const PRELOADED_QUESTIONS=[{"id":"pdf-conlaw-001","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Intergovernmental Immunity","question":"A state imposes a tax on the \"income\" of each of its residents. As defined in the taxing statute, \"income\" includes the fair rental value of the use of any automobile provided by the taxpayer's employer for the taxpayer's personal use. The federal government supplies automobiles to some of its employees who are residents in the state so that they may perform their jobs properly. A federal government employee supplied with an automobile for this purpose may also use it for the employee's own personal business. Assume there is no federal legislation on this subject. May the state collect this tax on the fair rental value of the personal use of the automobiles furnished by the federal government to these employees?","choices":{"A":"No, because such a tax would be a tax on the United States.","B":"No, because such a tax would be a tax upon activities performed on behalf of the United States, since the automobiles are primarily used by these federal employees in the discharge of their official duties.","C":"Yes, because an exemption from such state taxes for federal employees would be a denial to others of the equal protection of the laws.","D":"Yes, because the tax is imposed on the employees rather than on the United States, and the tax does not discriminate against persons who are employed by the United States."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations\n\nTax/regulation Definition Permissibility\n\nDirect Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities\n• Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal\n• Congress grants immunity or government\n• tax/regulation discriminates against or substantially interferes with federal duties Under the Article VI supremacy clause, states are prohibited from taxing the federal government—including its agencies and instrumentalities—without congressional consent. But states can tax the federal government's affiliates—including federal employees (as seen here)—unless:\n• Congress has granted the affiliate immunity\n• the tax discriminates against the federal government or its affiliates or\n• the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's ability to accomplish its federal purpose or duties. Here, the state seeks to collect an income tax on the value of the personal use of employer- provided automobiles from federal employees (not the U.S. government) (Choice A). Congress did not grant these employees immunity, and the tax is nondiscriminatory since it applies to automobiles provided by any employer. Additionally, the tax does not substantially interfere with the employees' federal duties since it only applies to their personal use of the automobiles. Therefore, the state may collect this tax from the federal employees. (Choice B) Although the automobiles may primarily be used by these federal employees in the discharge of their official duties, the tax only applies to personal use of the automobiles. Therefore, it is not a tax upon activities performed on behalf of the United States. (Choice C) Under the equal protection clause, state actions that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny. State action almost always satisfies this standard, so a tax exemption for federal employees would likely be sustained under this clause.\n\nEducational objective: States can tax the affiliates of the federal government unless (1) Congress has granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.\n\nReferences\n• Graves v. New York, 306 U.S. 466, 486–87 (1939) (holding that a state income tax imposed on federal employees is not an unconstitutional burden).","educationalObjective":"States can tax the affiliates of the federal government unless (1) Congress has granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","references":"• Graves v. New York, 306 U.S. 466, 486–87 (1939) (holding that a state income tax imposed on federal employees is not an unconstitutional burden).","rule":"States can tax the affiliates of the federal government unless (1) Congress has granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","irac":{"issue":"May the state collect this tax on the fair rental value of the personal use of the automobiles furnished by the federal government to these employees?","rule":"States can tax the affiliates of the federal government unless (1) Congress has granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","analysis":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations\n\nTax/regulation Definition Permissibility\n\nDirect Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities\n• Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal\n• Congress grants immunity or government\n• tax/regulation discriminates against or substantially interferes with federal duties Under the Article VI supremacy clause, states are prohibited from taxing the federal government—including its agencies and instrumentalities—without congressional consent. But states can tax the federal government's affiliates—including federal employees (as seen here)—unless:\n• Congress has granted the affiliate immunity\n• the tax discriminates against the federal government or its affiliates or\n• the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's ability to accomplish its federal purpose or duties. Here, the state seeks to collect an income tax on the value of the personal use of employer- provided automobiles from federal employees (not the U.S. government) (Choice A). Congress did not grant these employees immunity, and the tax is nondiscriminatory since it applies to automobiles provided by any employer. Additionally, the tax does not substantially interfere with the employees' federal duties since it only applies to their personal use of the automobiles. Therefore, the state may collect this tax from the federal employees. (Choice B) Although the automobiles may primarily be used by these federal employees in the discharge of their official duties, the tax only applies to personal use of the automobiles. Therefore, it is not a tax upon activities performed on behalf of the United States. (Choice C) Under the equal protection clause, state actions that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny. State action almost always satisfies this standard, so a tax exemption for federal employees would likely be sustained under this clause.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the tax is imposed on the employees rather than on the United States, and the tax does not discriminate against persons who are employed by the United States."},"questionZh":"州对其每个居民的“收入”征税。根据税法的定义，“收入”包括纳税人雇主为纳税人个人使用提供的任何汽车的公平租金价值。联邦政府向该州居民的一些雇员提供汽车，以便他们能够正常工作。为此目的而提供汽车的联邦政府雇员也可以将其用于雇员自己的个人业务。假设没有关于此主题的联邦立法。州可以对联邦政府向这些雇员提供的个人使用汽车的公平租金价值征收这项税吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这样的税是对美国征税。","B":"不，因为这种税是对代表美国进行的活动征税，因为汽车主要由这些联邦雇员在履行公务时使用。","C":"是的，因为免除联邦雇员的州税将剥夺其他人受到法律的平等保护。","D":"是的，因为这个税是对雇员征收的，而不是对美国征收的，而且这个税收并不歧视受雇于美国的人。"},"explanationZh":"联邦免受州和地方税收/法规的影响\n\n税收/法规 定义 许可\n\n直接适用于联邦政府——豁免，除非： 包括其机构和部门\n• 国会同意税收/监管 间接 适用于附属机构——包括 不豁免，除非： 受雇于联邦或与联邦有业务往来的个人/实体\n• 国会授予豁免权或政府\n• 税收/监管歧视或严重干扰联邦职责 根据第六条至上条款，未经国会同意，各州不得向联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）征税。但各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构——包括联邦雇员（如此处所示）——征税，除非：\n• 国会已授予附属机构豁免权\n• 该税收歧视联邦政府或其附属机构，或\n• 税收严重干扰附属机构实现其联邦目的或职责的能力。在此，州政府寻求对联邦雇员（而非美国政府）提供的雇主提供的汽车的个人使用价值征收所得税（选择 A）。国会没有授予这些雇员豁免权，而且该税是非歧视性的，因为它适用于任何雇主提供的汽车。此外，该税不会实质上干扰雇员的联邦义务，因为它仅适用于他们个人使用汽车。因此，州可以向联邦雇员征收这项税。 （选择 B）虽然汽车主要由这些联邦雇员在履行公务时使用，但该税仅适用于汽车的个人使用。因此，它不是对代表美国进行的活动征税。 （选项C）根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），不会对基本权利产生实质性影响或故意歧视受保护群体（如此处所示）的国家行为仅受到合理基础的审查。州政府的行动几乎总是满足这一标准，因此根据该条款，联邦雇员的免税可能会得到维持。\n\n教育目标：各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构征税，除非 (1) 国会授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。\n\n参考文献\n• Graves v. New York, 306 U.S. 466, 486–87 (1939)（认为对联邦雇员征收的州所得税不是违宪负担）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构征税，除非 (1) 国会已授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。","iracZh":{"issue":"州可以对联邦政府向这些雇员提供的个人使用汽车的公平租金价值征收这项税吗？","rule":"各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构征税，除非 (1) 国会已授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。","analysis":"联邦免受州和地方税收/法规的影响\n\n税收/法规 定义 许可\n\n直接适用于联邦政府——豁免，除非： 包括其机构和部门\n• 国会同意税收/监管 间接 适用于附属机构——包括 不豁免，除非： 受雇于联邦或与联邦有业务往来的个人/实体\n• 国会授予豁免权或政府\n• 税收/监管歧视或严重干扰联邦职责 根据第六条至上条款，未经国会同意，各州不得向联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）征税。但各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构——包括联邦雇员（如此处所示）——征税，除非：\n• 国会已授予附属机构豁免权\n• 该税收歧视联邦政府或其附属机构，或\n• 税收严重干扰附属机构实现其联邦目的或职责的能力。在此，州政府寻求对联邦雇员（而非美国政府）提供的雇主提供的汽车的个人使用价值征收所得税（选择 A）。国会没有授予这些雇员豁免权，而且该税是非歧视性的，因为它适用于任何雇主提供的汽车。此外，该税不会实质上干扰雇员的联邦义务，因为它仅适用于他们个人使用汽车。因此，州可以向联邦雇员征收这项税。 （选择 B）虽然汽车主要由这些联邦雇员在履行公务时使用，但该税仅适用于汽车的个人使用。因此，它不是对代表美国进行的活动征税。 （选项C）根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），不会对基本权利产生实质性影响或故意歧视受保护群体（如此处所示）的国家行为仅受到合理基础的审查。州政府的行动几乎总是满足这一标准，因此根据该条款，联邦雇员的免税可能会得到维持。\n\n教育目标：各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构征税，除非 (1) 国会授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。\n\n参考文献\n• Graves v. New York, 306 U.S. 466, 486–87 (1939)（认为对联邦雇员征收的州所得税不是违宪负担）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为这个税是对雇员征收的，而不是对美国征收的，而且这个税收并不歧视受雇于美国的人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"rational basis scrutiny","ipa":"/ɹˈæʃənəl bˈeɪsəs skɹˈutəni/","syllables":"ra·tio·nal ba·sis scrutiny","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合理基础审查：措施只需与合法政府利益合理相关","contextualMeaning":"合理基础审查：措施只需与合法政府利益合理相关","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Under the equal protection clause, state actions that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny.","synonyms":["rational basis examination"]},{"term":"supremacy clause","ipa":"/sʌpɹˈɛməsi klˈɔz/","syllables":"su·premacy clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"最高性条款：联邦宪法、法律与条约优先于冲突的州法","contextualMeaning":"最高性条款：联邦宪法、法律与条约优先于冲突的州法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations Tax/regulation Definition Permissibility Direct Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities • Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal • Congress grants immunity or government • tax/regulation discriminates","synonyms":["supremacy article"]},{"term":"equal protection clause","ipa":"/ˈikwəl pɹʌtˈɛkʃən klˈɔz/","syllables":"equal pro·tec·tion clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"平等保护条款：要求州对处境相同者给予平等法律保护","contextualMeaning":"平等保护条款：要求州对处境相同者给予平等法律保护","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Under the equal protection clause, state actions that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny.","synonyms":["equal protection article"]},{"term":"state action","ipa":"/stˈeɪt ˈækʃən/","syllables":"state ac·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"国家行为：可归责于政府、从而触发宪法限制的行为","contextualMeaning":"国家行为：可归责于政府、从而触发宪法限制的行为","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Under the equal protection clause, state actions that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny.","synonyms":["state military action","state legal action","state action at law"]},{"term":"fundamental right","ipa":"/fˌʌndʌmˈɛntəl ɹˈaɪt/","syllables":"fun·da·men·tal right","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"基本权利：触发更严格宪法审查的重要自由","contextualMeaning":"基本权利：触发更严格宪法审查的重要自由","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Under the equal protection clause, state actions that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny.","synonyms":["fundamental right field","fundamental rightfield","fundamental rightfulness"]},{"term":"instrumentalities","ipa":"/ˌɪnstɹʌmɛntˈælɪti/","syllables":"in·stru·men·tal·i·ties","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 手段( instrumentality的名词复数 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 手段( instrumentality的名词复数 )","wordForm":"词；原形 instrumentality；词形 0:instrumentality/1:s","sourceSentence":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations Tax/regulation Definition Permissibility Direct Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities • Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal • Congress grants immunity or government • tax/regulation discriminates","synonyms":["instrumentation"]},{"term":"discriminatory","ipa":"/dɪskɹˈɪmʌnʌtˌɔɹi/","syllables":"dis·crim·i·na·tory","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 有辨识力的, 有差别的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 有辨识力的, 有差别的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"States can tax the affiliates of the federal government unless (1) Congress has granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","synonyms":["preferential","prejudiced","discriminative","invidious"]},{"term":"congressional","ipa":"/kʌnɡɹˈɛʃənəl/","syllables":"con·gres·sional","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 会议的, 议会的, 国会的；[法] 代表大会的, 大会的, 议会的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 会议的, 议会的, 国会的；[法] 代表大会的, 大会的, 议会的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations Tax/regulation Definition Permissibility Direct Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities • Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal • Congress grants immunity or government • tax/regulation discriminates","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"legislation","ipa":"/lˌɛdʒʌslˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"leg·is·la·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 立法, 法律；[医] 立法, 法规","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 立法, 法律；[医] 立法, 法规","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:legislations","sourceSentence":"Assume there is no federal legislation on this subject.","synonyms":["legislating","lawmaking","statute law"]},{"term":"regulation","ipa":"/ɹˌɛɡjʌlˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"reg·u·la·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 规则, 管理, 调整；[计] 调整； 规章","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 规则, 管理, 调整；[计] 调整； 规章","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:regulations","sourceSentence":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations Tax/regulation Definition Permissibility Direct Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities • Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal • Congress grants immunity or government • tax/regulation discriminates","synonyms":["regulating","regularization","regularisation","rule"]},{"term":"residents","ipa":"/ɹˈɛzɪdənts/","syllables":"res·i·dents","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 居民；房客；住院医生（resident的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 居民；房客；住院医生（resident的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 resident；词形 0:resident/1:s","sourceSentence":"A state imposes a tax on the \"income\" of each of its residents.","synonyms":["house physician","resident physician","occupant","occupier"]},{"term":"congress","ipa":"/kˈɑŋɡɹəs/","syllables":"con·gress","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 国会, 会议, 讨论会；[医] 会议, 会合","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 国会, 会议, 讨论会；[医] 会议, 会合","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:congresses","sourceSentence":"States can tax the affiliates of the federal government unless (1) Congress has granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","synonyms":["meeting","coming together","United States Congress","U.S. Congress"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":1,"sourceQuestionPage":1,"sourceQuestionPages":[1],"sourceExplanationPages":[2,3],"sourceAnswerPages":[2,3],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-002","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federalism & Anti-Commandeering","question":"A federal statute authorizes a federal agency to issue rules requiring that state legislatures adopt laws of limited duration to reduce water pollution from gasoline-powered boat motors. The purpose of these rules is to assist the agency in attaining the clean water standards required by the statute. After the agency issued such rules, several states filed an action challenging the rules on the sole ground that they are unconstitutional. Should the court uphold the constitutionality of the agency's rules?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal government may not compel a state legislature to enact into state law a federally mandated regulatory program.","B":"No, because the Tenth Amendment grants states immunity from all direct federal regulation.","C":"Yes, because the rules serve an important purpose, and the requirements they impose on the states are only temporary and do not excessively interfere with the functioning of the state governments.","D":"Yes, because the supremacy clause of Article VI requires states to enforce federal law."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Federalism\n\n(power distribution between federal & state governments)\n\nConstitutional Effect provision\n\nArticle VI\n• Prioritizes U.S. Constitution, treaties & federal laws over state supremacy clause constitutions & laws\n• Prohibits states from regulating/taxing federal government or its agencies/instrumentalities Tenth Amendment\n• Reserves all powers not assigned to federal government by U.S. Constitution to states/people\n• Prohibits federal government from requiring states to enact laws or enforce federal laws\n• Immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions The principles of federalism—ie, the power distribution between the federal and state governments—are embodied in the supremacy clause and the Tenth Amendment. The supremacy clause places federal law (including agency rules) above state law. But the Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government and its agencies from requiring states to enact laws or to enforce federal laws. Therefore, the federal agency rules are unconstitutional because they require a state legislature to enact a federal regulatory program into state law. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment only grants states immunity from federal regulation that compels them to enact state laws or enforce federal ones. But states still must comply with other types of direct federal regulation (eg, EPA pollution standards). (Choice C) The federal government cannot require states to enact state laws or enforce federal laws—even if it would serve an important purpose or impose a minimal burden on the states. (Choice D) Under the supremacy clause, federal law preempts conflicting state law. But this clause does not require states to enforce federal law. And the Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from imposing such a requirement.\n\nEducational objective: The Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from requiring states to (1) enact state laws or (2) enforce federal laws.\n\nReferences\n• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992) (holding that Congress cannot commandeer the legislative process of the states by directly compelling them to enact and enforce a federal regulatory program).","educationalObjective":"The Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from requiring states to (1) enact state laws or (2) enforce federal laws.","references":"• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992) (holding that Congress cannot commandeer the legislative process of the states by directly compelling them to enact and enforce a federal regulatory program).","rule":"The Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from requiring states to (1) enact state laws or (2) enforce federal laws.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court uphold the constitutionality of the agency's rules?","rule":"The Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from requiring states to (1) enact state laws or (2) enforce federal laws.","analysis":"Federalism\n\n(power distribution between federal & state governments)\n\nConstitutional Effect provision\n\nArticle VI\n• Prioritizes U.S. Constitution, treaties & federal laws over state supremacy clause constitutions & laws\n• Prohibits states from regulating/taxing federal government or its agencies/instrumentalities Tenth Amendment\n• Reserves all powers not assigned to federal government by U.S. Constitution to states/people\n• Prohibits federal government from requiring states to enact laws or enforce federal laws\n• Immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions The principles of federalism—ie, the power distribution between the federal and state governments—are embodied in the supremacy clause and the Tenth Amendment. The supremacy clause places federal law (including agency rules) above state law. But the Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government and its agencies from requiring states to enact laws or to enforce federal laws. Therefore, the federal agency rules are unconstitutional because they require a state legislature to enact a federal regulatory program into state law. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment only grants states immunity from federal regulation that compels them to enact state laws or enforce federal ones. But states still must comply with other types of direct federal regulation (eg, EPA pollution standards). (Choice C) The federal government cannot require states to enact state laws or enforce federal laws—even if it would serve an important purpose or impose a minimal burden on the states. (Choice D) Under the supremacy clause, federal law preempts conflicting state law. But this clause does not require states to enforce federal law. And the Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from imposing such a requirement.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the federal government may not compel a state legislature to enact into state law a federally mandated regulatory program."},"questionZh":"一项联邦法规授权联邦机构颁布规则，要求州立法机构通过限期法律，以减少汽油动力船用发动机造成的水污染。这些规则的目的是协助该机构达到法规要求的清洁水标准。该机构发布此类规则后，几个州提起诉讼，质疑这些规则，理由只是这些规则违宪。法院是否应该维护该机构规则的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为联邦政府可能不会强迫州立法机构将联邦政府授权的监管计划纳入州法律。","B":"不，因为第十修正案赋予各州不受所有直接联邦监管的豁免权。","C":"是的，因为这些规则有一个重要的目的，它们对各州提出的要求只是暂时的，不会过度干扰各州政府的运作。","D":"是的，因为第六条的至上条款要求各州执行联邦法律。"},"explanationZh":"联邦制\n\n（联邦和州政府之间的权力分配）\n\n宪法效力条款\n\n第六条\n• 优先考虑美国宪法、条约和联邦法律，而不是州至上条款宪法和法律\n• 禁止各州对联邦政府或其机构/机构进行监管/征税 第十修正案\n• 将美国宪法未授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州/人民\n• 禁止联邦政府要求各州颁布法律或执行联邦法律\n• 使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干涉国家基本职能的联邦税。 联邦制原则，即联邦政府与州政府之间的权力分配，体现在至上条款和第十修正案中。至上条款将联邦法律（包括机构规则）置于州法律之上。但第十修正案禁止联邦政府及其机构要求各州制定法律或执行联邦法律。因此，联邦机构规则是违宪的，因为它们要求州立法机关将联邦监管计划纳入州法律。 （选项 B）第十修正案仅授予各州免受联邦法规的约束，这些法规迫使它们制定州法律或执行联邦法律。但各州仍然必须遵守其他类型的直接联邦法规（例如，EPA 污染标准）。 （选项 C）联邦政府不能要求各州颁布州法律或执行联邦法律，即使这会起到重要作用或给各州带来最小的负担。 （选项 D）根据至上条款，联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律。但该条款并不要求各州执行联邦法律。第十修正案禁止联邦政府提出这样的要求。\n\n教育目标：第十修正案禁止联邦政府要求各州 (1) 颁布州法律或 (2) 执行联邦法律。\n\n参考文献\n• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992)（认为国会不能通过直接迫使各州颁布和执行联邦监管计划来支配各州的立法程序）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十修正案禁止联邦政府要求各州 (1) 颁布州法律或 (2) 执行联邦法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该维护该机构规则的合宪性？","rule":"第十修正案禁止联邦政府要求各州 (1) 颁布州法律或 (2) 执行联邦法律。","analysis":"联邦制\n\n（联邦和州政府之间的权力分配）\n\n宪法效力条款\n\n第六条\n• 优先考虑美国宪法、条约和联邦法律，而不是州至上条款宪法和法律\n• 禁止各州对联邦政府或其机构/机构进行监管/征税 第十修正案\n• 将美国宪法未授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州/人民\n• 禁止联邦政府要求各州颁布法律或执行联邦法律\n• 使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干涉国家基本职能的联邦税。 联邦制原则，即联邦政府与州政府之间的权力分配，体现在至上条款和第十修正案中。至上条款将联邦法律（包括机构规则）置于州法律之上。但第十修正案禁止联邦政府及其机构要求各州制定法律或执行联邦法律。因此，联邦机构规则是违宪的，因为它们要求州立法机关将联邦监管计划纳入州法律。 （选项 B）第十修正案仅授予各州免受联邦法规的约束，这些法规迫使它们制定州法律或执行联邦法律。但各州仍然必须遵守其他类型的直接联邦法规（例如，EPA 污染标准）。 （选项 C）联邦政府不能要求各州颁布州法律或执行联邦法律，即使这会起到重要作用或给各州带来最小的负担。 （选项 D）根据至上条款，联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律。但该条款并不要求各州执行联邦法律。第十修正案禁止联邦政府提出这样的要求。\n\n教育目标：第十修正案禁止联邦政府要求各州 (1) 颁布州法律或 (2) 执行联邦法律。\n\n参考文献\n• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992)（认为国会不能通过直接迫使各州颁布和执行联邦监管计划来支配各州的立法程序）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为联邦政府可能不会强迫州立法机构将联邦政府授权的监管计划纳入州法律。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"tenth amendment","ipa":"/tˈɛnθ ʌmˈɛndmənt/","syllables":"tenth amend·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"第十修正案：未授予联邦且未禁止州行使的权力保留给州或人民","contextualMeaning":"第十修正案：未授予联邦且未禁止州行使的权力保留给州或人民","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the Tenth Amendment grants states immunity from all direct federal regulation.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"constitutionality","ipa":"/kˌɑnstɪtˌuʃʌnˈælɪti/","syllables":"con·sti·tu·tion·al·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 合宪法性；[法] 符合宪法, 合宪性, 立宪","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 合宪法性；[法] 符合宪法, 合宪性, 立宪","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Should the court uphold the constitutionality of the agency's rules?","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"federalism","ipa":"/fˈɛdɝʌlˌɪzəm/","syllables":"fed·er·al·ism","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 联邦制, 联邦主义；[法] 联邦制","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 联邦制, 联邦主义；[法] 联邦制","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Federalism (power distribution between federal & state governments) Constitutional Effect provision Article VI • Prioritizes U.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"principles","ipa":"/pɹˈɪnsəpəlz/","syllables":"prin·ci·ples","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 原则, 原理, 道义, 节操","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 原则, 原理, 道义, 节操","wordForm":"词；原形 principle；词形 0:principle/1:s","sourceSentence":"Constitution to states/people • Prohibits federal government from requiring states to enact laws or enforce federal laws • Immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions The principles of federalism—ie, the power distribution between the federal and state governments—are embodied in the supremacy clause and the Tenth Amendment.","synonyms":["rationale","rule","precept"]},{"term":"federally","ipa":"/fˈɛdɝəli/","syllables":"fed·er·ally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 同盟地, 联邦地, 联邦政府地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 同盟地, 联邦地, 联邦政府地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"No, because the federal government may not compel a state legislature to enact into state law a federally mandated regulatory program.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"immunizes","ipa":"/ˈɪmjʌnˌaɪzɪz/","syllables":"im·mu·nizes","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 使某人免疫( immunize的名词复数 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 使某人免疫( immunize的名词复数 )","wordForm":"词；原形 immunize；词形 1:3/0:immunize","sourceSentence":"Constitution to states/people • Prohibits federal government from requiring states to enact laws or enforce federal laws • Immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions The principles of federalism—ie, the power distribution between the federal and state governments—are embodied in the supremacy clause and the Tenth Amendment.","synonyms":["immunise","inoculate","vaccinate"]},{"term":"treaties","ipa":"/tɹˈitiz/","syllables":"treaties","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 条约；协定问题（treaty的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 条约；协定问题（treaty的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 treaty；词形 0:treaty/1:s","sourceSentence":"Constitution, treaties & federal laws over state supremacy clause constitutions & laws • Prohibits states from regulating/taxing federal government or its agencies/instrumentalities Tenth Amendment • Reserves all powers not assigned to federal government by U.","synonyms":["pact","accord"]},{"term":"agency","ipa":"/ˈeɪdʒənsi/","syllables":"agency","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 代理机构, 经销商, 中介；[化] 办事处","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 代理机构, 经销商, 中介；[化] 办事处","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:agencies","sourceSentence":"A federal statute authorizes a federal agency to issue rules requiring that state legislatures adopt laws of limited duration to reduce water pollution from gasoline-powered boat motors.","synonyms":["means","way","federal agency","government agency"]},{"term":"preempts","ipa":"/pɹˈiˌɛmpts/","syllables":"pre·empts","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 先占( preempt的第三人称单数 )； 取代； 先取","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 先占( preempt的第三人称单数 )； 取代； 先取","wordForm":"词；原形 preempt；词形 0:preempt/1:3","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) Under the supremacy clause, federal law preempts conflicting state law.","synonyms":["preemptive bid","pre-empt","displace"]},{"term":"compel","ipa":"/kʌmpˈɛl/","syllables":"com·pel","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 强迫, 迫使","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 强迫, 迫使","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:compelled/i:compelling/p:compelled/3:compels/s:compels","sourceSentence":"No, because the federal government may not compel a state legislature to enact into state law a federally mandated regulatory program.","synonyms":["oblige","obligate"]},{"term":"grants","ipa":"/ɡɹˈænts/","syllables":"grants","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[经] 补助金, 拨款, 津贴","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[经] 补助金, 拨款, 津贴","wordForm":"词；原形 grant；词形 0:grant/1:s3","sourceSentence":"No, because the Tenth Amendment grants states immunity from all direct federal regulation.","synonyms":["subsidization","subsidisation","concession","Ulysses Grant"]},{"term":"taxes","ipa":"/tˈæksəz/","syllables":"taxes","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 税务；税捐；税收, 税金","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 税务；税捐；税收, 税金","wordForm":"词；原形 tax；词形 1:s3/0:tax","sourceSentence":"Constitution to states/people • Prohibits federal government from requiring states to enact laws or enforce federal laws • Immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions The principles of federalism—ie, the power distribution between the federal and state governments—are embodied in the supremacy clause and the Tenth Amendment.","synonyms":["taxation","revenue enhancement","task","assess"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":2,"sourceQuestionPage":4,"sourceQuestionPages":[4],"sourceExplanationPages":[5,6],"sourceAnswerPages":[5,6],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-003","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Import-Export Clause","question":"A state statute requires corporations located in the state to pay an annual property tax based on the value of their inventory that is stored there. A tire distributor that imports tires manufactured in foreign countries and stores them at warehouses located in the state has sued to challenge the state tax, arguing that it is unconstitutional. Is the state tax constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the import-export clause prohibits state taxes on imported goods.","B":"No, because the state tax discriminates against foreign commerce.","C":"Yes, because states have absolute authority to tax goods within their borders.","D":"Yes, because the state tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The import-export clause generally prohibits state taxation of imported goods unless Congress consents or the tax is absolutely necessary for the state to execute its inspection laws. Additionally, a state tax on imported goods is valid if:\n• the federal government is still able to speak with one voice in foreign commerce\n• there is interstate harmony because the tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements and\n• the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government. Here, the state tax does not impair the federal government's ability to speak with one voice because the tax applies after the goods have gone through the import process. This means that the federal government may still tax the goods as they are imported. The tax also does not disturb interstate harmony because it:\n• applies to inventory that is stored at the distributor's warehouses in the state (substantial nexus)\n• is based on a rational formula that only taxes the value of the inventory in the state (fairly apportioned)\n• applies equally to imported and domestic goods (nondiscriminatory) (Choice B) and\n• is reasonable compared to services and benefits provided by the state (fairly related). Finally, the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government since the tax applies after the goods have been imported. It merely compensates the state for goods stored in the state. Therefore, the state tax is constitutional (Choice A). (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including the power to generally tax goods within their borders. But this power is not absolute because states must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, the import-export clause).\n\nEducational objective: A state tax on imported goods is valid if (1) the federal government is still able to speak with one voice in foreign commerce, (2) there is interstate harmony because the tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements, and (3) the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. 1, § 10, cl. 2 (state import-export clause).\n\n• Itel Containers Int'l Corp. v. Huddleston, 507 U.S. 60, 76–78 (1993) (setting forth the components of the modern import-export clause test).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 74 (explaining when a state tax violates the import-export clause).","educationalObjective":"A state tax on imported goods is valid if (1) the federal government is still able to speak with one voice in foreign commerce, (2) there is interstate harmony because the tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements, and (3) the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. 1, § 10, cl. 2 (state import-export clause).\n\n• Itel Containers Int'l Corp. v. Huddleston, 507 U.S. 60, 76–78 (1993) (setting forth the components of the modern import-export clause test).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 74 (explaining when a state tax violates the import-export clause).","rule":"A state tax on imported goods is valid if (1) the federal government is still able to speak with one voice in foreign commerce, (2) there is interstate harmony because the tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements, and (3) the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state tax constitutional?","rule":"A state tax on imported goods is valid if (1) the federal government is still able to speak with one voice in foreign commerce, (2) there is interstate harmony because the tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements, and (3) the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government.","analysis":"The import-export clause generally prohibits state taxation of imported goods unless Congress consents or the tax is absolutely necessary for the state to execute its inspection laws. Additionally, a state tax on imported goods is valid if:\n• the federal government is still able to speak with one voice in foreign commerce\n• there is interstate harmony because the tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements and\n• the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government. Here, the state tax does not impair the federal government's ability to speak with one voice because the tax applies after the goods have gone through the import process. This means that the federal government may still tax the goods as they are imported. The tax also does not disturb interstate harmony because it:\n• applies to inventory that is stored at the distributor's warehouses in the state (substantial nexus)\n• is based on a rational formula that only taxes the value of the inventory in the state (fairly apportioned)\n• applies equally to imported and domestic goods (nondiscriminatory) (Choice B) and\n• is reasonable compared to services and benefits provided by the state (fairly related). Finally, the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government since the tax applies after the goods have been imported. It merely compensates the state for goods stored in the state. Therefore, the state tax is constitutional (Choice A). (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including the power to generally tax goods within their borders. But this power is not absolute because states must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, the import-export clause).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the state tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government."},"questionZh":"州法规要求位于该州的公司根据存储在该州的库存价值缴纳年度财产税。一家进口外国制造的轮胎并将其储存在该州仓库的轮胎经销商提起诉讼，质疑州税，认为这是违宪的。州税符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为进出口条款禁止国家对进口货物征税。","B":"不，因为州税歧视外国商业。","C":"是的，因为各州拥有对其境内商品征税的绝对权力。","D":"是的，因为州税不会转移联邦政府的进口收入。"},"explanationZh":"进出口条款一般禁止国家对进口货物征税，除非国会同意或该税对于国家执行其检验法是绝对必要的。此外，在以下情况下，对进口货物征收州税是有效的：\n• 联邦政府在对外贸易中仍然能够统一发言权\n• 州际和谐，因为税收满足Complete Auto 要件，并且\n• 该税收不会挪用联邦政府的进口收入。在这里，州税并不会损害联邦政府的统一发言的能力，因为该税是在货物完成进口流程后征收的。这意味着联邦政府仍可能对进口货物征税。该税收也不会扰乱州际和谐，因为它：\n• 适用于存储在该州经销商仓库的库存（实质性联系）\n• 基于合理的公式，仅对州内的库存价值征税（公平分配）\n• 同样适用于进口和国内商品（非歧视）（选择 B）并且\n• 与州提供的服务和福利相比是合理的（公平相关）。最后，该税不会转移联邦政府的进口收入，因为该税在货物进口后征收。它只是对州内储存的货物进行补偿。因此，州税符合宪法（选项A）。 （选项 C）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括对其境内的商品普遍征税的权力。但这种权力并不是绝对的，因为各州必须遵守其他宪法规定（例如进出口条款）。\n\n教育目标：如果满足以下条件，对进口商品征收的州税是有效的：(1) 联邦政府仍然能够在对外贸易中统一发言权；(2) 由于税收满足Complete Auto 要件，因此州际和谐；(3) 该税收不会挪用联邦政府的进口收入。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 1，§ 10，cl。 2（注明进出口条款）。\n\n• Itel Containers Int'l Corp. v. Huddleston, 507 U.S. 60, 76–78 (1993)（阐述了现代进出口条款测试的组成部分）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 74（解释州税何时违反进出口条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果满足以下条件，则对进口商品征收的州税是有效的：(1) 联邦政府仍然能够在对外贸易中统一发言权；(2) 由于该税满足Complete Auto 要件，因此州际和谐；(3) 该税不会挪用联邦政府的进口收入。","iracZh":{"issue":"州税符合宪法吗？","rule":"如果满足以下条件，则对进口商品征收的州税是有效的：(1) 联邦政府仍然能够在对外贸易中统一发言权；(2) 由于该税满足Complete Auto 要件，因此州际和谐；(3) 该税不会挪用联邦政府的进口收入。","analysis":"进出口条款一般禁止国家对进口货物征税，除非国会同意或该税对于国家执行其检验法是绝对必要的。此外，在以下情况下，对进口货物征收州税是有效的：\n• 联邦政府在对外贸易中仍然能够统一发言权\n• 州际和谐，因为税收满足Complete Auto 要件，并且\n• 该税收不会挪用联邦政府的进口收入。在这里，州税并不会损害联邦政府的统一发言的能力，因为该税是在货物完成进口流程后征收的。这意味着联邦政府仍可能对进口货物征税。该税收也不会扰乱州际和谐，因为它：\n• 适用于存储在该州经销商仓库的库存（实质性联系）\n• 基于合理的公式，仅对州内的库存价值征税（公平分配）\n• 同样适用于进口和国内商品（非歧视）（选择 B）并且\n• 与州提供的服务和福利相比是合理的（公平相关）。最后，该税不会转移联邦政府的进口收入，因为该税在货物进口后征收。它只是对州内储存的货物进行补偿。因此，州税符合宪法（选项A）。 （选项 C）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括对其境内的商品普遍征税的权力。但这种权力并不是绝对的，因为各州必须遵守其他宪法规定（例如进出口条款）。\n\n教育目标：如果满足以下条件，对进口商品征收的州税是有效的：(1) 联邦政府仍然能够在对外贸易中统一发言权；(2) 由于税收满足Complete Auto 要件，因此州际和谐；(3) 该税收不会挪用联邦政府的进口收入。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 1，§ 10，cl。 2（注明进出口条款）。\n\n• Itel Containers Int'l Corp. v. Huddleston, 507 U.S. 60, 76–78 (1993)（阐述了现代进出口条款测试的组成部分）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 74（解释州税何时违反进出口条款）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为州税不会转移联邦政府的进口收入。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"import-export clause","ipa":"/'impɔ:t'ekspɔ:t klˈɔz/","syllables":"im·port-·ex·port clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"进出口条款：原则上禁止州对进出口货物课税","contextualMeaning":"进出口条款：原则上禁止州对进出口货物课税","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the import-export clause prohibits state taxes on imported goods.","synonyms":["import-export article"]},{"term":"substantial nexus","ipa":"/sʌbstˈænʃəl nˈɛksəs/","syllables":"sub·stan·tial nexus","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实质联系：州与被征税主体、财产或活动之间的充分联系","contextualMeaning":"实质联系：州与被征税主体、财产或活动之间的充分联系","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The tax also does not disturb interstate harmony because it: • applies to inventory that is stored at the distributor's warehouses in the state (substantial nexus) • is based on a rational formula that only taxes the value of the inventory in the state (fairly apportioned) • applies equally to imported and domestic goods (nondiscriminatory) (Choice B) and • is reasonable compared to services and benefits provided by ","synonyms":["substantial link"]},{"term":"fairly apportioned","ipa":"/fˈɛɹli ʌpˈɔɹʃənd/","syllables":"fairly ap·por·tioned","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公平分配：税负按与该州相关的适当份额计算","contextualMeaning":"公平分配：税负按与该州相关的适当份额计算","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The tax also does not disturb interstate harmony because it: • applies to inventory that is stored at the distributor's warehouses in the state (substantial nexus) • is based on a rational formula that only taxes the value of the inventory in the state (fairly apportioned) • applies equally to imported and domestic goods (nondiscriminatory) (Choice B) and • is reasonable compared to services and benefits provided by ","synonyms":["fairly dealt out","fairly doled out","fairly meted out"]},{"term":"corporations","ipa":"/kˌɔɹpɝˈeɪʃənz/","syllables":"cor·po·ra·tions","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 公司, 企业（corporation的复数形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 公司, 企业（corporation的复数形式）","wordForm":"词；原形 corporation；词形 0:corporation/1:s","sourceSentence":"A state statute requires corporations located in the state to pay an annual property tax based on the value of their inventory that is stored there.","synonyms":["pot","potbelly","bay window","tummy"]},{"term":"compensates","ipa":"/kˈɑmpʌnsˌeɪts/","syllables":"com·pen·sates","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 补偿, 报酬( compensate的第三人称单数 )； 给（某人）赔偿（或赔款）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 补偿, 报酬( compensate的第三人称单数 )； 给（某人）赔偿（或赔款）","wordForm":"词；原形 compensate；词形 0:compensate/1:3","sourceSentence":"It merely compensates the state for goods stored in the state.","synonyms":["recompense","remunerate","repair","indemnify"]},{"term":"interstate","ipa":"/ˌɪntɝstˈeɪt/","syllables":"in·ter·state","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 州际的；[法] 国与国之间的, 州际的, 州与州之间的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 州际的；[法] 国与国之间的, 州际的, 州与州之间的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:interstates","sourceSentence":"A state tax on imported goods is valid if (1) the federal government is still able to speak with one voice in foreign commerce, (2) there is interstate harmony because the tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements, and (3) the tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government.","synonyms":["interstate highway"]},{"term":"property","ipa":"/pɹˈɑpɝti/","syllables":"prop·erty","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 财产, 所有权, 性质, 属性；[计] 属性","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 财产, 所有权, 性质, 属性；[计] 属性","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:properties","sourceSentence":"A state statute requires corporations located in the state to pay an annual property tax based on the value of their inventory that is stored there.","synonyms":["prop","attribute","dimension","place"]},{"term":"import","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈɔɹt/","syllables":"im·port","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 进口货, 进口, 输入, 含义, 重要性；vt. 输入, 引入, 进口, 含...的意思, 重要；vi. 有关系","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 进口货, 进口, 输入, 含义, 重要性；vt. 输入, 引入, 进口, 含...的意思, 重要；vi. 有关系","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:imports/d:imported/i:importing/p:imported/3:imports","sourceSentence":"A tire distributor that imports tires manufactured in foreign countries and stores them at warehouses located in the state has sued to challenge the state tax, arguing that it is unconstitutional.","synonyms":["importation","consequence","moment","significance"]},{"term":"commerce","ipa":"/kˈɑmɝs/","syllables":"com·merce","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 商业, 商务, 贸易；[经] 商业, 贸易, 商务","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 商业, 商务, 贸易；[经] 商业, 贸易, 商务","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"No, because the state tax discriminates against foreign commerce.","synonyms":["commercialism","mercantilism","Department of Commerce","Commerce Department"]},{"term":"revenue","ipa":"/ɹˈɛvʌnˌu/","syllables":"rev·enue","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 收入, 岁入, 税收, 税务局；[经] 岁入, 税收, 税务局","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 收入, 岁入, 税收, 税务局；[经] 岁入, 税收, 税务局","wordForm":"词；词形 s:revenues","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the state tax does not divert import revenue from the federal government.","synonyms":["gross","receipts","tax income","taxation"]},{"term":"sued","ipa":"/sˈud/","syllables":"sued","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 要求, 请求（sue过去分词形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 要求, 请求（sue过去分词形式）","wordForm":"词；原形 sue；词形 0:sue/1:dp","sourceSentence":"A tire distributor that imports tires manufactured in foreign countries and stores them at warehouses located in the state has sued to challenge the state tax, arguing that it is unconstitutional.","synonyms":["Eugene Sue","action","litigate","process"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0e956a3773619023"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":3,"sourceQuestionPage":7,"sourceQuestionPages":[7],"sourceExplanationPages":[8,9],"sourceAnswerPages":[8,9],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-004","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Legislative Immunity","question":"A state representative made a speech on the floor of the state legislature in which she asserted that a state senator had violated the legislature's code of ethics by having a sexual relationship with a staff member. In making this assertion, the representative played a recording of a phone conversation between the senator and staff member in which they admitted having such a relationship. The representative obtained the recording from an acquaintance, who had placed an illegal wiretap on the staff member's phone. One year later, the federal government initiated a prosecution against the state representative for violating a federal criminal statute that prohibits the intentional publication of communications that were unlawfully recorded. A person found guilty under the statute is subject to a one-year prison sentence and a monetary fine. The representative has moved to dismiss the action. Is the court likely to dismiss the action?","choices":{"A":"No.","B":"Yes, because state legislators are absolutely immune from liability for actions taken pursuant to their official legislative functions.","C":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment bars actions of this kind in federal court.","D":"Yes, because the speech or debate clause wholly insulates state legislators from civil and criminal liability for legislative activity."},"answer":"A","explanation":"State legislative immunity\n\nAbsolute immunity Legislative acts that are subject of:\n\n• state civil or criminal action\n• federal civil action No immunity Legislative acts that are subject of:\n\n• federal criminal action State legislators generally possess absolute immunity from state civil or criminal liability for actions taken pursuant to official legislative functions (eg, making speeches in the legislature).* In the context of federal civil and criminal liability, the Supreme Court has held that a state legislator's absolute immunity:\n• extends to federal civil liability because the federal government's interest in ensuring private civil actions is outweighed by the substantial impact such actions have on state legislative functions but\n\n• does not extend to federal criminal liability because the federal government's important interest in enforcing criminal statutes outweighs the minimal impact prosecutions have on state legislative functions.\n\nAs a result, the state representative is not immune from federal prosecution for violating the federal statute, and the court will likely not dismiss the action (Choice B). *State legislative immunity derives from the common law. Today, most states have codified legislative immunity in their constitutions or statutes. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state or state official for money damages in federal court. However, this amendment does not apply to actions initiated by another state or the United States (as seen here). (Choice D) The speech or debate clause of the U.S. Constitution wholly insulates members of Congress, but not state legislators, from civil and criminal liability for any official legislative activity.\n\nEducational objective: A state legislator has absolute immunity from federal civil liability for official legislative actions. However, this immunity does not extend to federal criminal liability.\n\nReferences\n• Tenney v. Brandhove, 341 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1951) (holding that state legislators are immune from civil liability for acts performed in the regular course of the legislative process).\n\n• United States v. Gillock, 445 U.S. 360, 373 (1980) (stating that a state legislator is subject to criminal prosecution for a violation of federal law).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d States, Territories, and Dependencies § 62 (2020) (explaining state legislative immunity).","educationalObjective":"A state legislator has absolute immunity from federal civil liability for official legislative actions. However, this immunity does not extend to federal criminal liability.","references":"• Tenney v. Brandhove, 341 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1951) (holding that state legislators are immune from civil liability for acts performed in the regular course of the legislative process).\n\n• United States v. Gillock, 445 U.S. 360, 373 (1980) (stating that a state legislator is subject to criminal prosecution for a violation of federal law).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d States, Territories, and Dependencies § 62 (2020) (explaining state legislative immunity).","rule":"A state legislator has absolute immunity from federal civil liability for official legislative actions. However, this immunity does not extend to federal criminal liability.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to dismiss the action?","rule":"A state legislator has absolute immunity from federal civil liability for official legislative actions. However, this immunity does not extend to federal criminal liability.","analysis":"State legislative immunity\n\nAbsolute immunity Legislative acts that are subject of:\n\n• state civil or criminal action\n• federal civil action No immunity Legislative acts that are subject of:\n\n• federal criminal action State legislators generally possess absolute immunity from state civil or criminal liability for actions taken pursuant to official legislative functions (eg, making speeches in the legislature).* In the context of federal civil and criminal liability, the Supreme Court has held that a state legislator's absolute immunity:\n• extends to federal civil liability because the federal government's interest in ensuring private civil actions is outweighed by the substantial impact such actions have on state legislative functions but\n\n• does not extend to federal criminal liability because the federal government's important interest in enforcing criminal statutes outweighs the minimal impact prosecutions have on state legislative functions.\n\nAs a result, the state representative is not immune from federal prosecution for violating the federal statute, and the court will likely not dismiss the action (Choice B). *State legislative immunity derives from the common law. Today, most states have codified legislative immunity in their constitutions or statutes. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state or state official for money damages in federal court. However, this amendment does not apply to actions initiated by another state or the United States (as seen here). (Choice D) The speech or debate clause of the U.S. Constitution wholly insulates members of Congress, but not state legislators, from civil and criminal liability for any official legislative activity.","conclusion":"Answer A: No."},"questionZh":"一名州代表在州立法机关发表讲话，声称一名州参议员与一名工作人员发生性关系，违反了立法机关的道德准则。在做出这一断言时，该代表播放了参议员和工作人员之间的电话交谈录音，其中他们承认有这样的关系。该代表从一位熟人那里获得了录音，该熟人非法窃听了该工作人员的电话。一年后，联邦政府对州代表提起诉讼，指控其违反了联邦刑事法规，该法规禁止故意发布非法记录的通信。根据该法规被判有罪的人将被判处一年监禁和罚款。该代表已提出驳回该诉讼的动议。法院有可能驳回诉讼吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不。","B":"是的，因为州立法者绝对不承担根据其官方立法职能采取的行动的责任。","C":"是的，因为第十一条修正案禁止在联邦法院采取此类行动。","D":"是的，因为演讲或辩论条款完全使州立法者免受立法活动的民事和刑事责任。"},"explanationZh":"国家立法豁免权\n\n绝对豁免权 受以下法律约束的立法行为：\n\n• 国家民事或刑事诉讼\n• 联邦民事诉讼 无豁免权 立法行为涉及：\n\n• 联邦刑事诉讼 对于根据官方立法职能（例如，在立法机关发表演讲）采取的行动，州立法者通常拥有绝对的州民事或刑事责任豁免权。*在联邦民事和刑事责任的背景下，最高法院认为州立法者的绝对豁免权：\n• 延伸至联邦民事责任，因为联邦政府确保私人民事诉讼的利益超过了此类行为对州立法职能的重大影响，但\n\n• 不延伸至联邦刑事责任，因为联邦政府在执行刑事法规方面的重要利益超过了起诉对州立法职能的最小影响。\n\n因此，州代表不能因违反联邦法规而免受联邦起诉，法院可能不会驳回该诉讼（选择 B）。 *国家立法豁免权源自普通法。如今，大多数州已将立法豁免权写入宪法或法规。 （选项 C）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉州或州官员要求金钱赔偿。但是，该修正案不适用于其他州或美国发起的行动（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）美国宪法的演讲或辩论条款完全使国会议员（但不是州立法者）免受任何官方立法活动的民事和刑事责任。\n\n教育目标：州立法者对官方立法行为享有绝对的联邦民事责任豁免权。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于联邦刑事责任。\n\n参考文献\n• Tenney v. Brandhove, 341 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1951)（认为州立法者对于在立法程序的正常过程中实施的行为免于承担民事责任）。\n\n• United States v. Gillock, 445 U.S. 360, 373 (1980)（规定州立法者因违反联邦法律而受到刑事起诉）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 州、领土和属地 § 62 (2020)（解释州立法豁免权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"州立法者对官方立法行为享有绝对的联邦民事责任豁免权。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于联邦刑事责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院有可能驳回诉讼吗？","rule":"州立法者对官方立法行为享有绝对的联邦民事责任豁免权。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于联邦刑事责任。","analysis":"国家立法豁免权\n\n绝对豁免权 受以下法律约束的立法行为：\n\n• 国家民事或刑事诉讼\n• 联邦民事诉讼 无豁免权 立法行为涉及：\n\n• 联邦刑事诉讼 对于根据官方立法职能（例如，在立法机关发表演讲）采取的行动，州立法者通常拥有绝对的州民事或刑事责任豁免权。*在联邦民事和刑事责任的背景下，最高法院认为州立法者的绝对豁免权：\n• 延伸至联邦民事责任，因为联邦政府确保私人民事诉讼的利益超过了此类行为对州立法职能的重大影响，但\n\n• 不延伸至联邦刑事责任，因为联邦政府在执行刑事法规方面的重要利益超过了起诉对州立法职能的最小影响。\n\n因此，州代表不能因违反联邦法规而免受联邦起诉，法院可能不会驳回该诉讼（选择 B）。 *国家立法豁免权源自普通法。如今，大多数州已将立法豁免权写入宪法或法规。 （选项 C）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉州或州官员要求金钱赔偿。但是，该修正案不适用于其他州或美国发起的行动（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）美国宪法的演讲或辩论条款完全使国会议员（但不是州立法者）免受任何官方立法活动的民事和刑事责任。\n\n教育目标：州立法者对官方立法行为享有绝对的联邦民事责任豁免权。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于联邦刑事责任。\n\n参考文献\n• Tenney v. Brandhove, 341 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1951)（认为州立法者对于在立法程序的正常过程中实施的行为免于承担民事责任）。\n\n• United States v. Gillock, 445 U.S. 360, 373 (1980)（规定州立法者因违反联邦法律而受到刑事起诉）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 州、领土和属地 § 62 (2020)（解释州立法豁免权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"speech or debate clause","ipa":"/spˈitʃ ˈɔɹ dʌbˈeɪt klˈɔz/","syllables":"speech or de·bate clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"言论或辩论条款：保护国会议员及其助手的立法行为免受诉讼","contextualMeaning":"言论或辩论条款：保护国会议员及其助手的立法行为免受诉讼","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the speech or debate clause wholly insulates state legislators from civil and criminal liability for legislative activity.","synonyms":["speech or debate article"]},{"term":"eleventh amendment","ipa":"/ɪlˈɛvənθ ʌmˈɛndmənt/","syllables":"eleventh amend·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"第十一修正案：限制私人在联邦法院起诉州","contextualMeaning":"第十一修正案：限制私人在联邦法院起诉州","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment bars actions of this kind in federal court.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"prosecution","ipa":"/pɹˌɑsʌkjˈuʃən/","syllables":"pros·e·cu·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 执行, 经营, 起诉","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 执行, 经营, 起诉","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:prosecutions","sourceSentence":"One year later, the federal government initiated a prosecution against the state representative for violating a federal criminal statute that prohibits the intentional publication of communications that were unlawfully recorded.","synonyms":["pursuance","criminal prosecution"]},{"term":"publication","ipa":"/pˌʌblɪkˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"pub·li·ca·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 出版物, 出版, 公布；[经] 发布","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 出版物, 出版, 公布；[经] 发布","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:publications","sourceSentence":"One year later, the federal government initiated a prosecution against the state representative for violating a federal criminal statute that prohibits the intentional publication of communications that were unlawfully recorded.","synonyms":["publishing","issue"]},{"term":"legislator","ipa":"/lˈɛdʒʌslˌeɪtɝ/","syllables":"leg·is·la·tor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 立法者, 立法官, 立法委员；[法] 立法者, 立法机关成员, 立法委员","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 立法者, 立法官, 立法委员；[法] 立法者, 立法机关成员, 立法委员","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:legislators","sourceSentence":"Yes, because state legislators are absolutely immune from liability for actions taken pursuant to their official legislative functions.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"unlawfully","ipa":"/ʌnlˈɔfəli/","syllables":"un·law·fully","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 不正当地；不合法地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 不正当地；不合法地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"One year later, the federal government initiated a prosecution against the state representative for violating a federal criminal statute that prohibits the intentional publication of communications that were unlawfully recorded.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"liability","ipa":"/lˌaɪʌbˈɪlɪti/","syllables":"li·a·bil·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 责任, 债务, 倾向；[经] 责任, 义务, 负债","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 责任, 债务, 倾向；[经] 责任, 义务, 负债","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:liabilities","sourceSentence":"Yes, because state legislators are absolutely immune from liability for actions taken pursuant to their official legislative functions.","synonyms":["indebtedness","financial obligation"]},{"term":"criminal","ipa":"/kɹˈɪmənəl/","syllables":"crim·i·nal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 罪犯, 犯人, 刑事；a. 犯了罪的, 刑事的, 有罪的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 罪犯, 犯人, 刑事；a. 犯了罪的, 刑事的, 有罪的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:criminals","sourceSentence":"One year later, the federal government initiated a prosecution against the state representative for violating a federal criminal statute that prohibits the intentional publication of communications that were unlawfully recorded.","synonyms":["felon","crook","outlaw","malefactor"]},{"term":"sentence","ipa":"/sˈɛntəns/","syllables":"sen·tence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 句子, 命题, 宣判；vt. 宣判, 判决；[计] 句子","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 句子, 命题, 宣判；vt. 宣判, 判决；[计] 句子","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:sentences/d:sentenced/p:sentenced/i:sentencing/3:sentences","sourceSentence":"A person found guilty under the statute is subject to a one-year prison sentence and a monetary fine.","synonyms":["conviction","judgment of conviction","condemnation","prison term"]},{"term":"illegal","ipa":"/ˌɪlˈiɡəl/","syllables":"il·le·gal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 违法的, 不合规定的；[经] 非法的, 犯规的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 违法的, 不合规定的；[经] 非法的, 犯规的","wordForm":"形容词；词形 s:illegals","sourceSentence":"The representative obtained the recording from an acquaintance, who had placed an illegal wiretap on the staff member's phone.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"dismiss","ipa":"/dɪsmˈɪs/","syllables":"dis·miss","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 解散, 开除, 解职；vi. 解散；[计] 解散","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 解散, 开除, 解职；vi. 解散；[计] 解散","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:dismissed/p:dismissed/i:dismissing/3:dismisses","sourceSentence":"The representative has moved to dismiss the action.","synonyms":["dissolve","disregard","brush aside","brush off"]},{"term":"guilty","ipa":"/ɡˈɪlti/","syllables":"guilty","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 犯罪的, 有过失的, 自觉有错的, 心虚的；[法] 有罪的, 犯罪的, 自觉有罪的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 犯罪的, 有过失的, 自觉有错的, 心虚的；[法] 有罪的, 犯罪的, 自觉有罪的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"A person found guilty under the statute is subject to a one-year prison sentence and a monetary fine.","synonyms":["hangdog","shamefaced","shamed"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":4,"sourceQuestionPage":10,"sourceQuestionPages":[10],"sourceExplanationPages":[11,12],"sourceAnswerPages":[11,12],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-005","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"A state law makes it a crime for an individual to intentionally provide false information on state tax forms when applying for employment. A federal statute prohibits employers from hiring individuals who provide false Social Security numbers when applying for employment. The statute's purpose is to prevent individuals from assuming a false identity to obtain employment. A provision of the federal statute states that the statute expressly preempts state laws imposing civil or criminal punishments on employers who hire individuals who provide fraudulent Social Security numbers. A man was charged in state court with violating the state law by intentionally providing a false Social Security number on his state tax withholding form when he applied for employment in the state. The man argued that the prosecution should not proceed because the federal statute preempts the state law. Assume that no other federal statute applies. How should the state court proceed with the case?","choices":{"A":"Determine whether the man intentionally provided false information on his state tax form and, if so, find him guilty of violating the state law.","B":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the case and dismiss it, because the federal statute expressly preempts the state law.","C":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the case and dismiss it, because the federal statute impliedly preempts the state law.","D":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the case and dismiss it, because the federal statute expressly and impliedly preempts the state law."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The supremacy clause renders a state law void and without effect when a federal law expressly or impliedly preempts it. Express preemption occurs when a federal law:\n• was enacted pursuant to a power exclusively reserved to the federal government by the U.S. Constitution (eg, power to declare war) or\n\n• explicitly prohibits state regulation.\n\nThe federal government shares power with state governments to regulate hiring practices within the respective state. And though the federal statute here expressly preempts state laws that punish employers, it does not mention applicants. As a result, the federal statute does not expressly preempt the state law (Choices B & D). The federal statute also does not impliedly preempt the state law. Implied preemption occurs through:\n• field preemption – when Congress intended to completely occupy a particular field by legislating so thoroughly that it has left no room for supplementary state regulations\n\n• direct conflict preemption – when it is impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both federal and state law or\n\n• indirect conflict preemption – when the state law frustrates the purpose of the federal law.\n\nHere, there is no field preemption since the federal statute punishes employers but leaves room for states to punish applicants. There is no direct conflict preemption because individuals can comply with both statutes. And there is no indirect conflict preemption since the state law furthers the federal statute's purpose of preventing individuals from assuming a false identity to obtain employment (Choices C & D). Therefore, the state court should adjudicate the merits to determine whether the man intentionally provided false information on his state tax form and, if so, find him guilty.\n\nEducational objective: Express preemption occurs when a federal law is enacted under an exclusive federal power or explicitly bars state regulation. Implied preemption occurs when there is no room for state regulation (field), it is nearly impossible to comply with both laws (direct conflict), or the state law frustrates the federal law's purpose (indirect conflict).\n\nReferences\n• Kansas v. Garcia, 140 S. Ct. 791, 804–07 (2020) (holding that a federal law regulating employment hiring practices does not expressly or impliedly preempt a state law criminalizing fraud when applying for employment).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 234 (2020) (explaining the tests used to determine preemption).","educationalObjective":"Express preemption occurs when a federal law is enacted under an exclusive federal power or explicitly bars state regulation. Implied preemption occurs when there is no room for state regulation (field), it is nearly impossible to comply with both laws (direct conflict), or the state law frustrates the federal law's purpose (indirect conflict).","references":"• Kansas v. Garcia, 140 S. Ct. 791, 804–07 (2020) (holding that a federal law regulating employment hiring practices does not expressly or impliedly preempt a state law criminalizing fraud when applying for employment).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 234 (2020) (explaining the tests used to determine preemption).","rule":"Express preemption occurs when a federal law is enacted under an exclusive federal power or explicitly bars state regulation. Implied preemption occurs when there is no room for state regulation (field), it is nearly impossible to comply with both laws (direct conflict), or the state law frustrates the federal law's purpose (indirect conflict).","irac":{"issue":"How should the state court proceed with the case?","rule":"Express preemption occurs when a federal law is enacted under an exclusive federal power or explicitly bars state regulation. Implied preemption occurs when there is no room for state regulation (field), it is nearly impossible to comply with both laws (direct conflict), or the state law frustrates the federal law's purpose (indirect conflict).","analysis":"The supremacy clause renders a state law void and without effect when a federal law expressly or impliedly preempts it. Express preemption occurs when a federal law:\n• was enacted pursuant to a power exclusively reserved to the federal government by the U.S. Constitution (eg, power to declare war) or\n\n• explicitly prohibits state regulation.\n\nThe federal government shares power with state governments to regulate hiring practices within the respective state. And though the federal statute here expressly preempts state laws that punish employers, it does not mention applicants. As a result, the federal statute does not expressly preempt the state law (Choices B & D). The federal statute also does not impliedly preempt the state law. Implied preemption occurs through:\n• field preemption – when Congress intended to completely occupy a particular field by legislating so thoroughly that it has left no room for supplementary state regulations\n\n• direct conflict preemption – when it is impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both federal and state law or\n\n• indirect conflict preemption – when the state law frustrates the purpose of the federal law.\n\nHere, there is no field preemption since the federal statute punishes employers but leaves room for states to punish applicants. There is no direct conflict preemption because individuals can comply with both statutes. And there is no indirect conflict preemption since the state law furthers the federal statute's purpose of preventing individuals from assuming a false identity to obtain employment (Choices C & D). Therefore, the state court should adjudicate the merits to determine whether the man intentionally provided false information on his state tax form and, if so, find him guilty.","conclusion":"Answer A: Determine whether the man intentionally provided false information on his state tax form and, if so, find him guilty of violating the state law."},"questionZh":"州法律规定，个人在申请就业时故意在州税表上提供虚假信息属于犯罪行为。联邦法规禁止雇主雇用在申请就业时提供虚假社会安全号码的个人。该法规的目的是防止个人冒充虚假身份以获得就业机会。联邦法规的一项条款规定，该法规明确优先于州法律对雇用提供欺诈性社会安全号码的个人实施民事或刑事处罚的雇主。一名男子在该州申请就业时故意在州税预扣表上提供虚假的社会安全号码，因此被州法院指控违反州法律。该男子辩称，不应继续起诉，因为联邦法规优先于州法律。假设没有其他联邦法规适用。州法院应如何审理此案？","choicesZh":{"A":"确定该男子是否故意在州税表上提供虚假信息，如果是，则判定他违反州法律。","B":"拒绝裁定案件的是非曲直并将其驳回，因为联邦法规明确优先于州法律。","C":"拒绝裁定案件的是非曲直并将其驳回，因为联邦法规隐含优先于州法律。","D":"拒绝裁定案件的是非曲直并将其驳回，因为联邦法规明示和暗示优先于州法律。"},"explanationZh":"当联邦法律明示或暗示优先于州法律时，至上条款使州法律无效且无效。当联邦法律出现以下情况时，就会发生明确优先购买权：\n• 根据美国宪法专门保留给联邦政府的权力（例如宣战权）颁布，或\n\n• 明确禁止国家监管。\n\n联邦政府与州政府共享权力，监管各自州内的招聘行为。尽管这里的联邦法规明确优先于惩罚雇主的州法律，但它没有提及申请人。因此，联邦法规并未明确优先于州法律（Choices B & D）。联邦法规也不默示优先于州法律。隐式抢占通过以下方式发生：\n• 领域先发制人——国会打算通过立法彻底占领某个特定领域，以至于没有留下补充性国家法规的空间\n\n• 直接冲突先发制人——当不可能或几乎不可能同时遵守联邦和州法律或\n\n• 间接冲突先发制人——当州法律阻碍联邦法律的目的时。\n\n在这里，不存在先发制人的情况，因为联邦法规惩罚雇主，但为各州惩罚申请人留下了空间。不存在直接的冲突先发制人，因为个人可以遵守这两项法规。而且不存在间接冲突先发制人，因为州法律进一步推进了联邦法规的目的，即防止个人冒充虚假身份获得就业（选择 C ​​和 D）。因此，州法院应该对案情进行裁决，以确定该男子是否故意在州税表上提供虚假信息，如果是，则判他有罪。\n\n教育目标：当联邦法律根据联邦专有权力颁布或明确禁止州监管时，就会发生明确的先发制人。当没有州监管空间（领域）、几乎不可能同时遵守这两项法律（直接冲突）或州法律阻碍了联邦法律的目的（间接冲突）时，就会发生默示先发制人。\n\n参考文献\n• 堪萨斯诉加西亚，140 S. Ct. 791, 804–07 (2020)（认为规范雇佣行为的联邦法律并不明示或暗示优先于将申请就业时的欺诈行为定为刑事犯罪的州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 234 条 (2020)（解释用于确定优先权的测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当联邦法律根据联邦专有权力颁布或明确禁止州监管时，就会发生明确的先发制人。当没有州监管空间（领域）、几乎不可能同时遵守这两项法律（直接冲突）或州法律阻碍了联邦法律的目的（间接冲突）时，就会发生默示先发制人。","iracZh":{"issue":"州法院应如何审理此案？","rule":"当联邦法律根据联邦专有权力颁布或明确禁止州监管时，就会发生明确的先发制人。当没有州监管空间（领域）、几乎不可能同时遵守这两项法律（直接冲突）或州法律阻碍了联邦法律的目的（间接冲突）时，就会发生默示先发制人。","analysis":"当联邦法律明示或暗示优先于州法律时，至上条款使州法律无效且无效。当联邦法律出现以下情况时，就会发生明确优先购买权：\n• 根据美国宪法专门保留给联邦政府的权力（例如宣战权）颁布，或\n\n• 明确禁止国家监管。\n\n联邦政府与州政府共享权力，监管各自州内的招聘行为。尽管这里的联邦法规明确优先于惩罚雇主的州法律，但它没有提及申请人。因此，联邦法规并未明确优先于州法律（Choices B & D）。联邦法规也不默示优先于州法律。隐式抢占通过以下方式发生：\n• 领域先发制人——国会打算通过立法彻底占领某个特定领域，以至于没有留下补充性国家法规的空间\n\n• 直接冲突先发制人——当不可能或几乎不可能同时遵守联邦和州法律或\n\n• 间接冲突先发制人——当州法律阻碍联邦法律的目的时。\n\n在这里，不存在先发制人的情况，因为联邦法规惩罚雇主，但为各州惩罚申请人留下了空间。不存在直接的冲突先发制人，因为个人可以遵守这两项法规。而且不存在间接冲突先发制人，因为州法律进一步推进了联邦法规的目的，即防止个人冒充虚假身份获得就业（选择 C ​​和 D）。因此，州法院应该对案情进行裁决，以确定该男子是否故意在州税表上提供虚假信息，如果是，则判他有罪。\n\n教育目标：当联邦法律根据联邦专有权力颁布或明确禁止州监管时，就会发生明确的先发制人。当没有州监管空间（领域）、几乎不可能同时遵守这两项法律（直接冲突）或州法律阻碍了联邦法律的目的（间接冲突）时，就会发生默示先发制人。\n\n参考文献\n• 堪萨斯诉加西亚，140 S. Ct. 791, 804–07 (2020)（认为规范雇佣行为的联邦法律并不明示或暗示优先于将申请就业时的欺诈行为定为刑事犯罪的州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 234 条 (2020)（解释用于确定优先权的测试）。","conclusion":"答案 A：确定该男子是否故意在州税表上提供虚假信息，如果是，则判定他违反州法律。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"field preemption","ipa":"/fˈild pɹˌiˈɛmpʃən/","syllables":"field pre·emp·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"领域优先：联邦规制密集到占据整个法律领域","contextualMeaning":"领域优先：联邦规制密集到占据整个法律领域","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Implied preemption occurs through: • field preemption – when Congress intended to completely occupy a particular field by legislating so thoroughly that it has left no room for supplementary state regulations • direct conflict preemption – when it is impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both federal and state law or • indirect conflict preemption – when the state law frustrates the purpose of the federal la","synonyms":["field pre-emption"]},{"term":"conflict preemption","ipa":"/kˈɑnflɪkt pɹˌiˈɛmpʃən/","syllables":"con·flict pre·emp·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"冲突优先：州法与联邦法无法同时遵守或妨碍联邦目的","contextualMeaning":"冲突优先：州法与联邦法无法同时遵守或妨碍联邦目的","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Implied preemption occurs through: • field preemption – when Congress intended to completely occupy a particular field by legislating so thoroughly that it has left no room for supplementary state regulations • direct conflict preemption – when it is impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both federal and state law or • indirect conflict preemption – when the state law frustrates the purpose of the federal la","synonyms":["conflict pre-emption"]},{"term":"adjudicate","ipa":"/ʌdʒˈudɪkˌeɪt/","syllables":"ad·ju·di·cate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 判决, 宣告, 裁定","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 判决, 宣告, 裁定","wordForm":"词；词形 d:adjudicated/i:adjudicating/p:adjudicated/3:adjudicates/s:adjudicates","sourceSentence":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the case and dismiss it, because the federal statute expressly preempts the state law.","synonyms":["decide","settle","resolve","judge"]},{"term":"charged","ipa":"/tʃˈɑɹdʒd/","syllables":"charged","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 充满强烈感情的, 带电的, 气氛紧张的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 充满强烈感情的, 带电的, 气氛紧张的","wordForm":"形容词；原形 charge；词形 0:charge/1:dp/d:charged/p:charged","sourceSentence":"A man was charged in state court with violating the state law by intentionally providing a false Social Security number on his state tax withholding form when he applied for employment in the state.","synonyms":["mission","commission","care","tutelage"]},{"term":"proceed","ipa":"/pɹʌsˈid/","syllables":"pro·ceed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vi. 继续进行, 进行, 开始, 发出, 起诉；[法] 所得, 收入, 收益","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vi. 继续进行, 进行, 开始, 发出, 起诉；[法] 所得, 收入, 收益","wordForm":"动词；词形 s:proceeds/p:proceeded/i:proceeding/3:proceeds/d:proceeded","sourceSentence":"The man argued that the prosecution should not proceed because the federal statute preempts the state law.","synonyms":["go","continue","go on","carry on"]},{"term":"enacted","ipa":"/ɛnˈæktəd/","syllables":"en·acted","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 制定（法律）, 通过（法案）( enact的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 制定（法律）, 通过（法案）( enact的过去式和过去分词 )","wordForm":"词；原形 enact；词形 0:enact/1:dp","sourceSentence":"Express preemption occurs when a federal law is enacted under an exclusive federal power or explicitly bars state regulation.","synonyms":["reenact","act out","ordain"]},{"term":"crime","ipa":"/kɹˈaɪm/","syllables":"crime","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 犯罪, 罪行, 罪恶；[法] 犯罪, 罪, 罪恶","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 犯罪, 罪行, 罪恶；[法] 犯罪, 罪, 罪恶","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:crimes","sourceSentence":"A state law makes it a crime for an individual to intentionally provide false information on state tax forms when applying for employment.","synonyms":["criminal offense","criminal offence","law-breaking"]},{"term":"civil","ipa":"/sˈɪvəl/","syllables":"civil","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 市民的, 公民的, 有礼貌的；[法] 公民的, 国民的, 民用的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 市民的, 公民的, 有礼貌的；[法] 公民的, 国民的, 民用的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"A provision of the federal statute states that the statute expressly preempts state laws imposing civil or criminal punishments on employers who hire individuals who provide fraudulent Social Security numbers.","synonyms":["polite","civic"]},{"term":"case","ipa":"/kˈeɪs/","syllables":"case","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 情形, 情况, 箱, 容器, 事实, 病例, 案例, 框子；vt. 装箱, 包盖","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 情形, 情况, 箱, 容器, 事实, 病例, 案例, 框子；vt. 装箱, 包盖","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:cases/i:casing/p:cased/3:cases/d:cased","sourceSentence":"How should the state court proceed with the case?","synonyms":["lawsuit","suit","cause","causa"]},{"term":"bars","ipa":"/bˈɑɹz/","syllables":"bars","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 马齿龈；（木、金属等的）条；栅栏","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 马齿龈；（木、金属等的）条；栅栏","wordForm":"词；词形 0:bar/1:s3","sourceSentence":"Express preemption occurs when a federal law is enacted under an exclusive federal power or explicitly bars state regulation.","synonyms":["parallel bars"]},{"term":"reserved","ipa":"/ɹɪzˈɝvd/","syllables":"re·served","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 保留的, 预备的, 预定的, 腼腆的, 不露感情的, 含蓄的, 缄默的, 冷淡的；[法] 用作储备的, 保留的, 预定的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 保留的, 预备的, 预定的, 腼腆的, 不露感情的, 含蓄的, 缄默的, 冷淡的；[法] 用作储备的, 保留的, 预定的","wordForm":"形容词；原形 reserve；词形 0:reserve/1:dp/d:reserved/p:reserved","sourceSentence":"Express preemption occurs when a federal law: • was enacted pursuant to a power exclusively reserved to the federal government by the U.","synonyms":["reticence","taciturnity","modesty","military reserve"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-94e33cea9d282c70"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":5,"sourceQuestionPage":13,"sourceQuestionPages":[13],"sourceExplanationPages":[14,15],"sourceAnswerPages":[14,15],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-006","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A county in State A is located adjacent to the border of State B. The communities located in the county are principally suburbs of a large city located in State B, and therefore there is a large volume of traffic between that city and the county. While most of that traffic is by private passenger automobiles, some of it is by taxicabs and other kinds of commercial vehicles. A county ordinance provides that only taxicabs registered in the county may pick up or discharge passengers in the county. The stated purpose of this ordinance is to reduce traffic congestion. The ordinance also provides that only residents of the county may register taxicabs in that county. Which of the following is the proper result in a suit brought by State B taxicab owners challenging the constitutionality of this county ordinance?","choices":{"A":"Judgment for the county, because State B taxicab owners do not constitute a suspect or quasi-suspect class and the ordinance is reasonably related to the legitimate governmental purpose of reducing traffic congestion.","B":"Judgment for the county, because the ordinance forbids taxicabs registered in other counties of State A as well as in states other than State A to operate in the county and, therefore, it does not discriminate against interstate commerce.","C":"Judgment for the State B taxicab owners, because the fact that private passenger automobiles contribute more to the traffic congestion problem in the county than do taxicabs indicates that the ordinance is not a reasonable means by which to solve that problem.","D":"Judgment for the State B taxicab owners, because the ordinance unduly burdens interstate commerce by insulating the county taxicab owners from out-of-state competition without adequate justification."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The ability of state and local governments to regulate interstate commerce is limited by the dormant commerce clause. Under this clause, these entities are generally prohibited from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. As a result, a state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce—ie, an action that protects in-state over out-of-state economic interests—is invalid unless:\n• it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and\n• no reasonable alternative is available to achieve this interest. Here, the State A county ordinance discriminates against interstate commerce since it only allows county residents to register and operate taxicabs in the county—thereby insulating them from out-of-state competition. The ordinance furthers the county's legitimate, noneconomic interest in reducing traffic congestion. But reasonable alternatives are available (eg, investing in public transportation). Therefore, the ordinance unduly burdens interstate commerce without adequate justification, and judgment for the State B taxicab owners is proper. (Choice A) Taxicab owners are not a suspect or quasi-suspect class (ie, protected class), so the ordinance likely satisfies the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause under mere rational basis scrutiny. Nevertheless, the ordinance is invalid because it violates the dormant commerce clause. (Choice B) The ordinance's unlawful discrimination against interstate commerce is not negated by the fact that it also discriminates against residents of other State A counties.\n\n(Choice C) Although private passenger automobiles contribute more to traffic congestion in the county than taxicabs, the ordinance is a reasonable means to solve that problem since it alleviates some congestion. But it is still invalid because it discriminates against interstate commerce despite the availability of reasonable alternatives.\n\nEducational objective: A state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce violates the dormant commerce clause unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and (2) no reasonable alternatives exist.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Dean Milk Co. v. Madison, 340 U.S. 349, 354–55 (1951) (holding that a city's ban on the sale of milk processed outside city limits discriminates against interstate commerce and violates the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 105 (2019) (explaining the proper standard of review for a law that discriminates against interstate commerce).","educationalObjective":"A state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce violates the dormant commerce clause unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and (2) no reasonable alternatives exist.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Dean Milk Co. v. Madison, 340 U.S. 349, 354–55 (1951) (holding that a city's ban on the sale of milk processed outside city limits discriminates against interstate commerce and violates the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 105 (2019) (explaining the proper standard of review for a law that discriminates against interstate commerce).","rule":"A state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce violates the dormant commerce clause unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and (2) no reasonable alternatives exist.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the proper result in a suit brought by State B taxicab owners challenging the constitutionality of this county ordinance?","rule":"A state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce violates the dormant commerce clause unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and (2) no reasonable alternatives exist.","analysis":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The ability of state and local governments to regulate interstate commerce is limited by the dormant commerce clause. Under this clause, these entities are generally prohibited from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. As a result, a state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce—ie, an action that protects in-state over out-of-state economic interests—is invalid unless:\n• it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and\n• no reasonable alternative is available to achieve this interest. Here, the State A county ordinance discriminates against interstate commerce since it only allows county residents to register and operate taxicabs in the county—thereby insulating them from out-of-state competition. The ordinance furthers the county's legitimate, noneconomic interest in reducing traffic congestion. But reasonable alternatives are available (eg, investing in public transportation). Therefore, the ordinance unduly burdens interstate commerce without adequate justification, and judgment for the State B taxicab owners is proper. (Choice A) Taxicab owners are not a suspect or quasi-suspect class (ie, protected class), so the ordinance likely satisfies the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause under mere rational basis scrutiny. Nevertheless, the ordinance is invalid because it violates the dormant commerce clause. (Choice B) The ordinance's unlawful discrimination against interstate commerce is not negated by the fact that it also discriminates against residents of other State A counties.\n\n(Choice C) Although private passenger automobiles contribute more to traffic congestion in the county than taxicabs, the ordinance is a reasonable means to solve that problem since it alleviates some congestion. But it is still invalid because it discriminates against interstate commerce despite the availability of reasonable alternatives.","conclusion":"Answer D: Judgment for the State B taxicab owners, because the ordinance unduly burdens interstate commerce by insulating the county taxicab owners from out-of-state competition without adequate justification."},"questionZh":"A 州的一个县毗邻 B 州的边界。该县的社区主要是 B 州某个大城市的郊区，因此该城市和县之间的交通量很大。虽然大部分交通是私人乘用车，但其中一些是出租车和其他类型的商用车辆。县法令规定，只有在该县注册的出租车才可以在县内接送乘客。该条例的既定目的是减少交通拥堵。该条例还规定，只有该县的居民才能在该县注册出租车。 B 州出租车车主提起诉讼质疑该县法令的合宪性时，以下哪一项是正确结果？","choicesZh":{"A":"县的判决是，因为 B 州出租车车主不构成嫌疑或准嫌疑类别，并且该法令与减少交通拥堵的合法政府目的合理相关。","B":"县的判决，因为该法令禁止在 A 州其他县以及 A 州以外的州注册的出租车在该县运营，因此，它不存在对州际商业的歧视。","C":"B州出租车车主的判断是，因为私人客运汽车比出租车对县内交通拥堵问题的贡献更大，这一事实表明该条例不是解决该问题的合理手段。","D":"B 州出租车车主的判决，因为该法令在没有充分理由的情况下使县出租车车主免受州外竞争，从而过度加重了州际贸易的负担。"},"explanationZh":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（负担过重）超过了当地所有商业运输利益 州和地方政府监管州际商业的能力受到消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制。根据本条款，这些实体通常被禁止歧视州际贸易或以其他方式对州际贸易造成不当负担。因此，歧视州际商业的州或地方行为（即保护州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益的行为）无效，除非：\n• 它促进合法的、非经济的国家或地方利益，并且\n• 没有合理的替代方案可以实现这一利益。在这里，A 州县法令歧视州际商业，因为它只允许县居民在县内注册和经营出租车，从而使他们免受州外竞争。该法令进一步促进了该县在减少交通拥堵方面的合法非经济利益。但还有合理的替代方案（例如，投资公共交通）。因此，该法令在没有充分理由的情况下过度加重了州际贸易的负担，对 B 州出租车车主的判断是正确的。 （选项A）出租车车主不是嫌疑人或准嫌疑人（即受保护群体），因此，仅在合理基础上进行审查，该条例很可能满足第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。尽管如此，该法令仍然无效，因为它违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。 （选项 B） 该法令对州际商业的非法歧视并不能因为它还歧视 A 州其他县的居民而被否定。\n\n（选项 C） 虽然私人客运汽车比出租车对县内的交通拥堵造成的影响更大，但该条例是解决该问题的合理手段，因为它缓解了一些拥堵。但它仍然无效，因为尽管存在合理的替代方案，但它仍然歧视州际贸易。\n\n教育目标：州或地方歧视州际商业的行为违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的州或地方利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Dean Milk Co. v. Madison, 340 U.S. 349, 354–55 (1951)（认为一个城市禁止销售在城市范围外加工的牛奶是对州际贸易的歧视，并违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 105 (2019)（解释歧视州际商业的法律的适当审查标准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"州或地方歧视州际商业的行为违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的州或地方利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。","iracZh":{"issue":"B 州出租车车主提起诉讼质疑该县法令的合宪性时，以下哪一项是正确结果？","rule":"州或地方歧视州际商业的行为违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的州或地方利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。","analysis":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（负担过重）超过了当地所有商业运输利益 州和地方政府监管州际商业的能力受到消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制。根据本条款，这些实体通常被禁止歧视州际贸易或以其他方式对州际贸易造成不当负担。因此，歧视州际商业的州或地方行为（即保护州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益的行为）无效，除非：\n• 它促进合法的、非经济的国家或地方利益，并且\n• 没有合理的替代方案可以实现这一利益。在这里，A 州县法令歧视州际商业，因为它只允许县居民在县内注册和经营出租车，从而使他们免受州外竞争。该法令进一步促进了该县在减少交通拥堵方面的合法非经济利益。但还有合理的替代方案（例如，投资公共交通）。因此，该法令在没有充分理由的情况下过度加重了州际贸易的负担，对 B 州出租车车主的判断是正确的。 （选项A）出租车车主不是嫌疑人或准嫌疑人（即受保护群体），因此，仅在合理基础上进行审查，该条例很可能满足第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。尽管如此，该法令仍然无效，因为它违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。 （选项 B） 该法令对州际商业的非法歧视并不能因为它还歧视 A 州其他县的居民而被否定。\n\n（选项 C） 虽然私人客运汽车比出租车对县内的交通拥堵造成的影响更大，但该条例是解决该问题的合理手段，因为它缓解了一些拥堵。但它仍然无效，因为尽管存在合理的替代方案，但它仍然歧视州际贸易。\n\n教育目标：州或地方歧视州际商业的行为违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的州或地方利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Dean Milk Co. v. Madison, 340 U.S. 349, 354–55 (1951)（认为一个城市禁止销售在城市范围外加工的牛奶是对州际贸易的歧视，并违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 105 (2019)（解释歧视州际商业的法律的适当审查标准）。","conclusion":"答案 D：B 州出租车车主的判决，因为该法令在没有充分理由的情况下使县出租车车主免受州外竞争，从而过度加重了州际贸易的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"dormant commerce clause","ipa":"/dˈɔɹmənt kˈɑmɝs klˈɔz/","syllables":"dor·mant com·merce clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"消极商业条款：限制州歧视或不当负担州际商业","contextualMeaning":"消极商业条款：限制州歧视或不当负担州际商业","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce violates the dormant commerce clause unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and (2) no reasonable alternatives exist.","synonyms":["negative Commerce Clause","implied Commerce Clause limitation"]},{"term":"commerce clause","ipa":"/kˈɑmɝs klˈɔz/","syllables":"com·merce clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"商业条款：授权国会规制州际、对外及与印第安部落的商业","contextualMeaning":"商业条款：授权国会规制州际、对外及与印第安部落的商业","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce violates the dormant commerce clause unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and (2) no reasonable alternatives exist.","synonyms":["commerce article"]},{"term":"fourteenth amendment","ipa":"/fˈɔɹtˈinθ ʌmˈɛndmənt/","syllables":"four·teenth amend·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"第十四修正案：规定公民权、正当程序和平等保护等保障","contextualMeaning":"第十四修正案：规定公民权、正当程序和平等保护等保障","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Taxicab owners are not a suspect or quasi-suspect class (ie, protected class), so the ordinance likely satisfies the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause under mere rational basis scrutiny.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"constitute","ipa":"/kˈɑnstʌtˌut/","syllables":"con·sti·tute","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 构成, 组成, 任命；[建] 构造, 组成","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 构成, 组成, 任命；[建] 构造, 组成","wordForm":"动词；词形 3:constitutes/p:constituted/d:constituted/i:constituting","sourceSentence":"Judgment for the county, because State B taxicab owners do not constitute a suspect or quasi-suspect class and the ordinance is reasonably related to the legitimate governmental purpose of reducing traffic congestion.","synonyms":["establish","found","plant","institute"]},{"term":"burdensome","ipa":"/bˈɝdənsəm/","syllables":"bur·den·some","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 累赘的, 恼人的, 繁重的；[法] 难于负担的, 压抑的, 累赘的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 累赘的, 恼人的, 繁重的；[法] 难于负担的, 压抑的, 累赘的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Dormant commerce clause (state regulation of interstate commerce) Type of regulation Examples Standard Discriminatory • Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state • furthers legitimate • Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services • no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory • Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state high","synonyms":["onerous","taxing"]},{"term":"ordinance","ipa":"/ˈɔɹdənəns/","syllables":"or·di·nance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 法令, 条例, 圣餐礼；[经] 法令, 条款, 条例","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 法令, 条例, 圣餐礼；[经] 法令, 条款, 条例","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:ordinances","sourceSentence":"A county ordinance provides that only taxicabs registered in the county may pick up or discharge passengers in the county.","synonyms":["ordination","regulation"]},{"term":"burden","ipa":"/bˈɝdən/","syllables":"bur·den","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 负担, 重载, 担子, 责任；vt. 装货于, 烦扰, 使负担","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 负担, 重载, 担子, 责任；vt. 装货于, 烦扰, 使负担","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:burdens/d:burdened/i:burdening/p:burdened/3:burdens","sourceSentence":"Judgment for the State B taxicab owners, because the ordinance unduly burdens interstate commerce by insulating the county taxicab owners from out-of-state competition without adequate justification.","synonyms":["load","loading","encumbrance","incumbrance"]},{"term":"taxicab","ipa":"/tˈæksikˌæb/","syllables":"taxi·cab","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 出租车","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 出租车","wordForm":"词；词形 s:taxicabs","sourceSentence":"While most of that traffic is by private passenger automobiles, some of it is by taxicabs and other kinds of commercial vehicles.","synonyms":["cab","hack","taxi"]},{"term":"judgment","ipa":"/dʒˈədʒmənt/","syllables":"judg·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 裁判, 宣告, 判决书；[医] 判断","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 裁判, 宣告, 判决书；[医] 判断","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:judgments","sourceSentence":"Judgment for the county, because State B taxicab owners do not constitute a suspect or quasi-suspect class and the ordinance is reasonably related to the legitimate governmental purpose of reducing traffic congestion.","synonyms":["judgement","assessment","judicial decision","sound judgment"]},{"term":"unlawful","ipa":"/ʌnlˈɔfəl/","syllables":"un·law·ful","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 非法的, 不正当的；[法] 不法, 不法的, 非法的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 非法的, 不正当的；[法] 不法, 不法的, 非法的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) The ordinance's unlawful discrimination against interstate commerce is not negated by the fact that it also discriminates against residents of other State A counties.","synonyms":["illegitimate","illicit","outlaw","outlawed"]},{"term":"suspect","ipa":"/sʌspˈɛkt/","syllables":"sus·pect","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 被怀疑者, 嫌疑犯；a. 令人怀疑的, 不可信的, 可疑的；v. 怀疑, 猜想","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 被怀疑者, 嫌疑犯；a. 令人怀疑的, 不可信的, 可疑的；v. 怀疑, 猜想","wordForm":"名词；词形 p:suspected/d:suspected/s:suspects/3:suspects/i:suspecting","sourceSentence":"Judgment for the county, because State B taxicab owners do not constitute a suspect or quasi-suspect class and the ordinance is reasonably related to the legitimate governmental purpose of reducing traffic congestion.","synonyms":["defendant","distrust","mistrust","surmise"]},{"term":"invalid","ipa":"/ˈɪnvələd/","syllables":"in·valid","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 病人, 残废者；a. 有病的, 无效的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 病人, 残废者；a. 有病的, 无效的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:invalids/i:invaliding/p:invalided/3:invalids/d:invalided","sourceSentence":"As a result, a state or local action that discriminates against interstate commerce—ie, an action that protects in-state over out-of-state economic interests—is invalid unless: • it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state or local interest and • no reasonable alternative is available to achieve this interest.","synonyms":["shut-in","disable","incapacitate","handicap"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":6,"sourceQuestionPage":16,"sourceQuestionPages":[16],"sourceExplanationPages":[17,18],"sourceAnswerPages":[17,18],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-007","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A federal statute established a national lottery and created a new federal agency to administer it. In order to sell lottery tickets directly to the public, the agency established outlets throughout the country. Some of these outlets are within a state that levies a general tax on the gross receipts from all in-state gaming operations. The state seeks to tax the agency on the sales of lottery tickets from its outlets within the state. Must the agency pay the state gross receipts tax?","choices":{"A":"No, because a federal agency may not pay any monies to a state without explicit federal statutory authorization to do so.","B":"No, because a state may not impose a tax directly on the federal government or any of its agencies or instrumentalities.","C":"Yes, because it is a generally applicable tax that does not discriminate against the agency.","D":"Yes, because the federal government is not exempt from state taxation on its commercial activities."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the supremacy clause, the federal government is immune (ie, exempt) from any direct taxation by the states—including taxes on its commercial activities (Choice D). As a result, states may not impose taxes on the federal government—or any of its agencies or instrumentalities—without the express consent of Congress. Here, the state levies a general tax on the gross receipts (ie, total income) from all gaming operations within the state. And the state seeks to impose that tax on a new federal agency created to administer a national lottery. But since the agency is immune from direct state taxation, it is not required to pay the gross receipts tax. (Choice A) A federal agency can pay monies to a state without explicit statutory authorization if Congress has granted the agency broad discretion to spend its funds. But even if a federal agency has such discretion, a state cannot force the agency to pay a tax. (Choice C) A state can never impose a direct tax on a federal agency without Congress's explicit consent—even if that tax is generally applicable and therefore does not discriminate against the agency (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The federal government, its agencies, and its instrumentalities are immune from direct taxation by the states unless Congress expressly consents.\n\nReferences\n• McCullough v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819) (striking down a state tax imposed on the Bank of the United States).","educationalObjective":"The federal government, its agencies, and its instrumentalities are immune from direct taxation by the states unless Congress expressly consents.","references":"• McCullough v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819) (striking down a state tax imposed on the Bank of the United States).","rule":"The federal government, its agencies, and its instrumentalities are immune from direct taxation by the states unless Congress expressly consents.","irac":{"issue":"Must the agency pay the state gross receipts tax?","rule":"The federal government, its agencies, and its instrumentalities are immune from direct taxation by the states unless Congress expressly consents.","analysis":"Under the supremacy clause, the federal government is immune (ie, exempt) from any direct taxation by the states—including taxes on its commercial activities (Choice D). As a result, states may not impose taxes on the federal government—or any of its agencies or instrumentalities—without the express consent of Congress. Here, the state levies a general tax on the gross receipts (ie, total income) from all gaming operations within the state. And the state seeks to impose that tax on a new federal agency created to administer a national lottery. But since the agency is immune from direct state taxation, it is not required to pay the gross receipts tax. (Choice A) A federal agency can pay monies to a state without explicit statutory authorization if Congress has granted the agency broad discretion to spend its funds. But even if a federal agency has such discretion, a state cannot force the agency to pay a tax. (Choice C) A state can never impose a direct tax on a federal agency without Congress's explicit consent—even if that tax is generally applicable and therefore does not discriminate against the agency (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because a state may not impose a tax directly on the federal government or any of its agencies or instrumentalities."},"questionZh":"一项联邦法规建立了国家彩票并设立了一个新的联邦机构来管理它。为了直接向公众销售彩票，该机构在全国各地设立了销售点。其中一些网点所在的州对所有州内博彩业务的总收入征收一般税。该州寻求对该机构在州内销售彩票的行为征税。该机构必须缴纳州总收入税吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为未经联邦法定明确授权，联邦机构不得向州支付任何款项。","B":"不可以，因为州不得直接向联邦政府或其任何机构或部门征税。","C":"是的，因为这是一项普遍适用的税收，不会歧视该机构。","D":"是的，因为联邦政府不能免除其商业活动的州税。"},"explanationZh":"根据至上条款，联邦政府免受各州的任何直接征税，包括对其商业活动征税（选择 D）。因此，未经国会明确同意，各州不得对联邦政府或其任何机构或部门征税。在这里，州政府对州内所有博彩业务的总收入（即总收入）征收一般税。该州寻求对一个新成立的负责管理国家彩票的联邦机构征收该税。但由于该机构免于直接州税，因此无需缴纳总收入税。 （选择 A）如果国会授予联邦机构广泛的自由裁量权来使用其资金，则联邦机构可以在没有明确法定授权的情况下向州付款。但即使联邦机构有这样的自由裁量权，州也不能强迫该机构纳税。 （选项 C）未经国会明确同意，州永远不能对联邦机构征收直接税，即使该税普遍适用，因此不会歧视该机构（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：除非国会明确同意，否则联邦政府、其机构及其部门不受各州直接征税。\n\n参考文献\n• McCullough 诉马里兰州，17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819)（取消对美国银行征收的州税）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非国会明确同意，否则联邦政府、其机构及其部门不受各州直接征税。","iracZh":{"issue":"该机构必须缴纳州总收入税吗？","rule":"除非国会明确同意，否则联邦政府、其机构及其部门不受各州直接征税。","analysis":"根据至上条款，联邦政府免受各州的任何直接征税，包括对其商业活动征税（选择 D）。因此，未经国会明确同意，各州不得对联邦政府或其任何机构或部门征税。在这里，州政府对州内所有博彩业务的总收入（即总收入）征收一般税。该州寻求对一个新成立的负责管理国家彩票的联邦机构征收该税。但由于该机构免于直接州税，因此无需缴纳总收入税。 （选择 A）如果国会授予联邦机构广泛的自由裁量权来使用其资金，则联邦机构可以在没有明确法定授权的情况下向州付款。但即使联邦机构有这样的自由裁量权，州也不能强迫该机构纳税。 （选项 C）未经国会明确同意，州永远不能对联邦机构征收直接税，即使该税普遍适用，因此不会歧视该机构（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：除非国会明确同意，否则联邦政府、其机构及其部门不受各州直接征税。\n\n参考文献\n• McCullough 诉马里兰州，17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819)（取消对美国银行征收的州税）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为州不得直接向联邦政府或其任何机构或部门征税。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"established","ipa":"/ɪstˈæblɪʃt/","syllables":"es·tab·lished","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. (被)建立的, 固定的, 既定的, 确定的, 确认的, 确立的, (被)制定的；[计] 确定的, 既定的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. (被)建立的, 固定的, 既定的, 确定的, 确认的, 确立的, (被)制定的；[计] 确定的, 既定的","wordForm":"形容词；原形 establish；词形 0:establish/1:dp/d:established/p:established","sourceSentence":"A federal statute established a national lottery and created a new federal agency to administer it.","synonyms":["base","ground","found","prove"]},{"term":"statutory","ipa":"/stˈætʃʌtˌɔɹi/","syllables":"statu·tory","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 法令的, 法定的, 可依法惩处的；[经] 法定的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 法令的, 法定的, 可依法惩处的；[经] 法定的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"No, because a federal agency may not pay any monies to a state without explicit federal statutory authorization to do so.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"levies","ipa":"/lˈɛviz/","syllables":"levies","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. （部队）征兵( levy的名词复数 )； 募捐； 被征募的军队","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. （部队）征兵( levy的名词复数 )； 募捐； 被征募的军队","wordForm":"词；原形 levy；词形 0:levy/1:s3","sourceSentence":"Some of these outlets are within a state that levies a general tax on the gross receipts from all in-state gaming operations.","synonyms":["levy en masse","recruit","raise","impose"]},{"term":"exempt","ipa":"/ɪɡzˈɛmpt/","syllables":"ex·empt","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 免税者, 被免除义务者；a. 免除的；vt. 使免除, 豁免","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 免税者, 被免除义务者；a. 免除的；vt. 使免除, 豁免","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:exempted/i:exempting/3:exempts/p:exempted","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the federal government is not exempt from state taxation on its commercial activities.","synonyms":["excuse","relieve","let off","free"]},{"term":"order","ipa":"/ˈɔɹdɝ/","syllables":"or·der","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 次序, 规则, 命令；vi. 命令, 定货；vt. 整理, 命令, 定购","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 次序, 规则, 命令；vi. 命令, 定货；vt. 整理, 命令, 定购","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:orders/d:ordered/p:ordered/i:ordering/3:orders","sourceSentence":"In order to sell lottery tickets directly to the public, the agency established outlets throughout the country.","synonyms":["ordering","order of magnitude","purchase order","decree"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-4d098859ba1d4085"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":7,"sourceQuestionPage":19,"sourceQuestionPages":[19],"sourceExplanationPages":[20,21],"sourceAnswerPages":[20,21],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-008","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"A federal law provides that all motor vehicle tires discarded in this country must be disposed of in facilities licensed by the federal Environmental Protection Agency. Pursuant to this federal law and all proper federal procedural requirements, that agency has adopted very strict standards for the licensing of such facilities. As a result, the cost of disposing of tires in licensed facilities is substantial. A state has a very large fleet of motor vehicles, including trucks used to support state- owned commercial activities and police cars. The state disposes of used tires from both kinds of state motor vehicles in a state-owned and operated facility. This state facility is unlicensed, but its operation in actual practice meets most of the standards imposed by the federal Environmental Protection Agency on facilities it licenses to dispose of tires. Is it consistent with United States Supreme Court precedent for the state to continue disposing of its used tires in this manner?","choices":{"A":"No, because a state must comply with valid federal laws that regulate matters substantially affecting interstate commerce.","B":"No, because some of the tires come from vehicles that are used by the state solely in its commercial activities.","C":"Yes, because some of the tires come from vehicles that are used by the state in the performance of core state governmental functions such as law enforcement.","D":"Yes, because the legitimate needs of the federal government are satisfied by the fact that the unlicensed state disposal scheme meets, in actual practice, most of the federal standards for the licensing of such facilities."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Commerce clause\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, § 8)\n\nCongress can regulate:\n\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons/things moving in interstate commerce\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce The commerce clause gives Congress the broad authority to regulate interstate commerce. This power extends to nearly any activity that involves two or more states— including in-state activities that, in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce. And under the supremacy clause, these regulations are the supreme law of the land. Therefore, states must comply* with federal regulations just as any other entity or person must do. Here, a federal law requires the disposal of all motor vehicle tires at facilities licensed by the EPA. The state disposes of used tires at in-state facilities, but the aggregate effect of this activity likely has a substantial effect on interstate commerce. This means that the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's commerce powers. And since that law is supreme, the state must comply with it and cannot continue to dispose of its tires at the unlicensed facility. *Under the Tenth Amendment anti-commandeering principle, the state would have been immune from the federal law had it required the state to (1) enact state legislation or (2) enforce federal law. (Choice B) Although some of the disposed tires come from vehicles that are used solely in the state's commercial activities, the aggregate effect of tire disposal on interstate commerce is likely substantial. Therefore, the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's commerce power with which states must comply. (Choice C) The dormant commerce clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state economic interests. And though such discrimination is permitted when states perform core government functions (eg, law enforcement, waste disposal), this does not authorize states to violate valid federal laws. (Choice D) A state must fully comply with a federal law even if the state could satisfy the legitimate needs of the federal government by mostly complying with that law.\n\nEducational objective: States must comply with all valid federal laws. This includes laws that are passed under Congress's broad commerce powers, which extend to any activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.\n\nReferences\n• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 151 (2000) (holding that a state must comply with a federal law regulating commerce since it does not require the state to enact or enforce any laws).","educationalObjective":"States must comply with all valid federal laws. This includes laws that are passed under Congress's broad commerce powers, which extend to any activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.","references":"• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 151 (2000) (holding that a state must comply with a federal law regulating commerce since it does not require the state to enact or enforce any laws).","rule":"States must comply with all valid federal laws. This includes laws that are passed under Congress's broad commerce powers, which extend to any activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.","irac":{"issue":"Is it consistent with United States Supreme Court precedent for the state to continue disposing of its used tires in this manner?","rule":"States must comply with all valid federal laws. This includes laws that are passed under Congress's broad commerce powers, which extend to any activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.","analysis":"Commerce clause\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, § 8)\n\nCongress can regulate:\n\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons/things moving in interstate commerce\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce The commerce clause gives Congress the broad authority to regulate interstate commerce. This power extends to nearly any activity that involves two or more states— including in-state activities that, in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce. And under the supremacy clause, these regulations are the supreme law of the land. Therefore, states must comply* with federal regulations just as any other entity or person must do. Here, a federal law requires the disposal of all motor vehicle tires at facilities licensed by the EPA. The state disposes of used tires at in-state facilities, but the aggregate effect of this activity likely has a substantial effect on interstate commerce. This means that the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's commerce powers. And since that law is supreme, the state must comply with it and cannot continue to dispose of its tires at the unlicensed facility. *Under the Tenth Amendment anti-commandeering principle, the state would have been immune from the federal law had it required the state to (1) enact state legislation or (2) enforce federal law. (Choice B) Although some of the disposed tires come from vehicles that are used solely in the state's commercial activities, the aggregate effect of tire disposal on interstate commerce is likely substantial. Therefore, the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's commerce power with which states must comply. (Choice C) The dormant commerce clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state economic interests. And though such discrimination is permitted when states perform core government functions (eg, law enforcement, waste disposal), this does not authorize states to violate valid federal laws. (Choice D) A state must fully comply with a federal law even if the state could satisfy the legitimate needs of the federal government by mostly complying with that law.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because a state must comply with valid federal laws that regulate matters substantially affecting interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"联邦法律规定，在该国丢弃的所有机动车辆轮胎必须在联邦环境保护局许可的设施中进行处置。根据本联邦法律和所有适当的联邦程序要求，该机构对此类设施的许可采用了非常严格的标准。因此，在获得许可的设施中处理轮胎的成本很高。一个州拥有非常庞大的机动车辆车队，包括用于支持国有商业活动的卡车和警车。国家在国有和运营的设施中处理两种国有机动车辆的废旧轮胎。该州设施没有获得许可，但其实际运营符合联邦环境保护局对其许可处理轮胎的设施规定的大部分标准。该州继续以这种方式处理废旧轮胎是否符合美国最高法院的先例？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为州必须遵守有效的联邦法律，这些法律对严重影响州际贸易的事项进行监管。","B":"不会，因为有些轮胎来自国家仅用于商业活动的车辆。","C":"是的，因为一些轮胎来自国家用于履行核心州政府职能（例如执法）的车辆。","D":"是的，因为未经许可的州处置计划实际上满足了此类设施许可的大部分联邦标准，这一事实满足了联邦政府的合法需求。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条）\n\n国会可以监管：\n\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 州际贸易中移动的人/物\n• 对州际商业产生重大影响的活动 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的广泛权力。这种权力几乎适用于涉及两个或多个州的任何活动，包括总体上严重影响州际贸易的州内活动。根据至上条款，这些规定是该国的最高法律。因此，各州必须遵守*联邦法规，就像任何其他实体或个人必须遵守的那样。在此，联邦法律要求在 EPA 许可的设施中处置所有机动车辆轮胎。该州在州内设施中处理废旧轮胎，但这一活动的总体影响可能会对州际贸易产生重大影响。这意味着联邦法律是国会商业权力的有效行使。由于该法律具有至高无上的地位，因此国家必须遵守该法律，并且不能继续在未经许可的设施中处理其轮胎。 *根据第十修正案反征用原则，如果要求州 (1) 颁布州立法或 (2) 执行联邦法律，则州将不受联邦法律的约束。 （选择 B）虽然一些被处置的轮胎来自仅用于该州商业活动的车辆，但轮胎处置对州际商业的总体影响可能是巨大的。因此，联邦法律是国会商业权力的有效行使，各州必须遵守。 （选项C） 消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）一般禁止各州歧视州外经济利益。尽管当各州履行核心政府职能（例如执法、废物处理）时，这种歧视是允许的，但这并不授权各州违反有效的联邦法律。 （选项 D）即使州可以通过大部分遵守联邦法律来满足联邦政府的合法需要，也必须完全遵守该法律。\n\n教育目标：各州必须遵守所有有效的联邦法律。这包括根据国会广泛的商业权力通过的法律，这些权力延伸到任何严重影响州际商业的活动。\n\n参考文献\n• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 151 (2000)（认为州必须遵守监管商业的联邦法律，因为它不要求州颁布或执行任何法律）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"各州必须遵守所有有效的联邦法律。这包括根据国会广泛的商业权力通过的法律，这些权力延伸到任何严重影响州际商业的活动。","iracZh":{"issue":"该州继续以这种方式处理废旧轮胎是否符合美国最高法院的先例？","rule":"各州必须遵守所有有效的联邦法律。这包括根据国会广泛的商业权力通过的法律，这些权力延伸到任何严重影响州际商业的活动。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条）\n\n国会可以监管：\n\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 州际贸易中移动的人/物\n• 对州际商业产生重大影响的活动 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的广泛权力。这种权力几乎适用于涉及两个或多个州的任何活动，包括总体上严重影响州际贸易的州内活动。根据至上条款，这些规定是该国的最高法律。因此，各州必须遵守*联邦法规，就像任何其他实体或个人必须遵守的那样。在此，联邦法律要求在 EPA 许可的设施中处置所有机动车辆轮胎。该州在州内设施中处理废旧轮胎，但这一活动的总体影响可能会对州际贸易产生重大影响。这意味着联邦法律是国会商业权力的有效行使。由于该法律具有至高无上的地位，因此国家必须遵守该法律，并且不能继续在未经许可的设施中处理其轮胎。 *根据第十修正案反征用原则，如果要求州 (1) 颁布州立法或 (2) 执行联邦法律，则州将不受联邦法律的约束。 （选择 B）虽然一些被处置的轮胎来自仅用于该州商业活动的车辆，但轮胎处置对州际商业的总体影响可能是巨大的。因此，联邦法律是国会商业权力的有效行使，各州必须遵守。 （选项C） 消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）一般禁止各州歧视州外经济利益。尽管当各州履行核心政府职能（例如执法、废物处理）时，这种歧视是允许的，但这并不授权各州违反有效的联邦法律。 （选项 D）即使州可以通过大部分遵守联邦法律来满足联邦政府的合法需要，也必须完全遵守该法律。\n\n教育目标：各州必须遵守所有有效的联邦法律。这包括根据国会广泛的商业权力通过的法律，这些权力延伸到任何严重影响州际商业的活动。\n\n参考文献\n• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 151 (2000)（认为州必须遵守监管商业的联邦法律，因为它不要求州颁布或执行任何法律）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为州必须遵守有效的联邦法律，这些法律对严重影响州际贸易的事项进行监管。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"procedural","ipa":"/pɹʌsˈidʒɝəl/","syllables":"pro·ce·dural","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 程序上的；[经] 程序上的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 程序上的；[经] 程序上的","wordForm":"形容词；词形 s:procedurals","sourceSentence":"Pursuant to this federal law and all proper federal procedural requirements, that agency has adopted very strict standards for the licensing of such facilities.","synonyms":["adjective"]},{"term":"licensed","ipa":"/lˈaɪsənst/","syllables":"li·censed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 得到许可的；[经] 领有执照的, 领到许可证的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 得到许可的；[经] 领有执照的, 领到许可证的","wordForm":"词；原形 license；词形 0:license/1:dp/d:licensed/p:licensed","sourceSentence":"A federal law provides that all motor vehicle tires discarded in this country must be disposed of in facilities licensed by the federal Environmental Protection Agency.","synonyms":["permission","permit","licence","certify"]},{"term":"precedent","ipa":"/pɹˈɛsɪdənt/","syllables":"prece·dent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 先例, 前例；a. 在先的, 在前的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 先例, 前例；a. 在先的, 在前的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:precedents","sourceSentence":"Is it consistent with United States Supreme Court precedent for the state to continue disposing of its used tires in this manner?","synonyms":["case in point","case law","common law"]},{"term":"permitted","ipa":"/pɝmˈɪtɪd/","syllables":"per·mit·ted","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 允许( permit的过去式和过去分词 )； 许可； 许用","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 允许( permit的过去式和过去分词 )； 许可； 许用","wordForm":"词；原形 permit；词形 0:permit/1:dp/p:permitted/d:permitted","sourceSentence":"And though such discrimination is permitted when states perform core government functions (eg, law enforcement, waste disposal), this does not authorize states to violate valid federal laws.","synonyms":["license","permission","Trachinotus falcatus","licence"]},{"term":"police","ipa":"/pʌlˈis/","syllables":"po·lice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 警察, 警察当局, 治安；vt. 维持治安, 管辖","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 警察, 警察当局, 治安；vt. 维持治安, 管辖","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:police/0:police/1:s/i:policing/d:policed/p:policed/3:polices","sourceSentence":"A state has a very large fleet of motor vehicles, including trucks used to support state- owned commercial activities and police cars.","synonyms":["police force","constabulary","law","patrol"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":8,"sourceQuestionPage":22,"sourceQuestionPages":[22],"sourceExplanationPages":[23,24],"sourceAnswerPages":[23,24],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-009","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Appointments & Removal","question":"A state enacted a statute requiring the state's appointed presidential electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral votes for the presidential and vice-presidential nominees chosen by the popular vote of the state. A provision of the statute provides that an elector who breaks this oath and votes for another candidate will be removed and replaced as an elector and fined $1,000. Three appointed electors in the state swore the oath as required by the statute. When the time came to cast their electoral votes, however, the electors voted for a presidential candidate who was not chosen by the state's popular vote. The state removed the electors and replaced them with alternates. The state also fined each elector $1,000. The three electors sued the state in state court, claiming that the statute governing presidential electors violates the United States Constitution. How should the court rule on the constitutionality of the state statute?","choices":{"A":"The provision imposing a monetary fine on the electors is an unconstitutional bill of attainder.","B":"The provision allowing the removal of electors violates their fundamental right to vote.","C":"The provision requiring electors to swear an oath violates their First Amendment right to free speech and association.","D":"The statute is constitutional in its entirety."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Every four years, the United States holds a presidential and vice-presidential election. When citizens cast their ballots, they are selecting members of the Electoral College. These presidential electors then vote to determine the next President and Vice President. This process is governed by the following constitutional provisions:\n• Article II – grants state legislatures the power to appoint an allotted number of presidential electors in any manner.*\n\n• Twelfth Amendment – requires electors to meet in their states, vote separately for President and Vice President, and transmit sealed lists of their votes to the U.S. Senate to be officially counted.\n\nThe power to appoint electors also permits states to place conditions on such appointments so long as the conditions do not violate another constitutional provision. Permissible conditions include requiring electors to pledge or swear an oath that they will cast their electoral vote for the candidate chosen by the popular vote. States can also punish an elector who violates the pledge or oath by removing the elector and/or imposing a monetary fine. Therefore, the state statute here is constitutional in its entirety. *States cannot appoint as electors those who serve in the U.S. Senate or House of Representatives or who hold any other federal office. (Choice A) A bill of attainder is an unconstitutional legislative act that declares a named person or group guilty of a crime (not seen here) and imposes punishment without a trial. (Choice B) U.S. citizens who are 18 or older have the fundamental right to vote in federal, state, and local elections. However, this fundamental right does not extend to presidential electors casting electoral votes. (Choice C) The First Amendment right to free speech and association bars the government from conditioning government employment or office on an overbroad or vague loyalty oath. Here, the oath is not overbroad since it focuses on how electors must vote. It is also not vague since a reasonable person would understand it.\n\nEducational objective: States have power to appoint presidential electors and place conditions on such appointments. Such conditions may include requiring electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral vote for the candidate chosen by the state's popular vote and punishing electors who violate this oath through removal and/or a monetary fine.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 1, cl. 2 (appointing presidential electors).\n\n• U.S. Const. amend. XII (electoral college voting procedure).\n\n• Chiafalo v. Washington, 140 S. Ct. 2316, 2320 (2020) (holding that a state may penalize a presidential elector for breaking his/her pledge to cast a vote for the candidate who wins that state's popular vote).","educationalObjective":"States have power to appoint presidential electors and place conditions on such appointments. Such conditions may include requiring electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral vote for the candidate chosen by the state's popular vote and punishing electors who violate this oath through removal and/or a monetary fine.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. II, § 1, cl. 2 (appointing presidential electors).\n\n• U.S. Const. amend. XII (electoral college voting procedure).\n\n• Chiafalo v. Washington, 140 S. Ct. 2316, 2320 (2020) (holding that a state may penalize a presidential elector for breaking his/her pledge to cast a vote for the candidate who wins that state's popular vote).","rule":"States have power to appoint presidential electors and place conditions on such appointments. Such conditions may include requiring electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral vote for the candidate chosen by the state's popular vote and punishing electors who violate this oath through removal and/or a monetary fine.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the constitutionality of the state statute?","rule":"States have power to appoint presidential electors and place conditions on such appointments. Such conditions may include requiring electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral vote for the candidate chosen by the state's popular vote and punishing electors who violate this oath through removal and/or a monetary fine.","analysis":"Every four years, the United States holds a presidential and vice-presidential election. When citizens cast their ballots, they are selecting members of the Electoral College. These presidential electors then vote to determine the next President and Vice President. This process is governed by the following constitutional provisions:\n• Article II – grants state legislatures the power to appoint an allotted number of presidential electors in any manner.*\n\n• Twelfth Amendment – requires electors to meet in their states, vote separately for President and Vice President, and transmit sealed lists of their votes to the U.S. Senate to be officially counted.\n\nThe power to appoint electors also permits states to place conditions on such appointments so long as the conditions do not violate another constitutional provision. Permissible conditions include requiring electors to pledge or swear an oath that they will cast their electoral vote for the candidate chosen by the popular vote. States can also punish an elector who violates the pledge or oath by removing the elector and/or imposing a monetary fine. Therefore, the state statute here is constitutional in its entirety. *States cannot appoint as electors those who serve in the U.S. Senate or House of Representatives or who hold any other federal office. (Choice A) A bill of attainder is an unconstitutional legislative act that declares a named person or group guilty of a crime (not seen here) and imposes punishment without a trial. (Choice B) U.S. citizens who are 18 or older have the fundamental right to vote in federal, state, and local elections. However, this fundamental right does not extend to presidential electors casting electoral votes. (Choice C) The First Amendment right to free speech and association bars the government from conditioning government employment or office on an overbroad or vague loyalty oath. Here, the oath is not overbroad since it focuses on how electors must vote. It is also not vague since a reasonable person would understand it.","conclusion":"Answer D: The statute is constitutional in its entirety."},"questionZh":"某州制定了一项法规，要求该州指定的总统选举人宣誓，将选举人票投给该州普选产生的总统和副总统候选人。该法规的一项规定规定，违反誓言并投票给另一位候选人的选民将被除名并更换为选民，并处以 1,000 美元罚款。该州三名指定选举人按照法规要求宣誓。然而，当投票的时候，选举人投票给了一位没有通过该州普选选出的总统候选人。国家取消了选举人，并用候补人取而代之。州政府还对每位选民处以 1,000 美元的罚款。三名选举人向州法院起诉该州，声称管理总统选举人的法规违反了美国宪法。法院应当如何判定国家立法的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"对选民处以罚款的条款是一项违宪的剥夺公民权法案。","B":"允许罢免选民的规定侵犯了选民的基本投票权。","C":"要求选民宣誓的规定侵犯了第一修正案的言论自由和结社自由权。","D":"该法规整体上符合宪法。"},"explanationZh":"美国每四年举行一次总统和副总统选举。当公民投票时，他们正在选择选举团成员。然后，这些总统选举人投票决定下一任总统和副总统。这一过程受以下宪法条款管辖：\n• 第二条——授予州立法机关以任何方式任命一定数量的总统选举人的权力。*\n\n• 第十二修正案——要求选举人在各自的州举行会议，分别投票选举总统和副总统，并将密封的选票名单传送给美国参议院进行正式计票。\n\n任命选举人的权力还允许各州对此类任命设定条件，只要这些条件不违反其他宪法条款。允许的条件包括要求选民承诺或宣誓，他们将把选举人票投给普选的候选人。各州还可以通过罢免选民和/或处以罚款来惩罚违反承诺或宣誓的选民。因此，这里的州法规完全符合宪法。 *各州不能任命在美国参议院或众议院任职或担任任何其他联邦职务的人为选举人。 （选项 A）剥夺公民权法案是一项违宪的立法行为，宣布指定的个人或团体有罪（此处未示出），并在未经审判的情况下实施惩罚。 （选项B）年满18岁的美国公民拥有在联邦、州和地方选举中投票的基本权利。然而，这项基本权利并不适用于投票的总统选举人。 （选项 C）第一修正案的言论自由和结社自由权禁止政府以过于宽泛或模糊的忠诚宣誓来限制政府就业或担任公职。在这里，宣誓并不过分宽泛，因为它的重点是选民必须如何投票。它也并不含糊，因为一个有理智的人会理解它。\n\n教育目标：各国有权任命总统选举人并为此类任命设定条件。这些条件可能包括要求选民宣誓，他们将把选举人票投给该州普选的候选人，并通过罢免和/或罚款来惩罚违反誓言的选民。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 1，cl。 2（任命总统选举人）。\n\n• 美国宪法。修正。十二（选举团投票程序）。\n\n• Chiafalo 诉华盛顿，140 S. Ct. 2316, 2320 (2020)（规定州可以对总统选举人违反其为赢得该州普选的候选人投票的承诺进行惩罚）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"各州有权任命总统选举人并为此类任命设定条件。这些条件可能包括要求选民宣誓，他们将把选举人票投给该州普选的候选人，并通过罢免和/或罚款来惩罚违反誓言的选民。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应当如何判定国家立法的合宪性？","rule":"各州有权任命总统选举人并为此类任命设定条件。这些条件可能包括要求选民宣誓，他们将把选举人票投给该州普选的候选人，并通过罢免和/或罚款来惩罚违反誓言的选民。","analysis":"美国每四年举行一次总统和副总统选举。当公民投票时，他们正在选择选举团成员。然后，这些总统选举人投票决定下一任总统和副总统。这一过程受以下宪法条款管辖：\n• 第二条——授予州立法机关以任何方式任命一定数量的总统选举人的权力。*\n\n• 第十二修正案——要求选举人在各自的州举行会议，分别投票选举总统和副总统，并将密封的选票名单传送给美国参议院进行正式计票。\n\n任命选举人的权力还允许各州对此类任命设定条件，只要这些条件不违反其他宪法条款。允许的条件包括要求选民承诺或宣誓，他们将把选举人票投给普选的候选人。各州还可以通过罢免选民和/或处以罚款来惩罚违反承诺或宣誓的选民。因此，这里的州法规完全符合宪法。 *各州不能任命在美国参议院或众议院任职或担任任何其他联邦职务的人为选举人。 （选项 A）剥夺公民权法案是一项违宪的立法行为，宣布指定的个人或团体有罪（此处未示出），并在未经审判的情况下实施惩罚。 （选项B）年满18岁的美国公民拥有在联邦、州和地方选举中投票的基本权利。然而，这项基本权利并不适用于投票的总统选举人。 （选项 C）第一修正案的言论自由和结社自由权禁止政府以过于宽泛或模糊的忠诚宣誓来限制政府就业或担任公职。在这里，宣誓并不过分宽泛，因为它的重点是选民必须如何投票。它也并不含糊，因为一个有理智的人会理解它。\n\n教育目标：各国有权任命总统选举人并为此类任命设定条件。这些条件可能包括要求选民宣誓，他们将把选举人票投给该州普选的候选人，并通过罢免和/或罚款来惩罚违反誓言的选民。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 1，cl。 2（任命总统选举人）。\n\n• 美国宪法。修正。十二（选举团投票程序）。\n\n• Chiafalo 诉华盛顿，140 S. Ct. 2316, 2320 (2020)（规定州可以对总统选举人违反其为赢得该州普选的候选人投票的承诺进行惩罚）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该法规整体上符合宪法。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"first amendment","ipa":"/fˈɝst ʌmˈɛndmənt/","syllables":"first amend·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"第一修正案：保护宗教、言论、出版、集会和请愿自由","contextualMeaning":"第一修正案：保护宗教、言论、出版、集会和请愿自由","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The provision requiring electors to swear an oath violates their First Amendment right to free speech and association.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"bill of attainder","ipa":"/bˈɪl ˈəv ʌtˈeɪndɝ/","syllables":"bill of at·tain·der","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"剥夺公权法案：立法机关未经司法审判即惩罚特定个人或群体","contextualMeaning":"剥夺公权法案：立法机关未经司法审判即惩罚特定个人或群体","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The provision imposing a monetary fine on the electors is an unconstitutional bill of attainder.","synonyms":["bill of civil death"]},{"term":"presidential","ipa":"/pɹˌɛzʌdˈɛnʃəl/","syllables":"pres·i·den·tial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 总统制的, 总统的, 首长的, 统辖的；[法] 总统的, 议长的, 总经理的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 总统制的, 总统的, 首长的, 统辖的；[法] 总统的, 议长的, 总经理的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"A state enacted a statute requiring the state's appointed presidential electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral votes for the presidential and vice-presidential nominees chosen by the popular vote of the state.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"appointments","ipa":"/ʌpˈɔɪntmənts/","syllables":"ap·point·ments","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 约会( appointment的复数形式 )； 任命； 职务","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 约会( appointment的复数形式 )； 任命； 职务","wordForm":"名词；原形 appointment；词形 0:appointment/1:s","sourceSentence":"States have power to appoint presidential electors and place conditions on such appointments.","synonyms":["assignment","designation","naming","fitting"]},{"term":"association","ipa":"/ʌsˌoʊsiˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"as·so·ci·a·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 协会；[计] 关联","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 协会；[计] 关联","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:associations","sourceSentence":"The provision requiring electors to swear an oath violates their First Amendment right to free speech and association.","synonyms":["connection","connexion","affiliation","tie"]},{"term":"appoint","ipa":"/ʌpˈɔɪnt/","syllables":"ap·point","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 任命, 指定, 下令；[法] 派, 派任, 任命","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 任命, 指定, 下令；[法] 派, 派任, 任命","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:appointed/p:appointed/i:appointing/3:appoints","sourceSentence":"A state enacted a statute requiring the state's appointed presidential electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral votes for the presidential and vice-presidential nominees chosen by the popular vote of the state.","synonyms":["name","nominate","constitute","charge"]},{"term":"electoral","ipa":"/ɪlˈɛktɝəl/","syllables":"elec·toral","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 选举人的, 选举的, (有关)选举的；[法] 选举的, 选举人的, 由选举人组成的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 选举人的, 选举的, (有关)选举的；[法] 选举的, 选举人的, 由选举人组成的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"A state enacted a statute requiring the state's appointed presidential electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral votes for the presidential and vice-presidential nominees chosen by the popular vote of the state.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"candidate","ipa":"/kˈændədeɪt/","syllables":"can·di·date","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 候选人, 投考者；[法] 候选, 候补者","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 候选人, 投考者；[法] 候选, 候补者","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:candidates","sourceSentence":"A provision of the statute provides that an elector who breaks this oath and votes for another candidate will be removed and replaced as an elector and fined $1,000.","synonyms":["campaigner","nominee","prospect"]},{"term":"president","ipa":"/pɹˈɛzʌdˌɛnt/","syllables":"pres·i·dent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 总统, 总裁, 董事长, (学院)院长, (大学)校长, 主管人, 主持人；[经] 总经理, 董事长, 总裁","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 总统, 总裁, 董事长, (学院)院长, (大学)校长, 主管人, 主持人；[经] 总经理, 董事长, 总裁","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:presidents","sourceSentence":"A state enacted a statute requiring the state's appointed presidential electors to swear an oath that they will cast their electoral votes for the presidential and vice-presidential nominees chosen by the popular vote of the state.","synonyms":["President of the United States","Chief Executive","United States President","prexy"]},{"term":"election","ipa":"/ɪlˈɛkʃən/","syllables":"elec·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 选举, 当选, 选择权；[法] 选举, 当选","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 选举, 当选, 选择权；[法] 选举, 当选","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:elections","sourceSentence":"Every four years, the United States holds a presidential and vice-presidential election.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"citizens","ipa":"/sˈɪtəzənz/","syllables":"cit·i·zens","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 市民；公民（citizen的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 市民；公民（citizen的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 citizen；词形 0:citizen/1:s","sourceSentence":"When citizens cast their ballots, they are selecting members of the Electoral College.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"removal","ipa":"/ɹɪmˈuvəl/","syllables":"re·moval","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 移动, 移居, 迁移, 排除, 切除；[医] 切除, 除去","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 移动, 移居, 迁移, 排除, 切除；[医] 切除, 除去","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:removals","sourceSentence":"The provision allowing the removal of electors violates their fundamental right to vote.","synonyms":["remotion"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-525e8e83076cd6c3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":9,"sourceQuestionPage":25,"sourceQuestionPages":[25],"sourceExplanationPages":[26,27],"sourceAnswerPages":[26,27],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-010","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"A federal statute authorizes the Interstate Commerce Commission (ICC) to permit interstate carriers to discontinue any unprofitable route. An interstate bus company that operates in a five-state area applied to the ICC for permission to drop a very unprofitable route through a sparsely populated mountain region. The ICC granted that permission even though the bus company provided the only public transportation into that region. A woman owns a mountain resort in this region. The resort's customers usually arrived on buses operated by the company. After exhausting all available federal administrative remedies, the woman filed suit against the bus company in the trial court of the state in which the resort is located to enjoin the discontinuance by the bus company of its service to that area. The woman alleged that the discontinuance of service would violate a state statute that prohibits common carriers from abandoning service to communities having no alternate form of public transportation. What action should the state court take?","choices":{"A":"Deny the injunction, because a valid federal law preempts the state statute on which the woman relies.","B":"Direct the removal of the case to federal court, because this suit involves a substantial federal question.","C":"Dismiss the action, because the woman lacks standing to sue.","D":"Grant the injunction because, on these facts, a federal agency is interfering with essential state functions."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Preemption\n\n(federal law supersedes state law)\n\nType Applicability\n\nExpress\n• Constitution grants federal government exclusive power to regulate area or\n• Federal law expressly prohibits state regulation in same area Implied Field preemption:\n\n• Congressional intent to occupy entire field inferred from pervasive regulations that leave no room for state involvement Conflict preemption:\n\n• Direct conflict makes it impossible (or nearly impossible) to comply with federal & state law or\n• Indirect conflict frustrates accomplishment of federal law's purpose Under the Article VI supremacy clause, a valid federal law preempts (ie, supersedes) conflicting state law. Such preemption can be express or implied. One type of implied preemption is conflict preemption, which stems from either:\n• direct conflict – when it is impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both the federal and state laws or\n\n• indirect conflict – when the state law frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose.\n\nHere, the Interstate Commerce Commission (ICC) acted pursuant to a federal statute when it permitted the bus company to drop an unprofitable route. But a state statute prohibits the bus company from dropping that route since the area has no alternate form of public transportation. This frustrates the federal statute's purpose to let the ICC determine whether an interstate carrier may discontinue a given route. And since the federal statute impliedly preempts the conflicting state one, the woman's request to enjoin the discontinuance of the route should be denied. (Choice B) A state court cannot remove a case to federal court on its own initiative—even if the suit involves a substantial federal question. Instead, the defendant must request removal (not seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The woman has standing because she can allege an injury-in-fact (loss of customers) that is caused by the challenged action (dropped bus route) and redressable by the remedy sought (injunction). (Choice D) An essential function of a state is to enact and enforce laws to protect the health, safety, and welfare of the public (ie, police power). But a federal agency can interfere with such functions when acting pursuant to a valid federal law (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A federal law will impliedly preempt a conflicting state law that makes it impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both laws (ie, direct conflict) OR frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).\n\nReferences\n• Hines v. Davidowitz, 312 U.S. 52, 67 (1941) (stating that implied preemption occurs when a state law \"stands as an obstacle to the accomplishment and execution of the full purposes and objectives of Congress\").\n\n• Chi. & Nw. Transp. Co. v. Kalo Brick & Tile Co., 450 U.S. 311, 331 (1981) (holding that ICC approval of an abandonment of a common-carrier route preempts conflicting state law).","educationalObjective":"A federal law will impliedly preempt a conflicting state law that makes it impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both laws (ie, direct conflict) OR frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).","references":"• Hines v. Davidowitz, 312 U.S. 52, 67 (1941) (stating that implied preemption occurs when a state law \"stands as an obstacle to the accomplishment and execution of the full purposes and objectives of Congress\").\n\n• Chi. & Nw. Transp. Co. v. Kalo Brick & Tile Co., 450 U.S. 311, 331 (1981) (holding that ICC approval of an abandonment of a common-carrier route preempts conflicting state law).","rule":"A federal law will impliedly preempt a conflicting state law that makes it impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both laws (ie, direct conflict) OR frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).","irac":{"issue":"What action should the state court take?","rule":"A federal law will impliedly preempt a conflicting state law that makes it impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both laws (ie, direct conflict) OR frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).","analysis":"Preemption\n\n(federal law supersedes state law)\n\nType Applicability\n\nExpress\n• Constitution grants federal government exclusive power to regulate area or\n• Federal law expressly prohibits state regulation in same area Implied Field preemption:\n\n• Congressional intent to occupy entire field inferred from pervasive regulations that leave no room for state involvement Conflict preemption:\n\n• Direct conflict makes it impossible (or nearly impossible) to comply with federal & state law or\n• Indirect conflict frustrates accomplishment of federal law's purpose Under the Article VI supremacy clause, a valid federal law preempts (ie, supersedes) conflicting state law. Such preemption can be express or implied. One type of implied preemption is conflict preemption, which stems from either:\n• direct conflict – when it is impossible or nearly impossible to comply with both the federal and state laws or\n\n• indirect conflict – when the state law frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose.\n\nHere, the Interstate Commerce Commission (ICC) acted pursuant to a federal statute when it permitted the bus company to drop an unprofitable route. But a state statute prohibits the bus company from dropping that route since the area has no alternate form of public transportation. This frustrates the federal statute's purpose to let the ICC determine whether an interstate carrier may discontinue a given route. And since the federal statute impliedly preempts the conflicting state one, the woman's request to enjoin the discontinuance of the route should be denied. (Choice B) A state court cannot remove a case to federal court on its own initiative—even if the suit involves a substantial federal question. Instead, the defendant must request removal (not seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The woman has standing because she can allege an injury-in-fact (loss of customers) that is caused by the challenged action (dropped bus route) and redressable by the remedy sought (injunction). (Choice D) An essential function of a state is to enact and enforce laws to protect the health, safety, and welfare of the public (ie, police power). But a federal agency can interfere with such functions when acting pursuant to a valid federal law (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: Deny the injunction, because a valid federal law preempts the state statute on which the woman relies."},"questionZh":"一项联邦法规授权州际商务委员会 (ICC) 允许州际承运人停止任何无利可图的航线。一家在五个州地区运营的州际巴士公司向国际刑事法院申请允许放弃一条穿越人口稀少山区的非常无利可图的路线。尽管巴士公司提供进入该地区的唯一公共交通，但国际刑事法院还是授予了该许可。一名妇女在该地区拥有一座山区度假村。度假村的顾客通常乘坐该公司运营的巴士抵达。在用尽所有可用的联邦行政补救措施后，该女子向度假村所在州的初审法院对巴士公司提起诉讼，要求巴士公司停止向该地区提供服务。该女子声称，停止服务将违反一项州法规，该法规禁止公共交通公司放弃向没有其他公共交通形式的社区提供服务。州法院应该采取什么行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"拒绝该禁令，因为有效的联邦法律优先于该妇女所依赖的州法律。","B":"直接将案件移交联邦法院，因为该诉讼涉及重大联邦问题。","C":"驳回该诉讼，因为该女子没有起诉资格。","D":"批准该禁令是因为，根据这些事实，联邦机构正在干涉国家的基本职能。"},"explanationZh":"抢占\n\n（联邦法律取代州法律）\n\n类型适用性\n\n快递\n• 宪法赋予联邦政府管理地区或地区的专属权力\n• 联邦法律明确禁止同一领域的州监管 隐含字段优先：\n\n• 国会有意占领整个领域，这是从普遍存在的法规中推断出来的，这些法规没有给国家参与留出空间 冲突先发制人：\n\n• 直接冲突使得不可能（或几乎不可能）遵守联邦和州法律或\n• 间接冲突阻碍联邦法律目的的实现 根据第六条至上条款，有效的联邦法律优先于（即取代）相冲突的州法律。这种先发制人可以是明示的或暗示的。隐含抢占的一种类型是冲突抢占，它源于以下任一原因：\n• 直接冲突——当不可能或几乎不可能同时遵守联邦和州法律时，或者\n\n• 间接冲突——当州法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现时。\n\n在这里，州际商业委员会 (ICC) 根据联邦法规行事，允许巴士公司放弃一条无利可图的路线。但州法规禁止公交公司放弃这条路线，因为该地区没有替代的公共交通。这违背了联邦法规的目的，即让国际刑事法院决定州际航空公司是否可以中断特定航线。由于联邦法规隐含地优先于相互冲突的州法规，因此该妇女要求停止该路线的请求应被拒绝。 （选择 B）州法院不能主动将案件移交联邦法院，即使该诉讼涉及重大联邦问题。相反，被告必须请求驱逐（此处未显示）。\n\n（选项 C）该妇女具有诉讼资格，因为她可以指控因受质疑的行为（取消公交车路线）造成的事实上的伤害（客户流失），并且可以通过寻求补救措施（禁令）予以纠正。 （选项D） 国家的一项基本职能是制定和执行法律以保护公众的健康、安全和福利（即警察权力）。但是，联邦机构在根据有效的联邦法律行事时可以干预此类职能（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：联邦法律将隐含地优先于相互冲突的州法律，这使得不可能或几乎不可能遵守这两个法律（即直接冲突）或阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）。\n\n参考文献\n• Hines v. Davidowitz, 312 U.S. 52, 67 (1941)（指出当州法律“成为实现和执行国会全部宗旨和目标的障碍”时，就会发生默示先发制人）。\n\n• 气。 ＆西北。运输。 Co. v. Kalo Brick & Tile Co., 450 U.S. 311, 331 (1981)（认为国际商会批准放弃公共承运人路线优先于相冲突的州法律）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联邦法律将隐含地优先于相互冲突的州法律，这使得不可能或几乎不可能遵守这两个法律（即直接冲突）或阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）。","iracZh":{"issue":"州法院应该采取什么行动？","rule":"联邦法律将隐含地优先于相互冲突的州法律，这使得不可能或几乎不可能遵守这两个法律（即直接冲突）或阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）。","analysis":"抢占\n\n（联邦法律取代州法律）\n\n类型适用性\n\n快递\n• 宪法赋予联邦政府管理地区或地区的专属权力\n• 联邦法律明确禁止同一领域的州监管 隐含字段优先：\n\n• 国会有意占领整个领域，这是从普遍存在的法规中推断出来的，这些法规没有给国家参与留出空间 冲突先发制人：\n\n• 直接冲突使得不可能（或几乎不可能）遵守联邦和州法律或\n• 间接冲突阻碍联邦法律目的的实现 根据第六条至上条款，有效的联邦法律优先于（即取代）相冲突的州法律。这种先发制人可以是明示的或暗示的。隐含抢占的一种类型是冲突抢占，它源于以下任一原因：\n• 直接冲突——当不可能或几乎不可能同时遵守联邦和州法律时，或者\n\n• 间接冲突——当州法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现时。\n\n在这里，州际商业委员会 (ICC) 根据联邦法规行事，允许巴士公司放弃一条无利可图的路线。但州法规禁止公交公司放弃这条路线，因为该地区没有替代的公共交通。这违背了联邦法规的目的，即让国际刑事法院决定州际航空公司是否可以中断特定航线。由于联邦法规隐含地优先于相互冲突的州法规，因此该妇女要求停止该路线的请求应被拒绝。 （选择 B）州法院不能主动将案件移交联邦法院，即使该诉讼涉及重大联邦问题。相反，被告必须请求驱逐（此处未显示）。\n\n（选项 C）该妇女具有诉讼资格，因为她可以指控因受质疑的行为（取消公交车路线）造成的事实上的伤害（客户流失），并且可以通过寻求补救措施（禁令）予以纠正。 （选项D） 国家的一项基本职能是制定和执行法律以保护公众的健康、安全和福利（即警察权力）。但是，联邦机构在根据有效的联邦法律行事时可以干预此类职能（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：联邦法律将隐含地优先于相互冲突的州法律，这使得不可能或几乎不可能遵守这两个法律（即直接冲突）或阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）。\n\n参考文献\n• Hines v. Davidowitz, 312 U.S. 52, 67 (1941)（指出当州法律“成为实现和执行国会全部宗旨和目标的障碍”时，就会发生默示先发制人）。\n\n• 气。 ＆西北。运输。 Co. v. Kalo Brick & Tile Co., 450 U.S. 311, 331 (1981)（认为国际商会批准放弃公共承运人路线优先于相冲突的州法律）。","conclusion":"答案 A：拒绝该禁令，因为有效的联邦法律优先于该妇女所依赖的州法律。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"police power","ipa":"/pʌlˈis pˈaʊɝ/","syllables":"po·lice power","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"警察权：州为健康、安全、福利和道德制定一般法规的权力","contextualMeaning":"警察权：州为健康、安全、福利和道德制定一般法规的权力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) An essential function of a state is to enact and enforce laws to protect the health, safety, and welfare of the public (ie, police power).","synonyms":["police might","police mightiness","police powerfulness"]},{"term":"injunction","ipa":"/ˌɪndʒˈəŋkʃən/","syllables":"in·junc·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 命令, 禁令, 劝告；[经] 指令, 禁令","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 命令, 禁令, 劝告；[经] 指令, 禁令","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:injunctions","sourceSentence":"Deny the injunction, because a valid federal law preempts the state statute on which the woman relies.","synonyms":["enjoining","enjoinment","cease and desist order"]},{"term":"defendant","ipa":"/dɪfˈɛndənt/","syllables":"de·fen·dant","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 被告；[经] 被告方","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 被告；[经] 被告方","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:defendants","sourceSentence":"Instead, the defendant must request removal (not seen here).","synonyms":["suspect"]},{"term":"remedy","ipa":"/ɹˈɛmədi/","syllables":"rem·edy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 药物, 治疗法, 治疗, 补救, 赔偿；vt. 治疗, 补救, 矫正, 改善, 修补, 修缮","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 药物, 治疗法, 治疗, 补救, 赔偿；vt. 治疗, 补救, 矫正, 改善, 修补, 修缮","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:remedies/d:remedied/i:remedying/p:remedied/3:remedies","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The woman has standing because she can allege an injury-in-fact (loss of customers) that is caused by the challenged action (dropped bus route) and redressable by the remedy sought (injunction).","synonyms":["redress","remediation","curative","cure"]},{"term":"standing","ipa":"/stˈændɪŋ/","syllables":"stand·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 起立, 持续, 身分；a. 立着的, 不动的, 经常的, 持续的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 起立, 持续, 身分；a. 立着的, 不动的, 经常的, 持续的","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:stand/1:i/i:standing/s:standings","sourceSentence":"Dismiss the action, because the woman lacks standing to sue.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"service","ipa":"/sˈɝvəs/","syllables":"ser·vice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 服务, 贡献, 雇佣, 公职, 服役, 功劳, 仪式, 送达, 行政部门；vt. 保养, 维修；a. 武装部队的, 服务性的, 仆人的, 耐用的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 服务, 贡献, 雇佣, 公职, 服役, 功劳, 仪式, 送达, 行政部门；vt. 保养, 维修；a. 武装部队的, 服务性的, 仆人的, 耐用的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:services/d:serviced/i:servicing/p:serviced/3:services","sourceSentence":"After exhausting all available federal administrative remedies, the woman filed suit against the bus company in the trial court of the state in which the resort is located to enjoin the discontinuance by the bus company of its service to that area.","synonyms":["overhaul","inspection and repair","serving","service of process"]},{"term":"trial","ipa":"/tɹˈaɪəl/","syllables":"trial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 审判, 试验, 艰苦, 麻烦事, 考验；a. 审讯的, 试验性的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 审判, 试验, 艰苦, 麻烦事, 考验；a. 审讯的, 试验性的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:trials/p:trialled","sourceSentence":"After exhausting all available federal administrative remedies, the woman filed suit against the bus company in the trial court of the state in which the resort is located to enjoin the discontinuance by the bus company of its service to that area.","synonyms":["test","run","trial run","tryout"]},{"term":"suit","ipa":"/sˈut/","syllables":"suit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 套装, 诉讼, 请求, 起诉, 套, 组；vt. 适合, 使适应；vi. 合适, 相称","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 套装, 诉讼, 请求, 起诉, 套, 组；vt. 适合, 使适应；vi. 合适, 相称","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:suited/3:suits/s:suits/p:suited/i:suiting","sourceSentence":"After exhausting all available federal administrative remedies, the woman filed suit against the bus company in the trial court of the state in which the resort is located to enjoin the discontinuance by the bus company of its service to that area.","synonyms":["lawsuit","case","cause","causa"]},{"term":"challenged","ipa":"/tʃˈæləndʒd/","syllables":"chal·lenged","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 受到挑战的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 受到挑战的","wordForm":"词；原形 challenge；词形 0:challenge/1:dp/d:challenged/p:challenged","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The woman has standing because she can allege an injury-in-fact (loss of customers) that is caused by the challenged action (dropped bus route) and redressable by the remedy sought (injunction).","synonyms":["take exception","dispute","gainsay"]},{"term":"filed","ipa":"/fˈaɪld/","syllables":"filed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. [专利]申请；存档（file的过去分词形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. [专利]申请；存档（file的过去分词形式）","wordForm":"词；原形 file；词形 0:file/1:dp/p:filed/d:filed","sourceSentence":"After exhausting all available federal administrative remedies, the woman filed suit against the bus company in the trial court of the state in which the resort is located to enjoin the discontinuance by the bus company of its service to that area.","synonyms":["file cabinet","filing cabinet","data file","single file"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":10,"sourceQuestionPage":28,"sourceQuestionPages":[28],"sourceExplanationPages":[29,30],"sourceAnswerPages":[29,30],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-011","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"Due to the high cost of labor, several large corporations shut down their manufacturing plants in a state and moved their operations overseas. This caused severe and continuous unemployment in the state, which relies heavily on manufacturing jobs and manufactured goods to spur its economy. In response, the state legislature enacted a statute that requires certain businesses selling goods in the state to purchase goods produced in the state in an amount equal to at least half of the business's annual sales in the state. This statute only applies to businesses with annual sales in the state that exceed $3 million. A company that has annual sales in the state of $5 million challenges the state statute in federal court. Which constitutional provision provides the strongest ground on which to attack this statute?","choices":{"A":"The Article I commerce clause.","B":"The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause.","C":"The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause.","D":"The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The Article I commerce clause gives Congress extensive authority to regulate interstate commerce. This clause also carries a negative implication (ie, the dormant commerce clause), which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. Discrimination against interstate commerce—ie, favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests—is unconstitutional unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists. Here, the state statute discriminates against interstate commerce since it requires certain businesses to purchase substantial goods in the state at the expense of out-of-state competitors. The statute was enacted to relieve severe and continuous unemployment in the state. But since that interest is economic in nature, it cannot justify discrimination against interstate commerce. Therefore, the commerce clause is the strongest constitutional basis on which to attack this statute. (Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right are almost always found invalid under strict scrutiny. But since there is no fundamental right to engage in business, a due process challenge would likely fail. (Choice C) Discriminatory state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class generally violate the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But since no fundamental right is implicated and businesses are not a protected class, the statute likely satisfies this clause.\n\n(Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from infringing on the very limited rights of national citizenship—none of which are at issue here.\n\nEducational objective: State laws that favor in-state over out-of-state economic interests violate the dormant commerce clause unless (1) they further a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986) (explaining that the appropriate test for a dormant commerce clause challenge depends on whether the burden on interstate commerce is discriminatory or nondiscriminatory).","educationalObjective":"State laws that favor in-state over out-of-state economic interests violate the dormant commerce clause unless (1) they further a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986) (explaining that the appropriate test for a dormant commerce clause challenge depends on whether the burden on interstate commerce is discriminatory or nondiscriminatory).","rule":"State laws that favor in-state over out-of-state economic interests violate the dormant commerce clause unless (1) they further a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","irac":{"issue":"Which constitutional provision provides the strongest ground on which to attack this statute?","rule":"State laws that favor in-state over out-of-state economic interests violate the dormant commerce clause unless (1) they further a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","analysis":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The Article I commerce clause gives Congress extensive authority to regulate interstate commerce. This clause also carries a negative implication (ie, the dormant commerce clause), which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. Discrimination against interstate commerce—ie, favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests—is unconstitutional unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists. Here, the state statute discriminates against interstate commerce since it requires certain businesses to purchase substantial goods in the state at the expense of out-of-state competitors. The statute was enacted to relieve severe and continuous unemployment in the state. But since that interest is economic in nature, it cannot justify discrimination against interstate commerce. Therefore, the commerce clause is the strongest constitutional basis on which to attack this statute. (Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right are almost always found invalid under strict scrutiny. But since there is no fundamental right to engage in business, a due process challenge would likely fail. (Choice C) Discriminatory state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class generally violate the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But since no fundamental right is implicated and businesses are not a protected class, the statute likely satisfies this clause.\n\n(Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from infringing on the very limited rights of national citizenship—none of which are at issue here.","conclusion":"Answer A: The Article I commerce clause."},"questionZh":"由于劳动力成本高昂，几家大公司关闭了在某个州的制造工厂，并将业务转移到海外。这导致该州严重且持续的失业，该州严重依赖制造业就业和制成品来刺激经济。作为回应，州立法机关颁布了一项法规，要求某些在该州销售商品的企业购买该州生产的商品，金额至少相当于该企业在该州年销售额的一半。该法规仅适用于该州年销售额超过 300 万美元的企业。一家年销售额为 500 万美元的州公司向联邦法院挑战州法规。哪一项宪法条款为攻击该法规提供了最有力的依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。","B":"第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","C":"第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。","D":"第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款。"},"explanationZh":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（负担过重）超出了所有商业运输利益的本地范围。第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。该条款还具有负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）），它禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。对州际商业的歧视——即偏向州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益——是违宪的，除非（1）它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。在这里，州法规歧视州际贸易，因为它要求某些企业以牺牲州外竞争对手的利益为代价在州内购买大量商品。该法规的制定是为了缓解该州严重且持续的失业问题。但由于这种利益本质上是经济利益，因此不能成为歧视州际贸易的理由。因此，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）是攻击该法规的最有力的宪法依据。 （选项B）根据第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），对基本权利产生重大影响的州法律在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是被认定为无效。但由于没有从事商业活动的基本权利，正当程序挑战可能会失败。 （选项 C）对基本权利或受保护群体产生重大影响的歧视性州法律通常违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但由于不涉及任何基本权利，而且企业不是受保护群体，因此该法规可能满足这一条款。\n\n（选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州侵犯国民公民的非常有限的权利——这些权利都不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：有利于州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益的州法律违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非（1）它们促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986)（解释说，对消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）质疑的适当测试取决于州际商业的负担是歧视性的还是非歧视性的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"有利于州内而非州外经济利益的州法律违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非（1）它们促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。","iracZh":{"issue":"哪一项宪法条款为攻击该法规提供了最有力的依据？","rule":"有利于州内而非州外经济利益的州法律违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非（1）它们促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。","analysis":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（负担过重）超出了所有商业运输利益的本地范围。第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。该条款还具有负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）），它禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。对州际商业的歧视——即偏向州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益——是违宪的，除非（1）它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。在这里，州法规歧视州际贸易，因为它要求某些企业以牺牲州外竞争对手的利益为代价在州内购买大量商品。该法规的制定是为了缓解该州严重且持续的失业问题。但由于这种利益本质上是经济利益，因此不能成为歧视州际贸易的理由。因此，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）是攻击该法规的最有力的宪法依据。 （选项B）根据第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），对基本权利产生重大影响的州法律在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是被认定为无效。但由于没有从事商业活动的基本权利，正当程序挑战可能会失败。 （选项 C）对基本权利或受保护群体产生重大影响的歧视性州法律通常违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但由于不涉及任何基本权利，而且企业不是受保护群体，因此该法规可能满足这一条款。\n\n（选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州侵犯国民公民的非常有限的权利——这些权利都不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：有利于州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益的州法律违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非（1）它们促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986)（解释说，对消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）质疑的适当测试取决于州际商业的负担是歧视性的还是非歧视性的）。","conclusion":"答案 A：第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"strict scrutiny","ipa":"/stɹˈɪkt skɹˈutəni/","syllables":"strict scrutiny","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"严格审查：政府必须证明措施为实现重大利益所必需且狭义量身定制","contextualMeaning":"严格审查：政府必须证明措施为实现重大利益所必需且狭义量身定制","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right are almost always found invalid under strict scrutiny.","synonyms":["most exacting review","heightened constitutional review"]},{"term":"due process clause","ipa":"/dˈu pɹˈɑsˌɛs klˈɔz/","syllables":"due process clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"正当程序条款：限制政府未经适当程序剥夺生命、自由或财产","contextualMeaning":"正当程序条款：限制政府未经适当程序剥夺生命、自由或财产","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause.","synonyms":["due process article"]},{"term":"citizenship","ipa":"/sˈɪtɪzʌnʃˌɪp/","syllables":"cit·i·zen·ship","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 国籍, 市民权, 市民的身份；[法] 公民权, 公民资格, 公民身分","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 国籍, 市民权, 市民的身份；[法] 公民权, 公民资格, 公民身分","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:citizenships","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from infringing on the very limited rights of national citizenship—none of which are at issue here.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"privileges","ipa":"/pɹˈɪvlədʒəz/","syllables":"priv·i·leges","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 特权( privilege的名词复数 )； （因财富和社会地位而仅有部分人享有的）权益； 免责特权","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 特权( privilege的名词复数 )； （因财富和社会地位而仅有部分人享有的）权益； 免责特权","wordForm":"词；原形 privilege；词形 1:s3/0:privilege","sourceSentence":"The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause.","synonyms":["prerogative","perquisite","exclusive right","favor"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":11,"sourceQuestionPage":31,"sourceQuestionPages":[31],"sourceExplanationPages":[32,33],"sourceAnswerPages":[32,33],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-012","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"The United States Department of Energy regularly transports nuclear materials through a city on the way to a nuclear weapons processing plant it operates in a nearby state. The city recently adopted an ordinance prohibiting the transportation of any nuclear materials in or through the city. The ordinance declares that its purpose is to protect the health and safety of the residents of that city. May the Department of Energy continue to transport these nuclear materials through the city?","choices":{"A":"No, because the ordinance is rationally related to the public health and safety of the city's residents.","B":"No, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states certain unenumerated sovereign powers.","C":"Yes, because the Department of Energy is a federal agency engaged in a lawful federal function and, therefore, its activities may not be regulated by a local government without the consent of Congress.","D":"Yes, because the ordinance enacted by the city is invalid because it denies persons transporting such materials the equal protection of the laws."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations\n\nTax/regulation Definition Permissibility\n\nDirect Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities\n• Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal\n• Congress grants immunity or government\n• tax/regulation discriminates against or substantially interferes with federal duties Under the Article VI supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. A corollary of this principle is that the federal government— including its agencies and instrumentalities—need not comply with a local tax or regulation unless Congress consents. This is because local laws could hinder or destroy the operations of the federal government. Here, a city recently adopted an ordinance prohibiting the transportation of nuclear materials in or through the city. This hinders the operations of the U.S. Department of Energy since this federal agency is engaged in the lawful federal function of regularly transporting nuclear materials through the city. But since Congress did not consent to this regulation, the Department of Energy cannot be regulated by the city and may continue its operations. (Choices A & D) Under the equal protection clause, state and local laws that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny. The city's ordinance satisfies this standard since it is rationally related to the public health and safety of its residents. Nevertheless, the federal agency is not subject to this regulation without congressional consent. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment reserves to state and local governments all powers not given to the federal government by the Constitution—including the police power to protect residents' health and safety. But when exercising such powers, these entities must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, the supremacy clause).\n\nEducational objective: The federal government—including its agencies and instrumentalities—is not subject to state and local taxes or regulations in the absence of congressional consent.\n\nReferences\n• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819) (establishing that states cannot directly tax or regulate the federal government, its agencies, or its instrumentalities).\n\n• Envtl. Prot. Agency v. California ex rel. St. Water Res. Control Bd., 426 U.S. 200, 211 (1976) (holding that states can directly regulate a federal agency only when Congress consents).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d States, Territories, and Dependencies § 23 (2019) (explaining that federal agencies do not need to comply with state regulations).","educationalObjective":"The federal government—including its agencies and instrumentalities—is not subject to state and local taxes or regulations in the absence of congressional consent.","references":"• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819) (establishing that states cannot directly tax or regulate the federal government, its agencies, or its instrumentalities).\n\n• Envtl. Prot. Agency v. California ex rel. St. Water Res. Control Bd., 426 U.S. 200, 211 (1976) (holding that states can directly regulate a federal agency only when Congress consents).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d States, Territories, and Dependencies § 23 (2019) (explaining that federal agencies do not need to comply with state regulations).","rule":"The federal government—including its agencies and instrumentalities—is not subject to state and local taxes or regulations in the absence of congressional consent.","irac":{"issue":"May the Department of Energy continue to transport these nuclear materials through the city?","rule":"The federal government—including its agencies and instrumentalities—is not subject to state and local taxes or regulations in the absence of congressional consent.","analysis":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations\n\nTax/regulation Definition Permissibility\n\nDirect Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities\n• Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal\n• Congress grants immunity or government\n• tax/regulation discriminates against or substantially interferes with federal duties Under the Article VI supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. A corollary of this principle is that the federal government— including its agencies and instrumentalities—need not comply with a local tax or regulation unless Congress consents. This is because local laws could hinder or destroy the operations of the federal government. Here, a city recently adopted an ordinance prohibiting the transportation of nuclear materials in or through the city. This hinders the operations of the U.S. Department of Energy since this federal agency is engaged in the lawful federal function of regularly transporting nuclear materials through the city. But since Congress did not consent to this regulation, the Department of Energy cannot be regulated by the city and may continue its operations. (Choices A & D) Under the equal protection clause, state and local laws that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class (as seen here) are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny. The city's ordinance satisfies this standard since it is rationally related to the public health and safety of its residents. Nevertheless, the federal agency is not subject to this regulation without congressional consent. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment reserves to state and local governments all powers not given to the federal government by the Constitution—including the police power to protect residents' health and safety. But when exercising such powers, these entities must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, the supremacy clause).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the Department of Energy is a federal agency engaged in a lawful federal function and, therefore, its activities may not be regulated by a local government without the consent of Congress."},"questionZh":"美国能源部定期通过一个城市将核材料运送到其在附近州运营的核武器加工厂。该市最近通过了一项法令，禁止在该市内或通过该市运输任何核材料。该条例宣称其目的是保护该城市居民的健康和安全。能源部可以继续通过城市运输这些核材料吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该条例与城市居民的公共卫生和安全合理相关。","B":"不，因为第十修正案保留给各州某些未列举的主权权力。","C":"是的，因为能源部是履行合法联邦职能的联邦机构，因此，未经国会同意，地方政府不得对其活动进行监管。","D":"是的，因为该市颁布的法令是无效的，因为它剥夺了运输此类材料的人受到法律的平等保护。"},"explanationZh":"联邦免受州和地方税收/法规的影响\n\n税收/法规 定义 许可\n\n直接适用于联邦政府——豁免，除非： 包括其机构和部门\n• 国会同意税收/监管 间接 适用于附属机构——包括 不豁免，除非： 受雇于联邦或与联邦有业务往来的个人/实体\n• 国会授予豁免权或政府\n• 税收/监管歧视或严重干扰联邦义务 根据第六条至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约是该国的最高法律。这一原则的推论是，除非国会同意，否则联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）无需遵守地方税收或法规。这是因为当地法律可能会阻碍或破坏联邦政府的运作。在这里，一座城市最近通过了一项法令，禁止在该城市内或经过该城市运输核材料。这阻碍了美国能源部的运作，因为该联邦机构负责定期在该市运输核材料的合法联邦职能。但由于国会不同意这项规定，能源部无法受到该市的监管，可能会继续运作。 （选择 A 和 D）根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），不会对基本权利或受保护群体（如此处所示）产生重大影响的州和地方法律仅受到合理基础的审查。该市的条例满足这一标准，因为它与居民的公共健康和安全合理相关。然而，未经国会同意，联邦机构不受该法规的约束。 （选项B）第十修正案向州和地方政府保留宪法未赋予联邦政府的所有权力，包括保护居民健康和安全的警察权力。但在行使此类权力时，这些实体必须遵守其他宪法条款（例如至上条款）。\n\n教育目标：未经国会同意，联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）不受州和地方税收或法规的约束。\n\n参考文献\n• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819)（规定各州不能直接向联邦政府、其机构或其机构征税或对其进行监管）。\n\n• 环境。普罗特。机构诉加利福尼亚州前关系。圣沃特水库。 Control Bd., 426 U.S. 200, 211 (1976)（认为只有在国会同意的情况下，各州才能直接监管联邦机构）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 州、领地和属地 § 23 (2019)（解释联邦机构不需要遵守州法规）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"未经国会同意，联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）不受州和地方税收或法规的约束。","iracZh":{"issue":"能源部可以继续通过城市运输这些核材料吗？","rule":"未经国会同意，联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）不受州和地方税收或法规的约束。","analysis":"联邦免受州和地方税收/法规的影响\n\n税收/法规 定义 许可\n\n直接适用于联邦政府——豁免，除非： 包括其机构和部门\n• 国会同意税收/监管 间接 适用于附属机构——包括 不豁免，除非： 受雇于联邦或与联邦有业务往来的个人/实体\n• 国会授予豁免权或政府\n• 税收/监管歧视或严重干扰联邦义务 根据第六条至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约是该国的最高法律。这一原则的推论是，除非国会同意，否则联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）无需遵守地方税收或法规。这是因为当地法律可能会阻碍或破坏联邦政府的运作。在这里，一座城市最近通过了一项法令，禁止在该城市内或经过该城市运输核材料。这阻碍了美国能源部的运作，因为该联邦机构负责定期在该市运输核材料的合法联邦职能。但由于国会不同意这项规定，能源部无法受到该市的监管，可能会继续运作。 （选择 A 和 D）根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），不会对基本权利或受保护群体（如此处所示）产生重大影响的州和地方法律仅受到合理基础的审查。该市的条例满足这一标准，因为它与居民的公共健康和安全合理相关。然而，未经国会同意，联邦机构不受该法规的约束。 （选项B）第十修正案向州和地方政府保留宪法未赋予联邦政府的所有权力，包括保护居民健康和安全的警察权力。但在行使此类权力时，这些实体必须遵守其他宪法条款（例如至上条款）。\n\n教育目标：未经国会同意，联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）不受州和地方税收或法规的约束。\n\n参考文献\n• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819)（规定各州不能直接向联邦政府、其机构或其机构征税或对其进行监管）。\n\n• 环境。普罗特。机构诉加利福尼亚州前关系。圣沃特水库。 Control Bd., 426 U.S. 200, 211 (1976)（认为只有在国会同意的情况下，各州才能直接监管联邦机构）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 州、领地和属地 § 23 (2019)（解释联邦机构不需要遵守州法规）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为能源部是履行合法联邦职能的联邦机构，因此，未经国会同意，地方政府不得对其活动进行监管。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"sovereign","ipa":"/sˈɑvɹən/","syllables":"sov·er·eign","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 元首, 独立国；a. 具有主权的, 至高无上的, 国王的, 完全的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 元首, 独立国；a. 具有主权的, 至高无上的, 国王的, 完全的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:sovereigns","sourceSentence":"No, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states certain unenumerated sovereign powers.","synonyms":["crowned head","monarch","supreme","autonomous"]},{"term":"duties","ipa":"/dˈutiz/","syllables":"du·ties","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 关税（duty的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 关税（duty的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 duty；词形 0:duty/1:s","sourceSentence":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations Tax/regulation Definition Permissibility Direct Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities • Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal • Congress grants immunity or government • tax/regulation discriminates","synonyms":["responsibility","obligation","tariff"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":12,"sourceQuestionPage":34,"sourceQuestionPages":[34],"sourceExplanationPages":[35,36],"sourceAnswerPages":[35,36],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-013","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"National statistics revealed a dramatic increase in the number of elementary and secondary school students bringing controlled substances to school for sale. In response, Congress enacted a statute requiring each state's legislature to enact a law making it a crime for any person to sell, within 1,000 feet of any elementary or secondary school, any controlled substance that had previously been transported in interstate commerce. Is the federal statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress has no authority to require a state legislature to enact any specified legislation.","B":"No, because the sale of a controlled substance in close proximity to a school does not have a sufficiently close nexus to interstate commerce to justify its regulation by Congress.","C":"Yes, because Congress possesses broad authority under the commerce clause to regulate any activities affecting education that also have, in inseverable aggregates, a substantial effect on interstate commerce.","D":"Yes, because it contains a jurisdictional provision that will ensure, on a case-by-case basis, that any particular controlled substance subject to the terms of this statute will in fact affect interstate commerce."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The Article I commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. But when exercising this power, Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions. This includes the Tenth Amendment, which prohibits Congress from commandeering state or local legislatures by requiring them to enact laws (or to enforce federal laws). Here, the federal statute requires state legislatures to criminalize the sale, near a school, of any controlled substance that had previously been transported in interstate commerce. But even if Congress could regulate this conduct pursuant to its commerce power, it has no authority to require a state legislature to enact any specified legislation. Therefore, the federal statute is unconstitutional. (Choices B & C) Congress can regulate any in-state activity that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially impacts interstate commerce. Here, national statistics show a dramatic increase in the number of students selling controlled substances at school. This suggests that the combined (ie, aggregated) sales have a substantial effect on interstate commerce. As a result, Congress can likely regulate that activity—but it cannot require states to do so. (Choice D) The commerce clause also empowers Congress to regulate instrumentalities of, and persons or things moving in, interstate commerce—including controlled substances that have been transported in interstate commerce (as seen here). Nevertheless, the statute is invalid since it violates the Tenth Amendment.\n\nEducational objective: The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment prohibits Congress from requiring state or local legislatures to enact laws (or to enforce federal laws) that do so.\n\nReferences\n• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992) (holding that Congress cannot require state legislatures to enact or enforce certain legislation).","educationalObjective":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment prohibits Congress from requiring state or local legislatures to enact laws (or to enforce federal laws) that do so.","references":"• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992) (holding that Congress cannot require state legislatures to enact or enforce certain legislation).","rule":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment prohibits Congress from requiring state or local legislatures to enact laws (or to enforce federal laws) that do so.","irac":{"issue":"Is the federal statute constitutional?","rule":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment prohibits Congress from requiring state or local legislatures to enact laws (or to enforce federal laws) that do so.","analysis":"The Article I commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. But when exercising this power, Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions. This includes the Tenth Amendment, which prohibits Congress from commandeering state or local legislatures by requiring them to enact laws (or to enforce federal laws). Here, the federal statute requires state legislatures to criminalize the sale, near a school, of any controlled substance that had previously been transported in interstate commerce. But even if Congress could regulate this conduct pursuant to its commerce power, it has no authority to require a state legislature to enact any specified legislation. Therefore, the federal statute is unconstitutional. (Choices B & C) Congress can regulate any in-state activity that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially impacts interstate commerce. Here, national statistics show a dramatic increase in the number of students selling controlled substances at school. This suggests that the combined (ie, aggregated) sales have a substantial effect on interstate commerce. As a result, Congress can likely regulate that activity—but it cannot require states to do so. (Choice D) The commerce clause also empowers Congress to regulate instrumentalities of, and persons or things moving in, interstate commerce—including controlled substances that have been transported in interstate commerce (as seen here). Nevertheless, the statute is invalid since it violates the Tenth Amendment.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because Congress has no authority to require a state legislature to enact any specified legislation."},"questionZh":"国家统计数据显示，携带管制药物到学校出售的中小学生人数急剧增加。作为回应，国会颁布了一项法规，要求每个州的立法机关颁布一项法律，规定任何人在任何小学或中学 1,000 英尺范围内出售任何以前在州际贸易中运输的受管制物质均属犯罪。联邦法规是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会无权要求州立法机关颁布任何特定的立法。","B":"不会，因为在学校附近销售受管制物质与州际贸易没有足够密切的联系，不足以证明国会对其监管的合理性。","C":"是的，因为根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause），国会拥有广泛的权力来监管影响教育的任何活动，这些活动在不可分割的总体上也会对州际商业产生重大影响。","D":"是的，因为它包含一项管辖权条款，将根据具体情况确保任何受本法规条款约束的特定受控物质实际上会影响州际贸易。"},"explanationZh":"第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业，即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法规定。这包括第十修正案，该修正案禁止国会通过要求州或地方立法机构颁布法律（或执行联邦法律）来支配它们。在这里，联邦法规要求州立法机构将在学校附近销售任何先前在州际贸易中运输的受管制物质定为犯罪。但即使国会可以根据其商业权力规范这种行为，它也无权要求州立法机关制定任何特定的立法。因此，该联邦法规是违宪的。 （选择 B 和 C）国会可以监管任何单独或总体上对州际商业产生重大影响的州内活动。国家统计数据显示，在学校出售管制药物的学生人数急剧增加。这表明合并（即合计）销售额对州际贸易具有重大影响。因此，国会可能会监管该活动，但不能要求各州这样做。 （选项 D）商务条款还授权国会监管州际商务的工具以及州际商务中流动的人员或物品，包括在州际商务中运输的受控物质（如此处所示）。然而，该法规因违反第十修正案而无效。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业。但第十修正案禁止国会要求州或地方立法机构颁布这样做的法律（或执行联邦法律）。\n\n参考文献\n• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992)（认为国会不能要求州立法机构颁布或执行某些立法）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业。但第十修正案禁止国会要求州或地方立法机构颁布这样做的法律（或执行联邦法律）。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法规是否符合宪法？","rule":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业。但第十修正案禁止国会要求州或地方立法机构颁布这样做的法律（或执行联邦法律）。","analysis":"第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业，即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法规定。这包括第十修正案，该修正案禁止国会通过要求州或地方立法机构颁布法律（或执行联邦法律）来支配它们。在这里，联邦法规要求州立法机构将在学校附近销售任何先前在州际贸易中运输的受管制物质定为犯罪。但即使国会可以根据其商业权力规范这种行为，它也无权要求州立法机关制定任何特定的立法。因此，该联邦法规是违宪的。 （选择 B 和 C）国会可以监管任何单独或总体上对州际商业产生重大影响的州内活动。国家统计数据显示，在学校出售管制药物的学生人数急剧增加。这表明合并（即合计）销售额对州际贸易具有重大影响。因此，国会可能会监管该活动，但不能要求各州这样做。 （选项 D）商务条款还授权国会监管州际商务的工具以及州际商务中流动的人员或物品，包括在州际商务中运输的受控物质（如此处所示）。然而，该法规因违反第十修正案而无效。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业。但第十修正案禁止国会要求州或地方立法机构颁布这样做的法律（或执行联邦法律）。\n\n参考文献\n• New York v. United States, 505 U.S. 144, 161 (1992)（认为国会不能要求州立法机构颁布或执行某些立法）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为国会无权要求州立法机关颁布任何特定的立法。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"jurisdictional","ipa":"/dʒˌʊɹʌsdˈɪkʃənəl/","syllables":"ju·ris·dic·tional","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 司法权的, 裁决权的, 管辖权的；[法] 司法权的, 裁判权的, 管辖权的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 司法权的, 裁决权的, 管辖权的；[法] 司法权的, 裁判权的, 管辖权的","wordForm":"词；词形 s:jurisdictionals","sourceSentence":"Yes, because it contains a jurisdictional provision that will ensure, on a case-by-case basis, that any particular controlled substance subject to the terms of this statute will in fact affect interstate commerce.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"commandeering","ipa":"/kˌɑmʌndˈɪɹ/","syllables":"com·man·deer·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 征用( commandeer的现在分词 )； 强占","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 征用( commandeer的现在分词 )； 强占","wordForm":"词；原形 commandeer；词形 0:commandeer/1:i","sourceSentence":"This includes the Tenth Amendment, which prohibits Congress from commandeering state or local legislatures by requiring them to enact laws (or to enforce federal laws).","synonyms":["hijack","highjack","pirate"]},{"term":"criminalize","ipa":"/kɹˈɪmʌnʌlˌaɪz/","syllables":"crim·i·nal·ize","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 使有罪, 定罪","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[法] 使有罪, 定罪","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:criminalized/i:criminalizing/p:criminalized/3:criminalizes","sourceSentence":"Here, the federal statute requires state legislatures to criminalize the sale, near a school, of any controlled substance that had previously been transported in interstate commerce.","synonyms":["outlaw","criminalise","illegalize","illegalise"]},{"term":"controlled","ipa":"/kʌntɹˈoʊld/","syllables":"con·trolled","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 受约束的, 克制的；[法] 受管制的, 受控制的, 受管辖的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 受约束的, 克制的；[法] 受管制的, 受控制的, 受管辖的","wordForm":"形容词；原形 control；词形 0:control/1:dp/p:controlled/d:controlled","sourceSentence":"National statistics revealed a dramatic increase in the number of elementary and secondary school students bringing controlled substances to school for sale.","synonyms":["controller","restraint","command","mastery"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-3570284481164771"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":13,"sourceQuestionPage":37,"sourceQuestionPages":[37],"sourceExplanationPages":[38,39],"sourceAnswerPages":[38,39],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-014","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"Radon is a harmful gas found in the soil of certain regions of the United States. A state statute requires occupants of residences with basements susceptible to the intrusion of radon to have their residences tested for the presence of radon and to take specified remedial steps if the test indicates the presence of radon above specified levels. The statute also provides that the testing for radon may be done only by testers licensed by a state agency. According to the statute, a firm may be licensed to test for radon only if it meets specified rigorous standards relating to the accuracy of its testing. These standards may easily be achieved with current technology; but the technology required to meet them is 50% more expensive than the technology required to measure radon accumulations in a slightly less accurate manner. The United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) does not license radon testers. However, a federal statute authorizes the EPA to advise on the accuracy of various methods of radon testing and to provide to the general public a list of testers that use methods it believes to be reasonably accurate. A recently established firm in the state uses a testing method that the EPA has stated is reasonably accurate. The firm is also included by the EPA on the list of testers using methods of testing it believes to be reasonably accurate. The firm applies for a state radon- testing license, but its application is denied because the firm cannot demonstrate that the method it uses to test for radon is sufficiently accurate to meet the rigorous state statutory standards. The firm sues appropriate state officials in federal court claiming that the state may not constitutionally exclude the firm from performing the required radon tests in the state. Who will the court likely rule in favor of in this suit?","choices":{"A":"The firm, because the full faith and credit clause of the Constitution requires the state to respect and give effect to the action of the EPA in including the firm on its list of testers that use reasonably accurate methods.","B":"The firm, because the supremacy clause of the Constitution requires the state to respect and give effect to the action of the EPA in including the firm on its list of testers that use reasonably accurate methods.","C":"The state, because radon exposure is limited to basement areas that, by their very nature, cannot move in interstate commerce.","D":"The state, because the federal statute and the action of the EPA in including the firm on its list of testers that use reasonably accurate methods are not inconsistent with the more rigorous state licensing requirement, and that requirement is reasonably related to a legitimate public interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Preemption\n\n(federal law supersedes state law)\n\nType Applicability\n\nExpress\n• Constitution grants federal government exclusive power to regulate area or\n• Federal law expressly prohibits state regulation in same area Implied Field preemption:\n\n• Congressional intent to occupy entire field inferred from pervasive regulations that leave no room for state involvement Conflict preemption:\n\n• Direct conflict makes it impossible (or nearly impossible) to comply with federal & state law or\n• Indirect conflict frustrates accomplishment of federal law's purpose The federal government and states can regulate the same subject matter. But the Article VI supremacy clause renders a state law void when a federal law expressly or impliedly preempts it. Here, the federal statute authorizing the EPA's actions does not expressly preempt the state statute since neither the Constitution nor federal law explicitly forbids the state from regulating radon testers. And there is no implied preemption because:\n• Congress only regulated the accuracy—not the entire field—of radon testing (no field preemption)\n\n• radon testers can comply with both federal and state requirements (no direct conflict) and\n\n• the state statute does not frustrate the federal law or EPA's purpose to prevent radon poisoning (no indirect conflict).\n\nSince the federal statute and the EPA's actions are not inconsistent with the more rigorous state licensing requirement, they do not preempt the state statute. Therefore, the state does not need to give effect to the EPA's inclusion of the firm on its list of testers (Choice B). The state's exclusion of the firm also complies with substantive due process, which requires all laws to at least satisfy rational basis scrutiny. That is because the state's testing requirement is reasonably related to the state's legitimate interest in preventing radon poisoning. Therefore, the court will likely rule in the state's favor.\n\n(Choice A) Under the Article IV full faith and credit clause, a state must respect public acts (eg, legislation), records, and judicial proceedings of other states—not federal actions. (Choice C) The commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate persons and things moving through interstate commerce. And though radon exposure is limited to immovable basement areas, Congress may still regulate radon testing since radon testers can move between states. But the state can also regulate radon testing since there is no preemption.\n\nEducational objective: Under the supremacy clause, a state law is void if it is expressly or impliedly preempted by a federal law. And all state laws must at least be reasonably related to a legitimate state interest to comply with substantive due process.\n\nReferences\n• Gade v. Nat'l Solid Wastes Mgmt. Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992) (explaining when federal law expressly or impliedly preempts state law).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 234 (2019) (setting forth the preemption tests).","educationalObjective":"Under the supremacy clause, a state law is void if it is expressly or impliedly preempted by a federal law. And all state laws must at least be reasonably related to a legitimate state interest to comply with substantive due process.","references":"• Gade v. Nat'l Solid Wastes Mgmt. Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992) (explaining when federal law expressly or impliedly preempts state law).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 234 (2019) (setting forth the preemption tests).","rule":"Under the supremacy clause, a state law is void if it is expressly or impliedly preempted by a federal law. And all state laws must at least be reasonably related to a legitimate state interest to comply with substantive due process.","irac":{"issue":"Who will the court likely rule in favor of in this suit?","rule":"Under the supremacy clause, a state law is void if it is expressly or impliedly preempted by a federal law. And all state laws must at least be reasonably related to a legitimate state interest to comply with substantive due process.","analysis":"Preemption\n\n(federal law supersedes state law)\n\nType Applicability\n\nExpress\n• Constitution grants federal government exclusive power to regulate area or\n• Federal law expressly prohibits state regulation in same area Implied Field preemption:\n\n• Congressional intent to occupy entire field inferred from pervasive regulations that leave no room for state involvement Conflict preemption:\n\n• Direct conflict makes it impossible (or nearly impossible) to comply with federal & state law or\n• Indirect conflict frustrates accomplishment of federal law's purpose The federal government and states can regulate the same subject matter. But the Article VI supremacy clause renders a state law void when a federal law expressly or impliedly preempts it. Here, the federal statute authorizing the EPA's actions does not expressly preempt the state statute since neither the Constitution nor federal law explicitly forbids the state from regulating radon testers. And there is no implied preemption because:\n• Congress only regulated the accuracy—not the entire field—of radon testing (no field preemption)\n\n• radon testers can comply with both federal and state requirements (no direct conflict) and\n\n• the state statute does not frustrate the federal law or EPA's purpose to prevent radon poisoning (no indirect conflict).\n\nSince the federal statute and the EPA's actions are not inconsistent with the more rigorous state licensing requirement, they do not preempt the state statute. Therefore, the state does not need to give effect to the EPA's inclusion of the firm on its list of testers (Choice B). The state's exclusion of the firm also complies with substantive due process, which requires all laws to at least satisfy rational basis scrutiny. That is because the state's testing requirement is reasonably related to the state's legitimate interest in preventing radon poisoning. Therefore, the court will likely rule in the state's favor.\n\n(Choice A) Under the Article IV full faith and credit clause, a state must respect public acts (eg, legislation), records, and judicial proceedings of other states—not federal actions. (Choice C) The commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate persons and things moving through interstate commerce. And though radon exposure is limited to immovable basement areas, Congress may still regulate radon testing since radon testers can move between states. But the state can also regulate radon testing since there is no preemption.","conclusion":"Answer D: The state, because the federal statute and the action of the EPA in including the firm on its list of testers that use reasonably accurate methods are not inconsistent with the more rigorous state licensing requirement, and that requirement is reasonably related to a legitimate public interest."},"questionZh":"氡气是在美国某些地区的土壤中发现的一种有害气体。州法规要求地下室易受氡气侵入的住宅的居住者对其住宅进行氡气检测，如果测试表明氡气浓度超过规定水平，则采取指定的补救措施。该法规还规定，氡气测试只能由国家机构许可的测试人员进行。根据该法规，只有满足与测试准确性相关的指定严格标准的公司才能获得氡气测试许可。利用现有技术可以轻松实现这些标准；但满足这些要求所需的技术比以稍微不太准确的方式测量氡气积累所需的技术昂贵 50%。美国环境保护署 (EPA) 没有获得氡气测试仪的许可。然而，一项联邦法规授权美国环保局就各种氡气测试方法的准确性提出建议，并向公众提供一份使用其认为相当准确的方法的测试人员名单。该州一家最近成立的公司使用了 EPA 声称相当准确的测试方法。该公司还被 EPA 列入使用其认为相当准确的测试方法的测试人员名单中。该公司申请了州氡气测试许可证，但其申请被拒绝，因为该公司无法证明其用于测试氡气的方法足够准确，足以满足严格的州法定标准。该公司在联邦法院起诉相应的州官员，声称该州不得根据宪法排除该公司在该州进行所需的氡气测试。法院可能会在这起诉讼中做出有利于谁的裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"该公司，因为宪法的完整信用条款要求州尊重并实施 EPA 的行动，将该公司纳入其使用相当准确方法的测试人员名单中。","B":"公司，因为宪法的至高无上条款要求国家尊重并实施 EPA 的行动，将公司纳入其使用相当准确方法的测试人员名单中。","C":"该州，因为氡暴露仅限于地下室区域，从本质上讲，这些区域无法在州际贸易中移动。","D":"州，因为联邦法规和 EPA 将该公司纳入其使用相当准确方法的测试人员名单的行动，与更严格的州许可要求并不矛盾，而且该要求与合法的公共利益合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"抢占\n\n（联邦法律取代州法律）\n\n类型适用性\n\n快递\n• 宪法赋予联邦政府管理地区或地区的专属权力\n• 联邦法律明确禁止同一领域的州监管 隐含字段优先：\n\n• 国会有意占领整个领域，这是从普遍存在的法规中推断出来的，这些法规没有给国家参与留出空间 冲突先发制人：\n\n• 直接冲突使得不可能（或几乎不可能）遵守联邦和州法律或\n• 间接冲突阻碍联邦法律目的的实现 联邦政府和各州可以监管相同的主题。但是，当联邦法律明示或暗示优先于州法律时，第六条至上条款将使州法律无效。在这里，授权美国环保局行动的联邦法规并没有明确优先于州法规，因为宪法和联邦法律都没有明确禁止州监管氡气测试仪。并且不存在隐含的抢占，因为：\n• 国会仅规定氡气测试的准确性（而非整个领域）（无现场优先权）\n\n• 氡气测试仪可以同时符合联邦和州的要求（无直接冲突）并且\n\n• 州法规不会妨碍联邦法律或EPA 预防氡气中毒的目的（无间接冲突）。\n\n由于联邦法规和环保局的行动与更严格的州许可要求并不矛盾，因此它们不会优先于州法规。因此，国家不需要批准 EPA 将公司列入其测试者名单（选择 B）。国家对企业的排除也符合实质性正当程序，这要求所有法律至少满足合理基础的审查。这是因为国家的检测要求与国家预防氡中毒的合法利益合理相关。因此，法院可能会做出有利于该州的裁决。\n\n（选项A）根据第四条完全诚信条款，州必须尊重其他州的公共行为（例如立法）、记录和司法程序，而不是联邦行为。 （选项 C） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管通过州际商业流动的人员和物品。尽管氡气暴露仅限于不可移动的地下室区域，但国会仍可能对氡气测试进行监管，因为氡气测试人员可以在各州之间流动。但国家也可以监管氡气测试，因为没有先发制人的权力。\n\n教育目标：根据至上条款，如果州法律明示或暗示地被联邦法律取代，则该州法律无效。所有州法律至少必须与合法的国家利益合理相关，以遵守实质性正当程序。\n\n参考文献\n• Gade 诉国家固体废物管理公司。 Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992)（解释联邦法律何时明示或暗示优先于州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 234 条 (2019)（规定了优先权测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据至上条款，如果州法律明示或暗示地被联邦法律取代，则该州法律无效。所有州法律至少必须与合法的国家利益合理相关，以遵守实质性正当程序。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会在这起诉讼中做出有利于谁的裁决？","rule":"根据至上条款，如果州法律明示或暗示地被联邦法律取代，则该州法律无效。所有州法律至少必须与合法的国家利益合理相关，以遵守实质性正当程序。","analysis":"抢占\n\n（联邦法律取代州法律）\n\n类型适用性\n\n快递\n• 宪法赋予联邦政府管理地区或地区的专属权力\n• 联邦法律明确禁止同一领域的州监管 隐含字段优先：\n\n• 国会有意占领整个领域，这是从普遍存在的法规中推断出来的，这些法规没有给国家参与留出空间 冲突先发制人：\n\n• 直接冲突使得不可能（或几乎不可能）遵守联邦和州法律或\n• 间接冲突阻碍联邦法律目的的实现 联邦政府和各州可以监管相同的主题。但是，当联邦法律明示或暗示优先于州法律时，第六条至上条款将使州法律无效。在这里，授权美国环保局行动的联邦法规并没有明确优先于州法规，因为宪法和联邦法律都没有明确禁止州监管氡气测试仪。并且不存在隐含的抢占，因为：\n• 国会仅规定氡气测试的准确性（而非整个领域）（无现场优先权）\n\n• 氡气测试仪可以同时符合联邦和州的要求（无直接冲突）并且\n\n• 州法规不会妨碍联邦法律或EPA 预防氡气中毒的目的（无间接冲突）。\n\n由于联邦法规和环保局的行动与更严格的州许可要求并不矛盾，因此它们不会优先于州法规。因此，国家不需要批准 EPA 将公司列入其测试者名单（选择 B）。国家对企业的排除也符合实质性正当程序，这要求所有法律至少满足合理基础的审查。这是因为国家的检测要求与国家预防氡中毒的合法利益合理相关。因此，法院可能会做出有利于该州的裁决。\n\n（选项A）根据第四条完全诚信条款，州必须尊重其他州的公共行为（例如立法）、记录和司法程序，而不是联邦行为。 （选项 C） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管通过州际商业流动的人员和物品。尽管氡气暴露仅限于不可移动的地下室区域，但国会仍可能对氡气测试进行监管，因为氡气测试人员可以在各州之间流动。但国家也可以监管氡气测试，因为没有先发制人的权力。\n\n教育目标：根据至上条款，如果州法律明示或暗示地被联邦法律取代，则该州法律无效。所有州法律至少必须与合法的国家利益合理相关，以遵守实质性正当程序。\n\n参考文献\n• Gade 诉国家固体废物管理公司。 Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992)（解释联邦法律何时明示或暗示优先于州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 234 条 (2019)（规定了优先权测试）。","conclusion":"答案 D：州，因为联邦法规和 EPA 将该公司纳入其使用相当准确方法的测试人员名单的行动，与更严格的州许可要求并不矛盾，而且该要求与合法的公共利益合理相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"substantive due process","ipa":"/sˈəbstəntɪv dˈu pɹˈɑsˌɛs/","syllables":"sub·stan·tive due process","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实体性正当程序：限制政府无充分理由侵犯特定基本自由","contextualMeaning":"实体性正当程序：限制政府无充分理由侵犯特定基本自由","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"And all state laws must at least be reasonably related to a legitimate state interest to comply with substantive due process.","synonyms":["substantive due physical process","substantive due procedure","substantive due outgrowth"]},{"term":"full faith and credit","ipa":"/fˈʊl fˈeɪθ ənd kɹˈɛdət/","syllables":"full faith and credit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"充分信任与尊重：州须承认其他州的公共行为、记录和司法程序","contextualMeaning":"充分信任与尊重：州须承认其他州的公共行为、记录和司法程序","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The firm, because the full faith and credit clause of the Constitution requires the state to respect and give effect to the action of the EPA in including the firm on its list of testers that use reasonably accurate methods.","synonyms":["full faith and course credit","full faith and credit rating","full faith and recognition"]},{"term":"appropriate","ipa":"/ʌpɹˈoʊpɹiət/","syllables":"ap·pro·pri·ate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 适当的；[经] 适当的, 拨出, 占用","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 适当的；[经] 适当的, 拨出, 占用","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:appropriated/3:appropriates/p:appropriated/i:appropriating","sourceSentence":"The firm sues appropriate state officials in federal court claiming that the state may not constitutionally exclude the firm from performing the required radon tests in the state.","synonyms":["allow","earmark","set aside","reserve"]},{"term":"proceedings","ipa":"/pɹoʊsˈidɪŋz/","syllables":"pro·ceed·ings","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 诉讼, 事项, 行径, 事件, 活动, 项目, 会议录, 记录汇编, 活动记录","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 诉讼, 事项, 行径, 事件, 活动, 项目, 会议录, 记录汇编, 活动记录","wordForm":"词；原形 proceeding；词形 0:proceeding/1:s","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Under the Article IV full faith and credit clause, a state must respect public acts (eg, legislation), records, and judicial proceedings of other states—not federal actions.","synonyms":["legal proceeding","proceedings"]},{"term":"remedial","ipa":"/ɹɪmˈidiəl/","syllables":"re·me·dial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 治疗的, 补救的, 矫正的；[医] 治疗的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 治疗的, 补救的, 矫正的；[医] 治疗的","wordForm":"词；词形 s:remedials","sourceSentence":"A state statute requires occupants of residences with basements susceptible to the intrusion of radon to have their residences tested for the presence of radon and to take specified remedial steps if the test indicates the presence of radon above specified levels.","synonyms":["curative","healing","alterative","sanative"]},{"term":"judicial","ipa":"/dʒudˈɪʃəl/","syllables":"ju·di·cial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 法庭的, 公正的, 审判上的, 司法的；[法] 司法的, 审判上的, 法官的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 法庭的, 公正的, 审判上的, 司法的；[法] 司法的, 审判上的, 法官的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Under the Article IV full faith and credit clause, a state must respect public acts (eg, legislation), records, and judicial proceedings of other states—not federal actions.","synonyms":["discriminative","juridical","juridic"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":14,"sourceQuestionPage":40,"sourceQuestionPages":[40],"sourceExplanationPages":[41,42],"sourceAnswerPages":[41,42],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-015","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"State A requires commercial airlines to pay an annual property tax based on the value of the airline's fleet of planes that operate in the state. The tax is calculated by multiplying the percentage of the airline's annual arrivals and departures in the state by the fleet's overall value. The airline's fleet of planes is based in State B. Ten percent of the airline's annual arrivals and departures occur in State A. The airline has sued to challenge the State A tax, arguing that it is unconstitutional. Is the state tax likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress has the sole power to tax property moving in interstate commerce.","B":"No, because the airline's fleet is based in State B.","C":"Yes, because it does not place an undue burden on interstate commerce in violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause.","D":"Yes, because states have plenary authority to construct their tax system in any manner they choose."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A state tax on the value of property in the state (ie, ad valorem property tax) is generally valid. But when this property moves in interstate commerce (eg, planes), the state tax must comply with the negative implication of the commerce clause.* This prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. As a result, this type of state tax is valid when:\n• the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed property, which is established when the property has sufficient contacts with the state such that the property has acquired a taxable situs in the state\n\n• the tax is fairly apportioned based on the amount of time that the taxed property is in the state\n\n• the tax is not discriminatory against interstate commerce and\n\n• the tax is fairly related to the benefits or services provided by the taxing state (Choice A).\n\nHere, there is a substantial nexus between State A and the airline since 10% of the airline's annual arrivals and departures occur in the state (taxable situs) (Choice B). The tax is fairly apportioned because it is based on the percentage of the airline's annual arrivals and departures in the state. It is nondiscriminatory since it applies equally to intrastate and interstate commerce. And the tax is fairly related to the benefits provided by the state (eg, use of airport). Therefore, the state tax does not unduly burden interstate commerce and is likely constitutional. *A state tax on property permanently located in another state is unconstitutional since the taxing state lacks jurisdiction over the property. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including the power to construct a state tax system. But this power is not plenary (ie, absolute) because states must comply with other constitutional provisions.\n\nEducational objective: A state tax on property moving in interstate commerce is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed property such that the property has a taxable situs in the state, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.\n\nReferences\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state tax violates the commerce clause).\n\n• Braniff Airways, Inc. v. Neb. State Bd. of Equalization and Assessment, 347 U.S. 590, 599–601 (1954) (holding that a state ad valorem property tax on an airline's fleet was valid since the airline had a taxable situs in the state).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 163 (2020) (explaining that states may levy taxes on instrumentalities of commerce when they have a taxable situs in the state and the tax is fairly apportioned to their time in the state).","educationalObjective":"A state tax on property moving in interstate commerce is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed property such that the property has a taxable situs in the state, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.","references":"• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state tax violates the commerce clause).\n\n• Braniff Airways, Inc. v. Neb. State Bd. of Equalization and Assessment, 347 U.S. 590, 599–601 (1954) (holding that a state ad valorem property tax on an airline's fleet was valid since the airline had a taxable situs in the state).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 163 (2020) (explaining that states may levy taxes on instrumentalities of commerce when they have a taxable situs in the state and the tax is fairly apportioned to their time in the state).","rule":"A state tax on property moving in interstate commerce is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed property such that the property has a taxable situs in the state, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state tax likely constitutional?","rule":"A state tax on property moving in interstate commerce is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed property such that the property has a taxable situs in the state, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.","analysis":"A state tax on the value of property in the state (ie, ad valorem property tax) is generally valid. But when this property moves in interstate commerce (eg, planes), the state tax must comply with the negative implication of the commerce clause.* This prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. As a result, this type of state tax is valid when:\n• the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed property, which is established when the property has sufficient contacts with the state such that the property has acquired a taxable situs in the state\n\n• the tax is fairly apportioned based on the amount of time that the taxed property is in the state\n\n• the tax is not discriminatory against interstate commerce and\n\n• the tax is fairly related to the benefits or services provided by the taxing state (Choice A).\n\nHere, there is a substantial nexus between State A and the airline since 10% of the airline's annual arrivals and departures occur in the state (taxable situs) (Choice B). The tax is fairly apportioned because it is based on the percentage of the airline's annual arrivals and departures in the state. It is nondiscriminatory since it applies equally to intrastate and interstate commerce. And the tax is fairly related to the benefits provided by the state (eg, use of airport). Therefore, the state tax does not unduly burden interstate commerce and is likely constitutional. *A state tax on property permanently located in another state is unconstitutional since the taxing state lacks jurisdiction over the property. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including the power to construct a state tax system. But this power is not plenary (ie, absolute) because states must comply with other constitutional provisions.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it does not place an undue burden on interstate commerce in violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause."},"questionZh":"A 州要求商业航空公司根据在该州运营的航空公司机队的价值缴纳年度财产税。该税的计算方法是将航空公司每年到达和离开该州的百分比乘以机队的总价值。该航空公司的机队以 B 州为基地。该航空公司每年到达和起飞的航班有 10% 在 A 州发生。该航空公司已提起诉讼，质疑 A 州税收，认为该税收违宪。州税可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会拥有对州际贸易中的财产征税的唯一权力。","B":"不，因为该航空公司的机队位于 B 州。","C":"是的，因为它不会违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响，给州际商业带来不应有的负担。","D":"是的，因为各州拥有完全权力以他们选择的任何方式构建税收制度。"},"explanationZh":"对州内财产价值征收的州税（即从价财产税）通常有效。但是，当该财产在州际贸易（例如飞机）中移动时，州税必须遵守商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。*这禁止各州歧视州际贸易或对州际贸易造成过度负担。因此，这种类型的州税在以下情况下有效：\n• 国家与应税财产有实质性联系，当该财产与国家有充分联系以致该财产已在该州获得应税所在地时，这种联系就成立了\n\n• 根据应税财产在该州的时间长度公平分配税收\n\n• 该税收不歧视州际贸易，并且\n\n• 税收与征税州提供的福利或服务公平相关（选择A）。\n\n在这里，A 州与航空公司之间存在着密切的联系，因为航空公司每年 10% 的进港和离港发生在该州（纳税地点）（选择 B）。该税收是公平分配的，因为它是根据航空公司每年到达和离开该州的百分比计算的。它是非歧视性的，因为它同样适用于州内和州际贸易。而且税收与国家提供的福利（例如，使用机场）公平相关。因此，州税不会对州际贸易造成过度负担，并且可能符合宪法。 *对永久位于另一个州的财产征收州税是违宪的，因为征税州对该财产缺乏管辖权。 （选项D）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括建立州税收制度的权力。但这种权力不是全权（即绝对），因为各州必须遵守其他宪法规定。\n\n教育目标：如果满足以下条件，对州际贸易中的财产流动征收州税是有效的：(1) 州与应税财产有实质性联系，使得该财产在州内具有应税地点；(2) 税收公平分配；(3) 税收是非歧视性的；(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• Braniff Airways, Inc. 诉 Neb. State Bd. of Equalization and Assessment, 347 U.S. 590, 599–601 (1954)（认为对航空公司机队征收的州从价财产税是有效的，因为航空公司在该州拥有应税地点）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 163 (2020)（解释说，当商业工具在该州有应税地点并且税收按照其在该州的时间公平分配时，各州可以对这些商业工具征税）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，对州际贸易中的财产转移征收的州税是有效的：(1) 州与应税财产有实质性联系，使得该财产在州内具有应税地点，(2) 税收公平分配，(3) 税收是非歧视性的，(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"州税可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"在以下情况下，对州际贸易中的财产转移征收的州税是有效的：(1) 州与应税财产有实质性联系，使得该财产在州内具有应税地点，(2) 税收公平分配，(3) 税收是非歧视性的，(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关。","analysis":"对州内财产价值征收的州税（即从价财产税）通常有效。但是，当该财产在州际贸易（例如飞机）中移动时，州税必须遵守商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。*这禁止各州歧视州际贸易或对州际贸易造成过度负担。因此，这种类型的州税在以下情况下有效：\n• 国家与应税财产有实质性联系，当该财产与国家有充分联系以致该财产已在该州获得应税所在地时，这种联系就成立了\n\n• 根据应税财产在该州的时间长度公平分配税收\n\n• 该税收不歧视州际贸易，并且\n\n• 税收与征税州提供的福利或服务公平相关（选择A）。\n\n在这里，A 州与航空公司之间存在着密切的联系，因为航空公司每年 10% 的进港和离港发生在该州（纳税地点）（选择 B）。该税收是公平分配的，因为它是根据航空公司每年到达和离开该州的百分比计算的。它是非歧视性的，因为它同样适用于州内和州际贸易。而且税收与国家提供的福利（例如，使用机场）公平相关。因此，州税不会对州际贸易造成过度负担，并且可能符合宪法。 *对永久位于另一个州的财产征收州税是违宪的，因为征税州对该财产缺乏管辖权。 （选项D）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括建立州税收制度的权力。但这种权力不是全权（即绝对），因为各州必须遵守其他宪法规定。\n\n教育目标：如果满足以下条件，对州际贸易中的财产流动征收州税是有效的：(1) 州与应税财产有实质性联系，使得该财产在州内具有应税地点；(2) 税收公平分配；(3) 税收是非歧视性的；(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• Braniff Airways, Inc. 诉 Neb. State Bd. of Equalization and Assessment, 347 U.S. 590, 599–601 (1954)（认为对航空公司机队征收的州从价财产税是有效的，因为航空公司在该州拥有应税地点）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 163 (2020)（解释说，当商业工具在该州有应税地点并且税收按照其在该州的时间公平分配时，各州可以对这些商业工具征税）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为它不会违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响，给州际商业带来不应有的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"negative implications of the commerce clause","ipa":"/nˈɛɡətɪv ˌɪmplʌkˈeɪʃənz ˈəv ðə kˈɑmɝs klˈɔz/","syllables":"neg·a·tive im·pli·ca·tions of the com·merce clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"商业条款的消极含义：州不得歧视或不当负担州际商业","contextualMeaning":"商业条款的消极含义：州不得歧视或不当负担州际商业","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because it does not place an undue burden on interstate commerce in violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause.","synonyms":["negative implications of the commerce article"]},{"term":"undue burden","ipa":"/ʌndˈu bˈɝdən/","syllables":"un·due bur·den","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不当负担：对受保护活动造成超出允许范围的实质障碍","contextualMeaning":"不当负担：对受保护活动造成超出允许范围的实质障碍","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because it does not place an undue burden on interstate commerce in violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause.","synonyms":["excessive burden","impermissible obstacle"]},{"term":"intrastate","ipa":"/ˌɪntɹʌstˈeɪt/","syllables":"in·trastate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 州内的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 州内的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"It is nondiscriminatory since it applies equally to intrastate and interstate commerce.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"violation","ipa":"/vaɪʌlˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"vi·o·la·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 违反, 违背, 妨碍；[法] 违犯, 违背, 违反","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 违反, 违背, 妨碍；[法] 违犯, 违背, 违反","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:violations","sourceSentence":"Yes, because it does not place an undue burden on interstate commerce in violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause.","synonyms":["trespass","encroachment","intrusion","usurpation"]},{"term":"plenary","ipa":"/plˈɛnɝi/","syllables":"ple·nary","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 充分的, 完全的, 全体出席的；[经] 正式的, 充分的, 全体出席的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 充分的, 完全的, 全体出席的；[经] 正式的, 充分的, 全体出席的","wordForm":"词；词形 s:plenaries","sourceSentence":"Yes, because states have plenary authority to construct their tax system in any manner they choose.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f538b753d1abd083"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":15,"sourceQuestionPage":43,"sourceQuestionPages":[43],"sourceExplanationPages":[44,45,46],"sourceAnswerPages":[44,45,46],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-016","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"In recent years, several large corporations incorporated and headquartered in State A have been acquired by out-of-state corporations that have moved all of their operations out of State A. Other corporations incorporated and headquartered in State A have successfully resisted such acquisition attempts by out-of-state corporations, but they have suffered severe economic injury as a result. In an effort to preserve jobs in State A and to protect its domestic corporations against their sudden acquisition by out-of-state purchasers, the legislature of State A enacts a statute governing acquisitions of shares in all corporations incorporated in State A. This statute requires that any acquisition of more than 25% of the voting shares of a corporation incorporated in State A that occurs over a period of less than one year must be approved by the holders of record of a majority of the shares of the corporation as of the day before the acquisition of those shares commences. The statute expressly applies to acquisitions of State A corporations by both in-state and out-of-state entities. Assume that no federal statute applies. Is this statute of State A constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because one of the purposes of the statute is to prevent out-of-state entities from acquiring corporations incorporated and headquartered in State A.","B":"No, because the effect of the statute will necessarily be to hinder the acquisition of State A corporations by other corporations, many of whose shareholders are not residents of State A and, therefore, it will adversely affect the interstate sale of securities.","C":"Yes, because corporations exist only by virtue of state law and, therefore, the negative implications of the commerce clause do not apply to state regulations governing their creation and acquisition.","D":"Yes, because the statute imposes the same burden on both in-state and out-of-state entities wishing to acquire a State A corporation, it regulates only the acquisition of State A corporations, and it does not create an impermissible risk of inconsistent regulation on this subject by different states."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states are prohibited from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state regulation is not discriminatory if it treats in-state and out-of-state activities equally. But the nondiscriminatory regulation is unduly burdensome if its burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its legitimate, local benefits. This often occurs when there is a risk of inconsistent regulations on the same activity by different states. Here, the State A statute is not discriminatory since it imposes an equal burden on acquisitions by both in-state and out-of-state entities. And the statute is not unduly burdensome because there is no risk of inconsistent regulations by different states. That is because, as the Supreme Court reasoned in CTS Corp. v. Dynamics Corp. of Am., corporations are typically governed by the law of the state in which they are incorporated, and this statute regulates only the acquisition of State A corporations. Therefore, the statute is constitutional. (Choices A & B) The statute's purpose is to prevent out-of-state entities from acquiring State A corporations. And it will likely hinder acquisitions by corporations with out-of-state shareholders—adversely affecting the interstate sale of securities. But the statute's impact on interstate commerce is permissible because it is not discriminatory or unduly burdensome. (Choice C) The negative implications of the commerce clause (ie, dormant commerce clause) do apply to state regulations governing the creation and acquisition of corporations if those regulations discriminate against or unduly burden interstate commerce. But the State A statute does not do so.\n\nEducational objective: The dormant commerce clause bars states from enacting nondiscriminatory regulations that unduly burden interstate commerce. This occurs when the regulation's burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its legitimate, local benefits—eg, when it creates a risk of inconsistent regulations by different states.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• CTS Corp. v. Dynamics Corp. of Am., 481 U.S. 69, 88–91 (1987) (explaining that a nondiscriminatory law governing the acquisition of a state corporation does not unduly burden interstate commerce).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 39 (2019) (setting forth that a nondiscriminatory law violates the dormant commerce clause if its burdens on interstate commerce clearly exceed the law's local benefits).","educationalObjective":"The dormant commerce clause bars states from enacting nondiscriminatory regulations that unduly burden interstate commerce. This occurs when the regulation's burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its legitimate, local benefits—eg, when it creates a risk of inconsistent regulations by different states.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• CTS Corp. v. Dynamics Corp. of Am., 481 U.S. 69, 88–91 (1987) (explaining that a nondiscriminatory law governing the acquisition of a state corporation does not unduly burden interstate commerce).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 39 (2019) (setting forth that a nondiscriminatory law violates the dormant commerce clause if its burdens on interstate commerce clearly exceed the law's local benefits).","rule":"The dormant commerce clause bars states from enacting nondiscriminatory regulations that unduly burden interstate commerce. This occurs when the regulation's burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its legitimate, local benefits—eg, when it creates a risk of inconsistent regulations by different states.","irac":{"issue":"Is this statute of State A constitutional?","rule":"The dormant commerce clause bars states from enacting nondiscriminatory regulations that unduly burden interstate commerce. This occurs when the regulation's burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its legitimate, local benefits—eg, when it creates a risk of inconsistent regulations by different states.","analysis":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states are prohibited from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state regulation is not discriminatory if it treats in-state and out-of-state activities equally. But the nondiscriminatory regulation is unduly burdensome if its burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its legitimate, local benefits. This often occurs when there is a risk of inconsistent regulations on the same activity by different states. Here, the State A statute is not discriminatory since it imposes an equal burden on acquisitions by both in-state and out-of-state entities. And the statute is not unduly burdensome because there is no risk of inconsistent regulations by different states. That is because, as the Supreme Court reasoned in CTS Corp. v. Dynamics Corp. of Am., corporations are typically governed by the law of the state in which they are incorporated, and this statute regulates only the acquisition of State A corporations. Therefore, the statute is constitutional. (Choices A & B) The statute's purpose is to prevent out-of-state entities from acquiring State A corporations. And it will likely hinder acquisitions by corporations with out-of-state shareholders—adversely affecting the interstate sale of securities. But the statute's impact on interstate commerce is permissible because it is not discriminatory or unduly burdensome. (Choice C) The negative implications of the commerce clause (ie, dormant commerce clause) do apply to state regulations governing the creation and acquisition of corporations if those regulations discriminate against or unduly burden interstate commerce. But the State A statute does not do so.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the statute imposes the same burden on both in-state and out-of-state entities wishing to acquire a State A corporation, it regulates only the acquisition of State A corporations, and it does not create an impermissible risk of inconsistent regulation on this subject by different states."},"questionZh":"近年来，一些在 A 州注册成立并总部位于 A 州的大型公司被州外公司收购，这些公司已将其所有业务迁出 A 州。其他在 A 州注册成立并总部位于 A 州的公司也成功抵制了州外公司的此类收购尝试，但也因此遭受了严重的经济损失。为了保住 A 国的就业机会，并保护其国内公司免受州外收购者的突然收购，A 国立法机构颁布了一项法规，管辖在 A 国注册成立的所有公司的股份收购。该法规要求，在不到一年的时间内收购在 A 国注册成立的公司 25% 以上有表决权股份的行为，必须得到截至该收购前一天在册的该公司过半数股份持有人的批准。开始收购这些股份。该法规明确适用于州内和州外实体对 A 州公司的收购。假设没有适用的联邦法规。 A国的这条法规合宪吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该法规的目的之一是防止州外实体收购在 A 州注册成立并总部位于 A 州的公司。","B":"不会，因为该法规的效果必然会阻碍其他公司收购 A 州公司，而这些公司的许多股东不是 A 州居民，因此，它将对州际证券销售产生不利影响。","C":"是的，因为公司仅依据州法律而存在，因此，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响不适用于管理其创建和收购的州法规。","D":"是的，由于该法规对希望收购 A 州公司的州内和州外实体施加了相同的负担，因此它仅规范 A 州公司的收购，并且不会造成不同州对此问题监管不一致的不允许的风险。"},"explanationZh":"根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。如果州法规平等对待州内和州外的活动，则该州法规不具有歧视性。但如果非歧视性监管对州际贸易的负担明显超过其合法的当地利益，那么它的负担就会过大。当不同州对同一活动存在不一致的监管风险时，通常会发生这种情况。在这里，A 州法规不具有歧视性，因为它对州内和州外实体的收购施加了同等的负担。而且该法规并没有造成过分的负担，因为不存在不同州监管不一致的风险。这是因为，正如最高法院在 CTS Corp. 诉 Dynamics Corp. of Am. 案中所论证的那样，公司通常受其注册所在州的法律管辖，而该法规仅规范 A 州公司的收购。因此，该法规是符合宪法的。 （选项 A 和 B）该法规的目的是防止州外实体收购 A 州公司。这可能会阻碍拥有州外股东的公司进行收购，从而对州际证券销售产生不利影响。但该法规对州际贸易的影响是允许的，因为它不具有歧视性或造成过度负担。 （选项C） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面影响确实适用于管理公司创建和收购的州法规，如果这些法规歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成不当负担。但A州法规并没有这样做。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州颁布对州际商业造成过度负担的非歧视性法规。当法规对州际贸易的负担明显超过其合法的当地利益时，例如，当它产生不同州法规不一致的风险时，就会发生这种情况。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• CTS Corp. v. Dynamics Corp. of Am., 481 U.S. 69, 88–91 (1987)（解释说管辖国有公司收购的非歧视性法律不会对州际贸易造成不当负担）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 39 (2019)（规定，如果非歧视性法律对州际商业的负担明显超过该法律的当地利益，则该法律违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州颁布对州际商业造成过度负担的非歧视性法规。当法规对州际贸易的负担明显超过其合法的当地利益时，例如，当它产生不同州法规不一致的风险时，就会发生这种情况。","iracZh":{"issue":"A国的这条法规合宪吗？","rule":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州颁布对州际商业造成过度负担的非歧视性法规。当法规对州际贸易的负担明显超过其合法的当地利益时，例如，当它产生不同州法规不一致的风险时，就会发生这种情况。","analysis":"根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。如果州法规平等对待州内和州外的活动，则该州法规不具有歧视性。但如果非歧视性监管对州际贸易的负担明显超过其合法的当地利益，那么它的负担就会过大。当不同州对同一活动存在不一致的监管风险时，通常会发生这种情况。在这里，A 州法规不具有歧视性，因为它对州内和州外实体的收购施加了同等的负担。而且该法规并没有造成过分的负担，因为不存在不同州监管不一致的风险。这是因为，正如最高法院在 CTS Corp. 诉 Dynamics Corp. of Am. 案中所论证的那样，公司通常受其注册所在州的法律管辖，而该法规仅规范 A 州公司的收购。因此，该法规是符合宪法的。 （选项 A 和 B）该法规的目的是防止州外实体收购 A 州公司。这可能会阻碍拥有州外股东的公司进行收购，从而对州际证券销售产生不利影响。但该法规对州际贸易的影响是允许的，因为它不具有歧视性或造成过度负担。 （选项C） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面影响确实适用于管理公司创建和收购的州法规，如果这些法规歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成不当负担。但A州法规并没有这样做。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州颁布对州际商业造成过度负担的非歧视性法规。当法规对州际贸易的负担明显超过其合法的当地利益时，例如，当它产生不同州法规不一致的风险时，就会发生这种情况。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• CTS Corp. v. Dynamics Corp. of Am., 481 U.S. 69, 88–91 (1987)（解释说管辖国有公司收购的非歧视性法律不会对州际贸易造成不当负担）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 39 (2019)（规定，如果非歧视性法律对州际商业的负担明显超过该法律的当地利益，则该法律违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，由于该法规对希望收购 A 州公司的州内和州外实体施加了相同的负担，因此它仅规范 A 州公司的收购，并且不会造成不同州对此问题监管不一致的不允许的风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"injury","ipa":"/ˈɪndʒɝi/","syllables":"in·jury","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 伤害, 侮辱；[医] 伤, 损伤","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 伤害, 侮辱；[医] 伤, 损伤","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:injuries","sourceSentence":"Other corporations incorporated and headquartered in State A have successfully resisted such acquisition attempts by out-of-state corporations, but they have suffered severe economic injury as a result.","synonyms":["accidental injury","wound","combat injury","hurt"]},{"term":"voting","ipa":"/vˈoʊtɪŋ/","syllables":"vot·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 投票, 选举；a. 投票的, 选举的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 投票, 选举；a. 投票的, 选举的","wordForm":"词；词形 0:vote/1:i/i:voting","sourceSentence":"This statute requires that any acquisition of more than 25% of the voting shares of a corporation incorporated in State A that occurs over a period of less than one year must be approved by the holders of record of a majority of the shares of the corporation as of the day before the acquisition of those shares commences.","synonyms":["vote","ballot","balloting"]},{"term":"prohibited","ipa":"/pɹoʊhˈɪbətəd/","syllables":"pro·hib·ited","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 禁止, 被禁止的（prohibite的过去分词形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 禁止, 被禁止的（prohibite的过去分词形式）","wordForm":"词；原形 prohibit；词形 0:prohibit/1:dp/p:prohibited/d:prohibited","sourceSentence":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states are prohibited from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce.","synonyms":["forbid","interdict","proscribe","veto"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-694e4fa879806d20"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":16,"sourceQuestionPage":47,"sourceQuestionPages":[47],"sourceExplanationPages":[48,49],"sourceAnswerPages":[48,49],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-017","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state statute requires corporations located in the state to pay a use tax on equipment they purchased out-of-state for in-state use. The statute specifies that the use-tax rate is the same as the state's sales-tax rate for in-state equipment purchases. The statute also provides tax credits to corporations in the amount of the sales or use tax they paid on the same equipment in other states. A construction company that builds homes in the state has refused to pay the state's use tax on equipment it purchased outside the state. The company has sued to challenge the state tax, arguing it is unconstitutional. Is the state tax likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it discriminates against interstate commerce.","B":"No, because the commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because it does not unduly burden interstate commerce.","D":"Yes, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states the power to construct their own tax system."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including the power to construct a state tax system. But when exercising this power, states must comply with other constitutional provisions (Choice D). This includes the negative implication of the commerce clause (ie, dormant commerce clause), which prohibits states from unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state tax on goods used in the state but purchased in another state (ie, use tax) affects interstate commerce. Therefore, to comply with the dormant commerce clause, the state tax must be:\n• applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the taxing state\n\n• fairly apportioned so that goods are not subject to multiple taxation in other states\n\n• not discriminatory against interstate commerce, which is established when the use-tax rate on goods purchased out-of-state is not higher than the sales-tax rate on goods purchased in-state and\n\n• fairly related to the benefits or services provided by the taxing state.\n\nHere, there is a substantial nexus between the company and the state since the company builds homes there. The use tax is fairly apportioned because tax credits are provided for the sales or use tax that corporations paid on equipment in other states. The use tax is not discriminatory since it equals the state's sales tax for in-state equipment purchases (Choice A). It is also fairly related to the benefits provided by the state (eg, use of public roads). Therefore, the state tax does not unduly burden interstate commerce and is constitutional. (Choice B) The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate interstate commerce. However, states may still tax goods within their borders if the tax satisfies the negative implication of this clause (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A state tax on goods purchased out-of-state for in-state use is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed person or activity, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.\n\nReferences\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state tax violates the commerce clause).\n\n• Henneford v. Silas Mason Co., 300 U.S. 577, 584–85 (1937) (holding that a state's use tax on goods purchased out-of-state for in-state use is valid as long as the use- tax rate is not higher than the state's sales-tax rate).\n\n• 67B Am. Jur. 2d Sales and Use Taxes §§ 155–59 (explaining the four-factor test for determining whether a use tax violates the commerce clause).","educationalObjective":"A state tax on goods purchased out-of-state for in-state use is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed person or activity, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.","references":"• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state tax violates the commerce clause).\n\n• Henneford v. Silas Mason Co., 300 U.S. 577, 584–85 (1937) (holding that a state's use tax on goods purchased out-of-state for in-state use is valid as long as the use- tax rate is not higher than the state's sales-tax rate).\n\n• 67B Am. Jur. 2d Sales and Use Taxes §§ 155–59 (explaining the four-factor test for determining whether a use tax violates the commerce clause).","rule":"A state tax on goods purchased out-of-state for in-state use is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed person or activity, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state tax likely constitutional?","rule":"A state tax on goods purchased out-of-state for in-state use is valid if (1) the state has a substantial nexus with the taxed person or activity, (2) the tax is fairly apportioned, (3) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (4) the tax is fairly related to benefits or services provided by the state.","analysis":"The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including the power to construct a state tax system. But when exercising this power, states must comply with other constitutional provisions (Choice D). This includes the negative implication of the commerce clause (ie, dormant commerce clause), which prohibits states from unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state tax on goods used in the state but purchased in another state (ie, use tax) affects interstate commerce. Therefore, to comply with the dormant commerce clause, the state tax must be:\n• applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the taxing state\n\n• fairly apportioned so that goods are not subject to multiple taxation in other states\n\n• not discriminatory against interstate commerce, which is established when the use-tax rate on goods purchased out-of-state is not higher than the sales-tax rate on goods purchased in-state and\n\n• fairly related to the benefits or services provided by the taxing state.\n\nHere, there is a substantial nexus between the company and the state since the company builds homes there. The use tax is fairly apportioned because tax credits are provided for the sales or use tax that corporations paid on equipment in other states. The use tax is not discriminatory since it equals the state's sales tax for in-state equipment purchases (Choice A). It is also fairly related to the benefits provided by the state (eg, use of public roads). Therefore, the state tax does not unduly burden interstate commerce and is constitutional. (Choice B) The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate interstate commerce. However, states may still tax goods within their borders if the tax satisfies the negative implication of this clause (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it does not unduly burden interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"州法规要求位于该州的公司对其在州外购买供州内使用的设备缴纳使用税。该法规规定，州内设备采购的使用税率与州销售税率相同。该法规还为公司在其他州为相同设备缴纳的销售或使用税金额提供税收抵免。一家在该州建造房屋的建筑公司拒绝为其在州外购买的设备缴纳州使用税。该公司已提起诉讼，质疑州税，称其违宪。州税可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为它歧视州际贸易。","B":"不，因为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的权力。","C":"是的，因为它不会过度加重州际贸易的负担。","D":"是的，因为第十修正案保留了各州建立自己的税收制度的权力。"},"explanationZh":"第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括建立州税收制度的权力。但在行使这一权力时，各州必须遵守其他宪法规定（选项D）。这包括商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面影响，该条款禁止各州过度加重州际商业负担。对在本州使用但在另一个州购买的商品征收的州税（即使用税）会影响州际贸易。因此，为了遵守消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），州税必须：\n• 适用于与征税州有密切联系的个人或活动\n\n• 公平分配，使商品不会在其他州受到多重征税\n\n• 不歧视州际贸易，当在州外购买的商品的使用税率不高于在州内购买的商品的销售税率时，即成立。\n\n• 与征税州提供的福利或服务公平相关。\n\n由于公司在这里建造房屋，因此公司与国家之间存在着密切的联系。使用税是公平分配的，因为企业在其他州为设备支付的销售税或使用税可以享​​受税收抵免。使用税不具有歧视性，因为它等于州内购买设备的销售税（选择 A）。它还与国家提供的福利（例如，公共道路的使用）公平相关。因此，州税不会对州际贸易造成过度负担，而且符合宪法。 （选项 B） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的权力。然而，如果税收满足本条款的负面含义（如此处所示），各州仍然可以在其境内对商品征税。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 州与纳税人或纳税活动有实质性联系，(2) 税收公平分配，(3) 税收不歧视，(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关，则对在州外购买供州内使用的商品征收州税是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• Henneford v. Silas Mason Co., 300 U.S. 577, 584–85 (1937)（主张州对在州外购买供州内使用的商品征收的使用税有效，只要使用税率不高于该州的销售税率）。\n\n• 67B 上午。尤尔。 2d 销售和使用税 §§ 155–59（解释了确定使用税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对在州外购买供州内使用的商品征收的州税是有效的，如果 (1) 该州与纳税人或纳税活动有实质性联系，(2) 税收分配公平，(3) 税收是非歧视性的，(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"州税可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"对在州外购买供州内使用的商品征收的州税是有效的，如果 (1) 该州与纳税人或纳税活动有实质性联系，(2) 税收分配公平，(3) 税收是非歧视性的，(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关。","analysis":"第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括建立州税收制度的权力。但在行使这一权力时，各州必须遵守其他宪法规定（选项D）。这包括商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面影响，该条款禁止各州过度加重州际商业负担。对在本州使用但在另一个州购买的商品征收的州税（即使用税）会影响州际贸易。因此，为了遵守消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），州税必须：\n• 适用于与征税州有密切联系的个人或活动\n\n• 公平分配，使商品不会在其他州受到多重征税\n\n• 不歧视州际贸易，当在州外购买的商品的使用税率不高于在州内购买的商品的销售税率时，即成立。\n\n• 与征税州提供的福利或服务公平相关。\n\n由于公司在这里建造房屋，因此公司与国家之间存在着密切的联系。使用税是公平分配的，因为企业在其他州为设备支付的销售税或使用税可以享​​受税收抵免。使用税不具有歧视性，因为它等于州内购买设备的销售税（选择 A）。它还与国家提供的福利（例如，公共道路的使用）公平相关。因此，州税不会对州际贸易造成过度负担，而且符合宪法。 （选项 B） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的权力。然而，如果税收满足本条款的负面含义（如此处所示），各州仍然可以在其境内对商品征税。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 州与纳税人或纳税活动有实质性联系，(2) 税收公平分配，(3) 税收不歧视，(4) 税收与州提供的福利或服务公平相关，则对在州外购买供州内使用的商品征收州税是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• Henneford v. Silas Mason Co., 300 U.S. 577, 584–85 (1937)（主张州对在州外购买供州内使用的商品征收的使用税有效，只要使用税率不高于该州的销售税率）。\n\n• 67B 上午。尤尔。 2d 销售和使用税 §§ 155–59（解释了确定使用税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为它不会过度加重州际贸易的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f538b753d1abd083"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":17,"sourceQuestionPage":50,"sourceQuestionPages":[50],"sourceExplanationPages":[51,52,53],"sourceAnswerPages":[51,52,53],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-018","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state statute requires each insurance company that offers burglary insurance policies in the state to charge a uniform rate for such insurance to all of its customers residing within the same county in that state. So long as a company complies with this requirement, it is free to charge whatever rate the market will bear for its burglary insurance policies. An insurance company located in the state files suit in federal district court against appropriate state officials to challenge this statute on constitutional grounds. The insurance company wishes to charge customers residing within the same county in the state rates for burglary insurance policies that will vary because they would be based on the specific nature of the customer's business, on its precise location, and on its past claims record. How should the court rule in this suit?","choices":{"A":"Abstain from ruling on the merits of this case until the state courts have had an opportunity to pass on the constitutionality of this state statute.","B":"Hold the statute constitutional, because the statute is a reasonable exercise of the state's police power.","C":"Hold the statute unconstitutional, because the statute deprives the insurance company of its liberty or property without due process of law.","D":"Hold the statute unconstitutional, because the statute imposes an undue burden on interstate commerce."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the Tenth Amendment, any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government is reserved to the states. This includes the broad police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare—eg, requiring insurers to charge uniform rates to residents is reasonably related to protecting their financial welfare (as seen here). But when exercising this power, states cannot violate other constitutional provisions like the due process and dormant commerce clauses. The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause prohibits state deprivations of life, liberty, or property unless adequately justified. A deprivation impacting an ordinary right (eg, an economic right—as seen here) is justified if it satisfies mere rational basis scrutiny. Here, the statute satisfies this standard because it is reasonably related to the state's legitimate interest in protecting its citizens from varying insurance rates. As a result, the statute does not deprive the insurance company of due process of law (Choice C). Additionally, the Article I dormant commerce clause prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. A nondiscriminatory law is unduly burdensome if its burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its local benefits. Here, the statute is nondiscriminatory since it applies to all insurance companies. And it is not unduly burdensome because there is no indication that it has any effect outside the state (Choice D). Therefore, the statute is a constitutional exercise of the state's police power. (Choice A) A federal court should abstain from hearing a case that involves an unsettled issue of state law so that state courts can sort out the issue. But since this case concerns whether the statute violates the federal Constitution, the court need not abstain.\n\nEducational objective: States possess the police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare. But states cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising this power.\n\nReferences\n• O'Gorman & Young, Inc. v. Hartford Fire Ins. Co., 282 U.S. 251, 257–58 (1931) (holding that a state regulation of insurance rates is a reasonable exercise of the state's police power and does not violate the due process clause).\n\n• Kassel v. Consol. Freightways Corp., 450 U.S. 662, 669–70 (1981) (describing how a state acting pursuant to its police power can violate the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 379 (2019) (explaining that a state cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising its police powers).","educationalObjective":"States possess the police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare. But states cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising this power.","references":"• O'Gorman & Young, Inc. v. Hartford Fire Ins. Co., 282 U.S. 251, 257–58 (1931) (holding that a state regulation of insurance rates is a reasonable exercise of the state's police power and does not violate the due process clause).\n\n• Kassel v. Consol. Freightways Corp., 450 U.S. 662, 669–70 (1981) (describing how a state acting pursuant to its police power can violate the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 379 (2019) (explaining that a state cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising its police powers).","rule":"States possess the police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare. But states cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising this power.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule in this suit?","rule":"States possess the police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare. But states cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising this power.","analysis":"Under the Tenth Amendment, any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government is reserved to the states. This includes the broad police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare—eg, requiring insurers to charge uniform rates to residents is reasonably related to protecting their financial welfare (as seen here). But when exercising this power, states cannot violate other constitutional provisions like the due process and dormant commerce clauses. The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause prohibits state deprivations of life, liberty, or property unless adequately justified. A deprivation impacting an ordinary right (eg, an economic right—as seen here) is justified if it satisfies mere rational basis scrutiny. Here, the statute satisfies this standard because it is reasonably related to the state's legitimate interest in protecting its citizens from varying insurance rates. As a result, the statute does not deprive the insurance company of due process of law (Choice C). Additionally, the Article I dormant commerce clause prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. A nondiscriminatory law is unduly burdensome if its burden on interstate commerce clearly exceeds its local benefits. Here, the statute is nondiscriminatory since it applies to all insurance companies. And it is not unduly burdensome because there is no indication that it has any effect outside the state (Choice D). Therefore, the statute is a constitutional exercise of the state's police power. (Choice A) A federal court should abstain from hearing a case that involves an unsettled issue of state law so that state courts can sort out the issue. But since this case concerns whether the statute violates the federal Constitution, the court need not abstain.","conclusion":"Answer B: Hold the statute constitutional, because the statute is a reasonable exercise of the state's police power."},"questionZh":"州法规要求在该州提供入室盗窃保险的各保险公司向居住在该州同一县的所有客户收取统一的此类保险费率。只要公司遵守这一要求，就可以自由地收取市场对其盗窃保险保单所承受的任何费率。该州的一家保险公司向联邦地区法院对相关州官员提起诉讼，以宪法为由对该法规提出质疑。保险公司希望向居住在同一县的客户收取入室盗窃保单的州费率，该费率会有所不同，因为它们将基于客户业务的具体性质、其精确位置以及其过去的索赔记录。法院该如何判决这起诉讼呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"在州法院有机会通过该州法规的合宪性之前，不要就本案的是非曲直作出裁决。","B":"坚持该法规符合宪法，因为该法规是国家警察权力的合理行使。","C":"认为该法规违宪，因为该法规未经正当法律程序就剥夺了保险公司的自由或财产。","D":"认为该法规违宪，因为该法规给州际贸易带来了不应有的负担。"},"explanationZh":"根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力均保留给各州。这包括广泛的警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律，例如，要求保险公司向居民收取统一费率与保护他们的财务福利合理相关（如此处所示）。但在行使这一权力时，各州不能违反其他宪法条款，例如正当程序和消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止国家剥夺生命、自由或财产，除非有充分理由。影响普通权利（例如，经济权利——如此处所见）的剥夺如果满足纯粹的合理基础审查，就是合理的。在这里，该法规满足了这一标准，因为它与国家保护其公民免受不同保险费率影响的合法利益合理相关。因此，该法规并没有剥夺保险公司的正当法律程序（选择C）。此外，第一条消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。如果一项非歧视性法律对州际贸易的负担明显超过其当地利益，那么它的负担就过于沉重。在这里，该法规是非歧视性的，因为它适用于所有保险公司。而且它并没有造成过分的负担，因为没有迹象表明它在州外有任何影响（选择 D）。因此，该法规是国家警察权力的宪法行使。 （选择A）联邦法院应该放弃审理涉及州法律未解决问题的案件，以便州法院能够解决该问题。但由于本案涉及该法规是否违反联邦宪法，法院无需弃权。\n\n教育目标：国家拥有警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律。但各州在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法规定。\n\n参考文献\n• O'Gorman & Young, Inc. 诉 Hartford Fire Ins。 Co., 282 U.S. 251, 257–58 (1931)（认为州对保险费率的监管是州警察权力的合理行使，并且不违反正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause））。\n\n• 卡塞尔诉康索尔案。 Freightways Corp., 450 U.S. 662, 669–70 (1981)（描述国家如何根据其警察权力行事来违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 § 379 (2019)（解释说国家在行使其警察权力时不得违反其他宪法条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"各州拥有警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律。但各州在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法规定。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院该如何判决这起诉讼呢？","rule":"各州拥有警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律。但各州在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法规定。","analysis":"根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力均保留给各州。这包括广泛的警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律，例如，要求保险公司向居民收取统一费率与保护他们的财务福利合理相关（如此处所示）。但在行使这一权力时，各州不能违反其他宪法条款，例如正当程序和消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止国家剥夺生命、自由或财产，除非有充分理由。影响普通权利（例如，经济权利——如此处所见）的剥夺如果满足纯粹的合理基础审查，就是合理的。在这里，该法规满足了这一标准，因为它与国家保护其公民免受不同保险费率影响的合法利益合理相关。因此，该法规并没有剥夺保险公司的正当法律程序（选择C）。此外，第一条消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。如果一项非歧视性法律对州际贸易的负担明显超过其当地利益，那么它的负担就过于沉重。在这里，该法规是非歧视性的，因为它适用于所有保险公司。而且它并没有造成过分的负担，因为没有迹象表明它在州外有任何影响（选择 D）。因此，该法规是国家警察权力的宪法行使。 （选择A）联邦法院应该放弃审理涉及州法律未解决问题的案件，以便州法院能够解决该问题。但由于本案涉及该法规是否违反联邦宪法，法院无需弃权。\n\n教育目标：国家拥有警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律。但各州在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法规定。\n\n参考文献\n• O'Gorman & Young, Inc. 诉 Hartford Fire Ins。 Co., 282 U.S. 251, 257–58 (1931)（认为州对保险费率的监管是州警察权力的合理行使，并且不违反正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause））。\n\n• 卡塞尔诉康索尔案。 Freightways Corp., 450 U.S. 662, 669–70 (1981)（描述国家如何根据其警察权力行事来违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 § 379 (2019)（解释说国家在行使其警察权力时不得违反其他宪法条款）。","conclusion":"答案 B：坚持该法规符合宪法，因为该法规是国家警察权力的合理行使。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"deprivation","ipa":"/dˌɛpɹʌvˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"de·pri·va·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 剥夺, 剥夺官职, 免职；[医] 丧失, 剥夺, 缺乏","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 剥夺, 剥夺官职, 免职；[医] 丧失, 剥夺, 缺乏","wordForm":"词；词形 s:deprivations","sourceSentence":"The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause prohibits state deprivations of life, liberty, or property unless adequately justified.","synonyms":["privation","loss","want","neediness"]},{"term":"deprive","ipa":"/dɪpɹˈaɪv/","syllables":"de·prive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 剥夺, 使丧失；[法] 剥夺, 剥夺, 夺去","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 剥夺, 使丧失；[法] 剥夺, 剥夺, 夺去","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:deprived/i:depriving/p:deprived/3:deprives","sourceSentence":"Hold the statute unconstitutional, because the statute deprives the insurance company of its liberty or property without due process of law.","synonyms":["impoverish","strip","divest"]},{"term":"liberty","ipa":"/lˈɪbɝtˌi/","syllables":"lib·erty","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 自由, 特权, 许可, 冒失；[法] 自由, 自由权, 自由区域","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 自由, 特权, 许可, 冒失；[法] 自由, 自由权, 自由区域","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:liberties","sourceSentence":"Hold the statute unconstitutional, because the statute deprives the insurance company of its liberty or property without due process of law.","synonyms":["familiarity","impropriety","indecorum","autonomy"]},{"term":"hearing","ipa":"/hˈiɹɪŋ/","syllables":"hear·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 听, 听觉, 听讯；[医] 听, 听觉","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 听, 听觉, 听讯；[医] 听, 听觉","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:hear/1:i/i:hearing/s:hearings","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) A federal court should abstain from hearing a case that involves an unsettled issue of state law so that state courts can sort out the issue.","synonyms":["listening","audition","auditory sense","sense of hearing"]},{"term":"ruling","ipa":"/ɹˈulɪŋ/","syllables":"rul·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 判决, 裁定, 统治；a. 统治的, 支配的, 普遍的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 判决, 裁定, 统治；a. 统治的, 支配的, 普遍的","wordForm":"词；词形 0:rule/1:i/s:rulings/i:ruling","sourceSentence":"Abstain from ruling on the merits of this case until the state courts have had an opportunity to pass on the constitutionality of this state statute.","synonyms":["opinion","regnant","reigning"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-ab7697e3bd1a81f7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":18,"sourceQuestionPage":54,"sourceQuestionPages":[54],"sourceExplanationPages":[55,56],"sourceAnswerPages":[55,56],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-019","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"A toy manufacturer that has its headquarters and sole manufacturing plant in a particular state has developed a \"Martian\" toy that simulates the exploration of Mars by a remote- controlled vehicle. It accurately depicts the Martian landscape and the unmanned exploratory vehicle traversing it. The toy is of high quality, is safe and durable, and has sold very well. Other toy manufacturers, all located outside of the state, have developed similar toys that are lower in price. These manufacturers have contracts to sell their Martian toys to outlets in the state. Although these toys are safe and durable, they depict the Martian landscape less realistically than the toys manufactured in the state. Nevertheless, because of the price difference, sales of these toys have cut severely into the sales of the Martian toys manufactured in the state. The state legislature has recently enacted a law \"to protect the children of this state from faulty science and to protect in-state toy manufacturers from unfair competition.\" The law forbids the sale in the state of any toy that purports to represent extraterrestrial objects and does not satisfy specified scientific criteria. The Martian toy manufactured in the state satisfies all of these criteria; none of the Martian toys of the competing manufacturers meet the requirements. Is the state law constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it abrogates the obligations of the contracts between the other toy manufacturers and the in-state outlets that have agreed to sell their Martian toys.","B":"No, because it imposes an undue burden on interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because it deals only with a local matter, the sale of toys in stores located within the state.","D":"Yes, because the state's interest in protecting the state's children from faulty science justifies this burden on interstate commerce."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Discrimination against out-of-state commerce\n\nType Description Example\n\nFacial Regulation's language explicitly \"Corn grown out-of-state must discrimination favors in-state commerce satisfy more extensive safety measures\"\n\nDiscriminatory Facially neutral regulation applied State inspects corn grown out- application to favor in-state over out-of-state of-state but not corn grown in- commerce state\n\nDiscriminatory Otherwise neutral regulation Enforcement of safety effect disproportionately affects out-of- measures results in ban of corn state commerce grown out-of-state only\n\nThe commerce clause grants Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. In the inverse, the dormant commerce clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state commerce or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state regulation can discriminate three ways:\n• on its face – when language of a regulation expressly favors in-state interests\n• in its application – when a facially neutral regulation is applied in favor of in-state interests\n• in its effect – when an otherwise neutral law disproportionately impacts out-of- state commerce A discriminatory regulation violates the dormant commerce clause unless the state proves that the regulation (1) furthers a legitimate, non-economic state interest (2) that could not be achieved by reasonable alternatives. Here, the state law is neutral on its face and in its application since it applies equally to in- state and out-of-state toy manufacturers. But since only out-of-state toy manufacturers are impacted by the law, it has a discriminatory effect. The state does have a legitimate, non- economic interest in protecting children from faulty science. But there are reasonable alternatives to achieve this interest—eg, requiring a disclaimer that the toy is not scientifically accurate (Choice D). Therefore, the law unduly burdens interstate commerce and is unconstitutional. (Choice A) Under the contracts clause, state laws that substantially impair existing private contracts are valid if the impairment is (1) reasonable and narrowly tailored (2) to achieve an important state interest. This narrowly-tailored standard gives more deference to states\n\nthan the dormant commerce clause's no-reasonable-alternative standard. As a result, the statute is less likely to be invalidated under the contracts clause. (Choice C) Although the law deals with the local matter of selling toys in stores located within the state, it also unduly burdens interstate commerce by discriminating against out- of-state manufacturers.\n\nEducational objective: A state regulation that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its effect—against interstate commerce is invalid unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, non-economic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Bacchus Imps. v. Dias, 468 U.S. 263, 273 (1984) (holding that a state discriminated against interstate commerce when its regulation only negatively impacted out-of- state economic activities).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 103 (2019) (explaining the types of discrimination against interstate commerce).","educationalObjective":"A state regulation that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its effect—against interstate commerce is invalid unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, non-economic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Bacchus Imps. v. Dias, 468 U.S. 263, 273 (1984) (holding that a state discriminated against interstate commerce when its regulation only negatively impacted out-of- state economic activities).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 103 (2019) (explaining the types of discrimination against interstate commerce).","rule":"A state regulation that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its effect—against interstate commerce is invalid unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, non-economic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state law constitutional?","rule":"A state regulation that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its effect—against interstate commerce is invalid unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, non-economic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","analysis":"Discrimination against out-of-state commerce\n\nType Description Example\n\nFacial Regulation's language explicitly \"Corn grown out-of-state must discrimination favors in-state commerce satisfy more extensive safety measures\"\n\nDiscriminatory Facially neutral regulation applied State inspects corn grown out- application to favor in-state over out-of-state of-state but not corn grown in- commerce state\n\nDiscriminatory Otherwise neutral regulation Enforcement of safety effect disproportionately affects out-of- measures results in ban of corn state commerce grown out-of-state only\n\nThe commerce clause grants Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. In the inverse, the dormant commerce clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state commerce or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state regulation can discriminate three ways:\n• on its face – when language of a regulation expressly favors in-state interests\n• in its application – when a facially neutral regulation is applied in favor of in-state interests\n• in its effect – when an otherwise neutral law disproportionately impacts out-of- state commerce A discriminatory regulation violates the dormant commerce clause unless the state proves that the regulation (1) furthers a legitimate, non-economic state interest (2) that could not be achieved by reasonable alternatives. Here, the state law is neutral on its face and in its application since it applies equally to in- state and out-of-state toy manufacturers. But since only out-of-state toy manufacturers are impacted by the law, it has a discriminatory effect. The state does have a legitimate, non- economic interest in protecting children from faulty science. But there are reasonable alternatives to achieve this interest—eg, requiring a disclaimer that the toy is not scientifically accurate (Choice D). Therefore, the law unduly burdens interstate commerce and is unconstitutional. (Choice A) Under the contracts clause, state laws that substantially impair existing private contracts are valid if the impairment is (1) reasonable and narrowly tailored (2) to achieve an important state interest. This narrowly-tailored standard gives more deference to states\n\nthan the dormant commerce clause's no-reasonable-alternative standard. As a result, the statute is less likely to be invalidated under the contracts clause. (Choice C) Although the law deals with the local matter of selling toys in stores located within the state, it also unduly burdens interstate commerce by discriminating against out- of-state manufacturers.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because it imposes an undue burden on interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"一家总部和唯一制造厂位于某个州的玩具制造商开发了一款“火星人”玩具，模拟遥控车辆对火星的探索。它准确地描绘了火星的景观和穿越火星的无人探索飞行器。该玩具品质优良，安全耐用，销量非常好。其他位于该州以外的玩具制造商也开发了价格较低的类似玩具。这些制造商签订了向该州经销商销售火星玩具的合同。尽管这些玩具安全耐用，但它们描绘的火星景观不如该州制造的玩具真实。然而，由于价格差异，这些玩具的销量严重削弱了该州生产的火星玩具的销量。州立法机关最近颁布了一项法律，“保护本州儿童免受错误科学的影响，并保护州内玩具制造商免受不公平竞争”。法律禁止在该州销售任何声称代表外星物体且不符合特定科学标准的玩具。该州生产的火星玩具满足所有这些标准；竞争制造商的火星玩具均不符合要求。国家法律是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为它废除了其他玩具制造商与同意销售火星玩具的州内商店之间的合同义务。","B":"不，因为它给州际贸易带来了过度的负担。","C":"是的，因为它只处理本地事务，即在州内商店销售玩具。","D":"是的，因为国家保护国家儿童免受错误科学侵害的利益证明了州际贸易的这种负担是合理的。"},"explanationZh":"对州外商业的歧视\n\n类型 说明 示例\n\n面部法规的语言明确“州外种植的玉米必须歧视有利于州内商业，满足更广泛的安全措施”\n\n实施歧视性表面中立法规 州对在州外种植的玉米进行检查，以利于州内种植而不是州外种植的玉米，但不包括在商业州种植的玉米\n\n歧视性否则中立的监管 安全效应的执行不成比例地影响措施外的结果，禁止仅在州外种植的玉米州商业\n\n商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业——即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。相反，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州歧视州外商业或以其他方式过度加重州际商业负担。国家法规可以通过三种方式进行区别对待：\n• 从表面上看——当法规的语言明确有利于州内利益时\n• 在其应用中——当表面中立的法规被应用于有利于州内利益时\n• 就其效果而言——当原本中立的法律对州外商业产生不成比例的影响时 歧视性法规违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非州证明该法规 (1) 促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益 (2)，而该利益无法通过合理的替代方案实现。在这里，州法律在表面上和应用上都是中立的，因为它同样适用于州内和州外的玩具制造商。但由于只有州外的玩具制造商受到该法律的影响，因此具有歧视性效果。国家确实有合法的、非经济的利益来保护儿童免受错误科学的侵害。但有一些合理的替代方案可以实现这种兴趣，例如，要求声明该玩具在科学上不准确（选择 D）。因此，该法律过度加重了州际贸易的负担，并且是违宪的。 （选项 A）根据合同条款，如果损害是 (1) 合理且针对性强 (2) 是为了实现重要的国家利益，则严重损害现有私人合同的州法律是有效的。这种量身定制的标准更加尊重各州\n\n高于消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的无合理替代标准。因此，根据合同条款，该法规不太可能无效。 （选项 C）虽然该法律涉及在州内商店销售玩具的当地问题，但它也通过歧视州外制造商而给州际贸易带来过度负担。\n\n教育目标：在表面、应用或效果上歧视州际贸易的州法规是无效的，除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 巴克斯小鬼。 v. Dias, 468 U.S. 263, 273 (1984)（认为当一个州的监管仅对州外经济活动产生负面影响时，该州就歧视州际商业）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 103 (2019)（解释针对州际商业的歧视类型）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"表面上、应用中或效果上歧视州际贸易的州法规是无效的，除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。","iracZh":{"issue":"国家法律是否符合宪法？","rule":"表面上、应用中或效果上歧视州际贸易的州法规是无效的，除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。","analysis":"对州外商业的歧视\n\n类型 说明 示例\n\n面部法规的语言明确“州外种植的玉米必须歧视有利于州内商业，满足更广泛的安全措施”\n\n实施歧视性表面中立法规 州对在州外种植的玉米进行检查，以利于州内种植而不是州外种植的玉米，但不包括在商业州种植的玉米\n\n歧视性否则中立的监管 安全效应的执行不成比例地影响措施外的结果，禁止仅在州外种植的玉米州商业\n\n商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业——即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。相反，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州歧视州外商业或以其他方式过度加重州际商业负担。国家法规可以通过三种方式进行区别对待：\n• 从表面上看——当法规的语言明确有利于州内利益时\n• 在其应用中——当表面中立的法规被应用于有利于州内利益时\n• 就其效果而言——当原本中立的法律对州外商业产生不成比例的影响时 歧视性法规违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非州证明该法规 (1) 促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益 (2)，而该利益无法通过合理的替代方案实现。在这里，州法律在表面上和应用上都是中立的，因为它同样适用于州内和州外的玩具制造商。但由于只有州外的玩具制造商受到该法律的影响，因此具有歧视性效果。国家确实有合法的、非经济的利益来保护儿童免受错误科学的侵害。但有一些合理的替代方案可以实现这种兴趣，例如，要求声明该玩具在科学上不准确（选择 D）。因此，该法律过度加重了州际贸易的负担，并且是违宪的。 （选项 A）根据合同条款，如果损害是 (1) 合理且针对性强 (2) 是为了实现重要的国家利益，则严重损害现有私人合同的州法律是有效的。这种量身定制的标准更加尊重各州\n\n高于消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的无合理替代标准。因此，根据合同条款，该法规不太可能无效。 （选项 C）虽然该法律涉及在州内商店销售玩具的当地问题，但它也通过歧视州外制造商而给州际贸易带来过度负担。\n\n教育目标：在表面、应用或效果上歧视州际贸易的州法规是无效的，除非 (1) 它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 巴克斯小鬼。 v. Dias, 468 U.S. 263, 273 (1984)（认为当一个州的监管仅对州外经济活动产生负面影响时，该州就歧视州际商业）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 103 (2019)（解释针对州际商业的歧视类型）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为它给州际贸易带来了过度的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contracts clause","ipa":"/kˈɑntɹˌækts klˈɔz/","syllables":"con·tracts clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合同条款：限制州以法律严重损害既有合同义务","contextualMeaning":"合同条款：限制州以法律严重损害既有合同义务","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Under the contracts clause, state laws that substantially impair existing private contracts are valid if the impairment is (1) reasonable and narrowly tailored (2) to achieve an important state interest.","synonyms":["contracts article"]},{"term":"narrowly tailored","ipa":"/nˈɛɹoʊli tˈeɪlɝd/","syllables":"nar·rowly tai·lored","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"狭义量身定制：措施精准针对目标且不过度扩张","contextualMeaning":"狭义量身定制：措施精准针对目标且不过度扩张","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Under the contracts clause, state laws that substantially impair existing private contracts are valid if the impairment is (1) reasonable and narrowly tailored (2) to achieve an important state interest.","synonyms":["closely fitted","precisely targeted"]},{"term":"disclaimer","ipa":"/dɪsklˈeɪmɝ/","syllables":"dis·claimer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 放弃, 拒负责任, 不承诺；[经] 弃权或不申诉条款","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 放弃, 拒负责任, 不承诺；[经] 弃权或不申诉条款","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:disclaimers","sourceSentence":"But there are reasonable alternatives to achieve this interest—eg, requiring a disclaimer that the toy is not scientifically accurate (Choice D).","synonyms":["disavowal"]},{"term":"impairment","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈɛɹmənt/","syllables":"im·pair·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 削弱, 减少, 损害, 损伤, 危害；[计] 减损","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 削弱, 减少, 损害, 损伤, 危害；[计] 减损","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:impairments","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Under the contracts clause, state laws that substantially impair existing private contracts are valid if the impairment is (1) reasonable and narrowly tailored (2) to achieve an important state interest.","synonyms":["stultification","constipation","deadening","damage"]},{"term":"abrogates","ipa":"/ˈæbɹʌɡˌeɪt/","syllables":"ab·ro·gates","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 废除（法律等）( abrogate的第三人称单数 )； 取消； 去掉","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 废除（法律等）( abrogate的第三人称单数 )； 取消； 去掉","wordForm":"词；原形 abrogate；词形 0:abrogate/1:3","sourceSentence":"No, because it abrogates the obligations of the contracts between the other toy manufacturers and the in-state outlets that have agreed to sell their Martian toys.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"ban","ipa":"/bˈæn/","syllables":"ban","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 禁令；vt. 禁止, 取缔","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 禁令；vt. 禁止, 取缔","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:banned/i:banning/p:banned/s:bans/3:bans","sourceSentence":"Discrimination against out-of-state commerce Type Description Example Facial Regulation's language explicitly \"Corn grown out-of-state must discrimination favors in-state commerce satisfy more extensive safety measures\" Discriminatory Facially neutral regulation applied State inspects corn grown out- application to favor in-state over out-of-state of-state but not corn grown in- commerce state Discriminatory Otherwis","synonyms":["prohibition","proscription","Bachelor of Arts in Nursing","banning"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":19,"sourceQuestionPage":57,"sourceQuestionPages":[57],"sourceExplanationPages":[58,59],"sourceAnswerPages":[58,59],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-020","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"Small retailers located in a state are concerned about the loss of business to certain large retailers located nearby in bordering states. In an effort to deal with this concern, the state legislature enacted a statute requiring all manufacturers and wholesalers that sell goods to retailers in the state to do so at prices that are no higher than the lowest prices at which they sell them to retailers in any of the bordering states. Several manufacturers and wholesalers that are located in bordering states and that sell their goods to retailers in the state bring an action in federal court to challenge the constitutionality of this statute. Which of the following arguments offered by these plaintiffs is likely to be most persuasive in light of applicable precedent?","choices":{"A":"The state statute denies them the equal protection of the laws.","B":"The state statute deprives them of a privilege or immunity of national citizenship.","C":"The state statute deprives them of their property or liberty without due process of law.","D":"The state statute imposes an unreasonable burden on interstate commerce."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate interstate commerce. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) is that states cannot discriminate against or otherwise unduly burden interstate commerce. As a result, state regulations that have the practical effect of controlling wholly out-of-state conduct (ie, extraterritorial regulations) are unconstitutional—even if the regulated conduct affects commerce within the state. Here, the state statute is an extraterritorial regulation since it has the practical effect of regulating wholly out-of-state conduct. That is because the statute requires all manufacturers and wholesalers selling goods in the state to do so at prices that are no higher than the lowest prices they charge retailers in bordering states. This requires out-of- state commerce to be conducted in accordance with the state statute. Therefore, the most persuasive argument that the statute is unconstitutional is that it imposes an unreasonable burden on interstate commerce. (Choice A) State laws that substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class almost always violate the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But there is no fundamental right to sell goods, and out-of-state businesses are not a protected class. As a result, the state statute does not deny the plaintiffs the equal protection of the laws. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause* bars states from depriving individuals of the very limited rights of national citizenship—which do not include the right to sell goods. *The privileges or immunities clause is almost always an incorrect answer choice on the MBE. (Choice C) Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, state laws cannot deprive an individual of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. Laws that substantially impact a fundamental right are generally unjustified under strict scrutiny. But since no fundamental right is implicated here, the state statute is likely justified under rational basis scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: The dormant commerce clause prohibits states from enacting extraterritorial regulations that have the practical effect of controlling wholly out-of-state conduct—eg, by requiring out-of-state commerce to be conducted in accordance with a state statute.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Healy v. Beer Inst., 491 U.S. 324, 336–39 (1989) (holding that price-affirmation statutes violate the dormant commerce clause since they regulate commerce in other states).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 40 (2019) (discussing impermissible extraterritorial regulation).","educationalObjective":"The dormant commerce clause prohibits states from enacting extraterritorial regulations that have the practical effect of controlling wholly out-of-state conduct—eg, by requiring out-of-state commerce to be conducted in accordance with a state statute.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Healy v. Beer Inst., 491 U.S. 324, 336–39 (1989) (holding that price-affirmation statutes violate the dormant commerce clause since they regulate commerce in other states).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 40 (2019) (discussing impermissible extraterritorial regulation).","rule":"The dormant commerce clause prohibits states from enacting extraterritorial regulations that have the practical effect of controlling wholly out-of-state conduct—eg, by requiring out-of-state commerce to be conducted in accordance with a state statute.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments offered by these plaintiffs is likely to be most persuasive in light of applicable precedent?","rule":"The dormant commerce clause prohibits states from enacting extraterritorial regulations that have the practical effect of controlling wholly out-of-state conduct—eg, by requiring out-of-state commerce to be conducted in accordance with a state statute.","analysis":"The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate interstate commerce. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) is that states cannot discriminate against or otherwise unduly burden interstate commerce. As a result, state regulations that have the practical effect of controlling wholly out-of-state conduct (ie, extraterritorial regulations) are unconstitutional—even if the regulated conduct affects commerce within the state. Here, the state statute is an extraterritorial regulation since it has the practical effect of regulating wholly out-of-state conduct. That is because the statute requires all manufacturers and wholesalers selling goods in the state to do so at prices that are no higher than the lowest prices they charge retailers in bordering states. This requires out-of- state commerce to be conducted in accordance with the state statute. Therefore, the most persuasive argument that the statute is unconstitutional is that it imposes an unreasonable burden on interstate commerce. (Choice A) State laws that substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class almost always violate the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But there is no fundamental right to sell goods, and out-of-state businesses are not a protected class. As a result, the state statute does not deny the plaintiffs the equal protection of the laws. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause* bars states from depriving individuals of the very limited rights of national citizenship—which do not include the right to sell goods. *The privileges or immunities clause is almost always an incorrect answer choice on the MBE. (Choice C) Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, state laws cannot deprive an individual of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. Laws that substantially impact a fundamental right are generally unjustified under strict scrutiny. But since no fundamental right is implicated here, the state statute is likely justified under rational basis scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer D: The state statute imposes an unreasonable burden on interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"位于某个州的小型零售商担心业务会流失到位于邻近州的某些大型零售商。为了解决这一问题，州立法机关颁布了一项法规，要求所有向该州零售商出售商品的制造商和批发商的价格不得高于向任何邻州零售商出售商品的最低价格。位于边境各州并向该州零售商销售商品的几家制造商和批发商向联邦法院提起诉讼，质疑该法规的合宪性。根据适用的先例，这些原告提出的以下哪项论点可能最有说服力？","choicesZh":{"A":"州法规否认他们受到法律的平等保护。","B":"国家法规剥夺了他们的公民特权或豁免权。","C":"州法规未经正当法律程序就剥夺了他们的财产或自由。","D":"该州法规对州际贸易施加了不合理的负担。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的权力。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是，各州不得歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。因此，具有控制完全州外行为的实际效果的州法规（即域外法规）是违宪的——即使受监管的行为影响州内的商业。在这里，州法规是域外法规，因为它具有规范完全域外行为的实际效果。这是因为该法规要求所有在该州销售商品的制造商和批发商的销售价格不得高于他们向毗邻州零售商收取的最低价格。这要求州外商业活动必须按照州法规进行。因此，认为该法规违宪的最有说服力的论点是它给州际贸易带来了不合理的负担。 （选项 A）严重影响基本权利或故意歧视受保护群体的州法律几乎总是违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但没有销售商品的基本权利，州外企业也不是受保护阶层。因此，州法规并不否认原告受到法律的平等保护。 （选项 B）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款*禁止各州剥夺个人非常有限的公民权利，其中不包括销售商品的权利。 *MBE 中的特权或豁免条款几乎总是错误的答案选项。 （选项C）根据第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），州法律不得在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，对基本权利产生重大影响的法律通常是不合理的。但由于这里不涉及基本权利，因此在合理基础的审查下，国家法规可能是合理的。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州制定具有控制完全州外行为实际效果的域外法规，例如要求州外商业按照州法规进行。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Healy v. Beer Inst., 491 U.S. 324, 336–39 (1989)（认为价格确认法规违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），因为它们规范其他州的商业）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 40 (2019)（讨论不允许的域外监管）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州制定具有控制完全州外行为实际效果的域外法规，例如要求州外商业按照州法规进行。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据适用的先例，这些原告提出的以下哪项论点可能最有说服力？","rule":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州制定具有控制完全州外行为实际效果的域外法规，例如要求州外商业按照州法规进行。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的权力。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是，各州不得歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。因此，具有控制完全州外行为的实际效果的州法规（即域外法规）是违宪的——即使受监管的行为影响州内的商业。在这里，州法规是域外法规，因为它具有规范完全域外行为的实际效果。这是因为该法规要求所有在该州销售商品的制造商和批发商的销售价格不得高于他们向毗邻州零售商收取的最低价格。这要求州外商业活动必须按照州法规进行。因此，认为该法规违宪的最有说服力的论点是它给州际贸易带来了不合理的负担。 （选项 A）严重影响基本权利或故意歧视受保护群体的州法律几乎总是违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但没有销售商品的基本权利，州外企业也不是受保护阶层。因此，州法规并不否认原告受到法律的平等保护。 （选项 B）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款*禁止各州剥夺个人非常有限的公民权利，其中不包括销售商品的权利。 *MBE 中的特权或豁免条款几乎总是错误的答案选项。 （选项C）根据第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），州法律不得在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，对基本权利产生重大影响的法律通常是不合理的。但由于这里不涉及基本权利，因此在合理基础的审查下，国家法规可能是合理的。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州制定具有控制完全州外行为实际效果的域外法规，例如要求州外商业按照州法规进行。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Healy v. Beer Inst., 491 U.S. 324, 336–39 (1989)（认为价格确认法规违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），因为它们规范其他州的商业）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 40 (2019)（讨论不允许的域外监管）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该州法规对州际贸易施加了不合理的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"plaintiffs","ipa":"/plˈeɪntɪfs/","syllables":"plain·tiffs","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. <律>原告( plaintiff的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. <律>原告( plaintiff的复数形式 )","wordForm":"名词；原形 plaintiff；词形 0:plaintiff/1:s","sourceSentence":"Which of the following arguments offered by these plaintiffs is likely to be most persuasive in light of applicable precedent?","synonyms":["complainant"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-6f0bf4e3e78163ab"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":20,"sourceQuestionPage":60,"sourceQuestionPages":[60],"sourceExplanationPages":[61,62],"sourceAnswerPages":[61,62],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-021","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Foreign Affairs & War Powers","question":"A state requires all corporations that operate in the state to file their annual income tax returns with the state's tax board for the purpose of calculating the amount of income tax that each corporation owes. The board requires corporations to provide reasonable approximations of their income earned on a worldwide basis. The board then calculates the income tax that a corporation owes based on the amount of income attributable to its operations within the state. Several foreign corporations that operate in the state and do not use the state's method of accounting have sued the board in state court, arguing that the state tax is unconstitutional. Is the state tax constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress has the sole power to tax foreign corporations.","B":"No, because the state tax discriminates against foreign commerce.","C":"Yes, because the state tax was enacted pursuant to the state's police power, which takes precedence over the negative implications of the commerce clause.","D":"Yes, if the court determines that the state tax does not impede federal uniformity in foreign commerce."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The commerce clause grants Congress the power to regulate foreign commerce. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening foreign commerce. However, a state may still tax foreign persons and entities that earn income in the state if the state tax satisfies the Complete Auto and federal uniformity requirements (Choice A). Here, the state tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements (see image above) because the tax:\n• is applied to the foreign corporations' business activities in the state (substantial nexus)\n• is based on a rational formula that only taxes income earned in the state (fairly apportioned)\n• applies equally to domestic and foreign corporations (nondiscriminatory) (Choice B) and\n• is reasonable compared to services and benefits provided by the state, like infrastructure (fairly related). Additionally, the state tax must satisfy federal uniformity requirements. This means that the tax must not create an inevitable risk of international multiple taxation OR prevent the federal government from speaking with one voice in international trade or foreign affairs. Therefore, the state tax is constitutional if the court determines that it satisfies these additional requirements. (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment gives states all powers that are not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution—eg, police power to impose taxes on activities in the state. But pursuant to the supremacy clause, state police powers do not take precedence over other constitutional provisions, including the negative implications of the commerce clause.\n\nEducational objective: A state tax affecting foreign commerce must satisfy the Complete Auto requirements (substantial nexus, fairly apportioned, nondiscriminatory, and fairly related). Additionally, such a tax must not create an inevitable risk of international multiple taxation OR prevent the federal government from speaking with one voice in foreign affairs.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. 1, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Barclays Bank Pub. Ltd. Co. v. Franchise Tax Bd. of Cal., 512 U.S. 298, 311 (1994) (setting forth the requirements for determining whether a state tax that affects foreign commerce violates the commerce clause).","educationalObjective":"A state tax affecting foreign commerce must satisfy the Complete Auto requirements (substantial nexus, fairly apportioned, nondiscriminatory, and fairly related). Additionally, such a tax must not create an inevitable risk of international multiple taxation OR prevent the federal government from speaking with one voice in foreign affairs.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. 1, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Barclays Bank Pub. Ltd. Co. v. Franchise Tax Bd. of Cal., 512 U.S. 298, 311 (1994) (setting forth the requirements for determining whether a state tax that affects foreign commerce violates the commerce clause).","rule":"A state tax affecting foreign commerce must satisfy the Complete Auto requirements (substantial nexus, fairly apportioned, nondiscriminatory, and fairly related). Additionally, such a tax must not create an inevitable risk of international multiple taxation OR prevent the federal government from speaking with one voice in foreign affairs.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state tax constitutional?","rule":"A state tax affecting foreign commerce must satisfy the Complete Auto requirements (substantial nexus, fairly apportioned, nondiscriminatory, and fairly related). Additionally, such a tax must not create an inevitable risk of international multiple taxation OR prevent the federal government from speaking with one voice in foreign affairs.","analysis":"The commerce clause grants Congress the power to regulate foreign commerce. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening foreign commerce. However, a state may still tax foreign persons and entities that earn income in the state if the state tax satisfies the Complete Auto and federal uniformity requirements (Choice A). Here, the state tax satisfies the Complete Auto requirements (see image above) because the tax:\n• is applied to the foreign corporations' business activities in the state (substantial nexus)\n• is based on a rational formula that only taxes income earned in the state (fairly apportioned)\n• applies equally to domestic and foreign corporations (nondiscriminatory) (Choice B) and\n• is reasonable compared to services and benefits provided by the state, like infrastructure (fairly related). Additionally, the state tax must satisfy federal uniformity requirements. This means that the tax must not create an inevitable risk of international multiple taxation OR prevent the federal government from speaking with one voice in international trade or foreign affairs. Therefore, the state tax is constitutional if the court determines that it satisfies these additional requirements. (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment gives states all powers that are not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution—eg, police power to impose taxes on activities in the state. But pursuant to the supremacy clause, state police powers do not take precedence over other constitutional provisions, including the negative implications of the commerce clause.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, if the court determines that the state tax does not impede federal uniformity in foreign commerce."},"questionZh":"州要求所有在该州运营的公司向州税务委员会提交年度所得税申报表，以便计算每家公司所欠的所得税金额。该委员会要求公司提供其在全球范围内赚取的收入的合理近似值。然后，委员会根据公司在州内运营的收入金额计算公司所欠的所得税。几家在该州运营且不使用该州会计方法的外国公司已向州法院起诉该委员会，认为州税违宪。州税符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会拥有对外国公司征税的唯一权力。","B":"不，因为州税歧视外国商业。","C":"是的，因为州税是根据州警察权力制定的，它优先于商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。","D":"是的，如果法院确定州税不妨碍联邦对外贸易的统一。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管外国商业的权力。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是禁止各国歧视外国商业或以其他方式对外国商业造成不当负担。然而，如果州税满足完整汽车和联邦统一要求（选择 A），则州仍可对在该州赚取收入的外国人和实体征税。在这里，州税满足Complete Auto 要件（见上图），因为该税：\n• 适用于外国公司在该州的商业活动（实质性联系）\n• 基于合理的公式，仅对在该州赚取的收入征税（公平分配）\n• 同样适用于国内和国外公司（非歧视）（选择 B）并且\n• 与州提供的服务和福利（如基础设施）相比是合理的（公平相关）。此外，州税必须满足联邦统一要求。这意味着该税收不得造成不可避免的国际多重征税风险，也不得妨碍联邦政府在国际贸易或外交事务中统一发言权。因此，如果法院判定州税满足这些附加要求，则州税是符合宪法的。 （选项C） 第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力，例如警察对该州的活动征税的权力。但根据至上条款，州警察权力并不优先于其他宪法条款，包括商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。\n\n教育目标：影响对外贸易的州税必须满足Complete Auto 要件（实质性联系、公平分配、非歧视和公平相关）。此外，此类税收不得造成不可避免的国际多重征税风险，也不得妨碍联邦政府在外交事务中统一发言权。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 1，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 巴克莱银行酒吧。有限公司诉特许经营税局。 of Cal., 512 U.S. 298, 311 (1994)（规定了确定影响对外贸易的州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"影响对外贸易的州税必须满足Complete Auto 要件（实质性联系、公平分配、非歧视性和公平相关）。此外，此类税收不得造成不可避免的国际多重征税风险，也不得妨碍联邦政府在外交事务中统一发言权。","iracZh":{"issue":"州税符合宪法吗？","rule":"影响对外贸易的州税必须满足Complete Auto 要件（实质性联系、公平分配、非歧视性和公平相关）。此外，此类税收不得造成不可避免的国际多重征税风险，也不得妨碍联邦政府在外交事务中统一发言权。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管外国商业的权力。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是禁止各国歧视外国商业或以其他方式对外国商业造成不当负担。然而，如果州税满足完整汽车和联邦统一要求（选择 A），则州仍可对在该州赚取收入的外国人和实体征税。在这里，州税满足Complete Auto 要件（见上图），因为该税：\n• 适用于外国公司在该州的商业活动（实质性联系）\n• 基于合理的公式，仅对在该州赚取的收入征税（公平分配）\n• 同样适用于国内和国外公司（非歧视）（选择 B）并且\n• 与州提供的服务和福利（如基础设施）相比是合理的（公平相关）。此外，州税必须满足联邦统一要求。这意味着该税收不得造成不可避免的国际多重征税风险，也不得妨碍联邦政府在国际贸易或外交事务中统一发言权。因此，如果法院判定州税满足这些附加要求，则州税是符合宪法的。 （选项C） 第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力，例如警察对该州的活动征税的权力。但根据至上条款，州警察权力并不优先于其他宪法条款，包括商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。\n\n教育目标：影响对外贸易的州税必须满足Complete Auto 要件（实质性联系、公平分配、非歧视和公平相关）。此外，此类税收不得造成不可避免的国际多重征税风险，也不得妨碍联邦政府在外交事务中统一发言权。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 1，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 巴克莱银行酒吧。有限公司诉特许经营税局。 of Cal., 512 U.S. 298, 311 (1994)（规定了确定影响对外贸易的州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，如果法院确定州税不妨碍联邦对外贸易的统一。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-2fa27aff733339c2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":21,"sourceQuestionPage":63,"sourceQuestionPages":[63],"sourceExplanationPages":[64,65],"sourceAnswerPages":[64,65],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-022","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A state owned and operated an electric power system, which included a nuclear power plant. In order to ensure the availability of sites for the disposal of spent fuel from the nuclear power plant, the state refused to supply electric power to out-of-state purchasers residing in states that would not accept spent fuel from the plant for storage or disposal. Assume that no federal statute applies. Which of the following is the strongest argument that the state's action is constitutional?","choices":{"A":"A state may condition the sale to out-of-state purchasers of any products produced in that state on the willingness of those purchasers to bear their fair share of the environmental costs of producing those products.","B":"The generation of electricity is intrastate by nature and therefore subject to plenary state control.","C":"The state itself owns and operates the power system, and therefore its refusal to supply power to out-of-state purchasers is not subject to the negative implications of the commerce clause.","D":"The state's action is rationally related to the health, safety, and welfare of state citizens."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive authority to regulate interstate commerce. And the negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But states are not restrained by this clause when they participate in the market by, for example, buying or selling goods or services (ie, market-participant exception). Here, the state discriminated against interstate commerce by refusing to supply electricity to out-of-state purchasers residing in states that would not store or dispose of spent fuel. But since the state itself owns and operates the power system, the market-participant exception applies. As a result, the state's refusal to supply power is not subject to the negative implications of the commerce clause. Therefore, this is the strongest argument that its action is constitutional. (Choice A) Discrimination against interstate commerce may be justified if it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and no reasonable alternative exists. But a state's economic interest in sharing the environmental costs of production with out-of-state purchasers cannot justify such discrimination. (Choice B) Although generating electricity is intrastate (ie, within a state) by nature, supplying electricity to out-of-state purchasers is an interstate activity. But a state does not violate the dormant commerce clause by refusing to supply electricity to out-of-state purchasers when, as here, the state is acting as a market participant. (Choice D) All powers not explicitly granted to the federal government by the Constitution are reserved to the states through the Tenth Amendment. This includes the states' police power to take action that is rationally related to protecting the health, safety, and welfare of its citizens. But this power does not allow states to violate other constitutional provisions— including the dormant commerce clause.\n\nEducational objective: A state's activities are not restricted by the dormant commerce clause when the state is acting as a market participant (as opposed to a market regulator).\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980) (holding that a state is not subject to the dormant commerce clause when it acts as a market participant).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 33 (2019) (explaining the market-participant exception to the dormant commerce clause).","educationalObjective":"A state's activities are not restricted by the dormant commerce clause when the state is acting as a market participant (as opposed to a market regulator).","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980) (holding that a state is not subject to the dormant commerce clause when it acts as a market participant).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 33 (2019) (explaining the market-participant exception to the dormant commerce clause).","rule":"A state's activities are not restricted by the dormant commerce clause when the state is acting as a market participant (as opposed to a market regulator).","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest argument that the state's action is constitutional?","rule":"A state's activities are not restricted by the dormant commerce clause when the state is acting as a market participant (as opposed to a market regulator).","analysis":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive authority to regulate interstate commerce. And the negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But states are not restrained by this clause when they participate in the market by, for example, buying or selling goods or services (ie, market-participant exception). Here, the state discriminated against interstate commerce by refusing to supply electricity to out-of-state purchasers residing in states that would not store or dispose of spent fuel. But since the state itself owns and operates the power system, the market-participant exception applies. As a result, the state's refusal to supply power is not subject to the negative implications of the commerce clause. Therefore, this is the strongest argument that its action is constitutional. (Choice A) Discrimination against interstate commerce may be justified if it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and no reasonable alternative exists. But a state's economic interest in sharing the environmental costs of production with out-of-state purchasers cannot justify such discrimination. (Choice B) Although generating electricity is intrastate (ie, within a state) by nature, supplying electricity to out-of-state purchasers is an interstate activity. But a state does not violate the dormant commerce clause by refusing to supply electricity to out-of-state purchasers when, as here, the state is acting as a market participant. (Choice D) All powers not explicitly granted to the federal government by the Constitution are reserved to the states through the Tenth Amendment. This includes the states' police power to take action that is rationally related to protecting the health, safety, and welfare of its citizens. But this power does not allow states to violate other constitutional provisions— including the dormant commerce clause.","conclusion":"Answer C: The state itself owns and operates the power system, and therefore its refusal to supply power to out-of-state purchasers is not subject to the negative implications of the commerce clause."},"questionZh":"一家国有并经营一个电力系统，其中包括一座核电站。为了确保有地方处理核电站乏燃料，该州拒绝向居住在不接受核电站乏燃料进行储存或处置的州的州外购买者提供电力。假设没有适用的联邦法规。以下哪一项是证明国家行为符合宪法的最有力论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"州可以将在该州生产的任何产品出售给州外购买者，条件是这些购买者是否愿意承担生产这些产品的环境成本的公平份额。","B":"发电本质上是州内的，因此受到国家的全面控制。","C":"州本身拥有并运营电力系统，因此拒绝向州外购买者供电不受商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。","D":"国家的行为与国家公民的健康、安全和福利合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。但国家在通过购买或销售商品或服务等方式参与市场时不受本条款的限制（即市场参与者例外）。在这里，该州拒绝向居住在不会储存或处置乏燃料的州的州外购买者提供电力，从而歧视州际贸易。但由于国家本身拥有并经营电力系统，因此市场参与者例外。因此，国家拒绝供电不会受到商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。因此，这是其行为符合宪法的最有力论据。 （选择 A）如果州际贸易促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益并且不存在合理的替代方案，那么对州际贸易的歧视可能是合理的。但一州与州外购买者分担生产环境成本的经济利益并不能成为这种歧视的理由。 （选择B） 虽然发电本质上是州内（即州内）的活动，但向州外购买者供电是一项州际活动。但是，当州政府作为市场参与者时，拒绝向州外购买者供电并不违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。 （选项 D）宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力均通过第十修正案保留给各州。这包括各州警察有权采取与保护公民健康、安全和福利合理相关的行动。但这项权力不允许各州违反其他宪法条款——包括消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。\n\n教育目标：当国家作为市场参与者（而不是市场监管者）时，其活动不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980)（认为国家作为市场参与者时不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的约束）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 33 (2019)（解释消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的市场参与者例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当国家作为市场参与者（而不是市场监管者）时，其活动不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是证明国家行为符合宪法的最有力论据？","rule":"当国家作为市场参与者（而不是市场监管者）时，其活动不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。但国家在通过购买或销售商品或服务等方式参与市场时不受本条款的限制（即市场参与者例外）。在这里，该州拒绝向居住在不会储存或处置乏燃料的州的州外购买者提供电力，从而歧视州际贸易。但由于国家本身拥有并经营电力系统，因此市场参与者例外。因此，国家拒绝供电不会受到商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。因此，这是其行为符合宪法的最有力论据。 （选择 A）如果州际贸易促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益并且不存在合理的替代方案，那么对州际贸易的歧视可能是合理的。但一州与州外购买者分担生产环境成本的经济利益并不能成为这种歧视的理由。 （选择B） 虽然发电本质上是州内（即州内）的活动，但向州外购买者供电是一项州际活动。但是，当州政府作为市场参与者时，拒绝向州外购买者供电并不违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。 （选项 D）宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力均通过第十修正案保留给各州。这包括各州警察有权采取与保护公民健康、安全和福利合理相关的行动。但这项权力不允许各州违反其他宪法条款——包括消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。\n\n教育目标：当国家作为市场参与者（而不是市场监管者）时，其活动不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980)（认为国家作为市场参与者时不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的约束）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 33 (2019)（解释消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的市场参与者例外）。","conclusion":"答案 C：州本身拥有并运营电力系统，因此拒绝向州外购买者供电不受商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"regulator","ipa":"/ɹˈɛɡjʌlˌeɪtɝ/","syllables":"reg·u·la·tor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 调整者, 校准者, 校准器, 调整器, 标准钟；[化] 调节剂； 调节器","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 调整者, 校准者, 校准器, 调整器, 标准钟；[化] 调节剂； 调节器","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:regulators","sourceSentence":"A state's activities are not restricted by the dormant commerce clause when the state is acting as a market participant (as opposed to a market regulator).","synonyms":["governor"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-92ecbf48937cf0df"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":22,"sourceQuestionPage":66,"sourceQuestionPages":[66],"sourceExplanationPages":[67,68],"sourceAnswerPages":[67,68],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-023","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"A state law requires any lawn mower sold in the state to meet a specified minimum level of fuel efficiency. A new federal statute requires all power equipment, including lawn mowers, to be labeled with energy efficiency stickers to permit purchasers to make informed choices when buying such equipment. The statute does not expressly preempt state law. Assume that no other federal statute or administrative regulation addresses the energy efficiency of power equipment. Which of the following is the best argument the state can make for the continued validity of its law?","choices":{"A":"Congress cannot preempt state laws requiring a specified minimum level of fuel efficiency for lawn mowers, because the use of such equipment is a wholly local event and, therefore, is beyond the regulatory authority vested in Congress by the commerce clause.","B":"The law is unaffected by the federal statute, because Congress did not expressly prohibit state laws requiring power equipment to meet specified levels of fuel efficiency.","C":"The purpose of the law is consistent with the purpose of the federal statute, enforcement of the law would not interfere with the full execution of the statute, and there is no evidence that Congress intended to preclude the states from enacting supplemental laws on this subject.","D":"There is a very strong presumption that a specific state law on a subject normally within the state's police power prevails over a more general federal statute, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states primary authority over matters affecting public health, welfare, and safety."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The Article VI supremacy clause allows a federal law to preempt a conflicting state law, thereby rendering the state law void and without effect. But there is a presumption against preemption—whether express or implied—that can only be overcome when Congress clearly intended to supersede the state law. Here, the federal statute requiring power equipment (including lawn mowers) to have energy efficiency stickers does not expressly preempt the state law requiring lawn mowers to meet a minimum fuel-efficiency level. But the state must also show that the federal statute does not impliedly preempt the state law (Choice B). It can do so here because:\n• there is no evidence that Congress intended to fully occupy the field of power equipment, leaving no room for supplemental state laws (no field preemption)\n\n• enforcement of the state law would not directly interfere with the full execution of the federal statute since a lawn mower can satisfy both laws' requirements (no direct conflict preemption) and\n\n• the state and federal laws have consistent purposes since both aim to promote energy efficiency (no indirect conflict preemption).\n\nTherefore, this is the state's best argument for the continued validity of its law. (Choice A) The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate local activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially impact interstate commerce. Although the use of lawn mowers is a local activity, their aggregated sales likely have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. As a result, they are within Congress's regulatory authority. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the police power to enact and enforce laws that protect the public's health, safety, and welfare. But there is no presumption that state laws prevail over federal laws.\n\nEducational objective: The supremacy clause allows federal laws to expressly or impliedly preempt conflicting state laws. But there is a presumption against preemption that is only overcome when Congress clearly intended to supersede state laws.\n\nReferences\n• Gade v. Nat'l Solid Wastes Mgmt. Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992) (explaining when federal law expressly or impliedly preempts state law).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 234 (2019) (stating the tests used to determine preemption).","educationalObjective":"The supremacy clause allows federal laws to expressly or impliedly preempt conflicting state laws. But there is a presumption against preemption that is only overcome when Congress clearly intended to supersede state laws.","references":"• Gade v. Nat'l Solid Wastes Mgmt. Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992) (explaining when federal law expressly or impliedly preempts state law).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 234 (2019) (stating the tests used to determine preemption).","rule":"The supremacy clause allows federal laws to expressly or impliedly preempt conflicting state laws. But there is a presumption against preemption that is only overcome when Congress clearly intended to supersede state laws.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the best argument the state can make for the continued validity of its law?","rule":"The supremacy clause allows federal laws to expressly or impliedly preempt conflicting state laws. But there is a presumption against preemption that is only overcome when Congress clearly intended to supersede state laws.","analysis":"The Article VI supremacy clause allows a federal law to preempt a conflicting state law, thereby rendering the state law void and without effect. But there is a presumption against preemption—whether express or implied—that can only be overcome when Congress clearly intended to supersede the state law. Here, the federal statute requiring power equipment (including lawn mowers) to have energy efficiency stickers does not expressly preempt the state law requiring lawn mowers to meet a minimum fuel-efficiency level. But the state must also show that the federal statute does not impliedly preempt the state law (Choice B). It can do so here because:\n• there is no evidence that Congress intended to fully occupy the field of power equipment, leaving no room for supplemental state laws (no field preemption)\n\n• enforcement of the state law would not directly interfere with the full execution of the federal statute since a lawn mower can satisfy both laws' requirements (no direct conflict preemption) and\n\n• the state and federal laws have consistent purposes since both aim to promote energy efficiency (no indirect conflict preemption).\n\nTherefore, this is the state's best argument for the continued validity of its law. (Choice A) The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate local activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially impact interstate commerce. Although the use of lawn mowers is a local activity, their aggregated sales likely have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. As a result, they are within Congress's regulatory authority. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the police power to enact and enforce laws that protect the public's health, safety, and welfare. But there is no presumption that state laws prevail over federal laws.","conclusion":"Answer C: The purpose of the law is consistent with the purpose of the federal statute, enforcement of the law would not interfere with the full execution of the statute, and there is no evidence that Congress intended to preclude the states from enacting supplemental laws on this subject."},"questionZh":"州法律要求在该州销售的所有割草机必须满足规定的最低燃油效率水平。一项新的联邦法规要求所有电力设备（包括割草机）都贴上能效标签，以便购买者在购买此类设备时做出明智的选择。该法规没有明确优先于州法律。假设没有其他联邦法规或行政法规涉及电力设备的能源效率。以下哪一项是国家可以为其法律的持续有效性提出的最佳论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"国会不能优先于要求割草机规定最低燃油效率的州法律，因为此类设备的使用完全是本地事件，因此超出了商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会的监管权限。","B":"该法律不受联邦法规的影响，因为国会没有明确禁止州法律要求电力设备满足规定的燃油效率水平。","C":"该法律的目的与联邦法规的目的一致，该法律的执行不会干扰该法规的全面执行，并且没有证据表明国会打算阻止各州就此问题制定补充法律。","D":"有一个非常强烈的推定，即通常在州警察权力范围内的具体州法律优先于更一般的联邦法规，因为第十修正案保留了州对影响公共健康、福利和安全事务的主要权力。"},"explanationZh":"第六条至上条款允许联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律，从而使州法律无效且无效。但有一个反对先发制人的推定——无论是明示的还是暗示的——只有当国会明确打算取代州法律时才能克服这一推定。在这里，要求动力设备（包括割草机）贴有能效标签的联邦法规并没有明确优先于要求割草机满足最低燃油效率水平的州法律。但州还必须证明联邦法规并不暗示优先于州法律（选择 B）。它可以在这里这样做，因为：\n• 没有证据表明国会有意全面占领电力设备领域，没有留下补充州法律的空间（无领域抢占）\n\n• 州法律的执行不会直接干扰联邦法规的全面执行，因为割草机可以同时满足法律的要求（无直接冲突优先权）和\n\n• 州法律和联邦法律具有一致的目的，因为两者都旨在提高能源效率（无间接冲突先发制人）。\n\n因此，这是该州为其法律持续有效的最佳论据。 （选项 A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管当地活动的权力，这些活动无论是单独还是总体上都会对州际商业产生重大影响。尽管割草机的使用是本地活动，但其总销量可能对州际贸易产生重大影响。因此，它们属于国会的监管权限。 （选项 D）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力，包括警察制定和执行保护公众健康、安全和福利的法律的权力。但不存在州法律优先于联邦法律的假设。\n\n教育目标：至上条款允许联邦法律明示或暗示地优先于相冲突的州法律。但是，只有当国会明确打算取代州法律时，才会克服反对先发制人的推定。\n\n参考文献\n• Gade 诉国家固体废物管理公司。 Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992)（解释联邦法律何时明示或暗示优先于州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 234 条 (2019)（规定了用于确定优先权的测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"至上条款允许联邦法律明示或暗示地优先于相冲突的州法律。但是，只有当国会明确打算取代州法律时，才会克服反对先发制人的推定。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是国家可以为其法律的持续有效性提出的最佳论据？","rule":"至上条款允许联邦法律明示或暗示地优先于相冲突的州法律。但是，只有当国会明确打算取代州法律时，才会克服反对先发制人的推定。","analysis":"第六条至上条款允许联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律，从而使州法律无效且无效。但有一个反对先发制人的推定——无论是明示的还是暗示的——只有当国会明确打算取代州法律时才能克服这一推定。在这里，要求动力设备（包括割草机）贴有能效标签的联邦法规并没有明确优先于要求割草机满足最低燃油效率水平的州法律。但州还必须证明联邦法规并不暗示优先于州法律（选择 B）。它可以在这里这样做，因为：\n• 没有证据表明国会有意全面占领电力设备领域，没有留下补充州法律的空间（无领域抢占）\n\n• 州法律的执行不会直接干扰联邦法规的全面执行，因为割草机可以同时满足法律的要求（无直接冲突优先权）和\n\n• 州法律和联邦法律具有一致的目的，因为两者都旨在提高能源效率（无间接冲突先发制人）。\n\n因此，这是该州为其法律持续有效的最佳论据。 （选项 A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管当地活动的权力，这些活动无论是单独还是总体上都会对州际商业产生重大影响。尽管割草机的使用是本地活动，但其总销量可能对州际贸易产生重大影响。因此，它们属于国会的监管权限。 （选项 D）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力，包括警察制定和执行保护公众健康、安全和福利的法律的权力。但不存在州法律优先于联邦法律的假设。\n\n教育目标：至上条款允许联邦法律明示或暗示地优先于相冲突的州法律。但是，只有当国会明确打算取代州法律时，才会克服反对先发制人的推定。\n\n参考文献\n• Gade 诉国家固体废物管理公司。 Ass'n, 505 U.S. 88, 98 (1992)（解释联邦法律何时明示或暗示优先于州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 234 条 (2019)（规定了用于确定优先权的测试）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该法律的目的与联邦法规的目的一致，该法律的执行不会干扰该法规的全面执行，并且没有证据表明国会打算阻止各州就此问题制定补充法律。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"presumption","ipa":"/pɹɪzˈəmpʃən/","syllables":"pre·sump·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 专横, 放肆, 假定, 推测；[法] 假定, 推测, 设想","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 专横, 放肆, 假定, 推测；[法] 假定, 推测, 设想","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:presumptions","sourceSentence":"There is a very strong presumption that a specific state law on a subject normally within the state's police power prevails over a more general federal statute, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states primary authority over matters affecting public health, welfare, and safety.","synonyms":["presumptuousness","effrontery","assumption","given"]},{"term":"execution","ipa":"/ˌɛksʌkjˈuʃən/","syllables":"ex·e·cu·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 实行, 完成, 执行, 死刑；[计] 执行","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 实行, 完成, 执行, 死刑；[计] 执行","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:executions","sourceSentence":"The purpose of the law is consistent with the purpose of the federal statute, enforcement of the law would not interfere with the full execution of the statute, and there is no evidence that Congress intended to preclude the states from enacting supplemental laws on this subject.","synonyms":["performance","carrying out","carrying into action","murder"]},{"term":"validity","ipa":"/vʌlˈɪdəti/","syllables":"va·lid·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 有效性, 正确性；[计] 有效性","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 有效性, 正确性；[计] 有效性","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Which of the following is the best argument the state can make for the continued validity of its law?","synonyms":["cogency","rigor","rigour","validness"]},{"term":"evidence","ipa":"/ˈɛvədəns/","syllables":"ev·i·dence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 根据, 证据, 迹象；[经] 证据, 凭证","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 根据, 证据, 迹象；[经] 证据, 凭证","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:evidenced/s:evidences/i:evidencing/p:evidenced/3:evidences","sourceSentence":"The purpose of the law is consistent with the purpose of the federal statute, enforcement of the law would not interfere with the full execution of the statute, and there is no evidence that Congress intended to preclude the states from enacting supplemental laws on this subject.","synonyms":["grounds","attest","certify","manifest"]},{"term":"vested","ipa":"/vˈɛstɪd/","syllables":"vested","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 穿衣服的, 既得的, 既定的；[经] 既得的, 法定的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 穿衣服的, 既得的, 既定的；[经] 既得的, 法定的","wordForm":"词；原形 vest；词形 0:vest/1:dp/d:vested","sourceSentence":"Congress cannot preempt state laws requiring a specified minimum level of fuel efficiency for lawn mowers, because the use of such equipment is a wholly local event and, therefore, is beyond the regulatory authority vested in Congress by the commerce clause.","synonyms":["singlet","undershirt","waistcoat","robe"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-94e33cea9d282c70"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":23,"sourceQuestionPage":69,"sourceQuestionPages":[69],"sourceExplanationPages":[70,71],"sourceAnswerPages":[70,71],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-024","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"A plaintiff has sued a defendant in a court of the state in which both parties reside. The plaintiff alleges only a cause of action arising under a federal statute, although state law provides a similar cause of action. The federal statute provides that claims under the statute can be brought in any court of competent jurisdiction. The statute has not yet been interpreted by any federal court. Should the state court hear the case?","choices":{"A":"No, because cases arising under federal law must be decided in federal court.","B":"No, because state courts must abstain in cases arising under federal law until a federal court has decided the federal issue.","C":"Yes, because state courts may not discriminate against cases arising under federal law.","D":"Yes, because the parties cannot proceed in federal court since there is no diversity of citizenship."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Concurrent jurisdiction\n\nType of Federal court State court claim\n\nFederal Jurisdiction always proper Jurisdiction proper unless: law\n• Congress granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction or\n• neutral state rule bars state court jurisdiction State law Jurisdiction proper if: Jurisdiction always proper\n\n• diverse opposing parties and\n• amount in controversy > $75,000 State and federal courts share concurrent jurisdiction over claims arising under federal law (Choice A). But under the supremacy clause, state courts cannot discriminate against cases arising under federal law. As a result, state courts can only refuse to hear such a case in two narrow circumstances:\n• when Congress expressly granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction over the subject matter (eg, bankruptcy) or\n\n• when a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from adjudicating the case (eg, a rule barring state courts from deciding cases that do not involve state residents or property).\n\nHere, the plaintiff sued the defendant in state court, alleging only a cause of action arising under federal law. The relevant statute provides that claims under the statute can be brought in any court of competent jurisdiction, and there is no indication that a state rule prohibits the state court from hearing the case. And since state courts cannot discriminate against cases arising under federal law, the state court should hear the case. (Choice B) A federal court should abstain from hearing a case involving an unsettled issue of state law so that state courts can sort out the issue. But state courts need not abstain from hearing unsettled issues of federal law (as seen here). (Choice D) Federal courts can obtain subject-matter jurisdiction under either (1) diversity jurisdiction or (2) federal-question jurisdiction—ie, when the case arises under the Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (as seen here). Therefore, the parties could have\n\nproceeded in federal court even though they lack diversity of citizenship—ie, are citizens of the same state.\n\nEducational objective: The supremacy clause bars state courts from discriminating against cases arising under federal law. As a result, state courts can only refuse to hear federal-law claims if (1) Congress expressly granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction or (2) a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from hearing the case.\n\nReferences\n• Claflin v. Houseman, 93 U.S. 130, 136 (1876) (holding that state courts share concurrent jurisdiction with federal courts over cases arising under federal law).\n\n• Haywood v. Drown, 556 U.S. 729, 735–36 (2009) (explaining that state courts can refuse to hear a case involving a federal law only when federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction or a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from hearing it).\n\n• 20 Am. Jur. 2d Courts § 88 (2019) (describing federal and state courts' concurrent jurisdiction).","educationalObjective":"The supremacy clause bars state courts from discriminating against cases arising under federal law. As a result, state courts can only refuse to hear federal-law claims if (1) Congress expressly granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction or (2) a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from hearing the case.","references":"• Claflin v. Houseman, 93 U.S. 130, 136 (1876) (holding that state courts share concurrent jurisdiction with federal courts over cases arising under federal law).\n\n• Haywood v. Drown, 556 U.S. 729, 735–36 (2009) (explaining that state courts can refuse to hear a case involving a federal law only when federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction or a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from hearing it).\n\n• 20 Am. Jur. 2d Courts § 88 (2019) (describing federal and state courts' concurrent jurisdiction).","rule":"The supremacy clause bars state courts from discriminating against cases arising under federal law. As a result, state courts can only refuse to hear federal-law claims if (1) Congress expressly granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction or (2) a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from hearing the case.","irac":{"issue":"Should the state court hear the case?","rule":"The supremacy clause bars state courts from discriminating against cases arising under federal law. As a result, state courts can only refuse to hear federal-law claims if (1) Congress expressly granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction or (2) a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from hearing the case.","analysis":"Concurrent jurisdiction\n\nType of Federal court State court claim\n\nFederal Jurisdiction always proper Jurisdiction proper unless: law\n• Congress granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction or\n• neutral state rule bars state court jurisdiction State law Jurisdiction proper if: Jurisdiction always proper\n\n• diverse opposing parties and\n• amount in controversy > $75,000 State and federal courts share concurrent jurisdiction over claims arising under federal law (Choice A). But under the supremacy clause, state courts cannot discriminate against cases arising under federal law. As a result, state courts can only refuse to hear such a case in two narrow circumstances:\n• when Congress expressly granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction over the subject matter (eg, bankruptcy) or\n\n• when a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from adjudicating the case (eg, a rule barring state courts from deciding cases that do not involve state residents or property).\n\nHere, the plaintiff sued the defendant in state court, alleging only a cause of action arising under federal law. The relevant statute provides that claims under the statute can be brought in any court of competent jurisdiction, and there is no indication that a state rule prohibits the state court from hearing the case. And since state courts cannot discriminate against cases arising under federal law, the state court should hear the case. (Choice B) A federal court should abstain from hearing a case involving an unsettled issue of state law so that state courts can sort out the issue. But state courts need not abstain from hearing unsettled issues of federal law (as seen here). (Choice D) Federal courts can obtain subject-matter jurisdiction under either (1) diversity jurisdiction or (2) federal-question jurisdiction—ie, when the case arises under the Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (as seen here). Therefore, the parties could have\n\nproceeded in federal court even though they lack diversity of citizenship—ie, are citizens of the same state.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because state courts may not discriminate against cases arising under federal law."},"questionZh":"原告在双方居住州的法院起诉被告。原告声称仅根据联邦法规提出诉讼理由，尽管州法律提供了类似的诉讼理由。联邦法规规定，根据该法规提出的索赔可以向任何有管辖权的法院提出。该法规尚未得到任何联邦法院的解释。州法院应该审理此案吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为根据联邦法律产生的案件必须由联邦法院裁决。","B":"不可以，因为在联邦法院对联邦问题做出裁决之前，州法院必须对联邦法律下的案件弃权。","C":"是的，因为州法院不得歧视根据联邦法律提起的案件。","D":"是的，因为由于不存在公民身份多样性，双方无法在联邦法院进行诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"并行管辖\n\n联邦法院 州法院索赔的类型\n\n联邦管辖权始终为正当管辖权 除非： 法律\n• 国会授予联邦法院专属管辖权或\n• 中立州规则禁止州法院管辖 州法律 管辖权适当，如果： 管辖权始终适当\n\n• 不同的反对派和\n• 争议金额> 75,000 美元 州法院和联邦法院对根据联邦法律提出的索赔具有共同管辖权（选择A）。但根据至上条款，州法院不能歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件。因此，州法院只能在两种狭隘的情况下拒绝审理此类案件：\n• 当国会明确授予联邦法院对相关事项（例如破产）的专属管辖权时或\n\n• 当中立州规则禁止州法院裁决案件时（例如，规则禁止州法院裁决不涉及州居民或财产的案件）。\n\n在此，原告在州法院起诉被告，仅指控根据联邦法律提出诉讼理由。相关法规规定，根据法规提出的索赔可以向任何有管辖权的法院提出，并且没有迹象表明州规则禁止州法院审理此案。由于州法院不能歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件，因此州法院应该审理此案。 （选择B）联邦法院应该放弃审理涉及未解决的州法律问题的案件，以便州法院能够解决该问题。但州法院不必回避审理联邦法律中未解决的问题（如此处所示）。 （选择 D）联邦法院可以根据 (1) 多样性管辖权或 (2) 联邦问题管辖权获得主题管辖权，即当案件根据宪法、条约或联邦法律（如此处所示）产生时。因此，当事人可以\n\n即使他们缺乏公民身份的多样性（即，是同一州的公民），也会在联邦法院进行审理。\n\n教育目标：至上条款禁止州法院歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件。因此，州法院只能在以下情况下拒绝审理联邦法律索赔：(1) 国会明确授予联邦法院专属管辖权，或 (2) 中立的州规则禁止州法院审理案件。\n\n参考文献\n• Claflin v. Houseman, 93 U.S. 130, 136 (1876)（认为州法院与联邦法院对根据联邦法律产生的案件享有共同管辖权）。\n\n• Haywood v. Drown, 556 U.S. 729, 735–36 (2009)（解释说，只有当联邦法院拥有专属管辖权或中立的州规则禁止州法院审理涉及联邦法律的案件时，州法院才能拒绝审理）。\n\n• 上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 法院 § 88 (2019)（描述联邦法院和州法院的并行管辖权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"至上条款禁止州法院歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件。因此，州法院只能在以下情况下拒绝审理联邦法律索赔：(1) 国会明确授予联邦法院专属管辖权，或 (2) 中立的州规则禁止州法院审理案件。","iracZh":{"issue":"州法院应该审理此案吗？","rule":"至上条款禁止州法院歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件。因此，州法院只能在以下情况下拒绝审理联邦法律索赔：(1) 国会明确授予联邦法院专属管辖权，或 (2) 中立的州规则禁止州法院审理案件。","analysis":"并行管辖\n\n联邦法院 州法院索赔的类型\n\n联邦管辖权始终为正当管辖权 除非： 法律\n• 国会授予联邦法院专属管辖权或\n• 中立州规则禁止州法院管辖 州法律 管辖权适当，如果： 管辖权始终适当\n\n• 不同的反对派和\n• 争议金额> 75,000 美元 州法院和联邦法院对根据联邦法律提出的索赔具有共同管辖权（选择A）。但根据至上条款，州法院不能歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件。因此，州法院只能在两种狭隘的情况下拒绝审理此类案件：\n• 当国会明确授予联邦法院对相关事项（例如破产）的专属管辖权时或\n\n• 当中立州规则禁止州法院裁决案件时（例如，规则禁止州法院裁决不涉及州居民或财产的案件）。\n\n在此，原告在州法院起诉被告，仅指控根据联邦法律提出诉讼理由。相关法规规定，根据法规提出的索赔可以向任何有管辖权的法院提出，并且没有迹象表明州规则禁止州法院审理此案。由于州法院不能歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件，因此州法院应该审理此案。 （选择B）联邦法院应该放弃审理涉及未解决的州法律问题的案件，以便州法院能够解决该问题。但州法院不必回避审理联邦法律中未解决的问题（如此处所示）。 （选择 D）联邦法院可以根据 (1) 多样性管辖权或 (2) 联邦问题管辖权获得主题管辖权，即当案件根据宪法、条约或联邦法律（如此处所示）产生时。因此，当事人可以\n\n即使他们缺乏公民身份的多样性（即，是同一州的公民），也会在联邦法院进行审理。\n\n教育目标：至上条款禁止州法院歧视根据联邦法律产生的案件。因此，州法院只能在以下情况下拒绝审理联邦法律索赔：(1) 国会明确授予联邦法院专属管辖权，或 (2) 中立的州规则禁止州法院审理案件。\n\n参考文献\n• Claflin v. Houseman, 93 U.S. 130, 136 (1876)（认为州法院与联邦法院对根据联邦法律产生的案件享有共同管辖权）。\n\n• Haywood v. Drown, 556 U.S. 729, 735–36 (2009)（解释说，只有当联邦法院拥有专属管辖权或中立的州规则禁止州法院审理涉及联邦法律的案件时，州法院才能拒绝审理）。\n\n• 上午 20 点。尤尔。 2d 法院 § 88 (2019)（描述联邦法院和州法院的并行管辖权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为州法院不得歧视根据联邦法律提起的案件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"controversy","ipa":"/kˈɑntɹʌvˌɝsi/","syllables":"con·tro·versy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 论争, 辩论, 论战, 争论；[法] 论战, 争论, 争吵","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 论争, 辩论, 论战, 争论；[法] 论战, 争论, 争吵","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:controversies","sourceSentence":"Concurrent jurisdiction Type of Federal court State court claim Federal Jurisdiction always proper Jurisdiction proper unless: law • Congress granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction or • neutral state rule bars state court jurisdiction State law Jurisdiction proper if: Jurisdiction always proper • diverse opposing parties and • amount in controversy > $75,000 State and federal courts share concurrent jurisdicti","synonyms":["contention","contestation","disputation","disceptation"]},{"term":"bankruptcy","ipa":"/bˈæŋkɹəpsi/","syllables":"bank·ruptcy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 破产者；[经] 破产, 倒闭","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 破产者；[经] 破产, 倒闭","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:bankruptcies","sourceSentence":"As a result, state courts can only refuse to hear such a case in two narrow circumstances: • when Congress expressly granted federal courts exclusive jurisdiction over the subject matter (eg, bankruptcy) or • when a neutral state rule prohibits the state court from adjudicating the case (eg, a rule barring state courts from deciding cases that do not involve state residents or property).","synonyms":["failure"]},{"term":"decided","ipa":"/dˌɪsˈaɪdɪd/","syllables":"de·cided","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 确定的, 坚决的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 确定的, 坚决的","wordForm":"词；原形 decide；词形 0:decide/1:pd/p:decided/d:decided","sourceSentence":"No, because cases arising under federal law must be decided in federal court.","synonyms":["make up one's mind","determine","settle","resolve"]},{"term":"hear","ipa":"/hˈiɹ/","syllables":"hear","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 听到, 倾听, 听说, 审理；vi. 听见, 听","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 听到, 倾听, 听说, 审理；vi. 听见, 听","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:heard/p:heard/i:hearing/3:hears","sourceSentence":"Should the state court hear the case?","synonyms":["learn","get word","get wind","pick up"]},{"term":"parties","ipa":"/pˈɑɹtiz/","syllables":"par·ties","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 当事人；聚会；党派","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 当事人；聚会；党派","wordForm":"词；原形 party；词形 0:party/1:s","sourceSentence":"A plaintiff has sued a defendant in a court of the state in which both parties reside.","synonyms":["political party","company"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":24,"sourceQuestionPage":72,"sourceQuestionPages":[72],"sourceExplanationPages":[73,74],"sourceAnswerPages":[73,74],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-025","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A city in State X is a center for businesses that assemble personal computers. Components for these computers are manufactured elsewhere in State X and in other states, then shipped to the city, where the computers are assembled. A city ordinance imposes a special license tax on all of the many companies engaged in the business of assembling computers in that city. The tax payable by each such company is a percentage of the company's gross receipts earned in the state. A State X statute that authorizes municipalities to impose this license tax has a \"State X content\" provision. To comply with this provision of the state statute, the city license tax ordinance provides that the tax paid by any assembler of computers subject to this tax ordinance will be reduced by a percentage equal to the proportion of computer components manufactured in State X. A company assembles computers in the city and sells them from its offices in the city to buyers throughout the United States. All of the components of its computers come from outside State X. Therefore, the company must pay the city license tax in full without receiving any refund. Other computer assemblers in the city use components manufactured in State X in varying proportions and, therefore, are entitled to partial reductions of their city license tax payments. Following prescribed procedure, the company brings an action in a proper court asking to have the city's special license tax declared unconstitutional on the ground that it is inconsistent with the negative implications of the commerce clause. In this case, should the court rule in favor of the company?","choices":{"A":"No, because the commerce clause does not interfere with the right of a state to foster and support businesses located within its borders by encouraging its residents to purchase the products of those businesses.","B":"No, because the tax falls only on companies resident in State X and, therefore, does not discriminate against or otherwise adversely affect interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because any tax on a company engaged in interstate commerce, measured in whole or in part by its gross receipts earned in the state, is a per se violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause.","D":"Yes, because the tax improperly discriminates against interstate commerce by treating in-state products more favorably than out-of-state products."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The commerce clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce, which encompasses nearly all activities that affect two or more states. As a result, the negative implication of this clause—ie, the dormant commerce clause—prohibits states and municipalities from unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state or municipal tax on interstate commerce is an undue burden unless the tax:\n• is applied to persons or activities with a substantial nexus to the taxing state or municipality\n• is fairly apportioned to avoid taxing interstate activities performed in other states\n• does not discriminate by favoring local over out-of-state entities and\n• is fairly related to services and benefits provided by the taxing state or municipality. Here, the city's license tax applies to all companies that assemble computers in the city (substantial nexus) at a percentage of their gross receipts (fairly apportioned). The tax is likely reasonable compared to the services provided by the city (fairly related). But it discriminates against interstate commerce by allowing companies to reduce their taxes by using computer components manufactured in the state, thereby treating in-state products more favorably than out-of-state products (Choice B). Therefore, the court should rule in favor of the company. (Choice A) States and municipalities can encourage their residents to purchase products from businesses located within their borders. But these entities cannot violate the dormant commerce clause when doing so. (Choice C) A tax on a company engaged in interstate commerce, measured in whole or in part by its gross receipts, is not a per se (ie, automatic) violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause. Instead, such a tax complies with the \"fairly apportioned\" requirement imposed by this clause.\n\nEducational objective: A state or municipal tax on interstate commerce is valid if it is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the taxing entity, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to the services provided by the taxing entity.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state or municipal tax violates the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 168 (2019) (explaining that a state tax favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests is invalid).","educationalObjective":"A state or municipal tax on interstate commerce is valid if it is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the taxing entity, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to the services provided by the taxing entity.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state or municipal tax violates the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 168 (2019) (explaining that a state tax favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests is invalid).","rule":"A state or municipal tax on interstate commerce is valid if it is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the taxing entity, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to the services provided by the taxing entity.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, should the court rule in favor of the company?","rule":"A state or municipal tax on interstate commerce is valid if it is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the taxing entity, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to the services provided by the taxing entity.","analysis":"The commerce clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce, which encompasses nearly all activities that affect two or more states. As a result, the negative implication of this clause—ie, the dormant commerce clause—prohibits states and municipalities from unduly burdening interstate commerce. A state or municipal tax on interstate commerce is an undue burden unless the tax:\n• is applied to persons or activities with a substantial nexus to the taxing state or municipality\n• is fairly apportioned to avoid taxing interstate activities performed in other states\n• does not discriminate by favoring local over out-of-state entities and\n• is fairly related to services and benefits provided by the taxing state or municipality. Here, the city's license tax applies to all companies that assemble computers in the city (substantial nexus) at a percentage of their gross receipts (fairly apportioned). The tax is likely reasonable compared to the services provided by the city (fairly related). But it discriminates against interstate commerce by allowing companies to reduce their taxes by using computer components manufactured in the state, thereby treating in-state products more favorably than out-of-state products (Choice B). Therefore, the court should rule in favor of the company. (Choice A) States and municipalities can encourage their residents to purchase products from businesses located within their borders. But these entities cannot violate the dormant commerce clause when doing so. (Choice C) A tax on a company engaged in interstate commerce, measured in whole or in part by its gross receipts, is not a per se (ie, automatic) violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause. Instead, such a tax complies with the \"fairly apportioned\" requirement imposed by this clause.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the tax improperly discriminates against interstate commerce by treating in-state products more favorably than out-of-state products."},"questionZh":"X 州的一座城市是组装个人电脑的企业中心。这些计算机的组件在 X 州和其他州的其他地方制造，然后运到城市，在那里组装计算机。一项城市法令对该市从事计算机组装业务的所有公司征收特别许可证税。每个此类公司的应缴税款是该公司在该州赚取的总收入的百分比。授权各市征收许可证税的 X 州法规有“X 州内容”条款。为了遵守该州法规的这一规定，城市许可证税条例规定，任何受该税收条例管辖的计算机组装商所缴纳的税款将减少相当于在 X 州制造的计算机组件比例的百分比。一家公司在该市组装计算机，然后从其在该市的办事处将其出售给美国各地的买家。其计算机的所有组件均来自 X 州以外。因此，该公司必须全额缴纳城市许可证税，且不会收到任何退款。该市的其他计算机组装商以不同比例使用 X 州制造的组件，因此有权部分减免其城市许可证税。按照规定的程序，该公司向适当的法院提起诉讼，要求宣布该市的特别许可税违宪，理由是它不符合商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。在这种情况下，法院是否应该做出有利于公司的判决呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）不会妨碍国家通过鼓励居民购买企业产品来培育和支持位于其境内的企业的权利。","B":"不会，因为该税项仅适用于 X 州居民公司，因此不会歧视或以其他方式对州际贸易产生不利影响。","C":"是的，因为对从事州际贸易的公司征收的任何税款（全部或部分以其在州内赚取的总收入来衡量）本身就违反了商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。","D":"是的，因为该税收通过给予州内产品比州外产品更优惠的待遇，对州际贸易产生了不当歧视。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业，其中几乎涵盖了影响两个或多个州的所有活动。因此，该条款的负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））禁止州和市政府对州际商业造成过度负担。对州际商业征收的州或市税属于不当负担，除非该税：\n• 适用于与征税州或市有重大联系的个人或活动\n• 公平分配以避免对在其他州进行的州际活动征税\n• 不会因偏袒本地实体而不是州外实体而进行歧视，并且\n• 与征税州或市提供的服务和福利公平相关。在这里，该市的许可证税适用于在该市组装计算机（实质性联系）的所有公司，按其总收入的一定比例（公平分配）。与城市提供的服务相比，税收可能是合理的（公平相关）。但它歧视州际贸易，允许公司通过使用州内制造的计算机组件来减少税收，从而比州外产品更优惠地对待州内产品（选项B）。因此，法院应作出有利于公司的判决。 （选择 A）州和市政府可以鼓励其居民从位于其境内的企业购买产品。但这些实体这样做时不能违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。 （选项C）对从事州际贸易的公司征税，全部或部分按照其总收入来衡量，本身（即自动）并不违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。相反，这种税收符合本条款规定的“公平分配”要求。\n\n教育目标：州或市对州际商业征收的税在以下情况下是有效的：(1) 对与征税实体有重大联系的个人或活动征收，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与征税实体提供的服务公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州或市税是否违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 168 (2019)（解释说有利于州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益的州税是无效的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对州际商业征收的州或市税是有效的，如果它满足以下条件：(1) 对与征税实体有重大联系的个人或活动征收，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与征税实体提供的服务公平相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种情况下，法院是否应该做出有利于公司的判决呢？","rule":"对州际商业征收的州或市税是有效的，如果它满足以下条件：(1) 对与征税实体有重大联系的个人或活动征收，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与征税实体提供的服务公平相关。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业，其中几乎涵盖了影响两个或多个州的所有活动。因此，该条款的负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））禁止州和市政府对州际商业造成过度负担。对州际商业征收的州或市税属于不当负担，除非该税：\n• 适用于与征税州或市有重大联系的个人或活动\n• 公平分配以避免对在其他州进行的州际活动征税\n• 不会因偏袒本地实体而不是州外实体而进行歧视，并且\n• 与征税州或市提供的服务和福利公平相关。在这里，该市的许可证税适用于在该市组装计算机（实质性联系）的所有公司，按其总收入的一定比例（公平分配）。与城市提供的服务相比，税收可能是合理的（公平相关）。但它歧视州际贸易，允许公司通过使用州内制造的计算机组件来减少税收，从而比州外产品更优惠地对待州内产品（选项B）。因此，法院应作出有利于公司的判决。 （选择 A）州和市政府可以鼓励其居民从位于其境内的企业购买产品。但这些实体这样做时不能违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。 （选项C）对从事州际贸易的公司征税，全部或部分按照其总收入来衡量，本身（即自动）并不违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。相反，这种税收符合本条款规定的“公平分配”要求。\n\n教育目标：州或市对州际商业征收的税在以下情况下是有效的：(1) 对与征税实体有重大联系的个人或活动征收，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与征税实体提供的服务公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州或市税是否违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 168 (2019)（解释说有利于州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益的州税是无效的）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该税收通过给予州内产品比州外产品更优惠的待遇，对州际贸易产生了不当歧视。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"municipality","ipa":"/mjˌunɪsʌpˈæləti/","syllables":"mu·nic·i·pal·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 自治区, 市当局, 市民；[法] 市, 自治市, 自治地区","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 自治区, 市当局, 市民；[法] 市, 自治市, 自治地区","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:municipalities","sourceSentence":"A state or municipal tax on interstate commerce is an undue burden unless the tax: • is applied to persons or activities with a substantial nexus to the taxing state or municipality • is fairly apportioned to avoid taxing interstate activities performed in other states • does not discriminate by favoring local over out-of-state entities and • is fairly related to services and benefits provided by the taxing state or ","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"procedure","ipa":"/pɹʌsˈidʒɝ/","syllables":"pro·ce·dure","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 程序, 过程, 手续；[计] 规程； 过程","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 程序, 过程, 手续；[计] 规程； 过程","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:procedures","sourceSentence":"Following prescribed procedure, the company brings an action in a proper court asking to have the city's special license tax declared unconstitutional on the ground that it is inconsistent with the negative implications of the commerce clause.","synonyms":["operation","process","routine","subroutine"]},{"term":"municipal","ipa":"/mjunˈɪsəpəl/","syllables":"mu·nic·i·pal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 市政的, 自治区的, 内政的；[经] 市政的, 市的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 市政的, 自治区的, 内政的；[经] 市政的, 市的","wordForm":"形容词；词形 s:municipals","sourceSentence":"A State X statute that authorizes municipalities to impose this license tax has a \"State X content\" provision.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-bce916e23e3a59a4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":25,"sourceQuestionPage":75,"sourceQuestionPages":[75],"sourceExplanationPages":[76,77],"sourceAnswerPages":[76,77],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-026","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A fatal virus recently infected poultry in several nations. Some scientific evidence indicates that the virus can be transmitted from poultry to humans. Poultry farming is a major industry in several U.S. states. In one such state, the legislature has enacted a law imposing a fee of two cents per bird on all poultry farming and processing operations in the state. The purpose of the fee is to pay for a state inspection system to ensure that no poultry raised or processed in the state is infected with the virus. A company that has poultry processing plants both in the state and in other states has sued to challenge the fee. Is the fee constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because although it attaches only to intrastate activity, in the aggregate, the fee substantially affects interstate commerce.","B":"No, because it places an undue burden on interstate commerce in violation of the negative implications of the commerce clause.","C":"Yes, because it applies only to activities that take place wholly within the state, and it does not unduly burden interstate commerce.","D":"Yes, because it was enacted pursuant to the state's police power, which takes precedence over the negative implications of the commerce clause."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. This clause also carries a negative implication (ie, the dormant commerce clause) that prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. But a state tax that affects interstate commerce is not unduly burdensome if the tax is:\n• applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the state\n• fairly apportioned to avoid taxing activities performed in other states\n• not discriminatory against interstate commerce and\n• fairly related to services or benefits provided by the state. Here, the state law imposes a fee (ie, tax) on all poultry farming and processing operations in the state. Although the fee only attaches to intrastate activity, in the aggregate, the fee likely has a substantial effect on interstate commerce (Choice A). But it does not unduly burden interstate commerce—and is therefore constitutional—because:\n• the entities taxed have poultry farming and processing operations in the state (substantial nexus)\n\n• the fee only applies to birds raised or processed in the state (fairly apportioned)\n\n• the fee applies to all poultry farming and processing operations in the state (nondiscriminatory) and\n\n• the fee appears reasonable compared to the poultry inspection system that the state will provide (fairly related) (Choice B).\n\n(Choice D) The Tenth Amendment gives states all powers that are not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution—eg, police power to impose taxes on state activities. But pursuant to the supremacy clause, state police powers do not take precedence over other constitutional provisions, including the negative implications of the commerce clause.\n\nEducational objective: A state tax that affects interstate commerce does not violate the dormant commerce clause if it is (1) applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to services or benefits provided by the state.\n\nReferences\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test for determining whether a state tax violates the commerce clause).","educationalObjective":"A state tax that affects interstate commerce does not violate the dormant commerce clause if it is (1) applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to services or benefits provided by the state.","references":"• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test for determining whether a state tax violates the commerce clause).","rule":"A state tax that affects interstate commerce does not violate the dormant commerce clause if it is (1) applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to services or benefits provided by the state.","irac":{"issue":"Is the fee constitutional?","rule":"A state tax that affects interstate commerce does not violate the dormant commerce clause if it is (1) applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to services or benefits provided by the state.","analysis":"The commerce clause gives Congress the power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. This clause also carries a negative implication (ie, the dormant commerce clause) that prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. But a state tax that affects interstate commerce is not unduly burdensome if the tax is:\n• applied to a person or activity that has a substantial nexus with the state\n• fairly apportioned to avoid taxing activities performed in other states\n• not discriminatory against interstate commerce and\n• fairly related to services or benefits provided by the state. Here, the state law imposes a fee (ie, tax) on all poultry farming and processing operations in the state. Although the fee only attaches to intrastate activity, in the aggregate, the fee likely has a substantial effect on interstate commerce (Choice A). But it does not unduly burden interstate commerce—and is therefore constitutional—because:\n• the entities taxed have poultry farming and processing operations in the state (substantial nexus)\n\n• the fee only applies to birds raised or processed in the state (fairly apportioned)\n\n• the fee applies to all poultry farming and processing operations in the state (nondiscriminatory) and\n\n• the fee appears reasonable compared to the poultry inspection system that the state will provide (fairly related) (Choice B).\n\n(Choice D) The Tenth Amendment gives states all powers that are not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution—eg, police power to impose taxes on state activities. But pursuant to the supremacy clause, state police powers do not take precedence over other constitutional provisions, including the negative implications of the commerce clause.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it applies only to activities that take place wholly within the state, and it does not unduly burden interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"最近，一种致命病毒感染了多个国家的家禽。一些科学证据表明该病毒可以从家禽传播给人类。家禽养殖是美国几个州的主要产业。在其中一个州，立法机关颁布了一项法律，对该州所有家禽养殖和加工业务征收每只鸡两美分的费用。这笔费用的目的是支付州检查系统的费用，以确保在该州饲养或加工的家禽没有感染该病毒。一家在该州和其他州都设有家禽加工厂的公司已提起诉讼，对这笔费用提出质疑。收费是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为虽然它只适用于州内活动，但总的来说，该费用极大地影响了州际贸易。","B":"不可以，因为它给州际贸易带来了过度的负担，违反了商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。","C":"是的，因为它仅适用于完全在州内进行的活动，并且不会过度加重州际贸易的负担。","D":"是的，因为它是根据州警察权力制定的，该权力优先于商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的权力，即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。该条款还具有负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）），禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。但是，影响州际贸易的州税如果满足以下条件，则不会造成过度负担：\n• 适用于与国家有密切联系的个人或活动\n• 公平分配以避免对在其他州进行的活动征税\n• 不歧视州际贸易，并且\n• 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。在这里，州法律对该州所有家禽养殖和加工业务征收费用（即税）。尽管该费用仅适用于州内活动，但总体而言，该费用可能对州际贸易产生重大影响（选择 A）。但它不会对州际贸易造成过度负担，因此符合宪法，因为：\n• 纳税实体在该州拥有家禽养殖和加工业务（实质性联系）\n\n• 该费用仅适用于在该州饲养或加工的鸟类（公平分配）\n\n• 该费用适用于该州所有家禽养殖和加工业务（非歧视性）并且\n\n• 与国家提供的家禽检验系统相比，该费用显得合理（公平相关）（选择B）。\n\n（选项D） 第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力，例如警察对州活动征税的权力。但根据至上条款，州警察权力并不优先于其他宪法条款，包括商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。\n\n教育目标：影响州际商业的州税不违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），如果它 (1) 适用于与州有实质性联系的个人或活动，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（提出了确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"影响州际商业的州税不违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），如果它 (1) 适用于与州有重大联系的个人或活动，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"收费是否符合宪法？","rule":"影响州际商业的州税不违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），如果它 (1) 适用于与州有重大联系的个人或活动，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的权力，即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。该条款还具有负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）），禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。但是，影响州际贸易的州税如果满足以下条件，则不会造成过度负担：\n• 适用于与国家有密切联系的个人或活动\n• 公平分配以避免对在其他州进行的活动征税\n• 不歧视州际贸易，并且\n• 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。在这里，州法律对该州所有家禽养殖和加工业务征收费用（即税）。尽管该费用仅适用于州内活动，但总体而言，该费用可能对州际贸易产生重大影响（选择 A）。但它不会对州际贸易造成过度负担，因此符合宪法，因为：\n• 纳税实体在该州拥有家禽养殖和加工业务（实质性联系）\n\n• 该费用仅适用于在该州饲养或加工的鸟类（公平分配）\n\n• 该费用适用于该州所有家禽养殖和加工业务（非歧视性）并且\n\n• 与国家提供的家禽检验系统相比，该费用显得合理（公平相关）（选择B）。\n\n（选项D） 第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力，例如警察对州活动征税的权力。但根据至上条款，州警察权力并不优先于其他宪法条款，包括商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。\n\n教育目标：影响州际商业的州税不违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），如果它 (1) 适用于与州有实质性联系的个人或活动，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（提出了确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为它仅适用于完全在州内进行的活动，并且不会过度加重州际贸易的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-bce916e23e3a59a4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":26,"sourceQuestionPage":78,"sourceQuestionPages":[78],"sourceExplanationPages":[79,80],"sourceAnswerPages":[79,80],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-027","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Article IV & Interstate Relations","question":"State A sent three of its employees to a city located in State B to consult with a chemical laboratory there about matters of state business. While in the course of their employment, the three State A employees negligently released into local State B waterways some of the chemical samples they had received from the laboratory in State B. Persons in State B injured by the release of the chemicals sued the three State A employees and State A in a State B court for the damages they suffered. After a trial in which all of the defendants admitted jurisdiction of the State B court and fully participated, the plaintiffs received a judgment against all of the defendants for $5 million, which became final. Subsequently, the plaintiffs sought to enforce their State B court judgment by commencing a proper proceeding in an appropriate State A court. In that enforcement proceeding, State A argued, as it had done unsuccessfully in the earlier action in the State B court, that its liability is limited by a State A law to $100,000 in any tort case. Because the three individual employees of State A are able to pay only $50,000 of the judgment, the only way the plaintiffs can fully satisfy their State B court judgment is from the funds of State A. Can the plaintiffs recover the full balance of their State B court judgment from State A in the enforcement proceeding they filed in the State A court?","choices":{"A":"No, because the State A employees were negligent and, therefore, their actions were not authorized by State A.","B":"No, because the Tenth Amendment preserves the right of a state to have its courts enforce the state's public policy limiting its tort liability.","C":"Yes, because a limitation on damage awards against State A for tortious actions of its agents would violate the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"Yes, because the final judgment of the State B court is entitled to full faith and credit in the State A courts."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The Article IV full faith and credit clause requires states to respect the public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of other states. Under this clause, a state court must recognize and enforce judicial decisions rendered by a court in another state when:\n• that court had personal jurisdiction and subject-matter jurisdiction\n• the case was decided on its merits—ie, the substantive elements or grounds of a claim or defense—and\n• a final judgment was entered. Here, the State B residents (plaintiffs) sued the State A employees and State A (defendants) in a State B court. The defendants admitted that the court had jurisdiction. The case was then decided on its merits when the court issued a $5 million judgment against the defendants. And since the State B judgment became final, it is entitled to full faith and credit in the State A courts. Therefore, the plaintiffs can recover the full balance of that judgment from State A in the enforcement proceeding they filed in the State A court. (Choice A) Since the employees acted in the course of their employment, their actions were authorized by State A—even if the employees were negligent. And State A is vicariously liable for that negligence under the doctrine of respondeat superior. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment gives states any power that is not expressly given to the federal government by the Constitution—eg, the power of state courts to enforce the state's public policy limiting its tort liability. But when exercising such powers, states must comply with other constitutional provisions—eg, the full faith and credit clause. (Choice C) A limitation on damage awards against State A likely would not violate the equal protection clause because it would not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class.\n\nEducational objective: Under the full faith and credit clause, a state court must recognize and enforce a judicial decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.\n\nReferences\n• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998) (explaining when the full faith and credit clause applies to state court judgments).\n\n• V.L. v. E.L., 136 S. Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016) (holding that a state court cannot relitigate a case that has already been decided on its merits in a court of another state).","educationalObjective":"Under the full faith and credit clause, a state court must recognize and enforce a judicial decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.","references":"• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998) (explaining when the full faith and credit clause applies to state court judgments).\n\n• V.L. v. E.L., 136 S. Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016) (holding that a state court cannot relitigate a case that has already been decided on its merits in a court of another state).","rule":"Under the full faith and credit clause, a state court must recognize and enforce a judicial decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.","irac":{"issue":"Can the plaintiffs recover the full balance of their State B court judgment from State A in the enforcement proceeding they filed in the State A court?","rule":"Under the full faith and credit clause, a state court must recognize and enforce a judicial decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.","analysis":"The Article IV full faith and credit clause requires states to respect the public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of other states. Under this clause, a state court must recognize and enforce judicial decisions rendered by a court in another state when:\n• that court had personal jurisdiction and subject-matter jurisdiction\n• the case was decided on its merits—ie, the substantive elements or grounds of a claim or defense—and\n• a final judgment was entered. Here, the State B residents (plaintiffs) sued the State A employees and State A (defendants) in a State B court. The defendants admitted that the court had jurisdiction. The case was then decided on its merits when the court issued a $5 million judgment against the defendants. And since the State B judgment became final, it is entitled to full faith and credit in the State A courts. Therefore, the plaintiffs can recover the full balance of that judgment from State A in the enforcement proceeding they filed in the State A court. (Choice A) Since the employees acted in the course of their employment, their actions were authorized by State A—even if the employees were negligent. And State A is vicariously liable for that negligence under the doctrine of respondeat superior. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment gives states any power that is not expressly given to the federal government by the Constitution—eg, the power of state courts to enforce the state's public policy limiting its tort liability. But when exercising such powers, states must comply with other constitutional provisions—eg, the full faith and credit clause. (Choice C) A limitation on damage awards against State A likely would not violate the equal protection clause because it would not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the final judgment of the State B court is entitled to full faith and credit in the State A courts."},"questionZh":"A 国派出三名雇员前往 B 国的一座城市，向那里的化学实验室咨询国家业务事宜。在受雇期间，三名 A 国雇员疏忽地将他们从 B 国实验室收到的一些化学样品排放到当地 B 国水道中。因化学品泄漏而受伤的 B 国人员在 B 国法院起诉三名 A 国雇员和 A 国，要求赔偿他们所遭受的损失。经过所有被告均承认 B 州法院管辖权并充分参与的审判后，原告收到了针对所有被告赔偿 500 万美元的终审判决。随后，原告寻求通过在适当的 A 州法院启动适当的诉讼程序来执行 B 州法院的判决。在该执行程序中，A 州辩称，正如其在 B 州法院之前的诉讼中未成功所做的那样，A 州法律将其在任何侵权案件中的赔偿责任限制为 100,000 美元。由于 A 州的三名雇员只能支付 50,000 美元的判决金额，原告完全履行 B 州法院判决的唯一途径是使用 A 州的资金。原告在向 A 州法院提起的执行程序中能否从 A 州收回 B 州法院判决的全部余额？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为 A 国雇员存在疏忽，因此他们的行为未经 A 国授权。","B":"不，因为第十修正案保留了州让其法院执行限制其侵权责任的公共政策的权利。","C":"是的，因为限制 A 国因其代理人的侵权行为而获得的损害赔偿将违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。","D":"是的，因为B国法院的最终判决有权得到A国法院的充分信任和信任。"},"explanationZh":"第四条完全诚信条款要求各国尊重其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。根据本条款，在以下情况下，州法院必须承认并执行另一州法院做出的司法判决：\n• 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权\n• 案件是根据其案情（即索赔或抗辩的实质性要素或理由）做出决定的，并且\n• 作出最终判决。在此，B 州居民（原告）在 B 州法院起诉 A 州雇员和 A 州（被告）。被告承认法院有管辖权。随后，法院根据案件的是非曲直对被告做出了 500 万美元的判决。由于B国的判决成为最终判决，它有权获得A国法院的充分信任和信任。因此，原告可以在向 A 州法院提起的执行程序中追回 A 州判决的全部余额。 （选项A）由于雇员是在其受雇过程中采取行动的，因此他们的行为得到了A国的授权——即使雇员有疏忽。根据上级回应原则，A 国对该过失负有替代责任。 （选项B） 第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确赋予联邦政府的任何权力，例如州法院执行限制其侵权责任的州公共政策的权力。但在行使此类权力时，各州必须遵守其他宪法条款，例如完整的信用条款。 （选项 C）对 A 国损害赔偿的限制可能不会违反平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响。\n\n教育目标：根据完整的诚信条款，在以下情况下，州法院必须承认并执行另一州法院的司法裁决：(1) 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。\n\n参考文献\n• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998)（解释了完整的信用条款何时适用于州法院的判决）。\n\n• V.L. v.E.L.，136 S.Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016)（认为州法院不能对已经在另一州法院就案情作出裁决的案件重新提起诉讼）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据完整的诚信条款，在以下情况下，州法院必须承认并执行另一州法院的司法裁决：(1) 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。","iracZh":{"issue":"原告在向 A 州法院提起的执行程序中能否从 A 州收回 B 州法院判决的全部余额？","rule":"根据完整的诚信条款，在以下情况下，州法院必须承认并执行另一州法院的司法裁决：(1) 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。","analysis":"第四条完全诚信条款要求各国尊重其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。根据本条款，在以下情况下，州法院必须承认并执行另一州法院做出的司法判决：\n• 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权\n• 案件是根据其案情（即索赔或抗辩的实质性要素或理由）做出决定的，并且\n• 作出最终判决。在此，B 州居民（原告）在 B 州法院起诉 A 州雇员和 A 州（被告）。被告承认法院有管辖权。随后，法院根据案件的是非曲直对被告做出了 500 万美元的判决。由于B国的判决成为最终判决，它有权获得A国法院的充分信任和信任。因此，原告可以在向 A 州法院提起的执行程序中追回 A 州判决的全部余额。 （选项A）由于雇员是在其受雇过程中采取行动的，因此他们的行为得到了A国的授权——即使雇员有疏忽。根据上级回应原则，A 国对该过失负有替代责任。 （选项B） 第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确赋予联邦政府的任何权力，例如州法院执行限制其侵权责任的州公共政策的权力。但在行使此类权力时，各州必须遵守其他宪法条款，例如完整的信用条款。 （选项 C）对 A 国损害赔偿的限制可能不会违反平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响。\n\n教育目标：根据完整的诚信条款，在以下情况下，州法院必须承认并执行另一州法院的司法裁决：(1) 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。\n\n参考文献\n• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998)（解释了完整的信用条款何时适用于州法院的判决）。\n\n• V.L. v.E.L.，136 S.Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016)（认为州法院不能对已经在另一州法院就案情作出裁决的案件重新提起诉讼）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为B国法院的最终判决有权得到A国法院的充分信任和信任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"limitation","ipa":"/lˌɪmɪtˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"lim·i·ta·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 限制, 缺陷, 限额；[医] 限界, 限制, 限度","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 限制, 缺陷, 限额；[医] 限界, 限制, 限度","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:limitations","sourceSentence":"Yes, because a limitation on damage awards against State A for tortious actions of its agents would violate the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","synonyms":["restriction","limit"]},{"term":"release","ipa":"/ɹilˈis/","syllables":"re·lease","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 释放, 发泄, 豁免, 发行, 释放证书；vt. 释放, 解除, 放松, 豁免, 免除, 发布, 放弃, 让与；n. 发布","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 释放, 发泄, 豁免, 发行, 释放证书；vt. 释放, 解除, 放松, 豁免, 免除, 发布, 放弃, 让与；n. 发布","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:released/i:releasing/p:released/s:releases/3:releases","sourceSentence":"While in the course of their employment, the three State A employees negligently released into local State B waterways some of the chemical samples they had received from the laboratory in State B.","synonyms":["liberation","freeing","tone ending","waiver"]},{"term":"tortious","ipa":"/'tɒ:ʃəs/","syllables":"tor·tious","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. 侵权行为的；[法] 不法占有的, 民事上侵害的, 侵犯行为的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 侵权行为的；[法] 不法占有的, 民事上侵害的, 侵犯行为的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Yes, because a limitation on damage awards against State A for tortious actions of its agents would violate the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"injured","ipa":"/ˈɪndʒɝd/","syllables":"in·jured","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 受伤的, 受损害的, 被触怒的；[法] 受害的, 被害的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 受伤的, 受损害的, 被触怒的；[法] 受害的, 被害的","wordForm":"形容词；原形 injure；词形 0:injure/1:dp/p:injured/d:injured","sourceSentence":"Persons in State B injured by the release of the chemicals sued the three State A employees and State A in a State B court for the damages they suffered.","synonyms":["wound","hurt","bruise","offend"]},{"term":"awards","ipa":"/ʌwˈɔɹdz/","syllables":"awards","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 奖项；判决；奖赏（award的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 奖项；判决；奖赏（award的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 award；词形 0:award/1:s3","sourceSentence":"Yes, because a limitation on damage awards against State A for tortious actions of its agents would violate the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","synonyms":["awarding","accolade","honor","honour"]},{"term":"liable","ipa":"/lˈaɪəbəl/","syllables":"li·able","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 有义务的, 应负责的, 有...倾向的；[法] 有责任的, 可受处理的, 应受罚的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 有义务的, 应负责的, 有...倾向的；[法] 有责任的, 可受处理的, 应受罚的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"And State A is vicariously liable for that negligence under the doctrine of respondeat superior.","synonyms":["apt","nonimmune","nonresistant","unresistant"]},{"term":"tort","ipa":"/tˈɔɹt/","syllables":"tort","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 民事侵权行为；[经] 侵犯行为(不包括违约), 非法行为","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 民事侵权行为；[经] 侵犯行为(不包括违约), 非法行为","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:torts/3:torts","sourceSentence":"In that enforcement proceeding, State A argued, as it had done unsuccessfully in the earlier action in the State B court, that its liability is limited by a State A law to $100,000 in any tort case.","synonyms":["civil wrong"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-bcdfd17850c6c661"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":27,"sourceQuestionPage":81,"sourceQuestionPages":[81],"sourceExplanationPages":[82,83],"sourceAnswerPages":[82,83],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-028","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A state statute provides that only corporations whose owners have been residents of the state for at least two years may obtain a retail license to sell liquor in the state. The stated purpose of the statute is to promote responsible alcohol sales and consumption through the local control of retail liquor stores. A corporation's application for a liquor store license was denied because its owners had been state residents for less than two years. The corporation has sued to challenge the statute. Is the state statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress has exclusive authority over interstate commerce.","B":"No, because it unduly burdens interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because the issuance of licenses is a traditional government function.","D":"Yes, because the Twenty-first Amendment grants states authority to regulate alcohol."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Unconstitutional state alcohol regulations\n\nConstitutional Example provision\n\nDormant commerce Permitting only in-state businesses to ship alcohol to in-state clause consumers\n\nFree speech clause Banning advertisements featuring price of alcohol\n\nEstablishment clause Granting churches power to veto liquor license applications for businesses within 500-foot radius of church\n\nEqual protection Permitting alcohol sales to females at age 18 but not to males until clause age 21\n\nThe Twenty-first Amendment grants states broad authority to regulate the use of alcohol within their own borders.* However, this authority is restricted by other constitutional provisions, including the dormant commerce clause (Choice D). The dormant commerce clause prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. A discriminatory state statute favors in-state over out-of-state interests. Such a statute is subject to strict scrutiny and is unconstitutional unless the state proves that:\n• the statute furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest (eg, public health or safety) and\n• no reasonable alternative exists to further that interest. Here, the state statute discriminates against interstate commerce by granting licenses to sell liquor only to corporations whose owners have been state residents for at least two years. Even if the statute furthers the state's legitimate, noneconomic interest in promoting responsible alcohol consumption, reasonable alternatives exist. For example, the state could limit the amount of alcohol that may be sold to individuals at one time. Therefore, the statute unduly burdens interstate commerce and is unconstitutional. *The Twenty-first Amendment also repealed the Eighteenth Amendment's prohibition on the manufacture, transportation, and sale of alcohol in the United States. (Choice A) Congress does have exclusive authority over interstate commerce. But if Congress has not enacted legislation in a particular area of interstate commerce, then the states are free to regulate that area so long as it does not discriminate against or unduly burden interstate commerce or regulate extraterritorial (ie, wholly out-of-state) activity. (Choice C) The \"traditional government function\" exception allows states to favor government (not private) entities when those entities are performing a traditional\n\ngovernment function (eg, waste disposal). Here, issuing licenses may be a traditional government function. But since the state is favoring in-state residents (as opposed to government entities), this exception does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: The Twenty-first Amendment grants states broad authority to regulate alcohol within their borders. When doing so, states cannot violate the dormant commerce clause by discriminating against interstate commerce unless (1) the state statute furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists to do so.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XXI (state's power to regulate alcohol).\n\n• Tenn. Wine & Spirits Retailers Ass'n v. Thomas, 139 S. Ct. 2449, 2476 (2019) (holding that a state statute requiring liquor store operators to be state residents for at least two years violates the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d § 109 (2020) (explaining that the dormant commerce clause applies to state alcohol regulations).","educationalObjective":"The Twenty-first Amendment grants states broad authority to regulate alcohol within their borders. When doing so, states cannot violate the dormant commerce clause by discriminating against interstate commerce unless (1) the state statute furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists to do so.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XXI (state's power to regulate alcohol).\n\n• Tenn. Wine & Spirits Retailers Ass'n v. Thomas, 139 S. Ct. 2449, 2476 (2019) (holding that a state statute requiring liquor store operators to be state residents for at least two years violates the dormant commerce clause).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d § 109 (2020) (explaining that the dormant commerce clause applies to state alcohol regulations).","rule":"The Twenty-first Amendment grants states broad authority to regulate alcohol within their borders. When doing so, states cannot violate the dormant commerce clause by discriminating against interstate commerce unless (1) the state statute furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists to do so.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state statute constitutional?","rule":"The Twenty-first Amendment grants states broad authority to regulate alcohol within their borders. When doing so, states cannot violate the dormant commerce clause by discriminating against interstate commerce unless (1) the state statute furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists to do so.","analysis":"Unconstitutional state alcohol regulations\n\nConstitutional Example provision\n\nDormant commerce Permitting only in-state businesses to ship alcohol to in-state clause consumers\n\nFree speech clause Banning advertisements featuring price of alcohol\n\nEstablishment clause Granting churches power to veto liquor license applications for businesses within 500-foot radius of church\n\nEqual protection Permitting alcohol sales to females at age 18 but not to males until clause age 21\n\nThe Twenty-first Amendment grants states broad authority to regulate the use of alcohol within their own borders.* However, this authority is restricted by other constitutional provisions, including the dormant commerce clause (Choice D). The dormant commerce clause prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. A discriminatory state statute favors in-state over out-of-state interests. Such a statute is subject to strict scrutiny and is unconstitutional unless the state proves that:\n• the statute furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest (eg, public health or safety) and\n• no reasonable alternative exists to further that interest. Here, the state statute discriminates against interstate commerce by granting licenses to sell liquor only to corporations whose owners have been state residents for at least two years. Even if the statute furthers the state's legitimate, noneconomic interest in promoting responsible alcohol consumption, reasonable alternatives exist. For example, the state could limit the amount of alcohol that may be sold to individuals at one time. Therefore, the statute unduly burdens interstate commerce and is unconstitutional. *The Twenty-first Amendment also repealed the Eighteenth Amendment's prohibition on the manufacture, transportation, and sale of alcohol in the United States. (Choice A) Congress does have exclusive authority over interstate commerce. But if Congress has not enacted legislation in a particular area of interstate commerce, then the states are free to regulate that area so long as it does not discriminate against or unduly burden interstate commerce or regulate extraterritorial (ie, wholly out-of-state) activity. (Choice C) The \"traditional government function\" exception allows states to favor government (not private) entities when those entities are performing a traditional\n\ngovernment function (eg, waste disposal). Here, issuing licenses may be a traditional government function. But since the state is favoring in-state residents (as opposed to government entities), this exception does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because it unduly burdens interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"州法规规定，只有所有者是该州居民至少两年的公司才能获得在该州销售酒类的零售许可证。该法规的既定目的是通过对零售酒类商店的地方控制来促进负责任的酒类销售和消费。一家公司申请酒类商店执照被拒绝，因为其所有者成为该州居民还不到两年。该公司已提起诉讼，质疑该法规。国家法律是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会对州际贸易拥有专属权力。","B":"不，因为它过度加重了州际贸易的负担。","C":"是的，因为颁发许可证是传统的政府职能。","D":"是的，因为第二十一条修正案赋予各州监管酒精的权力。"},"explanationZh":"违反宪法的州酒精法规\n\n宪法范例条款\n\n休眠商业仅允许州内企业向州内条款消费者运送酒精\n\n言论自由条款 禁止以酒类价格为主题的广告\n\n设立条款 赋予教会否决教堂半径 500 英尺范围内企业酒类许可证申请的权力\n\n平等保护 允许向 18 岁女性销售酒精饮料，但在 21 岁之前不允许向男性销售酒精饮料\n\n第二十一条修正案赋予各州广泛的权力来监管其境内的酒精使用。*但是，这一权力受到其他宪法条款的限制，包括消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）（选项 D）。消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。歧视性的州法规有利于州内利益而不是州外利益。此类法规受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且是违宪的，除非国家证明：\n• 该法规促进合法的、非经济的国家利益（例如公共卫生或安全）并且\n• 不存在任何合理的替代方案来促进这种兴趣。在这里，州法规歧视州际贸易，只向所有者已成为该州居民至少两年的公司授予销售酒类的许可证。即使该法规促进了国家在促进负责任的酒精消费方面的合法、非经济利益，也存在合理的替代方案。例如，国家可以限制一次向个人出售的酒精数量。因此，该法规过度加重了州际贸易的负担，并且是违宪的。 *第二十一条修正案还废除了第十八修正案关于在美国制造、运输和销售酒精的禁令。 （选择 A）国会确实对州际贸易拥有专属权力。但如果国会尚未在州际贸易的特定领域颁布立法，那么各州可以自由地监管该领域，只要它不歧视州际贸易或给州际贸易带来不当负担或监管域外（即完全在州外）活动。 （选项 C）“传统政府职能”例外允许国家在政府（非私人）实体执行传统职能时支持这些实体。\n\n政府职能（例如废物处理）。在这里，颁发许可证可能是传统的政府职能。但由于该州有利于州内居民（而不是政府实体），因此此例外情况不适用。\n\n教育目标：第二十一条修正案赋予各州广泛的权力来监管其境内的酒精饮料。这样做时，各州不能通过歧视州际商业来违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 州法规促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XXI（国家监管酒精的权力）。\n\n• 田纳西州葡萄酒与烈酒零售商协会诉托马斯案，139 S. Ct. 2449, 2476 (2019)（认为要求酒类商店经营者必须成为州居民至少两年的州法规违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d § 109 (2020)（解释消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）适用于州酒精法规）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第二十一条修正案赋予各州广泛的权力来监管其境内的酒精饮料。这样做时，各州不能通过歧视州际商业来违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 州法规促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。","iracZh":{"issue":"国家法律是否符合宪法？","rule":"第二十一条修正案赋予各州广泛的权力来监管其境内的酒精饮料。这样做时，各州不能通过歧视州际商业来违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 州法规促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。","analysis":"违反宪法的州酒精法规\n\n宪法范例条款\n\n休眠商业仅允许州内企业向州内条款消费者运送酒精\n\n言论自由条款 禁止以酒类价格为主题的广告\n\n设立条款 赋予教会否决教堂半径 500 英尺范围内企业酒类许可证申请的权力\n\n平等保护 允许向 18 岁女性销售酒精饮料，但在 21 岁之前不允许向男性销售酒精饮料\n\n第二十一条修正案赋予各州广泛的权力来监管其境内的酒精使用。*但是，这一权力受到其他宪法条款的限制，包括消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）（选项 D）。消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。歧视性的州法规有利于州内利益而不是州外利益。此类法规受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且是违宪的，除非国家证明：\n• 该法规促进合法的、非经济的国家利益（例如公共卫生或安全）并且\n• 不存在任何合理的替代方案来促进这种兴趣。在这里，州法规歧视州际贸易，只向所有者已成为该州居民至少两年的公司授予销售酒类的许可证。即使该法规促进了国家在促进负责任的酒精消费方面的合法、非经济利益，也存在合理的替代方案。例如，国家可以限制一次向个人出售的酒精数量。因此，该法规过度加重了州际贸易的负担，并且是违宪的。 *第二十一条修正案还废除了第十八修正案关于在美国制造、运输和销售酒精的禁令。 （选择 A）国会确实对州际贸易拥有专属权力。但如果国会尚未在州际贸易的特定领域颁布立法，那么各州可以自由地监管该领域，只要它不歧视州际贸易或给州际贸易带来不当负担或监管域外（即完全在州外）活动。 （选项 C）“传统政府职能”例外允许国家在政府（非私人）实体执行传统职能时支持这些实体。\n\n政府职能（例如废物处理）。在这里，颁发许可证可能是传统的政府职能。但由于该州有利于州内居民（而不是政府实体），因此此例外情况不适用。\n\n教育目标：第二十一条修正案赋予各州广泛的权力来监管其境内的酒精饮料。这样做时，各州不能通过歧视州际商业来违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），除非 (1) 州法规促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XXI（国家监管酒精的权力）。\n\n• 田纳西州葡萄酒与烈酒零售商协会诉托马斯案，139 S. Ct. 2449, 2476 (2019)（认为要求酒类商店经营者必须成为州居民至少两年的州法规违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d § 109 (2020)（解释消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）适用于州酒精法规）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为它过度加重了州际贸易的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"establishment clause","ipa":"/ɪstˈæblɪʃmənt klˈɔz/","syllables":"es·tab·lish·ment clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"政教分离条款：限制政府建立、偏袒或强迫宗教","contextualMeaning":"政教分离条款：限制政府建立、偏袒或强迫宗教","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Unconstitutional state alcohol regulations Constitutional Example provision Dormant commerce Permitting only in-state businesses to ship alcohol to in-state clause consumers Free speech clause Banning advertisements featuring price of alcohol Establishment clause Granting churches power to veto liquor license applications for businesses within 500-foot radius of church Equal protection Permitting alcohol sales to fem","synonyms":["establishment article"]},{"term":"prohibition","ipa":"/pɹˌoʊʌbˈɪʃən/","syllables":"pro·hi·bi·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 禁令, 禁止","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 禁令, 禁止","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:prohibitions","sourceSentence":"*The Twenty-first Amendment also repealed the Eighteenth Amendment's prohibition on the manufacture, transportation, and sale of alcohol in the United States.","synonyms":["inhibition","forbiddance","ban","proscription"]},{"term":"church","ipa":"/tʃˈɝtʃ/","syllables":"church","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 教堂, 礼拜, 教会；vt. 使人接受宗教仪式；a. 教堂的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 教堂, 礼拜, 教会；vt. 使人接受宗教仪式；a. 教堂的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:churches/i:churching/p:churched/3:churches/d:churched","sourceSentence":"Unconstitutional state alcohol regulations Constitutional Example provision Dormant commerce Permitting only in-state businesses to ship alcohol to in-state clause consumers Free speech clause Banning advertisements featuring price of alcohol Establishment clause Granting churches power to veto liquor license applications for businesses within 500-foot radius of church Equal protection Permitting alcohol sales to fem","synonyms":["church service","church building","Christian church"]},{"term":"repealed","ipa":"/ɹɪpˈild/","syllables":"re·pealed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 撤销, 废除( repeal的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 撤销, 废除( repeal的过去式和过去分词 )","wordForm":"词；原形 repeal；词形 0:repeal/1:dp/d:repealed/p:repealed","sourceSentence":"*The Twenty-first Amendment also repealed the Eighteenth Amendment's prohibition on the manufacture, transportation, and sale of alcohol in the United States.","synonyms":["abrogation","annulment","revoke","annul"]},{"term":"banning","ipa":"/bˈænɪŋ/","syllables":"ban·ning","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[人名] 班宁； [地名] [美国] 班宁","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[人名] 班宁； [地名] [美国] 班宁","wordForm":"词；词形 0:ban/1:i/i:banning/s:bannings","sourceSentence":"Unconstitutional state alcohol regulations Constitutional Example provision Dormant commerce Permitting only in-state businesses to ship alcohol to in-state clause consumers Free speech clause Banning advertisements featuring price of alcohol Establishment clause Granting churches power to veto liquor license applications for businesses within 500-foot radius of church Equal protection Permitting alcohol sales to fem","synonyms":["ban","forbiddance","forbidding"]},{"term":"veto","ipa":"/vˈitˌoʊ/","syllables":"veto","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 否决权；vt. 否决, 禁止","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 否决权；vt. 否决, 禁止","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:vetoed/p:vetoed/i:vetoing/3:vetoes","sourceSentence":"Unconstitutional state alcohol regulations Constitutional Example provision Dormant commerce Permitting only in-state businesses to ship alcohol to in-state clause consumers Free speech clause Banning advertisements featuring price of alcohol Establishment clause Granting churches power to veto liquor license applications for businesses within 500-foot radius of church Equal protection Permitting alcohol sales to fem","synonyms":["forbid","prohibit","interdict","proscribe"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":28,"sourceQuestionPage":84,"sourceQuestionPages":[84],"sourceExplanationPages":[85,86],"sourceAnswerPages":[85,86],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-029","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A state legislature conducted an investigation into a series of fatal accidents in the state involving commercial trucks with exteriors made of polished aluminum. The investigation revealed that the sun's glare reflecting off these trucks blinded the drivers of other vehicles. In response, the state's legislature enacted a law prohibiting commercial trucks with polished aluminum exteriors from traveling on the state's highways. Litigation over the state law resulted in a final decision by the United States Supreme Court that the law impermissibly burdened interstate commerce and therefore was unconstitutional. Congress later enacted a statute permitting any state to enact a law regulating the degree of light reflectiveness of the exteriors of commercial trucks using the state's highways. Is this federal statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because Article III vests the judicial power in the federal courts, the essence of judicial power is the ability to render a final judgment, and this statute overrules a final judgment of the U.S. Supreme Court.","B":"No, because the U.S. Supreme Court has already determined that state laws of this type impermissibly burden interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because Article I, Section 8 grants Congress authority to enact statutes authorizing states to impose burdens on interstate commerce that would otherwise be prohibited.","D":"Yes, because Article I, Section 8 grants Congress authority to enact statutes for the general welfare, and Congress could reasonably believe that state laws regulating the light reflectiveness of the exteriors of commercial trucks promote the general welfare."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Commerce clause\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, § 8)\n\nCommerce clause Congress can regulate:\n\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons or things moving in interstate commerce\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce Dormant commerce States cannot: clause\n• discriminate against interstate commerce\n• unduly burden interstate commerce The state legislature enacted a law banning commercial trucks with aluminum exteriors from the state's highways. The U.S. Supreme Court then held that the law impermissibly burdened interstate commerce in violation of the dormant commerce clause. But the commerce clause (in Article I, Section 8) gives Congress substantial authority to regulate interstate commerce. This includes the ability to share commerce power with the states by authorizing them to impose burdens on interstate commerce that would otherwise be prohibited by the dormant commerce clause. And since Congress used that power to later enact a statute permitting states to regulate the reflectiveness of commercial trucks on state highways, that statute is constitutional. (Choices A & B) Article III grants federal courts judicial power, including the ability to render a final judgment. And here, the U.S. Supreme Court exercised that power by determining that the state law impermissibly burdened interstate commerce. But this did not preclude Congress from later exercising its commerce powers by authorizing states to impose such burdens. (Choice D) The taxing and spending clause (in Article I, Section 8) only grants Congress the power to enact statutes to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to enact any type of statute.\n\nEducational objective: The commerce clause grants Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce, including the power to authorize state legislation that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.\n\nReferences\n• S.-Cent. Timber Dev. v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 87–88 (1984) (explaining that Congress can authorize states to regulate interstate commerce in a manner that would otherwise not be permissible).","educationalObjective":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce, including the power to authorize state legislation that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.","references":"• S.-Cent. Timber Dev. v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 87–88 (1984) (explaining that Congress can authorize states to regulate interstate commerce in a manner that would otherwise not be permissible).","rule":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce, including the power to authorize state legislation that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.","irac":{"issue":"Is this federal statute constitutional?","rule":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive power to regulate interstate commerce, including the power to authorize state legislation that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.","analysis":"Commerce clause\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, § 8)\n\nCommerce clause Congress can regulate:\n\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons or things moving in interstate commerce\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce Dormant commerce States cannot: clause\n• discriminate against interstate commerce\n• unduly burden interstate commerce The state legislature enacted a law banning commercial trucks with aluminum exteriors from the state's highways. The U.S. Supreme Court then held that the law impermissibly burdened interstate commerce in violation of the dormant commerce clause. But the commerce clause (in Article I, Section 8) gives Congress substantial authority to regulate interstate commerce. This includes the ability to share commerce power with the states by authorizing them to impose burdens on interstate commerce that would otherwise be prohibited by the dormant commerce clause. And since Congress used that power to later enact a statute permitting states to regulate the reflectiveness of commercial trucks on state highways, that statute is constitutional. (Choices A & B) Article III grants federal courts judicial power, including the ability to render a final judgment. And here, the U.S. Supreme Court exercised that power by determining that the state law impermissibly burdened interstate commerce. But this did not preclude Congress from later exercising its commerce powers by authorizing states to impose such burdens. (Choice D) The taxing and spending clause (in Article I, Section 8) only grants Congress the power to enact statutes to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to enact any type of statute.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because Article I, Section 8 grants Congress authority to enact statutes authorizing states to impose burdens on interstate commerce that would otherwise be prohibited."},"questionZh":"州立法机构对该州发生的一系列致命事故进行了调查，这些事故涉及外部由抛光铝制成的商用卡车。调查显示，这些卡车反射的阳光使其他车辆的司机失明。作为回应，该州立法机关颁布了一项法律，禁止具有抛光铝制外观的商用卡车在该州高速公路上行驶。针对州法律的诉讼导致美国最高法院做出最终裁决，认为该法律不允许地加重州际贸易负担，因此违宪。国会后来颁布了一项法规，允许任何州颁布法律来规范使用该州高速公路的商用卡车外部的光反射程度。该联邦法规是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为第三条赋予联邦法院司法权，司法权的本质是做出最终判决的能力，而该法规推翻了美国最高法院的最终判决。","B":"不可以，因为美国最高法院已经裁定，此类州法律不允许给州际贸易带来负担。","C":"是的，因为第一条第 8 节授权国会颁布法规，授权各州对州际商业施加负担，否则这些负担将被禁止。","D":"是的，因为第一条第 8 节赋予国会为公共福利制定法规的权力，并且国会有理由相信规范商用卡车外部光反射性的州法律促进了公共福利。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条）\n\n国会可以规范商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）：\n\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 州际贸易中移动的人或物\n• 严重影响州际贸易的活动 休眠贸易 各州不能： 条款\n• 歧视州际贸易\n• 给州际贸易造成过度负担 州立法机构颁布了一项法律，禁止铝制外饰的商用卡车在该州高速公路上行驶。美国最高法院随后裁定，该法律不允许州际商业承受负担，违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。但商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（第一条第 8 节）赋予国会监管州际商业的实质性权力。这包括通过授权各州对州际商业施加负担来与各州分享商业权力的能力，否则这些负担将受到消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的禁止。由于国会后来利用这项权力颁布了一项法规，允许各州监管州高速公路上商用卡车的反光度，因此该法规是符合宪法的。 （选项 A 和 B） 第三条赋予联邦法院司法权，包括做出最终判决的能力。在这里，美国最高法院通过裁定州法律不允许地加重州际贸易的负担来行使这一权力。但这并不妨碍国会后来通过授权各州施加此类负担来行使其商业权力。 （选项 D）征税和支出条款（第一条第 8 节）仅授予国会为公共福利制定征税和支出法规的权力，而不是制定任何类型的法规。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业，包括授权州立法，否则将违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。\n\n参考文献\n• S.-Cent。木材开发。 v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 87–88 (1984)（解释说国会可以授权各州以其他方式不允许的方式监管州际贸易）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业，包括授权州立法，否则将违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。","iracZh":{"issue":"该联邦法规是否符合宪法？","rule":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业，包括授权州立法，否则将违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条）\n\n国会可以规范商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）：\n\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 州际贸易中移动的人或物\n• 严重影响州际贸易的活动 休眠贸易 各州不能： 条款\n• 歧视州际贸易\n• 给州际贸易造成过度负担 州立法机构颁布了一项法律，禁止铝制外饰的商用卡车在该州高速公路上行驶。美国最高法院随后裁定，该法律不允许州际商业承受负担，违反了消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。但商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（第一条第 8 节）赋予国会监管州际商业的实质性权力。这包括通过授权各州对州际商业施加负担来与各州分享商业权力的能力，否则这些负担将受到消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的禁止。由于国会后来利用这项权力颁布了一项法规，允许各州监管州高速公路上商用卡车的反光度，因此该法规是符合宪法的。 （选项 A 和 B） 第三条赋予联邦法院司法权，包括做出最终判决的能力。在这里，美国最高法院通过裁定州法律不允许地加重州际贸易的负担来行使这一权力。但这并不妨碍国会后来通过授权各州施加此类负担来行使其商业权力。 （选项 D）征税和支出条款（第一条第 8 节）仅授予国会为公共福利制定征税和支出法规的权力，而不是制定任何类型的法规。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业，包括授权州立法，否则将违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。\n\n参考文献\n• S.-Cent。木材开发。 v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 87–88 (1984)（解释说国会可以授权各州以其他方式不允许的方式监管州际贸易）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为第一条第 8 节授权国会颁布法规，授权各州对州际商业施加负担，否则这些负担将被禁止。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"litigation","ipa":"/lˌɪtʌɡˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"lit·i·ga·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 诉讼, 起诉；[经] 诉讼, 纠葛","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 诉讼, 起诉；[经] 诉讼, 纠葛","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:litigations","sourceSentence":"Litigation over the state law resulted in a final decision by the United States Supreme Court that the law impermissibly burdened interstate commerce and therefore was unconstitutional.","synonyms":["judicial proceeding"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":29,"sourceQuestionPage":87,"sourceQuestionPages":[87],"sourceExplanationPages":[88,89],"sourceAnswerPages":[88,89],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-030","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Article IV & Interstate Relations","question":"A man bought an antique car from a car dealer in State A. Under State A law, a person who buys from such a dealer acquires good title, even if the property was stolen from a previous owner. The man showed the car at an antique car show in State B. A woman recognized the car as having been stolen from her. Under State B law, a person whose property is stolen may reclaim it, even if the current possessor is an innocent purchaser. The woman sued the man in a State B court to reclaim the car. In his defense, the man claimed that he had good title under the law of State A. Nevertheless, the State B court applied State B law, and the woman prevailed. The man did not appeal. The sheriff had the car returned to State B and gave the woman possession of the car. Several months later, the woman moved to State A, bringing the car with her. The man brought a new suit against the woman in a State A court, claiming that the State B court in the prior suit should have applied the State A law, which protects innocent purchasers. The woman appeared and moved to dismiss the suit. What should the State A court do?","choices":{"A":"Apply State A law, because the car is currently located in State A.","B":"Apply the federal law on sale of goods, because the car has moved in interstate commerce.","C":"Dismiss the suit, because the State A court must give full faith and credit to the State B judgment.","D":"Remove the case to federal court, because the car has moved in interstate commerce, and therefore the case raises a federal question."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the Article IV full faith and credit clause, states have a duty to respect the public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of other states. This requires state courts to recognize and enforce decisions rendered by a court in another state when:\n• that court had personal jurisdiction and subject-matter jurisdiction\n• the case was decided on its merits—ie, the substantive elements or grounds of the claim or defense—and\n• a final judgment was entered. Here, the woman sued the man in a State B court to reclaim her car. And the State A court must give full faith and credit to the State B judgment since:\n• the man waived any right to challenge personal jurisdiction by appearing in the State B court and presenting a defense\n\n• state courts, including the State B court, have subject matter jurisdiction over virtually any case arising under federal or state law\n\n• the case was decided on its merits when the State B court applied State B's substantive law and ruled in the woman's favor and\n\n• the judgment became final when the man failed to appeal.\n\nTherefore, the State A court should dismiss the man's suit. (Choices A & B) The fact that the car is currently located in State A and has moved in interstate commerce is irrelevant in determining whether the State A court must give full faith and credit to the State B judgment. (Choice D) Even if the car's movement in interstate commerce created a federal question, the state court cannot remove the case to federal court on its own initiative. Removal is only proper when the defendant requests it (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The full faith and credit clause requires a state court to recognize and enforce a decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.\n\nReferences\n• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998) (explaining when the full faith and credit clause applies to state court judgments).\n\n• V.L. v. E.L., 136 S. Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016) (holding that a state court cannot relitigate a case that has already been decided on its merits in a court of another state).","educationalObjective":"The full faith and credit clause requires a state court to recognize and enforce a decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.","references":"• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998) (explaining when the full faith and credit clause applies to state court judgments).\n\n• V.L. v. E.L., 136 S. Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016) (holding that a state court cannot relitigate a case that has already been decided on its merits in a court of another state).","rule":"The full faith and credit clause requires a state court to recognize and enforce a decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.","irac":{"issue":"What should the State A court do?","rule":"The full faith and credit clause requires a state court to recognize and enforce a decision by a court in another state when (1) that court had personal and subject matter jurisdiction, (2) the case was decided on its merits, and (3) a final judgment was entered.","analysis":"Under the Article IV full faith and credit clause, states have a duty to respect the public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of other states. This requires state courts to recognize and enforce decisions rendered by a court in another state when:\n• that court had personal jurisdiction and subject-matter jurisdiction\n• the case was decided on its merits—ie, the substantive elements or grounds of the claim or defense—and\n• a final judgment was entered. Here, the woman sued the man in a State B court to reclaim her car. And the State A court must give full faith and credit to the State B judgment since:\n• the man waived any right to challenge personal jurisdiction by appearing in the State B court and presenting a defense\n\n• state courts, including the State B court, have subject matter jurisdiction over virtually any case arising under federal or state law\n\n• the case was decided on its merits when the State B court applied State B's substantive law and ruled in the woman's favor and\n\n• the judgment became final when the man failed to appeal.\n\nTherefore, the State A court should dismiss the man's suit. (Choices A & B) The fact that the car is currently located in State A and has moved in interstate commerce is irrelevant in determining whether the State A court must give full faith and credit to the State B judgment. (Choice D) Even if the car's movement in interstate commerce created a federal question, the state court cannot remove the case to federal court on its own initiative. Removal is only proper when the defendant requests it (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Dismiss the suit, because the State A court must give full faith and credit to the State B judgment."},"questionZh":"一名男子从 A 州的汽车经销商那里购买了一辆古董车。根据 A 州法律，从此类经销商处购买的人获得了良好的所有权，即使该财产是从前车主那里偷来的。该男子在 B 州的古董车展上展示了这辆车。一名女子认出这辆车是从她那里偷来的。根据 B 州法律，财产被盗的人可以取回财产，即使当前所有者是无辜的购买者。该女子在 B 州法院起诉该男子，要求收回汽车。该男子在辩护中声称，根据 A 州法律，他拥有良好的所有权。然而，B 州法院适用了 B 州法律，该女子胜诉。该男子没有上诉。治安官将汽车送回 B 州，并将汽车交给了该女子。几个月后，这名妇女带着汽车搬到了 A 州。该男子向 A 州法院对女子提起新诉讼，声称 B 州法院在之前的诉讼中应该适用 A 州法律，该法律保护无辜购买者。该女子出现并提出驳回诉讼。 A州法院应该做什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"应用 A 州法律，因为汽车当前位于 A 州。","B":"适用关于商品销售的联邦法律，因为汽车已在州际贸易中流通。","C":"驳回诉讼，因为 A 州法院必须充分信任 B 州的判决。","D":"将案件移交联邦法院，因为该汽车已在州际贸易中流通，因此该案件引发了联邦问题。"},"explanationZh":"根据第四条充分诚信条款，各国有义务尊重其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。这要求州法院在以下情况下承认并执行另一州法院做出的决定：\n• 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权\n• 案件是根据其案情（即索赔或辩护的实质性要素或理由）做出裁决的，并且\n• 作出最终判决。在这里，该女子在 B 州法院起诉该男子，要求收回她的汽车。 A 州法院必须充分信任 B 州的判决，因为：\n• 该男子通过在 B 州法院出庭并提出辩护，放弃了质疑属人管辖权的任何权利\n\n• 州法院，包括 B 州法院，对几乎所有根据联邦或州法律引起的案件拥有主题管辖权\n\n• 当 B 州法院适用 B 州实体法并做出对妇女有利的裁决时，根据案情作出了裁决；\n\n• 当该男子未能上诉时，判决成为最终判决。\n\n因此，A州法院应驳回该男子的诉讼。 （选项 A 和 B）汽车目前位于 A 州并已在州际贸易中移动这一事实与确定 A 州法院是否必须充分信任 B 州判决无关。 （选项 D）即使汽车在州际贸易中的流动引起了联邦问题，州法院也不能主动将案件移交联邦法院。仅当被告提出要求时，驱逐才是适当的（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标：完整的信用条款要求州法院在以下情况下承认并执行另一州法院的裁决：(1) 该法院拥有个人和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。\n\n参考文献\n• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998)（解释了完整的信用条款何时适用于州法院的判决）。\n\n• V.L. v.E.L.，136 S.Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016)（认为州法院不能对已经在另一州法院就案情作出裁决的案件重新提起诉讼）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"完整的信用条款要求州法院在以下情况下承认并执行另一州法院的裁决：(1) 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。","iracZh":{"issue":"A州法院应该做什么？","rule":"完整的信用条款要求州法院在以下情况下承认并执行另一州法院的裁决：(1) 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。","analysis":"根据第四条充分诚信条款，各国有义务尊重其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。这要求州法院在以下情况下承认并执行另一州法院做出的决定：\n• 该法院拥有属人管辖权和标的管辖权\n• 案件是根据其案情（即索赔或辩护的实质性要素或理由）做出裁决的，并且\n• 作出最终判决。在这里，该女子在 B 州法院起诉该男子，要求收回她的汽车。 A 州法院必须充分信任 B 州的判决，因为：\n• 该男子通过在 B 州法院出庭并提出辩护，放弃了质疑属人管辖权的任何权利\n\n• 州法院，包括 B 州法院，对几乎所有根据联邦或州法律引起的案件拥有主题管辖权\n\n• 当 B 州法院适用 B 州实体法并做出对妇女有利的裁决时，根据案情作出了裁决；\n\n• 当该男子未能上诉时，判决成为最终判决。\n\n因此，A州法院应驳回该男子的诉讼。 （选项 A 和 B）汽车目前位于 A 州并已在州际贸易中移动这一事实与确定 A 州法院是否必须充分信任 B 州判决无关。 （选项 D）即使汽车在州际贸易中的流动引起了联邦问题，州法院也不能主动将案件移交联邦法院。仅当被告提出要求时，驱逐才是适当的（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标：完整的信用条款要求州法院在以下情况下承认并执行另一州法院的裁决：(1) 该法院拥有个人和标的管辖权，(2) 案件根据案情作出裁决，以及 (3) 作出最终判决。\n\n参考文献\n• Baker v. Gen. Motors Corp., 522 U.S. 222, 231–33 (1998)（解释了完整的信用条款何时适用于州法院的判决）。\n\n• V.L. v.E.L.，136 S.Ct. 1017, 1020–22 (2016)（认为州法院不能对已经在另一州法院就案情作出裁决的案件重新提起诉讼）。","conclusion":"答案 C：驳回诉讼，因为 A 州法院必须充分信任 B 州的判决。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"sheriff","ipa":"/ʃˈɛɹəf/","syllables":"sher·iff","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 郡治安官, 县治安官；[法] 司法行政官, 郡长, 执行吏","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 郡治安官, 县治安官；[法] 司法行政官, 郡长, 执行吏","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:sheriffs","sourceSentence":"The sheriff had the car returned to State B and gave the woman possession of the car.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"appeal","ipa":"/ʌpˈil/","syllables":"ap·peal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 恳求, 诉请, 上诉, 吸引力；vi. 呼吁, 诉请, 要求, 上诉, 有吸引力；vt. 将...上诉","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 恳求, 诉请, 上诉, 吸引力；vi. 呼吁, 诉请, 要求, 上诉, 有吸引力；vt. 将...上诉","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:appeals/p:appealed/i:appealing/d:appealed/3:appeals","sourceSentence":"The man did not appeal.","synonyms":["appealingness","charm","solicitation","collection"]},{"term":"title","ipa":"/tˈaɪtəl/","syllables":"ti·tle","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 头衔, 名称, 标题, 书名, 扉页, 权利, 资格, 冠军, 字幕；vt. 授予头衔, 加标题于；[计] 标题","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 头衔, 名称, 标题, 书名, 扉页, 权利, 资格, 冠军, 字幕；vt. 授予头衔, 加标题于；[计] 标题","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:titles/d:titled/i:titling/p:titled/3:titles","sourceSentence":"Under State A law, a person who buys from such a dealer acquires good title, even if the property was stolen from a previous owner.","synonyms":["claim","title of respect","form of address","statute title"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-bcdfd17850c6c661"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":30,"sourceQuestionPage":90,"sourceQuestionPages":[90],"sourceExplanationPages":[91,92],"sourceAnswerPages":[91,92],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-031","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"Congress enacted a federal statute providing that any state may \"require labeling to show the state or other geographic origin of citrus fruit that is imported into the receiving state.\" Pursuant to the federal statute, a state that produced large quantities of citrus fruit enacted a law requiring all citrus fruit imported into the state to be stamped with a two-letter postal abbreviation signifying the state of the fruit's origin. The law did not impose any such requirement for citrus fruit grown within the state. When it adopted the law, the state legislature declared that its purpose was to reduce the risks of infection of local citrus crops by itinerant diseases that have been found to attack citrus fruit. A national association of citrus growers has sued to have the state law declared unconstitutional. The association claims that the law is prohibited by the negative implications of the commerce clause of the Constitution. Which of the following is the best argument in favor of the state's effort to have this lawsuit dismissed?","choices":{"A":"Any burden on interstate commerce imposed by the state law is outweighed by a legitimate state interest.","B":"Congress has the authority to authorize specified state regulations that would otherwise be prohibited by the negative implications of the commerce clause, and it has done so in this situation.","C":"The state law does not discriminate against out-of-state citrus growers or producers.","D":"The state law furthers a legitimate state interest, the burden it imposes on interstate commerce is only incidental, and the state's interest cannot be satisfied by other means that are less burdensome to interstate commerce."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The negative implication of the commerce clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. As a result, states generally cannot favor in-state over out-of-state economic interests unless:\n• the state law furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and\n• no reasonable, nondiscriminatory alternatives are available. But this analysis is unnecessary—and states can engage in such discrimination—when Congress explicitly authorizes the state's conduct (or some other exception to the dormant commerce clause applies). Here, the state law discriminates against interstate commerce by requiring citrus fruit imported into the state—but not citrus fruit grown within the state—to have a stamp showing its origin (Choice C). This law would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause since there are reasonable alternatives to prevent citrus fruit infections (eg, conducting safety inspections) (Choice D). But since Congress explicitly authorized states to require this labeling, this is the state's best argument to have the lawsuit dismissed. (Choice A) Under the undue burden test, a state regulation is justified if its legitimate, local benefits exceed the burdens imposed on interstate commerce. But this test does not apply to discriminatory state regulations (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The dormant commerce clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. But states can do so when Congress has explicitly authorized that conduct.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (commerce clause).\n\n• Ne. Bancorp, Inc. v. Bd. of Governors of Fed. Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985) (explaining that states can discriminate against interstate commerce when Congress authorizes them to do so).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 27 (2019) (discussing congressional authorization to regulate interstate commerce).","educationalObjective":"The dormant commerce clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. But states can do so when Congress has explicitly authorized that conduct.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (commerce clause).\n\n• Ne. Bancorp, Inc. v. Bd. of Governors of Fed. Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985) (explaining that states can discriminate against interstate commerce when Congress authorizes them to do so).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 27 (2019) (discussing congressional authorization to regulate interstate commerce).","rule":"The dormant commerce clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. But states can do so when Congress has explicitly authorized that conduct.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the best argument in favor of the state's effort to have this lawsuit dismissed?","rule":"The dormant commerce clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against or unduly burdening interstate commerce. But states can do so when Congress has explicitly authorized that conduct.","analysis":"The negative implication of the commerce clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. As a result, states generally cannot favor in-state over out-of-state economic interests unless:\n• the state law furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and\n• no reasonable, nondiscriminatory alternatives are available. But this analysis is unnecessary—and states can engage in such discrimination—when Congress explicitly authorizes the state's conduct (or some other exception to the dormant commerce clause applies). Here, the state law discriminates against interstate commerce by requiring citrus fruit imported into the state—but not citrus fruit grown within the state—to have a stamp showing its origin (Choice C). This law would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause since there are reasonable alternatives to prevent citrus fruit infections (eg, conducting safety inspections) (Choice D). But since Congress explicitly authorized states to require this labeling, this is the state's best argument to have the lawsuit dismissed. (Choice A) Under the undue burden test, a state regulation is justified if its legitimate, local benefits exceed the burdens imposed on interstate commerce. But this test does not apply to discriminatory state regulations (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: Congress has the authority to authorize specified state regulations that would otherwise be prohibited by the negative implications of the commerce clause, and it has done so in this situation."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项联邦法规，规定任何州都可以“要求贴标签以显示进口到接收州的柑橘类水果的州或其他地理来源”。根据联邦法规，生产大量柑橘类水果的州颁布了一项法律，要求所有进口到该州的柑橘类水果都必须加盖两个字母的邮政缩写，表示该水果的原产地州。该法律没有对该州内种植的柑橘类水果施加任何此类要求。在通过该法律时，州立法机关宣布其目的是减少当地柑橘作物被已发现的攻击柑橘类水果的流动疾病感染的风险。全国柑橘种植者协会提起诉讼，要求宣布该州法律违宪。该协会声称，该法律因宪法商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响而被禁止。以下哪一项是支持该州努力驳回该诉讼的最佳论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"州法律对州际贸易施加的任何负担都比合法的州利益更重要。","B":"国会有权批准特定的州法规，否则这些法规将因商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响而被禁止，并且在这种情况下它已经这样做了。","C":"州法律不歧视州外柑橘种植者或生产者。","D":"州法促进了合法的国家利益，它对州际贸易施加的负担只是偶然的，并且国家利益不能通过其他对州际贸易负担较轻的方式来满足。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。因此，各州通常不能偏袒州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益，除非：\n• 国家法律促进合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且\n• 没有合理的、非歧视性的替代方案。但当国会明确授权各州的行为（或适用消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的其他例外情况）时，这种分析是不必要的，并且各州可以参与此类歧视。在这里，州法律歧视州际贸易，要求进口到该州的柑橘类水果（而不是在该州种植的柑橘类水果）贴有显示其原产地的印章（选项 C）。该法律通常会违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），因为有合理的替代方案可以防止柑橘类水果感染（例如进行安全检查）（选择D）。但由于国会明确授权各州要求这种标签，这是该州驳回诉讼的最佳论据。 （选项 A）根据不当负担测试，如果州法规的合法本地利益超过了州际贸易所承受的负担，那么该州法规就是合理的。但此测试不适用于歧视性的国家法规（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）通常禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。但当国会明确授权该行为时，各州可以这样做。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 氖。 Bancorp, Inc. 诉 Bd.美联储理事。 Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985)（解释说，当国会授权时，各州可以歧视州际贸易）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 27 (2019)（讨论国会授权监管州际商业）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）通常禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。但当国会明确授权该行为时，各州可以这样做。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是支持该州努力驳回该诉讼的最佳论据？","rule":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）通常禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。但当国会明确授权该行为时，各州可以这样做。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。因此，各州通常不能偏袒州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益，除非：\n• 国家法律促进合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且\n• 没有合理的、非歧视性的替代方案。但当国会明确授权各州的行为（或适用消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的其他例外情况）时，这种分析是不必要的，并且各州可以参与此类歧视。在这里，州法律歧视州际贸易，要求进口到该州的柑橘类水果（而不是在该州种植的柑橘类水果）贴有显示其原产地的印章（选项 C）。该法律通常会违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），因为有合理的替代方案可以防止柑橘类水果感染（例如进行安全检查）（选择D）。但由于国会明确授权各州要求这种标签，这是该州驳回诉讼的最佳论据。 （选项 A）根据不当负担测试，如果州法规的合法本地利益超过了州际贸易所承受的负担，那么该州法规就是合理的。但此测试不适用于歧视性的国家法规（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）通常禁止各州歧视州际商业或对州际商业造成过度负担。但当国会明确授权该行为时，各州可以这样做。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 氖。 Bancorp, Inc. 诉 Bd.美联储理事。 Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985)（解释说，当国会授权时，各州可以歧视州际贸易）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 27 (2019)（讨论国会授权监管州际商业）。","conclusion":"答案 B：国会有权批准特定的州法规，否则这些法规将因商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响而被禁止，并且在这种情况下它已经这样做了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"lawsuit","ipa":"/lˈɔsˌut/","syllables":"law·suit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 诉讼；[法] 诉讼, 诉讼案件","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 诉讼；[法] 诉讼, 诉讼案件","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:lawsuits","sourceSentence":"Which of the following is the best argument in favor of the state's effort to have this lawsuit dismissed?","synonyms":["suit","case","cause","causa"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-92ecbf48937cf0df"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":31,"sourceQuestionPage":93,"sourceQuestionPages":[93],"sourceExplanationPages":[94,95],"sourceAnswerPages":[94,95],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-032","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"After several well-publicized deaths caused by fires in products made from highly flammable fabrics, State A enacted a statute prohibiting \"the manufacture or assembly of any product in this state which contains any fabric that has not been tested and approved for flame retardancy by a specific privately owned and operated testing company located in State A.\" For many years, a fabric mill located in State A has had its fabric tested for flame retardancy by a privately owned and operated testing company located in State B. This testing company is a reliable organization that uses a process for testing and approving fabrics for flame retardancy identical in all respects to that used by the State A testing company. Because the fabric mill wishes to continue to have its fabric tested solely by the State B testing company, the fabric mill files an action in a State A court challenging the constitutionality of the State A statute as applied to its circumstances. In this suit, should the court hold the statute to be constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it denies the fabric mill the equal protection of the laws.","B":"No, because it imposes an unreasonable burden on interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because it is a legitimate means of protecting the safety of the public.","D":"Yes, because it is reasonably related to the protection of the reputation of the fabric industry located in State A."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The states' power to regulate interstate commerce is restricted by the dormant commerce clause, which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. Discrimination against interstate commerce—ie, favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests—is invalid unless:\n• it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest (eg, public health and safety) and\n• no reasonable alternative exists. Here, the State A statute discriminates against interstate commerce since it requires that an in-state company test flame retardancy. The statute promotes the state's legitimate, noneconomic interest in ensuring safety. But reasonable alternatives are available—eg, allowing any reliable organization, including the State B company, to conduct these tests. Therefore, the statute is unconstitutional as applied to these circumstances because it unreasonably burdens interstate commerce (Choice C). (Choice A) State laws that substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class almost always violate the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But since no fundamental right is implicated and businesses are not a protected class, the statute likely does not deny the fabric mill the equal protection of the laws. (Choice D) A state law that discriminates against interstate commerce must further a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and no reasonable alternatives can exist. Therefore, the fact that the statute is reasonably related to State A's economic interest in protecting the reputation of State A's fabric industry does not justify the statute.\n\nEducational objective: Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot discriminate against interstate commerce unless (1) the discrimination furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986) (explaining that state discrimination against interstate commerce is invalid unless it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and no reasonable alternatives exist).","educationalObjective":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot discriminate against interstate commerce unless (1) the discrimination furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986) (explaining that state discrimination against interstate commerce is invalid unless it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and no reasonable alternatives exist).","rule":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot discriminate against interstate commerce unless (1) the discrimination furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","irac":{"issue":"In this suit, should the court hold the statute to be constitutional?","rule":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot discriminate against interstate commerce unless (1) the discrimination furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists.","analysis":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The states' power to regulate interstate commerce is restricted by the dormant commerce clause, which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. Discrimination against interstate commerce—ie, favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests—is invalid unless:\n• it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest (eg, public health and safety) and\n• no reasonable alternative exists. Here, the State A statute discriminates against interstate commerce since it requires that an in-state company test flame retardancy. The statute promotes the state's legitimate, noneconomic interest in ensuring safety. But reasonable alternatives are available—eg, allowing any reliable organization, including the State B company, to conduct these tests. Therefore, the statute is unconstitutional as applied to these circumstances because it unreasonably burdens interstate commerce (Choice C). (Choice A) State laws that substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class almost always violate the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But since no fundamental right is implicated and businesses are not a protected class, the statute likely does not deny the fabric mill the equal protection of the laws. (Choice D) A state law that discriminates against interstate commerce must further a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and no reasonable alternatives can exist. Therefore, the fact that the statute is reasonably related to State A's economic interest in protecting the reputation of State A's fabric industry does not justify the statute.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because it imposes an unreasonable burden on interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"在发生了几起广为人知的由高度易燃织物制成的产品火灾造成的死亡事件后，A 州颁布了一项法规，禁止“在该州制造或组装任何含有未经 A 州特定私人拥有和经营的测试公司进行阻燃性测试和批准的织物的产品”。多年来，位于 A 州的一家织物厂一直由位于 B 州的一家私营测试公司对其织物进行阻燃性测试。该测试公司是一家可靠的组织，其测试和批准织物阻燃性的流程在所有方面均与 A 州测试公司所使用的流程相同。由于面料厂希望继续仅由 B 州测试公司对其面料进行测试，因此面料厂向 A 州法院提起诉讼，质疑 A 州法规适用于其情况的合宪性。在这起诉讼中，法院是否应该认定该法规符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为它剥夺了布料厂受到法律的平等保护。","B":"不，因为它给州际贸易带来了不合理的负担。","C":"是的，因为这是保护公众安全的合法手段。","D":"是的，因为这与保护位于 A 国的面料行业的声誉合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（繁重）超过当地所有商业运输利益 各州监管州际贸易的权力受到消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制，该条款禁止各州歧视州际贸易或以其他方式对州际贸易造成过度负担。对州际商业的歧视——即有利于州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益——是无效的，除非：\n• 它促进合法的、非经济的国家利益（例如，公共健康和安全）并且\n• 不存在合理的替代方案。在这里，A 州法规对州际贸易存在歧视，因为它要求州内公司测试阻燃性。该法规促进国家在确保安全方面的合法、非经济利益。但合理的替代方案是可行的——例如，允许任何可靠的组织，包括 B 国公司，来进行这些测试。因此，该法规适用于这些情况是违宪的，因为它不合理地加重了州际贸易的负担（选项 C）。 （选项 A）严重影响基本权利或故意歧视受保护群体的州法律几乎总是违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但由于不涉及基本权利，而且企业不是受保护阶层，因此该法规可能不会否认布料厂受到法律的平等保护。 （选项 D）歧视州际商业的州法律必须促进合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且不存在合理的替代方案。因此，该法规与 A 国保护 A 国织物工业声誉的经济利益合理相关这一事实并不能证明该法规的合理性。\n\n教育目标：根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得歧视州际商业，除非 (1) 歧视促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986)（解释说国家对州际贸易的歧视是无效的，除非它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益并且不存在合理的替代方案）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得歧视州际商业，除非（1）这种歧视促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起诉讼中，法院是否应该认定该法规符合宪法？","rule":"根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得歧视州际商业，除非（1）这种歧视促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。","analysis":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（繁重）超过当地所有商业运输利益 各州监管州际贸易的权力受到消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的限制，该条款禁止各州歧视州际贸易或以其他方式对州际贸易造成过度负担。对州际商业的歧视——即有利于州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益——是无效的，除非：\n• 它促进合法的、非经济的国家利益（例如，公共健康和安全）并且\n• 不存在合理的替代方案。在这里，A 州法规对州际贸易存在歧视，因为它要求州内公司测试阻燃性。该法规促进国家在确保安全方面的合法、非经济利益。但合理的替代方案是可行的——例如，允许任何可靠的组织，包括 B 国公司，来进行这些测试。因此，该法规适用于这些情况是违宪的，因为它不合理地加重了州际贸易的负担（选项 C）。 （选项 A）严重影响基本权利或故意歧视受保护群体的州法律几乎总是违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但由于不涉及基本权利，而且企业不是受保护阶层，因此该法规可能不会否认布料厂受到法律的平等保护。 （选项 D）歧视州际商业的州法律必须促进合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且不存在合理的替代方案。因此，该法规与 A 国保护 A 国织物工业声誉的经济利益合理相关这一事实并不能证明该法规的合理性。\n\n教育目标：根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得歧视州际商业，除非 (1) 歧视促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且 (2) 不存在合理的替代方案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Maine v. Taylor, 477 U.S. 131, 138 (1986)（解释说国家对州际贸易的歧视是无效的，除非它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益并且不存在合理的替代方案）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为它给州际贸易带来了不合理的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"assembly","ipa":"/ʌsˈɛmbli/","syllables":"as·sem·bly","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 与会者, 集会, 装配, 组件；[化] 装配","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 与会者, 集会, 装配, 组件；[化] 装配","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:assemblies","sourceSentence":"After several well-publicized deaths caused by fires in products made from highly flammable fabrics, State A enacted a statute prohibiting \"the manufacture or assembly of any product in this state which contains any fabric that has not been tested and approved for flame retardancy by a specific privately owned and operated testing company located in State A.","synonyms":["fabrication","assemblage","gathering","forum"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":32,"sourceQuestionPage":96,"sourceQuestionPages":[96],"sourceExplanationPages":[97,98],"sourceAnswerPages":[97,98],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-033","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A state owned a large natural gas field and took bids for its exploitation. The highest bid came from an interstate pipeline company that distributed natural gas to providers throughout the country. A local gas company submitted the next highest bid, which included the commitment that it would pass along to local customers any savings if it was awarded the contract. The state awarded the contract to the local company. The interstate company sued to overturn this decision. Should the interstate company prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the state acted as a market participant.","B":"No, because the state has a compelling interest in reducing the cost of gas for state citizens.","C":"Yes, because the state acted irrationally by not choosing the highest bidder and thus denied the interstate company due process of law.","D":"Yes, because the state discriminated against interstate commerce."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) is that states are generally prohibited from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But under the market-participant exception, states are not subject to the dormant commerce clause when they act as a market participant—ie, buy or sell goods or services—rather than as a market regulator. Here, the state discriminated against interstate commerce when it awarded the contract to the local company even though the interstate company was the highest bidder. But since the state was selling the right to exploit the natural gas field that it owned, it acted as a market participant. As a result, the dormant commerce clause does not apply, and the interstate company should not prevail in its suit to overturn the state's decision (Choice D). (Choice B) Had the dormant commerce clause applied, this state decision would have been subject to strict scrutiny. Under this test, state action that discriminates against interstate commerce is invalid unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists. And since the state's interest in reducing local customers' gas costs is economic in nature, the decision likely would have failed strict scrutiny. (Choice C) Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, most state deprivations of life, liberty, or property are justified if they are rationally related to a legitimate state interest. And here, choosing the local company's lower bid was rational since any savings would be passed on to local customers—a legitimate state interest. Therefore, the state did not deny the interstate company due process of law.\n\nEducational objective: The dormant commerce clause—which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce—does not apply when a state acts as a market participant (eg, buyer or seller).\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980) (holding that a state acts as a market participant and is not subject to the dormant commerce clause when the state buys or sells goods or services).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 33 (2019) (explaining that the dormant commerce clause does not apply when a state acts as a market participant as opposed to a market regulator).","educationalObjective":"The dormant commerce clause—which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce—does not apply when a state acts as a market participant (eg, buyer or seller).","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980) (holding that a state acts as a market participant and is not subject to the dormant commerce clause when the state buys or sells goods or services).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 33 (2019) (explaining that the dormant commerce clause does not apply when a state acts as a market participant as opposed to a market regulator).","rule":"The dormant commerce clause—which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce—does not apply when a state acts as a market participant (eg, buyer or seller).","irac":{"issue":"Should the interstate company prevail?","rule":"The dormant commerce clause—which prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce—does not apply when a state acts as a market participant (eg, buyer or seller).","analysis":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) is that states are generally prohibited from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But under the market-participant exception, states are not subject to the dormant commerce clause when they act as a market participant—ie, buy or sell goods or services—rather than as a market regulator. Here, the state discriminated against interstate commerce when it awarded the contract to the local company even though the interstate company was the highest bidder. But since the state was selling the right to exploit the natural gas field that it owned, it acted as a market participant. As a result, the dormant commerce clause does not apply, and the interstate company should not prevail in its suit to overturn the state's decision (Choice D). (Choice B) Had the dormant commerce clause applied, this state decision would have been subject to strict scrutiny. Under this test, state action that discriminates against interstate commerce is invalid unless (1) it furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest and (2) no reasonable alternative exists. And since the state's interest in reducing local customers' gas costs is economic in nature, the decision likely would have failed strict scrutiny. (Choice C) Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, most state deprivations of life, liberty, or property are justified if they are rationally related to a legitimate state interest. And here, choosing the local company's lower bid was rational since any savings would be passed on to local customers—a legitimate state interest. Therefore, the state did not deny the interstate company due process of law.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the state acted as a market participant."},"questionZh":"某国有拥有一个大型天然气田，并对其开采进行了招标。最高出价来自一家州际管道公司，该公司向全国各地的供应商分销天然气。一家当地天然气公司提交了第二高的出价，其中包括承诺，如果获得合同，它将把节省的任何费用转嫁给当地客户。国家将合同授予当地公司。州际公司提起诉讼，要求推翻这一决定。州际公司应该占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国家充当市场参与者。","B":"不，因为该州对降低本州公民的天然气成本有着强烈的兴趣。","C":"是的，因为州政府的行为不合理，没有选择最高出价者，从而拒绝了州际公司的正当法律程序。","D":"是的，因为国家歧视州际贸易。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业——即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是，一般禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。但根据市场参与者例外，当国家作为市场参与者（即购买或出售商品或服务）而不是市场监管者时，不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的约束。在这里，当州将合同授予当地公司时，尽管州际公司是出价最高者，但它还是对州际商业的歧视。但由于国家出售其拥有的天然气田的开采权，因此它充当了市场参与者。因此，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）不适用，州际公司不应在推翻州决定的诉讼中胜诉（选项 D）。 （选项 B）如果适用消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），该州的决定将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。根据这一测试，歧视州际贸易的国家行为是无效的，除非（1）它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。由于该州降低当地客户天然气成本的利益本质上是经济性的，因此该决定很可能无法通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。 （选项C）根据第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），大多数国家剥夺生命、自由或财产的行为都是合理的，如果它们与合法的国家利益合理相关的话。在这里，选择当地公司的较低出价是合理的，因为任何节省都将转嫁给当地客户——这是合法的国家利益。因此，州政府并没有否认州际公司的正当法律程序。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）（禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担）不适用于当州作为市场参与者（例如买方或卖方）时。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980)（认为国家作为市场参与者，在购买或出售商品或服务时不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的约束）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 33 (2019)（解释说，当国家充当市场参与者而不是市场监管者时，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）不适用）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当国家作为市场参与者（例如买方或卖方）时，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）（禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担）不适用。","iracZh":{"issue":"州际公司应该占上风吗？","rule":"当国家作为市场参与者（例如买方或卖方）时，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）（禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担）不适用。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业——即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是，一般禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。但根据市场参与者例外，当国家作为市场参与者（即购买或出售商品或服务）而不是市场监管者时，不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的约束。在这里，当州将合同授予当地公司时，尽管州际公司是出价最高者，但它还是对州际商业的歧视。但由于国家出售其拥有的天然气田的开采权，因此它充当了市场参与者。因此，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）不适用，州际公司不应在推翻州决定的诉讼中胜诉（选项 D）。 （选项 B）如果适用消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），该州的决定将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。根据这一测试，歧视州际贸易的国家行为是无效的，除非（1）它促进了合法的、非经济的国家利益，并且（2）不存在合理的替代方案。由于该州降低当地客户天然气成本的利益本质上是经济性的，因此该决定很可能无法通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。 （选项C）根据第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），大多数国家剥夺生命、自由或财产的行为都是合理的，如果它们与合法的国家利益合理相关的话。在这里，选择当地公司的较低出价是合理的，因为任何节省都将转嫁给当地客户——这是合法的国家利益。因此，州政府并没有否认州际公司的正当法律程序。\n\n教育目标：消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）（禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担）不适用于当州作为市场参与者（例如买方或卖方）时。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, 447 U.S. 429, 439–40 (1980)（认为国家作为市场参与者，在购买或出售商品或服务时不受消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的约束）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 33 (2019)（解释说，当国家充当市场参与者而不是市场监管者时，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）不适用）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为国家充当市场参与者。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-92ecbf48937cf0df"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":33,"sourceQuestionPage":99,"sourceQuestionPages":[99],"sourceExplanationPages":[100,101],"sourceAnswerPages":[100,101],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-034","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A state law prohibits the withdrawal of groundwater from any well within the state for use in another state. The express purpose of the law is to safeguard the supply of water for state citizens. Adoption of this state law followed enactment of a federal statute providing that \"the transport of groundwater from one state to another may be restricted or prohibited in accordance with the laws of the state in which the water originates.\" An association of water users in a neighboring state has filed suit to have the state law declared unconstitutional and enjoined on the ground that it violates the negative implications of the commerce clause. Which of the following is the best argument supporting a motion to dismiss the lawsuit?","choices":{"A":"Groundwater located within a state is not itself an article of interstate commerce, and therefore state regulation of the withdrawal of such groundwater does not implicate the commerce clause.","B":"The federal statute explicitly consents to a state's regulation of its groundwater in a way that would otherwise violate the negative implications of the commerce clause.","C":"The law promotes a compelling state interest that outweighs any burden on interstate commercial activity that might result from this state regulation of its groundwater.","D":"The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states plenary authority over the regulation of the natural resources located within their respective borders."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the negative implications of the commerce clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause), states are generally prohibited from unduly burdening interstate commerce. An undue burden can arise when a state discriminates against interstate commerce by favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests. But such discrimination is permitted when Congress explicitly consents to the state's regulation. Here, the state law discriminates against interstate commerce by prohibiting the withdrawal of in-state groundwater for out-of-state use. But since this law was adopted after the enactment of a federal statute that explicitly consents to such a regulation, the state law does not violate the negative implications of the commerce clause. Therefore, this is the best argument supporting the state's motion to dismiss the lawsuit. (Choice A) An article of interstate commerce is anything that can be sold, exchanged, or transported between states—including natural resources like groundwater. (Choice C) A nondiscriminatory state law is justified if the burdens on interstate commerce do not clearly exceed the law's local benefits. But a discriminatory state law (as seen here) cannot be justified on this basis. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the power to regulate in- state natural resources. But this power is not plenary (ie, absolute) since states must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, the dormant commerce clause).\n\nEducational objective: A state law that would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause is valid if an exception exists—eg, when Congress explicitly consents to such a law.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Ne. Bancorp, Inc. v. Bd. of Governors of Fed. Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985) (asserting that states can discriminate against interstate commerce when Congress authorizes such action).","educationalObjective":"A state law that would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause is valid if an exception exists—eg, when Congress explicitly consents to such a law.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Ne. Bancorp, Inc. v. Bd. of Governors of Fed. Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985) (asserting that states can discriminate against interstate commerce when Congress authorizes such action).","rule":"A state law that would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause is valid if an exception exists—eg, when Congress explicitly consents to such a law.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the best argument supporting a motion to dismiss the lawsuit?","rule":"A state law that would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause is valid if an exception exists—eg, when Congress explicitly consents to such a law.","analysis":"Under the negative implications of the commerce clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause), states are generally prohibited from unduly burdening interstate commerce. An undue burden can arise when a state discriminates against interstate commerce by favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests. But such discrimination is permitted when Congress explicitly consents to the state's regulation. Here, the state law discriminates against interstate commerce by prohibiting the withdrawal of in-state groundwater for out-of-state use. But since this law was adopted after the enactment of a federal statute that explicitly consents to such a regulation, the state law does not violate the negative implications of the commerce clause. Therefore, this is the best argument supporting the state's motion to dismiss the lawsuit. (Choice A) An article of interstate commerce is anything that can be sold, exchanged, or transported between states—including natural resources like groundwater. (Choice C) A nondiscriminatory state law is justified if the burdens on interstate commerce do not clearly exceed the law's local benefits. But a discriminatory state law (as seen here) cannot be justified on this basis. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the power to regulate in- state natural resources. But this power is not plenary (ie, absolute) since states must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, the dormant commerce clause).","conclusion":"Answer B: The federal statute explicitly consents to a state's regulation of its groundwater in a way that would otherwise violate the negative implications of the commerce clause."},"questionZh":"州法律禁止从本州内的任何井中抽取地下水供另一个州使用。该法律的明确目的是保障州公民的供水。该州法律的通过是在一项联邦法规颁布之后颁布的，该法规规定“根据水源地州的法律，可以限制或禁止将地下水从一个州运输到另一个州”。邻州的一个用水者协会已提起诉讼，要求宣布该州法律违宪并予以禁止，理由是它违反了商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。以下哪一项是支持驳回诉讼动议的最佳论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"位于州内的地下水本身并不是州际贸易的物品，因此州对此类地下水抽取的监管并不涉及商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。","B":"联邦法规明确同意各州对其地下水进行监管，否则会违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。","C":"该法律促进了令人信服的国家利益，该利益超过了州对地下水的监管可能给州际商业活动带来的任何负担。","D":"第十修正案保留各州对其各自境内自然资源进行监管的全部权力。"},"explanationZh":"在商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面影响下，各州通常被禁止对州际商业造成过度负担。当一个州通过偏袒州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益来歧视州际贸易时，可能会产生不适当的负担。但当国会明确同意州的监管时，这种歧视是被允许的。在这里，州法律禁止提取州内地下水供州外使用，从而歧视州际商业。但由于该法律是在明确同意此类规定的联邦法规颁布后通过的，因此州法律并未违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。因此，这是支持州政府驳回诉讼动议的最佳论据。 （选项 A）州际贸易是指可以在州之间出售、交换或运输的任何物品，包括地下水等自然资源。 （选项 C）如果州际贸易的负担没有明显超过该法律给当地带来的利益，那么非歧视性的州法律就是合理的。但歧视性的州法律（如此处所示）不能以此为依据。 （选项 D）第十修正案将宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州，包括管理州内自然资源的权力。但这种权力不是全权（即绝对），因为各州必须遵守其他宪法条款（例如，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n教育目标：如果存在例外情况，例如，当国会明确同意此类法律时，通常会违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的州法律是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 氖。 Bancorp, Inc. 诉 Bd.美联储理事。 Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985)（声称当国会授权时，各州可以歧视州际贸易）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果存在例外情况（例如，当国会明确同意此类法律时），通常会违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的州法律是有效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是支持驳回诉讼动议的最佳论据？","rule":"如果存在例外情况（例如，当国会明确同意此类法律时），通常会违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的州法律是有效的。","analysis":"在商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面影响下，各州通常被禁止对州际商业造成过度负担。当一个州通过偏袒州内经济利益而不是州外经济利益来歧视州际贸易时，可能会产生不适当的负担。但当国会明确同意州的监管时，这种歧视是被允许的。在这里，州法律禁止提取州内地下水供州外使用，从而歧视州际商业。但由于该法律是在明确同意此类规定的联邦法规颁布后通过的，因此州法律并未违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。因此，这是支持州政府驳回诉讼动议的最佳论据。 （选项 A）州际贸易是指可以在州之间出售、交换或运输的任何物品，包括地下水等自然资源。 （选项 C）如果州际贸易的负担没有明显超过该法律给当地带来的利益，那么非歧视性的州法律就是合理的。但歧视性的州法律（如此处所示）不能以此为依据。 （选项 D）第十修正案将宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州，包括管理州内自然资源的权力。但这种权力不是全权（即绝对），因为各州必须遵守其他宪法条款（例如，消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））。\n\n教育目标：如果存在例外情况，例如，当国会明确同意此类法律时，通常会违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的州法律是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 氖。 Bancorp, Inc. 诉 Bd.美联储理事。 Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985)（声称当国会授权时，各州可以歧视州际贸易）。","conclusion":"答案 B：联邦法规明确同意各州对其地下水进行监管，否则会违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的负面影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"enactment","ipa":"/ɛnˈæktmənt/","syllables":"en·act·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 制定, 条例, 法规, 法令, 颁布, 规定；[法] 制定, 规定, 颁布","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 制定, 条例, 法规, 法令, 颁布, 规定；[法] 制定, 规定, 颁布","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:enactments","sourceSentence":"Adoption of this state law followed enactment of a federal statute providing that \"the transport of groundwater from one state to another may be restricted or prohibited in accordance with the laws of the state in which the water originates.","synonyms":["portrayal","characterization","personation","passage"]},{"term":"adoption","ipa":"/ʌdˈɑpʃən/","syllables":"adop·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 采用, 采纳, 收养；[法] 收养, 采纳, 采用","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 采用, 采纳, 收养；[法] 收养, 采纳, 采用","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:adoptions","sourceSentence":"Adoption of this state law followed enactment of a federal statute providing that \"the transport of groundwater from one state to another may be restricted or prohibited in accordance with the laws of the state in which the water originates.","synonyms":["borrowing","acceptance","acceptation","espousal"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-92ecbf48937cf0df"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":34,"sourceQuestionPage":102,"sourceQuestionPages":[102],"sourceExplanationPages":[103,104],"sourceAnswerPages":[103,104],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-035","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"The childhood home of a former U.S. president is part of a national park located in a city. The National Park Service entered into a contract with an independent antique collector to acquire items owned by residents of the city during the former president's lifetime. According to the contract, the collector purchases items and then sells them to the Park Service at a price equal to the collector's cost plus a 10% commission. Purchases by antique collectors are ordinarily subject to the sales tax of the state in which the city is located. The collector has filed suit in state court to enjoin collection of the tax on these purchases for the Park Service, claiming that the sales tax is unconstitutional as applied to them. Should the state court issue the injunction?","choices":{"A":"No, because as the purchaser of the antiques, the collector, rather than the federal government, is liable for the tax.","B":"No, because the suit is within the exclusive jurisdiction of the federal courts.","C":"Yes, because the federal government is contractually obligated to pay the amount of the sales tax when the government covers the collector's cost of the antiques.","D":"Yes, because under the supremacy clause, the federal program to acquire the antiques preempts the state sales tax on the purchase of these items."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the Article VI supremacy clause, the federal government is generally immune from direct taxation by the states. This is because such taxes could hinder or destroy the operations of the federal government. But states can tax the federal government's affiliates, including persons or entities employed by or doing business with the federal government (eg, contractors—as seen here). This is true even if the cost of the tax is ultimately passed on to the federal government. As a result, an affiliate must pay these taxes unless:\n• Congress has granted the affiliate immunity\n\n• the tax discriminates against the federal government or the affiliate or\n\n• the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties—eg, when a high tax burden makes it impossible for the affiliate to accomplish its federal functions.\n\nHere, the independent antique collector (federal government affiliate) is responsible for paying the state sales tax when purchasing items to sell to the National Park Service. Although the federal government is contractually obligated to pay the amount of the sales tax when it covers the collector's cost, this is not a direct tax (Choice C). And since (1) Congress has not granted the collector immunity, (2) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (3) there is no indication that it imposes a prohibitively high tax burden, the state should not be enjoined from collecting this tax. (Choice B) Federal courts do not have exclusive jurisdiction over suits arising under federal law (as seen here) since state courts also have jurisdiction over such suits. (Choice D) The supremacy clause also allows federal law to preempt conflicting state law. But there is no direct conflict here since the antique collector can pay the sales tax without violating federal law. And there is no indirect conflict since the collector can still acquire antiques to accomplish the federal program's purpose.\n\nEducational objective: States can tax federal government affiliates (eg, employees) unless (1) Congress granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.\n\nReferences\n• Alabama v. King & Boozer, 314 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1941) (holding that a state can tax a federal contractor even if the tax is passed on to the federal government).","educationalObjective":"States can tax federal government affiliates (eg, employees) unless (1) Congress granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","references":"• Alabama v. King & Boozer, 314 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1941) (holding that a state can tax a federal contractor even if the tax is passed on to the federal government).","rule":"States can tax federal government affiliates (eg, employees) unless (1) Congress granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","irac":{"issue":"Should the state court issue the injunction?","rule":"States can tax federal government affiliates (eg, employees) unless (1) Congress granted the affiliate immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties.","analysis":"Under the Article VI supremacy clause, the federal government is generally immune from direct taxation by the states. This is because such taxes could hinder or destroy the operations of the federal government. But states can tax the federal government's affiliates, including persons or entities employed by or doing business with the federal government (eg, contractors—as seen here). This is true even if the cost of the tax is ultimately passed on to the federal government. As a result, an affiliate must pay these taxes unless:\n• Congress has granted the affiliate immunity\n\n• the tax discriminates against the federal government or the affiliate or\n\n• the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties—eg, when a high tax burden makes it impossible for the affiliate to accomplish its federal functions.\n\nHere, the independent antique collector (federal government affiliate) is responsible for paying the state sales tax when purchasing items to sell to the National Park Service. Although the federal government is contractually obligated to pay the amount of the sales tax when it covers the collector's cost, this is not a direct tax (Choice C). And since (1) Congress has not granted the collector immunity, (2) the tax is nondiscriminatory, and (3) there is no indication that it imposes a prohibitively high tax burden, the state should not be enjoined from collecting this tax. (Choice B) Federal courts do not have exclusive jurisdiction over suits arising under federal law (as seen here) since state courts also have jurisdiction over such suits. (Choice D) The supremacy clause also allows federal law to preempt conflicting state law. But there is no direct conflict here since the antique collector can pay the sales tax without violating federal law. And there is no indirect conflict since the collector can still acquire antiques to accomplish the federal program's purpose.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because as the purchaser of the antiques, the collector, rather than the federal government, is liable for the tax."},"questionZh":"美国前总统的童年故居是位于城市的国家公园的一部分。国家公园管理局与一位独立古董收藏家签订了一份合同，收购前总统在世期间该市居民拥有的物品。根据合同，收藏家购买物品，然后以收藏家成本加上 10% 的佣金的价格出售给公园管理局。古董收藏家的购买通常需要缴纳该城市所在州的销售税。收税员已向州法院提起诉讼，要求禁止对公园管理局的这些购买商品征收税款，声称适用于他们的销售税是违宪的。州法院是否应该发布禁令？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为作为古董的购买者，收藏家而不是联邦政府，需要缴纳税款。","B":"不可以，因为该诉讼属于联邦法院的专属管辖范围。","C":"是的，因为根据合同，当政府承担古董收藏家的费用时，联邦政府有义务支付销售税。","D":"是的，因为根据至上条款，购买古董的联邦计划优先于购买这些物品的州销售税。"},"explanationZh":"根据第六条至上条款，联邦政府通常免受各州的直接征税。这是因为此类税收可能会阻碍或破坏联邦政府的运作。但各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构征税，包括受雇于联邦政府或与联邦政府有业务往来的个人或实体（例如承包商——如此处所示）。即使税收成本最终转嫁给联邦政府，情况也是如此。因此，附属公司必须缴纳这些税款，除非：\n• 国会已授予附属机构豁免权\n\n• 税收歧视联邦政府或其附属机构，或\n\n• 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责——例如，当高税收负担使附属机构无法履行其联邦职能时。\n\n在这里，独立古董收藏家（联邦政府附属机构）在购买出售给国家公园管理局的物品时负责缴纳州销售税。尽管联邦政府在合同上有义务支付销售税，但它不是直接税（选项 C）。由于（1）国会没有授予征收者豁免权，（2）该税是非歧视性的，并且（3）没有迹象表明它施加了过高的税收负担，因此不应禁止国家征收该税。 （选择 B）联邦法院对根据联邦法律提起的诉讼（如此处所示）没有专属管辖权，因为州法院也对此类诉讼拥有管辖权。 （选项 D） 至上条款还允许联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律。但这里并没有直接的冲突，因为古董收藏家可以在不违反联邦法律的情况下缴纳销售税。并且不存在间接冲突，因为收藏家仍然可以购买古董来实现联邦计划的目的。\n\n教育目标：各州可以对联邦政府附属机构（例如雇员）征税，除非 (1) 国会授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。\n\n参考文献\n• Alabama v. King & Boozer, 314 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1941)（主张州可以向联邦承包商征税，即使税款转嫁给联邦政府）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"各州可以对联邦政府附属机构（例如雇员）征税，除非 (1) 国会授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。","iracZh":{"issue":"州法院是否应该发布禁令？","rule":"各州可以对联邦政府附属机构（例如雇员）征税，除非 (1) 国会授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。","analysis":"根据第六条至上条款，联邦政府通常免受各州的直接征税。这是因为此类税收可能会阻碍或破坏联邦政府的运作。但各州可以对联邦政府的附属机构征税，包括受雇于联邦政府或与联邦政府有业务往来的个人或实体（例如承包商——如此处所示）。即使税收成本最终转嫁给联邦政府，情况也是如此。因此，附属公司必须缴纳这些税款，除非：\n• 国会已授予附属机构豁免权\n\n• 税收歧视联邦政府或其附属机构，或\n\n• 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责——例如，当高税收负担使附属机构无法履行其联邦职能时。\n\n在这里，独立古董收藏家（联邦政府附属机构）在购买出售给国家公园管理局的物品时负责缴纳州销售税。尽管联邦政府在合同上有义务支付销售税，但它不是直接税（选项 C）。由于（1）国会没有授予征收者豁免权，（2）该税是非歧视性的，并且（3）没有迹象表明它施加了过高的税收负担，因此不应禁止国家征收该税。 （选择 B）联邦法院对根据联邦法律提起的诉讼（如此处所示）没有专属管辖权，因为州法院也对此类诉讼拥有管辖权。 （选项 D） 至上条款还允许联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律。但这里并没有直接的冲突，因为古董收藏家可以在不违反联邦法律的情况下缴纳销售税。并且不存在间接冲突，因为收藏家仍然可以购买古董来实现联邦计划的目的。\n\n教育目标：各州可以对联邦政府附属机构（例如雇员）征税，除非 (1) 国会授予附属机构豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。\n\n参考文献\n• Alabama v. King & Boozer, 314 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1941)（主张州可以向联邦承包商征税，即使税款转嫁给联邦政府）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为作为古董的购买者，收藏家而不是联邦政府，需要缴纳税款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contractually","ipa":"/kʌntɹˈæktʃuəli/","syllables":"con·trac·tu·ally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 合约地；合同性地；契约规定地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 合约地；合同性地；契约规定地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the federal government is contractually obligated to pay the amount of the sales tax when the government covers the collector's cost of the antiques.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"contractors","ipa":"/kˈɑntɹˌæktɝz/","syllables":"con·trac·tors","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. （建筑、监造中的）承包人( contractor的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. （建筑、监造中的）承包人( contractor的复数形式 )","wordForm":"名词；原形 contractor；词形 0:contractor/1:s","sourceSentence":"But states can tax the federal government's affiliates, including persons or entities employed by or doing business with the federal government (eg, contractors—as seen here).","synonyms":["contractile organ","declarer"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-4d098859ba1d4085"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":35,"sourceQuestionPage":105,"sourceQuestionPages":[105],"sourceExplanationPages":[106,107],"sourceAnswerPages":[106,107],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-036","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Foreign Affairs & War Powers","question":"In response to massive layoffs of employees of automobile assembly plants located in State A, the state legislature enacted a statute which prohibits the parking of automobiles manufactured outside of the United States in any parking lot or parking structure located within State A. This statute does not apply to parking on public streets. Which of the following is the strongest argument with which to challenge the constitutionality of this statute?","choices":{"A":"The statute denies the owners of foreign-made automobiles the equal protection of the laws.","B":"The statute deprives the owners of foreign-made automobiles of liberty or property without due process of law.","C":"The statute imposes an undue burden on foreign commerce.","D":"The statute is inconsistent with the privileges or immunities clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The commerce clause gives Congress extensive authority to regulate interstate and foreign commerce. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate or foreign commerce. Therefore, state laws that discriminate against interstate commerce by favoring in-state over out-of-state interests are invalid unless:\n• the law furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest (eg, public health) and\n• no reasonable alternative exists to achieve that interest. State laws that discriminate against foreign commerce are subject to even greater scrutiny due to the federal government's substantial interest in speaking with one voice in foreign affairs. Here, the state statute discriminates against foreign commerce by preventing owners of foreign-made automobiles from parking in lots or structures in the state. This is to encourage consumers to buy domestic-made automobiles, many of which are manufactured in State A. And since the statute furthers an economic state interest, it unduly burdens foreign commerce. Therefore, this is the strongest argument to challenge the statute's constitutionality. (Choices A & B) Discriminatory laws implicate the constitutional guarantee of equal protection of the laws. And a law cannot deprive persons of a life, liberty, or property interest without due process of law. But such laws are almost always upheld when they do not impact a fundamental right or protected class (as seen here) since they need only pass rational basis scrutiny.\n\n(Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause bars states from depriving individuals of the very limited rights of national citizenship—none of which apply here.\n\nEducational objective: State laws that discriminate against foreign commerce are reviewed with the utmost scrutiny and are almost always invalid since it is imperative that the country speak with one voice in foreign affairs.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• South-Central Timber Dev., Inc. v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 99–100 (1984) (explaining that state discrimination against foreign commerce is subject to greater scrutiny than state discrimination against interstate commerce).","educationalObjective":"State laws that discriminate against foreign commerce are reviewed with the utmost scrutiny and are almost always invalid since it is imperative that the country speak with one voice in foreign affairs.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• South-Central Timber Dev., Inc. v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 99–100 (1984) (explaining that state discrimination against foreign commerce is subject to greater scrutiny than state discrimination against interstate commerce).","rule":"State laws that discriminate against foreign commerce are reviewed with the utmost scrutiny and are almost always invalid since it is imperative that the country speak with one voice in foreign affairs.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest argument with which to challenge the constitutionality of this statute?","rule":"State laws that discriminate against foreign commerce are reviewed with the utmost scrutiny and are almost always invalid since it is imperative that the country speak with one voice in foreign affairs.","analysis":"Dormant commerce clause\n\n(state regulation of interstate commerce)\n\nType of regulation Examples Standard\n\nDiscriminatory\n• Taxing out-of-state products Unconstitutional unless: more heavily (favors in-state\n• furthers legitimate\n• Requiring use of in-state interests) state interest and products/services\n• no reasonable alternative Nondiscriminatory\n• Prohibiting all commercial Unconstitutional if: vehicles on state highways (otherwise\n• burden clearly\n• Overly strict requirements burdensome) exceeds local for all commercial shipments benefits The commerce clause gives Congress extensive authority to regulate interstate and foreign commerce. The negative implication of this clause (ie, the dormant commerce clause) prohibits states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate or foreign commerce. Therefore, state laws that discriminate against interstate commerce by favoring in-state over out-of-state interests are invalid unless:\n• the law furthers a legitimate, noneconomic state interest (eg, public health) and\n• no reasonable alternative exists to achieve that interest. State laws that discriminate against foreign commerce are subject to even greater scrutiny due to the federal government's substantial interest in speaking with one voice in foreign affairs. Here, the state statute discriminates against foreign commerce by preventing owners of foreign-made automobiles from parking in lots or structures in the state. This is to encourage consumers to buy domestic-made automobiles, many of which are manufactured in State A. And since the statute furthers an economic state interest, it unduly burdens foreign commerce. Therefore, this is the strongest argument to challenge the statute's constitutionality. (Choices A & B) Discriminatory laws implicate the constitutional guarantee of equal protection of the laws. And a law cannot deprive persons of a life, liberty, or property interest without due process of law. But such laws are almost always upheld when they do not impact a fundamental right or protected class (as seen here) since they need only pass rational basis scrutiny.\n\n(Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause bars states from depriving individuals of the very limited rights of national citizenship—none of which apply here.","conclusion":"Answer C: The statute imposes an undue burden on foreign commerce."},"questionZh":"针对A州汽车装配厂大规模裁员的情况，州立法机关颁布了一项法规，禁止在A州境内的任何停车场或停车设施内停放美国境外制造的汽车。该法规不适用于公共街道上的停车。以下哪一项是质疑该法规合宪性的最有力论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法规剥夺了外国制造汽车的车主受到法律的平等保护。","B":"该法规未经正当法律程序就剥夺了外国制造汽车车主的自由或财产。","C":"该法规给对外贸易带来了不适当的负担。","D":"该法规与第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款不一致。"},"explanationZh":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（负担过重）超出了所有商业运输利益的本地要求 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际和外国商业。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是禁止各州歧视州际或外国商业或以其他方式对州际或外国商业造成不当负担。因此，通过偏袒州内利益而非州外利益而歧视州际贸易的州法律无效，除非：\n• 法律促进合法的、非经济的国家利益（例如公共卫生）并且\n• 不存在实现该利益的合理替代方案。由于联邦政府在外交事务中以统一的声音发言的重大利益，歧视外国商业的州法律受到更严格的审查。在这里，该州法规禁止外国制造汽车的车主将其停放在该州的停车场或建筑物内，从而歧视外国商业。这是为了鼓励消费者购买国产汽车，其中许多汽车是在 A 国制造的。由于该法规促进了国家经济利益，因此过度加重了外国商业的负担。因此，这是质疑该法规合宪性的最有力论据。 （选项 A 和 B） 歧视性法律意味着宪法对法律的平等保护的保障。未经正当法律程序，法律不得剥夺人的生命、自由或财产利益。但当这些法律不影响基本权利或受保护阶层（如此处所示）时，它们几乎总是会得到维护，因为它们只需要通过合理基础的审查。\n\n（选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州剥夺个人非常有限的国家公民权利——这些权利都不适用。\n\n教育目标：歧视外国商业的国家法律经过最严格的审查，并且几乎总是无效的，因为国家在外交事务中必须发出一个声音。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• South-Central Timber Dev., Inc. v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 99–100 (1984)（解释说国家对外国商业的歧视比国家对州际商业的歧视受到更严格的审查）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"歧视外国商业的国家法律都经过最严格的审查，并且几乎总是无效的，因为国家必须在外交事务上用一个声音说话。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是质疑该法规合宪性的最有力论据？","rule":"歧视外国商业的国家法律都经过最严格的审查，并且几乎总是无效的，因为国家必须在外交事务上用一个声音说话。","analysis":"消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）\n\n（州际贸易的州监管）\n\n监管类型 示例 标准\n\n歧视性的\n• 对州外产品征税违宪，除非： 更严厉（有利于州内产品）\n• 进一步合法化\n• 要求使用州内利益）州内利益和产品/服务\n• 没有合理的替代方案 非歧视\n• 禁止所有商业违宪，如果： 车辆在国道上行驶（否则\n• 负担明确\n• 过于严格的要求（负担过重）超出了所有商业运输利益的本地要求 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际和外国商业。该条款（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause））的负面含义是禁止各州歧视州际或外国商业或以其他方式对州际或外国商业造成不当负担。因此，通过偏袒州内利益而非州外利益而歧视州际贸易的州法律无效，除非：\n• 法律促进合法的、非经济的国家利益（例如公共卫生）并且\n• 不存在实现该利益的合理替代方案。由于联邦政府在外交事务中以统一的声音发言的重大利益，歧视外国商业的州法律受到更严格的审查。在这里，该州法规禁止外国制造汽车的车主将其停放在该州的停车场或建筑物内，从而歧视外国商业。这是为了鼓励消费者购买国产汽车，其中许多汽车是在 A 国制造的。由于该法规促进了国家经济利益，因此过度加重了外国商业的负担。因此，这是质疑该法规合宪性的最有力论据。 （选项 A 和 B） 歧视性法律意味着宪法对法律的平等保护的保障。未经正当法律程序，法律不得剥夺人的生命、自由或财产利益。但当这些法律不影响基本权利或受保护阶层（如此处所示）时，它们几乎总是会得到维护，因为它们只需要通过合理基础的审查。\n\n（选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州剥夺个人非常有限的国家公民权利——这些权利都不适用。\n\n教育目标：歧视外国商业的国家法律经过最严格的审查，并且几乎总是无效的，因为国家在外交事务中必须发出一个声音。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• South-Central Timber Dev., Inc. v. Wunnicke, 467 U.S. 82, 99–100 (1984)（解释说国家对外国商业的歧视比国家对州际商业的歧视受到更严格的审查）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该法规给对外贸易带来了不适当的负担。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":36,"sourceQuestionPage":108,"sourceQuestionPages":[108],"sourceExplanationPages":[109,110],"sourceAnswerPages":[109,110],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-037","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"A man contracted with a moving company to ship household goods from the man's old home in State A to his new home in State B. A federal statute provides that all liability of an interstate mover to a shipper for loss of or damage to the shipper's goods in transit is governed exclusively by the contract between them. The statute also requires the mover to offer a shipper at least two contracts with different levels of liability. In full compliance with that federal statute, the moving company offered the man a choice between two shipping agreements that provided different levels of liability on the part of the moving company. The more expensive contract provided that the moving company was fully liable in case of loss or damage. The less expensive contract limited the moving company's liability in case of loss or damage to less than full value. The man voluntarily signed the less expensive contract, fixing the moving company's liability at less than the full value of the shipment. The moving company's truck was involved in an accident in State B. The accident was entirely a product of the negligence of the moving company's driver. The man's household goods were destroyed. In accordance with the contract, the moving company reimbursed the man for less than the full value of the goods. The man then brought suit against the moving company under the tort law of State B claiming that he was entitled to be reimbursed for the full value of the destroyed goods. The moving company filed a motion to dismiss. In this suit, what action should the court take?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion to dismiss, because it is unconstitutional for a federal statute to authorize the moving company to contract out of any degree of liability for its own negligence.","B":"Deny the motion to dismiss, because the full faith and credit clause of the Constitution requires that state tort law be given effect.","C":"Dismiss the case, because the contractual relationship between the man and the moving company is governed by the obligation of contracts clause of the Constitution.","D":"Dismiss the case, because the federal statute governing liability of interstate carriers is the supreme law of the land and preempts state tort law."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The federal government and states share many powers, so they often regulate the same subject matter. But under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. As a result, they preempt (ie, supersede) conflicting state laws, state constitutions, and local laws. Here, the man and the moving company entered into a contract that limited the moving company's liability. The man then sued under state tort law to hold the moving company fully liable. But a ruling in the man's favor would conflict with a federal statute requiring that such disputes be governed exclusively by their contract. And since the federal statute is the supreme law of the land and preempts state tort law, the court should dismiss the case. (Choice A) The federal statute can authorize the moving company to contract out of any degree of liability for its own negligence. This is because the commerce clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate nearly any activity involving two or more states— including an interstate mover's degree of liability. (Choice B) The Article IV full faith and credit clause requires states to respect public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of all other states. But it does not require courts to give effect to state laws that conflict with federal law (as seen here). (Choice C) The contracts clause restricts the ability of state legislatures to impair existing contracts. But this clause does not apply to Congress or federal statutes that enforce existing contracts (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state laws, state constitutions, and local laws.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• Allis-Chalmers Corp. v. Lueck, 471 U.S. 202, 210–11 (1985) (holding that a federal law governing contracts preempts a conflicting state law on the same subject).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 235 (2019) (discussing tests to determine if there is a conflict between federal and state law).","educationalObjective":"Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state laws, state constitutions, and local laws.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• Allis-Chalmers Corp. v. Lueck, 471 U.S. 202, 210–11 (1985) (holding that a federal law governing contracts preempts a conflicting state law on the same subject).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 235 (2019) (discussing tests to determine if there is a conflict between federal and state law).","rule":"Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state laws, state constitutions, and local laws.","irac":{"issue":"In this suit, what action should the court take?","rule":"Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state laws, state constitutions, and local laws.","analysis":"The federal government and states share many powers, so they often regulate the same subject matter. But under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. As a result, they preempt (ie, supersede) conflicting state laws, state constitutions, and local laws. Here, the man and the moving company entered into a contract that limited the moving company's liability. The man then sued under state tort law to hold the moving company fully liable. But a ruling in the man's favor would conflict with a federal statute requiring that such disputes be governed exclusively by their contract. And since the federal statute is the supreme law of the land and preempts state tort law, the court should dismiss the case. (Choice A) The federal statute can authorize the moving company to contract out of any degree of liability for its own negligence. This is because the commerce clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate nearly any activity involving two or more states— including an interstate mover's degree of liability. (Choice B) The Article IV full faith and credit clause requires states to respect public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of all other states. But it does not require courts to give effect to state laws that conflict with federal law (as seen here). (Choice C) The contracts clause restricts the ability of state legislatures to impair existing contracts. But this clause does not apply to Congress or federal statutes that enforce existing contracts (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Dismiss the case, because the federal statute governing liability of interstate carriers is the supreme law of the land and preempts state tort law."},"questionZh":"一名男子与一家搬家公司签订合同，将家庭用品从该男子在 A 州的旧家运送到他在 B 州的新家。联邦法规规定，州际搬家公司对托运人在运输途中的货物丢失或损坏所承担的所有责任仅受他们之间的合同管辖。该法规还要求搬家公司向托运人提供至少两份具有不同责任级别的合同。搬家公司完全遵守该联邦法规，为该男子提供了两种运输协议之间的选择，这两种运输协议规定了搬家公司不同程度的责任。更昂贵的合同规定，搬家公司对丢失或损坏承担全部责任。较便宜的合同将搬家公司在丢失或损坏时的责任限制为低于全部价值。该男子自愿签署了较便宜的合同，将搬家公司的责任确定为低于货物的全部价值。搬家公司的卡车在B州发生了事故，这起事故完全是搬家公司司机疏忽造成的。该男子的家庭用品被毁。按照合同规定，搬家公司向男子赔偿的金额低于货物的全部价值。该男子随后根据B国侵权法对搬家公司提起诉讼，声称他有权获得被毁坏货物的全部价值赔偿。搬家公司提出驳回动议。在这起诉讼中，法院应该采取什么行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回驳回动议，因为联邦法规授权搬家公司签订合同免除其自身疏忽的任何程度的责任，这是违宪的。","B":"驳回驳回动议，因为宪法的完整信用条款要求州侵权法生效。","C":"驳回此案，因为该男子与搬家公司之间的合同关系受宪法合同义务条款管辖。","D":"驳回此案，因为管辖州际承运人责任的联邦法规是该国的最高法律，并优先于州侵权法。"},"explanationZh":"联邦政府和各州共享许多权力，因此它们经常监管相同的主题。但根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约是该国的最高法律。因此，它们优先于（即取代）相互冲突的州法律、州宪法和地方法律。在此，男子与搬家公司签订了一份限制搬家公司责任的合同。该男子随后根据州侵权法提起诉讼，要求搬家公司承担全部责任。但对男子有利的裁决将与联邦法规相冲突，该法规要求此类纠纷仅受他们的合同管辖。由于联邦法规是该国的最高法律并优先于州侵权法，因此法院应该驳回此案。 （选择A）联邦法规可以授权搬家公司签订合同，免除其自身疏忽的任何程度的责任。这是因为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管几乎所有涉及两个或多个州的活动，包括州际搬家者的责任程度。 （选项B） 第四条完全诚信条款要求各国尊重所有其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。但它并不要求法院执行与联邦法律相冲突的州法律（如此处所示）。 （选项 C） 合同条款限制州立法机关损害现有合同的能力。但该条款不适用于执行现有合同的国会或联邦法规（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相冲突的州法律、州宪法和地方法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。六、六类。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• Allis-Chalmers Corp. v. Lueck, 471 U.S. 202, 210–11 (1985)（认为管理合同的联邦法律优先于同一主题的冲突州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 235 条 (2019)（讨论确定联邦法律和州法律之间是否存在冲突的测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相冲突的州法律、州宪法和地方法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起诉讼中，法院应该采取什么行动？","rule":"根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相冲突的州法律、州宪法和地方法律。","analysis":"联邦政府和各州共享许多权力，因此它们经常监管相同的主题。但根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约是该国的最高法律。因此，它们优先于（即取代）相互冲突的州法律、州宪法和地方法律。在此，男子与搬家公司签订了一份限制搬家公司责任的合同。该男子随后根据州侵权法提起诉讼，要求搬家公司承担全部责任。但对男子有利的裁决将与联邦法规相冲突，该法规要求此类纠纷仅受他们的合同管辖。由于联邦法规是该国的最高法律并优先于州侵权法，因此法院应该驳回此案。 （选择A）联邦法规可以授权搬家公司签订合同，免除其自身疏忽的任何程度的责任。这是因为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管几乎所有涉及两个或多个州的活动，包括州际搬家者的责任程度。 （选项B） 第四条完全诚信条款要求各国尊重所有其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。但它并不要求法院执行与联邦法律相冲突的州法律（如此处所示）。 （选项 C） 合同条款限制州立法机关损害现有合同的能力。但该条款不适用于执行现有合同的国会或联邦法规（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相冲突的州法律、州宪法和地方法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。六、六类。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• Allis-Chalmers Corp. v. Lueck, 471 U.S. 202, 210–11 (1985)（认为管理合同的联邦法律优先于同一主题的冲突州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 235 条 (2019)（讨论确定联邦法律和州法律之间是否存在冲突的测试）。","conclusion":"答案 D：驳回此案，因为管辖州际承运人责任的联邦法规是该国的最高法律，并优先于州侵权法。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contractual","ipa":"/kʌntɹˈæktʃuəl/","syllables":"con·trac·tual","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 契约的；[经] 契约的, 契约性的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 契约的；[经] 契约的, 契约性的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Dismiss the case, because the contractual relationship between the man and the moving company is governed by the obligation of contracts clause of the Constitution.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"reimbursed","ipa":"/ɹˌiɪmbˈɝst/","syllables":"re·im·bursed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 偿还, 付还( reimburse的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 偿还, 付还( reimburse的过去式和过去分词 )","wordForm":"词；原形 reimburse；词形 0:reimburse/1:dp/d:reimbursed/p:reimbursed","sourceSentence":"In accordance with the contract, the moving company reimbursed the man for less than the full value of the goods.","synonyms":["recoup"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-391fcc2fa8798419"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":37,"sourceQuestionPage":111,"sourceQuestionPages":[111],"sourceExplanationPages":[112,113],"sourceAnswerPages":[112,113],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-038","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"A federal statute provides that the cities in which certain specified airports are located may regulate the rates and services of all limousines that serve those airports, without regard to the origin or destination of the passengers who use the limousines. A city in State A is located adjacent to a city in State B. The airport serving both of them is located in the State A city and is one of those airports specified in the federal statute. The State A city council has adopted a rule that requires any limousines serving the airport to charge only the rates authorized by the State A city council. An airline limousine service has a lucrative business transporting passengers between the State B city and the airport in the State A city, at much lower rates than those required by the State A city council. It transports passengers in interstate traffic only; it does not provide local service within the State A city. The new rule adopted by the State A city council will require the limousine service to charge the same rates as limousines operating only in the State A city. Must the airline limousine service comply with the new rule of the State A city council?","choices":{"A":"No, because the airline limousine service is engaged in interstate commerce and this rule is an undue burden on that commerce.","B":"No, because the rule would arbitrarily destroy a lucrative existing business and, therefore, would amount to a taking without just compensation.","C":"Yes, because Congress has authorized this form of regulation by the State A city and, therefore, removed any constitutional impediments to it that may have otherwise existed.","D":"Yes, because the airport is located in the State A city and, therefore, its city council has exclusive regulatory authority over all transportation to and from the airport."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. This clause also carries a negative implication (ie, the dormant commerce clause) that prohibits states and municipalities from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But Congress can exercise its broad commerce power to explicitly authorize conduct that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause. Here, the State A city council rule requires all airport limousine services to charge only the rates authorized by the city council. It can be presumed that this rule unduly burdens interstate commerce because it regulates economic activities between State A and State B. But since Congress explicitly authorized this type of regulation by the State A city (via the federal statute), any constitutional impediments that may have existed were removed (Choice A). As a result, the interstate limousine service must comply with the State A city rule. (Choice B) A regulation that destroys all economic value of a business will be deemed a taking. Here, there is no evidence that the interstate limousine service's business will be destroyed by the higher rates, so the State A city council rule is not a taking. And even if it was, this would only allow the limousine service to seek just compensation (ie, fair market value at the time of the taking)—not to ignore the rule. (Choice D) The city council's regulatory authority over transportation to and from the airport located in the city is not exclusive. Instead, this authority is shared with State A and the federal government.\n\nEducational objective: Congress can explicitly authorize states and municipalities to act in ways that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Ne. Bancorp v. Bd. of Governors of Fed. Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985) (setting forth that states can discriminate against interstate commerce when Congress authorizes such action).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 27 (2019) (explaining that Congress can authorize state action that would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause).","educationalObjective":"Congress can explicitly authorize states and municipalities to act in ways that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 3 (commerce clause).\n\n• Ne. Bancorp v. Bd. of Governors of Fed. Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985) (setting forth that states can discriminate against interstate commerce when Congress authorizes such action).\n\n• 15A Am. Jur. 2d Commerce § 27 (2019) (explaining that Congress can authorize state action that would ordinarily violate the dormant commerce clause).","rule":"Congress can explicitly authorize states and municipalities to act in ways that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.","irac":{"issue":"Must the airline limousine service comply with the new rule of the State A city council?","rule":"Congress can explicitly authorize states and municipalities to act in ways that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause.","analysis":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. This clause also carries a negative implication (ie, the dormant commerce clause) that prohibits states and municipalities from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But Congress can exercise its broad commerce power to explicitly authorize conduct that would otherwise violate the dormant commerce clause. Here, the State A city council rule requires all airport limousine services to charge only the rates authorized by the city council. It can be presumed that this rule unduly burdens interstate commerce because it regulates economic activities between State A and State B. But since Congress explicitly authorized this type of regulation by the State A city (via the federal statute), any constitutional impediments that may have existed were removed (Choice A). As a result, the interstate limousine service must comply with the State A city rule. (Choice B) A regulation that destroys all economic value of a business will be deemed a taking. Here, there is no evidence that the interstate limousine service's business will be destroyed by the higher rates, so the State A city council rule is not a taking. And even if it was, this would only allow the limousine service to seek just compensation (ie, fair market value at the time of the taking)—not to ignore the rule. (Choice D) The city council's regulatory authority over transportation to and from the airport located in the city is not exclusive. Instead, this authority is shared with State A and the federal government.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because Congress has authorized this form of regulation by the State A city and, therefore, removed any constitutional impediments to it that may have otherwise existed."},"questionZh":"联邦法规规定，某些特定机场所在的城市可以规范为这些机场提供服务的所有豪华轿车的费率和服务，而不考虑使用豪华轿车的乘客的出发地或目的地。 A 州的一个城市与 B 州的一个城市相邻。为这两个城市提供服务的机场都位于 A 州的城市，并且是联邦法规规定的机场之一。 A 州市议会通过了一项规定，要求为机场提供服务的任何豪华轿车仅按 A 州市议会批准的费率收费。航空公司豪华轿车服务在 B 州城市和 A 州城市机场之间运送乘客是一项利润丰厚的业务，其价格远低于 A 州市议会要求的价格。它仅在州际交通中运送乘客；不提供A州城市内的本地服务。 A州市议会通过的新规则将要求豪华轿车服务收取与仅在A州城市运营的豪华轿车相同的费率。航空豪华轿车服务必须遵守A州市议会的新规定吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为航空公司豪华轿车服务从事州际贸易，而该规则对该贸易造成了不应有的负担。","B":"不可以，因为该规则会任意破坏利润丰厚的现有业务，因此相当于在没有公正补偿的情况下进行夺取。","C":"是的，因为国会已授权 A 州城市实施这种形式的监管，因此消除了可能存在的任何宪法障碍。","D":"是的，因为机场位于 A 州城市，因此其市议会对所有往返机场的交通拥有专属监管权。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业，即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。该条款还具有负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）），禁止州和市政府歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。但国会可以行使其广泛的商业权力，明确授权违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的行为。在这里，A 州市议会的规定要求所有机场豪华轿车服务仅按市议会批准的价格收费。可以推测，该规则过度加重了州际贸易的负担，因为它规范 A 州和 B 州之间的经济活动。但由于国会明确授权 A 州城市（通过联邦法规）进行此类监管，因此消除了可能存在的任何宪法障碍（选项 A）。因此，州际豪华轿车服务必须遵守 A 州城市的规定。 （选择 B）破坏企业所有经济价值的法规将被视为征收。在这里，没有证据表明州际豪华轿车服务的业务会因较高的费率而受到破坏，因此 A 州市议会的规定并不是一种征收。即使是这样，这也只能让豪华轿车服务寻求公正的补偿（即拿走时的公平市场价值），而不是忽视规则。 （选项 D） 市议会对往返城市机场的交通的监管权不是排他性的。相反，该权力是与 A 州和联邦政府共享的。\n\n教育目标：国会可以明确授权各州和市政府以违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的方式行事。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 氖。 Bancorp 诉 Bd.美联储理事。 Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985)（规定当国会授权时，各州可以歧视州际贸易）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 27 (2019)（解释国会可以授权通常违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的国家行为（state action））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会可以明确授权各州和市政府以违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的方式行事。","iracZh":{"issue":"航空豪华轿车服务必须遵守A州市议会的新规定吗？","rule":"国会可以明确授权各州和市政府以违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的方式行事。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管州际商业，即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。该条款还具有负面含义（即消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）），禁止州和市政府歧视州际商业或以其他方式对州际商业造成不当负担。但国会可以行使其广泛的商业权力，明确授权违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的行为。在这里，A 州市议会的规定要求所有机场豪华轿车服务仅按市议会批准的价格收费。可以推测，该规则过度加重了州际贸易的负担，因为它规范 A 州和 B 州之间的经济活动。但由于国会明确授权 A 州城市（通过联邦法规）进行此类监管，因此消除了可能存在的任何宪法障碍（选项 A）。因此，州际豪华轿车服务必须遵守 A 州城市的规定。 （选择 B）破坏企业所有经济价值的法规将被视为征收。在这里，没有证据表明州际豪华轿车服务的业务会因较高的费率而受到破坏，因此 A 州市议会的规定并不是一种征收。即使是这样，这也只能让豪华轿车服务寻求公正的补偿（即拿走时的公平市场价值），而不是忽视规则。 （选项 D） 市议会对往返城市机场的交通的监管权不是排他性的。相反，该权力是与 A 州和联邦政府共享的。\n\n教育目标：国会可以明确授权各州和市政府以违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的方式行事。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 3（商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause））。\n\n• 氖。 Bancorp 诉 Bd.美联储理事。 Reserve Sys., 472 U.S. 159, 174 (1985)（规定当国会授权时，各州可以歧视州际贸易）。\n\n• 15A 上午。尤尔。 2d Commerce § 27 (2019)（解释国会可以授权通常违反消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）的国家行为（state action））。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为国会已授权 A 州城市实施这种形式的监管，因此消除了可能存在的任何宪法障碍。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"just compensation","ipa":"/dʒˈəst kˌɑmpʌnsˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"just com·pen·sa·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公平补偿：政府征收私人财产时应支付的公平市场价值","contextualMeaning":"公平补偿：政府征收私人财产时应支付的公平市场价值","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the rule would arbitrarily destroy a lucrative existing business and, therefore, would amount to a taking without just compensation.","synonyms":["just recompense"]},{"term":"taking","ipa":"/tˈeɪkɪŋ/","syllables":"tak·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 取得, 捕获, 进款；a. 迷人的, 可爱的, 会传染的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 取得, 捕获, 进款；a. 迷人的, 可爱的, 会传染的","wordForm":"词；词形 0:take/1:i/s:takings/i:taking","sourceSentence":"No, because the rule would arbitrarily destroy a lucrative existing business and, therefore, would amount to a taking without just compensation.","synonyms":["pickings","fetching","winning"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-92ecbf48937cf0df"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":38,"sourceQuestionPage":114,"sourceQuestionPages":[114],"sourceExplanationPages":[115,116],"sourceAnswerPages":[115,116],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-039","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"The United States Department of the Interior granted a man the food and drink concession in a federal park located in a state. The man operated his concession out of federally owned facilities in the park. The federal statute authorizing the Interior Department to grant such concessions provided that the grantees would pay only a nominal rental for use of these federal facilities because of the great benefit their concessions would provide to the people of the United States. The state legislature enacted a statute imposing an occupancy tax on the occupants of real estate within that state that is not subject to state real estate taxes. The statute was intended to equalize the state tax burden on such occupants with that on people occupying real estate that is subject to state real estate taxes. Pursuant to that statute, the state's Department of Revenue attempted to collect the state occupancy tax from the man because the federal facilities occupied by the man were not subject to state real estate taxes. The man sued to invalidate the state occupancy tax as applied to him. What is the strongest constitutional ground upon which the man could challenge the occupancy tax?","choices":{"A":"The tax violates the commerce clause by unduly burdening the interstate tourist trade.","B":"The tax violates the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment because the tax treats him less favorably than federal concessionaires in other states who do not have to pay such occupancy taxes.","C":"The tax violates the privileges or immunities clause of the Fourteenth Amendment by interfering with the fundamental right to do business on federal property.","D":"The tax violates the supremacy clause of Article VI and the federal statute authorizing such concessions."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations\n\nTax/regulation Definition Permissibility\n\nDirect Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities\n• Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal\n• Congress grants immunity or government\n• tax/regulation discriminates against or substantially interferes with federal duties The supremacy clause immunizes the federal government, including its agencies and instrumentalities, from state and municipal taxation unless Congress consents. But the federal government's affiliates—ie, persons or entities employed by or doing business with the federal government—are only immune if:\n• Congress grants the affiliate immunity\n• the tax discriminates against the federal government or its affiliates or\n• the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties. Here, the state attempted to collect an occupancy tax from the man—an affiliate of the federal government who was granted the food and drink concession in a federal park. The federal statute authorizing this grant provided that the man would only pay nominal rent due to the great public benefit provided by concessions. And since the state tax may substantially interfere with the man's federal purpose or duties by raising his costs, his strongest argument is that the tax violates the supremacy clause and the federal statute as applied to him. (Choice A) Under the dormant commerce clause, state taxes are invalid if they unduly burden interstate commerce. But the state tax here does not unduly burden the interstate tourist trade because it (1) applies to activities with a substantial nexus to the state, (2) is fairly apportioned, (3) does not discriminate against interstate commerce, and (4) is fairly related to the services and benefits provided by the state. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause is triggered when a state treats similarly situated persons differently. But state laws that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are almost always upheld under mere rational basis scrutiny.\n\n(Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause bars states from interfering with the very limited rights of national citizenship—which do not include a right to do business on federal property.\n\nEducational objective: The federal government's affiliates are subject to state and municipal taxation unless (1) Congress grants them immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with their federal purpose or duties.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819) (establishing federal immunity from state taxation and regulation).\n\n• United States v. New Mexico, 455 U.S. 720, 735 n.11 (1982) (explaining that state taxes on affiliates of the federal government must be nondiscriminatory and cannot substantially interfere with its activities).","educationalObjective":"The federal government's affiliates are subject to state and municipal taxation unless (1) Congress grants them immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with their federal purpose or duties.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819) (establishing federal immunity from state taxation and regulation).\n\n• United States v. New Mexico, 455 U.S. 720, 735 n.11 (1982) (explaining that state taxes on affiliates of the federal government must be nondiscriminatory and cannot substantially interfere with its activities).","rule":"The federal government's affiliates are subject to state and municipal taxation unless (1) Congress grants them immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with their federal purpose or duties.","irac":{"issue":"What is the strongest constitutional ground upon which the man could challenge the occupancy tax?","rule":"The federal government's affiliates are subject to state and municipal taxation unless (1) Congress grants them immunity, (2) the tax is discriminatory, or (3) the tax substantially interferes with their federal purpose or duties.","analysis":"Federal immunity from state & local taxes/regulations\n\nTax/regulation Definition Permissibility\n\nDirect Applies to federal government— Immune unless: including its agencies & instrumentalities\n• Congress consents to tax/regulation Indirect Applies to affiliates—including Not immune unless: persons/entities employed by or doing business with federal\n• Congress grants immunity or government\n• tax/regulation discriminates against or substantially interferes with federal duties The supremacy clause immunizes the federal government, including its agencies and instrumentalities, from state and municipal taxation unless Congress consents. But the federal government's affiliates—ie, persons or entities employed by or doing business with the federal government—are only immune if:\n• Congress grants the affiliate immunity\n• the tax discriminates against the federal government or its affiliates or\n• the tax substantially interferes with the affiliate's federal purpose or duties. Here, the state attempted to collect an occupancy tax from the man—an affiliate of the federal government who was granted the food and drink concession in a federal park. The federal statute authorizing this grant provided that the man would only pay nominal rent due to the great public benefit provided by concessions. And since the state tax may substantially interfere with the man's federal purpose or duties by raising his costs, his strongest argument is that the tax violates the supremacy clause and the federal statute as applied to him. (Choice A) Under the dormant commerce clause, state taxes are invalid if they unduly burden interstate commerce. But the state tax here does not unduly burden the interstate tourist trade because it (1) applies to activities with a substantial nexus to the state, (2) is fairly apportioned, (3) does not discriminate against interstate commerce, and (4) is fairly related to the services and benefits provided by the state. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause is triggered when a state treats similarly situated persons differently. But state laws that do not substantially impact a fundamental right or intentionally discriminate against a protected class (as seen here) are almost always upheld under mere rational basis scrutiny.\n\n(Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause bars states from interfering with the very limited rights of national citizenship—which do not include a right to do business on federal property.","conclusion":"Answer D: The tax violates the supremacy clause of Article VI and the federal statute authorizing such concessions."},"questionZh":"美国内政部授予一名男子在某州联邦公园内的食品和饮料特许权。该男子在公园内的联邦政府拥有的设施内经营他的特许经营权。授权内政部授予此类特许权的联邦法规规定，受赠者只需支付象征性的租金即可使用这些联邦设施，因为他们的特许权将为美国人民带来巨大利益。州立法机关颁布了一项法规，对该州内不需缴纳州房地产税的房地产占用者征收占用税。该法规的目的是使这些居住者的州税负担与占用应缴纳国家房地产税的房地产的人的州税负担相等。根据该法规，该州税务局试图向该男子征收州占用税，因为该男子占用的联邦设施无需缴纳州房地产税。该男子提起诉讼，要求使适用于他的州占用税无效。该男子可以挑战占用税的最有力的宪法依据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该税收对州际旅游贸易造成过度负担，违反了商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。","B":"该税违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为该税对他的待遇不如其他州的联邦特许经营者，后者不必缴纳此类占用税。","C":"该税侵犯了在联邦财产上开展业务的基本权利，违反了第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款。","D":"该税收违反了第六条的至上条款以及授权此类优惠的联邦法规。"},"explanationZh":"联邦免受州和地方税收/法规的影响\n\n税收/法规 定义 许可\n\n直接适用于联邦政府——豁免，除非： 包括其机构和部门\n• 国会同意税收/监管 间接 适用于附属机构——包括 不豁免，除非： 受雇于联邦或与联邦有业务往来的个人/实体\n• 国会授予豁免权或政府\n• 税收/监管歧视或严重干扰联邦职责 至高无上条款使联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）免受州和市税收的影响，除非国会同意。但联邦政府的附属机构（即受雇于联邦政府或与联邦政府有业务往来的个人或实体）仅在以下情况下才享有豁免权：\n• 国会授予附属机构豁免权\n• 该税收歧视联邦政府或其附属机构，或\n• 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。在这里，州政府试图向这名男子征收占用税，这名男子是联邦政府的下属，获得了联邦公园的食品和饮料特许权。授权这项补助金的联邦法规规定，由于特许权提供了巨大的公共利益，该男子只需支付象征性的租金。由于州税可能会增加该人的成本，从而严重干扰该人的联邦目的或职责，因此他最有力的论点是，该税违反了适用于他的至上条款和联邦法规。 （选项 A）根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），如果州税过度加重州际贸易负担，则州税无效。但这里的州税不会对州际旅游贸易造成过度负担，因为它（1）适用于与州有重大联系的活动，（2）公平分配，（3）不歧视州际贸易，（4）与州提供的服务和福利公平相关。 （选项B）当一个州以不同方式对待处境相似的人时，就会触发第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但是，不会对基本权利产生实质性影响或故意歧视受保护群体（如此处所示）的州法律几乎总是在纯粹的合理基础审查下得到维护。\n\n（选项 C）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州干涉国民公民的非常有限的权利，其中不包括在联邦财产上开展业务的权利。\n\n教育目标：联邦政府的附属机构须缴纳州税和市税，除非 (1) 国会授予他们豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰其联邦目的或职责。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。六、六类。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819)（确立联邦免受州税收和监管的豁免）。\n\n• 美国诉新墨西哥州，455 U.S. 720, 735 n.11 (1982)（解释说，对联邦政府附属机构征收的州税必须是非歧视性的，并且不能实质性干扰其活动）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联邦政府的附属机构须缴纳州税和市税，除非 (1) 国会授予他们豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰其联邦目的或职责。","iracZh":{"issue":"该男子可以挑战占用税的最有力的宪法依据是什么？","rule":"联邦政府的附属机构须缴纳州税和市税，除非 (1) 国会授予他们豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰其联邦目的或职责。","analysis":"联邦免受州和地方税收/法规的影响\n\n税收/法规 定义 许可\n\n直接适用于联邦政府——豁免，除非： 包括其机构和部门\n• 国会同意税收/监管 间接 适用于附属机构——包括 不豁免，除非： 受雇于联邦或与联邦有业务往来的个人/实体\n• 国会授予豁免权或政府\n• 税收/监管歧视或严重干扰联邦职责 至高无上条款使联邦政府（包括其机构和部门）免受州和市税收的影响，除非国会同意。但联邦政府的附属机构（即受雇于联邦政府或与联邦政府有业务往来的个人或实体）仅在以下情况下才享有豁免权：\n• 国会授予附属机构豁免权\n• 该税收歧视联邦政府或其附属机构，或\n• 税收严重干扰附属机构的联邦目的或职责。在这里，州政府试图向这名男子征收占用税，这名男子是联邦政府的下属，获得了联邦公园的食品和饮料特许权。授权这项补助金的联邦法规规定，由于特许权提供了巨大的公共利益，该男子只需支付象征性的租金。由于州税可能会增加该人的成本，从而严重干扰该人的联邦目的或职责，因此他最有力的论点是，该税违反了适用于他的至上条款和联邦法规。 （选项 A）根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），如果州税过度加重州际贸易负担，则州税无效。但这里的州税不会对州际旅游贸易造成过度负担，因为它（1）适用于与州有重大联系的活动，（2）公平分配，（3）不歧视州际贸易，（4）与州提供的服务和福利公平相关。 （选项B）当一个州以不同方式对待处境相似的人时，就会触发第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但是，不会对基本权利产生实质性影响或故意歧视受保护群体（如此处所示）的州法律几乎总是在纯粹的合理基础审查下得到维护。\n\n（选项 C）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州干涉国民公民的非常有限的权利，其中不包括在联邦财产上开展业务的权利。\n\n教育目标：联邦政府的附属机构须缴纳州税和市税，除非 (1) 国会授予他们豁免权，(2) 税收具有歧视性，或 (3) 税收严重干扰其联邦目的或职责。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。六、六类。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316, 436 (1819)（确立联邦免受州税收和监管的豁免）。\n\n• 美国诉新墨西哥州，455 U.S. 720, 735 n.11 (1982)（解释说，对联邦政府附属机构征收的州税必须是非歧视性的，并且不能实质性干扰其活动）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该税收违反了第六条的至上条款以及授权此类优惠的联邦法规。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":39,"sourceQuestionPage":117,"sourceQuestionPages":[117],"sourceExplanationPages":[118,119],"sourceAnswerPages":[118,119],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-040","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"A state spends several million dollars a year on an oyster conservation program. As part of that program, the state limits, by statute, oyster fishing in its coastal waters to persons who have state oyster permits. In order to promote conservation, it issues only a limited number of oyster permits each year. The permits are effective for only one year from the date of their issuance and are awarded on the basis of a lottery, in which there is no differentiation between resident and nonresident applicants. However, each nonresident who obtains a permit is charged an annual permit fee that is $5 more than the fee charged residents. A large fishing company operates from a port in another state and is incorporated in that other state. Each of the company's boats has a federal shipping license that permits it \"to engage in all aspects of the coastal trade, to fish and to carry cargo from place to place along the coast, and to engage in other lawful activities along the coast of the United States.\" These shipping licenses are authorized by federal statute. Assume no other federal statutes or administrative rules apply. Although it had previously held a state oyster permit, the company did not obtain a permit in that state's lottery this year. Which of the following is the strongest argument that can be made in support of a continued right of the company to fish for oysters this year in the state's coastal waters?","choices":{"A":"The company has a right to fish for oysters in the state's waters despite the state law because it holds a federal shipping license.","B":"The refusal to grant the company a permit this year is a taking of its property without due process of law because the company previously held a state oyster permit and the state knows that the company is engaged in a continuing business operation.","C":"The state law denies the company the privileges and immunities of state citizenship because it provides higher permit charges for nonresidents.","D":"The state law is an undue burden on interstate commerce because it provides higher permit charges for nonresidents."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the supremacy clause, the provisions of the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. As a result, these provisions preempt conflicting provisions in state constitutions, state laws, and local laws—rendering such provisions void and without effect. Here, the fishing company possesses a shipping license authorized by a federal statute that permits it to fish in the state's waters. But the state statute prohibits the company from fishing since it lacks a state-issued permit. Since these statutes conflict, the company's strongest argument is that its federal shipping license gives it a right to fish in the state's waters despite the state law. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires a state to provide notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard to those it deprives of life, liberty, or property. But the company has no property interest in its expired state permit, so the state did not violate this clause. (Choice C) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship. But noncitizens and corporations (like the fishing company) are not considered citizens under this clause. Therefore, this clause does not apply. (Choice D) The dormant commerce clause generally bars states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But discrimination is permitted when the state participates in the market—ie, buys or sells goods or services. And since the state is selling permits to fish in its coastal waters, it can charge nonresidents more for a permit.\n\nEducational objective: Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state constitutions, state laws, and local laws.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• Douglas v. Seacoast Prods., Inc., 431 U.S. 265, 283 (1977) (holding that a state fishing law that prohibits federally licensed ships from fishing in the state's waters violates the supremacy clause).","educationalObjective":"Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state constitutions, state laws, and local laws.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• Douglas v. Seacoast Prods., Inc., 431 U.S. 265, 283 (1977) (holding that a state fishing law that prohibits federally licensed ships from fishing in the state's waters violates the supremacy clause).","rule":"Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state constitutions, state laws, and local laws.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest argument that can be made in support of a continued right of the company to fish for oysters this year in the state's coastal waters?","rule":"Under the supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties preempt conflicting state constitutions, state laws, and local laws.","analysis":"Under the supremacy clause, the provisions of the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. As a result, these provisions preempt conflicting provisions in state constitutions, state laws, and local laws—rendering such provisions void and without effect. Here, the fishing company possesses a shipping license authorized by a federal statute that permits it to fish in the state's waters. But the state statute prohibits the company from fishing since it lacks a state-issued permit. Since these statutes conflict, the company's strongest argument is that its federal shipping license gives it a right to fish in the state's waters despite the state law. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires a state to provide notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard to those it deprives of life, liberty, or property. But the company has no property interest in its expired state permit, so the state did not violate this clause. (Choice C) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship. But noncitizens and corporations (like the fishing company) are not considered citizens under this clause. Therefore, this clause does not apply. (Choice D) The dormant commerce clause generally bars states from discriminating against or otherwise unduly burdening interstate commerce. But discrimination is permitted when the state participates in the market—ie, buys or sells goods or services. And since the state is selling permits to fish in its coastal waters, it can charge nonresidents more for a permit.","conclusion":"Answer A: The company has a right to fish for oysters in the state's waters despite the state law because it holds a federal shipping license."},"questionZh":"一个州每年花费数百万美元用于牡蛎保护计划。作为该计划的一部分，该州通过法规限制拥有州牡蛎许可证的人在其沿海水域进行牡蛎捕捞。为了促进保护，每年只发放有限数量的牡蛎许可证。这些许可证自颁发之日起有效期仅为一年，并通过抽签方式颁发，居民和非居民申请人之间没有区别。然而，每位获得许可证的非居民每年需缴纳的许可证费比居民多缴纳 5 美元。一家大型渔业公司在另一个州的港口开展业务，并在该州注册成立。该公司的每艘船只都拥有联邦航运许可证，允许其“从事沿海贸易的各个方面，捕鱼和沿着海岸从一个地方运到另一个地方，以及沿着美国海岸从事其他合法活动。”这些运输许可证由联邦法规授权。假设没有其他联邦法规或行政规则适用。尽管该公司此前持有州牡蛎许可证，但今年该公司并未获得该州的彩票许可证。以下哪一项是支持该公司今年继续在该州沿海水域捕捞牡蛎的权利的最有力论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"尽管有州法律规定，该公司仍有权在该州水域捕捞牡蛎，因为它拥有联邦航运许可证。","B":"今年拒绝向该公司颁发许可证是在未经正当法律程序的情况下征用其财产，因为该公司之前持有州牡蛎许可证，并且州政府知道该公司从事持续性业务运营。","C":"州法律剥夺了公司的州公民特权和豁免权，因为它向非居民提供了更高的许可费用。","D":"州法律对州际贸易造成了不适当的负担，因为它对非居民规定了更高的许可费用。"},"explanationZh":"根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约的规定是该国的最高法律。因此，这些条款优先于州宪法、州法律和地方法律中的冲突条款，从而使这些条款无效且没有任何效力。在这里，渔业公司拥有联邦法规授权的航运许可证，允许其在该州水域捕鱼。但州法规禁止该公司捕鱼，因为它没有州颁发的许可证。由于这些法规相互冲突，该公司最有力的论点是，尽管有州法律规定，其联邦航运许可证赋予其在该州水域捕鱼的权利。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求各州向那些被其剥夺生命、自由或财产的人提供通知和有意义的机会，让其表达意见。但该公司在其过期的州许可证中没有财产权益，因此州政府并没有违反这一条款。 （选项C） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的权利来歧视其他国家的公民。但根据本条款，非公民和公司（如渔业公司）不被视为公民。因此，本条款不适用。 （选项 D） 消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）通常禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式过度加重州际商业负担。但当国家参与市场（即购买或出售商品或服务）时，歧视是允许的。由于该州正在出售其沿海水域的捕鱼许可证，因此可以向非居民收取更高的许可证费用。\n\n教育目标：根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相冲突的州宪法、州法律和地方法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。六、六类。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• Douglas v. Seacoast Prods., Inc., 431 U.S. 265, 283 (1977)（认为禁止联邦许可的船只在该州水域捕鱼的州捕鱼法违反了至上条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相互冲突的州宪法、州法律和地方法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是支持该公司今年继续在该州沿海水域捕捞牡蛎的权利的最有力论据？","rule":"根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相互冲突的州宪法、州法律和地方法律。","analysis":"根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约的规定是该国的最高法律。因此，这些条款优先于州宪法、州法律和地方法律中的冲突条款，从而使这些条款无效且没有任何效力。在这里，渔业公司拥有联邦法规授权的航运许可证，允许其在该州水域捕鱼。但州法规禁止该公司捕鱼，因为它没有州颁发的许可证。由于这些法规相互冲突，该公司最有力的论点是，尽管有州法律规定，其联邦航运许可证赋予其在该州水域捕鱼的权利。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求各州向那些被其剥夺生命、自由或财产的人提供通知和有意义的机会，让其表达意见。但该公司在其过期的州许可证中没有财产权益，因此州政府并没有违反这一条款。 （选项C） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的权利来歧视其他国家的公民。但根据本条款，非公民和公司（如渔业公司）不被视为公民。因此，本条款不适用。 （选项 D） 消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）通常禁止各州歧视州际商业或以其他方式过度加重州际商业负担。但当国家参与市场（即购买或出售商品或服务）时，歧视是允许的。由于该州正在出售其沿海水域的捕鱼许可证，因此可以向非居民收取更高的许可证费用。\n\n教育目标：根据至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约优先于相冲突的州宪法、州法律和地方法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。六、六类。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• Douglas v. Seacoast Prods., Inc., 431 U.S. 265, 283 (1977)（认为禁止联邦许可的船只在该州水域捕鱼的州捕鱼法违反了至上条款）。","conclusion":"答案 A：尽管有州法律规定，该公司仍有权在该州水域捕捞牡蛎，因为它拥有联邦航运许可证。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"privileges and immunities clause","ipa":"/pɹˈɪvlədʒəz ənd ˌɪmjˈunɪtiz klˈɔz/","syllables":"priv·i·leges and im·mu·ni·ties clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"特权与豁免条款：限制州歧视外州公民的重要活动","contextualMeaning":"特权与豁免条款：限制州歧视外州公民的重要活动","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship.","synonyms":["privileges and immunities article"]},{"term":"notice","ipa":"/nˈoʊtəs/","syllables":"no·tice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 注意, 布告, 通知, 预告, 短评；vt. 注意, 通知, 评论, 提及, 关注；vi. 注意","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 注意, 布告, 通知, 预告, 短评；vt. 注意, 通知, 评论, 提及, 关注；vi. 注意","wordForm":"名词；词形 p:noticed/d:noticed/i:noticing/s:notices/3:notices","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires a state to provide notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard to those it deprives of life, liberty, or property.","synonyms":["observation","observance","poster","posting"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-71592e9660542d2f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":40,"sourceQuestionPage":120,"sourceQuestionPages":[120],"sourceExplanationPages":[121,122],"sourceAnswerPages":[121,122],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-041","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A federally owned and operated office building located in a state is heated with a new, pollution-free heating system. However, in the coldest season of the year, this new system is sometimes insufficient to supply adequate heat to the building. The federal appropriation statute providing the money for construction of the new heating system permitted use of the old, pollution-generating system when necessary to supply additional heat. When the old heating system operates (only about two days in any year), the smokestack of the building emits smoke that exceeds the state's pollution-control standards. May the operators of the federal office building be prosecuted successfully by state authorities for violating that state's pollution-control standards?","choices":{"A":"No, because the operations of the federal government are immune from state regulation in the absence of federal consent.","B":"No, because the violations of the state pollution-control standards involved here are so insignificant that they are beyond the legitimate reach of state law.","C":"Yes, because the regulation of pollution is a joint concern of the federal government and the state and, therefore, both of them may regulate conduct causing pollution.","D":"Yes, because the regulation of pollution is a legitimate state police power concern."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the Article VI supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. As a result, the operations of the federal government— including those of its agencies and instrumentalities—are completely immune from state regulation in the absence of congressional consent. And since Congress did not consent to the state's pollution-control standards in this instance, the operators of the federal office building cannot be successfully prosecuted for violating them. (Choice B) All federal government violations of state regulations—insignificant or otherwise—are beyond the reach of state law unless Congress consented to the regulation. (Choice C) Both the federal and state governments may regulate pollution. But the supremacy clause prevents states from enforcing their regulations against the federal government without congressional consent. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to states the broad police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare—eg, regulating pollution. But when exercising this power, states cannot violate other constitutional provisions like the supremacy clause (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The operations of the federal government—including those of its agencies and instrumentalities—are completely immune from state regulation unless Congress consents.\n\nReferences\n• Arizona v. California, 283 U.S. 423, 451 (1931) (explaining that \"[t]he United States may perform its functions without conforming to the police regulations of a State\").","educationalObjective":"The operations of the federal government—including those of its agencies and instrumentalities—are completely immune from state regulation unless Congress consents.","references":"• Arizona v. California, 283 U.S. 423, 451 (1931) (explaining that \"[t]he United States may perform its functions without conforming to the police regulations of a State\").","rule":"The operations of the federal government—including those of its agencies and instrumentalities—are completely immune from state regulation unless Congress consents.","irac":{"issue":"May the operators of the federal office building be prosecuted successfully by state authorities for violating that state's pollution-control standards?","rule":"The operations of the federal government—including those of its agencies and instrumentalities—are completely immune from state regulation unless Congress consents.","analysis":"Under the Article VI supremacy clause, the U.S. Constitution, federal laws, and treaties are the supreme law of the land. As a result, the operations of the federal government— including those of its agencies and instrumentalities—are completely immune from state regulation in the absence of congressional consent. And since Congress did not consent to the state's pollution-control standards in this instance, the operators of the federal office building cannot be successfully prosecuted for violating them. (Choice B) All federal government violations of state regulations—insignificant or otherwise—are beyond the reach of state law unless Congress consented to the regulation. (Choice C) Both the federal and state governments may regulate pollution. But the supremacy clause prevents states from enforcing their regulations against the federal government without congressional consent. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to states the broad police power to enact and enforce laws that are reasonably related to protecting public health, safety, and welfare—eg, regulating pollution. But when exercising this power, states cannot violate other constitutional provisions like the supremacy clause (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the operations of the federal government are immune from state regulation in the absence of federal consent."},"questionZh":"位于某个州的一栋联邦政府拥有和运营的办公楼采用新型无污染供暖系统供暖。然而，在一年中最冷的季节，这个新系统有时不足以为建筑物提供足够的热量。为建设新供暖系统提供资金的联邦拨款法规允许在必要时使用旧的、产生污染的系统来提供额外的热量。旧供暖系统运行时（每年只有两天左右），大楼烟囱排放的烟雾超过国家污染控制标准。联邦办公楼的经营者是否可以因违反该州的污染控制标准而被州当局成功起诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为在没有联邦政府同意的情况下，联邦政府的运作不受州监管。","B":"不会，因为这里涉及的违反国家污染控制标准的行为非常轻微，超出了国家法律的合法管辖范围。","C":"是的，因为对污染的监管是联邦政府和州政府的共同关注，因此，两者都可以对造成污染的行为进行监管。","D":"是的，因为污染监管是国家警察权力的合法关注点。"},"explanationZh":"根据第六条至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约是美国的最高法律。因此，在没有国会同意的情况下，联邦政府的运作（包括其机构和部门的运作）完全不受州监管。由于国会没有同意该州的污染控制标准，因此联邦办公楼的运营商无法因违反这些标准而被成功起诉。 （选择 B）联邦政府对州法规的所有违反行为（无论是否严重）都超出了州法律的管辖范围，除非国会同意该法规。 （选择C）联邦和州政府都可以监管污染。但至高无上的条款禁止各州在未经国会同意的情况下对联邦政府实施监管。 （选项D）第十修正案保留了广泛的警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律，例如监管污染。但在行使这一权力时，各州不能违反其他宪法条款，例如至上条款（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：除非国会同意，否则联邦政府的运作（包括其机构和部门的运作）完全不受州监管。\n\n参考文献\n• 亚利桑那州诉加利福尼亚州，283 U.S. 423, 451 (1931)（解释说“美国可以在不遵守州警察法规的情况下履行其职能”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非国会同意，否则联邦政府的运作（包括其机构和部门的运作）完全不受州监管。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦办公楼的经营者是否可以因违反该州的污染控制标准而被州当局成功起诉？","rule":"除非国会同意，否则联邦政府的运作（包括其机构和部门的运作）完全不受州监管。","analysis":"根据第六条至上条款，美国宪法、联邦法律和条约是美国的最高法律。因此，在没有国会同意的情况下，联邦政府的运作（包括其机构和部门的运作）完全不受州监管。由于国会没有同意该州的污染控制标准，因此联邦办公楼的运营商无法因违反这些标准而被成功起诉。 （选择 B）联邦政府对州法规的所有违反行为（无论是否严重）都超出了州法律的管辖范围，除非国会同意该法规。 （选择C）联邦和州政府都可以监管污染。但至高无上的条款禁止各州在未经国会同意的情况下对联邦政府实施监管。 （选项D）第十修正案保留了广泛的警察权力来制定和执行与保护公众健康、安全和福利合理相关的法律，例如监管污染。但在行使这一权力时，各州不能违反其他宪法条款，例如至上条款（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：除非国会同意，否则联邦政府的运作（包括其机构和部门的运作）完全不受州监管。\n\n参考文献\n• 亚利桑那州诉加利福尼亚州，283 U.S. 423, 451 (1931)（解释说“美国可以在不遵守州警察法规的情况下履行其职能”）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为在没有联邦政府同意的情况下，联邦政府的运作不受州监管。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"appropriation","ipa":"/ʌpɹˌoʊpɹiˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"ap·pro·pri·a·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 拨用, 挪用, 拨款；[经] 拨款, 挪用","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 拨用, 挪用, 拨款；[经] 拨款, 挪用","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:appropriations","sourceSentence":"The federal appropriation statute providing the money for construction of the new heating system permitted use of the old, pollution-generating system when necessary to supply additional heat.","synonyms":["annexation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-4d098859ba1d4085"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":41,"sourceQuestionPage":123,"sourceQuestionPages":[123],"sourceExplanationPages":[124,125],"sourceAnswerPages":[124,125],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-042","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Taxing & Spending Powers","question":"Congress enacted a statute that annually awards federal funds to states that must be used to improve the quality of public education. Every state agreed to participate in this federal program. On average, the funds awarded by the federal program comprise 15% of a state's overall annual budget. Several years later, Congress enacted another statute that expanded this federal program to reduce the academic achievement gap between high-performing and low-performing public schools. A provision of this federal statute requires states to administer a statewide academic test to students that is then used to assess the academic performance of public schools. States that choose not to comply with this testing requirement will no longer receive any federal funding from the program and may suffer budgetary shortfalls as a result. A state that has chosen not to comply with the testing requirement has sued the federal government, arguing that the statute's provision is unconstitutional. Which of the following is the state's best argument?","choices":{"A":"Incentivizing states to adopt federal policies exceeds the scope of Congress's spending power.","B":"The condition placed on the receipt of federal funding is unduly coercive.","C":"The funding of public education has traditionally been performed by the states.","D":"The Tenth Amendment grants states immunity from direct federal regulation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Conditional federal funding\n\n(Article I, section 8 spending clause)\n\nConditions must meet all the following criteria:\n\n• clearly stated & unambiguous\n• reasonably related to federal interest in funded program\n• do not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity\n• do not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad discretion to spend for the general welfare, such as improving the quality of public education. Congress can use this spending power to incentivize states to adopt federal policies by placing appropriate conditions on the receipt of federal funds (Choice A). A condition is appropriate when it:\n• is clear and unambiguous (eg, requiring states to comply with the testing requirement or lose federal funding)\n\n• is reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended (eg, requiring academic testing to reduce the academic achievement gap)\n\n• does not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity (eg, developing and administering a statewide academic test is not unconstitutional) and\n\n• is not unduly coercive.\n\nA condition is unduly coercive if it amounts to a \"gun to the head\" of a state. Although SCOTUS has not provided concrete guidance on how to make this determination, it has invalidated a condition that would have withheld 10% of a state's overall budget.* Here, the condition on the receipt of federal funding would withhold 15% of an average state's annual budget. This amounts to a \"gun to the head\" and is unduly coercive since states that do not comply with this condition may suffer significant budgetary shortfalls. Therefore, this is the state's best argument that the provision is unconstitutional. *In contrast, SCOTUS has upheld a condition that withheld less than 1% of a state's overall budget. (Choice C) Although the funding of public education has traditionally been performed by the states, the spending clause gives Congress the power to spend for the general welfare— including public education.\n\n(Choice D) The Tenth Amendment grants states immunity from federal regulations that compel or unduly coerce them to enact state laws or enforce federal laws. However, states must comply with other types of direct federal regulation (eg, EPA pollution standards).\n\nEducational objective: Congress may use its spending power to regulate states by placing conditions on the receipt of federal funds that (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States; EPA = Environmental Protection Agency.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 1 (taxing and spending clause).\n\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 212 (1987) (holding that Congress can place a condition on the receipt of federal highway funding that amounts to less than 1% of a state's overall budget).\n\n• Nat'l Fed'n of Indep. Bus. v. Sebelius, 567 U.S. 519, 581–82 (2012) (explaining that Congress cannot place a condition on the receipt of Medicaid funds that amounts to 10% of a state's overall spending).","educationalObjective":"Congress may use its spending power to regulate states by placing conditions on the receipt of federal funds that (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States; EPA = Environmental Protection Agency.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 1 (taxing and spending clause).\n\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 212 (1987) (holding that Congress can place a condition on the receipt of federal highway funding that amounts to less than 1% of a state's overall budget).\n\n• Nat'l Fed'n of Indep. Bus. v. Sebelius, 567 U.S. 519, 581–82 (2012) (explaining that Congress cannot place a condition on the receipt of Medicaid funds that amounts to 10% of a state's overall spending).","rule":"Congress may use its spending power to regulate states by placing conditions on the receipt of federal funds that (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States; EPA = Environmental Protection Agency.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the state's best argument?","rule":"Congress may use its spending power to regulate states by placing conditions on the receipt of federal funds that (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States; EPA = Environmental Protection Agency.","analysis":"Conditional federal funding\n\n(Article I, section 8 spending clause)\n\nConditions must meet all the following criteria:\n\n• clearly stated & unambiguous\n• reasonably related to federal interest in funded program\n• do not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity\n• do not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad discretion to spend for the general welfare, such as improving the quality of public education. Congress can use this spending power to incentivize states to adopt federal policies by placing appropriate conditions on the receipt of federal funds (Choice A). A condition is appropriate when it:\n• is clear and unambiguous (eg, requiring states to comply with the testing requirement or lose federal funding)\n\n• is reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended (eg, requiring academic testing to reduce the academic achievement gap)\n\n• does not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity (eg, developing and administering a statewide academic test is not unconstitutional) and\n\n• is not unduly coercive.\n\nA condition is unduly coercive if it amounts to a \"gun to the head\" of a state. Although SCOTUS has not provided concrete guidance on how to make this determination, it has invalidated a condition that would have withheld 10% of a state's overall budget.* Here, the condition on the receipt of federal funding would withhold 15% of an average state's annual budget. This amounts to a \"gun to the head\" and is unduly coercive since states that do not comply with this condition may suffer significant budgetary shortfalls. Therefore, this is the state's best argument that the provision is unconstitutional. *In contrast, SCOTUS has upheld a condition that withheld less than 1% of a state's overall budget. (Choice C) Although the funding of public education has traditionally been performed by the states, the spending clause gives Congress the power to spend for the general welfare— including public education.\n\n(Choice D) The Tenth Amendment grants states immunity from federal regulations that compel or unduly coerce them to enact state laws or enforce federal laws. However, states must comply with other types of direct federal regulation (eg, EPA pollution standards).","conclusion":"Answer B: The condition placed on the receipt of federal funding is unduly coercive."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，每年向各州提供联邦资金，这些资金必须用于提高公共教育质量。每个州都同意参与这项联邦计划。平均而言，联邦计划授予的资金占州年度总体预算的 15%。几年后，国会颁布了另一项法规，扩大了这项联邦计划，以缩小表现优异和表现不佳的公立学校之间的学业成绩差距。该联邦法规的一项规定要求各州对学生进行全州范围的学业测试，然后用于评估公立学校的学业成绩。选择不遵守此测试要求的州将不再从该计划获得任何联邦资金，并可能因此遭受预算短缺。选择不遵守测试要求的州已起诉联邦政府，认为该法规的规定违宪。以下哪一项是该州最好的论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"激励各州采取联邦政策超出了国会的支出能力范围。","B":"接受联邦资助的条件具有过度强制性。","C":"公共教育的资金传统上由各州承担。","D":"第十修正案赋予各州不受直接联邦监管的豁免权。"},"explanationZh":"有条件的联邦资助\n\n（第一条第8款支出条款）\n\n条件必须满足以下所有标准：\n\n• 明确表述且不含糊\n• 与资助计划中的联邦利益合理相关\n• 不要求各州从事违宪活动\n• 不要过度强迫各州接受税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的自由裁量权，可以在公共福利方面进行支出，例如提高公共教育的质量。国会可以利用这种支出能力，通过对联邦资金的接收设定适当的条件来激励各州采取联邦政策（选择 A）。当满足以下条件时，条件是适当的：\n• 清晰明确（例如，要求各州遵守测试要求，否则将失去联邦资助）\n\n• 与资金支出的目的合理相关（例如，要求学术测试以缩小学术成绩差距）\n\n• 不要求接受者从事违宪活动（例如，制定和管理全州范围的学术测试并不违宪）并且\n\n• 没有过度强制。\n\n如果一项条件相当于“用枪指着一个国家的头部”，则该条件具有过度强制性。尽管 SCOTUS 没有就如何做出这一决定提供具体指导，但它已经使扣留州总体预算 10% 的条件无效。*在这里，收到联邦资金的条件将扣留州平均年度预算的 15%。这相当于“用枪指着头”，具有过度强制性，因为不遵守这一条件的国家可能会遭受严重的预算短缺。因此，这是该州证明该条款违宪的最佳论据。 *相比之下，最高法院维持了扣留州总预算不到 1% 的条件。 （选项 C）虽然公共教育的资金传统上由各州承担，但支出条款赋予国会为公共福利（包括公共教育）支出的权力。\n\n（选项 D）第十修正案赋予各州豁免权，使其免受强迫或不当强迫其制定州法律或执行联邦法律的联邦法规的影响。然而，各州必须遵守其他类型的直接联邦法规（例如，EPA 污染标准）。\n\n教育目标：国会可以利用其支出权力来监管各州，对联邦资金的接收设定以下条件：(1) 明确且不含糊，(2) 与资金支出目的合理相关，(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动，(4) 不具有过度胁迫性。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院； EPA = 环境保护署。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 1（税收和支出条款）。\n\n• 南达科他州诉多尔案 (South Dakota v. Dole)，483 U.S. 203, 212 (1987)（认为国会可以对联邦公路资金少于州总预算 1% 的接收提出条件）。\n\n• 独立国家联邦储备银行。公共汽车。 v. Sebelius, 567 U.S. 519, 581–82 (2012)（解释说国会不能对收到相当于州总支出 10% 的医疗补助资金设定条件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会可以利用其支出权力来监管各州，对联邦资金的接收设定以下条件：(1) 明确且不含糊，(2) 与资金支出的目的合理相关，(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动，(4) 不具有过度胁迫性。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院； EPA = 环境保护署。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是该州最好的论据？","rule":"国会可以利用其支出权力来监管各州，对联邦资金的接收设定以下条件：(1) 明确且不含糊，(2) 与资金支出的目的合理相关，(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动，(4) 不具有过度胁迫性。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院； EPA = 环境保护署。","analysis":"有条件的联邦资助\n\n（第一条第8款支出条款）\n\n条件必须满足以下所有标准：\n\n• 明确表述且不含糊\n• 与资助计划中的联邦利益合理相关\n• 不要求各州从事违宪活动\n• 不要过度强迫各州接受税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的自由裁量权，可以在公共福利方面进行支出，例如提高公共教育的质量。国会可以利用这种支出能力，通过对联邦资金的接收设定适当的条件来激励各州采取联邦政策（选择 A）。当满足以下条件时，条件是适当的：\n• 清晰明确（例如，要求各州遵守测试要求，否则将失去联邦资助）\n\n• 与资金支出的目的合理相关（例如，要求学术测试以缩小学术成绩差距）\n\n• 不要求接受者从事违宪活动（例如，制定和管理全州范围的学术测试并不违宪）并且\n\n• 没有过度强制。\n\n如果一项条件相当于“用枪指着一个国家的头部”，则该条件具有过度强制性。尽管 SCOTUS 没有就如何做出这一决定提供具体指导，但它已经使扣留州总体预算 10% 的条件无效。*在这里，收到联邦资金的条件将扣留州平均年度预算的 15%。这相当于“用枪指着头”，具有过度强制性，因为不遵守这一条件的国家可能会遭受严重的预算短缺。因此，这是该州证明该条款违宪的最佳论据。 *相比之下，最高法院维持了扣留州总预算不到 1% 的条件。 （选项 C）虽然公共教育的资金传统上由各州承担，但支出条款赋予国会为公共福利（包括公共教育）支出的权力。\n\n（选项 D）第十修正案赋予各州豁免权，使其免受强迫或不当强迫其制定州法律或执行联邦法律的联邦法规的影响。然而，各州必须遵守其他类型的直接联邦法规（例如，EPA 污染标准）。\n\n教育目标：国会可以利用其支出权力来监管各州，对联邦资金的接收设定以下条件：(1) 明确且不含糊，(2) 与资金支出目的合理相关，(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动，(4) 不具有过度胁迫性。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院； EPA = 环境保护署。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 1（税收和支出条款）。\n\n• 南达科他州诉多尔案 (South Dakota v. Dole)，483 U.S. 203, 212 (1987)（认为国会可以对联邦公路资金少于州总预算 1% 的接收提出条件）。\n\n• 独立国家联邦储备银行。公共汽车。 v. Sebelius, 567 U.S. 519, 581–82 (2012)（解释说国会不能对收到相当于州总支出 10% 的医疗补助资金设定条件）。","conclusion":"答案 B：接受联邦资助的条件具有过度强制性。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"coercive","ipa":"/koʊˈɝsɪv/","syllables":"co·er·cive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 强制的, 强压的, 强迫的；[法] 强制的, 强迫的, 强行的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 强制的, 强压的, 强迫的；[法] 强制的, 强迫的, 强行的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The condition placed on the receipt of federal funding is unduly coercive.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"coerce","ipa":"/koʊˈɝs/","syllables":"co·erce","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 强制, 强迫","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 强制, 强迫","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:coerced/p:coerced/i:coercing/3:coerces","sourceSentence":"Conditional federal funding (Article I, section 8 spending clause) Conditions must meet all the following criteria: • clearly stated & unambiguous • reasonably related to federal interest in funded program • do not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity • do not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad discretion to spend for the general welfare, such as i","synonyms":["hale","squeeze","pressure","force"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":42,"sourceQuestionPage":126,"sourceQuestionPages":[126],"sourceExplanationPages":[127,128],"sourceAnswerPages":[127,128],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-043","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Dormant Commerce Clause","question":"Widgets are manufactured wholly from raw materials mined and processed in a state. The only two manufacturers of widgets in the United States are also located in that state. However, their widgets are purchased by retailers located in every state. The state legislature is considering the adoption of a statute that would impose a tax solely on the manufacture of widgets. The tax is to be calculated at 3% of their wholesale value. Which of the following arguments would be LEAST helpful to the state in defending the constitutionality of this proposed state tax on widgets?","choices":{"A":"A tax on the manufacture of widgets may be imposed only by the state in which the manufacturing occurs and, therefore, it is not likely to create the danger of a multiple tax burden on interstate commerce.","B":"At the time widgets are manufactured and taxed they have not yet entered the channels of interstate commerce.","C":"Because of the powers reserved to them by the Tenth Amendment, states have plenary authority to construct their tax system in any manner they choose.","D":"The economic impact of this tax will be passed on to both in-state and out-of-state purchasers of widgets and, therefore, the tax is wholly nondiscriminatory in its effect."},"answer":"C","explanation":"State taxation of interstate commerce\n\n(Complete Auto test)\n\nState tax on interstate commerce valid if tax:\n\n• applied to person or activity with substantial nexus to state\n• fairly apportioned to avoid taxing interstate activities performed in other states\n• does not discriminate against interstate commerce AND\n• fairly related to services & benefits provided by state Under the Tenth Amendment, any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the power to construct a state tax system—is reserved to the states. But here, this is the least helpful argument for the state because this power is not plenary (ie, absolute). Instead, states must comply with other constitutional provisions. For example, a state tax on interstate commerce (as seen here) must comply with the dormant commerce clause and is only valid if it:\n• is applied to persons or activities with a substantial nexus to the state—eg, widgets manufactured from raw materials mined and processed in the state\n\n• is fairly apportioned to avoid taxing interstate activities performed in other states and thereby imposing multiple tax burdens—eg, tax imposed only in state where manufacturing occurs (Choice A), tax applied before widgets enter channels of interstate commerce (Choice B)\n\n• does not discriminate by favoring in-state over out-of-state entities—eg, economic impact passed on to both in-state and out-of-state purchasers (Choice D) and\n\n• is fairly related (ie, reasonable compared) to services and benefits provided by the state—eg, police protection, public roads.\n\nEducational objective: Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot tax interstate commerce unless the tax is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to state-provided services or benefits.\n\nReferences\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state tax violates the commerce clause).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 157 (2019) (explaining how state taxation cannot unduly burden interstate commerce).","educationalObjective":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot tax interstate commerce unless the tax is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to state-provided services or benefits.","references":"• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977) (setting forth the four-factor test used to determine if a state tax violates the commerce clause).\n\n• 71 Am. Jur. 2d State and Local Taxation § 157 (2019) (explaining how state taxation cannot unduly burden interstate commerce).","rule":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot tax interstate commerce unless the tax is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to state-provided services or benefits.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments would be LEAST helpful to the state in defending the constitutionality of this proposed state tax on widgets?","rule":"Under the dormant commerce clause, states cannot tax interstate commerce unless the tax is (1) levied on persons or activities that have a substantial nexus with the state, (2) fairly apportioned, (3) nondiscriminatory, and (4) fairly related to state-provided services or benefits.","analysis":"State taxation of interstate commerce\n\n(Complete Auto test)\n\nState tax on interstate commerce valid if tax:\n\n• applied to person or activity with substantial nexus to state\n• fairly apportioned to avoid taxing interstate activities performed in other states\n• does not discriminate against interstate commerce AND\n• fairly related to services & benefits provided by state Under the Tenth Amendment, any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the power to construct a state tax system—is reserved to the states. But here, this is the least helpful argument for the state because this power is not plenary (ie, absolute). Instead, states must comply with other constitutional provisions. For example, a state tax on interstate commerce (as seen here) must comply with the dormant commerce clause and is only valid if it:\n• is applied to persons or activities with a substantial nexus to the state—eg, widgets manufactured from raw materials mined and processed in the state\n\n• is fairly apportioned to avoid taxing interstate activities performed in other states and thereby imposing multiple tax burdens—eg, tax imposed only in state where manufacturing occurs (Choice A), tax applied before widgets enter channels of interstate commerce (Choice B)\n\n• does not discriminate by favoring in-state over out-of-state entities—eg, economic impact passed on to both in-state and out-of-state purchasers (Choice D) and\n\n• is fairly related (ie, reasonable compared) to services and benefits provided by the state—eg, police protection, public roads.","conclusion":"Answer C: Because of the powers reserved to them by the Tenth Amendment, states have plenary authority to construct their tax system in any manner they choose."},"questionZh":"小部件完全由在某个州开采和加工的原材料制成。美国仅有的两家小部件制造商也位于该州。然而，他们的小部件是由位于每个州的零售商购买的。州立法机关正在考虑通过一项法规，仅对小部件的制造征税。税款按其批发价值的3%计算。以下哪项论点对州捍卫拟议的小部件州税的合宪性最没有帮助？","choicesZh":{"A":"小部件制造税只能由制造所在州征收，因此，不太可能对州际贸易造成多重税收负担的危险。","B":"在小部件被制造并征税时，它们尚未进入州际贸易渠道。","C":"由于第十修正案保留给各州的权力，各州拥有以自己选择的任何方式构建税收制度的全权。","D":"该税的经济影响将传递给州内和州外的小部件购买者，因此，该税的效果完全是非歧视性的。"},"explanationZh":"州际贸易的州税\n\n（完整的自动测试）\n\n州际商业税在以下情况下有效：\n\n• 适用于与国家有重大联系的个人或活动\n• 公平分配以避免对在其他州进行的州际活动征税\n• 不歧视州际贸易并且\n• 与州提供的服务和福利公平相关 根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力（包括建立州税收制度的权力）均保留给各州。但在这里，这对国家来说是最没有帮助的论据，因为这种权力不是全权（即绝对）。相反，各州必须遵守其他宪法条款。例如，州际商业税（如此处所示）必须符合消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），并且仅在满足以下条件时才有效：\n• 适用于与国家有重大联系的个人或活动——例如，用在该州开采和加工的原材料制造的小部件\n\n• 公平分配，以避免对在其他州进行的州际活动征税，从而造成多重税收负担——例如，仅在制造发生的州征收税款（选择A），在小部件进入州际贸易渠道之前征收税款（选择B）\n\n• 不偏向州内实体而不是州外实体——例如，经济影响同时传递给州内和州外购买者（选择D）以及\n\n• 与州提供的服务和福利公平相关（即比较合理），例如警察保护、公共道路。\n\n教育目标：根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得对州际商业征税，除非税款 (1) 对与州有重大联系的个人或活动征税，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 157 (2019)（解释州税如何不能对州际贸易造成过度负担）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得对州际商业征税，除非该税款 (1) 对与该州有重大联系的个人或活动征税，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项论点对州捍卫拟议的小部件州税的合宪性最没有帮助？","rule":"根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得对州际商业征税，除非该税款 (1) 对与该州有重大联系的个人或活动征税，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。","analysis":"州际贸易的州税\n\n（完整的自动测试）\n\n州际商业税在以下情况下有效：\n\n• 适用于与国家有重大联系的个人或活动\n• 公平分配以避免对在其他州进行的州际活动征税\n• 不歧视州际贸易并且\n• 与州提供的服务和福利公平相关 根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力（包括建立州税收制度的权力）均保留给各州。但在这里，这对国家来说是最没有帮助的论据，因为这种权力不是全权（即绝对）。相反，各州必须遵守其他宪法条款。例如，州际商业税（如此处所示）必须符合消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），并且仅在满足以下条件时才有效：\n• 适用于与国家有重大联系的个人或活动——例如，用在该州开采和加工的原材料制造的小部件\n\n• 公平分配，以避免对在其他州进行的州际活动征税，从而造成多重税收负担——例如，仅在制造发生的州征收税款（选择A），在小部件进入州际贸易渠道之前征收税款（选择B）\n\n• 不偏向州内实体而不是州外实体——例如，经济影响同时传递给州内和州外购买者（选择D）以及\n\n• 与州提供的服务和福利公平相关（即比较合理），例如警察保护、公共道路。\n\n教育目标：根据消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause），各州不得对州际商业征税，除非税款 (1) 对与州有重大联系的个人或活动征税，(2) 公平分配，(3) 非歧视性，以及 (4) 与州提供的服务或福利公平相关。\n\n参考文献\n• Complete Auto Transit, Inc. v. Brady, 430 U.S. 274, 279 (1977)（规定了用于确定州税是否违反商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的四因素测试）。\n\n• 上午 71 点。尤尔。 2d 州和地方税收 § 157 (2019)（解释州税如何不能对州际贸易造成过度负担）。","conclusion":"答案 C：由于第十修正案保留给各州的权力，各州拥有以自己选择的任何方式构建税收制度的全权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":43,"sourceQuestionPage":129,"sourceQuestionPages":[129],"sourceExplanationPages":[130,131],"sourceAnswerPages":[130,131],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-044","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"Congressional hearings determined that the use of mechanical power hammers is very dangerous to the persons using them and to persons in the vicinity of the persons using them. As a result, Congress enacted a statute prohibiting the use of mechanical power hammers on all construction projects in the United States. Subsequently, a study conducted by a private research firm concluded that nails driven by mechanical power hammers have longer-lasting joining power than hand-driven nails. After learning about this study, a city council enacted an amendment to its building safety code requiring the use of mechanical power hammers in the construction of all buildings intended for human habitation. Is this amendment to the city's building safety code constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it conflicts with the provisions of the federal statute.","B":"No, because it was enacted subsequent to the federal statute.","C":"Yes, because the federal statute does not expressly indicate that it supersedes inconsistent state or local laws.","D":"Yes, because the long-term safety of human habitations justifies some additional risk to the people engaged in their construction."},"answer":"A","explanation":"It is common for the federal government to regulate the same subject matter as states and/or municipalities because these governments share many of the same powers. But under the supremacy clause, federal law is the supreme law of the land. Therefore, a federal law can preempt (ie, supersede) conflicting state or municipal laws and render them void. One example is implied conflict preemption, which occurs when there is:\n• direct conflict – a state/municipal law conflicts with a federal law to such a degree that it is impossible (or nearly impossible) for a person or entity to comply with both laws or\n\n• indirect conflict – a state/municipal law frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose.\n\nHere, the federal statute prohibits the use of mechanized hammers on all construction projects. But the city council amendment requires the use of such hammers on all construction projects for human habitation. These laws directly conflict since it is impossible for a company constructing human habitations in the city to comply with both laws. Therefore, the federal statute impliedly preempts the city council amendment and renders it unconstitutional. (Choice B) In determining preemption, the timing of the laws is irrelevant. Therefore, it makes no difference whether the city council enacted the amendment before or after the federal statute. (Choice C) Although the federal statute does not expressly state that it supersedes inconsistent state or local laws, it impliedly preempts them. (Choice D) Federal laws always preempt inconsistent state and local laws—regardless of their justification.\n\nEducational objective: Implied conflict preemption exists when (1) it is impossible to comply with a federal law and a conflicting state/municipal law (ie, direct conflict) or (2) a state/municipal law frustrates the accomplishment of a federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).\n\nReferences\n• English v. Gen. Elec. Co., 496 U.S. 72, 79 (1990) (stating that federal law preempts state law when \"it is impossible for a private party to comply with both state and federal requirements\").\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 235 (2019) (explaining when federal and state laws conflict).","educationalObjective":"Implied conflict preemption exists when (1) it is impossible to comply with a federal law and a conflicting state/municipal law (ie, direct conflict) or (2) a state/municipal law frustrates the accomplishment of a federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).","references":"• English v. Gen. Elec. Co., 496 U.S. 72, 79 (1990) (stating that federal law preempts state law when \"it is impossible for a private party to comply with both state and federal requirements\").\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 235 (2019) (explaining when federal and state laws conflict).","rule":"Implied conflict preemption exists when (1) it is impossible to comply with a federal law and a conflicting state/municipal law (ie, direct conflict) or (2) a state/municipal law frustrates the accomplishment of a federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).","irac":{"issue":"Is this amendment to the city's building safety code constitutional?","rule":"Implied conflict preemption exists when (1) it is impossible to comply with a federal law and a conflicting state/municipal law (ie, direct conflict) or (2) a state/municipal law frustrates the accomplishment of a federal law's purpose (ie, indirect conflict).","analysis":"It is common for the federal government to regulate the same subject matter as states and/or municipalities because these governments share many of the same powers. But under the supremacy clause, federal law is the supreme law of the land. Therefore, a federal law can preempt (ie, supersede) conflicting state or municipal laws and render them void. One example is implied conflict preemption, which occurs when there is:\n• direct conflict – a state/municipal law conflicts with a federal law to such a degree that it is impossible (or nearly impossible) for a person or entity to comply with both laws or\n\n• indirect conflict – a state/municipal law frustrates the accomplishment of the federal law's purpose.\n\nHere, the federal statute prohibits the use of mechanized hammers on all construction projects. But the city council amendment requires the use of such hammers on all construction projects for human habitation. These laws directly conflict since it is impossible for a company constructing human habitations in the city to comply with both laws. Therefore, the federal statute impliedly preempts the city council amendment and renders it unconstitutional. (Choice B) In determining preemption, the timing of the laws is irrelevant. Therefore, it makes no difference whether the city council enacted the amendment before or after the federal statute. (Choice C) Although the federal statute does not expressly state that it supersedes inconsistent state or local laws, it impliedly preempts them. (Choice D) Federal laws always preempt inconsistent state and local laws—regardless of their justification.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because it conflicts with the provisions of the federal statute."},"questionZh":"国会听证会确定，使用机械动力锤对于使用它们的人和使用它们的人附近的人来说非常危险。因此，国会颁布了一项法规，禁止在美国的所有建筑项目中使用机械动力锤。随后，一家私人研究公司进行的一项研究得出结论，机械动力锤驱动的钉子比手工钉子具有更持久的连接力。在了解这项研究后，市议会颁布了一项建筑安全规范修正案，要求在建造所有供人类居住的建筑时使用机械动力锤。该市建筑安全法规的修正案是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为这与联邦法规的规定相冲突。","B":"不，因为它是在联邦法规之后颁布的。","C":"是的，因为联邦法规没有明确表明它取代不一致的州或地方法律。","D":"是的，因为人类居住区的长期安全证明，参与其建设的人们面临一些额外的风险是合理的。"},"explanationZh":"联邦政府与州和/或市政府监管相同的主题是很常见的，因为这些政府拥有许多相同的权力。但根据至上条款，联邦法律是该国的最高法律。因此，联邦法律可以优先于（即取代）相冲突的州或市法律并使其无效。一个例子是隐含冲突抢占，当存在以下情况时就会发生这种情况：\n• 直接冲突——州/市法律与联邦法律的冲突程度达到个人或实体不可能（或几乎不可能）同时遵守这两项法律或\n\n• 间接冲突——州/市法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现。\n\n在这里，联邦法规禁止在所有建筑项目中使用机械化锤。但市议会修正案要求在所有人类居住建设项目上使用这种锤子。这些法律直接冲突，因为在城市建造人类住宅的公司不可能同时遵守这两项法律。因此，联邦法规隐含地优先于市议会修正案并使其违宪。 （选项B） 在确定优先购买权时，法律的时间安排是无关紧要的。因此，市议会在联邦法规之前或之后颁布修正案并没有什么区别。 （选项 C）虽然联邦法规没有明确规定它取代不一致的州或地方法律，但它隐含地优先于它们。 （选项 D）联邦法律总是优先于不一致的州和地方法律——无论其理由如何。\n\n教育目标：当 (1) 不可能遵守联邦法律和相冲突的州/市法律（即直接冲突）或 (2) 州/市法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）时，就存在隐含冲突先发制人。\n\n参考文献\n• English 诉 Gen. Elec。 Co., 496 U.S. 72, 79 (1990)（指出当“私人当事人不可能同时遵守州和联邦要求时”，联邦法律优先于州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 235 条 (2019)（解释联邦法律和州法律何时发生冲突）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当 (1) 不可能遵守联邦法律和相冲突的州/市法律（即直接冲突）或 (2) 州/市法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）时，就存在默示冲突先发制人。","iracZh":{"issue":"该市建筑安全法规的修正案是否符合宪法？","rule":"当 (1) 不可能遵守联邦法律和相冲突的州/市法律（即直接冲突）或 (2) 州/市法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）时，就存在默示冲突先发制人。","analysis":"联邦政府与州和/或市政府监管相同的主题是很常见的，因为这些政府拥有许多相同的权力。但根据至上条款，联邦法律是该国的最高法律。因此，联邦法律可以优先于（即取代）相冲突的州或市法律并使其无效。一个例子是隐含冲突抢占，当存在以下情况时就会发生这种情况：\n• 直接冲突——州/市法律与联邦法律的冲突程度达到个人或实体不可能（或几乎不可能）同时遵守这两项法律或\n\n• 间接冲突——州/市法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现。\n\n在这里，联邦法规禁止在所有建筑项目中使用机械化锤。但市议会修正案要求在所有人类居住建设项目上使用这种锤子。这些法律直接冲突，因为在城市建造人类住宅的公司不可能同时遵守这两项法律。因此，联邦法规隐含地优先于市议会修正案并使其违宪。 （选项B） 在确定优先购买权时，法律的时间安排是无关紧要的。因此，市议会在联邦法规之前或之后颁布修正案并没有什么区别。 （选项 C）虽然联邦法规没有明确规定它取代不一致的州或地方法律，但它隐含地优先于它们。 （选项 D）联邦法律总是优先于不一致的州和地方法律——无论其理由如何。\n\n教育目标：当 (1) 不可能遵守联邦法律和相冲突的州/市法律（即直接冲突）或 (2) 州/市法律阻碍联邦法律目的的实现（即间接冲突）时，就存在隐含冲突先发制人。\n\n参考文献\n• English 诉 Gen. Elec。 Co., 496 U.S. 72, 79 (1990)（指出当“私人当事人不可能同时遵守州和联邦要求时”，联邦法律优先于州法律）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 235 条 (2019)（解释联邦法律和州法律何时发生冲突）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为这与联邦法规的规定相冲突。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-94e33cea9d282c70"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":44,"sourceQuestionPage":132,"sourceQuestionPages":[132],"sourceExplanationPages":[133,134],"sourceAnswerPages":[133,134],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-045","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federalism & Anti-Commandeering","question":"A federal statute requires all owners of mineral springs to pay a sales tax on the mineral water they sell. A state that sells mineral water from the springs that it owns has refused to pay the tax. The United States has sued the state in federal court, seeking payment of the tax. The state has moved to dismiss the action. Is the court likely to dismiss the action?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress has unlimited power to tax and spend for the general welfare.","B":"No, because the nondiscriminatory tax does not interfere with the essential functions of state government.","C":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits states from being sued in federal court without the state's consent.","D":"Yes, because the Tenth Amendment grants states immunity from all direct federal taxation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Federalism\n\n(power distribution between federal & state governments)\n\nConstitutional Effect provision\n\nArticle VI\n• Prioritizes U.S. Constitution, treaties & federal laws over state supremacy clause constitutions & laws\n• Prohibits states from regulating/taxing federal government or its agencies/instrumentalities Tenth Amendment\n• Reserves all powers not assigned to federal government by U.S. Constitution to states/people\n• Prohibits federal government from requiring states to enact laws or enforce federal laws\n• Immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions Article I grants Congress the broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But this power is not unlimited (Choice A). Under the Tenth Amendment, Congress is prohibited from imposing federal taxes on states that:\n• discriminate against states because the taxes apply to states and their agencies but not private individuals or entities or\n\n• would unduly interfere with the states' essential functions, including property used for or income received from the state's performance of traditional government actions (eg, public schools, state parks).\n\nHowever, the Tenth Amendment does not immunize states from nondiscriminatory federal taxes that apply to the public and private sectors and do not interfere with essential state functions (Choice D). Here, the federal sales tax is nondiscriminatory since it applies to all private and public owners of mineral springs who sell mineral water. The tax also does not unduly interfere with essential state functions because selling mineral water (or any other product) is a business action—not a traditional government action. As a result, the Tenth Amendment does not immunize the state from the tax, so the court is not likely to dismiss the action. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. However, this amendment does not bar actions brought by the United States (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The Tenth Amendment immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions.\n\nReferences\n• New York v. United States, 326 U.S. 572, 582 (1946) (holding that a state is not immune from a nondiscriminatory federal tax that does not interfere with essential state functions).","educationalObjective":"The Tenth Amendment immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions.","references":"• New York v. United States, 326 U.S. 572, 582 (1946) (holding that a state is not immune from a nondiscriminatory federal tax that does not interfere with essential state functions).","rule":"The Tenth Amendment immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to dismiss the action?","rule":"The Tenth Amendment immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions.","analysis":"Federalism\n\n(power distribution between federal & state governments)\n\nConstitutional Effect provision\n\nArticle VI\n• Prioritizes U.S. Constitution, treaties & federal laws over state supremacy clause constitutions & laws\n• Prohibits states from regulating/taxing federal government or its agencies/instrumentalities Tenth Amendment\n• Reserves all powers not assigned to federal government by U.S. Constitution to states/people\n• Prohibits federal government from requiring states to enact laws or enforce federal laws\n• Immunizes states from federal taxes that discriminate against states or unduly interfere with essential state functions Article I grants Congress the broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But this power is not unlimited (Choice A). Under the Tenth Amendment, Congress is prohibited from imposing federal taxes on states that:\n• discriminate against states because the taxes apply to states and their agencies but not private individuals or entities or\n\n• would unduly interfere with the states' essential functions, including property used for or income received from the state's performance of traditional government actions (eg, public schools, state parks).\n\nHowever, the Tenth Amendment does not immunize states from nondiscriminatory federal taxes that apply to the public and private sectors and do not interfere with essential state functions (Choice D). Here, the federal sales tax is nondiscriminatory since it applies to all private and public owners of mineral springs who sell mineral water. The tax also does not unduly interfere with essential state functions because selling mineral water (or any other product) is a business action—not a traditional government action. As a result, the Tenth Amendment does not immunize the state from the tax, so the court is not likely to dismiss the action. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. However, this amendment does not bar actions brought by the United States (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the nondiscriminatory tax does not interfere with the essential functions of state government."},"questionZh":"联邦法规要求所有矿泉水所有者对其销售的矿泉水缴纳销售税。一个出售其拥有的泉水矿泉水的州拒绝缴纳税款。美国已向联邦法院起诉该州，要求缴纳税款。该州已采取行动驳回该诉讼。法院有可能驳回诉讼吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会拥有无限的权力，可以为公共福利征税和支出。","B":"不会，因为非歧视性税收不会干扰州政府的基本职能。","C":"是的，因为第十一条修正案禁止未经各州同意在联邦法院起诉各州。","D":"是的，因为第十修正案赋予各州免除所有直接联邦税的权利。"},"explanationZh":"联邦制\n\n（联邦和州政府之间的权力分配）\n\n宪法效力条款\n\n第六条\n• 优先考虑美国宪法、条约和联邦法律，而不是州至上条款宪法和法律\n• 禁止各州对联邦政府或其机构/机构进行监管/征税 第十修正案\n• 将美国宪法未授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州/人民\n• 禁止联邦政府要求各州颁布法律或执行联邦法律\n• 使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干预国家基本职能的联邦税。第一条赋予国会广泛的权力，为公共福利征税和支出。但这种力量并不是无限的（选择A）。根据第十修正案，国会不得向以下州征收联邦税：\n• 歧视国家，因为税收适用于国家及其机构，而不是私人或实体，或\n\n• 会不适当地干扰各州的基本职能，包括用于州执行传统政府行为（例如公立学校、州立公园）的财产或从该州获得的收入。\n\n然而，第十修正案并没有使各州免受适用于公共和私营部门的非歧视性联邦税，并且不干涉基本的国家职能（选项 D）。在这里，联邦销售税是非歧视性的，因为它适用于所有销售矿泉水的私人和公共矿泉所有者。该税收也不会过度干扰国家的基本职能，因为销售矿泉水（或任何其他产品）是一种商业行为，而不是传统的政府行为。因此，第十修正案并未使各州免于征税，因此法院不太可能驳回该诉讼。 （选项C）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。然而，该修正案并不禁止美国提起的诉讼（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：第十修正案使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干扰国家基本职能的联邦税。\n\n参考文献\n• New York v. United States, 326 U.S. 572, 582 (1946)（认为州不能免受不干扰基本州职能的非歧视性联邦税的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十修正案使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干扰国家基本职能的联邦税。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院有可能驳回诉讼吗？","rule":"第十修正案使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干扰国家基本职能的联邦税。","analysis":"联邦制\n\n（联邦和州政府之间的权力分配）\n\n宪法效力条款\n\n第六条\n• 优先考虑美国宪法、条约和联邦法律，而不是州至上条款宪法和法律\n• 禁止各州对联邦政府或其机构/机构进行监管/征税 第十修正案\n• 将美国宪法未授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州/人民\n• 禁止联邦政府要求各州颁布法律或执行联邦法律\n• 使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干预国家基本职能的联邦税。第一条赋予国会广泛的权力，为公共福利征税和支出。但这种力量并不是无限的（选择A）。根据第十修正案，国会不得向以下州征收联邦税：\n• 歧视国家，因为税收适用于国家及其机构，而不是私人或实体，或\n\n• 会不适当地干扰各州的基本职能，包括用于州执行传统政府行为（例如公立学校、州立公园）的财产或从该州获得的收入。\n\n然而，第十修正案并没有使各州免受适用于公共和私营部门的非歧视性联邦税，并且不干涉基本的国家职能（选项 D）。在这里，联邦销售税是非歧视性的，因为它适用于所有销售矿泉水的私人和公共矿泉所有者。该税收也不会过度干扰国家的基本职能，因为销售矿泉水（或任何其他产品）是一种商业行为，而不是传统的政府行为。因此，第十修正案并未使各州免于征税，因此法院不太可能驳回该诉讼。 （选项C）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。然而，该修正案并不禁止美国提起的诉讼（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：第十修正案使各州免于缴纳歧视各州或不适当地干扰国家基本职能的联邦税。\n\n参考文献\n• New York v. United States, 326 U.S. 572, 582 (1946)（认为州不能免受不干扰基本州职能的非歧视性联邦税的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为非歧视性税收不会干扰州政府的基本职能。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"bar","ipa":"/bˈɑɹ/","syllables":"bar","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 条, 棒, 酒吧, 栅, 障碍物；vt. 禁止, 阻挡, 妨碍；[计] 棒形图","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 条, 棒, 酒吧, 栅, 障碍物；vt. 禁止, 阻挡, 妨碍；[计] 棒形图","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:bars/d:barred/p:barred/i:barring/3:bars","sourceSentence":"However, this amendment does not bar actions brought by the United States (as seen here).","synonyms":["prevention","barroom","saloon","ginmill"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":45,"sourceQuestionPage":135,"sourceQuestionPages":[135],"sourceExplanationPages":[136,137],"sourceAnswerPages":[136,137],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-046","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"The legislatures of several states entered into an agreement in which each state pledged to curb its pollution emissions. The agreement authorized a commission that contained a representative of each state to investigate how the states could reach their pollution emission goals and propose solutions for the states to adopt. The commission determined that each state needed to commit a fixed percentage of its energy consumption to clean energy sources, and each state enacted legislation that implemented the commission's suggestion. As a result, the states' demand for electricity produced by a company that uses coal-burning plants has significantly declined. The company has filed suit against the commission in a federal district court, seeking a declaratory judgment that the states' agreement is unconstitutional. Assume that no federal statute applies. What action should the federal court take?","choices":{"A":"Dismiss the suit without reaching the merits, because the Eleventh Amendment bars suits of this kind in federal court.","B":"Dismiss the suit without reaching the merits, because the suit presents a nonjusticiable political question.","C":"Invalidate the states' agreement, because the states failed to obtain congressional consent.","D":"Uphold the states' agreement, because the states validly entered into an interstate compact."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Congressional consent for interstate compacts\n\nRequired States' political power increased at federal government's expense—eg, states:\n\n• cede territory to each other\n• exercise powers they cannot exercise on own Not States do not encroach on federal government supremacy—eg, states: required\n• enact reciprocal legislation for benefit of each state's residents (eg, state college tuition reciprocity)\n• form administrative body to study joint problems and make recommendations\n• create entity to operate transportation facility (eg, port authority) States generally may enter into agreements with each other (ie, interstates compacts) without permission from the federal government.* However, the compact clause requires that states obtain Congress's consent for interstate compacts that increase the compacting states' political power at the expense of the federal government. This situation arises when the compact grants states or an administrative body powers that they could not exercise on their own. Here, several states entered into an interstate compact to curb pollution emissions and created a commission that suggested that each state commit a fixed percentage of its energy consumption to clean energy sources. Each state did so. Since each state could have adopted energy guidelines without the commission's suggestion, the compact does not grant power to the states at the expense of the federal government. As a result, congressional consent was not required, and the federal court is likely to uphold the states' agreement (Choice C). *The compact clause also permits states to enter into agreements with foreign governments if Congress consents. (Choice A) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars suits in federal court against state governments. However, it does not prohibit suits against commissions created by interstate compacts (as seen here). (Choice B) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from hearing the merits of suits that present issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution—neither of which are seen here.\n\nEducational objective: The compact clause requires states to obtain Congress's consent for agreements between the states (ie, interstate compacts) that increase the political power of the compacting states at the expense of the federal government.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 10, cl. 3 (compact clause).\n\n• U.S. Steel Corp. v. Multistate Tax Comm'n, 434 U.S. 452, 473–76 (1978) (holding that congressional consent was not required when an interstate compact granted powers that compacting states could exercise on their own).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d States, Territories, and Dependencies § 10 (2020) (explaining when congressional consent is required for an interstate compact).","educationalObjective":"The compact clause requires states to obtain Congress's consent for agreements between the states (ie, interstate compacts) that increase the political power of the compacting states at the expense of the federal government.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 10, cl. 3 (compact clause).\n\n• U.S. Steel Corp. v. Multistate Tax Comm'n, 434 U.S. 452, 473–76 (1978) (holding that congressional consent was not required when an interstate compact granted powers that compacting states could exercise on their own).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d States, Territories, and Dependencies § 10 (2020) (explaining when congressional consent is required for an interstate compact).","rule":"The compact clause requires states to obtain Congress's consent for agreements between the states (ie, interstate compacts) that increase the political power of the compacting states at the expense of the federal government.","irac":{"issue":"What action should the federal court take?","rule":"The compact clause requires states to obtain Congress's consent for agreements between the states (ie, interstate compacts) that increase the political power of the compacting states at the expense of the federal government.","analysis":"Congressional consent for interstate compacts\n\nRequired States' political power increased at federal government's expense—eg, states:\n\n• cede territory to each other\n• exercise powers they cannot exercise on own Not States do not encroach on federal government supremacy—eg, states: required\n• enact reciprocal legislation for benefit of each state's residents (eg, state college tuition reciprocity)\n• form administrative body to study joint problems and make recommendations\n• create entity to operate transportation facility (eg, port authority) States generally may enter into agreements with each other (ie, interstates compacts) without permission from the federal government.* However, the compact clause requires that states obtain Congress's consent for interstate compacts that increase the compacting states' political power at the expense of the federal government. This situation arises when the compact grants states or an administrative body powers that they could not exercise on their own. Here, several states entered into an interstate compact to curb pollution emissions and created a commission that suggested that each state commit a fixed percentage of its energy consumption to clean energy sources. Each state did so. Since each state could have adopted energy guidelines without the commission's suggestion, the compact does not grant power to the states at the expense of the federal government. As a result, congressional consent was not required, and the federal court is likely to uphold the states' agreement (Choice C). *The compact clause also permits states to enter into agreements with foreign governments if Congress consents. (Choice A) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars suits in federal court against state governments. However, it does not prohibit suits against commissions created by interstate compacts (as seen here). (Choice B) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from hearing the merits of suits that present issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution—neither of which are seen here.","conclusion":"Answer D: Uphold the states' agreement, because the states validly entered into an interstate compact."},"questionZh":"几个州的立法机构达成了一项协议，每个州都承诺遏制其污染排放。该协议授权成立一个由各州代表组成的委员会，调查各州如何实现污染排放目标，并提出解决方案供各州采用。该委员会确定每个州都需要将其能源消耗的固定比例用于清洁能源，并且每个州都颁布了立法来实施该委员会的建议。结果，各州对燃煤电厂公司生产的电力的需求大幅下降。该公司已向联邦地区法院对该委员会提起诉讼，寻求宣告性判决，认定各州的协议违宪。假设没有适用的联邦法规。联邦法院应该采取什么行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"在未确定实质内容的情况下驳回诉讼，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院提起此类诉讼。","B":"在未确定实质内容的情况下驳回诉讼，因为该诉讼提出了不可审理的政治问题。","C":"各州的协议无效，因为各州未能获得国会的同意。","D":"维护各州的协议，因为各州有效地签订了州际契约。"},"explanationZh":"国会同意州际契约\n\n要求各州的政治权力以牺牲联邦政府为代价而增加，例如，各州：\n\n• 互相割让领土\n• 行使他们无法自行行使的权力 否 各州不侵犯联邦政府的至高无上权——例如各州：必需\n• 为各州居民的利益制定互惠立法（例如，州立大学学费互惠）\n• 成立行政机构来研究共同问题并提出建议\n• 创建实体来运营运输设施（例如港务局） 各州通常可以在未经联邦政府许可的情况下相互签订协议（即州际契约）。*但是，契约条款要求各州就州际契约获得国会的同意，这会以联邦政府的利益为代价增加各州的政治权力。当契约授予国家或行政机构无法自行行使的权力时，就会出现这种情况。在这里，几个州签订了州际契约以遏制污染排放，并成立了一个委员会，建议每个州将其能源消耗的固定百分比用于清洁能源。每个州都这样做了。由于每个州都可以在没有委员会建议的情况下采用能源指南，因此该契约不会以牺牲联邦政府为代价来授予各州权力。因此，不需要国会同意，联邦法院很可能维持各州的协议（选项C）。 *如果国会同意，该契约条款还允许各州与外国政府签订协议。 （选项 A）第十一修正案一般禁止在联邦法院针对州政府提起诉讼。然而，它并不禁止对州际契约设立的委员会提起诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项 B）政治问题原则阻止联邦法院审理提出以下问题的诉讼的是非曲直：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准——这两种问题在这里都没有看到。\n\n教育目标：契约条款要求各州就各州之间的协议（即州际契约）获得国会的同意，以牺牲联邦政府的利益来增加契约州的政治权力。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 10，cl。 3（紧凑条款）。\n\n• U.S. Steel Corp. v. Multistate Tax Comm'n, 434 U.S. 452, 473–76 (1978)（认为当州际契约授予契约州可以自行行使的权力时，不需要国会同意）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 州、领地和属地 § 10 (2020)（解释州际契约何时需要国会同意）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"契约条款要求各州就州间协议（即州际契约）获得国会的同意，以牺牲联邦政府的利益来增加契约州的政治权力。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法院应该采取什么行动？","rule":"契约条款要求各州就州间协议（即州际契约）获得国会的同意，以牺牲联邦政府的利益来增加契约州的政治权力。","analysis":"国会同意州际契约\n\n要求各州的政治权力以牺牲联邦政府为代价而增加，例如，各州：\n\n• 互相割让领土\n• 行使他们无法自行行使的权力 否 各州不侵犯联邦政府的至高无上权——例如各州：必需\n• 为各州居民的利益制定互惠立法（例如，州立大学学费互惠）\n• 成立行政机构来研究共同问题并提出建议\n• 创建实体来运营运输设施（例如港务局） 各州通常可以在未经联邦政府许可的情况下相互签订协议（即州际契约）。*但是，契约条款要求各州就州际契约获得国会的同意，这会以联邦政府的利益为代价增加各州的政治权力。当契约授予国家或行政机构无法自行行使的权力时，就会出现这种情况。在这里，几个州签订了州际契约以遏制污染排放，并成立了一个委员会，建议每个州将其能源消耗的固定百分比用于清洁能源。每个州都这样做了。由于每个州都可以在没有委员会建议的情况下采用能源指南，因此该契约不会以牺牲联邦政府为代价来授予各州权力。因此，不需要国会同意，联邦法院很可能维持各州的协议（选项C）。 *如果国会同意，该契约条款还允许各州与外国政府签订协议。 （选项 A）第十一修正案一般禁止在联邦法院针对州政府提起诉讼。然而，它并不禁止对州际契约设立的委员会提起诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项 B）政治问题原则阻止联邦法院审理提出以下问题的诉讼的是非曲直：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准——这两种问题在这里都没有看到。\n\n教育目标：契约条款要求各州就各州之间的协议（即州际契约）获得国会的同意，以牺牲联邦政府的利益来增加契约州的政治权力。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 10，cl。 3（紧凑条款）。\n\n• U.S. Steel Corp. v. Multistate Tax Comm'n, 434 U.S. 452, 473–76 (1978)（认为当州际契约授予契约州可以自行行使的权力时，不需要国会同意）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 州、领地和属地 § 10 (2020)（解释州际契约何时需要国会同意）。","conclusion":"答案 D：维护各州的协议，因为各州有效地签订了州际契约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"political question","ipa":"/pʌlˈɪtəkəl kwˈɛstʃən/","syllables":"po·lit·i·cal ques·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"政治问题：因宪法交由政治部门或缺乏司法标准而不可审理的问题","contextualMeaning":"政治问题：因宪法交由政治部门或缺乏司法标准而不可审理的问题","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Dismiss the suit without reaching the merits, because the suit presents a nonjusticiable political question.","synonyms":["political doubt","political dubiousness","political doubtfulness"]},{"term":"declaratory","ipa":"/dɪklˈɛɹʌtˌɔɹi/","syllables":"de·clara·tory","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 宣言的；公布的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 宣言的；公布的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The company has filed suit against the commission in a federal district court, seeking a declaratory judgment that the states' agreement is unconstitutional.","synonyms":["declarative","asserting"]},{"term":"judicially","ipa":"/dʒudˈɪʃəli/","syllables":"ju·di·cially","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 依法判决地, 公平地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 依法判决地, 公平地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from hearing the merits of suits that present issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution—neither of which are seen here.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"executive","ipa":"/ɪɡzˈɛkjətɪv/","syllables":"ex·ec·u·tive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 执行部门, 执行委员会, 执行者, 经理主管人员；a. 执行的, 善于执行的, 善于经营的；[计] 执行程序","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 执行部门, 执行委员会, 执行者, 经理主管人员；a. 执行的, 善于执行的, 善于经营的；[计] 执行程序","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:executives","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from hearing the merits of suits that present issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution—neither of which are seen here.","synonyms":["administrator","executive director"]},{"term":"commit","ipa":"/kʌmˈɪt/","syllables":"com·mit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 委托(托付), 犯罪, 指派...作战, 使承担义务；[法] 犯, 做, 把...交托给","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 委托(托付), 犯罪, 指派...作战, 使承担义务；[法] 犯, 做, 把...交托给","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:committed/i:committing/p:committed/3:commits","sourceSentence":"The commission determined that each state needed to commit a fixed percentage of its energy consumption to clean energy sources, and each state enacted legislation that implemented the commission's suggestion.","synonyms":["give","dedicate","consecrate","devote"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":46,"sourceQuestionPage":138,"sourceQuestionPages":[138],"sourceExplanationPages":[139,140],"sourceAnswerPages":[139,140],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-047","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Appointments & Removal","question":"A federal statute with inseverable provisions established a new five-member National Prosperity Board with broad regulatory powers over the operation of the securities, banking, and commodities industries, including the power to issue rules with the force of law. The statute provides for three of the board members to be appointed by the President with the advice and consent of the Senate. They serve seven-year terms and are removable only for good cause. The other two members of the board were designated in the statute to be the respective general counsel of the Senate and House of Representatives Committees on Government Operations. The statute stipulated that they were to serve on the board for as long as they continued in those positions. Following all required administrative procedures, the board issued an elaborate set of rules regulating the operations of all banks, securities dealers, and commodities brokers. A securities company, which was subject to the board's rules, sought a declaratory judgment that the rules were invalid because the statute establishing the board was unconstitutional. In this case, should the court rule that the statute establishing the National Prosperity Board is constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because all members of federal boards exercising executive powers must be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the appointments clause of Article II.","B":"No, because all members of federal boards having broad powers that are quasi- legislative in nature, such as rulemaking, must be appointed by Congress.","C":"Yes, because the necessary and proper clause authorizes Congress to determine the means by which members are appointed to boards created by Congress under its power to regulate commerce among the states.","D":"Yes, because there is a substantial nexus between the power of Congress to legislate for the general welfare and the means specified by Congress in this statute for the appointment of board members."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Appointment of federal officers\n\n(Art. II, § 2 appointments clause)\n\nType Examples Appointment\n\nPrincipal\n• Ambassador Appointed by President (with Senate approval)\n• Supreme Court justice\n• Cabinet official Inferior\n• Independent counsel Appointed by President (with Senate approval)\n• Judicial clerk unless Congress delegates appointment to:\n• Administrative law\n• President alone judge\n• federal courts or\n• heads of executive departments Federal officers are persons who:\n• hold a continuing public office (eg, for a term of years) and\n• have significant discretionary authority to administer and/or enforce laws (ie, executive powers). All federal officers must be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the Article II appointments clause. Under this clause, Congress cannot appoint federal officers. Here, the National Prosperity Board comprises three members who serve seven-year terms and two who serve indefinitely—all of whom hold a continuing public office. And they all exercise executive powers since they have significant discretionary authority to issue rules with the force of law. As a result, they are federal officers who must be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the appointments clause. And since the applicable statute allows Congress to appoint two members, it is unconstitutional. (Choice B) Congress may appoint persons to exercise quasi-legislative powers that are investigative or informative in nature. But in Buckley v. Valeo, the Supreme Court held that rulemaking is an administrative power that can only be exercised by federal officers (who can never be appointed by Congress). (Choice C) The necessary and proper clause authorizes Congress to enact laws that are reasonably appropriate to carry out its express powers—including the power to regulate commerce among the states. But Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions when doing so.\n\n(Choice D) Article I, section 8 grants Congress the power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But Congress is not taxing or spending here, so this power does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: Federal officers are persons who hold a continuing public office AND have significant discretionary authority to administer and/or enforce laws (ie, executive powers). They can only be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the Article II appointments clause—never by Congress.\n\nReferences\n• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 132–33 (1976) (holding that federal officers must be appointed by the President or in a manner consistent with the appointments clause, which does not allow congressional appointments).\n\n• Lucia v. SEC, 138 S. Ct. 2044, 2053 (2018) (describing the requirements for a federal officer).","educationalObjective":"Federal officers are persons who hold a continuing public office AND have significant discretionary authority to administer and/or enforce laws (ie, executive powers). They can only be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the Article II appointments clause—never by Congress.","references":"• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 132–33 (1976) (holding that federal officers must be appointed by the President or in a manner consistent with the appointments clause, which does not allow congressional appointments).\n\n• Lucia v. SEC, 138 S. Ct. 2044, 2053 (2018) (describing the requirements for a federal officer).","rule":"Federal officers are persons who hold a continuing public office AND have significant discretionary authority to administer and/or enforce laws (ie, executive powers). They can only be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the Article II appointments clause—never by Congress.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, should the court rule that the statute establishing the National Prosperity Board is constitutional?","rule":"Federal officers are persons who hold a continuing public office AND have significant discretionary authority to administer and/or enforce laws (ie, executive powers). They can only be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the Article II appointments clause—never by Congress.","analysis":"Appointment of federal officers\n\n(Art. II, § 2 appointments clause)\n\nType Examples Appointment\n\nPrincipal\n• Ambassador Appointed by President (with Senate approval)\n• Supreme Court justice\n• Cabinet official Inferior\n• Independent counsel Appointed by President (with Senate approval)\n• Judicial clerk unless Congress delegates appointment to:\n• Administrative law\n• President alone judge\n• federal courts or\n• heads of executive departments Federal officers are persons who:\n• hold a continuing public office (eg, for a term of years) and\n• have significant discretionary authority to administer and/or enforce laws (ie, executive powers). All federal officers must be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the Article II appointments clause. Under this clause, Congress cannot appoint federal officers. Here, the National Prosperity Board comprises three members who serve seven-year terms and two who serve indefinitely—all of whom hold a continuing public office. And they all exercise executive powers since they have significant discretionary authority to issue rules with the force of law. As a result, they are federal officers who must be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the appointments clause. And since the applicable statute allows Congress to appoint two members, it is unconstitutional. (Choice B) Congress may appoint persons to exercise quasi-legislative powers that are investigative or informative in nature. But in Buckley v. Valeo, the Supreme Court held that rulemaking is an administrative power that can only be exercised by federal officers (who can never be appointed by Congress). (Choice C) The necessary and proper clause authorizes Congress to enact laws that are reasonably appropriate to carry out its express powers—including the power to regulate commerce among the states. But Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions when doing so.\n\n(Choice D) Article I, section 8 grants Congress the power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But Congress is not taxing or spending here, so this power does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because all members of federal boards exercising executive powers must be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the appointments clause of Article II."},"questionZh":"一项具有不可分割条款的联邦法规设立了一个由五名成员组成的新国家繁荣委员会，对证券、银行和大宗商品行业的运营拥有广泛的监管权力，包括发布具有法律效力的规则的权力。该法规规定，三名董事会成员由总统在参议院的建议和同意下任命。他们的任期为七年，只有在有正当理由的情况下才可以罢免。章程中指定董事会的另外两名成员分别担任参议院和众议院政府运作委员会的总法律顾问。法规规定，只要他们继续担任这些职位，他们就必须在董事会任职。按照所有必要的行政程序，董事会发布了一套详细的规则来规范所有银行、证券交易商和商品经纪人的运营。一家受董事会规则约束的证券公司因设立董事会的法规违宪，请求宣告该规则无效。在这种情况下，法院是否应该裁定设立国家繁荣委员会的法规符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为行使行政权力的联邦委员会的所有成员都必须由总统任命，或者以符合第二条任命条款的方式任命。","B":"不可以，因为联邦委员会的所有成员都拥有准立法性质的广泛权力，例如制定规则，必须由国会任命。","C":"是的，因为必要且适当的条款授权国会决定任命国会成员进入由国会根据其监管各州之间商业权力而设立的委员会的方式。","D":"是的，因为国会为公共福利立法的权力与国会在本法规中指定的董事会成员任命方式之间存在着实质性的联系。"},"explanationZh":"任命联邦官员\n\n（第 II 条第 2 款任命条款）\n\n类型 示例 预约\n\n校长\n• 大使由总统任命（经参议院批准）\n• 最高法院法官\n• 内阁官员下级\n• 独立律师由总统任命（经参议院批准）\n• 司法书记员，除非国会将任命委托给：\n• 行政法\n• 总统独自判断\n• 联邦法院或\n• 行政部门负责人 联邦官员是：\n• 担任持续的公职（例如，任期数年）并且\n• 拥有管理和/或执行法律的重要自由裁量权（即行政权力）。所有联邦官员必须由总统任命或以符合第二条任命条款的方式任命。根据该条款，国会不能任命联邦官员。在这里，国家繁荣委员会由三名任期七年的成员和两名无限期任职的成员组成，他们都担任连续的公职。他们都行使行政权力，因为他们拥有重大的自由裁量权，可以发布具有法律效力的规则。因此，他们是联邦官员，必须由总统任命或以符合任命条款的方式任命。由于适用的法规允许国会任命两名成员，因此这是违宪的。 （选择 B）国会可以任命人员行使调查或提供信息性质的准立法权。但在巴克利诉法雷奥案中，最高法院认为，规则制定是一项行政权力，只能由联邦官员（永远不能由国会任命）行使。 （选项 C）必要且适当的条款授权国会颁布合理适当的法律来履行其明确的权力，包括规范各州之间商业的权力。但国会这样做时不能违反其他宪法条款。\n\n（选项 D）第一条第 8 节赋予国会为公共福利征税和支出的权力。但国会不在这里征税或支出，因此这项权力不适用。\n\n教育目标：联邦官员是担任持续公职并拥有管理和/或执行法律的重大自由裁量权（即行政权）的人员。他们只能由总统或以符合第二条任命条款的其他方式任命，而不能由国会任命。\n\n参考文献\n• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 132–33 (1976)（认为联邦官员必须由总统任命或以符合任命条款的方式任命，该条款不允许国会任命）。\n\n• Lucia 诉 SEC，138 S. Ct. 2044、2053 (2018)（描述了对联邦官员的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联邦官员是持续担任公职并拥有管理和/或执行法律的重大自由裁量权（即行政权力）的人员。他们只能由总统或以符合第二条任命条款的其他方式任命，而不能由国会任命。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种情况下，法院是否应该裁定设立国家繁荣委员会的法规符合宪法？","rule":"联邦官员是持续担任公职并拥有管理和/或执行法律的重大自由裁量权（即行政权力）的人员。他们只能由总统或以符合第二条任命条款的其他方式任命，而不能由国会任命。","analysis":"任命联邦官员\n\n（第 II 条第 2 款任命条款）\n\n类型 示例 预约\n\n校长\n• 大使由总统任命（经参议院批准）\n• 最高法院法官\n• 内阁官员下级\n• 独立律师由总统任命（经参议院批准）\n• 司法书记员，除非国会将任命委托给：\n• 行政法\n• 总统独自判断\n• 联邦法院或\n• 行政部门负责人 联邦官员是：\n• 担任持续的公职（例如，任期数年）并且\n• 拥有管理和/或执行法律的重要自由裁量权（即行政权力）。所有联邦官员必须由总统任命或以符合第二条任命条款的方式任命。根据该条款，国会不能任命联邦官员。在这里，国家繁荣委员会由三名任期七年的成员和两名无限期任职的成员组成，他们都担任连续的公职。他们都行使行政权力，因为他们拥有重大的自由裁量权，可以发布具有法律效力的规则。因此，他们是联邦官员，必须由总统任命或以符合任命条款的方式任命。由于适用的法规允许国会任命两名成员，因此这是违宪的。 （选择 B）国会可以任命人员行使调查或提供信息性质的准立法权。但在巴克利诉法雷奥案中，最高法院认为，规则制定是一项行政权力，只能由联邦官员（永远不能由国会任命）行使。 （选项 C）必要且适当的条款授权国会颁布合理适当的法律来履行其明确的权力，包括规范各州之间商业的权力。但国会这样做时不能违反其他宪法条款。\n\n（选项 D）第一条第 8 节赋予国会为公共福利征税和支出的权力。但国会不在这里征税或支出，因此这项权力不适用。\n\n教育目标：联邦官员是担任持续公职并拥有管理和/或执行法律的重大自由裁量权（即行政权）的人员。他们只能由总统或以符合第二条任命条款的其他方式任命，而不能由国会任命。\n\n参考文献\n• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 132–33 (1976)（认为联邦官员必须由总统任命或以符合任命条款的方式任命，该条款不允许国会任命）。\n\n• Lucia 诉 SEC，138 S. Ct. 2044、2053 (2018)（描述了对联邦官员的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为行使行政权力的联邦委员会的所有成员都必须由总统任命，或者以符合第二条任命条款的方式任命。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"necessary and proper clause","ipa":"/nˈɛsʌsˌɛɹi ənd pɹˈɑpɝ klˈɔz/","syllables":"nec·es·sary and proper clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"必要与适当条款：允许国会采用适当手段执行列举权力","contextualMeaning":"必要与适当条款：允许国会采用适当手段执行列举权力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the necessary and proper clause authorizes Congress to determine the means by which members are appointed to boards created by Congress under its power to regulate commerce among the states.","synonyms":["necessary and proper article"]},{"term":"appointments clause","ipa":"/ʌpˈɔɪntmənts klˈɔz/","syllables":"ap·point·ments clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"任命条款：规定主要与次要联邦官员的任命方式","contextualMeaning":"任命条款：规定主要与次要联邦官员的任命方式","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because all members of federal boards exercising executive powers must be appointed by the President or in a manner otherwise consistent with the appointments clause of Article II.","synonyms":["appointments article"]},{"term":"delegates","ipa":"/dˈɛlʌɡˌeɪts/","syllables":"del·e·gates","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 代表, 代表团成员( delegate的名词复数 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 代表, 代表团成员( delegate的名词复数 )","wordForm":"词；原形 delegate；词形 0:delegate/1:s3","sourceSentence":"II, § 2 appointments clause) Type Examples Appointment Principal • Ambassador Appointed by President (with Senate approval) • Supreme Court justice • Cabinet official Inferior • Independent counsel Appointed by President (with Senate approval) • Judicial clerk unless Congress delegates appointment to: • Administrative law • President alone judge • federal courts or • heads of executive departments Federal officers ar","synonyms":["designate","depute","assign"]},{"term":"officers","ipa":"/ˈɔfəsɝz/","syllables":"of·fi·cers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 军官, 人员（officer复数形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 军官, 人员（officer复数形式）","wordForm":"词；原形 officer；词形 1:s3/0:officer","sourceSentence":"Federal officers are persons who hold a continuing public office AND have significant discretionary authority to administer and/or enforce laws (ie, executive powers).","synonyms":["military officer","officeholder","ship's officer","policeman"]},{"term":"counsel","ipa":"/kˈaʊnsəl/","syllables":"coun·sel","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 商议, 忠告, 法律顾问；v. 商议, 劝告","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 商议, 忠告, 法律顾问；v. 商议, 劝告","wordForm":"词；词形 i:counselling/p:counselled/s:counsels/d:counselled/3:counsels","sourceSentence":"The other two members of the board were designated in the statute to be the respective general counsel of the Senate and House of Representatives Committees on Government Operations.","synonyms":["guidance","counseling","counselling","direction"]},{"term":"justice","ipa":"/dʒˈəstəs/","syllables":"jus·tice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 正义, 公平, 公正, 正确, 司法, 审判","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 正义, 公平, 公正, 正确, 司法, 审判","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:justices","sourceSentence":"II, § 2 appointments clause) Type Examples Appointment Principal • Ambassador Appointed by President (with Senate approval) • Supreme Court justice • Cabinet official Inferior • Independent counsel Appointed by President (with Senate approval) • Judicial clerk unless Congress delegates appointment to: • Administrative law • President alone judge • federal courts or • heads of executive departments Federal officers ar","synonyms":["justness","Department of Justice","Justice Department","DoJ"]},{"term":"clerk","ipa":"/klˈɝk/","syllables":"clerk","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 办事员, 职员, 文书；vi. 当店员","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 办事员, 职员, 文书；vi. 当店员","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:clerks/i:clerking/p:clerked/3:clerks/d:clerked","sourceSentence":"II, § 2 appointments clause) Type Examples Appointment Principal • Ambassador Appointed by President (with Senate approval) • Supreme Court justice • Cabinet official Inferior • Independent counsel Appointed by President (with Senate approval) • Judicial clerk unless Congress delegates appointment to: • Administrative law • President alone judge • federal courts or • heads of executive departments Federal officers ar","synonyms":["salesclerk","shop clerk","shop assistant"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":47,"sourceQuestionPage":141,"sourceQuestionPages":[141],"sourceExplanationPages":[142,143],"sourceAnswerPages":[142,143],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-048","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Eleventh Amendment & State Sovereign Immunity","question":"A drawbridge owned, operated, and maintained by a county government malfunctioned. A portion of the drawbridge fell and collided with a boat owned by a man who resides in another state. The man filed a lawsuit against the county in a federal district court to recover for the damage caused to his boat. The county has moved to dismiss the suit. Is the court likely to grant the county's motion?","choices":{"A":"No.","B":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits suits against county governments in federal court.","C":"Yes, because the Tenth Amendment gives state courts exclusive jurisdiction over suits against state, county, and municipal governments.","D":"Yes, because the U.S. Supreme Court has original and exclusive jurisdiction over suits against governments."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment prohibits foreign governments and private parties from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. However, this immunity does not extend to actions brought against local governments (eg, municipalities, counties). Therefore, the Eleventh Amendment does not apply to the man's suit against the county, and the federal court will likely deny the county's motion to dismiss (Choice B). (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government. However, it does not give state courts exclusive jurisdiction over suits against state, county, and municipal governments. Instead, state and federal courts have concurrent jurisdiction over such suits. (Choice D) The U.S. Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases that affect ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls OR that involve a state as a party (neither seen here). Additionally, the Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction over suits between states (also not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state governments in federal court. However, this immunity does not extend to suits against local governments (eg, counties, municipalities).\n\nReferences\n• N. Ins. Co. v. Chatham County, 547 U.S. 189, 193–95 (2006) (holding that the Eleventh Amendment does not prohibit suits against local governments).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 918 (2020) (explaining that the Eleventh Amendment does not bar suits against local governments in federal court).","educationalObjective":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state governments in federal court. However, this immunity does not extend to suits against local governments (eg, counties, municipalities).","references":"• N. Ins. Co. v. Chatham County, 547 U.S. 189, 193–95 (2006) (holding that the Eleventh Amendment does not prohibit suits against local governments).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 918 (2020) (explaining that the Eleventh Amendment does not bar suits against local governments in federal court).","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state governments in federal court. However, this immunity does not extend to suits against local governments (eg, counties, municipalities).","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to grant the county's motion?","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state governments in federal court. However, this immunity does not extend to suits against local governments (eg, counties, municipalities).","analysis":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment prohibits foreign governments and private parties from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. However, this immunity does not extend to actions brought against local governments (eg, municipalities, counties). Therefore, the Eleventh Amendment does not apply to the man's suit against the county, and the federal court will likely deny the county's motion to dismiss (Choice B). (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government. However, it does not give state courts exclusive jurisdiction over suits against state, county, and municipal governments. Instead, state and federal courts have concurrent jurisdiction over such suits. (Choice D) The U.S. Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases that affect ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls OR that involve a state as a party (neither seen here). Additionally, the Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction over suits between states (also not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: No."},"questionZh":"一座由县政府拥有、运营和维护的吊桥发生故障。吊桥的一部分倒塌并与居住在另一个州的一名男子拥有的一艘船相撞。该男子向联邦地区法院向该县提起诉讼，要求赔偿对其船只造成的损失。该县已采取行动驳回诉讼。法院可能会批准县政府的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不。","B":"是的，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院对县政府提起诉讼。","C":"是的，因为第十修正案赋予州法院对针对州、县和市政府的诉讼的专属管辖权。","D":"是的，因为美国最高法院对针对政府的诉讼拥有原始和专属管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案禁止外国政府和私人团体在未经州政府同意的情况下向联邦法院起诉该州。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于针对地方政府（例如市、县）提起的诉讼。因此，第十一修正案不适用于该男子对县的诉讼，联邦法院很可能会驳回县的驳回动议（选择 B）。 （选项 C）第十修正案将宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州。然而，它并没有赋予州法院对针对州、县和市政府的诉讼的专属管辖权。相反，州法院和联邦法院对此类诉讼具有并行管辖权。 （选项 D）美国最高法院对影响大使、公共部长或领事的案件或涉及州作为一方的案件（此处均未示出）拥有初审管辖权。此外，最高法院对州之间的诉讼拥有专属管辖权（此处也未显示）。\n\n教育目标：第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院对州政府提起诉讼。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• N.Ins。 Co. v. Chatham County, 547 U.S. 189, 193–95 (2006)（认为第十一修正案不禁止针对地方政府提起诉讼）。\n\n• 32A 上午。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 918 (2020)（解释第十一修正案并不禁止在联邦法院针对地方政府提起诉讼）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十一条修正案禁止在联邦法院对州政府提起诉讼。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会批准县政府的动议吗？","rule":"第十一条修正案禁止在联邦法院对州政府提起诉讼。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼。","analysis":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案禁止外国政府和私人团体在未经州政府同意的情况下向联邦法院起诉该州。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于针对地方政府（例如市、县）提起的诉讼。因此，第十一修正案不适用于该男子对县的诉讼，联邦法院很可能会驳回县的驳回动议（选择 B）。 （选项 C）第十修正案将宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州。然而，它并没有赋予州法院对针对州、县和市政府的诉讼的专属管辖权。相反，州法院和联邦法院对此类诉讼具有并行管辖权。 （选项 D）美国最高法院对影响大使、公共部长或领事的案件或涉及州作为一方的案件（此处均未示出）拥有初审管辖权。此外，最高法院对州之间的诉讼拥有专属管辖权（此处也未显示）。\n\n教育目标：第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院对州政府提起诉讼。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• N.Ins。 Co. v. Chatham County, 547 U.S. 189, 193–95 (2006)（认为第十一修正案不禁止针对地方政府提起诉讼）。\n\n• 32A 上午。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 918 (2020)（解释第十一修正案并不禁止在联邦法院针对地方政府提起诉讼）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"retroactive","ipa":"/ɹˌɛtɹoʊˈæktɪv/","syllables":"retroac·tive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 反动的, 追溯的, 有追溯效力的；[经] 有追溯效力的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 反动的, 追溯的, 有追溯效力的；[经] 有追溯效力的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Eleventh Amendment (state immunity from suit in federal court) Immunity • Suit brought by private party or foreign government • Suit against state official violating state law • Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued fo","synonyms":["ex post facto","retro"]},{"term":"injunctive","ipa":"/ˌɪndʒˈəŋktɪv/","syllables":"in·junc·tive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 命令的, 禁令的, 指令的；[法] 禁令的, 指令的, 命令的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 命令的, 禁令的, 指令的；[法] 禁令的, 指令的, 命令的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Eleventh Amendment (state immunity from suit in federal court) Immunity • Suit brought by private party or foreign government • Suit against state official violating state law • Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued fo","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"ministers","ipa":"/mˈɪnəstɝz/","syllables":"min·is·ters","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 大臣( minister的名词复数 )； 部长； 公使","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 大臣( minister的名词复数 )； 部长； 公使","wordForm":"词；原形 minister；词形 1:s3/0:minister","sourceSentence":"Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases that affect ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls OR that involve a state as a party (neither seen here).","synonyms":["curate","minister of religion","parson","pastor"]},{"term":"relief","ipa":"/ɹɪlˈif/","syllables":"re·lief","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 减轻, 解除, 救济, 安慰, 调剂, 浮雕, 换班, (地势的)起伏；[医] 缓减, 减轻, 浮雕(绘画中)","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 减轻, 解除, 救济, 安慰, 调剂, 浮雕, 换班, (地势的)起伏；[医] 缓减, 减轻, 浮雕(绘画中)","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:reliefs","sourceSentence":"Eleventh Amendment (state immunity from suit in federal court) Immunity • Suit brought by private party or foreign government • Suit against state official violating state law • Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued fo","synonyms":["easing","easement","alleviation","succor"]},{"term":"consuls","ipa":"/kˈɑnsəlz/","syllables":"con·suls","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 领事( consul的复数形式 )； （古罗马共和国时期）执政官 （古罗马共和国及其军队的最高首长, 同时共有两位, 每年选举一次）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 领事( consul的复数形式 )； （古罗马共和国时期）执政官 （古罗马共和国及其军队的最高首长, 同时共有两位, 每年选举一次）","wordForm":"名词；原形 consul；词形 0:consul/1:s","sourceSentence":"Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases that affect ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls OR that involve a state as a party (neither seen here).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":48,"sourceQuestionPage":144,"sourceQuestionPages":[144],"sourceExplanationPages":[145,146],"sourceAnswerPages":[145,146],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-049","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"A state law that restricted access to contraception was challenged in state court as a violation of the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment to the U.S. Constitution and as a violation of a similar due process provision of the state constitution. The case made its way to the state's highest court, which ruled that the law violated the due process provisions of both the U.S. and the state constitutions. If petitioned to do so, may the U.S. Supreme Court exercise jurisdiction to review the state court decision?","choices":{"A":"No, because the state court's decision in this case rests on adequate and independent state law grounds.","B":"No, because the U.S. Supreme Court has appellate jurisdiction only over state court decisions that determine the constitutionality of federal laws.","C":"Yes, because the U.S. Supreme Court has appellate jurisdiction over any ruling of a state's highest court based on an interpretation of federal law.","D":"Yes, because the U.S. Supreme Court has appellate jurisdiction over decisions that find state laws in violation of the federal Constitution."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Appellate jurisdiction of SCOTUS\n\nType of review Applicability\n\nCertiorari\n• Appeal from lower federal court or highest state court by filing petition for writ of certiorari (discretionary\n• SCOTUS will review if ≥ 4 Justices vote to accept appeal review)\n• Very common – how most cases come before SCOTUS Direct appeal\n• Appeal from decision on injunction by special three-judge federal district court panel (mandatory\n• Extremely rare – limited to cases brought under specific review) statutes (eg, Voting Rights Act) SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has discretionary jurisdiction to review final state court decisions that involve federal law. But SCOTUS cannot exercise jurisdiction to review those decisions if they rest on adequate and independent state grounds—ie, when:\n• state law completely resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case (adequate) and\n\n• the state court did not rely on federal law to decide the state law issue (independent).\n\nThis limit on SCOTUS's jurisdiction promotes judicial economy by barring SCOTUS from deciding a federal question when it would not change the outcome of the case. Here, the highest state court's ruling rests on an adequate state ground because the court found the law invalid under the state constitution. So even if SCOTUS reversed the state court's ruling on the U.S. Constitution, the law would still violate the state constitution and the outcome of the case would remain the same. The ruling also rests on an independent state ground because the state court did not rely on federal law in ruling on the similar state constitutional issue.* As a result, SCOTUS may not exercise jurisdiction to review the state court decision. *A state court ruling is not independent if the state court relied on an \"identical\" federal provision to rule on the state provision. Here, the federal and state due process provisions are similar but not the same, so the ruling rests on an independent state ground. (Choice B) SCOTUS has appellate jurisdiction over state court decisions determining the constitutionality of federal laws AND the validity of state laws under the U.S. Constitution.\n\n(Choices C & D) SCOTUS has appellate jurisdiction over state court decisions based on federal law—including decisions that find state laws in violation of the federal constitution—unless that decision rests on adequate and independent state grounds.\n\nEducational objective: The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review state court decisions based on adequate and independent state grounds—ie, when state grounds completely resolve the matter and the court does not rely on federal law to reach that decision.\n\nReferences\n• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983) (explaining when a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state grounds).","educationalObjective":"The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review state court decisions based on adequate and independent state grounds—ie, when state grounds completely resolve the matter and the court does not rely on federal law to reach that decision.","references":"• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983) (explaining when a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state grounds).","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review state court decisions based on adequate and independent state grounds—ie, when state grounds completely resolve the matter and the court does not rely on federal law to reach that decision.","irac":{"issue":"Supreme Court exercise jurisdiction to review the state court decision?","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review state court decisions based on adequate and independent state grounds—ie, when state grounds completely resolve the matter and the court does not rely on federal law to reach that decision.","analysis":"Appellate jurisdiction of SCOTUS\n\nType of review Applicability\n\nCertiorari\n• Appeal from lower federal court or highest state court by filing petition for writ of certiorari (discretionary\n• SCOTUS will review if ≥ 4 Justices vote to accept appeal review)\n• Very common – how most cases come before SCOTUS Direct appeal\n• Appeal from decision on injunction by special three-judge federal district court panel (mandatory\n• Extremely rare – limited to cases brought under specific review) statutes (eg, Voting Rights Act) SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has discretionary jurisdiction to review final state court decisions that involve federal law. But SCOTUS cannot exercise jurisdiction to review those decisions if they rest on adequate and independent state grounds—ie, when:\n• state law completely resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case (adequate) and\n\n• the state court did not rely on federal law to decide the state law issue (independent).\n\nThis limit on SCOTUS's jurisdiction promotes judicial economy by barring SCOTUS from deciding a federal question when it would not change the outcome of the case. Here, the highest state court's ruling rests on an adequate state ground because the court found the law invalid under the state constitution. So even if SCOTUS reversed the state court's ruling on the U.S. Constitution, the law would still violate the state constitution and the outcome of the case would remain the same. The ruling also rests on an independent state ground because the state court did not rely on federal law in ruling on the similar state constitutional issue.* As a result, SCOTUS may not exercise jurisdiction to review the state court decision. *A state court ruling is not independent if the state court relied on an \"identical\" federal provision to rule on the state provision. Here, the federal and state due process provisions are similar but not the same, so the ruling rests on an independent state ground. (Choice B) SCOTUS has appellate jurisdiction over state court decisions determining the constitutionality of federal laws AND the validity of state laws under the U.S. Constitution.\n\n(Choices C & D) SCOTUS has appellate jurisdiction over state court decisions based on federal law—including decisions that find state laws in violation of the federal constitution—unless that decision rests on adequate and independent state grounds.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the state court's decision in this case rests on adequate and independent state law grounds."},"questionZh":"一项限制避孕的州法律在州法院受到质疑，因为该法律违反了美国宪法第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），并且违反了州宪法中类似的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。该案件已提交该州最高法院，该法院裁定该法律违反了美国和州宪法的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。如果请求这样做，美国最高法院是否可以行使管辖权来审查州法院的裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为州法院在本案中的判决是基于充分且独立的州法律依据。","B":"不可以，因为美国最高法院仅对确定联邦法律是否符合宪法的州法院判决拥有上诉管辖权。","C":"是的，因为美国最高法院对州最高法院根据联邦法律的解释做出的任何裁决拥有上诉管辖权。","D":"是的，因为美国最高法院对认定州法律违反联邦宪法的决定拥有上诉管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"最高法院的上诉管辖权\n\n审查类型 适用性\n\n调卷卷\n• 通过提交调卷令状申请，向下级联邦法院或最高州法院提出上诉（酌情决定）\n• 如果≥ 4 位法官投票接受上诉审查，最高法院将进行审查）\n• 非常常见——大多数案件都会提交 SCOTUS 直接上诉\n• 对由三名法官组成的联邦地方法院特别小组作出的禁令决定提出上诉（强制\n• 极其罕见——仅限于受到特定审查的案件）法规（例如投票权法案） SCOTUS = 美国最高法院\n\n美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有自由裁量权，可以审查涉及联邦法律的州法院的最终裁决。但如果这些决定基于充分且独立的州理由，即当：\n• 州法律完全解决了该问题，因此联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果（充分）并且\n\n• 州法院不依赖联邦法律来裁决州法律问题（独立）。\n\n对最高法院管辖权的这种限制禁止最高法院在不会改变案件结果的情况下决定联邦问题，从而促进了司法经济。在这里，最高州法院的裁决有充分的州理由，因为法院认为该法律根据州宪法无效。因此，即使最高法院推翻了州法院对美国宪法的裁决，该法律仍然违反州宪法，案件的结果也将保持不变。该裁决还基于独立的州理由，因为州法院在对类似的州宪法问题做出裁决时不依赖联邦法律。*因此，最高法院可能无法行使管辖权来审查州法院的裁决。 *如果州法院依据“相同”的联邦条款对州条款做出裁决，则州法院的裁决不具有独立性。在此，联邦和州的正当程序规定相似但不相同，因此裁决基于独立的州理由。 （选择 B）最高法院对州法院决定联邦法律的合宪性和州法律根据美国宪法的有效性的裁决拥有上诉管辖权。\n\n（选择 C ​​和 D）最高法院对州法院根据联邦法律做出的决定（包括认定州法律违反联邦宪法的决定）拥有上诉管辖权，除非该决定基于充分且独立的州理由。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院无权根据充分且独立的州理由审查州法院的决定，即当州理由完全解决问题并且法院不依赖联邦法律做出该决定时。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983)（解释州最高法院的判决何时基于充分且独立的州理由）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"美国最高法院无权根据充分且独立的州理由审查州法院的决定，即当州理由完全解决问题并且法院不依赖联邦法律做出该决定时。","iracZh":{"issue":"最高法院行使管辖权来审查州法院的判决吗？","rule":"美国最高法院无权根据充分且独立的州理由审查州法院的决定，即当州理由完全解决问题并且法院不依赖联邦法律做出该决定时。","analysis":"最高法院的上诉管辖权\n\n审查类型 适用性\n\n调卷卷\n• 通过提交调卷令状申请，向下级联邦法院或最高州法院提出上诉（酌情决定）\n• 如果≥ 4 位法官投票接受上诉审查，最高法院将进行审查）\n• 非常常见——大多数案件都会提交 SCOTUS 直接上诉\n• 对由三名法官组成的联邦地方法院特别小组作出的禁令决定提出上诉（强制\n• 极其罕见——仅限于受到特定审查的案件）法规（例如投票权法案） SCOTUS = 美国最高法院\n\n美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有自由裁量权，可以审查涉及联邦法律的州法院的最终裁决。但如果这些决定基于充分且独立的州理由，即当：\n• 州法律完全解决了该问题，因此联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果（充分）并且\n\n• 州法院不依赖联邦法律来裁决州法律问题（独立）。\n\n对最高法院管辖权的这种限制禁止最高法院在不会改变案件结果的情况下决定联邦问题，从而促进了司法经济。在这里，最高州法院的裁决有充分的州理由，因为法院认为该法律根据州宪法无效。因此，即使最高法院推翻了州法院对美国宪法的裁决，该法律仍然违反州宪法，案件的结果也将保持不变。该裁决还基于独立的州理由，因为州法院在对类似的州宪法问题做出裁决时不依赖联邦法律。*因此，最高法院可能无法行使管辖权来审查州法院的裁决。 *如果州法院依据“相同”的联邦条款对州条款做出裁决，则州法院的裁决不具有独立性。在此，联邦和州的正当程序规定相似但不相同，因此裁决基于独立的州理由。 （选择 B）最高法院对州法院决定联邦法律的合宪性和州法律根据美国宪法的有效性的裁决拥有上诉管辖权。\n\n（选择 C ​​和 D）最高法院对州法院根据联邦法律做出的决定（包括认定州法律违反联邦宪法的决定）拥有上诉管辖权，除非该决定基于充分且独立的州理由。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院无权根据充分且独立的州理由审查州法院的决定，即当州理由完全解决问题并且法院不依赖联邦法律做出该决定时。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983)（解释州最高法院的判决何时基于充分且独立的州理由）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为州法院在本案中的判决是基于充分且独立的州法律依据。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"adequate and independent state ground","ipa":"/ˈædəkwət ənd ˌɪndɪpˈɛndənt stˈeɪt ɡɹˈaʊnd/","syllables":"ad·e·quate and in·de·pen·dent state ground","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"充分且独立的州法依据：足以决定结果且不依赖联邦法的州法理由","contextualMeaning":"充分且独立的州法依据：足以决定结果且不依赖联邦法的州法理由","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review state court decisions based on adequate and independent state grounds—ie, when state grounds completely resolve the matter and the court does not rely on federal law to reach that decision.","synonyms":["adequate and independent state flat coat","adequate and independent state primer","adequate and independent state priming"]},{"term":"petition","ipa":"/pʌtˈɪʃən/","syllables":"pe·ti·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 请愿, 诉状, 陈情书, 申请, 祈求, 祷文；v. 正式请求, 恳求, 请愿","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 请愿, 诉状, 陈情书, 申请, 祈求, 祷文；v. 正式请求, 恳求, 请愿","wordForm":"动词；词形 s:petitions/p:petitioned/d:petitioned/i:petitioning/3:petitions","sourceSentence":"If petitioned to do so, may the U.","synonyms":["request","postulation","prayer","orison"]},{"term":"certiorari","ipa":"/,sə:tiɔ:'reərai/","syllables":"cer·tio·rari","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 上级法院向下级法院/准司法机构调取案卷的令状；[经] 复审令","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 上级法院向下级法院/准司法机构调取案卷的令状；[经] 复审令","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:certiorari/0:certiorari/1:s","sourceSentence":"Appellate jurisdiction of SCOTUS Type of review Applicability Certiorari • Appeal from lower federal court or highest state court by filing petition for writ of certiorari (discretionary • SCOTUS will review if ≥ 4 Justices vote to accept appeal review) • Very common – how most cases come before SCOTUS Direct appeal • Appeal from decision on injunction by special three-judge federal district court panel (mandatory • ","synonyms":["writ of certiorari"]},{"term":"appellate","ipa":"/ʌpˈɛlɪt/","syllables":"ap·pel·late","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 上诉的, 有权受理上诉的；[法] 受理上诉的, 上诉的, 控诉的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 上诉的, 有权受理上诉的；[法] 受理上诉的, 上诉的, 控诉的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Supreme Court has appellate jurisdiction only over state court decisions that determine the constitutionality of federal laws.","synonyms":["appellant"]},{"term":"filing","ipa":"/fˈaɪlɪŋ/","syllables":"fil·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 锉, 琢磨, 锉屑；[计] 编档； 文件编排","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 锉, 琢磨, 锉屑；[计] 编档； 文件编排","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:file/1:i/i:filing/s:filings","sourceSentence":"Appellate jurisdiction of SCOTUS Type of review Applicability Certiorari • Appeal from lower federal court or highest state court by filing petition for writ of certiorari (discretionary • SCOTUS will review if ≥ 4 Justices vote to accept appeal review) • Very common – how most cases come before SCOTUS Direct appeal • Appeal from decision on injunction by special three-judge federal district court panel (mandatory • ","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"panel","ipa":"/pˈænəl/","syllables":"panel","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 嵌板, 仪表板, 专题讨论小组, 全体陪审员；vt. 嵌镶板","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 嵌板, 仪表板, 专题讨论小组, 全体陪审员；vt. 嵌镶板","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:panels/d:panelled/i:panelling/p:panelled/3:panels","sourceSentence":"Appellate jurisdiction of SCOTUS Type of review Applicability Certiorari • Appeal from lower federal court or highest state court by filing petition for writ of certiorari (discretionary • SCOTUS will review if ≥ 4 Justices vote to accept appeal review) • Very common – how most cases come before SCOTUS Direct appeal • Appeal from decision on injunction by special three-judge federal district court panel (mandatory • ","synonyms":["control panel","instrument panel","control board","board"]},{"term":"writ","ipa":"/ɹˈɪt/","syllables":"writ","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 令状, 文书, 文件, 书面命令；[经] 法院命令","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 令状, 文书, 文件, 书面命令；[经] 法院命令","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:writs/d:writ/0:writ/1:dp/p:writ","sourceSentence":"Appellate jurisdiction of SCOTUS Type of review Applicability Certiorari • Appeal from lower federal court or highest state court by filing petition for writ of certiorari (discretionary • SCOTUS will review if ≥ 4 Justices vote to accept appeal review) • Very common – how most cases come before SCOTUS Direct appeal • Appeal from decision on injunction by special three-judge federal district court panel (mandatory • ","synonyms":["judicial writ"]},{"term":"vote","ipa":"/vˈoʊt/","syllables":"vote","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 投票, 选举, 选票, 表决, 选举权, 得票数；vi. 投票, 选举；vt. 投票选举, 投票决定, 公认, 使投票","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 投票, 选举, 选票, 表决, 选举权, 得票数；vi. 投票, 选举；vt. 投票选举, 投票决定, 公认, 使投票","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:votes/d:voted/p:voted/i:voting/3:votes","sourceSentence":"Appellate jurisdiction of SCOTUS Type of review Applicability Certiorari • Appeal from lower federal court or highest state court by filing petition for writ of certiorari (discretionary • SCOTUS will review if ≥ 4 Justices vote to accept appeal review) • Very common – how most cases come before SCOTUS Direct appeal • Appeal from decision on injunction by special three-judge federal district court panel (mandatory • ","synonyms":["ballot","voting","balloting","right to vote"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":49,"sourceQuestionPage":147,"sourceQuestionPages":[147],"sourceExplanationPages":[148,149],"sourceAnswerPages":[148,149],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-050","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Mootness & Ripeness","question":"A state legislature recently enacted a statute forbidding public utilities regulated by the State Public Service Commission from increasing their rates more than once every two years. A public utility company regulated by that commission has just obtained approval from the commission for a general rate increase. The company has routinely filed for a rate increase every 10 to 14 months during the last 20 years. Because of uncertainties about future fuel prices, the company cannot ascertain with any certainty the date when it will need a further rate increase, but it thinks that it may need such an increase sometime within the next 18 months. The company files an action in the federal district court in the state requesting a declaratory judgment that this new state statute forbidding public utility rate increases more than once every two years is unconstitutional. Assume no federal statute is relevant. How should the court rule in this case?","choices":{"A":"Dismiss the complaint, because controversies over state-regulated utility rates are outside of the jurisdiction conferred on federal courts by Article III of the Constitution.","B":"Dismiss the complaint, because this action is not ripe for decision.","C":"Hold the statute constitutional, because the judgment of a legislature on a matter involving economic regulation is entitled to great deference.","D":"Hold the statute unconstitutional, because such a moratorium on rate increases deprives utilities of their property without due process of law."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Article III, Section 2 of the Constitution allows federal courts to hear cases and controversies that are ripe for adjudication (ie, capable of judicial resolution). This requirement is met when the plaintiff has suffered an actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. But a suit cannot be heard by a federal court if the plaintiff faces the mere possibility of harm (unripe) or the plaintiff's harm is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot). Here, the company filed a complaint in federal court requesting a declaratory judgment regarding the new statute limiting rate increases for public utilities to once every two years. But since the company had just obtained approval for a rate increase and merely thinks that it may need another rate increase in the next 18 months, it has suffered no actual harm or immediate threat of harm. Therefore, this action is unripe and should be dismissed. (Choice A) Article III grants federal courts subject-matter jurisdiction over claims arising under the Constitution, federal laws, or treaties. Therefore, the company's complaint regarding the constitutionality of the new statute falls within the court's conferred jurisdiction and will not be dismissed on this basis. (Choices C & D) A determination as to whether (1) the court should defer to the legislature's economic judgment or (2) the moratorium is a deprivation of property without due process of law involves the merits of the suit. But since the action is not ripe for adjudication, the court cannot consider the merits and must dismiss the suit.\n\nEducational objective: Federal courts can only hear cases and controversies that are ripe for adjudication—ie, claims that concern an actual or immediate threat of harm suffered by the plaintiff. Therefore, courts must dismiss claims that allege the mere possibility of harm (unripe) or harm that is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot).\n\nReferences\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998) (explaining when a claim is unripe for adjudication).","educationalObjective":"Federal courts can only hear cases and controversies that are ripe for adjudication—ie, claims that concern an actual or immediate threat of harm suffered by the plaintiff. Therefore, courts must dismiss claims that allege the mere possibility of harm (unripe) or harm that is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot).","references":"• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998) (explaining when a claim is unripe for adjudication).","rule":"Federal courts can only hear cases and controversies that are ripe for adjudication—ie, claims that concern an actual or immediate threat of harm suffered by the plaintiff. Therefore, courts must dismiss claims that allege the mere possibility of harm (unripe) or harm that is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot).","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule in this case?","rule":"Federal courts can only hear cases and controversies that are ripe for adjudication—ie, claims that concern an actual or immediate threat of harm suffered by the plaintiff. Therefore, courts must dismiss claims that allege the mere possibility of harm (unripe) or harm that is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot).","analysis":"Article III, Section 2 of the Constitution allows federal courts to hear cases and controversies that are ripe for adjudication (ie, capable of judicial resolution). This requirement is met when the plaintiff has suffered an actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. But a suit cannot be heard by a federal court if the plaintiff faces the mere possibility of harm (unripe) or the plaintiff's harm is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot). Here, the company filed a complaint in federal court requesting a declaratory judgment regarding the new statute limiting rate increases for public utilities to once every two years. But since the company had just obtained approval for a rate increase and merely thinks that it may need another rate increase in the next 18 months, it has suffered no actual harm or immediate threat of harm. Therefore, this action is unripe and should be dismissed. (Choice A) Article III grants federal courts subject-matter jurisdiction over claims arising under the Constitution, federal laws, or treaties. Therefore, the company's complaint regarding the constitutionality of the new statute falls within the court's conferred jurisdiction and will not be dismissed on this basis. (Choices C & D) A determination as to whether (1) the court should defer to the legislature's economic judgment or (2) the moratorium is a deprivation of property without due process of law involves the merits of the suit. But since the action is not ripe for adjudication, the court cannot consider the merits and must dismiss the suit.","conclusion":"Answer B: Dismiss the complaint, because this action is not ripe for decision."},"questionZh":"州立法机构最近颁布了一项法规，禁止州公共服务委员会监管的公用事业公司每两年提高费率一次以上。受该委员会监管的一家公用事业公司刚刚获得该委员会批准普遍上调费率。在过去 20 年中，该公司通常每 10 至 14 个月申请一次加息。由于未来燃油价格的不确定性，该公司无法确定何时需要进一步加息，但它认为可能需要在未来 18 个月内的某个时候进行加息。该公司向该州联邦地方法院提起诉讼，要求作出宣告性判决，裁定这项禁止公用事业费率每两年增加一次以上的新州法规违宪。假设没有相关的联邦法规。对于本案，法院应当如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回申诉，因为有关国家监管的公用事业费率的争议不属于宪法第三条赋予联邦法院的管辖范围。","B":"驳回投诉，因为该行动尚未成熟。","C":"坚持立法符合宪法，因为立法机关对涉及经济监管的问题的判断应该得到极大的尊重。","D":"认为该法规违宪，因为暂停加息会在未经正当法律程序的情况下剥夺公用事业公司的财产。"},"explanationZh":"宪法第三条第二款允许联邦法院审理成熟的案件和争议（即能够司法解决）。当原告遭受实际伤害或直接威胁时，就满足了这一要求。但是，如果原告仅面临伤害的可能性（不成熟）或原告的伤害不再能够通过司法解决（没有实际意义），则联邦法院不能审理诉讼。在此，该公司向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求就新法规将公​​用事业费率上涨限制为每两年一次做出宣告性判决。但由于该公司刚刚获得加息批准，只是认为未来 18 个月内可能需要再次加息，因此并未遭受实际损害或直接损害威胁。因此，该行为不成熟，应予驳回。 （选项 A） 第三条授予联邦法院对根据宪法、联邦法律或条约提出的索赔的主题管辖权。因此，该公司关于新法规是否符合宪法的申诉属于法院赋予的管辖权，不会据此予以驳回。 （选项 C 和 D）决定是否 (1) 法院应服从立法机关的经济判决或 (2) 暂停是未经正当法律程序剥夺财产，涉及诉讼的是非曲直。但由于诉讼尚未成熟，法院无法考虑案情，必须驳回诉讼。\n\n教育目标：联邦法院只能审理判决时机成熟的案件和争议，即涉及原告遭受的实际或直接伤害威胁的索赔。因此，法院必须驳回声称仅存在损害可能性（不成熟）或损害不再能够通过司法解决（没有实际意义）的主张。\n\n参考文献\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998)（解释何时裁决尚不成熟）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联邦法院只能审理成熟的案件和争议，即涉及原告遭受的实际或直接伤害威胁的索赔。因此，法院必须驳回声称仅存在损害可能性（不成熟）或损害不再能够通过司法解决（没有实际意义）的主张。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于本案，法院应当如何判决？","rule":"联邦法院只能审理成熟的案件和争议，即涉及原告遭受的实际或直接伤害威胁的索赔。因此，法院必须驳回声称仅存在损害可能性（不成熟）或损害不再能够通过司法解决（没有实际意义）的主张。","analysis":"宪法第三条第二款允许联邦法院审理成熟的案件和争议（即能够司法解决）。当原告遭受实际伤害或直接威胁时，就满足了这一要求。但是，如果原告仅面临伤害的可能性（不成熟）或原告的伤害不再能够通过司法解决（没有实际意义），则联邦法院不能审理诉讼。在此，该公司向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求就新法规将公​​用事业费率上涨限制为每两年一次做出宣告性判决。但由于该公司刚刚获得加息批准，只是认为未来 18 个月内可能需要再次加息，因此并未遭受实际损害或直接损害威胁。因此，该行为不成熟，应予驳回。 （选项 A） 第三条授予联邦法院对根据宪法、联邦法律或条约提出的索赔的主题管辖权。因此，该公司关于新法规是否符合宪法的申诉属于法院赋予的管辖权，不会据此予以驳回。 （选项 C 和 D）决定是否 (1) 法院应服从立法机关的经济判决或 (2) 暂停是未经正当法律程序剥夺财产，涉及诉讼的是非曲直。但由于诉讼尚未成熟，法院无法考虑案情，必须驳回诉讼。\n\n教育目标：联邦法院只能审理判决时机成熟的案件和争议，即涉及原告遭受的实际或直接伤害威胁的索赔。因此，法院必须驳回声称仅存在损害可能性（不成熟）或损害不再能够通过司法解决（没有实际意义）的主张。\n\n参考文献\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998)（解释何时裁决尚不成熟）。","conclusion":"答案 B：驳回投诉，因为该行动尚未成熟。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"adjudication","ipa":"/ʌdʒˌudʌkˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"ad·ju·di·ca·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 判决, 裁定, 宣告；[经] 判决, 裁决","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 判决, 裁定, 宣告；[经] 判决, 裁决","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:adjudications","sourceSentence":"Federal courts can only hear cases and controversies that are ripe for adjudication—ie, claims that concern an actual or immediate threat of harm suffered by the plaintiff.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"complaint","ipa":"/kʌmplˈeɪnt/","syllables":"com·plaint","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 诉苦, 抱怨, 控诉；[医] 陈诉； 病","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 诉苦, 抱怨, 控诉；[医] 陈诉； 病","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:complaints","sourceSentence":"Dismiss the complaint, because controversies over state-regulated utility rates are outside of the jurisdiction conferred on federal courts by Article III of the Constitution.","synonyms":["charge","ailment","ill"]},{"term":"ripe","ipa":"/ɹˈaɪp/","syllables":"ripe","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 成熟的, 熟练的, 成年的；[医] 成熟的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 成熟的, 熟练的, 成年的；[医] 成熟的","wordForm":"形容词；词形 t:ripest/r:riper","sourceSentence":"Dismiss the complaint, because this action is not ripe for decision.","synonyms":["advanced","mature","good","right"]},{"term":"moot","ipa":"/mˈut/","syllables":"moot","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 大会, 模拟案件, 辩论会；a. 未决议的, 无实际意义的；vt. 讨论, 争论","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 大会, 模拟案件, 辩论会；a. 未决议的, 无实际意义的；vt. 讨论, 争论","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:mooted/s:moots/3:moots/i:mooting/p:mooted/r:mooter","sourceSentence":"Therefore, courts must dismiss claims that allege the mere possibility of harm (unripe) or harm that is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot).","synonyms":["consider","debate","turn over","deliberate"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-fcb4aaed424130d1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":50,"sourceQuestionPage":150,"sourceQuestionPages":[150],"sourceExplanationPages":[151,152],"sourceAnswerPages":[151,152],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-051","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"A national coalition of activists announced that it would be holding a rally at the Capitol to protest federal laws that criminalize the possession or use of cannabis. The rally has been advertised as a \"smoke-in,\" where the activists would smoke cannabis during the protest. The President, who has recently announced her support for the decriminalization of recreational cannabis under federal law, does not want any of the activists to face criminal charges for participating in the rally. The President has proposed to grant a group pardon to the activists before the rally begins. May the President constitutionally grant this pardon?","choices":{"A":"No, because the President may not grant a pardon before a federal offense has been committed.","B":"No, because the President may not grant a pardon to a group of people.","C":"Yes, because the President may grant a pardon for federal offenses.","D":"Yes, because the President's power to pardon is plenary."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Exclusive executive powers\n\n• Nominate principal officers\n• Veto bills\n• Prosecute & pardon federal offenses\n• Communicate & negotiate with foreign governments\n• Recognize foreign governments\n• Enter executive agreements Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary (ie, absolute) power to grant reprieves and pardons to persons or groups of people that have committed federal offenses—except in cases of impeachment (Choices C & D). But the President may grant a pardon only after the federal offense has been committed—not before. Since the President here seeks to grant a group pardon to the activists before they commit a federal offense by smoking cannabis at the rally, she may not constitutionally grant this pardon. (Choice B) A President may grant a pardon to a group of people who have committed a federal offense. For example, President Carter granted a group pardon to every person who avoided the military draft during the Vietnam War.\n\nEducational objective: Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary power to generally grant pardons to individuals or groups that have committed federal offenses. But a pardon cannot be granted before the federal offense has been committed.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2, cl. 1 (vesting the President with the power to grant reprieves and pardons).\n\n• Ex Parte Garland, 71 U.S. (4 Wall) 333, 380 (1866) (explaining that a pardon can be granted only after a federal offense has been committed).","educationalObjective":"Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary power to generally grant pardons to individuals or groups that have committed federal offenses. But a pardon cannot be granted before the federal offense has been committed.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2, cl. 1 (vesting the President with the power to grant reprieves and pardons).\n\n• Ex Parte Garland, 71 U.S. (4 Wall) 333, 380 (1866) (explaining that a pardon can be granted only after a federal offense has been committed).","rule":"Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary power to generally grant pardons to individuals or groups that have committed federal offenses. But a pardon cannot be granted before the federal offense has been committed.","irac":{"issue":"May the President constitutionally grant this pardon?","rule":"Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary power to generally grant pardons to individuals or groups that have committed federal offenses. But a pardon cannot be granted before the federal offense has been committed.","analysis":"Exclusive executive powers\n\n• Nominate principal officers\n• Veto bills\n• Prosecute & pardon federal offenses\n• Communicate & negotiate with foreign governments\n• Recognize foreign governments\n• Enter executive agreements Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary (ie, absolute) power to grant reprieves and pardons to persons or groups of people that have committed federal offenses—except in cases of impeachment (Choices C & D). But the President may grant a pardon only after the federal offense has been committed—not before. Since the President here seeks to grant a group pardon to the activists before they commit a federal offense by smoking cannabis at the rally, she may not constitutionally grant this pardon. (Choice B) A President may grant a pardon to a group of people who have committed a federal offense. For example, President Carter granted a group pardon to every person who avoided the military draft during the Vietnam War.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the President may not grant a pardon before a federal offense has been committed."},"questionZh":"一个全国活动人士联盟宣布，将在国会大厦举行集会，抗议将持有或使用大麻定为犯罪的联邦法律。这次集会被宣传为“吸烟”，活动人士在抗议期间吸食大麻。总统最近宣布支持根据联邦法律将休闲大麻合法化，她不希望任何活动人士因参加集会而面临刑事指控。总统提议在集会开始前对活动人士进行集体赦免。总统可以根据宪法批准赦免吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为在联邦犯罪发生之前，总统不得给予赦免。","B":"不可以，因为总统可能不会赦免一群人。","C":"是的，因为总统可能会赦免联邦罪行。","D":"是的，因为总统拥有全部赦免权。"},"explanationZh":"独家行政权力\n\n• 提名主要官员\n• 否决法案\n• 起诉和赦免联邦罪行\n• 与外国政府的沟通和谈判\n• 承认外国政府\n• 签订行政协议 《宪法》第二条赋予总统全权（即绝对）权力，可以对犯有联邦罪行的个人或群体给予缓刑和赦免——弹劾情况除外（选择C 和D）。但总统只有在联邦犯罪发生之后才能给予赦免，而不是之前。由于总统寻求在活动人士在集会上吸食大麻犯下联邦罪行之前给予集体赦免，她可能不会根据宪法批准这一赦免。 （选择 B）总统可以赦免一群犯有联邦罪行的人。例如，卡特总统对所有在越南战争期间避免入伍的人进行了集体赦免。\n\n教育目标：《宪法》第二条赋予总统全权，可以普遍赦免犯下联邦罪行的个人或团体。但在联邦犯罪发生之前，不能给予赦免。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2，cl。 1（赋予总统缓刑和赦免的权力）。\n\n• Ex Parte Garland, 71 U.S. (4 Wall) 333, 380 (1866)（解释说只有在犯下联邦罪行后才能授予赦免）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"《宪法》第二条赋予总统全权，可以普遍赦免犯下联邦罪行的个人或团体。但在联邦犯罪发生之前，不能给予赦免。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统可以根据宪法批准赦免吗？","rule":"《宪法》第二条赋予总统全权，可以普遍赦免犯下联邦罪行的个人或团体。但在联邦犯罪发生之前，不能给予赦免。","analysis":"独家行政权力\n\n• 提名主要官员\n• 否决法案\n• 起诉和赦免联邦罪行\n• 与外国政府的沟通和谈判\n• 承认外国政府\n• 签订行政协议 《宪法》第二条赋予总统全权（即绝对）权力，可以对犯有联邦罪行的个人或群体给予缓刑和赦免——弹劾情况除外（选择C 和D）。但总统只有在联邦犯罪发生之后才能给予赦免，而不是之前。由于总统寻求在活动人士在集会上吸食大麻犯下联邦罪行之前给予集体赦免，她可能不会根据宪法批准这一赦免。 （选择 B）总统可以赦免一群犯有联邦罪行的人。例如，卡特总统对所有在越南战争期间避免入伍的人进行了集体赦免。\n\n教育目标：《宪法》第二条赋予总统全权，可以普遍赦免犯下联邦罪行的个人或团体。但在联邦犯罪发生之前，不能给予赦免。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2，cl。 1（赋予总统缓刑和赦免的权力）。\n\n• Ex Parte Garland, 71 U.S. (4 Wall) 333, 380 (1866)（解释说只有在犯下联邦罪行后才能授予赦免）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为在联邦犯罪发生之前，总统不得给予赦免。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"plenary power","ipa":"/plˈɛnɝi pˈaʊɝ/","syllables":"ple·nary power","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"全权：在特定领域内范围广泛或完整的政府权力","contextualMeaning":"全权：在特定领域内范围广泛或完整的政府权力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary power to generally grant pardons to individuals or groups that have committed federal offenses.","synonyms":["plenary might","plenary mightiness","plenary powerfulness"]},{"term":"impeachment","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈitʃmənt/","syllables":"im·peach·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 控告, 检举, 弹劾","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[法] 控告, 检举, 弹劾","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:impeachments","sourceSentence":"Exclusive executive powers • Nominate principal officers • Veto bills • Prosecute & pardon federal offenses • Communicate & negotiate with foreign governments • Recognize foreign governments • Enter executive agreements Article II of the Constitution vests the President with plenary (ie, absolute) power to grant reprieves and pardons to persons or groups of people that have committed federal offenses—except in cases ","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"offense","ipa":"/ʌfˈɛns/","syllables":"of·fense","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 犯罪, 伤感情, 攻击","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 犯罪, 伤感情, 攻击","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:offenses","sourceSentence":"No, because the President may not grant a pardon before a federal offense has been committed.","synonyms":["offence","offensive","discourtesy","offensive activity"]},{"term":"military","ipa":"/mˈɪlʌtˌɛɹi/","syllables":"mil·i·tary","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 军队；a. 军事的, 军人的, 适于战争的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 军队；a. 军事的, 军人的, 适于战争的","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"For example, President Carter granted a group pardon to every person who avoided the military draft during the Vietnam War.","synonyms":["armed forces","armed services","military machine","war machine"]},{"term":"holding","ipa":"/hˈoʊldɪŋ/","syllables":"hold·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 把持, 支持, 保持；[法] 租借地, 占有物, 拥有的财产","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 把持, 支持, 保持；[法] 租借地, 占有物, 拥有的财产","wordForm":"词；词形 0:hold/1:i/s:holdings/i:holding","sourceSentence":"A national coalition of activists announced that it would be holding a rally at the Capitol to protest federal laws that criminalize the possession or use of cannabis.","synonyms":["retention","keeping","property","belongings"]},{"term":"capitol","ipa":"/kˈæpɪtəl/","syllables":"capi·tol","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 国会大厦, 州议会大厦, 朱比特神庙","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 国会大厦, 州议会大厦, 朱比特神庙","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:capitols","sourceSentence":"A national coalition of activists announced that it would be holding a rally at the Capitol to protest federal laws that criminalize the possession or use of cannabis.","synonyms":["Capitol Building"]},{"term":"protest","ipa":"/pɹˈoʊtˌɛst/","syllables":"protest","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 抗议, 反对, 抗议书, 断言；v. 反对, 抗议, 断言","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 抗议, 反对, 抗议书, 断言；v. 反对, 抗议, 断言","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:protests/p:protested/i:protesting/d:protested/3:protests","sourceSentence":"A national coalition of activists announced that it would be holding a rally at the Capitol to protest federal laws that criminalize the possession or use of cannabis.","synonyms":["objection","dissent","protestation","resist"]},{"term":"pardon","ipa":"/pˈɑɹdən/","syllables":"par·don","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 原谅, 赦免；vt. 宽恕, 原谅","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 原谅, 赦免；vt. 宽恕, 原谅","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:pardoned/s:pardons/p:pardoned/i:pardoning/3:pardons","sourceSentence":"The President has proposed to grant a group pardon to the activists before the rally begins.","synonyms":["forgiveness","amnesty","free pardon","excuse"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":51,"sourceQuestionPage":153,"sourceQuestionPages":[153],"sourceExplanationPages":[154],"sourceAnswerPages":[154],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-052","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federal Judicial Power","question":"Under the authority of a federal voting rights statute, some states drew congressional districts in a manner calculated to increase the likelihood that members of historically disadvantaged minority racial groups would be elected. The U.S. Supreme Court declared these districts to be unconstitutional, as improper racial gerrymanders. In response to this ruling, Congress passed a new statute that explicitly denies the Supreme Court appellate jurisdiction over all future cases challenging the constitutionality of action taken under the authority of the federal voting rights statute. Which of the following is the most persuasive argument for the constitutionality of the new statute restricting the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction?","choices":{"A":"Article III of the Constitution explicitly states that the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction is subject to such exceptions and regulations as Congress shall make.","B":"The constitutional principle of separation of powers authorizes Congress to pass statutes calculated to reduce the effects of Supreme Court decisions that interfere with the exercise of powers that have been delegated to the legislative branch.","C":"The establishment and apportionment of congressional districts directly affect interstate commerce, and the Constitution authorizes Congress to use its plenary authority over such commerce for any purpose it believes will promote the general welfare.","D":"The Fifteenth Amendment authorizes Congress to enforce the amendment's voting rights provisions by appropriate legislation, and Congress could reasonably determine that this restriction on the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction is an appropriate means to that end."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Article III exceptions clause\n\nCongress can modify SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction unless modification would violate:\n\n• separation-of-powers doctrine (eg, by usurping judicial power) or\n• other constitutional provisions (eg, violating equal protection clause by only allowing men to appeal) SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States\n\nAppellate jurisdiction is a court's authority to overturn, modify, or affirm another court's decision. The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction over decisions by (1) lower federal courts and (2) the highest state courts when the decision turns on federal law. But Article III explicitly provides that this jurisdiction is subject to such exceptions and regulations as Congress shall make. Therefore, that is the most persuasive argument supporting the constitutionality of the statute restricting SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. (Choice B) The constitutional principle of separation of powers bars each federal government branch from usurping another branch's powers. And since a congressional statute calculated to reduce the effects of SCOTUS decisions may usurp judicial power, this argument does not support the statute's constitutionality. (Choice C) The Article I commerce clause grants Congress plenary (ie, absolute) authority to regulate activities that, singly or in the aggregate, have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. But since establishing and apportioning congressional districts only has a mild impact on interstate commerce, this argument would fail. (Choice D) The Fifteenth Amendment authorizes Congress to enact legislation to ensure that the federal and state governments do not deny or abridge the right to vote based on race, color, or previous servitude. But Article III specifically allows Congress to alter SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction, so it provides the more persuasive argument.\n\nEducational objective: Congress has the power to regulate and make exceptions to the U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction under Article III of the Constitution.\n\nReferences\n• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869) (establishing that Congress has almost unlimited power to regulate and make exceptions to the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction).","educationalObjective":"Congress has the power to regulate and make exceptions to the U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction under Article III of the Constitution.","references":"• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869) (establishing that Congress has almost unlimited power to regulate and make exceptions to the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction).","rule":"Congress has the power to regulate and make exceptions to the U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction under Article III of the Constitution.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most persuasive argument for the constitutionality of the new statute restricting the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction?","rule":"Congress has the power to regulate and make exceptions to the U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction under Article III of the Constitution.","analysis":"Article III exceptions clause\n\nCongress can modify SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction unless modification would violate:\n\n• separation-of-powers doctrine (eg, by usurping judicial power) or\n• other constitutional provisions (eg, violating equal protection clause by only allowing men to appeal) SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States\n\nAppellate jurisdiction is a court's authority to overturn, modify, or affirm another court's decision. The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction over decisions by (1) lower federal courts and (2) the highest state courts when the decision turns on federal law. But Article III explicitly provides that this jurisdiction is subject to such exceptions and regulations as Congress shall make. Therefore, that is the most persuasive argument supporting the constitutionality of the statute restricting SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. (Choice B) The constitutional principle of separation of powers bars each federal government branch from usurping another branch's powers. And since a congressional statute calculated to reduce the effects of SCOTUS decisions may usurp judicial power, this argument does not support the statute's constitutionality. (Choice C) The Article I commerce clause grants Congress plenary (ie, absolute) authority to regulate activities that, singly or in the aggregate, have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. But since establishing and apportioning congressional districts only has a mild impact on interstate commerce, this argument would fail. (Choice D) The Fifteenth Amendment authorizes Congress to enact legislation to ensure that the federal and state governments do not deny or abridge the right to vote based on race, color, or previous servitude. But Article III specifically allows Congress to alter SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction, so it provides the more persuasive argument.","conclusion":"Answer A: Article III of the Constitution explicitly states that the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction is subject to such exceptions and regulations as Congress shall make."},"questionZh":"根据联邦投票权法规的授权，一些州以一种精心设计的方式划分国会选区，以增加历史上处于不利地位的少数族裔群体成员当选的可能性。美国最高法院宣布这些选区违宪，属于不当种族选区。作为对这一裁决的回应，国会通过了一项新法规，明确否认最高法院对未来所有质疑联邦投票权法规授权下所采取行动合宪性的案件具有上诉管辖权。以下哪一项是限制最高法院上诉管辖权的新法规合宪性最有说服力的论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"宪法第三条明确规定，最高法院的上诉管辖权受国会制定的例外和规定的约束。","B":"权力分立的宪法原则授权国会通过旨在减少最高法院判决干扰立法部门行使权力的影响的法规。","C":"国会选区的设立和分配直接影响州际贸易，宪法授权国会将其对此类贸易的全体权力用于其认为会促进公众福利的任何目的。","D":"第十五修正案授权国会通过适当的立法强制执行修正案的投票权条款，国会可以合理地确定对最高法院上诉管辖权的限制是实现这一目的的适当手段。"},"explanationZh":"第三条例外条款\n\n国会可以修改最高法院的上诉管辖权，除非修改会违反：\n\n• 权力分立原则（例如，通过篡夺司法权）或\n• 其他宪法条款（例如，只允许男性上诉，从而违反平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）） SCOTUS = 美国最高法院\n\n上诉管辖权是法院推翻、修改或确认另一法院判决的权力。当 (1) 下级联邦法院和 (2) 最高州法院依据联邦法律做出的决定时，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有上诉管辖权。但第三条明确规定，该管辖权受国会制定的例外和规定的约束。因此，这是支持限制最高法院上诉管辖权的法规合宪性的最有说服力的论据。 （选项B） 宪法权力分立原则禁止联邦政府各部门篡夺另一部门的权力。由于旨在减少最高法院判决影响的国会法规可能会篡夺司法权，因此这一论点并不支持该法规的合宪性。 （选项C）第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授予国会全体（即绝对）权力来监管对州际商业产生重大影响的活动，无论是单独的还是总体的。但由于建立和分配国会选区只对州际贸易产生轻微影响，因此这一论点是站不住脚的。 （选项 D）第十五修正案授权国会颁布立法，以确保联邦和州政府不会因种族、肤色或以前的奴役而否认或剥夺投票权。但第三条明确允许国会改变最高法院的上诉管辖权，因此它提供了更有说服力的论据。\n\n教育目标：国会有权根据宪法第三条对美国最高法院的上诉管辖权进行监管和制定例外情况。\n\n参考文献\n• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869)（规定国会几乎拥有无限的权力来监管最高法院的上诉管辖权并对其作出例外处理）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据宪法第三条，国会有权对美国最高法院的上诉管辖权进行监管和例外。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是限制最高法院上诉管辖权的新法规合宪性最有说服力的论据？","rule":"根据宪法第三条，国会有权对美国最高法院的上诉管辖权进行监管和例外。","analysis":"第三条例外条款\n\n国会可以修改最高法院的上诉管辖权，除非修改会违反：\n\n• 权力分立原则（例如，通过篡夺司法权）或\n• 其他宪法条款（例如，只允许男性上诉，从而违反平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）） SCOTUS = 美国最高法院\n\n上诉管辖权是法院推翻、修改或确认另一法院判决的权力。当 (1) 下级联邦法院和 (2) 最高州法院依据联邦法律做出的决定时，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有上诉管辖权。但第三条明确规定，该管辖权受国会制定的例外和规定的约束。因此，这是支持限制最高法院上诉管辖权的法规合宪性的最有说服力的论据。 （选项B） 宪法权力分立原则禁止联邦政府各部门篡夺另一部门的权力。由于旨在减少最高法院判决影响的国会法规可能会篡夺司法权，因此这一论点并不支持该法规的合宪性。 （选项C）第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授予国会全体（即绝对）权力来监管对州际商业产生重大影响的活动，无论是单独的还是总体的。但由于建立和分配国会选区只对州际贸易产生轻微影响，因此这一论点是站不住脚的。 （选项 D）第十五修正案授权国会颁布立法，以确保联邦和州政府不会因种族、肤色或以前的奴役而否认或剥夺投票权。但第三条明确允许国会改变最高法院的上诉管辖权，因此它提供了更有说服力的论据。\n\n教育目标：国会有权根据宪法第三条对美国最高法院的上诉管辖权进行监管和制定例外情况。\n\n参考文献\n• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869)（规定国会几乎拥有无限的权力来监管最高法院的上诉管辖权并对其作出例外处理）。","conclusion":"答案 A：宪法第三条明确规定，最高法院的上诉管辖权受国会制定的例外和规定的约束。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"apportionment","ipa":"/ʌpˈɔɹʃənmənt/","syllables":"ap·por·tion·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 分配, 分摊, 分派；[经] 分派, 分配, 分摊","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 分配, 分摊, 分派；[经] 分派, 分配, 分摊","wordForm":"词；词形 s:apportionments","sourceSentence":"The establishment and apportionment of congressional districts directly affect interstate commerce, and the Constitution authorizes Congress to use its plenary authority over such commerce for any purpose it believes will promote the general welfare.","synonyms":["allotment","apportioning","allocation","parceling"]},{"term":"gerrymanders","ipa":"/dʒˈɛɹimˌændɝz/","syllables":"ger·ry·man·ders","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 为本党利益改划选举区分, 不公正操纵, 欺骗（gerrymander的第三人称单数形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 为本党利益改划选举区分, 不公正操纵, 欺骗（gerrymander的第三人称单数形式）","wordForm":"词；原形 gerrymander；词形 1:3/0:gerrymander","sourceSentence":"Supreme Court declared these districts to be unconstitutional, as improper racial gerrymanders.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"improper","ipa":"/ˌɪmpɹˈɑpɝ/","syllables":"im·proper","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 不合适的, 错误的, 不成体统的, 不道德的；[法] 不适当的, 不合适的, 不正确的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 不合适的, 错误的, 不成体统的, 不道德的；[法] 不适当的, 不合适的, 不正确的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Supreme Court declared these districts to be unconstitutional, as improper racial gerrymanders.","synonyms":["wrong","unconventional","unlawful"]},{"term":"racial","ipa":"/ɹˈeɪʃəl/","syllables":"racial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 人种的, 种族的；[医] 种族的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 人种的, 种族的；[医] 种族的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Under the authority of a federal voting rights statute, some states drew congressional districts in a manner calculated to increase the likelihood that members of historically disadvantaged minority racial groups would be elected.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"race","ipa":"/ɹˈeɪs/","syllables":"race","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 种族, 人种, 赛跑, 比赛, 急流, 人类, 同道, 姜根；vi. 赛跑, 竞赛, 疾走；vt. 与...赛跑, 使疾走, 使猛转","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 种族, 人种, 赛跑, 比赛, 急流, 人类, 同道, 姜根；vi. 赛跑, 竞赛, 疾走；vt. 与...赛跑, 使疾走, 使猛转","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:races/i:racing/p:raced/d:raced/3:races","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) The Fifteenth Amendment authorizes Congress to enact legislation to ensure that the federal and state governments do not deny or abridge the right to vote based on race, color, or previous servitude.","synonyms":["raceway","subspecies","slipstream","airstream"]},{"term":"elected","ipa":"/ɪlˈɛktəd/","syllables":"elected","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 选举, 推选","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 选举, 推选","wordForm":"词；原形 elect；词形 0:elect/1:dp/d:elected/p:elected","sourceSentence":"Under the authority of a federal voting rights statute, some states drew congressional districts in a manner calculated to increase the likelihood that members of historically disadvantaged minority racial groups would be elected.","synonyms":["chosen","elite"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":52,"sourceQuestionPage":155,"sourceQuestionPages":[155],"sourceExplanationPages":[156,157],"sourceAnswerPages":[156,157],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-053","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"A county district attorney opened a criminal investigation into the President's business transactions conducted before the President assumed office. The district attorney brought the case before a grand jury, which validly issued a subpoena duces tecum requiring the President to produce various financial records related to the business transactions at issue. The President has refused to produce the financial records, citing absolute immunity and executive privilege. Under what circumstances, if any, may the grand jury compel production of the financial records over the President's assertions of absolute immunity and executive privilege?","choices":{"A":"It may compel production only if the subpoena seeks critical evidence that cannot be obtained by any other means.","B":"It may compel production without providing any additional justification.","C":"It may not compel production under any circumstances because the disclosure of financial records is protected by executive privilege.","D":"It may not compel production under any circumstances because the President has absolute immunity from complying with a subpoena."},"answer":"B","explanation":"[Current-law update — answer unchanged] Trump v. United States (2024) distinguishes core constitutional acts, other official acts, and unofficial acts. This subpoena seeks private records concerning transactions before the President took office, so Trump v. Vance remains controlling and the grand jury may compel production.\n\nPresidential immunity & privilege\n\nImmunity Absolute immunity from civil suits seeking damages for official executive actions\n\n• No immunity for actions that occurred before taking office\n• No immunity for actions unrelated to executive functions\n• No immunity from criminal subpoena Privilege Power to withhold official documents & communications:\n\n• National security secrets – afforded greatest deference\n• Civil case – afforded greater deference than in criminal trial\n• Criminal case – afforded least deference (disclosure required if prosecution shows need) As the head of the executive branch, the President possesses all Article II powers, including the power to execute federal laws and serve as the commander in chief of the armed forces. To safeguard the President's ability to perform these important duties, the President has the following protections in judicial proceedings:\n• Executive privilege – prevents the disclosure of confidential documents and communications made in performance of official duties and responsibilities\n• Absolute immunity – prevents civil suits for damages against the President for any act performed as part of the President's official duties and responsibilities* However, these protections are not infinite. For example, executive privilege does not extend to documents and communications that were made in performing private functions (ie, private papers). Since the financial records at issue here relate to the President's private affairs before taking office—not official conduct while in office—the records are not protected by executive privilege (Choice C). Additionally, the President is not absolutely immune from complying with a validly issued subpoena that seeks the President's private papers in a criminal proceeding (Choice D). Instead, the President must comply with the subpoena just like any other citizen. Since the grand jury validly issued the subpoena duces tecum in this case, the President must produce the requested financial records. As a result, the grand jury need not provide any additional justification to compel production of those records. *The U.S. Supreme Court has never ruled whether a President can be indicted for a crime while in office. However, Congress can use the impeachment process to remove the President from office for a high crime or misdemeanor.\n\n(Choice A) The grand jury does not need to meet a heightened standard of need by showing that the subpoena seeks critical evidence that cannot be obtained by any other means. The reason is that, in the absence of a need to protect the President, the public interest in fair and effective law enforcement favors access to such evidence.\n\nEducational objective: Executive privilege and absolute immunity safeguard a President from liability when performing official duties and responsibilities. However, these protections are not absolute and will not shield the President from complying with a criminal subpoena that seeks the President's private papers.\n\nReferences\n• Trump v. Vance, 140 S. Ct. 2412, 2430 (2020) (holding that the President is not absolutely immune from state criminal subpoenas seeking his private papers and is not entitled to a heightened standard of need).","educationalObjective":"A President has immunity from criminal prosecution for core constitutional acts and at least presumptive immunity for other official acts, but no immunity for unofficial acts. Executive privilege and immunity do not shield private, pre-office financial records from a valid criminal subpoena.","references":"• Trump v. Vance, 140 S. Ct. 2412, 2430 (2020) (holding that the President is not absolutely immune from state criminal subpoenas seeking his private papers and is not entitled to a heightened standard of need).","rule":"A President has immunity from criminal prosecution for core constitutional acts and at least presumptive immunity for other official acts, but no immunity for unofficial acts. Executive privilege and immunity do not shield private, pre-office financial records from a valid criminal subpoena.","irac":{"issue":"Under what circumstances, if any, may the grand jury compel production of the financial records over the President's assertions of absolute immunity and executive privilege?","rule":"A President has immunity from criminal prosecution for core constitutional acts and at least presumptive immunity for other official acts, but no immunity for unofficial acts. Executive privilege and immunity do not shield private, pre-office financial records from a valid criminal subpoena.","analysis":"Presidential immunity & privilege\n\nImmunity Absolute immunity from civil suits seeking damages for official executive actions\n\n• No immunity for actions that occurred before taking office\n• No immunity for actions unrelated to executive functions\n• No immunity from criminal subpoena Privilege Power to withhold official documents & communications:\n\n• National security secrets – afforded greatest deference\n• Civil case – afforded greater deference than in criminal trial\n• Criminal case – afforded least deference (disclosure required if prosecution shows need) As the head of the executive branch, the President possesses all Article II powers, including the power to execute federal laws and serve as the commander in chief of the armed forces. To safeguard the President's ability to perform these important duties, the President has the following protections in judicial proceedings:\n• Executive privilege – prevents the disclosure of confidential documents and communications made in performance of official duties and responsibilities\n• Absolute immunity – prevents civil suits for damages against the President for any act performed as part of the President's official duties and responsibilities* However, these protections are not infinite. For example, executive privilege does not extend to documents and communications that were made in performing private functions (ie, private papers). Since the financial records at issue here relate to the President's private affairs before taking office—not official conduct while in office—the records are not protected by executive privilege (Choice C). Additionally, the President is not absolutely immune from complying with a validly issued subpoena that seeks the President's private papers in a criminal proceeding (Choice D). Instead, the President must comply with the subpoena just like any other citizen. Since the grand jury validly issued the subpoena duces tecum in this case, the President must produce the requested financial records. As a result, the grand jury need not provide any additional justification to compel production of those records. *The U.S. Supreme Court has never ruled whether a President can be indicted for a crime while in office. However, Congress can use the impeachment process to remove the President from office for a high crime or misdemeanor.\n\n(Choice A) The grand jury does not need to meet a heightened standard of need by showing that the subpoena seeks critical evidence that cannot be obtained by any other means. The reason is that, in the absence of a need to protect the President, the public interest in fair and effective law enforcement favors access to such evidence.","conclusion":"Answer B: It may compel production without providing any additional justification."},"questionZh":"一名县地方检察官对总统就职前进行的商业交易展开刑事调查。地区检察官将此案提交给大陪审团，大陪审团有效地发出了传票，要求总统出示与所涉商业交易相关的各种财务记录。总统以绝对豁免权和行政特权为由，拒绝提供财务记录。在什么情况下（如果有的话），大陪审团可以强制提供总统声称的绝对豁免权和行政特权的财务记录？","choicesZh":{"A":"仅当传票寻求通过任何其他方式无法获得的关键证据时，才可能强制出示。","B":"它可能会在不提供任何额外理由的情况下强制生产。","C":"在任何情况下都不得强制生产，因为财务记录的披露受到行政特权的保护。","D":"在任何情况下都不得强制制作，因为总统拥有绝对的豁免权，可以遵守传票。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法更新——答案不变】Trump v. United States（2024）区分核心宪法职权行为、其他公务行为与非公务行为。本题传票针对总统就任前私人交易记录，故 Trump v. Vance 的规则仍适用，大陪审团可以强制提交。\n\n总统豁免权和特权\n\n豁免权 绝对豁免因官方行政行为而寻求损害赔偿的民事诉讼\n\n• 上任前发生的行为不享有豁免权\n• 与执行职能无关的行为不享有豁免权\n• 不享有刑事传票豁免权 扣留官方文件和通讯的特权：\n\n• 国家安全机密——给予最大程度的尊重\n• 民事案件——比刑事审判受到更大的尊重\n• 刑事案件——给予最少的尊重（如果起诉表明有必要，则要求披露） 作为行政部门的首脑，总统拥有第二条规定的所有权力，包括执行联邦法律和担任武装部队总司令的权力。为了保障总统履行这些重要职责的能力，总统在司法程序中享有以下保护：\n• 行政特权——防止泄露在履行官方职责和责任时进行的机密文件和通信\n• 绝对豁免权——防止因总统履行公务和责任而采取的任何行为而对总统提起民事损害赔偿诉讼* 然而，这些保护并不是无限的。例如，行政特权不适用于执行私人职能时所制作的文件和通信（即私人文件）。由于此处涉及的财务记录涉及总统上任前的私人事务，而不是在职期间的官方行为，因此这些记录不受行政特权的保护（选项 C）。此外，总统并非绝对不受遵守在刑事诉讼中寻求总统私人文件的有效发出的传票的约束（选项 D）。相反，总统必须像其他公民一样遵守传票。由于大陪审团在本案中有效发出了传票，总统必须提供所要求的财务记录。因此，大陪审团无需提供任何额外的理由来强制提供这些记录。 *美国最高法院从未裁定总统在任期间是否可以因犯罪而被起诉。然而，国会可以利用弹劾程序将总统因重罪或轻罪免职。\n\n（选择 A）大陪审团不需要通过证明传票寻求通过任何其他方式无法获得的关键证据来满足更高的需求标准。原因是，在不需要保护总统的情况下，公平有效执法的公众利益有利于获取此类证据。\n\n教育目标：行政特权和绝对豁免权保障总统在履行公务和责任时免于承担责任。然而，这些保护并不是绝对的，也不会阻止总统遵守寻求总统私人文件的刑事传票。\n\n参考文献\n• 特朗普诉万斯，140 S. Ct. 2412, 2430 (2020)（认为总统并非绝对免受寻求其私人文件的国家刑事传票的影响，并且无权获得更高的需求标准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"总统对核心宪法职权行为享有刑事起诉绝对豁免，对其他公务行为至少享有推定豁免，但对非公务行为不享有豁免。行政特权及总统豁免不阻却针对其就任前私人财务记录的有效刑事传票。","iracZh":{"issue":"在什么情况下（如果有的话），大陪审团可以强制提供总统声称的绝对豁免权和行政特权的财务记录？","rule":"总统对核心宪法职权行为享有刑事起诉绝对豁免，对其他公务行为至少享有推定豁免，但对非公务行为不享有豁免。行政特权及总统豁免不阻却针对其就任前私人财务记录的有效刑事传票。","analysis":"总统豁免权和特权\n\n豁免权 绝对豁免因官方行政行为而寻求损害赔偿的民事诉讼\n\n• 上任前发生的行为不享有豁免权\n• 与执行职能无关的行为不享有豁免权\n• 不享有刑事传票豁免权 扣留官方文件和通讯的特权：\n\n• 国家安全机密——给予最大程度的尊重\n• 民事案件——比刑事审判受到更大的尊重\n• 刑事案件——给予最少的尊重（如果起诉表明有必要，则要求披露） 作为行政部门的首脑，总统拥有第二条规定的所有权力，包括执行联邦法律和担任武装部队总司令的权力。为了保障总统履行这些重要职责的能力，总统在司法程序中享有以下保护：\n• 行政特权——防止泄露在履行官方职责和责任时进行的机密文件和通信\n• 绝对豁免权——防止因总统履行公务和责任而采取的任何行为而对总统提起民事损害赔偿诉讼* 然而，这些保护并不是无限的。例如，行政特权不适用于执行私人职能时所制作的文件和通信（即私人文件）。由于此处涉及的财务记录涉及总统上任前的私人事务，而不是在职期间的官方行为，因此这些记录不受行政特权的保护（选项 C）。此外，总统并非绝对不受遵守在刑事诉讼中寻求总统私人文件的有效发出的传票的约束（选项 D）。相反，总统必须像其他公民一样遵守传票。由于大陪审团在本案中有效发出了传票，总统必须提供所要求的财务记录。因此，大陪审团无需提供任何额外的理由来强制提供这些记录。 *美国最高法院从未裁定总统在任期间是否可以因犯罪而被起诉。然而，国会可以利用弹劾程序将总统因重罪或轻罪免职。\n\n（选择 A）大陪审团不需要通过证明传票寻求通过任何其他方式无法获得的关键证据来满足更高的需求标准。原因是，在不需要保护总统的情况下，公平有效执法的公众利益有利于获取此类证据。\n\n教育目标：行政特权和绝对豁免权保障总统在履行公务和责任时免于承担责任。然而，这些保护并不是绝对的，也不会阻止总统遵守寻求总统私人文件的刑事传票。\n\n参考文献\n• 特朗普诉万斯，140 S. Ct. 2412, 2430 (2020)（认为总统并非绝对免受寻求其私人文件的国家刑事传票的影响，并且无权获得更高的需求标准）。","conclusion":"答案 B：它可能会在不提供任何额外理由的情况下强制生产。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"补充 2024 年总统刑事豁免框架；本题涉及就任前私人记录，原答案不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Trump v. United States, 603 U.S. 593 (2024)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/23-939_e2pg.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"executive privilege","ipa":"/ɪɡzˈɛkjətɪv pɹˈɪvlədʒ/","syllables":"ex·ec·u·tive priv·i·lege","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"行政特权：总统对特定机密行政沟通主张保密的权力","contextualMeaning":"行政特权：总统对特定机密行政沟通主张保密的权力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The President has refused to produce the financial records, citing absolute immunity and executive privilege.","synonyms":["executive prerogative","executive perquisite","executive exclusive right"]},{"term":"misdemeanor","ipa":"/mˌɪsdʌmˈinɝ/","syllables":"mis·de·meanor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 行为不检, 品行不良, 轻罪, 不端行为","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 行为不检, 品行不良, 轻罪, 不端行为","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:misdemeanors","sourceSentence":"However, Congress can use the impeachment process to remove the President from office for a high crime or misdemeanor.","synonyms":["misdemeanour","infraction","violation","infringement"]},{"term":"subpoena","ipa":"/sʌpˈinə/","syllables":"sub·poena","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 传票；vt. 传审, 传唤","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 传票；vt. 传审, 传唤","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:subpoenas/d:subpoenaed/p:subpoenaed/i:subpoenaing/3:subpoenas","sourceSentence":"The district attorney brought the case before a grand jury, which validly issued a subpoena duces tecum requiring the President to produce various financial records related to the business transactions at issue.","synonyms":["subpoena ad testificandum"]},{"term":"indicted","ipa":"/ˌɪndˈaɪtɪd/","syllables":"in·dicted","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 控告, 起诉( indict的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 控告, 起诉( indict的过去式和过去分词 )","wordForm":"词；原形 indict；词形 0:indict/1:dp/d:indicted","sourceSentence":"Supreme Court has never ruled whether a President can be indicted for a crime while in office.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"damages","ipa":"/dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 赔偿金；[经] 损害赔偿； 伤害","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 赔偿金；[经] 损害赔偿； 伤害","wordForm":"名词；原形 damage；词形 0:damage/1:s3","sourceSentence":"Presidential immunity & privilege Immunity Absolute immunity from civil suits seeking damages for official executive actions • No immunity for actions that occurred before taking office • No immunity for actions unrelated to executive functions • No immunity from criminal subpoena Privilege Power to withhold official documents & communications: • National security secrets – afforded greatest deference • Civil case – ","synonyms":["harm","hurt","scathe","wrong"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":53,"sourceQuestionPage":158,"sourceQuestionPages":[158],"sourceExplanationPages":[159,160],"sourceAnswerPages":[159,160],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-054","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Mootness & Ripeness","question":"A woman contracted for expensive cable television service for a period of six months solely to view the televised trial of a man who was on trial for murder in a state court. In the midst of the trial, the judge prohibited any further televising of the man's trial because the judge concluded that the presence of television cameras was disruptive. The woman brought an action in a federal district court against the judge in the man's case and sought an injunction that would require the judge to resume the televising of the man's trial. The woman alleged that the judge's order to stop televising the trial deprived her of property—her investment in cable television service—without due process of law. Before the woman's case came to trial, the man's criminal trial concluded in a conviction and sentencing. There do not appear to be any obvious errors in the proceeding that led to the result in the man's case. After the man's conviction and sentencing, the judge moved to dismiss the woman's suit. What is the most proper disposition of this motion by the federal court?","choices":{"A":"Defer action on the motion until after any appellate proceedings in the man's case have concluded, because the man might appeal, his conviction might be set aside, he might be tried again, and television cameras might be barred from the new trial.","B":"Defer action on the motion until after the state's supreme court expresses a view on its proper disposition, because the state law of mootness governs suits in federal court when the federal case is inexorably intertwined with a state proceeding.","C":"Deny the motion, because the woman has raised an important constitutional question—whether her investment in cable service solely to view the man's trial is protected by the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause.","D":"Grant the motion, because the subject matter of the controversy between the woman and the judge has ceased to exist and there is no strong likelihood that it will be revived."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Federal courts have the power to decide actual cases and controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. But once the plaintiff's harm is resolved, his/her claim becomes moot and should be dismissed unless:\n• the harm is capable of repetition yet evading review due to a reasonable chance that the same harm will (1) affect the plaintiff again and (2) not last long enough to be fully litigated (eg, pregnancy) or\n• the defendant voluntarily stops the challenged action but can resume it at any time. Here, the woman sued in federal court to force the judge to resume televising the man's murder trial. But before the court could consider her claim, this controversy ceased to exist because the man was convicted and sentenced. And the controversy is not likely to be revived since there were no obvious errors in the man's trial warranting a new trial. Therefore, this controversy is moot, and the federal court should grant the judge's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) It is highly unlikely that the man will succeed on appeal. But even if he does and obtains a new trial, it is pure speculation whether television cameras will again be barred from the courtroom. Therefore, the woman's suit is moot and should be dismissed—not deferred. (Choice B) Federal courts always apply federal law to decide procedural issues like mootness—even when the federal case is inexorably intertwined with a state proceeding. (Choice C) The woman's claim would require the court to determine whether her investment in the cable service is a property interest protected by the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. But the fact that a claim raises an important constitutional question does not make it an actual case or controversy subject to judicial review.\n\nEducational objective: When the plaintiff's harm is resolved, his/her claim becomes moot and must be dismissed unless (1) the harm is capable of repetition yet evading review or (2) the defendant voluntarily stops the challenged action but can resume it at any time.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Turner v. Rogers, 564 U.S. 431, 439–40 (2011) (explaining the mootness doctrine and its exceptions).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 575 (2019) (defining mootness and its exceptions).","educationalObjective":"When the plaintiff's harm is resolved, his/her claim becomes moot and must be dismissed unless (1) the harm is capable of repetition yet evading review or (2) the defendant voluntarily stops the challenged action but can resume it at any time.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Turner v. Rogers, 564 U.S. 431, 439–40 (2011) (explaining the mootness doctrine and its exceptions).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 575 (2019) (defining mootness and its exceptions).","rule":"When the plaintiff's harm is resolved, his/her claim becomes moot and must be dismissed unless (1) the harm is capable of repetition yet evading review or (2) the defendant voluntarily stops the challenged action but can resume it at any time.","irac":{"issue":"What is the most proper disposition of this motion by the federal court?","rule":"When the plaintiff's harm is resolved, his/her claim becomes moot and must be dismissed unless (1) the harm is capable of repetition yet evading review or (2) the defendant voluntarily stops the challenged action but can resume it at any time.","analysis":"Federal courts have the power to decide actual cases and controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. But once the plaintiff's harm is resolved, his/her claim becomes moot and should be dismissed unless:\n• the harm is capable of repetition yet evading review due to a reasonable chance that the same harm will (1) affect the plaintiff again and (2) not last long enough to be fully litigated (eg, pregnancy) or\n• the defendant voluntarily stops the challenged action but can resume it at any time. Here, the woman sued in federal court to force the judge to resume televising the man's murder trial. But before the court could consider her claim, this controversy ceased to exist because the man was convicted and sentenced. And the controversy is not likely to be revived since there were no obvious errors in the man's trial warranting a new trial. Therefore, this controversy is moot, and the federal court should grant the judge's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) It is highly unlikely that the man will succeed on appeal. But even if he does and obtains a new trial, it is pure speculation whether television cameras will again be barred from the courtroom. Therefore, the woman's suit is moot and should be dismissed—not deferred. (Choice B) Federal courts always apply federal law to decide procedural issues like mootness—even when the federal case is inexorably intertwined with a state proceeding. (Choice C) The woman's claim would require the court to determine whether her investment in the cable service is a property interest protected by the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. But the fact that a claim raises an important constitutional question does not make it an actual case or controversy subject to judicial review.","conclusion":"Answer D: Grant the motion, because the subject matter of the controversy between the woman and the judge has ceased to exist and there is no strong likelihood that it will be revived."},"questionZh":"一名妇女签订了为期六个月的昂贵有线电视服务合同，只是为了观看一名在州法院因谋杀罪受审的男子的电视审判。在审判过程中，法官禁止进一步电视转播该男子的审判，因为法官得出的结论是电视摄像机的存在具有干扰性。该女子向联邦地区法院对男子案件的法官提起诉讼，并寻求禁令，要求法官恢复对该男子审判的电视转播。该女子声称，法官停止电视转播庭审的命令剥夺了她的财产——她对有线电视服务的投资——而没有经过正当的法律程序。在女子的案件开庭审理之前，男子的刑事审判以定罪和量刑结束。在导致该男子案件结果的诉讼程序中似乎没有任何明显的错误。男子定罪并判刑后，法官提出驳回女子的诉讼。联邦法院对这项动议最恰当的处理是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"推迟对该动议采取行动，直到该男子案件的任何上诉程序结束后，因为该男子可能会上诉，他的定罪可能会被搁置，他可能会再次受审，并且电视摄像机可能会被禁止观看新的审判。","B":"推迟对该动议采取行动，直到州最高法院就其适当处置表达意见之后，因为当联邦案件与州诉讼不可避免地交织在一起时，州无实际意义的法律管辖联邦法院的诉讼。","C":"否决该动议，因为这位女士提出了一个重要的宪法问题——她仅为了观看该男子的审判而对有线电视服务的投资是否受到第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）的保护。","D":"批准该动议，因为该妇女与法官之间的争议主题已不复存在，并且不太可能重新出现。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院有权裁决实际案件和争议，即能够通过司法解决的争议。但一旦原告的损害得到解决，他/她的索赔就变得毫无意义，应被驳回，除非：\n• 由于同样的伤害有可能 (1) 再次影响原告，并且 (2) 持续时间不够长，无法充分提起诉讼（例如，怀孕），因此伤害可能会重复，但会逃避审查，或者\n• 被告自愿停止受质疑的行动，但可以随时恢复。在这里，该女子向联邦法院提起诉讼，迫使法官恢复电视转播该男子的谋杀案审判。但在法庭考虑她的主张之前，这场争议已经不复存在，因为该男子已被定罪并被判刑。由于对该男子的审判没有明显错误，因此争议不太可能再次发生，因此需要重新审判。因此，这场争议没有实际意义，联邦法院应该批准法官的驳回动议。 （选项A）该男子上诉成功的可能性极小。但即使他这样做并获得新的审判，电视摄像机是否会再次被禁止进入法庭也纯粹是猜测。因此，该女子的诉讼没有实际意义，应该被驳回——而不是推迟。 （选择 B）联邦法院始终适用联邦法律来决定程序性问题，例如无实际意义的问题，即使联邦案件与州诉讼程序不可避免地交织在一起。 （选项 C）该妇女的主张将要求法院确定她对有线电视服务的投资是否属于受第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护的财产权益。但一项主张提出了重要的宪法问题这一事实并不意味着它成为需要司法审查的实际案件或争议。\n\n教育目标：当原告的损害得到解决后，他/她的主张就变得毫无意义，必须被驳回，除非（1）损害能够重复但逃避审查，或（2）被告自愿停止受质疑的行动但可以随时恢复。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Turner v. Rogers, 564 U.S. 431, 439–40 (2011)（解释无实际意义原则及其例外情况）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 575 (2019)（定义无意义及其例外情况）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当原告的损害得到解决后，他/她的主张就变得毫无意义，必须被驳回，除非（1）损害能够重复但逃避审查，或（2）被告自愿停止受质疑的行动但可以随时恢复。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法院对这项动议最恰当的处理是什么？","rule":"当原告的损害得到解决后，他/她的主张就变得毫无意义，必须被驳回，除非（1）损害能够重复但逃避审查，或（2）被告自愿停止受质疑的行动但可以随时恢复。","analysis":"联邦法院有权裁决实际案件和争议，即能够通过司法解决的争议。但一旦原告的损害得到解决，他/她的索赔就变得毫无意义，应被驳回，除非：\n• 由于同样的伤害有可能 (1) 再次影响原告，并且 (2) 持续时间不够长，无法充分提起诉讼（例如，怀孕），因此伤害可能会重复，但会逃避审查，或者\n• 被告自愿停止受质疑的行动，但可以随时恢复。在这里，该女子向联邦法院提起诉讼，迫使法官恢复电视转播该男子的谋杀案审判。但在法庭考虑她的主张之前，这场争议已经不复存在，因为该男子已被定罪并被判刑。由于对该男子的审判没有明显错误，因此争议不太可能再次发生，因此需要重新审判。因此，这场争议没有实际意义，联邦法院应该批准法官的驳回动议。 （选项A）该男子上诉成功的可能性极小。但即使他这样做并获得新的审判，电视摄像机是否会再次被禁止进入法庭也纯粹是猜测。因此，该女子的诉讼没有实际意义，应该被驳回——而不是推迟。 （选择 B）联邦法院始终适用联邦法律来决定程序性问题，例如无实际意义的问题，即使联邦案件与州诉讼程序不可避免地交织在一起。 （选项 C）该妇女的主张将要求法院确定她对有线电视服务的投资是否属于受第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护的财产权益。但一项主张提出了重要的宪法问题这一事实并不意味着它成为需要司法审查的实际案件或争议。\n\n教育目标：当原告的损害得到解决后，他/她的主张就变得毫无意义，必须被驳回，除非（1）损害能够重复但逃避审查，或（2）被告自愿停止受质疑的行动但可以随时恢复。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Turner v. Rogers, 564 U.S. 431, 439–40 (2011)（解释无实际意义原则及其例外情况）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 575 (2019)（定义无意义及其例外情况）。","conclusion":"答案 D：批准该动议，因为该妇女与法官之间的争议主题已不复存在，并且不太可能重新出现。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"case or controversy","ipa":"/kˈeɪs ˈɔɹ kˈɑntɹʌvˌɝsi/","syllables":"case or con·tro·versy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"案件或争议：Article III 对联邦司法权的实际争议要求","contextualMeaning":"案件或争议：Article III 对联邦司法权的实际争议要求","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"But the fact that a claim raises an important constitutional question does not make it an actual case or controversy subject to judicial review.","synonyms":["case or contention","case or contestation","case or disputation"]},{"term":"conviction","ipa":"/kʌnvˈɪkʃən/","syllables":"con·vic·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 定罪, 信服, 坚信；[法] 定罪, 证明有罪, 判罪","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 定罪, 信服, 坚信；[法] 定罪, 证明有罪, 判罪","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:convictions","sourceSentence":"Before the woman's case came to trial, the man's criminal trial concluded in a conviction and sentencing.","synonyms":["judgment of conviction","condemnation","sentence","strong belief"]},{"term":"warranting","ipa":"/wˈɔɹənt/","syllables":"war·rant·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 保证, 担保（warrant的现在分词形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 保证, 担保（warrant的现在分词形式）","wordForm":"词；原形 warrant；词形 0:warrant/1:i","sourceSentence":"And the controversy is not likely to be revived since there were no obvious errors in the man's trial warranting a new trial.","synonyms":["guarantee","warrantee","warranty","sanction"]},{"term":"litigated","ipa":"/lˈɪtɪɡˌeɪtɪd/","syllables":"lit·i·gated","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 提出诉讼( litigate的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 提出诉讼( litigate的过去式和过去分词 )","wordForm":"词；原形 litigate；词形 0:litigate/1:dp","sourceSentence":"But once the plaintiff's harm is resolved, his/her claim becomes moot and should be dismissed unless: • the harm is capable of repetition yet evading review due to a reasonable chance that the same harm will (1) affect the plaintiff again and (2) not last long enough to be fully litigated (eg, pregnancy) or • the defendant voluntarily stops the challenged action but can resume it at any time.","synonyms":["action","sue","process"]},{"term":"convicted","ipa":"/kʌnvˈɪktəd/","syllables":"con·victed","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 宣判有罪( convict的过去式 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 宣判有罪( convict的过去式 )","wordForm":"词；原形 convict；词形 0:convict/1:dp/d:convicted/p:convicted","sourceSentence":"But before the court could consider her claim, this controversy ceased to exist because the man was convicted and sentenced.","synonyms":["con","inmate","yard bird","yardbird"]},{"term":"tried","ipa":"/tɹˈaɪd/","syllables":"tried","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 试验过的, 可靠的；[法] 已经审讯的, 已经查验的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 试验过的, 可靠的；[法] 已经审讯的, 已经查验的","wordForm":"词；原形 try；词形 0:try/1:pd/p:tried/d:tried","sourceSentence":"Defer action on the motion until after any appellate proceedings in the man's case have concluded, because the man might appeal, his conviction might be set aside, he might be tried again, and television cameras might be barred from the new trial.","synonyms":["attempt","effort","endeavor","endeavour"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-fcb4aaed424130d1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":54,"sourceQuestionPage":161,"sourceQuestionPages":[161],"sourceExplanationPages":[162,163],"sourceAnswerPages":[162,163],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-055","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federal Judicial Power","question":"The House of Representatives created an investigatory committee to look into the judicial administrative processes of federal judges. In particular, the committee was tasked with investigating the issuance of court orders, the management of trial schedules, and the drafting of basic court documents by judicial assistants. The goal of the committee was to ensure that the administrative processes were carried out efficiently, accurately, and pursuant to federal legislation regarding the judicial administrative process. After much investigation, the committee concluded that a federal district court judge was negligently mismanaging his trial schedule, causing undue delays in his case log. Based upon its findings, the committee made a recommendation to the House of Representatives to remove the federal judge. Can the House of Representatives remove the federal judge based on the committee's recommendation?","choices":{"A":"No, because the House of Representatives cannot investigate a federal judge's administrative processes.","B":"No, because the federal judge can be removed only by impeachment.","C":"Yes, because the House of Representatives can remove federal judges with or without cause.","D":"Yes, because the federal judge mismanaged his trial schedule."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Federal judges\n\nType Attributes Examples\n\nArticle I\n• Implement Congress's enumerated\n• Tax court powers\n• Bankruptcy court\n• Not entitled to life tenure &\n• Veterans' claims court undiminished pay Article\n• Decide actual cases or controversies\n• District court III\n• Entitled to life tenure & undiminished\n• Circuit appellate court pay\n• SCOTUS SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nThere are two types of federal judges:\n• Article I judges – who serve on specialized courts created by Congress under Article I to perform functions deemed helpful in carrying out Congress's enumerated powers (e.g., veterans' claims courts)\n\n• Article III judges – who serve on courts created under Article III to decide the merits of actual cases or controversies (e.g., federal district courts)\n\nArticle III judges are independent of Congress, the President, and the political process. To help guarantee their judicial independence, Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished pay during their service. This has been interpreted to mean that Article III judges can be removed from office only through impeachment. This process requires the House of Representatives to adopt articles of impeachment by a majority vote and the Senate to convict by a two-thirds vote. Here, the House of Representatives created a committee to investigate the work of federal judges. After concluding that a federal district court judge negligently mismanaged his trial schedule, the committee recommended that the House of Representatives remove him (Choice D). But since the federal district court judge can be removed only by impeachment—which also requires Senate action—the House of Representatives cannot unilaterally remove him based on the committee's recommendation. (Choice A) Both the House of Representatives and the Senate have the power to investigate a federal judge's administrative processes. But a federal district court judge can be removed only through impeachment. (Choice C) A federal district court judge can be removed through impeachment— regardless of whether adequate cause exists for removal.\n\nEducational objective: Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished pay during their service—and therefore can be removed from office only through impeachment. This process requires the House of Representatives to adopt articles of impeachment by a majority vote and the Senate to convict by a two-thirds vote.\n\nReferences\n• Glidden Co. v. Zdanok, 370 U.S. 530, 534 (1962) (stating that only Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished salaries while in service).","educationalObjective":"Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished pay during their service—and therefore can be removed from office only through impeachment. This process requires the House of Representatives to adopt articles of impeachment by a majority vote and the Senate to convict by a two-thirds vote.","references":"• Glidden Co. v. Zdanok, 370 U.S. 530, 534 (1962) (stating that only Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished salaries while in service).","rule":"Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished pay during their service—and therefore can be removed from office only through impeachment. This process requires the House of Representatives to adopt articles of impeachment by a majority vote and the Senate to convict by a two-thirds vote.","irac":{"issue":"Can the House of Representatives remove the federal judge based on the committee's recommendation?","rule":"Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished pay during their service—and therefore can be removed from office only through impeachment. This process requires the House of Representatives to adopt articles of impeachment by a majority vote and the Senate to convict by a two-thirds vote.","analysis":"Federal judges\n\nType Attributes Examples\n\nArticle I\n• Implement Congress's enumerated\n• Tax court powers\n• Bankruptcy court\n• Not entitled to life tenure &\n• Veterans' claims court undiminished pay Article\n• Decide actual cases or controversies\n• District court III\n• Entitled to life tenure & undiminished\n• Circuit appellate court pay\n• SCOTUS SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nThere are two types of federal judges:\n• Article I judges – who serve on specialized courts created by Congress under Article I to perform functions deemed helpful in carrying out Congress's enumerated powers (e.g., veterans' claims courts)\n\n• Article III judges – who serve on courts created under Article III to decide the merits of actual cases or controversies (e.g., federal district courts)\n\nArticle III judges are independent of Congress, the President, and the political process. To help guarantee their judicial independence, Article III judges are entitled to life tenure during good behavior and undiminished pay during their service. This has been interpreted to mean that Article III judges can be removed from office only through impeachment. This process requires the House of Representatives to adopt articles of impeachment by a majority vote and the Senate to convict by a two-thirds vote. Here, the House of Representatives created a committee to investigate the work of federal judges. After concluding that a federal district court judge negligently mismanaged his trial schedule, the committee recommended that the House of Representatives remove him (Choice D). But since the federal district court judge can be removed only by impeachment—which also requires Senate action—the House of Representatives cannot unilaterally remove him based on the committee's recommendation. (Choice A) Both the House of Representatives and the Senate have the power to investigate a federal judge's administrative processes. But a federal district court judge can be removed only through impeachment. (Choice C) A federal district court judge can be removed through impeachment— regardless of whether adequate cause exists for removal.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the federal judge can be removed only by impeachment."},"questionZh":"众议院成立了一个调查委员会来调查联邦法官的司法行政程序。特别是，该委员会的任务是调查法院命令的发布、审判时间表的管理以及司法助理起草基本法院文件的情况。该委员会的目标是确保行政程序高效、准确地进行，并符合有关司法行政程序的联邦立法。经过大量调查后，委员会得出结论认为，一名联邦地区法院法官疏忽大意，对审判时间表管理不善，导致案件日志过度延误。根据调查结果，该委员会向众议院建议罢免联邦法官。众议院能否根据委员会的建议罢免联邦法官？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为众议院不能调查联邦法官的行政程序。","B":"不可以，因为联邦法官只能通过弹劾才能被免职。","C":"是的，因为众议院可以有理由或无理由罢免联邦法官。","D":"是的，因为联邦法官对审判日程安排不当。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法官\n\n类型属性示例\n\n第一条\n• 实施国会列举的\n• 税务法庭的权力\n• 破产法庭\n• 无权享有终身任期&\n• 退伍军人索赔法院不减薪条款\n• 决定实际案例或争议\n• 第三区法院\n• 享有终身任期且不受减损\n• 巡回上诉法院薪酬\n• SCOTUS SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n联邦法官有两种类型：\n• 第一条法官——在国会根据第一条设立的专门法院任职，履行被认为有助于履行国会列举的权力的职能（例如，退伍军人索赔法院）\n\n• 第三条法官——在根据第三条设立的法院任职，负责裁决实际案件或争议的是非曲直（例如联邦地区法院）\n\n第三条 法官独立于国会、总统和政治进程。为了保证司法独立，第三条规定，法官在表现良好的情况下有权终身任职，并在服务期间享受不减薪的待遇。这被解释为意味着第三条法官只能通过弹劾才能被免职。这一过程需要众议院以多数票通过弹劾条款，参议院以三分之二票数定罪。在这里，众议院成立了一个委员会来调查联邦法官的工作。在得出联邦地区法院法官疏忽对审判日程管理不善的结论后，委员会建议众议院罢免他（选项 D）。但由于联邦地区法院法官只能通过弹劾来罢免——这也需要参议院采取行动——众议院不能根据委员会的建议单方面罢免他。 （选项A）众议院和参议院都有权调查联邦法官的行政程序。但联邦地区法院法官只能通过弹劾才能被免职。 （选择 C）联邦地区法院法官可以通过弹劾被免职——无论是否存在充分的免职理由。\n\n教育目标：第三条规定，法官在行为良好的情况下有权终身任职，并在服务期间享受不减薪的权利，因此只能通过弹劾将其免职。这一过程需要众议院以多数票通过弹劾条款，参议院以三分之二票数定罪。\n\n参考文献\n• Glidden Co. v. Zdanok, 370 U.S. 530, 534 (1962)（规定只有第 III 条规定的法官在行为良好的情况下才有权享有终身任期，并且在服务期间工资不减）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第三条 法官在行为良好的情况下有权享有终身职位，并在服务期间享有不减薪的权利，因此只能通过弹劾将其免职。这一过程需要众议院以多数票通过弹劾条款，参议院以三分之二票数定罪。","iracZh":{"issue":"众议院能否根据委员会的建议罢免联邦法官？","rule":"第三条 法官在行为良好的情况下有权享有终身职位，并在服务期间享有不减薪的权利，因此只能通过弹劾将其免职。这一过程需要众议院以多数票通过弹劾条款，参议院以三分之二票数定罪。","analysis":"联邦法官\n\n类型属性示例\n\n第一条\n• 实施国会列举的\n• 税务法庭的权力\n• 破产法庭\n• 无权享有终身任期&\n• 退伍军人索赔法院不减薪条款\n• 决定实际案例或争议\n• 第三区法院\n• 享有终身任期且不受减损\n• 巡回上诉法院薪酬\n• SCOTUS SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n联邦法官有两种类型：\n• 第一条法官——在国会根据第一条设立的专门法院任职，履行被认为有助于履行国会列举的权力的职能（例如，退伍军人索赔法院）\n\n• 第三条法官——在根据第三条设立的法院任职，负责裁决实际案件或争议的是非曲直（例如联邦地区法院）\n\n第三条 法官独立于国会、总统和政治进程。为了保证司法独立，第三条规定，法官在表现良好的情况下有权终身任职，并在服务期间享受不减薪的待遇。这被解释为意味着第三条法官只能通过弹劾才能被免职。这一过程需要众议院以多数票通过弹劾条款，参议院以三分之二票数定罪。在这里，众议院成立了一个委员会来调查联邦法官的工作。在得出联邦地区法院法官疏忽对审判日程管理不善的结论后，委员会建议众议院罢免他（选项 D）。但由于联邦地区法院法官只能通过弹劾来罢免——这也需要参议院采取行动——众议院不能根据委员会的建议单方面罢免他。 （选项A）众议院和参议院都有权调查联邦法官的行政程序。但联邦地区法院法官只能通过弹劾才能被免职。 （选择 C）联邦地区法院法官可以通过弹劾被免职——无论是否存在充分的免职理由。\n\n教育目标：第三条规定，法官在行为良好的情况下有权终身任职，并在服务期间享受不减薪的权利，因此只能通过弹劾将其免职。这一过程需要众议院以多数票通过弹劾条款，参议院以三分之二票数定罪。\n\n参考文献\n• Glidden Co. v. Zdanok, 370 U.S. 530, 534 (1962)（规定只有第 III 条规定的法官在行为良好的情况下才有权享有终身任期，并且在服务期间工资不减）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为联邦法官只能通过弹劾才能被免职。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"enumerated","ipa":"/ɪnˈumɝˌeɪtɪd/","syllables":"enu·mer·ated","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 列举, 枚举, 数( enumerate的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 列举, 枚举, 数( enumerate的过去式和过去分词 )","wordForm":"词；原形 enumerate；词形 0:enumerate/1:dp","sourceSentence":"Federal judges Type Attributes Examples Article I • Implement Congress's enumerated • Tax court powers • Bankruptcy court • Not entitled to life tenure & • Veterans' claims court undiminished pay Article • Decide actual cases or controversies • District court III • Entitled to life tenure & undiminished • Circuit appellate court pay • SCOTUS SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","synonyms":["recite","itemize","itemise","count"]},{"term":"findings","ipa":"/fˈaɪndɪŋz/","syllables":"find·ings","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 发现, 调查结果；检验发现的情况（finding复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 发现, 调查结果；检验发现的情况（finding复数）","wordForm":"名词；原形 finding；词形 0:finding/1:s","sourceSentence":"Based upon its findings, the committee made a recommendation to the House of Representatives to remove the federal judge.","synonyms":["determination"]},{"term":"circuit","ipa":"/sˈɝkət/","syllables":"cir·cuit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 电路, 环(行)道, 巡回；[计] 线路； 电路","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 电路, 环(行)道, 巡回；[计] 线路； 电路","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:circuits/d:circuited/i:circuiting/p:circuited/3:circuits","sourceSentence":"Federal judges Type Attributes Examples Article I • Implement Congress's enumerated • Tax court powers • Bankruptcy court • Not entitled to life tenure & • Veterans' claims court undiminished pay Article • Decide actual cases or controversies • District court III • Entitled to life tenure & undiminished • Circuit appellate court pay • SCOTUS SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","synonyms":["lap","circle","tour","electrical circuit"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":55,"sourceQuestionPage":164,"sourceQuestionPages":[164],"sourceExplanationPages":[165,166],"sourceAnswerPages":[165,166],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-056","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Appointments & Removal","question":"A statute passed by both houses of Congress and signed by the President created a federal agency to regulate consumer financial products such as credit cards, student loans, and mortgages. The federal statute authorizes the agency to issue regulatory rules, conduct administrative proceedings, and initiate suits in federal court on behalf of consumers. A statutory provision establishes that the agency is led by a single director who must be appointed by the President with the advice and consent of the Senate. The provision also states that the director serves a five-year term and can only be removed from office by the President for good cause. Is the provision allowing the director to be removed by the President for good cause constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the President may remove a federal executive officer only with the advice and consent of the Senate.","B":"No, because the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function.","C":"Yes, because Congress may require the President to show good cause to remove a federal executive officer.","D":"Yes, because the provision is part of a statute that was passed by both houses of Congress and signed by the President."},"answer":"B","explanation":"[Current-law update — answer unchanged] Trump v. Slaughter (2026) invalidated the FTC's for-cause removal provision and rejected the former broad Humphrey's Executor exception. The single-director restriction here was already unconstitutional under Seila Law and remains so.\n\nThe Constitution divides the federal government into three coequal branches that possess distinct powers (ie, separation of powers). Article II vests the executive power in the President, including the power to faithfully execute federal laws. Since it is impossible for one person to carry out this power, federal executive officers may be appointed pursuant to the appointments clause to assist the President. The Constitution is silent about the removal of federal executive officers, but the U.S. Supreme Court has repeatedly held that the President has absolute authority to remove such officers absent two exceptions. This means that Congress cannot impose any removal restrictions, including good-cause requirements, except for officers who either:\n• belong to a multimember body that is balanced along partisan lines and exercises no executive power (eg, committees that conduct investigations on behalf of Congress) or\n• lack policymaking or administrative authority (eg, independent counsel who investigates and prosecutes alleged crimes by government officials). Here, Congress created a federal agency headed by a single director. The director exercises significant executive power and administrative authority by issuing regulatory rules, conducting administrative proceedings, and initiating suits in federal court. As a result, the President has the absolute authority to remove the director. The provision requiring good cause for removal therefore amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function (Choice C). (Choice A) The President must appoint certain federal executive officers (eg, cabinet secretaries) with the advice and consent of the Senate. However, the Senate's advice and consent is not required for the President to remove such officers. (Choice D) The removal provision is part of a statute that satisfied the federal legislative process because it was passed by both houses of Congress and signed by the President. But the provision is still an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive power.\n\nEducational objective: The President has absolute authority to remove federal executive officers. Congress may impose restrictions on this power only for officers who (1) belong to a multimember body that is balanced along partisan lines and exercises no executive power or (2) lack policymaking or administrative authority.\n\nReferences\n• Seila Law Ltd. Liab. Corp. v. Consumer Fin. Prot. Bureau, 140 S. Ct. 2183, 2197–2200 (2020) (explaining that the President has the absolute power to remove federal executive officers subject to very narrow exceptions).","educationalObjective":"Article II generally gives the President authority to remove executive officers at will. Trump v. Slaughter (2026) held the FTC for-cause removal restriction unconstitutional; statutory for-cause protection for a single director exercising substantial executive power is likewise invalid.","references":"• Seila Law Ltd. Liab. Corp. v. Consumer Fin. Prot. Bureau, 140 S. Ct. 2183, 2197–2200 (2020) (explaining that the President has the absolute power to remove federal executive officers subject to very narrow exceptions).","rule":"Article II generally gives the President authority to remove executive officers at will. Trump v. Slaughter (2026) held the FTC for-cause removal restriction unconstitutional; statutory for-cause protection for a single director exercising substantial executive power is likewise invalid.","irac":{"issue":"Is the provision allowing the director to be removed by the President for good cause constitutional?","rule":"Article II generally gives the President authority to remove executive officers at will. Trump v. Slaughter (2026) held the FTC for-cause removal restriction unconstitutional; statutory for-cause protection for a single director exercising substantial executive power is likewise invalid.","analysis":"The Constitution divides the federal government into three coequal branches that possess distinct powers (ie, separation of powers). Article II vests the executive power in the President, including the power to faithfully execute federal laws. Since it is impossible for one person to carry out this power, federal executive officers may be appointed pursuant to the appointments clause to assist the President. The Constitution is silent about the removal of federal executive officers, but the U.S. Supreme Court has repeatedly held that the President has absolute authority to remove such officers absent two exceptions. This means that Congress cannot impose any removal restrictions, including good-cause requirements, except for officers who either:\n• belong to a multimember body that is balanced along partisan lines and exercises no executive power (eg, committees that conduct investigations on behalf of Congress) or\n• lack policymaking or administrative authority (eg, independent counsel who investigates and prosecutes alleged crimes by government officials). Here, Congress created a federal agency headed by a single director. The director exercises significant executive power and administrative authority by issuing regulatory rules, conducting administrative proceedings, and initiating suits in federal court. As a result, the President has the absolute authority to remove the director. The provision requiring good cause for removal therefore amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function (Choice C). (Choice A) The President must appoint certain federal executive officers (eg, cabinet secretaries) with the advice and consent of the Senate. However, the Senate's advice and consent is not required for the President to remove such officers. (Choice D) The removal provision is part of a statute that satisfied the federal legislative process because it was passed by both houses of Congress and signed by the President. But the provision is still an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive power.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function."},"questionZh":"国会两院通过并由总统签署的一项法规成立了一个联邦机构来监管信用卡、学生贷款和抵押贷款等消费金融产品。联邦法规授权该机构发布监管规则、开展行政诉讼以及代表消费者向联邦法院提起诉讼。一项法定条款规定，该机构由一名董事领导，该董事必须由总统在参议院的建议和同意下任命。该条款还规定，主任的任期为五年，只有在有正当理由的情况下才能由总统免职。允许总统因正当理由罢免董事的规定是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为总统只有在参议院建议和同意的情况下才能罢免联邦行政官员。","B":"不可以，因为该规定相当于对行政职能的立法干预，违宪。","C":"是的，因为国会可能要求总统拿出充分理由罢免联邦行政官员。","D":"是的，因为该条款是国会两院通过并由总统签署的法规的一部分。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法更新——答案不变】Trump v. Slaughter（2026）认定 FTC 的“仅因故免职”限制违宪，并否定原先宽泛的 Humphrey’s Executor 例外。本题单一负责人的免职限制依 Seila Law 已属违宪，现行法结论相同。\n\n宪法将联邦政府分为三个平等的部门，拥有不同的权力（即权力分立）。第二条赋予总统行政权，包括忠实执行联邦法律的权力。由于不可能由一个人行使这一权力，因此可以根据任命条款任命联邦行政官员协助总统。 《宪法》没有提及罢免联邦行政官员，但美国最高法院一再裁定，总统拥有罢免此类官员的绝对权力，除非有两个例外。这意味着国会不能施加任何驱逐限制，包括正当理由要求，但以下官员除外：\n• 属于一个党派平衡且不行使行政权力的多成员机构（例如，代表国会进行调查的委员会）或\n• 缺乏决策或行政权力（例如，调查和起诉政府官员涉嫌犯罪的独立律师）。在这里，国会设立了一个由一名主任领导的联邦机构。董事通过发布监管规则、进行行政程序以及向联邦法院提起诉讼来行使重要的行政权力和行政权力。因此，总统拥有罢免董事的绝对权力。因此，要求有充分理由罢免的条款相当于对行政职能的违宪立法干预（选项 C）。 （选择A）总统必须在参议院的建议和同意下任命某些联邦行政官员（例如内阁部长）。然而，总统无需征得参议院的建议和同意即可罢免此类官员。 （选项D） 罢免条款是满足联邦立法程序的法规的一部分，因为它是由国会两院通过并由总统签署的。但该规定仍然是违宪的立法干涉行政权。\n\n教育目标：总统拥有罢免联邦行政官员的绝对权力。国会只能对以下官员的这一权力施加限制：(1) 属于一个按党派平衡的多成员机构，不行使行政权力，或 (2) 缺乏决策或行政权力。\n\n参考文献\n• Seila Law Ltd. Liab。公司诉消费者金融公司。普罗特。局，140 S.Ct. 2183, 2197–2200 (2020)（解释总统拥有罢免联邦行政官员的绝对权力，但有极少数例外情况）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"Article II 原则上赋予总统任意免除行政官员的权力。Trump v. Slaughter（2026）认定 FTC 委员的法定“仅因故免职”保护违宪；对行使重大行政权的单一负责人设置同类保护亦属无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"允许总统因正当理由罢免董事的规定是否符合宪法？","rule":"Article II 原则上赋予总统任意免除行政官员的权力。Trump v. Slaughter（2026）认定 FTC 委员的法定“仅因故免职”保护违宪；对行使重大行政权的单一负责人设置同类保护亦属无效。","analysis":"宪法将联邦政府分为三个平等的部门，拥有不同的权力（即权力分立）。第二条赋予总统行政权，包括忠实执行联邦法律的权力。由于不可能由一个人行使这一权力，因此可以根据任命条款任命联邦行政官员协助总统。 《宪法》没有提及罢免联邦行政官员，但美国最高法院一再裁定，总统拥有罢免此类官员的绝对权力，除非有两个例外。这意味着国会不能施加任何驱逐限制，包括正当理由要求，但以下官员除外：\n• 属于一个党派平衡且不行使行政权力的多成员机构（例如，代表国会进行调查的委员会）或\n• 缺乏决策或行政权力（例如，调查和起诉政府官员涉嫌犯罪的独立律师）。在这里，国会设立了一个由一名主任领导的联邦机构。董事通过发布监管规则、进行行政程序以及向联邦法院提起诉讼来行使重要的行政权力和行政权力。因此，总统拥有罢免董事的绝对权力。因此，要求有充分理由罢免的条款相当于对行政职能的违宪立法干预（选项 C）。 （选择A）总统必须在参议院的建议和同意下任命某些联邦行政官员（例如内阁部长）。然而，总统无需征得参议院的建议和同意即可罢免此类官员。 （选项D） 罢免条款是满足联邦立法程序的法规的一部分，因为它是由国会两院通过并由总统签署的。但该规定仍然是违宪的立法干涉行政权。\n\n教育目标：总统拥有罢免联邦行政官员的绝对权力。国会只能对以下官员的这一权力施加限制：(1) 属于一个按党派平衡的多成员机构，不行使行政权力，或 (2) 缺乏决策或行政权力。\n\n参考文献\n• Seila Law Ltd. Liab。公司诉消费者金融公司。普罗特。局，140 S.Ct. 2183, 2197–2200 (2020)（解释总统拥有罢免联邦行政官员的绝对权力，但有极少数例外情况）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为该规定相当于对行政职能的立法干预，违宪。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-2026-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"以 2026 年 Trump v. Slaughter 更新总统免职权规则；原答案 B 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Trump v. Slaughter, 609 U.S. ___ (2026)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/25-332_new_geil.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-3192c014878839d5"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":56,"sourceQuestionPage":167,"sourceQuestionPages":[167],"sourceExplanationPages":[168,169],"sourceAnswerPages":[168,169],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-057","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Mootness & Ripeness","question":"A nightclub owner applied for a required zoning permit to open a nude-dancing nightclub in the theater district of a city. An organization of influential city residents began an intensive lobbying effort to persuade the city council to deny the owner a permit to operate any type of nude-dancing facility at any time or in any place in the city. The owner has sued the city in an appropriate federal court, seeking an injunction that would prohibit the city council from considering the organization's views, on the ground that if the organization is successful in its lobbying efforts, the owner's First and Fourteenth Amendment rights would be violated. The city has moved to dismiss the action. Should the court dismiss the owner's action?","choices":{"A":"No, because nude dancing is symbolic speech and is therefore protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","B":"No, because the organization does not seek a reasonable time, place, and manner regulation of nude dancing, but instead seeks a total ban on the owner's opening any type of nude-dancing facility at any time or in any place in the city.","C":"Yes, because the action is not ripe.","D":"Yes, because the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not protect obscenity, and nude dancing is obscene."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Article III of the Constitution limits the jurisdiction of federal courts to actual cases or controversies. An actual case or controversy exists when a dispute between adverse parties is capable of judicial resolution—ie, ripe for adjudication. A suit is ripe when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. But a suit based on potential future harm is unripe and should be dismissed. Here, the nightclub owner applied for a permit to open a nude-dancing facility. An organization then lobbied the city council to deny the permit. In response, the owner sued in federal court to enjoin the city council from considering the organization's views. But the city council has not yet denied his permit request (no actual harm), nor is there any indication that it will do so soon (no immediate threat). And since the owner's action is not ripe, the court should grant the city's motion and dismiss the suit. (Choices A & B) Nude dancing is symbolic speech that is protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments. As a result, a total ban on nude dancing (ie, a content-based restriction) would be presumptively unconstitutional. In contrast, a reasonable time, place, and manner (ie, content-neutral) regulation would likely be valid. But the court will not consider the constitutionality of the potential regulation since the suit is unripe. (Choice D) The First and Fourteenth Amendments do not protect certain forms of speech, including obscenity. But mere nudity (including nude dancing) is not obscene.\n\nEducational objective: For a suit to be ripe for adjudication, the plaintiff must have suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. Therefore, a claim based on potential future harm is unripe and will be dismissed.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998) (explaining when a claim is unripe for adjudication).","educationalObjective":"For a suit to be ripe for adjudication, the plaintiff must have suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. Therefore, a claim based on potential future harm is unripe and will be dismissed.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998) (explaining when a claim is unripe for adjudication).","rule":"For a suit to be ripe for adjudication, the plaintiff must have suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. Therefore, a claim based on potential future harm is unripe and will be dismissed.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court dismiss the owner's action?","rule":"For a suit to be ripe for adjudication, the plaintiff must have suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. Therefore, a claim based on potential future harm is unripe and will be dismissed.","analysis":"Article III of the Constitution limits the jurisdiction of federal courts to actual cases or controversies. An actual case or controversy exists when a dispute between adverse parties is capable of judicial resolution—ie, ripe for adjudication. A suit is ripe when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. But a suit based on potential future harm is unripe and should be dismissed. Here, the nightclub owner applied for a permit to open a nude-dancing facility. An organization then lobbied the city council to deny the permit. In response, the owner sued in federal court to enjoin the city council from considering the organization's views. But the city council has not yet denied his permit request (no actual harm), nor is there any indication that it will do so soon (no immediate threat). And since the owner's action is not ripe, the court should grant the city's motion and dismiss the suit. (Choices A & B) Nude dancing is symbolic speech that is protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments. As a result, a total ban on nude dancing (ie, a content-based restriction) would be presumptively unconstitutional. In contrast, a reasonable time, place, and manner (ie, content-neutral) regulation would likely be valid. But the court will not consider the constitutionality of the potential regulation since the suit is unripe. (Choice D) The First and Fourteenth Amendments do not protect certain forms of speech, including obscenity. But mere nudity (including nude dancing) is not obscene.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the action is not ripe."},"questionZh":"一名夜总会老板申请了所需的分区许可证，以便在某城市的剧院区开设一家裸舞夜总会。一个由有影响力的城市居民组成的组织开始密集游说，说服市议会拒绝业主在城市任何时间、任何地点经营任何类型的裸体舞蹈设施的许可证。业主已向适当的联邦法院起诉该市，寻求一项禁令，禁止市议会考虑该组织的观点，理由是如果该组织的游说工作成功，业主的第一和第十四修正案权利将受到侵犯。该市已采取行动驳回这一诉讼。法院是否应该驳回业主的诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为裸体舞蹈是象征性的言论，因此受到第一修正案和第十四修正案的保护。","B":"不，因为该组织并不寻求对裸体舞蹈进行合理的时间、地点和方式规定，而是寻求全面禁止业主在任何时间、任何地点在城市内开设任何类型的裸体舞蹈设施。","C":"是的，因为行动还不成熟。","D":"是的，因为第一和第十四修正案不保护淫秽，而裸体跳舞是淫秽的。"},"explanationZh":"宪法第三条将联邦法院的管辖权限于实际案件或争议。当敌对双方之间的争议能够通过司法解决（即裁决时机成熟）时，就存在实际案件或争议。当原告遭受实际伤害或直接威胁时，诉讼就成熟了。但基于未来潜在危害的诉讼还不成熟，应该被驳回。在这里，夜总会老板申请了开设裸体舞蹈设施的许可证。随后，一个组织游说市议会拒绝发放许可证。作为回应，业主向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求禁止市议会考虑该组织的观点。但市议会尚未拒绝他的许可请求（没有实际伤害），也没有任何迹象表明它将很快拒绝（没有立即威胁）。由于业主的诉讼还不成熟，法院应批准市政府的动议并驳回诉讼。 （选项 A 和 B） 裸体舞蹈是受第一修正案和第十四修正案保护的象征性言论。因此，全面禁止裸体跳舞（即基于内容的限制）将被推定为违宪。相反，合理的时间、地点和方式（即内容中立）监管可能是有效的。但由于诉讼尚未成熟，法院不会考虑潜在法规的合宪性。 （选项 D）第一修正案和第十四修正案不保护某些形式的言论，包括淫秽言论。但单纯的裸体（包括裸体跳舞）并不猥亵。\n\n教育目标：为了使诉讼的裁决时机成熟，原告必须遭受实际伤害或直接威胁。因此，基于未来潜在损害的索赔尚不成熟，将被驳回。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998)（解释何时裁决尚不成熟）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了使诉讼的裁决时机成熟，原告必须遭受实际伤害或直接威胁。因此，基于未来潜在损害的索赔尚不成熟，将被驳回。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该驳回业主的诉讼？","rule":"为了使诉讼的裁决时机成熟，原告必须遭受实际伤害或直接威胁。因此，基于未来潜在损害的索赔尚不成熟，将被驳回。","analysis":"宪法第三条将联邦法院的管辖权限于实际案件或争议。当敌对双方之间的争议能够通过司法解决（即裁决时机成熟）时，就存在实际案件或争议。当原告遭受实际伤害或直接威胁时，诉讼就成熟了。但基于未来潜在危害的诉讼还不成熟，应该被驳回。在这里，夜总会老板申请了开设裸体舞蹈设施的许可证。随后，一个组织游说市议会拒绝发放许可证。作为回应，业主向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求禁止市议会考虑该组织的观点。但市议会尚未拒绝他的许可请求（没有实际伤害），也没有任何迹象表明它将很快拒绝（没有立即威胁）。由于业主的诉讼还不成熟，法院应批准市政府的动议并驳回诉讼。 （选项 A 和 B） 裸体舞蹈是受第一修正案和第十四修正案保护的象征性言论。因此，全面禁止裸体跳舞（即基于内容的限制）将被推定为违宪。相反，合理的时间、地点和方式（即内容中立）监管可能是有效的。但由于诉讼尚未成熟，法院不会考虑潜在法规的合宪性。 （选项 D）第一修正案和第十四修正案不保护某些形式的言论，包括淫秽言论。但单纯的裸体（包括裸体跳舞）并不猥亵。\n\n教育目标：为了使诉讼的裁决时机成熟，原告必须遭受实际伤害或直接威胁。因此，基于未来潜在损害的索赔尚不成熟，将被驳回。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998)（解释何时裁决尚不成熟）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为行动还不成熟。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"symbolic speech","ipa":"/sɪmbˈɑlɪk spˈitʃ/","syllables":"sym·bolic speech","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"象征性言论：以行为传达特定信息的受保护表达","contextualMeaning":"象征性言论：以行为传达特定信息的受保护表达","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because nude dancing is symbolic speech and is therefore protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","synonyms":["symbolic language","symbolic lecture","symbolic talking to"]},{"term":"obscenity","ipa":"/ʌbsˈɛnɪti/","syllables":"ob·scen·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 淫秽, 淫秽的语言(或行为、动作), 可憎的事；[法] 猥亵, 淫诲, 猥亵的言语或行动","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 淫秽, 淫秽的语言(或行为、动作), 可憎的事；[法] 猥亵, 淫诲, 猥亵的言语或行动","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:obscenities","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not protect obscenity, and nude dancing is obscene.","synonyms":["lewdness","bawdiness","salaciousness","salacity"]},{"term":"obscene","ipa":"/ɑbsˈin/","syllables":"ob·scene","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 淫秽的, 猥亵的, 讨人厌的, 可憎的；[法] 猥亵的, 海洋的, 污秽的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 淫秽的, 猥亵的, 讨人厌的, 可憎的；[法] 猥亵的, 海洋的, 污秽的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not protect obscenity, and nude dancing is obscene.","synonyms":["lewd","raunchy","salacious","abhorrent"]},{"term":"zoning","ipa":"/zˈoʊnɪŋ/","syllables":"zon·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 分区制；[医] 带现象(补体结合)","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 分区制；[医] 带现象(补体结合)","wordForm":"名词；词形 i:zoning/0:zoning/1:i","sourceSentence":"A nightclub owner applied for a required zoning permit to open a nude-dancing nightclub in the theater district of a city.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-fcb4aaed424130d1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":57,"sourceQuestionPage":170,"sourceQuestionPages":[170],"sourceExplanationPages":[171,172],"sourceAnswerPages":[171,172],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-058","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Mootness & Ripeness","question":"The federal parole commission denied a federal prisoner's request for parole based on the commission's parole guidelines. The prisoner subsequently filed a class action against the commission in federal court, challenging the facial validity of the commission's parole guidelines on the basis that the guidelines violate federal law. After the court certified the class action, the prisoner was released from prison. The commission has moved to dismiss the case. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because an actual case or controversy exists for the court to decide.","B":"No, because the prisoner's suit is not ripe for decision.","C":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits this type of action in federal court.","D":"Yes, because the prisoner's claim has become moot."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Ripeness v. Mootness\n\nRipe Action will not be heard until plaintiff has experienced actual injury or imminent threat thereof (too soon)\n\nMoot Action will be dismissed if further proceedings would have no effect unless:\n\n(too late)\n• plaintiff's harm is capable of repetition yet evading review (eg, pregnancy)\n• defendant voluntarily stops action but can resume it anytime\n• plaintiff is named plaintiff in class action & claims of unnamed plaintiffs are still viable Article III of the Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide actual cases and controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. If further legal proceedings would no longer have an effect—often because the plaintiff's harm has been resolved—then an actual case or controversy typically ceases to exist. When this occurs, the plaintiff's claim is typically rendered moot and the case must then be dismissed. However, one exception to this rule applies when the plaintiff whose claim has become moot is the named plaintiff in a certified class action (as seen here). If the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs in the class are still viable, then the class action will not be dismissed as moot. That is because, while the named plaintiff's personal claim has become moot, an actual case or controversy still exists for the unnamed plaintiffs' claims. Here, the prisoner filed a class action in federal court, thereby serving as the named plaintiff. The prisoner's personal claim—that the commission's parole guidelines violate federal law—became moot when he was released from prison. However, an actual case or controversy still exists for the unnamed plaintiffs who have not been released (Choice D). Therefore, the court should deny the commission's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) For a suit to be ripe for decision, the plaintiff must have experienced a real injury or an immediate threat thereof. Here, the prisoner suffered actual harm when the commission denied the prisoner's request for parole, so this suit is ripe for decision. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment prohibits foreign governments and private parties from suing a state in federal court. It does not prohibit private parties from suing the federal government in federal court (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A federal court typically must dismiss a case when the plaintiff's claim becomes moot. However, one exception applies when (1) a plaintiff whose claim is moot is the named plaintiff in a certified class action and (2) the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs in that class action are still viable.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• U.S. Parole Comm'n v. Geraghty, 445 U.S. 388, 404 (1980) (holding that federal courts must not dismiss a class action when the named plaintiff's claim becomes moot so long as the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs are viable).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 575 (2020) (defining mootness and its exceptions).","educationalObjective":"A federal court typically must dismiss a case when the plaintiff's claim becomes moot. However, one exception applies when (1) a plaintiff whose claim is moot is the named plaintiff in a certified class action and (2) the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs in that class action are still viable.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• U.S. Parole Comm'n v. Geraghty, 445 U.S. 388, 404 (1980) (holding that federal courts must not dismiss a class action when the named plaintiff's claim becomes moot so long as the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs are viable).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 575 (2020) (defining mootness and its exceptions).","rule":"A federal court typically must dismiss a case when the plaintiff's claim becomes moot. However, one exception applies when (1) a plaintiff whose claim is moot is the named plaintiff in a certified class action and (2) the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs in that class action are still viable.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"A federal court typically must dismiss a case when the plaintiff's claim becomes moot. However, one exception applies when (1) a plaintiff whose claim is moot is the named plaintiff in a certified class action and (2) the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs in that class action are still viable.","analysis":"Ripeness v. Mootness\n\nRipe Action will not be heard until plaintiff has experienced actual injury or imminent threat thereof (too soon)\n\nMoot Action will be dismissed if further proceedings would have no effect unless:\n\n(too late)\n• plaintiff's harm is capable of repetition yet evading review (eg, pregnancy)\n• defendant voluntarily stops action but can resume it anytime\n• plaintiff is named plaintiff in class action & claims of unnamed plaintiffs are still viable Article III of the Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide actual cases and controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. If further legal proceedings would no longer have an effect—often because the plaintiff's harm has been resolved—then an actual case or controversy typically ceases to exist. When this occurs, the plaintiff's claim is typically rendered moot and the case must then be dismissed. However, one exception to this rule applies when the plaintiff whose claim has become moot is the named plaintiff in a certified class action (as seen here). If the claims of the unnamed plaintiffs in the class are still viable, then the class action will not be dismissed as moot. That is because, while the named plaintiff's personal claim has become moot, an actual case or controversy still exists for the unnamed plaintiffs' claims. Here, the prisoner filed a class action in federal court, thereby serving as the named plaintiff. The prisoner's personal claim—that the commission's parole guidelines violate federal law—became moot when he was released from prison. However, an actual case or controversy still exists for the unnamed plaintiffs who have not been released (Choice D). Therefore, the court should deny the commission's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) For a suit to be ripe for decision, the plaintiff must have experienced a real injury or an immediate threat thereof. Here, the prisoner suffered actual harm when the commission denied the prisoner's request for parole, so this suit is ripe for decision. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment prohibits foreign governments and private parties from suing a state in federal court. It does not prohibit private parties from suing the federal government in federal court (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because an actual case or controversy exists for the court to decide."},"questionZh":"联邦假释委员会根据委员会的假释指南拒绝了联邦囚犯的假释请求。该囚犯随后向联邦法院对该委员会提起集体诉讼，质疑该委员会假释指南的表面有效性，理由是该指南违反了联邦法律。法院批准集体诉讼后，该囚犯被释放出狱。该委员会已采取行动驳回此案。法院应该批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为存在实际案件或争议，需要法院裁决。","B":"不，因为囚犯诉讼的判决时机还不成熟。","C":"是的，因为第十一修正案禁止联邦法院采取此类行动。","D":"是的，因为囚犯的主张已经变得毫无意义。"},"explanationZh":"成熟与无意义\n\n在原告经历实际伤害或迫在眉睫的威胁之前，不会听到成熟诉讼（太早）\n\n如果进一步的程序无效，模拟诉讼将被驳回，除非：\n\n（太晚了）\n• 原告的伤害可能会重复但逃避审查（例如怀孕）\n• 被告自愿停止行动，但可以随时恢复行动\n• 原告在集体诉讼中被指定为原告，且未指定姓名的原告的主张仍然有效 《宪法》第三条赋予联邦法院裁决实际案件和争议的权力，即能够通过司法解决的争议。如果进一步的法律诉讼不再产生影响（通常是因为原告的伤害已经解决），那么实际的案件或争议通常不复存在。当这种情况发生时，原告的索赔通常变得毫无意义，案件必须被驳回。但是，当索赔已变得毫无意义的原告是经认证的集体诉讼中指定的原告（如此处所示）时，则适用此规则的一个例外。如果集体诉讼中未透露姓名的原告的主张仍然可行，那么集体诉讼就不会因为没有实际意义而被驳回。这是因为，虽然具名原告的个人索赔已变得毫无意义，但未具名原告的索赔仍然存在实际案件或争议。在此，囚犯向联邦法院提起集体诉讼，从而成为指定原告。囚犯的个人主张——委员会的假释指导方针违反了联邦法律——在他出狱后就变得毫无意义。但对于未获释的未透露姓名的原告来说，实际案件或争议仍然存在（选项D）。因此，法院应驳回委员会的驳回动议。 （选择B）为了使诉讼的判决时机成熟，原告必须经历过真正的伤害或直接的伤害威胁。在这里，当委员会拒绝囚犯的假释请求时，囚犯受到了实际伤害，因此该诉讼的判决时机已经成熟。 （选项 C）第十一修正案禁止外国政府和私人团体在联邦法院起诉州政府。它并不禁止私人团体在联邦法院起诉联邦政府（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当原告的主张变得毫无意义时，联邦法院通常必须驳回案件。但是，有一个例外情况适用：(1) 索赔无实际意义的原告是经过认证的集体诉讼中的指定原告，并且 (2) 该集体诉讼中未指定姓名的原告的索赔仍然可行。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• U.S. Parole Comm'n v. Geraghty, 445 U.S. 388, 404 (1980)（认为只要未透露姓名的原告的主张成立，当指定的原告的主张变得毫无意义时，联邦法院不得驳回集体诉讼）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 575 (2020)（定义无意义及其例外情况）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当原告的主张变得毫无意义时，联邦法院通常必须驳回案件。但是，有一个例外情况适用：(1) 索赔无实际意义的原告是经过认证的集体诉讼中的指定原告，并且 (2) 该集体诉讼中未指定姓名的原告的索赔仍然可行。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该批准该动议吗？","rule":"当原告的主张变得毫无意义时，联邦法院通常必须驳回案件。但是，有一个例外情况适用：(1) 索赔无实际意义的原告是经过认证的集体诉讼中的指定原告，并且 (2) 该集体诉讼中未指定姓名的原告的索赔仍然可行。","analysis":"成熟与无意义\n\n在原告经历实际伤害或迫在眉睫的威胁之前，不会听到成熟诉讼（太早）\n\n如果进一步的程序无效，模拟诉讼将被驳回，除非：\n\n（太晚了）\n• 原告的伤害可能会重复但逃避审查（例如怀孕）\n• 被告自愿停止行动，但可以随时恢复行动\n• 原告在集体诉讼中被指定为原告，且未指定姓名的原告的主张仍然有效 《宪法》第三条赋予联邦法院裁决实际案件和争议的权力，即能够通过司法解决的争议。如果进一步的法律诉讼不再产生影响（通常是因为原告的伤害已经解决），那么实际的案件或争议通常不复存在。当这种情况发生时，原告的索赔通常变得毫无意义，案件必须被驳回。但是，当索赔已变得毫无意义的原告是经认证的集体诉讼中指定的原告（如此处所示）时，则适用此规则的一个例外。如果集体诉讼中未透露姓名的原告的主张仍然可行，那么集体诉讼就不会因为没有实际意义而被驳回。这是因为，虽然具名原告的个人索赔已变得毫无意义，但未具名原告的索赔仍然存在实际案件或争议。在此，囚犯向联邦法院提起集体诉讼，从而成为指定原告。囚犯的个人主张——委员会的假释指导方针违反了联邦法律——在他出狱后就变得毫无意义。但对于未获释的未透露姓名的原告来说，实际案件或争议仍然存在（选项D）。因此，法院应驳回委员会的驳回动议。 （选择B）为了使诉讼的判决时机成熟，原告必须经历过真正的伤害或直接的伤害威胁。在这里，当委员会拒绝囚犯的假释请求时，囚犯受到了实际伤害，因此该诉讼的判决时机已经成熟。 （选项 C）第十一修正案禁止外国政府和私人团体在联邦法院起诉州政府。它并不禁止私人团体在联邦法院起诉联邦政府（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当原告的主张变得毫无意义时，联邦法院通常必须驳回案件。但是，有一个例外情况适用：(1) 索赔无实际意义的原告是经过认证的集体诉讼中的指定原告，并且 (2) 该集体诉讼中未指定姓名的原告的索赔仍然可行。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• U.S. Parole Comm'n v. Geraghty, 445 U.S. 388, 404 (1980)（认为只要未透露姓名的原告的主张成立，当指定的原告的主张变得毫无意义时，联邦法院不得驳回集体诉讼）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 575 (2020)（定义无意义及其例外情况）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为存在实际案件或争议，需要法院裁决。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"ripeness","ipa":"/'raipnis/","syllables":"ripeness","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 成熟, 圆通, 完成","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 成熟, 圆通, 完成","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Ripeness v.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"parole","ipa":"/pɝˈoʊl/","syllables":"pa·role","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 假释, 誓言, 释放宣言；vt. 宣誓后释放, 使假释出狱","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 假释, 誓言, 释放宣言；vt. 宣誓后释放, 使假释出狱","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:paroled/s:paroles/p:paroled/i:paroling/3:paroles","sourceSentence":"The federal parole commission denied a federal prisoner's request for parole based on the commission's parole guidelines.","synonyms":["password","watchword","word","countersign"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":58,"sourceQuestionPage":173,"sourceQuestionPages":[173],"sourceExplanationPages":[174,175],"sourceAnswerPages":[174,175],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-059","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"A federal statute provides that the United States Supreme Court has authority to review any case filed in a United States court of appeals, even if that case has not yet been decided by the court of appeals. The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), an agency in the executive branch of the federal government, issued an important environmental rule. Although the rule had not yet been enforced against them, companies that would be adversely affected by the rule filed a petition for review of the rule in a court of appeals, seeking a declaration that the rule was invalid solely because it was beyond the statutory authority of the EPA. The companies made no constitutional claim. A statute specifically provides for direct review of EPA rules by a court of appeals without any initial action in a district court. The companies have filed a petition for a writ of certiorari in the Supreme Court requesting immediate review of this case by the Supreme Court before the court of appeals has actually decided the case. The EPA acknowledges that the case is important enough to warrant Supreme Court review and that it should be decided promptly, but it asks the Supreme Court to dismiss the petition on jurisdictional grounds. What is the best constitutional argument in support of the EPA's request?","choices":{"A":"Article III precludes federal courts from reviewing the validity of any federal agency rule in any proceeding other than an action to enforce the rule.","B":"Article III provides that all federal cases, except those within the original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court, must be initiated by an action in a federal district court.","C":"The case is appellate in nature, but it is beyond the appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court, because Article III states that its jurisdiction extends only to cases arising under the Constitution.","D":"The case is not within the original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court as defined by Article III, and it is not a proper subject of that court's appellate jurisdiction because it has not yet been decided by any lower court."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article III of the Constitution states that the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has both:\n• original jurisdiction – the authority to directly review (1) cases involving an ambassador, public minister, or consul and (2) cases in which a state is a party and\n\n• appellate jurisdiction – the authority to overturn, modify, or affirm a decision made by a lower federal court or a state's highest court in all other cases falling within federal judicial power.\n\nHere, the companies filed a petition for a writ of certiorari requesting that SCOTUS immediately review their case against the EPA. But the case is not within SCOTUS's original jurisdiction as narrowly defined by Article III. And since the case has not yet been decided by a lower court—ie, there is no decision being appealed from—it is not a proper subject of SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. Accordingly, that is the best constitutional argument in support of the EPA's request. (Choice A) Article III allows federal courts to review the validity of a federal rule in a proceeding other than an action to enforce the rule. For example, federal courts may issue a declaratory judgment that a rule is invalid before it has been enforced if the plaintiff faces imminent harm. (Choice B) Under Article III, Congress has broad power to regulate the original and appellate jurisdiction of lower federal courts. As a result, a federal case can be initiated by an action in a U.S. court of appeals rather than a U.S. district court if authorized by Congress. (Choice C) SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction extends to all cases arising under the Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (as seen here). But this case is not appellate in nature since there is no lower court decision to review.\n\nEducational objective: The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has original jurisdiction in (1) cases involving an ambassador, public minister, or consul and (2) cases in which a state is a party. But in all other cases, SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction requires a lower court decision.\n\nReferences\n• Balt. & Ohio R.R. Co. v. Interstate Com. Comm'n, 215 U.S. 216, 224–25 (1909) (explaining that SCOTUS has original jurisdiction in limited circumstances and that appellate jurisdiction requires a lower court decision).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 453 (2019) (defining original and appellate jurisdiction).","educationalObjective":"The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has original jurisdiction in (1) cases involving an ambassador, public minister, or consul and (2) cases in which a state is a party. But in all other cases, SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction requires a lower court decision.","references":"• Balt. & Ohio R.R. Co. v. Interstate Com. Comm'n, 215 U.S. 216, 224–25 (1909) (explaining that SCOTUS has original jurisdiction in limited circumstances and that appellate jurisdiction requires a lower court decision).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 453 (2019) (defining original and appellate jurisdiction).","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has original jurisdiction in (1) cases involving an ambassador, public minister, or consul and (2) cases in which a state is a party. But in all other cases, SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction requires a lower court decision.","irac":{"issue":"What is the best constitutional argument in support of the EPA's request?","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has original jurisdiction in (1) cases involving an ambassador, public minister, or consul and (2) cases in which a state is a party. But in all other cases, SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction requires a lower court decision.","analysis":"Article III of the Constitution states that the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has both:\n• original jurisdiction – the authority to directly review (1) cases involving an ambassador, public minister, or consul and (2) cases in which a state is a party and\n\n• appellate jurisdiction – the authority to overturn, modify, or affirm a decision made by a lower federal court or a state's highest court in all other cases falling within federal judicial power.\n\nHere, the companies filed a petition for a writ of certiorari requesting that SCOTUS immediately review their case against the EPA. But the case is not within SCOTUS's original jurisdiction as narrowly defined by Article III. And since the case has not yet been decided by a lower court—ie, there is no decision being appealed from—it is not a proper subject of SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. Accordingly, that is the best constitutional argument in support of the EPA's request. (Choice A) Article III allows federal courts to review the validity of a federal rule in a proceeding other than an action to enforce the rule. For example, federal courts may issue a declaratory judgment that a rule is invalid before it has been enforced if the plaintiff faces imminent harm. (Choice B) Under Article III, Congress has broad power to regulate the original and appellate jurisdiction of lower federal courts. As a result, a federal case can be initiated by an action in a U.S. court of appeals rather than a U.S. district court if authorized by Congress. (Choice C) SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction extends to all cases arising under the Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (as seen here). But this case is not appellate in nature since there is no lower court decision to review.","conclusion":"Answer D: The case is not within the original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court as defined by Article III, and it is not a proper subject of that court's appellate jurisdiction because it has not yet been decided by any lower court."},"questionZh":"联邦法规规定，美国最高法院有权审查向美国上诉法院提起的任何案件，即使该案件尚未由上诉法院裁决。环境保护局（EPA）是联邦政府行政部门的一个机构，发布了一项重要的环境规则。尽管该规则尚未针对他们执行，但将受到该规则不利影响的公司向上诉法院提交了对该规则进行审查的请愿书，寻求宣布该规则无效，仅仅是因为它超出了美国环保署的法定权限。这些公司没有提出宪法主张。法规明确规定上诉法院可以直接审查 EPA 规则，而无需在地区法院进行任何初步行动。这些公司已向最高法院提交调卷令状请愿书，要求最高法院在上诉法院实际裁决此案之前立即复审此案。环保局承认此案非常重要，需要最高法院审查，并且应该立即做出裁决，但它要求最高法院以管辖权为由驳回请愿书。支持 EPA 要求的最佳宪法论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"第三条禁止联邦法院在除执行规则的诉讼之外的任何诉讼中审查任何联邦机构规则的有效性。","B":"第三条规定，除最高法院初审管辖范围内的案件外，所有联邦案件都必须由联邦地区法院提起诉讼。","C":"该案件本质上是上诉案件，但它超出了最高法院的上诉管辖权，因为第三条规定其管辖权仅适用于根据宪法产生的案件。","D":"该案不属于第三条规定的最高法院的初审管辖范围，并且由于尚未得到任何下级法院的裁决，因此不属于该法院上诉管辖权的适当主题。"},"explanationZh":"宪法第三条规定，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有：\n• 初审管辖权 – 直接审查 (1) 涉及大使、公共部长或领事的案件和 (2) 国家为当事方的案件的权力\n\n• 上诉管辖权——有权推翻、修改或确认下级联邦法院或州最高法院在联邦司法权力范围内的所有其他案件中作出的裁决。\n\n在此，这些公司提交了一份调卷令状请愿书，要求 SCOTUS 立即审查他们针对 EPA 的案件。但根据第三条狭义的规定，该案并不属于最高法院的原始管辖范围。由于该案尚未由下级法院裁决，即没有可上诉的裁决，因此它不是最高法院上诉管辖权的适当主题。因此，这是支持美国环保局要求的最佳宪法论据。 （选项 A） 第三条允许联邦法院在除执行规则的诉讼之外的诉讼中审查联邦规则的有效性。例如，如果原告面临迫在眉睫的伤害，联邦法院可能会发布宣告性判决，判定某项规则在执行之前无效。 （选择 B）根据第三条，国会拥有广泛的权力来规范下级联邦法院的初审管辖权和上诉管辖权。因此，如果得到国会授权，联邦案件可以通过美国上诉法院而不是美国地区法院提起诉讼。 （选项 C）最高法院的上诉管辖权延伸至根据宪法、条约或联邦法律（如此处所示）引起的所有案件。但本案本质上不属于上诉案，因为没有下级法院决定进行审查。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对 (1) 涉及大使、公务部长或领事的案件和 (2) 以州为一方的案件拥有初审管辖权。但在所有其他案件中，最高法院的上诉管辖权需要下级法院的裁决。\n\n参考文献\n• 巴尔特。 & 俄亥俄州 R.R. Co. 诉州际公路委员会。 Comm'n, 215 U.S. 216, 224–25 (1909)（解释 SCOTUS 在有限情况下拥有初审管辖权，而上诉管辖权需要下级法院的裁决）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 453 (2019)（定义初审管辖权和上诉管辖权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对 (1) 涉及大使、公共部长或领事的案件以及 (2) 以州为一方的案件拥有初审管辖权。但在所有其他案件中，最高法院的上诉管辖权需要下级法院的裁决。","iracZh":{"issue":"支持 EPA 要求的最佳宪法论据是什么？","rule":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对 (1) 涉及大使、公共部长或领事的案件以及 (2) 以州为一方的案件拥有初审管辖权。但在所有其他案件中，最高法院的上诉管辖权需要下级法院的裁决。","analysis":"宪法第三条规定，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有：\n• 初审管辖权 – 直接审查 (1) 涉及大使、公共部长或领事的案件和 (2) 国家为当事方的案件的权力\n\n• 上诉管辖权——有权推翻、修改或确认下级联邦法院或州最高法院在联邦司法权力范围内的所有其他案件中作出的裁决。\n\n在此，这些公司提交了一份调卷令状请愿书，要求 SCOTUS 立即审查他们针对 EPA 的案件。但根据第三条狭义的规定，该案并不属于最高法院的原始管辖范围。由于该案尚未由下级法院裁决，即没有可上诉的裁决，因此它不是最高法院上诉管辖权的适当主题。因此，这是支持美国环保局要求的最佳宪法论据。 （选项 A） 第三条允许联邦法院在除执行规则的诉讼之外的诉讼中审查联邦规则的有效性。例如，如果原告面临迫在眉睫的伤害，联邦法院可能会发布宣告性判决，判定某项规则在执行之前无效。 （选择 B）根据第三条，国会拥有广泛的权力来规范下级联邦法院的初审管辖权和上诉管辖权。因此，如果得到国会授权，联邦案件可以通过美国上诉法院而不是美国地区法院提起诉讼。 （选项 C）最高法院的上诉管辖权延伸至根据宪法、条约或联邦法律（如此处所示）引起的所有案件。但本案本质上不属于上诉案，因为没有下级法院决定进行审查。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对 (1) 涉及大使、公务部长或领事的案件和 (2) 以州为一方的案件拥有初审管辖权。但在所有其他案件中，最高法院的上诉管辖权需要下级法院的裁决。\n\n参考文献\n• 巴尔特。 & 俄亥俄州 R.R. Co. 诉州际公路委员会。 Comm'n, 215 U.S. 216, 224–25 (1909)（解释 SCOTUS 在有限情况下拥有初审管辖权，而上诉管辖权需要下级法院的裁决）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 453 (2019)（定义初审管辖权和上诉管辖权）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该案不属于第三条规定的最高法院的初审管辖范围，并且由于尚未得到任何下级法院的裁决，因此不属于该法院上诉管辖权的适当主题。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"declaration","ipa":"/dˌɛklɝˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"de·c·la·ra·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 宣告, 说明, 宣布；[计] 说明","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 宣告, 说明, 宣布；[计] 说明","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:declarations","sourceSentence":"Although the rule had not yet been enforced against them, companies that would be adversely affected by the rule filed a petition for review of the rule in a court of appeals, seeking a declaration that the rule was invalid solely because it was beyond the statutory authority of the EPA.","synonyms":["resolution","resolve","announcement","proclamation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-63e7be27eba330e7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":59,"sourceQuestionPage":176,"sourceQuestionPages":[176],"sourceExplanationPages":[177,178],"sourceAnswerPages":[177,178],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-060","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federal Judicial Power","question":"Assume that Congress passed and the President signed the following statute: \"The appellate jurisdiction of the United States Supreme Court shall not extend to any case involving the constitutionality of any state statute limiting the circumstances in which a person may obtain contraception, or involving the constitutionality of this statute.\" What is the strongest argument against the constitutionality of this statute?","choices":{"A":"Congress may not exercise its authority over the appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court in a way that seriously interferes with the establishment of a supreme and uniform body of federal constitutional law.","B":"Congress may only regulate the appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court over cases initially arising in federal courts.","C":"The appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court may only be altered by constitutional amendment.","D":"The statute violates the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Most cases come before the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) through its appellate jurisdiction. This allows SCOTUS to review certain cases that have been decided by a lower federal court or a state's highest court. Article III grants Congress the power to regulate and make exceptions to SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. But when exercising this almost unlimited power, Congress cannot violate:\n• the separation-of-powers doctrine—eg, usurping judicial power to decide cases— or\n\n• other constitutional provisions—eg, giving only men the right to appeal, in violation of the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment.\n\nHere, the federal statute denies SCOTUS appellate jurisdiction over any case involving the constitutionality of a state statute limiting access to contraception. As a result, the federal statute may violate the separation-of-powers doctrine since it could usurp SCOTUS's power to establish a supreme and uniform body of federal constitutional law on this issue. Therefore, this is the strongest argument against the constitutionality of the federal statute. (Choices B & C) Since Article III of the Constitution gives Congress the power to limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction over cases initially arising in federal or state court, no constitutional amendment is required. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause applies when states treat similarly situated persons differently. But since the challenged statute was enacted by the federal government, this clause does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: Article III grants Congress the power to regulate and limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. But when exercising this power, Congress cannot violate the separation-of- powers doctrine or other constitutional provisions.\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Klein, 80 U.S. 128, 147–48 (1871) (establishing that Congress cannot alter the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction if it would violate the separation-of-powers doctrine).","educationalObjective":"Article III grants Congress the power to regulate and limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. But when exercising this power, Congress cannot violate the separation-of- powers doctrine or other constitutional provisions.","references":"• United States v. Klein, 80 U.S. 128, 147–48 (1871) (establishing that Congress cannot alter the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction if it would violate the separation-of-powers doctrine).","rule":"Article III grants Congress the power to regulate and limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. But when exercising this power, Congress cannot violate the separation-of- powers doctrine or other constitutional provisions.","irac":{"issue":"Assume that Congress passed and the President signed the following statute: \"The appellate jurisdiction of the United States Supreme Court shall not extend to any case involving the constitutionality of any state statute limiting the circumstances in which a person may obtain contraception, or involving the constitutionality of this statute.\" What is the strongest argument against the constitutionality of this statute?","rule":"Article III grants Congress the power to regulate and limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. But when exercising this power, Congress cannot violate the separation-of- powers doctrine or other constitutional provisions.","analysis":"Most cases come before the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) through its appellate jurisdiction. This allows SCOTUS to review certain cases that have been decided by a lower federal court or a state's highest court. Article III grants Congress the power to regulate and make exceptions to SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. But when exercising this almost unlimited power, Congress cannot violate:\n• the separation-of-powers doctrine—eg, usurping judicial power to decide cases— or\n\n• other constitutional provisions—eg, giving only men the right to appeal, in violation of the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment.\n\nHere, the federal statute denies SCOTUS appellate jurisdiction over any case involving the constitutionality of a state statute limiting access to contraception. As a result, the federal statute may violate the separation-of-powers doctrine since it could usurp SCOTUS's power to establish a supreme and uniform body of federal constitutional law on this issue. Therefore, this is the strongest argument against the constitutionality of the federal statute. (Choices B & C) Since Article III of the Constitution gives Congress the power to limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction over cases initially arising in federal or state court, no constitutional amendment is required. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause applies when states treat similarly situated persons differently. But since the challenged statute was enacted by the federal government, this clause does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer A: Congress may not exercise its authority over the appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court in a way that seriously interferes with the establishment of a supreme and uniform body of federal constitutional law."},"questionZh":"假设国会通过且总统签署以下法规：“美国最高法院的上诉管辖权不得延伸至涉及限制个人获得避孕的情况的任何州法规的合宪性或涉及本法规的合宪性的任何案件。”反对该法规合宪性的最有力论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"国会不得以严重干扰建立最高统一联邦宪法机构的方式行使对最高法院上诉管辖权的权力。","B":"国会只能规定最高法院对最初在联邦法院审理的案件的上诉管辖权。","C":"最高法院的上诉管辖权只能通过宪法修正案来改变。","D":"该法规违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。"},"explanationZh":"大多数案件通过其上诉管辖权提交给美国最高法院 (SCOTUS)。这使得最高法院能够审查由下级联邦法院或州最高法院裁决的某些案件。第三条赋予国会对最高法院上诉管辖权进行监管和例外的权力。但在行使这种几乎无限的权力时，国会不能违反：\n• 权力分立原则——例如，篡夺司法裁决权——或\n\n• 其他宪法条款——例如，只赋予男性上诉权，这违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分。\n\n在这里，联邦法规否认最高法院对任何涉及限制避孕的州法规是否合宪的案件具有上诉管辖权。因此，联邦法规可能违反权力分立原则，因为它可能篡夺最高法院在这个问题上建立最高和统一的联邦宪法机构的权力。因此，这是反对联邦法规合宪性的最有力论据。 （选项 B 和 C）由于宪法第三条赋予国会限制最高法院对联邦或州法院最初审理的案件的上诉管辖权的权力，因此不需要修改宪法。 （选项 D）当各州以不同方式对待处境相似的人时，适用第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但由于受到质疑的法规是由联邦政府颁布的，因此该条款不适用。\n\n教育目标：第三条赋予国会规范和限制最高法院上诉管辖权的权力。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反分权原则或其他宪法规定。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Klein, 80 U.S. 128, 147–48 (1871)（规定国会不能改变最高法院的上诉管辖权，如果这会违反权力分立原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第三条赋予国会规范和限制最高法院上诉管辖权的权力。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反分权原则或其他宪法规定。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设国会通过且总统签署以下法规：“美国最高法院的上诉管辖权不得延伸至涉及限制个人获得避孕的情况的任何州法规的合宪性或涉及本法规的合宪性的任何案件。”反对该法规合宪性的最有力论据是什么？","rule":"第三条赋予国会规范和限制最高法院上诉管辖权的权力。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反分权原则或其他宪法规定。","analysis":"大多数案件通过其上诉管辖权提交给美国最高法院 (SCOTUS)。这使得最高法院能够审查由下级联邦法院或州最高法院裁决的某些案件。第三条赋予国会对最高法院上诉管辖权进行监管和例外的权力。但在行使这种几乎无限的权力时，国会不能违反：\n• 权力分立原则——例如，篡夺司法裁决权——或\n\n• 其他宪法条款——例如，只赋予男性上诉权，这违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分。\n\n在这里，联邦法规否认最高法院对任何涉及限制避孕的州法规是否合宪的案件具有上诉管辖权。因此，联邦法规可能违反权力分立原则，因为它可能篡夺最高法院在这个问题上建立最高和统一的联邦宪法机构的权力。因此，这是反对联邦法规合宪性的最有力论据。 （选项 B 和 C）由于宪法第三条赋予国会限制最高法院对联邦或州法院最初审理的案件的上诉管辖权的权力，因此不需要修改宪法。 （选项 D）当各州以不同方式对待处境相似的人时，适用第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但由于受到质疑的法规是由联邦政府颁布的，因此该条款不适用。\n\n教育目标：第三条赋予国会规范和限制最高法院上诉管辖权的权力。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反分权原则或其他宪法规定。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Klein, 80 U.S. 128, 147–48 (1871)（规定国会不能改变最高法院的上诉管辖权，如果这会违反权力分立原则）。","conclusion":"答案 A：国会不得以严重干扰建立最高统一联邦宪法机构的方式行使对最高法院上诉管辖权的权力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-63e7be27eba330e7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":60,"sourceQuestionPage":179,"sourceQuestionPages":[179],"sourceExplanationPages":[180,181],"sourceAnswerPages":[180,181],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-061","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"Congress enacted a statute over the President's veto establishing a program to protect areas in the United States that are rich in biological diversity. The program is consistent with the terms of an environmental treaty that the President objected to and did not sign. The statute creates an executive agency and authorizes it to designate parts of federal lands for inclusion in the program in accordance with criteria taken from the treaty. In an inseverable provision, the statute further provides that the agency must report each designation to a committee of Congress and that the committee may overturn the agency's designation by a majority vote. Why is the statute unconstitutional?","choices":{"A":"It authorizes a committee of Congress to overturn an executive decision.","B":"It constitutes an invalid delegation of legislative authority to an executive agency.","C":"It interferes with the exercise of the President's paramount authority in foreign affairs.","D":"It requires an executive agency to report its decisions to Congress."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The nondelegation doctrine prohibits Congress from delegating purely legislative powers (eg, making or repealing laws). But it allows Congress to delegate its incidental powers (eg, regulating federal lands) to executive agencies if it provides an intelligible principle— ie, a clear statement defining:\n• the policy Congress seeks to advance (eg, protect areas in the United States that are rich in biodiversity)\n\n• the agency to carry out that policy (eg, the agency created by statute here) and\n\n• the scope of that agency's authority (eg, designate parts of federal lands for inclusion in the environmental protection program) (Choice B).\n\nBut once Congress has delegated authority to an executive agency, Congress cannot authorize a committee to veto (ie, overturn) that agency's executive decisions. Such decisions can only be overturned through new legislation, which requires:\n• bicameralism—which entails a majority vote in both the House of Representatives and the Senate (not merely a congressional committee)—and\n\n• presentment to the President for approval or disapproval (which did not occur here).\n\nTherefore, the statute is unconstitutional because it authorizes a committee of Congress to overturn an executive decision. (Choice C) Article II gives the President paramount authority in foreign affairs, so Congress could not have forced the President to sign the environmental treaty. But since Congress has the power to enact domestic laws, it can implement this program protecting areas in the United States despite the President's objection to the treaty. (Choice D) Congress can require executive agencies to report their decisions to Congress if this is necessary and proper for (ie, reasonably related to) implementing federal law. But Congress cannot veto executive agency decisions without adhering to bicameralism and presentment.\n\nEducational objective: Congress can only overturn an executive agency's actions by enacting new legislation. This requires both (1) passage by majority vote in the House of Representatives and the Senate (ie, bicameralism) and (2) presentation to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).\n\nReferences\n• Gundy v. United States, 139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019) (explaining that the nondelegation doctrine allows Congress to delegate power as long as it provides an intelligible principle to guide the delegate's use of discretion).\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 946–59 (1982) (striking down the congressional-veto provision of an immigration statute since it bypasses bicameralism and presentment).","educationalObjective":"Congress can only overturn an executive agency's actions by enacting new legislation. This requires both (1) passage by majority vote in the House of Representatives and the Senate (ie, bicameralism) and (2) presentation to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).","references":"• Gundy v. United States, 139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019) (explaining that the nondelegation doctrine allows Congress to delegate power as long as it provides an intelligible principle to guide the delegate's use of discretion).\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 946–59 (1982) (striking down the congressional-veto provision of an immigration statute since it bypasses bicameralism and presentment).","rule":"Congress can only overturn an executive agency's actions by enacting new legislation. This requires both (1) passage by majority vote in the House of Representatives and the Senate (ie, bicameralism) and (2) presentation to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).","irac":{"issue":"Why is the statute unconstitutional?","rule":"Congress can only overturn an executive agency's actions by enacting new legislation. This requires both (1) passage by majority vote in the House of Representatives and the Senate (ie, bicameralism) and (2) presentation to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).","analysis":"The nondelegation doctrine prohibits Congress from delegating purely legislative powers (eg, making or repealing laws). But it allows Congress to delegate its incidental powers (eg, regulating federal lands) to executive agencies if it provides an intelligible principle— ie, a clear statement defining:\n• the policy Congress seeks to advance (eg, protect areas in the United States that are rich in biodiversity)\n\n• the agency to carry out that policy (eg, the agency created by statute here) and\n\n• the scope of that agency's authority (eg, designate parts of federal lands for inclusion in the environmental protection program) (Choice B).\n\nBut once Congress has delegated authority to an executive agency, Congress cannot authorize a committee to veto (ie, overturn) that agency's executive decisions. Such decisions can only be overturned through new legislation, which requires:\n• bicameralism—which entails a majority vote in both the House of Representatives and the Senate (not merely a congressional committee)—and\n\n• presentment to the President for approval or disapproval (which did not occur here).\n\nTherefore, the statute is unconstitutional because it authorizes a committee of Congress to overturn an executive decision. (Choice C) Article II gives the President paramount authority in foreign affairs, so Congress could not have forced the President to sign the environmental treaty. But since Congress has the power to enact domestic laws, it can implement this program protecting areas in the United States despite the President's objection to the treaty. (Choice D) Congress can require executive agencies to report their decisions to Congress if this is necessary and proper for (ie, reasonably related to) implementing federal law. But Congress cannot veto executive agency decisions without adhering to bicameralism and presentment.","conclusion":"Answer A: It authorizes a committee of Congress to overturn an executive decision."},"questionZh":"国会不顾总统的否决，颁布了一项法规，制定一项保护美国生物多样性丰富地区的计划。该计划符合总统反对且未签署的环境条约的条款。该法规设立了一个执行机构，并授权其根据条约中的标准指定部分联邦土地纳入该计划。在一项不可分割的条款中，该法规进一步规定，该机构必须向国会委员会报告每项指定，并且该委员会可以以多数票推翻该机构的指定。为什么该法规违宪？","choicesZh":{"A":"它授权国会委员会推翻行政决定。","B":"它构成对行政机构无效的立法权授权。","C":"它干涉总统在外交事务中行使最高权力。","D":"它要求执行机构向国会报告其决定。"},"explanationZh":"不授权原则禁止国会授予纯粹的立法权（例如制定或废除法律）。但它允许国会将其附带权力（例如，管理联邦土地）委托给行政机构，前提是它提供了一个易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义：\n• 国会寻求推进的政策（例如，保护美国生物多样性丰富的地区）\n\n• 执行该政策的机构（例如，此处法规创建的机构）以及\n\n• 该机构的权力范围（例如，指定部分联邦土地纳入环境保护计划）（选择B）。\n\n但一旦国会将权力授予某个执行机构，国会就不能授权委员会否决（即推翻）该机构的执行决定。此类决定只能通过新立法推翻，新立法要求：\n• 两院制——需要众议院和参议院（不仅仅是国会委员会）的多数票——以及\n\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（这里没有发生）。\n\n因此，该法规违宪，因为它授权国会委员会推翻行政决定。 （选项C）第二条赋予总统在外交事务上的最高权力，因此国会不能强迫总统签署环境条约。但由于国会有权制定国内法，因此尽管总统反对该条约，它仍可以实施这一保护美国境内地区的计划。 （选择 D）如果对于实施联邦法律来说是必要且适当的（即合理相关），国会可以要求行政机构向国会报告其决定。但如果不遵守两院制和代表制，国会就不能否决行政机构的决定。\n\n教育目标：国会只能通过颁布新的立法来推翻行政机构的行为。这需要 (1) 在众议院和参议院以多数票通过（即两院制），以及 (2) 提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。\n\n参考文献\n• Gundy 诉美国，139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019)（解释说，非授权原则允许国会授权，只要它提供了一个易于理解的原则来指导代表使用自由裁量权）。\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 946–59 (1982)（废除移民法规中国会否决权条款，因为它绕过了两院制和陈述）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会只能通过颁布新的立法来推翻行政机构的行为。这需要 (1) 在众议院和参议院以多数票通过（即两院制），以及 (2) 提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。","iracZh":{"issue":"为什么该法规违宪？","rule":"国会只能通过颁布新的立法来推翻行政机构的行为。这需要 (1) 在众议院和参议院以多数票通过（即两院制），以及 (2) 提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。","analysis":"不授权原则禁止国会授予纯粹的立法权（例如制定或废除法律）。但它允许国会将其附带权力（例如，管理联邦土地）委托给行政机构，前提是它提供了一个易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义：\n• 国会寻求推进的政策（例如，保护美国生物多样性丰富的地区）\n\n• 执行该政策的机构（例如，此处法规创建的机构）以及\n\n• 该机构的权力范围（例如，指定部分联邦土地纳入环境保护计划）（选择B）。\n\n但一旦国会将权力授予某个执行机构，国会就不能授权委员会否决（即推翻）该机构的执行决定。此类决定只能通过新立法推翻，新立法要求：\n• 两院制——需要众议院和参议院（不仅仅是国会委员会）的多数票——以及\n\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（这里没有发生）。\n\n因此，该法规违宪，因为它授权国会委员会推翻行政决定。 （选项C）第二条赋予总统在外交事务上的最高权力，因此国会不能强迫总统签署环境条约。但由于国会有权制定国内法，因此尽管总统反对该条约，它仍可以实施这一保护美国境内地区的计划。 （选择 D）如果对于实施联邦法律来说是必要且适当的（即合理相关），国会可以要求行政机构向国会报告其决定。但如果不遵守两院制和代表制，国会就不能否决行政机构的决定。\n\n教育目标：国会只能通过颁布新的立法来推翻行政机构的行为。这需要 (1) 在众议院和参议院以多数票通过（即两院制），以及 (2) 提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。\n\n参考文献\n• Gundy 诉美国，139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019)（解释说，非授权原则允许国会授权，只要它提供了一个易于理解的原则来指导代表使用自由裁量权）。\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 946–59 (1982)（废除移民法规中国会否决权条款，因为它绕过了两院制和陈述）。","conclusion":"答案 A：它授权国会委员会推翻行政决定。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"presentment","ipa":"/pɹizˈɛntmənt/","syllables":"pre·sent·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 陈述, 描写, 叙述, 呈现, 展示物, 描画, 画像, 赠送, 赠送物, 演出, 提出, 呈递；[法] 陪审员的直接控诉书, 公诉, 控诉状","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 陈述, 描写, 叙述, 呈现, 展示物, 描画, 画像, 赠送, 赠送物, 演出, 提出, 呈递；[法] 陪审员的直接控诉书, 公诉, 控诉状","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:presentments","sourceSentence":"This requires both (1) passage by majority vote in the House of Representatives and the Senate (ie, bicameralism) and (2) presentation to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).","synonyms":["presentation","demonstration","notification"]},{"term":"delegation","ipa":"/dˌɛlʌɡˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"del·e·ga·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 代表团, 派遣代表团, 代表的地位(或权力)；[经] 派遣, 委任, 委托","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 代表团, 派遣代表团, 代表的地位(或权力)；[经] 派遣, 委任, 委托","wordForm":"词；词形 s:delegations","sourceSentence":"It constitutes an invalid delegation of legislative authority to an executive agency.","synonyms":["delegating","relegating","relegation","deputation"]},{"term":"delegating","ipa":"/dˈɛlʌɡˌeɪtɪŋ/","syllables":"del·e·gat·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 委派代表（delegate的现在分词形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 委派代表（delegate的现在分词形式）","wordForm":"词；词形 0:delegate/1:i/i:delegating","sourceSentence":"The nondelegation doctrine prohibits Congress from delegating purely legislative powers (eg, making or repealing laws).","synonyms":["delegation","relegating","relegation","deputation"]},{"term":"objection","ipa":"/ʌbdʒˈɛkʃən/","syllables":"ob·jec·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 异议, 反对, 不喜欢, 缺点, 缺陷, 妨碍, 拒绝之理由；[法] 异议, 反对, 抗议","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 异议, 反对, 不喜欢, 缺点, 缺陷, 妨碍, 拒绝之理由；[法] 异议, 反对, 抗议","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:objections","sourceSentence":"But since Congress has the power to enact domestic laws, it can implement this program protecting areas in the United States despite the President's objection to the treaty.","synonyms":["protest","dissent","expostulation","remonstrance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-dca63f85819110f7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":61,"sourceQuestionPage":182,"sourceQuestionPages":[182],"sourceExplanationPages":[183,184],"sourceAnswerPages":[183,184],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-062","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"State A suffers from a high unemployment rate. To combat this problem, the State A legislature passed a statute requiring every business that sells over $5 million worth of goods each year in State A to annually buy goods produced in State A in an amount equal to half of its State A sales. Several persons and entities seek to challenge the constitutionality of this state statute in federal court. Which of the following persons or entities most clearly has standing to do so?","choices":{"A":"A company that sells $3 million worth of goods in State A annually but currently purchases only $1 million in State A goods.","B":"A distributor that currently does not sell or purchase goods in State A but expects to sell $15 million worth of goods in State A next fiscal year.","C":"A State B manufacturer that supplies 75 percent of the goods bought by a corporation with annual sales in State A of $10 million.","D":"The governor of State B on behalf of her state's businesses."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A federal court will not hear a case unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. This requires the plaintiff to allege three elements:\n• Injury-in-fact – actual or imminent harm that is concrete and particularized\n• Causation – the injury is fairly traceable to the defendant's challenged conduct\n• Redressability – a favorable judicial decision will likely remedy or prevent the injury Here, the State A statute requires businesses selling over $5 million worth of goods each year in State A to buy State A produced goods in an amount equal to half of their State A sales. As a result, a corporation annually selling $10 million worth of goods in State A but purchasing 75% of its goods from a State B manufacturer must now buy more State A goods. This will cause the State B manufacturer imminent financial harm that can be prevented if the statute is found unconstitutional. Therefore, the State B manufacturer likely has standing to contest the statute. (Choice A) The statute does not apply to the company that annually sells $3 million (not over $5 million) worth of goods in State A. Therefore, this company does not have standing to contest the statute. (Choice B) The distributor that currently does not sell or purchase goods in State A but expects to sell $15 million worth of goods in State A next year will need to purchase $7.5 million worth of State A goods if its prediction is accurate. But it lacks standing to contest the statute because this harm is speculative—not concrete. (Choice D) The third-party standing doctrine allows an injured party to assert a third party's rights in limited circumstances. But the State B governor does not have standing to sue on behalf of her state's businesses because there is no evidence that the statute injured the governor.\n\nEducational objective: A plaintiff has standing to sue in federal court if he/she allegedly (1) suffered an injury-in- fact that (2) was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and (3) is redressable by a favorable judicial decision.\n\nReferences\n• Lujan v. Defs. of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992) (holding that standing requires proof of an injury-in-fact, causation, and redressability).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 577 (2019) (explaining the injury-in-fact element of standing).","educationalObjective":"A plaintiff has standing to sue in federal court if he/she allegedly (1) suffered an injury-in- fact that (2) was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and (3) is redressable by a favorable judicial decision.","references":"• Lujan v. Defs. of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992) (holding that standing requires proof of an injury-in-fact, causation, and redressability).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 577 (2019) (explaining the injury-in-fact element of standing).","rule":"A plaintiff has standing to sue in federal court if he/she allegedly (1) suffered an injury-in- fact that (2) was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and (3) is redressable by a favorable judicial decision.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following persons or entities most clearly has standing to do so?","rule":"A plaintiff has standing to sue in federal court if he/she allegedly (1) suffered an injury-in- fact that (2) was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and (3) is redressable by a favorable judicial decision.","analysis":"A federal court will not hear a case unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. This requires the plaintiff to allege three elements:\n• Injury-in-fact – actual or imminent harm that is concrete and particularized\n• Causation – the injury is fairly traceable to the defendant's challenged conduct\n• Redressability – a favorable judicial decision will likely remedy or prevent the injury Here, the State A statute requires businesses selling over $5 million worth of goods each year in State A to buy State A produced goods in an amount equal to half of their State A sales. As a result, a corporation annually selling $10 million worth of goods in State A but purchasing 75% of its goods from a State B manufacturer must now buy more State A goods. This will cause the State B manufacturer imminent financial harm that can be prevented if the statute is found unconstitutional. Therefore, the State B manufacturer likely has standing to contest the statute. (Choice A) The statute does not apply to the company that annually sells $3 million (not over $5 million) worth of goods in State A. Therefore, this company does not have standing to contest the statute. (Choice B) The distributor that currently does not sell or purchase goods in State A but expects to sell $15 million worth of goods in State A next year will need to purchase $7.5 million worth of State A goods if its prediction is accurate. But it lacks standing to contest the statute because this harm is speculative—not concrete. (Choice D) The third-party standing doctrine allows an injured party to assert a third party's rights in limited circumstances. But the State B governor does not have standing to sue on behalf of her state's businesses because there is no evidence that the statute injured the governor.","conclusion":"Answer C: A State B manufacturer that supplies 75 percent of the goods bought by a corporation with annual sales in State A of $10 million."},"questionZh":"A 州失业率很高。为了解决这个问题，A 州立法机关通过了一项法规，要求每年在 A 州销售价值超过 500 万美元商品的每个企业每年购买 A 州生产的商品，数量相当于其 A 州销售额的一半。一些个人和实体试图在联邦法院质疑该州法规的合宪性。以下哪一个个人或实体最明显有资格这样做？","choicesZh":{"A":"一家公司每年在 A 州销售价值 300 万美元的商品，但目前仅采购 A 州商品 100 万美元。","B":"目前不在 A 州销售或购买商品的经销商，但预计下一财年在 A 州销售价值 1,500 万美元的商品。","C":"一家 B 州制造商，供应 A 州年销售额为 1,000 万美元的公司购买的 75% 的商品。","D":"B 州州长代表该州的企业。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院不会审理案件，除非原告有资格——即案件结果与个人利益相关。这要求原告提出三个要素：\n• 事实伤害——实际或迫在眉睫的具体且具体的伤害\n• 因果关系——伤害完全可以追溯到被告受到质疑的行为\n• 可赔偿性——有利的司法判决可能会补救或防止伤害 在此，A 州法规要求每年在 A 州销售价值超过 500 万美元的商品的企业购买 A 州生产的商品，其数量相当于其 A 州销售额的一半。因此，一家公司每年在 A 州销售价值 1000 万美元的商品，但其 75% 的商品是从 B 州制造商那里购买的，现在必须购买更多的 A 州商品。这将给 B 州制造商带来迫在眉睫的经济损失，如果该法规被发现违宪，这种损失是可以避免的。因此，B 州制造商可能有资格对该法规提出异议。 （选项A） 该法规不适用于每年在A 州销售价值300 万美元（不超过500 万美元）商品的公司。因此，该公司没有资格对该法规提出异议。 （选择 B） 目前不在 A 州销售或购买商品，但预计明年在 A 州销售价值 1500 万美元商品的分销商，如果其预测准确，则需要购买价值 750 万美元的 A 州商品。但它缺乏质疑该法规的资格，因为这种损害是推测性的，而不是具体的。 （选项D） 第三方地位原则允许受害方在有限的情况下主张第三方的权利。但 B 州州长没有资格代表本州企业提起诉讼，因为没有证据表明该法规损害了州长的利益。\n\n教育目标：如果原告据称 (1) 遭受了事实上的伤害，(2) 是由被告所质疑的行为造成的，并且 (3) 可通过有利的司法判决予以纠正，则原告有权在联邦法院提起诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• Lujan 诉Defs。 of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992)（认为资格要求提供事实伤害、因果关系和可补救性的证据）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 577 (2019)（解释了诉讼资格的事实伤害要素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果原告据称 (1) 遭受了事实上的伤害 (2) 是由被告所质疑的行为造成的，并且 (3) 可通过有利的司法判决予以补救，则原告有资格向联邦法院提起诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一个个人或实体最明显有资格这样做？","rule":"如果原告据称 (1) 遭受了事实上的伤害 (2) 是由被告所质疑的行为造成的，并且 (3) 可通过有利的司法判决予以补救，则原告有资格向联邦法院提起诉讼。","analysis":"联邦法院不会审理案件，除非原告有资格——即案件结果与个人利益相关。这要求原告提出三个要素：\n• 事实伤害——实际或迫在眉睫的具体且具体的伤害\n• 因果关系——伤害完全可以追溯到被告受到质疑的行为\n• 可赔偿性——有利的司法判决可能会补救或防止伤害 在此，A 州法规要求每年在 A 州销售价值超过 500 万美元的商品的企业购买 A 州生产的商品，其数量相当于其 A 州销售额的一半。因此，一家公司每年在 A 州销售价值 1000 万美元的商品，但其 75% 的商品是从 B 州制造商那里购买的，现在必须购买更多的 A 州商品。这将给 B 州制造商带来迫在眉睫的经济损失，如果该法规被发现违宪，这种损失是可以避免的。因此，B 州制造商可能有资格对该法规提出异议。 （选项A） 该法规不适用于每年在A 州销售价值300 万美元（不超过500 万美元）商品的公司。因此，该公司没有资格对该法规提出异议。 （选择 B） 目前不在 A 州销售或购买商品，但预计明年在 A 州销售价值 1500 万美元商品的分销商，如果其预测准确，则需要购买价值 750 万美元的 A 州商品。但它缺乏质疑该法规的资格，因为这种损害是推测性的，而不是具体的。 （选项D） 第三方地位原则允许受害方在有限的情况下主张第三方的权利。但 B 州州长没有资格代表本州企业提起诉讼，因为没有证据表明该法规损害了州长的利益。\n\n教育目标：如果原告据称 (1) 遭受了事实上的伤害，(2) 是由被告所质疑的行为造成的，并且 (3) 可通过有利的司法判决予以纠正，则原告有权在联邦法院提起诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• Lujan 诉Defs。 of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992)（认为资格要求提供事实伤害、因果关系和可补救性的证据）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 577 (2019)（解释了诉讼资格的事实伤害要素）。","conclusion":"答案 C：一家 B 州制造商，供应 A 州年销售额为 1,000 万美元的公司购买的 75% 的商品。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-e4d2f96689bf3a25"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":62,"sourceQuestionPage":185,"sourceQuestionPages":[185],"sourceExplanationPages":[186,187],"sourceAnswerPages":[186,187],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-063","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A federal statute imposes an excise tax of $100 on each new computer sold in the United States. It also appropriates the entire proceeds of that tax to a special fund, which is required to be used to purchase licenses for computer software that will be made available for use, free of charge, to any resident of the United States. Is this statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal government may not impose any direct taxes on citizens of the United States.","B":"No, because the statute takes, without just compensation, the property of persons who hold patents or copyrights on computer software.","C":"Yes, because it is a reasonable exercise of Congress's powers to tax and spend for the general welfare.","D":"Yes, because the patent power authorizes Congress to impose reasonable charges on the sale of technology and to spend the proceeds of those charges to advance the use of technology in the United States."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Congressional taxing power\n\nType of Definition Constitutional requirements tax\n\nDirect\n• Taxes on persons or their Allowed if: property\n• apportioned\n• Payment cannot be avoided proportionately among states\n• reasonably related to revenue production Indirect\n• Taxes on goods/transactions Allowed if:\n• Payment can be avoided or shifted\n• identical taxation in every to another (eg, consumer) state\n• reasonably related to revenue production Export\n• Taxes on goods/services leaving Not allowed the country The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare (ie, any public purpose). Congress can use its taxing power:\n• directly (ie, on persons or real property) – so long as the tax is apportioned proportionately among the states and reasonably related to revenue production (Choice A) or\n\n• indirectly (ie, on goods or transactions) – so long as the tax is identical in every state and reasonably related to revenue production.\n\nHere, a federal statute imposes an indirect excise tax on new computers sold in the U.S. This tax is identical in every state since it applies to every sale of a new computer. Excise taxes are a common and reasonable way to raise revenue, and the proceeds of this tax are used for the general welfare—to provide free computer-software licenses to U.S. residents. Therefore, the statute is constitutional. (Choice B) The takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. But here, the federal government is purchasing licenses to use the computer software—not taking property from the holders of patents or copyrights on that software.\n\n(Choice D) Under the copyright and patent clause, Congress can enact laws encouraging the production of creative works (copyright laws) and inventions (patent laws). But this clause does not authorize Congress to impose reasonable charges on, or spend proceeds from, the sale of these works or inventions.\n\nEducational objective: Congress has broad power to impose (1) direct taxes that are apportioned proportionately among the states and reasonably related to revenue production or (2) indirect taxes that are identical in every state and reasonably related to revenue production.\n\nReferences\n• Thomas v. United States, 192 U.S. 363, 371 (1904) (holding that a tax on the sale of stock is an indirect tax).\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 360–63 (1945) (upholding an indirect tax that is identical in every state).","educationalObjective":"Congress has broad power to impose (1) direct taxes that are apportioned proportionately among the states and reasonably related to revenue production or (2) indirect taxes that are identical in every state and reasonably related to revenue production.","references":"• Thomas v. United States, 192 U.S. 363, 371 (1904) (holding that a tax on the sale of stock is an indirect tax).\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 360–63 (1945) (upholding an indirect tax that is identical in every state).","rule":"Congress has broad power to impose (1) direct taxes that are apportioned proportionately among the states and reasonably related to revenue production or (2) indirect taxes that are identical in every state and reasonably related to revenue production.","irac":{"issue":"Is this statute constitutional?","rule":"Congress has broad power to impose (1) direct taxes that are apportioned proportionately among the states and reasonably related to revenue production or (2) indirect taxes that are identical in every state and reasonably related to revenue production.","analysis":"Congressional taxing power\n\nType of Definition Constitutional requirements tax\n\nDirect\n• Taxes on persons or their Allowed if: property\n• apportioned\n• Payment cannot be avoided proportionately among states\n• reasonably related to revenue production Indirect\n• Taxes on goods/transactions Allowed if:\n• Payment can be avoided or shifted\n• identical taxation in every to another (eg, consumer) state\n• reasonably related to revenue production Export\n• Taxes on goods/services leaving Not allowed the country The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare (ie, any public purpose). Congress can use its taxing power:\n• directly (ie, on persons or real property) – so long as the tax is apportioned proportionately among the states and reasonably related to revenue production (Choice A) or\n\n• indirectly (ie, on goods or transactions) – so long as the tax is identical in every state and reasonably related to revenue production.\n\nHere, a federal statute imposes an indirect excise tax on new computers sold in the U.S. This tax is identical in every state since it applies to every sale of a new computer. Excise taxes are a common and reasonable way to raise revenue, and the proceeds of this tax are used for the general welfare—to provide free computer-software licenses to U.S. residents. Therefore, the statute is constitutional. (Choice B) The takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. But here, the federal government is purchasing licenses to use the computer software—not taking property from the holders of patents or copyrights on that software.\n\n(Choice D) Under the copyright and patent clause, Congress can enact laws encouraging the production of creative works (copyright laws) and inventions (patent laws). But this clause does not authorize Congress to impose reasonable charges on, or spend proceeds from, the sale of these works or inventions.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it is a reasonable exercise of Congress's powers to tax and spend for the general welfare."},"questionZh":"联邦法规对在美国销售的每台新计算机征收 100 美元的消费税。它还将该税收的全部收入拨入一项特别基金，该基金需要用于购买计算机软件的许可证，该软件将免费提供给任何美国居民使用。该法规是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为联邦政府不得对美国公民征收任何直接税。","B":"不可以，因为该法规在没有公正补偿的情况下剥夺了拥有计算机软件专利或版权的人的财产。","C":"是的，因为这是国会为了公共福利而征税和支出的权力的合理行使。","D":"是的，因为专利权授权国会对技术销售征收合理的费用，并将这些费用的收益用于促进技术在美国的使用。"},"explanationZh":"国会征税权\n\n定义类型 宪法要求税\n\n直接\n• 对个人或其财产征税 允许的情况：\n• 分配的\n• 各州之间无法按比例避免付款\n• 与收入产生合理相关 间接\n• 在以下情况下允许对商品/交易征税：\n• 付款可以避免或转移\n• 每个州（例如消费者）的税收相同\n• 与收入生产合理相关 出口\n• 对离开该国的商品/服务征税 不允许 征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力，可以为一般福利（即任何公共目的）征税和支出。国会可以使用其征税权：\n• 直接（即针对个人或不动产）——只要税收按比例在各州之间分配并且与收入生产合理相关（选择A）或\n\n• 间接（即针对货物或交易）——只要每个州的税收都是相同的，并且与收入生产合理相关。\n\n在这里，联邦法规对在美国销售的新计算机征收间接消费税。该税在每个州都是相同的，因为它适用于每台新计算机的销售。消费税是一种常见且合理的增加收入的方式，这种税收的收益用于公共福利——向美国居民提供免费的计算机软件许可证。因此，该法规是符合宪法的。 （选项 B） 征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下将私有财产征用为公共用途。但在这里，联邦政府正在购买计算机软件的使用许可证，而不是从该软件的专利或版权持有者手中夺取财产。\n\n（选项D）根据版权和专利条款，国会可以颁布鼓励创意作品生产的法律（版权法）和发明（专利法）。但该条款并未授权国会对这些作品或发明的销售收取合理费用或支出收益。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有广泛的权力征收（1）在各州之间按比例分配且与收入生产合理相关的直接税，或（2）每个州相同且与收入生产合理相关的间接税。\n\n参考文献\n• Thomas v. United States, 192 U.S. 363, 371 (1904)（认为股票销售税是间接税）。\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 360–63 (1945)（维持每个州都相同的间接税）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会拥有广泛的权力来征收（1）直接税，这些税在各州之间按比例分配，并与收入生产合理相关；（2）间接税，每个州都相同，并且与收入生产合理相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"该法规是否符合宪法？","rule":"国会拥有广泛的权力来征收（1）直接税，这些税在各州之间按比例分配，并与收入生产合理相关；（2）间接税，每个州都相同，并且与收入生产合理相关。","analysis":"国会征税权\n\n定义类型 宪法要求税\n\n直接\n• 对个人或其财产征税 允许的情况：\n• 分配的\n• 各州之间无法按比例避免付款\n• 与收入产生合理相关 间接\n• 在以下情况下允许对商品/交易征税：\n• 付款可以避免或转移\n• 每个州（例如消费者）的税收相同\n• 与收入生产合理相关 出口\n• 对离开该国的商品/服务征税 不允许 征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力，可以为一般福利（即任何公共目的）征税和支出。国会可以使用其征税权：\n• 直接（即针对个人或不动产）——只要税收按比例在各州之间分配并且与收入生产合理相关（选择A）或\n\n• 间接（即针对货物或交易）——只要每个州的税收都是相同的，并且与收入生产合理相关。\n\n在这里，联邦法规对在美国销售的新计算机征收间接消费税。该税在每个州都是相同的，因为它适用于每台新计算机的销售。消费税是一种常见且合理的增加收入的方式，这种税收的收益用于公共福利——向美国居民提供免费的计算机软件许可证。因此，该法规是符合宪法的。 （选项 B） 征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下将私有财产征用为公共用途。但在这里，联邦政府正在购买计算机软件的使用许可证，而不是从该软件的专利或版权持有者手中夺取财产。\n\n（选项D）根据版权和专利条款，国会可以颁布鼓励创意作品生产的法律（版权法）和发明（专利法）。但该条款并未授权国会对这些作品或发明的销售收取合理费用或支出收益。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有广泛的权力征收（1）在各州之间按比例分配且与收入生产合理相关的直接税，或（2）每个州相同且与收入生产合理相关的间接税。\n\n参考文献\n• Thomas v. United States, 192 U.S. 363, 371 (1904)（认为股票销售税是间接税）。\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 360–63 (1945)（维持每个州都相同的间接税）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为这是国会为了公共福利而征税和支出的权力的合理行使。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"takings clause","ipa":"/tˈeɪkɪŋz klˈɔz/","syllables":"tak·ings clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"征收条款：政府为公共用途征收私人财产须支付公平补偿","contextualMeaning":"征收条款：政府为公共用途征收私人财产须支付公平补偿","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) The takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation.","synonyms":["takings article"]},{"term":"public use","ipa":"/pˈəblɪk jˈus/","syllables":"pub·lic use","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公共用途：征收须服务公众使用或公共目的","contextualMeaning":"公共用途：征收须服务公众使用或公共目的","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) The takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation.","synonyms":["public manipulation","public habit","public usage"]},{"term":"export","ipa":"/ˈɛkspɔɹt/","syllables":"ex·port","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 输出品, 输出；vt. 输出, 出口；vi. 输出物资","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 输出品, 输出；vt. 输出, 出口；vi. 输出物资","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:exports/d:exported/3:exports/i:exporting/p:exported","sourceSentence":"Congressional taxing power Type of Definition Constitutional requirements tax Direct • Taxes on persons or their Allowed if: property • apportioned • Payment cannot be avoided proportionately among states • reasonably related to revenue production Indirect • Taxes on goods/transactions Allowed if: • Payment can be avoided or shifted • identical taxation in every to another (eg, consumer) state • reasonably related to","synonyms":["exportation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":63,"sourceQuestionPage":188,"sourceQuestionPages":[188],"sourceExplanationPages":[189,190],"sourceAnswerPages":[189,190],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-064","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Eleventh Amendment & State Sovereign Immunity","question":"Congress passed a statute that expressly authorizes suits for damages in federal courts against state governments that violate federal copyright laws. A photographer sued a state in a federal district court for damages pursuant to the federal statute. The photographer claimed that the state published her copyrighted photographs and videos on its website without her permission. The state has moved to dismiss the photographer's suit. Should the court grant the state's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because Article I grants Congress the power to enact laws that protect copyrighted materials.","B":"No, because Articles I and III grant Congress the power to regulate the jurisdiction and procedures of the federal courts.","C":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits the action for damages against the state in federal court.","D":"Yes, because the Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from compelling states to act."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state for money damages or equitable relief in federal court. Congress can abrogate (ie, repeal) this immunity if it is clearly acting to enforce a Civil War Amendment—ie, the Thirteenth, Fourteenth, or Fifteenth Amendment (not seen here). Congress may then enact appropriate remedial legislation expressly subjecting the states to private suits for damages in federal court. However, Congress cannot abrogate a state's Eleventh Amendment immunity through its other powers.* This includes its Article I power to enact laws protecting copyrighted materials (Choice A). This also includes its power under Articles I and III to regulate the jurisdiction of the federal courts (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the state's motion to dismiss the photographer's suit pursuant to the Eleventh Amendment. *The lone exception to this rule is that Congress may subject nonconsenting states to bankruptcy proceedings through the Article I bankruptcy clause. The Supreme Court has justified this exception based on the bankruptcy clause's unique history and exceptionalism. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from compelling states to enact state laws or enforce federal laws. However, states can be subjected to suits for damages in federal court if Congress validly abrogates state immunity.\n\nEducational objective: The Eleventh Amendment generally grants states immunity from suits for damages. Congress can abrogate this immunity when it clearly acts to enforce a Civil War Amendment but cannot do so through its other powers—eg, its powers to protect copyrights and regulate federal court jurisdiction.\n\nReferences\n• Allen v. Cooper, 140 S. Ct. 994, 1007 (2020) (holding that Congress cannot abrogate the states' Eleventh Amendment immunity through the Article I copyright clause).","educationalObjective":"The Eleventh Amendment generally grants states immunity from suits for damages. Congress can abrogate this immunity when it clearly acts to enforce a Civil War Amendment but cannot do so through its other powers—eg, its powers to protect copyrights and regulate federal court jurisdiction.","references":"• Allen v. Cooper, 140 S. Ct. 994, 1007 (2020) (holding that Congress cannot abrogate the states' Eleventh Amendment immunity through the Article I copyright clause).","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment generally grants states immunity from suits for damages. Congress can abrogate this immunity when it clearly acts to enforce a Civil War Amendment but cannot do so through its other powers—eg, its powers to protect copyrights and regulate federal court jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the state's motion?","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment generally grants states immunity from suits for damages. Congress can abrogate this immunity when it clearly acts to enforce a Civil War Amendment but cannot do so through its other powers—eg, its powers to protect copyrights and regulate federal court jurisdiction.","analysis":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state for money damages or equitable relief in federal court. Congress can abrogate (ie, repeal) this immunity if it is clearly acting to enforce a Civil War Amendment—ie, the Thirteenth, Fourteenth, or Fifteenth Amendment (not seen here). Congress may then enact appropriate remedial legislation expressly subjecting the states to private suits for damages in federal court. However, Congress cannot abrogate a state's Eleventh Amendment immunity through its other powers.* This includes its Article I power to enact laws protecting copyrighted materials (Choice A). This also includes its power under Articles I and III to regulate the jurisdiction of the federal courts (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the state's motion to dismiss the photographer's suit pursuant to the Eleventh Amendment. *The lone exception to this rule is that Congress may subject nonconsenting states to bankruptcy proceedings through the Article I bankruptcy clause. The Supreme Court has justified this exception based on the bankruptcy clause's unique history and exceptionalism. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment prohibits the federal government from compelling states to enact state laws or enforce federal laws. However, states can be subjected to suits for damages in federal court if Congress validly abrogates state immunity.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits the action for damages against the state in federal court."},"questionZh":"国会通过了一项法规，明确授权向联邦法院对违反联邦版权法的州政府提起损害赔偿诉讼。一名摄影师根据联邦法规向联邦地区法院起诉一个州，要求赔偿损失。该摄影师声称，国家未经她的许可，在其网站上发布了她受版权保护的照片和视频。该州已采取行动驳回摄影师的诉讼。法院应该批准州政府的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为第一条赋予国会颁布保护受版权保护材料的法律的权力。","B":"不，因为第一条和第三条赋予国会规范联邦法院管辖权和程序的权力。","C":"是的，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院针对州提出损害赔偿诉讼。","D":"是的，因为第十修正案禁止联邦政府强迫各州采取行动。"},"explanationZh":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉国家要求金钱赔偿或公平救济。如果国会明显是为了执行内战修正案，即第十三、第十四或第十五修正案（此处未看到），则可以废除（即废除）这种豁免权。然后，国会可能会颁布适当的补救立法，明确要求各州向联邦法院提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。然而，国会不能通过其其他权力废除州的第十一修正案豁免权。*这包括其第一条颁布保护版权材料的法律的权力（选择 A）。这还包括其根据第一条和第三条规定的权力，以规范联邦法院的管辖权（选择 B）。因此，法院很可能会根据第十一条修正案批准该州提出的驳回摄影师诉讼的动议。 *该规则的唯一例外是，国会可以通过第一条破产条款对不同意的州进行破产程序。最高法院根据破产条款的独特历史和例外论证明了这一例外的合理性。 （选项 D） 第十修正案禁止联邦政府强迫各州制定州法律或执行联邦法律。然而，如果国会有效废除各州豁免权，各州可能会向联邦法院提起损害赔偿诉讼。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案一般赋予各州免受损害赔偿诉讼的豁免权。当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案但无法通过其其他权力（例如保护版权和监管联邦法院管辖权的权力）来这样做时，国会可以废除这种豁免权。\n\n参考文献\n• Allen 诉 Cooper，140 S. Ct. 994, 1007 (2020)（认为国会不能通过第一条版权条款废除各州的第十一修正案豁免权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十一条修正案一般赋予各州免于损害赔偿诉讼的权利。当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案但无法通过其其他权力（例如保护版权和监管联邦法院管辖权的权力）来这样做时，国会可以废除这种豁免权。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该批准州政府的动议吗？","rule":"第十一条修正案一般赋予各州免于损害赔偿诉讼的权利。当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案但无法通过其其他权力（例如保护版权和监管联邦法院管辖权的权力）来这样做时，国会可以废除这种豁免权。","analysis":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉国家要求金钱赔偿或公平救济。如果国会明显是为了执行内战修正案，即第十三、第十四或第十五修正案（此处未看到），则可以废除（即废除）这种豁免权。然后，国会可能会颁布适当的补救立法，明确要求各州向联邦法院提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。然而，国会不能通过其其他权力废除州的第十一修正案豁免权。*这包括其第一条颁布保护版权材料的法律的权力（选择 A）。这还包括其根据第一条和第三条规定的权力，以规范联邦法院的管辖权（选择 B）。因此，法院很可能会根据第十一条修正案批准该州提出的驳回摄影师诉讼的动议。 *该规则的唯一例外是，国会可以通过第一条破产条款对不同意的州进行破产程序。最高法院根据破产条款的独特历史和例外论证明了这一例外的合理性。 （选项 D） 第十修正案禁止联邦政府强迫各州制定州法律或执行联邦法律。然而，如果国会有效废除各州豁免权，各州可能会向联邦法院提起损害赔偿诉讼。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案一般赋予各州免受损害赔偿诉讼的豁免权。当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案但无法通过其其他权力（例如保护版权和监管联邦法院管辖权的权力）来这样做时，国会可以废除这种豁免权。\n\n参考文献\n• Allen 诉 Cooper，140 S. Ct. 994, 1007 (2020)（认为国会不能通过第一条版权条款废除各州的第十一修正案豁免权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院针对州提出损害赔偿诉讼。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":64,"sourceQuestionPage":191,"sourceQuestionPages":[191],"sourceExplanationPages":[192,193],"sourceAnswerPages":[192,193],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-065","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"Congress recently enacted a statute imposing severe criminal penalties on anyone engaged in trading in the stock market who, in the course of that trading, takes \"unfair advantage\" of other investors who are also trading in the stock market. The statute does not define the term \"unfair advantage.\" There have been no prosecutions under this new statute. The members of an association of law school professors that is dedicated to increasing the clarity of the language used in criminal statutes believe that this statute is unconstitutionally vague. Neither the association nor any of its members is currently engaged in, or intends in the future to engage in, trading in the stock market. The association and its members bring suit against the Attorney General of the United States in a federal district court, seeking an injunction against the enforcement of this statute on the ground that it is unconstitutional. May the federal court determine the merits of this suit?","choices":{"A":"No, because a suit for an injunction against enforcement of a criminal statute may not be brought in federal court at any time prior to a bona fide effort to enforce that statute.","B":"No, because the plaintiffs do not have an interest in invalidating this statute that is adequate to ensure that the suit presents an Article III controversy.","C":"Yes, because the plaintiffs seek real relief of a conclusive nature—an injunction against enforcement of this statute.","D":"Yes, because the suit involves a dispute over the constitutionality of a federal statute."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Article III only allows federal courts to determine the merits of actual cases or controversies. The doctrine of standing ensures that this requirement is met by requiring the plaintiff to show that he/she has a personal stake in the outcome of the case. This exists when the plaintiff has suffered an injury-in-fact—ie, concrete and particularized harm that is actual or imminent—that was caused by the challenged conduct and is redressable by a favorable decision. Here, the association and its members sued in federal court to challenge the constitutionality of the stock market statute. But the statute has not caused these plaintiffs an injury-in-fact because neither is currently engaged in, or intends to engage in, stock market trading (no actual or imminent harm). This means that the plaintiffs do not have an interest in invalidating the statute that would satisfy Article III's case-or-controversy requirement. As a result, the court cannot determine the merits of their suit. (Choice A) Even if a criminal statute has not been enforced, a suit for an injunction to prevent such enforcement may be brought in federal court if the plaintiff shows that the statute poses an immediate threat of harm (not seen here). (Choice C) The injunction would provide real and conclusive relief. But the federal court does not have the power to issue an injunction in a suit that does not meet the case-or- controversy requirement (as seen here). (Choice D) Federal courts have subject-matter jurisdiction over suits involving the constitutionality of federal statutes. But the court cannot hear the merits of such a suit when, as here, the plaintiffs lack standing to assert their constitutional challenge.\n\nEducational objective: Under the doctrine of standing, an injury-in-fact exists when the plaintiff has suffered concrete and particularized harm that is actual or imminent.\n\nReferences\n• Lujan v. Defs. of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992) (holding that standing requires proof of an injury-in-fact, causation, and redressability).","educationalObjective":"Under the doctrine of standing, an injury-in-fact exists when the plaintiff has suffered concrete and particularized harm that is actual or imminent.","references":"• Lujan v. Defs. of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992) (holding that standing requires proof of an injury-in-fact, causation, and redressability).","rule":"Under the doctrine of standing, an injury-in-fact exists when the plaintiff has suffered concrete and particularized harm that is actual or imminent.","irac":{"issue":"May the federal court determine the merits of this suit?","rule":"Under the doctrine of standing, an injury-in-fact exists when the plaintiff has suffered concrete and particularized harm that is actual or imminent.","analysis":"Article III only allows federal courts to determine the merits of actual cases or controversies. The doctrine of standing ensures that this requirement is met by requiring the plaintiff to show that he/she has a personal stake in the outcome of the case. This exists when the plaintiff has suffered an injury-in-fact—ie, concrete and particularized harm that is actual or imminent—that was caused by the challenged conduct and is redressable by a favorable decision. Here, the association and its members sued in federal court to challenge the constitutionality of the stock market statute. But the statute has not caused these plaintiffs an injury-in-fact because neither is currently engaged in, or intends to engage in, stock market trading (no actual or imminent harm). This means that the plaintiffs do not have an interest in invalidating the statute that would satisfy Article III's case-or-controversy requirement. As a result, the court cannot determine the merits of their suit. (Choice A) Even if a criminal statute has not been enforced, a suit for an injunction to prevent such enforcement may be brought in federal court if the plaintiff shows that the statute poses an immediate threat of harm (not seen here). (Choice C) The injunction would provide real and conclusive relief. But the federal court does not have the power to issue an injunction in a suit that does not meet the case-or- controversy requirement (as seen here). (Choice D) Federal courts have subject-matter jurisdiction over suits involving the constitutionality of federal statutes. But the court cannot hear the merits of such a suit when, as here, the plaintiffs lack standing to assert their constitutional challenge.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the plaintiffs do not have an interest in invalidating this statute that is adequate to ensure that the suit presents an Article III controversy."},"questionZh":"国会最近颁布了一项法规，对任何在股票市场交易的人在交易过程中利用也在股票市场交易的其他投资者的“不公平优势”的人实施严厉的刑事处罚。该法规没有定义“不公平优势”一词。根据这项新法规，尚未有任何起诉。一个致力于提高刑事法规中使用的语言清晰度的法学院教授协会的成员认为，该法规含糊不清，违宪。该协会及其任何成员目前均未从事或将来打算从事股票市场交易。该协会及其成员向联邦地区法院起诉美国司法部长，以该法规违宪为由，寻求禁止执行该法规的禁令。联邦法院可以确定该诉讼的案情吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为在善意执行刑事法规之前的任何时间都不得向联邦法院提起针对执行刑事法规的禁令的诉讼。","B":"不，因为原告没有利益使该法规无效，这足以确保诉讼提出第三条争议。","C":"是的，因为原告寻求具有决定性性质的真正救济——禁止执行该法规的禁令。","D":"是的，因为该诉讼涉及联邦法规的合宪性争议。"},"explanationZh":"第三条仅允许联邦法院确定实际案件或争议的实质。诉讼资格原则要求原告证明他/她在案件结果中拥有个人利益，从而确保满足这一要求。当原告遭受了事实上的伤害（即实际或迫在眉睫的具体和特殊伤害）时，这种情况就存在，这种伤害是由受质疑的行为造成的，并且可以通过有利的决定予以纠正。在这里，该协会及其成员向联邦法院提起诉讼，质疑股票市场法规的合宪性。但该法规并未对这些原告造成事实上的伤害，因为他们目前都没有从事或打算从事股票市场交易（没有实际或迫在眉睫的伤害）。这意味着原告没有兴趣使满足第三条案件或争议要求的法规无效。因此，法院无法确定他们的诉讼是非曲直。 （选择 A）即使刑事法规尚未得到执行，如果原告表明该法规构成了直接的伤害威胁（此处未见），也可以向联邦法院提起禁止此类执行的诉讼。 （选择 C） 禁令将提供真正且决定性的救济。但联邦法院无权对不符合案件或争议要求的诉讼发布禁令（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）联邦法院对涉及联邦法规合宪性的诉讼拥有主题管辖权。但是，当原告没有资格提出宪法质疑时，法院无法审理此类诉讼的实质问题。\n\n教育目标：根据原告原则，当原告遭受实际或迫在眉睫的具体和特殊伤害时，就存在事实伤害。\n\n参考文献\n• Lujan 诉Defs。 of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992)（认为资格要求提供事实伤害、因果关系和可补救性的证据）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据原告原则，当原告遭受实际或迫在眉睫的具体和特殊伤害时，即存在事实伤害。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法院可以确定该诉讼的案情吗？","rule":"根据原告原则，当原告遭受实际或迫在眉睫的具体和特殊伤害时，即存在事实伤害。","analysis":"第三条仅允许联邦法院确定实际案件或争议的实质。诉讼资格原则要求原告证明他/她在案件结果中拥有个人利益，从而确保满足这一要求。当原告遭受了事实上的伤害（即实际或迫在眉睫的具体和特殊伤害）时，这种情况就存在，这种伤害是由受质疑的行为造成的，并且可以通过有利的决定予以纠正。在这里，该协会及其成员向联邦法院提起诉讼，质疑股票市场法规的合宪性。但该法规并未对这些原告造成事实上的伤害，因为他们目前都没有从事或打算从事股票市场交易（没有实际或迫在眉睫的伤害）。这意味着原告没有兴趣使满足第三条案件或争议要求的法规无效。因此，法院无法确定他们的诉讼是非曲直。 （选择 A）即使刑事法规尚未得到执行，如果原告表明该法规构成了直接的伤害威胁（此处未见），也可以向联邦法院提起禁止此类执行的诉讼。 （选择 C） 禁令将提供真正且决定性的救济。但联邦法院无权对不符合案件或争议要求的诉讼发布禁令（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）联邦法院对涉及联邦法规合宪性的诉讼拥有主题管辖权。但是，当原告没有资格提出宪法质疑时，法院无法审理此类诉讼的实质问题。\n\n教育目标：根据原告原则，当原告遭受实际或迫在眉睫的具体和特殊伤害时，就存在事实伤害。\n\n参考文献\n• Lujan 诉Defs。 of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992)（认为资格要求提供事实伤害、因果关系和可补救性的证据）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为原告没有利益使该法规无效，这足以确保诉讼提出第三条争议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"penalties","ipa":"/pˈɛnəltiz/","syllables":"penal·ties","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 惩罚( penalty的名词复数 )； 刑罚； 害处","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 惩罚( penalty的名词复数 )； 刑罚； 害处","wordForm":"词；原形 penalty；词形 0:penalty/1:s","sourceSentence":"Congress recently enacted a statute imposing severe criminal penalties on anyone engaged in trading in the stock market who, in the course of that trading, takes \"unfair advantage\" of other investors who are also trading in the stock market.","synonyms":["punishment","penalization","penalisation"]},{"term":"vague","ipa":"/vˈeɪɡ/","syllables":"vague","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 含糊的, 不清楚的, 茫然的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 含糊的, 不清楚的, 茫然的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The members of an association of law school professors that is dedicated to increasing the clarity of the language used in criminal statutes believe that this statute is unconstitutionally vague.","synonyms":["obscure","undefined","dim","faint"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-e4d2f96689bf3a25"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":65,"sourceQuestionPage":194,"sourceQuestionPages":[194],"sourceExplanationPages":[195,196],"sourceAnswerPages":[195,196],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-066","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Taxing & Spending Powers","question":"Congress enacted a statute providing grants of federal funds for the restoration and preservation of courthouses that were built before 1900 and are still in use. The statute contains an inseverable condition requiring that any courthouse restored with the aid of such a grant must be equipped with ramps and other facilities necessary to accommodate physically handicapped people. A state law requires public buildings in the state to have ramps and other facilities for handicapped people. It exempts from those requirements any building that is more than 70 years old if the State Board of Architects finds that the installation of such facilities would destroy the architectural integrity of the building. A county courthouse in the state was built in 1895 and is still in use. It does not contain ramps or other special facilities for handicapped people. The State Board of Architects has determined that the installation of those facilities would destroy the architectural integrity of the building. Nevertheless, the county applies for a federal grant to restore and preserve that county's courthouse. If the county restores the courthouse with the aid of a federal restoration and preservation grant, is the county bound to install ramps and other facilities for handicapped people in that building?","choices":{"A":"No, because any acceptance of this condition by the county would, as a matter of law, be considered to be under duress.","B":"No, because the Constitution does not authorize the federal government to direct the actions of the states or any of their political subdivisions with respect to matters affecting their own governmental buildings.","C":"Yes, because Congress may impose reasonable conditions related to the public welfare on grants of federal funds to public bodies when the public bodies are free to accept or reject the grants.","D":"Yes, because the rights of handicapped and disabled people are fundamental rights that take precedence, as a constitutional matter, over considerations of architectural integrity."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Conditional federal funding\n\n(Article I, section 8 spending clause)\n\nConditions must meet all the following criteria:\n\n• clearly stated & unambiguous\n• reasonably related to federal interest in funded program\n• do not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity\n• do not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad discretion to tax and spend for the general welfare (ie, for any public purpose). Congress may use its spending power to incentivize state or local governments to act in certain ways by placing conditions on their receipt of federal funds. But these conditions are only valid if they:\n• are clearly stated and unambiguous so that potential recipients may make an informed decision\n• are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended\n• do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity and\n• are not unduly coercive—ie, are not so extreme that they amount to a \"gun to the head.\" Here, Congress imposed a condition on the receipt of federal grants for the restoration and preservation of historic courthouses (public welfare). That condition is valid—and the county will be bound to that condition if it restores its courthouse with the aid of such a grant—because:\n• it unambiguously requires any courthouse restored with the aid of such a grant to be equipped with facilities for handicapped people\n\n• installing handicap facilities in courthouses is reasonably related to restoring and preserving those buildings\n\n• recipients are not required to do anything unconstitutional and\n\n• being required to install handicap facilities is a relatively minor condition for receiving funds to restore and preserve an entire courthouse (ie, no undue coercion) (Choice A).\n\n(Choice B) Under the Tenth Amendment, the federal government cannot direct states or their political subdivisions (eg, counties) to enact legislation or enforce federal law. But Congress may incentivize them to act through conditional federal funding (as seen here).\n\n(Choice D) Handicapped and disabled people enjoy the same fundamental rights as everyone else—but no such rights are implicated here.\n\nEducational objective: Conditional federal funding is valid if the conditions (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds are expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive.\n\nReferences\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207–12 (1987) (holding that conditions on states' receipt of federal funds must be unambiguous, related to the purpose of the funding, and noncoercive).","educationalObjective":"Conditional federal funding is valid if the conditions (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds are expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive.","references":"• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207–12 (1987) (holding that conditions on states' receipt of federal funds must be unambiguous, related to the purpose of the funding, and noncoercive).","rule":"Conditional federal funding is valid if the conditions (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds are expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive.","irac":{"issue":"If the county restores the courthouse with the aid of a federal restoration and preservation grant, is the county bound to install ramps and other facilities for handicapped people in that building?","rule":"Conditional federal funding is valid if the conditions (1) are clear and unambiguous, (2) are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds are expended, (3) do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity, and (4) are not unduly coercive.","analysis":"Conditional federal funding\n\n(Article I, section 8 spending clause)\n\nConditions must meet all the following criteria:\n\n• clearly stated & unambiguous\n• reasonably related to federal interest in funded program\n• do not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity\n• do not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad discretion to tax and spend for the general welfare (ie, for any public purpose). Congress may use its spending power to incentivize state or local governments to act in certain ways by placing conditions on their receipt of federal funds. But these conditions are only valid if they:\n• are clearly stated and unambiguous so that potential recipients may make an informed decision\n• are reasonably related to the purpose for which the funds will be expended\n• do not require recipients to engage in unconstitutional activity and\n• are not unduly coercive—ie, are not so extreme that they amount to a \"gun to the head.\" Here, Congress imposed a condition on the receipt of federal grants for the restoration and preservation of historic courthouses (public welfare). That condition is valid—and the county will be bound to that condition if it restores its courthouse with the aid of such a grant—because:\n• it unambiguously requires any courthouse restored with the aid of such a grant to be equipped with facilities for handicapped people\n\n• installing handicap facilities in courthouses is reasonably related to restoring and preserving those buildings\n\n• recipients are not required to do anything unconstitutional and\n\n• being required to install handicap facilities is a relatively minor condition for receiving funds to restore and preserve an entire courthouse (ie, no undue coercion) (Choice A).\n\n(Choice B) Under the Tenth Amendment, the federal government cannot direct states or their political subdivisions (eg, counties) to enact legislation or enforce federal law. But Congress may incentivize them to act through conditional federal funding (as seen here).\n\n(Choice D) Handicapped and disabled people enjoy the same fundamental rights as everyone else—but no such rights are implicated here.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because Congress may impose reasonable conditions related to the public welfare on grants of federal funds to public bodies when the public bodies are free to accept or reject the grants."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，提供联邦资金拨款，用于修复和保护 1900 年之前建造且仍在使用的法院大楼。该法规包含一个不可分割的条件，要求在此类拨款的帮助下恢复的任何法院大楼都必须配备坡道和其他必要的设施，以适应身体残疾人士的需要。州法律要求该州的公共建筑必须为残疾人士提供坡道和其他设施。如果国家建筑师委员会认为安装此类设施会破坏建筑物的建筑完整性，则任何楼龄超过 70 年的建筑物都可以免受这些要求的约束。该州的一座县法院建于 1895 年，至今仍在使用。它不包含坡道或其他供残疾人使用的特殊设施。国家建筑师委员会已确定，安装这些设施将破坏建筑物的建筑完整性。尽管如此，该县还是申请了联邦拨款来恢复和保护该县的法院大楼。如果县在联邦修复和保护拨款的帮助下修复法院大楼，该县是否必须在该建筑中为残疾人安装坡道和其他设施？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为根据法律，该县接受这一条件将被视为受到胁迫。","B":"不可以，因为宪法没有授权联邦政府指导各州或其任何政治部门就影响其政府大楼的事务采取行动。","C":"是的，因为当公共机构可以自由接受或拒绝拨款时，国会可以对向公共机构拨款的联邦资金施加与公共福利相关的合理条件。","D":"是的，因为残疾人的权利是基本权利，作为宪法问题，优先于建筑完整性的考虑。"},"explanationZh":"有条件的联邦资助\n\n（第一条第8款支出条款）\n\n条件必须满足以下所有标准：\n\n• 明确表述且不含糊\n• 与资助计划中的联邦利益合理相关\n• 不要求各州从事违宪活动\n• 不要过度强迫各州接受税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的自由裁量权，可以为公共福利（即任何公共目的）征税和支出。国会可以利用其支出能力，通过对收到联邦资金设定条件来激励州或地方政府以某些方式采取行动。但这些条件只有在满足以下条件时才有效：\n• 明确表述且不含糊，以便潜在接收者可以做出明智的决定\n• 与资金支出的目的合理相关\n• 不要求接收者从事违宪活动，并且\n• 没有过度强制——即，没有极端到相当于“用枪指着头部”。在这里，国会对获得用于修复和保护历史法院大楼的联邦拨款（公共福利）施加了一个条件。该条件是有效的，如果县在此类拨款的帮助下恢复法院大楼，则将受到该条件的约束，因为：\n• 明确要求在此类拨款的帮助下修复的任何法院大楼均配备供残疾人使用的设施\n\n• 在法院大楼安装残疾人设施与恢复和保护这些建筑物有合理的关系\n\n• 接收者无需做任何违反宪法的事情\n\n• 要求安装残疾人设施是获得修复和保护整个法院的资金的一个相对次要的条件（即没有不当胁迫）（选择A）。\n\n（选项B） 根据第十修正案，联邦政府不能指示各州或其政治分支机构（例如县）制定立法或执行联邦法律。但国会可能会通过有条件的联邦资助来激励他们采取行动（如此处所示）。\n\n（选项D）残障人士和残障人士享有与其他人相同的基本权利——但这里不涉及此类权利。\n\n教育目标：如果条件 (1) 明确且不含糊，(2) 与资金支出的目的合理相关，(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动，并且 (4) 没有过度胁迫，则有条件联邦资助是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207-12 (1987)（认为各州接收联邦资金的条件必须明确、与资金目的相关且非强制性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果满足以下条件，有条件联邦资助是有效的：(1) 明确且不含糊；(2) 与资金支出的目的合理相关；(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动；(4) 没有过度强制。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果县在联邦修复和保护拨款的帮助下修复法院大楼，该县是否必须在该建筑中为残疾人安装坡道和其他设施？","rule":"如果满足以下条件，有条件联邦资助是有效的：(1) 明确且不含糊；(2) 与资金支出的目的合理相关；(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动；(4) 没有过度强制。","analysis":"有条件的联邦资助\n\n（第一条第8款支出条款）\n\n条件必须满足以下所有标准：\n\n• 明确表述且不含糊\n• 与资助计划中的联邦利益合理相关\n• 不要求各州从事违宪活动\n• 不要过度强迫各州接受税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的自由裁量权，可以为公共福利（即任何公共目的）征税和支出。国会可以利用其支出能力，通过对收到联邦资金设定条件来激励州或地方政府以某些方式采取行动。但这些条件只有在满足以下条件时才有效：\n• 明确表述且不含糊，以便潜在接收者可以做出明智的决定\n• 与资金支出的目的合理相关\n• 不要求接收者从事违宪活动，并且\n• 没有过度强制——即，没有极端到相当于“用枪指着头部”。在这里，国会对获得用于修复和保护历史法院大楼的联邦拨款（公共福利）施加了一个条件。该条件是有效的，如果县在此类拨款的帮助下恢复法院大楼，则将受到该条件的约束，因为：\n• 明确要求在此类拨款的帮助下修复的任何法院大楼均配备供残疾人使用的设施\n\n• 在法院大楼安装残疾人设施与恢复和保护这些建筑物有合理的关系\n\n• 接收者无需做任何违反宪法的事情\n\n• 要求安装残疾人设施是获得修复和保护整个法院的资金的一个相对次要的条件（即没有不当胁迫）（选择A）。\n\n（选项B） 根据第十修正案，联邦政府不能指示各州或其政治分支机构（例如县）制定立法或执行联邦法律。但国会可能会通过有条件的联邦资助来激励他们采取行动（如此处所示）。\n\n（选项D）残障人士和残障人士享有与其他人相同的基本权利——但这里不涉及此类权利。\n\n教育目标：如果条件 (1) 明确且不含糊，(2) 与资金支出的目的合理相关，(3) 不要求接受者从事违宪活动，并且 (4) 没有过度胁迫，则有条件联邦资助是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207-12 (1987)（认为各州接收联邦资金的条件必须明确、与资金目的相关且非强制性）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为当公共机构可以自由接受或拒绝拨款时，国会可以对向公共机构拨款的联邦资金施加与公共福利相关的合理条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"courthouse","ipa":"/kˈɔɹthˌaʊs/","syllables":"cour·t·house","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 法院, 郡政府所在地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 法院, 郡政府所在地","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:courthouses","sourceSentence":"Congress enacted a statute providing grants of federal funds for the restoration and preservation of courthouses that were built before 1900 and are still in use.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"coercion","ipa":"/koʊˈɝʃən/","syllables":"co·er·cion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 强迫, 威压, 高压统治；[法] 强迫, 强制, 高压统治","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 强迫, 威压, 高压统治；[法] 强迫, 强制, 高压统治","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:coercions","sourceSentence":"That condition is valid—and the county will be bound to that condition if it restores its courthouse with the aid of such a grant—because: • it unambiguously requires any courthouse restored with the aid of such a grant to be equipped with facilities for handicapped people • installing handicap facilities in courthouses is reasonably related to restoring and preserving those buildings • recipients are not required to","synonyms":["compulsion"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":66,"sourceQuestionPage":197,"sourceQuestionPages":[197],"sourceExplanationPages":[198,199],"sourceAnswerPages":[198,199],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-067","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Taxing & Spending Powers","question":"A newly enacted federal statute appropriates $100 million in federal funds to support basic research by universities located in the United States. The statute provides that \"the 10 best universities in the United States\" will each receive $10 million. It also provides that \"the 10 best universities\" shall be \"determined by a poll of the presidents of all the universities in the nation, to be conducted by the United States Department of Education.\" In responding to that poll, each university president is required to apply the well-recognized and generally accepted standards of academic quality that are specified in the statute. The provisions of the statute are inseverable. Which of the following statements about this statute is correct?","choices":{"A":"The statute is constitutional, because Congress has plenary authority to determine the objects of its spending and the methods used to achieve them, so long as they may reasonably be deemed to serve the general welfare and do not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution.","B":"The statute is unconstitutional, because the limitation on recipients to the 10 best universities is arbitrary and capricious and denies other high-quality universities the equal protection of the laws.","C":"The statute is unconstitutional, because the reliance by Congress on a poll of individuals who are not federal officials to determine the recipients of its appropriated funds is an unconstitutional delegation of legislative power.","D":"The validity of the statute is nonjusticiable, because the use by Congress of its spending power necessarily involves political considerations that must be resolved finally by those branches of the government that are closest to the political process."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Congress's taxing & spending powers\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, sec. 8, cl. 1)\n\nTaxing\n• Direct taxes – must be: – apportioned evenly among states and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Indirect taxes – must be: – uniformly applied in every state and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Export taxes – always unconstitutional Spending\n• Funds must be spent for general welfare\n• Conditions on receipt of funds must: – be clearly stated & unambiguous – be reasonably related to federal interest in funded program – not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity and – not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress the power to spend federal revenue for the general welfare—ie, for any public purpose. Aside from this narrow constraint, Congress has plenary (ie, absolute) authority to determine the objects of its spending and the methods used to achieve them. But when doing so, Congress cannot violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution (eg, equal protection clause). Here, the federal statute appropriates $100 million in federal funds to support basic research by universities located in the United States. Such research may reasonably be deemed to serve the general welfare, and the statute does not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution. And since Congress has plenary authority to determine its spending goals and the methods for achieving them, the statute is constitutional. (Choice B) Equal protection principles extend to all persons and organizations. And though the statute limits funding to the 10 best universities, all universities are eligible to receive votes and have their presidents' votes counted. Therefore, the statute does not deny other high-quality universities the equal protection of the laws. (Choice C) Congress can delegate legislative power if it provides intelligible guidelines for the delegates to follow. This standard is met here since Congress has clearly directed the Department of Education to conduct a poll of university presidents and specified the standards that must be applied in responding to that poll.\n\n(Choice D) Federal courts are prohibited from adjudicating political questions—ie, issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since courts regularly determine the constitutionality of federal statutes, this prohibition does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to spend federal revenue, as long as the spending (1) is for the general welfare and (2) does not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution.\n\nReferences\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207–08 (1987) (explaining that Congress's spending power must serve a public purpose and cannot violate any prohibitory language of the Constitution).","educationalObjective":"The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to spend federal revenue, as long as the spending (1) is for the general welfare and (2) does not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution.","references":"• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207–08 (1987) (explaining that Congress's spending power must serve a public purpose and cannot violate any prohibitory language of the Constitution).","rule":"The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to spend federal revenue, as long as the spending (1) is for the general welfare and (2) does not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements about this statute is correct?","rule":"The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to spend federal revenue, as long as the spending (1) is for the general welfare and (2) does not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution.","analysis":"Congress's taxing & spending powers\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, sec. 8, cl. 1)\n\nTaxing\n• Direct taxes – must be: – apportioned evenly among states and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Indirect taxes – must be: – uniformly applied in every state and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Export taxes – always unconstitutional Spending\n• Funds must be spent for general welfare\n• Conditions on receipt of funds must: – be clearly stated & unambiguous – be reasonably related to federal interest in funded program – not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity and – not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress the power to spend federal revenue for the general welfare—ie, for any public purpose. Aside from this narrow constraint, Congress has plenary (ie, absolute) authority to determine the objects of its spending and the methods used to achieve them. But when doing so, Congress cannot violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution (eg, equal protection clause). Here, the federal statute appropriates $100 million in federal funds to support basic research by universities located in the United States. Such research may reasonably be deemed to serve the general welfare, and the statute does not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution. And since Congress has plenary authority to determine its spending goals and the methods for achieving them, the statute is constitutional. (Choice B) Equal protection principles extend to all persons and organizations. And though the statute limits funding to the 10 best universities, all universities are eligible to receive votes and have their presidents' votes counted. Therefore, the statute does not deny other high-quality universities the equal protection of the laws. (Choice C) Congress can delegate legislative power if it provides intelligible guidelines for the delegates to follow. This standard is met here since Congress has clearly directed the Department of Education to conduct a poll of university presidents and specified the standards that must be applied in responding to that poll.\n\n(Choice D) Federal courts are prohibited from adjudicating political questions—ie, issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since courts regularly determine the constitutionality of federal statutes, this prohibition does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer A: The statute is constitutional, because Congress has plenary authority to determine the objects of its spending and the methods used to achieve them, so long as they may reasonably be deemed to serve the general welfare and do not violate any prohibitory language in the Constitution."},"questionZh":"新颁布的联邦法规拨款 1 亿美元的联邦资金支持美国大学的基础研究。该法规规定“美国最好的10所大学”每所将获得1000万美元。它还规定，“最好的10所大学”应“由美国教育部对全国所有大学的校长进行民意调查来确定”。在回应这项民意调查时，每所大学校长都必须采用法规中规定的公认且普遍接受的学术质量标准。该法规的规定是不可分割的。关于该法规，下列哪项表述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法规符合宪法，因为国会拥有决定其支出目的和实现这些目的的方法的全权，只要它们可以合理地被认为是服务于公众福利并且不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。","B":"该法规是违宪的，因为对10所最佳大学的获奖者的限制是任意且反复无常的，并且剥夺了其他高质量大学受到法律的平等保护。","C":"该法规违宪，因为国会依靠非联邦官员的个人民意调查来确定其拨款的接受者，这是违宪的立法权授权。","D":"该法规的有效性是不可审理的，因为国会对其支出权的使用必然涉及政治考虑，而这些考虑必须最终由最接近政治进程的政府部门来解决。"},"explanationZh":"国会的征税和支出权力\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条第 1 条）\n\n征税\n• 直接税 – 必须： – 在各州之间平均分配，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 间接税 – 必须： – 在每个州统一适用，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 出口税——总是违宪支出\n• 资金必须用于公共福利\n• 收到资金的条件必须： – 明确规定且不含糊 – 与资助项目中的联邦利益合理相关 – 不要求各州参与违宪活动以及 – 不过度强迫各州接受 征税和支出条款赋予国会将联邦收入用于公共福利（即任何公共目的）的权力。除了这一狭隘的限制之外，国会拥有全体（即绝对）权力来决定其支出的目的以及实现这些目的的方法。但这样做时，国会不能违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言（例如平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。在此，联邦法规拨款 1 亿美元的联邦资金来支持美国大学的基础研究。此类研究可以合理地被视为服务于公众福利，并且该法规不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。由于国会拥有决定其支出目标和实现这些目标的方法的全权，因此该法规符合宪法。 （选择B）平等保护原则适用于所有个人和组织。尽管该法规限制向前 10 所最好的大学提供资助，但所有大学都有资格获得选票并计算其校长的选票。因此，该章程并不否认其他优质大学受到法律的平等保护。 （选择 C）如果国会为代表们提供了可理解的指导方针，那么它可以委托立法权。这里满足了这一标准，因为国会明确指示教育部对大学校长进行民意调查，并指定了回应该民意调查时必须采用的标准。\n\n（选项 D）联邦法院被禁止裁决政治问题，即 (1) 保留给行政或立法部门或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准的问题。但由于法院定期确定联邦法规的合宪性，因此这项禁令并不适用。\n\n教育目标：征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力来支出联邦收入，只要支出（1）用于公共福利并且（2）不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。\n\n参考文献\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207–08 (1987)（解释国会的支出能力必须服务于公共目的，并且不能违反宪法的任何禁止性语言）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力来支出联邦收入，只要支出（1）用于公共福利并且（2）不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于该法规，下列哪项表述是正确的？","rule":"税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力来支出联邦收入，只要支出（1）用于公共福利并且（2）不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。","analysis":"国会的征税和支出权力\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条第 1 条）\n\n征税\n• 直接税 – 必须： – 在各州之间平均分配，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 间接税 – 必须： – 在每个州统一适用，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 出口税——总是违宪支出\n• 资金必须用于公共福利\n• 收到资金的条件必须： – 明确规定且不含糊 – 与资助项目中的联邦利益合理相关 – 不要求各州参与违宪活动以及 – 不过度强迫各州接受 征税和支出条款赋予国会将联邦收入用于公共福利（即任何公共目的）的权力。除了这一狭隘的限制之外，国会拥有全体（即绝对）权力来决定其支出的目的以及实现这些目的的方法。但这样做时，国会不能违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言（例如平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。在此，联邦法规拨款 1 亿美元的联邦资金来支持美国大学的基础研究。此类研究可以合理地被视为服务于公众福利，并且该法规不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。由于国会拥有决定其支出目标和实现这些目标的方法的全权，因此该法规符合宪法。 （选择B）平等保护原则适用于所有个人和组织。尽管该法规限制向前 10 所最好的大学提供资助，但所有大学都有资格获得选票并计算其校长的选票。因此，该章程并不否认其他优质大学受到法律的平等保护。 （选择 C）如果国会为代表们提供了可理解的指导方针，那么它可以委托立法权。这里满足了这一标准，因为国会明确指示教育部对大学校长进行民意调查，并指定了回应该民意调查时必须采用的标准。\n\n（选项 D）联邦法院被禁止裁决政治问题，即 (1) 保留给行政或立法部门或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准的问题。但由于法院定期确定联邦法规的合宪性，因此这项禁令并不适用。\n\n教育目标：征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力来支出联邦收入，只要支出（1）用于公共福利并且（2）不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。\n\n参考文献\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207–08 (1987)（解释国会的支出能力必须服务于公共目的，并且不能违反宪法的任何禁止性语言）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该法规符合宪法，因为国会拥有决定其支出目的和实现这些目的的方法的全权，只要它们可以合理地被认为是服务于公众福利并且不违反宪法中的任何禁止性语言。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":67,"sourceQuestionPage":200,"sourceQuestionPages":[200],"sourceExplanationPages":[201,202],"sourceAnswerPages":[201,202],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-068","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"The U.S. House of Representatives authorized a House committee to investigate current election laws and develop new legislation to close any loopholes that permit foreign governments to interfere in the U.S. election process. The House authorized this investigation because of widespread reports of foreign interference in the most recent presidential election. As part of its investigation, the House committee issued subpoenas to various financial institutions that sought the President's financial records for the years preceding her election to the presidency. The Internal Revenue Service (IRS) possesses nearly the same information as the financial records sought by the House committee. The President filed suit against the financial institutions in federal court to enjoin them from complying with the congressional subpoenas. Which of the following is the best argument in favor of the President's effort to obtain the injunction?","choices":{"A":"A congressional subpoena that seeks the President's personal information violates the separation of powers.","B":"A subpoena is unnecessary when the information that Congress seeks can be reasonably obtained from a source other than the President.","C":"The power to conduct investigations or issue subpoenas is not among the powers of Congress enumerated in Article I.","D":"The President's private information from before taking office is protected by executive privilege."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. Although conducting investigations and issuing subpoenas are not enumerated powers, investigations are reasonably necessary to obtain information that will help Congress carry out its enumerated powers (Choice C). As part of an investigation, a congressional committee may issue subpoenas to compel testimony and/or the production of documents and tangible things related to the investigation. However, a congressional subpoena that seeks the President's personal information raises significant separation of powers issues between these two branches of government. Without proper limitations, Congress could usurp presidential authority. But prohibiting all subpoenas seeking the President's personal information would undercut Congress's investigatory power (Choice A). As a result, a congressional committee seeking the President's personal information must provide detailed evidence that the subpoena:\n• is necessary to obtain the information sought because that information cannot be reasonably obtained from another source\n\n• is limited in scope to a valid legislative purpose (not vague or overbroad) and\n\n• does not impose an undue burden on the President's time and attention.\n\nHere, the IRS possesses nearly the same information as the financial records sought by the House committee. Therefore, the President's best argument to enjoin the financial institutions from complying with the congressional subpoenas is that they are unnecessary since the information that Congress seeks can be reasonably obtained from a source other than the President—the IRS. (Choice D) The President may assert executive privilege to prevent the disclosure of confidential documents and communications made in performance of official duties and responsibilities—not private functions (eg, the President's personal financial records sought here).\n\nEducational objective: A congressional committee seeking the President's personal information must provide detailed evidence that a subpoena (1) is necessary to obtain the information, (2) is limited in scope to a valid legislative purpose, and (3) does not unduly burden the President's time and attention.\n\nReferences\n• Trump v. Mazars, USA, Ltd. Liab. P'ship, 140 S. Ct. 2019, 2035–36 (2020) (setting forth the requirements for a congressional subpoena of the President's personal documents).","educationalObjective":"A congressional committee seeking the President's personal information must provide detailed evidence that a subpoena (1) is necessary to obtain the information, (2) is limited in scope to a valid legislative purpose, and (3) does not unduly burden the President's time and attention.","references":"• Trump v. Mazars, USA, Ltd. Liab. P'ship, 140 S. Ct. 2019, 2035–36 (2020) (setting forth the requirements for a congressional subpoena of the President's personal documents).","rule":"A congressional committee seeking the President's personal information must provide detailed evidence that a subpoena (1) is necessary to obtain the information, (2) is limited in scope to a valid legislative purpose, and (3) does not unduly burden the President's time and attention.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the best argument in favor of the President's effort to obtain the injunction?","rule":"A congressional committee seeking the President's personal information must provide detailed evidence that a subpoena (1) is necessary to obtain the information, (2) is limited in scope to a valid legislative purpose, and (3) does not unduly burden the President's time and attention.","analysis":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. Although conducting investigations and issuing subpoenas are not enumerated powers, investigations are reasonably necessary to obtain information that will help Congress carry out its enumerated powers (Choice C). As part of an investigation, a congressional committee may issue subpoenas to compel testimony and/or the production of documents and tangible things related to the investigation. However, a congressional subpoena that seeks the President's personal information raises significant separation of powers issues between these two branches of government. Without proper limitations, Congress could usurp presidential authority. But prohibiting all subpoenas seeking the President's personal information would undercut Congress's investigatory power (Choice A). As a result, a congressional committee seeking the President's personal information must provide detailed evidence that the subpoena:\n• is necessary to obtain the information sought because that information cannot be reasonably obtained from another source\n\n• is limited in scope to a valid legislative purpose (not vague or overbroad) and\n\n• does not impose an undue burden on the President's time and attention.\n\nHere, the IRS possesses nearly the same information as the financial records sought by the House committee. Therefore, the President's best argument to enjoin the financial institutions from complying with the congressional subpoenas is that they are unnecessary since the information that Congress seeks can be reasonably obtained from a source other than the President—the IRS. (Choice D) The President may assert executive privilege to prevent the disclosure of confidential documents and communications made in performance of official duties and responsibilities—not private functions (eg, the President's personal financial records sought here).","conclusion":"Answer B: A subpoena is unnecessary when the information that Congress seeks can be reasonably obtained from a source other than the President."},"questionZh":"美国众议院授权众议院委员会调查现行选举法并制定新立法，以堵住允许外国政府干预美国选举进程的任何漏洞。由于广泛报道外国干预最近的总统选举，众议院批准了这项调查。作为调查的一部分，众议院委员会向多家金融机构发出传票，要求提供总统当选总统前几年的财务记录。美国国税局 (IRS) 拥有与众议院委员会要求的财务记录几乎相同的信息。总统向联邦法院对金融​​机构提起诉讼，禁止它们遵守国会传票。以下哪一项是支持总统努力获得禁令的最佳论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"寻求总统个人信息的国会传票违反了权力分立。","B":"当国会寻求的信息可以从总统以外的来源合理获得时，就不需要传票。","C":"进行调查或发出传票的权力不属于第一条列举的国会权力之列。","D":"总统上任前的私人信息受到行政特权的保护。"},"explanationZh":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。尽管进行调查和发出传票不属于列举的权力，但为了获取有助于国会履行其列举的权力的信息，调查是合理必要的（选项 C）。作为调查的一部分，国会委员会可能会发出传票以强制作证和/或出示与调查相关的文件和有形物品。然而，寻求总统个人信息的国会传票引发了这两个政府部门之间的重大权力分离问题。如果没有适当的限制，国会可能会篡夺总统权力。但禁止所有寻求总统个人信息的传票将削弱国会的调查权（选择 A）。因此，寻求总统个人信息的国会委员会必须提供详细证据，证明传票：\n• 对于获取所寻求的信息是必要的，因为无法从其他来源合理地获取该信息\n\n• 范围仅限于有效的立法目的（不模糊或过于宽泛）并且\n\n• 不会对总统的时间和注意力造成不应有的负担。\n\n在这里，美国国税局拥有与众议院委员会寻求的财务记录几乎相同的信息。因此，总统禁止金融机构遵守国会传票的最佳理由是，这些传票是不必要的，因为国会寻求的信息可以从总统以外的其他来源（国税局）合理获得。 （选择D）总统可以主张行政特权，以防止披露在履行公务和责任时——而非私人职能（例如，此处寻求的总统个人财务记录）中进行的机密文件和通信。\n\n教育目标：寻求总统个人信息的国会委员会必须提供详细证据，证明传票 (1) 是获取信息所必需的，(2) 范围仅限于有效的立法目的，并且 (3) 不会过度增加总统的时间和注意力。\n\n参考文献\n• 特朗普诉玛泽 (Mazars)，美国有限公司 Liab。 P'ship, 140 S. Ct. 2019、2035–36 (2020)（规定了国会传票获取总统个人文件的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"寻求总统个人信息的国会委员会必须提供详细证据，证明传票 (1) 是获取信息所必需的，(2) 范围仅限于有效的立法目的，并且 (3) 不会过度增加总统的时间和注意力。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是支持总统努力获得禁令的最佳论据？","rule":"寻求总统个人信息的国会委员会必须提供详细证据，证明传票 (1) 是获取信息所必需的，(2) 范围仅限于有效的立法目的，并且 (3) 不会过度增加总统的时间和注意力。","analysis":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。尽管进行调查和发出传票不属于列举的权力，但为了获取有助于国会履行其列举的权力的信息，调查是合理必要的（选项 C）。作为调查的一部分，国会委员会可能会发出传票以强制作证和/或出示与调查相关的文件和有形物品。然而，寻求总统个人信息的国会传票引发了这两个政府部门之间的重大权力分离问题。如果没有适当的限制，国会可能会篡夺总统权力。但禁止所有寻求总统个人信息的传票将削弱国会的调查权（选择 A）。因此，寻求总统个人信息的国会委员会必须提供详细证据，证明传票：\n• 对于获取所寻求的信息是必要的，因为无法从其他来源合理地获取该信息\n\n• 范围仅限于有效的立法目的（不模糊或过于宽泛）并且\n\n• 不会对总统的时间和注意力造成不应有的负担。\n\n在这里，美国国税局拥有与众议院委员会寻求的财务记录几乎相同的信息。因此，总统禁止金融机构遵守国会传票的最佳理由是，这些传票是不必要的，因为国会寻求的信息可以从总统以外的其他来源（国税局）合理获得。 （选择D）总统可以主张行政特权，以防止披露在履行公务和责任时——而非私人职能（例如，此处寻求的总统个人财务记录）中进行的机密文件和通信。\n\n教育目标：寻求总统个人信息的国会委员会必须提供详细证据，证明传票 (1) 是获取信息所必需的，(2) 范围仅限于有效的立法目的，并且 (3) 不会过度增加总统的时间和注意力。\n\n参考文献\n• 特朗普诉玛泽 (Mazars)，美国有限公司 Liab。 P'ship, 140 S. Ct. 2019、2035–36 (2020)（规定了国会传票获取总统个人文件的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：当国会寻求的信息可以从总统以外的来源合理获得时，就不需要传票。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0bb4411256c84f23"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":68,"sourceQuestionPage":203,"sourceQuestionPages":[203],"sourceExplanationPages":[204,205],"sourceAnswerPages":[204,205],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-069","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A man mailed thousands of letters to individuals residing in his state that falsely claimed that the individuals were delinquent on their state tax payments. The letters further stated that the individuals needed to send a set amount of money to the listed address in order to avoid criminal prosecution. One of the individuals who received this letter contacted a federal agency. After investigating, the federal government charged the man with violating a federal statute that prohibits mailing misleading or false materials to obtain money. At trial, the man argues that Congress had no authority to enact the federal statute and that the statute is a violation of his free speech rights protected by the First Amendment. Is the court likely to find the statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because although Congress may regulate mail sent interstate, it lacks the power to regulate mail sent intrastate.","B":"No, because the statute violates the man's First Amendment rights.","C":"Yes, because Congress has the power to provide for the general welfare, and the statute directly advances and is narrowly tailored to achieve a substantial government interest.","D":"Yes, because Congress has the power to regulate the mail, and the letters are not constitutionally protected speech."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. As a result, Congress may regulate and restrict what is sent through the mail to carry out its enumerated power to establish post offices and post roads (as seen here). However, Congress must comply with other constitutional provisions when exercising this power, including the First Amendment free speech clause. Under the free speech clause, Congress may freely regulate and restrict materials containing (1) unprotected speech or (2) commercial speech that is false or misleading or concerns unlawful activity. Here, the man's letters are false commercial speech because they falsely claimed that the individuals were delinquent on their state tax payments and needed to pay money to avoid criminal prosecution. As a result, the letters are not protected by the First Amendment, and the court will likely find the statute constitutional (Choice B). (Choice A) Congress has the power to regulate mail sent interstate (ie, between two or more states) as well as mail sent intrastate (ie, within a state). (Choice C) Congress has the power to spend for the general welfare, but this power does not apply here since the federal statute is not a spending measure. Additionally, the statute need not directly advance or be narrowly tailored to achieve a substantial government interest. This intermediate level of First Amendment scrutiny only applies to commercial speech that is true, is not misleading, and concerns a lawful activity (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Congress has broad power to establish post offices and post roads, including regulating and restricting what may be sent by mail. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the First Amendment free speech clause by restricting unprotected speech OR commercial speech that is false or misleading or concerns unlawful activity.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 7 (power to establish post offices).\n\n• U.S. Postal Serv. v. Council of Greenburgh Civic Ass'ns, 453 U.S. 114, 126 (1981) (explaining that Congress cannot exercise its postal power in a manner that violates the First Amendment).\n\n• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980) (stating that false and misleading commercial speech is not protected by the First Amendment).","educationalObjective":"Congress has broad power to establish post offices and post roads, including regulating and restricting what may be sent by mail. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the First Amendment free speech clause by restricting unprotected speech OR commercial speech that is false or misleading or concerns unlawful activity.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 7 (power to establish post offices).\n\n• U.S. Postal Serv. v. Council of Greenburgh Civic Ass'ns, 453 U.S. 114, 126 (1981) (explaining that Congress cannot exercise its postal power in a manner that violates the First Amendment).\n\n• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980) (stating that false and misleading commercial speech is not protected by the First Amendment).","rule":"Congress has broad power to establish post offices and post roads, including regulating and restricting what may be sent by mail. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the First Amendment free speech clause by restricting unprotected speech OR commercial speech that is false or misleading or concerns unlawful activity.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to find the statute constitutional?","rule":"Congress has broad power to establish post offices and post roads, including regulating and restricting what may be sent by mail. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the First Amendment free speech clause by restricting unprotected speech OR commercial speech that is false or misleading or concerns unlawful activity.","analysis":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. As a result, Congress may regulate and restrict what is sent through the mail to carry out its enumerated power to establish post offices and post roads (as seen here). However, Congress must comply with other constitutional provisions when exercising this power, including the First Amendment free speech clause. Under the free speech clause, Congress may freely regulate and restrict materials containing (1) unprotected speech or (2) commercial speech that is false or misleading or concerns unlawful activity. Here, the man's letters are false commercial speech because they falsely claimed that the individuals were delinquent on their state tax payments and needed to pay money to avoid criminal prosecution. As a result, the letters are not protected by the First Amendment, and the court will likely find the statute constitutional (Choice B). (Choice A) Congress has the power to regulate mail sent interstate (ie, between two or more states) as well as mail sent intrastate (ie, within a state). (Choice C) Congress has the power to spend for the general welfare, but this power does not apply here since the federal statute is not a spending measure. Additionally, the statute need not directly advance or be narrowly tailored to achieve a substantial government interest. This intermediate level of First Amendment scrutiny only applies to commercial speech that is true, is not misleading, and concerns a lawful activity (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because Congress has the power to regulate the mail, and the letters are not constitutionally protected speech."},"questionZh":"一名男子向居住在该州的个人邮寄了数千封信件，谎称这些人拖欠州税。信件进一步指出，这些人需要向所列地址发送一定金额的资金，以避免刑事起诉。其中一位收到这封信的人联系了联邦机构。经过调查后，联邦政府指控该男子违反了禁止邮寄误导性或虚假材料以获取金钱的联邦法规。在审判中，该男子辩称国会无权颁布联邦法规，并且该法规侵犯了他受第一修正案保护的言论自由权。法院可能认为该法规符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为尽管国会可以监管州际发送的邮件，但它没有权力监管州内发送的邮件。","B":"不可以，因为该法规侵犯了男子的第一修正案权利。","C":"是的，因为国会有权提供普遍福利，并且该法规直接推进并狭隘地定制以实现重大的政府利益。","D":"是的，因为国会有权监管邮件，而信件不属于受宪法保护的言论。"},"explanationZh":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。因此，国会可以规范和限制通过邮件发送的内容，以履行其建立邮局和邮路的列举权力（如此处所示）。然而，国会在行使这一权力时必须遵守其他宪法条款，包括第一修正案的言论自由条款。根据言论自由条款，国会可以自由监管和限制包含 (1) 不受保护的言论或 (2) 虚假或误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论的材料。在这里，该男子的信件是虚假的商业演讲，因为他们错误地声称这些人拖欠州税，需要付款以避免刑事起诉。因此，这些信件不受第一修正案的保护，法院可能会认为该法规符合宪法（选择 B）。 （选择 A）国会有权监管州际（即两个或多个州之间）发送的邮件以及州内（即一个州内）发送的邮件。 （选项C）国会有权为公共福利支出，但此权力不适用于此处，因为联邦法规不是一项支出措施。此外，该法规不需要直接推进或狭隘地调整以实现重大的政府利益。第一修正案的这种中间级别的审查仅适用于真实的、不具误导性的、涉及合法活动的商业言论（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有建立邮局和邮路的广泛权力，包括规范和限制邮件发送的内容。在行使这一权力时，国会不会通过限制不受保护的言论或虚假或误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论来违反第一修正案的言论自由条款。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 7（设立邮局的权力）。\n\n• 美国邮政服务。 v. Council of Greenburgh Civic Ass'ns, 453 U.S. 114, 126 (1981)（解释说国会不能以违反第一修正案的方式行使邮政权）。\n\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980)（指出虚假和误导性商业言论不受第一修正案保护）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会拥有建立邮局和邮路的广泛权力，包括监管和限制邮件发送的内容。在行使这一权力时，国会不会通过限制不受保护的言论或虚假或误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论来违反第一修正案的言论自由条款。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能认为该法规符合宪法吗？","rule":"国会拥有建立邮局和邮路的广泛权力，包括监管和限制邮件发送的内容。在行使这一权力时，国会不会通过限制不受保护的言论或虚假或误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论来违反第一修正案的言论自由条款。","analysis":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。因此，国会可以规范和限制通过邮件发送的内容，以履行其建立邮局和邮路的列举权力（如此处所示）。然而，国会在行使这一权力时必须遵守其他宪法条款，包括第一修正案的言论自由条款。根据言论自由条款，国会可以自由监管和限制包含 (1) 不受保护的言论或 (2) 虚假或误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论的材料。在这里，该男子的信件是虚假的商业演讲，因为他们错误地声称这些人拖欠州税，需要付款以避免刑事起诉。因此，这些信件不受第一修正案的保护，法院可能会认为该法规符合宪法（选择 B）。 （选择 A）国会有权监管州际（即两个或多个州之间）发送的邮件以及州内（即一个州内）发送的邮件。 （选项C）国会有权为公共福利支出，但此权力不适用于此处，因为联邦法规不是一项支出措施。此外，该法规不需要直接推进或狭隘地调整以实现重大的政府利益。第一修正案的这种中间级别的审查仅适用于真实的、不具误导性的、涉及合法活动的商业言论（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有建立邮局和邮路的广泛权力，包括规范和限制邮件发送的内容。在行使这一权力时，国会不会通过限制不受保护的言论或虚假或误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论来违反第一修正案的言论自由条款。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 7（设立邮局的权力）。\n\n• 美国邮政服务。 v. Council of Greenburgh Civic Ass'ns, 453 U.S. 114, 126 (1981)（解释说国会不能以违反第一修正案的方式行使邮政权）。\n\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980)（指出虚假和误导性商业言论不受第一修正案保护）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为国会有权监管邮件，而信件不属于受宪法保护的言论。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"commercial speech","ipa":"/kʌmˈɝʃəl spˈitʃ/","syllables":"com·mer·cial speech","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"商业言论：主要提出商业交易或与商业活动有关的表达","contextualMeaning":"商业言论：主要提出商业交易或与商业活动有关的表达","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the First Amendment free speech clause by restricting unprotected speech OR commercial speech that is false or misleading or concerns unlawful activity.","synonyms":["commercial language","commercial lecture","commercial talking to"]},{"term":"delinquent","ipa":"/dɪlˈɪŋkwənt/","syllables":"delin·quent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 行为不良的人, 违法者；a. 怠忽的, 有过失的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 行为不良的人, 违法者；a. 怠忽的, 有过失的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:delinquents","sourceSentence":"A man mailed thousands of letters to individuals residing in his state that falsely claimed that the individuals were delinquent on their state tax payments.","synonyms":["juvenile delinquent","overdue","derelict","neglectful"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-6b7a858f57441c17"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":69,"sourceQuestionPage":206,"sourceQuestionPages":[206],"sourceExplanationPages":[207,208],"sourceAnswerPages":[207,208],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-070","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Taxing & Spending Powers","question":"Congress enacted a statute that authorized the construction of a monument commemorating the role of the United States in liberating a particular foreign nation during World War II. Another statute appropriated $3 million for the construction. When the United States became involved in a bitter trade dispute with the foreign nation, the President announced that he was canceling the monument's construction and that he would not spend the appropriated funds. Although the actual reason for the President's decision was the trade dispute, the announcement stated that the reason was an unexpected rise in the federal deficit. Assume that no other statutes apply. Is the President's decision constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the President failed to invoke his foreign affairs powers in his announcement.","B":"No, because the President is obligated to spend funds in accordance with congressional directions.","C":"Yes, because the President is vested with inherent executive power to control federal expenditures.","D":"Yes, because the President's decision is a valid exercise of his foreign affairs powers."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Congress's taxing & spending powers\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, sec. 8, cl. 1)\n\nTaxing\n• Direct taxes – must be: – apportioned evenly among states and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Indirect taxes – must be: – uniformly applied in every state and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Export taxes – always unconstitutional Spending\n• Funds must be spent for general welfare\n• Conditions on receipt of funds must: – be clearly stated & unambiguous – be reasonably related to federal interest in funded program – not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity and – not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress very broad spending power, which it exercises by proposing appropriations bills. Once an appropriations bill becomes law, the President must spend the appropriated funds as specifically directed in the law. That is because Article II obligates the President to take care that the laws are faithfully executed. Therefore, the President's decision not to spend the appropriated funds for the monument's construction is unconstitutional. (Choices A & D) The President can never refuse to spend funds that are subject to a mandatory appropriation—even where foreign affairs are directly involved. (Choice C) The Constitution does not vest the President with inherent executive power to control federal expenditures. Instead, the President must spend federal funds in accordance with congressional directives.\n\nEducational objective: An appropriations statute is a valid exercise of Congress's spending power. And the President must take care that the law is faithfully executed by spending the appropriated funds as specifically directed in the statute.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 3 (take care clause).\n\n• Train v. New York, 420 U.S. 35, 41 (1975) (requiring the EPA Administrator to spend the full amount authorized by the valid federal statute despite the President's order to withhold such funds).\n\n• 63C Am. Jur. 2d Public Funds § 37 (2019) (explaining that the President and the executive branch do not have the power to impound congressionally appropriated funds).","educationalObjective":"An appropriations statute is a valid exercise of Congress's spending power. And the President must take care that the law is faithfully executed by spending the appropriated funds as specifically directed in the statute.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. II, § 3 (take care clause).\n\n• Train v. New York, 420 U.S. 35, 41 (1975) (requiring the EPA Administrator to spend the full amount authorized by the valid federal statute despite the President's order to withhold such funds).\n\n• 63C Am. Jur. 2d Public Funds § 37 (2019) (explaining that the President and the executive branch do not have the power to impound congressionally appropriated funds).","rule":"An appropriations statute is a valid exercise of Congress's spending power. And the President must take care that the law is faithfully executed by spending the appropriated funds as specifically directed in the statute.","irac":{"issue":"Is the President's decision constitutional?","rule":"An appropriations statute is a valid exercise of Congress's spending power. And the President must take care that the law is faithfully executed by spending the appropriated funds as specifically directed in the statute.","analysis":"Congress's taxing & spending powers\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, sec. 8, cl. 1)\n\nTaxing\n• Direct taxes – must be: – apportioned evenly among states and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Indirect taxes – must be: – uniformly applied in every state and – reasonably related to revenue raising\n• Export taxes – always unconstitutional Spending\n• Funds must be spent for general welfare\n• Conditions on receipt of funds must: – be clearly stated & unambiguous – be reasonably related to federal interest in funded program – not require states to engage in unconstitutional activity and – not unduly coerce states into accepting The taxing and spending clause gives Congress very broad spending power, which it exercises by proposing appropriations bills. Once an appropriations bill becomes law, the President must spend the appropriated funds as specifically directed in the law. That is because Article II obligates the President to take care that the laws are faithfully executed. Therefore, the President's decision not to spend the appropriated funds for the monument's construction is unconstitutional. (Choices A & D) The President can never refuse to spend funds that are subject to a mandatory appropriation—even where foreign affairs are directly involved. (Choice C) The Constitution does not vest the President with inherent executive power to control federal expenditures. Instead, the President must spend federal funds in accordance with congressional directives.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the President is obligated to spend funds in accordance with congressional directions."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，授权建造一座纪念碑，以纪念美国在第二次世界大战期间解放特定外国的作用。另一项法规拨款 300 万美元用于建设。当美国卷入与外国的激烈贸易争端时，总统宣布取消纪念碑的建设，并且不会使用拨款。尽管总统做出这一决定的实际原因是贸易争端，但公告称，原因是联邦赤字意外上升。假设没有其他法规适用。总统的决定是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为总统在他的声明中没有援引他的外交权力。","B":"不，因为总统有义务按照国会的指示支出资金。","C":"是的，因为总统拥有控制联邦支出的固有行政权力。","D":"是的，因为总统的决定是他有效行使外交权力。"},"explanationZh":"国会的征税和支出权力\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条第 1 条）\n\n征税\n• 直接税 – 必须： – 在各州之间平均分配，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 间接税 – 必须： – 在每个州统一适用，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 出口税——总是违宪支出\n• 资金必须用于公共福利\n• 收到资金的条件必须： – 明确规定且不含糊 – 与资助项目中的联邦利益合理相关 – 不要求各州参与违宪活动以及 – 不过度强迫各州接受 征税和支出条款赋予国会非常广泛的支出权力，国会通过提出拨款法案来行使这一权力。一旦拨款法案成为法律，总统必须按照法律的具体指示使用拨款。这是因为第二条规定总统有义务确保法律得到忠实执行。因此，总统决定不将拨款用于纪念碑的建设是违宪的。 （选择 A 和 D）总统永远不能拒绝使用强制拨款的资金——即使直接涉及外交事务。 （选项C） 宪法没有赋予总统控制联邦支出的固有行政权力。相反，总统必须根据国会指令使用联邦资金。\n\n教育目标：拨款法规是国会支出权的有效行使。总统必须按照法规的具体指示使用拨款，确保法律得到忠实执行。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 3（照顾条款）。\n\n• Train v. New York, 420 U.S. 35, 41 (1975)（要求 EPA 署长花费有效联邦法规授权的全额资金，尽管总统下令扣留此类资金）。\n\n• 63C 上午。尤尔。 2d 公共基金 § 37 (2019)（解释说总统和行政部门无权扣押国会拨款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"拨款法规是国会支出权的有效行使。总统必须按照法规的具体指示使用拨款，确保法律得到忠实执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统的决定是否符合宪法？","rule":"拨款法规是国会支出权的有效行使。总统必须按照法规的具体指示使用拨款，确保法律得到忠实执行。","analysis":"国会的征税和支出权力\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条第 1 条）\n\n征税\n• 直接税 – 必须： – 在各州之间平均分配，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 间接税 – 必须： – 在每个州统一适用，并且 – 与收入增加合理相关\n• 出口税——总是违宪支出\n• 资金必须用于公共福利\n• 收到资金的条件必须： – 明确规定且不含糊 – 与资助项目中的联邦利益合理相关 – 不要求各州参与违宪活动以及 – 不过度强迫各州接受 征税和支出条款赋予国会非常广泛的支出权力，国会通过提出拨款法案来行使这一权力。一旦拨款法案成为法律，总统必须按照法律的具体指示使用拨款。这是因为第二条规定总统有义务确保法律得到忠实执行。因此，总统决定不将拨款用于纪念碑的建设是违宪的。 （选择 A 和 D）总统永远不能拒绝使用强制拨款的资金——即使直接涉及外交事务。 （选项C） 宪法没有赋予总统控制联邦支出的固有行政权力。相反，总统必须根据国会指令使用联邦资金。\n\n教育目标：拨款法规是国会支出权的有效行使。总统必须按照法规的具体指示使用拨款，确保法律得到忠实执行。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 3（照顾条款）。\n\n• Train v. New York, 420 U.S. 35, 41 (1975)（要求 EPA 署长花费有效联邦法规授权的全额资金，尽管总统下令扣留此类资金）。\n\n• 63C 上午。尤尔。 2d 公共基金 § 37 (2019)（解释说总统和行政部门无权扣押国会拨款）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为总统有义务按照国会的指示支出资金。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"expenditures","ipa":"/ɪkspˈɛndətʃɝz/","syllables":"ex·pen·di·tures","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 花费( expenditure的复数形式 )； 使用； （尤指金钱的）支出额","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 花费( expenditure的复数形式 )； 使用； （尤指金钱的）支出额","wordForm":"名词；原形 expenditure；词形 0:expenditure/1:s","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the President is vested with inherent executive power to control federal expenditures.","synonyms":["consumption","using up","expending","outgo"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":70,"sourceQuestionPage":209,"sourceQuestionPages":[209],"sourceExplanationPages":[210,211],"sourceAnswerPages":[210,211],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-071","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Appointments & Removal","question":"A recently enacted federal statute requires the President to make each appointment of a United States ambassador to a foreign country from a list of three individuals. The list is to be compiled by the Senate Foreign Relations Committee and approved by the full Senate in advance of the appointment. The statute also provides that Senate confirmation of the appointment is deemed to occur automatically 30 days after the time the President names an appointee from the list, unless the full Senate determines otherwise within the 30-day period. Is this statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the statute impermissibly restricts the plenary foreign affairs powers of the President.","B":"No, because the statute violates the constitutional requirements for appointment of ambassadors of the United States.","C":"Yes, because the statute is a necessary and proper measure in furtherance of Congress's power to regulate commerce with foreign states.","D":"Yes, because the statute is consistent with the constitutional requirement that the presidential appointment of ambassadors be with the advice and consent of the Senate."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The appointments clause grants the President the power to appoint ambassadors, Supreme Court justices, and other principal federal officers with the Senate's advice and consent. This clause gives the President plenary (ie, exclusive) nomination power that cannot be limited by Congress. Therefore, the statute unconstitutionally infringes upon that power by limiting the President's ability to select ambassadorial nominees to a list compiled by a Senate committee. This statute also provides for automatic Senate confirmation of any individual who had been named on the list of nominees approved by the Senate before the appointment. But Senate confirmation should only occur after a nomination, when the Senate gives advice (eg, by holding hearings) and then chooses to consent to the nomination by majority vote. Therefore, this statute also violates the majority-vote requirement (Choice D). (Choice A) The President does not have plenary power over foreign affairs since Congress also possesses powers in this area (eg, declare war, regulate foreign commerce). (Choice C) The necessary and proper clause (article I, section 8) grants Congress the power to employ reasonably appropriate measures to carry out its other powers—eg, regulating commerce with foreign nations. But Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising this power (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The appointments clause gives the President the power to appoint ambassadors, Supreme Court justices, and other principal federal officers with the advice and consent of the Senate. Congress cannot limit the President's plenary power to select nominees, and the Senate can only choose to consent to the nomination by majority vote.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2, cl. 2 (appointments clause).\n\n• Edmond v. United States, 520 U.S. 651, 659 (1997) (stating that principal officers, including ambassadors, must be nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate).","educationalObjective":"The appointments clause gives the President the power to appoint ambassadors, Supreme Court justices, and other principal federal officers with the advice and consent of the Senate. Congress cannot limit the President's plenary power to select nominees, and the Senate can only choose to consent to the nomination by majority vote.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2, cl. 2 (appointments clause).\n\n• Edmond v. United States, 520 U.S. 651, 659 (1997) (stating that principal officers, including ambassadors, must be nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate).","rule":"The appointments clause gives the President the power to appoint ambassadors, Supreme Court justices, and other principal federal officers with the advice and consent of the Senate. Congress cannot limit the President's plenary power to select nominees, and the Senate can only choose to consent to the nomination by majority vote.","irac":{"issue":"Is this statute constitutional?","rule":"The appointments clause gives the President the power to appoint ambassadors, Supreme Court justices, and other principal federal officers with the advice and consent of the Senate. Congress cannot limit the President's plenary power to select nominees, and the Senate can only choose to consent to the nomination by majority vote.","analysis":"The appointments clause grants the President the power to appoint ambassadors, Supreme Court justices, and other principal federal officers with the Senate's advice and consent. This clause gives the President plenary (ie, exclusive) nomination power that cannot be limited by Congress. Therefore, the statute unconstitutionally infringes upon that power by limiting the President's ability to select ambassadorial nominees to a list compiled by a Senate committee. This statute also provides for automatic Senate confirmation of any individual who had been named on the list of nominees approved by the Senate before the appointment. But Senate confirmation should only occur after a nomination, when the Senate gives advice (eg, by holding hearings) and then chooses to consent to the nomination by majority vote. Therefore, this statute also violates the majority-vote requirement (Choice D). (Choice A) The President does not have plenary power over foreign affairs since Congress also possesses powers in this area (eg, declare war, regulate foreign commerce). (Choice C) The necessary and proper clause (article I, section 8) grants Congress the power to employ reasonably appropriate measures to carry out its other powers—eg, regulating commerce with foreign nations. But Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising this power (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the statute violates the constitutional requirements for appointment of ambassadors of the United States."},"questionZh":"最近颁布的一项联邦法规要求总统从三人名单中任命一名美国驻外国大使。该名单将由参议院外交关系委员会编制，并在任命前得到参议院全体会议的批准。该法规还规定，参议院对任命的确认被视为在总统从名单中指定任命人后 30 天自动发生，除非参议院全体会议在 30 天内另有决定。该法规是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该法规不允许地限制总统的全体外交权力。","B":"不可以，因为该法规违反了任命美国大使的宪法要求。","C":"是的，因为该法规是加强国会监管与外国贸易的权力的必要且适当的措施。","D":"是的，因为该法规符合宪法要求总统任命大使须经参议院建议和同意。"},"explanationZh":"任命条款赋予总统在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命大使、最高法院法官和其他主要联邦官员的权力。该条款赋予总统全权（即排他性）提名权，不受国会限制。因此，该法规限制了总统根据参议院委员会编制的名单选择大使提名人的能力，从而违宪地侵犯了这一权力。该法规还规定参议院自动确认任命前参议院批准的提名人名单上的任何个人。但参议院的确认只能在提名之后进行，参议院给出建议（例如通过举行听证会），然后选择以多数票同意提名。因此，该法规也违反了多数票要求（选项D）。 （选项A） 总统对外交事务没有全权，因为国会也拥有该领域的权力（例如宣战、管理对外贸易）。 （选项C）必要且适当的条款（第一条第8节）赋予国会采取合理适当措施来履行其其他权力的权力——例如，规范与外国的贸易。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法条款（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：任命条款赋予总统在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命大使、最高法院法官和其他主要联邦官员的权力。国会不能限制总统全会选择提名人的权力，参议院只能选择以多数票同意提名。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2，cl。 2（任命条款）。\n\n• Edmond v. United States, 520 U.S. 651, 659 (1997)（规定主要官员，包括大使，必须由总统提名并经参议院确认）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"任命条款赋予总统在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命大使、最高法院法官和其他主要联邦官员的权力。国会不能限制总统全会选择提名人的权力，参议院只能选择以多数票同意提名。","iracZh":{"issue":"该法规是否符合宪法？","rule":"任命条款赋予总统在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命大使、最高法院法官和其他主要联邦官员的权力。国会不能限制总统全会选择提名人的权力，参议院只能选择以多数票同意提名。","analysis":"任命条款赋予总统在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命大使、最高法院法官和其他主要联邦官员的权力。该条款赋予总统全权（即排他性）提名权，不受国会限制。因此，该法规限制了总统根据参议院委员会编制的名单选择大使提名人的能力，从而违宪地侵犯了这一权力。该法规还规定参议院自动确认任命前参议院批准的提名人名单上的任何个人。但参议院的确认只能在提名之后进行，参议院给出建议（例如通过举行听证会），然后选择以多数票同意提名。因此，该法规也违反了多数票要求（选项D）。 （选项A） 总统对外交事务没有全权，因为国会也拥有该领域的权力（例如宣战、管理对外贸易）。 （选项C）必要且适当的条款（第一条第8节）赋予国会采取合理适当措施来履行其其他权力的权力——例如，规范与外国的贸易。但国会在行使这一权力时不能违反其他宪法条款（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：任命条款赋予总统在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命大使、最高法院法官和其他主要联邦官员的权力。国会不能限制总统全会选择提名人的权力，参议院只能选择以多数票同意提名。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2，cl。 2（任命条款）。\n\n• Edmond v. United States, 520 U.S. 651, 659 (1997)（规定主要官员，包括大使，必须由总统提名并经参议院确认）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为该法规违反了任命美国大使的宪法要求。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"nomination","ipa":"/nˌɑmʌnˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"nom·i·na·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 提名, 任命, 提名权","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 提名, 任命, 提名权","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:nominations","sourceSentence":"Congress cannot limit the President's plenary power to select nominees, and the Senate can only choose to consent to the nomination by majority vote.","synonyms":["nominating speech","nominating address"]},{"term":"appointee","ipa":"/ʌpɔɪntˈi/","syllables":"ap·pointee","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 被任命者；[法] 被指定的人, 被任命者","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 被任命者；[法] 被指定的人, 被任命者","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:appointees","sourceSentence":"The statute also provides that Senate confirmation of the appointment is deemed to occur automatically 30 days after the time the President names an appointee from the list, unless the full Senate determines otherwise within the 30-day period.","synonyms":["appointment"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-98ef24aeaef044be"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":71,"sourceQuestionPage":212,"sourceQuestionPages":[212],"sourceExplanationPages":[213,214],"sourceAnswerPages":[213,214],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-072","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Delegation & Legislative Veto","question":"Congress passed a statute directing the United States Forest Service, a federal agency, to issue regulations to control campfires on federal public lands and to establish a schedule of penalties for those who violate the new regulations. The statute provides that the Forest Service regulations should \"reduce, to the maximum extent feasible, all potential hazards that arise from campfires on Forest Service lands.\" The Forest Service issued the regulations and the schedule of penalties directed by Congress. The regulations include a rule that provides for the doubling of the fine for any negligent or prohibited use of fire if the user is under the influence of alcohol or drugs. Which of the following arguments best supports a finding that the rule providing for the fines is constitutional?","choices":{"A":"The executive branch of government, of which the Forest Service is part, has inherent rule-making authority over public lands.","B":"The rule is justified by a compelling government interest in safeguarding forest resources.","C":"The rule relates directly to law enforcement, which is an executive rather than a legislative function, and hence it does not need specific congressional authorization.","D":"The rule was issued pursuant to a valid exercise of Congress's power to delegate rule- making authority to federal agencies."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The nondelegation doctrine prohibits Congress from delegating its exclusive legislative powers (eg, making or repealing laws). But Congress can delegate its incidentalpowers (eg, rule-making authority) if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining:\n• the policy Congress seeks to advance (eg, reduce campfire hazards on Forest Service lands)\n• the agency to carry out that policy (eg, the United States Forest Service) and\n• the scope of that agency's authority (eg, issue campfire regulations and establish a penalty schedule). Since Congress properly delegated its rule-making authority to the United States Forest Service, its rule doubling the fine for a fire if the user is under the influence of alcohol or drugs is likely constitutional. (Choice A) The Article IV property clause gives Congress (not the executive branch) inherent rule-making authority over federal lands. But Congress can delegate this incidental power to executive agencies. (Choice B) The rule would only have to be justified by a compelling government interest— ie, satisfy strict scrutiny—if it substantially impacted a fundamental right or intentionally discriminated against a suspect class (not seen here). (Choice C) Although law enforcement is an executive function, it must be exercised pursuant to congressional authorization (ie, federal law).\n\nEducational objective: Congress can delegate incidental legislative powers to federal agencies if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining (1) the policy Congress seeks to advance, (2) the agency to carry out that policy, and (3) the scope of that agency's authority.\n\nReferences\n• Gundy v. United States, 139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019) (explaining that the nondelegation doctrine allows Congress to delegate power as long as it provides an intelligible principle to guide the delegate's use of discretion).","educationalObjective":"Congress can delegate incidental legislative powers to federal agencies if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining (1) the policy Congress seeks to advance, (2) the agency to carry out that policy, and (3) the scope of that agency's authority.","references":"• Gundy v. United States, 139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019) (explaining that the nondelegation doctrine allows Congress to delegate power as long as it provides an intelligible principle to guide the delegate's use of discretion).","rule":"Congress can delegate incidental legislative powers to federal agencies if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining (1) the policy Congress seeks to advance, (2) the agency to carry out that policy, and (3) the scope of that agency's authority.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments best supports a finding that the rule providing for the fines is constitutional?","rule":"Congress can delegate incidental legislative powers to federal agencies if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining (1) the policy Congress seeks to advance, (2) the agency to carry out that policy, and (3) the scope of that agency's authority.","analysis":"The nondelegation doctrine prohibits Congress from delegating its exclusive legislative powers (eg, making or repealing laws). But Congress can delegate its incidentalpowers (eg, rule-making authority) if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining:\n• the policy Congress seeks to advance (eg, reduce campfire hazards on Forest Service lands)\n• the agency to carry out that policy (eg, the United States Forest Service) and\n• the scope of that agency's authority (eg, issue campfire regulations and establish a penalty schedule). Since Congress properly delegated its rule-making authority to the United States Forest Service, its rule doubling the fine for a fire if the user is under the influence of alcohol or drugs is likely constitutional. (Choice A) The Article IV property clause gives Congress (not the executive branch) inherent rule-making authority over federal lands. But Congress can delegate this incidental power to executive agencies. (Choice B) The rule would only have to be justified by a compelling government interest— ie, satisfy strict scrutiny—if it substantially impacted a fundamental right or intentionally discriminated against a suspect class (not seen here). (Choice C) Although law enforcement is an executive function, it must be exercised pursuant to congressional authorization (ie, federal law).","conclusion":"Answer D: The rule was issued pursuant to a valid exercise of Congress's power to delegate rule- making authority to federal agencies."},"questionZh":"国会通过了一项法规，指示联邦机构美国林务局发布法规来控制联邦公共土地上的篝火，并制定对违反新法规的人的处罚表。该法规规定，林务局法规应“在可行的最大范围内减少林务局土地上篝火引起的所有潜在危害”。林务局发布了国会指示的法规和处罚表。该法规包括一项规定，如果使用者在酒精或毒品的影响下，对任何疏忽或禁止使用火的行为处以双倍罚款。以下哪项论据最能支持罚款规定符合宪法的裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"林务局所属的政府行政部门对公共土地拥有固有的规则制定权。","B":"该规则的合理性在于政府对保护森林资源的强烈兴趣。","C":"该规则与执法直接相关，属于行政职能而非立法职能，因此不需要国会的具体授权。","D":"该规则是根据国会有效行使将规则制定权委托给联邦机构的权力而发布的。"},"explanationZh":"不授权原则禁止国会授权其专有立法权（例如制定或废除法律）。但如果国会提供了一项易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义：\n• 国会寻求推进的政策（例如，减少林务局土地上的篝火危害）\n• 执行该政策的机构（例如美国林务局）以及\n• 该机构的权力范围（例如，发布篝火规则并制定处罚表）。由于国会将其规则制定权适当地委托给了美国林务局，因此如果用户受到酒精或毒品的影响，其对火灾的罚款加倍的规定可能符合宪法。 （选项 A）第四条财产条款赋予国会（而非行政部门）对联邦土地固有的规则制定权。但国会可以将这种附带权力委托给行政机构。 （选择B）如果该规则对基本权利产生重大影响或故意歧视可疑阶层（此处未见），则该规则只需以令人信服的政府利益为依据，即满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即可证明其合理性。 （选项C） 虽然执法是行政职能，但必须根据国会授权（即联邦法律）行使。\n\n教育目标：国会可以将附带的立法权委托给联邦机构，前提是它提供了一项易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义（1）国会寻求推进的政策，（2）执行该政策的机构，以及（3）该机构的权力范围。\n\n参考文献\n• Gundy 诉美国，139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019)（解释说，非授权原则允许国会授权，只要它提供了一个易于理解的原则来指导代表使用自由裁量权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会可以将附带的立法权委托给联邦机构，前提是它提供了一个易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义（1）国会寻求推进的政策，（2）执行该政策的机构，以及（3）该机构的权力范围。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项论据最能支持罚款规定符合宪法的裁决？","rule":"国会可以将附带的立法权委托给联邦机构，前提是它提供了一个易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义（1）国会寻求推进的政策，（2）执行该政策的机构，以及（3）该机构的权力范围。","analysis":"不授权原则禁止国会授权其专有立法权（例如制定或废除法律）。但如果国会提供了一项易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义：\n• 国会寻求推进的政策（例如，减少林务局土地上的篝火危害）\n• 执行该政策的机构（例如美国林务局）以及\n• 该机构的权力范围（例如，发布篝火规则并制定处罚表）。由于国会将其规则制定权适当地委托给了美国林务局，因此如果用户受到酒精或毒品的影响，其对火灾的罚款加倍的规定可能符合宪法。 （选项 A）第四条财产条款赋予国会（而非行政部门）对联邦土地固有的规则制定权。但国会可以将这种附带权力委托给行政机构。 （选择B）如果该规则对基本权利产生重大影响或故意歧视可疑阶层（此处未见），则该规则只需以令人信服的政府利益为依据，即满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即可证明其合理性。 （选项C） 虽然执法是行政职能，但必须根据国会授权（即联邦法律）行使。\n\n教育目标：国会可以将附带的立法权委托给联邦机构，前提是它提供了一项易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义（1）国会寻求推进的政策，（2）执行该政策的机构，以及（3）该机构的权力范围。\n\n参考文献\n• Gundy 诉美国，139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019)（解释说，非授权原则允许国会授权，只要它提供了一个易于理解的原则来指导代表使用自由裁量权）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该规则是根据国会有效行使将规则制定权委托给联邦机构的权力而发布的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"fine","ipa":"/fˈaɪn/","syllables":"fine","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 罚款, 罚金, 晴天, 精细；a. 好的, 晴朗的, 健康的, 细小的, 精细的；vt. 罚款, 精炼, 澄清","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 罚款, 罚金, 晴天, 精细；a. 好的, 晴朗的, 健康的, 细小的, 精细的；vt. 罚款, 精炼, 澄清","wordForm":"名词；词形 t:finest/d:fined/s:fines/r:finer/p:fined/3:fines/i:fining","sourceSentence":"The regulations include a rule that provides for the doubling of the fine for any negligent or prohibited use of fire if the user is under the influence of alcohol or drugs.","synonyms":["mulct","amercement","ticket","all right"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-36d518c98d17df68"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":72,"sourceQuestionPage":215,"sourceQuestionPages":[215],"sourceExplanationPages":[216,217],"sourceAnswerPages":[216,217],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-073","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"The Federal Drone Safety Act establishes the Federal Drone Safety Commission, authorizes the Commission to issue licenses for the sale of unmanned aerial drones on terms that are consistent with the purposes of the Act, and makes the unlicensed sale of a drone a crime. The provisions of the Federal Drone Safety Act are inseverable. A hobby store owner applied to the Federal Drone Safety Commission for a license to sell drones. The Commission held, and the store owner participated in, a trial-type proceeding on his license application. In that proceeding it was demonstrated that the store owner had repeatedly and intentionally sold drones without a license. As a result, the Commission denied the store owner's application for a license. The license denial was based on a Commission rule authorized by the Drone Safety Act that prohibited the issuance of drone licenses to persons who had engaged in such conduct. Nevertheless, the store owner continued to sell drones in his store. The store owner was subsequently convicted of the crime of unlicensed sale of a drone. On appeal, he challenges the constitutionality of the Federal Drone Safety Act. What will be the most likely holding of the reviewing court?","choices":{"A":"The Act is constitutional, because Congress may use the authority vested in it by the commerce clause to regulate the sale of drones and the provisions of the Act do not violate any prohibitory provision of the Constitution.","B":"The Act is constitutional, because the Constitution generally authorizes Congress to enact all laws that are necessary and proper to advance the general welfare, and Congress could reasonably believe that the sale of drones by people like the store owner constitutes a threat to the general welfare.","C":"The Act is unconstitutional, because Congress may not impose a criminal penalty on action that is improper only because it is inconsistent with an agency rule.","D":"The Act is unconstitutional, because the mere sale of a drone is a wholly local matter that is beyond the regulatory authority of Congress."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad authority to regulate interstate commerce. This power even extends to in-state activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce. A substantial effect is presumed when the activity is economic in nature. So, though drone sales are wholly local, it is presumed that this economic activity substantially affects interstate commerce (Choice D). Therefore, Congress can regulate these sales so long as it complies with prohibitory constitutional provisions. One such provision is Article I, section 1 and its implied nondelegation doctrine. This doctrine prohibits Congress from delegating purely legislative powers. But it allows Congress to delegate its incidental powers (eg, rulemaking, legislative enforcement) if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining:\n• the policy Congress seeks to advance (eg, drone safety)\n• the agency to carry out that policy (eg, the Federal Drone Safety Commission) and\n• the scope of that agency's authority (eg, issue licenses on terms consistent with the Act's purpose). Since Congress properly delegated its regulatory authority to the Federal Drone Safety Commission—and there is no evidence that the Act violates any other prohibitory provision of the Constitution—the reviewing court will likely hold that the Act is constitutional. (Choice B) Article I authorizes Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare and to enact legislation necessary and proper to exercise this enumerated power. The Act here seeks to regulate drone sales—not tax or spend—for the general welfare. And since the commerce clause gives Congress direct authority to regulate this economic activity, its powers under the necessary and proper clause are unnecessary here. (Choice C) The unlicensed sale of a drone is criminalized by the Act itself—not an agency rule. And Congress could impose a criminal penalty on an action that is inconsistent with an agency rule so long as the rule was created within the scope of the agency's delegated authority.\n\nEducational objective: Congress has broad authority to regulate economic activities since it is presumed that they substantially affect interstate commerce. But Congress can only delegate its incidental powers to another entity if it gives that entity an intelligible principle (policy, agency, scope) on which to base its regulations.\n\nReferences\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005) (holding that Congress can regulate purely local activities that are part of an economic class of activities that substantially affect interstate commerce).\n\n• Gundy v. United States, 139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019) (explaining that the nondelegation doctrine allows Congress to delegate power as long as it provides an intelligible principle to guide the delegee's use of discretion).","educationalObjective":"Congress has broad authority to regulate economic activities since it is presumed that they substantially affect interstate commerce. But Congress can only delegate its incidental powers to another entity if it gives that entity an intelligible principle (policy, agency, scope) on which to base its regulations.","references":"• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005) (holding that Congress can regulate purely local activities that are part of an economic class of activities that substantially affect interstate commerce).\n\n• Gundy v. United States, 139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019) (explaining that the nondelegation doctrine allows Congress to delegate power as long as it provides an intelligible principle to guide the delegee's use of discretion).","rule":"Congress has broad authority to regulate economic activities since it is presumed that they substantially affect interstate commerce. But Congress can only delegate its incidental powers to another entity if it gives that entity an intelligible principle (policy, agency, scope) on which to base its regulations.","irac":{"issue":"What will be the most likely holding of the reviewing court?","rule":"Congress has broad authority to regulate economic activities since it is presumed that they substantially affect interstate commerce. But Congress can only delegate its incidental powers to another entity if it gives that entity an intelligible principle (policy, agency, scope) on which to base its regulations.","analysis":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad authority to regulate interstate commerce. This power even extends to in-state activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce. A substantial effect is presumed when the activity is economic in nature. So, though drone sales are wholly local, it is presumed that this economic activity substantially affects interstate commerce (Choice D). Therefore, Congress can regulate these sales so long as it complies with prohibitory constitutional provisions. One such provision is Article I, section 1 and its implied nondelegation doctrine. This doctrine prohibits Congress from delegating purely legislative powers. But it allows Congress to delegate its incidental powers (eg, rulemaking, legislative enforcement) if it provides an intelligible principle—ie, a clear statement defining:\n• the policy Congress seeks to advance (eg, drone safety)\n• the agency to carry out that policy (eg, the Federal Drone Safety Commission) and\n• the scope of that agency's authority (eg, issue licenses on terms consistent with the Act's purpose). Since Congress properly delegated its regulatory authority to the Federal Drone Safety Commission—and there is no evidence that the Act violates any other prohibitory provision of the Constitution—the reviewing court will likely hold that the Act is constitutional. (Choice B) Article I authorizes Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare and to enact legislation necessary and proper to exercise this enumerated power. The Act here seeks to regulate drone sales—not tax or spend—for the general welfare. And since the commerce clause gives Congress direct authority to regulate this economic activity, its powers under the necessary and proper clause are unnecessary here. (Choice C) The unlicensed sale of a drone is criminalized by the Act itself—not an agency rule. And Congress could impose a criminal penalty on an action that is inconsistent with an agency rule so long as the rule was created within the scope of the agency's delegated authority.","conclusion":"Answer A: The Act is constitutional, because Congress may use the authority vested in it by the commerce clause to regulate the sale of drones and the provisions of the Act do not violate any prohibitory provision of the Constitution."},"questionZh":"《联邦无人机安全法》设立了联邦无人机安全委员会，授权该委员会按照符合该法宗旨的条款颁发销售无人机的许可证，并将未经许可销售无人机定为犯罪行为。 《联邦无人机安全法》的规定是不可分割的。一位业余爱好商店老板向联邦无人机安全委员会申请了销售无人机的许可证。委员会就其许可证申请进行了审判型程序，店主也参与了该程序。在该诉讼中，证明店主多次故意在没有许可证的情况下销售无人机。结果，委员会拒绝了店主的许可证申请。拒绝颁发许可证的依据是《无人机安全法》授权的委员会规则，该规则禁止向从事此类行为的人颁发无人机许可证。尽管如此，店主还是继续在店里销售无人机。该店老板随后被判无证销售无人机罪。在上诉中，他质疑《联邦无人机安全法》的合宪性。复审法院最有可能做出什么裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法案符合宪法，因为国会可以利用商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予它的权力来规范无人机的销售，并且该法案的条款不违反宪法的任何禁止性条款。","B":"该法案是符合宪法的，因为宪法一般授权国会颁布所有必要且适当的法律来促进公共福利，并且国会有理由相信像商店老板这样的人销售无人机对公共福利构成威胁。","C":"该法案违宪，因为国会不得仅因为不符合机构规则而对不当行为处以刑事处罚。","D":"该法案违宪，因为无人机的销售完全是地方事务，超出了国会的监管权限。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。这种权力甚至延伸到州内活动，这些活动无论是单独还是总体上都会对州际贸易产生重大影响。当该活动本质上是经济活动时，就会假定产生重大影响。因此，尽管无人机销售完全是本地的，但据推测这种经济活动会极大地影响州际贸易（选项 D）。因此，只要符合宪法禁止性规定，国会就可以对这些销售进行监管。其中一项规定是第一条第一节及其隐含的不授权原则。该学说禁止国会授予纯粹的立法权。但如果它提供了一个易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义：\n• 国会寻求推进的政策（例如，无人机安全）\n• 执行该政策的机构（例如联邦无人机安全委员会）以及\n• 该机构的权力范围（例如，按照符合该法案目的的条款颁发许可证）。由于国会正确地将其监管权委托给联邦无人机安全委员会，并且没有证据表明该法案违反了宪法的任何其他禁止性条款，因此审查法院可能会认为该法案符合宪法。 （选项B）第一条授权国会为公共福利征税和支出，并颁布必要且适当的立法来行使这一列举的权力。该法案旨在为了公共福利而监管无人机销售，而不是税收或支出。由于商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会直接权力来监管这种经济活动，因此其在必要和适当条款下的权力在这里是不必要的。 （选项 C）该法案本身将无证销售无人机定为犯罪行为，而不是机构规则。只要该规则是在该机构的授权范围内制定的，国会就可以对不符合该机构规则的行为处以刑事处罚。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有监管经济活动的广泛权力，因为人们认为经济活动对州际贸易有重大影响。但国会只能将其附带权力委托给另一个实体，前提是它给予该实体一个可理解的原则（政策、机构、范围）作为其监管的基础。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005)（认为国会可以监管纯粹的地方活动，这些活动是对州际贸易产生重大影响的经济活动类别的一部分）。\n\n• Gundy 诉美国，139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019)（解释说，非授权原则允许国会授权，只要它提供了一个易于理解的原则来指导被授权者使用自由裁量权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会拥有监管经济活动的广泛权力，因为人们认为经济活动对州际贸易有重大影响。但国会只能将其附带权力委托给另一个实体，前提是它给予该实体一个可理解的原则（政策、机构、范围）作为其监管的基础。","iracZh":{"issue":"复审法院最有可能做出什么裁决？","rule":"国会拥有监管经济活动的广泛权力，因为人们认为经济活动对州际贸易有重大影响。但国会只能将其附带权力委托给另一个实体，前提是它给予该实体一个可理解的原则（政策、机构、范围）作为其监管的基础。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。这种权力甚至延伸到州内活动，这些活动无论是单独还是总体上都会对州际贸易产生重大影响。当该活动本质上是经济活动时，就会假定产生重大影响。因此，尽管无人机销售完全是本地的，但据推测这种经济活动会极大地影响州际贸易（选项 D）。因此，只要符合宪法禁止性规定，国会就可以对这些销售进行监管。其中一项规定是第一条第一节及其隐含的不授权原则。该学说禁止国会授予纯粹的立法权。但如果它提供了一个易于理解的原则，即明确的声明，定义：\n• 国会寻求推进的政策（例如，无人机安全）\n• 执行该政策的机构（例如联邦无人机安全委员会）以及\n• 该机构的权力范围（例如，按照符合该法案目的的条款颁发许可证）。由于国会正确地将其监管权委托给联邦无人机安全委员会，并且没有证据表明该法案违反了宪法的任何其他禁止性条款，因此审查法院可能会认为该法案符合宪法。 （选项B）第一条授权国会为公共福利征税和支出，并颁布必要且适当的立法来行使这一列举的权力。该法案旨在为了公共福利而监管无人机销售，而不是税收或支出。由于商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会直接权力来监管这种经济活动，因此其在必要和适当条款下的权力在这里是不必要的。 （选项 C）该法案本身将无证销售无人机定为犯罪行为，而不是机构规则。只要该规则是在该机构的授权范围内制定的，国会就可以对不符合该机构规则的行为处以刑事处罚。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有监管经济活动的广泛权力，因为人们认为经济活动对州际贸易有重大影响。但国会只能将其附带权力委托给另一个实体，前提是它给予该实体一个可理解的原则（政策、机构、范围）作为其监管的基础。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005)（认为国会可以监管纯粹的地方活动，这些活动是对州际贸易产生重大影响的经济活动类别的一部分）。\n\n• Gundy 诉美国，139 S. Ct. 2116, 2123 (2019)（解释说，非授权原则允许国会授权，只要它提供了一个易于理解的原则来指导被授权者使用自由裁量权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该法案符合宪法，因为国会可以利用商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予它的权力来规范无人机的销售，并且该法案的条款不违反宪法的任何禁止性条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-36d518c98d17df68"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":73,"sourceQuestionPage":218,"sourceQuestionPages":[218],"sourceExplanationPages":[219,220],"sourceAnswerPages":[219,220],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-074","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"A federal statute provides funds to private organizations that develop online learning materials for schools. A provision of the statute requires organizations that receive such funds to provide their educational materials to private, religious schools for use in their curriculum on the same terms that the organizations provide to public schools. A taxpayer brought a suit in federal court to challenge the federal statutory provision as violating the establishment clause of the First Amendment. The taxpayer does not work in the education industry, nor does he have any children. Does the taxpayer have standing to challenge the provision?","choices":{"A":"No, because the private organizations receiving the federal funds are not state actors.","B":"No, because the provision did not cause the taxpayer to suffer a concrete and particularized injury.","C":"Yes, because taxpayers have the right to challenge how Congress spends federal funds.","D":"Yes, because the taxpayer's challenge is based on the establishment clause."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on one's own behalf)\n\nGeneral Plaintiff must prove: rule\n• injury-in-fact – concrete & particularized harm (actual or imminent)\n• causation – harm traceable to defendant's conduct AND\n• redressability – favorable judicial decision can remedy harm Citizen No standing as mere citizen\n\nLegislator Must prove personal stake in dispute & concrete injury to challenge constitutionality of government action\n\nTaxpayer Standing to challenge taxes owed—but not government spending unless:\n\n• challenging legislation enacted under taxing & spending power AND\n• legislation exceeds limits imposed by establishment clause A federal court will not hear a case unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. To have standing, the plaintiff must assert an injury-in-fact that is concrete and particularized—a generalized grievance shared by many or all citizens is insufficient. Therefore, taxpayers lack standing to challenge the way the government spends tax dollars unless the taxpayer:\n• challenges legislation enacted under Congress's taxing and spending power and\n• alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on this power—to date, the only limitation that the U.S. Supreme Court has found is the First Amendment establishment clause. Here, the taxpayer is challenging a federal statute that provides funds to private organizations to develop online learning materials for schools (exercise of congressional spending power). He is specifically challenging the statutory provision requiring such organizations to provide materials to private, religious schools as a violation of the First Amendment establishment clause (specific constitutional limitation). The taxpayer therefore has standing to challenge this provision. (Choice A) With the exception of the Thirteenth Amendment, the Constitution applies to government (ie, state) action. Private action is considered government action only if the state-action doctrine applies. But here, the taxpayer is challenging a federal law, so there is no need to determine whether the private organizations are engaging in state action. (Choice B) The statutory provision did not cause the taxpayer to suffer a concrete and particularized injury since he does not work in the education industry and does not have\n\nchildren who may be affected by the provision. However, as a taxpayer, he has standing to challenge the provision as violative of the establishment clause. (Choice C) Taxpayers have the right to challenge how Congress spends federal funds only when the challenge alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on Congress's taxing and spending power (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A plaintiff-taxpayer has standing when his/her suit (1) challenges legislation enacted under Congress's taxing and spending power and (2) alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on that power (ie, the establishment clause).\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (defining the case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83, 102–04 (1968) (establishing the narrow exception to the rule against taxpayer standing).","educationalObjective":"A plaintiff-taxpayer has standing when his/her suit (1) challenges legislation enacted under Congress's taxing and spending power and (2) alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on that power (ie, the establishment clause).","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (defining the case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83, 102–04 (1968) (establishing the narrow exception to the rule against taxpayer standing).","rule":"A plaintiff-taxpayer has standing when his/her suit (1) challenges legislation enacted under Congress's taxing and spending power and (2) alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on that power (ie, the establishment clause).","irac":{"issue":"Does the taxpayer have standing to challenge the provision?","rule":"A plaintiff-taxpayer has standing when his/her suit (1) challenges legislation enacted under Congress's taxing and spending power and (2) alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on that power (ie, the establishment clause).","analysis":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on one's own behalf)\n\nGeneral Plaintiff must prove: rule\n• injury-in-fact – concrete & particularized harm (actual or imminent)\n• causation – harm traceable to defendant's conduct AND\n• redressability – favorable judicial decision can remedy harm Citizen No standing as mere citizen\n\nLegislator Must prove personal stake in dispute & concrete injury to challenge constitutionality of government action\n\nTaxpayer Standing to challenge taxes owed—but not government spending unless:\n\n• challenging legislation enacted under taxing & spending power AND\n• legislation exceeds limits imposed by establishment clause A federal court will not hear a case unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. To have standing, the plaintiff must assert an injury-in-fact that is concrete and particularized—a generalized grievance shared by many or all citizens is insufficient. Therefore, taxpayers lack standing to challenge the way the government spends tax dollars unless the taxpayer:\n• challenges legislation enacted under Congress's taxing and spending power and\n• alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on this power—to date, the only limitation that the U.S. Supreme Court has found is the First Amendment establishment clause. Here, the taxpayer is challenging a federal statute that provides funds to private organizations to develop online learning materials for schools (exercise of congressional spending power). He is specifically challenging the statutory provision requiring such organizations to provide materials to private, religious schools as a violation of the First Amendment establishment clause (specific constitutional limitation). The taxpayer therefore has standing to challenge this provision. (Choice A) With the exception of the Thirteenth Amendment, the Constitution applies to government (ie, state) action. Private action is considered government action only if the state-action doctrine applies. But here, the taxpayer is challenging a federal law, so there is no need to determine whether the private organizations are engaging in state action. (Choice B) The statutory provision did not cause the taxpayer to suffer a concrete and particularized injury since he does not work in the education industry and does not have\n\nchildren who may be affected by the provision. However, as a taxpayer, he has standing to challenge the provision as violative of the establishment clause. (Choice C) Taxpayers have the right to challenge how Congress spends federal funds only when the challenge alleges a violation of a specific constitutional limitation on Congress's taxing and spending power (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the taxpayer's challenge is based on the establishment clause."},"questionZh":"联邦法规向为学校开发在线学习材料的私人组织提供资金。该法规的一项规定要求接受此类资金的组织向私立宗教学校提供教育材料，以在其课程中使用，其条件与该组织向公立学校提供的条件相同。纳税人向联邦法院提起诉讼，质疑联邦法律规定违反了第一修正案的设立条款。纳税人不从事教育行业，也没有孩子。纳税人是否有资格质疑该规定？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为接受联邦资金的私人组织不是国家行为者。","B":"不会，因为该规定并未导致纳税人遭受具体的、特殊的损害。","C":"是的，因为纳税人有权质疑国会如何使用联邦资金。","D":"是的，因为纳税人的质疑是基于设立条款。"},"explanationZh":"第三条 地位\n\n（以自己的名义提起诉讼）\n\n一般原告必须证明：规则\n• 事实伤害——具体和特殊的伤害（实际的或迫在眉睫的）\n• 因果关系——伤害可追溯到被告的行为并且\n• 可补救性——有利的司法判决可以补救伤害 公民 不具备单纯公民的资格\n\n立法者必须证明个人在争议和具体伤害中的利害关系，以质疑政府行为的合宪性\n\n纳税人有权质疑所欠税款，但不能质疑政府支出，除非：\n\n• 挑战根据税收和消费能力颁布的立法以及\n• 立法超出了设立条款所施加的限制 联邦法院不会审理案件，除非原告有资格——即，在案件结果中存在个人利害关系。为了获得诉讼资格，原告必须主张具体且具体的事实伤害——许多或所有公民共同的普遍不满是不够的。因此，纳税人没有资格质疑政府支出税款的方式，除非纳税人：\n• 挑战根据国会征税和支出权力颁布的立法，\n• 声称违反了宪法对该权力的特定限制——迄今为止，美国最高法院认定的唯一限制是第一修正案的设立条款。在这里，纳税人正在挑战一项联邦法规，该法规向私人组织提供资金，为学校开发在线学习材料（行使国会的支出权）。他特别质疑要求此类组织向私立宗教学校提供材料的法律规定，认为这违反了第一修正案的设立条款（特定的宪法限制）。因此，纳税人有权质疑这一规定。 （选项A） 除第十三修正案外，宪法适用于政府（即州）行为。仅当国家行为原则适用时，私人行为才被视为政府行为。但在这里，纳税人正在挑战联邦法律，因此无需确定私人组织是否参与了国家行为（state action）。 （选项B）由于纳税人并不从事教育行业，也没有从事教育行业的工作，因此该法定条款并未导致纳税人遭受具体和特殊的损害。\n\n可能受到该规定影响的儿童。然而，作为纳税人，他有权质疑该规定违反了设立条款。 （选项 C）只有当质疑违反了宪法对国会征税和支出权力的特定限制（如此处所示）时，纳税人才有权质疑国会如何支出联邦资金。\n\n教育目标：当原告纳税人的诉讼 (1) 质疑根据国会征税和支出权力颁布的立法，以及 (2) 指控违反宪法对该权力的具体限制（即建国条款）时，他/她就具有诉讼资格。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（定义案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83, 102–04 (1968)（确立了针对纳税人资格的规则的狭隘例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当原告纳税人的诉讼 (1) 对根据国会征税和支出权力颁布的立法提出质疑，以及 (2) 指控违反宪法对该权力的具体限制（即设立条款）时，他/她就具有诉讼资格。","iracZh":{"issue":"纳税人是否有资格质疑该规定？","rule":"当原告纳税人的诉讼 (1) 对根据国会征税和支出权力颁布的立法提出质疑，以及 (2) 指控违反宪法对该权力的具体限制（即设立条款）时，他/她就具有诉讼资格。","analysis":"第三条 地位\n\n（以自己的名义提起诉讼）\n\n一般原告必须证明：规则\n• 事实伤害——具体和特殊的伤害（实际的或迫在眉睫的）\n• 因果关系——伤害可追溯到被告的行为并且\n• 可补救性——有利的司法判决可以补救伤害 公民 不具备单纯公民的资格\n\n立法者必须证明个人在争议和具体伤害中的利害关系，以质疑政府行为的合宪性\n\n纳税人有权质疑所欠税款，但不能质疑政府支出，除非：\n\n• 挑战根据税收和消费能力颁布的立法以及\n• 立法超出了设立条款所施加的限制 联邦法院不会审理案件，除非原告有资格——即，在案件结果中存在个人利害关系。为了获得诉讼资格，原告必须主张具体且具体的事实伤害——许多或所有公民共同的普遍不满是不够的。因此，纳税人没有资格质疑政府支出税款的方式，除非纳税人：\n• 挑战根据国会征税和支出权力颁布的立法，\n• 声称违反了宪法对该权力的特定限制——迄今为止，美国最高法院认定的唯一限制是第一修正案的设立条款。在这里，纳税人正在挑战一项联邦法规，该法规向私人组织提供资金，为学校开发在线学习材料（行使国会的支出权）。他特别质疑要求此类组织向私立宗教学校提供材料的法律规定，认为这违反了第一修正案的设立条款（特定的宪法限制）。因此，纳税人有权质疑这一规定。 （选项A） 除第十三修正案外，宪法适用于政府（即州）行为。仅当国家行为原则适用时，私人行为才被视为政府行为。但在这里，纳税人正在挑战联邦法律，因此无需确定私人组织是否参与了国家行为（state action）。 （选项B）由于纳税人并不从事教育行业，也没有从事教育行业的工作，因此该法定条款并未导致纳税人遭受具体和特殊的损害。\n\n可能受到该规定影响的儿童。然而，作为纳税人，他有权质疑该规定违反了设立条款。 （选项 C）只有当质疑违反了宪法对国会征税和支出权力的特定限制（如此处所示）时，纳税人才有权质疑国会如何支出联邦资金。\n\n教育目标：当原告纳税人的诉讼 (1) 质疑根据国会征税和支出权力颁布的立法，以及 (2) 指控违反宪法对该权力的具体限制（即建国条款）时，他/她就具有诉讼资格。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（定义案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83, 102–04 (1968)（确立了针对纳税人资格的规则的狭隘例外）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为纳税人的质疑是基于设立条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"religious","ipa":"/ɹɪlˈɪdʒəs/","syllables":"re·li·gious","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 宗教性的, 虔诚的, 宗教上的, 严谨的；n. 修道士, 出家人","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 宗教性的, 虔诚的, 宗教上的, 严谨的；n. 修道士, 出家人","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"A provision of the statute requires organizations that receive such funds to provide their educational materials to private, religious schools for use in their curriculum on the same terms that the organizations provide to public schools.","synonyms":["spiritual"]},{"term":"taxpayer","ipa":"/tˈækspˌeɪɝ/","syllables":"tax·payer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 纳税人；[法] 纳税人, 纳税义务人","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 纳税人；[法] 纳税人, 纳税义务人","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:taxpayers","sourceSentence":"A taxpayer brought a suit in federal court to challenge the federal statutory provision as violating the establishment clause of the First Amendment.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"grievance","ipa":"/ɡɹˈivəns/","syllables":"griev·ance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 委屈, 冤情, 苦况；[经] 不满(对雇用条件的)","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 委屈, 冤情, 苦况；[经] 不满(对雇用条件的)","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:grievances","sourceSentence":"To have standing, the plaintiff must assert an injury-in-fact that is concrete and particularized—a generalized grievance shared by many or all citizens is insufficient.","synonyms":["grudge","score"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":74,"sourceQuestionPage":221,"sourceQuestionPages":[221],"sourceExplanationPages":[222,223],"sourceAnswerPages":[222,223],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-075","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"Terrorists in a foreign country kidnapped the United States ambassador to that country. They threatened to kill her unless the President of the United States secured the release of an identified person who was a citizen of the foreign country and was being held in a state prison in the United States pursuant to a valid conviction by that state. The President responded by entering into an agreement with the foreign country which provided that the foreign country would secure the release of the United States ambassador on a specified date in return for action by the President that would secure the release of the identified person held in the state prison. The President then ordered the governor of the state to release the prisoner in question. The governor refused. No federal statutes are applicable. Which of the following is the strongest constitutional argument for the authority of the President to take action in these circumstances requiring the state governor to release the prisoner?","choices":{"A":"The duty of the President to faithfully execute the laws authorizes him to resolve any conflicts between state and federal interests, making the determination of such matters wholly nonjusticiable.","B":"The power of the President to appoint ambassadors authorizes him to take any action that he may think desirable to protect them from injury because those officials become agents of the President upon appointment.","C":"The power of the President to conduct the foreign affairs of the United States includes a plenary authority to take whatever action the President deems wise to protect the safety of our diplomatic agents.","D":"The power of the President to negotiate with foreign nations impliedly authorizes him to make executive agreements with them which prevail over state law."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article II executive powers\n\nDomestic\n• Manage federal executive agencies & officers powers\n• Execute federal laws\n• Recommend legislation to Congress\n• Sign or veto bills\n• Appoint/remove federal officers\n• Prosecute & grant pardons for federal crimes Foreign\n• Serve as commander in chief powers\n• Negotiate/enter treaties (with 2/3 Senate approval) & executive agreements\n• Recognize foreign governments\n• Appoint/receive foreign diplomats Article II gives the President a wide range of domestic and foreign powers. In foreign affairs, the President's power is at its zenith because it is imperative that the nation speak with one voice on the international stage. One such power is that the President can negotiate and enter into agreements with foreign nations. These agreements can take the form of either:\n• treaties – formal agreements that take effect once the Senate gives advice and consents by a two-thirds vote or\n\n• executive agreements – less formal agreements that take effect as soon as the President enters into them (ie, no Senate advice and consent required).\n\nHere, the President entered into an executive agreement with the foreign country to secure the release of that country's citizen from state prison. And under the supremacy clause, executive agreements—which carry the force of federal law—prevail over conflicting state laws. Therefore, this is the strongest constitutional argument for the President's authority to require the governor to release the foreign country's citizen from state prison. (Choice A) The President's duty to faithfully execute federal laws does not authorize him/her to resolve conflicts between state and federal interests. Instead, these conflicts are justiciable and must be resolved in court. (Choices B & C) Article II grants the President the power to appoint ambassadors, who then become agents of the President. But this does not give the President plenary (ie, absolute) authority to take any action that he/she deems desirable or wise to protect them because the President must comply with the Constitution, treaties, and federal laws.\n\nEducational objective: The President has extensive powers in foreign affairs, including the power to negotiate and enter into executive agreements with foreign countries. And under the supremacy clause, executive agreements prevail over conflicting state laws.\n\nReferences\n• Am. Ins. Assoc. v. Garamendi, 539 U.S. 396, 415–17 (2003) (explaining the creation of executive agreements and their supremacy over conflicting state laws).","educationalObjective":"The President has extensive powers in foreign affairs, including the power to negotiate and enter into executive agreements with foreign countries. And under the supremacy clause, executive agreements prevail over conflicting state laws.","references":"• Am. Ins. Assoc. v. Garamendi, 539 U.S. 396, 415–17 (2003) (explaining the creation of executive agreements and their supremacy over conflicting state laws).","rule":"The President has extensive powers in foreign affairs, including the power to negotiate and enter into executive agreements with foreign countries. And under the supremacy clause, executive agreements prevail over conflicting state laws.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest constitutional argument for the authority of the President to take action in these circumstances requiring the state governor to release the prisoner?","rule":"The President has extensive powers in foreign affairs, including the power to negotiate and enter into executive agreements with foreign countries. And under the supremacy clause, executive agreements prevail over conflicting state laws.","analysis":"Article II executive powers\n\nDomestic\n• Manage federal executive agencies & officers powers\n• Execute federal laws\n• Recommend legislation to Congress\n• Sign or veto bills\n• Appoint/remove federal officers\n• Prosecute & grant pardons for federal crimes Foreign\n• Serve as commander in chief powers\n• Negotiate/enter treaties (with 2/3 Senate approval) & executive agreements\n• Recognize foreign governments\n• Appoint/receive foreign diplomats Article II gives the President a wide range of domestic and foreign powers. In foreign affairs, the President's power is at its zenith because it is imperative that the nation speak with one voice on the international stage. One such power is that the President can negotiate and enter into agreements with foreign nations. These agreements can take the form of either:\n• treaties – formal agreements that take effect once the Senate gives advice and consents by a two-thirds vote or\n\n• executive agreements – less formal agreements that take effect as soon as the President enters into them (ie, no Senate advice and consent required).\n\nHere, the President entered into an executive agreement with the foreign country to secure the release of that country's citizen from state prison. And under the supremacy clause, executive agreements—which carry the force of federal law—prevail over conflicting state laws. Therefore, this is the strongest constitutional argument for the President's authority to require the governor to release the foreign country's citizen from state prison. (Choice A) The President's duty to faithfully execute federal laws does not authorize him/her to resolve conflicts between state and federal interests. Instead, these conflicts are justiciable and must be resolved in court. (Choices B & C) Article II grants the President the power to appoint ambassadors, who then become agents of the President. But this does not give the President plenary (ie, absolute) authority to take any action that he/she deems desirable or wise to protect them because the President must comply with the Constitution, treaties, and federal laws.","conclusion":"Answer D: The power of the President to negotiate with foreign nations impliedly authorizes him to make executive agreements with them which prevail over state law."},"questionZh":"外国的恐怖分子绑架了美国驻该国大使。他们威胁要杀死她，除非美国总统释放一名已被确认身份的外国公民，并根据该州的有效定罪被关押在美国的州立监狱中。作为回应，总统与外国签订了一项协议，规定外国将确保在指定日期释放美国大使，以换取总统采取行动，确保释放被关押在国家监狱的已确认人员。总统随后命令该州州长释放涉案囚犯。州长拒绝了。没有适用的联邦法规。以下哪一项是总统有权在要求州长释放囚犯的情况下采取行动的最有力的宪法论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"总统忠实执行法律的责任授权他解决州和联邦利益之间的任何冲突，从而使此类问题的裁决完全不可由司法管辖。","B":"总统任命大使的权力授权他采取他认为适当的任何行动来保护他们免受伤害，因为这些官员在任命后就成为总统的代理人。","C":"总统处理美国外交事务的权力包括采取总统认为明智的任何行动来保护我们外交人员安全的全体权力。","D":"总统与外国谈判的权力隐含地授权他与外国达成优先于州法律的行政协议。"},"explanationZh":"第二条 行政权力\n\n国内\n• 管理联邦执行机构和官员的权力\n• 执行联邦法律\n• 向国会建议立法\n• 签署或否决法案\n• 任命/罢免联邦官员\n• 起诉联邦罪行并给予赦免\n• 担任总司令\n• 谈判/签订条约（经 2/3 参议院批准）和执行协议\n• 承认外国政府\n• 任命/接待外国外交官 第二条赋予总统广泛的国内和国外权力。在外交事务中，总统的权力达到了顶峰，因为国家必须在国际舞台上以一个声音说话。其中一项权力是总统可以与外国进行谈判并达成协议。这些协议可以采取以下形式之一：\n• 条约——正式协议，一旦参议院提出建议并以三分之二票数同意即生效，或\n\n• 行政协议——不太正式的协议，总统签署后立即生效（即无需参议院建议和同意）。\n\n在这里，总统与外国签署了一项行政协议，以确保该国公民从国家监狱获释。根据至上条款，具有联邦法律效力的行政协议优先于相互冲突的州法律。因此，这是总统有权要求州长从州监狱释放外国公民的最有力的宪法论据。 （选项 A）总统忠实执行联邦法律的职责并不授权他/她解决州和联邦利益之间的冲突。相反，这些冲突可以通过法庭审理，必须在法庭上解决。 （选项 B 和 C） 第二条赋予总统任命大使的权力，大使随后成为总统的代理人。但这并不赋予总统全权（即绝对）权力来采取他/她认为适当或明智的任何行动来保护他们，因为总统必须遵守宪法、条约和联邦法律。\n\n教育目标：总统在外交事务中拥有广泛的权力，包括与外国谈判和签订行政协议的权力。根据至上条款，执行协议优先于相冲突的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 上午。插入。副教授。 v. Garamendi, 539 U.S. 396, 415–17 (2003)（解释行政协议的制定及其相对于相冲突的州法律的至高无上的地位）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"总统在外交事务上拥有广泛的权力，包括与外国谈判和签订行政协议的权力。根据至上条款，执行协议优先于相冲突的州法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是总统有权在要求州长释放囚犯的情况下采取行动的最有力的宪法论据？","rule":"总统在外交事务上拥有广泛的权力，包括与外国谈判和签订行政协议的权力。根据至上条款，执行协议优先于相冲突的州法律。","analysis":"第二条 行政权力\n\n国内\n• 管理联邦执行机构和官员的权力\n• 执行联邦法律\n• 向国会建议立法\n• 签署或否决法案\n• 任命/罢免联邦官员\n• 起诉联邦罪行并给予赦免\n• 担任总司令\n• 谈判/签订条约（经 2/3 参议院批准）和执行协议\n• 承认外国政府\n• 任命/接待外国外交官 第二条赋予总统广泛的国内和国外权力。在外交事务中，总统的权力达到了顶峰，因为国家必须在国际舞台上以一个声音说话。其中一项权力是总统可以与外国进行谈判并达成协议。这些协议可以采取以下形式之一：\n• 条约——正式协议，一旦参议院提出建议并以三分之二票数同意即生效，或\n\n• 行政协议——不太正式的协议，总统签署后立即生效（即无需参议院建议和同意）。\n\n在这里，总统与外国签署了一项行政协议，以确保该国公民从国家监狱获释。根据至上条款，具有联邦法律效力的行政协议优先于相互冲突的州法律。因此，这是总统有权要求州长从州监狱释放外国公民的最有力的宪法论据。 （选项 A）总统忠实执行联邦法律的职责并不授权他/她解决州和联邦利益之间的冲突。相反，这些冲突可以通过法庭审理，必须在法庭上解决。 （选项 B 和 C） 第二条赋予总统任命大使的权力，大使随后成为总统的代理人。但这并不赋予总统全权（即绝对）权力来采取他/她认为适当或明智的任何行动来保护他们，因为总统必须遵守宪法、条约和联邦法律。\n\n教育目标：总统在外交事务中拥有广泛的权力，包括与外国谈判和签订行政协议的权力。根据至上条款，执行协议优先于相冲突的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 上午。插入。副教授。 v. Garamendi, 539 U.S. 396, 415–17 (2003)（解释行政协议的制定及其相对于相冲突的州法律的至高无上的地位）。","conclusion":"答案 D：总统与外国谈判的权力隐含地授权他与外国达成优先于州法律的行政协议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"justiciable","ipa":"/dʒʌs'tiʃiəbl/","syllables":"jus·ti·cia·ble","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. 可在法庭裁决的；[法] 可受法院裁判的, 受法院管辖的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 可在法庭裁决的；[法] 可受法院裁判的, 受法院管辖的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"The duty of the President to faithfully execute the laws authorizes him to resolve any conflicts between state and federal interests, making the determination of such matters wholly nonjusticiable.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":75,"sourceQuestionPage":224,"sourceQuestionPages":[224],"sourceExplanationPages":[225,226],"sourceAnswerPages":[225,226],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-076","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"Congress passed a bill prohibiting the President from granting a pardon to any person who has not served at least one-third of the sentence imposed by the court that convicted the person. The President vetoed the bill, claiming that it was unconstitutional. Nevertheless, Congress passed it over his veto by a two-thirds vote of each house. Is this act of Congress constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because a presidential veto based upon constitutional grounds may be overridden only with the concurrence of three-fourths of the state legislatures.","B":"No, because it interferes with the plenary power of the President to grant pardons.","C":"Yes, because it is a necessary and proper means of carrying out the powers of Congress.","D":"Yes, because it was enacted over the President's veto by a two-thirds vote of each house."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The Constitution gives each branch of government separate and distinct powers that generally cannot be exercised by another branch. This establishes a system of checks and balances that prevents concentration of power. The President (ie, executive branch) has the distinct power to enforce laws. This includes the plenary (ie, absolute) power to grant reprievesand pardons to persons who commit federal offenses (except in cases of impeachment). And since the other branches cannot interfere with this power in any way, this act of Congress is unconstitutional. (Choice A) Article II grants the President the power to veto a bill for any reason, which can only be overridden by a two-thirds vote in each house of Congress. A three-fourths vote of the state legislatures is generally part of the constitutional amendment process—not the federal legislative process. (Choice C) The necessary and proper clause gives Congress the power to use reasonably appropriate means to carry out its other powers. But it cannot violate other constitutional provisions when doing so (as seen here). (Choice D) Although the bill was enacted into law over the President's veto by a two-thirds vote of each house of Congress, it is unconstitutional since it interferes with the President's plenary power to issue pardons.\n\nEducational objective: Under the separation-of-powers doctrine, each branch of government has separate and distinct powers that generally cannot be exercised by another branch—eg, Congress and the judiciary cannot interfere with the President's plenary power to grant reprieves and pardons.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2, cl. 1 (pardon power).\n\n• Schick v. Reed, 419 U.S. 256, 266 (1974) (stating that Congress cannot alter the President's pardon power).","educationalObjective":"Under the separation-of-powers doctrine, each branch of government has separate and distinct powers that generally cannot be exercised by another branch—eg, Congress and the judiciary cannot interfere with the President's plenary power to grant reprieves and pardons.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2, cl. 1 (pardon power).\n\n• Schick v. Reed, 419 U.S. 256, 266 (1974) (stating that Congress cannot alter the President's pardon power).","rule":"Under the separation-of-powers doctrine, each branch of government has separate and distinct powers that generally cannot be exercised by another branch—eg, Congress and the judiciary cannot interfere with the President's plenary power to grant reprieves and pardons.","irac":{"issue":"Is this act of Congress constitutional?","rule":"Under the separation-of-powers doctrine, each branch of government has separate and distinct powers that generally cannot be exercised by another branch—eg, Congress and the judiciary cannot interfere with the President's plenary power to grant reprieves and pardons.","analysis":"The Constitution gives each branch of government separate and distinct powers that generally cannot be exercised by another branch. This establishes a system of checks and balances that prevents concentration of power. The President (ie, executive branch) has the distinct power to enforce laws. This includes the plenary (ie, absolute) power to grant reprievesand pardons to persons who commit federal offenses (except in cases of impeachment). And since the other branches cannot interfere with this power in any way, this act of Congress is unconstitutional. (Choice A) Article II grants the President the power to veto a bill for any reason, which can only be overridden by a two-thirds vote in each house of Congress. A three-fourths vote of the state legislatures is generally part of the constitutional amendment process—not the federal legislative process. (Choice C) The necessary and proper clause gives Congress the power to use reasonably appropriate means to carry out its other powers. But it cannot violate other constitutional provisions when doing so (as seen here). (Choice D) Although the bill was enacted into law over the President's veto by a two-thirds vote of each house of Congress, it is unconstitutional since it interferes with the President's plenary power to issue pardons.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because it interferes with the plenary power of the President to grant pardons."},"questionZh":"国会通过了一项法案，禁止总统赦免任何未服完定罪法院至少三分之一刑期的人。总统否决了该法案，声称该法案违宪。尽管如此，国会还是以参众两院三分之二的票数通过了他的否决。国会的这项法案是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为基于宪法的总统否决只有在四分之三的州立法机构同意的情况下才能被推翻。","B":"不，因为它干扰了总统赦免的全权。","C":"是的，因为这是履行国会权力的必要且适当的手段。","D":"是的，因为它是在总统否决后以参众两院三分之二票数通过的。"},"explanationZh":"《宪法》赋予政府每个部门单独且独特的权力，通常其他部门不能行使这些权力。这建立了一个防止权力集中的制衡体系。总统（即行政部门）拥有执行法律的独特权力。这包括对犯有联邦罪行的人给予缓刑和赦免的全体（即绝对）权力（弹劾案件除外）。由于其他部门不能以任何方式干涉这一权力，因此国会的这项法案是违宪的。 （选项A）第二条赋予总统以任何理由否决一项法案的权力，该法案只能由国会两院三分之二多数票才能推翻。州立法机构四分之三的投票通常是宪法修正案程序的一部分，而不是联邦立法程序。 （选项C）必要且适当的条款赋予国会使用合理适当的手段来履行其其他权力的权力。但这样做时不能违反其他宪法条款（如此处所示）。 （选项D）虽然该法案经国会参众两院三分之二票数通过总统否决而成为法律，但它违反宪法，因为它干扰了总统发布赦免的全体权力。\n\n教育目标：根据权力分立原则，政府的每个部门都有单独且独特的权力，通常其他部门不能行使这些权力，例如，国会和司法部门不能干涉总统授予缓刑和赦免的全权。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2，cl。 1（赦免权）。\n\n• Schick v. Reed, 419 U.S. 256, 266 (1974)（指出国会不能改变总统的赦免权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据权力分立原则，政府的每个部门都拥有单独且独特的权力，通常其他部门不能行使这些权力，例如，国会和司法部门不能干涉总统授予缓刑和赦免的全权。","iracZh":{"issue":"国会的这项法案是否符合宪法？","rule":"根据权力分立原则，政府的每个部门都拥有单独且独特的权力，通常其他部门不能行使这些权力，例如，国会和司法部门不能干涉总统授予缓刑和赦免的全权。","analysis":"《宪法》赋予政府每个部门单独且独特的权力，通常其他部门不能行使这些权力。这建立了一个防止权力集中的制衡体系。总统（即行政部门）拥有执行法律的独特权力。这包括对犯有联邦罪行的人给予缓刑和赦免的全体（即绝对）权力（弹劾案件除外）。由于其他部门不能以任何方式干涉这一权力，因此国会的这项法案是违宪的。 （选项A）第二条赋予总统以任何理由否决一项法案的权力，该法案只能由国会两院三分之二多数票才能推翻。州立法机构四分之三的投票通常是宪法修正案程序的一部分，而不是联邦立法程序。 （选项C）必要且适当的条款赋予国会使用合理适当的手段来履行其其他权力的权力。但这样做时不能违反其他宪法条款（如此处所示）。 （选项D）虽然该法案经国会参众两院三分之二票数通过总统否决而成为法律，但它违反宪法，因为它干扰了总统发布赦免的全体权力。\n\n教育目标：根据权力分立原则，政府的每个部门都有单独且独特的权力，通常其他部门不能行使这些权力，例如，国会和司法部门不能干涉总统授予缓刑和赦免的全权。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2，cl。 1（赦免权）。\n\n• Schick v. Reed, 419 U.S. 256, 266 (1974)（指出国会不能改变总统的赦免权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为它干扰了总统赦免的全权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"judiciary","ipa":"/dʒudˈɪʃiˌɛɹi/","syllables":"ju·di·ciary","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 司法的, 法院的, 法官的；n. 司法部, 司法系统, 法官","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 司法的, 法院的, 法官的；n. 司法部, 司法系统, 法官","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:judiciaries","sourceSentence":"Under the separation-of-powers doctrine, each branch of government has separate and distinct powers that generally cannot be exercised by another branch—eg, Congress and the judiciary cannot interfere with the President's plenary power to grant reprieves and pardons.","synonyms":["bench","judicature","judicatory","judicial system"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-960dc7a7fa0f633f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":76,"sourceQuestionPage":227,"sourceQuestionPages":[227],"sourceExplanationPages":[228,229],"sourceAnswerPages":[228,229],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-077","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"A baseball fan has a fierce temper and an extremely loud voice. Attending a baseball game in which a number of calls went against the home team, the fan repeatedly stood up, brandished his fist, and angrily shouted, \"Kill the umpires.\" The fourth time he engaged in this conduct, many other spectators followed the fan in rising from their seats, brandishing fists, and shouting, \"Kill the umpires.\" The home team lost the game. Although no violence ensued, spectators crowded menacingly around the umpires after the game. As a result, the umpires were able to leave the field and stadium only with the help of a massive police escort. For his conduct, the fan was charged with inciting to riot and was convicted in a jury trial in state court. He appealed. The state supreme court reversed his conviction. In its opinion, the court discussed in detail decisions of the United States Supreme Court dealing with the First Amendment free speech clause as incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment. At the end of that discussion, however, the court stated that it \"need not resolve how, on the basis of these cases,\" the United States Supreme Court would decide the fan's case. \"Instead,\" the court stated, \"this court has always given the free-speech guarantee of the state's constitution the broadest possible interpretation. As a result, we hold that in this case, where no riot or other violence actually occurred, the state constitution does not permit this conviction for incitement to riot to stand.\" The United States Supreme Court grants a writ of certiorari to review this decision of the state supreme court. In this case, how should the United States Supreme Court rule?","choices":{"A":"Affirm the state supreme court's decision, because the fan's ballpark shout is commonplace hyperbole that cannot, consistent with the First and Fourteenth Amendments, be punished.","B":"Dismiss the writ as improvidently granted, because the state supreme court's decision rests on an independent and adequate state-law ground.","C":"Remand the case to the state supreme court with directions that it resolve the First and Fourteenth Amendment free-speech issue that it discussed in such detail.","D":"Reverse the decision of the state supreme court, because incitement to violent action is not speech protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The Supreme Court of the United States (SCOTUS) may use its discretionary appellate jurisdiction and grant a writ of certiorari to review state court judgments. But since SCOTUS may only review such judgments if they are based on federal law, it cannot review a judgment based on adequate and independent state grounds. This occurs when:\n• state law fully resolves the matter, so the outcome of the case is not affected by federal law (adequate) and\n\n• the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its decision (independent).\n\nIf a writ of certiorari was improvidently (ie, incorrectly) granted in such a case, SCOTUS must dismiss the writ. Here, the state supreme court reversed the fan's criminal conviction for inciting a riot on a state constitutional ground. The court held that the state constitution grants broader free- speech protection than the U.S. Constitution (adequate ground). And though the court discussed First Amendment cases in detail, it clearly stated that its decision was not based on that caselaw (independent ground). Therefore, the state supreme court's decision rests on an independent and adequate state ground, and SCOTUS must dismiss the writ as improvidently granted. (Choices A & D) When a case is decided on adequate and independent state grounds (as seen here), SCOTUS has no jurisdiction to affirm or reverse that case on its merits. As a result, SCOTUS cannot decide whether the fan's ballpark shout is commonplace hyperbole or unprotected speech subject to punishment. (Choice C) The state court need not resolve the federal constitutional issue since its decision was based on an adequate and independent state ground. But even if the decision was not based on such a ground, SCOTUS would not ask the state court to resolve the federal constitutional issue. Instead, it would rule on that issue and remand the case to state court to resolve any remaining state-law issues.\n\nEducational objective: SCOTUS only has jurisdiction to review a state supreme court decision based on federal law—not adequate and independent state grounds.\n\nReferences\n• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983) (explaining when a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state grounds).","educationalObjective":"SCOTUS only has jurisdiction to review a state supreme court decision based on federal law—not adequate and independent state grounds.","references":"• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983) (explaining when a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state grounds).","rule":"SCOTUS only has jurisdiction to review a state supreme court decision based on federal law—not adequate and independent state grounds.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, how should the United States Supreme Court rule?","rule":"SCOTUS only has jurisdiction to review a state supreme court decision based on federal law—not adequate and independent state grounds.","analysis":"The Supreme Court of the United States (SCOTUS) may use its discretionary appellate jurisdiction and grant a writ of certiorari to review state court judgments. But since SCOTUS may only review such judgments if they are based on federal law, it cannot review a judgment based on adequate and independent state grounds. This occurs when:\n• state law fully resolves the matter, so the outcome of the case is not affected by federal law (adequate) and\n\n• the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its decision (independent).\n\nIf a writ of certiorari was improvidently (ie, incorrectly) granted in such a case, SCOTUS must dismiss the writ. Here, the state supreme court reversed the fan's criminal conviction for inciting a riot on a state constitutional ground. The court held that the state constitution grants broader free- speech protection than the U.S. Constitution (adequate ground). And though the court discussed First Amendment cases in detail, it clearly stated that its decision was not based on that caselaw (independent ground). Therefore, the state supreme court's decision rests on an independent and adequate state ground, and SCOTUS must dismiss the writ as improvidently granted. (Choices A & D) When a case is decided on adequate and independent state grounds (as seen here), SCOTUS has no jurisdiction to affirm or reverse that case on its merits. As a result, SCOTUS cannot decide whether the fan's ballpark shout is commonplace hyperbole or unprotected speech subject to punishment. (Choice C) The state court need not resolve the federal constitutional issue since its decision was based on an adequate and independent state ground. But even if the decision was not based on such a ground, SCOTUS would not ask the state court to resolve the federal constitutional issue. Instead, it would rule on that issue and remand the case to state court to resolve any remaining state-law issues.","conclusion":"Answer B: Dismiss the writ as improvidently granted, because the state supreme court's decision rests on an independent and adequate state-law ground."},"questionZh":"棒球迷脾气暴躁，声音特别大。在一场棒球比赛中，多次出现对主队不利的判罚，这名球迷多次站起来，挥舞拳头，愤怒地喊道：“杀了裁判。”当他第四次做出这种行为时，许多其他观众也跟着球迷从座位上站起来，挥舞着拳头，高喊“杀掉裁判”。主队输掉了比赛。尽管没有发生暴力事件，但比赛结束后，观众们威胁性地围拢在裁判周围。结果，裁判员只能在大批警察的护送下才能离开赛场和体育场。由于他的行为，这名球迷被指控煽动骚乱，并在州法院的陪审团审判中被定罪。他提出上诉。州最高法院推翻了对他的定罪。在其意见中，法院详细讨论了美国最高法院关于将第一修正案言论自由条款纳入第十四修正案的判决。然而，在讨论结束时，法院表示，“无需解决美国最高法院如何根据这些案件来裁决球迷的案件”。 “相反，”法院表示，“本法院一直给予州宪法的言论自由保证尽可能最广泛的解释。因此，我们认为，在本案中，如果没有实际发生骚乱或其他暴力，州宪法不允许煽动骚乱的定罪成立。”美国最高法院授予调卷令状以复审州最高法院的这一决定。对于这种情况，美国最高法院应该如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持州最高法院的裁决，因为球迷的球场喊叫是常见的夸张说法，根据第一修正案和第十四修正案，不能受到惩罚。","B":"驳回该令状，因为州最高法院的决定是基于独立且充分的州法律依据。","C":"将案件发回州最高法院重审，并指示其解决其详细讨论的第一和第十四修正案的言论自由问题。","D":"推翻州最高法院的判决，因为煽动暴力行为不属于受第一修正案和第十四修正案保护的言论。"},"explanationZh":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 可以使用其酌情上诉管辖权并授予调卷令状以审查州法院的判决。但由于最高法院只能审查基于联邦法律的此类判决，因此它无法审查基于充分和独立的州理由的判决。出现这种情况时：\n• 州法律完全解决了该问题，因此案件的结果不受联邦法律（充分）的影响，并且\n\n• 州法院不依赖联邦法律做出裁决（独立）。\n\n如果在这种情况下，调卷令状是无意地（即不正确地）授予的，最高法院必须驳回该令状。在这里，州最高法院以州宪法为由推翻了这名球迷煽动骚乱的刑事定罪。法院认为，州宪法比美国宪法赋予更广泛的言论自由保护（充分理由）。尽管法院详细讨论了第一修正案案件，但它明确表示其决定并非基于该判例法（独立依据）。因此，州最高法院的决定基于独立且充分的州理由，最高法院必须驳回该令状，因为该令状的授予是不明智的。 （选择 A 和 D）当案件根据充分且独立的州理由做出裁决时（如此处所示），最高法院无权根据案情确认或推翻该案件。因此，最高法院无法判定球迷的球场喊叫是常见的夸张说法还是不受保护的言论，会受到惩罚。 （选项C）州法院不需要解决联邦宪法问题，因为其决定是基于充分且独立的州理由。但即使该决定不是基于这样的理由，最高法院也不会要求州法院解决联邦宪法问题。相反，它将就该问题做出裁决，并将案件发回州法院，以解决任何剩余的州法律问题。\n\n教育目标：最高法院仅有权根据联邦法律审查州最高法院的裁决，而不是充分且独立的州理由。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983)（解释州最高法院的判决何时基于充分且独立的州理由）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"SCOTUS 仅有权根据联邦法律审查州最高法院的决定，而不是充分且独立的州理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于这种情况，美国最高法院应该如何裁决？","rule":"SCOTUS 仅有权根据联邦法律审查州最高法院的决定，而不是充分且独立的州理由。","analysis":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 可以使用其酌情上诉管辖权并授予调卷令状以审查州法院的判决。但由于最高法院只能审查基于联邦法律的此类判决，因此它无法审查基于充分和独立的州理由的判决。出现这种情况时：\n• 州法律完全解决了该问题，因此案件的结果不受联邦法律（充分）的影响，并且\n\n• 州法院不依赖联邦法律做出裁决（独立）。\n\n如果在这种情况下，调卷令状是无意地（即不正确地）授予的，最高法院必须驳回该令状。在这里，州最高法院以州宪法为由推翻了这名球迷煽动骚乱的刑事定罪。法院认为，州宪法比美国宪法赋予更广泛的言论自由保护（充分理由）。尽管法院详细讨论了第一修正案案件，但它明确表示其决定并非基于该判例法（独立依据）。因此，州最高法院的决定基于独立且充分的州理由，最高法院必须驳回该令状，因为该令状的授予是不明智的。 （选择 A 和 D）当案件根据充分且独立的州理由做出裁决时（如此处所示），最高法院无权根据案情确认或推翻该案件。因此，最高法院无法判定球迷的球场喊叫是常见的夸张说法还是不受保护的言论，会受到惩罚。 （选项C）州法院不需要解决联邦宪法问题，因为其决定是基于充分且独立的州理由。但即使该决定不是基于这样的理由，最高法院也不会要求州法院解决联邦宪法问题。相反，它将就该问题做出裁决，并将案件发回州法院，以解决任何剩余的州法律问题。\n\n教育目标：最高法院仅有权根据联邦法律审查州最高法院的裁决，而不是充分且独立的州理由。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983)（解释州最高法院的判决何时基于充分且独立的州理由）。","conclusion":"答案 B：驳回该令状，因为州最高法院的决定是基于独立且充分的州法律依据。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"incitement","ipa":"/ˌɪnsˈaɪtmənt/","syllables":"in·cite·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 煽动, 刺激, 怂恿犯罪","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[法] 煽动, 刺激, 怂恿犯罪","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:incitements","sourceSentence":"As a result, we hold that in this case, where no riot or other violence actually occurred, the state constitution does not permit this conviction for incitement to riot to stand.","synonyms":["incitation","exhortation","provocation"]},{"term":"inciting","ipa":"/ˌɪnsˈaɪtɪŋ/","syllables":"in·cit·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 刺激的, 煽动的；v. 刺激, 激励, 煽动( incite的现在分词 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 刺激的, 煽动的；v. 刺激, 激励, 煽动( incite的现在分词 )","wordForm":"词；原形 incite；词形 0:incite/1:i","sourceSentence":"For his conduct, the fan was charged with inciting to riot and was convicted in a jury trial in state court.","synonyms":["instigate","set off","stir up","prod"]},{"term":"opinion","ipa":"/ʌpˈɪnjən/","syllables":"opin·ion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 意见, 评价, 主张；[经] 意见","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 意见, 评价, 主张；[经] 意见","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:opinions","sourceSentence":"In its opinion, the court discussed in detail decisions of the United States Supreme Court dealing with the First Amendment free speech clause as incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment.","synonyms":["ruling","impression","feeling","belief"]},{"term":"remand","ipa":"/ɹɪmˈænd/","syllables":"re·mand","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 遣回, 还押；vt. 遣回, 还押候审","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 遣回, 还押；vt. 遣回, 还押候审","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:remanded/s:remands/p:remanded/i:remanding/3:remands","sourceSentence":"Remand the case to the state supreme court with directions that it resolve the First and Fourteenth Amendment free-speech issue that it discussed in such detail.","synonyms":["remit","send back","imprison","incarcerate"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-1d5548e6940e58b3"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":77,"sourceQuestionPage":230,"sourceQuestionPages":[230],"sourceExplanationPages":[231,232],"sourceAnswerPages":[231,232],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-078","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"A plaintiff challenged the constitutionality of a state tax law, alleging that it violated the equal protection clauses of both the United States Constitution and the state constitution. The state supreme court agreed and held the tax law to be invalid. It said: \"We hold that this state tax law violates the equal protection clause of the United States Constitution and also the equal protection clause of the state constitution because we interpret that provision of the state constitution to contain exactly the same prohibition against discriminatory legislation as is contained in the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution.\" The state sought review of this decision in the United States Supreme Court, alleging that the state supreme court's determination of the federal constitutional issue was incorrect. How should the United States Supreme Court dispose of the case if it believes that this interpretation of the federal Constitution by the state supreme court raises an important federal question and is incorrect on the merits?","choices":{"A":"Refuse to review the decision of the state supreme court, because a state government may not seek review of decisions of its own courts in the United States Supreme Court.","B":"Refuse to review the decision of the state supreme court, because it is based on an adequate and independent ground of state law.","C":"Reverse the state supreme court decision, because the equal protection clause of a state constitution must be construed by the state supreme court in a manner that is congruent with the meaning of the equal protection clause of the federal Constitution.","D":"Reverse the state supreme court decision with respect to the equal protection clause of the federal Constitution and remand the case to the state supreme court for further proceedings, because the state and federal constitutional issues are so intertwined that the federal issue must be decided so that this case may be disposed of properly."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The Supreme Court of the United States (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction over most final state-court decisions that present a federal question. But SCOTUS cannot exercise jurisdiction over, or review the merits of, such decisions if they are based on adequate and independent state grounds. This occurs when:\n• state law fully resolves the issue and the outcome of the case is not affected by federal law (ie, adequate state grounds) and\n\n• the final state-court decision is reached without relying on federal precedent (ie, independent state grounds).\n\nHere, the state supreme court held that the state tax law violates the equal protection clauses of both the federal and state constitutions (adequate state grounds). But since that decision relied on the federal constitution to interpret the state constitution (no independent state grounds), SCOTUS has jurisdiction to reverse* the state's interpretation of the federal constitution (Choice B). Once it does so (resolving the federal question), it must then remand the case to the state supreme court for further proceedings on the remaining state-law issues. *A remand would be unnecessary if SCOTUS agreed with (ie, affirmed) the state's interpretation of the federal constitution since there would be no issues remaining to be settled. (Choice A) A state government can seek SCOTUS review of its own state-court decisions so long as they are not based on adequate and independent state grounds (as seen here). (Choice C) States are not required to construe their constitutions in a manner congruent (ie, consistent) with the U.S. Constitution because states can always grant broader constitutional protections.\n\nEducational objective: SCOTUS has discretion to exercise appellate jurisdiction over state supreme court decisions that present a federal question—unless those decisions are based on adequate and independent state grounds. But once it resolves the federal question, SCOTUS must remand the case to the state supreme court if any state-law issues remain.\n\nReferences\n• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 46 (2019) (explaining that state and federal grounds are interwoven when the state court decides that the state provision is identical to the federal provision).","educationalObjective":"SCOTUS has discretion to exercise appellate jurisdiction over state supreme court decisions that present a federal question—unless those decisions are based on adequate and independent state grounds. But once it resolves the federal question, SCOTUS must remand the case to the state supreme court if any state-law issues remain.","references":"• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 46 (2019) (explaining that state and federal grounds are interwoven when the state court decides that the state provision is identical to the federal provision).","rule":"SCOTUS has discretion to exercise appellate jurisdiction over state supreme court decisions that present a federal question—unless those decisions are based on adequate and independent state grounds. But once it resolves the federal question, SCOTUS must remand the case to the state supreme court if any state-law issues remain.","irac":{"issue":"How should the United States Supreme Court dispose of the case if it believes that this interpretation of the federal Constitution by the state supreme court raises an important federal question and is incorrect on the merits?","rule":"SCOTUS has discretion to exercise appellate jurisdiction over state supreme court decisions that present a federal question—unless those decisions are based on adequate and independent state grounds. But once it resolves the federal question, SCOTUS must remand the case to the state supreme court if any state-law issues remain.","analysis":"The Supreme Court of the United States (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction over most final state-court decisions that present a federal question. But SCOTUS cannot exercise jurisdiction over, or review the merits of, such decisions if they are based on adequate and independent state grounds. This occurs when:\n• state law fully resolves the issue and the outcome of the case is not affected by federal law (ie, adequate state grounds) and\n\n• the final state-court decision is reached without relying on federal precedent (ie, independent state grounds).\n\nHere, the state supreme court held that the state tax law violates the equal protection clauses of both the federal and state constitutions (adequate state grounds). But since that decision relied on the federal constitution to interpret the state constitution (no independent state grounds), SCOTUS has jurisdiction to reverse* the state's interpretation of the federal constitution (Choice B). Once it does so (resolving the federal question), it must then remand the case to the state supreme court for further proceedings on the remaining state-law issues. *A remand would be unnecessary if SCOTUS agreed with (ie, affirmed) the state's interpretation of the federal constitution since there would be no issues remaining to be settled. (Choice A) A state government can seek SCOTUS review of its own state-court decisions so long as they are not based on adequate and independent state grounds (as seen here). (Choice C) States are not required to construe their constitutions in a manner congruent (ie, consistent) with the U.S. Constitution because states can always grant broader constitutional protections.","conclusion":"Answer D: Reverse the state supreme court decision with respect to the equal protection clause of the federal Constitution and remand the case to the state supreme court for further proceedings, because the state and federal constitutional issues are so intertwined that the federal issue must be decided so that this case may be disposed of properly."},"questionZh":"原告质疑州税法的合宪性，声称该法违反了美国宪法和州宪法的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。州最高法院同意并裁定税法无效。它说：“我们认为这项州税法违反了美国宪法的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），也违反了州宪法的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为我们将州宪法的该条款解释为包含与美国宪法第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）完全相同的禁止歧视性立法的规定。”该州寻求美国最高法院对这一判决进行复审，声称州最高法院对联邦宪法问题的裁决不正确。如果美国最高法院认为州最高法院对联邦宪法的解释提出了重要的联邦问题并且实质内容不正确，应如何处理此案？","choicesZh":{"A":"拒绝审查州最高法院的决定，因为州政府不得向美国最高法院寻求对其本州法院的决定进行审查。","B":"拒绝审查州最高法院的决定，因为它是基于充分且独立的州法律依据。","C":"推翻州最高法院的判决，因为州宪法的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）必须由州最高法院以与联邦宪法的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的含义一致的方式解释。","D":"推翻州最高法院关于联邦宪法平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的判决，并将案件发回州最高法院进一步审理，因为州和联邦宪法问题相互交织，必须决定联邦问题，才能妥善处理此案。"},"explanationZh":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对大多数涉及联邦问题的州法院最终裁决拥有上诉管辖权。但如果此类决定是基于充分且独立的国家理由，最高法院就不能对此类决定行使管辖权或审查其优点。出现这种情况时：\n• 州法律完全解决了该问题，并且案件的结果不受联邦法律的影响（即有充分的州理由）并且\n\n• 州法院的最终裁决不依赖联邦先例（即独立州理由）。\n\n在此，州最高法院认为州税法违反了联邦和州宪法的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）（充分的州理由）。但由于该决定依赖联邦宪法来解释州宪法（没有独立的州理由），因此最高法院有权推翻*州对联邦宪法的解释（选项B）。一旦这样做（解决联邦问题），它必须将案件发回州最高法院，以就剩余的州法律问题进行进一步的诉讼。 *如果最高法院同意（即确认）州对联邦宪法的解释，则没有必要还押，因为没有任何问题需要解决。 （选择 A）州政府可以寻求 SCOTUS 对其州法院的决定进行审查，只要这些决定不是基于充分且独立的州理由（如此处所示）。 （选择 C）各州不需要以与美国宪法一致（即一致）的方式解释其宪法，因为各州总是可以授予更广泛的宪法保护。\n\n教育目标：最高法院有权对涉及联邦问题的州最高法院判决行使上诉管辖权，除非这些判决是基于充分且独立的州理由。但一旦解决了联邦问题，如果仍然存在任何州法律问题，最高法院必须将案件发回州最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 46 (2019)（解释当州法院裁定州条款与联邦条款相同时，州和联邦理由是交织在一起的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"最高法院有权对涉及联邦问题的州最高法院判决行使上诉管辖权，除非这些判决是基于充分且独立的州理由。但一旦解决了联邦问题，如果仍然存在任何州法律问题，最高法院必须将案件发回州最高法院。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果美国最高法院认为州最高法院对联邦宪法的解释提出了重要的联邦问题并且实质内容不正确，应如何处理此案？","rule":"最高法院有权对涉及联邦问题的州最高法院判决行使上诉管辖权，除非这些判决是基于充分且独立的州理由。但一旦解决了联邦问题，如果仍然存在任何州法律问题，最高法院必须将案件发回州最高法院。","analysis":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对大多数涉及联邦问题的州法院最终裁决拥有上诉管辖权。但如果此类决定是基于充分且独立的国家理由，最高法院就不能对此类决定行使管辖权或审查其优点。出现这种情况时：\n• 州法律完全解决了该问题，并且案件的结果不受联邦法律的影响（即有充分的州理由）并且\n\n• 州法院的最终裁决不依赖联邦先例（即独立州理由）。\n\n在此，州最高法院认为州税法违反了联邦和州宪法的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）（充分的州理由）。但由于该决定依赖联邦宪法来解释州宪法（没有独立的州理由），因此最高法院有权推翻*州对联邦宪法的解释（选项B）。一旦这样做（解决联邦问题），它必须将案件发回州最高法院，以就剩余的州法律问题进行进一步的诉讼。 *如果最高法院同意（即确认）州对联邦宪法的解释，则没有必要还押，因为没有任何问题需要解决。 （选择 A）州政府可以寻求 SCOTUS 对其州法院的决定进行审查，只要这些决定不是基于充分且独立的州理由（如此处所示）。 （选择 C）各州不需要以与美国宪法一致（即一致）的方式解释其宪法，因为各州总是可以授予更广泛的宪法保护。\n\n教育目标：最高法院有权对涉及联邦问题的州最高法院判决行使上诉管辖权，除非这些判决是基于充分且独立的州理由。但一旦解决了联邦问题，如果仍然存在任何州法律问题，最高法院必须将案件发回州最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 46 (2019)（解释当州法院裁定州条款与联邦条款相同时，州和联邦理由是交织在一起的）。","conclusion":"答案 D：推翻州最高法院关于联邦宪法平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的判决，并将案件发回州最高法院进一步审理，因为州和联邦宪法问题相互交织，必须决定联邦问题，才能妥善处理此案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-63e7be27eba330e7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":78,"sourceQuestionPage":233,"sourceQuestionPages":[233],"sourceExplanationPages":[234,235],"sourceAnswerPages":[234,235],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-079","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"Because of a sudden and unanticipated severe shortage of heating fuel, the President has ordered all offices of federal executive agencies to be open only four days per week. The President's order allows an exception to the extent that emergency circumstances require different hours of operation (as in the case of federal hospitals). When Congress enacted the appropriations statute for operating all federal executive agencies, its members assumed that the offices of those agencies would be open five days per week, but Congress did not include such a requirement in its appropriations statute or in any other statute. Is the President's order constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the heads of the various executive agencies have final responsibility for the operation of those agencies' offices.","B":"No, because when they passed the statute appropriating monies for the operation of executive agencies, members of Congress assumed that those agencies' offices would be open five days per week.","C":"Yes, because the Constitution vests the President with plenary authority to direct the administration of all federal agencies in any manner the President deems expedient.","D":"Yes, because the order relates to the management of the executive branch and is not prohibited by any statute."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article II gives the President a wide range of powers, which includes the power to manage federal executive agency operations by issuing executive orders. But Congress can limit those powers when these two branches share authority over a particular area (eg, executive agencies). When this occurs, the Youngstownframework is used to determine the extent of the President's authority to act by considering whether there was congressional:\n• authorization – in which case the President had maximum authority to act and that act is likely valid\n\n• silence – in which case the act is valid so long as it (1) fell within the President's Article II powers and (2) did not interfere with another branch's powers or\n\n• opposition – in which case the President had minimal authority to act and that act is likely invalid.\n\nHere, the President ordered all federal executive agency offices to be open only four days per week. Although Congress had assumed that agency offices would be open five days per week, it remained silent by not including such a requirement in any statute (Choice B). And since the order falls within the President's Article II power to manage executive agencies and does not interfere with another branch's powers, the order is constitutional. (Choice A) The heads of executive agencies have final responsibility for the operation of those agencies' offices. But this does not affect the constitutionality of the President's order since agency heads must comply with operational requirements validly imposed by Congress or the President when fulfilling that responsibility. (Choice C) The President does not have plenary (ie, absolute) authority to direct the administration of federal agencies in any manner the President deems expedient. Instead, the President must comply with the Constitution and relevant federal statutes.\n\nEducational objective: When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.\n\nReferences\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).","educationalObjective":"When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.","references":"• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).","rule":"When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.","irac":{"issue":"Is the President's order constitutional?","rule":"When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.","analysis":"Article II gives the President a wide range of powers, which includes the power to manage federal executive agency operations by issuing executive orders. But Congress can limit those powers when these two branches share authority over a particular area (eg, executive agencies). When this occurs, the Youngstownframework is used to determine the extent of the President's authority to act by considering whether there was congressional:\n• authorization – in which case the President had maximum authority to act and that act is likely valid\n\n• silence – in which case the act is valid so long as it (1) fell within the President's Article II powers and (2) did not interfere with another branch's powers or\n\n• opposition – in which case the President had minimal authority to act and that act is likely invalid.\n\nHere, the President ordered all federal executive agency offices to be open only four days per week. Although Congress had assumed that agency offices would be open five days per week, it remained silent by not including such a requirement in any statute (Choice B). And since the order falls within the President's Article II power to manage executive agencies and does not interfere with another branch's powers, the order is constitutional. (Choice A) The heads of executive agencies have final responsibility for the operation of those agencies' offices. But this does not affect the constitutionality of the President's order since agency heads must comply with operational requirements validly imposed by Congress or the President when fulfilling that responsibility. (Choice C) The President does not have plenary (ie, absolute) authority to direct the administration of federal agencies in any manner the President deems expedient. Instead, the President must comply with the Constitution and relevant federal statutes.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the order relates to the management of the executive branch and is not prohibited by any statute."},"questionZh":"由于取暖燃料突然出现意外严重短缺，总统已下令联邦执行机构的所有办公室每周仅开放四天。总统命令允许例外情况，即紧急情况需要不同的营业时间（如联邦医院的情况）。当国会颁布用于运营所有联邦行政机构的拨款法规时，其成员假设这些机构的办公室每周开放五天，但国会并未将这一要求纳入其拨款法规或任何其他法规中。总统的命令是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为各执行机构的负责人对这些机构办公室的运作负有最终责任。","B":"不，因为当国会议员通过为行政机构运营拨款的法规时，他们认为这些机构的办公室每周开放五天。","C":"是的，因为宪法赋予总统全权，以总统认为有利的任何方式指导所有联邦机构的行政管理。","D":"是的，因为该命令涉及行政部门的管理，并且不受任何法规的禁止。"},"explanationZh":"第二条赋予总统广泛的权力，其中包括通过发布行政命令来管理联邦行政机构运作的权力。但当这两个部门在特定领域（例如行政机构）共享权力时，国会可以限制这些权力。当这种情况发生时，扬斯敦框架用于通过考虑国会是否存在以下情况来确定总统采取行动的权力范围：\n• 授权——在这种情况下，总统拥有采取行动的最大权力，并且该行动可能有效\n\n• 沉默——在这种情况下，只要该行为(1) 属于总统第二条权力范围内并且(2) 不干涉其他部门的权力或\n\n• 反对——在这种情况下，总统的行动权力极小，而且该行动可能无效。\n\n总统在此下令所有联邦执行机构办公室每周仅开放四天。尽管国会假定机构办公室每周开放五天，但它保持沉默，没有在任何法规中纳入这样的要求（选择 B）。由于该命令属于总统管理行政机构的第二条权力，并且不干涉其他部门的权力，因此该命令是宪法性的。 （选择A） 执行机构的负责人对这些机构办公室的运作负有最终责任。但这并不影响总统命令的合宪性，因为机构负责人在履行职责时必须遵守国会或总统有效施加的操作要求。 （选择 C） 总统没有全权（即绝对）权力以总统认为有利的任何方式指导联邦机构的管理。相反，总统必须遵守宪法和相关联邦法规。\n\n教育目标：当国会对总统的行为保持沉默时，只要这些行为 (1) 属于总统的第二条权力范围，并且 (2) 不干涉其他部门的权力，则这些行为有效。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当国会对总统的行动保持沉默时，只要这些行动 (1) 属于总统的第二条权力范围内，并且 (2) 不干涉其他部门的权力，那么这些行动就是有效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统的命令是否符合宪法？","rule":"当国会对总统的行动保持沉默时，只要这些行动 (1) 属于总统的第二条权力范围内，并且 (2) 不干涉其他部门的权力，那么这些行动就是有效的。","analysis":"第二条赋予总统广泛的权力，其中包括通过发布行政命令来管理联邦行政机构运作的权力。但当这两个部门在特定领域（例如行政机构）共享权力时，国会可以限制这些权力。当这种情况发生时，扬斯敦框架用于通过考虑国会是否存在以下情况来确定总统采取行动的权力范围：\n• 授权——在这种情况下，总统拥有采取行动的最大权力，并且该行动可能有效\n\n• 沉默——在这种情况下，只要该行为(1) 属于总统第二条权力范围内并且(2) 不干涉其他部门的权力或\n\n• 反对——在这种情况下，总统的行动权力极小，而且该行动可能无效。\n\n总统在此下令所有联邦执行机构办公室每周仅开放四天。尽管国会假定机构办公室每周开放五天，但它保持沉默，没有在任何法规中纳入这样的要求（选择 B）。由于该命令属于总统管理行政机构的第二条权力，并且不干涉其他部门的权力，因此该命令是宪法性的。 （选择A） 执行机构的负责人对这些机构办公室的运作负有最终责任。但这并不影响总统命令的合宪性，因为机构负责人在履行职责时必须遵守国会或总统有效施加的操作要求。 （选择 C） 总统没有全权（即绝对）权力以总统认为有利的任何方式指导联邦机构的管理。相反，总统必须遵守宪法和相关联邦法规。\n\n教育目标：当国会对总统的行为保持沉默时，只要这些行为 (1) 属于总统的第二条权力范围，并且 (2) 不干涉其他部门的权力，则这些行为有效。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该命令涉及行政部门的管理，并且不受任何法规的禁止。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-39b2a747323b7ff1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":79,"sourceQuestionPage":236,"sourceQuestionPages":[236],"sourceExplanationPages":[237,238],"sourceAnswerPages":[237,238],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-080","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"An employer owed an employee $200 in unpaid wages. A law of the state in which the employer and the employee reside and in which the employee works provides that the courts of that state must decide claims for unpaid wages within 10 days of filing. After the employee filed a claim in state court pursuant to this law, the employer filed a voluntary bankruptcy petition in federal bankruptcy court. In the bankruptcy proceeding, the employer sought to stay further proceedings in the unpaid wages claim on the basis of a federal statute which provides that a person who files a federal bankruptcy petition receives an automatic stay of all proceedings against him or her in all federal and state courts. No other federal laws apply. In addition to the supremacy clause of Article VI, what is the most obvious constitutional basis for the imposition of a stay of the unpaid wages claim in the state court?","choices":{"A":"Congress's power to provide for the general welfare.","B":"Congress's power to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy.","C":"Congress's power to regulate commerce among the states.","D":"Congress's power to regulate the jurisdiction and procedures of the courts."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Enumerated congressional powers\n\n(Article I, Section 8)\n\n• Taxing & spending\n• Interstate & foreign commerce\n• War, armed forces, militia\n• Coin & borrow money\n• Immigration & naturalization\n• Mail\n• Copyright & patent laws\n• Federal courts\n• District of Columbia\n• Bankruptcy\n• Rules concerning captures Here, a state law requires that a state court decide unpaid-wages claims within 10 days of filing. But a federal statute requires an automatic stay of such claims if a party files a federal bankruptcy petition. Since the supremacy clause establishes that federal laws take precedence over conflicting state laws, the federal statute will trump the state law so long as the statute is a valid exercise of Congress's enumerated powers. One enumerated power is the authority to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy across the country, which prevent inconsistent laws and judgments in different states. Therefore, Congress's bankruptcy power provides the most obvious basis to stay the state court proceedings. (Choice A) The taxing and spending clause gives Congress the power to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to regulate judicial procedures (eg, requiring a stay of all proceedings). (Choice C) The commerce clause does not support a federal statute providing for a stay of court proceedings against a person who has filed a federal bankruptcy petition. That is because the statute does not regulate (1) the channels or instrumentalities of interstate commerce or (2) activities that substantially affect interstate commerce (eg, economic activities). (Choice D) Articles I and III give Congress the power to regulate the jurisdiction and procedures of federal courts—not state courts. Therefore, this power does not allow Congress to require a stay of state court proceedings.\n\nEducational objective: The supremacy clause establishes that federal law takes precedence over conflicting state laws so long as the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's enumerated powers—eg, the power to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 4 (bankruptcy clause).\n\n• U.S. Const. art VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• Puerto Rico v. Franklin Cal. Tax-Free Trust, 136 S. Ct. 1938, 1942 (2016) (holding that federal bankruptcy law preempts a municipal bankruptcy scheme).","educationalObjective":"The supremacy clause establishes that federal law takes precedence over conflicting state laws so long as the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's enumerated powers—eg, the power to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 4 (bankruptcy clause).\n\n• U.S. Const. art VI, cl. 2 (supremacy clause).\n\n• Puerto Rico v. Franklin Cal. Tax-Free Trust, 136 S. Ct. 1938, 1942 (2016) (holding that federal bankruptcy law preempts a municipal bankruptcy scheme).","rule":"The supremacy clause establishes that federal law takes precedence over conflicting state laws so long as the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's enumerated powers—eg, the power to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy.","irac":{"issue":"In addition to the supremacy clause of Article VI, what is the most obvious constitutional basis for the imposition of a stay of the unpaid wages claim in the state court?","rule":"The supremacy clause establishes that federal law takes precedence over conflicting state laws so long as the federal law is a valid exercise of Congress's enumerated powers—eg, the power to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy.","analysis":"Enumerated congressional powers\n\n(Article I, Section 8)\n\n• Taxing & spending\n• Interstate & foreign commerce\n• War, armed forces, militia\n• Coin & borrow money\n• Immigration & naturalization\n• Mail\n• Copyright & patent laws\n• Federal courts\n• District of Columbia\n• Bankruptcy\n• Rules concerning captures Here, a state law requires that a state court decide unpaid-wages claims within 10 days of filing. But a federal statute requires an automatic stay of such claims if a party files a federal bankruptcy petition. Since the supremacy clause establishes that federal laws take precedence over conflicting state laws, the federal statute will trump the state law so long as the statute is a valid exercise of Congress's enumerated powers. One enumerated power is the authority to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy across the country, which prevent inconsistent laws and judgments in different states. Therefore, Congress's bankruptcy power provides the most obvious basis to stay the state court proceedings. (Choice A) The taxing and spending clause gives Congress the power to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to regulate judicial procedures (eg, requiring a stay of all proceedings). (Choice C) The commerce clause does not support a federal statute providing for a stay of court proceedings against a person who has filed a federal bankruptcy petition. That is because the statute does not regulate (1) the channels or instrumentalities of interstate commerce or (2) activities that substantially affect interstate commerce (eg, economic activities). (Choice D) Articles I and III give Congress the power to regulate the jurisdiction and procedures of federal courts—not state courts. Therefore, this power does not allow Congress to require a stay of state court proceedings.","conclusion":"Answer B: Congress's power to provide uniform rules of bankruptcy."},"questionZh":"雇主拖欠雇员 200 美元的工资。雇主和雇员居住地以及雇员工作所在州的法律规定，该州法院必须在提出拖欠工资索赔后 10 天内做出裁决。雇员根据本法向州法院提出索赔后，雇主向联邦破产法院提出自愿破产申请。在破产程序中，雇主根据联邦法规寻求中止未付工资索赔的进一步诉讼，该法规规定，提交联邦破产申请的人将自动中止所有联邦和州法院针对他或她的所有诉讼。没有其他联邦法律适用。除了第六条的至高无上条款之外，州法院中止未付工资索赔的最明显的宪法依据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"国会提供公共福利的权力。","B":"国会有权制定统一的破产规则。","C":"国会监管各州之间商业的权力。","D":"国会有权规范法院的管辖权和程序。"},"explanationZh":"列举国会权力\n\n（第一条第八节）\n\n• 税收和支出\n• 州际及对外贸易\n• 战争、武装部队、民兵\n• 投币和借钱\n• 移民和入籍\n• 邮件\n• 版权和专利法\n• 联邦法院\n• 哥伦比亚特区\n• 破产\n• 有关扣押的规则 在此，州法律要求州法院在提出拖欠工资索赔后 10 天内做出裁决。但联邦法规要求，如果一方提交联邦破产申请，此类索赔将自动中止。由于至上条款规定联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律，因此只要该法规有效行使了国会列举的权力，联邦法规就将优先于州法律。其中一项列举的权力是在全国范围内提供统一破产规则的权力，以防止不同州的法律和判决不一致。因此，国会的破产权为中止州法院诉讼提供了最明显的依据。 （选择A）征税和支出条款赋予国会为公共福利征税和支出的权力，而不是规范司法程序（例如，要求中止所有诉讼程序）。 （选项 C）商务条款不支持联邦法规规定针对已提交联邦破产申请的人暂缓法庭诉讼。这是因为该法规没有规范（1）州际贸易的渠道或工具或（2）实质性影响州际贸易的活动（例如经济活动）。 （选项 D）第一条和第三条赋予国会规范联邦法院（而非州法院）的管辖权和程序的权力。因此，这项权力不允许国会要求中止州法院诉讼程序。\n\n教育目标：至上条款规定，只要联邦法律有效行使国会列举的权力（例如，提供统一破产规则的权力），联邦法律就优先于相互冲突的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 4（破产条款）。\n\n• 美国宪法。第六条，第一节。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• 波多黎各诉富兰克林加州案。免税信托，136 S. Ct. 1938、1942 (2016)（认为联邦破产法优先于市政破产计划）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"至上条款规定，只要联邦法律有效行使国会列举的权力（例如，提供统一破产规则的权力），联邦法律就优先于相互冲突的州法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"除了第六条的至高无上条款之外，州法院中止未付工资索赔的最明显的宪法依据是什么？","rule":"至上条款规定，只要联邦法律有效行使国会列举的权力（例如，提供统一破产规则的权力），联邦法律就优先于相互冲突的州法律。","analysis":"列举国会权力\n\n（第一条第八节）\n\n• 税收和支出\n• 州际及对外贸易\n• 战争、武装部队、民兵\n• 投币和借钱\n• 移民和入籍\n• 邮件\n• 版权和专利法\n• 联邦法院\n• 哥伦比亚特区\n• 破产\n• 有关扣押的规则 在此，州法律要求州法院在提出拖欠工资索赔后 10 天内做出裁决。但联邦法规要求，如果一方提交联邦破产申请，此类索赔将自动中止。由于至上条款规定联邦法律优先于相冲突的州法律，因此只要该法规有效行使了国会列举的权力，联邦法规就将优先于州法律。其中一项列举的权力是在全国范围内提供统一破产规则的权力，以防止不同州的法律和判决不一致。因此，国会的破产权为中止州法院诉讼提供了最明显的依据。 （选择A）征税和支出条款赋予国会为公共福利征税和支出的权力，而不是规范司法程序（例如，要求中止所有诉讼程序）。 （选项 C）商务条款不支持联邦法规规定针对已提交联邦破产申请的人暂缓法庭诉讼。这是因为该法规没有规范（1）州际贸易的渠道或工具或（2）实质性影响州际贸易的活动（例如经济活动）。 （选项 D）第一条和第三条赋予国会规范联邦法院（而非州法院）的管辖权和程序的权力。因此，这项权力不允许国会要求中止州法院诉讼程序。\n\n教育目标：至上条款规定，只要联邦法律有效行使国会列举的权力（例如，提供统一破产规则的权力），联邦法律就优先于相互冲突的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 4（破产条款）。\n\n• 美国宪法。第六条，第一节。 2（至上条款）。\n\n• 波多黎各诉富兰克林加州案。免税信托，136 S. Ct. 1938、1942 (2016)（认为联邦破产法优先于市政破产计划）。","conclusion":"答案 B：国会有权制定统一的破产规则。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"stay","ipa":"/stˈeɪ/","syllables":"stay","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 停留, 逗留, 制止, 延缓, 停止, 支柱, 支撑物, 支索；vt. 制止, 延缓, 坚持, 支持, 支撑, 用支索固定；vi. 停留, 逗留, 暂停, 坚持, 中止","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 停留, 逗留, 制止, 延缓, 停止, 支柱, 支撑物, 支索；vt. 制止, 延缓, 坚持, 支持, 支撑, 用支索固定；vi. 停留, 逗留, 暂停, 坚持, 中止","wordForm":"名词；词形 p:stayed/i:staying/d:stayed/3:stays/s:stays","sourceSentence":"In the bankruptcy proceeding, the employer sought to stay further proceedings in the unpaid wages claim on the basis of a federal statute which provides that a person who files a federal bankruptcy petition receives an automatic stay of all proceedings against him or her in all federal and state courts.","synonyms":["arrest","check","halt","hitch"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":80,"sourceQuestionPage":239,"sourceQuestionPages":[239],"sourceExplanationPages":[240,241],"sourceAnswerPages":[240,241],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-081","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"Congress wishes to enact legislation prohibiting discrimination in the sale or rental of housing on the basis of the affectional preference or sexual orientation of the potential purchaser or renter. Congress wishes this statute to apply to all public and private vendors and lessors of residential property in this country, with a few narrowly drawn exceptions. Which of the following clauses would provide the most credible basis for congressional authority to enact such a statute?","choices":{"A":"The commerce clause of Article I, Section 8, because the sale or rental of almost all housing in this country could reasonably be deemed to have a substantial effect on interstate commerce.","B":"The enforcement clause of the Fourteenth Amendment, because that amendment prohibits all public and private actors from engaging in irrational discrimination.","C":"The enforcement clause of the Thirteenth Amendment, because that amendment clearly prohibits discrimination against the class of persons protected by this statute.","D":"The general welfare clause of Article I, Section 8, because the conduct the statute prohibits could reasonably be deemed to be harmful to the national interest."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The Article I, section 8 commerce clause vests Congress with broad authority to regulate interstate commerce (ie, activities affecting two or more states). This includes:\n• the channels of interstate commerce (eg, railroads)\n• the instrumentalities of interstate commerce (eg, trains)\n• persons or things moving in interstate commerce (eg, cargo) and\n• any activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce. Federal statutes enacted under this broad power are constitutional so long as there is a rational or reasonable basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, regulations involving economic activities are presumptively constitutional. Here, Congress wishes to enact a statute prohibiting discrimination based on affectional preference or sexual orientation in the sale or rental of almost all housing in this country. These activities presumably have a substantial effect on interstate commerce since they are economic in nature. Therefore, the commerce clause provides a credible basis for congressional authority to enact the proposed statute. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment enforcement clause gives Congress the authority to enforce the amendment's other provisions. But these provisions (eg, the equal protection clause) only apply to state actors—not all private actors. (Choice C) Under the Thirteenth Amendment enforcement clause, Congress can enact legislation enforcing the amendment's prohibition of slavery and involuntary servitude— which are not at issue here. (Choice D) The Article I, section 8 taxing and spending clause only authorizes Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to prohibit conduct harmful to the national interest.\n\nEducational objective: Federal statutes enacted under the commerce clause are constitutional so long as there is a rational basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, regulations that involve economic activities are presumptively constitutional.\n\nReferences\n• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964) (holding that the commerce clause allows Congress to prohibit racial discrimination by local motels serving travelers).","educationalObjective":"Federal statutes enacted under the commerce clause are constitutional so long as there is a rational basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, regulations that involve economic activities are presumptively constitutional.","references":"• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964) (holding that the commerce clause allows Congress to prohibit racial discrimination by local motels serving travelers).","rule":"Federal statutes enacted under the commerce clause are constitutional so long as there is a rational basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, regulations that involve economic activities are presumptively constitutional.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following clauses would provide the most credible basis for congressional authority to enact such a statute?","rule":"Federal statutes enacted under the commerce clause are constitutional so long as there is a rational basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, regulations that involve economic activities are presumptively constitutional.","analysis":"The Article I, section 8 commerce clause vests Congress with broad authority to regulate interstate commerce (ie, activities affecting two or more states). This includes:\n• the channels of interstate commerce (eg, railroads)\n• the instrumentalities of interstate commerce (eg, trains)\n• persons or things moving in interstate commerce (eg, cargo) and\n• any activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce. Federal statutes enacted under this broad power are constitutional so long as there is a rational or reasonable basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, regulations involving economic activities are presumptively constitutional. Here, Congress wishes to enact a statute prohibiting discrimination based on affectional preference or sexual orientation in the sale or rental of almost all housing in this country. These activities presumably have a substantial effect on interstate commerce since they are economic in nature. Therefore, the commerce clause provides a credible basis for congressional authority to enact the proposed statute. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment enforcement clause gives Congress the authority to enforce the amendment's other provisions. But these provisions (eg, the equal protection clause) only apply to state actors—not all private actors. (Choice C) Under the Thirteenth Amendment enforcement clause, Congress can enact legislation enforcing the amendment's prohibition of slavery and involuntary servitude— which are not at issue here. (Choice D) The Article I, section 8 taxing and spending clause only authorizes Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to prohibit conduct harmful to the national interest.","conclusion":"Answer A: The commerce clause of Article I, Section 8, because the sale or rental of almost all housing in this country could reasonably be deemed to have a substantial effect on interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"国会希望颁布立法，禁止在房屋销售或租赁中基于潜在购买者或承租人的情感偏好或性取向的歧视。国会希望该法规适用于该国住宅物业的所有公共和私人供应商和出租人，但少数例外情况除外。以下哪一项条款将为国会授权颁布此类法规提供最可信的基础？","choicesZh":{"A":"第一条第 8 节的商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause），因为该国几乎所有住房的出售或出租都可以合理地被视为对州际商业产生重大影响。","B":"第十四修正案的执行条款，因为该修正案禁止所有公共和私人行为者从事非理性歧视。","C":"第十三修正案的执行条款，因为该修正案明确禁止歧视受该法规保护的人群。","D":"第一条第八款的一般福利条款，因为该法规所禁止的行为可以合理地被视为损害国家利益。"},"explanationZh":"第一条第 8 款商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业（即影响两个或多个州的活动）。这包括：\n• 州际商业渠道（例如铁路）\n• 州际贸易的工具（例如火车）\n• 在州际贸易中移动的人或物（例如货物）以及\n• 任何单独或总体对州际贸易产生重大影响的活动。只要有合理或合理的基础可以断定受监管的活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，根据这一广泛权力颁布的联邦法规就是合宪的。因此，涉及经济活动的法规被推定是符合宪法的。在这里，国会希望颁布一项法规，禁止在该国几乎所有住房的销售或租赁中基于情感偏好或性取向的歧视。这些活动可能对州际贸易产生重大影响，因为它们本质上是经济活动。因此，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）为国会授权颁布拟议法规提供了可靠的基础。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案执行条款赋予国会执行修正案其他条款的权力。但这些条款（例如，平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））仅适用于国家行为者，而不是所有私人行为者。 （选项C）根据第十三修正案的执行条款，国会可以颁布立法，强制执行该修正案禁止奴隶制和非自愿奴役的规定——这不是这里讨论的问题。 （选项D）第一条第8款征税和支出条款仅授权国会为公共福利征税和支出，而不是禁止损害国家利益的行为。\n\n教育目标：只要有合理的依据得出结论认为受监管的活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）颁布的联邦法规就是合宪的。因此，涉及经济活动的法规被推定是符合宪法的。\n\n参考文献\n• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964)（认为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）允许国会禁止当地汽车旅馆为旅客提供种族歧视）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只要有合理的基础可以断定受监管的活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）颁布的联邦法规就是合宪的。因此，涉及经济活动的法规被推定是符合宪法的。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项条款将为国会授权颁布此类法规提供最可信的基础？","rule":"只要有合理的基础可以断定受监管的活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）颁布的联邦法规就是合宪的。因此，涉及经济活动的法规被推定是符合宪法的。","analysis":"第一条第 8 款商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业（即影响两个或多个州的活动）。这包括：\n• 州际商业渠道（例如铁路）\n• 州际贸易的工具（例如火车）\n• 在州际贸易中移动的人或物（例如货物）以及\n• 任何单独或总体对州际贸易产生重大影响的活动。只要有合理或合理的基础可以断定受监管的活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，根据这一广泛权力颁布的联邦法规就是合宪的。因此，涉及经济活动的法规被推定是符合宪法的。在这里，国会希望颁布一项法规，禁止在该国几乎所有住房的销售或租赁中基于情感偏好或性取向的歧视。这些活动可能对州际贸易产生重大影响，因为它们本质上是经济活动。因此，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）为国会授权颁布拟议法规提供了可靠的基础。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案执行条款赋予国会执行修正案其他条款的权力。但这些条款（例如，平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））仅适用于国家行为者，而不是所有私人行为者。 （选项C）根据第十三修正案的执行条款，国会可以颁布立法，强制执行该修正案禁止奴隶制和非自愿奴役的规定——这不是这里讨论的问题。 （选项D）第一条第8款征税和支出条款仅授权国会为公共福利征税和支出，而不是禁止损害国家利益的行为。\n\n教育目标：只要有合理的依据得出结论认为受监管的活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）颁布的联邦法规就是合宪的。因此，涉及经济活动的法规被推定是符合宪法的。\n\n参考文献\n• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964)（认为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）允许国会禁止当地汽车旅馆为旅客提供种族歧视）。","conclusion":"答案 A：第一条第 8 节的商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause），因为该国几乎所有住房的出售或出租都可以合理地被视为对州际商业产生重大影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"residential","ipa":"/ɹˌɛzɪdˈɛnʃəl/","syllables":"res·i·den·tial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 住宅的, 与居住有关的；[法] 有关居住的, 房产的:居所的, 适于居住的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 住宅的, 与居住有关的；[法] 有关居住的, 房产的:居所的, 适于居住的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Congress wishes this statute to apply to all public and private vendors and lessors of residential property in this country, with a few narrowly drawn exceptions.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-9e02bd68d0baa40f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":81,"sourceQuestionPage":242,"sourceQuestionPages":[242],"sourceExplanationPages":[243,244],"sourceAnswerPages":[243,244],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-082","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"In the wake of massive terrorist attacks carried out inside the United States by foreign citizens, Congress declared war on the terrorists' nation of origin. It also passed a statute requiring every permanent resident who is a citizen of the enemy nation to either immediately leave the United States voluntarily or be subject to deportation. An inseverable provision of the new statute provides that the United States Supreme Court will have original and exclusive jurisdiction over any action brought to challenge the validity of the statute. Is the new statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because cases brought under the statute do not fall within the categories of cases specified in Article III as those over which the Supreme Court shall have original jurisdiction.","B":"No, because the statute violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment.","C":"Yes, because among the powers of Congress enumerated in Article I, Section 8, is the power to enact laws governing immigration and naturalization.","D":"Yes, because Article III specifically provides that the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court shall be subject to such exceptions and regulations as Congress shall make."},"answer":"A","explanation":"SCOTUS jurisdiction\n\nType Definition Applicability\n\nOriginal Case filed directly in Cases involving either: SCOTUS (Congress cannot\n• ambassador/public adjust) minister/consul or\n• state as party Appellate Case filed after decision by All other cases by either: either: (Congress may\n• writ of certiorari or limit)\n• lower federal\n• direct appeal (rare) court or\n• highest state court SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nOriginal jurisdiction is a court's power to hear and decide a case first—ie, before any appellate review. Article III provides that the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has original jurisdiction over:\n• cases involving foreign ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls and\n• cases in which a state is a party. And though Congress has the power to limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction, Congress cannot alter SCOTUS's original jurisdiction (Choice D). Here, Congress passed a statute requiring every permanent resident who is a citizen of the enemy nation to leave the United States voluntarily or be subject to deportation. The statute provides that SCOTUS will have original and exclusive jurisdiction over any action challenging the statute's validity. Since this inseverable provision purports to expand SCOTUS's original jurisdiction beyond the categories of cases specified in Article III, the statute is unconstitutional. (Choice B) Federal classifications based on citizenship are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny under the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment. That is because Congress has plenary (ie, exclusive) authority to make immigration policy. And since the deportation order is rationally related to the government's legitimate interest in national security, the statute does not violate this component. (Choice C) Article I, Section 8 enumerates Congress's powers, including the power to enact laws governing immigration and naturalization. But Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising these powers (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The United States Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over (1) cases in which a state is a party and (2) cases involving foreign ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls. Congress cannot expand or limit this jurisdiction.\n\nReferences\n• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803) (holding that Congress cannot expand the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 453 (2019) (defining original and appellate jurisdiction).","educationalObjective":"The United States Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over (1) cases in which a state is a party and (2) cases involving foreign ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls. Congress cannot expand or limit this jurisdiction.","references":"• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803) (holding that Congress cannot expand the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 453 (2019) (defining original and appellate jurisdiction).","rule":"The United States Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over (1) cases in which a state is a party and (2) cases involving foreign ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls. Congress cannot expand or limit this jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"Is the new statute constitutional?","rule":"The United States Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over (1) cases in which a state is a party and (2) cases involving foreign ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls. Congress cannot expand or limit this jurisdiction.","analysis":"SCOTUS jurisdiction\n\nType Definition Applicability\n\nOriginal Case filed directly in Cases involving either: SCOTUS (Congress cannot\n• ambassador/public adjust) minister/consul or\n• state as party Appellate Case filed after decision by All other cases by either: either: (Congress may\n• writ of certiorari or limit)\n• lower federal\n• direct appeal (rare) court or\n• highest state court SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nOriginal jurisdiction is a court's power to hear and decide a case first—ie, before any appellate review. Article III provides that the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has original jurisdiction over:\n• cases involving foreign ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls and\n• cases in which a state is a party. And though Congress has the power to limit SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction, Congress cannot alter SCOTUS's original jurisdiction (Choice D). Here, Congress passed a statute requiring every permanent resident who is a citizen of the enemy nation to leave the United States voluntarily or be subject to deportation. The statute provides that SCOTUS will have original and exclusive jurisdiction over any action challenging the statute's validity. Since this inseverable provision purports to expand SCOTUS's original jurisdiction beyond the categories of cases specified in Article III, the statute is unconstitutional. (Choice B) Federal classifications based on citizenship are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny under the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment. That is because Congress has plenary (ie, exclusive) authority to make immigration policy. And since the deportation order is rationally related to the government's legitimate interest in national security, the statute does not violate this component. (Choice C) Article I, Section 8 enumerates Congress's powers, including the power to enact laws governing immigration and naturalization. But Congress cannot violate other constitutional provisions when exercising these powers (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because cases brought under the statute do not fall within the categories of cases specified in Article III as those over which the Supreme Court shall have original jurisdiction."},"questionZh":"在外国公民在美国境内实施大规模恐怖袭击后，国会向恐怖分子的原籍国宣战。它还通过了一项法规，要求每一位敌国公民的永久居民要么立即自愿离开美国，要么被驱逐出境。新法规的一项不可分割的条款规定，美国最高法院将对任何质疑该法规有效性的诉讼拥有原始和专属管辖权。新法规合宪吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为根据本法提起的案件不属于第三条规定的最高法院具有初审管辖权的案件类别。","B":"不，因为该法规违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分。","C":"是的，因为第一条第 8 节列举的国会权力包括制定移民和入籍法律的权力。","D":"是的，因为第三条明确规定，最高法院的管辖权应遵守国会制定的例外和规定。"},"explanationZh":"最高法院管辖权\n\n类型 定义 适用性\n\n直接提交的原始案件涉及以下任一案件： SCOTUS（国会不能\n• 大使/公共调整）部长/领事或\n• 州作为当事方 在做出决定后提起上诉案件 所有其他案件均通过以下方式之一：（国会可以\n• 调卷令或限制令）\n• 下级联邦\n• 直接上诉（罕见）法院或\n• 最高州法院 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n初审管辖权是法院首先审理并裁决案件的权力，即在任何上诉审查之前。第三条规定，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对以下事项拥有初审管辖权：\n• 涉及外国大使、公务部长或领事的案件以及\n• 国家为当事方的案件。尽管国会有权限制 SCOTUS 的上诉管辖权，但国会不能改变 SCOTUS 的原始管辖权（选项 D）。在这里，国会通过了一项法规，要求所有敌国公民的永久居民自愿离开美国，否则将被驱逐出境。该法规规定，最高法院将对任何质疑该法规有效性的行动拥有原始和专属管辖权。由于这一不可分割的条款旨在将最高法院的原始管辖权扩大到第三条规定的案件类别之外，因此该法规违宪。 （选择 B）基于公民身份的联邦分类仅受第五修正案平等保护部分的合理基础审查（rational basis review）审查（rational basis review）审查。这是因为国会拥有制定移民政策的全权（即排他性）权力。由于驱逐令与政府在国家安全方面的合法利益合理相关，因此该法规并不违反这一组成部分。 （选项 C）第一条第 8 节列举了国会的权力，包括颁布移民和入籍法律的权力。但国会在行使这些权力时不能违反其他宪法条款（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院对 (1) 以州为一方的案件和 (2) 涉及外国大使、公使或领事的案件拥有初审管辖权。国会不能扩大或限制这一管辖权。\n\n参考文献\n• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803)（认为国会不能扩大最高法院的原始管辖权）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 453 (2019)（定义初审管辖权和上诉管辖权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"美国最高法院对 (1) 以州为当事人的案件和 (2) 涉及外国大使、公使或领事的案件拥有初审管辖权。国会不能扩大或限制这一管辖权。","iracZh":{"issue":"新法规合宪吗？","rule":"美国最高法院对 (1) 以州为当事人的案件和 (2) 涉及外国大使、公使或领事的案件拥有初审管辖权。国会不能扩大或限制这一管辖权。","analysis":"最高法院管辖权\n\n类型 定义 适用性\n\n直接提交的原始案件涉及以下任一案件： SCOTUS（国会不能\n• 大使/公共调整）部长/领事或\n• 州作为当事方 在做出决定后提起上诉案件 所有其他案件均通过以下方式之一：（国会可以\n• 调卷令或限制令）\n• 下级联邦\n• 直接上诉（罕见）法院或\n• 最高州法院 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n初审管辖权是法院首先审理并裁决案件的权力，即在任何上诉审查之前。第三条规定，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 对以下事项拥有初审管辖权：\n• 涉及外国大使、公务部长或领事的案件以及\n• 国家为当事方的案件。尽管国会有权限制 SCOTUS 的上诉管辖权，但国会不能改变 SCOTUS 的原始管辖权（选项 D）。在这里，国会通过了一项法规，要求所有敌国公民的永久居民自愿离开美国，否则将被驱逐出境。该法规规定，最高法院将对任何质疑该法规有效性的行动拥有原始和专属管辖权。由于这一不可分割的条款旨在将最高法院的原始管辖权扩大到第三条规定的案件类别之外，因此该法规违宪。 （选择 B）基于公民身份的联邦分类仅受第五修正案平等保护部分的合理基础审查（rational basis review）审查（rational basis review）审查。这是因为国会拥有制定移民政策的全权（即排他性）权力。由于驱逐令与政府在国家安全方面的合法利益合理相关，因此该法规并不违反这一组成部分。 （选项 C）第一条第 8 节列举了国会的权力，包括颁布移民和入籍法律的权力。但国会在行使这些权力时不能违反其他宪法条款（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院对 (1) 以州为一方的案件和 (2) 涉及外国大使、公使或领事的案件拥有初审管辖权。国会不能扩大或限制这一管辖权。\n\n参考文献\n• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803)（认为国会不能扩大最高法院的原始管辖权）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 453 (2019)（定义初审管辖权和上诉管辖权）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不能，因为根据本法提起的案件不属于第三条规定的最高法院具有初审管辖权的案件类别。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"classifications","ipa":"/klˌæsʌfʌkˈeɪʃənz/","syllables":"clas·si·fi·ca·tions","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 分类( classification的复数形式 )； 分级； 类别","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 分类( classification的复数形式 )； 分级； 类别","wordForm":"名词；原形 classification；词形 0:classification/1:s","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Federal classifications based on citizenship are subject to mere rational basis scrutiny under the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment.","synonyms":["categorization","categorisation","compartmentalization","compartmentalisation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":82,"sourceQuestionPage":245,"sourceQuestionPages":[245],"sourceExplanationPages":[246,247],"sourceAnswerPages":[246,247],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-083","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"The President issued an executive order in an effort to encourage U.S. citizens to use the metric (Celsius) system of temperatures. Section 1 of the executive order requires the U.S. Weather Bureau, a federal executive agency, to state temperatures only in Celsius in all weather reports. Section 2 of the executive order requires all privately owned federally licensed radio and television stations giving weather reports to report temperatures only in Celsius. No federal statute is applicable. Is the President's executive order constitutional?","choices":{"A":"Section 1 is constitutional, but section 2 is not.","B":"Section 2 is constitutional, but section 1 is not.","C":"Sections 1 and 2 are constitutional.","D":"Sections 1 and 2 are unconstitutional."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Article II gives the President, as the country's chief executive, the authority to direct the actions of federal executive agencies (eg, the U.S. Weather Bureau). However, Congress also has authority over these agencies under the nondelegation doctrine, so the President must act with congressional approval or in a manner consistent with an act of Congress. And since there is no applicable statute that conflicts with the executive order, section 1 of that order is constitutional (Choices B & D). Additionally, the President generally has no Article II authority to direct the actions of persons outside of the executive branch (eg, privately owned companies) without congressional approval. There are no circumstances presented in the facts that might justify an exception to this general rule (eg, a sudden attack on the United States). Therefore, section 2 of the executive order is unconstitutional (Choices B & C).\n\nEducational objective: When the President and Congress share authority over a particular area, the President's Article II powers must be exercised with congressional approval or in a manner that is consistent with an act of Congress.\n\nReferences\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).","educationalObjective":"When the President and Congress share authority over a particular area, the President's Article II powers must be exercised with congressional approval or in a manner that is consistent with an act of Congress.","references":"• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).","rule":"When the President and Congress share authority over a particular area, the President's Article II powers must be exercised with congressional approval or in a manner that is consistent with an act of Congress.","irac":{"issue":"Is the President's executive order constitutional?","rule":"When the President and Congress share authority over a particular area, the President's Article II powers must be exercised with congressional approval or in a manner that is consistent with an act of Congress.","analysis":"Article II gives the President, as the country's chief executive, the authority to direct the actions of federal executive agencies (eg, the U.S. Weather Bureau). However, Congress also has authority over these agencies under the nondelegation doctrine, so the President must act with congressional approval or in a manner consistent with an act of Congress. And since there is no applicable statute that conflicts with the executive order, section 1 of that order is constitutional (Choices B & D). Additionally, the President generally has no Article II authority to direct the actions of persons outside of the executive branch (eg, privately owned companies) without congressional approval. There are no circumstances presented in the facts that might justify an exception to this general rule (eg, a sudden attack on the United States). Therefore, section 2 of the executive order is unconstitutional (Choices B & C).","conclusion":"Answer A: Section 1 is constitutional, but section 2 is not."},"questionZh":"总统发布了一项行政命令，鼓励美国公民使用公制（摄氏度）温度系统。该行政命令的第 1 条要求联邦执行机构美国气象局在所有天气报告中仅以摄氏度表示气温。该行政命令的第 2 条要求所有提供天气预报的私营联邦许可广播电台和电视台只能以摄氏度报告温度。没有适用的联邦法规。总统的行政命令是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"第 1 条符合宪法，但第 2 条则不符合宪法。","B":"第 2 条符合宪法，但第 1 条则不符合宪法。","C":"第 1 条和第 2 条符合宪法。","D":"第 1 条和第 2 条违宪。"},"explanationZh":"第二条赋予总统作为国家行政长官指导联邦执行机构（例如美国气象局）行动的权力。然而，根据非授权原则，国会也对这些机构拥有权力，因此总统必须得到国会批准或以符合国会法案的方式行事。由于不存在与行政命令相冲突的适用法规，因此该命令的第 1 条符合宪法（选择 B 和 D）。此外，未经国会批准，总统通常没有第二条权力来指导行政部门以外的人员（例如私营公司）的行动。事实中没有任何情况可以证明这一一般规则的例外（例如，对美国的突然袭击）。因此，行政命令第 2 条违宪（选项 B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：当总统和国会在某一特定领域分享权力时，总统的第二条权力必须得到国会批准或以符合国会法案的方式行使。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当总统和国会在某一特定领域分享权力时，总统的第二条权力必须经国会批准或以符合国会法案的方式行使。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统的行政命令是否符合宪法？","rule":"当总统和国会在某一特定领域分享权力时，总统的第二条权力必须经国会批准或以符合国会法案的方式行使。","analysis":"第二条赋予总统作为国家行政长官指导联邦执行机构（例如美国气象局）行动的权力。然而，根据非授权原则，国会也对这些机构拥有权力，因此总统必须得到国会批准或以符合国会法案的方式行事。由于不存在与行政命令相冲突的适用法规，因此该命令的第 1 条符合宪法（选择 B 和 D）。此外，未经国会批准，总统通常没有第二条权力来指导行政部门以外的人员（例如私营公司）的行动。事实中没有任何情况可以证明这一一般规则的例外（例如，对美国的突然袭击）。因此，行政命令第 2 条违宪（选项 B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：当总统和国会在某一特定领域分享权力时，总统的第二条权力必须得到国会批准或以符合国会法案的方式行使。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。","conclusion":"答案 A：第 1 条符合宪法，但第 2 条则不符合宪法。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-988f8704cd7cc299"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":83,"sourceQuestionPage":248,"sourceQuestionPages":[248],"sourceExplanationPages":[249,250],"sourceAnswerPages":[249,250],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-084","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A U.S. senator made a speech on the floor of the Senate accusing a low-level purchasing officer employed by a federal agency of wasting millions of dollars of taxpayer money by purchasing many more office supplies than the agency needed. The accusation was demonstrably false, and the senator was negligent in making it. The purchasing officer has sued the senator for defamation, alleging only that the accusation was false and that the senator was negligent. What is the most appropriate ground for the court to dismiss the purchasing officer's complaint?","choices":{"A":"The federal government is constitutionally immune from suit without its consent, and it has not consented to suits of this kind.","B":"The First Amendment guarantees members of Congress an unqualified right to speak on matters of public concern at any place and time without having to fear adverse legal consequences.","C":"The First Amendment protects public officials from defamation liability for statements made in their official capacity, unless the plaintiff alleges and proves that the statement was false and uttered with actual malice.","D":"The speech or debate clause of Article I, section 6 of the Constitution wholly insulates members of Congress from tort liability for statements made on the floor of Congress."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Speech or debate clause\n\n(immunity for members of Congress & their aides)\n\nConduct Examples Immunity\n\nLegislative\n• Statements made on House or Immunity from criminal activity Senate floor & civil liability\n• Drafting & enacting legislation\n• Conducting investigations & research\n• Committee hearings & reports\n• Preparing & distributing reports within Congress Nonlegislative\n• Statements made outside No immunity activity Congress\n• Republishing statements made in Congress\n• Writing newsletters & press releases\n• Bribery Under the speech or debate clause of Article I, section 6, members of Congress are immune from criminal and civil liability for any official legislative activity—eg, speeches given on the House or Senate floor. This ensures that members of Congress are free to represent their constituents' interests without fear that they will later be liable in court for that representation. Here, the senator made a speech on the Senate floor that falsely and negligently accused the purchasing officer of wasting millions of dollars of taxpayer money. Therefore, the most appropriate ground for the court to dismiss the officer's complaint is that the speech or debate clause wholly insulates the senator from tort liability. (Choice A) The federal government is constitutionally immune from suit without its consent. However, this immunity does not extend to suits against federal legislators in their individual capacities (as seen here). (Choice B) Members of Congress do not have an unqualified right to speak on matters of public concern without fear of adverse legal consequences. But the speech or debate clause insulates them from liability for any speech that occurs in the course of official legislative activity.\n\n(Choice C) The First Amendment heightens the standard for proving defamation when the plaintiff is a prominent public official. In that case, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant's statement was false and made with actual malice. But the speech or debate clause protects members of Congress from defamation liability for statements made in their official capacity—regardless of whether the plaintiff can meet that heightened standard.\n\nEducational objective: The speech or debate clause immunizes members of Congress from criminal and civil liability for official legislative activities—eg, speeches given on the House or Senate floor.\n\nReferences\n• Hutchinson v. Proxmire, 443 U.S. 111, 130 (1979) (stating that a speech made on the Senate floor would be wholly immune from a defamation suit).\n\n• 77 Am. Jur. 2d United States § 7 (2019) (explaining that the speech or debate clause grants legislative immunity).","educationalObjective":"The speech or debate clause immunizes members of Congress from criminal and civil liability for official legislative activities—eg, speeches given on the House or Senate floor.","references":"• Hutchinson v. Proxmire, 443 U.S. 111, 130 (1979) (stating that a speech made on the Senate floor would be wholly immune from a defamation suit).\n\n• 77 Am. Jur. 2d United States § 7 (2019) (explaining that the speech or debate clause grants legislative immunity).","rule":"The speech or debate clause immunizes members of Congress from criminal and civil liability for official legislative activities—eg, speeches given on the House or Senate floor.","irac":{"issue":"What is the most appropriate ground for the court to dismiss the purchasing officer's complaint?","rule":"The speech or debate clause immunizes members of Congress from criminal and civil liability for official legislative activities—eg, speeches given on the House or Senate floor.","analysis":"Speech or debate clause\n\n(immunity for members of Congress & their aides)\n\nConduct Examples Immunity\n\nLegislative\n• Statements made on House or Immunity from criminal activity Senate floor & civil liability\n• Drafting & enacting legislation\n• Conducting investigations & research\n• Committee hearings & reports\n• Preparing & distributing reports within Congress Nonlegislative\n• Statements made outside No immunity activity Congress\n• Republishing statements made in Congress\n• Writing newsletters & press releases\n• Bribery Under the speech or debate clause of Article I, section 6, members of Congress are immune from criminal and civil liability for any official legislative activity—eg, speeches given on the House or Senate floor. This ensures that members of Congress are free to represent their constituents' interests without fear that they will later be liable in court for that representation. Here, the senator made a speech on the Senate floor that falsely and negligently accused the purchasing officer of wasting millions of dollars of taxpayer money. Therefore, the most appropriate ground for the court to dismiss the officer's complaint is that the speech or debate clause wholly insulates the senator from tort liability. (Choice A) The federal government is constitutionally immune from suit without its consent. However, this immunity does not extend to suits against federal legislators in their individual capacities (as seen here). (Choice B) Members of Congress do not have an unqualified right to speak on matters of public concern without fear of adverse legal consequences. But the speech or debate clause insulates them from liability for any speech that occurs in the course of official legislative activity.\n\n(Choice C) The First Amendment heightens the standard for proving defamation when the plaintiff is a prominent public official. In that case, the plaintiff must prove that the defendant's statement was false and made with actual malice. But the speech or debate clause protects members of Congress from defamation liability for statements made in their official capacity—regardless of whether the plaintiff can meet that heightened standard.","conclusion":"Answer D: The speech or debate clause of Article I, section 6 of the Constitution wholly insulates members of Congress from tort liability for statements made on the floor of Congress."},"questionZh":"一位美国参议员在参议院发表讲话，指责联邦机构雇用的一名低级采购官员购买了超出该机构所需的办公用品，浪费了数百万美元纳税人的钱。这一指控显然是错误的，而且参议员的指控也存在疏忽。采购官员以诽谤罪起诉参议员，仅指控指控不实且参议员疏忽大意。法院驳回采购官员申诉的最适当理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"根据宪法，联邦政府在未经其同意的情况下免于提起诉讼，而且联邦政府也没有同意此类诉讼。","B":"第一修正案保障国会议员在任何地点和时间拥有就公众关心的问题发表言论的无条件权利，而不必担心不利的法律后果。","C":"第一修正案保护公职人员以其官方身份发表的言论免受诽谤责任，除非原告声称并证明该言论是虚假的并且是出于真正的恶意而发表的。","D":"宪法第一条第六节的演讲或辩论条款完全使国会议员免受在国会发言的侵权责任。"},"explanationZh":"演讲或辩论条款\n\n（国会议员及其助手的豁免权）\n\n行为示例 免疫力\n\n立法\n• 关于众议院或犯罪活动豁免权参议院发言及民事责任的声明\n• 起草和颁布立法\n• 进行调查和研究\n• 委员会听证会和报告\n• 在国会非立法部门内准备和分发报告\n• 在国会外发表的声明 无豁免活动\n• 重新发表国会声明\n• 撰写时事通讯和新闻稿\n• 贿赂 根据第一条第6 节的演讲或辩论条款，国会议员对于任何官方立法活动（例如在众议院或参议院发表的演讲）均免于承担刑事和民事责任。这确保了国会议员可以自由地代表其选民的利益，而不必担心他们以后会因这种代表而在法庭上承担责任。在这里，参议员在参议院发表演讲，错误地、疏忽地指责采购官员浪费了数百万美元的纳税人的钱。因此，法院驳回该官员申诉的最合适理由是演讲或辩论条款完全使参议员免于承担侵权责任。 （选择 A） 根据宪法，联邦政府在未经其同意的情况下免受诉讼。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于以个人身份针对联邦立法者提起的诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选择B）国会议员没有无条件的权利就公众关心的问题发表意见，而不必担心不利的法律后果。但言论或辩论条款（Speech or Debate Clause）（Speech or Debate Clause）使他们免于对官方立法活动过程中发生的任何言论承担责任。\n\n（选项 C） 第一修正案提高了当原告是知名公职人员时证明诽谤的标准。在这种情况下，原告必须证明被告的陈述是虚假的并且是出于真正的恶意。但演讲或辩论条款保护国会议员免受以官方身份发表的言论的诽谤责任，无论原告是否能够满足这一更高的标准。\n\n教育目标：演讲或辩论条款使国会议员免受官方立法活动（例如在众议院或参议院发表演讲）的刑事和民事责任。\n\n参考文献\n• Hutchinson v. Proxmire, 443 U.S. 111, 130 (1979)（指出在参议院发表的演讲将完全免受诽谤诉讼）。\n\n• 上午 77 点。尤尔。 2d United States § 7 (2019)（解释演讲或辩论条款授予立法豁免权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"演讲或辩论条款使国会议员免受官方立法活动（例如在众议院或参议院发表演讲）的刑事和民事责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院驳回采购官员申诉的最适当理由是什么？","rule":"演讲或辩论条款使国会议员免受官方立法活动（例如在众议院或参议院发表演讲）的刑事和民事责任。","analysis":"演讲或辩论条款\n\n（国会议员及其助手的豁免权）\n\n行为示例 免疫力\n\n立法\n• 关于众议院或犯罪活动豁免权参议院发言及民事责任的声明\n• 起草和颁布立法\n• 进行调查和研究\n• 委员会听证会和报告\n• 在国会非立法部门内准备和分发报告\n• 在国会外发表的声明 无豁免活动\n• 重新发表国会声明\n• 撰写时事通讯和新闻稿\n• 贿赂 根据第一条第6 节的演讲或辩论条款，国会议员对于任何官方立法活动（例如在众议院或参议院发表的演讲）均免于承担刑事和民事责任。这确保了国会议员可以自由地代表其选民的利益，而不必担心他们以后会因这种代表而在法庭上承担责任。在这里，参议员在参议院发表演讲，错误地、疏忽地指责采购官员浪费了数百万美元的纳税人的钱。因此，法院驳回该官员申诉的最合适理由是演讲或辩论条款完全使参议员免于承担侵权责任。 （选择 A） 根据宪法，联邦政府在未经其同意的情况下免受诉讼。然而，这种豁免权并不适用于以个人身份针对联邦立法者提起的诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选择B）国会议员没有无条件的权利就公众关心的问题发表意见，而不必担心不利的法律后果。但言论或辩论条款（Speech or Debate Clause）（Speech or Debate Clause）使他们免于对官方立法活动过程中发生的任何言论承担责任。\n\n（选项 C） 第一修正案提高了当原告是知名公职人员时证明诽谤的标准。在这种情况下，原告必须证明被告的陈述是虚假的并且是出于真正的恶意。但演讲或辩论条款保护国会议员免受以官方身份发表的言论的诽谤责任，无论原告是否能够满足这一更高的标准。\n\n教育目标：演讲或辩论条款使国会议员免受官方立法活动（例如在众议院或参议院发表演讲）的刑事和民事责任。\n\n参考文献\n• Hutchinson v. Proxmire, 443 U.S. 111, 130 (1979)（指出在参议院发表的演讲将完全免受诽谤诉讼）。\n\n• 上午 77 点。尤尔。 2d United States § 7 (2019)（解释演讲或辩论条款授予立法豁免权）。","conclusion":"答案 D：宪法第一条第六节的演讲或辩论条款完全使国会议员免受在国会发言的侵权责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"accusation","ipa":"/ˌækjʌzˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"ac·cu·sa·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 控告, 指控, 指责；[法] 控告, 起诉, 告发","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 控告, 指控, 指责；[法] 控告, 起诉, 告发","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:accusations","sourceSentence":"The accusation was demonstrably false, and the senator was negligent in making it.","synonyms":["charge","accusal"]},{"term":"defamation","ipa":"/dˌɛfʌmˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"defama·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 破坏名誉, 中伤, 诽谤；[法] 破坏名誉, 中伤, 毁谤","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 破坏名誉, 中伤, 诽谤；[法] 破坏名誉, 中伤, 毁谤","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:defamations","sourceSentence":"The purchasing officer has sued the senator for defamation, alleging only that the accusation was false and that the senator was negligent.","synonyms":["aspersion","calumny","slander","denigration"]},{"term":"accused","ipa":"/ʌkjˈuzd/","syllables":"ac·cused","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 被告","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 被告","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:accuse/1:dp/d:accused/p:accused","sourceSentence":"Here, the senator made a speech on the Senate floor that falsely and negligently accused the purchasing officer of wasting millions of dollars of taxpayer money.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"press","ipa":"/pɹˈɛs/","syllables":"press","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 压, 揿, 按, 人群, 印刷机, 压力, 出版社, 记者, 报刊, 新闻舆论, 紧迫；vt. 压, 压榨, 紧抱, 逼迫, 推进, 强迫征募, 催逼；vi. 压, 重压, 催促, 拥挤, 奋力前进, 受压","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 压, 揿, 按, 人群, 印刷机, 压力, 出版社, 记者, 报刊, 新闻舆论, 紧迫；vt. 压, 压榨, 紧抱, 逼迫, 推进, 强迫征募, 催逼；vi. 压, 重压, 催促, 拥挤, 奋力前进, 受压","wordForm":"名词；词形 p:pressed/d:pressed/i:pressing/s:presses/3:presses","sourceSentence":"Speech or debate clause (immunity for members of Congress & their aides) Conduct Examples Immunity Legislative • Statements made on House or Immunity from criminal activity Senate floor & civil liability • Drafting & enacting legislation • Conducting investigations & research • Committee hearings & reports • Preparing & distributing reports within Congress Nonlegislative • Statements made outside No immunity activity","synonyms":["pressure","pressing","military press","mechanical press"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":84,"sourceQuestionPage":251,"sourceQuestionPages":[251],"sourceExplanationPages":[252,253],"sourceAnswerPages":[252,253],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-085","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Political Question","question":"The United States had long recognized the ruling faction in a foreign country as that country's government, despite an ongoing civil war. Throughout the civil war, the ruling faction controlled the majority of the country's territory, and the United States afforded diplomatic immunity to the ambassador representing the ruling faction. A newly elected President of the United States decided to recognize a rebel group as the government of the foreign country and notified the ambassador from the ruling faction that she must leave the United States within 10 days. The ambassador filed an action in federal district court for a declaration that the ruling faction was the true government of the foreign country and for an injunction against enforcement of the President's order that she leave the United States. The United States has moved to dismiss the action. If the court dismisses the action, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The action involves a nonjusticiable political question.","B":"The action is not ripe.","C":"The action is within the original jurisdiction of the U.S. Supreme Court.","D":"The ambassador does not have standing."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Article III of the Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide actual cases and controversies. But the political-question doctrine prohibits federal courts from adjudicating political questions—ie, issues that (1) the Constitution reserves to the legislative and executive branches or (2) lack judicially discoverable and manageable standards for resolution. When this occurs, the federal court must dismiss the suit. Here, the ambassador's suit for a declaratory judgment and an injunction concerned the President's recognition of a rebel group as the foreign country's true government. But such power is reserved to the executive branch under the Constitution, and there are no standards to help a court resolve this dispute. Therefore, the court will most likely dismiss this action because it involves a nonjusticiable political question. (Choice B) Under Article III's case-or-controversy requirement, a suit must be ripe for adjudication. This means that the plaintiff must have suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. Here, the ambassador's action is ripe because she has been ordered to leave the U.S. within 10 days (immediate threat of harm). (Choice C) The U.S. Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases affecting ambassadors, so the ambassador's suit could have been filed directly in the Supreme Court. But original jurisdiction over ambassadors is not exclusive, so a lower federal court could also adjudicate this case if it was justiciable. (Choice D) The ambassador does have standing because she suffered an injury-in-fact (expulsion) that was caused by the challenged action (President's order) and is redressable by the remedy sought (injunction).\n\nEducational objective: Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot decide issues that (1) the Constitution reserves to the other branches of government or (2) lack judicially discoverable and manageable standards for resolution. Therefore, suits presenting these nonjusticiable political questions must be dismissed.\n\nReferences\n• Jones v. United States, 137 U.S. 202, 212 (1890) (stating that the recognition of a foreign government and its representatives is a nonjusticiable political question).\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962) (discussing the political-question doctrine).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Fed. Courts § 574 (2019) (listing examples of political questions).","educationalObjective":"Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot decide issues that (1) the Constitution reserves to the other branches of government or (2) lack judicially discoverable and manageable standards for resolution. Therefore, suits presenting these nonjusticiable political questions must be dismissed.","references":"• Jones v. United States, 137 U.S. 202, 212 (1890) (stating that the recognition of a foreign government and its representatives is a nonjusticiable political question).\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962) (discussing the political-question doctrine).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Fed. Courts § 574 (2019) (listing examples of political questions).","rule":"Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot decide issues that (1) the Constitution reserves to the other branches of government or (2) lack judicially discoverable and manageable standards for resolution. Therefore, suits presenting these nonjusticiable political questions must be dismissed.","irac":{"issue":"If the court dismisses the action, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot decide issues that (1) the Constitution reserves to the other branches of government or (2) lack judicially discoverable and manageable standards for resolution. Therefore, suits presenting these nonjusticiable political questions must be dismissed.","analysis":"Article III of the Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide actual cases and controversies. But the political-question doctrine prohibits federal courts from adjudicating political questions—ie, issues that (1) the Constitution reserves to the legislative and executive branches or (2) lack judicially discoverable and manageable standards for resolution. When this occurs, the federal court must dismiss the suit. Here, the ambassador's suit for a declaratory judgment and an injunction concerned the President's recognition of a rebel group as the foreign country's true government. But such power is reserved to the executive branch under the Constitution, and there are no standards to help a court resolve this dispute. Therefore, the court will most likely dismiss this action because it involves a nonjusticiable political question. (Choice B) Under Article III's case-or-controversy requirement, a suit must be ripe for adjudication. This means that the plaintiff must have suffered actual harm or an immediate threat thereof. Here, the ambassador's action is ripe because she has been ordered to leave the U.S. within 10 days (immediate threat of harm). (Choice C) The U.S. Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases affecting ambassadors, so the ambassador's suit could have been filed directly in the Supreme Court. But original jurisdiction over ambassadors is not exclusive, so a lower federal court could also adjudicate this case if it was justiciable. (Choice D) The ambassador does have standing because she suffered an injury-in-fact (expulsion) that was caused by the challenged action (President's order) and is redressable by the remedy sought (injunction).","conclusion":"Answer A: The action involves a nonjusticiable political question."},"questionZh":"尽管内战仍在继续，美国长期以来一直承认外国的统治派系是该国的政府。整个内战期间，统治派控制着该国大部分领土，美国向代表统治派的大使提供外交豁免权。美国一位新当选的总统决定承认一个反叛组织为外国政府，并通知执政派大使必须在10天内离开美国。大使向联邦地方法院提起诉讼，要求宣布执政派别才是外国的真正政府，并要求颁布禁令，禁止执行总统让她离开美国的命令。美国已采取行动驳回这一行动。如果法院驳回诉讼，最可能的理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该行动涉及一个不可审理的政治问题。","B":"行动还不成熟。","C":"该诉讼属于美国最高法院的初审管辖范围。","D":"大使没有地位。"},"explanationZh":"宪法第三条赋予联邦法院裁决实际案件和争议的权力。但政治问题原则禁止联邦法院裁决政治问题，即以下问题：(1) 宪法保留给立法和行政部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的解决标准。当这种情况发生时，联邦法院必须驳回诉讼。在此，大使要求宣告性判决和禁令的诉讼涉及总统承认叛乱组织是外国的真正政府。但根据宪法，这种权力保留给行政部门，并且没有标准可以帮助法院解决这一纠纷。因此，法院很可能会驳回这一诉讼，因为它涉及不可审理的政治问题。 （选择B） 根据第三条“案件或争议”的要求，诉讼的裁决必须成熟。这意味着原告必须遭受实际伤害或直接威胁。在这里，大使的行动已经成熟，因为她已被命令在 10 天内离开美国（立即受到伤害威胁）。 （选项C）美国最高法院对影响大使的案件拥有初审管辖权，因此大使的诉讼可以直接向最高法院提起。但对大使的最初管辖权并不具有排他性，因此，如果可以由法院审理，下级联邦法院也可以对本案进行裁决。 （选项 D）大使确实有资格，因为她受到了因受质疑的行为（总统命令）造成的事实上的伤害（驱逐），并且可以通过寻求的补救措施（禁令）予以纠正。\n\n教育目标：根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能裁决以下问题：(1) 宪法保留给政府其他部门的权利或 (2) 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的解决标准。因此，提出这些不可审理的政治问题的诉讼必须被驳回。\n\n参考文献\n• Jones v. United States, 137 U.S. 202, 212 (1890)（指出承认外国政府及其代表是一个不可审理的政治问题）。\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962)（讨论政治问题原则）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 美联储。法院 § 574 (2019)（列出政治问题的示例）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能裁决以下问题：(1) 宪法保留给政府其他部门的权利或 (2) 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的解决标准。因此，提出这些不可审理的政治问题的诉讼必须被驳回。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院驳回诉讼，最可能的理由是什么？","rule":"根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能裁决以下问题：(1) 宪法保留给政府其他部门的权利或 (2) 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的解决标准。因此，提出这些不可审理的政治问题的诉讼必须被驳回。","analysis":"宪法第三条赋予联邦法院裁决实际案件和争议的权力。但政治问题原则禁止联邦法院裁决政治问题，即以下问题：(1) 宪法保留给立法和行政部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的解决标准。当这种情况发生时，联邦法院必须驳回诉讼。在此，大使要求宣告性判决和禁令的诉讼涉及总统承认叛乱组织是外国的真正政府。但根据宪法，这种权力保留给行政部门，并且没有标准可以帮助法院解决这一纠纷。因此，法院很可能会驳回这一诉讼，因为它涉及不可审理的政治问题。 （选择B） 根据第三条“案件或争议”的要求，诉讼的裁决必须成熟。这意味着原告必须遭受实际伤害或直接威胁。在这里，大使的行动已经成熟，因为她已被命令在 10 天内离开美国（立即受到伤害威胁）。 （选项C）美国最高法院对影响大使的案件拥有初审管辖权，因此大使的诉讼可以直接向最高法院提起。但对大使的最初管辖权并不具有排他性，因此，如果可以由法院审理，下级联邦法院也可以对本案进行裁决。 （选项 D）大使确实有资格，因为她受到了因受质疑的行为（总统命令）造成的事实上的伤害（驱逐），并且可以通过寻求的补救措施（禁令）予以纠正。\n\n教育目标：根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能裁决以下问题：(1) 宪法保留给政府其他部门的权利或 (2) 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的解决标准。因此，提出这些不可审理的政治问题的诉讼必须被驳回。\n\n参考文献\n• Jones v. United States, 137 U.S. 202, 212 (1890)（指出承认外国政府及其代表是一个不可审理的政治问题）。\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962)（讨论政治问题原则）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 美联储。法院 § 574 (2019)（列出政治问题的示例）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该行动涉及一个不可审理的政治问题。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-4f78ce54591f9d96"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":85,"sourceQuestionPage":254,"sourceQuestionPages":[254],"sourceExplanationPages":[255,256],"sourceAnswerPages":[255,256],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-086","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Eleventh Amendment & State Sovereign Immunity","question":"A federal statute provides states with funds to establish a disability benefits program. The statute requires a participating state to disburse the benefits to eligible applicants within one month after an application is approved. A commissioner of a State A agency that established a disability benefits program pursuant to the federal statute issued a regulation that delayed the disbursement of benefits until six months after an application is approved. Hundreds of residents of State A whose disability benefits were delayed because of the regulation have filed a class action against the commissioner in federal district court, arguing that the regulation violates the federal statute. The residents seek retroactive damages for the payment of the withheld benefits to be paid by the state treasury and an injunction to compel payment of future benefits. The commissioner has moved to dismiss the action and alleges that the requested relief is a violation of the Eleventh Amendment. Should the federal court hear the merits of the residents' suit?","choices":{"A":"No, because the commissioner is immune from suit under the Eleventh Amendment.","B":"Yes, but only on the request for retroactive damages for the withheld benefits.","C":"Yes, but only on the request for the injunction to compel payment of future benefits.","D":"Yes, on both because the suit is not subject to the Eleventh Amendment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. This immunity extends to suits against a state official for violating federal law when the state treasury, rather than the official, will be forced to pay damages that allegedly occurred before the action commenced (ie, retroactive damages). However, the Eleventh Amendment does not prohibit suits against a state official when:\n• the official will pay the damages from his/her own pocket, which occurs when the official is sued for actions taken as an individual rather than as a government official or\n\n• the suit seeks a declaratory judgment or injunction, even if such relief would force the state treasury to pay money in the future to comply with the court order (ie, prospective damages).\n\nHere, the residents filed a class action in federal court against the state commissioner, arguing that the regulation delaying their disability benefits violates the federal statute. The court may hear their request for an injunction to compel payment of future benefits because such payments would constitute prospective damages (Choice A). However, the court may not hear the merits of their request for payment of the withheld benefits since such payments would constitute retroactive damages that would be paid by the state treasury (Choices B & D).\n\nEducational objective: The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state officials for violating federal law when the state would pay retroactive damages. But it allows such suits when (1) the official will pay the damages or (2) the suit seeks declaratory or injunctive relief, even if the state would have to pay prospective damages under the court order.\n\nReferences\n• Ex parte Young, 209 U.S. 123, 159–60 (1908) (describing when the Eleventh Amendment does not bar suits in federal court against state officials for violations of federal law).\n\n• Edelman v. Jordan, 415 U.S. 651, 675–77 (1974) (holding that actions for injunctive relief against state officials in federal court are limited to those that result in the future payment of monetary damages from the state treasury).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 916 (2020) (explaining that the Eleventh Amendment bars citizens from suing state officials in federal court unless certain exceptions apply).","educationalObjective":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state officials for violating federal law when the state would pay retroactive damages. But it allows such suits when (1) the official will pay the damages or (2) the suit seeks declaratory or injunctive relief, even if the state would have to pay prospective damages under the court order.","references":"• Ex parte Young, 209 U.S. 123, 159–60 (1908) (describing when the Eleventh Amendment does not bar suits in federal court against state officials for violations of federal law).\n\n• Edelman v. Jordan, 415 U.S. 651, 675–77 (1974) (holding that actions for injunctive relief against state officials in federal court are limited to those that result in the future payment of monetary damages from the state treasury).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 916 (2020) (explaining that the Eleventh Amendment bars citizens from suing state officials in federal court unless certain exceptions apply).","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state officials for violating federal law when the state would pay retroactive damages. But it allows such suits when (1) the official will pay the damages or (2) the suit seeks declaratory or injunctive relief, even if the state would have to pay prospective damages under the court order.","irac":{"issue":"Should the federal court hear the merits of the residents' suit?","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits against state officials for violating federal law when the state would pay retroactive damages. But it allows such suits when (1) the official will pay the damages or (2) the suit seeks declaratory or injunctive relief, even if the state would have to pay prospective damages under the court order.","analysis":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. This immunity extends to suits against a state official for violating federal law when the state treasury, rather than the official, will be forced to pay damages that allegedly occurred before the action commenced (ie, retroactive damages). However, the Eleventh Amendment does not prohibit suits against a state official when:\n• the official will pay the damages from his/her own pocket, which occurs when the official is sued for actions taken as an individual rather than as a government official or\n\n• the suit seeks a declaratory judgment or injunction, even if such relief would force the state treasury to pay money in the future to comply with the court order (ie, prospective damages).\n\nHere, the residents filed a class action in federal court against the state commissioner, arguing that the regulation delaying their disability benefits violates the federal statute. The court may hear their request for an injunction to compel payment of future benefits because such payments would constitute prospective damages (Choice A). However, the court may not hear the merits of their request for payment of the withheld benefits since such payments would constitute retroactive damages that would be paid by the state treasury (Choices B & D).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, but only on the request for the injunction to compel payment of future benefits."},"questionZh":"联邦法规为各州提供资金来建立残疾人福利计划。该法规要求参与州在申请获得批准后一个月内向符合条件的申请人发放福利。根据联邦法规建立残疾福利计划的 A 州机构的专员发布了一项规定，将福利的支付推迟到申请获得批准后六个月。由于该规定而导致残疾福利被推迟的 A 州数百名居民已向联邦地区法院对专员提起集体诉讼，认为该规定违反了联邦法规。居民寻求追溯损害赔偿金，以支付国库应支付的扣留福利，并发出强制支付未来福利的禁令。专员已提出驳回该诉讼的请求，并声称所请求的救济违反了第十一条修正案。联邦法院是否应该听取居民诉讼的实质内容？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为根据第十一修正案，专员可以免受诉讼。","B":"可以，但仅限于要求对扣留的福利进行追溯损害赔偿时。","C":"是的，但仅限于请求强制支付未来福利的禁令。","D":"是的，两者都适用，因为该诉讼不受第十一修正案的约束。"},"explanationZh":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体在未经州政府同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉州政府。这种豁免权适用于针对州官员违反联邦法律的诉讼，当州财政部而不是官员将被迫支付据称在诉讼开始之前发生的损害赔偿（即追溯损害赔偿）时。然而，第十一修正案并不禁止在以下情况下对州官员提起诉讼：\n• 当该官员因个人而非政府官员的行为而被起诉时，该官员将自掏腰包支付损害赔偿金，或者\n\n• 该诉讼寻求宣告性判决或禁令，即使此类救济将迫使国库在未来支付款项以遵守法院命令（即预期损害赔偿）。\n\n在这里，居民向联邦法院对州专员提起集体诉讼，认为延迟发放残疾福利的规定违反了联邦法规。法院可能会听取他们提出的强制支付未来福利的禁令请求，因为此类付款将构成预期损害（选择 A）。然而，法院可能不会听取他们要求支付扣留福利的请求的理由，因为此类付款将构成由国库支付的追溯性损害赔偿（Choices B & D）。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案禁止在州支付追溯赔偿金的情况下对州官员违反联邦法律提起诉讼。但在以下情况下，它允许此类诉讼：（1）官员将支付损害赔偿金或（2）诉讼寻求宣告性或禁令性救济，即使州必须根据法院命令支付预期损害赔偿金。\n\n参考文献\n• Ex parte Young, 209 U.S. 123, 159–60 (1908)（描述第十一修正案何时不禁止在联邦法院针对违反联邦法律的州官员提起诉讼）。\n\n• Edelman v. Jordan, 415 U.S. 651, 675–77 (1974)（认为联邦法院针对州官员的禁令救济诉讼仅限于导致未来从州财政部支付金钱赔偿的诉讼）。\n\n• 32A 上午。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 916 (2020)（解释说，第十一修正案禁止公民在联邦法院起诉州官员，除非适用某些例外情况）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十一修正案禁止在州支付追溯赔偿金时对州官员违反联邦法律提起诉讼。但在以下情况下，它允许此类诉讼：（1）官员将支付损害赔偿金或（2）诉讼寻求宣告性或禁令性救济，即使州必须根据法院命令支付预期损害赔偿金。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法院是否应该听取居民诉讼的实质内容？","rule":"第十一修正案禁止在州支付追溯赔偿金时对州官员违反联邦法律提起诉讼。但在以下情况下，它允许此类诉讼：（1）官员将支付损害赔偿金或（2）诉讼寻求宣告性或禁令性救济，即使州必须根据法院命令支付预期损害赔偿金。","analysis":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体在未经州政府同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉州政府。这种豁免权适用于针对州官员违反联邦法律的诉讼，当州财政部而不是官员将被迫支付据称在诉讼开始之前发生的损害赔偿（即追溯损害赔偿）时。然而，第十一修正案并不禁止在以下情况下对州官员提起诉讼：\n• 当该官员因个人而非政府官员的行为而被起诉时，该官员将自掏腰包支付损害赔偿金，或者\n\n• 该诉讼寻求宣告性判决或禁令，即使此类救济将迫使国库在未来支付款项以遵守法院命令（即预期损害赔偿）。\n\n在这里，居民向联邦法院对州专员提起集体诉讼，认为延迟发放残疾福利的规定违反了联邦法规。法院可能会听取他们提出的强制支付未来福利的禁令请求，因为此类付款将构成预期损害（选择 A）。然而，法院可能不会听取他们要求支付扣留福利的请求的理由，因为此类付款将构成由国库支付的追溯性损害赔偿（Choices B & D）。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案禁止在州支付追溯赔偿金的情况下对州官员违反联邦法律提起诉讼。但在以下情况下，它允许此类诉讼：（1）官员将支付损害赔偿金或（2）诉讼寻求宣告性或禁令性救济，即使州必须根据法院命令支付预期损害赔偿金。\n\n参考文献\n• Ex parte Young, 209 U.S. 123, 159–60 (1908)（描述第十一修正案何时不禁止在联邦法院针对违反联邦法律的州官员提起诉讼）。\n\n• Edelman v. Jordan, 415 U.S. 651, 675–77 (1974)（认为联邦法院针对州官员的禁令救济诉讼仅限于导致未来从州财政部支付金钱赔偿的诉讼）。\n\n• 32A 上午。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 916 (2020)（解释说，第十一修正案禁止公民在联邦法院起诉州官员，除非适用某些例外情况）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，但仅限于请求强制支付未来福利的禁令。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"disability","ipa":"/dˌɪsʌbˈɪlɪti/","syllables":"dis·abil·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 无力, 无能, 残疾；[医] 劳动能力丧失, 病废","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 无力, 无能, 残疾；[医] 劳动能力丧失, 病废","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:disabilities","sourceSentence":"A federal statute provides states with funds to establish a disability benefits program.","synonyms":["disablement","handicap","impairment"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":86,"sourceQuestionPage":257,"sourceQuestionPages":[257],"sourceExplanationPages":[258,259],"sourceAnswerPages":[258,259],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-087","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federal Judicial Power","question":"In response to the need for additional toxic waste landfills in a state, the state's legislature enacted a law authorizing a state agency to establish five new state-owned and state- operated toxic waste landfills. The law provided that the agency would decide the locations and sizes of the landfills after an investigation of all potential sites and a determination that the particular sites chosen would not endanger public health and would be consistent with the public welfare. A community in the state was scheduled for inspection by the agency as a potential toxic waste landfill site. Because the community's residents obtained most of their drinking water from an aquifer that ran under the entire community, a citizens' group, made up of residents of that community, sued the appropriate officials of the agency in federal court. The group sought a declaratory judgment that selecting their community as the site of a toxic waste landfill would be unconstitutional and an injunction preventing the agency from doing so. The agency officials moved to dismiss. Which of the following is the most appropriate basis for the court to dismiss this suit?","choices":{"A":"The case is not ripe for a decision on the merits.","B":"The case presents a nonjusticiable political question.","C":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits of this kind in the federal courts.","D":"The interest of the state in obtaining suitable sites for toxic waste landfills is sufficiently compelling to justify the selection of the community as a location for such a facility."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Justiciability\n\n(suitability for judicial resolution)\n\nCase or controversy Prohibits advisory opinions under three doctrines:\n\n• Standing – plaintiff must allege injury-in-fact caused by challenged action & redressable by remedy sought\n• Ripeness – claim must present actual controversy involving past injury or present threat of injury\n• Mootness – claim must be unresolved Adequate & SCOTUS cannot review final state court judgment that rests on independent state adequate & independent state grounds grounds\n\nPolitical question Court cannot hear case if issue presented:\n\n• is reserved for executive/legislative branches or\n• lacks judicially manageable standards for resolution SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nA suit may only be heard by a federal court if it is justiciable. A suit is justiciable if it presents an actual case or controversy that is ripe for (and capable of) judicial resolution—ie, when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an imminent threat of harm. Therefore, a suit based on potential harm (unripe) or harm that is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot) should be dismissed. Here, the group sued to prevent agency officials from selecting its community as a toxic waste landfill site. But the suit is based on potential—not actual or imminent—harm since the agency is still investigating where to place these landfills. Therefore, the most appropriate basis to dismiss this suit is that it is not ripe for a decision on the merits. But the group's claim may become justiciable and can be re-filed in federal court if its community is later selected as a landfill site. (Choice B) The political-question doctrine prohibits federal courts from hearing cases that present issues that (1) are reserved for the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since federal courts often determine the constitutionality of state laws, this doctrine is not a basis for dismissal. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court for monetary relief. But it does not bar suits against state officials for declaratory or injunctive relief (as seen here).\n\n(Choice D) Federal courts can only determine the merits of a suit—eg, whether a government action is necessary to serve a compelling government interest (strict scrutiny)—if that suit is justiciable. And since this suit is not ripe for judicial resolution, the court cannot determine the merits of the group's claim.\n\nEducational objective: To be justiciable, a suit must present an actual case or controversy. This occurs when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an imminent threat thereof—not a mere possibility of harm.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Abbot Labs. v. Gardner, 387 U.S. 136, 148 (1967) (stating that federal courts cannot adjudicate unripe suits).","educationalObjective":"To be justiciable, a suit must present an actual case or controversy. This occurs when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an imminent threat thereof—not a mere possibility of harm.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Abbot Labs. v. Gardner, 387 U.S. 136, 148 (1967) (stating that federal courts cannot adjudicate unripe suits).","rule":"To be justiciable, a suit must present an actual case or controversy. This occurs when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an imminent threat thereof—not a mere possibility of harm.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most appropriate basis for the court to dismiss this suit?","rule":"To be justiciable, a suit must present an actual case or controversy. This occurs when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an imminent threat thereof—not a mere possibility of harm.","analysis":"Justiciability\n\n(suitability for judicial resolution)\n\nCase or controversy Prohibits advisory opinions under three doctrines:\n\n• Standing – plaintiff must allege injury-in-fact caused by challenged action & redressable by remedy sought\n• Ripeness – claim must present actual controversy involving past injury or present threat of injury\n• Mootness – claim must be unresolved Adequate & SCOTUS cannot review final state court judgment that rests on independent state adequate & independent state grounds grounds\n\nPolitical question Court cannot hear case if issue presented:\n\n• is reserved for executive/legislative branches or\n• lacks judicially manageable standards for resolution SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nA suit may only be heard by a federal court if it is justiciable. A suit is justiciable if it presents an actual case or controversy that is ripe for (and capable of) judicial resolution—ie, when the plaintiff has suffered actual harm or an imminent threat of harm. Therefore, a suit based on potential harm (unripe) or harm that is no longer capable of judicial resolution (moot) should be dismissed. Here, the group sued to prevent agency officials from selecting its community as a toxic waste landfill site. But the suit is based on potential—not actual or imminent—harm since the agency is still investigating where to place these landfills. Therefore, the most appropriate basis to dismiss this suit is that it is not ripe for a decision on the merits. But the group's claim may become justiciable and can be re-filed in federal court if its community is later selected as a landfill site. (Choice B) The political-question doctrine prohibits federal courts from hearing cases that present issues that (1) are reserved for the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since federal courts often determine the constitutionality of state laws, this doctrine is not a basis for dismissal. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court for monetary relief. But it does not bar suits against state officials for declaratory or injunctive relief (as seen here).\n\n(Choice D) Federal courts can only determine the merits of a suit—eg, whether a government action is necessary to serve a compelling government interest (strict scrutiny)—if that suit is justiciable. And since this suit is not ripe for judicial resolution, the court cannot determine the merits of the group's claim.","conclusion":"Answer A: The case is not ripe for a decision on the merits."},"questionZh":"为了满足各州对更多有毒废物填埋场的需求，该州立法机关颁布了一项法律，授权州机构建立五个新的国有和国有运营的有毒废物填埋场。该法律规定，该机构将在对所有潜在地点进行调查并确定所选择的特定地点不会危害公众健康且符合公共福利后，决定垃圾填埋场的位置和规模。该州的一个社区计划接受该机构的检查，认为该社区是一个潜在的有毒废物填埋场。由于该社区居民的大部分饮用水来自整个社区地下的含水层，因此由该社区居民组成的一个公民团体向联邦法院起诉了该机构的相关官员。该组织寻求一项宣告性判决，认定选择他们的社区作为有毒废物填埋场是违宪的，并寻求一项禁止该机构这样做的禁令。该机构官员动议解职。以下哪一项是法院驳回本案的最适当的依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"就案情作出裁决的时机尚不成熟。","B":"此案提出了一个不可审理的政治问题。","C":"第十一条修正案禁止在联邦法院提起此类诉讼。","D":"国家对获得合适的有毒废物填埋场址的兴趣足以证明选择社区作为此类设施的地点是合理的。"},"explanationZh":"可审理性\n\n（是否适合司法解决）\n\n案例或争议 禁止根据以下三种原则提供咨询意见：\n\n• 诉讼资格——原告必须声称受到质疑的行为造成了事实上的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以纠正\n• 成熟度——索赔必须提出涉及过去受伤或当前受伤威胁的实际争议\n• 没有实际意义 – 索赔必须未得到解决 充分且 SCOTUS 无法审查基于独立州充分和独立州理由的最终州法院判决\n\n政治问题 如果提出以下问题，法院将无法审理案件：\n\n• 保留给行政/立法部门或\n• 缺乏司法上可管理的决议标准 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n诉讼只有在可审理的情况下才能由联邦法院审理。如果诉讼提出了一个成熟的（并且能够）司法解决的实际案件或争议，即当原告遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的伤害威胁时，则该诉讼是可审理的。因此，基于潜在损害（不成熟）或不再能够通过司法解决的损害（没有实际意义）的诉讼应被驳回。在这里，该组织提起诉讼，阻止机构官员选择其社区作为有毒废物填埋场。但该诉讼是基于潜在的（而不是实际的或迫在眉睫的）危害，因为该机构仍在调查这些垃圾填埋场的地点。因此，驳回本案最恰当的理由是，就案情作出裁决的时机尚不成熟。但如果其社区后来被选为垃圾填埋场，该组织的索赔可能会受到司法管辖，并可以向联邦法院重新提起诉讼。 （选择 B）政治问题原则禁止联邦法院审理以下问题的案件：(1) 属于行政或立法部门的问题，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于联邦法院经常判定州法律是否符合宪法，因此这一原则并不是解雇的依据。 （选项 C）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉一个州以寻求金钱救济。但它并不禁止针对州官员提出宣告性或禁令性救济的诉讼（如此处所示）。\n\n（选择D）联邦法院只能确定诉讼的是非曲直——例如，政府行为是否有必要服务于令人信服的政府利益（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））——如果该诉讼是可审理的。由于司法解决此诉讼的时机尚不成熟，法院无法确定该组织索赔的实质。\n\n教育目标：为了可审理，诉讼必须提出实际案例或争议。当原告遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的伤害威胁（而不仅仅是伤害的可能性）时，就会发生这种情况。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• 艾伯特实验室。 v. Gardner, 387 U.S. 136, 148 (1967)（指出联邦法院不能裁决不成熟的诉讼）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了具有可审理性，诉讼必须提出实际案件或争议。当原告遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的伤害威胁（而不仅仅是伤害的可能性）时，就会发生这种情况。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是法院驳回本案的最适当的依据？","rule":"为了具有可审理性，诉讼必须提出实际案件或争议。当原告遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的伤害威胁（而不仅仅是伤害的可能性）时，就会发生这种情况。","analysis":"可审理性\n\n（是否适合司法解决）\n\n案例或争议 禁止根据以下三种原则提供咨询意见：\n\n• 诉讼资格——原告必须声称受到质疑的行为造成了事实上的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以纠正\n• 成熟度——索赔必须提出涉及过去受伤或当前受伤威胁的实际争议\n• 没有实际意义 – 索赔必须未得到解决 充分且 SCOTUS 无法审查基于独立州充分和独立州理由的最终州法院判决\n\n政治问题 如果提出以下问题，法院将无法审理案件：\n\n• 保留给行政/立法部门或\n• 缺乏司法上可管理的决议标准 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n诉讼只有在可审理的情况下才能由联邦法院审理。如果诉讼提出了一个成熟的（并且能够）司法解决的实际案件或争议，即当原告遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的伤害威胁时，则该诉讼是可审理的。因此，基于潜在损害（不成熟）或不再能够通过司法解决的损害（没有实际意义）的诉讼应被驳回。在这里，该组织提起诉讼，阻止机构官员选择其社区作为有毒废物填埋场。但该诉讼是基于潜在的（而不是实际的或迫在眉睫的）危害，因为该机构仍在调查这些垃圾填埋场的地点。因此，驳回本案最恰当的理由是，就案情作出裁决的时机尚不成熟。但如果其社区后来被选为垃圾填埋场，该组织的索赔可能会受到司法管辖，并可以向联邦法院重新提起诉讼。 （选择 B）政治问题原则禁止联邦法院审理以下问题的案件：(1) 属于行政或立法部门的问题，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于联邦法院经常判定州法律是否符合宪法，因此这一原则并不是解雇的依据。 （选项 C）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉一个州以寻求金钱救济。但它并不禁止针对州官员提出宣告性或禁令性救济的诉讼（如此处所示）。\n\n（选择D）联邦法院只能确定诉讼的是非曲直——例如，政府行为是否有必要服务于令人信服的政府利益（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））——如果该诉讼是可审理的。由于司法解决此诉讼的时机尚不成熟，法院无法确定该组织索赔的实质。\n\n教育目标：为了可审理，诉讼必须提出实际案例或争议。当原告遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的伤害威胁（而不仅仅是伤害的可能性）时，就会发生这种情况。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• 艾伯特实验室。 v. Gardner, 387 U.S. 136, 148 (1967)（指出联邦法院不能裁决不成熟的诉讼）。","conclusion":"答案 A：就案情作出裁决的时机尚不成熟。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"justiciability","ipa":"/,dʒʌ,stiʃiə'biləti/","syllables":"jus·ti·cia·bil·ity","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 可诉性；可否审理；可审判性（justiciable的名词形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 可诉性；可否审理；可审判性（justiciable的名词形式）","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Justiciability (suitability for judicial resolution) Case or controversy Prohibits advisory opinions under three doctrines: • Standing – plaintiff must allege injury-in-fact caused by challenged action & redressable by remedy sought • Ripeness – claim must present actual controversy involving past injury or present threat of injury • Mootness – claim must be unresolved Adequate & SCOTUS cannot review final state cour","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"dismissal","ipa":"/dɪsmˈɪsəl/","syllables":"dis·missal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 免职, 解雇；[经] 解雇","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 免职, 解雇；[经] 解雇","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:dismissals","sourceSentence":"But since federal courts often determine the constitutionality of state laws, this doctrine is not a basis for dismissal.","synonyms":["dismission","discharge","firing","liberation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":87,"sourceQuestionPage":260,"sourceQuestionPages":[260],"sourceExplanationPages":[261,262],"sourceAnswerPages":[261,262],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-088","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Eleventh Amendment & State Sovereign Immunity","question":"A federal statute provides states with funds for child welfare programs, subject to the condition that such programs be administered in accordance with federal standards. The United States sued a state in federal court for injunctive relief, arguing that the state's child welfare programs, which were funded in part by federal funds disbursed under this statute, failed to comply with federal standards. The state has moved to dismiss the action. Is the court likely to dismiss the action?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress can place any condition on the receipt of federal funds.","B":"No, because the Eleventh Amendment does not bar actions brought by the United States.","C":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment bars actions against a state in federal court.","D":"Yes, because the protection of child welfare is reserved to the states."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent.* However, this amendment does not bar actions brought by the United States or another state. And since the United States sued the state here for injunctive relief, the Eleventh Amendment does not apply. As a result, the federal court likely will not dismiss this action (Choice C). *Additionally, the doctrine of sovereign immunity bars private federal-law actions against a state sued in the courts of that state or any other state without the state's consent. (Choice A) Congress may place certain conditions on the receipt of federal funds. Such conditions are valid if they (1) are unambiguous, (2) relate to the funded program's purpose, and (3) are not unduly coercive. Therefore, the federal court will likely retain this suit and determine if this funding condition satisfies these requirements. (Choice D) Under the Tenth Amendment, all powers not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution are reserved to the states—eg, the power over child welfare. But the federal government can use its taxing and spending power to encourage states to adopt certain policies by placing conditions on the receipt of federal funds—eg, requiring state-recipients to comply with federal standards (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. But it does not bar suits brought by the United States or another state.\n\nReferences\n• Principality of Monaco v. Mississippi, 292 U.S. 313, 329 (1934) (stating that the Eleventh Amendment does not bar a suit by the United States against a state in federal court).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 911 (2019) (explaining that the United States may sue a state in federal court without the state's consent).","educationalObjective":"The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. But it does not bar suits brought by the United States or another state.","references":"• Principality of Monaco v. Mississippi, 292 U.S. 313, 329 (1934) (stating that the Eleventh Amendment does not bar a suit by the United States against a state in federal court).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 911 (2019) (explaining that the United States may sue a state in federal court without the state's consent).","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. But it does not bar suits brought by the United States or another state.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to dismiss the action?","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent. But it does not bar suits brought by the United States or another state.","analysis":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment prohibits private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court without the state's consent.* However, this amendment does not bar actions brought by the United States or another state. And since the United States sued the state here for injunctive relief, the Eleventh Amendment does not apply. As a result, the federal court likely will not dismiss this action (Choice C). *Additionally, the doctrine of sovereign immunity bars private federal-law actions against a state sued in the courts of that state or any other state without the state's consent. (Choice A) Congress may place certain conditions on the receipt of federal funds. Such conditions are valid if they (1) are unambiguous, (2) relate to the funded program's purpose, and (3) are not unduly coercive. Therefore, the federal court will likely retain this suit and determine if this funding condition satisfies these requirements. (Choice D) Under the Tenth Amendment, all powers not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution are reserved to the states—eg, the power over child welfare. But the federal government can use its taxing and spending power to encourage states to adopt certain policies by placing conditions on the receipt of federal funds—eg, requiring state-recipients to comply with federal standards (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the Eleventh Amendment does not bar actions brought by the United States."},"questionZh":"联邦法规为各州提供儿童福利计划资金，但条件是此类计划必须按照联邦标准进行管理。美国向联邦法院起诉一个州，要求颁布禁令救济，理由是该州的儿童福利计划不符合联邦标准，该计划的部分资金来自根据该法规支付的联邦资金。该州已采取行动驳回该诉讼。法院有可能驳回诉讼吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为国会可以对联邦资金的接收提出任何条件。","B":"不，因为第十一条修正案并不禁止美国提起的诉讼。","C":"是的，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院针对州提起诉讼。","D":"是的，因为儿童福利的保护权属于各州。"},"explanationZh":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案禁止私人团体和外国政府在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。*但是，该修正案并不禁止美国或其他州提起的诉讼。而且由于美国在这里起诉该州要求禁令救济，第十一条修正案并不适用。因此，联邦法院可能不会驳回该诉讼（选项 C）。 *此外，主权豁免原则禁止在未经州同意的情况下，在该州或任何其他州的法院对被起诉的州采取私人联邦法行动。 （选择 A）国会可以对联邦资金的接收设定某些条件。如果这些条件 (1) 明确，(2) 与受资助项目的目的相关，并且 (3) 没有过度强制性，则这些条件有效。因此，联邦法院可能会保留该诉讼并确定该资助条件是否满足这些要求。 （选项D） 根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力均保留给各州，例如儿童福利权。但联邦政府可以利用其征税和支出权力，通过对联邦资金的接收设定条件来鼓励各州采取某些政策，例如，要求州接受者遵守联邦标准（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案禁止私人团体和外国政府未经州同意在联邦法院起诉该州。但它并不禁止美国或其他国家提起诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• 摩纳哥公国诉密西西比州，292 U.S. 313, 329 (1934)（指出第十一修正案并不禁止美国在联邦法院对州提起诉讼）。\n\n• 32A 上午。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 911 (2019)（解释说美国可以在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十一修正案禁止私人团体和外国政府在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。但它并不禁止美国或其他国家提起诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院有可能驳回诉讼吗？","rule":"第十一修正案禁止私人团体和外国政府在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。但它并不禁止美国或其他国家提起诉讼。","analysis":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案禁止私人团体和外国政府在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。*但是，该修正案并不禁止美国或其他州提起的诉讼。而且由于美国在这里起诉该州要求禁令救济，第十一条修正案并不适用。因此，联邦法院可能不会驳回该诉讼（选项 C）。 *此外，主权豁免原则禁止在未经州同意的情况下，在该州或任何其他州的法院对被起诉的州采取私人联邦法行动。 （选择 A）国会可以对联邦资金的接收设定某些条件。如果这些条件 (1) 明确，(2) 与受资助项目的目的相关，并且 (3) 没有过度强制性，则这些条件有效。因此，联邦法院可能会保留该诉讼并确定该资助条件是否满足这些要求。 （选项D） 根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力均保留给各州，例如儿童福利权。但联邦政府可以利用其征税和支出权力，通过对联邦资金的接收设定条件来鼓励各州采取某些政策，例如，要求州接受者遵守联邦标准（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案禁止私人团体和外国政府未经州同意在联邦法院起诉该州。但它并不禁止美国或其他国家提起诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• 摩纳哥公国诉密西西比州，292 U.S. 313, 329 (1934)（指出第十一修正案并不禁止美国在联邦法院对州提起诉讼）。\n\n• 32A 上午。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 911 (2019)（解释说美国可以在未经州同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为第十一条修正案并不禁止美国提起的诉讼。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":88,"sourceQuestionPage":263,"sourceQuestionPages":[263],"sourceExplanationPages":[264,265],"sourceAnswerPages":[264,265],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-089","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"Congress enacted a statute directing U.S. ambassadors to send formal letters to the governments of their host countries, protesting any violations by those governments of international treaties on weapons sales. The President prefers to handle violations by certain countries in a less formal manner and has directed ambassadors not to comply with the statute. Is the President's action constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress has the power to implement treaties, and therefore the statute is binding on the President.","B":"No, because Congress has the power to regulate commerce with foreign nations, and therefore the statute is binding on the President.","C":"Yes, because Congress has no jurisdiction over matters outside the U.S. borders.","D":"Yes, because the President and his subordinates are the exclusive official representatives of the United States in foreign affairs."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article II vests the President with authority over a wide array of domestic and foreign policy areas. When the President shares authority with Congress over a particular area (eg, federal agencies), the validity of the President's actions is determined by the Youngstown framework. But when the President does not share authority with Congress, the President can exercise that exclusive Article II power without adhering to congressional directives. One exclusive Article II power is the authority to communicate and negotiate with foreign governments as the official U.S. representative in foreign affairs. The President can exercise this power directly or through his/her subordinates—including ambassadors and other executive-branch officials. Therefore, it was constitutional for the President to direct the ambassadors not to comply with the federal statute regarding formal letters to foreign governments. (Choice A) Congress has the power to implement treaties through appropriate legislation under the necessary and proper clause. But such legislation cannot restrict the President's ability to exercise an exclusive Article II power (as seen here). (Choices B & C) Congress has some jurisdiction over matters outside U.S. borders—eg, the power to declare war and to regulate commerce with foreign nations. But this does not give Congress the power to bind the President (or his/her subordinates) on matters within the President's exclusive authority.\n\nEducational objective: When the President does not share power with Congress over a particular area—eg, communicating with foreign governments—the President can exercise that exclusive Article II power without congressional interference.\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Curtiss-Wright Exp. Corp., 299 U.S. 304, 319 (1936) (stating that \"the President alone has the power to speak or listen as a representative of the nation\").","educationalObjective":"When the President does not share power with Congress over a particular area—eg, communicating with foreign governments—the President can exercise that exclusive Article II power without congressional interference.","references":"• United States v. Curtiss-Wright Exp. Corp., 299 U.S. 304, 319 (1936) (stating that \"the President alone has the power to speak or listen as a representative of the nation\").","rule":"When the President does not share power with Congress over a particular area—eg, communicating with foreign governments—the President can exercise that exclusive Article II power without congressional interference.","irac":{"issue":"Is the President's action constitutional?","rule":"When the President does not share power with Congress over a particular area—eg, communicating with foreign governments—the President can exercise that exclusive Article II power without congressional interference.","analysis":"Article II vests the President with authority over a wide array of domestic and foreign policy areas. When the President shares authority with Congress over a particular area (eg, federal agencies), the validity of the President's actions is determined by the Youngstown framework. But when the President does not share authority with Congress, the President can exercise that exclusive Article II power without adhering to congressional directives. One exclusive Article II power is the authority to communicate and negotiate with foreign governments as the official U.S. representative in foreign affairs. The President can exercise this power directly or through his/her subordinates—including ambassadors and other executive-branch officials. Therefore, it was constitutional for the President to direct the ambassadors not to comply with the federal statute regarding formal letters to foreign governments. (Choice A) Congress has the power to implement treaties through appropriate legislation under the necessary and proper clause. But such legislation cannot restrict the President's ability to exercise an exclusive Article II power (as seen here). (Choices B & C) Congress has some jurisdiction over matters outside U.S. borders—eg, the power to declare war and to regulate commerce with foreign nations. But this does not give Congress the power to bind the President (or his/her subordinates) on matters within the President's exclusive authority.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the President and his subordinates are the exclusive official representatives of the United States in foreign affairs."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，指示美国大使向东道国政府发送正式信函，抗议这些政府违反国际武器销售条约的行为。总统更喜欢以不太正式的方式处理某些国家的违规行为，并指示大使不要遵守该法规。总统的行动是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会有权执行条约，因此该法规对总统具有约束力。","B":"不，因为国会有权监管与外国的贸易，因此该法规对总统具有约束力。","C":"是的，因为国会对美国境外的事务没有管辖权。","D":"是的，因为总统及其下属是美国在外交事务上的唯一官方代表。"},"explanationZh":"第二条赋予总统在广泛的国内和外交政策领域的权力。当总统与国会在特定领域（例如联邦机构）分享权力时，总统行为的有效性由扬斯敦框架决定。但是，当总统不与国会分享权力时，总统可以行使第二条的专属权力，而无需遵守国会指令。第二条的一项专有权力是作为美国外交事务官方代表与外国政府沟通和谈判的权力。总统可以直接或通过他/她的下属——包括大使和其他行政部门官员——行使这一权力。因此，总统指示大使不遵守有关致外国政府正式信件的联邦法规是符合宪法的。 （选择A）国会有权根据必要和适当的条款通过适当的立法来实施条约。但此类立法不能限制总统行使第二条专有权力的能力（如此处所示）。 （选择 B 和 C）国会对美国境外的事务拥有一定的管辖权，例如宣战和监管与外国贸易的权力。但这并不赋予国会在总统专属权力范围内的事务上约束总统（或其下属）的权力。\n\n教育目标：当总统在某一特定领域（例如与外国政府沟通）不与国会分享权力时，总统可以行使第二条的专属权力，而无需国会干预。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国诉Curtiss-Wright Exp。 Corp.，299 U.S. 304, 319 (1936)（指出“只有总统才有权作为国家代表发言或聆听”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当总统在特定领域（例如与外国政府沟通）不与国会分享权力时，总统可以行使第二条的专属权力，而无需国会干预。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统的行动是否符合宪法？","rule":"当总统在特定领域（例如与外国政府沟通）不与国会分享权力时，总统可以行使第二条的专属权力，而无需国会干预。","analysis":"第二条赋予总统在广泛的国内和外交政策领域的权力。当总统与国会在特定领域（例如联邦机构）分享权力时，总统行为的有效性由扬斯敦框架决定。但是，当总统不与国会分享权力时，总统可以行使第二条的专属权力，而无需遵守国会指令。第二条的一项专有权力是作为美国外交事务官方代表与外国政府沟通和谈判的权力。总统可以直接或通过他/她的下属——包括大使和其他行政部门官员——行使这一权力。因此，总统指示大使不遵守有关致外国政府正式信件的联邦法规是符合宪法的。 （选择A）国会有权根据必要和适当的条款通过适当的立法来实施条约。但此类立法不能限制总统行使第二条专有权力的能力（如此处所示）。 （选择 B 和 C）国会对美国境外的事务拥有一定的管辖权，例如宣战和监管与外国贸易的权力。但这并不赋予国会在总统专属权力范围内的事务上约束总统（或其下属）的权力。\n\n教育目标：当总统在某一特定领域（例如与外国政府沟通）不与国会分享权力时，总统可以行使第二条的专属权力，而无需国会干预。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国诉Curtiss-Wright Exp。 Corp.，299 U.S. 304, 319 (1936)（指出“只有总统才有权作为国家代表发言或聆听”）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为总统及其下属是美国在外交事务上的唯一官方代表。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"binding","ipa":"/bˈaɪndɪŋ/","syllables":"bind·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 装订；[计] 联编； 汇集","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 装订；[计] 联编； 汇集","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:bind/1:i/i:binding/s:bindings","sourceSentence":"No, because Congress has the power to implement treaties, and therefore the statute is binding on the President.","synonyms":["dressing","bandaging","book binding","cover"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-a32150d53e521eb1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":89,"sourceQuestionPage":266,"sourceQuestionPages":[266],"sourceExplanationPages":[267,268],"sourceAnswerPages":[267,268],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-090","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Political Question","question":"The constitution of State A grants the state legislature the power to redraw the boundaries of the state's federal congressional districts every 10 years. In the last redistricting cycle, the legislature's majority political party redrew the equally populated districts with the express intent to increase the party's representation in Congress. During the most recent election, the legislature's majority political party won 10 of the 13 available congressional seats despite receiving less than 50 percent of the overall statewide vote. A resident of State A who voted for a congressional candidate representing another political party filed suit in a State A federal court against the state legislators who drafted the redistricting map. The resident argued that the court should enjoin the use of the map in future elections because the legislators violated the U.S. Constitution by engaging in partisan gerrymandering. Should the federal court hear the merits of the resident's suit?","choices":{"A":"No, because the case presents a nonjusticiable issue.","B":"No, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits suits against state officials in federal court.","C":"Yes, because the court must guarantee that the state has a republican form of government.","D":"Yes, because the map violates the \"one person, one vote\" principle."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Nonjusticiable political questions\n\n(issues incapable of judicial resolution)\n\n• Recognition of foreign governments\n• Reception of ambassadors\n• Nature of wars/hostilities\n• Partisan gerrymandering\n• Guarantee of republican form of government\n• Recognition of Native American tribes\n• House/Senate rules for impeachment\n• Ratification process for constitutional amendments Article III of the U.S. Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide justiciable cases and controversies. However, the political-question doctrine bars a federal court from hearing the merits of nonjusticiable political questions. A case presents a political question when:\n• the Constitution reserves the issue to the legislative or executive branch and/or\n• there is a lack of judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue. Here, the resident filed suit in federal court to enjoin the use of the state's redistricting map in future elections due to partisan gerrymandering—ie, drawing electoral districts to favor or weaken a political party. However, SCOTUS held in Rucho v. Common Cause that partisan gerrymandering suits present nonjusticiable political questions because there are no judicial standards to resolve them. Therefore, the court should not hear the merits of the resident's suit. (Choice B) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court for monetary relief. But it does not bar suits against state officials for injunctive relief (as seen here). (Choice C) Under Article IV of the Constitution, the federal government must guarantee a republican form of government to every state. However, the issue of what constitutes a republican form of government is a nonjusticiable political question reserved to Congress. (Choice D) The \"one person, one vote\" principle holds that one person's vote must be essentially equal to any other person's vote. When a state establishes congressional districts, this principle requires nearly precise mathematical equality between the districts. Since State A's 13 congressional districts are equally populated, the map does not violate the \"one person, one vote\" principle.\n\nEducational objective: Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot hear the merits of a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question—ie, an issue that (1) the Constitution reserves to another branch of government and/or (2) lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards for the issue to be resolved by a court. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nReferences\n• Rucho v. Common Cause, 139 S. Ct. 2484, 2506–07 (2019) (holding that partisan gerrymandering claims are nonjusticiable because there is no judicially discoverable and manageable standard to review them).\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962) (explaining the political-question doctrine).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Fed. Courts § 574 (2020) (listing common examples of political questions).","educationalObjective":"Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot hear the merits of a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question—ie, an issue that (1) the Constitution reserves to another branch of government and/or (2) lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards for the issue to be resolved by a court. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","references":"• Rucho v. Common Cause, 139 S. Ct. 2484, 2506–07 (2019) (holding that partisan gerrymandering claims are nonjusticiable because there is no judicially discoverable and manageable standard to review them).\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962) (explaining the political-question doctrine).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Fed. Courts § 574 (2020) (listing common examples of political questions).","rule":"Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot hear the merits of a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question—ie, an issue that (1) the Constitution reserves to another branch of government and/or (2) lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards for the issue to be resolved by a court. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","irac":{"issue":"Should the federal court hear the merits of the resident's suit?","rule":"Under the political-question doctrine, federal courts cannot hear the merits of a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question—ie, an issue that (1) the Constitution reserves to another branch of government and/or (2) lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards for the issue to be resolved by a court. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","analysis":"Nonjusticiable political questions\n\n(issues incapable of judicial resolution)\n\n• Recognition of foreign governments\n• Reception of ambassadors\n• Nature of wars/hostilities\n• Partisan gerrymandering\n• Guarantee of republican form of government\n• Recognition of Native American tribes\n• House/Senate rules for impeachment\n• Ratification process for constitutional amendments Article III of the U.S. Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide justiciable cases and controversies. However, the political-question doctrine bars a federal court from hearing the merits of nonjusticiable political questions. A case presents a political question when:\n• the Constitution reserves the issue to the legislative or executive branch and/or\n• there is a lack of judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue. Here, the resident filed suit in federal court to enjoin the use of the state's redistricting map in future elections due to partisan gerrymandering—ie, drawing electoral districts to favor or weaken a political party. However, SCOTUS held in Rucho v. Common Cause that partisan gerrymandering suits present nonjusticiable political questions because there are no judicial standards to resolve them. Therefore, the court should not hear the merits of the resident's suit. (Choice B) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court for monetary relief. But it does not bar suits against state officials for injunctive relief (as seen here). (Choice C) Under Article IV of the Constitution, the federal government must guarantee a republican form of government to every state. However, the issue of what constitutes a republican form of government is a nonjusticiable political question reserved to Congress. (Choice D) The \"one person, one vote\" principle holds that one person's vote must be essentially equal to any other person's vote. When a state establishes congressional districts, this principle requires nearly precise mathematical equality between the districts. Since State A's 13 congressional districts are equally populated, the map does not violate the \"one person, one vote\" principle.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the case presents a nonjusticiable issue."},"questionZh":"A州宪法赋予州立法机关每10年重新划定该州联邦国会选区边界的权力。在上一个选区重新划分周期中，立法机关的多数党重新划分了人口均等的选区，明确表示要增加该党在国会的代表性。在最近的一次选举中，立法机构的多数党尽管获得的全州选票不到 50%，但赢得了 13 个可用国会席位中的 10 个。一名投票给代表另一个政党的国会候选人的 A 州居民向 A 州联邦法院对起草选区重新划分地图的州立法者提起诉讼。该居民辩称，法院应禁止在未来的选举中使用该地图，因为立法者进行党派选区划分，违反了美国宪法。联邦法院是否应该听取居民诉讼的实质内容？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为此案存在不可审理的问题。","B":"不可以，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院对州官员提起诉讼。","C":"是的，因为法院必须保证国家实行共和政体。","D":"是的，因为该地图违反了“一人一票”的原则。"},"explanationZh":"不可审理的政治问题\n\n（无法司法解决的问题）\n\n• 外国政府的承认\n• 大使接待\n• 战争/敌对行动的性质\n• 党派不公正划分选区\n• 共和政体的保障\n• 对美洲原住民部落的认可\n• 众议院/参议院弹劾规则\n• 宪法修正案的批准程序 美国宪法第三条赋予联邦法院裁决可审理案件和争议的权力。然而，政治问题原则禁止联邦法院审理不可审理的政治问题的案情。在以下情况下，案件会提出政治问题：\n• 宪法将问题保留给立法或行政部门和/或\n• 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题。在这里，该居民向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求禁止在未来的选举中使用该州的选区重划地图，因为该地图存在党派不公正的选区划分，即绘制选区以支持或削弱某个政党。然而，最高法院在《鲁乔诉共同事业》一案中认为，党派不公正划分选区诉讼提出了不可司法审理的政治问题，因为没有司法标准来解决这些问题。因此，法院不应审理居民诉讼的案情。 （选项 B）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉一个州以获得金钱救济。但它并不禁止针对州官员提出禁令救济诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项C） 根据宪法第四条，联邦政府必须保证每个州都实行共和政体。然而，共和政府形式的构成问题是一个由国会保留的不可审理的政治问题。 （选项D） “一人一票”原则认为一个人的选票必须与任何其他人的选票基本相等。当一个州设立国会选区时，这一原则要求选区之间在数学上近乎精确的平等。由于A州13个国会选区人口相等，因此该地图并不违反“一人一票”原则。\n\n教育目标：根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能审理提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼的案情，即（1）宪法保留给另一个政府部门的问题和/或（2）缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的法院解决问题的标准。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• Rucho 诉 Common Cause，139 S. Ct. 2484, 2506–07 (2019)（认为党派不公正选区划分的主张是不可审理的，因为没有司法上可发现和可管理的标准来审查它们）。\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962)（解释政治问题原则）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 美联储。法院 § 574 (2020)（列出政治问题的常见示例）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能审理提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼的案情，即（1）宪法保留给政府另一个部门的问题和/或（2）缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的法院解决问题的标准。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法院是否应该听取居民诉讼的实质内容？","rule":"根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能审理提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼的案情，即（1）宪法保留给政府另一个部门的问题和/或（2）缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的法院解决问题的标准。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。","analysis":"不可审理的政治问题\n\n（无法司法解决的问题）\n\n• 外国政府的承认\n• 大使接待\n• 战争/敌对行动的性质\n• 党派不公正划分选区\n• 共和政体的保障\n• 对美洲原住民部落的认可\n• 众议院/参议院弹劾规则\n• 宪法修正案的批准程序 美国宪法第三条赋予联邦法院裁决可审理案件和争议的权力。然而，政治问题原则禁止联邦法院审理不可审理的政治问题的案情。在以下情况下，案件会提出政治问题：\n• 宪法将问题保留给立法或行政部门和/或\n• 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题。在这里，该居民向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求禁止在未来的选举中使用该州的选区重划地图，因为该地图存在党派不公正的选区划分，即绘制选区以支持或削弱某个政党。然而，最高法院在《鲁乔诉共同事业》一案中认为，党派不公正划分选区诉讼提出了不可司法审理的政治问题，因为没有司法标准来解决这些问题。因此，法院不应审理居民诉讼的案情。 （选项 B）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉一个州以获得金钱救济。但它并不禁止针对州官员提出禁令救济诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项C） 根据宪法第四条，联邦政府必须保证每个州都实行共和政体。然而，共和政府形式的构成问题是一个由国会保留的不可审理的政治问题。 （选项D） “一人一票”原则认为一个人的选票必须与任何其他人的选票基本相等。当一个州设立国会选区时，这一原则要求选区之间在数学上近乎精确的平等。由于A州13个国会选区人口相等，因此该地图并不违反“一人一票”原则。\n\n教育目标：根据政治问题原则，联邦法院不能审理提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼的案情，即（1）宪法保留给另一个政府部门的问题和/或（2）缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的法院解决问题的标准。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• Rucho 诉 Common Cause，139 S. Ct. 2484, 2506–07 (2019)（认为党派不公正选区划分的主张是不可审理的，因为没有司法上可发现和可管理的标准来审查它们）。\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962)（解释政治问题原则）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 美联储。法院 § 574 (2020)（列出政治问题的常见示例）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为此案存在不可审理的问题。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":90,"sourceQuestionPage":269,"sourceQuestionPages":[269],"sourceExplanationPages":[270,271],"sourceAnswerPages":[270,271],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-091","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"The vaccination of children against childhood contagious diseases (such as measles, diphtheria, and whooping cough) has traditionally been a function of private doctors and local and state health departments. Because vaccination rates have declined in recent years, especially in urban areas, the President proposes to appoint a Presidential Advisory Commission on Vaccination which would be charged with conducting a national publicity campaign to encourage vaccination as a public health measure. No federal statute authorizes or prohibits this action by the President. The activities of the Presidential Advisory Commission on Vaccination would be financed entirely from funds appropriated by Congress to the Office of the President for \"such other purposes as the President may think appropriate.\" May the President constitutionally create such a commission for this purpose?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress has not specifically authorized the creation and support of such a new federal agency.","B":"No, because the protection of children against common diseases by vaccination is a traditional state function and, therefore, is reserved to the states by the Tenth Amendment.","C":"Yes, because the President has plenary authority to provide for the health, safety, and welfare of the people of the United States.","D":"Yes, because this action is within the scope of executive authority vested in the President by the Constitution, and no federal statute prohibits it."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article II gives the President the power to manage the executive branch. Pursuant to this power, the President can use congressional funds to create advisory commissions that will recommend and promote policies. Congress can limit the President's powers when they share authority over an area (eg, use of federal funds). The Youngstown framework is then used to determine the extent of the President's authority. And when Congress is silent, the President's action is valid so long as it:\n• falls within the President's Article II powers and\n• does not interfere with another branch's powers. Here, the President proposes an advisory commission that would encourage vaccination and be financed entirely from funds already appropriated for Presidential use. Congress is silent on the President's proposal since no federal statute authorizes or prohibits it. And since the President is acting within the scope of his Article II executive authority and is not interfering with another branch's powers, he may constitutionally create the commission. (Choice A) Specific congressional authorization is not needed since the President is establishing an advisory commission—not a new federal agency with rule-making powers (eg, Securities and Exchange Commission). (Choice B) All powers that the Constitution does not expressly give to the federal government—including the police power to protect children against common diseases—are reserved to the states by the Tenth Amendment. But the President has the power to establish advisory commissions on traditional state functions like protecting child welfare. (Choice C) The President does not have plenary (ie, absolute) authority to provide for the health, safety, and welfare of the people of the United States. Instead, the President must comply with the Constitution and relevant federal statutes.\n\nEducational objective: When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions in an area over which they share authority, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.\n\nReferences\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).","educationalObjective":"When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions in an area over which they share authority, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.","references":"• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).","rule":"When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions in an area over which they share authority, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.","irac":{"issue":"The vaccination of children against childhood contagious diseases (such as measles, diphtheria, and whooping cough) has traditionally been a function of private doctors and local and state health departments. Because vaccination rates have declined in recent years, especially in urban areas, the President proposes to appoint a Presidential Advisory Commission on Vaccination which would be charged with conducting a national publicity campaign to encourage vaccination as a public health measure. No federal statute authorizes or prohibits this action by the President. The activities of the Presidential Advisory Commission on Vaccination would be financed entirely from funds appropriated by Congress to the Office of the President for \"such other purposes as the President may think appropriate.\" May the President constitutionally create such a commission for this purpose?","rule":"When Congress has remained silent about the President's actions in an area over which they share authority, those actions are valid so long as they (1) fall within the President's Article II powers and (2) do not interfere with another branch's powers.","analysis":"Article II gives the President the power to manage the executive branch. Pursuant to this power, the President can use congressional funds to create advisory commissions that will recommend and promote policies. Congress can limit the President's powers when they share authority over an area (eg, use of federal funds). The Youngstown framework is then used to determine the extent of the President's authority. And when Congress is silent, the President's action is valid so long as it:\n• falls within the President's Article II powers and\n• does not interfere with another branch's powers. Here, the President proposes an advisory commission that would encourage vaccination and be financed entirely from funds already appropriated for Presidential use. Congress is silent on the President's proposal since no federal statute authorizes or prohibits it. And since the President is acting within the scope of his Article II executive authority and is not interfering with another branch's powers, he may constitutionally create the commission. (Choice A) Specific congressional authorization is not needed since the President is establishing an advisory commission—not a new federal agency with rule-making powers (eg, Securities and Exchange Commission). (Choice B) All powers that the Constitution does not expressly give to the federal government—including the police power to protect children against common diseases—are reserved to the states by the Tenth Amendment. But the President has the power to establish advisory commissions on traditional state functions like protecting child welfare. (Choice C) The President does not have plenary (ie, absolute) authority to provide for the health, safety, and welfare of the people of the United States. Instead, the President must comply with the Constitution and relevant federal statutes.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because this action is within the scope of executive authority vested in the President by the Constitution, and no federal statute prohibits it."},"questionZh":"传统上，为儿童接种疫苗以预防儿童传染病（如麻疹、白喉和百日咳）一直是私人医生以及地方和州卫生部门的职责。由于近年来疫苗接种率下降，特别是在城市地区，总统提议任命一个总统疫苗接种咨询委员会，负责开展全国宣传活动，鼓励将疫苗接种作为一项公共卫生措施。没有任何联邦法规授权或禁止总统采取这一行动。总统疫苗接种咨询委员会的活动将完全由国会拨给总统办公室的资金资助，用于“总统认为适当的其他目的”。总统可以为此目的根据宪法设立这样一个委员会吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会尚未明确授权创建和支持这样一个新的联邦机构。","B":"不，因为通过疫苗接种来保护儿童免受常见疾病的侵害是一项传统的国家职能，因此第十修正案保留给各州。","C":"是的，因为总统拥有保障美国人民健康、安全和福利的全权。","D":"是的，因为这一行动属于宪法赋予总统的行政权力范围，没有任何联邦法规禁止这样做。"},"explanationZh":"第二条赋予总统管理行政部门的权力。根据这项权力，总统可以利用国会资金设立咨询委员会，以推荐和推广政策。当国会在某个领域分享权力时（例如，使用联邦资金），可以限制总统的权力。然后使用扬斯敦框架来确定总统的权力范围。当国会保持沉默时，只要满足以下条件，总统的行动就是有效的：\n• 属于总统第二条权力范围，并且\n• 不干涉其他部门的权力。在此，总统提议成立一个咨询委员会，该委员会将鼓励接种疫苗，并完全由已拨给总统使用的资金提供资金。国会对总统的提议保持沉默，因为没有联邦法规授权或禁止它。由于总统是在其第二条行政权力的范围内行事，并且不干涉其他部门的权力，因此他可以根据宪法设立该委员会。 （选择A）不需要国会的具体授权，因为总统正在建立一个咨询委员会，而不是一个具有规则制定权的新联邦机构（例如，证券交易委员会）。 （选项 B）宪法未明确赋予联邦政府的所有权力（包括保护儿童免受常见疾病侵害的警察权力）均由第十修正案保留给各州。但总统有权就保护儿童福利等传统国家职能设立咨询委员会。 （选项 C）总统没有全权（即绝对）权力来保障美国人民的健康、安全和福利。相反，总统必须遵守宪法和相关联邦法规。\n\n教育目标：当国会对总统在他们共同权力领域的行动保持沉默时，只要这些行动（1）属于总统的第二条权力范围内，并且（2）不干涉其他部门的权力，那么这些行动就是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当国会对总统在他们共同拥有权力的领域的行动保持沉默时，只要这些行动（1）属于总统的第二条权力范围内，并且（2）不干涉其他部门的权力，那么这些行动就是有效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"传统上，为儿童接种疫苗以预防儿童传染病（如麻疹、白喉和百日咳）一直是私人医生以及地方和州卫生部门的职责。由于近年来疫苗接种率下降，特别是在城市地区，总统提议任命一个总统疫苗接种咨询委员会，负责开展全国宣传活动，鼓励将疫苗接种作为一项公共卫生措施。没有任何联邦法规授权或禁止总统采取这一行动。总统疫苗接种咨询委员会的活动将完全由国会拨给总统办公室的资金资助，用于“总统认为适当的其他目的”。总统可以为此目的根据宪法设立这样一个委员会吗？","rule":"当国会对总统在他们共同拥有权力的领域的行动保持沉默时，只要这些行动（1）属于总统的第二条权力范围内，并且（2）不干涉其他部门的权力，那么这些行动就是有效的。","analysis":"第二条赋予总统管理行政部门的权力。根据这项权力，总统可以利用国会资金设立咨询委员会，以推荐和推广政策。当国会在某个领域分享权力时（例如，使用联邦资金），可以限制总统的权力。然后使用扬斯敦框架来确定总统的权力范围。当国会保持沉默时，只要满足以下条件，总统的行动就是有效的：\n• 属于总统第二条权力范围，并且\n• 不干涉其他部门的权力。在此，总统提议成立一个咨询委员会，该委员会将鼓励接种疫苗，并完全由已拨给总统使用的资金提供资金。国会对总统的提议保持沉默，因为没有联邦法规授权或禁止它。由于总统是在其第二条行政权力的范围内行事，并且不干涉其他部门的权力，因此他可以根据宪法设立该委员会。 （选择A）不需要国会的具体授权，因为总统正在建立一个咨询委员会，而不是一个具有规则制定权的新联邦机构（例如，证券交易委员会）。 （选项 B）宪法未明确赋予联邦政府的所有权力（包括保护儿童免受常见疾病侵害的警察权力）均由第十修正案保留给各州。但总统有权就保护儿童福利等传统国家职能设立咨询委员会。 （选项 C）总统没有全权（即绝对）权力来保障美国人民的健康、安全和福利。相反，总统必须遵守宪法和相关联邦法规。\n\n教育目标：当国会对总统在他们共同权力领域的行动保持沉默时，只要这些行动（1）属于总统的第二条权力范围内，并且（2）不干涉其他部门的权力，那么这些行动就是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为这一行动属于宪法赋予总统的行政权力范围，没有任何联邦法规禁止这样做。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"campaign","ipa":"/kæmpˈeɪn/","syllables":"cam·paign","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 战役, 运动, 竞选运动；vi. 参加运动, 作战","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 战役, 运动, 竞选运动；vi. 参加运动, 作战","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:campaigns/i:campaigning/p:campaigned/d:campaigned/3:campaigns","sourceSentence":"Because vaccination rates have declined in recent years, especially in urban areas, the President proposes to appoint a Presidential Advisory Commission on Vaccination which would be charged with conducting a national publicity campaign to encourage vaccination as a public health measure.","synonyms":["hunting expedition","safari","cause","crusade"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-988f8704cd7cc299"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":91,"sourceQuestionPage":272,"sourceQuestionPages":[272],"sourceExplanationPages":[273,274],"sourceAnswerPages":[273,274],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-092","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A federal statute prohibits the construction of nuclear energy plants in this country without a license from the Federal Nuclear Plant Siting Commission. The statute provides that the Commission may issue a license authorizing the construction of a proposed nuclear energy plant 30 days after the Commission makes a finding that the plant will comply with specified standards of safety, technological and commercial feasibility, and public convenience. In a severable provision, the Commission's enabling statute also provides that Congress, by simple majorities in each house, may veto the issuance of a particular license by the Commission if such a veto occurs within 30 days following the required Commission finding. Early last year, the Commission found that an energy corporation met all statutory requirements and, therefore, voted to issue the corporation a license authorizing it to construct a nuclear energy plant. Because they believed that the issuance of a license to the corporation was not in accord with the applicable statutory criteria, a majority of each of the two houses of Congress voted, within the specified 30-day period, to veto the license. On the basis of that veto, the Commission refused to issue the license. Subsequently, the corporation sued the Commission in an appropriate federal district court, challenging the constitutionality of the Commission's refusal to issue the license. In this suit, how should the court rule regarding the congressional veto of the energy corporation's license?","choices":{"A":"The veto is invalid, because any determination by Congress that particular agency action does not satisfy statutory criteria violates Article III, Section 1 of the Constitution because it constitutes the performance of a judicial function by the legislative branch.","B":"The veto is invalid, because Article I, Section 7 of the Constitution has been interpreted to mean that any action of Congress purporting to alter the legal rights of persons outside of the legislative branch must be presented to the President for his signature or veto.","C":"The veto is valid, because Congress has authority under the commerce clause to regulate the construction of nuclear energy plants.","D":"The veto is valid, because there is a compelling national interest in the close congressional supervision of nuclear-plant siting in light of the grave dangers to the public health and safety that are associated with the operation of such plants."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Article I grants all federal legislative power to Congress, which consists of the House of Representatives and the Senate. Article I also prescribes the method Congress must use to take legislative action—ie, any action that alters the legal rights of persons outside of the legislative branch. Under this method, all legislative action must be:\n• approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) and\n• presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment). As a result, congressional vetoes are always invalid since they attempt to overturn agency actions without adhering to the proper method of bicameralism and presentment. Here, the federal statute authorizes an agency to issue licenses for the construction of nuclear energy plants. Pursuant to this statute, the agency voted to issue a license to an energy corporation. But the statute also includes a provision allowing Congress, by majority vote of both houses, to veto the agency's issuance of a license. And though a majority of each house of Congress voted to veto the corporation's license (bicameralism), the veto was not presented to the President (no presentment). Therefore, this legislative action is invalid. (Choice A) Article III, section 1 gives federal courts the power to resolve cases and controversies. This precludes Congress from issuing a judgment in individual cases. But Congress can still make a nonbinding determination (eg, through a resolution) that particular agency action does not satisfy statutory criteria. (Choice C) Congress can regulate the construction of nuclear energy plants under the commerce clause since the production of energy substantially impacts interstate commerce. But Congress must do so through bicameralism and presentment. (Choice D) Congressional vetoes are always invalid—even when they are supported by a compelling national interest (eg, public health and safety).\n\nEducational objective: Congressional vetoes are always invalid since they attempt to overturn agency actions without adhering to the proper method for legislative action—ie, bicameralism and presentment.\n\nReferences\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 959 (1983) (striking down a congressional-veto provision in a federal immigration statute).","educationalObjective":"Congressional vetoes are always invalid since they attempt to overturn agency actions without adhering to the proper method for legislative action—ie, bicameralism and presentment.","references":"• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 959 (1983) (striking down a congressional-veto provision in a federal immigration statute).","rule":"Congressional vetoes are always invalid since they attempt to overturn agency actions without adhering to the proper method for legislative action—ie, bicameralism and presentment.","irac":{"issue":"In this suit, how should the court rule regarding the congressional veto of the energy corporation's license?","rule":"Congressional vetoes are always invalid since they attempt to overturn agency actions without adhering to the proper method for legislative action—ie, bicameralism and presentment.","analysis":"Article I grants all federal legislative power to Congress, which consists of the House of Representatives and the Senate. Article I also prescribes the method Congress must use to take legislative action—ie, any action that alters the legal rights of persons outside of the legislative branch. Under this method, all legislative action must be:\n• approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) and\n• presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment). As a result, congressional vetoes are always invalid since they attempt to overturn agency actions without adhering to the proper method of bicameralism and presentment. Here, the federal statute authorizes an agency to issue licenses for the construction of nuclear energy plants. Pursuant to this statute, the agency voted to issue a license to an energy corporation. But the statute also includes a provision allowing Congress, by majority vote of both houses, to veto the agency's issuance of a license. And though a majority of each house of Congress voted to veto the corporation's license (bicameralism), the veto was not presented to the President (no presentment). Therefore, this legislative action is invalid. (Choice A) Article III, section 1 gives federal courts the power to resolve cases and controversies. This precludes Congress from issuing a judgment in individual cases. But Congress can still make a nonbinding determination (eg, through a resolution) that particular agency action does not satisfy statutory criteria. (Choice C) Congress can regulate the construction of nuclear energy plants under the commerce clause since the production of energy substantially impacts interstate commerce. But Congress must do so through bicameralism and presentment. (Choice D) Congressional vetoes are always invalid—even when they are supported by a compelling national interest (eg, public health and safety).","conclusion":"Answer B: The veto is invalid, because Article I, Section 7 of the Constitution has been interpreted to mean that any action of Congress purporting to alter the legal rights of persons outside of the legislative branch must be presented to the President for his signature or veto."},"questionZh":"联邦法规禁止未经联邦核电站选址委员会许可在该国建设核电站。该法规规定，在委员会认定核电站将符合安全、技术和商业可行性以及公共便利性的规定标准后 30 天，委员会可以颁发许可证，授权建设拟议的核电站。在一项可分割的条款中，委员会的授权法规还规定，国会可以通过两院的简单多数否决委员会颁发的特定许可证，如果这种否决发生在委员会要求的调查结果后 30 天内。去年年初，委员会发现一家能源公司符合所有法定要求，因此投票决定向该公司颁发许可证，授权其建造核电站。由于认为向该公司颁发许可证不符合适用的法定标准，国会两院均以多数票在规定的 30 天期限内否决了该许可证。根据该否决权，委员会拒绝颁发许可证。随后，该公司在适当的联邦地区法院起诉委员会，质疑委员会拒绝颁发许可证的合宪性。在这起诉讼中，对于国会否决能源公司牌照的问题，法院应该如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"否决权是无效的，因为国会对特定机构行动不符合法定标准的任何决定都违反了宪法第三条第一节，因为它构成了立法部门履行司法职能。","B":"否决权是无效的，因为宪法第一条第七节被解释为国会任何旨在改变立法部门之外的人的合法权利的行动都必须提交总统签署或否决。","C":"否决权是有效的，因为根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause），国会有权监管核电站的建设。","D":"否决权是有效的，因为国会对核电站选址进行密切监督符合令人信服的国家利益，因为核电站的运营对公众健康和安全构成严重威胁。"},"explanationZh":"第一条将所有联邦立法权授予国会，国会由众议院和参议院组成。第一条还规定了国会采取立法行动必须采用的方法，即任何改变立法部门之外的人的合法权利的行动。根据这种方法，所有立法行动必须：\n• 经国会两院批准（即两院制）并且\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（即陈述）。因此，国会否决权总是无效的，因为它们试图在不遵守两院制和陈述的正确方法的情况下推翻机构的行动。在这里，联邦法规授权一个机构颁发核电站建设许可证。根据该法规，该机构投票决定向一家能源公司颁发许可证。但该法规还包括一项条款，允许国会以两院多数票否决该机构颁发许可证。尽管国会两院的多数票否决了公司的许可证（两院制），但否决权并未提交给总统（没有提交）。因此，该立法行为无效。 （选项 A） 第三条第一节赋予联邦法院解决案件和争议的权力。这使得国会无法对个别案件作出判决。但国会仍然可以做出不具约束力的决定（例如，通过决议）特定机构的行动不符合法定标准。 （选择 C）国会可以根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）规范核能工厂的建设，因为能源生产极大地影响州际贸易。但国会必须通过两院制和代表制来做到这一点。 （选项D）国会的否决权总是无效的——即使它们得到了令人信服的国家利益（例如，公共卫生和安全）的支持。\n\n教育目标：国会否决权总是无效的，因为它们试图推翻机构的行动，而不遵守立法行动的正确方法，即两院制和陈述。\n\n参考文献\n• INS 诉 Chadha，462 U.S. 919, 959 (1983)（推翻联邦移民法规中的国会否决条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会否决权总是无效的，因为它们试图推翻机构的行动，而不遵守立法行动的正确方法，即两院制和陈述。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起诉讼中，对于国会否决能源公司牌照的问题，法院应该如何裁决？","rule":"国会否决权总是无效的，因为它们试图推翻机构的行动，而不遵守立法行动的正确方法，即两院制和陈述。","analysis":"第一条将所有联邦立法权授予国会，国会由众议院和参议院组成。第一条还规定了国会采取立法行动必须采用的方法，即任何改变立法部门之外的人的合法权利的行动。根据这种方法，所有立法行动必须：\n• 经国会两院批准（即两院制）并且\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（即陈述）。因此，国会否决权总是无效的，因为它们试图在不遵守两院制和陈述的正确方法的情况下推翻机构的行动。在这里，联邦法规授权一个机构颁发核电站建设许可证。根据该法规，该机构投票决定向一家能源公司颁发许可证。但该法规还包括一项条款，允许国会以两院多数票否决该机构颁发许可证。尽管国会两院的多数票否决了公司的许可证（两院制），但否决权并未提交给总统（没有提交）。因此，该立法行为无效。 （选项 A） 第三条第一节赋予联邦法院解决案件和争议的权力。这使得国会无法对个别案件作出判决。但国会仍然可以做出不具约束力的决定（例如，通过决议）特定机构的行动不符合法定标准。 （选择 C）国会可以根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）规范核能工厂的建设，因为能源生产极大地影响州际贸易。但国会必须通过两院制和代表制来做到这一点。 （选项D）国会的否决权总是无效的——即使它们得到了令人信服的国家利益（例如，公共卫生和安全）的支持。\n\n教育目标：国会否决权总是无效的，因为它们试图推翻机构的行动，而不遵守立法行动的正确方法，即两院制和陈述。\n\n参考文献\n• INS 诉 Chadha，462 U.S. 919, 959 (1983)（推翻联邦移民法规中的国会否决条款）。","conclusion":"答案 B：否决权是无效的，因为宪法第一条第七节被解释为国会任何旨在改变立法部门之外的人的合法权利的行动都必须提交总统签署或否决。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"associated","ipa":"/ʌsˈoʊsiˌeɪtɪd/","syllables":"as·so·ci·ated","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 联合的；[计] 关联的； 结合的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 联合的；[计] 关联的； 结合的","wordForm":"词；原形 associate；词形 0:associate/1:dp/d:associated/p:associated","sourceSentence":"The veto is valid, because there is a compelling national interest in the close congressional supervision of nuclear-plant siting in light of the grave dangers to the public health and safety that are associated with the operation of such plants.","synonyms":["associate degree","companion","comrade","fellow"]},{"term":"vetoes","ipa":"/vˈitˌoʊz/","syllables":"ve·toes","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 否决权, 否认权( veto的名词复数 )； 行使否决权","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 否决权, 否认权( veto的名词复数 )； 行使否决权","wordForm":"词；原形 vetoe；词形 0:vetoe/1:s3","sourceSentence":"Congressional vetoes are always invalid since they attempt to overturn agency actions without adhering to the proper method for legislative action—ie, bicameralism and presentment.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"enabling","ipa":"/ɛnˈeɪbəlɪŋ/","syllables":"en·abling","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 有可能, 或有条件完成的；v. 使能够, 提供做…的权利[措施]( enable的现在分词 )； 使可能","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 有可能, 或有条件完成的；v. 使能够, 提供做…的权利[措施]( enable的现在分词 )； 使可能","wordForm":"形容词；原形 enable；词形 0:enable/1:i/i:enabling","sourceSentence":"In a severable provision, the Commission's enabling statute also provides that Congress, by simple majorities in each house, may veto the issuance of a particular license by the Commission if such a veto occurs within 30 days following the required Commission finding.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-dca63f85819110f7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":92,"sourceQuestionPage":275,"sourceQuestionPages":[275],"sourceExplanationPages":[276,277],"sourceAnswerPages":[276,277],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-093","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"The House of Representatives passed a bill imposing a tax on all insurance premiums for policies covering shipments of goods to foreign countries. The legislative record states that the purpose of this export tax is to subsidize the marine insurance industry. The tax applies to all goods manufactured or grown in every state. The bill passed the Senate and was signed into law by the President. Pursuant to the statute, a company paid the tax on insurance premiums for policies that cover goods exported to its foreign subsidiaries. The company filed a claim for a tax refund and was denied. The company filed suit alleging that the tax is unconstitutional. Is this federal tax likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress does not have the power to tax exported goods or closely related services.","B":"No, because the tax does not reasonably relate to raising revenue.","C":"Yes, because Congress's taxing power is unlimited.","D":"Yes, because the tax is necessary to serve a compelling government interest."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare (ie, any public purpose). However, this power is not unlimited and must comply with other constitutional provisions—including the export clause (Choice C). The export clause prohibits federal taxation of:\n• exported goods, which are goods leaving the U.S. and shipped to foreign countries and\n\n• services and activities closely related to the export process—eg, insurance premiums paid to cover the shipment of exported goods (as seen here).\n\nTherefore, the federal tax on insurance premiums for policies covering shipments of goods to foreign countries is not within Congress's power to tax and is unconstitutional. (Choice B) The tax in this case is reasonably related to raising revenue because its purpose is to raise funds that will be used to subsidize the marine insurance industry. But this issue is irrelevant because the tax is an unconstitutional export tax. (Choice D) Federal export taxes are never valid, even if the government shows that they are necessary to serve a compelling interest (ie, satisfy strict scrutiny).\n\nEducational objective: Congress has broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare. However, Congress can never impose taxes on exported goods or on services and activities closely related to the export process.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 9 (export clause).\n\n• United States v. Int'l Bus. Machs. Corp., 517 U.S. 843, 846–47 (1996) (explaining that the export clause prohibits federal taxation of exported goods and services and activities closely related to the export process).","educationalObjective":"Congress has broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare. However, Congress can never impose taxes on exported goods or on services and activities closely related to the export process.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 9 (export clause).\n\n• United States v. Int'l Bus. Machs. Corp., 517 U.S. 843, 846–47 (1996) (explaining that the export clause prohibits federal taxation of exported goods and services and activities closely related to the export process).","rule":"Congress has broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare. However, Congress can never impose taxes on exported goods or on services and activities closely related to the export process.","irac":{"issue":"Is this federal tax likely constitutional?","rule":"Congress has broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare. However, Congress can never impose taxes on exported goods or on services and activities closely related to the export process.","analysis":"The taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to tax and spend for the general welfare (ie, any public purpose). However, this power is not unlimited and must comply with other constitutional provisions—including the export clause (Choice C). The export clause prohibits federal taxation of:\n• exported goods, which are goods leaving the U.S. and shipped to foreign countries and\n\n• services and activities closely related to the export process—eg, insurance premiums paid to cover the shipment of exported goods (as seen here).\n\nTherefore, the federal tax on insurance premiums for policies covering shipments of goods to foreign countries is not within Congress's power to tax and is unconstitutional. (Choice B) The tax in this case is reasonably related to raising revenue because its purpose is to raise funds that will be used to subsidize the marine insurance industry. But this issue is irrelevant because the tax is an unconstitutional export tax. (Choice D) Federal export taxes are never valid, even if the government shows that they are necessary to serve a compelling interest (ie, satisfy strict scrutiny).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because Congress does not have the power to tax exported goods or closely related services."},"questionZh":"众议院通过了一项法案，对所有涉及运往国外的货物的保险保费征税。立法记录表明，征收出口税的目的是补贴海上保险业。该税适用于每个州制造或种植的所有商品。该法案在参议院获得通过，并由总统签署成为法律。根据该法规，公司为其向其外国子公司出口货物的保单缴纳保险费税。该公司提出退税申请，但遭到拒绝。该公司提起诉讼，指控该税收违宪。这项联邦税可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国会无权对出口商品或密切相关的服务征税。","B":"不，因为税收与增加收入没有合理的关系。","C":"是的，因为国会的征税权是无限的。","D":"是的，因为税收对于服务政府的迫切利益是必要的。"},"explanationZh":"征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力，可以为公共福利（即任何公共目的）征税和支出。然而，这种权力并不是无限的，并且必须遵守其他宪法条款——包括出口条款（选项C）。出口条款禁止对以下各项征收联邦税：\n• 出口货物，即离开美国并运往国外的货物，以及\n\n• 与出口流程密切相关的服务和活动——例如，为出口货物的运输支付的保险费（如此处所示）。\n\n因此，对涵盖运往国外的货物的保单的保险费征收联邦税不属于国会征税的权力，并且是违宪的。 （选项B）本案中的税收与增加收入合理相关，因为其目的是筹集资金用于补贴海上保险业。但这个问题无关紧要，因为该税是违宪的出口税。 （选择D）联邦出口税永远无效，即使政府表明它们对于服务于引人注目的利益（即满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有广泛的权力，可以为公共福利征税和支出。然而，国会永远不能对出口货物或与出口过程密切相关的服务和活动征税。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 9（出口条款）。\n\n• 美国诉国际巴士。马赫数。 Corp., 517 U.S. 843, 846–47 (1996)（解释说出口条款禁止对出口货物和服务以及与出口过程密切相关的活动征联邦税）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会拥有广泛的权力，可以为公共福利征税和支出。然而，国会永远不能对出口货物或与出口过程密切相关的服务和活动征税。","iracZh":{"issue":"这项联邦税可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"国会拥有广泛的权力，可以为公共福利征税和支出。然而，国会永远不能对出口货物或与出口过程密切相关的服务和活动征税。","analysis":"征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力，可以为公共福利（即任何公共目的）征税和支出。然而，这种权力并不是无限的，并且必须遵守其他宪法条款——包括出口条款（选项C）。出口条款禁止对以下各项征收联邦税：\n• 出口货物，即离开美国并运往国外的货物，以及\n\n• 与出口流程密切相关的服务和活动——例如，为出口货物的运输支付的保险费（如此处所示）。\n\n因此，对涵盖运往国外的货物的保单的保险费征收联邦税不属于国会征税的权力，并且是违宪的。 （选项B）本案中的税收与增加收入合理相关，因为其目的是筹集资金用于补贴海上保险业。但这个问题无关紧要，因为该税是违宪的出口税。 （选择D）联邦出口税永远无效，即使政府表明它们对于服务于引人注目的利益（即满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有广泛的权力，可以为公共福利征税和支出。然而，国会永远不能对出口货物或与出口过程密切相关的服务和活动征税。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 9（出口条款）。\n\n• 美国诉国际巴士。马赫数。 Corp., 517 U.S. 843, 846–47 (1996)（解释说出口条款禁止对出口货物和服务以及与出口过程密切相关的活动征联邦税）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为国会无权对出口商品或密切相关的服务征税。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-8da788f266b12b32"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":93,"sourceQuestionPage":278,"sourceQuestionPages":[278],"sourceExplanationPages":[279,280],"sourceAnswerPages":[279,280],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-094","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"A valid treaty between the United States and a foreign country provides for the elimination of all tariff barriers between the two countries. It authorizes the president of either country to issue a proclamation nullifying any state or local laws in that country that have the effect of impeding imports from the other country. The foreign country uses the metric system of measurement, and thus all goods produced there and exported to the United States are packaged in metric sizes, such as liters and kilograms. A law of a state in the United States requires all goods sold in that state to be packaged in traditional American sizes, such as quarts or pounds. Because the state law substantially impedes imports from the foreign country, the President of the United States has issued a proclamation nullifying the state law pursuant to the treaty. Is the President's proclamation valid?","choices":{"A":"No, because the Constitution vests in Congress the exclusive authority to specify binding legal standards for weights and measures, and the President therefore lacks constitutional authority for the proclamation.","B":"No, because the principles of federalism embedded in the Constitution prohibit the President from taking action to invalidate a state law.","C":"Yes, because it is authorized by a valid treaty of the United States and is not prohibited by any provision of the Constitution and, therefore, is the supreme law of the land.","D":"Yes, because the President has inherent authority to nullify any state law that substantially impedes commerce between the United States and another country."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Article II gives the President extensive power over foreign policy and international relations—including the power to negotiate and enter into treaties with foreign nations. But a treaty cannot be ratified (ie, signed into law) until it has been approved by a two-thirds Senate vote (no action by the House of Representatives required). It then becomes valid and binding on the federal and state governments:\n• immediately, if the treaty is self-executing—ie, contains explicit enforcement provisions (as seen here)—or\n\n• after implementing legislation or judicial action, if the treaty is non-self- executing—ie, lacks explicit enforcement provisions.\n\nAnd under the supremacy clause, an executed treaty supersedes any conflicting state law so long as the treaty is consistent with other constitutional provisions. Here, the President issued a proclamation nullifying a state law that substantially impeded imports from a foreign country. This proclamation was authorized by an explicit enforcement provision in a self-executing treaty between the U.S. and the foreign nation. And since the proclamation is not prohibited by any constitutional provision, it is the supreme law of the land and nullifies the state law. (Choice A) Article I, section 8 gives Congress the exclusive authority to specify standards for weights and measures. The President's proclamation does not interfere with that authority because it was merely used to eliminate a state-imposed packaging requirement—not to adjust national measurement standards. (Choice B) The principles of federalism allocate power between the federal and state governments. But federalism does not prohibit the President from invalidating state laws pursuant to a federal statute or treaty (as seen here). (Choice D) The President has no inherent authority to nullify a state law that substantially impedes commerce between the U.S. and another country. But the President can do so if authorized by Congress (eg, under its commerce power) or a valid treaty (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A treaty must be approved by a two-thirds vote in the Senate (not the House of Representatives) before it can be ratified. Once this occurs, the treaty takes precedence over any conflicting state law pursuant to the supremacy clause.\n\nReferences\n• Medellin v. Texas, 552 U.S. 491, 526–30 (2008) (explaining the President's powers to implement self-executing and non-self-executing treaties).\n\n• Ware v. Hylton, 3 U.S. 199, 236–37 (1796) (declaring that treaties take precedence over conflicting state laws).","educationalObjective":"A treaty must be approved by a two-thirds vote in the Senate (not the House of Representatives) before it can be ratified. Once this occurs, the treaty takes precedence over any conflicting state law pursuant to the supremacy clause.","references":"• Medellin v. Texas, 552 U.S. 491, 526–30 (2008) (explaining the President's powers to implement self-executing and non-self-executing treaties).\n\n• Ware v. Hylton, 3 U.S. 199, 236–37 (1796) (declaring that treaties take precedence over conflicting state laws).","rule":"A treaty must be approved by a two-thirds vote in the Senate (not the House of Representatives) before it can be ratified. Once this occurs, the treaty takes precedence over any conflicting state law pursuant to the supremacy clause.","irac":{"issue":"Is the President's proclamation valid?","rule":"A treaty must be approved by a two-thirds vote in the Senate (not the House of Representatives) before it can be ratified. Once this occurs, the treaty takes precedence over any conflicting state law pursuant to the supremacy clause.","analysis":"Article II gives the President extensive power over foreign policy and international relations—including the power to negotiate and enter into treaties with foreign nations. But a treaty cannot be ratified (ie, signed into law) until it has been approved by a two-thirds Senate vote (no action by the House of Representatives required). It then becomes valid and binding on the federal and state governments:\n• immediately, if the treaty is self-executing—ie, contains explicit enforcement provisions (as seen here)—or\n\n• after implementing legislation or judicial action, if the treaty is non-self- executing—ie, lacks explicit enforcement provisions.\n\nAnd under the supremacy clause, an executed treaty supersedes any conflicting state law so long as the treaty is consistent with other constitutional provisions. Here, the President issued a proclamation nullifying a state law that substantially impeded imports from a foreign country. This proclamation was authorized by an explicit enforcement provision in a self-executing treaty between the U.S. and the foreign nation. And since the proclamation is not prohibited by any constitutional provision, it is the supreme law of the land and nullifies the state law. (Choice A) Article I, section 8 gives Congress the exclusive authority to specify standards for weights and measures. The President's proclamation does not interfere with that authority because it was merely used to eliminate a state-imposed packaging requirement—not to adjust national measurement standards. (Choice B) The principles of federalism allocate power between the federal and state governments. But federalism does not prohibit the President from invalidating state laws pursuant to a federal statute or treaty (as seen here). (Choice D) The President has no inherent authority to nullify a state law that substantially impedes commerce between the U.S. and another country. But the President can do so if authorized by Congress (eg, under its commerce power) or a valid treaty (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it is authorized by a valid treaty of the United States and is not prohibited by any provision of the Constitution and, therefore, is the supreme law of the land."},"questionZh":"美国与外国之间的有效条约规定消除两国之间的所有关税壁垒。它授权任一国家的总统发布公告，废除该国任何具有阻碍从另一国家进口的州或地方法律。外国使用公制计量系统，因此该国生产并出口到美国的所有商品均以公制尺寸包装，例如升和公斤。美国某个州的法律要求在该州销售的所有商品都必须按照传统的美国尺寸进行包装，例如夸脱或磅。由于该州法律严重阻碍从外国进口，美国总统根据该条约发布了一项公告，宣布该州法律无效。总统的公告有效吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为宪法赋予国会指定具有约束力的度量衡法律标准的专属权力，因此总统缺乏宪法颁布的权力。","B":"不可以，因为宪法中的联邦制原则禁止总统采取行动使州法律无效。","C":"是的，因为它是由美国有效条约授权的，并且不受宪法任何条款的禁止，因此是美国的最高法律。","D":"是的，因为总统拥有废除任何严重阻碍美国与其他国家之间商业往来的州法律的固有权力。"},"explanationZh":"第二条赋予总统广泛的外交政策和国际关系权力，包括与外国谈判和签订条约的权力。但条约必须得到参议院三分之二投票的批准（无需众议院采取行动）才能获得批准（即签署成为法律）。然后它就变得有效并对联邦政府和州政府具有约束力：\n• 立即，如果条约是自动执行的——即包含明确的执行条款（如此处所示）——或\n\n• 在实施立法或司法行动后，如果条约不能自动执行，即缺乏明确的执行条款。\n\n根据至上条款，只要条约与其他宪法条款一致，已执行的条约就取代任何冲突的州法律。总统在此发布公告，宣布一项严重阻碍外国进口的州法律无效。该公告是由美国与外国之间的自动执行条约中的明确执行条款授权的。由于该公告不受任何宪法条款的禁止，因此它是该国的最高法律，并使州法律无效。 （选项 A）第一条第 8 节赋予国会指定度量衡标准的专属权力。总统的公告不会干扰该权力，因为它只是用来消除国家规定的包装要求，而不是调整国家测量标准。 （选项 B） 联邦制原则在联邦政府和州政府之间分配权力。但联邦制并不禁止总统根据联邦法规或条约使州法律无效（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）总统没有固有权力废除严重阻碍美国与其他国家之间商业往来的州法律。但如果得到国会授权（例如，根据其商业权力）或有效条约（如此处所示），总统可以这样做。\n\n教育目标：条约必须获得参议院（而不是众议院）三分之二票数的批准才能获得批准。一旦发生这种情况，根据至上条款，该条约优先于任何冲突的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• Medellin v. Texas, 552 U.S. 491, 526–30 (2008)（解释总统执行自动执行和非自动执行条约的权力）。\n\n• Ware v. Hylton, 3 U.S. 199, 236–37 (1796)（声明条约优先于相互冲突的州法律）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"条约必须获得参议院（而非众议院）三分之二多数票的批准才能获得批准。一旦发生这种情况，根据至上条款，该条约优先于任何冲突的州法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统的公告有效吗？","rule":"条约必须获得参议院（而非众议院）三分之二多数票的批准才能获得批准。一旦发生这种情况，根据至上条款，该条约优先于任何冲突的州法律。","analysis":"第二条赋予总统广泛的外交政策和国际关系权力，包括与外国谈判和签订条约的权力。但条约必须得到参议院三分之二投票的批准（无需众议院采取行动）才能获得批准（即签署成为法律）。然后它就变得有效并对联邦政府和州政府具有约束力：\n• 立即，如果条约是自动执行的——即包含明确的执行条款（如此处所示）——或\n\n• 在实施立法或司法行动后，如果条约不能自动执行，即缺乏明确的执行条款。\n\n根据至上条款，只要条约与其他宪法条款一致，已执行的条约就取代任何冲突的州法律。总统在此发布公告，宣布一项严重阻碍外国进口的州法律无效。该公告是由美国与外国之间的自动执行条约中的明确执行条款授权的。由于该公告不受任何宪法条款的禁止，因此它是该国的最高法律，并使州法律无效。 （选项 A）第一条第 8 节赋予国会指定度量衡标准的专属权力。总统的公告不会干扰该权力，因为它只是用来消除国家规定的包装要求，而不是调整国家测量标准。 （选项 B） 联邦制原则在联邦政府和州政府之间分配权力。但联邦制并不禁止总统根据联邦法规或条约使州法律无效（如此处所示）。 （选项 D）总统没有固有权力废除严重阻碍美国与其他国家之间商业往来的州法律。但如果得到国会授权（例如，根据其商业权力）或有效条约（如此处所示），总统可以这样做。\n\n教育目标：条约必须获得参议院（而不是众议院）三分之二票数的批准才能获得批准。一旦发生这种情况，根据至上条款，该条约优先于任何冲突的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• Medellin v. Texas, 552 U.S. 491, 526–30 (2008)（解释总统执行自动执行和非自动执行条约的权力）。\n\n• Ware v. Hylton, 3 U.S. 199, 236–37 (1796)（声明条约优先于相互冲突的州法律）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为它是由美国有效条约授权的，并且不受宪法任何条款的禁止，因此是美国的最高法律。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"tariff","ipa":"/tˈɛɹəf/","syllables":"tar·iff","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 关税, 关税表, 价格表, 收费表；vt. 课以关税；[计] 价目表","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 关税, 关税表, 价格表, 收费表；vt. 课以关税；[计] 价目表","wordForm":"动词；词形 s:tariffs/i:tariffing/p:tariffed/3:tariffs/d:tariffed","sourceSentence":"A valid treaty between the United States and a foreign country provides for the elimination of all tariff barriers between the two countries.","synonyms":["duty"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-489cb8172b1519e5"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":94,"sourceQuestionPage":281,"sourceQuestionPages":[281],"sourceExplanationPages":[282,283],"sourceAnswerPages":[282,283],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-095","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Delegation & Legislative Veto","question":"A statute passed by both houses of Congress and signed by the President authorizes a federal agency to select a site for and to construct a monument honoring members of the capitol police force killed in the line of duty. The statute appropriates the necessary funds but provides that the funds may not be expended until both houses of Congress have adopted a concurrent resolution, not subject to presentment to the President, approving the agency's plans for the monument's location and design. Is the provision requiring further congressional approval before expenditure of the funds constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because decisions regarding the placement and design of government-owned structures are an exclusively executive function with which Congress may not interfere by any means.","B":"No, because the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function.","C":"Yes, because Congress may attach reasonable conditions to its appropriation of funds to executive departments, and its special interest in the members of its own police force makes the provision a reasonable condition.","D":"Yes, because the provision is part of a statute that was passed by both houses of Congress and signed by the President."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Article I entrusts Congress (ie, the House of Representatives and the Senate) with the power to take legislative action. Action is legislative when its purpose or effect is to alter the legal rights or duties of persons outside of Congress. And legislative action, absent narrow exceptions, must be:\n• approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) and\n• presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment). Through this process, Congress can delegate some of its powers (eg, rulemaking) to federal executive agencies. But once Congress does so, it cannot interfere with the agencies' functions unless it again complies with the legislative-action process. Any attempt to bypass this process amounts to an unconstitutional legislative veto. Here, Congress delegated power to the federal agency to select a site for and construct a monument. But a statutory provision prevents the agency from expending the appropriated funds until Congress adopts a concurrent resolution approving the agency's plans. And since the resolution will not be presented to the President, the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function (ie, legislative veto). (Choice A) Decisions regarding the placement and design of government structures are not an exclusively executive function because Congress also has the authority to make these determinations. (Choice C) Congress may attach reasonable conditions to its appropriations of funds—eg, directing how executive departments can spend funds. But such conditions must comply with the Constitution (not seen here). (Choice D) The provision is part of a statute that passed both houses of Congress and was signed by the President. However, the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative veto since it grants Congress the future power to approve or disapprove the agency's plans without presentment to the President.\n\nEducational objective: Once Congress delegates power to an executive agency, it cannot interfere with the agency's functions without satisfying the legislative-action process—ie, bicameralism and presentment. Any attempt to bypass this process is an unconstitutional legislative veto.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 7, cl. 2 (bicameralism and presentment requirements).\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 951–52 (1983) (explaining that almost all legislative acts must satisfy the constitutional requirements of bicameralism and presentment to have effect).","educationalObjective":"Once Congress delegates power to an executive agency, it cannot interfere with the agency's functions without satisfying the legislative-action process—ie, bicameralism and presentment. Any attempt to bypass this process is an unconstitutional legislative veto.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 7, cl. 2 (bicameralism and presentment requirements).\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 951–52 (1983) (explaining that almost all legislative acts must satisfy the constitutional requirements of bicameralism and presentment to have effect).","rule":"Once Congress delegates power to an executive agency, it cannot interfere with the agency's functions without satisfying the legislative-action process—ie, bicameralism and presentment. Any attempt to bypass this process is an unconstitutional legislative veto.","irac":{"issue":"Is the provision requiring further congressional approval before expenditure of the funds constitutional?","rule":"Once Congress delegates power to an executive agency, it cannot interfere with the agency's functions without satisfying the legislative-action process—ie, bicameralism and presentment. Any attempt to bypass this process is an unconstitutional legislative veto.","analysis":"Article I entrusts Congress (ie, the House of Representatives and the Senate) with the power to take legislative action. Action is legislative when its purpose or effect is to alter the legal rights or duties of persons outside of Congress. And legislative action, absent narrow exceptions, must be:\n• approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) and\n• presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment). Through this process, Congress can delegate some of its powers (eg, rulemaking) to federal executive agencies. But once Congress does so, it cannot interfere with the agencies' functions unless it again complies with the legislative-action process. Any attempt to bypass this process amounts to an unconstitutional legislative veto. Here, Congress delegated power to the federal agency to select a site for and construct a monument. But a statutory provision prevents the agency from expending the appropriated funds until Congress adopts a concurrent resolution approving the agency's plans. And since the resolution will not be presented to the President, the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function (ie, legislative veto). (Choice A) Decisions regarding the placement and design of government structures are not an exclusively executive function because Congress also has the authority to make these determinations. (Choice C) Congress may attach reasonable conditions to its appropriations of funds—eg, directing how executive departments can spend funds. But such conditions must comply with the Constitution (not seen here). (Choice D) The provision is part of a statute that passed both houses of Congress and was signed by the President. However, the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative veto since it grants Congress the future power to approve or disapprove the agency's plans without presentment to the President.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the provision amounts to an unconstitutional legislative interference with an executive function."},"questionZh":"国会参众两院通过并由总统签署的一项法规授权联邦机构为因公殉职的国会警察部队成员选择地点并建造一座纪念碑。该法规拨款必要的资金，但规定在国会两院同时通过一项决议（无需向总统提交批准该机构的纪念碑选址和设计计划）之前，不得支出这些资金。在资金支出之前需要国会进一步批准的规定是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为有关政府所有结构的布局和设计的决定完全是行政职能，国会不得以任何方式干预。","B":"不可以，因为该规定相当于对行政职能的立法干预，违宪。","C":"是的，因为国会可以在向行政部门拨款时附加合理的条件，而且国会对自己警察部队成员的特殊利益使这一规定成为合理的条件。","D":"是的，因为该条款是国会两院通过并由总统签署的法规的一部分。"},"explanationZh":"第一条赋予国会（即众议院和参议院）采取立法行动的权力。当其目的或效果是改变国会以外人员的合法权利或义务时，该行动就是立法行动。除少数例外情况外，立法行动必须：\n• 经国会两院批准（即两院制）并且\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（即陈述）。通过这一过程，国会可以将其部分权力（例如，规则制定）委托给联邦执行机构。但一旦国会这样做，它就不能干预这些机构的职能，除非它再次遵守立法行动程序。任何绕过这一程序的尝试都相当于违宪的立法否决。在这里，国会将权力委托给联邦机构来选择纪念碑的地点和建造纪念碑。但一项法定条款禁止该机构在国会通过同时决议批准该机构的计划之前支出拨款。由于该决议不会提交给总统，因此该条款相当于对行政职能的违宪立法干预（即立法否决）。 （选择 A）有关政府结构的布局和设计的决定并不完全是行政职能，因为国会也有权做出这些决定。 （选择C）国会可以对其资金拨款附加合理的条件，例如，指导行政部门如何使用资金。但这样的条件必须符合宪法（这里没有看到）。 （选项 D） 该条款是国会参众两院通过并由总统签署的法规的一部分。然而，该条款相当于违宪的立法否决权，因为它赋予国会未来批准或否决该机构计划的权力，而无需向总统提交。\n\n教育目标：一旦国会将权力委托给执行机构，在不满足立法行动程序（即两院制和代表制）的情况下，它就不能干涉该机构的职能。任何绕过这一程序的尝试都是违宪的立法否决。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 7，cl。 2（两院制和表现要求）。\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 951–52 (1983)（解释说几乎所有立法行为都必须满足两院制和提交的宪法要求才能生效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一旦国会将权力委托给一个行政机构，它就不能在不满足立法行动程序（即两院制和代表制）的情况下干预该机构的职能。任何绕过这一程序的尝试都是违宪的立法否决。","iracZh":{"issue":"在资金支出之前需要国会进一步批准的规定是否符合宪法？","rule":"一旦国会将权力委托给一个行政机构，它就不能在不满足立法行动程序（即两院制和代表制）的情况下干预该机构的职能。任何绕过这一程序的尝试都是违宪的立法否决。","analysis":"第一条赋予国会（即众议院和参议院）采取立法行动的权力。当其目的或效果是改变国会以外人员的合法权利或义务时，该行动就是立法行动。除少数例外情况外，立法行动必须：\n• 经国会两院批准（即两院制）并且\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（即陈述）。通过这一过程，国会可以将其部分权力（例如，规则制定）委托给联邦执行机构。但一旦国会这样做，它就不能干预这些机构的职能，除非它再次遵守立法行动程序。任何绕过这一程序的尝试都相当于违宪的立法否决。在这里，国会将权力委托给联邦机构来选择纪念碑的地点和建造纪念碑。但一项法定条款禁止该机构在国会通过同时决议批准该机构的计划之前支出拨款。由于该决议不会提交给总统，因此该条款相当于对行政职能的违宪立法干预（即立法否决）。 （选择 A）有关政府结构的布局和设计的决定并不完全是行政职能，因为国会也有权做出这些决定。 （选择C）国会可以对其资金拨款附加合理的条件，例如，指导行政部门如何使用资金。但这样的条件必须符合宪法（这里没有看到）。 （选项 D） 该条款是国会参众两院通过并由总统签署的法规的一部分。然而，该条款相当于违宪的立法否决权，因为它赋予国会未来批准或否决该机构计划的权力，而无需向总统提交。\n\n教育目标：一旦国会将权力委托给执行机构，在不满足立法行动程序（即两院制和代表制）的情况下，它就不能干涉该机构的职能。任何绕过这一程序的尝试都是违宪的立法否决。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 7，cl。 2（两院制和表现要求）。\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 951–52 (1983)（解释说几乎所有立法行为都必须满足两院制和提交的宪法要求才能生效）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为该规定相当于对行政职能的立法干预，违宪。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"legislative veto","ipa":"/lˈɛdʒʌslˌeɪtɪv vˈitˌoʊ/","syllables":"leg·isla·tive veto","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"立法否决：国会一院或委员会试图否决行政行为的机制","contextualMeaning":"立法否决：国会一院或委员会试图否决行政行为的机制","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Any attempt to bypass this process is an unconstitutional legislative veto.","synonyms":["legislative forbid","legislative prohibit","legislative interdict"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-dca63f85819110f7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":95,"sourceQuestionPage":284,"sourceQuestionPages":[284],"sourceExplanationPages":[285,286],"sourceAnswerPages":[285,286],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-096","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"A trade association in State A was formed for the purpose of protecting the state's lucrative cattle industry. The association's members, who are cattle breeders and dealers, use a grading system to grade the quality of cattle bred in the state. The grades assigned by the association are nationally recognized and prominently displayed on cattle to promote the state's cattle industry. The commissioner of the agency that regulates cattle in State B has adopted a regulation that requires cattle sold in the state to display a grade approved by the federal government or no grade at all. The sales in State B of several members of the trade association have declined as a result of complying with this regulation. As a result, the association has filed suit against the commissioner in a federal district court, seeking an injunction against the enforcement of the regulation on the ground that it is unconstitutional. May the court determine the merits of this suit?","choices":{"A":"No, because the association itself has suffered no injury.","B":"No, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits suits against state officials in federal court.","C":"Yes, because federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over federal-law claims.","D":"Yes, because the association has standing to sue on behalf of its members."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on another's behalf)\n\nOrganization Organization has standing if: members\n• members have standing to sue on their own behalf\n• their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND\n• lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when:\n\n• parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND\n• obstacle prevents third party from suing (eg, privacy/retaliation concern) Child Parent has standing unless parental rights have been limited by court OR lawsuit may adversely affect child\n\nAssignor Assignee has standing if assigned legal claim for ordinary & good faith business purpose\n\nA federal court cannot hear a case unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a concrete interest in the outcome of the case—to bring it. When the plaintiff is an organization (eg, trade association), the organization has standing to sue when it has suffered an injury. It also has standing to bring an action on behalf of its members when:\n• the members themselves would have standing to sue (ie, members suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant and is redressable by the remedy sought)\n\n• the interests at stake (eg, members' injuries) relate to the organization's purpose and\n\n• if damages are sought, the lawsuit does not require member participation (eg, to prove monetary damages).\n\nHere, the association's members would have standing because the decline in sales (injury- in-fact) caused by the commissioner's regulation (causation) will be remedied if the court grants an injunction (redressability). The decline in sales relates to the association's purpose of protecting State A's cattle industry. And since the association seeks an injunction (not damages), member participation is not needed. Therefore, the association\n\nhas standing to sue on behalf of its members, and the court may determine the merits of this suit. (Choice A) An organization must suffer an injury-in-fact to have standing to sue on its own behalf. But an organization may have standing to sue on its members' behalf even if it has not suffered an injury. (Choice B) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court for monetary relief. But it does not bar suits against state officials for injunctive relief (as seen here). (Choice C) Federal and state courts have concurrent jurisdiction over federal-law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: An organization has standing to sue on its members' behalf when (1) the members themselves would have standing to sue, (2) the interests at stake relate to the organization's purpose, and (3) if damages are sought, the lawsuit does not require member participation.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• Hunt v. Wash. State Apple Adver. Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 343–45 (1977) (holding that organizations may bring actions on behalf of their members when the requirements for organizational standing are met.)","educationalObjective":"An organization has standing to sue on its members' behalf when (1) the members themselves would have standing to sue, (2) the interests at stake relate to the organization's purpose, and (3) if damages are sought, the lawsuit does not require member participation.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• Hunt v. Wash. State Apple Adver. Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 343–45 (1977) (holding that organizations may bring actions on behalf of their members when the requirements for organizational standing are met.)","rule":"An organization has standing to sue on its members' behalf when (1) the members themselves would have standing to sue, (2) the interests at stake relate to the organization's purpose, and (3) if damages are sought, the lawsuit does not require member participation.","irac":{"issue":"May the court determine the merits of this suit?","rule":"An organization has standing to sue on its members' behalf when (1) the members themselves would have standing to sue, (2) the interests at stake relate to the organization's purpose, and (3) if damages are sought, the lawsuit does not require member participation.","analysis":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on another's behalf)\n\nOrganization Organization has standing if: members\n• members have standing to sue on their own behalf\n• their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND\n• lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when:\n\n• parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND\n• obstacle prevents third party from suing (eg, privacy/retaliation concern) Child Parent has standing unless parental rights have been limited by court OR lawsuit may adversely affect child\n\nAssignor Assignee has standing if assigned legal claim for ordinary & good faith business purpose\n\nA federal court cannot hear a case unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a concrete interest in the outcome of the case—to bring it. When the plaintiff is an organization (eg, trade association), the organization has standing to sue when it has suffered an injury. It also has standing to bring an action on behalf of its members when:\n• the members themselves would have standing to sue (ie, members suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant and is redressable by the remedy sought)\n\n• the interests at stake (eg, members' injuries) relate to the organization's purpose and\n\n• if damages are sought, the lawsuit does not require member participation (eg, to prove monetary damages).\n\nHere, the association's members would have standing because the decline in sales (injury- in-fact) caused by the commissioner's regulation (causation) will be remedied if the court grants an injunction (redressability). The decline in sales relates to the association's purpose of protecting State A's cattle industry. And since the association seeks an injunction (not damages), member participation is not needed. Therefore, the association\n\nhas standing to sue on behalf of its members, and the court may determine the merits of this suit. (Choice A) An organization must suffer an injury-in-fact to have standing to sue on its own behalf. But an organization may have standing to sue on its members' behalf even if it has not suffered an injury. (Choice B) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars private parties and foreign governments from suing a state in federal court for monetary relief. But it does not bar suits against state officials for injunctive relief (as seen here). (Choice C) Federal and state courts have concurrent jurisdiction over federal-law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the association has standing to sue on behalf of its members."},"questionZh":"A 州成立了一个贸易协会，其目的是保护该州利润丰厚的养牛业。该协会的成员是牛饲养者和经销商，他们使用分级系统对该州饲养的牛的质量进行分级。该协会制定的等级得到全国认可，并在牛身上显着位置展示，以促进该州的养牛业发展。 B 州牲畜监管机构的专员通过了一项法规，要求在该州出售的牲畜必须显示联邦政府批准的等级，或者根本不显示等级。由于遵守这一规定，该行业协会的几个成员在 B 国的销售额有所下降。因此，该协会已向联邦地区法院对专员提起诉讼，以违宪为由寻求禁止执行该法规的禁令。法院可以裁定该诉讼的是非曲直吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为协会本身并没有受到伤害。","B":"不可以，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院对州官员提起诉讼。","C":"是的，因为联邦法院对联邦法律索赔拥有专属管辖权。","D":"是的，因为该协会有资格代表其会员提起诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"第三条 地位\n\n（代表他人提起诉讼）\n\n组织 组织具有地位，如果： 成员\n• 会员有资格代表自己提起诉讼\n• 他们的伤害与组织的目的有关并且\n• 诉讼不需要会员参与（如果寻求损害赔偿） 第三方受伤原告在以下情况下具有诉讼资格：\n\n• 各方有着密不可分的密切关系（例如医生/患者）并且\n• 障碍阻止第三方起诉（例如，隐私/报复问题） 儿童 父母有资格，除非父母权利受到法院限制或诉讼可能对儿童产生不利影响\n\n如果转让人出于普通和善意商业目的而提出法律索赔，则受让人享有资格\n\n除非原告有资格（即对案件结果有具体利益）提起诉讼，否则联邦法院无法审理案件。当原告是一个组织（例如行业协会）时，该组织在遭受损害时有资格提起诉讼。在以下情况下，它还有权代表其成员提起诉讼：\n• 会员本身有资格提起诉讼（即，会员实际上受到了由被告造成的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以补救）\n\n• 所涉及的利益（例如，成员的伤害）与组织的宗旨有关，并且\n\n• 如果寻求损害赔偿，诉讼不需要会员参与（例如，证明金钱损害）。\n\n在这里，协会的成员将有资格，因为如果法院颁布禁令（可补救性），由专员的监管（因果关系）造成的销售下降（事实上的损害）将得到补救。销量下降与该协会保护A国养牛业的目的有关。由于该协会寻求禁令（而不是损害赔偿），因此不需要会员参与。因此，协会\n\n具有代表其成员提起诉讼的资格，法院可以确定该诉讼的实质内容。 （选择 A）组织必须遭受事实上的伤害才能有资格代表自己提起诉讼。但一个组织即使没有受到伤害，也可能有资格代表其成员提起诉讼。 （选项 B）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉一个州以获得金钱救济。但它并不禁止针对州官员提出禁令救济诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项 C）联邦法院和州法院对联邦法律主张具有并行管辖权，除非宪法或国会明确规定联邦法院具有专属管辖权（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，组织有权代表其成员提起诉讼：(1) 成员本身有权提起诉讼，(2) 所涉及的利益与组织的宗旨相关，以及 (3) 如果寻求损害赔偿，则诉讼不需要成员参与。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• 亨特诉华盛顿州苹果广告公司。 Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 343–45 (1977)（认为当满足组织地位的要求时，组织可以代表其成员提起诉讼。）","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，组织有权代表其成员提起诉讼：(1) 成员本身有权提起诉讼，(2) 所涉及的利益与组织的宗旨相关，以及 (3) 如果寻求损害赔偿，则诉讼不需要成员参与。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可以裁定该诉讼的是非曲直吗？","rule":"在以下情况下，组织有权代表其成员提起诉讼：(1) 成员本身有权提起诉讼，(2) 所涉及的利益与组织的宗旨相关，以及 (3) 如果寻求损害赔偿，则诉讼不需要成员参与。","analysis":"第三条 地位\n\n（代表他人提起诉讼）\n\n组织 组织具有地位，如果： 成员\n• 会员有资格代表自己提起诉讼\n• 他们的伤害与组织的目的有关并且\n• 诉讼不需要会员参与（如果寻求损害赔偿） 第三方受伤原告在以下情况下具有诉讼资格：\n\n• 各方有着密不可分的密切关系（例如医生/患者）并且\n• 障碍阻止第三方起诉（例如，隐私/报复问题） 儿童 父母有资格，除非父母权利受到法院限制或诉讼可能对儿童产生不利影响\n\n如果转让人出于普通和善意商业目的而提出法律索赔，则受让人享有资格\n\n除非原告有资格（即对案件结果有具体利益）提起诉讼，否则联邦法院无法审理案件。当原告是一个组织（例如行业协会）时，该组织在遭受损害时有资格提起诉讼。在以下情况下，它还有权代表其成员提起诉讼：\n• 会员本身有资格提起诉讼（即，会员实际上受到了由被告造成的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以补救）\n\n• 所涉及的利益（例如，成员的伤害）与组织的宗旨有关，并且\n\n• 如果寻求损害赔偿，诉讼不需要会员参与（例如，证明金钱损害）。\n\n在这里，协会的成员将有资格，因为如果法院颁布禁令（可补救性），由专员的监管（因果关系）造成的销售下降（事实上的损害）将得到补救。销量下降与该协会保护A国养牛业的目的有关。由于该协会寻求禁令（而不是损害赔偿），因此不需要会员参与。因此，协会\n\n具有代表其成员提起诉讼的资格，法院可以确定该诉讼的实质内容。 （选择 A）组织必须遭受事实上的伤害才能有资格代表自己提起诉讼。但一个组织即使没有受到伤害，也可能有资格代表其成员提起诉讼。 （选项 B）第十一修正案一般禁止私人团体和外国政府向联邦法院起诉一个州以获得金钱救济。但它并不禁止针对州官员提出禁令救济诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项 C）联邦法院和州法院对联邦法律主张具有并行管辖权，除非宪法或国会明确规定联邦法院具有专属管辖权（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，组织有权代表其成员提起诉讼：(1) 成员本身有权提起诉讼，(2) 所涉及的利益与组织的宗旨相关，以及 (3) 如果寻求损害赔偿，则诉讼不需要成员参与。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• 亨特诉华盛顿州苹果广告公司。 Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 343–45 (1977)（认为当满足组织地位的要求时，组织可以代表其成员提起诉讼。）","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该协会有资格代表其会员提起诉讼。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"retaliation","ipa":"/ɹitˌæliˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"re·tal·i·a·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 报复；[法] 报仇, 报复, 回敬","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 报复；[法] 报仇, 报复, 回敬","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:retaliations","sourceSentence":"Article III standing (suing on another's behalf) Organization Organization has standing if: members • members have standing to sue on their own behalf • their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND • lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when: • parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND • obstacle prevents third","synonyms":["revenge"]},{"term":"assignor","ipa":"/ə'sainə/","syllables":"as·signor","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 分配人, 指定人, 转让者；[经] 转让人, 让与人","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 分配人, 指定人, 转让者；[经] 转让人, 让与人","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:assignors","sourceSentence":"Article III standing (suing on another's behalf) Organization Organization has standing if: members • members have standing to sue on their own behalf • their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND • lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when: • parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND • obstacle prevents third","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"assignee","ipa":"/,æsi'ni:/","syllables":"as·signee","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 受让人, 受托者, 代理人；[经] 受让人","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 受让人, 受托者, 代理人；[经] 受让人","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:assignees","sourceSentence":"Article III standing (suing on another's behalf) Organization Organization has standing if: members • members have standing to sue on their own behalf • their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND • lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when: • parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND • obstacle prevents third","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"privacy","ipa":"/pɹˈaɪvəsi/","syllables":"pri·vacy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 隐私, 隐居, 秘密；[计] 个人保密权","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 隐私, 隐居, 秘密；[计] 个人保密权","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:privacies","sourceSentence":"Article III standing (suing on another's behalf) Organization Organization has standing if: members • members have standing to sue on their own behalf • their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND • lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when: • parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND • obstacle prevents third","synonyms":["privateness","seclusion","secrecy","concealment"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":96,"sourceQuestionPage":287,"sourceQuestionPages":[287],"sourceExplanationPages":[288,289],"sourceAnswerPages":[288,289],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-097","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Appointments & Removal","question":"A senator made a speech on the floor of the U.S. Senate in which she asserted that a federal civil servant with minor responsibilities was twice convicted of fraud by a state. In making this assertion, the senator relied wholly on research done by her chief legislative assistant. In fact, it was a different man with the same name, and not the civil servant, who was convicted of these crimes in the state court proceedings. This mistake was the result of carelessness on the legislative assistant's part. No legislation affecting the appointment or discipline of civil servants or the program of the federal agency for which the civil servant works was under consideration at the time the senator made her speech about him on the floor of the Senate. The civil servant sues the senator and her legislative assistant for defamation. Both defendants move to dismiss the complaint. As a matter of constitutional law, how should the court proceed on the defendants' motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny it as to both defendants, because any immunity of the senator under the speech or debate clause does not attach to a speech that is not germane to pending legislative business, and her legislative assistant is entitled to no greater immunity than the legislator he was assisting.","B":"Deny it as to the legislative assistant, because he is not a legislator protected by the speech or debate clause; but grant it as to the senator, because she is immune from suit for her speech by virtue of that clause.","C":"Grant it as to both defendants, because the senator is immune to suit for any speech she makes in the Senate under the speech or debate clause of Article I, section 6, and her legislative assistant may assert the senator's immunity for his assistance to her in preparing the speech.","D":"Grant it as to the legislative assistant, because he is protected by the freedom of speech guarantee against defamation actions by government officials based on his mere carelessness; but deny it as to the senator, because, as an officer of the United States, she is a constituent part of the government and, therefore, has no freedom of speech rights in that capacity."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Federal government immunities\n\nBranch Actor Immunity Applicable suit\n\nLegislative Member of\n• Absolute immunity for official Civil & Congress legislative acts criminal\n\nAide\n• Absolute immunity for acts that would be immune if performed by member of Congress Executive President\n• Absolute immunity for official Civil executive acts\n• No immunity for acts before taking office or unrelated to executive function Aide/official\n• Qualified immunity for discretionary acts Judicial Judge/prosecutor\n• Absolute immunity for official Civil judicial acts unless clearly lacks subject matter jurisdiction Officer/employee\n• Absolute immunity for acts pursuant to court order\n• Qualified immunity for discretionary acts The Article I, section 6 speech or debate clause gives members of Congress absolute immunity from criminal and civil liability for official legislative activities. This includes any speech made on the House or Senate floor—regardless of whether the speech relates to pending legislative business (Choice A). And this immunity extends to legislative aides who engage in activities that would be immune if performed by a member of Congress. Here, the senator relied on the legislative assistant's careless research and falsely asserted that the civil servant had previously been convicted of fraud. The civil servant then sued both for defamation. But since the senator made the assertion during a speech on the Senate floor, she is immune from suit for that speech. And the legislative assistant may assert the senator's immunity for his assistance to her in preparing that speech. Therefore, the court should grant the motion to dismiss as to both defendants (Choice B). (Choice D) All persons—including legislators—have freedom of speech. But defamatory statements fall outside First Amendment protections. And though prominent government\n\nofficials can only prevail in a defamation action if they prove actual malice, minor government officials need only prove negligence (as seen here). Therefore, only the speech or debate clause will immunize the defendants' conduct.\n\nEducational objective: Under the speech or debate clause, members of Congress are immune from criminal and civil liability for any official legislative activity (eg, a speech given on the floor of Congress). And this immunity extends to legislative aides who engage in conduct that would be immune if performed by a member of Congress.\n\nReferences\n• Gravel v. United States, 408 U.S. 606, 621–22 (1972) (holding that the speech or debate clause grants immunity to members of Congress and legislative aides for certain conduct).\n\n• 77 Am. Jur. 2d United States § 7 (2019) (explaining that the speech or debate clause grants legislative immunity).","educationalObjective":"Under the speech or debate clause, members of Congress are immune from criminal and civil liability for any official legislative activity (eg, a speech given on the floor of Congress). And this immunity extends to legislative aides who engage in conduct that would be immune if performed by a member of Congress.","references":"• Gravel v. United States, 408 U.S. 606, 621–22 (1972) (holding that the speech or debate clause grants immunity to members of Congress and legislative aides for certain conduct).\n\n• 77 Am. Jur. 2d United States § 7 (2019) (explaining that the speech or debate clause grants legislative immunity).","rule":"Under the speech or debate clause, members of Congress are immune from criminal and civil liability for any official legislative activity (eg, a speech given on the floor of Congress). And this immunity extends to legislative aides who engage in conduct that would be immune if performed by a member of Congress.","irac":{"issue":"As a matter of constitutional law, how should the court proceed on the defendants' motion?","rule":"Under the speech or debate clause, members of Congress are immune from criminal and civil liability for any official legislative activity (eg, a speech given on the floor of Congress). And this immunity extends to legislative aides who engage in conduct that would be immune if performed by a member of Congress.","analysis":"Federal government immunities\n\nBranch Actor Immunity Applicable suit\n\nLegislative Member of\n• Absolute immunity for official Civil & Congress legislative acts criminal\n\nAide\n• Absolute immunity for acts that would be immune if performed by member of Congress Executive President\n• Absolute immunity for official Civil executive acts\n• No immunity for acts before taking office or unrelated to executive function Aide/official\n• Qualified immunity for discretionary acts Judicial Judge/prosecutor\n• Absolute immunity for official Civil judicial acts unless clearly lacks subject matter jurisdiction Officer/employee\n• Absolute immunity for acts pursuant to court order\n• Qualified immunity for discretionary acts The Article I, section 6 speech or debate clause gives members of Congress absolute immunity from criminal and civil liability for official legislative activities. This includes any speech made on the House or Senate floor—regardless of whether the speech relates to pending legislative business (Choice A). And this immunity extends to legislative aides who engage in activities that would be immune if performed by a member of Congress. Here, the senator relied on the legislative assistant's careless research and falsely asserted that the civil servant had previously been convicted of fraud. The civil servant then sued both for defamation. But since the senator made the assertion during a speech on the Senate floor, she is immune from suit for that speech. And the legislative assistant may assert the senator's immunity for his assistance to her in preparing that speech. Therefore, the court should grant the motion to dismiss as to both defendants (Choice B). (Choice D) All persons—including legislators—have freedom of speech. But defamatory statements fall outside First Amendment protections. And though prominent government\n\nofficials can only prevail in a defamation action if they prove actual malice, minor government officials need only prove negligence (as seen here). Therefore, only the speech or debate clause will immunize the defendants' conduct.","conclusion":"Answer C: Grant it as to both defendants, because the senator is immune to suit for any speech she makes in the Senate under the speech or debate clause of Article I, section 6, and her legislative assistant may assert the senator's immunity for his assistance to her in preparing the speech."},"questionZh":"一名参议员在美国参议院发表讲话，声称一名责任较轻的联邦公务员曾两次被州定罪犯有欺诈罪。在做出这一断言时，参议员完全依赖于她的首席立法助理所做的研究。事实上，在州法院诉讼中被判犯有这些罪行的是另一个同名男子，而不是公务员。这个错误是立法助理的粗心造成的。当参议员在参议院发表有关他的演讲时，没有正在考虑影响公务员任命或纪律或公务员所工作的联邦机构计划的立法。公务员以诽谤罪起诉参议员和她的立法助理。两名被告均提出驳回申诉的动议。作为宪法问题，法院应如何审理被告的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"对两名被告均予以否认，因为参议员根据演讲或辩论条款享有的任何豁免权并不适用于与未决立法事务无关的演讲，而且她的立法助理所享有的豁免权并不比他所协助的立法者更大。","B":"否认立法助理，因为他不是受发言或辩论条款保护的立法会议员；但授予参议员这一权利，因为根据该条款，她的演讲不受诉讼。","C":"授予两名被告，因为参议员根据第一条第 6 节的演讲或辩论条款在参议院发表的任何演讲均享有豁免权，并且她的立法助理可以主张参议员因协助她准备演讲而享有豁免权。","D":"对于立法助理，因为他受到言论自由保障的保护，不会因为他的粗心而被政府官员诽谤；但对于参议员则否认这一点，因为作为美国官员，她是政府的组成部分，因此没有言论自由权。"},"explanationZh":"联邦政府豁免权\n\n分支演员免疫适用套装\n\n立法会议员\n• 官方民事和国会立法行为的绝对刑事豁免权\n\n爱德\n• 对于由国会议员执行主席实施的行为享有绝对豁免权\n• 官方民事行政行为的绝对豁免权\n• 就职前或与行政职能无关的行为不享有豁免权 助手/官员\n• 酌情行为的合格豁免权 法官/检察官\n• 官方民事司法行为的绝对豁免权，除非明确缺乏主题管辖权 官员/雇员\n• 根据法院命令的行为享有绝对豁免权\n• 酌情行为的合格豁免权 第一条第 6 节演讲或辩论条款赋予国会议员绝对豁免权，使其免受官方立法活动的刑事和民事责任。这包括在众议院或参议院发表的任何演讲，无论该演讲是否与悬而未决的立法事务有关（选择 A）。这种豁免权也适用于立法助手，他们从事的活动如果由国会议员进行则可以豁免。在这里，参议员依靠立法助理的粗心研究，错误地断言该公务员以前曾被判犯有欺诈罪。该公务员随后以诽谤罪起诉两人。但由于这位参议员是在参议院演讲时提出这一主张的，因此她不会因该演讲而受到诉讼。立法助理可以主张参议员因协助她准备演讲而享有豁免权。因此，法院应批准驳回两名被告的动议（选择B）。 （选项 D）所有人——包括立法者——都有言论自由。但诽谤性言论不属于第一修正案的保护范围。尽管显赫的政府\n\n官员只有证明确实存在恶意，才能在诽谤诉讼中胜诉，小政府官员只需要证明疏忽（如此处所示）。因此，只有演讲或辩论条款才能使被告的行为免受影响。\n\n教育目标：根据演讲或辩论条款，国会议员对任何官方立法活动（例如，在国会发表演讲）免受刑事和民事责任。这种豁免权也适用于立法助手，他们的行为如果由国会议员进行则可以豁免。\n\n参考文献\n• Gravel v. United States, 408 U.S. 606, 621–22 (1972)（认为演讲或辩论条款授予国会议员和立法助理某些行为的豁免权）。\n\n• 上午 77 点。尤尔。 2d United States § 7 (2019)（解释演讲或辩论条款授予立法豁免权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据演讲或辩论条款，国会议员对任何官方立法活动（例如，在国会发表演讲）免受刑事和民事责任。这种豁免权也适用于立法助手，他们的行为如果由国会议员进行则可以豁免。","iracZh":{"issue":"作为宪法问题，法院应如何审理被告的动议？","rule":"根据演讲或辩论条款，国会议员对任何官方立法活动（例如，在国会发表演讲）免受刑事和民事责任。这种豁免权也适用于立法助手，他们的行为如果由国会议员进行则可以豁免。","analysis":"联邦政府豁免权\n\n分支演员免疫适用套装\n\n立法会议员\n• 官方民事和国会立法行为的绝对刑事豁免权\n\n爱德\n• 对于由国会议员执行主席实施的行为享有绝对豁免权\n• 官方民事行政行为的绝对豁免权\n• 就职前或与行政职能无关的行为不享有豁免权 助手/官员\n• 酌情行为的合格豁免权 法官/检察官\n• 官方民事司法行为的绝对豁免权，除非明确缺乏主题管辖权 官员/雇员\n• 根据法院命令的行为享有绝对豁免权\n• 酌情行为的合格豁免权 第一条第 6 节演讲或辩论条款赋予国会议员绝对豁免权，使其免受官方立法活动的刑事和民事责任。这包括在众议院或参议院发表的任何演讲，无论该演讲是否与悬而未决的立法事务有关（选择 A）。这种豁免权也适用于立法助手，他们从事的活动如果由国会议员进行则可以豁免。在这里，参议员依靠立法助理的粗心研究，错误地断言该公务员以前曾被判犯有欺诈罪。该公务员随后以诽谤罪起诉两人。但由于这位参议员是在参议院演讲时提出这一主张的，因此她不会因该演讲而受到诉讼。立法助理可以主张参议员因协助她准备演讲而享有豁免权。因此，法院应批准驳回两名被告的动议（选择B）。 （选项 D）所有人——包括立法者——都有言论自由。但诽谤性言论不属于第一修正案的保护范围。尽管显赫的政府\n\n官员只有证明确实存在恶意，才能在诽谤诉讼中胜诉，小政府官员只需要证明疏忽（如此处所示）。因此，只有演讲或辩论条款才能使被告的行为免受影响。\n\n教育目标：根据演讲或辩论条款，国会议员对任何官方立法活动（例如，在国会发表演讲）免受刑事和民事责任。这种豁免权也适用于立法助手，他们的行为如果由国会议员进行则可以豁免。\n\n参考文献\n• Gravel v. United States, 408 U.S. 606, 621–22 (1972)（认为演讲或辩论条款授予国会议员和立法助理某些行为的豁免权）。\n\n• 上午 77 点。尤尔。 2d United States § 7 (2019)（解释演讲或辩论条款授予立法豁免权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：授予两名被告，因为参议员根据第一条第 6 节的演讲或辩论条款在参议院发表的任何演讲均享有豁免权，并且她的立法助理可以主张参议员因协助她准备演讲而享有豁免权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"defamatory","ipa":"/dɪfˈæmʌtˌɔɹi/","syllables":"defam·a·tory","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 破坏名誉的, 诽谤的；[法] 破坏名誉的, 诽谤的, 中伤的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 破坏名誉的, 诽谤的；[法] 破坏名誉的, 诽谤的, 中伤的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"But defamatory statements fall outside First Amendment protections.","synonyms":["calumniatory","calumnious","denigrative","denigrating"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":97,"sourceQuestionPage":290,"sourceQuestionPages":[290],"sourceExplanationPages":[291,292],"sourceAnswerPages":[291,292],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-098","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"A state constitution provides that in every criminal trial \"the accused shall have the right to confront all witnesses against him face to face.\" A defendant was convicted in state court of child abuse based on testimony from a six-year-old child. The child testified while she was seated behind one-way glass, which allowed the defendant to see the child but did not allow the child to see the defendant. The defendant appealed his conviction to the state's highest court, claiming that the inability of the child to see the defendant while she testified violated both the United States Constitution and the state constitution. Without addressing the federal constitutional issue, the state's highest court reversed the defendant's conviction and ordered a new trial. The court held that \"the constitution of this state is clear, and it requires that while testifying in a criminal trial, a witness must be able to see the defendant.\" The state petitioned the United States Supreme Court for a writ of certiorari. On which ground should the United States Supreme Court DENY the state's petition?","choices":{"A":"A state may not seek appellate review in the United States Supreme Court of the reversal of a criminal conviction by its highest court.","B":"The decision of the state's highest court requires a new trial, and therefore it is not a final judgment.","C":"The decision of the state's highest court was based on an adequate and independent state ground.","D":"The Sixth Amendment to the United States Constitution does not require that a witness against a criminal defendant be able to see the defendant while the witness testifies."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has discretion to review a highest state court judgment that turns on federal law. But SCOTUS must decline to review a state court judgment based on state grounds that are:\n• adequate – the state grounds fully resolve the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and\n\n• independent – it is clear from the opinion that the state court did not rely on federal law in reaching its conclusion.\n\nHere, the defendant appealed his conviction to the state's highest court, claiming violations of both the U.S. Constitution and the state constitution. Without addressing the federal constitutional issue, the court reversed his conviction on the ground that the state constitution clearly requires that a witness be able to see a criminal defendant while testifying. Since the court's decision was based on an adequate and independent state ground, SCOTUS should deny the state's petition for a writ of certiorari (ie, request for appellate review). (Choice A) A state may seek appellate review in the Supreme Court of the reversal of a criminal conviction by the state's highest court. But SCOTUS must decline to review the reversal if it rests on adequate and independent state grounds. (Choice B) Although the decision of the state's highest court requires a new trial, it is a final judgment since it fully resolves the confrontation issue—ie, whether witnesses must be able to see criminal defendants while testifying—and that issue will not arise on retrial. (Choice D) A criminal defendant's Sixth Amendment right to confront adverse witnesses does not require that a witness be able to see the defendant while testifying. But here, SCOTUS would not reach the merits of the defendant's Sixth Amendment claim since the decision of the state's highest court was based entirely on state law.\n\nEducational objective: The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment based on state-law grounds that are adequate (ie, the state grounds fully resolve the matter) AND independent (ie, the state court did not rely on federal law).\n\nReferences\n• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983) (holding that a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state-law grounds if the state court makes a plain statement to that effect).\n\n• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 44 (2019) (explaining the plain statement rule).","educationalObjective":"The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment based on state-law grounds that are adequate (ie, the state grounds fully resolve the matter) AND independent (ie, the state court did not rely on federal law).","references":"• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983) (holding that a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state-law grounds if the state court makes a plain statement to that effect).\n\n• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 44 (2019) (explaining the plain statement rule).","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment based on state-law grounds that are adequate (ie, the state grounds fully resolve the matter) AND independent (ie, the state court did not rely on federal law).","irac":{"issue":"On which ground should the United States Supreme Court DENY the state's petition?","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment based on state-law grounds that are adequate (ie, the state grounds fully resolve the matter) AND independent (ie, the state court did not rely on federal law).","analysis":"The U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has discretion to review a highest state court judgment that turns on federal law. But SCOTUS must decline to review a state court judgment based on state grounds that are:\n• adequate – the state grounds fully resolve the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and\n\n• independent – it is clear from the opinion that the state court did not rely on federal law in reaching its conclusion.\n\nHere, the defendant appealed his conviction to the state's highest court, claiming violations of both the U.S. Constitution and the state constitution. Without addressing the federal constitutional issue, the court reversed his conviction on the ground that the state constitution clearly requires that a witness be able to see a criminal defendant while testifying. Since the court's decision was based on an adequate and independent state ground, SCOTUS should deny the state's petition for a writ of certiorari (ie, request for appellate review). (Choice A) A state may seek appellate review in the Supreme Court of the reversal of a criminal conviction by the state's highest court. But SCOTUS must decline to review the reversal if it rests on adequate and independent state grounds. (Choice B) Although the decision of the state's highest court requires a new trial, it is a final judgment since it fully resolves the confrontation issue—ie, whether witnesses must be able to see criminal defendants while testifying—and that issue will not arise on retrial. (Choice D) A criminal defendant's Sixth Amendment right to confront adverse witnesses does not require that a witness be able to see the defendant while testifying. But here, SCOTUS would not reach the merits of the defendant's Sixth Amendment claim since the decision of the state's highest court was based entirely on state law.","conclusion":"Answer C: The decision of the state's highest court was based on an adequate and independent state ground."},"questionZh":"州宪法规定，在每次刑事审判中，“被告有权与所有对他不利的证人面对面对峙”。根据一名六岁儿童的证词，一名被告在州法院被判虐待儿童罪。孩子作证时，她坐在单向玻璃后面，这让被告可以看到孩子，但不允许孩子看到被告。被告向州最高法院提出上诉，声称孩子在作证时无法见被告违反了美国宪法和州宪法。在没有解决联邦宪法问题的情况下，该州最高法院推翻了对被告的定罪并下令重新审判。法院认为，“本州宪法明确规定，在刑事审判中作证时，证人必须能够见到被告”。该州向美国最高法院请求调卷令。美国最高法院应以什么理由驳回该州的请愿书？","choicesZh":{"A":"州不得向美国最高法院寻求上诉审查以推翻其最高法院的刑事定罪。","B":"该州最高法院的判决需要重新审理，因此不是最终判决。","C":"该州最高法院的裁决是基于充分且独立的州理由。","D":"美国宪法第六修正案没有要求刑事被告的证人在作证时能够见到被告。"},"explanationZh":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 有权审查涉及联邦法律的最高法院判决。但最高法院必须拒绝审查州法院基于以下州理由的判决：\n• 充分——州理由充分解决问题，联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，并且\n\n• 独立——从意见中可以清楚地看出，州法院在得出结论时并未依赖联邦法律。\n\n在此，被告向州最高法院提出上诉，声称其违反了美国宪法和州宪法。在没有解决联邦宪法问题的情况下，法院推翻了对他的定罪，理由是州宪法明确要求证人在作证时能够见到刑事被告。由于法院的判决是基于充分且独立的州理由，最高法院应驳回该州的调卷令状申请（即上诉审查请求）。 （选择 A）州可以向最高法院寻求上诉复审，以推翻州最高法院的刑事定罪。但如果最高法院有充分且独立的州理由，则最高法院必须拒绝审查这一逆转。 （选项B）该州最高法院的判决虽然需要重新审理，但它是终审判决，因为它完全解决了对抗问题，即证人作证时是否必须能够见到刑事被告人，而重审时不会出现该问题。 （选项 D）刑事被告人面对不利证人的第六修正案权利并不要求证人在作证时能够看到被告人。但在这里，最高法院不会讨论被告第六修正案主张的实质，因为该州最高法院的判决完全基于州法律。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院不能根据充分（即州法律充分解决问题）和独立（即州法院不依赖联邦法律）的州法律依据来审查州法院的判决。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983)（认为州最高法院的判决基于充分且独立的州法律依据，如果州法院对此作出明确声明）。\n\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 44 (2019)（解释简单声明规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"美国最高法院不能根据充分（即州法律充分解决问题）和独立（即州法院不依赖联邦法律）的州法律依据来审查州法院的判决。","iracZh":{"issue":"美国最高法院应以什么理由驳回该州的请愿书？","rule":"美国最高法院不能根据充分（即州法律充分解决问题）和独立（即州法院不依赖联邦法律）的州法律依据来审查州法院的判决。","analysis":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 有权审查涉及联邦法律的最高法院判决。但最高法院必须拒绝审查州法院基于以下州理由的判决：\n• 充分——州理由充分解决问题，联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，并且\n\n• 独立——从意见中可以清楚地看出，州法院在得出结论时并未依赖联邦法律。\n\n在此，被告向州最高法院提出上诉，声称其违反了美国宪法和州宪法。在没有解决联邦宪法问题的情况下，法院推翻了对他的定罪，理由是州宪法明确要求证人在作证时能够见到刑事被告。由于法院的判决是基于充分且独立的州理由，最高法院应驳回该州的调卷令状申请（即上诉审查请求）。 （选择 A）州可以向最高法院寻求上诉复审，以推翻州最高法院的刑事定罪。但如果最高法院有充分且独立的州理由，则最高法院必须拒绝审查这一逆转。 （选项B）该州最高法院的判决虽然需要重新审理，但它是终审判决，因为它完全解决了对抗问题，即证人作证时是否必须能够见到刑事被告人，而重审时不会出现该问题。 （选项 D）刑事被告人面对不利证人的第六修正案权利并不要求证人在作证时能够看到被告人。但在这里，最高法院不会讨论被告第六修正案主张的实质，因为该州最高法院的判决完全基于州法律。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院不能根据充分（即州法律充分解决问题）和独立（即州法院不依赖联邦法律）的州法律依据来审查州法院的判决。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983)（认为州最高法院的判决基于充分且独立的州法律依据，如果州法院对此作出明确声明）。\n\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 44 (2019)（解释简单声明规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该州最高法院的裁决是基于充分且独立的州理由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"witness","ipa":"/wˈɪtnəs/","syllables":"wit·ness","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 证人, 目击者, 证据, 证词；vt. 目击, 作证, 证明, 表明；vi. 作证人, 作为证据","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 证人, 目击者, 证据, 证词；vt. 目击, 作证, 证明, 表明；vi. 作证人, 作为证据","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:witnesses/d:witnessed/p:witnessed/i:witnessing/3:witnesses","sourceSentence":"A state constitution provides that in every criminal trial \"the accused shall have the right to confront all witnesses against him face to face.","synonyms":["spectator","viewer","watcher","looker"]},{"term":"retrial","ipa":"/ɹitɹˈaɪəl/","syllables":"re·trial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 再审, 复试, 再试验；[法] 再审, 复审","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 再审, 复试, 再试验；[法] 再审, 复审","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:retrials","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Although the decision of the state's highest court requires a new trial, it is a final judgment since it fully resolves the confrontation issue—ie, whether witnesses must be able to see criminal defendants while testifying—and that issue will not arise on retrial.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-63e7be27eba330e7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":98,"sourceQuestionPage":293,"sourceQuestionPages":[293],"sourceExplanationPages":[294,295],"sourceAnswerPages":[294,295],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-099","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"The United States found itself involved in a foreign conflict. Congress enacted a statute that established a military court in a foreign country to determine if individuals captured in an active combat zone should be classified as enemy combatants and detained until the cessation of hostilities. The statute provides that the military court may hear evidence presented by the captured individual at its discretion. A man, a U.S. citizen, was captured in an active combat zone and tried by the military court. After the government presented its evidence, the court determined that the man was an enemy combatant and ordered that he be detained until the end of hostilities. The man was not permitted to provide a defense. The man seeks to overturn his detention in a habeas corpus proceeding in a federal district court, claiming that the statute is unconstitutional as applied to him. How is the federal court likely to rule in this action?","choices":{"A":"It will set aside the military court's ruling, because Congress has no enumerated power to establish military courts.","B":"It will set aside the military court's ruling, because the man was entitled to a fair opportunity to rebut the government's factual assertions before a neutral decision- maker.","C":"It will uphold the military court's ruling, because an individual in a foreign country does not possess constitutional rights.","D":"It will uphold the military court's ruling, because federal courts do not have jurisdiction to review decisions issued by military courts."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. Congress may therefore establish military courts and tribunals to carry out its enumerated powers to regulate the military and make rules regarding captures on land and sea (Choice A). Congress may grant military courts jurisdiction over enemy combatants, enemy civilians, and current members of the U.S. military.* However, Congress must afford procedural due process to U.S. citizens classified and detained as enemy combatants. Procedural due process requires that U.S. citizens be given:\n• notice of the factual basis for the citizen's classification as an enemy combatant and\n• a fair opportunity to rebut the government's factual assertions before a neutral decision-maker. A U.S. citizen detained as an enemy combatant who does not receive these protections may file a petition for a writ of habeas corpus in a federal court unless Congress has explicitly suspended the writ (not seen here). The federal court has jurisdiction to then set aside an unlawful detention (Choice D). Here, the man was captured in an active combat zone and classified as an enemy combatant by the military court. The statute gives the military court the discretion whether to allow a captured individual to provide evidence in his/her defense. However, the man is entitled to rebut the government's factual assertions because he is a U.S. citizen. Since the military court did not provide the man that opportunity, the statute is unconstitutional as applied to him. Therefore, the federal court will likely set aside the military court's ruling. *Military courts and tribunals do not have jurisdiction over U.S. civilians who are not classified as enemy combatants unless hostilities have forced state and federal civilian courts to close. (Choice C) U.S. citizens, including citizens in foreign countries, always possess constitutional rights.\n\nEducational objective: Congress may establish military courts and tribunals but must afford U.S. citizens classified as enemy combatants procedural due process. This requires (1) notice of the factual basis for the citizen's classification and (2) a fair opportunity to rebut those facts before a neutral decision-maker.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 14 (power to make rules regarding capture).\n\n• Hamdi v. Rumsfeld, 542 U.S. 507, 533 (2004) (O'Connor, J., plurality) (holding that detained U.S. citizens seeking to challenge enemy combatant status are entitled to procedural due process).","educationalObjective":"Congress may establish military courts and tribunals but must afford U.S. citizens classified as enemy combatants procedural due process. This requires (1) notice of the factual basis for the citizen's classification and (2) a fair opportunity to rebut those facts before a neutral decision-maker.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 14 (power to make rules regarding capture).\n\n• Hamdi v. Rumsfeld, 542 U.S. 507, 533 (2004) (O'Connor, J., plurality) (holding that detained U.S. citizens seeking to challenge enemy combatant status are entitled to procedural due process).","rule":"Congress may establish military courts and tribunals but must afford U.S. citizens classified as enemy combatants procedural due process. This requires (1) notice of the factual basis for the citizen's classification and (2) a fair opportunity to rebut those facts before a neutral decision-maker.","irac":{"issue":"How is the federal court likely to rule in this action?","rule":"Congress may establish military courts and tribunals but must afford U.S. citizens classified as enemy combatants procedural due process. This requires (1) notice of the factual basis for the citizen's classification and (2) a fair opportunity to rebut those facts before a neutral decision-maker.","analysis":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. Congress may therefore establish military courts and tribunals to carry out its enumerated powers to regulate the military and make rules regarding captures on land and sea (Choice A). Congress may grant military courts jurisdiction over enemy combatants, enemy civilians, and current members of the U.S. military.* However, Congress must afford procedural due process to U.S. citizens classified and detained as enemy combatants. Procedural due process requires that U.S. citizens be given:\n• notice of the factual basis for the citizen's classification as an enemy combatant and\n• a fair opportunity to rebut the government's factual assertions before a neutral decision-maker. A U.S. citizen detained as an enemy combatant who does not receive these protections may file a petition for a writ of habeas corpus in a federal court unless Congress has explicitly suspended the writ (not seen here). The federal court has jurisdiction to then set aside an unlawful detention (Choice D). Here, the man was captured in an active combat zone and classified as an enemy combatant by the military court. The statute gives the military court the discretion whether to allow a captured individual to provide evidence in his/her defense. However, the man is entitled to rebut the government's factual assertions because he is a U.S. citizen. Since the military court did not provide the man that opportunity, the statute is unconstitutional as applied to him. Therefore, the federal court will likely set aside the military court's ruling. *Military courts and tribunals do not have jurisdiction over U.S. civilians who are not classified as enemy combatants unless hostilities have forced state and federal civilian courts to close. (Choice C) U.S. citizens, including citizens in foreign countries, always possess constitutional rights.","conclusion":"Answer B: It will set aside the military court's ruling, because the man was entitled to a fair opportunity to rebut the government's factual assertions before a neutral decision- maker."},"questionZh":"美国发现自己卷入了一场外国冲突。国会颁布了一项法规，在外国设立军事法庭，以确定在活跃战区被俘的个人是否应被归类为敌方战斗人员并被拘留直至敌对行动停止。该法规规定，军事法庭可以酌情听取被俘人员提出的证据。一名美国公民在活跃的战区被抓获并接受军事法庭审判。政府出示证据后，法院认定该男子是敌方战斗人员，并下令拘留他直至敌对行动结束。该男子不被允许提供辩护。该男子试图在联邦地区法院提起的人身保护令诉讼中推翻对他的拘留，声称该法规适用于他是违宪的。联邦法院可能如何对此诉讼做出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"它将推翻军事法庭的裁决，因为国会没有设立军事法庭的列举权力。","B":"它将推翻军事法庭的裁决，因为该男子有权在中立决策者面前反驳政府的事实主张。","C":"它将维持军事法庭的裁决，因为外国个人不享有宪法权利。","D":"它将维持军事法院的裁决，因为联邦法院无权审查军事法院做出的裁决。"},"explanationZh":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。因此，国会可以设立军事法院和法庭，以履行其规定的权力来监管军队并制定有关陆地和海上捕获的规则（选择 A）。国会可以授予军事法庭对敌方战斗人员、敌方平民和美国现役军人的管辖权。*但是，国会必须为被归类和拘留为敌方战斗人员的美国公民提供程序性正当程序。程序正当程序要求美国公民获得：\n• 关于将公民归类为敌方战斗人员的事实依据的通知；\n• 在中立决策者面前反驳政府事实主张的公平机会。作为敌方战斗人员而被拘留且未获得这些保护的美国公民可以向联邦法院提交人身保护令状请愿书，除非国会明确暂停该令状（此处未见）。联邦法院有权撤销非法拘留（选择 D）。在这里，该男子在活跃的战斗地区被俘，并被军事法庭归类为敌方战斗人员。该法规赋予军事法庭是否允许被俘人员为其辩护提供证据的自由裁量权。然而，该男子有权反驳政府的事实主张，因为他是美国公民。由于军事法庭没有为该男子提供这个机会，因此该法规适用于他是违宪的。因此，联邦法院很可能会推翻军事法庭的裁决。 *军事法院和法庭对未被归类为敌方战斗人员的美国平民没有管辖权，除非敌对行动迫使州和联邦民事法院关闭。 （选项C）美国公民，包括外国公民，始终享有宪法权利。\n\n教育目标：国会可以设立军事法院和法庭，但必须为被归类为敌方战斗人员的美国公民提供程序正当程序。这需要（1）通知公民分类的事实依据，以及（2）有公平的机会在中立决策者面前反驳这些事实。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 14（制定有关捕获的规则的权力）。\n\n• Hamdi v. Rumsfeld, 542 U.S. 507, 533 (2004)（O'Connor, J.，复数）（认为寻求挑战敌方战斗人员身份的被拘留美国公民有权获得程序性正当程序）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会可以设立军事法院和法庭，但必须为被列为敌方战斗人员的美国公民提供程序正当程序。这需要（1）通知公民分类的事实依据，以及（2）有公平的机会在中立决策者面前反驳这些事实。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法院可能如何对此诉讼做出裁决？","rule":"国会可以设立军事法院和法庭，但必须为被列为敌方战斗人员的美国公民提供程序正当程序。这需要（1）通知公民分类的事实依据，以及（2）有公平的机会在中立决策者面前反驳这些事实。","analysis":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。因此，国会可以设立军事法院和法庭，以履行其规定的权力来监管军队并制定有关陆地和海上捕获的规则（选择 A）。国会可以授予军事法庭对敌方战斗人员、敌方平民和美国现役军人的管辖权。*但是，国会必须为被归类和拘留为敌方战斗人员的美国公民提供程序性正当程序。程序正当程序要求美国公民获得：\n• 关于将公民归类为敌方战斗人员的事实依据的通知；\n• 在中立决策者面前反驳政府事实主张的公平机会。作为敌方战斗人员而被拘留且未获得这些保护的美国公民可以向联邦法院提交人身保护令状请愿书，除非国会明确暂停该令状（此处未见）。联邦法院有权撤销非法拘留（选择 D）。在这里，该男子在活跃的战斗地区被俘，并被军事法庭归类为敌方战斗人员。该法规赋予军事法庭是否允许被俘人员为其辩护提供证据的自由裁量权。然而，该男子有权反驳政府的事实主张，因为他是美国公民。由于军事法庭没有为该男子提供这个机会，因此该法规适用于他是违宪的。因此，联邦法院很可能会推翻军事法庭的裁决。 *军事法院和法庭对未被归类为敌方战斗人员的美国平民没有管辖权，除非敌对行动迫使州和联邦民事法院关闭。 （选项C）美国公民，包括外国公民，始终享有宪法权利。\n\n教育目标：国会可以设立军事法院和法庭，但必须为被归类为敌方战斗人员的美国公民提供程序正当程序。这需要（1）通知公民分类的事实依据，以及（2）有公平的机会在中立决策者面前反驳这些事实。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 14（制定有关捕获的规则的权力）。\n\n• Hamdi v. Rumsfeld, 542 U.S. 507, 533 (2004)（O'Connor, J.，复数）（认为寻求挑战敌方战斗人员身份的被拘留美国公民有权获得程序性正当程序）。","conclusion":"答案 B：它将推翻军事法庭的裁决，因为该男子有权在中立决策者面前反驳政府的事实主张。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"procedural due process","ipa":"/pɹʌsˈidʒɝəl dˈu pɹˈɑsˌɛs/","syllables":"pro·ce·dural due process","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"程序性正当程序：剥夺生命、自由或财产前后须提供适当程序","contextualMeaning":"程序性正当程序：剥夺生命、自由或财产前后须提供适当程序","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"citizens classified as enemy combatants procedural due process.","synonyms":["procedural due physical process","procedural due procedure","procedural due outgrowth"]},{"term":"classified","ipa":"/klˈæsʌfˌaɪd/","syllables":"clas·si·fied","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 分类的, 被归入某类的, 被指定为机密的；[建] 分了类的, 分了级的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 分类的, 被归入某类的, 被指定为机密的；[建] 分了类的, 分了级的","wordForm":"形容词；词形 0:classify/1:dp/d:classified/s:classifieds/p:classified","sourceSentence":"Congress enacted a statute that established a military court in a foreign country to determine if individuals captured in an active combat zone should be classified as enemy combatants and detained until the cessation of hostilities.","synonyms":["classified ad","classified advertisement"]},{"term":"tribunals","ipa":"/tɹˌaɪbjˈunəlz/","syllables":"tri·bunals","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 审理委员会( tribunal的复数形式 )； 特别法庭； 法官席","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 审理委员会( tribunal的复数形式 )； 特别法庭； 法官席","wordForm":"名词；原形 tribunal；词形 0:tribunal/1:s","sourceSentence":"Congress may establish military courts and tribunals but must afford U.","synonyms":["court","judicature"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-c4dba56348078ce8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":99,"sourceQuestionPage":296,"sourceQuestionPages":[296],"sourceExplanationPages":[297,298],"sourceAnswerPages":[297,298],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-100","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"Congressional committees heard testimony from present and former holders of licenses issued by state vocational licensing boards. According to the testimony, the boards had unfairly manipulated their disciplinary proceedings in order to revoke the licenses of some license holders as a means of protecting favored licensees from competition. In response, Congress enacted a statute prescribing detailed procedural requirements for the disciplinary proceedings of all state vocational licensing boards. The statute requires the state boards to provide licensees with adequate notice and opportunity for an adjudicatory hearing in all disciplinary proceedings. The statute also prescribes membership criteria for state vocational licensing boards, designed to ensure that the boards are likely to be neutral. Which of the following provides the best source of authority for this federal statute?","choices":{"A":"Section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"The general welfare clause of Article I, Section 8.","C":"The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV, Section 2.","D":"The takings clause of the Fifth Amendment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Fourteenth Amendment protections\n\nClause Applicability\n\nEqual protection Discriminatory treatment of similarly situated people\n\nDue process Substantive – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification\n\nProcedural – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate process\n\nPrivileges or Interference with rights of national citizenship immunities*\n\n*Rarely a correct answer.\n\nSection 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment gives Congress the power to enact appropriate legislation to enforce the rights guaranteed by the amendment (eg, procedural due process). Legislation is appropriate under this section when it:\n• seeks to remedy or prevent state action that violates the Fourteenth Amendment and\n• is narrowly tailored—ie, its requirements are congruent with and proportional to the violations it addresses. A violation of procedural due process occurs when the state fails to follow certain procedures when it intentionally deprives a person of a life, liberty, or property interest (eg, vocational license). These procedures include giving the person notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker. Here, state vocational licensing boards apparently violated procedural due process by unfairly manipulating their disciplinary proceedings. Congress attempted to remedy this by enacting a statute prescribing detailed procedural requirements for such proceedings in all states. This statute is narrowly tailored since its requirements—ie, adequate notice and a hearing before a neutral board—are the basic standards of procedural fairness. Therefore, section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment is the best source of authority for this statute. (Choice B) The Article I, section 8 general welfare clause empowers Congress to spend for the general welfare (ie, for any public purpose). But this clause does not apply because the statute is not a spending measure.\n\n(Choice C) The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV, section 2 prohibits states from improperly discriminating against citizens of other states. Since this statute does not address discrimination against citizens of other states, this clause does not apply. (Choice D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause prohibits governmental taking of private property for public use without just compensation. But this clause only applies to real or personal property—not vocational licenses.\n\nEducational objective: Congress can enforce Fourteenth Amendment rights through legislation that (1) seeks to remedy or prevent state violations of the amendment and (2) is narrowly tailored—ie, its requirements are congruent with and proportional to the violations it addresses.\n\nReferences\n• Tennessee v. Lane, 541 U.S. 509, 520 (2004) (explaining that Congress can enact laws to remedy or prevent Fourteenth Amendment violations if there is congruence and proportionality between the injury and the law).","educationalObjective":"Congress can enforce Fourteenth Amendment rights through legislation that (1) seeks to remedy or prevent state violations of the amendment and (2) is narrowly tailored—ie, its requirements are congruent with and proportional to the violations it addresses.","references":"• Tennessee v. Lane, 541 U.S. 509, 520 (2004) (explaining that Congress can enact laws to remedy or prevent Fourteenth Amendment violations if there is congruence and proportionality between the injury and the law).","rule":"Congress can enforce Fourteenth Amendment rights through legislation that (1) seeks to remedy or prevent state violations of the amendment and (2) is narrowly tailored—ie, its requirements are congruent with and proportional to the violations it addresses.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following provides the best source of authority for this federal statute?","rule":"Congress can enforce Fourteenth Amendment rights through legislation that (1) seeks to remedy or prevent state violations of the amendment and (2) is narrowly tailored—ie, its requirements are congruent with and proportional to the violations it addresses.","analysis":"Fourteenth Amendment protections\n\nClause Applicability\n\nEqual protection Discriminatory treatment of similarly situated people\n\nDue process Substantive – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification\n\nProcedural – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate process\n\nPrivileges or Interference with rights of national citizenship immunities*\n\n*Rarely a correct answer.\n\nSection 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment gives Congress the power to enact appropriate legislation to enforce the rights guaranteed by the amendment (eg, procedural due process). Legislation is appropriate under this section when it:\n• seeks to remedy or prevent state action that violates the Fourteenth Amendment and\n• is narrowly tailored—ie, its requirements are congruent with and proportional to the violations it addresses. A violation of procedural due process occurs when the state fails to follow certain procedures when it intentionally deprives a person of a life, liberty, or property interest (eg, vocational license). These procedures include giving the person notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker. Here, state vocational licensing boards apparently violated procedural due process by unfairly manipulating their disciplinary proceedings. Congress attempted to remedy this by enacting a statute prescribing detailed procedural requirements for such proceedings in all states. This statute is narrowly tailored since its requirements—ie, adequate notice and a hearing before a neutral board—are the basic standards of procedural fairness. Therefore, section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment is the best source of authority for this statute. (Choice B) The Article I, section 8 general welfare clause empowers Congress to spend for the general welfare (ie, for any public purpose). But this clause does not apply because the statute is not a spending measure.\n\n(Choice C) The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV, section 2 prohibits states from improperly discriminating against citizens of other states. Since this statute does not address discrimination against citizens of other states, this clause does not apply. (Choice D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause prohibits governmental taking of private property for public use without just compensation. But this clause only applies to real or personal property—not vocational licenses.","conclusion":"Answer A: Section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"国会委员会听取了州职业执照委员会颁发的现任和前任执照持有者的证词。根据证词，委员会不公平地操纵了纪律程序，以吊销一些许可证持有者的许可证，以此作为保护受青睐的被许可人免受竞争的手段。作为回应，国会颁布了一项法规，规定了所有州职业许可委员会纪律程序的详细程序要求。该法规要求州委员会向被许可人提供充分的通知和在所有纪律程序中进行裁决听证会的机会。该法规还规定了州职业许可委员会的成员资格标准，旨在确保委员会保持中立。以下哪项为该联邦法规提供了最佳的权力来源？","choicesZh":{"A":"第十四修正案第 5 条。","B":"第一条第 8 节的一般福利条款。","C":"第四条第二节的特权和豁免条款。","D":"第五修正案的征收条款（Takings Clause）（Takings Clause）。"},"explanationZh":"第十四修正案的保护\n\n条款适用性\n\n平等保护 对处境相似的人的歧视性待遇\n\n正当程序 实质性 – 在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n程序性——未经充分程序剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n特权或干扰国家公民豁免权*\n\n*很少有正确答案。\n\n第十四修正案第 5 条赋予国会制定适当立法的权力，以执行修正案所保障的权利（例如程序性正当程序）。当满足以下条件时，根据本节立法是适当的：\n• 寻求补救或防止违反第十四修正案的国家行为，并且\n• 是严格定制的——即，其要求与其所解决的违规行为一致且成比例。当国家故意剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产利益（例如职业执照）时未能遵循某些程序，则违反了程序性正当程序。这些程序包括向相关人员发出通知，并提供让中立决策者听取意见的有意义的机会。在这里，州职业许可委员会不公平地操纵纪律程序，显然违反了程序正当程序。国会试图通过颁布一项法规来纠正这一问题，为所有州的此类诉讼规定详细的程序要求。该法规的制定范围很窄，因为它的要求——即充分的通知和中立委员会的听证会——是程序公平的基本标准。因此，第十四修正案第五条是该法规的最佳权威来源。 （选项 B）第一条第 8 节一般福利条款授权国会为一般福利（即任何公共目的）支出。但该条款并不适用，因为该法规不是支出措施。\n\n（选项C） 第四条第二节的特权和豁免条款禁止国家不当歧视其他国家的公民。由于该法规没有解决对其他州公民的歧视，因此该条款不适用。 （选项 D） 第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私人财产供公共使用。但该条款仅适用于不动产或动产，不适用于职业许可证。\n\n教育目标：国会可以通过立法来执行第十四修正案的权利，这些立法（1）旨在补救或防止各州违反该修正案的行为，并且（2）是狭隘定制的，即其要求与其所解决的违法行为一致并成比例。\n\n参考文献\n• Tennessee v. Lane, 541 U.S. 509, 520 (2004)（解释说，如果伤害与法律之间存在一致性和相称性，国会可以颁布法律来补救或防止违反第十四修正案）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会可以通过立法强制执行第十四修正案的权利，(1) 寻求补救或防止各州违反该修正案的行为，(2) 是严格定制的，即其要求与其所解决的违法行为一致并成比例。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项为该联邦法规提供了最佳的权力来源？","rule":"国会可以通过立法强制执行第十四修正案的权利，(1) 寻求补救或防止各州违反该修正案的行为，(2) 是严格定制的，即其要求与其所解决的违法行为一致并成比例。","analysis":"第十四修正案的保护\n\n条款适用性\n\n平等保护 对处境相似的人的歧视性待遇\n\n正当程序 实质性 – 在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n程序性——未经充分程序剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n特权或干扰国家公民豁免权*\n\n*很少有正确答案。\n\n第十四修正案第 5 条赋予国会制定适当立法的权力，以执行修正案所保障的权利（例如程序性正当程序）。当满足以下条件时，根据本节立法是适当的：\n• 寻求补救或防止违反第十四修正案的国家行为，并且\n• 是严格定制的——即，其要求与其所解决的违规行为一致且成比例。当国家故意剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产利益（例如职业执照）时未能遵循某些程序，则违反了程序性正当程序。这些程序包括向相关人员发出通知，并提供让中立决策者听取意见的有意义的机会。在这里，州职业许可委员会不公平地操纵纪律程序，显然违反了程序正当程序。国会试图通过颁布一项法规来纠正这一问题，为所有州的此类诉讼规定详细的程序要求。该法规的制定范围很窄，因为它的要求——即充分的通知和中立委员会的听证会——是程序公平的基本标准。因此，第十四修正案第五条是该法规的最佳权威来源。 （选项 B）第一条第 8 节一般福利条款授权国会为一般福利（即任何公共目的）支出。但该条款并不适用，因为该法规不是支出措施。\n\n（选项C） 第四条第二节的特权和豁免条款禁止国家不当歧视其他国家的公民。由于该法规没有解决对其他州公民的歧视，因此该条款不适用。 （选项 D） 第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私人财产供公共使用。但该条款仅适用于不动产或动产，不适用于职业许可证。\n\n教育目标：国会可以通过立法来执行第十四修正案的权利，这些立法（1）旨在补救或防止各州违反该修正案的行为，并且（2）是狭隘定制的，即其要求与其所解决的违法行为一致并成比例。\n\n参考文献\n• Tennessee v. Lane, 541 U.S. 509, 520 (2004)（解释说，如果伤害与法律之间存在一致性和相称性，国会可以颁布法律来补救或防止违反第十四修正案）。","conclusion":"答案 A：第十四修正案第 5 条。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"adjudicatory","ipa":"/ə'dʒuːdɪkətərɪ/","syllables":"ad·ju·di·ca·tory","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"裁决的； 宣告的； 审判的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：裁决的； 宣告的； 审判的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The statute requires the state boards to provide licensees with adequate notice and opportunity for an adjudicatory hearing in all disciplinary proceedings.","synonyms":["adjudicative"]},{"term":"licensees","ipa":"/lˌaɪsʌnsˈiz/","syllables":"li·censees","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 售酒执照持有者（英式英语）( licensee的复数形式 )； 特许制作（或使用）…的人（或公司）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 售酒执照持有者（英式英语）( licensee的复数形式 )； 特许制作（或使用）…的人（或公司）","wordForm":"名词；原形 licensee；词形 0:licensee/1:s","sourceSentence":"According to the testimony, the boards had unfairly manipulated their disciplinary proceedings in order to revoke the licenses of some license holders as a means of protecting favored licensees from competition.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":100,"sourceQuestionPage":299,"sourceQuestionPages":[299],"sourceExplanationPages":[300,301],"sourceAnswerPages":[300,301],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-101","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"Which of the following acts by the United States Senate would be constitutionally IMPROPER?","choices":{"A":"The Senate appoints a commission to adjudicate finally a boundary dispute between two states.","B":"The Senate decides, with the House of Representatives, that a disputed state ratification of a proposed constitutional amendment is valid.","C":"The Senate determines the eligibility of a person to serve as a senator.","D":"The Senate passes a resolution calling on the President to pursue a certain foreign policy."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Independent legislative powers\n\n(legislative-action process not required)\n\nLegislative body Powers\n\nHouse of\n• Impeach federal officials Representatives\n• Elect President (when electoral-college threshold not met)\n• Determine internal rules & members' eligibility\n• Punish/expel members for bad behavior Senate\n• Try impeached federal officials\n• Confirm/reject presidential appointments\n• Approve/reject treaties\n• Determine internal rules & members' eligibility\n• Punish/expel members for bad behavior Full Congress\n• Propose constitutional amendments\n• Decide disputes regarding ratification of amendments Article I prescribes the method Congress (ie, the House of Representatives and the Senate) must use to take any action that alters the legal rights of persons outside the legislative branch (ie, legislative action). Under this method, most legislative action must be:\n• approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) and\n• presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment). But certain legislative powers are independent and do not require bicameralism and/or presentment. These include:\n• the power of the Senate, together with the House of Representatives, to decide disputes regarding the ratification of amendments (no presentment required) (Choice B) and\n\n• the power of the Senate to determine a person's eligibility to serve as a senator (no bicameralism or presentment required) (Choice C).\n\nThese independent legislative powers do not include the power to appoint a commission to adjudicate boundary disputes between two states. Therefore, it would be constitutionally improper for the Senate to do so without adhering to bicameralism and presentment. (Choice D) A resolution does not amount to legislative action because it is a nonbinding recommendation that has no effect on the legal rights of persons outside the legislative\n\nbranch. Therefore, the Senate can pass a resolution calling on the President to pursue a certain foreign policy without adhering to bicameralism or presentment.\n\nEducational objective: Absent narrow exceptions, all legislative action must be approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) AND presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).\n\nReferences\n• Coleman v. Miller, 307 U.S. 433, 456 (1939) (explaining that Congress controls the promulgation of the adoption of constitutional amendments).\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 952 (1983) (explaining that legislative action is subject to bicameralism and presentment when Congress alters the legal rights, duties, and relations of persons outside the legislative branch).","educationalObjective":"Absent narrow exceptions, all legislative action must be approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) AND presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).","references":"• Coleman v. Miller, 307 U.S. 433, 456 (1939) (explaining that Congress controls the promulgation of the adoption of constitutional amendments).\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 952 (1983) (explaining that legislative action is subject to bicameralism and presentment when Congress alters the legal rights, duties, and relations of persons outside the legislative branch).","rule":"Absent narrow exceptions, all legislative action must be approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) AND presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following acts by the United States Senate would be constitutionally IMPROPER?","rule":"Absent narrow exceptions, all legislative action must be approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) AND presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment).","analysis":"Independent legislative powers\n\n(legislative-action process not required)\n\nLegislative body Powers\n\nHouse of\n• Impeach federal officials Representatives\n• Elect President (when electoral-college threshold not met)\n• Determine internal rules & members' eligibility\n• Punish/expel members for bad behavior Senate\n• Try impeached federal officials\n• Confirm/reject presidential appointments\n• Approve/reject treaties\n• Determine internal rules & members' eligibility\n• Punish/expel members for bad behavior Full Congress\n• Propose constitutional amendments\n• Decide disputes regarding ratification of amendments Article I prescribes the method Congress (ie, the House of Representatives and the Senate) must use to take any action that alters the legal rights of persons outside the legislative branch (ie, legislative action). Under this method, most legislative action must be:\n• approved by both houses of Congress (ie, bicameralism) and\n• presented to the President for approval or disapproval (ie, presentment). But certain legislative powers are independent and do not require bicameralism and/or presentment. These include:\n• the power of the Senate, together with the House of Representatives, to decide disputes regarding the ratification of amendments (no presentment required) (Choice B) and\n\n• the power of the Senate to determine a person's eligibility to serve as a senator (no bicameralism or presentment required) (Choice C).\n\nThese independent legislative powers do not include the power to appoint a commission to adjudicate boundary disputes between two states. Therefore, it would be constitutionally improper for the Senate to do so without adhering to bicameralism and presentment. (Choice D) A resolution does not amount to legislative action because it is a nonbinding recommendation that has no effect on the legal rights of persons outside the legislative\n\nbranch. Therefore, the Senate can pass a resolution calling on the President to pursue a certain foreign policy without adhering to bicameralism or presentment.","conclusion":"Answer A: The Senate appoints a commission to adjudicate finally a boundary dispute between two states."},"questionZh":"美国参议院的下列哪项行为违反宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"参议院任命一个委员会最终裁决两国之间的边界争端。","B":"参议院与众议院共同决定，有争议的州对拟议宪法修正案的批准是有效的。","C":"参议院决定某人担任参议员的资格。","D":"参议院通过一项决议，呼吁总统奉行某种外交政策。"},"explanationZh":"独立的立法权\n\n（不需要立法行动程序）\n\n立法机构权力\n\n之家\n• 弹劾联邦官员代表\n• 选举总统（未达到选举团门槛时）\n• 确定内部规则和会员资格\n• 惩罚/开除不良行为参议院成员\n• 尝试弹劾联邦官员\n• 确认/拒绝总统任命\n• 批准/拒绝条约\n• 确定内部规则和会员资格\n• 惩罚/开除不良行为的会员 国会全体会议\n• 提出宪法修正案\n• 解决有关批准修正案的争议 第一条规定了国会（即众议院和参议院）采取任何改变立法部门之外人员合法权利的行动（即立法行动）时必须采用的方法。根据这种方法，大多数立法行动必须是：\n• 经国会两院批准（即两院制）并且\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（即陈述）。但某些立法权是独立的，不需要两院制和/或代表权。这些包括：\n• 参议院与众议院共同决定有关批准修正案的争议的权力（无需陈述）（选择B）和\n\n• 参议院有权决定一个人担任参议员的资格（无需两院制或陈述）（选项C）。\n\n这些独立的立法权不包括任命一个委员会来裁决两国之间边界争端的权力。因此，参议院在不遵守两院制和代表制的情况下这样做，在宪法上是不适当的。 （选项D）决议不构成立法行动，因为它是一项不具约束力的建议，对立法之外的人的合法权利没有影响\n\n分支。因此，参议院可以通过一项决议，要求总统奉行某种外交政策，而无需坚持两院制或陈述。\n\n教育目标：除少数例外情况外，所有立法行动都必须得到国会两院的批准（即两院制），并提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。\n\n参考文献\n• Coleman v. Miller, 307 U.S. 433, 456 (1939)（解释国会控制宪法修正案的颁布）。\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 952 (1983)（解释说，当国会改变立法部门以外人员的合法权利、义务和关系时，立法行动须服从两院制和提交）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除少数例外情况外，所有立法行动都必须得到国会两院的批准（即两院制），并提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。","iracZh":{"issue":"美国参议院的下列哪项行为违反宪法？","rule":"除少数例外情况外，所有立法行动都必须得到国会两院的批准（即两院制），并提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。","analysis":"独立的立法权\n\n（不需要立法行动程序）\n\n立法机构权力\n\n之家\n• 弹劾联邦官员代表\n• 选举总统（未达到选举团门槛时）\n• 确定内部规则和会员资格\n• 惩罚/开除不良行为参议院成员\n• 尝试弹劾联邦官员\n• 确认/拒绝总统任命\n• 批准/拒绝条约\n• 确定内部规则和会员资格\n• 惩罚/开除不良行为的会员 国会全体会议\n• 提出宪法修正案\n• 解决有关批准修正案的争议 第一条规定了国会（即众议院和参议院）采取任何改变立法部门之外人员合法权利的行动（即立法行动）时必须采用的方法。根据这种方法，大多数立法行动必须是：\n• 经国会两院批准（即两院制）并且\n• 提交给总统批准或不批准（即陈述）。但某些立法权是独立的，不需要两院制和/或代表权。这些包括：\n• 参议院与众议院共同决定有关批准修正案的争议的权力（无需陈述）（选择B）和\n\n• 参议院有权决定一个人担任参议员的资格（无需两院制或陈述）（选项C）。\n\n这些独立的立法权不包括任命一个委员会来裁决两国之间边界争端的权力。因此，参议院在不遵守两院制和代表制的情况下这样做，在宪法上是不适当的。 （选项D）决议不构成立法行动，因为它是一项不具约束力的建议，对立法之外的人的合法权利没有影响\n\n分支。因此，参议院可以通过一项决议，要求总统奉行某种外交政策，而无需坚持两院制或陈述。\n\n教育目标：除少数例外情况外，所有立法行动都必须得到国会两院的批准（即两院制），并提交总统批准或否决（即提交）。\n\n参考文献\n• Coleman v. Miller, 307 U.S. 433, 456 (1939)（解释国会控制宪法修正案的颁布）。\n\n• INS v. Chadha, 462 U.S. 919, 952 (1983)（解释说，当国会改变立法部门以外人员的合法权利、义务和关系时，立法行动须服从两院制和提交）。","conclusion":"答案 A：参议院任命一个委员会最终裁决两国之间的边界争端。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"impeach","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈitʃ/","syllables":"im·peach","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 控告, 怀疑, 检举, 弹劾；[法] 控告, 检举, 弹劾","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 控告, 怀疑, 检举, 弹劾；[法] 控告, 检举, 弹劾","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:impeached/i:impeaching/p:impeached/3:impeaches","sourceSentence":"Independent legislative powers (legislative-action process not required) Legislative body Powers House of • Impeach federal officials Representatives • Elect President (when electoral-college threshold not met) • Determine internal rules & members' eligibility • Punish/expel members for bad behavior Senate • Try impeached federal officials • Confirm/reject presidential appointments • Approve/reject treaties • Determi","synonyms":["accuse","incriminate","criminate"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":101,"sourceQuestionPage":302,"sourceQuestionPages":[302],"sourceExplanationPages":[303,304],"sourceAnswerPages":[303,304],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-102","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Eleventh Amendment & State Sovereign Immunity","question":"A federal statute enacted pursuant to the power of Congress to enforce the Fourteenth Amendment prohibits any state from requiring any of its employees to retire from state employment solely because of their age. The statute expressly authorizes employees required by a state to retire from state employment solely because of their age to sue the state government in federal district court for any damages resulting from that state action. On the basis of this federal statute, a retiree sues State X in federal district court. State X moves to dismiss the suit on the ground that Congress lacks authority to authorize such suits against a state. Which of the following is the strongest argument that the retiree can offer in opposition to the state's motion to dismiss this suit?","choices":{"A":"When Congress exercises power vested in it by any provision of the Constitution, Congress has unlimited authority to authorize private actions for damages against a state.","B":"When Congress exercises power vested in it by the Fourteenth Amendment, Congress may enact appropriate remedial legislation expressly subjecting the states to private suits for damages in federal court.","C":"While the Eleventh Amendment applies to suits in federal court by citizens of one state against another state, it does not apply to such suits by citizens against their own states.","D":"While the Eleventh Amendment restrains the federal judiciary, that amendment does not limit the power of Congress to modify the sovereign immunity of the states."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment generally forbids the federal courts from hearing private suits for damages against the states. Congress cannot override this ban, even to exercise one of its constitutionally enumerated powers (Choices A & D). But there is a narrow exception to this rule when Congress is clearly acting to enforce the Civil War Amendments (eg, Fourteenth Amendment—as seen here). In that case, it may enact appropriate remedial legislation expressly subjecting the states to private suits for damages in federal court. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment applies to all private suits for damages against the states—including suits by citizens against their own state. But it does not bar Congress from authorizing such suits as a means of enforcing the Civil War Amendments.\n\nEducational objective: The Eleventh Amendment generally forbids the federal courts from hearing private suits for damages against the states. But Congress can authorize such suits when it is clearly acting to enforce the Civil War Amendments (ie, Thirteenth, Fourteenth, and Fifteenth Amendments).\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Georgia, 546 U.S. 151, 158–59 (2006) (explaining that the Fourteenth Amendment allows Congress to abrogate state sovereign immunity by authorizing private suits for damages against the states).","educationalObjective":"The Eleventh Amendment generally forbids the federal courts from hearing private suits for damages against the states. But Congress can authorize such suits when it is clearly acting to enforce the Civil War Amendments (ie, Thirteenth, Fourteenth, and Fifteenth Amendments).","references":"• United States v. Georgia, 546 U.S. 151, 158–59 (2006) (explaining that the Fourteenth Amendment allows Congress to abrogate state sovereign immunity by authorizing private suits for damages against the states).","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment generally forbids the federal courts from hearing private suits for damages against the states. But Congress can authorize such suits when it is clearly acting to enforce the Civil War Amendments (ie, Thirteenth, Fourteenth, and Fifteenth Amendments).","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest argument that the retiree can offer in opposition to the state's motion to dismiss this suit?","rule":"The Eleventh Amendment generally forbids the federal courts from hearing private suits for damages against the states. But Congress can authorize such suits when it is clearly acting to enforce the Civil War Amendments (ie, Thirteenth, Fourteenth, and Fifteenth Amendments).","analysis":"Eleventh Amendment\n\n(state immunity from suit in federal court)\n\nImmunity\n• Suit brought by private party or foreign government\n• Suit against state official violating state law\n• Exceptions: – State consents to suit – Immunity removed by 13th, 14th, or 15th Amendment – State official sued for injunctive or declaratory relief – Damages to be paid by state officer personally (not state treasury) – State official sued for prospective (not retroactive) damages to be paid by state treasury No\n• Suit brought by United States or other state immunity\n• Suit against local government (eg, counties, municipalities)\n• Bankruptcy proceedings The Eleventh Amendment generally forbids the federal courts from hearing private suits for damages against the states. Congress cannot override this ban, even to exercise one of its constitutionally enumerated powers (Choices A & D). But there is a narrow exception to this rule when Congress is clearly acting to enforce the Civil War Amendments (eg, Fourteenth Amendment—as seen here). In that case, it may enact appropriate remedial legislation expressly subjecting the states to private suits for damages in federal court. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment applies to all private suits for damages against the states—including suits by citizens against their own state. But it does not bar Congress from authorizing such suits as a means of enforcing the Civil War Amendments.","conclusion":"Answer B: When Congress exercises power vested in it by the Fourteenth Amendment, Congress may enact appropriate remedial legislation expressly subjecting the states to private suits for damages in federal court."},"questionZh":"根据国会执行第十四修正案的权力颁布的联邦法规禁止任何州仅因为年龄而要求其任何雇员从州就业中退休。该法规明确授权仅因年龄而被州要求从州就业退休的雇员可以在联邦地区法院起诉州政府，要求赔偿该州行为造成的任何损害。根据该联邦法规，退休人员在联邦地区法院起诉 X 州。 X 州提出驳回诉讼，理由是国会无权授权针对某州提起此类诉讼。以下哪一项是退休人员可以提出的最有力的论据来反对州政府驳回诉讼的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"当国会行使宪法任何条款赋予的权力时，国会拥有无限的权力授权私人针对州提出损害赔偿诉讼。","B":"当国会行使第十四修正案赋予的权力时，国会可以颁布适当的补救立法，明确规定各州可以向联邦法院提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。","C":"虽然第十一修正案适用于一个州的公民向另一州向联邦法院提起的诉讼，但不适用于公民向自己的州提起的此类诉讼。","D":"虽然第十一条修正案限制了联邦司法机构，但该修正案并不限制国会修改各州主权豁免权的权力。"},"explanationZh":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案一般禁止联邦法院审理针对各州的损害赔偿私人诉讼。国会不能推翻这项禁令，甚至不能行使宪法规定的权力之一（选择 A 和 D）。但当国会明显采取行动执行内战修正案（例如，第十四修正案——如此处所示）时，这条规则有一个狭隘的例外。在这种情况下，它可以颁布适当的补救立法，明确规定各州必须向联邦法院提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。 （选项 C）第十一修正案适用于所有针对各州提起的损害赔偿私人诉讼，包括公民针对本州提起的诉讼。但这并不妨碍国会授权此类诉讼作为执行内战修正案的手段。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案一般禁止联邦法院审理针对各州的损害赔偿私人诉讼。但当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案（即第十三、第十四和第十五修正案）时，它可以授权此类诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Georgia, 546 U.S. 151, 158–59 (2006)（解释第十四修正案允许国会通过授权对各州提起损害赔偿的私人诉讼来废除国家主权豁免）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十一条修正案一般禁止联邦法院审理针对各州的损害赔偿私人诉讼。但当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案（即第十三、第十四和第十五修正案）时，它可以授权此类诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是退休人员可以提出的最有力的论据来反对州政府驳回诉讼的动议？","rule":"第十一条修正案一般禁止联邦法院审理针对各州的损害赔偿私人诉讼。但当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案（即第十三、第十四和第十五修正案）时，它可以授权此类诉讼。","analysis":"第十一条修正案\n\n（国家免受联邦法院诉讼）\n\n免疫力\n• 私人团体或外国政府提起的诉讼\n• 起诉州政府官员违反州法律\n• 例外： – 州同意提起诉讼 – 根据第 13、14 或 15 修正案取消豁免权 – 州官员因禁令或宣告性救济而被起诉 – 损害赔偿由州官员个人（而非州财政部）支付 – 州官员因预期（非追溯性）损害由州财政部支付而被起诉 否\n• 美国或其他国家豁免权提起的诉讼\n• 针对地方政府（例如县、市）的诉讼\n• 破产程序 第十一修正案一般禁止联邦法院审理针对各州的损害赔偿私人诉讼。国会不能推翻这项禁令，甚至不能行使宪法规定的权力之一（选择 A 和 D）。但当国会明显采取行动执行内战修正案（例如，第十四修正案——如此处所示）时，这条规则有一个狭隘的例外。在这种情况下，它可以颁布适当的补救立法，明确规定各州必须向联邦法院提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。 （选项 C）第十一修正案适用于所有针对各州提起的损害赔偿私人诉讼，包括公民针对本州提起的诉讼。但这并不妨碍国会授权此类诉讼作为执行内战修正案的手段。\n\n教育目标：第十一条修正案一般禁止联邦法院审理针对各州的损害赔偿私人诉讼。但当国会明确采取行动执行内战修正案（即第十三、第十四和第十五修正案）时，它可以授权此类诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Georgia, 546 U.S. 151, 158–59 (2006)（解释第十四修正案允许国会通过授权对各州提起损害赔偿的私人诉讼来废除国家主权豁免）。","conclusion":"答案 B：当国会行使第十四修正案赋予的权力时，国会可以颁布适当的补救立法，明确规定各州可以向联邦法院提起私人损害赔偿诉讼。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":102,"sourceQuestionPage":305,"sourceQuestionPages":[305],"sourceExplanationPages":[306,307],"sourceAnswerPages":[306,307],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-103","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"A federal statute extends federal minimum wage requirements to all dry cleaning stores. The statute contains express findings that, when combined, the wages received by dry cleaning workers have a substantial impact on the national economy and on the flow of goods and services in interstate commerce. These findings are supported by information presented to Congress during committee hearings on the legislation. A small dry cleaning store operates exclusively within a community in the center of a geographically large state. It has no customers from outside the state. It employs three workers, each of whom is paid less than the federal minimum wage. Must this dry cleaning store comply with the statute imposing the federal minimum wage requirements on all dry cleaning stores?","choices":{"A":"No, because the store does no business in interstate commerce.","B":"No, because the wages of the store's three workers do not have a substantial impact on interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because the commerce clause vests Congress with plenary legislative authority over labor relations.","D":"Yes, because the wages paid by dry cleaning stores have a substantial impact on interstate commerce."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The Article I commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. This includes the power to regulate the following:\n• channels of interstate commerce (eg, highways, waterways)\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce (eg, trucks, trains)\n• persons or things moving in interstate commerce (eg, goods, electricity) and\n• in-state activities that—singly or in the aggregate—substantially impact interstate commerce. Here, the federal statute requires all dry cleaning stores to pay their employees a minimum wage. This includes the small dry cleaning store that employs three low-wage workers, whose wages alone do not have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. But when the wages paid by all dry cleaning stores are added together (ie, aggregated), they do have a substantial impact on interstate commerce (Choice B). Therefore, Congress can regulate these wages under the commerce clause, and the store must comply with the minimum wage requirement. (Choice A) Although the store does no business in interstate commerce, Congress can regulate the store's wages since the aggregate wages paid by all dry cleaning stores have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. (Choice C) The commerce clause does not vest Congress with plenary (ie, absolute) legislative authority over labor relations. Congress can only regulate labor relations that substantially affect interstate commerce, and states can also regulate labor relations so long as they comply with the dormant commerce clause.\n\nEducational objective: The commerce clause gives Congress broad authority to regulate interstate commerce— including in-state activities that substantially impact interstate commerce singly or in the aggregate.\n\nReferences\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005) (holding that Congress can regulate purely local activities that are part of an economic class of activities that have a substantial effect on interstate commerce).\n\n• NLRB v. Jones &amp; Laughlin Steel Corp., 301 U.S. 1, 36–37 (1937) (recognizing that Congress's commerce powers extend to activities that impact interstate commerce independently or in combination with other sources).","educationalObjective":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad authority to regulate interstate commerce— including in-state activities that substantially impact interstate commerce singly or in the aggregate.","references":"• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005) (holding that Congress can regulate purely local activities that are part of an economic class of activities that have a substantial effect on interstate commerce).\n\n• NLRB v. Jones &amp; Laughlin Steel Corp., 301 U.S. 1, 36–37 (1937) (recognizing that Congress's commerce powers extend to activities that impact interstate commerce independently or in combination with other sources).","rule":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad authority to regulate interstate commerce— including in-state activities that substantially impact interstate commerce singly or in the aggregate.","irac":{"issue":"Must this dry cleaning store comply with the statute imposing the federal minimum wage requirements on all dry cleaning stores?","rule":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad authority to regulate interstate commerce— including in-state activities that substantially impact interstate commerce singly or in the aggregate.","analysis":"The Article I commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate interstate commerce—ie, nearly all activities involving two or more states. This includes the power to regulate the following:\n• channels of interstate commerce (eg, highways, waterways)\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce (eg, trucks, trains)\n• persons or things moving in interstate commerce (eg, goods, electricity) and\n• in-state activities that—singly or in the aggregate—substantially impact interstate commerce. Here, the federal statute requires all dry cleaning stores to pay their employees a minimum wage. This includes the small dry cleaning store that employs three low-wage workers, whose wages alone do not have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. But when the wages paid by all dry cleaning stores are added together (ie, aggregated), they do have a substantial impact on interstate commerce (Choice B). Therefore, Congress can regulate these wages under the commerce clause, and the store must comply with the minimum wage requirement. (Choice A) Although the store does no business in interstate commerce, Congress can regulate the store's wages since the aggregate wages paid by all dry cleaning stores have a substantial impact on interstate commerce. (Choice C) The commerce clause does not vest Congress with plenary (ie, absolute) legislative authority over labor relations. Congress can only regulate labor relations that substantially affect interstate commerce, and states can also regulate labor relations so long as they comply with the dormant commerce clause.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the wages paid by dry cleaning stores have a substantial impact on interstate commerce."},"questionZh":"一项联邦法规将联邦最低工资要求扩展到所有干洗店。该法规明确指出，干洗工人的工资综合起来对国民经济以及州际商业中的商品和服务流动具有重大影响。这些调查结果得到了在立法委员会听证会上向国会提交的信息的支持。一家小型干洗店专门在一个地理较大的州中心的社区内经营。它没有来自州外的客户。它雇用了三名工人，每人的工资都低于联邦最低工资。该干洗店必须遵守对所有干洗店实行联邦最低工资要求的法规吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这家商店不从事州际贸易。","B":"不会，因为商店三名工人的工资对州际贸易没有实质性影响。","C":"是的，因为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会有关劳动关系的全部立法权。","D":"是的，因为干洗店支付的工资对州际贸易有重大影响。"},"explanationZh":"第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业——即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。这包括监管以下事项的权力：\n• 州际商业渠道（例如高速公路、水路）\n• 州际贸易工具（例如卡车、火车）\n• 州际贸易中移动的人或物（例如货物、电力）以及\n• 州内活动——单独或总体——对州际商业产生重大影响。在这里，联邦法规要求所有干洗店向员工支付最低工资。这包括雇用三名低薪工人的小型干洗店，仅靠他们的工资并不会对州际贸易产生重大影响。但当所有干洗店支付的工资加在一起（即合计）时，它们确实对州际贸易产生了重大影响（选项B）。因此，国会可以根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）来规范这些工资，而商店必须遵守最低工资要求。 （选项A）虽然该商店不从事州际贸易，但国会可以规范该商店的工资，因为所有干洗店支付的工资总额对州际贸易有重大影响。 （选项C） 商务条款并未赋予国会对劳动关系的全部（即绝对）立法权。国会只能规范对州际商业产生重大影响的劳动关系，各州也可以规范劳动关系，只要它们遵守消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。\n\n教育目标：商务条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商务，包括对州际商务单独或总体产生重大影响的州内活动。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005)（认为国会可以监管纯粹的地方活动，这些活动属于对州际贸易有重大影响的经济活动类别的一部分）。\n\n• NLRB 诉 Jones & Laughlin Steel Corp., 301 U.S. 1, 36–37 (1937)（承认国会的商业权力延伸至独立或与其他来源结合影响州际商业的活动）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商务条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商务，包括对州际商务单独或总体产生重大影响的州内活动。","iracZh":{"issue":"该干洗店必须遵守对所有干洗店实行联邦最低工资要求的法规吗？","rule":"商务条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商务，包括对州际商务单独或总体产生重大影响的州内活动。","analysis":"第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业——即涉及两个或多个州的几乎所有活动。这包括监管以下事项的权力：\n• 州际商业渠道（例如高速公路、水路）\n• 州际贸易工具（例如卡车、火车）\n• 州际贸易中移动的人或物（例如货物、电力）以及\n• 州内活动——单独或总体——对州际商业产生重大影响。在这里，联邦法规要求所有干洗店向员工支付最低工资。这包括雇用三名低薪工人的小型干洗店，仅靠他们的工资并不会对州际贸易产生重大影响。但当所有干洗店支付的工资加在一起（即合计）时，它们确实对州际贸易产生了重大影响（选项B）。因此，国会可以根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）来规范这些工资，而商店必须遵守最低工资要求。 （选项A）虽然该商店不从事州际贸易，但国会可以规范该商店的工资，因为所有干洗店支付的工资总额对州际贸易有重大影响。 （选项C） 商务条款并未赋予国会对劳动关系的全部（即绝对）立法权。国会只能规范对州际商业产生重大影响的劳动关系，各州也可以规范劳动关系，只要它们遵守消极商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）（Dormant Commerce Clause）。\n\n教育目标：商务条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商务，包括对州际商务单独或总体产生重大影响的州内活动。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005)（认为国会可以监管纯粹的地方活动，这些活动属于对州际贸易有重大影响的经济活动类别的一部分）。\n\n• NLRB 诉 Jones & Laughlin Steel Corp., 301 U.S. 1, 36–37 (1937)（承认国会的商业权力延伸至独立或与其他来源结合影响州际商业的活动）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为干洗店支付的工资对州际贸易有重大影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-d5423725ddc9b845"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":103,"sourceQuestionPage":308,"sourceQuestionPages":[308],"sourceExplanationPages":[309,310],"sourceAnswerPages":[309,310],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-104","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Political Question","question":"A jury convicted a federal district court judge of bribery. When the judge subsequently refused to resign from his office, the House of Representatives adopted and presented to the Senate an article of impeachment that charged the judge with bribery. Pursuant to its rules for impeachment, the Senate authorized a committee of senators to hold closed-door hearings to take testimony and receive evidence related to the impeachment charge. At the conclusion of the hearings, the committee reported the facts and summarized the evidence to the full Senate. Based on the committee's report, the Senate voted to convict the judge and remove him from office. The judge has filed suit in federal court to enjoin the Senate from removing him from office. The judge claims that the Senate violated Article I of the U.S. Constitution by prohibiting the full Senate from taking part in the hearings. If the court dismisses the judge's action, what will be the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"The action involves a nonjusticiable political question.","B":"The action is moot.","C":"The judge lacks standing.","D":"The U.S. Supreme Court has original and exclusive jurisdiction over the action."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Nonjusticiable political questions\n\n(issues incapable of judicial resolution)\n\n• Recognition of foreign governments\n• Reception of ambassadors\n• Nature of wars/hostilities\n• Partisan gerrymandering\n• Guarantee of republican form of government\n• Recognition of Native American tribes\n• House/Senate rules for impeachment\n• Ratification process for constitutional amendments Article III of the U.S. Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide justiciable cases and controversies. However, some actions are not capable of judicial resolution (ie, nonjusticiable). This includes actions implicating the political-question doctrine. Under this doctrine, federal courts are prohibited from deciding political questions that should be resolved by another branch of government. A case presents a nonjusticiable political question when:\n• the issue has been reserved to the executive or legislative branch and/or\n\n• there is a lack of judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue.\n\nHere, the judge seeks to enjoin the Senate from removing him from office. He claims that the Senate violated Article I of the U.S. Constitution by prohibiting the full Senate from participating in the evidentiary hearings. However, the impeachment and removal of federal judges is a power reserved solely to Congress,* and there are no standards to help a court resolve an impeachment process issue. Therefore, the court will most likely dismiss the judge's action because it involves a nonjusticiable political question. *Congress also has the exclusive power to impeach and remove the President, Vice President, and other federal officers (eg, the U.S. Attorney General). (Choice B) A suit is also nonjusticiable if it is moot—ie, when the suit no longer presents an actual controversy for the court to resolve. Here, the judge's action is not moot since his harm has not been resolved. However, the federal courts cannot provide relief because his claim presents a nonjusticiable political question.\n\n(Choice C) The judge has standing because he suffered an injury-in-fact (removal from office) that was caused by the challenged action (impeachment procedure) and is redressable by the remedy sought (injunction). (Choice D) The U.S. Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases that affect ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls OR involve a state as a party (neither seen here). Additionally, the Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction over suits between states (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Federal courts must dismiss a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question. This occurs when (1) the Constitution reserves the issue to another branch of government and/or (2) the court lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue.\n\nReferences\n• Nixon v. United States, 506 U.S. 224, 238 (1993) (holding that the impeachment process presents a nonjusticiable question that is reserved to the legislative branch).\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962) (explaining the political-question doctrine).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Fed. Courts § 574 (2020) (listing common examples of political questions).","educationalObjective":"Federal courts must dismiss a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question. This occurs when (1) the Constitution reserves the issue to another branch of government and/or (2) the court lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue.","references":"• Nixon v. United States, 506 U.S. 224, 238 (1993) (holding that the impeachment process presents a nonjusticiable question that is reserved to the legislative branch).\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962) (explaining the political-question doctrine).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Fed. Courts § 574 (2020) (listing common examples of political questions).","rule":"Federal courts must dismiss a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question. This occurs when (1) the Constitution reserves the issue to another branch of government and/or (2) the court lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue.","irac":{"issue":"If the court dismisses the judge's action, what will be the most likely reason?","rule":"Federal courts must dismiss a suit that presents a nonjusticiable political question. This occurs when (1) the Constitution reserves the issue to another branch of government and/or (2) the court lacks judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue.","analysis":"Nonjusticiable political questions\n\n(issues incapable of judicial resolution)\n\n• Recognition of foreign governments\n• Reception of ambassadors\n• Nature of wars/hostilities\n• Partisan gerrymandering\n• Guarantee of republican form of government\n• Recognition of Native American tribes\n• House/Senate rules for impeachment\n• Ratification process for constitutional amendments Article III of the U.S. Constitution grants federal courts the power to decide justiciable cases and controversies. However, some actions are not capable of judicial resolution (ie, nonjusticiable). This includes actions implicating the political-question doctrine. Under this doctrine, federal courts are prohibited from deciding political questions that should be resolved by another branch of government. A case presents a nonjusticiable political question when:\n• the issue has been reserved to the executive or legislative branch and/or\n\n• there is a lack of judicially discoverable and manageable standards to resolve the issue.\n\nHere, the judge seeks to enjoin the Senate from removing him from office. He claims that the Senate violated Article I of the U.S. Constitution by prohibiting the full Senate from participating in the evidentiary hearings. However, the impeachment and removal of federal judges is a power reserved solely to Congress,* and there are no standards to help a court resolve an impeachment process issue. Therefore, the court will most likely dismiss the judge's action because it involves a nonjusticiable political question. *Congress also has the exclusive power to impeach and remove the President, Vice President, and other federal officers (eg, the U.S. Attorney General). (Choice B) A suit is also nonjusticiable if it is moot—ie, when the suit no longer presents an actual controversy for the court to resolve. Here, the judge's action is not moot since his harm has not been resolved. However, the federal courts cannot provide relief because his claim presents a nonjusticiable political question.\n\n(Choice C) The judge has standing because he suffered an injury-in-fact (removal from office) that was caused by the challenged action (impeachment procedure) and is redressable by the remedy sought (injunction). (Choice D) The U.S. Supreme Court has original jurisdiction over cases that affect ambassadors, public ministers, or consuls OR involve a state as a party (neither seen here). Additionally, the Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction over suits between states (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: The action involves a nonjusticiable political question."},"questionZh":"陪审团判定一名联邦地区法院法官犯有贿赂罪。当该法官随后拒绝辞职时，众议院通过并向参议院提交了一项弹劾条款，指控该法官行贿。根据弹劾规则，参议院授权参议员委员会举行闭门听证会，听取证词并收集与弹劾指控相关的证据。听证会结束时，委员会向参议院全体会议报告了事实并总结了证据。根据该委员会的报告，参议院投票判定该法官有罪并将其免职。该法官已向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求禁止参议院罢免他的职务。法官声称参议院禁止参议院全体成员参加听证会，违反了美国宪法第一条。如果法院驳回法官的诉讼，最可能的理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该行动涉及一个不可审理的政治问题。","B":"该行动毫无意义。","C":"法官缺乏地位。","D":"美国最高法院对此诉讼拥有原始且专属管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"不可审理的政治问题\n\n（无法司法解决的问题）\n\n• 外国政府的承认\n• 大使接待\n• 战争/敌对行动的性质\n• 党派不公正划分选区\n• 共和政体的保障\n• 对美洲原住民部落的认可\n• 众议院/参议院弹劾规则\n• 宪法修正案的批准程序 美国宪法第三条赋予联邦法院裁决可审理案件和争议的权力。然而，有些行为无法通过司法解决（即不可审理）。这包括涉及政治问题学说的行为。根据这一原则，联邦法院不得决定应由政府其他部门解决的政治问题。在以下情况下，案件会提出不可审理的政治问题：\n• 该问题已保留给行政或立法部门和/或\n\n• 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题。\n\n在这里，法官试图禁止参议院罢免他的职务。他声称参议院禁止参议院全体成员参加证据听证会，违反了美国宪法第一条。然而，弹劾和罢免联邦法官是国会独有的权力，*并且没有标准可以帮助法院解决弹劾程序问题。因此，法院很可能会驳回法官的诉讼，因为它涉及不可审理的政治问题。 *国会还拥有弹劾和罢免总统、副总统和其他联邦官员（例如美国司法部长）的专属权力。 （选择 B）如果诉讼没有实际意义，即当诉讼不再提出需要法院解决的实际争议时，该诉讼也是不可审理的。在这里，法官的行为没有实际意义，因为他的伤害尚未得到解决。然而，联邦法院无法提供救济，因为他的主张提出了不可审理的政治问题。\n\n（选项 C）法官具有诉讼资格，因为他受到了因受质疑的行为（弹劾程序）造成的事实上的伤害（免职），并且可以通过寻求补救措施（禁令）予以纠正。 （选项 D）美国最高法院对影响大使、公共部长或领事或涉及州作为一方的案件（此处均未示出）拥有初审管辖权。此外，最高法院对州之间的诉讼拥有专属管辖权（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标：联邦法院必须驳回提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼。当（1）宪法将问题保留给政府的另一个部门和/或（2）法院缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题时，就会发生这种情况。\n\n参考文献\n• Nixon v. United States, 506 U.S. 224, 238 (1993)（认为弹劾程序提出了一个由立法部门保留的不可审理的问题）。\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962)（解释政治问题原则）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 美联储。法院 § 574 (2020)（列出政治问题的常见示例）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"联邦法院必须驳回提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼。当（1）宪法将问题保留给政府的另一个部门和/或（2）法院缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题时，就会发生这种情况。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院驳回法官的诉讼，最可能的理由是什么？","rule":"联邦法院必须驳回提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼。当（1）宪法将问题保留给政府的另一个部门和/或（2）法院缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题时，就会发生这种情况。","analysis":"不可审理的政治问题\n\n（无法司法解决的问题）\n\n• 外国政府的承认\n• 大使接待\n• 战争/敌对行动的性质\n• 党派不公正划分选区\n• 共和政体的保障\n• 对美洲原住民部落的认可\n• 众议院/参议院弹劾规则\n• 宪法修正案的批准程序 美国宪法第三条赋予联邦法院裁决可审理案件和争议的权力。然而，有些行为无法通过司法解决（即不可审理）。这包括涉及政治问题学说的行为。根据这一原则，联邦法院不得决定应由政府其他部门解决的政治问题。在以下情况下，案件会提出不可审理的政治问题：\n• 该问题已保留给行政或立法部门和/或\n\n• 缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题。\n\n在这里，法官试图禁止参议院罢免他的职务。他声称参议院禁止参议院全体成员参加证据听证会，违反了美国宪法第一条。然而，弹劾和罢免联邦法官是国会独有的权力，*并且没有标准可以帮助法院解决弹劾程序问题。因此，法院很可能会驳回法官的诉讼，因为它涉及不可审理的政治问题。 *国会还拥有弹劾和罢免总统、副总统和其他联邦官员（例如美国司法部长）的专属权力。 （选择 B）如果诉讼没有实际意义，即当诉讼不再提出需要法院解决的实际争议时，该诉讼也是不可审理的。在这里，法官的行为没有实际意义，因为他的伤害尚未得到解决。然而，联邦法院无法提供救济，因为他的主张提出了不可审理的政治问题。\n\n（选项 C）法官具有诉讼资格，因为他受到了因受质疑的行为（弹劾程序）造成的事实上的伤害（免职），并且可以通过寻求补救措施（禁令）予以纠正。 （选项 D）美国最高法院对影响大使、公共部长或领事或涉及州作为一方的案件（此处均未示出）拥有初审管辖权。此外，最高法院对州之间的诉讼拥有专属管辖权（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标：联邦法院必须驳回提出不可审理的政治问题的诉讼。当（1）宪法将问题保留给政府的另一个部门和/或（2）法院缺乏司法上可发现和可管理的标准来解决该问题时，就会发生这种情况。\n\n参考文献\n• Nixon v. United States, 506 U.S. 224, 238 (1993)（认为弹劾程序提出了一个由立法部门保留的不可审理的问题）。\n\n• Baker v. Carr, 369 U.S. 186, 210–17 (1962)（解释政治问题原则）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 美联储。法院 § 574 (2020)（列出政治问题的常见示例）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该行动涉及一个不可审理的政治问题。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":104,"sourceQuestionPage":311,"sourceQuestionPages":[311],"sourceExplanationPages":[312,313],"sourceAnswerPages":[312,313],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-105","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federal Judicial Power","question":"Congress passed a statute providing that parties could no longer seek review in the U.S. Supreme Court of final judgments in criminal matters made by the highest court in each state. What is the best argument supporting the constitutionality of the statute?","choices":{"A":"Congress has the power to make exceptions to the appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.","B":"Criminal matters are traditionally governed by state law.","C":"The proper means of federal judicial review of state criminal matters is by habeas corpus.","D":"The review of state court judgments is not within the original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court."},"answer":"A","explanation":"SCOTUS jurisdiction\n\nType Definition Applicability\n\nOriginal Case filed directly in Cases involving either: SCOTUS (Congress cannot\n• ambassador/public adjust) minister/consul or\n• state as party Appellate Case filed after decision by All other cases by either: either: (Congress may\n• writ of certiorari or limit)\n• lower federal\n• direct appeal (rare) court or\n• highest state court SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction to review a final judgment of lower federal courts and the highest state courts when the judgment turns on federal law. But the Article III exceptions clause gives Congress almost unlimited power to regulate or make exceptions to this jurisdiction. Therefore, that is the best argument supporting the constitutionality of the statute prohibiting SCOTUS from reviewing final judgments in criminal matters made by the highest court in each state. (Choice B) Although criminal matters are traditionally governed by state law, SCOTUS can review final judgments in state criminal cases involving an alleged violation of the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty. (Choice C) A person in custody can file a petition for a writ of habeas corpus in federal court to challenge the legality of his/her detention. But a habeas corpus petition is a separate civil suit—not part of the criminal appeals process. So, the better argument is that Congress can make exceptions to SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. (Choice D) The review of state court judgments is not within SCOTUS's very limited original jurisdiction (ie, power to hear a case first and render a decision). But it is within SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction.\n\nEducational objective: The U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction extends to final judgments of lower federal courts and the highest state courts when the judgment turns on federal law. But the Article III exceptions clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate and make exceptions to this jurisdiction.\n\nReferences\n• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869) (establishing that Congress has almost unlimited power to make exceptions to the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction).","educationalObjective":"The U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction extends to final judgments of lower federal courts and the highest state courts when the judgment turns on federal law. But the Article III exceptions clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate and make exceptions to this jurisdiction.","references":"• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869) (establishing that Congress has almost unlimited power to make exceptions to the Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction).","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction extends to final judgments of lower federal courts and the highest state courts when the judgment turns on federal law. But the Article III exceptions clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate and make exceptions to this jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"What is the best argument supporting the constitutionality of the statute?","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction extends to final judgments of lower federal courts and the highest state courts when the judgment turns on federal law. But the Article III exceptions clause gives Congress extensive power to regulate and make exceptions to this jurisdiction.","analysis":"SCOTUS jurisdiction\n\nType Definition Applicability\n\nOriginal Case filed directly in Cases involving either: SCOTUS (Congress cannot\n• ambassador/public adjust) minister/consul or\n• state as party Appellate Case filed after decision by All other cases by either: either: (Congress may\n• writ of certiorari or limit)\n• lower federal\n• direct appeal (rare) court or\n• highest state court SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction to review a final judgment of lower federal courts and the highest state courts when the judgment turns on federal law. But the Article III exceptions clause gives Congress almost unlimited power to regulate or make exceptions to this jurisdiction. Therefore, that is the best argument supporting the constitutionality of the statute prohibiting SCOTUS from reviewing final judgments in criminal matters made by the highest court in each state. (Choice B) Although criminal matters are traditionally governed by state law, SCOTUS can review final judgments in state criminal cases involving an alleged violation of the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty. (Choice C) A person in custody can file a petition for a writ of habeas corpus in federal court to challenge the legality of his/her detention. But a habeas corpus petition is a separate civil suit—not part of the criminal appeals process. So, the better argument is that Congress can make exceptions to SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction. (Choice D) The review of state court judgments is not within SCOTUS's very limited original jurisdiction (ie, power to hear a case first and render a decision). But it is within SCOTUS's appellate jurisdiction.","conclusion":"Answer A: Congress has the power to make exceptions to the appellate jurisdiction of the Supreme Court."},"questionZh":"国会通过了一项法规，规定各方不得再向美国最高法院寻求对各州最高法院就刑事案件做出的最终判决进行复审。支持该法规合宪性的最佳论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"国会有权对最高法院的上诉管辖权作出例外处理。","B":"刑事案件传统上受州法律管辖。","C":"联邦司法审查州刑事事项的正确手段是人身保护令。","D":"州法院判决的复核不属于最高法院的原始管辖范围。"},"explanationZh":"最高法院管辖权\n\n类型 定义 适用性\n\n直接提交的原始案件涉及以下任一案件： SCOTUS（国会不能\n• 大使/公共调整）部长/领事或\n• 州作为当事方 在做出决定后提起上诉案件 所有其他案件均通过以下方式之一：（国会可以\n• 调卷令或限制令）\n• 下级联邦\n• 直接上诉（罕见）法院或\n• 最高州法院 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有上诉管辖权，可以在下级联邦法院和最高州法院的最终判决适用联邦法律时对其进行复审。但第三条例外条款赋予国会几乎无限的权力来监管这一管辖权或对该管辖权做出例外。因此，这是支持禁止最高法院审查各州最高法院刑事案件最终判决的法规合宪性的最佳论据。 （选择 B）虽然刑事案件传统上受州法律管辖，但最高法院可以审查涉及涉嫌违反宪法、联邦法律或条约的州刑事案件的最终判决。 （选项 C）被拘留者可以向联邦法院申请人身保护令，以质疑其拘留的合法性。但人身保护令申请是一项单独的民事诉讼，不属于刑事上诉程序的一部分。因此，更好的论据是国会可以对最高法院的上诉管辖权做出例外。 （选项D） 对州法院判决的审查不属于SCOTUS 非常有限的原始管辖权（即首先审理案件并作出决定的权力）。但这属于最高法院的上诉管辖范围。\n\n教育目标：当判决依据联邦法律时，美国最高法院的上诉管辖权延伸至下级联邦法院和最高州法院的最终判决。但第三条例外条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管这一管辖权并制定例外情况。\n\n参考文献\n• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869)（规定国会几乎拥有无限的权力对最高法院的上诉管辖权做出例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当判决依据联邦法律时，美国最高法院的上诉管辖权延伸至下级联邦法院和最高州法院的最终判决。但第三条例外条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管这一管辖权并制定例外情况。","iracZh":{"issue":"支持该法规合宪性的最佳论据是什么？","rule":"当判决依据联邦法律时，美国最高法院的上诉管辖权延伸至下级联邦法院和最高州法院的最终判决。但第三条例外条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管这一管辖权并制定例外情况。","analysis":"最高法院管辖权\n\n类型 定义 适用性\n\n直接提交的原始案件涉及以下任一案件： SCOTUS（国会不能\n• 大使/公共调整）部长/领事或\n• 州作为当事方 在做出决定后提起上诉案件 所有其他案件均通过以下方式之一：（国会可以\n• 调卷令或限制令）\n• 下级联邦\n• 直接上诉（罕见）法院或\n• 最高州法院 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有上诉管辖权，可以在下级联邦法院和最高州法院的最终判决适用联邦法律时对其进行复审。但第三条例外条款赋予国会几乎无限的权力来监管这一管辖权或对该管辖权做出例外。因此，这是支持禁止最高法院审查各州最高法院刑事案件最终判决的法规合宪性的最佳论据。 （选择 B）虽然刑事案件传统上受州法律管辖，但最高法院可以审查涉及涉嫌违反宪法、联邦法律或条约的州刑事案件的最终判决。 （选项 C）被拘留者可以向联邦法院申请人身保护令，以质疑其拘留的合法性。但人身保护令申请是一项单独的民事诉讼，不属于刑事上诉程序的一部分。因此，更好的论据是国会可以对最高法院的上诉管辖权做出例外。 （选项D） 对州法院判决的审查不属于SCOTUS 非常有限的原始管辖权（即首先审理案件并作出决定的权力）。但这属于最高法院的上诉管辖范围。\n\n教育目标：当判决依据联邦法律时，美国最高法院的上诉管辖权延伸至下级联邦法院和最高州法院的最终判决。但第三条例外条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管这一管辖权并制定例外情况。\n\n参考文献\n• Ex parte McCardle, 74 U.S. 506, 514 (1869)（规定国会几乎拥有无限的权力对最高法院的上诉管辖权做出例外）。","conclusion":"答案 A：国会有权对最高法院的上诉管辖权作出例外处理。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"legality","ipa":"/liɡˈæləti/","syllables":"le·gal·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 合法, 正当；[经] 合法性","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 合法, 正当；[经] 合法性","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:legalities","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) A person in custody can file a petition for a writ of habeas corpus in federal court to challenge the legality of his/her detention.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":105,"sourceQuestionPage":314,"sourceQuestionPages":[314],"sourceExplanationPages":[315,316],"sourceAnswerPages":[315,316],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-106","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"The National AIDS Prevention and Control Act is a new, comprehensive federal statute that was enacted to deal with the public health crisis caused by the AIDS virus. Congress and the President were concerned that inconsistent lower court rulings with respect to the constitutionality, interpretation, and application of the statute might adversely affect or delay its enforcement and thereby jeopardize the public health. As a result, they included a provision in the statute providing that all legal challenges concerning those matters may be initiated only by filing suit directly in the United States Supreme Court. Is the provision authorizing direct review of the constitutionality, interpretation, or application of this statute only in the United States Supreme Court constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it denies persons who wish to challenge this statute the equal protection of the laws by requiring them to file suit in a court different from that in which persons who wish to challenge other statutes may file suit.","B":"No, because it is inconsistent with the specification in Article III of the original jurisdiction of the United States Supreme Court.","C":"Yes, because Article III provides that the jurisdiction of the United States Supreme Court is subject to such exceptions and such regulations as Congress shall make.","D":"Yes, because it is authorized by the Article I power of Congress to enact all laws that are \"necessary and proper\" to implement the general welfare."},"answer":"B","explanation":"SCOTUS jurisdiction\n\nType Definition Applicability\n\nOriginal Case filed directly in Cases involving either: SCOTUS (Congress cannot\n• ambassador/public adjust) minister/consul or\n• state as party Appellate Case filed after decision by All other cases by either: either: (Congress may\n• writ of certiorari or limit)\n• lower federal\n• direct appeal (rare) court or\n• highest state court SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nArticle III gives the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) original jurisdiction—ie, the authority to directly review a case—in two rare circumstances:\n• when a case affects an ambassador, public minister, or consul and\n• when a state is a party to the suit. Congress cannot expand or restrict SCOTUS's original jurisdiction beyond Article III's specifications (Choice C). Since the statutory provision attempts to do so by authorizing SCOTUS to directly review challenges to the National AIDS Prevention and Control Act, it is unconstitutional. (Choice A) This provision implicates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment because it treats persons challenging this statute differently than persons challenging other statutes. But since the provision does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, it likely does not violate this component. (Choice D) Article I allows Congress to enact laws that are \"necessary and proper\" (ie, reasonably appropriate) to carry out its enumerated powers—including the power to spend for the public's general welfare. But when doing so, it cannot violate other constitutional provisions (eg, Article III—as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Article III gives the U.S. Supreme Court original jurisdiction over cases (1) that affect an ambassador, public minister, or consul or (2) in which a state is a party. And Congress cannot alter SCOTUS's original jurisdiction.\n\nReferences\n• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803) (holding that Congress cannot alter the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 453 (2019) (defining original and appellate jurisdiction).","educationalObjective":"Article III gives the U.S. Supreme Court original jurisdiction over cases (1) that affect an ambassador, public minister, or consul or (2) in which a state is a party. And Congress cannot alter SCOTUS's original jurisdiction.","references":"• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803) (holding that Congress cannot alter the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 453 (2019) (defining original and appellate jurisdiction).","rule":"Article III gives the U.S. Supreme Court original jurisdiction over cases (1) that affect an ambassador, public minister, or consul or (2) in which a state is a party. And Congress cannot alter SCOTUS's original jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"Is the provision authorizing direct review of the constitutionality, interpretation, or application of this statute only in the United States Supreme Court constitutional?","rule":"Article III gives the U.S. Supreme Court original jurisdiction over cases (1) that affect an ambassador, public minister, or consul or (2) in which a state is a party. And Congress cannot alter SCOTUS's original jurisdiction.","analysis":"SCOTUS jurisdiction\n\nType Definition Applicability\n\nOriginal Case filed directly in Cases involving either: SCOTUS (Congress cannot\n• ambassador/public adjust) minister/consul or\n• state as party Appellate Case filed after decision by All other cases by either: either: (Congress may\n• writ of certiorari or limit)\n• lower federal\n• direct appeal (rare) court or\n• highest state court SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nArticle III gives the U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) original jurisdiction—ie, the authority to directly review a case—in two rare circumstances:\n• when a case affects an ambassador, public minister, or consul and\n• when a state is a party to the suit. Congress cannot expand or restrict SCOTUS's original jurisdiction beyond Article III's specifications (Choice C). Since the statutory provision attempts to do so by authorizing SCOTUS to directly review challenges to the National AIDS Prevention and Control Act, it is unconstitutional. (Choice A) This provision implicates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment because it treats persons challenging this statute differently than persons challenging other statutes. But since the provision does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, it likely does not violate this component. (Choice D) Article I allows Congress to enact laws that are \"necessary and proper\" (ie, reasonably appropriate) to carry out its enumerated powers—including the power to spend for the public's general welfare. But when doing so, it cannot violate other constitutional provisions (eg, Article III—as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because it is inconsistent with the specification in Article III of the original jurisdiction of the United States Supreme Court."},"questionZh":"《国家艾滋病预防和控制法》是一项新的、全面的联邦法规，旨在应对艾滋病病毒引起的公共卫生危机。国会和总统担心，下级法院在该法规的合宪性、解释和适用方面不一致的裁决可能会对其执行产生不利影响或延迟，从而危害公众健康。因此，他们在法规中纳入了一项条款，规定有关这些事项的所有法律挑战只能通过直接向美国最高法院提起诉讼来发起。仅在美国最高法院授权直接审查本法规的合宪性、解释或适用的条款是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为它要求挑战该法规的人向与希望挑战其他法规的人可以提起诉讼的法院不同的法院提起诉讼，从而剥夺了他们受到法律的平等保护。","B":"不可以，因为这不符合美国最高法院的原管辖权第三条的规定。","C":"是的，因为第三条规定，美国最高法院的管辖权受国会制定的例外和规定的约束。","D":"是的，因为国会第一条授权它颁布所有“必要且适当”的法律来实现公共福利。"},"explanationZh":"最高法院管辖权\n\n类型 定义 适用性\n\n直接提交的原始案件涉及以下任一案件： SCOTUS（国会不能\n• 大使/公共调整）部长/领事或\n• 州作为当事方 在做出决定后提起上诉案件 所有其他案件均通过以下方式之一：（国会可以\n• 调卷令或限制令）\n• 下级联邦\n• 直接上诉（罕见）法院或\n• 最高州法院 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n第三条在两种罕见的情况下赋予美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 初审管辖权，即直接审查案件的权力：\n• 当案件影响到大使、公共部长或领事时，并且\n• 当国家是诉讼当事方时。国会不能扩大或限制 SCOTUS 的原始管辖权超出第三条的规定（选项 C）。由于该法律条款试图通过授权最高法院直接审查对《国家艾滋病预防和控制法案》的挑战来做到这一点，因此它是违宪的。 （选项 A）该条款暗示了第五修正案的平等保护部分，因为它对待质疑该法规的人与质疑其他法规的人不同。但由于该条款不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，因此它可能不会违反该组成部分。 （选项D）第一条允许国会颁布“必要和适当”（即合理适当）的法律来履行其列举的权力，包括为公众的普遍福利支出的权力。但这样做时，它不能违反其他宪法条款（例如，第三条——如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：第三条赋予美国最高法院对以下案件的初审管辖权：(1) 影响大使、公务部长或领事的案件；或 (2) 州为当事方的案件。国会不能改变最高法院的原始管辖权。\n\n参考文献\n• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803)（认为国会不能改变最高法院的原始管辖权）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 453 (2019)（定义初审管辖权和上诉管辖权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第三条赋予美国最高法院对以下案件的初审管辖权：(1) 影响大使、公务部长或领事的案件；或 (2) 当事一方为州的案件。国会不能改变最高法院的原始管辖权。","iracZh":{"issue":"仅在美国最高法院授权直接审查本法规的合宪性、解释或适用的条款是否符合宪法？","rule":"第三条赋予美国最高法院对以下案件的初审管辖权：(1) 影响大使、公务部长或领事的案件；或 (2) 当事一方为州的案件。国会不能改变最高法院的原始管辖权。","analysis":"最高法院管辖权\n\n类型 定义 适用性\n\n直接提交的原始案件涉及以下任一案件： SCOTUS（国会不能\n• 大使/公共调整）部长/领事或\n• 州作为当事方 在做出决定后提起上诉案件 所有其他案件均通过以下方式之一：（国会可以\n• 调卷令或限制令）\n• 下级联邦\n• 直接上诉（罕见）法院或\n• 最高州法院 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n第三条在两种罕见的情况下赋予美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 初审管辖权，即直接审查案件的权力：\n• 当案件影响到大使、公共部长或领事时，并且\n• 当国家是诉讼当事方时。国会不能扩大或限制 SCOTUS 的原始管辖权超出第三条的规定（选项 C）。由于该法律条款试图通过授权最高法院直接审查对《国家艾滋病预防和控制法案》的挑战来做到这一点，因此它是违宪的。 （选项 A）该条款暗示了第五修正案的平等保护部分，因为它对待质疑该法规的人与质疑其他法规的人不同。但由于该条款不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，因此它可能不会违反该组成部分。 （选项D）第一条允许国会颁布“必要和适当”（即合理适当）的法律来履行其列举的权力，包括为公众的普遍福利支出的权力。但这样做时，它不能违反其他宪法条款（例如，第三条——如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：第三条赋予美国最高法院对以下案件的初审管辖权：(1) 影响大使、公务部长或领事的案件；或 (2) 州为当事方的案件。国会不能改变最高法院的原始管辖权。\n\n参考文献\n• Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. 137, 174–76 (1803)（认为国会不能改变最高法院的原始管辖权）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 联邦法院 § 453 (2019)（定义初审管辖权和上诉管辖权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为这不符合美国最高法院的原管辖权第三条的规定。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"specification","ipa":"/spˌɛsɪfɪkˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"spec·i·fi·ca·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 规格, 详述, 详细说明书；[计] 规范； 形式说明","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 规格, 详述, 详细说明书；[计] 规范； 形式说明","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:specifications","sourceSentence":"No, because it is inconsistent with the specification in Article III of the original jurisdiction of the United States Supreme Court.","synonyms":["stipulation","spec"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":106,"sourceQuestionPage":317,"sourceQuestionPages":[317],"sourceExplanationPages":[318,319],"sourceAnswerPages":[318,319],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-107","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"In order to reduce the federal deficit, Congress enacted a statute imposing a five percent national retail sales tax. The tax was levied upon all retail sales in the United States and applied equally to the sales of all kinds of goods. Is this tax constitutional as applied to retail sales of newspapers?","choices":{"A":"No, because retail sales taxes are within the taxing power of the states.","B":"No, because the imposition of a tax on the sale of newspapers violates the freedom of the press.","C":"Yes, because it is within Congress's power to tax.","D":"Yes, because the tax is necessary to serve the compelling interest of balancing the federal budget."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The taxing and spending clause (ie, general welfare clause) gives Congress broad authority to raise revenue through taxation. This includes the power to impose indirect taxes (eg, sales tax) if they are applied equally in every state AND are reasonably related to revenue production—as seen here. But a tax also must respect other constitutional provisions—including the First Amendment freedom of the press. The retail sales tax does so here because it is generally applicable to all goods and in no way targets press operations (Choice B). And since the sales tax does not implicate any other constitutional provisions, it is a constitutional exercise of Congress's taxing power—even as applied to retail sales of newspapers. (Choice A) Retail sales taxes are within the taxing power of the states as well as Congress's enumerated taxing power. (Choice D) The tax need not be necessary to serve a compelling interest because it is generally applicable and does not target the press.\n\nEducational objective: Indirect taxes must be applied equally in every state and reasonably related to revenue production. And such taxes cannot violate any other constitutional provision—eg, by targeting the press in violation of the First Amendment.\n\nReferences\n• Leathers v. Medlock, 499 U.S. 439, 447 (1991) (holding that the government may not exercise its taxing power to single out the press or target small groups of speakers).","educationalObjective":"Indirect taxes must be applied equally in every state and reasonably related to revenue production. And such taxes cannot violate any other constitutional provision—eg, by targeting the press in violation of the First Amendment.","references":"• Leathers v. Medlock, 499 U.S. 439, 447 (1991) (holding that the government may not exercise its taxing power to single out the press or target small groups of speakers).","rule":"Indirect taxes must be applied equally in every state and reasonably related to revenue production. And such taxes cannot violate any other constitutional provision—eg, by targeting the press in violation of the First Amendment.","irac":{"issue":"Is this tax constitutional as applied to retail sales of newspapers?","rule":"Indirect taxes must be applied equally in every state and reasonably related to revenue production. And such taxes cannot violate any other constitutional provision—eg, by targeting the press in violation of the First Amendment.","analysis":"The taxing and spending clause (ie, general welfare clause) gives Congress broad authority to raise revenue through taxation. This includes the power to impose indirect taxes (eg, sales tax) if they are applied equally in every state AND are reasonably related to revenue production—as seen here. But a tax also must respect other constitutional provisions—including the First Amendment freedom of the press. The retail sales tax does so here because it is generally applicable to all goods and in no way targets press operations (Choice B). And since the sales tax does not implicate any other constitutional provisions, it is a constitutional exercise of Congress's taxing power—even as applied to retail sales of newspapers. (Choice A) Retail sales taxes are within the taxing power of the states as well as Congress's enumerated taxing power. (Choice D) The tax need not be necessary to serve a compelling interest because it is generally applicable and does not target the press.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it is within Congress's power to tax."},"questionZh":"为了减少联邦赤字，国会颁布了一项法规，征收 5% 的全国零售税。该税对美国的所有零售销售征收，并且同样适用于各种商品的销售。适用于报纸零售的这项税收是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为零售税属于各州的征税权范围。","B":"不可以，因为对报纸销售征税侵犯了新闻自由。","C":"是的，因为国会有权征税。","D":"是的，因为税收对于服务于平衡联邦预算的迫切利益是必要的。"},"explanationZh":"税收和支出条款（即一般福利条款）赋予国会通过税收增加收入的广泛权力。这包括征收间接税（例如销售税）的权力，前提是这些税在每个州都平等适用，并且与收入生产合理相关——如此处所示。但税收还必须尊重其他宪法条款——包括第一修正案的新闻自由。零售销售税在这里这样做是因为它通常适用于所有商品，并且绝不针对印刷业务（选择 B）。由于销售税不涉及任何其他宪法条款，因此它是国会征税权的宪法行使——即使适用于报纸的零售。 （选择 A）零售税属于各州的征税权以及国会列举的征税权。 （选择 D）税收不一定是服务于引人注目的利益所必需的，因为它是普遍适用的并且不针对媒体。\n\n教育目标：间接税必须在每个州平等适用，并与收入生产合理相关。而且此类税收不能违反任何其他宪法条款，例如，针对媒体而违反第一修正案。\n\n参考文献\n• Leathers v. Medlock, 499 U.S. 439, 447 (1991)（认为政府不得行使其征税权力来挑衅媒体或针对一小群发言人）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"间接税必须在每个州平等适用，并与收入生产合理相关。而且此类税收不能违反任何其他宪法条款，例如，针对媒体而违反第一修正案。","iracZh":{"issue":"适用于报纸零售的这项税收是否符合宪法？","rule":"间接税必须在每个州平等适用，并与收入生产合理相关。而且此类税收不能违反任何其他宪法条款，例如，针对媒体而违反第一修正案。","analysis":"税收和支出条款（即一般福利条款）赋予国会通过税收增加收入的广泛权力。这包括征收间接税（例如销售税）的权力，前提是这些税在每个州都平等适用，并且与收入生产合理相关——如此处所示。但税收还必须尊重其他宪法条款——包括第一修正案的新闻自由。零售销售税在这里这样做是因为它通常适用于所有商品，并且绝不针对印刷业务（选择 B）。由于销售税不涉及任何其他宪法条款，因此它是国会征税权的宪法行使——即使适用于报纸的零售。 （选择 A）零售税属于各州的征税权以及国会列举的征税权。 （选择 D）税收不一定是服务于引人注目的利益所必需的，因为它是普遍适用的并且不针对媒体。\n\n教育目标：间接税必须在每个州平等适用，并与收入生产合理相关。而且此类税收不能违反任何其他宪法条款，例如，针对媒体而违反第一修正案。\n\n参考文献\n• Leathers v. Medlock, 499 U.S. 439, 447 (1991)（认为政府不得行使其征税权力来挑衅媒体或针对一小群发言人）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为国会有权征税。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-8da788f266b12b32"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":107,"sourceQuestionPage":320,"sourceQuestionPages":[320],"sourceExplanationPages":[321,322],"sourceAnswerPages":[321,322],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-108","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"\"Look-alike drugs\" is the term used to describe nonprescription drugs that look like narcotic drugs and are sold on the streets as narcotic drugs. After extensive hearings, Congress concluded that the sale of look-alike drugs was widespread in this country and was creating severe health and law-enforcement problems. To combat these problems, Congress enacted a comprehensive statute that regulates the manufacture, distribution, and sale of all look-alike drugs in the United States. Which of the following sources of constitutional authority can most easily be used to justify the authority of Congress to enact this statute?","choices":{"A":"The commerce clause.","B":"The enforcement powers of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"The general welfare clause.","D":"The spending power."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Commerce power to regulate\n\nactivities that substantially affect interstate commerce\n\nTo determine if Congress can regulate, consider whether:\n\n• activity is economic in nature (then substantial effect presumed)\n• jurisdictional element limits reach to activities with direct connection to interstate commerce\n• express congressional findings that activity substantially affects interstate commerce and\n• strong link between activity & effect on interstate commerce Congress's extensive power to regulate interstate commerce extends to nearly all activities that (1) involve two or more states or (2) have a substantial effect on interstate commerce. To determine whether activities have a substantial effect that triggers Congress's commerce power, courts consider whether:\n• the activities are economic in nature (if so, a substantial effect is presumed)\n\n• the regulation has a jurisdictional element that limits its reach to activities with a direct connection to or effect on interstate commerce\n\n• there are express congressional findings concerning the activities' effect on interstate commerce and\n\n• there is a strong link between the regulated activities and that effect.\n\nHere, the commerce clause most easily justifies Congress's enactment of a statute that regulates the manufacture, distribution, and sale of look-alike drugs. That is because, while some of these activities may only occur in one state (eg, manufacturing), all of them are economic in nature. It is therefore presumed that these activities have a substantial effect on interstate commerce and are subject to Congress's commerce powers. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment enforcement clause authorizes Congress to enact legislation enforcing the amendment's other provisions. But those other provisions only apply to state actors—not the private individuals likely selling look-alike drugs here. (Choices C & D) The taxing and spending clause (ie, general welfare clause) gives Congress the power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But here, the comprehensive statute regulating the manufacture, distribution, and sale of look-alike drugs goes beyond merely taxing and spending.\n\nEducational objective: An activity is presumed to have a substantial effect on interstate commerce that warrants regulation by Congress when the activity is economic in nature.\n\nReferences\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 32–33 (2005) (upholding the constitutionality of a federal drug law since in-state marijuana cultivation substantially affects interstate commerce).","educationalObjective":"An activity is presumed to have a substantial effect on interstate commerce that warrants regulation by Congress when the activity is economic in nature.","references":"• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 32–33 (2005) (upholding the constitutionality of a federal drug law since in-state marijuana cultivation substantially affects interstate commerce).","rule":"An activity is presumed to have a substantial effect on interstate commerce that warrants regulation by Congress when the activity is economic in nature.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following sources of constitutional authority can most easily be used to justify the authority of Congress to enact this statute?","rule":"An activity is presumed to have a substantial effect on interstate commerce that warrants regulation by Congress when the activity is economic in nature.","analysis":"Commerce power to regulate\n\nactivities that substantially affect interstate commerce\n\nTo determine if Congress can regulate, consider whether:\n\n• activity is economic in nature (then substantial effect presumed)\n• jurisdictional element limits reach to activities with direct connection to interstate commerce\n• express congressional findings that activity substantially affects interstate commerce and\n• strong link between activity & effect on interstate commerce Congress's extensive power to regulate interstate commerce extends to nearly all activities that (1) involve two or more states or (2) have a substantial effect on interstate commerce. To determine whether activities have a substantial effect that triggers Congress's commerce power, courts consider whether:\n• the activities are economic in nature (if so, a substantial effect is presumed)\n\n• the regulation has a jurisdictional element that limits its reach to activities with a direct connection to or effect on interstate commerce\n\n• there are express congressional findings concerning the activities' effect on interstate commerce and\n\n• there is a strong link between the regulated activities and that effect.\n\nHere, the commerce clause most easily justifies Congress's enactment of a statute that regulates the manufacture, distribution, and sale of look-alike drugs. That is because, while some of these activities may only occur in one state (eg, manufacturing), all of them are economic in nature. It is therefore presumed that these activities have a substantial effect on interstate commerce and are subject to Congress's commerce powers. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment enforcement clause authorizes Congress to enact legislation enforcing the amendment's other provisions. But those other provisions only apply to state actors—not the private individuals likely selling look-alike drugs here. (Choices C & D) The taxing and spending clause (ie, general welfare clause) gives Congress the power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But here, the comprehensive statute regulating the manufacture, distribution, and sale of look-alike drugs goes beyond merely taxing and spending.","conclusion":"Answer A: The commerce clause."},"questionZh":"“外观相似药物”是一个术语，用于描述看起来像麻醉药品并在街头作为麻醉药品出售的非处方药。经过广泛的听证会后，国会得出结论认为，相似药物的销售在该国非常普遍，并造成了严重的健康和执法问题。为了解决这些问题，国会颁布了一项全面的法规，规范美国境内所有相似药物的制造、分销和销售。以下哪项宪法权威来源最容易被用来证明国会颁布该法规的权力是正当的？","choicesZh":{"A":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。","B":"第十四修正案的执行权。","C":"一般福利条款。","D":"消费能力。"},"explanationZh":"商业监管权\n\n严重影响州际贸易的活动\n\n要确定国会是否可以监管，请考虑是否：\n\n• 活动本质上是经济性的（则假定有实质性影响）\n• 司法管辖因素限制了与州际贸易直接相关的活动\n• 表达国会的调查结果，即活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，并且\n• 州际商务活动与影响之间的紧密联系 国会监管州际商务的广泛权力延伸到几乎所有 (1) 涉及两个或多个州或 (2) 对州际商务有重大影响的活动。为了确定活动是否具有触发国会商业权力的实质性影响，法院考虑是否：\n• 这些活动本质上是经济性的（如果是这样，则假定产生重大影响）\n\n• 该法规具有管辖权要素，限制其适用于与州际贸易直接相关或影响的活动\n\n• 国会对这些活动对州际贸易的影响有明确的调查结果\n\n• 受监管的活动与该影响之间存在密切联系。\n\n在这里，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）最容易证明国会颁布一项法规来监管相似药物的制造、分销和销售。这是因为，虽然其中一些活动可能只发生在一个州（例如制造业），但所有这些活动本质上都是经济活动。因此，可以推测这些活动对州际贸易具有重大影响，并受国会商业权力的管辖。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案的执行条款授权国会颁布立法来执行该修正案的其他条款。但这些其他规定仅适用于国家行为者，而不适用于可能在这里销售相似药物的私人。 （选择C 和D） 征税和支出条款（即一般福利条款）赋予国会为一般福利征税和支出的权力。但在这里，监管相似药物的制造、分销和销售的综合法规不仅仅是税收和支出。\n\n教育目标：一项活动被认为对州际贸易有重大影响，当该活动本质上是经济活动时，需要国会进行监管。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 32–33 (2005)（维护联邦毒品法的合宪性，因为州内大麻种植严重影响州际贸易）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一项活动具有经济性质时，该活动被认为对州际贸易产生重大影响，需要国会进行监管。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项宪法权威来源最容易被用来证明国会颁布该法规的权力是正当的？","rule":"当一项活动具有经济性质时，该活动被认为对州际贸易产生重大影响，需要国会进行监管。","analysis":"商业监管权\n\n严重影响州际贸易的活动\n\n要确定国会是否可以监管，请考虑是否：\n\n• 活动本质上是经济性的（则假定有实质性影响）\n• 司法管辖因素限制了与州际贸易直接相关的活动\n• 表达国会的调查结果，即活动对州际贸易产生重大影响，并且\n• 州际商务活动与影响之间的紧密联系 国会监管州际商务的广泛权力延伸到几乎所有 (1) 涉及两个或多个州或 (2) 对州际商务有重大影响的活动。为了确定活动是否具有触发国会商业权力的实质性影响，法院考虑是否：\n• 这些活动本质上是经济性的（如果是这样，则假定产生重大影响）\n\n• 该法规具有管辖权要素，限制其适用于与州际贸易直接相关或影响的活动\n\n• 国会对这些活动对州际贸易的影响有明确的调查结果\n\n• 受监管的活动与该影响之间存在密切联系。\n\n在这里，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）最容易证明国会颁布一项法规来监管相似药物的制造、分销和销售。这是因为，虽然其中一些活动可能只发生在一个州（例如制造业），但所有这些活动本质上都是经济活动。因此，可以推测这些活动对州际贸易具有重大影响，并受国会商业权力的管辖。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案的执行条款授权国会颁布立法来执行该修正案的其他条款。但这些其他规定仅适用于国家行为者，而不适用于可能在这里销售相似药物的私人。 （选择C 和D） 征税和支出条款（即一般福利条款）赋予国会为一般福利征税和支出的权力。但在这里，监管相似药物的制造、分销和销售的综合法规不仅仅是税收和支出。\n\n教育目标：一项活动被认为对州际贸易有重大影响，当该活动本质上是经济活动时，需要国会进行监管。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 32–33 (2005)（维护联邦毒品法的合宪性，因为州内大麻种植严重影响州际贸易）。","conclusion":"答案 A：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"law-enforcement","ipa":"/'lɔ:enf'ɔ:smənt/","syllables":"law-en·force·ment","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 执法（机构或官员）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 执法（机构或官员）","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"After extensive hearings, Congress concluded that the sale of look-alike drugs was widespread in this country and was creating severe health and law-enforcement problems.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":108,"sourceQuestionPage":323,"sourceQuestionPages":[323],"sourceExplanationPages":[324,325],"sourceAnswerPages":[324,325],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-109","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"Congress enacted a statute prohibiting discrimination in the rental of residential property anywhere in the United States on the basis of sexual orientation or preference by any person or entity, public or private. Which of the following provisions provides the strongest basis for Congress's authority to enact this statute?","choices":{"A":"The commerce clause of Article I, Section 8.","B":"The enforcement clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"The general welfare clause of Article I, Section 8.","D":"The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The commerce clause of Article I, section 8 gives Congress broad regulatory authority over most activities involving two or more states. This includes the power to regulate:\n• the channels of interstate commerce (eg, airports)\n\n• the instrumentalities of interstate commerce (eg, planes)\n\n• people and things moving in interstate commerce (eg, commercial shipments) and\n\n• activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce (presumed when activity is economic in nature).\n\nHere, Congress enacted a statute prohibiting discrimination based on sexual orientation in the rental of residential property. Since renting is an economic activity, there is a strong presumption that this activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, the commerce clause provides the strongest basis for Congress's authority to enact this statute to regulate that activity. (Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment's enforcement clause, Congress can enact legislation to enforce the amendment's other provisions against state actors—but not private actors (eg, residential landlords). (Choice C) The Article I, section 8 taxing and spending clause (ie, general welfare clause) only empowers Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to prohibit rental discrimination. (Choice D) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state citizens by denying them a right of state citizenship. But this clause is a limitation on state power—not a basis for congressional authority.\n\nEducational objective: The commerce clause grants Congress extensive authority to regulate (1) the channels of interstate commerce, (2) the instrumentalities of interstate commerce, (3) persons and things moving in interstate commerce, and even (4) activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.\n\nReferences\n• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964) (holding that the commerce clause allows Congress to prohibit racial discrimination by local motels serving travelers).","educationalObjective":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive authority to regulate (1) the channels of interstate commerce, (2) the instrumentalities of interstate commerce, (3) persons and things moving in interstate commerce, and even (4) activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.","references":"• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964) (holding that the commerce clause allows Congress to prohibit racial discrimination by local motels serving travelers).","rule":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive authority to regulate (1) the channels of interstate commerce, (2) the instrumentalities of interstate commerce, (3) persons and things moving in interstate commerce, and even (4) activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following provisions provides the strongest basis for Congress's authority to enact this statute?","rule":"The commerce clause grants Congress extensive authority to regulate (1) the channels of interstate commerce, (2) the instrumentalities of interstate commerce, (3) persons and things moving in interstate commerce, and even (4) activities that substantially affect interstate commerce.","analysis":"The commerce clause of Article I, section 8 gives Congress broad regulatory authority over most activities involving two or more states. This includes the power to regulate:\n• the channels of interstate commerce (eg, airports)\n\n• the instrumentalities of interstate commerce (eg, planes)\n\n• people and things moving in interstate commerce (eg, commercial shipments) and\n\n• activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially affect interstate commerce (presumed when activity is economic in nature).\n\nHere, Congress enacted a statute prohibiting discrimination based on sexual orientation in the rental of residential property. Since renting is an economic activity, there is a strong presumption that this activity substantially affects interstate commerce. Therefore, the commerce clause provides the strongest basis for Congress's authority to enact this statute to regulate that activity. (Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment's enforcement clause, Congress can enact legislation to enforce the amendment's other provisions against state actors—but not private actors (eg, residential landlords). (Choice C) The Article I, section 8 taxing and spending clause (ie, general welfare clause) only empowers Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare—not to prohibit rental discrimination. (Choice D) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state citizens by denying them a right of state citizenship. But this clause is a limitation on state power—not a basis for congressional authority.","conclusion":"Answer A: The commerce clause of Article I, Section 8."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，禁止任何个人或实体（无论是公共还是私人）在美国任何地方的住宅物业租赁中基于性取向或偏好的歧视。以下哪项条款为国会颁布该法规的权力提供了最有力的基础？","choicesZh":{"A":"第一条第 8 节的商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。","B":"第十四修正案的执行条款。","C":"第一条第 8 节的一般福利条款。","D":"第四条的特权和豁免条款。"},"explanationZh":"第一条第 8 节的商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会对涉及两个或多个州的大多数活动的广泛监管权。这包括监管的权力：\n• 州际商业渠道（例如机场）\n\n• 州际贸易的工具（例如飞机）\n\n• 州际贸易中移动的人员和物品（例如商业运输）以及\n\n• 单独或总体对州际贸易产生重大影响的活动（假定活动本质上是经济活动）。\n\n在这里，国会颁布了一项法规，禁止在住宅物业租赁中基于性取向的歧视。由于租赁是一种经济活动，因此有一个强有力的假设，即该活动会严重影响州际贸易。因此，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）为国会有权颁布该法规来规范该活动提供了最强有力的基础。 （选项B）根据第十四修正案的执行条款，国会可以颁布立法来针对国家行为者执行该修正案的其他条款，但不能针对私人行为者（例如住宅房东）。 （选项C）第一条第8节征税和支出条款（即一般福利条款）仅授权国会为一般福利征税和支出，而不是禁止租金歧视。 （选项 D） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止各州通过剥夺本州公民权来歧视外州公民。但该条款是对国家权力的限制，而不是国会权力的基础。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管（1）州际商业的渠道，（2）州际商业的工具，（3）州际商业中流动的人和事物，甚至（4）对州际商业产生重大影响的活动。\n\n参考文献\n• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964)（认为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）允许国会禁止当地汽车旅馆为旅客提供种族歧视）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管（1）州际商业的渠道，（2）州际商业的工具，（3）州际商业中流动的人和事物，甚至（4）对州际商业产生重大影响的活动。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项条款为国会颁布该法规的权力提供了最有力的基础？","rule":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管（1）州际商业的渠道，（2）州际商业的工具，（3）州际商业中流动的人和事物，甚至（4）对州际商业产生重大影响的活动。","analysis":"第一条第 8 节的商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会对涉及两个或多个州的大多数活动的广泛监管权。这包括监管的权力：\n• 州际商业渠道（例如机场）\n\n• 州际贸易的工具（例如飞机）\n\n• 州际贸易中移动的人员和物品（例如商业运输）以及\n\n• 单独或总体对州际贸易产生重大影响的活动（假定活动本质上是经济活动）。\n\n在这里，国会颁布了一项法规，禁止在住宅物业租赁中基于性取向的歧视。由于租赁是一种经济活动，因此有一个强有力的假设，即该活动会严重影响州际贸易。因此，商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）为国会有权颁布该法规来规范该活动提供了最强有力的基础。 （选项B）根据第十四修正案的执行条款，国会可以颁布立法来针对国家行为者执行该修正案的其他条款，但不能针对私人行为者（例如住宅房东）。 （选项C）第一条第8节征税和支出条款（即一般福利条款）仅授权国会为一般福利征税和支出，而不是禁止租金歧视。 （选项 D） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止各州通过剥夺本州公民权来歧视外州公民。但该条款是对国家权力的限制，而不是国会权力的基础。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管（1）州际商业的渠道，（2）州际商业的工具，（3）州际商业中流动的人和事物，甚至（4）对州际商业产生重大影响的活动。\n\n参考文献\n• Heart of Atlanta Motel, Inc. v. United States, 379 U.S. 241, 258 (1964)（认为商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）允许国会禁止当地汽车旅馆为旅客提供种族歧视）。","conclusion":"答案 A：第一条第 8 节的商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"landlords","ipa":"/lˈændlˌɔɹdz/","syllables":"land·lords","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 房东； 房东, 地主( landlord的复数形式 )； 店主","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 房东； 房东, 地主( landlord的复数形式 )； 店主","wordForm":"名词；原形 landlord；词形 0:landlord/1:s","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment's enforcement clause, Congress can enact legislation to enforce the amendment's other provisions against state actors—but not private actors (eg, residential landlords).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-9e02bd68d0baa40f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":109,"sourceQuestionPage":326,"sourceQuestionPages":[326],"sourceExplanationPages":[327,328],"sourceAnswerPages":[327,328],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-110","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"A federal statute admitting a state to the Union granted the state certain public lands and established some very ambiguous conditions on the state's subsequent disposition of these lands. This federal statute also required the new state to write those exact same conditions into its state constitution. One hundred years later, a state statute dealing with the sale of these public lands was challenged in a state court lawsuit on the ground that it was inconsistent with the conditions contained in the federal statute and with the provisions of the state constitution that exactly copy the conditions contained in the federal statute. The trial court decision in this case was appealed to the state supreme court. In its opinion, the state supreme court dealt at length with the ambiguous language of the federal statute and with cases interpreting identical language in federal statutes admitting other states to the union. The state supreme court opinion did not discuss the similar provisions of the state constitution, but it did hold that the challenged state statute is invalid because it is \"inconsistent with the language of the federal statute and therefore is inconsistent with the identical provisions of our state constitution.\" If the losing party in the state supreme court seeks review of the decision of that court in the United States Supreme Court, how should the United States Supreme Court proceed?","choices":{"A":"The court should accept the case for review and determine the validity of the interpretation of the federal statute if it is an important and substantial question.","B":"The court should ask the state supreme court to indicate more clearly whether it relied on the state constitutional provision in rendering its decision.","C":"The court should decline to review the case because a decision by a state supreme court concerning the proper disposition of state public lands is not reviewable by the United States Supreme Court.","D":"The court should decline to review the case on the ground that the decision of the state supreme court rests on an adequate and independent state ground."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The Supreme Court of the United States (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction to review decisions issued by lower federal courts and the highest state courts that concern important and substantial issues of federal law. But SCOTUS has no jurisdiction to review a state court decision that is supported by state-law grounds that are both:\n• adequate – when state law completely resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and\n• independent – when the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its decision. Here, a state statute dealing with the sale of certain public lands was challenged in state court and held to be inconsistent with a federal statute and the state constitution (adequate state grounds). But the state supreme court's decision relied on federal case law that interpreted identical language in other federal statutes (no independent state grounds) (Choice D). Therefore, SCOTUS should review the state supreme court's interpretation if it is an important and substantial question. (Choice B) The state supreme court explicitly stated that it relied on federal case law, so no clarification is needed. But even if the grounds for the state court's decision were unclear, SCOTUS would not ask the state court for clarification. Instead, SCOTUS would assume that the state court used federal law to reach its decision, rule on the federal issue, and remand the case to state court to resolve any remaining state-law issues. (Choice C) SCOTUS can review a state supreme court decision—even if it involves the proper disposition of state public lands—as long as that decision was not based on adequate and independent state-law grounds.\n\nEducational objective: The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review a state court decision based on an adequate state-law ground that completely resolves the case independent of federal law.\n\nReferences\n• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983) (explaining when a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state-law grounds).\n\n• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 44 (2019) (listing the rules to determine whether a decision rests on adequate and independent state grounds).","educationalObjective":"The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review a state court decision based on an adequate state-law ground that completely resolves the case independent of federal law.","references":"• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983) (explaining when a state supreme court judgment rests on adequate and independent state-law grounds).\n\n• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 44 (2019) (listing the rules to determine whether a decision rests on adequate and independent state grounds).","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review a state court decision based on an adequate state-law ground that completely resolves the case independent of federal law.","irac":{"issue":"A federal statute admitting a state to the Union granted the state certain public lands and established some very ambiguous conditions on the state's subsequent disposition of these lands. This federal statute also required the new state to write those exact same conditions into its state constitution. One hundred years later, a state statute dealing with the sale of these public lands was challenged in a state court lawsuit on the ground that it was inconsistent with the conditions contained in the federal statute and with the provisions of the state constitution that exactly copy the conditions contained in the federal statute. The trial court decision in this case was appealed to the state supreme court. In its opinion, the state supreme court dealt at length with the ambiguous language of the federal statute and with cases interpreting identical language in federal statutes admitting other states to the union. The state supreme court opinion did not discuss the similar provisions of the state constitution, but it did hold that the challenged state statute is invalid because it is \"inconsistent with the language of the federal statute and therefore is inconsistent with the identical provisions of our state constitution.\" If the losing party in the state supreme court seeks review of the decision of that court in the United States Supreme Court, how should the United States Supreme Court proceed?","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court has no jurisdiction to review a state court decision based on an adequate state-law ground that completely resolves the case independent of federal law.","analysis":"The Supreme Court of the United States (SCOTUS) has appellate jurisdiction to review decisions issued by lower federal courts and the highest state courts that concern important and substantial issues of federal law. But SCOTUS has no jurisdiction to review a state court decision that is supported by state-law grounds that are both:\n• adequate – when state law completely resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and\n• independent – when the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its decision. Here, a state statute dealing with the sale of certain public lands was challenged in state court and held to be inconsistent with a federal statute and the state constitution (adequate state grounds). But the state supreme court's decision relied on federal case law that interpreted identical language in other federal statutes (no independent state grounds) (Choice D). Therefore, SCOTUS should review the state supreme court's interpretation if it is an important and substantial question. (Choice B) The state supreme court explicitly stated that it relied on federal case law, so no clarification is needed. But even if the grounds for the state court's decision were unclear, SCOTUS would not ask the state court for clarification. Instead, SCOTUS would assume that the state court used federal law to reach its decision, rule on the federal issue, and remand the case to state court to resolve any remaining state-law issues. (Choice C) SCOTUS can review a state supreme court decision—even if it involves the proper disposition of state public lands—as long as that decision was not based on adequate and independent state-law grounds.","conclusion":"Answer A: The court should accept the case for review and determine the validity of the interpretation of the federal statute if it is an important and substantial question."},"questionZh":"承认一个州加入联邦的联邦法规授予该州某些公共土地，并对该州随后处置这些土地设定了一些非常模糊的条件。该联邦法规还要求新州将这些完全相同的条件写入其州宪法。一百年后，一项涉及出售这些公共土地的州法规在州法院诉讼中受到质疑，理由是该法规与联邦法规中包含的条件以及与完全复制联邦法规中包含的条件的州宪法条款不一致。本案初审法院的判决已上诉至州最高法院。在其看来，州最高法院详细处理了联邦法规中含糊不清的语言，以及解释联邦法规中允许其他州加入联邦的相同语言的案件。州最高法院的意见没有讨论州宪法的类似条款，但确实认为受质疑的州法规无效，因为它“与联邦法规的语言不一致，因此与我们州宪法的相同条款不一致”。如果州最高法院的败诉方寻求美国最高法院对该法院的判决进行复审，美国最高法院应如何审理？","choicesZh":{"A":"如果这是一个重要且实质性的问题，法院应该接受案件进行审查并确定联邦法规解释的有效性。","B":"法院应要求州最高法院更明确地表明其是否依据州宪法条款做出决定。","C":"法院应拒绝审查此案，因为州最高法院关于适当处置州公共土地的决定不受美国最高法院审查。","D":"法院应拒绝复审该案，理由是州最高法院的裁决有充分且独立的州理由。"},"explanationZh":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有上诉管辖权，可以审查下级联邦法院和最高州法院做出的涉及联邦法律重要和实质性问题的裁决。但最高法院没有管辖权来审查州法院的决定，该决定得到州法律依据的支持，这些依据是：\n• 充分——当州法律完全解决问题时，联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，并且\n• 独立——州法院不依赖联邦法律做出裁决。在此，涉及某些公共土地出售的州法规在州法院受到质疑，并被认为与联邦法规和州宪法（充分的州理由）不一致。但州最高法院的判决依赖于对其他联邦法规中相同语言进行解释的联邦判例法（没有独立的州理由）（选项 D）。因此，如果这是一个重要且实质性的问题，最高法院应该审查州最高法院的解释。 （选项B）州最高法院明确表示其依据的是联邦判例法，因此无需澄清。但即使州法院判决的理由尚不清楚，最高法院也不会要求州法院做出澄清。相反，最高法院将假定州法院使用联邦法律做出决定，对联邦问题做出裁决，并将案件发回州法院以解决任何剩余的州法律问题。 （选择 C）最高法院可以审查州最高法院的决定——即使它涉及州公共土地的适当处置——只要该决定不是基于充分和独立的州法律依据。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院没有管辖权来审查州法院基于充分的州法律依据而独立于联邦法律完全解决案件的判决。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983)（解释州最高法院的判决何时基于充分且独立的州法律依据）。\n\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 44 (2019)（列出了确定决定是否基于充分且独立的州理由的规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"美国最高法院没有管辖权来审查州法院基于充分的州法律依据而完全独立于联邦法律解决案件的判决。","iracZh":{"issue":"承认一个州加入联邦的联邦法规授予该州某些公共土地，并对该州随后处置这些土地设定了一些非常模糊的条件。该联邦法规还要求新州将这些完全相同的条件写入其州宪法。一百年后，一项涉及出售这些公共土地的州法规在州法院诉讼中受到质疑，理由是该法规与联邦法规中包含的条件以及与完全复制联邦法规中包含的条件的州宪法条款不一致。本案初审法院的判决已上诉至州最高法院。在其看来，州最高法院详细处理了联邦法规中含糊不清的语言，以及解释联邦法规中允许其他州加入联邦的相同语言的案件。州最高法院的意见没有讨论州宪法的类似条款，但确实认为受质疑的州法规无效，因为它“与联邦法规的语言不一致，因此与我们州宪法的相同条款不一致”。如果州最高法院的败诉方寻求美国最高法院对该法院的判决进行复审，美国最高法院应如何审理？","rule":"美国最高法院没有管辖权来审查州法院基于充分的州法律依据而完全独立于联邦法律解决案件的判决。","analysis":"美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 拥有上诉管辖权，可以审查下级联邦法院和最高州法院做出的涉及联邦法律重要和实质性问题的裁决。但最高法院没有管辖权来审查州法院的决定，该决定得到州法律依据的支持，这些依据是：\n• 充分——当州法律完全解决问题时，联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，并且\n• 独立——州法院不依赖联邦法律做出裁决。在此，涉及某些公共土地出售的州法规在州法院受到质疑，并被认为与联邦法规和州宪法（充分的州理由）不一致。但州最高法院的判决依赖于对其他联邦法规中相同语言进行解释的联邦判例法（没有独立的州理由）（选项 D）。因此，如果这是一个重要且实质性的问题，最高法院应该审查州最高法院的解释。 （选项B）州最高法院明确表示其依据的是联邦判例法，因此无需澄清。但即使州法院判决的理由尚不清楚，最高法院也不会要求州法院做出澄清。相反，最高法院将假定州法院使用联邦法律做出决定，对联邦问题做出裁决，并将案件发回州法院以解决任何剩余的州法律问题。 （选择 C）最高法院可以审查州最高法院的决定——即使它涉及州公共土地的适当处置——只要该决定不是基于充分和独立的州法律依据。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院没有管辖权来审查州法院基于充分的州法律依据而独立于联邦法律完全解决案件的判决。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1040–41 (1983)（解释州最高法院的判决何时基于充分且独立的州法律依据）。\n\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 44 (2019)（列出了确定决定是否基于充分且独立的州理由的规则）。","conclusion":"答案 A：如果这是一个重要且实质性的问题，法院应该接受案件进行审查并确定联邦法规解释的有效性。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-42b299d1cbbec967"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":110,"sourceQuestionPage":329,"sourceQuestionPages":[329],"sourceExplanationPages":[330,331],"sourceAnswerPages":[330,331],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-111","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"The National Ecological Balance Act prohibits the destruction or removal of any wild animals located on lands owned by the United States without express permission from the Federal Bureau of Land Management. Violators are subject to fines of up to $1,000 per offense. After substantial property damage was inflicted on residents of a state by hungry coyotes, the state legislature passed the Coyote Bounty Bill, which offers $25 for each coyote killed or captured within the state. A national forest, owned by the federal government, is located entirely within the state. Many coyotes live in the national forest. Without seeking permission from the Bureau of Land Management, a hunter shot several coyotes in the national forest and collected the bounty from the state. As a result, he was subsequently tried in federal district court, convicted, and fined $1,000 for violating the National Ecological Balance Act. The hunter appealed his conviction to the United States Court of Appeals. On appeal, should the Court of Appeals hold the National Ecological Balance Act constitutional as applied to the hunter?","choices":{"A":"No, because Congress may not use its delegated powers to override the Tenth Amendment right of the state to legislate in areas of traditional state governmental functions, such as the protection of the property of its residents.","B":"No, because Congress violates the full faith and credit clause of Article IV when it punishes conduct that has been authorized by state action.","C":"Yes, because Article I, Section 8, of the Constitution authorizes Congress to enact all laws necessary and proper to advance the general welfare.","D":"Yes, because the property clause of Article IV, Section 3, of the Constitution authorizes such federal statutory controls and sanctions."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Article IV clauses\n\nClause Requirements\n\nFull faith & credit States must recognize public acts, records & judicial proceedings of other states\n\nPrivileges & States cannot deny rights of state citizenship to citizens of other immunities states\n\nProperty Congress can dispose of & regulate federal land/territories\n\nGuarantee Federal government must guarantee states republican form of government & protect states from invasion\n\nThe Article IV, section 3 property clause gives Congress complete power to dispose of and regulate federally owned land and territories—including the wildlife living thereon. As a result, Congress can prohibit the destruction or removal of wild animals on federal land without permission from the Federal Bureau of Land Management. And since the hunter shot coyotes in a national forest without permission, he can be prosecuted under this statute. Therefore, the court should hold this statute constitutional as applied to the hunter. (Choice A) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states (or to the people) all powers not expressly given to the federal government by the Constitution—including the police power to protect residents' property. But Congress can use its delegated powers to override a state's Tenth Amendment powers. And when it does so, the Article VI supremacy clause prescribes that the federal law supersedes the conflicting state law. (Choice B) The full faith and credit clause of Article IV requires states to respect public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of other states. Therefore, Congress does not violate this clause by punishing conduct that has been authorized by a state. (Choice C) Article I, section 8 authorizes Congress to enact laws that are \"necessary and proper\" to carry out its enumerated powers—including the power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But Congress is not taxing or spending here, so the necessary and proper clause cannot justify the statute.\n\nEducational objective: Under the property clause, Congress has absolute power over federally owned land and territories—including the power to prohibit the destruction or removal of wild animals living thereon.\n\nReferences\n• Kleppe v. New Mexico, 426 U.S. 529, 540–41 (1976) (stating that Congress's plenary power over federal lands \"includes the power to regulate and protect the wildlife living there\").","educationalObjective":"Under the property clause, Congress has absolute power over federally owned land and territories—including the power to prohibit the destruction or removal of wild animals living thereon.","references":"• Kleppe v. New Mexico, 426 U.S. 529, 540–41 (1976) (stating that Congress's plenary power over federal lands \"includes the power to regulate and protect the wildlife living there\").","rule":"Under the property clause, Congress has absolute power over federally owned land and territories—including the power to prohibit the destruction or removal of wild animals living thereon.","irac":{"issue":"On appeal, should the Court of Appeals hold the National Ecological Balance Act constitutional as applied to the hunter?","rule":"Under the property clause, Congress has absolute power over federally owned land and territories—including the power to prohibit the destruction or removal of wild animals living thereon.","analysis":"Article IV clauses\n\nClause Requirements\n\nFull faith & credit States must recognize public acts, records & judicial proceedings of other states\n\nPrivileges & States cannot deny rights of state citizenship to citizens of other immunities states\n\nProperty Congress can dispose of & regulate federal land/territories\n\nGuarantee Federal government must guarantee states republican form of government & protect states from invasion\n\nThe Article IV, section 3 property clause gives Congress complete power to dispose of and regulate federally owned land and territories—including the wildlife living thereon. As a result, Congress can prohibit the destruction or removal of wild animals on federal land without permission from the Federal Bureau of Land Management. And since the hunter shot coyotes in a national forest without permission, he can be prosecuted under this statute. Therefore, the court should hold this statute constitutional as applied to the hunter. (Choice A) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states (or to the people) all powers not expressly given to the federal government by the Constitution—including the police power to protect residents' property. But Congress can use its delegated powers to override a state's Tenth Amendment powers. And when it does so, the Article VI supremacy clause prescribes that the federal law supersedes the conflicting state law. (Choice B) The full faith and credit clause of Article IV requires states to respect public acts, records, and judicial proceedings of other states. Therefore, Congress does not violate this clause by punishing conduct that has been authorized by a state. (Choice C) Article I, section 8 authorizes Congress to enact laws that are \"necessary and proper\" to carry out its enumerated powers—including the power to tax and spend for the general welfare. But Congress is not taxing or spending here, so the necessary and proper clause cannot justify the statute.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the property clause of Article IV, Section 3, of the Constitution authorizes such federal statutory controls and sanctions."},"questionZh":"《国家生态平衡法》禁止在未经联邦土地管理局明确许可的情况下销毁或清除美国拥有的土地上的任何野生动物。违规者每次违规将被处以最高 1,000 美元的罚款。在饥饿的土狼对某州居民造成重大财产损失后，州立法机关通过了《土狼赏金法案》，该法案为该州内每只被杀死或捕获的土狼提供 25 美元的奖金。国家森林由联邦政府所有，完全位于该州境内。许多郊狼生活在国家森林中。一名猎人在未经土地管理局许可的情况下，在国家森林中射杀了几只土狼，并从国家那里收取了赏金。结果，他随后在联邦地区法院受审，被定罪，并因违反《国家生态平衡法》而被罚款 1000 美元。猎人向美国上诉法院提出上诉。在上诉中，上诉法院是否应该认定适用于猎人的《国家生态平衡法》符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为国会不得利用其授予的权力推翻第十修正案规定的州在传统州政府职能领域（例如保护居民财产）立法的权利。","B":"不，因为国会在惩罚经国家行为（state action）授权的行为时违反了第四条的全部信用条款。","C":"是的，因为宪法第一条第八节授权国会颁布所有必要和适当的法律来促进公共福利。","D":"是的，因为宪法第四条第三节的财产条款授权此类联邦法定控制和制裁。"},"explanationZh":"第四条条款\n\n条款要求\n\n充分的信任和信用 国家必须承认其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序\n\n特权和州不能否认其他豁免州公民的州公民权利\n\n财产国会可以处置和管理联邦土地/领土\n\n保证联邦政府必须保证各州实行共和政体并保护各州免受入侵\n\n第四条第 3 款财产条款赋予国会处置和管理联邦拥有的土地和领地（包括生活在其上的野生动物）的完全权力。因此，未经联邦土地管理局许可，国会可以禁止在联邦土地上破坏或清除野生动物。由于猎人未经许可在国家森林中射杀郊狼，因此他可以根据该法规受到起诉。因此，法院应认为适用于猎人的该法规符合宪法。 （选项A）第十修正案将宪法未明确赋予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州（或人民），包括警察保护居民财产的权力。但国会可以利用其授予的权力推翻各州的第十修正案权力。当这样做时，第六条至上条款规定联邦法律取代相冲突的州法律。 （选项B）第四条的完整诚信条款要求各国尊重其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。因此，国会通过惩罚州授权的行为并不违反该条款。 （选项 C）第一条第 8 节授权国会颁布“必要且适当”的法律，以履行其列举的权力，包括为公共福利征税和支出的权力。但国会并没有在这里征税或支出，因此必要且适当的条款不能证明该法规的合理性。\n\n教育目标：根据财产条款，国会对联邦拥有的土地和领土拥有绝对权力，包括禁止破坏或清除生活在其中的野生动物的权力。\n\n参考文献\n• Kleppe v. New Mexico, 426 U.S. 529, 540–41 (1976)（指出国会对联邦土地的全部权力“包括管理和保护生活在那里的野生动物的权力”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据财产条款，国会对联邦拥有的土地和领土拥有绝对权力，包括禁止破坏或清除生活在其中的野生动物的权力。","iracZh":{"issue":"在上诉中，上诉法院是否应该认定适用于猎人的《国家生态平衡法》符合宪法？","rule":"根据财产条款，国会对联邦拥有的土地和领土拥有绝对权力，包括禁止破坏或清除生活在其中的野生动物的权力。","analysis":"第四条条款\n\n条款要求\n\n充分的信任和信用 国家必须承认其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序\n\n特权和州不能否认其他豁免州公民的州公民权利\n\n财产国会可以处置和管理联邦土地/领土\n\n保证联邦政府必须保证各州实行共和政体并保护各州免受入侵\n\n第四条第 3 款财产条款赋予国会处置和管理联邦拥有的土地和领地（包括生活在其上的野生动物）的完全权力。因此，未经联邦土地管理局许可，国会可以禁止在联邦土地上破坏或清除野生动物。由于猎人未经许可在国家森林中射杀郊狼，因此他可以根据该法规受到起诉。因此，法院应认为适用于猎人的该法规符合宪法。 （选项A）第十修正案将宪法未明确赋予联邦政府的所有权力保留给各州（或人民），包括警察保护居民财产的权力。但国会可以利用其授予的权力推翻各州的第十修正案权力。当这样做时，第六条至上条款规定联邦法律取代相冲突的州法律。 （选项B）第四条的完整诚信条款要求各国尊重其他国家的公共行为、记录和司法程序。因此，国会通过惩罚州授权的行为并不违反该条款。 （选项 C）第一条第 8 节授权国会颁布“必要且适当”的法律，以履行其列举的权力，包括为公共福利征税和支出的权力。但国会并没有在这里征税或支出，因此必要且适当的条款不能证明该法规的合理性。\n\n教育目标：根据财产条款，国会对联邦拥有的土地和领土拥有绝对权力，包括禁止破坏或清除生活在其中的野生动物的权力。\n\n参考文献\n• Kleppe v. New Mexico, 426 U.S. 529, 540–41 (1976)（指出国会对联邦土地的全部权力“包括管理和保护生活在那里的野生动物的权力”）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为宪法第四条第三节的财产条款授权此类联邦法定控制和制裁。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":111,"sourceQuestionPage":332,"sourceQuestionPages":[332],"sourceExplanationPages":[333,334],"sourceAnswerPages":[333,334],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-112","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A proposed federal statute would prohibit all types of discrimination against minorities on the basis of their race in every business transaction executed anywhere in the United States by any person or entity, governmental or private. Is this proposed federal statute likely to be constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it would invade the powers reserved to the states by the Tenth Amendment.","B":"No, because it would regulate purely local transactions that are not in interstate commerce.","C":"Yes, because it could reasonably be viewed as a means of enforcing the provisions of the Thirteenth Amendment.","D":"Yes, because it could reasonably be viewed as an exercise of Congress's authority to enact laws for the general welfare."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Congress's power to enforce the Civil War Amendments\n\nAmendment Effects Congress's enforcement powers\n\nThirteenth Prohibits government & private\n• Prohibit badges/incidents actors from engaging in of slavery slavery/involuntary servitude\n• Override state 11th Amendment immunity Fourteenth Prohibits states from denying equal\n• Remedy/prevent violations protection, due process, or with proportional & privileges/immunities of national congruent legislation citizenship\n• Override state 11th Amendment immunity Fifteenth Prohibits states & federal government\n• Ban methods of voter from denying or abridging voting discrimination rights based on race, color, or\n• Override state 11th previous servitude Amendment immunity Section 1 of the Thirteenth Amendment prohibits all government and private entities from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude. And the Section 2 enforcement clause gives Congress the power to forbid any racial discrimination that can reasonably be viewed as a \"badge or incident of slavery\" (eg, discrimination in housing, education, or employment). Since racial discrimination against minorities in business transactions can reasonably be viewed as a relic of slavery, the proposed federal statute banning this conduct is likely constitutional. (Choice A) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government (eg, police powers). But the Thirteenth Amendment expressly grants Congress the authority to enact legislation addressing slavery and involuntary servitude (as seen here). (Choice B) The fact that the statute would regulate purely local transactions that do not have a substantial effect on interstate commerce shows that it cannot be supported by the commerce clause. But Congress can still enact this statute pursuant to its power to enforce the Thirteenth Amendment. (Choice D) The taxing and spending clause gives Congress the authority to tax or spend for the general welfare—not to enact any type of law for this purpose (eg, prohibiting discrimination).\n\nEducational objective: The Thirteenth Amendment (1) prohibits all government and private entities from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude and (2) empowers Congress to ban any type of racial discrimination that can reasonably be viewed as a badge or incident of slavery.\n\nReferences\n• Patterson v. McLean Credit Union, 491 U.S. 164, 192 (1989) (explaining that the Thirteenth Amendment empowers Congress to identify the badges and incidents of slavery and to legislate to end them).","educationalObjective":"The Thirteenth Amendment (1) prohibits all government and private entities from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude and (2) empowers Congress to ban any type of racial discrimination that can reasonably be viewed as a badge or incident of slavery.","references":"• Patterson v. McLean Credit Union, 491 U.S. 164, 192 (1989) (explaining that the Thirteenth Amendment empowers Congress to identify the badges and incidents of slavery and to legislate to end them).","rule":"The Thirteenth Amendment (1) prohibits all government and private entities from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude and (2) empowers Congress to ban any type of racial discrimination that can reasonably be viewed as a badge or incident of slavery.","irac":{"issue":"Is this proposed federal statute likely to be constitutional?","rule":"The Thirteenth Amendment (1) prohibits all government and private entities from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude and (2) empowers Congress to ban any type of racial discrimination that can reasonably be viewed as a badge or incident of slavery.","analysis":"Congress's power to enforce the Civil War Amendments\n\nAmendment Effects Congress's enforcement powers\n\nThirteenth Prohibits government & private\n• Prohibit badges/incidents actors from engaging in of slavery slavery/involuntary servitude\n• Override state 11th Amendment immunity Fourteenth Prohibits states from denying equal\n• Remedy/prevent violations protection, due process, or with proportional & privileges/immunities of national congruent legislation citizenship\n• Override state 11th Amendment immunity Fifteenth Prohibits states & federal government\n• Ban methods of voter from denying or abridging voting discrimination rights based on race, color, or\n• Override state 11th previous servitude Amendment immunity Section 1 of the Thirteenth Amendment prohibits all government and private entities from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude. And the Section 2 enforcement clause gives Congress the power to forbid any racial discrimination that can reasonably be viewed as a \"badge or incident of slavery\" (eg, discrimination in housing, education, or employment). Since racial discrimination against minorities in business transactions can reasonably be viewed as a relic of slavery, the proposed federal statute banning this conduct is likely constitutional. (Choice A) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states all powers that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government (eg, police powers). But the Thirteenth Amendment expressly grants Congress the authority to enact legislation addressing slavery and involuntary servitude (as seen here). (Choice B) The fact that the statute would regulate purely local transactions that do not have a substantial effect on interstate commerce shows that it cannot be supported by the commerce clause. But Congress can still enact this statute pursuant to its power to enforce the Thirteenth Amendment. (Choice D) The taxing and spending clause gives Congress the authority to tax or spend for the general welfare—not to enact any type of law for this purpose (eg, prohibiting discrimination).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it could reasonably be viewed as a means of enforcing the provisions of the Thirteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"拟议的联邦法规将禁止任何个人或实体（无论是政府还是私人）在美国任何地方执行的每项商业交易中基于种族对少数群体进行一切类型的歧视。这项拟议的联邦法规可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这会侵犯第十修正案保留给各州的权力。","B":"不，因为它将监管不属于州际贸易的纯粹本地交易。","C":"是的，因为它可以合理地被视为执行第十三修正案条款的一种手段。","D":"是的，因为它可以合理地被视为国会行使为公众福利颁布法律的权力。"},"explanationZh":"国会执行内战修正案的权力\n\n修正案影响国会的执行权\n\n第十三条 禁止政府和私人\n• 禁止徽章/事件参与者参与奴役/非自愿奴役\n• 推翻各州第十一条修正案豁免权 第十四条禁止各州否认平等\n• 补救/防止违规行为保护、正当程序或国家一致立法公民权的比例和特权/豁免权\n• 推翻州第十一修正案豁免权 第十五条禁止州和联邦政府\n• 禁止选民以种族、肤色或种族为由拒绝或剥夺投票歧视权利。\n• 推翻州第11 条先前的劳役修正案豁免权 第十三条修正案第1 条禁止所有政府和私人实体从事奴役或非自愿劳役。第 2 条执行条款赋予国会禁止任何可以合理地视为“奴隶制徽章或事件”的种族歧视（例如住房、教育或就业方面的歧视）的权力。由于商业交易中针对少数群体的种族歧视可以合理地视为奴隶制的遗迹，因此拟议的禁止这种行为的联邦法规可能符合宪法。 （选项A）第十修正案将宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力（例如警察权力）保留给各州。但第十三修正案明确授予国会颁布立法解决奴隶制和非自愿奴役问题的权力（如此处所示）。 （选项 B）该法规将规范纯粹的本地交易，对州际贸易没有实质性影响，这一事实表明它不能得到商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的支持。但国会仍然可以根据其执行第十三修正案的权力颁布该法规。 （选项D） 征税和支出条款赋予国会为公共福利征税或支出的权力，而不是为此目的颁布任何类型的法律（例如，禁止歧视）。\n\n教育目标：第十三修正案 (1) 禁止所有政府和私人实体从事奴隶制或非自愿奴役，(2) 授权国会禁止任何类型的可合理视为奴隶制徽章或事件的种族歧视。\n\n参考文献\n• Patterson v. McLean Credit Union, 491 U.S. 164, 192 (1989)（解释说，第十三修正案授权国会识别奴隶制的徽章和事件，并立法终止它们）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十三修正案 (1) 禁止所有政府和私人实体从事奴隶制或非自愿奴役，并且 (2) 授权国会禁止任何类型的可合理视为奴隶制徽章或事件的种族歧视。","iracZh":{"issue":"这项拟议的联邦法规可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"第十三修正案 (1) 禁止所有政府和私人实体从事奴隶制或非自愿奴役，并且 (2) 授权国会禁止任何类型的可合理视为奴隶制徽章或事件的种族歧视。","analysis":"国会执行内战修正案的权力\n\n修正案影响国会的执行权\n\n第十三条 禁止政府和私人\n• 禁止徽章/事件参与者参与奴役/非自愿奴役\n• 推翻各州第十一条修正案豁免权 第十四条禁止各州否认平等\n• 补救/防止违规行为保护、正当程序或国家一致立法公民权的比例和特权/豁免权\n• 推翻州第十一修正案豁免权 第十五条禁止州和联邦政府\n• 禁止选民以种族、肤色或种族为由拒绝或剥夺投票歧视权利。\n• 推翻州第11 条先前的劳役修正案豁免权 第十三条修正案第1 条禁止所有政府和私人实体从事奴役或非自愿劳役。第 2 条执行条款赋予国会禁止任何可以合理地视为“奴隶制徽章或事件”的种族歧视（例如住房、教育或就业方面的歧视）的权力。由于商业交易中针对少数群体的种族歧视可以合理地视为奴隶制的遗迹，因此拟议的禁止这种行为的联邦法规可能符合宪法。 （选项A）第十修正案将宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力（例如警察权力）保留给各州。但第十三修正案明确授予国会颁布立法解决奴隶制和非自愿奴役问题的权力（如此处所示）。 （选项 B）该法规将规范纯粹的本地交易，对州际贸易没有实质性影响，这一事实表明它不能得到商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）的支持。但国会仍然可以根据其执行第十三修正案的权力颁布该法规。 （选项D） 征税和支出条款赋予国会为公共福利征税或支出的权力，而不是为此目的颁布任何类型的法律（例如，禁止歧视）。\n\n教育目标：第十三修正案 (1) 禁止所有政府和私人实体从事奴隶制或非自愿奴役，(2) 授权国会禁止任何类型的可合理视为奴隶制徽章或事件的种族歧视。\n\n参考文献\n• Patterson v. McLean Credit Union, 491 U.S. 164, 192 (1989)（解释说，第十三修正案授权国会识别奴隶制的徽章和事件，并立法终止它们）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为它可以合理地被视为执行第十三修正案条款的一种手段。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"voter","ipa":"/vˈoʊtɝ/","syllables":"voter","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 选民, 投票人；[法] 选民, 选举人, 投票人","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 选民, 投票人；[法] 选民, 选举人, 投票人","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:voters","sourceSentence":"Congress's power to enforce the Civil War Amendments Amendment Effects Congress's enforcement powers Thirteenth Prohibits government & private • Prohibit badges/incidents actors from engaging in of slavery slavery/involuntary servitude • Override state 11th Amendment immunity Fourteenth Prohibits states from denying equal • Remedy/prevent violations protection, due process, or with proportional & privileges/immunitie","synonyms":["elector"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":112,"sourceQuestionPage":335,"sourceQuestionPages":[335],"sourceExplanationPages":[336,337],"sourceAnswerPages":[336,337],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-113","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supreme Court Review","question":"A state law provides for an award of damages against anyone who publishes the name of a rape victim. Pursuant to that law, a woman sued a local newspaper in state court after the newspaper identified her as a rape victim. The state trial and appellate courts rejected the claim, holding that the state law was invalid under both the state constitution and the First Amendment of the U.S. Constitution. The state supreme court affirmed, holding specifically: \"We think that this well-intentioned law very likely violates the First Amendment of the federal Constitution. We need not, however, decide that issue, because the law assuredly violates our state constitution, which provides even greater protection to the right of the press to report the news.\" The woman petitioned for review in the U.S. Supreme Court. Is the U.S. Supreme Court likely to review the state supreme court judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the First Amendment prohibits the imposition of liability for the publication of truthful information.","B":"No, because the judgment of the state supreme court rests upon an adequate and independent state-law ground.","C":"Yes, because the supremacy clause does not permit a state to create rights greater than those conferred by the federal Constitution.","D":"Yes, because the U.S. Supreme Court's appellate jurisdiction extends to cases arising under federal law."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Adequate & independent state ground\n\n(precludes SCOTUS review)\n\nAdequate State ground fully resolves matter & application of federal law would not affect outcome\n\nIndependent State court did not rely on federal law to reach conclusion\n\nSCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) may exercise its appellate jurisdiction to review a judgment of the highest court of a state if that judgment turned on federal law. But SCOTUS may not review a state court judgment that rests on an adequate and independent state ground—ie:\n• a state ground that fully resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case (adequate) and\n• the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its conclusion (independent). Here, the state supreme court indicated that the state law very likely violates the U.S. Constitution. But the court also stated that it need not decide that federal issue because the law assuredly violates the state constitution. Since the state ground fully resolves the matter and the court did not rely on federal law to interpret the state constitution, SCOTUS is unlikely to review the state court judgment (Choice D). (Choice A) The First Amendment freedom of the press generally bars liability for publicizing truthful information. But this does not provide a basis for SCOTUS to decline to review a case. Instead, review is improper here because the state court decision was based on an adequate and independent state ground. (Choice C) The Article VI supremacy clause establishes that the U.S. Constitution and federal laws trump conflicting state laws. Therefore, states cannot restrict rights conferred by the U.S. Constitution. However, states are always free to create greater rights and protections than the U.S. Constitution does.\n\nEducational objective: The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment that rests on an adequate and independent state ground—ie, (1) a state ground fully resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and (2) the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its conclusion.\n\nReferences\n• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983) (holding that a state supreme court can plainly state that its decision rests on adequate and independent state-law grounds).\n\n• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 42 (2019) (explaining the adequate and independent state ground doctrine).","educationalObjective":"The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment that rests on an adequate and independent state ground—ie, (1) a state ground fully resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and (2) the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its conclusion.","references":"• Michigan v. Long, 463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983) (holding that a state supreme court can plainly state that its decision rests on adequate and independent state-law grounds).\n\n• 4 Am. Jur. 2d Appellate Review § 42 (2019) (explaining the adequate and independent state ground doctrine).","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment that rests on an adequate and independent state ground—ie, (1) a state ground fully resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and (2) the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its conclusion.","irac":{"issue":"Supreme Court likely to review the state supreme court judgment?","rule":"The U.S. Supreme Court cannot review a state court judgment that rests on an adequate and independent state ground—ie, (1) a state ground fully resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case and (2) the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its conclusion.","analysis":"Adequate & independent state ground\n\n(precludes SCOTUS review)\n\nAdequate State ground fully resolves matter & application of federal law would not affect outcome\n\nIndependent State court did not rely on federal law to reach conclusion\n\nSCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States\n\nThe U.S. Supreme Court (SCOTUS) may exercise its appellate jurisdiction to review a judgment of the highest court of a state if that judgment turned on federal law. But SCOTUS may not review a state court judgment that rests on an adequate and independent state ground—ie:\n• a state ground that fully resolves the matter such that the application of federal law would not affect the outcome of the case (adequate) and\n• the state court did not rely on federal law to reach its conclusion (independent). Here, the state supreme court indicated that the state law very likely violates the U.S. Constitution. But the court also stated that it need not decide that federal issue because the law assuredly violates the state constitution. Since the state ground fully resolves the matter and the court did not rely on federal law to interpret the state constitution, SCOTUS is unlikely to review the state court judgment (Choice D). (Choice A) The First Amendment freedom of the press generally bars liability for publicizing truthful information. But this does not provide a basis for SCOTUS to decline to review a case. Instead, review is improper here because the state court decision was based on an adequate and independent state ground. (Choice C) The Article VI supremacy clause establishes that the U.S. Constitution and federal laws trump conflicting state laws. Therefore, states cannot restrict rights conferred by the U.S. Constitution. However, states are always free to create greater rights and protections than the U.S. Constitution does.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the judgment of the state supreme court rests upon an adequate and independent state-law ground."},"questionZh":"州法律规定对公布强奸受害者姓名的任何人给予损害赔偿。根据该法律，一名妇女在州法院起诉当地一家报纸，因为该报将她列为强奸受害者。州初审法院和上诉法院驳回了这一主张，认为该州法律根据州宪法和美国宪法第一修正案均无效。州最高法院确认并明确表示：“我们认为这项善意的法律很可能违反了联邦宪法第一修正案。但是，我们不需要就这个问题做出裁决，因为该法律肯定违反了我们的州宪法，而州宪法为媒体报道新闻的权利提供了更大的保护。”该女子向美国最高法院请求复审。美国最高法院是否有可能复审州最高法院的判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为第一修正案禁止对发布真实信息承担责任。","B":"不，因为州最高法院的判决有充分且独立的州法律依据。","C":"是的，因为至上条款不允许一个州创造比联邦宪法赋予的权利更大的权利。","D":"是的，因为美国最高法院的上诉管辖权延伸至联邦法律下产生的案件。"},"explanationZh":"充足且独立的国家基础\n\n（排除 SCOTUS 审查）\n\n充分的州理由完全解决问题，联邦法律的适用不会影响结果\n\n独立州法院不依赖联邦法律得出结论\n\nSCOTUS = 美国最高法院\n\n如果州最高法院的判决涉及联邦法律，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 可以行使其上诉管辖权来复审该判决。但最高法院可能不会审查基于充分且独立的州理由的州法院判决，即：\n• 完全解决问题的州理由，使得联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果（充分）以及\n• 州法院不依赖联邦法律得出结论（独立）。在此，州最高法院表示该州法律很可能违反美国宪法。但法院还表示，它不需要就联邦问题做出裁决，因为该法律肯定违反了州宪法。由于州理由完全解决了该问题，并且法院不依赖联邦法律来解释州宪法，因此 SCOTUS 不太可能审查州法院的判决（选项 D）。 （选项 A） 第一修正案的新闻自由一般禁止公开真实信息的责任。但这并不能为最高法院拒绝审查案件提供依据。相反，此处的审查是不适当的，因为州法院的判决是基于充分且独立的州理由。 （选项 C）第六条至上条款规定，美国宪法和联邦法律优先于相互冲突的州法律。因此，各州不能限制美国宪法赋予的权利。然而，各州始终可以自由地创造比美国宪法更大的权利和保护。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院不能审查基于充分且独立的州理由的州法院判决，即（1）州理由完全解决了问题，使得联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，以及（2）州法院不依赖联邦法律得出结论。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983)（认为州最高法院可以明确声明其判决基于充分且独立的州法律依据）。\n\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 42 (2019)（解释充分且独立的国家依据原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"美国最高法院不能审查基于充分且独立的州理由的州法院判决，即（1）州理由完全解决了问题，使得联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，以及（2）州法院没有依赖联邦法律得出结论。","iracZh":{"issue":"最高法院可能会审查州最高法院的判决？","rule":"美国最高法院不能审查基于充分且独立的州理由的州法院判决，即（1）州理由完全解决了问题，使得联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，以及（2）州法院没有依赖联邦法律得出结论。","analysis":"充足且独立的国家基础\n\n（排除 SCOTUS 审查）\n\n充分的州理由完全解决问题，联邦法律的适用不会影响结果\n\n独立州法院不依赖联邦法律得出结论\n\nSCOTUS = 美国最高法院\n\n如果州最高法院的判决涉及联邦法律，美国最高法院 (SCOTUS) 可以行使其上诉管辖权来复审该判决。但最高法院可能不会审查基于充分且独立的州理由的州法院判决，即：\n• 完全解决问题的州理由，使得联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果（充分）以及\n• 州法院不依赖联邦法律得出结论（独立）。在此，州最高法院表示该州法律很可能违反美国宪法。但法院还表示，它不需要就联邦问题做出裁决，因为该法律肯定违反了州宪法。由于州理由完全解决了该问题，并且法院不依赖联邦法律来解释州宪法，因此 SCOTUS 不太可能审查州法院的判决（选项 D）。 （选项 A） 第一修正案的新闻自由一般禁止公开真实信息的责任。但这并不能为最高法院拒绝审查案件提供依据。相反，此处的审查是不适当的，因为州法院的判决是基于充分且独立的州理由。 （选项 C）第六条至上条款规定，美国宪法和联邦法律优先于相互冲突的州法律。因此，各州不能限制美国宪法赋予的权利。然而，各州始终可以自由地创造比美国宪法更大的权利和保护。\n\n教育目标：美国最高法院不能审查基于充分且独立的州理由的州法院判决，即（1）州理由完全解决了问题，使得联邦法律的适用不会影响案件的结果，以及（2）州法院不依赖联邦法律得出结论。\n\n参考文献\n• 密歇根诉朗 (Michigan v. Long)，463 U.S. 1032, 1041 (1983)（认为州最高法院可以明确声明其判决基于充分且独立的州法律依据）。\n\n• 凌晨 4 点。尤尔。 2d 上诉审查 § 42 (2019)（解释充分且独立的国家依据原则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为州最高法院的判决有充分且独立的州法律依据。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":113,"sourceQuestionPage":338,"sourceQuestionPages":[338],"sourceExplanationPages":[339,340],"sourceAnswerPages":[339,340],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-114","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federal Judicial Power","question":"City police officers shot and killed a woman's friend as he attempted to escape arrest for an armed robbery he had committed. The woman brought suit in federal district court against the city police department and the police officers involved, seeking only a judgment declaring unconstitutional the state statute under which the police acted. That newly enacted statute authorized the police to use deadly force when necessary to apprehend a person who has committed a felony. In her suit, the woman alleged that the police would not have killed her friend if the use of deadly force had not been authorized by the statute. The defendants filed a motion to dismiss the woman's suit. How should the federal district court rule on this motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the suit raises a substantial federal question.","B":"Grant the motion, because the Eleventh Amendment prohibits federal courts from deciding cases of this type.","C":"Grant the motion, because the suit does not present a case or controversy.","D":"Grant the motion, because the suit involves a nonjusticiable political question."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Justiciability\n\n(suitability for judicial resolution)\n\nCase or controversy Prohibits advisory opinions under three doctrines:\n\n• Standing – plaintiff must allege injury-in-fact caused by challenged action & redressable by remedy sought\n• Ripeness – claim must present actual controversy involving past injury or present threat of injury\n• Mootness – claim must be unresolved Adequate & SCOTUS cannot review final state court judgment that rests on independent state adequate & independent state grounds grounds\n\nPolitical question Court cannot hear case if issue presented:\n\n• is reserved for executive/legislative branches or\n• lacks judicially manageable standards for resolution SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nArticle III restricts federal judicial power to actual cases or controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. As a result, federal courts can only issue a declaratory judgment if the dispute presents a real and immediate danger to a party's interests. Otherwise, the dispute would not satisfy Article III's case-or-controversy requirement since a judgment would amount to an impermissible advisory opinion—ie, a judicial opinion that determines the validity of a law without resolving an actual dispute. Here, the woman sought a declaratory judgment that the state statute under which the police had killed her friend was unconstitutional. But the statute does not present a real and immediate danger to the woman since there is no indication that it poses any present or future harm to her. This means that the woman does not have any interest at stake to satisfy Article III's case-or-controversy requirement. Therefore, the federal court should grant the defendants' motion to dismiss the suit to avoid rendering an advisory opinion. (Choice A) The woman's suit raises a federal question because she is challenging the constitutionality of a statute. However, the court must still dismiss her claim because no case or controversy exists. (Choice B) The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties (and foreign governments) from suing states in federal court. But it does not apply to suits against local governments (eg, the city) or their affiliates (eg, the police department and its officers).\n\n(Choice D) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from adjudicating issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branches or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since federal courts regularly determine the constitutionality of state statutes, this doctrine does not apply here.\n\nEducational objective: Article III's case-or-controversy requirement prohibits federal courts from issuing declaratory judgments unless the dispute presents a real and immediate danger to a party's interests. Otherwise, the judgment would amount to an impermissible advisory opinion.\n\nReferences\n• Ashcroft v. Mattis, 431 U.S. 171, 172–73 (1977) (holding that a father's emotional harm caused by the police shooting his son did not present an injury that would allow him to seek a declaratory judgment).","educationalObjective":"Article III's case-or-controversy requirement prohibits federal courts from issuing declaratory judgments unless the dispute presents a real and immediate danger to a party's interests. Otherwise, the judgment would amount to an impermissible advisory opinion.","references":"• Ashcroft v. Mattis, 431 U.S. 171, 172–73 (1977) (holding that a father's emotional harm caused by the police shooting his son did not present an injury that would allow him to seek a declaratory judgment).","rule":"Article III's case-or-controversy requirement prohibits federal courts from issuing declaratory judgments unless the dispute presents a real and immediate danger to a party's interests. Otherwise, the judgment would amount to an impermissible advisory opinion.","irac":{"issue":"How should the federal district court rule on this motion?","rule":"Article III's case-or-controversy requirement prohibits federal courts from issuing declaratory judgments unless the dispute presents a real and immediate danger to a party's interests. Otherwise, the judgment would amount to an impermissible advisory opinion.","analysis":"Justiciability\n\n(suitability for judicial resolution)\n\nCase or controversy Prohibits advisory opinions under three doctrines:\n\n• Standing – plaintiff must allege injury-in-fact caused by challenged action & redressable by remedy sought\n• Ripeness – claim must present actual controversy involving past injury or present threat of injury\n• Mootness – claim must be unresolved Adequate & SCOTUS cannot review final state court judgment that rests on independent state adequate & independent state grounds grounds\n\nPolitical question Court cannot hear case if issue presented:\n\n• is reserved for executive/legislative branches or\n• lacks judicially manageable standards for resolution SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nArticle III restricts federal judicial power to actual cases or controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. As a result, federal courts can only issue a declaratory judgment if the dispute presents a real and immediate danger to a party's interests. Otherwise, the dispute would not satisfy Article III's case-or-controversy requirement since a judgment would amount to an impermissible advisory opinion—ie, a judicial opinion that determines the validity of a law without resolving an actual dispute. Here, the woman sought a declaratory judgment that the state statute under which the police had killed her friend was unconstitutional. But the statute does not present a real and immediate danger to the woman since there is no indication that it poses any present or future harm to her. This means that the woman does not have any interest at stake to satisfy Article III's case-or-controversy requirement. Therefore, the federal court should grant the defendants' motion to dismiss the suit to avoid rendering an advisory opinion. (Choice A) The woman's suit raises a federal question because she is challenging the constitutionality of a statute. However, the court must still dismiss her claim because no case or controversy exists. (Choice B) The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties (and foreign governments) from suing states in federal court. But it does not apply to suits against local governments (eg, the city) or their affiliates (eg, the police department and its officers).\n\n(Choice D) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from adjudicating issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branches or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since federal courts regularly determine the constitutionality of state statutes, this doctrine does not apply here.","conclusion":"Answer C: Grant the motion, because the suit does not present a case or controversy."},"questionZh":"一名妇女的朋友因武装抢劫而试图逃脱逮捕，市警察开枪打死了他。该女子向联邦地区法院起诉市警察局和涉案警察，只寻求一项判决，宣布警察行事所依据的州法规违宪。新颁布的法规授权警方在必要时使用致命武力逮捕犯有重罪的人。该女子在诉讼中声称，如果没有法律授权使用致命武力，警察就不会杀死她的朋友。被告提出动议驳回该女子的诉讼。联邦地区法院该如何对此动议作出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"拒绝该动议，因为该诉讼提出了重大的联邦问题。","B":"批准该动议，因为第十一修正案禁止联邦法院裁决此类案件。","C":"批准该动议，因为该诉讼没有提出案例或争议。","D":"批准该动议，因为该诉讼涉及不可审理的政治问题。"},"explanationZh":"可审理性\n\n（是否适合司法解决）\n\n案例或争议 禁止根据以下三种原则提供咨询意见：\n\n• 诉讼资格——原告必须声称受到质疑的行为造成了事实上的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以纠正\n• 成熟度——索赔必须提出涉及过去受伤或当前受伤威胁的实际争议\n• 没有实际意义 – 索赔必须未得到解决 充分且 SCOTUS 无法审查基于独立州充分和独立州理由的最终州法院判决\n\n政治问题 如果提出以下问题，法院将无法审理案件：\n\n• 保留给行政/立法部门或\n• 缺乏司法上可管理的决议标准 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n第三条将联邦司法权限制在实际案件或争议上，即能够由司法解决的争议。因此，只有当争议对一方利益构成真正且直接的威胁时，联邦法院才能发布宣告性判决。否则，争议将不满足第三条的案件或争议要求，因为判决将相当于不被允许的咨询意见，即在不解决实际争议的情况下确定法律有效性的司法意见。在这里，该妇女寻求一项宣告性判决，即警察杀害她朋友所依据的州法律违宪。但该法规并未对这名妇女构成真正和直接的危险，因为没有迹象表明该法规对她造成任何当前或未来的伤害。这意味着该妇女没有任何利益关系来满足第三条的案件或争议要求。因此，联邦法院应该批准被告驳回诉讼的动议，以避免发表咨询意见。 （选项 A） 该妇女的诉讼提出了联邦问题，因为她正在挑战一项法规的合宪性。然而，法院仍必须驳回她的诉讼请求，因为不存在任何案件或争议。 （选项 B）第十一修正案禁止私人团体（和外国政府）在联邦法院起诉各州。但它不适用于针对地方政府（例如市）或其附属机构（例如警察部门及其官员）的诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）政治问题原则阻止联邦法院裁决以下问题：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于联邦法院定期确定州法规的合宪性，因此该原则不适用于此处。\n\n教育目标：第三条的案件或争议要求禁止联邦法院发布宣告性判决，除非争议对一方利益构成真正和直接的威胁。否则，该判决将构成不被允许的咨询意见。\n\n参考文献\n• Ashcroft v. Mattis, 431 U.S. 171, 172–73 (1977)（认为警察开枪射杀儿子对父亲造成的情感伤害并不构成允许他寻求宣告性判决的伤害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第三条的案件或争议要求禁止联邦法院发布宣告性判决，除非争议对一方利益构成真正和直接的威胁。否则，该判决将构成不被允许的咨询意见。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦地区法院该如何对此动议作出裁决？","rule":"第三条的案件或争议要求禁止联邦法院发布宣告性判决，除非争议对一方利益构成真正和直接的威胁。否则，该判决将构成不被允许的咨询意见。","analysis":"可审理性\n\n（是否适合司法解决）\n\n案例或争议 禁止根据以下三种原则提供咨询意见：\n\n• 诉讼资格——原告必须声称受到质疑的行为造成了事实上的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以纠正\n• 成熟度——索赔必须提出涉及过去受伤或当前受伤威胁的实际争议\n• 没有实际意义 – 索赔必须未得到解决 充分且 SCOTUS 无法审查基于独立州充分和独立州理由的最终州法院判决\n\n政治问题 如果提出以下问题，法院将无法审理案件：\n\n• 保留给行政/立法部门或\n• 缺乏司法上可管理的决议标准 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n第三条将联邦司法权限制在实际案件或争议上，即能够由司法解决的争议。因此，只有当争议对一方利益构成真正且直接的威胁时，联邦法院才能发布宣告性判决。否则，争议将不满足第三条的案件或争议要求，因为判决将相当于不被允许的咨询意见，即在不解决实际争议的情况下确定法律有效性的司法意见。在这里，该妇女寻求一项宣告性判决，即警察杀害她朋友所依据的州法律违宪。但该法规并未对这名妇女构成真正和直接的危险，因为没有迹象表明该法规对她造成任何当前或未来的伤害。这意味着该妇女没有任何利益关系来满足第三条的案件或争议要求。因此，联邦法院应该批准被告驳回诉讼的动议，以避免发表咨询意见。 （选项 A） 该妇女的诉讼提出了联邦问题，因为她正在挑战一项法规的合宪性。然而，法院仍必须驳回她的诉讼请求，因为不存在任何案件或争议。 （选项 B）第十一修正案禁止私人团体（和外国政府）在联邦法院起诉各州。但它不适用于针对地方政府（例如市）或其附属机构（例如警察部门及其官员）的诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）政治问题原则阻止联邦法院裁决以下问题：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于联邦法院定期确定州法规的合宪性，因此该原则不适用于此处。\n\n教育目标：第三条的案件或争议要求禁止联邦法院发布宣告性判决，除非争议对一方利益构成真正和直接的威胁。否则，该判决将构成不被允许的咨询意见。\n\n参考文献\n• Ashcroft v. Mattis, 431 U.S. 171, 172–73 (1977)（认为警察开枪射杀儿子对父亲造成的情感伤害并不构成允许他寻求宣告性判决的伤害）。","conclusion":"答案 C：批准该动议，因为该诉讼没有提出案例或争议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"felony","ipa":"/fˈɛləni/","syllables":"felony","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 重罪；[法] 重罪, 重刑罪","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 重罪；[法] 重罪, 重刑罪","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:felonies","sourceSentence":"That newly enacted statute authorized the police to use deadly force when necessary to apprehend a person who has committed a felony.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":114,"sourceQuestionPage":341,"sourceQuestionPages":[341],"sourceExplanationPages":[342,343],"sourceAnswerPages":[342,343],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-115","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"Congress provides funds to states that can be used to reimburse medical providers for procedures they perform for low-income individuals. A state that participates in this federal program enacted a statute that prohibits reimbursing providers for the cost of performing vasectomies for men. A doctor who routinely performs vasectomies for low-income men has submitted numerous requests for reimbursement pursuant to the program. However, the commissioner of the state agency administering the program has repeatedly denied the doctor's requests pursuant to the state statute. The doctor sued the commissioner in a federal district court, seeking an injunction against the enforcement of the statute on the ground that it violates men's right to privacy. Assuming that this federal district court has previously held that men have a privacy right to obtain vasectomies, how should the district court proceed with the doctor's suit?","choices":{"A":"Determine the merits of the suit, because federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over federal-law claims.","B":"Determine the merits of the suit, because the doctor has standing.","C":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the suit and dismiss it, because the Eleventh Amendment bars this type of action in federal court.","D":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the suit and stay the suit until a man seeking a vasectomy joins the suit."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on another's behalf)\n\nOrganization Organization has standing if: members\n• members have standing to sue on their own behalf\n• their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND\n• lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when:\n\n• parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND\n• obstacle prevents third party from suing (eg, privacy/retaliation concern) Child Parent has standing unless parental rights have been limited by court OR lawsuit may adversely affect child\n\nAssignor Assignee has standing if assigned legal claim for ordinary & good faith business purpose\n\nIn federal litigation, parties are generally limited to asserting their own rights and not the rights of a third party. That is because the third party will likely be the most effective advocate of his/her own interests. However, the third-party standing doctrine permits an injured plaintiff to assert the rights of a third party when:\n• the plaintiff and the third party share an inextricably close relationship and\n• genuine obstacles prevent the third party from asserting his/her rights. Here, enforcement of the statute harmed the doctor since he was denied reimbursement for performing vasectomies for men. Doctors share an inextricably close relationship with their patients. Additionally, the men's low-income status, fear of retaliation, and privacy concerns pose genuine obstacles that may prevent the men from challenging vasectomy restrictions. Therefore, the doctor has standing to assert that the statute violates men's right to privacy, and the court should hear the merits of the doctor's suit. (Choice A) Federal and state courts have concurrent jurisdiction over federal-law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars federal courts from adjudicating suits against a state government for monetary relief—not suits against a state official for injunctive relief (as seen here). (Choice D) A federal court can stay (ie, suspend) a suit to force a party to comply with a court order or protect a party's rights. But here, the federal court need not stay the action until a man seeking a vasectomy joins the suit because the doctor has third-party standing.\n\nEducational objective: Under the third-party standing doctrine, an injured plaintiff may assert a third party's rights when (1) the parties share an inextricably close relationship and (2) genuine obstacles prevent the third party from asserting his/her rights.\n\nReferences\n• Singleton v. Wulff, 428 U.S. 106, 118 (1976) (concluding that \"it generally is appropriate to allow a physician to assert the rights of women patients as against governmental interference\").\n\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• 16 Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 145 (2020) (explaining third-party standing requirements).","educationalObjective":"Under the third-party standing doctrine, an injured plaintiff may assert a third party's rights when (1) the parties share an inextricably close relationship and (2) genuine obstacles prevent the third party from asserting his/her rights.","references":"• Singleton v. Wulff, 428 U.S. 106, 118 (1976) (concluding that \"it generally is appropriate to allow a physician to assert the rights of women patients as against governmental interference\").\n\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• 16 Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 145 (2020) (explaining third-party standing requirements).","rule":"Under the third-party standing doctrine, an injured plaintiff may assert a third party's rights when (1) the parties share an inextricably close relationship and (2) genuine obstacles prevent the third party from asserting his/her rights.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that this federal district court has previously held that men have a privacy right to obtain vasectomies, how should the district court proceed with the doctor's suit?","rule":"Under the third-party standing doctrine, an injured plaintiff may assert a third party's rights when (1) the parties share an inextricably close relationship and (2) genuine obstacles prevent the third party from asserting his/her rights.","analysis":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on another's behalf)\n\nOrganization Organization has standing if: members\n• members have standing to sue on their own behalf\n• their injuries relate to organization's purpose AND\n• lawsuit does not require member participation (if damages sought) Third party Injured plaintiff has standing when:\n\n• parties share inextricably close relationship (eg, doctor/patient) AND\n• obstacle prevents third party from suing (eg, privacy/retaliation concern) Child Parent has standing unless parental rights have been limited by court OR lawsuit may adversely affect child\n\nAssignor Assignee has standing if assigned legal claim for ordinary & good faith business purpose\n\nIn federal litigation, parties are generally limited to asserting their own rights and not the rights of a third party. That is because the third party will likely be the most effective advocate of his/her own interests. However, the third-party standing doctrine permits an injured plaintiff to assert the rights of a third party when:\n• the plaintiff and the third party share an inextricably close relationship and\n• genuine obstacles prevent the third party from asserting his/her rights. Here, enforcement of the statute harmed the doctor since he was denied reimbursement for performing vasectomies for men. Doctors share an inextricably close relationship with their patients. Additionally, the men's low-income status, fear of retaliation, and privacy concerns pose genuine obstacles that may prevent the men from challenging vasectomy restrictions. Therefore, the doctor has standing to assert that the statute violates men's right to privacy, and the court should hear the merits of the doctor's suit. (Choice A) Federal and state courts have concurrent jurisdiction over federal-law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment generally bars federal courts from adjudicating suits against a state government for monetary relief—not suits against a state official for injunctive relief (as seen here). (Choice D) A federal court can stay (ie, suspend) a suit to force a party to comply with a court order or protect a party's rights. But here, the federal court need not stay the action until a man seeking a vasectomy joins the suit because the doctor has third-party standing.","conclusion":"Answer B: Determine the merits of the suit, because the doctor has standing."},"questionZh":"国会向各州提供资金，可用于补偿医疗服务提供者为低收入个人执行的手术。参与该联邦计划的一个州颁布了一项法规，禁止向提供者报销男性输精管结扎术的费用。一位经常为低收入男性进行输精管结扎术的医生根据该计划提交了许多报销请求。然而，管理该计划的国家机构专员根据州法规多次拒绝了医生的请求。该医生在联邦地方法院起诉专员，要求颁布禁止执行该法规的禁令，理由是该法规侵犯了男性的隐私权。假设该联邦地区法院此前裁定男性拥有获得输精管结扎术的隐私权，那么地区法院应如何审理医生的诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"确定诉讼的是非曲直，因为联邦法院对联邦法律索赔拥有专属管辖权。","B":"确定诉讼的是非曲直，因为医生有地位。","C":"拒绝裁定诉讼的是非曲直并将其驳回，因为第十一修正案禁止在联邦法院采取此类行动。","D":"拒绝裁定诉讼的是非曲直，并保留诉讼，直到寻求输精管结扎术的男性加入诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"第三条 地位\n\n（代表他人提起诉讼）\n\n组织 组织具有地位，如果： 成员\n• 会员有资格代表自己提起诉讼\n• 他们的伤害与组织的目的有关并且\n• 诉讼不需要会员参与（如果寻求损害赔偿） 第三方受伤原告在以下情况下具有诉讼资格：\n\n• 各方有着密不可分的密切关系（例如医生/患者）并且\n• 障碍阻止第三方起诉（例如，隐私/报复问题） 儿童 父母有资格，除非父母权利受到法院限制或诉讼可能对儿童产生不利影响\n\n如果转让人出于普通和善意商业目的而提出法律索赔，则受让人享有资格\n\n在联邦诉讼中，当事人通常仅限于维护自己的权利，而不是第三方的权利。这是因为第三方可能是其自身利益的最有效倡导者。然而，第三方地位原则允许受害原告在以下情况下主张第三方的权利：\n• 原告和第三方有着密不可分的密切关系，并且\n• 真正的障碍阻止第三方主张他/她的权利。在这里，该法规的执行伤害了医生，因为他因为男性进行输精管结扎术而被拒绝报销。医生与患者有着密不可分的密切关系。此外，这些男性的低收入状况、对报复的恐惧以及隐私问题构成了真正的障碍，可能会阻止这些男性挑战输精管结扎术的限制。因此，医生有资格主张该法规侵犯了男性的隐私权，法院应该听取医生诉讼的实质内容。 （选择 A）联邦法院和州法院对联邦法律主张具有并行管辖权，除非宪法或国会明确规定联邦法院具有专属管辖权（此处未示出）。\n\n（选项 C）第十一修正案一般禁止联邦法院裁决针对州政府的金钱救济诉讼，而不是针对州官员的禁令救济诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选择D）联邦法院可以中止（即中止）诉讼，以迫使一方遵守法院命令或保护一方的权利。但在这里，联邦法院不需要中止诉讼，直到寻求输精管结扎术的男性加入诉讼，因为该医生具有第三方地位。\n\n教育目标：根据第三方地位原则，在以下情况下，受害原告可以主张第三方的权利：(1) 双方有着密不可分的密切关系；(2) 存在真正的障碍阻止第三方主张其权利。\n\n参考文献\n• Singleton v. Wulff, 428 U.S. 106, 118 (1976)（结论是“允许医生维护女性患者的权利以反对政府干预通常是适当的”）。\n\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• 上午 16 点。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 145 条 (2020)（解释第三方资格要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据第三方地位原则，受害原告可以在下列情况下主张第三方的权利：(1) 双方有着密不可分的密切关系；(2) 存在真正的障碍阻止第三方主张其权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设该联邦地区法院此前裁定男性拥有获得输精管结扎术的隐私权，那么地区法院应如何审理医生的诉讼？","rule":"根据第三方地位原则，受害原告可以在下列情况下主张第三方的权利：(1) 双方有着密不可分的密切关系；(2) 存在真正的障碍阻止第三方主张其权利。","analysis":"第三条 地位\n\n（代表他人提起诉讼）\n\n组织 组织具有地位，如果： 成员\n• 会员有资格代表自己提起诉讼\n• 他们的伤害与组织的目的有关并且\n• 诉讼不需要会员参与（如果寻求损害赔偿） 第三方受伤原告在以下情况下具有诉讼资格：\n\n• 各方有着密不可分的密切关系（例如医生/患者）并且\n• 障碍阻止第三方起诉（例如，隐私/报复问题） 儿童 父母有资格，除非父母权利受到法院限制或诉讼可能对儿童产生不利影响\n\n如果转让人出于普通和善意商业目的而提出法律索赔，则受让人享有资格\n\n在联邦诉讼中，当事人通常仅限于维护自己的权利，而不是第三方的权利。这是因为第三方可能是其自身利益的最有效倡导者。然而，第三方地位原则允许受害原告在以下情况下主张第三方的权利：\n• 原告和第三方有着密不可分的密切关系，并且\n• 真正的障碍阻止第三方主张他/她的权利。在这里，该法规的执行伤害了医生，因为他因为男性进行输精管结扎术而被拒绝报销。医生与患者有着密不可分的密切关系。此外，这些男性的低收入状况、对报复的恐惧以及隐私问题构成了真正的障碍，可能会阻止这些男性挑战输精管结扎术的限制。因此，医生有资格主张该法规侵犯了男性的隐私权，法院应该听取医生诉讼的实质内容。 （选择 A）联邦法院和州法院对联邦法律主张具有并行管辖权，除非宪法或国会明确规定联邦法院具有专属管辖权（此处未示出）。\n\n（选项 C）第十一修正案一般禁止联邦法院裁决针对州政府的金钱救济诉讼，而不是针对州官员的禁令救济诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选择D）联邦法院可以中止（即中止）诉讼，以迫使一方遵守法院命令或保护一方的权利。但在这里，联邦法院不需要中止诉讼，直到寻求输精管结扎术的男性加入诉讼，因为该医生具有第三方地位。\n\n教育目标：根据第三方地位原则，在以下情况下，受害原告可以主张第三方的权利：(1) 双方有着密不可分的密切关系；(2) 存在真正的障碍阻止第三方主张其权利。\n\n参考文献\n• Singleton v. Wulff, 428 U.S. 106, 118 (1976)（结论是“允许医生维护女性患者的权利以反对政府干预通常是适当的”）。\n\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• 上午 16 点。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 145 条 (2020)（解释第三方资格要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：确定诉讼的是非曲直，因为医生有地位。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"advocate","ipa":"/ˈædvəkət/","syllables":"ad·vo·cate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 提倡者, 拥护者；vt. 主张, 提倡","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 提倡者, 拥护者；vt. 主张, 提倡","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:advocated/s:advocates/i:advocating/p:advocated/3:advocates","sourceSentence":"That is because the third party will likely be the most effective advocate of his/her own interests.","synonyms":["advocator","proponent","exponent","counsel"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":115,"sourceQuestionPage":344,"sourceQuestionPages":[344],"sourceExplanationPages":[345,346],"sourceAnswerPages":[345,346],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-116","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"A city ordinance requires that its mayor must have been continuously a resident of the city for at least five years at the time he or she takes office. The plaintiff, who is thinking about running for mayor in an election that will take place next year, will have been a resident of the city for only four and one-half years at the time the elected mayor would take office. Before he decides to run for mayor, the plaintiff wants to know whether he could lawfully assume that position if he were elected. As a result, the plaintiff files suit in the local federal district court for a declaratory judgment that the city's five-year-residence requirement is unconstitutional and that he is entitled to a place on his political party's primary election ballot for mayor. He names the chairman of his political party as the sole defendant and does not join any election officials. The chairman responds by joining the plaintiff in requesting the court to declare the city's residence requirement invalid. What should the court do in this case?","choices":{"A":"Issue the declaratory judgment, because a residency requirement of this type is a denial of the equal protection of the laws.","B":"Issue the declaratory judgment, because the plaintiff will have substantially complied with the residency requirement.","C":"Refuse to determine the merits of this suit, because there is no case or controversy.","D":"Refuse to issue the declaratory judgment, because an issue of this kind involving only a local election does not present a substantial federal constitutional question."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Article III of the Constitution limits federal judicial power to actual cases or controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. This case-or- controversy requirement is not met unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. To have standing, the plaintiff must allege three elements:\n• Injury-in-fact – actual or imminent harm that is concrete and particularized\n• Causation – the injury is fairly traceable to the defendant's challenged conduct\n• Redressability – a favorable judicial decision will likely remedy or prevent the alleged injury Here, the plaintiff sued to have the city's residency requirement invalidated. But he has not suffered actual or imminent harm since he has merely thought about running for mayor and any injury to him is purely hypothetical (no injury-in-fact). And even if the plaintiff could allege such harm, he still lacks standing because that injury would not be fairly traceable to the chairman of his political party (no causation). Therefore, there is no actual case or controversy, and the court should refuse to determine the merits of this suit. (Choice A) The residency requirement triggers the equal protection clause since it discriminates against persons who have lived in the city for less than five years. But this does not create an actual case or controversy since the plaintiff still lacks standing. (Choice B) The fact that the plaintiff will have \"substantially complied\" with the residency requirement if and when he runs for mayor is no basis for the court to declare the requirement invalid. (Choice D) The plaintiff's suit does present a substantial federal constitutional question since it challenges the constitutionality of the city's residency requirement. As a result, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction to hear this suit. But it cannot adjudicate the suit because there is no case or controversy.\n\nEducational objective: Article III's case-or-controversy requirement is not met unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and is redressable by the courts.\n\nReferences\n• Lujan v. Defs. of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992) (holding that standing requires proof of an injury-in-fact, causation, and redressability).","educationalObjective":"Article III's case-or-controversy requirement is not met unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and is redressable by the courts.","references":"• Lujan v. Defs. of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992) (holding that standing requires proof of an injury-in-fact, causation, and redressability).","rule":"Article III's case-or-controversy requirement is not met unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and is redressable by the courts.","irac":{"issue":"What should the court do in this case?","rule":"Article III's case-or-controversy requirement is not met unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant's challenged conduct and is redressable by the courts.","analysis":"Article III of the Constitution limits federal judicial power to actual cases or controversies—ie, disputes that are capable of judicial resolution. This case-or- controversy requirement is not met unless the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. To have standing, the plaintiff must allege three elements:\n• Injury-in-fact – actual or imminent harm that is concrete and particularized\n• Causation – the injury is fairly traceable to the defendant's challenged conduct\n• Redressability – a favorable judicial decision will likely remedy or prevent the alleged injury Here, the plaintiff sued to have the city's residency requirement invalidated. But he has not suffered actual or imminent harm since he has merely thought about running for mayor and any injury to him is purely hypothetical (no injury-in-fact). And even if the plaintiff could allege such harm, he still lacks standing because that injury would not be fairly traceable to the chairman of his political party (no causation). Therefore, there is no actual case or controversy, and the court should refuse to determine the merits of this suit. (Choice A) The residency requirement triggers the equal protection clause since it discriminates against persons who have lived in the city for less than five years. But this does not create an actual case or controversy since the plaintiff still lacks standing. (Choice B) The fact that the plaintiff will have \"substantially complied\" with the residency requirement if and when he runs for mayor is no basis for the court to declare the requirement invalid. (Choice D) The plaintiff's suit does present a substantial federal constitutional question since it challenges the constitutionality of the city's residency requirement. As a result, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction to hear this suit. But it cannot adjudicate the suit because there is no case or controversy.","conclusion":"Answer C: Refuse to determine the merits of this suit, because there is no case or controversy."},"questionZh":"城市法令要求市长在就职时必须连续居住该城市至少五年。原告正在考虑在明年举行的选举中竞选市长，在当选市长就职时，他在该市居住的时间只有四年半。在决定竞选市长之前，原告想知道如果当选，他是否可以合法担任该职位。因此，原告向当地联邦地区法院提起诉讼，要求作出宣告性判决，判定该市五年居住要求违宪，并有权在其政党的市长初选选票中获得一席之地。他将其政党的主席列为唯一被告，并且没有加入任何选举官员的行列。作为回应，主席与原告一起请求法院宣布该市的居住要求无效。对于这种情况，法院应该怎么做呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"发布宣告性判决，因为这种类型的居住要求是对法律平等保护的否定。","B":"发布宣告性判决，因为原告已基本遵守居住要求。","C":"拒绝确定该诉讼的是非曲直，因为没有案件或争议。","D":"拒绝发布宣告性判决，因为这种仅涉及地方选举的问题并不构成实质性的联邦宪法问题。"},"explanationZh":"宪法第三条将联邦司法权限制在实际案件或争议上，即能够由司法解决的争议。除非原告具有诉讼资格，即案件结果具有个人利益，否则就无法满足“案件或争议”的要求。为了获得诉讼资格，原告必须提出三个要素：\n• 事实伤害——实际或迫在眉睫的具体且具体的伤害\n• 因果关系——伤害完全可以追溯到被告受到质疑的行为\n• 可赔偿性——有利的司法判决可能会补救或防止所指控的伤害 在此，原告提起诉讼，要求取消该市的居住要求。但他并没有遭受实际或迫在眉睫的伤害，因为他只是考虑竞选市长，对他的任何伤害纯粹是假设的（没有事实上的伤害）。即使原告可以指控这种伤害，他仍然缺乏地位，因为这种伤害无法完全追溯到他所在政党的主席（没有因果关系）。因此，不存在实际案件或争议，法院应拒绝认定本案的是非曲直。 （选项A）居住要求触发了平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它歧视在城市居住不满五年的人。但这并不会产生实际的案件或争议，因为原告仍然缺乏地位。 （选项B） 原告竞选市长时将“实质上遵守”居住要求这一事实并不能成为法院宣布该要求无效的依据。 （选项D） 原告的诉讼确实提出了重大的联邦宪法问题，因为它挑战了该市居住要求的合宪性。因此，法院拥有审理该诉讼的标的管辖权。但由于没有案件或争议，它无法裁决该诉讼。\n\n教育目标：除非原告具有诉讼资格，即因被告所质疑的行为而遭受了事实上的伤害并且可以由法院赔偿，否则不满足第三条的案件或争议要求。\n\n参考文献\n• Lujan 诉Defs。 of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992)（认为资格要求提供事实伤害、因果关系和可补救性的证据）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非原告具有诉讼资格，即因被告所质疑的行为而遭受了事实上的伤害并且可以由法院赔偿，否则不满足第三条的案件或争议要求。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于这种情况，法院应该怎么做呢？","rule":"除非原告具有诉讼资格，即因被告所质疑的行为而遭受了事实上的伤害并且可以由法院赔偿，否则不满足第三条的案件或争议要求。","analysis":"宪法第三条将联邦司法权限制在实际案件或争议上，即能够由司法解决的争议。除非原告具有诉讼资格，即案件结果具有个人利益，否则就无法满足“案件或争议”的要求。为了获得诉讼资格，原告必须提出三个要素：\n• 事实伤害——实际或迫在眉睫的具体且具体的伤害\n• 因果关系——伤害完全可以追溯到被告受到质疑的行为\n• 可赔偿性——有利的司法判决可能会补救或防止所指控的伤害 在此，原告提起诉讼，要求取消该市的居住要求。但他并没有遭受实际或迫在眉睫的伤害，因为他只是考虑竞选市长，对他的任何伤害纯粹是假设的（没有事实上的伤害）。即使原告可以指控这种伤害，他仍然缺乏地位，因为这种伤害无法完全追溯到他所在政党的主席（没有因果关系）。因此，不存在实际案件或争议，法院应拒绝认定本案的是非曲直。 （选项A）居住要求触发了平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它歧视在城市居住不满五年的人。但这并不会产生实际的案件或争议，因为原告仍然缺乏地位。 （选项B） 原告竞选市长时将“实质上遵守”居住要求这一事实并不能成为法院宣布该要求无效的依据。 （选项D） 原告的诉讼确实提出了重大的联邦宪法问题，因为它挑战了该市居住要求的合宪性。因此，法院拥有审理该诉讼的标的管辖权。但由于没有案件或争议，它无法裁决该诉讼。\n\n教育目标：除非原告具有诉讼资格，即因被告所质疑的行为而遭受了事实上的伤害并且可以由法院赔偿，否则不满足第三条的案件或争议要求。\n\n参考文献\n• Lujan 诉Defs。 of Wildlife, 504 U.S. 555, 560–61 (1992)（认为资格要求提供事实伤害、因果关系和可补救性的证据）。","conclusion":"答案 C：拒绝确定该诉讼的是非曲直，因为没有案件或争议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"residency","ipa":"/ɹˈɛzɪdənsi/","syllables":"res·i·dency","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 住处；[法] 住处, 管辖区","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 住处；[法] 住处, 管辖区","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:residencies","sourceSentence":"Issue the declaratory judgment, because a residency requirement of this type is a denial of the equal protection of the laws.","synonyms":["residence","abidance"]},{"term":"ballot","ipa":"/bˈælət/","syllables":"bal·lot","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 投票, 投票用纸, 抽签；vi. 投票, 抽签；vt. 投票选出, 拉选票","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 投票, 投票用纸, 抽签；vi. 投票, 抽签；vt. 投票选出, 拉选票","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:ballots/i:balloting/p:balloted/3:ballots/d:balloted","sourceSentence":"As a result, the plaintiff files suit in the local federal district court for a declaratory judgment that the city's five-year-residence requirement is unconstitutional and that he is entitled to a place on his political party's primary election ballot for mayor.","synonyms":["vote","voting","balloting"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-e4d2f96689bf3a25"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":116,"sourceQuestionPage":347,"sourceQuestionPages":[347],"sourceExplanationPages":[348,349],"sourceAnswerPages":[348,349],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-117","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of the Press","question":"The President issued an executive order banning the extraction of natural gas on federal land. An oil and gas company that had been drilling for natural gas on federal land sued the President in federal court and sought a preliminary injunction that would prevent the executive order from going into effect. Before the federal judge ruled on the injunction, an employee of the oil company left an envelope of cash on the judge's doorstep with a note that stated, \"Remember who your friends are.\" The next day, the judge issued a nationwide injunction preventing the executive order from taking effect. Several months later, a local newspaper broke the story about the envelope of cash, the note, and the judge's subsequent ruling. The judge has not been indicted for bribery, but the President has called for the judge's immediate removal. May the President constitutionally remove the federal judge?","choices":{"A":"No, because a federal judge can only be removed by impeachment.","B":"No, because the federal judge has not been indicted for bribery.","C":"Yes, because bribery is an impeachable offense.","D":"Yes, because the President's removal power is absolute."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The Constitution divides the federal government into three coequal branches that possess distinct powers (ie, separation of powers). The President has the power to appoint federal judges with the Senate's advice and consent. Federal judges have lifetime tenure during good behavior, which helps guarantee judicial independence. And Congress has the sole power to impeach and remove federal judges for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors, which helps ensure judicial integrity. Here, the President seeks the removal of the federal judge, who has been accused of bribery. Although bribery is an impeachable offense, only Congress has the power to remove a federal judge by impeachment (Choice C). Therefore, the President may not constitutionally remove the federal judge. (Choice B) The fact that the federal judge has not been indicted for bribery has no bearing on whether the President can remove the federal judge from office.* That is because the power to impeach and remove federal judges belongs to Congress—not the President. *An indictment is not required for a federal judge to be impeached and removed from office because the House of Representatives—not a grand jury—determines whether the judge's conduct constitutes an impeachable offense. (Choice D) The Constitution is silent about the President's removal of federal executive officers, but the U.S. Supreme Court has repeatedly held that the President has near- absolute authority to remove such officers. But though the President's removal power is broad, it does not include the power to remove federal judges.\n\nEducational objective: The President has the power to appoint federal judges with the Senate's advice and consent, but Congress has the sole power to impeach and remove federal judges for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2 (vesting the President with the power to appoint federal judges).\n\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 4 (stating that Congress may impeach and remove federal judges from office for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors).\n\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 1 (granting lifetime tenure to federal judges during good behavior).","educationalObjective":"The President has the power to appoint federal judges with the Senate's advice and consent, but Congress has the sole power to impeach and remove federal judges for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. II, § 2 (vesting the President with the power to appoint federal judges).\n\n• U.S. Const. art. II, § 4 (stating that Congress may impeach and remove federal judges from office for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors).\n\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 1 (granting lifetime tenure to federal judges during good behavior).","rule":"The President has the power to appoint federal judges with the Senate's advice and consent, but Congress has the sole power to impeach and remove federal judges for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors.","irac":{"issue":"May the President constitutionally remove the federal judge?","rule":"The President has the power to appoint federal judges with the Senate's advice and consent, but Congress has the sole power to impeach and remove federal judges for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors.","analysis":"The Constitution divides the federal government into three coequal branches that possess distinct powers (ie, separation of powers). The President has the power to appoint federal judges with the Senate's advice and consent. Federal judges have lifetime tenure during good behavior, which helps guarantee judicial independence. And Congress has the sole power to impeach and remove federal judges for treason, bribery, or other high crimes and misdemeanors, which helps ensure judicial integrity. Here, the President seeks the removal of the federal judge, who has been accused of bribery. Although bribery is an impeachable offense, only Congress has the power to remove a federal judge by impeachment (Choice C). Therefore, the President may not constitutionally remove the federal judge. (Choice B) The fact that the federal judge has not been indicted for bribery has no bearing on whether the President can remove the federal judge from office.* That is because the power to impeach and remove federal judges belongs to Congress—not the President. *An indictment is not required for a federal judge to be impeached and removed from office because the House of Representatives—not a grand jury—determines whether the judge's conduct constitutes an impeachable offense. (Choice D) The Constitution is silent about the President's removal of federal executive officers, but the U.S. Supreme Court has repeatedly held that the President has near- absolute authority to remove such officers. But though the President's removal power is broad, it does not include the power to remove federal judges.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because a federal judge can only be removed by impeachment."},"questionZh":"总统发布了一项行政命令，禁止在联邦土地上开采天然气。一家在联邦土地上开采天然气的石油和天然气公司在联邦法院起诉总统，并寻求初步禁令，以阻止该行政命令生效。在联邦法官对禁令做出裁决之前，石油公司的一名员工在法官家门口留下了一个装满现金的信封，上面写着“记住你的朋友是谁”。第二天，法官发布了一项全国性禁令，阻止该行政命令生效。几个月后，当地一家报纸披露了关于现金信封、纸条以及法官随后裁决的故事。该法官尚未被指控受贿，但总统要求立即罢免该法官。总统可以根据宪法罢免联邦法官吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为联邦法官只能通过弹劾被免职。","B":"不，因为联邦法官尚未因贿赂而被起诉。","C":"是的，因为贿赂是一种可弹劾的罪行。","D":"是的，因为总统的罢免权是绝对的。"},"explanationZh":"宪法将联邦政府分为三个平等的部门，拥有不同的权力（即权力分立）。总统有权在参议院的建议和同意下任命联邦法官。联邦法官在行为良好的情况下终身任职，这有助于保证司法独立。国会拥有因叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪而弹劾和罢免联邦法官的唯一权力，这有助于确保司法公正。在这里，总统寻求罢免被指控受贿的联邦法官。尽管贿赂是一种可弹劾的罪行，但只有国会有权通过弹劾联邦法官免职（选项 C）。因此，总统不得根据宪法罢免联邦法官。 （选项B）联邦法官未被指控受贿这一事实与总统是否可以罢免联邦法官无关。*这是因为弹劾和罢免联邦法官的权力属于国会，而不是总统。 *联邦法官被弹劾和免职不需要起诉书，因为由众议院（而不是大陪审团）决定法官的行为是否构成可弹劾的罪行。 （选项D）宪法没有规定总统可以罢免联邦行政官员，但美国最高法院一再认为总统拥有几乎绝对的权力可以罢免这些官员。但尽管总统的罢免权很广泛，但并不包括罢免联邦法官的权力。\n\n教育目标：总统有权在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命联邦法官，但国会拥有弹劾和罢免联邦法官叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪的唯一权力。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2（赋予总统任命联邦法官的权力）。\n\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 4（规定国会可以因叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪而弹劾联邦法官并罢免其职务）。\n\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III, § 1（在行为良好的情况下授予联邦法官终身任期）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"总统有权在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命联邦法官，但国会拥有以叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪弹劾和罢免联邦法官的唯一权力。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统可以根据宪法罢免联邦法官吗？","rule":"总统有权在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命联邦法官，但国会拥有以叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪弹劾和罢免联邦法官的唯一权力。","analysis":"宪法将联邦政府分为三个平等的部门，拥有不同的权力（即权力分立）。总统有权在参议院的建议和同意下任命联邦法官。联邦法官在行为良好的情况下终身任职，这有助于保证司法独立。国会拥有因叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪而弹劾和罢免联邦法官的唯一权力，这有助于确保司法公正。在这里，总统寻求罢免被指控受贿的联邦法官。尽管贿赂是一种可弹劾的罪行，但只有国会有权通过弹劾联邦法官免职（选项 C）。因此，总统不得根据宪法罢免联邦法官。 （选项B）联邦法官未被指控受贿这一事实与总统是否可以罢免联邦法官无关。*这是因为弹劾和罢免联邦法官的权力属于国会，而不是总统。 *联邦法官被弹劾和免职不需要起诉书，因为由众议院（而不是大陪审团）决定法官的行为是否构成可弹劾的罪行。 （选项D）宪法没有规定总统可以罢免联邦行政官员，但美国最高法院一再认为总统拥有几乎绝对的权力可以罢免这些官员。但尽管总统的罢免权很广泛，但并不包括罢免联邦法官的权力。\n\n教育目标：总统有权在参议院建议和同意的情况下任命联邦法官，但国会拥有弹劾和罢免联邦法官叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪的唯一权力。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 2（赋予总统任命联邦法官的权力）。\n\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 II，§ 4（规定国会可以因叛国、贿赂或其他重罪和轻罪而弹劾联邦法官并罢免其职务）。\n\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III, § 1（在行为良好的情况下授予联邦法官终身任期）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为联邦法官只能通过弹劾被免职。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"removal power","ipa":"/ɹɪmˈuvəl pˈaʊɝ/","syllables":"re·moval power","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"免职权：总统解除行政官员职务的 Article II 权力","contextualMeaning":"免职权：总统解除行政官员职务的 Article II 权力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the President's removal power is absolute.","synonyms":["removal might","removal mightiness","removal powerfulness"]},{"term":"impeachable","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈitʃəbəl/","syllables":"im·peach·able","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 可控告的, 可弹劾的, 可怀疑的；[法] 可控告的, 可弹劾的, 可怀疑的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 可控告的, 可弹劾的, 可怀疑的；[法] 可控告的, 可弹劾的, 可怀疑的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Yes, because bribery is an impeachable offense.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"indictment","ipa":"/ˌɪndˈaɪtmənt/","syllables":"in·dict·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 起诉, 控告, 起诉状；[经] 起诉书","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 起诉, 控告, 起诉状；[经] 起诉书","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:indictments","sourceSentence":"*An indictment is not required for a federal judge to be impeached and removed from office because the House of Representatives—not a grand jury—determines whether the judge's conduct constitutes an impeachable offense.","synonyms":["bill of indictment"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-76b8cd146bcd619d"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":117,"sourceQuestionPage":350,"sourceQuestionPages":[350],"sourceExplanationPages":[351,352],"sourceAnswerPages":[351,352],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-118","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Presidential Powers","question":"In response to a terrorist attack that occurred within the United States, Congress authorized the President to use military force against nations, organizations, and persons the President determined were responsible for the attack. Pursuant to this authorization, enemy combatants were seized in a foreign country and transported to a foreign location under the de facto control of the United States, which was well outside the area of military activity. The President ordered that a military tribunal be convened in that location to try the enemy combatants. Prior to the attack, Congress, pursuant to its constitutional authority, had authorized the use of military tribunals and set out procedures to guarantee specific basic rights that the tribunals must provide, such as the right of a defendant to hear the evidence against him. The procedural rules for the military tribunal established by the presidential order did not provide for those basic rights. Does the President have the constitutional authority to convene this military tribunal?","choices":{"A":"No, because a military tribunal only has power over current members of the U.S. armed forces.","B":"No, because the procedures adopted for the President's military tribunal conflict with the procedures adopted by Congress.","C":"Yes, because Congress authorized the President to use military force that led to the seizure of the enemy combatants.","D":"Yes, because the President has plenary power over military tribunals as commander in chief."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Article II grants the President constitutional authority over a wide range of domestic and foreign policy areas, including serving as commander in chief of the U.S. military. Congress also has authority over military matters, including the power to establish military courts and tribunals. When these coequal branches of government share power, the Youngstown framework determines whether the President acted within his/her constitutional authority. This framework considers whether there was congressional:\n• authorization – in which case the President had maximum authority to act and that act is likely valid\n• silence – in which case the act is valid so long as it (1) fell within the President's Article II powers and (2) did not interfere with another branch's powers or\n• opposition – in which case the President had minimal authority to act and that act is likely invalid. Here, Congress authorized the use of military tribunals and set out procedures to guarantee defendants specific basic rights. However, these rights were not included in the President's executive order that provided procedural rules for a tribunal to try certain enemy combatants. Since the congressional authorization and the executive order are in opposition, the President does not have the constitutional authority to convene this military tribunal. (Choice A) A military tribunal can exercise jurisdiction over current members of the U.S. armed forces as well as enemy combatants and enemy civilians. (Choice C) Although Congress authorized the President to use military force that led to the seizure of the enemy combatants, Congress also guaranteed specific basic rights to defendants tried in a military tribunal. Since the executive order did not include these rights, the President cannot convene the tribunal. (Choice D) The President, acting as the commander in chief, does not have plenary (ie, exclusive) power over military tribunals. Instead, the President shares this power with Congress and must abide by congressional requirements.\n\nEducational objective: The President has minimal constitutional authority to act when Congress opposes the President's actions in an area over which the executive and legislative branches share authority.\n\nReferences\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).\n\n• Hamdan v. Rumsfeld, 548 U.S. 557, 613 (2006) (holding that a military tribunal created by an executive order is invalid when the procedures that will govern the tribunal contravene existing federal law).","educationalObjective":"The President has minimal constitutional authority to act when Congress opposes the President's actions in an area over which the executive and legislative branches share authority.","references":"• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952) (Jackson, J., concurring) (setting forth the tripartite framework for analyzing the constitutionality of presidential actions).\n\n• Hamdan v. Rumsfeld, 548 U.S. 557, 613 (2006) (holding that a military tribunal created by an executive order is invalid when the procedures that will govern the tribunal contravene existing federal law).","rule":"The President has minimal constitutional authority to act when Congress opposes the President's actions in an area over which the executive and legislative branches share authority.","irac":{"issue":"Does the President have the constitutional authority to convene this military tribunal?","rule":"The President has minimal constitutional authority to act when Congress opposes the President's actions in an area over which the executive and legislative branches share authority.","analysis":"Article II grants the President constitutional authority over a wide range of domestic and foreign policy areas, including serving as commander in chief of the U.S. military. Congress also has authority over military matters, including the power to establish military courts and tribunals. When these coequal branches of government share power, the Youngstown framework determines whether the President acted within his/her constitutional authority. This framework considers whether there was congressional:\n• authorization – in which case the President had maximum authority to act and that act is likely valid\n• silence – in which case the act is valid so long as it (1) fell within the President's Article II powers and (2) did not interfere with another branch's powers or\n• opposition – in which case the President had minimal authority to act and that act is likely invalid. Here, Congress authorized the use of military tribunals and set out procedures to guarantee defendants specific basic rights. However, these rights were not included in the President's executive order that provided procedural rules for a tribunal to try certain enemy combatants. Since the congressional authorization and the executive order are in opposition, the President does not have the constitutional authority to convene this military tribunal. (Choice A) A military tribunal can exercise jurisdiction over current members of the U.S. armed forces as well as enemy combatants and enemy civilians. (Choice C) Although Congress authorized the President to use military force that led to the seizure of the enemy combatants, Congress also guaranteed specific basic rights to defendants tried in a military tribunal. Since the executive order did not include these rights, the President cannot convene the tribunal. (Choice D) The President, acting as the commander in chief, does not have plenary (ie, exclusive) power over military tribunals. Instead, the President shares this power with Congress and must abide by congressional requirements.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the procedures adopted for the President's military tribunal conflict with the procedures adopted by Congress."},"questionZh":"为了应对美国境内发生的恐怖袭击，国会授权总统对总统确定应对此次袭击负责的国家、组织和个人使用武力。根据这项授权，敌方战斗人员在外国被抓获，并被运送到美国实际控制下的外国地点，该地点远远超出军事活动范围。总统下令在该地点召开军事法庭审判敌方战斗人员。在袭击发生之前，国会根据其宪法权力，授权使用军事法庭，并制定了程序来保障法庭必须提供的具体基本权利，例如被告听取对他不利的证据的权利。总统令设立的军事法庭程序规则没有规定这些基本权利。总统是否拥有宪法权力来召集军事法庭？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为军事法庭仅对美国武装部队的现任成员拥有权力。","B":"不可以，因为总统军事法庭采用的程序与国会采用的程序相冲突。","C":"是的，因为国会授权总统使用军事力量来扣押敌方战斗人员。","D":"是的，因为总统作为总司令拥有对军事法庭的全部权力。"},"explanationZh":"第二条赋予总统在广泛的国内和外交政策领域的宪法权力，包括担任美国军队总司令。国会还拥有军事事务的权力，包括设立军事法院和法庭的权力。当这些平等的政府部门分享权力时，扬斯敦框架决定总统是否在其宪法权力范围内行事。该框架考虑国会是否存在：\n• 授权——在这种情况下，总统拥有采取行动的最大权力，并且该行动可能有效\n• 沉默——在这种情况下，只要该行为(1) 属于总统第二条权力范围内并且(2) 不干涉其他部门的权力或\n• 反对——在这种情况下，总统的行动权力极小，而且该行动可能无效。在这里，国会授权使用军事法庭并制定程序来保障被告的具体基本权利。然而，这些权利并未包含在总统行政命令中，该命令为法庭审判某些敌方战斗人员提供了程序规则。由于国会授权和行政命令是对立的，总统没有宪法权力召集这个军事法庭。 （选项 A）军事法庭可以对美国武装部队现任成员以及敌方战斗人员和敌方平民行使管辖权。 （选项C）虽然国会授权总统使用武力扣押敌方战斗人员，但国会也保障在军事法庭受审的被告的特定基本权利。由于行政命令不包括这些权利，总统无法召集法庭。 （选项D） 总统作为总司令，对军事法庭没有全部（即排他性）权力。相反，总统与国会分享这一权力，并且必须遵守国会的要求。\n\n教育目标：当国会反对总统在行政和立法部门共享权力的领域采取行动时，总统拥有最低限度的宪法权力。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。\n\n• Hamdan v. Rumsfeld, 548 U.S. 557, 613 (2006)（认为当管辖法庭的程序违反现行联邦法律时，由行政命令设立的军事法庭无效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当国会反对总统在行政部门和立法部门共享权力的领域采取行动时，总统拥有最低限度的宪法权力。","iracZh":{"issue":"总统是否拥有宪法权力来召集军事法庭？","rule":"当国会反对总统在行政部门和立法部门共享权力的领域采取行动时，总统拥有最低限度的宪法权力。","analysis":"第二条赋予总统在广泛的国内和外交政策领域的宪法权力，包括担任美国军队总司令。国会还拥有军事事务的权力，包括设立军事法院和法庭的权力。当这些平等的政府部门分享权力时，扬斯敦框架决定总统是否在其宪法权力范围内行事。该框架考虑国会是否存在：\n• 授权——在这种情况下，总统拥有采取行动的最大权力，并且该行动可能有效\n• 沉默——在这种情况下，只要该行为(1) 属于总统第二条权力范围内并且(2) 不干涉其他部门的权力或\n• 反对——在这种情况下，总统的行动权力极小，而且该行动可能无效。在这里，国会授权使用军事法庭并制定程序来保障被告的具体基本权利。然而，这些权利并未包含在总统行政命令中，该命令为法庭审判某些敌方战斗人员提供了程序规则。由于国会授权和行政命令是对立的，总统没有宪法权力召集这个军事法庭。 （选项 A）军事法庭可以对美国武装部队现任成员以及敌方战斗人员和敌方平民行使管辖权。 （选项C）虽然国会授权总统使用武力扣押敌方战斗人员，但国会也保障在军事法庭受审的被告的特定基本权利。由于行政命令不包括这些权利，总统无法召集法庭。 （选项D） 总统作为总司令，对军事法庭没有全部（即排他性）权力。相反，总统与国会分享这一权力，并且必须遵守国会的要求。\n\n教育目标：当国会反对总统在行政和立法部门共享权力的领域采取行动时，总统拥有最低限度的宪法权力。\n\n参考文献\n• Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer, 343 U.S. 579, 635–38 (1952)（Jackson, J.，同意）（提出了分析总统行为合宪性的三方框架）。\n\n• Hamdan v. Rumsfeld, 548 U.S. 557, 613 (2006)（认为当管辖法庭的程序违反现行联邦法律时，由行政命令设立的军事法庭无效）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为总统军事法庭采用的程序与国会采用的程序相冲突。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"seizure","ipa":"/sˈiʒɝ/","syllables":"seizure","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 捕获, 夺取, 占领, 捕获物, 没收, 充公；[医] 发作； 癫痫发作","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 捕获, 夺取, 占领, 捕获物, 没收, 充公；[医] 发作； 癫痫发作","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:seizures","sourceSentence":"Yes, because Congress authorized the President to use military force that led to the seizure of the enemy combatants.","synonyms":["capture","gaining control","ictus","raptus"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-988f8704cd7cc299"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":118,"sourceQuestionPage":353,"sourceQuestionPages":[353],"sourceExplanationPages":[354,355],"sourceAnswerPages":[354,355],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-119","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Federal Judicial Power","question":"A federal statute requires the National Bureau of Standards to establish minimum quality standards for all beer sold in the United States. The statute also provides that public hearings must precede adoption of the standards, and that once they are adopted, the standards will be subject to judicial review. While the proposed standards have not yet been announced, several Bureau officials have publicly expressed opinions indicating a belief that pasteurized beer is safer than unpasteurized beer. However, these officials have not stated whether they intend to include a pasteurization requirement in the standards. A brewery that produces unpasteurized beer is concerned that, after the appropriate proceedings, the Bureau may adopt quality standards that will prohibit the sale of unpasteurized beer. The brewery has sued in federal district court to enjoin the Bureau from adopting standards that would prohibit the sale of unpasteurized beer. How should the district court proceed with the suit?","choices":{"A":"Determine whether the Bureau could reasonably believe that pasteurization is the safest process by which to brew beer and, if so, refuse to issue the injunction against the Bureau.","B":"Determine whether the process used by the brewery is as safe as pasteurization and, if so, issue the injunction against the Bureau.","C":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the suit and dismiss it, because it does not involve a justiciable case or controversy.","D":"Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the suit at this time and stay the action until the Bureau has actually issued beer-quality standards."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Justiciability\n\n(suitability for judicial resolution)\n\nCase or controversy Prohibits advisory opinions under three doctrines:\n\n• Standing – plaintiff must allege injury-in-fact caused by challenged action & redressable by remedy sought\n• Ripeness – claim must present actual controversy involving past injury or present threat of injury\n• Mootness – claim must be unresolved Adequate & SCOTUS cannot review final state court judgment that rests on independent state adequate & independent state grounds grounds\n\nPolitical question Court cannot hear case if issue presented:\n\n• is reserved for executive/legislative branches or\n• lacks judicially manageable standards for resolution SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nA federal court should not proceed with a suit unless it is justiciable—ie, appropriate for judicial resolution—based on the following Article III requirements: (1) case or controversy, (2) no adequate and independent state grounds, and (3) no political questions. The case-or-controversy requirement is met if the plaintiff has standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant and is redressable by the court. An injury-in-fact exists if the plaintiff alleges actual, or an immediate threat of, harm. Therefore, a court should dismiss a suit that is:\n• unripe – the suit is based on the mere potential for, or future threat of, harm or\n• moot – the suit no longer presents a live (ie, actual) controversy for the court to resolve. Here, the brewery filed suit in federal district court to enjoin the Bureau from adopting standards that would prohibit the brewery from selling unpasteurized beer. But this suit does not involve a justiciable case or controversy because the Bureau has not adopted such standards (no actual harm) and public hearings that must precede the adoption have yet to occur (no imminent harm). Therefore, the court should refuse to adjudicate the merits of this unripe suit and dismiss it.\n\n(Choices A & B) Since this suit is not justiciable, the court cannot consider its merits—eg, whether (1) the Bureau could reasonably believe that pasteurization is the safest process or (2) the brewery's process is as safe as pasteurization. (Choice D) A federal court can stay (ie, suspend) a suit to (1) force a party to comply with a court order or (2) protect a party's rights. However, a federal court must dismiss a suit when, as here, it does not present a justiciable case or controversy.\n\nEducational objective: Article III limits the jurisdiction of federal courts to justiciable cases or controversies. This requires proof that the plaintiff has suffered an injury-in-fact—ie, an actual, or immediate threat of, harm. Therefore, a federal court must dismiss a suit involving future or resolved harm.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998) (explaining when a claim is unripe for adjudication).","educationalObjective":"Article III limits the jurisdiction of federal courts to justiciable cases or controversies. This requires proof that the plaintiff has suffered an injury-in-fact—ie, an actual, or immediate threat of, harm. Therefore, a federal court must dismiss a suit involving future or resolved harm.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case or controversy requirement).\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998) (explaining when a claim is unripe for adjudication).","rule":"Article III limits the jurisdiction of federal courts to justiciable cases or controversies. This requires proof that the plaintiff has suffered an injury-in-fact—ie, an actual, or immediate threat of, harm. Therefore, a federal court must dismiss a suit involving future or resolved harm.","irac":{"issue":"How should the district court proceed with the suit?","rule":"Article III limits the jurisdiction of federal courts to justiciable cases or controversies. This requires proof that the plaintiff has suffered an injury-in-fact—ie, an actual, or immediate threat of, harm. Therefore, a federal court must dismiss a suit involving future or resolved harm.","analysis":"Justiciability\n\n(suitability for judicial resolution)\n\nCase or controversy Prohibits advisory opinions under three doctrines:\n\n• Standing – plaintiff must allege injury-in-fact caused by challenged action & redressable by remedy sought\n• Ripeness – claim must present actual controversy involving past injury or present threat of injury\n• Mootness – claim must be unresolved Adequate & SCOTUS cannot review final state court judgment that rests on independent state adequate & independent state grounds grounds\n\nPolitical question Court cannot hear case if issue presented:\n\n• is reserved for executive/legislative branches or\n• lacks judicially manageable standards for resolution SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nA federal court should not proceed with a suit unless it is justiciable—ie, appropriate for judicial resolution—based on the following Article III requirements: (1) case or controversy, (2) no adequate and independent state grounds, and (3) no political questions. The case-or-controversy requirement is met if the plaintiff has standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact that was caused by the defendant and is redressable by the court. An injury-in-fact exists if the plaintiff alleges actual, or an immediate threat of, harm. Therefore, a court should dismiss a suit that is:\n• unripe – the suit is based on the mere potential for, or future threat of, harm or\n• moot – the suit no longer presents a live (ie, actual) controversy for the court to resolve. Here, the brewery filed suit in federal district court to enjoin the Bureau from adopting standards that would prohibit the brewery from selling unpasteurized beer. But this suit does not involve a justiciable case or controversy because the Bureau has not adopted such standards (no actual harm) and public hearings that must precede the adoption have yet to occur (no imminent harm). Therefore, the court should refuse to adjudicate the merits of this unripe suit and dismiss it.\n\n(Choices A & B) Since this suit is not justiciable, the court cannot consider its merits—eg, whether (1) the Bureau could reasonably believe that pasteurization is the safest process or (2) the brewery's process is as safe as pasteurization. (Choice D) A federal court can stay (ie, suspend) a suit to (1) force a party to comply with a court order or (2) protect a party's rights. However, a federal court must dismiss a suit when, as here, it does not present a justiciable case or controversy.","conclusion":"Answer C: Refuse to adjudicate the merits of the suit and dismiss it, because it does not involve a justiciable case or controversy."},"questionZh":"联邦法规要求国家标准局为在美国销售的所有啤酒制定最低质量标准。该法规还规定，标准通过之前必须举行公开听证会，一旦通过，标准将接受司法审查。虽然拟议的标准尚未公布，但几位局官员公开表达了观点，表明巴氏灭菌啤酒比未经巴氏灭菌的啤酒更安全。然而，这些官员尚未说明他们是否打算将巴氏灭菌要求纳入标准中。生产未经高温消毒啤酒的啤酒厂担心，经过适当的程序后，该局可能会采用质量标准，禁止销售未经高温消毒的啤酒。该啤酒厂已向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，要求禁止该局采用禁止销售未经高温消毒啤酒的标准。地方法院应如何审理该诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"确定该局是否可以合理地相信巴氏灭菌是酿造啤酒的最安全方法，如果是，则拒绝向该局发出禁令。","B":"确定啤酒厂使用的工艺是否与巴氏灭菌一样安全，如果是，则向该局发出禁令。","C":"拒绝裁定诉讼的是非曲直并将其驳回，因为它不涉及可审理的案件或争议。","D":"此时拒绝裁决诉讼的是非曲直，并在该局实际发布啤酒质量标准之前暂停诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"可审理性\n\n（是否适合司法解决）\n\n案例或争议 禁止根据以下三种原则提供咨询意见：\n\n• 诉讼资格——原告必须声称受到质疑的行为造成了事实上的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以纠正\n• 成熟度——索赔必须提出涉及过去受伤或当前受伤威胁的实际争议\n• 没有实际意义 – 索赔必须未得到解决 充分且 SCOTUS 无法审查基于独立州充分和独立州理由的最终州法院判决\n\n政治问题 如果提出以下问题，法院将无法审理案件：\n\n• 保留给行政/立法部门或\n• 缺乏司法上可管理的决议标准 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n联邦法院不应提起诉讼，除非该诉讼符合以下第三条要求，即适合司法解决：(1) 案件或争议，(2) 没有充分和独立的州理由，以及 (3) 不存在政治问题。如果原告有资格，即遭受了由被告造成的事实上的伤害并且可以由法院赔偿，则满足案件或争议的要求。如果原告声称存在实际伤害或直接威胁伤害，则存在事实伤害。因此，法院应驳回以下诉讼：\n• 不成熟——诉讼仅基于潜在的伤害或未来的威胁\n• 没有实际意义——诉讼不再提出需要法院解决的现场（即实际）争议。在这里，该啤酒厂向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，要求禁止该局采用禁止该啤酒厂销售未经高温消毒的啤酒的标准。但该诉讼并不涉及可审理的案件或争议，因为该局尚未采用此类标准（没有实际损害），并且在采用之前必须举行的公开听证会尚未举行（没有迫在眉睫的损害）。因此，法院应拒绝对这起不成熟诉讼的案情作出裁决并予以驳回。\n\n（选项A和B）由于该诉讼不可审理，法院无法考虑其案情，例如，是否（1）局可以合理地相信巴氏灭菌是最安全的过程，或者（2）啤酒厂的过程与巴氏灭菌一样安全。 （选择 D）联邦法院可以中止（即中止）诉讼，以 (1) 迫使一方遵守法院命令或 (2) 保护一方的权利。然而，当诉讼没有提出可审理的案件或争议时，联邦法院必须驳回诉讼，就像这里一样。\n\n教育目标：第三条将联邦法院的管辖权限于可审理的案件或争议。这需要证明原告确实遭受了伤害，即实际的或直接的伤害威胁。因此，联邦法院必须驳回涉及未来或已解决损害的诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998)（解释何时裁决尚不成熟）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第三条将联邦法院的管辖权限于可审理的案件或争议。这需要证明原告确实遭受了伤害，即实际的或直接的伤害威胁。因此，联邦法院必须驳回涉及未来或已解决损害的诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"地方法院应如何审理该诉讼？","rule":"第三条将联邦法院的管辖权限于可审理的案件或争议。这需要证明原告确实遭受了伤害，即实际的或直接的伤害威胁。因此，联邦法院必须驳回涉及未来或已解决损害的诉讼。","analysis":"可审理性\n\n（是否适合司法解决）\n\n案例或争议 禁止根据以下三种原则提供咨询意见：\n\n• 诉讼资格——原告必须声称受到质疑的行为造成了事实上的伤害，并且可以通过寻求补救措施予以纠正\n• 成熟度——索赔必须提出涉及过去受伤或当前受伤威胁的实际争议\n• 没有实际意义 – 索赔必须未得到解决 充分且 SCOTUS 无法审查基于独立州充分和独立州理由的最终州法院判决\n\n政治问题 如果提出以下问题，法院将无法审理案件：\n\n• 保留给行政/立法部门或\n• 缺乏司法上可管理的决议标准 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n联邦法院不应提起诉讼，除非该诉讼符合以下第三条要求，即适合司法解决：(1) 案件或争议，(2) 没有充分和独立的州理由，以及 (3) 不存在政治问题。如果原告有资格，即遭受了由被告造成的事实上的伤害并且可以由法院赔偿，则满足案件或争议的要求。如果原告声称存在实际伤害或直接威胁伤害，则存在事实伤害。因此，法院应驳回以下诉讼：\n• 不成熟——诉讼仅基于潜在的伤害或未来的威胁\n• 没有实际意义——诉讼不再提出需要法院解决的现场（即实际）争议。在这里，该啤酒厂向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，要求禁止该局采用禁止该啤酒厂销售未经高温消毒的啤酒的标准。但该诉讼并不涉及可审理的案件或争议，因为该局尚未采用此类标准（没有实际损害），并且在采用之前必须举行的公开听证会尚未举行（没有迫在眉睫的损害）。因此，法院应拒绝对这起不成熟诉讼的案情作出裁决并予以驳回。\n\n（选项A和B）由于该诉讼不可审理，法院无法考虑其案情，例如，是否（1）局可以合理地相信巴氏灭菌是最安全的过程，或者（2）啤酒厂的过程与巴氏灭菌一样安全。 （选择 D）联邦法院可以中止（即中止）诉讼，以 (1) 迫使一方遵守法院命令或 (2) 保护一方的权利。然而，当诉讼没有提出可审理的案件或争议时，联邦法院必须驳回诉讼，就像这里一样。\n\n教育目标：第三条将联邦法院的管辖权限于可审理的案件或争议。这需要证明原告确实遭受了伤害，即实际的或直接的伤害威胁。因此，联邦法院必须驳回涉及未来或已解决损害的诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Texas v. United States, 523 U.S. 296, 300 (1998)（解释何时裁决尚不成熟）。","conclusion":"答案 C：拒绝裁定诉讼的是非曲直并将其驳回，因为它不涉及可审理的案件或争议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":119,"sourceQuestionPage":356,"sourceQuestionPages":[356],"sourceExplanationPages":[357,358],"sourceAnswerPages":[357,358],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-120","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"A federal statute appropriated $7 million for a nationwide essay contest on \"How the United States Can Best Stop Drug Abuse.\" The statute indicates that its purpose is to generate new, practical ideas for eliminating drug abuse in the United States. Contest rules set forth in the statute provide that winning essays are to be selected based on the \"originality, aptness, and feasibility of their ideas.\" The statute expressly authorizes a first prize of $1 million, 50 second prizes of $100,000 each, and 100 third prizes of $10,000 each. It also states that judges for the contest are to be appointed by the President of the United States with the advice and consent of the Senate and that all residents of the United States who are not employees of the federal government are eligible to enter and win the contest. A provision of the statute authorizes any taxpayer of the United States to challenge its constitutionality. In a suit by a federal taxpayer to challenge the constitutionality of the statute, how should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"Hold the statute constitutional, because it is reasonably related to the general welfare, it states concrete objectives, and it provides adequate criteria for conducting the essay contest and awarding the prize money.","B":"Hold the statute unconstitutional, because it does not provide sufficient guidelines for awarding the prize money appropriated by Congress and, therefore, unconstitutionally delegates legislative power to the contest judges.","C":"Hold the statute unconstitutional, because its relationship to legitimate purposes of the spending power of Congress is too tenuous and conjectural to satisfy the necessary and proper clause of Article I.","D":"Refuse to decide the merits, because a generalized taxpayer grievance is not a concrete and particularized Article III injury, and Congress cannot create standing merely by authorizing any taxpayer to sue."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The taxpayer lacks Article III standing. A generalized interest in seeing that Congress acts constitutionally is not a concrete and particularized injury. Congress's statement that any taxpayer may sue creates a cause of action, but it cannot replace the constitutional injury-in-fact requirement. TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez confirms that a statutory right and authorization to sue do not automatically establish standing. The narrow Flast taxpayer exception does not apply because this suit does not allege that a taxing-and-spending enactment violates a specific Establishment Clause limitation. Therefore, the court should refuse to reach the merits (Choice D).\n\n(Choice A) The spending program may be within Congress's substantive spending power, but a federal court cannot decide that merits question without Article III jurisdiction.\n\n(Choice B) The contest criteria likely supply an intelligible principle, but this issue also cannot be reached without standing.\n\n(Choice C) Congress's spending power is express; nevertheless, the standing defect is antecedent.\n\nEducational objective: Article III requires a concrete, particularized, actual or imminent injury that is traceable to the defendant and likely redressable by the court. Congress may create statutory rights, but it cannot eliminate the constitutional injury-in-fact requirement or confer standing on an uninjured taxpayer merely by authorizing suit.\n\nReferences\n• TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez, 594 U.S. 413, 426–27 (2021).\n• Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83, 102–04 (1968).","educationalObjective":"Article III requires a concrete, particularized, actual or imminent injury that is traceable to the defendant and likely redressable by the court. Congress may create statutory rights, but it cannot eliminate the constitutional injury-in-fact requirement or confer standing on an uninjured taxpayer merely by authorizing suit.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 1 (taxing and spending clause).\n\n• South Dakota v. Dole, 483 U.S. 203, 207–08 (1987) (holding that conditions on a federal expenditure must be unambiguous and reasonably related to the purpose of the expenditure).","rule":"Article III requires a concrete, particularized, actual or imminent injury that is traceable to the defendant and likely redressable by the court. Congress may create statutory rights, but it cannot eliminate the constitutional injury-in-fact requirement or confer standing on an uninjured taxpayer merely by authorizing suit.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by a federal taxpayer to challenge the constitutionality of the statute, how should the court rule?","rule":"Article III requires a concrete, particularized, actual or imminent injury that is traceable to the defendant and likely redressable by the court. Congress may create statutory rights, but it cannot eliminate the constitutional injury-in-fact requirement or confer standing on an uninjured taxpayer merely by authorizing suit.","analysis":"The taxpayer lacks Article III standing. A generalized interest in seeing that Congress acts constitutionally is not a concrete and particularized injury. Congress's statement that any taxpayer may sue creates a cause of action, but it cannot replace the constitutional injury-in-fact requirement. TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez confirms that a statutory right and authorization to sue do not automatically establish standing. The narrow Flast taxpayer exception does not apply because this suit does not allege that a taxing-and-spending enactment violates a specific Establishment Clause limitation. Therefore, the court should refuse to reach the merits (Choice D).\n\n(Choice A) The spending program may be within Congress's substantive spending power, but a federal court cannot decide that merits question without Article III jurisdiction.\n\n(Choice B) The contest criteria likely supply an intelligible principle, but this issue also cannot be reached without standing.\n\n(Choice C) Congress's spending power is express; nevertheless, the standing defect is antecedent.\n\nEducational objective: Article III requires a concrete, particularized, actual or imminent injury that is traceable to the defendant and likely redressable by the court. Congress may create statutory rights, but it cannot eliminate the constitutional injury-in-fact requirement or confer standing on an uninjured taxpayer merely by authorizing suit.\n\nReferences\n• TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez, 594 U.S. 413, 426–27 (2021).\n• Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83, 102–04 (1968).","conclusion":"Answer D: Refuse to decide the merits, because a generalized taxpayer grievance is not a concrete and particularized Article III injury, and Congress cannot create standing merely by authorizing any taxpayer to sue."},"questionZh":"一项联邦法规拨款 700 万美元用于举办“美国如何最好地制止药物滥用”的全国征文比赛。该法规表明其目的是为消除美国的药物滥用产生新的、实用的想法。法规中规定的竞赛规则规定，获奖论文的评选依据是“其想法的原创性、恰当性和可行性”。章程明确授予一等奖 100 万美元，二等奖 50 名，每人 10 万美元，三等奖 100 名，每人 1 万美元。它还规定，竞赛的法官由美国总统在参议院的建议和同意下任命，所有非联邦政府雇员的美国居民都有资格参加并赢得竞赛。该法规的一项条款授权美国任何纳税人质疑其合宪性。在联邦纳税人提起诉讼质疑该法规的合宪性时，法院应如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"遵守宪法，因为它与公共福利合理相关，它规定了具体目标，并为举办征文比赛和颁发奖金提供了充分的标准。","B":"认为该法规违宪，因为它没有为授予国会拨款的奖金提供足够的指导方针，因此违宪地将立法权委托给竞赛法官。","C":"认为该法规违宪，因为它与国会支出权合法目的的关系过于脆弱和推测，无法满足第一条的必要和适当条款。","D":"拒绝审理实体问题，因为一般化的纳税人异议并非 Article III 所要求的具体且个别化损害；国会不能仅凭授权任何纳税人起诉而创造诉讼资格。"},"explanationZh":"该纳税人不具备 Article III 诉讼资格。希望国会依宪行事的一般化利益，不是具体且个别化的损害。国会写明“任何纳税人均可起诉”只能创设诉因，不能取代宪法要求的事实损害。TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez 明确指出，法定权利及诉权授权并不会自动产生诉讼资格。Flast 的狭窄纳税人例外也不适用，因为本案并未主张征税与支出立法违反政教分离条款这一特定宪法限制。因此，法院应拒绝进入实体审理（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）该支出项目在实体上可能属于国会支出权，但联邦法院没有 Article III 管辖前不能裁判这一问题。\n\n（选项 B）竞赛标准可能已提供“可理解原则”，但同样不能绕过诉讼资格。\n\n（选项 C）支出权是明示权力；不过诉讼资格是必须先解决的门槛。\n\n教育目标：Article III 要求原告具有具体、个别化且现实或迫近的损害，该损害可归因于被告并可能由法院救济。国会可以创设法定权利，但不能取消宪法上的事实损害要求，也不能仅以授权起诉的方式赋予未受具体损害的纳税人诉讼资格。\n\n参考：TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez, 594 U.S. 413（2021）；Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83（1968）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"Article III 要求原告具有具体、个别化且现实或迫近的损害，该损害可归因于被告并可能由法院救济。国会可以创设法定权利，但不能取消宪法上的事实损害要求，也不能仅以授权起诉的方式赋予未受具体损害的纳税人诉讼资格。","iracZh":{"issue":"在联邦纳税人提起诉讼质疑该法规的合宪性时，法院应如何裁决？","rule":"Article III 要求原告具有具体、个别化且现实或迫近的损害，该损害可归因于被告并可能由法院救济。国会可以创设法定权利，但不能取消宪法上的事实损害要求，也不能仅以授权起诉的方式赋予未受具体损害的纳税人诉讼资格。","analysis":"该纳税人不具备 Article III 诉讼资格。希望国会依宪行事的一般化利益，不是具体且个别化的损害。国会写明“任何纳税人均可起诉”只能创设诉因，不能取代宪法要求的事实损害。TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez 明确指出，法定权利及诉权授权并不会自动产生诉讼资格。Flast 的狭窄纳税人例外也不适用，因为本案并未主张征税与支出立法违反政教分离条款这一特定宪法限制。因此，法院应拒绝进入实体审理（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）该支出项目在实体上可能属于国会支出权，但联邦法院没有 Article III 管辖前不能裁判这一问题。\n\n（选项 B）竞赛标准可能已提供“可理解原则”，但同样不能绕过诉讼资格。\n\n（选项 C）支出权是明示权力；不过诉讼资格是必须先解决的门槛。\n\n教育目标：Article III 要求原告具有具体、个别化且现实或迫近的损害，该损害可归因于被告并可能由法院救济。国会可以创设法定权利，但不能取消宪法上的事实损害要求，也不能仅以授权起诉的方式赋予未受具体损害的纳税人诉讼资格。\n\n参考：TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez, 594 U.S. 413（2021）；Flast v. Cohen, 392 U.S. 83（1968）。","conclusion":"答案 D：拒绝审理实体问题，因为一般化的纳税人异议并非 Article III 所要求的具体且个别化损害；国会不能仅凭授权任何纳税人起诉而创造诉讼资格。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"corrected-answer-current-law","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"原资料以 A 为答案，但其忽略 Article III 的具体损害门槛。依 TransUnion 与 Flast，已将答案修正为 D，并重写选项 D 与解析。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez, 594 U.S. 413 (2021)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/20-297_4g25.pdf"}],"originalAnswer":"A","revisedAnswer":"D"},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":120,"sourceQuestionPage":359,"sourceQuestionPages":[359],"sourceExplanationPages":[360,361],"sourceAnswerPages":[360,361],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-121","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"A federal statute prohibits the sale or resale, in any place in this country, of any product intended for human consumption or ingestion into the human body that contains designated chemicals known to cause cancer, unless the product is clearly labeled as dangerous. The constitutionality of this federal statute may be justified most easily on which of the following bases?","choices":{"A":"Congress's power to enforce the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"Congress's power to promote science and the useful arts.","C":"Congress's power to provide for the general welfare.","D":"Congress's power to regulate commerce among the states."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Commerce clause\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, § 8)\n\nCongress can regulate:\n\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons/things moving in interstate commerce\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce The Article I commerce clause grants Congress broad authority to regulate commerce among the states. This includes any activity that has a substantial effect on interstate commerce. If the regulated activity is economic in nature, a substantial effect is presumed. Here, the federal statute requires sellers to clearly label products that contain cancer- causing chemicals as dangerous. Selling dangerous consumables is an economic activity that is presumed to substantially affect interstate commerce. Therefore, Congress can regulate this activity under its commerce power. (Choice A) The Fourteenth Amendment's enforcement clause enables Congress to pass laws enforcing the amendment's other provisions. But since these provisions only apply to state action, Congress cannot regulate the labeling practices of private entities on this basis. (Choice B) The Article I copyright and patent clause empowers Congress to pass laws that promote science and the useful arts—which are not implicated here. (Choice C) The taxing and spending clause only allows Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare of the United States. Since the federal statute does not involve such activities, this would not justify the statute.\n\nEducational objective: The Article I commerce clause grants Congress broad authority to regulate commerce among the states. This includes any activity that substantially affects interstate commerce (eg, economic activities).\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Sullivan, 332 U.S. 689, 697–98 (1948) (holding that a federal drug- labeling statute is a proper exercise of Congress's commerce power).","educationalObjective":"The Article I commerce clause grants Congress broad authority to regulate commerce among the states. This includes any activity that substantially affects interstate commerce (eg, economic activities).","references":"• United States v. Sullivan, 332 U.S. 689, 697–98 (1948) (holding that a federal drug- labeling statute is a proper exercise of Congress's commerce power).","rule":"The Article I commerce clause grants Congress broad authority to regulate commerce among the states. This includes any activity that substantially affects interstate commerce (eg, economic activities).","irac":{"issue":"The constitutionality of this federal statute may be justified most easily on which of the following bases?","rule":"The Article I commerce clause grants Congress broad authority to regulate commerce among the states. This includes any activity that substantially affects interstate commerce (eg, economic activities).","analysis":"Commerce clause\n\n(U.S. Const. art. I, § 8)\n\nCongress can regulate:\n\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons/things moving in interstate commerce\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce The Article I commerce clause grants Congress broad authority to regulate commerce among the states. This includes any activity that has a substantial effect on interstate commerce. If the regulated activity is economic in nature, a substantial effect is presumed. Here, the federal statute requires sellers to clearly label products that contain cancer- causing chemicals as dangerous. Selling dangerous consumables is an economic activity that is presumed to substantially affect interstate commerce. Therefore, Congress can regulate this activity under its commerce power. (Choice A) The Fourteenth Amendment's enforcement clause enables Congress to pass laws enforcing the amendment's other provisions. But since these provisions only apply to state action, Congress cannot regulate the labeling practices of private entities on this basis. (Choice B) The Article I copyright and patent clause empowers Congress to pass laws that promote science and the useful arts—which are not implicated here. (Choice C) The taxing and spending clause only allows Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare of the United States. Since the federal statute does not involve such activities, this would not justify the statute.","conclusion":"Answer D: Congress's power to regulate commerce among the states."},"questionZh":"联邦法规禁止在该国任何地方销售或转售任何供人类食用或摄入人体的产品，其中含有已知会导致癌症的指定化学物质，除非该产品明确标记为危险品。该联邦法规的合宪性可以最容易地根据以下哪一个基础来证明是合理的？","choicesZh":{"A":"国会执行第十四修正案的权力。","B":"国会促进科学和实用艺术的权力。","C":"国会提供公共福利的权力。","D":"国会监管各州之间商业的权力。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条）\n\n国会可以监管：\n\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 州际贸易中移动的人/物\n• 严重影响州际贸易的活动 第一条贸易条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管各州之间的贸易。这包括对州际贸易有重大影响的任何活动。如果受监管的活动本质上是经济活动，则假定会产生重大影响。在这里，联邦法规要求卖家将含有致癌化学物质的产品明确标记为危险品。销售危险消费品是一项被认为会严重影响州际贸易的经济活动。因此，国会可以利用其商业权力来规范这一活动。 （选项 A） 第十四修正案的执行条款使国会能够通过法律来执行该修正案的其他条款。但由于这些规定仅适用于国家行为（state action），国会无法在此基础上规范私人实体的标签做法。 （选项 B）第一条版权和专利条款授权国会通过促进科学和实用艺术的法律（此处未涉及）。 （选项C）征税和支出条款只允许国会为美国的普遍福利征税和支出。由于联邦法规不涉及此类活动，因此这不能证明该法规的合理性。\n\n教育目标：第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管各州之间的商业。这包括任何严重影响州际贸易的活动（例如经济活动）。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Sullivan，332 U.S. 689, 697-98 (1948)（认为联邦药品标签法规是国会商业权力的适当行使）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管各州之间的商业。这包括任何严重影响州际贸易的活动（例如经济活动）。","iracZh":{"issue":"该联邦法规的合宪性可以最容易地根据以下哪一个基础来证明是合理的？","rule":"第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管各州之间的商业。这包括任何严重影响州际贸易的活动（例如经济活动）。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）\n\n（美国宪法第 I 条第 8 条）\n\n国会可以监管：\n\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 州际贸易中移动的人/物\n• 严重影响州际贸易的活动 第一条贸易条款赋予国会广泛的权力来监管各州之间的贸易。这包括对州际贸易有重大影响的任何活动。如果受监管的活动本质上是经济活动，则假定会产生重大影响。在这里，联邦法规要求卖家将含有致癌化学物质的产品明确标记为危险品。销售危险消费品是一项被认为会严重影响州际贸易的经济活动。因此，国会可以利用其商业权力来规范这一活动。 （选项 A） 第十四修正案的执行条款使国会能够通过法律来执行该修正案的其他条款。但由于这些规定仅适用于国家行为（state action），国会无法在此基础上规范私人实体的标签做法。 （选项 B）第一条版权和专利条款授权国会通过促进科学和实用艺术的法律（此处未涉及）。 （选项C）征税和支出条款只允许国会为美国的普遍福利征税和支出。由于联邦法规不涉及此类活动，因此这不能证明该法规的合理性。\n\n教育目标：第一条商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管各州之间的商业。这包括任何严重影响州际贸易的活动（例如经济活动）。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Sullivan，332 U.S. 689, 697-98 (1948)（认为联邦药品标签法规是国会商业权力的适当行使）。","conclusion":"答案 D：国会监管各州之间商业的权力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":121,"sourceQuestionPage":362,"sourceQuestionPages":[362],"sourceExplanationPages":[363,364],"sourceAnswerPages":[363,364],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-122","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"In order to provide funds for a system of new major airports near the 10 largest cities in the United States, Congress levies a tax of $25 on each airline ticket issued in the United States. The tax applies to every airline ticket, even those for travel that does not originate in, terminate at, or pass through any of those 10 large cities. Is this tax constitutional as applied to the issuance in the United States of an airline ticket for travel between two cities that will not be served by any of the new airports?","choices":{"A":"No, because the burden of the tax outweighs its benefits for passengers whose travel does not originate in, terminate at, or pass through any of the 10 largest cities.","B":"No, because the tax adversely affects the fundamental right to travel.","C":"Yes, because an exemption for the issuance of tickets for travel between cities that will not be served by the new airports would deny the purchasers of all other tickets the equal protection of the laws.","D":"Yes, because Congress has broad discretion in choosing the subjects of its taxation and may impose taxes on subjects that have no relation to the purpose for which those tax funds will be expended."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Congressional taxing power\n\nType of Definition Constitutional requirements tax\n\nDirect\n• Taxes on persons or their Allowed if: property\n• apportioned\n• Payment cannot be avoided proportionately among states\n• reasonably related to revenue production Indirect\n• Taxes on goods/transactions Allowed if:\n• Payment can be avoided or shifted\n• identical taxation in every to another (eg, consumer) state\n• reasonably related to revenue production Export\n• Taxes on goods/services leaving Not allowed the country The taxing and spending clause grants Congress the broad power to impose indirect taxes on the subject of its choosing. The taxed subject need not have any relation to the purpose for which the tax funds will be spent. Instead, an indirect tax is constitutional as long as it is (1) identical in every state and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. Here, Congress has imposed a $25 tax on airline tickets. This indirect tax is identical in every state since it applies to all airline tickets issued in the U.S. And the tax is reasonably related to raising revenues for a system of new major airports near the 10 largest cities. Therefore, this tax is constitutional—even as applied to airline tickets for travel between cities that will not be served by the new airports. (Choice A) Courts do not weigh the burdens and benefits of a tax to determine its constitutionality. (Choice B) The Fifth Amendment due process clause generally prohibits the federal government from substantially impairing persons' fundamental rights (eg, the right to travel). And though a tax on airline tickets may affect the right to travel, a minimal tax of $25 would not substantially impair this right. (Choice C) This type of exemption—which does not appear in the tax levied by Congress— may raise equal protection concerns since it treats similarly situated passengers differently. But the exemption would not deny passengers equal protection of the laws\n\nsince it (1) does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class and (2) would likely pass rational basis scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: Under the taxing and spending clause, Congress can impose indirect taxes on goods or transactions as long as the taxes are (1) identical in every state and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This is true even if the taxes have no relation to the purpose for which the funds will be spent.\n\nReferences\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 355–63 (1945) (holding that an IRS inheritance tax is constitutional since it is indirect, identical in every state, and reasonably related to revenue production).","educationalObjective":"Under the taxing and spending clause, Congress can impose indirect taxes on goods or transactions as long as the taxes are (1) identical in every state and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This is true even if the taxes have no relation to the purpose for which the funds will be spent.","references":"• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 355–63 (1945) (holding that an IRS inheritance tax is constitutional since it is indirect, identical in every state, and reasonably related to revenue production).","rule":"Under the taxing and spending clause, Congress can impose indirect taxes on goods or transactions as long as the taxes are (1) identical in every state and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This is true even if the taxes have no relation to the purpose for which the funds will be spent.","irac":{"issue":"Is this tax constitutional as applied to the issuance in the United States of an airline ticket for travel between two cities that will not be served by any of the new airports?","rule":"Under the taxing and spending clause, Congress can impose indirect taxes on goods or transactions as long as the taxes are (1) identical in every state and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This is true even if the taxes have no relation to the purpose for which the funds will be spent.","analysis":"Congressional taxing power\n\nType of Definition Constitutional requirements tax\n\nDirect\n• Taxes on persons or their Allowed if: property\n• apportioned\n• Payment cannot be avoided proportionately among states\n• reasonably related to revenue production Indirect\n• Taxes on goods/transactions Allowed if:\n• Payment can be avoided or shifted\n• identical taxation in every to another (eg, consumer) state\n• reasonably related to revenue production Export\n• Taxes on goods/services leaving Not allowed the country The taxing and spending clause grants Congress the broad power to impose indirect taxes on the subject of its choosing. The taxed subject need not have any relation to the purpose for which the tax funds will be spent. Instead, an indirect tax is constitutional as long as it is (1) identical in every state and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. Here, Congress has imposed a $25 tax on airline tickets. This indirect tax is identical in every state since it applies to all airline tickets issued in the U.S. And the tax is reasonably related to raising revenues for a system of new major airports near the 10 largest cities. Therefore, this tax is constitutional—even as applied to airline tickets for travel between cities that will not be served by the new airports. (Choice A) Courts do not weigh the burdens and benefits of a tax to determine its constitutionality. (Choice B) The Fifth Amendment due process clause generally prohibits the federal government from substantially impairing persons' fundamental rights (eg, the right to travel). And though a tax on airline tickets may affect the right to travel, a minimal tax of $25 would not substantially impair this right. (Choice C) This type of exemption—which does not appear in the tax levied by Congress— may raise equal protection concerns since it treats similarly situated passengers differently. But the exemption would not deny passengers equal protection of the laws\n\nsince it (1) does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class and (2) would likely pass rational basis scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because Congress has broad discretion in choosing the subjects of its taxation and may impose taxes on subjects that have no relation to the purpose for which those tax funds will be expended."},"questionZh":"为了为美国 10 个最大城市附近的新主要机场系统提供资金，国会对美国境内发行的每张机票征收 25 美元的税。该税适用于每张机票，即使是那些并非始发、终止或途经这 10 个大城市中任何一个的旅行机票。对于在美国发行在两个城市之间旅行且不会由任何新机场提供服务的机票，这种税收是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为对于旅行并非始发地、终止地或途经 10 个最大城市中任何一个的乘客来说，税收负担超过了其收益。","B":"不可以，因为税收会对基本旅行权利产生不利影响。","C":"是的，因为对于新机场不提供服务的城市之间旅行的机票的发行豁免将使所有其他机票的购买者无法获得法律的平等保护。","D":"是的，因为国会在选择征税对象方面拥有广泛的自由裁量权，并且可以对与这些税收资金的支出目的无关的对象征税。"},"explanationZh":"国会征税权\n\n定义类型 宪法要求税\n\n直接\n• 对个人或其财产征税 允许的情况：\n• 分配的\n• 各州之间无法按比例避免付款\n• 与收入产生合理相关 间接\n• 在以下情况下允许对商品/交易征税：\n• 付款可以避免或转移\n• 每个州（例如消费者）的税收相同\n• 与收入生产合理相关 出口\n• 对离开该国的商品/服务征税 不允许 征税和支出条款赋予国会对其选择的主体征收间接税的广泛权力。纳税主体不必与税收资金的用途有任何关系。相反，只要间接税（1）在每个州都是相同的并且（2）与收入生产合理相关，那么它就是符合宪法的。在这里，国会对机票征收 25 美元的税。这种间接税在每个州都是相同的，因为它适用于在美国发行的所有机票，并且该税与提高 10 个最大城市附近的新主要机场系统的收入合理相关。因此，这项税收是符合宪法的——即使适用于新机场不提供服务的城市之间旅行的机票。 （选择 A）法院不会权衡税收的负担和收益来确定其是否符合宪法。 （选项B） 第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）一般禁止联邦政府实质性损害个人的基本权利（例如旅行权）。尽管机票税可能会影响旅行权，但 25 美元的最低税不会严重损害这一权利。 （选择 C）这种类型的豁免（不会出现在国会征收的税款中）可能会引起平等保护问题，因为它对类似情况的乘客有不同的对待。但豁免不会剥夺乘客受到法律的平等保护\n\n因为它（1）不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，并且（2）可能会通过合理基础的审查。\n\n教育目标：根据税收和支出条款，国会可以对商品或交易征收间接税，只要税收（1）每个州的税收相同并且（2）与收入生产合理相关。即使税收与资金的用途无关，情况也是如此。\n\n参考文献\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 355–63 (1945)（认为国税局遗产税是符合宪法的，因为它是间接的，在每个州都是相同的，并且与收入生产合理相关）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据税收和支出条款，国会可以对商品或交易征收间接税，只要税收（1）每个州的税收相同并且（2）与收入生产合理相关。即使税收与资金的用途无关，情况也是如此。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于在美国发行在两个城市之间旅行且不会由任何新机场提供服务的机票，这种税收是否符合宪法？","rule":"根据税收和支出条款，国会可以对商品或交易征收间接税，只要税收（1）每个州的税收相同并且（2）与收入生产合理相关。即使税收与资金的用途无关，情况也是如此。","analysis":"国会征税权\n\n定义类型 宪法要求税\n\n直接\n• 对个人或其财产征税 允许的情况：\n• 分配的\n• 各州之间无法按比例避免付款\n• 与收入产生合理相关 间接\n• 在以下情况下允许对商品/交易征税：\n• 付款可以避免或转移\n• 每个州（例如消费者）的税收相同\n• 与收入生产合理相关 出口\n• 对离开该国的商品/服务征税 不允许 征税和支出条款赋予国会对其选择的主体征收间接税的广泛权力。纳税主体不必与税收资金的用途有任何关系。相反，只要间接税（1）在每个州都是相同的并且（2）与收入生产合理相关，那么它就是符合宪法的。在这里，国会对机票征收 25 美元的税。这种间接税在每个州都是相同的，因为它适用于在美国发行的所有机票，并且该税与提高 10 个最大城市附近的新主要机场系统的收入合理相关。因此，这项税收是符合宪法的——即使适用于新机场不提供服务的城市之间旅行的机票。 （选择 A）法院不会权衡税收的负担和收益来确定其是否符合宪法。 （选项B） 第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）一般禁止联邦政府实质性损害个人的基本权利（例如旅行权）。尽管机票税可能会影响旅行权，但 25 美元的最低税不会严重损害这一权利。 （选择 C）这种类型的豁免（不会出现在国会征收的税款中）可能会引起平等保护问题，因为它对类似情况的乘客有不同的对待。但豁免不会剥夺乘客受到法律的平等保护\n\n因为它（1）不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，并且（2）可能会通过合理基础的审查。\n\n教育目标：根据税收和支出条款，国会可以对商品或交易征收间接税，只要税收（1）每个州的税收相同并且（2）与收入生产合理相关。即使税收与资金的用途无关，情况也是如此。\n\n参考文献\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 355–63 (1945)（认为国税局遗产税是符合宪法的，因为它是间接的，在每个州都是相同的，并且与收入生产合理相关）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为国会在选择征税对象方面拥有广泛的自由裁量权，并且可以对与这些税收资金的支出目的无关的对象征税。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":122,"sourceQuestionPage":365,"sourceQuestionPages":[365],"sourceExplanationPages":[366,367],"sourceAnswerPages":[366,367],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-123","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Standing","question":"In order to reduce the federal deficit, Congress passed, and the President signed into law, a statute that grants the President line-item veto power. Pursuant to this power, the President has the authority to cancel specific spending and tax-benefit measures that have been enacted into law. Several members of Congress who voted against the legislation have sued the appropriate federal officials in a federal district court. The members of Congress claim that the statute is unconstitutional because it violates the requirements of the federal legislative process. The federal officials move to dismiss the suit. Which of the following is the most appropriate basis for the court to dismiss this suit?","choices":{"A":"The Eleventh Amendment bars suits of this kind in federal court.","B":"The members of Congress do not have standing.","C":"The suit presents a nonjusticiable political question.","D":"The U.S. Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction over the action."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on one's own behalf)\n\nGeneral Plaintiff must prove: rule\n• injury-in-fact – concrete & particularized harm (actual or imminent)\n• causation – harm traceable to defendant's conduct AND\n• redressability – favorable judicial decision can remedy harm Citizen No standing as mere citizen\n\nLegislator Must prove personal stake in dispute & concrete injury to challenge constitutionality of government action\n\nTaxpayer Standing to challenge taxes owed—but not government spending unless:\n\n• challenging legislation enacted under taxing & spending power AND\n• legislation exceeds limits imposed by establishment clause Federal courts can only hear suits in which the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. A federal or state legislator has standing to challenge the constitutionality of government action in the rare instance that the action caused the legislator personal and concrete harm—eg, by nullifying the legislator's vote. Institutional, abstract harm shared equally by all legislators in that respective body does not confer standing. Here, several members of Congress have challenged the constitutionality of the federal statute. However, the statute has not caused the members to suffer personal and concrete injuries since they were able to vote against the statute and can vote to repeal it in the future. Instead, they are asserting institutional, abstract harm that is shared equally by all other members of Congress. Therefore, the most appropriate basis to dismiss the suit is that the members of Congress lack standing.* *In Clinton v. City of New York, the U.S. Supreme Court held that plaintiffs who would lose funding or tax benefits have standing to challenge line-item veto power, which was subsequently found unconstitutional as a violation of the federal legislative process. (Choice A) The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits suits in federal court against state governments—not federal officials (as seen here). (Choice C) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from adjudicating issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since federal courts regularly determine the constitutionality of federal laws, this doctrine does not apply here.\n\n(Choice D) The U.S. Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction over suits between states (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A legislator has standing to challenge the constitutionality of government action when that action causes the legislator personal and concrete harm—not institutional, abstract harm shared equally by all legislators in that respective body.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• Raines v. Byrd, 521 U.S. 811, 829–30 (1997) (holding that members of Congress lack standing when the challenged government action causes an institutional and abstract harm instead of personal and concrete harm).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 153 (2020) (explaining legislator standing requirements).","educationalObjective":"A legislator has standing to challenge the constitutionality of government action when that action causes the legislator personal and concrete harm—not institutional, abstract harm shared equally by all legislators in that respective body.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. III, § 2 (case-or-controversy requirement).\n\n• Raines v. Byrd, 521 U.S. 811, 829–30 (1997) (holding that members of Congress lack standing when the challenged government action causes an institutional and abstract harm instead of personal and concrete harm).\n\n• 32 Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 153 (2020) (explaining legislator standing requirements).","rule":"A legislator has standing to challenge the constitutionality of government action when that action causes the legislator personal and concrete harm—not institutional, abstract harm shared equally by all legislators in that respective body.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most appropriate basis for the court to dismiss this suit?","rule":"A legislator has standing to challenge the constitutionality of government action when that action causes the legislator personal and concrete harm—not institutional, abstract harm shared equally by all legislators in that respective body.","analysis":"Article III standing\n\n(suing on one's own behalf)\n\nGeneral Plaintiff must prove: rule\n• injury-in-fact – concrete & particularized harm (actual or imminent)\n• causation – harm traceable to defendant's conduct AND\n• redressability – favorable judicial decision can remedy harm Citizen No standing as mere citizen\n\nLegislator Must prove personal stake in dispute & concrete injury to challenge constitutionality of government action\n\nTaxpayer Standing to challenge taxes owed—but not government spending unless:\n\n• challenging legislation enacted under taxing & spending power AND\n• legislation exceeds limits imposed by establishment clause Federal courts can only hear suits in which the plaintiff has standing—ie, a personal stake in the outcome of the case. A federal or state legislator has standing to challenge the constitutionality of government action in the rare instance that the action caused the legislator personal and concrete harm—eg, by nullifying the legislator's vote. Institutional, abstract harm shared equally by all legislators in that respective body does not confer standing. Here, several members of Congress have challenged the constitutionality of the federal statute. However, the statute has not caused the members to suffer personal and concrete injuries since they were able to vote against the statute and can vote to repeal it in the future. Instead, they are asserting institutional, abstract harm that is shared equally by all other members of Congress. Therefore, the most appropriate basis to dismiss the suit is that the members of Congress lack standing.* *In Clinton v. City of New York, the U.S. Supreme Court held that plaintiffs who would lose funding or tax benefits have standing to challenge line-item veto power, which was subsequently found unconstitutional as a violation of the federal legislative process. (Choice A) The Eleventh Amendment generally prohibits suits in federal court against state governments—not federal officials (as seen here). (Choice C) The political-question doctrine prevents federal courts from adjudicating issues that (1) are reserved to the executive or legislative branch or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since federal courts regularly determine the constitutionality of federal laws, this doctrine does not apply here.\n\n(Choice D) The U.S. Supreme Court has exclusive jurisdiction over suits between states (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: The members of Congress do not have standing."},"questionZh":"为了减少联邦赤字，国会通过了一项赋予总统单项否决权的法规，并由总统签署成为法律。根据这项权力，总统有权取消已颁布为法律的具体支出和税收优惠措施。几位投票反对该立法的国会议员已在联邦地区法院起诉相应的联邦官员。国会议员声称该法规违宪，因为它违反了联邦立法程序的要求。联邦官员决定驳回诉讼。以下哪一项是法院驳回本案的最适当的依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"第十一条修正案禁止在联邦法院提起此类诉讼。","B":"国会议员没有地位。","C":"该诉讼提出了一个不可审理的政治问题。","D":"美国最高法院对此诉讼拥有专属管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"第三条 地位\n\n（以自己的名义提起诉讼）\n\n一般原告必须证明：规则\n• 事实伤害——具体和特殊的伤害（实际的或迫在眉睫的）\n• 因果关系——伤害可追溯到被告的行为并且\n• 可补救性——有利的司法判决可以补救伤害 公民 不具备单纯公民的资格\n\n立法者必须证明个人在争议和具体伤害中的利害关系，以质疑政府行为的合宪性\n\n纳税人有权质疑所欠税款，但不能质疑政府支出，除非：\n\n• 挑战根据税收和消费能力颁布的立法以及\n• 立法超出了设立条款所施加的限制 联邦法院只能审理原告有资格的诉讼——即，与案件结果有个人利害关系的诉讼。在极少数情况下，联邦或州立法者有权质疑政府行为的合宪性，即政府行为对立法者造成个人和具体伤害（例如，取消立法者的投票）。由各自机构中的所有立法者平等分担的制度性、抽象伤害并不赋予地位。在这里，几位国会议员对联邦法规的合宪性提出了质疑。然而，该法规并没有导致会员遭受人身和具体伤害，因为他们可以投票反对该法规，并可以在未来投票废除该法规。相反，他们声称国会所有其他成员都平等地承受了制度性的、抽象的伤害。因此，驳回诉讼最合适的依据是国会议员缺乏资格。* *在克林顿诉纽约市一案中，美国最高法院认为，将失去资金或税收优惠的原告有资格挑战单项否决权，该决定随后被认定为违宪，违反了联邦立法程序。 （选项 A）第十一修正案一般禁止在联邦法院针对州政府而非联邦官员提起诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项 C）政治问题原则阻止联邦法院裁决以下问题：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于联邦法院定期确定联邦法律的合宪性，因此这一原则不适用于此处。\n\n（选项 D）美国最高法院对各州之间的诉讼拥有专属管辖权（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标：当政府行为对立法者造成个人和具体伤害，而不是该机构中所有立法者平等分担的制度性、抽象伤害时，立法者有权质疑政府行为的合宪性。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Raines v. Byrd, 521 U.S. 811, 829-30 (1997)（认为当受到质疑的政府行为造成制度性和抽象性损害而不是个人和具体损害时，国会议员缺乏地位）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 153 条 (2020)（解释立法者资格要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当政府行为对立法者造成个人和具体伤害，而不是由该机构的所有立法者平等分担的制度性、抽象伤害时，立法者就有资格质疑政府行为的合宪性。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是法院驳回本案的最适当的依据？","rule":"当政府行为对立法者造成个人和具体伤害，而不是由该机构的所有立法者平等分担的制度性、抽象伤害时，立法者就有资格质疑政府行为的合宪性。","analysis":"第三条 地位\n\n（以自己的名义提起诉讼）\n\n一般原告必须证明：规则\n• 事实伤害——具体和特殊的伤害（实际的或迫在眉睫的）\n• 因果关系——伤害可追溯到被告的行为并且\n• 可补救性——有利的司法判决可以补救伤害 公民 不具备单纯公民的资格\n\n立法者必须证明个人在争议和具体伤害中的利害关系，以质疑政府行为的合宪性\n\n纳税人有权质疑所欠税款，但不能质疑政府支出，除非：\n\n• 挑战根据税收和消费能力颁布的立法以及\n• 立法超出了设立条款所施加的限制 联邦法院只能审理原告有资格的诉讼——即，与案件结果有个人利害关系的诉讼。在极少数情况下，联邦或州立法者有权质疑政府行为的合宪性，即政府行为对立法者造成个人和具体伤害（例如，取消立法者的投票）。由各自机构中的所有立法者平等分担的制度性、抽象伤害并不赋予地位。在这里，几位国会议员对联邦法规的合宪性提出了质疑。然而，该法规并没有导致会员遭受人身和具体伤害，因为他们可以投票反对该法规，并可以在未来投票废除该法规。相反，他们声称国会所有其他成员都平等地承受了制度性的、抽象的伤害。因此，驳回诉讼最合适的依据是国会议员缺乏资格。* *在克林顿诉纽约市一案中，美国最高法院认为，将失去资金或税收优惠的原告有资格挑战单项否决权，该决定随后被认定为违宪，违反了联邦立法程序。 （选项 A）第十一修正案一般禁止在联邦法院针对州政府而非联邦官员提起诉讼（如此处所示）。 （选项 C）政治问题原则阻止联邦法院裁决以下问题：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于联邦法院定期确定联邦法律的合宪性，因此这一原则不适用于此处。\n\n（选项 D）美国最高法院对各州之间的诉讼拥有专属管辖权（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标：当政府行为对立法者造成个人和具体伤害，而不是该机构中所有立法者平等分担的制度性、抽象伤害时，立法者有权质疑政府行为的合宪性。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 III，§ 2（案例或争议要求）。\n\n• Raines v. Byrd, 521 U.S. 811, 829-30 (1997)（认为当受到质疑的政府行为造成制度性和抽象性损害而不是个人和具体损害时，国会议员缺乏地位）。\n\n• 上午 32 点。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 153 条 (2020)（解释立法者资格要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：国会议员没有地位。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":123,"sourceQuestionPage":368,"sourceQuestionPages":[368],"sourceExplanationPages":[369,370],"sourceAnswerPages":[369,370],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-124","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A certain mineral is added to bodies of fresh water to prevent the spread of freshwater parasites. The presence of those parasites threatens the health of the organisms living in rivers and streams throughout the country and imperils the freshwater commercial fishing industry. This mineral is currently mined only in State A. In order to raise needed revenue, Congress recently enacted a statute providing for the imposition of a $100 tax on each ton of the mineral mined in the United States. Because it will raise the cost of the mineral, this tax is likely to reduce the amount added to freshwater rivers and streams and, therefore, is likely to have an adverse effect on the interstate freshwater commercial fishing industry. The mineral producers in State A have filed a lawsuit in federal court challenging this tax solely on constitutional grounds. Is this tax constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it is likely to have an adverse effect on the freshwater commercial fishing industry and Congress has a responsibility under the commerce clause to protect, foster, and advance such interstate industries.","B":"No, because only producers in State A will pay the tax and, therefore, it is not uniform among the states and denies the mineral producers equal protection of the laws.","C":"Yes, because the power of Congress to impose taxes is plenary, this tax does not contain any provisions extraneous to tax needs or purposes, and it is not barred by any prohibitory language in the Constitution.","D":"Yes, because the tax is a necessary and proper means of exercising federal authority over the navigable waters of the United States."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Congressional taxing power\n\nType of Definition Constitutional requirements tax\n\nDirect\n• Taxes on persons or their Allowed if: property\n• apportioned\n• Payment cannot be avoided proportionately among states\n• reasonably related to revenue production Indirect\n• Taxes on goods/transactions Allowed if:\n• Payment can be avoided or shifted\n• identical taxation in every to another (eg, consumer) state\n• reasonably related to revenue production Export\n• Taxes on goods/services leaving Not allowed the country The taxing and spending clause gives Congress plenary (ie, exclusive) power to raise federal revenue by imposing taxes. But Congress can only impose an indirect federal tax if it is:\n• imposed identically in every state where the taxed goods are found (ie, geographically uniform) and\n• reasonably related to revenue production (ie, no provisions extraneous to tax purposes). Here, Congress imposed a $100 indirect tax on a mineral mined in the U.S. The tax is imposed uniformly in every state where the mineral is or may be found and does not contain provisions unrelated to the need for revenue production. Therefore, it complies with the taxing and spending clause. And though the tax only impacts the mineral producers in State A, there is no evidence of intentional discrimination that would raise equal protection concerns—or any other constitutional prohibition (Choice B). Therefore, the tax is constitutional. (Choice A) The commerce clause vests Congress with broad authority to regulate interstate commerce. But it does not obligate Congress to protect, foster, or advance interstate industries like freshwater commercial fishing.\n\n(Choice D) The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to enact legislation reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated powers (eg, taxation). But Congress can only use its taxing power to raise revenue—not to exercise authority over navigable waters.\n\nEducational objective: Indirect federal taxes comply with the taxing and spending clause so long as they are (1) identical in every state where the taxed goods are located and (2) reasonably related to revenue production.","educationalObjective":"Indirect federal taxes comply with the taxing and spending clause so long as they are (1) identical in every state where the taxed goods are located and (2) reasonably related to revenue production.","references":"","rule":"Indirect federal taxes comply with the taxing and spending clause so long as they are (1) identical in every state where the taxed goods are located and (2) reasonably related to revenue production.","irac":{"issue":"Is this tax constitutional?","rule":"Indirect federal taxes comply with the taxing and spending clause so long as they are (1) identical in every state where the taxed goods are located and (2) reasonably related to revenue production.","analysis":"Congressional taxing power\n\nType of Definition Constitutional requirements tax\n\nDirect\n• Taxes on persons or their Allowed if: property\n• apportioned\n• Payment cannot be avoided proportionately among states\n• reasonably related to revenue production Indirect\n• Taxes on goods/transactions Allowed if:\n• Payment can be avoided or shifted\n• identical taxation in every to another (eg, consumer) state\n• reasonably related to revenue production Export\n• Taxes on goods/services leaving Not allowed the country The taxing and spending clause gives Congress plenary (ie, exclusive) power to raise federal revenue by imposing taxes. But Congress can only impose an indirect federal tax if it is:\n• imposed identically in every state where the taxed goods are found (ie, geographically uniform) and\n• reasonably related to revenue production (ie, no provisions extraneous to tax purposes). Here, Congress imposed a $100 indirect tax on a mineral mined in the U.S. The tax is imposed uniformly in every state where the mineral is or may be found and does not contain provisions unrelated to the need for revenue production. Therefore, it complies with the taxing and spending clause. And though the tax only impacts the mineral producers in State A, there is no evidence of intentional discrimination that would raise equal protection concerns—or any other constitutional prohibition (Choice B). Therefore, the tax is constitutional. (Choice A) The commerce clause vests Congress with broad authority to regulate interstate commerce. But it does not obligate Congress to protect, foster, or advance interstate industries like freshwater commercial fishing.\n\n(Choice D) The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to enact legislation reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated powers (eg, taxation). But Congress can only use its taxing power to raise revenue—not to exercise authority over navigable waters.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the power of Congress to impose taxes is plenary, this tax does not contain any provisions extraneous to tax needs or purposes, and it is not barred by any prohibitory language in the Constitution."},"questionZh":"淡水中添加了某种矿物质，以防止淡水寄生虫的传播。这些寄生虫的存在威胁着全国河流和溪流中生物的健康，并危及淡水商业捕鱼业。这种矿物目前仅在 A 州开采。为了筹集所需的收入，国会最近颁布了一项法规，规定对在美国开采的每吨矿物征收 100 美元的税。由于该税会提高矿物成本，因此可能会减少淡水河流和溪流的添加量，因此可能对州际淡水商业捕鱼业产生不利影响。 A 州的矿产生产商已向联邦法院提起诉讼，仅以宪法为由质疑这项税收。这种税收符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这可能对淡水商业捕鱼业产生不利影响，并且根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause），国会有责任保护、培育和推动此类州际产业。","B":"不可以，因为只有 A 州的生产商才会缴纳税款，因此各州之间的规定并不统一，并且剥夺了矿产生产商受到法律的平等保护。","C":"是的，由于国会征税的权力是全权的，因此该税不包含任何与税收需要或目的无关的规定，并且不受宪法中任何禁止性语言的禁止。","D":"是的，因为税收是对美国通航水域行使联邦权力的必要且适当的手段。"},"explanationZh":"国会征税权\n\n定义类型 宪法要求税\n\n直接\n• 对个人或其财产征税 允许的情况：\n• 分配的\n• 各州之间无法按比例避免付款\n• 与收入产生合理相关 间接\n• 在以下情况下允许对商品/交易征税：\n• 付款可以避免或转移\n• 每个州（例如消费者）的税收相同\n• 与收入生产合理相关 出口\n• 对离开该国的商品/服务征税 不允许 征税和支出条款赋予国会通过征税来增加联邦收入的全部（即排他性）权力。但国会只能在以下情况下征收间接联邦税：\n• 在发现征税商品的每个州均征收相同的税款（即地理上统一）并且\n• 与收入产生合理相关（即没有与税收目的无关的规定）。在这里，国会对在美国开采的矿物征收 100 美元的间接税。该税在发现或可能发现该矿物的每个州统一征收，并且不包含与收入生产需求无关的条款。因此，它符合税收和支出条款。尽管该税收仅影响 A 州的矿产生产商，但没有证据表明故意歧视会引起平等保护问题或任何其他宪法禁止（选择 B）。因此，税收是符合宪法的。 （选项 A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。但它并没有要求国会保护、培育或推进淡水商业捕鱼等州际产业。\n\n（选项D）必要且适当的条款允许国会制定合理必要的立法以履行其列举的权力（例如税收）。但国会只能利用其征税权来增加收入，而不能对通航水域行使权力。\n\n教育目标：间接联邦税符合征税和支出条款，只要它们 (1) 在征税商品所在的每个州都是相同的，并且 (2) 与收入生产合理相关。","educationalObjectiveZh":"间接联邦税符合征税和支出条款，只要它们 (1) 在征税商品所在的每个州都是相同的，并且 (2) 与收入生产合理相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"这种税收符合宪法吗？","rule":"间接联邦税符合征税和支出条款，只要它们 (1) 在征税商品所在的每个州都是相同的，并且 (2) 与收入生产合理相关。","analysis":"国会征税权\n\n定义类型 宪法要求税\n\n直接\n• 对个人或其财产征税 允许的情况：\n• 分配的\n• 各州之间无法按比例避免付款\n• 与收入产生合理相关 间接\n• 在以下情况下允许对商品/交易征税：\n• 付款可以避免或转移\n• 每个州（例如消费者）的税收相同\n• 与收入生产合理相关 出口\n• 对离开该国的商品/服务征税 不允许 征税和支出条款赋予国会通过征税来增加联邦收入的全部（即排他性）权力。但国会只能在以下情况下征收间接联邦税：\n• 在发现征税商品的每个州均征收相同的税款（即地理上统一）并且\n• 与收入产生合理相关（即没有与税收目的无关的规定）。在这里，国会对在美国开采的矿物征收 100 美元的间接税。该税在发现或可能发现该矿物的每个州统一征收，并且不包含与收入生产需求无关的条款。因此，它符合税收和支出条款。尽管该税收仅影响 A 州的矿产生产商，但没有证据表明故意歧视会引起平等保护问题或任何其他宪法禁止（选择 B）。因此，税收是符合宪法的。 （选项 A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际商业。但它并没有要求国会保护、培育或推进淡水商业捕鱼等州际产业。\n\n（选项D）必要且适当的条款允许国会制定合理必要的立法以履行其列举的权力（例如税收）。但国会只能利用其征税权来增加收入，而不能对通航水域行使权力。\n\n教育目标：间接联邦税符合征税和支出条款，只要它们 (1) 在征税商品所在的每个州都是相同的，并且 (2) 与收入生产合理相关。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，由于国会征税的权力是全权的，因此该税不包含任何与税收需要或目的无关的规定，并且不受宪法中任何禁止性语言的禁止。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":124,"sourceQuestionPage":371,"sourceQuestionPages":[371],"sourceExplanationPages":[372,373],"sourceAnswerPages":[372,373],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-125","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"A federal statute requires any individual or entity owning more than 100 cars to ensure that at least 10 percent of those cars are electric-powered. A city that owns over 500 cars has sued the federal official responsible for enforcing this statute in federal district court, seeking an injunction prohibiting enforcement of the statute on the ground that it is unconstitutional. Should the court grant the injunction?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal government has sovereign immunity and cannot be sued without its explicit consent.","B":"No, because the statute is valid under the commerce clause and does not violate the Tenth Amendment.","C":"Yes, because as applied to state and local governments, the statute exceeds Congress's power under the commerce clause.","D":"Yes, because the statute violates the reserved rights of the states under the Tenth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The commerce clause grants Congress broad power to regulate interstate commerce, including:\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons/things moving in interstate commerce and\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce. But Congress must comply with other constitutional provisions when exercising its commerce power. This includes the Tenth Amendment, which prohibits Congress from commandeering state or local governments by requiring them to (1) enforce a federal law or (2) enact a state or local law. Here, Congress enacted a statute requiring individuals and entities owning over 100 cars (like the city) to ensure that at least 10 percent of those cars are electric-powered. This is a valid exercise of Congress's commerce power since cars are instrumentalities of interstate commerce (Choice C). And since the statute does not require the city to enforce it against others or enact related laws, the statute does not violate the Tenth Amendment. Therefore, the court should deny the injunction(Choice D). (Choice A) The federal government enjoys sovereign immunity from suits seeking monetary damages. But this immunity does not extend to suits seeking nonmonetary relief—eg, an injunction (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Congress has broad power to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment limits this power by prohibiting Congress from requiring state or local governments to (1) enforce a federal law or (2) enact a state or local law.\n\nReferences\n• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 149–51 (2000) (holding that a federal statute regulating states' commercial activity does not violate the Tenth Amendment since it does not require states to enact or enforce any laws).","educationalObjective":"Congress has broad power to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment limits this power by prohibiting Congress from requiring state or local governments to (1) enforce a federal law or (2) enact a state or local law.","references":"• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 149–51 (2000) (holding that a federal statute regulating states' commercial activity does not violate the Tenth Amendment since it does not require states to enact or enforce any laws).","rule":"Congress has broad power to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment limits this power by prohibiting Congress from requiring state or local governments to (1) enforce a federal law or (2) enact a state or local law.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the injunction?","rule":"Congress has broad power to regulate interstate commerce. But the Tenth Amendment limits this power by prohibiting Congress from requiring state or local governments to (1) enforce a federal law or (2) enact a state or local law.","analysis":"The commerce clause grants Congress broad power to regulate interstate commerce, including:\n• channels of interstate commerce\n• instrumentalities of interstate commerce\n• persons/things moving in interstate commerce and\n• activities that substantially affect interstate commerce. But Congress must comply with other constitutional provisions when exercising its commerce power. This includes the Tenth Amendment, which prohibits Congress from commandeering state or local governments by requiring them to (1) enforce a federal law or (2) enact a state or local law. Here, Congress enacted a statute requiring individuals and entities owning over 100 cars (like the city) to ensure that at least 10 percent of those cars are electric-powered. This is a valid exercise of Congress's commerce power since cars are instrumentalities of interstate commerce (Choice C). And since the statute does not require the city to enforce it against others or enact related laws, the statute does not violate the Tenth Amendment. Therefore, the court should deny the injunction(Choice D). (Choice A) The federal government enjoys sovereign immunity from suits seeking monetary damages. But this immunity does not extend to suits seeking nonmonetary relief—eg, an injunction (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the statute is valid under the commerce clause and does not violate the Tenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"联邦法规要求拥有 100 辆以上汽车的任何个人或实体必须确保其中至少 10% 是电动汽车。一个拥有超过 500 辆汽车的城市已向联邦地区法院起诉负责执行该法规的联邦官员，请求颁布禁令，以该法规违宪为由禁止执行该法规。法院是否应该发出禁令？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为联邦政府拥有主权豁免权，未经其明确同意不得起诉。","B":"不，因为该法规根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）有效，并且不违反第十修正案。","C":"是的，因为适用于州和地方政府时，该法规超出了国会在商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）下的权力。","D":"是的，因为该法规侵犯了第十修正案赋予各州的保留权利。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的广泛权力，包括：\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 在州际贸易中移动的人/物以及\n• 严重影响州际贸易的活动。但国会在行使商业权力时必须遵守其他宪法条款。这包括第十修正案，该修正案禁止国会通过要求州或地方政府（1）执行联邦法律或（2）制定州或地方法律来指挥州或地方政府。在这里，国会颁布了一项法规，要求拥有 100 辆以上汽车的个人和实体（例如城市）确保其中至少 10% 是电动汽车。这是国会商业权力的有效行使，因为汽车是州际商业的工具（选项 C）。而且由于该法规没有要求该市对他人强制执行或颁布相关法律，因此该法规并不违反第十修正案。因此，法院应驳回该禁令（选择D）。 （选择 A） 联邦政府享有主权豁免权，免受寻求金钱赔偿的诉讼。但这种豁免权并不适用于寻求非金钱救济的诉讼，例如禁令（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有监管州际贸易的广泛权力。但第十修正案限制了这一权力，禁止国会要求州或地方政府（1）执行联邦法律或（2）颁布州或地方法律。\n\n参考文献\n• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 149–51 (2000)（认为规范各州商业活动的联邦法规不违反第十修正案，因为它不要求各州颁布或执行任何法律）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会拥有监管州际贸易的广泛权力。但第十修正案限制了这一权力，禁止国会要求州或地方政府（1）执行联邦法律或（2）颁布州或地方法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该发出禁令？","rule":"国会拥有监管州际贸易的广泛权力。但第十修正案限制了这一权力，禁止国会要求州或地方政府（1）执行联邦法律或（2）颁布州或地方法律。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会监管州际商业的广泛权力，包括：\n• 州际贸易渠道\n• 州际贸易的工具\n• 在州际贸易中移动的人/物以及\n• 严重影响州际贸易的活动。但国会在行使商业权力时必须遵守其他宪法条款。这包括第十修正案，该修正案禁止国会通过要求州或地方政府（1）执行联邦法律或（2）制定州或地方法律来指挥州或地方政府。在这里，国会颁布了一项法规，要求拥有 100 辆以上汽车的个人和实体（例如城市）确保其中至少 10% 是电动汽车。这是国会商业权力的有效行使，因为汽车是州际商业的工具（选项 C）。而且由于该法规没有要求该市对他人强制执行或颁布相关法律，因此该法规并不违反第十修正案。因此，法院应驳回该禁令（选择D）。 （选择 A） 联邦政府享有主权豁免权，免受寻求金钱赔偿的诉讼。但这种豁免权并不适用于寻求非金钱救济的诉讼，例如禁令（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有监管州际贸易的广泛权力。但第十修正案限制了这一权力，禁止国会要求州或地方政府（1）执行联邦法律或（2）颁布州或地方法律。\n\n参考文献\n• Reno v. Condon, 528 U.S. 141, 149–51 (2000)（认为规范各州商业活动的联邦法规不违反第十修正案，因为它不要求各州颁布或执行任何法律）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为该法规根据商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）有效，并且不违反第十修正案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-3570284481164771"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":125,"sourceQuestionPage":374,"sourceQuestionPages":[374],"sourceExplanationPages":[375,376],"sourceAnswerPages":[375,376],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-126","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Commerce Clause","question":"A congressional committee investigated the pharmaceutical industry and found that the high cost of prescription drugs purchased and sold in the United States negatively impacted the nation's economy and the health of its citizens. In response, Congress passed a statute that regulates \"the retail prices of every purchase or sale of prescription drugs in the United States.\" A group of pharmaceutical companies challenged the constitutionality of this statute in federal court. What is the strongest argument in support of the constitutionality of this statute?","choices":{"A":"Congress may enact statutes for the general welfare.","B":"Congress may regulate the prices of all domestic purchases and sales of goods.","C":"The Constitution grants Congress the power to regulate the interstate transportation of prescription drugs.","D":"The purchases and sales of prescription drugs in the United States substantially impact interstate commerce in the aggregate."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate interstate and foreign commerce. This includes:\n• the channels of interstate and foreign commerce (eg, roadways)\n• the instrumentalities of interstate and foreign commerce (eg, vehicles)\n• persons and things moving in interstate or foreign commerce (eg, goods and services) and\n• in-state activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially impact interstate or foreign commerce. Since Congress's commerce power is broad, federal statutes are constitutional if there is any rational basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate or foreign commerce. This can be shown through express congressional findings. Here, the federal statute regulates the retail prices of prescription drugs in the United States. Congress has the authority to regulate such products' interstate transportation, but this statute also regulates in-state purchases and sales (Choice C). Since the congressional committee found that the high cost of prescription drugs negatively impacted the nation's economy, it is rational to conclude that their aggregated in-state purchases and sales substantially impact interstate commerce. Therefore, this is the strongest argument to support this statute. (Choice A) The taxing and spending clause empowers Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare. But regulating prices is not equivalent to taxing or spending. (Choice B) Congress cannot regulate the prices of every domestic purchase and sale of goods since it cannot regulate purely in-state sales that do not substantially affect interstate commerce.\n\nEducational objective: The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate (1) channels and instrumentalities of, (2) persons and things moving in, and (3) in-state activities that—singly or in the aggregate—substantially affect interstate or foreign commerce.\n\nReferences\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005) (explaining Congress's broad authority under the commerce clause).","educationalObjective":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate (1) channels and instrumentalities of, (2) persons and things moving in, and (3) in-state activities that—singly or in the aggregate—substantially affect interstate or foreign commerce.","references":"• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005) (explaining Congress's broad authority under the commerce clause).","rule":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate (1) channels and instrumentalities of, (2) persons and things moving in, and (3) in-state activities that—singly or in the aggregate—substantially affect interstate or foreign commerce.","irac":{"issue":"What is the strongest argument in support of the constitutionality of this statute?","rule":"The commerce clause empowers Congress to regulate (1) channels and instrumentalities of, (2) persons and things moving in, and (3) in-state activities that—singly or in the aggregate—substantially affect interstate or foreign commerce.","analysis":"The commerce clause gives Congress broad power to regulate interstate and foreign commerce. This includes:\n• the channels of interstate and foreign commerce (eg, roadways)\n• the instrumentalities of interstate and foreign commerce (eg, vehicles)\n• persons and things moving in interstate or foreign commerce (eg, goods and services) and\n• in-state activities that, singly or in the aggregate, substantially impact interstate or foreign commerce. Since Congress's commerce power is broad, federal statutes are constitutional if there is any rational basis for concluding that the regulated activity substantially affects interstate or foreign commerce. This can be shown through express congressional findings. Here, the federal statute regulates the retail prices of prescription drugs in the United States. Congress has the authority to regulate such products' interstate transportation, but this statute also regulates in-state purchases and sales (Choice C). Since the congressional committee found that the high cost of prescription drugs negatively impacted the nation's economy, it is rational to conclude that their aggregated in-state purchases and sales substantially impact interstate commerce. Therefore, this is the strongest argument to support this statute. (Choice A) The taxing and spending clause empowers Congress to tax and spend for the general welfare. But regulating prices is not equivalent to taxing or spending. (Choice B) Congress cannot regulate the prices of every domestic purchase and sale of goods since it cannot regulate purely in-state sales that do not substantially affect interstate commerce.","conclusion":"Answer D: The purchases and sales of prescription drugs in the United States substantially impact interstate commerce in the aggregate."},"questionZh":"国会委员会对制药行业进行了调查，发现在美国购买和销售处方药的高昂成本对国家经济和公民健康产生了负面影响。作为回应，国会通过了一项法规，规范“美国每次购买或销售处方药的零售价格”。一些制药公司在联邦法院质疑该法规的合宪性。支持该法规合宪性的最有力论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"国会可以制定公共福利法规。","B":"国会可以规范所有国内商品采购和销售的价格。","C":"宪法赋予国会监管处方药州际运输的权力。","D":"美国处方药的购买和销售总体上对州际贸易产生了重大影响。"},"explanationZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际和外国商业。这包括：\n• 州际和国外商业渠道（例如公路）\n• 州际和对外贸易的工具（例如车辆）\n• 在州际或对外贸易中流动的人和事物（例如，商品和服务）以及\n• 州内活动，无论是单独还是总体，都会对州际或国外商业产生重大影响。由于国会的商业权力很广泛，如果有合理的依据得出结论认为受监管的活动对州际或外国商业产生重大影响，联邦法规就是合宪的。这可以通过国会的明确调查结果来证明。在这里，联邦法规规范美国处方药的零售价格。国会有权规范此类产品的州际运输，但该法规还规范州内的购买和销售（选项 C）。由于国会委员会发现处方药的高成本对国家经济产生了负面影响，因此可以合理地得出结论，州内购买和销售的总和对州际贸易产生了重大影响。因此，这是支持该法规最有力的论据。 （选项 A） 征税和支出条款授权国会为公共福利征税和支出。但监管价格并不等同于征税或支出。 （选择 B）国会无法监管每项国内商品买卖的价格，因为它无法监管不会对州际贸易产生重大影响的纯粹州内销售。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管（1）渠道和工具，（2）人员和物品进入，以及（3）州内活动（单独或总体）对州际或外国商业产生重大影响。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005)（解释了国会在商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）下的广泛权力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管（1）渠道和工具，（2）进入的人员和物品，以及（3）对州际或外国商业产生重大影响的州内活动（无论是单独的还是总体的）。","iracZh":{"issue":"支持该法规合宪性的最有力论据是什么？","rule":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管（1）渠道和工具，（2）进入的人员和物品，以及（3）对州际或外国商业产生重大影响的州内活动（无论是单独的还是总体的）。","analysis":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的权力来监管州际和外国商业。这包括：\n• 州际和国外商业渠道（例如公路）\n• 州际和对外贸易的工具（例如车辆）\n• 在州际或对外贸易中流动的人和事物（例如，商品和服务）以及\n• 州内活动，无论是单独还是总体，都会对州际或国外商业产生重大影响。由于国会的商业权力很广泛，如果有合理的依据得出结论认为受监管的活动对州际或外国商业产生重大影响，联邦法规就是合宪的。这可以通过国会的明确调查结果来证明。在这里，联邦法规规范美国处方药的零售价格。国会有权规范此类产品的州际运输，但该法规还规范州内的购买和销售（选项 C）。由于国会委员会发现处方药的高成本对国家经济产生了负面影响，因此可以合理地得出结论，州内购买和销售的总和对州际贸易产生了重大影响。因此，这是支持该法规最有力的论据。 （选项 A） 征税和支出条款授权国会为公共福利征税和支出。但监管价格并不等同于征税或支出。 （选择 B）国会无法监管每项国内商品买卖的价格，因为它无法监管不会对州际贸易产生重大影响的纯粹州内销售。\n\n教育目标：商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）授权国会监管（1）渠道和工具，（2）人员和物品进入，以及（3）州内活动（单独或总体）对州际或外国商业产生重大影响。\n\n参考文献\n• Gonzales v. Raich, 545 U.S. 1, 17 (2005)（解释了国会在商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）下的广泛权力）。","conclusion":"答案 D：美国处方药的购买和销售总体上对州际贸易产生了重大影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-9e02bd68d0baa40f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":126,"sourceQuestionPage":377,"sourceQuestionPages":[377],"sourceExplanationPages":[378,379],"sourceAnswerPages":[378,379],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-127","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"Congress enacted a statute that requires all males between the ages of 18 and 30 to register with the Selective Service Agency, which is responsible for drafting individuals to serve in the armed forces in the event of war. Males who fail to register are not eligible to receive federal student aid for college. An 18-year-old male refused to register and was denied federal student aid pursuant to the statute. The student challenged the federal statute in federal court, arguing that the statute discriminated on the basis of sex and age. Is the statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the statute violates the due process clause of the Fifth Amendment.","B":"No, because the statute violates the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"Yes, because Congress has broad power to raise and regulate the armed forces.","D":"Yes, because federal student aid for college is a privilege, not a right."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. As a result, Congress may establish and require citizens to register for a military draft to carry out its enumerated power to raise and regulate the U.S. armed forces.* However, Congress must still comply with other constitutional provisions when exercising this broad power, including the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment due process clause. This component applies when the federal government discriminates against similarly situated persons. Sex-based discrimination is subject to intermediate scrutiny, which requires the government to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest. This standard is met here because the U.S. Supreme Court held in Rostker v. Goldberg that a male-only requirement for military draft registration is substantially related to the government's important interest in national defense. Age-based discrimination is subject to mere rational basis scrutiny, under which the challenger need only show that the challenged government action is not related to a legitimate government interest. Since the government will be able to overcome the higher hurdle of intermediate scrutiny to justify its sex-based discrimination, the student's age discrimination claim will also fail. Therefore, the statute complies with the Fifth Amendment due process clause and is constitutional (Choice A). *Congress also has the power to declare war and authorize the President to call state National Guard units into action without the approval of the state governor to execute federal laws, suppress insurrections, and repel invasions. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause applies to discrimination by state governments, not the federal government. (Choice D) Due process protections historically extended to rights—not privileges. But this distinction has been abandoned, so it is irrelevant whether federal student aid for college is a privilege or a right.\n\nEducational objective: Congress has broad power to raise and regulate the U.S. armed forces, including establishing and requiring citizens to register for a military draft. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment by restricting registration to males in a specified age range.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (power to raise and regulate armed forces).\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 83 (1981) (holding that Congress can use its broad authority to raise and regulate the armed forces to require only males in a certain age range to register for a military draft).","educationalObjective":"Congress has broad power to raise and regulate the U.S. armed forces, including establishing and requiring citizens to register for a military draft. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment by restricting registration to males in a specified age range.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (power to raise and regulate armed forces).\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 83 (1981) (holding that Congress can use its broad authority to raise and regulate the armed forces to require only males in a certain age range to register for a military draft).","rule":"Congress has broad power to raise and regulate the U.S. armed forces, including establishing and requiring citizens to register for a military draft. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment by restricting registration to males in a specified age range.","irac":{"issue":"Is the statute constitutional?","rule":"Congress has broad power to raise and regulate the U.S. armed forces, including establishing and requiring citizens to register for a military draft. When exercising this power, Congress does not violate the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment by restricting registration to males in a specified age range.","analysis":"The necessary and proper clause allows Congress to take actions that are reasonably necessary to carry out its enumerated Article I powers. As a result, Congress may establish and require citizens to register for a military draft to carry out its enumerated power to raise and regulate the U.S. armed forces.* However, Congress must still comply with other constitutional provisions when exercising this broad power, including the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment due process clause. This component applies when the federal government discriminates against similarly situated persons. Sex-based discrimination is subject to intermediate scrutiny, which requires the government to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest. This standard is met here because the U.S. Supreme Court held in Rostker v. Goldberg that a male-only requirement for military draft registration is substantially related to the government's important interest in national defense. Age-based discrimination is subject to mere rational basis scrutiny, under which the challenger need only show that the challenged government action is not related to a legitimate government interest. Since the government will be able to overcome the higher hurdle of intermediate scrutiny to justify its sex-based discrimination, the student's age discrimination claim will also fail. Therefore, the statute complies with the Fifth Amendment due process clause and is constitutional (Choice A). *Congress also has the power to declare war and authorize the President to call state National Guard units into action without the approval of the state governor to execute federal laws, suppress insurrections, and repel invasions. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause applies to discrimination by state governments, not the federal government. (Choice D) Due process protections historically extended to rights—not privileges. But this distinction has been abandoned, so it is irrelevant whether federal student aid for college is a privilege or a right.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because Congress has broad power to raise and regulate the armed forces."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，要求所有 18 岁至 30 岁之间的男性在义务兵役局登记，该机构负责征召个人在发生战争时加入武装部队。未能注册的男性没有资格获得联邦大学学生资助。一名 18 岁男性拒绝注册，并根据法规被拒绝获得联邦学生援助。该学生在联邦法院对联邦法规提出质疑，认为该法规存在性别和年龄歧视。该法规是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该法规违反了第五修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","B":"不可以，因为该法规违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。","C":"是的，因为国会拥有广泛的权力来组建和管理武装部队。","D":"是的，因为联邦大学学生资助是一种特权，而不是权利。"},"explanationZh":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。因此，国会可以制定并要求公民登记征兵，以履行其征召和监管美国武装部队的列举权力。*但是，国会在行使这一广泛权力时仍必须遵守其他宪法条款，包括第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中的平等保护部分。当联邦政府歧视处境相似的人时，该部分适用。基于性别的歧视需要接受中间审查，这要求政府证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。这里满足这一标准是因为美国最高法院在罗斯特克诉戈德伯格案中认为，征兵登记仅限男性的要求与政府在国防方面的重要利益密切相关。基于年龄的歧视仅受到合理基础的审查，挑战者只需证明受到挑战的政府行为与合法的政府利益无关。由于政府将能够克服更高的中间审查障碍来证明其性别歧视的合理性，因此学生的年龄歧视指控也将失败。因此，该法规符合第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）并且符合宪法（选项A）。 *国会还有权宣战并授权总统在未经州长批准的情况下召集州国民警卫队部队采取行动，以执行联邦法律、镇压叛乱和击退入侵。 （选项B） 第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）适用于州政府而非联邦政府的歧视。 （选项 D） 正当程序保护历来延伸至权利，而非特权。但这种区别已被放弃，因此联邦大学学生资助是特权还是权利并不重要。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有广泛的权力来组建和管理美国武装部队，包括建立和要求公民登记参加兵役。在行使这一权力时，国会将登记限制为特定年龄范围内的男性，并不违反第五修正案的平等保护部分。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8（组建和管理武装部队的权力）。\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 83 (1981)（认为国会可以利用其广泛的权力来提高和监管武装部队，只要求特定年龄范围的男性登记应征入伍）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会拥有广泛的权力来组建和管理美国武装部队，包括建立并要求公民登记征兵。在行使这一权力时，国会将登记限制为特定年龄范围内的男性，并不违反第五修正案的平等保护部分。","iracZh":{"issue":"该法规是否符合宪法？","rule":"国会拥有广泛的权力来组建和管理美国武装部队，包括建立并要求公民登记征兵。在行使这一权力时，国会将登记限制为特定年龄范围内的男性，并不违反第五修正案的平等保护部分。","analysis":"必要且适当的条款允许国会采取合理必要的行动来履行其第一条所列举的权力。因此，国会可以制定并要求公民登记征兵，以履行其征召和监管美国武装部队的列举权力。*但是，国会在行使这一广泛权力时仍必须遵守其他宪法条款，包括第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中的平等保护部分。当联邦政府歧视处境相似的人时，该部分适用。基于性别的歧视需要接受中间审查，这要求政府证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。这里满足这一标准是因为美国最高法院在罗斯特克诉戈德伯格案中认为，征兵登记仅限男性的要求与政府在国防方面的重要利益密切相关。基于年龄的歧视仅受到合理基础的审查，挑战者只需证明受到挑战的政府行为与合法的政府利益无关。由于政府将能够克服更高的中间审查障碍来证明其性别歧视的合理性，因此学生的年龄歧视指控也将失败。因此，该法规符合第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）并且符合宪法（选项A）。 *国会还有权宣战并授权总统在未经州长批准的情况下召集州国民警卫队部队采取行动，以执行联邦法律、镇压叛乱和击退入侵。 （选项B） 第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）适用于州政府而非联邦政府的歧视。 （选项 D） 正当程序保护历来延伸至权利，而非特权。但这种区别已被放弃，因此联邦大学学生资助是特权还是权利并不重要。\n\n教育目标：国会拥有广泛的权力来组建和管理美国武装部队，包括建立和要求公民登记参加兵役。在行使这一权力时，国会将登记限制为特定年龄范围内的男性，并不违反第五修正案的平等保护部分。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8（组建和管理武装部队的权力）。\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 83 (1981)（认为国会可以利用其广泛的权力来提高和监管武装部队，只要求特定年龄范围的男性登记应征入伍）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为国会拥有广泛的权力来组建和管理武装部队。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"intermediate scrutiny","ipa":"/ˌɪntɝmˈidiɪt skɹˈutəni/","syllables":"in·ter·me·di·ate scrutiny","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"中度审查：措施须与重要政府利益具有实质关联","contextualMeaning":"中度审查：措施须与重要政府利益具有实质关联","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Sex-based discrimination is subject to intermediate scrutiny, which requires the government to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest.","synonyms":["heightened review","middle-tier scrutiny"]},{"term":"substantially related","ipa":"/sʌbstˈænʃəli ɹɪlˈeɪtɪd/","syllables":"sub·stan·tially re·lated","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"具有实质关联：手段与重要政府利益之间须有紧密联系","contextualMeaning":"具有实质关联：手段与重要政府利益之间须有紧密联系","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Sex-based discrimination is subject to intermediate scrutiny, which requires the government to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest.","synonyms":["substantially related to"]},{"term":"sex","ipa":"/sˈɛks/","syllables":"sex","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 性别, 性欲；vt. 区别...的性别, 引起...的性欲","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 性别, 性欲；vt. 区别...的性别, 引起...的性欲","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:sexes/i:sexing/p:sexed/3:sexes/d:sexed","sourceSentence":"The student challenged the federal statute in federal court, arguing that the statute discriminated on the basis of sex and age.","synonyms":["sexual activity","sexual practice","sex activity","gender"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-b2efd6b666e25885"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":127,"sourceQuestionPage":380,"sourceQuestionPages":[380],"sourceExplanationPages":[381,382],"sourceAnswerPages":[381,382],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-128","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"The Sports Championship Revenue Enhancement Act is a federal statute that was enacted as part of a comprehensive program to eliminate the federal budget deficit. That act imposed, for a period of five years, a 50% excise tax on the price of tickets to championship sporting events. Such events included the World Series, the Super Bowl, major college bowl games, and similar championship sports events. Is this federal tax likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because a 50% tax is likely to reduce attendance at championship sporting events and, therefore, is not rationally related to the legitimate interest of Congress in eliminating the budget deficit.","B":"No, because Congress violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment by singling out championship sporting events for this tax while failing to tax other major sporting, artistic, or entertainment events to which tickets are sold.","C":"Yes, because an act of Congress that appears to be a revenue raising measure on its face is not rendered invalid because it may have adverse economic consequences for the activity taxed.","D":"Yes, because the compelling national interest in reducing the federal budget deficit justifies this tax as a temporary emergency measure."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The taxing and spending clause vests Congress with broad taxing power. This allows Congress to impose an indirect tax (eg, excise tax) so long as it is:\n• imposed uniformly in every state where the taxed goods are found and\n• reasonably related to revenue raising on its face. Such taxes are constitutional even if they have an adverse economic impact on, or otherwise burden, the thing taxed. Here, the federal statute imposed a 50% excise tax on tickets to championship sporting events. This indirect tax is applied uniformly to all such events, regardless of where they are held. And though the tax may create adverse economic consequences (eg, reduced attendance) for these events, it is reasonably related to raising revenues to eliminate the budget deficit (Choice A). Therefore, the tax is likely constitutional. (Choice B) This excise tax triggers equal protection concerns since it taxes championship sporting events differently than other major events. But since neither the events nor the attendees are a protected class, the tax need only pass rational basis scrutiny. Therefore, the tax likely complies with the equal protection component. (Choice D) Congress does not need a compelling national interest or emergency powers to justify the use of its broad taxing power.\n\nEducational objective: Congress can impose indirect taxes so long as they are (1) uniformly applied in every state where the taxed goods are found and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This power is not diminished by the fact that such taxes may burden or restrict the thing taxed.\n\nReferences\n• Flint v. Stone Tracy Co., 220 U.S. 107, 151 (1911) (\"[T]he terms duties, imposts and excises are generally treated as embracing the indirect forms of taxation\").\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 362 (1945) (\"It has long been settled that an Act of Congress which on its face purports to be an exercise of the taxing power, is not any the less so because the tax is burdensome or tends to restrict or suppress the thing taxed\").","educationalObjective":"Congress can impose indirect taxes so long as they are (1) uniformly applied in every state where the taxed goods are found and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This power is not diminished by the fact that such taxes may burden or restrict the thing taxed.","references":"• Flint v. Stone Tracy Co., 220 U.S. 107, 151 (1911) (\"[T]he terms duties, imposts and excises are generally treated as embracing the indirect forms of taxation\").\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 362 (1945) (\"It has long been settled that an Act of Congress which on its face purports to be an exercise of the taxing power, is not any the less so because the tax is burdensome or tends to restrict or suppress the thing taxed\").","rule":"Congress can impose indirect taxes so long as they are (1) uniformly applied in every state where the taxed goods are found and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This power is not diminished by the fact that such taxes may burden or restrict the thing taxed.","irac":{"issue":"Is this federal tax likely constitutional?","rule":"Congress can impose indirect taxes so long as they are (1) uniformly applied in every state where the taxed goods are found and (2) reasonably related to revenue production. This power is not diminished by the fact that such taxes may burden or restrict the thing taxed.","analysis":"The taxing and spending clause vests Congress with broad taxing power. This allows Congress to impose an indirect tax (eg, excise tax) so long as it is:\n• imposed uniformly in every state where the taxed goods are found and\n• reasonably related to revenue raising on its face. Such taxes are constitutional even if they have an adverse economic impact on, or otherwise burden, the thing taxed. Here, the federal statute imposed a 50% excise tax on tickets to championship sporting events. This indirect tax is applied uniformly to all such events, regardless of where they are held. And though the tax may create adverse economic consequences (eg, reduced attendance) for these events, it is reasonably related to raising revenues to eliminate the budget deficit (Choice A). Therefore, the tax is likely constitutional. (Choice B) This excise tax triggers equal protection concerns since it taxes championship sporting events differently than other major events. But since neither the events nor the attendees are a protected class, the tax need only pass rational basis scrutiny. Therefore, the tax likely complies with the equal protection component. (Choice D) Congress does not need a compelling national interest or emergency powers to justify the use of its broad taxing power.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because an act of Congress that appears to be a revenue raising measure on its face is not rendered invalid because it may have adverse economic consequences for the activity taxed."},"questionZh":"《体育锦标赛收入增强法案》是一项联邦法规，是作为消除联邦预算赤字综合计划的一部分而颁布的。该法案对锦标赛体育赛事的门票价格征收 50% 的消费税，为期五年。此类赛事包括世界大赛、超级碗、主要大学碗赛以及类似的锦标赛体育赛事。这项联邦税可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为 50% 的税收可能会减少锦标赛体育赛事的出席率，因此与国会消除预算赤字的合法利益没有合理关系。","B":"不可以，因为国会只对锦标赛体育赛事征税，却没有对出售门票的其他主要体育、艺术或娱乐赛事征税，违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分。","C":"是的，因为表面上看来是一项税收增加措施的国会法案并不会因为它可能对征税活动产生不利的经济后果而被视为无效。","D":"是的，因为减少联邦预算赤字的国家利益令人信服，这证明这项税收作为临时紧急措施是合理的。"},"explanationZh":"征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的征税权力。这使得国会可以征收间接税（例如消费税），只要它：\n• 在发现征税商品的每个州统一征收，并且\n• 表面上与收入增长合理相关。即使此类税收对征税对象产生不利的经济影响或造成其他负担，也是符合宪法的。在这里，联邦法规对锦标赛体育赛事的门票征收 50% 的消费税。这种间接税统一适用于所有此类活动，无论其在何处举行。尽管税收可能会对这些活动造成不利的经济后果（例如，出席人数减少），但它与增加收入以消除预算赤字有合理的关系（选择 A）。因此，这项税收很可能符合宪法。 （选择 B）这种消费税引发了同样的保护问题，因为它对锦标赛体育赛事的征税方式与其他重大赛事不同。但由于活动和参加者都不是受保护阶层，因此税收只需要通过合理基础的审查。因此，该税收可能符合平等保护的要求。 （选择 D）国会不需要令人信服的国家利益或紧急权力来证明其广泛征税权的使用是合理的。\n\n教育目标：国会可以征收间接税，只要它们 (1) 统一适用于发现征税商品的每个州，并且 (2) 与收入生产合理相关。这种权力不会因为此类税收可能给征税对象带来负担或限制而减弱。\n\n参考文献\n• Flint v. Stone Tracy Co., 220 U.S. 107, 151 (1911)（“关税、进口税和消费税等术语通常被视为包含间接税收形式”）。\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 362 (1945)（“长期以来的共识是，一项表面上声称行使征税权的国会法案，实际上并没有因为税收负担过重或倾向于限制或抑制征税对象而减弱”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会可以征收间接税，只要它们（1）统一适用于发现征税商品的每个州，并且（2）与收入生产合理相关。这种权力不会因为此类税收可能给征税对象带来负担或限制而减弱。","iracZh":{"issue":"这项联邦税可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"国会可以征收间接税，只要它们（1）统一适用于发现征税商品的每个州，并且（2）与收入生产合理相关。这种权力不会因为此类税收可能给征税对象带来负担或限制而减弱。","analysis":"征税和支出条款赋予国会广泛的征税权力。这使得国会可以征收间接税（例如消费税），只要它：\n• 在发现征税商品的每个州统一征收，并且\n• 表面上与收入增长合理相关。即使此类税收对征税对象产生不利的经济影响或造成其他负担，也是符合宪法的。在这里，联邦法规对锦标赛体育赛事的门票征收 50% 的消费税。这种间接税统一适用于所有此类活动，无论其在何处举行。尽管税收可能会对这些活动造成不利的经济后果（例如，出席人数减少），但它与增加收入以消除预算赤字有合理的关系（选择 A）。因此，这项税收很可能符合宪法。 （选择 B）这种消费税引发了同样的保护问题，因为它对锦标赛体育赛事的征税方式与其他重大赛事不同。但由于活动和参加者都不是受保护阶层，因此税收只需要通过合理基础的审查。因此，该税收可能符合平等保护的要求。 （选择 D）国会不需要令人信服的国家利益或紧急权力来证明其广泛征税权的使用是合理的。\n\n教育目标：国会可以征收间接税，只要它们 (1) 统一适用于发现征税商品的每个州，并且 (2) 与收入生产合理相关。这种权力不会因为此类税收可能给征税对象带来负担或限制而减弱。\n\n参考文献\n• Flint v. Stone Tracy Co., 220 U.S. 107, 151 (1911)（“关税、进口税和消费税等术语通常被视为包含间接税收形式”）。\n\n• Fernandez v. Wiener, 326 U.S. 340, 362 (1945)（“长期以来的共识是，一项表面上声称行使征税权的国会法案，实际上并没有因为税收负担过重或倾向于限制或抑制征税对象而减弱”）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为表面上看来是一项税收增加措施的国会法案并不会因为它可能对征税活动产生不利的经济后果而被视为无效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-8da788f266b12b32"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":128,"sourceQuestionPage":383,"sourceQuestionPages":[383],"sourceExplanationPages":[384,385],"sourceAnswerPages":[384,385],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-129","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"On the basis of scientific studies showing a causal relationship between the consumption of \"red meat\" (principally beef) and certain forms of cancer, a federal statute prohibits all commercial advertising of red-meat products. The statute does not, however, restrict the sale of red-meat products. Producers of red meat have challenged the statute as a violation of their free speech rights protected by the First Amendment. Is the court likely to find the statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it does not serve a substantial government interest.","B":"No, because it is more extensive than necessary to serve the government interest in preventing certain cancers.","C":"Yes, because it does not affect speech protected by the First Amendment.","D":"Yes, because it serves a legitimate government interest in protecting public health."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The government can ban commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity. But all other commercial speech is protected by the First Amendment, albeit to a lesser extent than other forms of expression (eg, political speech). This means that the government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the government proves that its regulation directly advances a substantial government interest and is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. Here, the red-meat advertisements are protected commercial speech since there is no evidence that they are false or misleading and red meat is a lawful product (Choice C). And the statute prohibiting these advertisements likely advances the government's substantial interest in protecting the public from certain cancers (Choice A). But the statute is more extensive than necessary to achieve that interest since a cancer warning in those advertisements (instead of a total ban) would suffice. Therefore, the statute will likely be found unconstitutional. (Choice D) A legitimate government interest is never sufficient to justify the regulation of protected speech.\n\nEducational objective: The government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest.\n\nReferences\n• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980) (providing the test for determining whether the government's regulation of protected commercial speech is constitutional).","educationalObjective":"The government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest.","references":"• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980) (providing the test for determining whether the government's regulation of protected commercial speech is constitutional).","rule":"The government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to find the statute constitutional?","rule":"The government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest.","analysis":"The government can ban commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity. But all other commercial speech is protected by the First Amendment, albeit to a lesser extent than other forms of expression (eg, political speech). This means that the government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the government proves that its regulation directly advances a substantial government interest and is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. Here, the red-meat advertisements are protected commercial speech since there is no evidence that they are false or misleading and red meat is a lawful product (Choice C). And the statute prohibiting these advertisements likely advances the government's substantial interest in protecting the public from certain cancers (Choice A). But the statute is more extensive than necessary to achieve that interest since a cancer warning in those advertisements (instead of a total ban) would suffice. Therefore, the statute will likely be found unconstitutional. (Choice D) A legitimate government interest is never sufficient to justify the regulation of protected speech.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because it is more extensive than necessary to serve the government interest in preventing certain cancers."},"questionZh":"科学研究表明，食用“红肉”（主要是牛肉）与某些癌症之间存在因果关系，联邦法规禁止所有红肉产品的商业广告。然而，该法规并不限制红肉产品的销售。红肉生产商对该法规提出质疑，认为该法规侵犯了他们受第一修正案保护的言论自由权。法院可能认为该法规符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为它不符合政府的重大利益。","B":"不，因为它的范围超出了服务于政府预防某些癌症的利益所需的范围。","C":"是的，因为它不影响受第一修正案保护的言论。","D":"是的，因为它符合政府保护公众健康的合法利益。"},"explanationZh":"政府可以禁止 (1) 虚假或误导性的或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论。但所有其他商业言论均受到第一修正案的保护，尽管程度低于其他形式的表达（例如政治言论）。这意味着，只有当政府证明其监管直接促进了政府的重大利益且不超过服务该利益所需的范围时，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论。在这里，红肉广告受到保护的商业言论，因为没有证据表明它们是虚假或误导性的，并且红肉是合法产品（选项C）。禁止这些广告的法规可能会增强政府保护公众免受某些癌症侵害的重大利益（选项 A）。但该法规的范围超出了实现这种兴趣所需的范围，因为这些广告中的癌症警告（而不是全面禁止）就足够了。因此，该法规很可能会被认定为违宪。 （选项 D）合法的政府利益永远不足以证明对受保护言论的监管是合理的。\n\n教育目标：只有在以下情况下，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论：法规（1）直接促进重大政府利益，并且（2）范围不超过服务该利益所需的范围。\n\n参考文献\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980)（提供了确定政府对受保护商业言论的监管是否符合宪法的测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只有在以下情况下，政府才能对受保护的商业言论进行监管：(1) 直接促进重大政府利益，并且 (2) 范围不超过服务该利益所需的范围。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能认为该法规符合宪法吗？","rule":"只有在以下情况下，政府才能对受保护的商业言论进行监管：(1) 直接促进重大政府利益，并且 (2) 范围不超过服务该利益所需的范围。","analysis":"政府可以禁止 (1) 虚假或误导性的或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论。但所有其他商业言论均受到第一修正案的保护，尽管程度低于其他形式的表达（例如政治言论）。这意味着，只有当政府证明其监管直接促进了政府的重大利益且不超过服务该利益所需的范围时，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论。在这里，红肉广告受到保护的商业言论，因为没有证据表明它们是虚假或误导性的，并且红肉是合法产品（选项C）。禁止这些广告的法规可能会增强政府保护公众免受某些癌症侵害的重大利益（选项 A）。但该法规的范围超出了实现这种兴趣所需的范围，因为这些广告中的癌症警告（而不是全面禁止）就足够了。因此，该法规很可能会被认定为违宪。 （选项 D）合法的政府利益永远不足以证明对受保护言论的监管是合理的。\n\n教育目标：只有在以下情况下，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论：法规（1）直接促进重大政府利益，并且（2）范围不超过服务该利益所需的范围。\n\n参考文献\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980)（提供了确定政府对受保护商业言论的监管是否符合宪法的测试）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为它的范围超出了服务于政府预防某些癌症的利益所需的范围。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"expression","ipa":"/ɪkspɹˈɛʃən/","syllables":"ex·pres·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 表达, 表现, 词语, 措辞；[计] 表达式","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 表达, 表现, 词语, 措辞；[计] 表达式","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:expressions","sourceSentence":"But all other commercial speech is protected by the First Amendment, albeit to a lesser extent than other forms of expression (eg, political speech).","synonyms":["look","aspect","facial expression","face"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-6b7a858f57441c17"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":129,"sourceQuestionPage":386,"sourceQuestionPages":[386],"sourceExplanationPages":[387,388],"sourceAnswerPages":[387,388],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-130","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"Several sites on a mountain within federal public lands are regarded as sacred to a group of people that has gathered there for years to perform religious ceremonies. The United States Forest Service recently issued a permit to a private developer to construct a ski facility in an area that includes the sites that are sacred to the group. The group has filed suit in federal district court against the Forest Service to force cancellation of the permit, claiming solely that the permit violates its First Amendment right to the free exercise of religion. The Forest Service has conceded that the group's religious beliefs are sincere and that construction of the ski facility will adversely affect the group's religious practices. What must the group show to prevail on its First Amendment claim?","choices":{"A":"Construction of the ski facility will have a discriminatory impact on the group's religious practices in relation to the practices of other religious groups.","B":"The burden on the group's religious practices that will be imposed by construction of the ski facility outweighs the government's interest in allowing the facility.","C":"The government can serve its legitimate interest in allowing the ski facility by issuing a permit that is less burdensome on the group's religious practices.","D":"The permit issued by the government is aimed at suppressing the religious practices of the group."},"answer":"D","explanation":"First Amendment free exercise of religion\n\nBurden Applicability Level of scrutiny\n\nIncidental Generally applicable to all persons Rational basis scrutiny:\n\n• burden rationally related to\n• legitimate government interest Direct Intentionally targets religious Strict scrutiny: belief/practice\n• burden necessary to achieve\n• compelling government interest The United States Forest Service issued a permit for the construction of a ski facility on land sacred to a religious group. As a result, the group filed suit to cancel the permit since the construction would adversely affect its First Amendment right to perform religious ceremonies. The group's likelihood of success depends on whether that burden is either:\n• incidental – triggering rational basis scrutiny, where the challenger will fail unless the challenger shows that the burden has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest or\n\n• direct – triggering strict scrutiny, where the challenger will prevail unless the government meets the nearly impossible task of showing that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nTherefore, to have the greatest chance of prevailing on its First Amendment claim, the group must show that the government-issued permit is aimed at suppressing (ie, directly burdens) the group's religious practices. (Choice A) The ski facility's discriminatory impact on the group's religious practices allows the group to bring a free exercise claim. But that claim will likely fail unless the group can show that the discriminatory impact was intentional, which would create a presumption of invalidity that would be difficult for the government to rebut. (Choice B) A free exercise challenge will be analyzed under rational basis or strict scrutiny, neither of which weighs the burden on religion against the government's interest. (Choice C) To prevail, the group must show that the permit directly burdens its religious practices. Once it does so, the burden shifts to the government to show that its compelling (not merely legitimate) interest in the ski facility cannot be achieved by less burdensome means.\n\nEducational objective: Incidental burdens on the free exercise of religion are typically upheld under rational basis review. But direct burdens are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.\n\nReferences\n• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990) (establishing the direct/incidental burden test).\n\n• Church of Lukumi Babalu Aye v. City of Hialeah, 508 U.S. 520, 546 (1993) (applying strict scrutiny to a city ordinance that intentionally targeted a religious practice).","educationalObjective":"Incidental burdens on the free exercise of religion are typically upheld under rational basis review. But direct burdens are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.","references":"• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990) (establishing the direct/incidental burden test).\n\n• Church of Lukumi Babalu Aye v. City of Hialeah, 508 U.S. 520, 546 (1993) (applying strict scrutiny to a city ordinance that intentionally targeted a religious practice).","rule":"Incidental burdens on the free exercise of religion are typically upheld under rational basis review. But direct burdens are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.","irac":{"issue":"What must the group show to prevail on its First Amendment claim?","rule":"Incidental burdens on the free exercise of religion are typically upheld under rational basis review. But direct burdens are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.","analysis":"First Amendment free exercise of religion\n\nBurden Applicability Level of scrutiny\n\nIncidental Generally applicable to all persons Rational basis scrutiny:\n\n• burden rationally related to\n• legitimate government interest Direct Intentionally targets religious Strict scrutiny: belief/practice\n• burden necessary to achieve\n• compelling government interest The United States Forest Service issued a permit for the construction of a ski facility on land sacred to a religious group. As a result, the group filed suit to cancel the permit since the construction would adversely affect its First Amendment right to perform religious ceremonies. The group's likelihood of success depends on whether that burden is either:\n• incidental – triggering rational basis scrutiny, where the challenger will fail unless the challenger shows that the burden has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest or\n\n• direct – triggering strict scrutiny, where the challenger will prevail unless the government meets the nearly impossible task of showing that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nTherefore, to have the greatest chance of prevailing on its First Amendment claim, the group must show that the government-issued permit is aimed at suppressing (ie, directly burdens) the group's religious practices. (Choice A) The ski facility's discriminatory impact on the group's religious practices allows the group to bring a free exercise claim. But that claim will likely fail unless the group can show that the discriminatory impact was intentional, which would create a presumption of invalidity that would be difficult for the government to rebut. (Choice B) A free exercise challenge will be analyzed under rational basis or strict scrutiny, neither of which weighs the burden on religion against the government's interest. (Choice C) To prevail, the group must show that the permit directly burdens its religious practices. Once it does so, the burden shifts to the government to show that its compelling (not merely legitimate) interest in the ski facility cannot be achieved by less burdensome means.","conclusion":"Answer D: The permit issued by the government is aimed at suppressing the religious practices of the group."},"questionZh":"联邦公共土地内的一座山上的几个地点被一群多年来聚集在那里举行宗教仪式的人们视为神圣的。美国林务局最近向一家私人开发商颁发了许可证，允许其在包括该团体神圣地点的地区建造滑雪设施。该组织已向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，要求林务局强制取消该许可证，仅声称该许可证违反了第一修正案中自由行使宗教信仰的权利。林务局承认该团体的宗教信仰是真诚的，滑雪设施的建设将对该团体的宗教活动产生不利影响。该团体必须证明什么才能在其第一修正案主张中获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"滑雪设施的建设将对该团体的宗教习俗相对于其他宗教团体的习俗产生歧视性影响。","B":"滑雪设施的建设给该团体的宗教活动带来的负担超过了政府允许该设施建设的利益。","C":"政府可以通过发放许可证来满足其允许滑雪设施的合法利益，从而减轻该团体的宗教活动负担。","D":"政府颁发许可证的目的是压制该团体的宗教活动。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案自由行使宗教信仰\n\n负担 适用性 审查级别\n\n附带条款 一般适用于所有人 理性依据审查：\n\n• 合理相关的负担\n• 合法的政府利益 直接 有意针对宗教 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：信仰/实践\n• 实现目标所需的负担\n• 政府的强烈兴趣 美国林务局颁发了在宗教团体圣地上建造滑雪设施的许可证。结果，该组织提起诉讼要求取消许可证，因为该建筑将对其根据第一修正案举行宗教仪式的权利产生不利影响。该小组成功的可能性取决于该负担是否是：\n• 偶然——引发合理基础审查，挑战者将失败，除非挑战者证明该负担与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系，或\n\n• 直接——引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），挑战者将获胜，除非政府完成几乎不可能完成的任务，证明这一负担对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n因此，为了最大程度地支持其第一修正案的主张，该团体必须证明政府颁发的许可证旨在压制（即直接加重）该团体的宗教活动。 （选项 A）滑雪设施对该团体宗教活动的歧视性影响允许该团体提出自由锻炼主张。但除非该组织能够证明歧视性影响是故意的，否则这一主张可能会失败，这将产生政府难以反驳的无效推定。 （选择B）自由行挑战将在合理基础或严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下进行分析，两者都不会权衡宗教负担与政府利益之间的关系。 （选项 C）要获胜，该团体必须证明该许可证直接对其宗教活动造成负担。一旦这样做，负担就会转移到政府身上，以表明其对滑雪设施的令人信服的（不仅仅是合法的）利益无法通过不那么繁重的手段来实现。\n\n教育目标：宗教自由的附带负担通常在合理基础审查下得到维护。但在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担几乎总是无效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990)（建立直接/附带负担测试）。\n\n• Lukumi Babalu Aye 教堂诉海厄利亚市，508 U.S. 520, 546 (1993)（对故意针对宗教活动的城市法令进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"宗教自由的附带负担通常在合理基础审查下得到维护。但在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担几乎总是无效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"该团体必须证明什么才能在其第一修正案主张中获胜？","rule":"宗教自由的附带负担通常在合理基础审查下得到维护。但在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担几乎总是无效的。","analysis":"第一修正案自由行使宗教信仰\n\n负担 适用性 审查级别\n\n附带条款 一般适用于所有人 理性依据审查：\n\n• 合理相关的负担\n• 合法的政府利益 直接 有意针对宗教 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：信仰/实践\n• 实现目标所需的负担\n• 政府的强烈兴趣 美国林务局颁发了在宗教团体圣地上建造滑雪设施的许可证。结果，该组织提起诉讼要求取消许可证，因为该建筑将对其根据第一修正案举行宗教仪式的权利产生不利影响。该小组成功的可能性取决于该负担是否是：\n• 偶然——引发合理基础审查，挑战者将失败，除非挑战者证明该负担与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系，或\n\n• 直接——引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），挑战者将获胜，除非政府完成几乎不可能完成的任务，证明这一负担对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n因此，为了最大程度地支持其第一修正案的主张，该团体必须证明政府颁发的许可证旨在压制（即直接加重）该团体的宗教活动。 （选项 A）滑雪设施对该团体宗教活动的歧视性影响允许该团体提出自由锻炼主张。但除非该组织能够证明歧视性影响是故意的，否则这一主张可能会失败，这将产生政府难以反驳的无效推定。 （选择B）自由行挑战将在合理基础或严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下进行分析，两者都不会权衡宗教负担与政府利益之间的关系。 （选项 C）要获胜，该团体必须证明该许可证直接对其宗教活动造成负担。一旦这样做，负担就会转移到政府身上，以表明其对滑雪设施的令人信服的（不仅仅是合法的）利益无法通过不那么繁重的手段来实现。\n\n教育目标：宗教自由的附带负担通常在合理基础审查下得到维护。但在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担几乎总是无效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990)（建立直接/附带负担测试）。\n\n• Lukumi Babalu Aye 教堂诉海厄利亚市，508 U.S. 520, 546 (1993)（对故意针对宗教活动的城市法令进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 D：政府颁发许可证的目的是压制该团体的宗教活动。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"rational basis review","ipa":"/ɹˈæʃənəl bˈeɪsəs ɹˌivjˈu/","syllables":"ra·tio·nal ba·sis re·view","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合理基础审查：措施须与合法政府利益合理相关","contextualMeaning":"合理基础审查：措施须与合法政府利益合理相关","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Incidental burdens on the free exercise of religion are typically upheld under rational basis review.","synonyms":["minimum scrutiny","deferential review"]},{"term":"conceded","ipa":"/kʌnsˈidɪd/","syllables":"con·ceded","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 承认( concede的过去式和过去分词 )； 出让, 容许； 承认(比赛、选举等失败","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 承认( concede的过去式和过去分词 )； 出让, 容许； 承认(比赛、选举等失败","wordForm":"词；原形 concede；词形 0:concede/1:pd","sourceSentence":"The Forest Service has conceded that the group's religious beliefs are sincere and that construction of the ski facility will adversely affect the group's religious practices.","synonyms":["yield","grant","profess","confess"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":130,"sourceQuestionPage":389,"sourceQuestionPages":[389],"sourceExplanationPages":[390,391],"sourceAnswerPages":[390,391],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-131","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Free Exercise Clause","question":"A federal statute requires all males between the ages of 18 and 26 to register with the Selective Service Agency, which is responsible for drafting individuals to serve in the armed forces in the event of war. The statute makes it a felony to fail to register but provides an exemption for individuals who hold a sincere religious objection to war. The statute allows the federal government to question the sincerity and reasonableness of religious objections. The provisions of the statute are inseverable. An 18-year-old man applying for federal student loans noted on the loan application that he had not registered with the Selective Service Agency. A representative of the Agency contacted the man, and the man said that he had no intention of registering due to his religious belief that all war is wrong. After refusing to register on several more occasions, the man was arrested and charged under the federal statute. What is the man's strongest argument that the federal statute is unconstitutional?","choices":{"A":"The statute denies him the equal protection of the laws because it only applies to men.","B":"The statute denies him the equal protection of the laws because it only applies to those between the ages of 18 and 26.","C":"The statute violates his right to freely exercise his religious beliefs by allowing the government to challenge the reasonableness of those beliefs.","D":"The statute violates his right to freely exercise his religious beliefs by allowing the government to challenge the sincerity of those beliefs."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The First Amendment free exercise clause prohibits the government from punishing an individual for any sincerely held religious belief. As a result, any government inquiry is limited to whether a professed belief is sincere—not whether that belief is reasonable or true (Choice D). Here, the federal statute allows the government to challenge the reasonableness of any religious objection to Selective Service registration. Therefore, this is the man's strongest argument that the federal statute is unconstitutional. (Choice A) A federal law that intentionally discriminates based on gender or nonmarital birth violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment unless it passes intermediate scrutiny. This standard of scrutiny is likely met here because, as the U.S. Supreme Court held in Rostker v. Goldberg, the male-only requirement is substantially related to the government's important interest in national defense. (Choice B) Under the Fifth Amendment's equal protection component, a federal law that does not impact a fundamental right or protected class is generally valid under mere rational basis scrutiny. Age is not a protected class, so the statute's application to those between the ages of 18 and 26 is likely valid.\n\nEducational objective: The free exercise clause allows the government to question whether a religious belief is sincerely held but not whether that belief is reasonable or true.\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Seeger, 380 U.S. 163, 184–85 (1965) (explaining that the government's only task is to determine whether a professed religious belief is sincerely held).\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 78–79 (1981) (holding that a law exempting women from the draft does not violate the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment).","educationalObjective":"The free exercise clause allows the government to question whether a religious belief is sincerely held but not whether that belief is reasonable or true.","references":"• United States v. Seeger, 380 U.S. 163, 184–85 (1965) (explaining that the government's only task is to determine whether a professed religious belief is sincerely held).\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 78–79 (1981) (holding that a law exempting women from the draft does not violate the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment).","rule":"The free exercise clause allows the government to question whether a religious belief is sincerely held but not whether that belief is reasonable or true.","irac":{"issue":"What is the man's strongest argument that the federal statute is unconstitutional?","rule":"The free exercise clause allows the government to question whether a religious belief is sincerely held but not whether that belief is reasonable or true.","analysis":"The First Amendment free exercise clause prohibits the government from punishing an individual for any sincerely held religious belief. As a result, any government inquiry is limited to whether a professed belief is sincere—not whether that belief is reasonable or true (Choice D). Here, the federal statute allows the government to challenge the reasonableness of any religious objection to Selective Service registration. Therefore, this is the man's strongest argument that the federal statute is unconstitutional. (Choice A) A federal law that intentionally discriminates based on gender or nonmarital birth violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment unless it passes intermediate scrutiny. This standard of scrutiny is likely met here because, as the U.S. Supreme Court held in Rostker v. Goldberg, the male-only requirement is substantially related to the government's important interest in national defense. (Choice B) Under the Fifth Amendment's equal protection component, a federal law that does not impact a fundamental right or protected class is generally valid under mere rational basis scrutiny. Age is not a protected class, so the statute's application to those between the ages of 18 and 26 is likely valid.","conclusion":"Answer C: The statute violates his right to freely exercise his religious beliefs by allowing the government to challenge the reasonableness of those beliefs."},"questionZh":"一项联邦法规要求所有 18 岁至 26 岁的男性在义务兵役局登记，该机构负责征召个人在战争时加入武装部队服役。该法规将未登记的行为定为重罪，但为真诚地反对战争的宗教人士提供了豁免。该法规允许联邦政府质疑宗教反对的诚意和合理性。该法规的规定是不可分割的。一名申请联邦学生贷款的 18 岁男子在贷款申请上注明，他没有在兵役局登记。该机构的一名代表联系了该男子，该男子表示，由于他的宗教信仰认为所有战争都是错误的，因此他无意登记。在多次拒绝登记后，该男子被捕并根据联邦法规受到指控。该男子认为联邦法规违宪的最有力论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法规剥夺了他受到法律的平等保护，因为它只适用于男性。","B":"该法规剥夺了他受到法律的平等保护，因为它只适用于18岁至26岁之间的人。","C":"该法规允许政府质疑这些信仰的合理性，从而侵犯了他自由行使宗教信仰的权利。","D":"该法规允许政府质疑这些信仰的真诚性，从而侵犯了他自由行使宗教信仰的权利。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）禁止政府因任何真诚的宗教信仰而惩罚个人。因此，任何政府调查都仅限于所声称的信念是否真诚，而不是该信念是否合理或真实（选项 D）。在这里，联邦法规允许政府对任何宗教反对兵役登记的合理性提出质疑。因此，这是该男子认为联邦法规违宪的最有力论据。 （选项 A）除非通过中间审查，否则故意基于性别或非婚生育进行歧视的联邦法律违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分。这种审查标准很可能在这里得到满足，因为正如美国最高法院在罗斯特克诉戈德堡一案中所判决的那样，仅限男性的要求与政府在国防方面的重要利益密切相关。 （选项B）根据第五修正案的平等保护部分，不影响基本权利或受保护群体的联邦法律在仅仅合理基础的审查下通常是有效的。年龄不是受保护群体，因此该法规适用于 18 岁至 26 岁之间的人可能是有效的。\n\n教育目标：宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）允许政府质疑宗教信仰是否真诚，但不能质疑该信仰是否合理或真实。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Seeger, 380 U.S. 163, 184–85 (1965)（解释说政府的唯一任务是确定所宣称的宗教信仰是否是真诚的）。\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 78–79 (1981)（认为免除妇女兵役的法律并不违反第五修正案的平等保护部分）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）允许政府质疑宗教信仰是否真诚，但不能质疑该信仰是否合理或真实。","iracZh":{"issue":"该男子认为联邦法规违宪的最有力论据是什么？","rule":"宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）允许政府质疑宗教信仰是否真诚，但不能质疑该信仰是否合理或真实。","analysis":"第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）禁止政府因任何真诚的宗教信仰而惩罚个人。因此，任何政府调查都仅限于所声称的信念是否真诚，而不是该信念是否合理或真实（选项 D）。在这里，联邦法规允许政府对任何宗教反对兵役登记的合理性提出质疑。因此，这是该男子认为联邦法规违宪的最有力论据。 （选项 A）除非通过中间审查，否则故意基于性别或非婚生育进行歧视的联邦法律违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分。这种审查标准很可能在这里得到满足，因为正如美国最高法院在罗斯特克诉戈德堡一案中所判决的那样，仅限男性的要求与政府在国防方面的重要利益密切相关。 （选项B）根据第五修正案的平等保护部分，不影响基本权利或受保护群体的联邦法律在仅仅合理基础的审查下通常是有效的。年龄不是受保护群体，因此该法规适用于 18 岁至 26 岁之间的人可能是有效的。\n\n教育目标：宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）允许政府质疑宗教信仰是否真诚，但不能质疑该信仰是否合理或真实。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Seeger, 380 U.S. 163, 184–85 (1965)（解释说政府的唯一任务是确定所宣称的宗教信仰是否是真诚的）。\n\n• Rostker v. Goldberg, 453 U.S. 57, 78–79 (1981)（认为免除妇女兵役的法律并不违反第五修正案的平等保护部分）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该法规允许政府质疑这些信仰的合理性，从而侵犯了他自由行使宗教信仰的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"free exercise clause","ipa":"/fɹˈi ˈɛksɝsˌaɪz klˈɔz/","syllables":"free ex·er·cise clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"宗教自由行使条款：保护真诚信仰与一定范围的宗教实践","contextualMeaning":"宗教自由行使条款：保护真诚信仰与一定范围的宗教实践","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The free exercise clause allows the government to question whether a religious belief is sincerely held but not whether that belief is reasonable or true.","synonyms":["free exercise article"]},{"term":"gender","ipa":"/dʒˈɛndɝ/","syllables":"gen·der","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 性；vt. 产生","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 性；vt. 产生","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:genders/d:gendered/p:gendered","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) A federal law that intentionally discriminates based on gender or nonmarital birth violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment unless it passes intermediate scrutiny.","synonyms":["sex","sexuality","grammatical gender"]},{"term":"wrong","ipa":"/ɹˈɔŋ/","syllables":"wrong","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 错误的, 不正当的, 失常的；adv. 错误地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 错误的, 不正当的, 失常的；adv. 错误地","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:wrongs/i:wronging/p:wronged/3:wrongs/d:wronged","sourceSentence":"A representative of the Agency contacted the man, and the man said that he had no intention of registering due to his religious belief that all war is wrong.","synonyms":["legal injury","damage","wrongfulness","faulty"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-41f19ec828afe0b4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":131,"sourceQuestionPage":392,"sourceQuestionPages":[392],"sourceExplanationPages":[393,394],"sourceAnswerPages":[393,394],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-132","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A city ordinance prohibits the location of \"adult theaters and bookstores\" (theaters and bookstores presenting sexually explicit performances or materials) in residential or commercial zones within the city. The ordinance was intended to protect surrounding property from the likely adverse secondary effects of such establishments. Adult theaters and bookstores are freely permitted in the areas of the city zoned industrial, where those adverse secondary effects are not as likely. A storekeeper was denied a zoning permit to open an adult theater and bookstore in a building owned by him in an area zoned commercial. As a result, the storekeeper brought suit in an appropriate court challenging the constitutionality of the zoning ordinance. Which of the following statements regarding the constitutionality of this city ordinance is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The ordinance is invalid, because a city may not enforce zoning regulations that deprive potential operators of adult theaters and bookstores of their freedom to choose the location of their businesses.","B":"The ordinance is invalid, because a city may not zone property in a manner calculated to protect property from the likely adverse secondary effects of adult theaters and bookstores.","C":"The ordinance is valid, because a city may enforce this type of time, place, and manner regulation on speech-related businesses, so long as the regulation is designed to serve a substantial governmental interest and does not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication.","D":"The ordinance is valid, because a city may enforce zoning restrictions on speech- related businesses to ensure that the messages they disseminate are acceptable to the residents of adjacent property."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Speech regulations\n\nType Definition Test\n\nContent-\n• Facial discrimination Strict scrutiny based\n• Primary purpose targets\n• Necessary & narrowly tailored message, subject, or idea to achieve compelling interest Content-\n• Regulates time, place, or Intermediate scrutiny neutral manner of speech\n• Narrowly tailored to achieve important interest\n• Alternative avenues of communication remain Adult theaters and bookstores are speech-related businesses that are protected by the First Amendment. As a result, government regulation of such businesses is subject to constitutional review. If the regulation targets what is being said (ie, content-based restriction), it is subject to strict scrutiny and presumptively unconstitutional. But if the regulation targets the time, place, or manner of speech (ie, content-neutral restriction), it is subject to intermediate scrutiny and generally acceptable. In Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., the Supreme Court held that zoning ordinances for adult-entertainment businesses are time, place, and manner restrictions. That is because such ordinances target the secondary effects that adult businesses have on the surrounding community—not the acceptability of their content. As a result, these ordinances are valid so long as they pass intermediate scrutiny—ie, are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial or important government interest and do not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication (Choice B). (Choice A) A city can enforce zoning regulations that restrict adult theater and bookstore operators' freedom to choose the location of their businesses so long as the regulation survives intermediate scrutiny. (Choice D) A zoning ordinance is a content-based restriction if its primary purpose is to ensure that the messages disseminated by speech-related businesses are acceptable to residents. Such a restriction is almost certainly invalid since it must pass strict scrutiny—ie, be necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nEducational objective: Time, place, and manner restrictions (eg, zoning ordinances) are valid so long as they are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and do not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication.\n\nReferences\n• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., 475 U.S. 41, 46–47 (1986) (explaining that zoning ordinances for adult-entertainment businesses are time, place, and manner restrictions).","educationalObjective":"Time, place, and manner restrictions (eg, zoning ordinances) are valid so long as they are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and do not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication.","references":"• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., 475 U.S. 41, 46–47 (1986) (explaining that zoning ordinances for adult-entertainment businesses are time, place, and manner restrictions).","rule":"Time, place, and manner restrictions (eg, zoning ordinances) are valid so long as they are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and do not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements regarding the constitutionality of this city ordinance is most accurate?","rule":"Time, place, and manner restrictions (eg, zoning ordinances) are valid so long as they are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and do not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication.","analysis":"Speech regulations\n\nType Definition Test\n\nContent-\n• Facial discrimination Strict scrutiny based\n• Primary purpose targets\n• Necessary & narrowly tailored message, subject, or idea to achieve compelling interest Content-\n• Regulates time, place, or Intermediate scrutiny neutral manner of speech\n• Narrowly tailored to achieve important interest\n• Alternative avenues of communication remain Adult theaters and bookstores are speech-related businesses that are protected by the First Amendment. As a result, government regulation of such businesses is subject to constitutional review. If the regulation targets what is being said (ie, content-based restriction), it is subject to strict scrutiny and presumptively unconstitutional. But if the regulation targets the time, place, or manner of speech (ie, content-neutral restriction), it is subject to intermediate scrutiny and generally acceptable. In Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., the Supreme Court held that zoning ordinances for adult-entertainment businesses are time, place, and manner restrictions. That is because such ordinances target the secondary effects that adult businesses have on the surrounding community—not the acceptability of their content. As a result, these ordinances are valid so long as they pass intermediate scrutiny—ie, are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial or important government interest and do not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication (Choice B). (Choice A) A city can enforce zoning regulations that restrict adult theater and bookstore operators' freedom to choose the location of their businesses so long as the regulation survives intermediate scrutiny. (Choice D) A zoning ordinance is a content-based restriction if its primary purpose is to ensure that the messages disseminated by speech-related businesses are acceptable to residents. Such a restriction is almost certainly invalid since it must pass strict scrutiny—ie, be necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","conclusion":"Answer C: The ordinance is valid, because a city may enforce this type of time, place, and manner regulation on speech-related businesses, so long as the regulation is designed to serve a substantial governmental interest and does not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of communication."},"questionZh":"一项城市法令禁止在城市内的住宅区或商业区开设“成人剧院和书店”（提供露骨色情表演或材料的剧院和书店）。该法令旨在保护周围财产免受此类设施可能产生的不利二次影响。成人剧院和书店可以在城市工业区自由开放，这些不利的次要影响不太可能发生。一名店主被拒绝获得分区许可证，无法在他拥有的位于商业区的一栋大楼内开设成人剧院和书店。因此，店主向适当的法院提起诉讼，质疑分区条例的合宪性。关于本市法令的合宪性，以下哪项陈述最准确？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法令无效，因为城市不得执行剥夺成人剧院和书店潜在经营者选择经营地点的自由的分区法规。","B":"该法令无效，因为城市不得以保护财产免受成人剧院和书店可能产生的不利二次影响的方式对财产进行分区。","C":"该条例是有效的，因为一个城市可以对与语音相关的业务执行此类时间、地点和方式的规定，只要该规定旨在服务于政府的重大利益，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径。","D":"该法令是有效的，因为城市可以对与语音相关的企业实施分区限制，以确保它们传播的信息能够为邻近房产的居民所接受。"},"explanationZh":"言论规定\n\n类型定义测试\n\n内容-\n• 面部歧视 基于严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）\n• 主要目的目标\n• 必要且精心定制的信息、主题或想法，以实现引人注目的兴趣 内容-\n• 规定时间、地点或中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）中立的言论方式\n• 为实现重要利益而进行的狭隘定制\n• 替代沟通途径仍然存在 成人剧院和书店是受第一修正案保护的与言论相关的行业。因此，政府对此类企业的监管须接受宪法审查。如果该法规针对的是所说内容（即基于内容的限制），则将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）并被推定为违宪。但如果该规定针对的是言论的时间、地点或方式（即内容中立的限制），则需要经过中间审查并且通常可以接受。在 Renton 诉 Playtime Theatres, Inc. 案中，最高法院认为成人娱乐业的分区条例是时间、地点和方式的限制。这是因为此类法令针对的是成人企业对周围社区产生的次要影响，而不是其内容的可接受性。因此，只要这些法令通过了中间审查，即是专门为服务实质性或重要的政府利益而制定的，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径（选择 B），那么它们就是有效的。 （选择 A） 城市可以执行分区法规，限制成人剧院和书店经营者选择其业务地点的自由，只要该法规能够通过中间审查。 （选项 D）如果分区条例的主要目的是确保与语音相关的企业传播的信息能够为居民所接受，那么该分区条例就是基于内容的限制。这种限制几乎肯定是无效的，因为它必须经过严格的审查——即，必须是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而进行的必要的和严格的调整。\n\n教育目标：时间、地点和方式限制（例如，分区条例）只要是专门为服务于政府的重大利益而制定的，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径，那么它们就是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., 475 U.S. 41, 46–47 (1986)（解释成人娱乐业的分区条例是时间、地点和方式限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"时间、地点和方式限制（例如，分区条例）只要是专门为服务于政府的重大利益而制定的，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径，那么它们就是有效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于本市法令的合宪性，以下哪项陈述最准确？","rule":"时间、地点和方式限制（例如，分区条例）只要是专门为服务于政府的重大利益而制定的，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径，那么它们就是有效的。","analysis":"言论规定\n\n类型定义测试\n\n内容-\n• 面部歧视 基于严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）\n• 主要目的目标\n• 必要且精心定制的信息、主题或想法，以实现引人注目的兴趣 内容-\n• 规定时间、地点或中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）中立的言论方式\n• 为实现重要利益而进行的狭隘定制\n• 替代沟通途径仍然存在 成人剧院和书店是受第一修正案保护的与言论相关的行业。因此，政府对此类企业的监管须接受宪法审查。如果该法规针对的是所说内容（即基于内容的限制），则将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）并被推定为违宪。但如果该规定针对的是言论的时间、地点或方式（即内容中立的限制），则需要经过中间审查并且通常可以接受。在 Renton 诉 Playtime Theatres, Inc. 案中，最高法院认为成人娱乐业的分区条例是时间、地点和方式的限制。这是因为此类法令针对的是成人企业对周围社区产生的次要影响，而不是其内容的可接受性。因此，只要这些法令通过了中间审查，即是专门为服务实质性或重要的政府利益而制定的，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径（选择 B），那么它们就是有效的。 （选择 A） 城市可以执行分区法规，限制成人剧院和书店经营者选择其业务地点的自由，只要该法规能够通过中间审查。 （选项 D）如果分区条例的主要目的是确保与语音相关的企业传播的信息能够为居民所接受，那么该分区条例就是基于内容的限制。这种限制几乎肯定是无效的，因为它必须经过严格的审查——即，必须是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而进行的必要的和严格的调整。\n\n教育目标：时间、地点和方式限制（例如，分区条例）只要是专门为服务于政府的重大利益而制定的，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径，那么它们就是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., 475 U.S. 41, 46–47 (1986)（解释成人娱乐业的分区条例是时间、地点和方式限制）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该条例是有效的，因为一个城市可以对与语音相关的业务执行此类时间、地点和方式的规定，只要该规定旨在服务于政府的重大利益，并且不会不合理地限制其他沟通途径。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":132,"sourceQuestionPage":395,"sourceQuestionPages":[395],"sourceExplanationPages":[396,397],"sourceAnswerPages":[396,397],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-133","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"Insurance is provided in a state only by private companies. Although the state insurance commissioner inspects insurance companies for solvency, the state does not regulate their rates or policies. An insurance company charges higher rates for burglary insurance to residents of one part of a county in the state than to residents of another section of the same county because of the different crime rates in those areas. A resident of that county was charged the higher rate by the insurance company because of the location of her residence. She sues the insurance company, alleging that the differential in insurance rates unconstitutionally denies her the equal protection of the laws. Will the resident's suit succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the constitutional guarantee of equal protection of the laws is not applicable to the actions of these insurance companies.","B":"No, because there is a rational basis for the differential in insurance rates.","C":"Yes, because the higher crime rate in the resident's neighborhood demonstrates that the county police are not giving persons who reside there the equal protection of the laws.","D":"Yes, because the insurance rate differential is inherently discriminatory."},"answer":"A","explanation":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control The Constitution (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) only applies to government actors. But in rare circumstances, a private actor is viewed as a government actor (and subject to the Constitution) under the state-action doctrine. This doctrine applies when:\n• the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or\n• the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Therefore, the resident can only challenge the private insurance company's discriminatory conduct on equal protection grounds if the state-action doctrine applies. Here, the company does not perform a traditional and exclusive government function since insurance has historically been provided by private entities. And though the state inspects all insurance companies for solvency, it does not regulate their rates and policies. But even if it did, state regulation (however substantial) does not amount to significant government involvement since this an ordinary government activity. Therefore, the resident's suit will fail because equal protection guarantees do not apply to the company's actions. (Choice B) Equal protection challenges undergo rational basis review when the challenged action does not impact a fundamental right or protected class. But since the insurance company's actions cannot be challenged on equal protection grounds, it does not matter whether there is a rational basis for the insurance rate differential. (Choices C & D) The higher crime rate in the resident's neighborhood may demonstrate that county police are not giving neighborhood residents the equal protection of the laws. And the insurance rate differential is inherently discriminatory. But these facts are irrelevant in this suit against a private insurance company.\n\nEducational objective: The state-action doctrine applies when (1) a private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private\n\nactor's activities. The private actor is then treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution.\n\nReferences\n• Am. Mfrs. Mut. Ins. Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999) (holding that private insurance companies are not government actors unless there is proof of significant government involvement in their activities).","educationalObjective":"The state-action doctrine applies when (1) a private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private\n\nactor's activities. The private actor is then treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution.","references":"• Am. Mfrs. Mut. Ins. Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999) (holding that private insurance companies are not government actors unless there is proof of significant government involvement in their activities).","rule":"The state-action doctrine applies when (1) a private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private\n\nactor's activities. The private actor is then treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution.","irac":{"issue":"Will the resident's suit succeed?","rule":"The state-action doctrine applies when (1) a private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private\n\nactor's activities. The private actor is then treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution.","analysis":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control The Constitution (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) only applies to government actors. But in rare circumstances, a private actor is viewed as a government actor (and subject to the Constitution) under the state-action doctrine. This doctrine applies when:\n• the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or\n• the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Therefore, the resident can only challenge the private insurance company's discriminatory conduct on equal protection grounds if the state-action doctrine applies. Here, the company does not perform a traditional and exclusive government function since insurance has historically been provided by private entities. And though the state inspects all insurance companies for solvency, it does not regulate their rates and policies. But even if it did, state regulation (however substantial) does not amount to significant government involvement since this an ordinary government activity. Therefore, the resident's suit will fail because equal protection guarantees do not apply to the company's actions. (Choice B) Equal protection challenges undergo rational basis review when the challenged action does not impact a fundamental right or protected class. But since the insurance company's actions cannot be challenged on equal protection grounds, it does not matter whether there is a rational basis for the insurance rate differential. (Choices C & D) The higher crime rate in the resident's neighborhood may demonstrate that county police are not giving neighborhood residents the equal protection of the laws. And the insurance rate differential is inherently discriminatory. But these facts are irrelevant in this suit against a private insurance company.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the constitutional guarantee of equal protection of the laws is not applicable to the actions of these insurance companies."},"questionZh":"在一个州，保险仅由私营公司提供。尽管国家保险专员检查保险公司的偿付能力，但国家并不规范其费率或保单。由于这些地区的犯罪率不同，保险公司对该州某个县的一个地区的居民收取的入室盗窃保险费率高于同一县另一个地区的居民。该县的一名居民因其居住地而被保险公司收取较高的费率。她起诉保险公司，声称保险费率的差异违反宪法，剥夺了她受到法律的平等保护。居民诉讼能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为法律平等保护的宪法保障并不适用于这些保险公司的行为。","B":"不会，因为保险费率的差别是有合理依据的。","C":"是的，因为居民社区较高的犯罪率表明县警察没有给予居住在那里的人平等的法律保护。","D":"是的，因为保险费率差异本质上是歧视性的。"},"explanationZh":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍参与私人实体的管理或控制 《宪法》（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为者。但在极少数情况下，根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），私人行为者被视为政府行为者（并受宪法约束）。该原则适用于以下情况：\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能，或者\n• 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。因此，只有在适用国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）的情况下，居民才能以平等保护为由质疑私人保险公司的歧视行为。在这里，该公司不履行传统的、排他性的政府职能，因为保险历来是由私人实体提供的。尽管国家检查所有保险公司的偿付能力，但并没有监管它们的费率和保单。但即使确实如此，国家监管（无论规模如何）也不等于政府的重大参与，因为这是一项普通的政府活动。因此，居民的诉讼将会失败，因为平等保护保证不适用于公司的行为。 （选择 B）当受到质疑的行动不影响基本权利或受保护阶层时，平等保护质疑将接受合理的基础审查。但由于保险公司的行为无法以平等保障为由受到质疑，因此保险费率差异是否有合理依据并不重要。 （选项 C 和 D） 居民社区较高的犯罪率可能表明县警察没有给予社区居民平等的法律保护。而保险费率差异本质上是歧视性的。但这些事实与这起针对私人保险公司的诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标：国家行为学说适用于以下情况：(1) 私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为\n\n演员的活动。然后，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。\n\n参考文献\n• 上午。制造商。穆特。插入。 Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999)（认为私人保险公司不是政府行为者，除非有证据表明政府大量参与其活动）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用于以下情况：(1) 私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或 (2) 政府大量参与私人活动\n\n演员的活动。然后，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。","iracZh":{"issue":"居民诉讼能成功吗？","rule":"国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用于以下情况：(1) 私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或 (2) 政府大量参与私人活动\n\n演员的活动。然后，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。","analysis":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍参与私人实体的管理或控制 《宪法》（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为者。但在极少数情况下，根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），私人行为者被视为政府行为者（并受宪法约束）。该原则适用于以下情况：\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能，或者\n• 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。因此，只有在适用国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）的情况下，居民才能以平等保护为由质疑私人保险公司的歧视行为。在这里，该公司不履行传统的、排他性的政府职能，因为保险历来是由私人实体提供的。尽管国家检查所有保险公司的偿付能力，但并没有监管它们的费率和保单。但即使确实如此，国家监管（无论规模如何）也不等于政府的重大参与，因为这是一项普通的政府活动。因此，居民的诉讼将会失败，因为平等保护保证不适用于公司的行为。 （选择 B）当受到质疑的行动不影响基本权利或受保护阶层时，平等保护质疑将接受合理的基础审查。但由于保险公司的行为无法以平等保障为由受到质疑，因此保险费率差异是否有合理依据并不重要。 （选项 C 和 D） 居民社区较高的犯罪率可能表明县警察没有给予社区居民平等的法律保护。而保险费率差异本质上是歧视性的。但这些事实与这起针对私人保险公司的诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标：国家行为学说适用于以下情况：(1) 私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为\n\n演员的活动。然后，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。\n\n参考文献\n• 上午。制造商。穆特。插入。 Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999)（认为私人保险公司不是政府行为者，除非有证据表明政府大量参与其活动）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为法律平等保护的宪法保障并不适用于这些保险公司的行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"affirmatively","ipa":"/ʌfˈɝmətɪvli/","syllables":"af·fir·ma·tively","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 肯定地；断然地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 肯定地；断然地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"State-action doctrine Traditional • Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections) Significant • Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement • Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law) • Gov","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":133,"sourceQuestionPage":398,"sourceQuestionPages":[398],"sourceExplanationPages":[399,400],"sourceAnswerPages":[399,400],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-134","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"A state law provides some funding for public schools on a per-student basis from general state revenues, which primarily come from the state income and sales taxes. The law also provides that all other public monies used to support public schools in the state come from locally levied real estate taxes. This results in a large disparity in per-student funding among the state's many public school districts because some districts have higher property values per student than other districts. Public school students who claim to be disadvantaged by this school-funding law have challenged the law solely on the ground that it violates the Fourteenth Amendment's equal protection clause. Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action?","choices":{"A":"The state must demonstrate that the law is necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest.","B":"The state must demonstrate that the law is rationally related to a legitimate state interest.","C":"The students must demonstrate that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest.","D":"The students must demonstrate that the law is not substantially related to an important state interest."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives An equal protection challenge requires proof that the law (1) is discriminatory—ie, treats similarly situated people differently—and (2) lacks adequate justification. The required justification depends on whether the law substantially impacts a suspect class or fundamental right (triggering strict scrutiny) or a quasi-suspect class (triggering intermediate scrutiny). If it does not, then the challenger must satisfy the rational basis test.\n\nHere, the students challenged a state law that results in a large disparity in per-student funding among the state's public school districts. But education is not a fundamental right, and the funding disparities are not alleged to affect a suspect or quasi-suspect class (no strict or intermediate scrutiny) (Choice A). Therefore, the court will apply the rational basis standard—ie, the burden of persuasion is on the students (not the state) to demonstrate that the school-funding law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest (Choice B). (Choice D) The burden would be on the state to demonstrate that the law is not substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny), had the law affected a quasi-suspect class.\n\nEducational objective: A discriminatory law can be challenged on equal protection grounds and is generally reviewed under the rational basis test. However, a more stringent level of review will be used when the law substantially impacts a quasi-suspect class, suspect class, or fundamental right.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1 (equal protection clause).\n\n• San Antonio Indep. Sch. Dist. v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973) (holding that disparities in public school funding do not implicate a protected class or fundamental right).","educationalObjective":"A discriminatory law can be challenged on equal protection grounds and is generally reviewed under the rational basis test. However, a more stringent level of review will be used when the law substantially impacts a quasi-suspect class, suspect class, or fundamental right.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1 (equal protection clause).\n\n• San Antonio Indep. Sch. Dist. v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973) (holding that disparities in public school funding do not implicate a protected class or fundamental right).","rule":"A discriminatory law can be challenged on equal protection grounds and is generally reviewed under the rational basis test. However, a more stringent level of review will be used when the law substantially impacts a quasi-suspect class, suspect class, or fundamental right.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action?","rule":"A discriminatory law can be challenged on equal protection grounds and is generally reviewed under the rational basis test. However, a more stringent level of review will be used when the law substantially impacts a quasi-suspect class, suspect class, or fundamental right.","analysis":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives An equal protection challenge requires proof that the law (1) is discriminatory—ie, treats similarly situated people differently—and (2) lacks adequate justification. The required justification depends on whether the law substantially impacts a suspect class or fundamental right (triggering strict scrutiny) or a quasi-suspect class (triggering intermediate scrutiny). If it does not, then the challenger must satisfy the rational basis test.\n\nHere, the students challenged a state law that results in a large disparity in per-student funding among the state's public school districts. But education is not a fundamental right, and the funding disparities are not alleged to affect a suspect or quasi-suspect class (no strict or intermediate scrutiny) (Choice A). Therefore, the court will apply the rational basis standard—ie, the burden of persuasion is on the students (not the state) to demonstrate that the school-funding law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest (Choice B). (Choice D) The burden would be on the state to demonstrate that the law is not substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny), had the law affected a quasi-suspect class.","conclusion":"Answer C: The students must demonstrate that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest."},"questionZh":"州法律根据每个学生的情况，从州一般收入中为公立学校提供一些资金，这些资金主要来自州所得税和销售税。该法律还规定，用于支持该州公立学校的所有其他公共资金均来自当地征收的房地产税。这导致该州许多公立学区的每个学生的资金存在巨大差异，因为某些学区的每个学生的财产价值高于其他学区。声称因这项学校资助法而处于不利地位的公立学校学生仅以该法违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）为由对该法提出了质疑。以下哪项最能说明此行动中的说服负担？","choicesZh":{"A":"国家必须证明法律对于维护令人信服的国家利益是必要的。","B":"国家必须证明法律与合法的国家利益合理相关。","C":"学生必须证明法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。","D":"学生必须证明该法律与重要的国家利益没有实质性关系。"},"explanationZh":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 平等保护挑战需要证明法律(1) 具有歧视性，即对处境相似的人进行不同对待，以及(2) 缺乏充分的理由。所需的理由取决于法律是否对可疑类别或基本权利（引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））或准可疑类别（引发中间审查）产生实质性影响。如果没有，那么挑战者必须满足合理基础测试。\n\n在这里，学生们对一项州法律提出了质疑，该法律导致该州公立学区之间每个学生的资金存在巨大差异。但教育并不是一项基本权利，而且资金差异并不影响嫌疑人或准嫌疑人阶层（没有严格或中间的审查）（选项 A）。因此，法院将适用合理依据标准，即由学生（而不是国家）承担说服责任，以证明学校资助法与任何合法的国家利益不存在合理关联（选项B）。 （选择D）如果法律影响了准可疑阶层，则国家有责任证明该法律与重要的国家利益没有实质性关系（即中间审查）。\n\n教育目标：歧视性法律可以在平等保护的基础上受到质疑，并且通常会在合理基础测试下进行审查。然而，当法律对准嫌疑阶层、嫌疑阶层或基本权利产生重大影响时，将采用更严格的审查。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XIV，§ 1（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• 圣安东尼奥独立。施。区。 v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973)（认为公立学校经费的差异并不意味着受保护阶层或基本权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"歧视性法律可以基于平等保护的理由受到质疑，并且通常会根据合理基础测试进行审查。然而，当法律对准嫌疑阶层、嫌疑阶层或基本权利产生重大影响时，将采用更严格的审查。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能说明此行动中的说服负担？","rule":"歧视性法律可以基于平等保护的理由受到质疑，并且通常会根据合理基础测试进行审查。然而，当法律对准嫌疑阶层、嫌疑阶层或基本权利产生重大影响时，将采用更严格的审查。","analysis":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 平等保护挑战需要证明法律(1) 具有歧视性，即对处境相似的人进行不同对待，以及(2) 缺乏充分的理由。所需的理由取决于法律是否对可疑类别或基本权利（引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））或准可疑类别（引发中间审查）产生实质性影响。如果没有，那么挑战者必须满足合理基础测试。\n\n在这里，学生们对一项州法律提出了质疑，该法律导致该州公立学区之间每个学生的资金存在巨大差异。但教育并不是一项基本权利，而且资金差异并不影响嫌疑人或准嫌疑人阶层（没有严格或中间的审查）（选项 A）。因此，法院将适用合理依据标准，即由学生（而不是国家）承担说服责任，以证明学校资助法与任何合法的国家利益不存在合理关联（选项B）。 （选择D）如果法律影响了准可疑阶层，则国家有责任证明该法律与重要的国家利益没有实质性关系（即中间审查）。\n\n教育目标：歧视性法律可以在平等保护的基础上受到质疑，并且通常会在合理基础测试下进行审查。然而，当法律对准嫌疑阶层、嫌疑阶层或基本权利产生重大影响时，将采用更严格的审查。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XIV，§ 1（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• 圣安东尼奥独立。施。区。 v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973)（认为公立学校经费的差异并不意味着受保护阶层或基本权利）。","conclusion":"答案 C：学生必须证明法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"nationality","ipa":"/nˌæʃʌnˈæləti/","syllables":"na·tion·al·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 国籍, 国家, 民族性；[法] 国家, 民族, 国民","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 国籍, 国家, 民族性；[法] 国家, 民族, 国民","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:nationalities","sourceSentence":"Equal protection (standards of review) Standard Applicability Test Rational basis Default test Challenger must show • action is not rationally related • to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class: • action is • Sex/gender substantially related • Legitimacy • to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class:","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":134,"sourceQuestionPage":401,"sourceQuestionPages":[401],"sourceExplanationPages":[402,403],"sourceAnswerPages":[402,403],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-135","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Establishment Clause","question":"A city operates a cemetery pursuant to a city ordinance. The ordinance requires the operation of the city cemetery to be supported primarily by revenues derived from the sale of cemetery lots to individuals. The ordinance further provides that the purchase of a cemetery lot entitles the owner to perpetual care of the lot, and entitles the owner to erect on the lot, at the owner's expense, a memorial monument or marker of the owner's choice, subject to certain size restrictions. The city ordinance requires the city to maintain the cemetery, including mowing the grass, watering flowers, and plowing snow, and provides for the expenditure of city tax funds for such maintenance if revenues from the sale of cemetery lots are insufficient. Although cemetery lots are sold at full fair market value, which includes the current value of perpetual care, the revenue from the sale of such lots has been insufficient in recent years to maintain the cemetery. As a result, a small amount of city tax funds has also been used for that purpose. A group of city taxpayers brings suit against the city challenging the constitutionality of the city ordinance insofar as it permits the owner of a cemetery lot to erect a religious memorial monument or marker on his or her lot. Is this suit likely to be successful?","choices":{"A":"No, because only a small amount of city tax funds has been used to maintain the cemetery.","B":"No, because the ordinance does not violate the establishment clause of the First Amendment.","C":"Yes, because city maintenance of any religious object is a violation of the establishment clause of the First Amendment.","D":"Yes, because no compelling governmental interest justifies authorizing private persons to erect religious monuments or markers in a city-operated cemetery."},"answer":"B","explanation":"City taxpayers have challenged the constitutionality of a city ordinance that permits persons to erect religious monuments or markers on cemetery lots maintained by the city. This challenge triggers the First Amendment establishment clause since it alleges that the government failed to remain neutral toward religion. As a result, the government action will be reviewed under the historical test and upheld if the action has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution. Historically, individuals have had the right to erect monuments or markers of their choice on individual cemetery lots, including religious monuments or markers on lots in city- operated cemeteries. And since the ordinance here adheres to this long-standing practice, it is in accord with the original meaning of the Constitution and does not violate the establishment clause (Choice C). As a result, the taxpayers' suit will likely be unsuccessful. (Choice A) The small amount of city tax funds used to maintain the cemetery helps establish the lack of government entanglement with religion required to satisfy the Lemon test. But this test has been abandoned in establishment-clause challenges. (Choice D) The city ordinance would have to be necessary to achieve a compelling government interest to survive strict scrutiny. But courts will only apply this heightened standard and forego the historical test when a law expressly favors or disfavors religion— which the ordinance here did not do. Therefore, no compelling interest is required.\n\nEducational objective: Establishment clause challenges are reviewed under the historical test, which upholds government actions that have historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.\n\nReferences\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School Dist., 142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022) (holding that challenges based on the establishment clause \"must be interpreted by reference to historical practices and understandings\").","educationalObjective":"Establishment clause challenges are reviewed under the historical test, which upholds government actions that have historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.","references":"• Kennedy v. Bremerton School Dist., 142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022) (holding that challenges based on the establishment clause \"must be interpreted by reference to historical practices and understandings\").","rule":"Establishment clause challenges are reviewed under the historical test, which upholds government actions that have historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.","irac":{"issue":"Is this suit likely to be successful?","rule":"Establishment clause challenges are reviewed under the historical test, which upholds government actions that have historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.","analysis":"City taxpayers have challenged the constitutionality of a city ordinance that permits persons to erect religious monuments or markers on cemetery lots maintained by the city. This challenge triggers the First Amendment establishment clause since it alleges that the government failed to remain neutral toward religion. As a result, the government action will be reviewed under the historical test and upheld if the action has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution. Historically, individuals have had the right to erect monuments or markers of their choice on individual cemetery lots, including religious monuments or markers on lots in city- operated cemeteries. And since the ordinance here adheres to this long-standing practice, it is in accord with the original meaning of the Constitution and does not violate the establishment clause (Choice C). As a result, the taxpayers' suit will likely be unsuccessful. (Choice A) The small amount of city tax funds used to maintain the cemetery helps establish the lack of government entanglement with religion required to satisfy the Lemon test. But this test has been abandoned in establishment-clause challenges. (Choice D) The city ordinance would have to be necessary to achieve a compelling government interest to survive strict scrutiny. But courts will only apply this heightened standard and forego the historical test when a law expressly favors or disfavors religion— which the ordinance here did not do. Therefore, no compelling interest is required.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the ordinance does not violate the establishment clause of the First Amendment."},"questionZh":"城市根据城市条例经营墓地。该条例要求城市公墓的运营主要靠向个人出售公墓地块获得的收入来支持。该法令还规定，购买墓地地块使所有者有权永久保管该地块，并有权自费在该地块上竖立纪念碑或由所有者选择的标记，但须遵守一定的尺寸限制。该市法令要求市政府对公墓进行维护，包括割草、浇花和除雪，并规定如果公墓地块销售收入不足，市税基金将用于此类维护。尽管墓地地块以完全公平的市场价值出售，其中包括永久护理的当前价值，但近年来出售此类地块的收入不足以维持墓地的维护。因此，少量的城市税收资金也被用于此目的。一群城市纳税人对市政府提起诉讼，质疑该城市法令的合宪性，因为该法令允许墓地所有者在其墓地上竖立宗教纪念纪念碑或标记。这场诉讼有可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为只使用了少量的城市税收资金来维护墓地。","B":"不，因为该条例并不违反第一修正案的成立条款。","C":"是的，因为城市对任何宗教物体的维护都违反了第一修正案的确立条款。","D":"是的，因为没有任何令人信服的政府利益可以证明授权私人在城市管理的墓地中竖立宗教纪念碑或标记是合理的。"},"explanationZh":"城市纳税人对一项城市法令的合宪性提出质疑，该法令允许人们在城市维护的墓地上竖立宗教纪念碑或标记。这一挑战触发了第一修正案的确立条款，因为它声称政府未能对宗教保持中立。因此，政府行为将接受历史检验，如果该行为在历史上被允许且符合宪法的原意，则该行为将得到维持。从历史上看，个人有权在个人墓地中竖立自己选择的纪念碑或标记，包括在城市管理的墓地中的宗教纪念碑或标记。而这里的条例由于秉承了这一长期以来的做法，因此符合宪法的本意，并不违反宪法设立条款（选项C）。因此，纳税人的诉讼很可能会失败。 （选项 A）用于维护墓地的少量城市税收资金有助于确定满足柠檬测试所需的政府与宗教的关系不存在。但这一测试在政教分离条款（Establishment Clause）挑战中已被放弃。 （选项 D） 城市法令必须是实现令人信服的政府利益才能经受住严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的必要条件。但只有当法律明确支持或反对宗教时，法院才会适用这一更高的标准并放弃历史检验——而这里的法令没有这样做。因此，不需要有强烈的兴趣。\n\n教育目标：在历史检验下审查建制条款的挑战，维护历史上根据宪法本义允许的政府行为。\n\n参考文献\n• 肯尼迪诉布雷默顿学区，142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022)（认为基于成立条款的质疑“必须参考历史实践和理解来解释”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"建制条款的挑战是在历史检验下进行审查的，它维护了历史上根据宪法本义允许的政府行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"这场诉讼有可能成功吗？","rule":"建制条款的挑战是在历史检验下进行审查的，它维护了历史上根据宪法本义允许的政府行为。","analysis":"城市纳税人对一项城市法令的合宪性提出质疑，该法令允许人们在城市维护的墓地上竖立宗教纪念碑或标记。这一挑战触发了第一修正案的确立条款，因为它声称政府未能对宗教保持中立。因此，政府行为将接受历史检验，如果该行为在历史上被允许且符合宪法的原意，则该行为将得到维持。从历史上看，个人有权在个人墓地中竖立自己选择的纪念碑或标记，包括在城市管理的墓地中的宗教纪念碑或标记。而这里的条例由于秉承了这一长期以来的做法，因此符合宪法的本意，并不违反宪法设立条款（选项C）。因此，纳税人的诉讼很可能会失败。 （选项 A）用于维护墓地的少量城市税收资金有助于确定满足柠檬测试所需的政府与宗教的关系不存在。但这一测试在政教分离条款（Establishment Clause）挑战中已被放弃。 （选项 D） 城市法令必须是实现令人信服的政府利益才能经受住严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的必要条件。但只有当法律明确支持或反对宗教时，法院才会适用这一更高的标准并放弃历史检验——而这里的法令没有这样做。因此，不需要有强烈的兴趣。\n\n教育目标：在历史检验下审查建制条款的挑战，维护历史上根据宪法本义允许的政府行为。\n\n参考文献\n• 肯尼迪诉布雷默顿学区，142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022)（认为基于成立条款的质疑“必须参考历史实践和理解来解释”）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为该条例并不违反第一修正案的成立条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"compelling governmental interest","ipa":"/kʌmpˈɛlɪŋ ɡˈʌvɝmˌɛntəl ˈɪntɹəst/","syllables":"com·pelling gov·ern·men·tal in·ter·est","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"重大政府利益：严格审查所要求的最高等级政府目标","contextualMeaning":"重大政府利益：严格审查所要求的最高等级政府目标","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because no compelling governmental interest justifies authorizing private persons to erect religious monuments or markers in a city-operated cemetery.","synonyms":["compelling governmental pastime","compelling governmental pursuit","compelling governmental sake"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-3b705c40dfe53746"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":135,"sourceQuestionPage":404,"sourceQuestionPages":[404],"sourceExplanationPages":[405,406],"sourceAnswerPages":[405,406],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-136","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"Congress enacted a statute authorizing the denial of all federal funding to public school districts in which a specified percentage of the students enrolled in the public schools fail to pass a national achievement test. According to the terms of the federal statute, the first national achievement test was scheduled for administration five years from the effective date of the statute. After reviewing then-current levels of public school student performance, the officials of a state became concerned that several of its public school districts would lose their federal funding after the administration of the first national achievement test. Then-current levels of private school student performance were substantially higher. In order to improve the chances of those school districts retaining their federal funding, the state recently enacted a law that requires all children of elementary and secondary school age to attend the schools operated by their respective local public school districts. The law is to take effect at the beginning of the next school year. Parents of children enrolled in private schools within the state have filed suit to challenge the constitutionality of this state law. Should the court uphold the law?","choices":{"A":"No, because it is not necessary to further a compelling state interest.","B":"No, because it is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest.","C":"Yes, because it is necessary to further a compelling state interest.","D":"Yes, because it is rationally related to a legitimate state interest."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Fourteenth Amendment substantive due process principles prevent states from depriving individuals of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. State laws that substantially impair a fundamental right—eg, the right to privacy, which encompasses parents' right to control their children's upbringing and education*—must undergo strict scrutiny. This difficult test requires the state to show that its law is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. Here, the state law substantially impairs parents' fundamental right to control their children's upbringing and education by requiring all children to attend public school. And though the state may have a compelling interest in raising test scores to retain federal funding, the law is not necessary to further that interest. This is because the state can do so with less restrictive measures—eg, improving teaching and test preparation in public schools (Choice C). Therefore, the law should not be upheld under strict scrutiny. *The right to education is an ordinary right that is subject to rational basis review. But this right has not been impaired by the state law because each child is still receiving an education through the public school system. (Choices B & D) The law is rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in raising test scores to retain federal funding. But the easy-to-pass rational basis test does not apply here since the law substantially impairs a fundamental right.\n\nEducational objective: Laws that substantially impair a fundamental right—eg, parents' right to control their children's upbringing and education—can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under strict scrutiny and rarely survive.\n\nReferences\n• Pierce v. Soc'y of Sisters, 268 U.S. 510, 534–35 (1925) (holding that a law eliminating private schools violates the fundamental right of parents to direct the upbringing and education of their children).","educationalObjective":"Laws that substantially impair a fundamental right—eg, parents' right to control their children's upbringing and education—can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under strict scrutiny and rarely survive.","references":"• Pierce v. Soc'y of Sisters, 268 U.S. 510, 534–35 (1925) (holding that a law eliminating private schools violates the fundamental right of parents to direct the upbringing and education of their children).","rule":"Laws that substantially impair a fundamental right—eg, parents' right to control their children's upbringing and education—can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under strict scrutiny and rarely survive.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court uphold the law?","rule":"Laws that substantially impair a fundamental right—eg, parents' right to control their children's upbringing and education—can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under strict scrutiny and rarely survive.","analysis":"Fourteenth Amendment substantive due process principles prevent states from depriving individuals of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. State laws that substantially impair a fundamental right—eg, the right to privacy, which encompasses parents' right to control their children's upbringing and education*—must undergo strict scrutiny. This difficult test requires the state to show that its law is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. Here, the state law substantially impairs parents' fundamental right to control their children's upbringing and education by requiring all children to attend public school. And though the state may have a compelling interest in raising test scores to retain federal funding, the law is not necessary to further that interest. This is because the state can do so with less restrictive measures—eg, improving teaching and test preparation in public schools (Choice C). Therefore, the law should not be upheld under strict scrutiny. *The right to education is an ordinary right that is subject to rational basis review. But this right has not been impaired by the state law because each child is still receiving an education through the public school system. (Choices B & D) The law is rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in raising test scores to retain federal funding. But the easy-to-pass rational basis test does not apply here since the law substantially impairs a fundamental right.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because it is not necessary to further a compelling state interest."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，授权拒绝向公立学校就读的特定比例的学生未能通过国家成绩测试的公立学区提供所有联邦资助。根据联邦法规的条款，第一次全国成绩测试计划在该法规生效之日起五年内实施。在审查了当时公立学校学生的表现水平后，州官员开始担心，在第一次全国成绩测试实施后，该州的几个公立学区将失去联邦资助。当时私立学校学生的表现水平要高得多。为了提高这些学区保留联邦资助的机会，该州最近颁布了一项法律，要求所有中小学适龄儿童就读各自当地公立学区开办的学校。该法律将于下一学年开始时生效。在该州私立学校就读的孩子的家长已提起诉讼，质疑该州法律的合宪性。法院是否应该维护法律？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为没有必要促进令人信服的国家利益。","B":"不，因为它与合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。","C":"是的，因为有必要促进令人信服的国家利益。","D":"是的，因为它与合法的国家利益合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"第十四修正案的实质性正当程序原则防止国家在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产。严重损害基本权利（例如隐私权，其中包括父母控制子女抚养和教育*的权利）的州法律必须接受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这一困难的考验要求国家证明其法律对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。在这里，州法律要求所有孩子就读公立学校，从而严重损害了父母控制孩子抚养和教育的基本权利。尽管各州可能对提高考试成绩以保留联邦资金有着强烈的兴趣，但法律没有必要促进这种兴趣。这是因为国家可以通过较少的限制性措施来做到这一点，例如改善公立学校的教学和考试准备（选项 C）。因此，法律不应受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。 *受教育权是一项普通权利，需要经过合理基础审查。但这项权利并没有受到州法律的损害，因为每个孩子仍然通过公立学校系统接受教育。 （选择 B 和 D）该法律与各州提高考试成绩以保留联邦资金的合法利益合理相关。但容易通过的合理基础测试在这里并不适用，因为该法律严重损害了一项基本权利。\n\n教育目标：严重损害基本权利（例如父母控制子女抚养和教育的权利）的法律可以基于实质性正当程序理由受到质疑。此类法律经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），很少能保留下来。\n\n参考文献\n• Pierce v. Soc'y of Sisters, 268 U.S. 510, 534–35 (1925)（认为取消私立学校的法律侵犯了父母指导子女抚养和教育的基本权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"严重损害基本权利（例如父母控制子女抚养和教育的权利）的法律可能会因实质性正当程序理由而受到质疑。此类法律经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），很少能保留下来。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该维护法律？","rule":"严重损害基本权利（例如父母控制子女抚养和教育的权利）的法律可能会因实质性正当程序理由而受到质疑。此类法律经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），很少能保留下来。","analysis":"第十四修正案的实质性正当程序原则防止国家在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产。严重损害基本权利（例如隐私权，其中包括父母控制子女抚养和教育*的权利）的州法律必须接受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这一困难的考验要求国家证明其法律对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。在这里，州法律要求所有孩子就读公立学校，从而严重损害了父母控制孩子抚养和教育的基本权利。尽管各州可能对提高考试成绩以保留联邦资金有着强烈的兴趣，但法律没有必要促进这种兴趣。这是因为国家可以通过较少的限制性措施来做到这一点，例如改善公立学校的教学和考试准备（选项 C）。因此，法律不应受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。 *受教育权是一项普通权利，需要经过合理基础审查。但这项权利并没有受到州法律的损害，因为每个孩子仍然通过公立学校系统接受教育。 （选择 B 和 D）该法律与各州提高考试成绩以保留联邦资金的合法利益合理相关。但容易通过的合理基础测试在这里并不适用，因为该法律严重损害了一项基本权利。\n\n教育目标：严重损害基本权利（例如父母控制子女抚养和教育的权利）的法律可以基于实质性正当程序理由受到质疑。此类法律经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），很少能保留下来。\n\n参考文献\n• Pierce v. Soc'y of Sisters, 268 U.S. 510, 534–35 (1925)（认为取消私立学校的法律侵犯了父母指导子女抚养和教育的基本权利）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为没有必要促进令人信服的国家利益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-268b9f4e798dce2e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":136,"sourceQuestionPage":407,"sourceQuestionPages":[407],"sourceExplanationPages":[408,409],"sourceAnswerPages":[408,409],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-137","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state prohibits voting for a write-in candidate in both primary and general elections. However, the state provides a mechanism by which individuals who want to appear as candidates on the primary ballot, as well as groups that want to be classified as a party, can do so. Of the following, which is the best argument that a voter challenging the constitutionality of this ban on write-in candidates can make?","choices":{"A":"The Fifteenth Amendment limits a state's power to regulate its own elections.","B":"The mechanism provided by the state does not provide for reasonable access to the ballot.","C":"The right to vote is a fundamental right, so the state ban is subject to strict scrutiny review.","D":"The state ban imposes a burden on the right of voters to select the candidates of their choice."},"answer":"B","explanation":"State-election restrictions\n\nRestriction Examples Test\n\nOrdinary\n• Voter registration Rational basis: challenger must show\n• Photo-ID restriction lacks rational relationship (nondiscriminatory) to legitimate state interest requirement\n• Disallowing write-in voting Severe\n• Poll tax Strict scrutiny: state must show\n• Disallowing third- restriction is necessary to achieve (discriminatory) compelling state interest party candidacies\n• Property-ownership requirement State electoral regulations impact an individual's fundamental right to vote and freely associate, so they can be challenged under the First Amendment or the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. The standard for evaluating such regulations under either amendment depends on the severity of the burden imposed. A court will apply either:\n• rational basis review – applies to ordinary (nondiscriminatory) burdens and requires the challenger to show that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny – applies to severe (discriminatory) burdens and requires the state to show that the regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest.\n\nHere, the state bans write-in candidates in both primary and general elections. The state instead provides a mechanism by which individuals who want to appear as candidates on the primary ballot, as well as groups that want to be classified as a party, can do so. Since this ban is an ordinary (nondiscriminatory) burden, it is subject to rational basis review— not strict scrutiny review (Choice C). Therefore, the best argument to challenge this ban is that the mechanism provided by the state does not provide for reasonable access to the ballot. (Choice A) The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits state and federal governments from abridging the right to vote on the basis of race, color, or previous condition of servitude. But since the state regulation here does not bar voting on any of these bases, this is not a strong argument.\n\n(Choice D) The fact that the state ban imposes a burden on the right of voters to select candidate of their choice does not, on its own, provide a strong argument to challenge the constitutionality of the ban. Instead, a challenger must also show that the ban does not satisfy rational basis scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: Severe (discriminatory) electoral regulations are subject to strict scrutiny. But ordinary (nondiscriminatory) electoral regulations (eg, ban on write-in candidates) are subject to the less stringent rational basis test.\n\nReferences\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 434 (1992) (holding that a state ban on write-in candidates is an ordinary burden subject to rational basis scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"Severe (discriminatory) electoral regulations are subject to strict scrutiny. But ordinary (nondiscriminatory) electoral regulations (eg, ban on write-in candidates) are subject to the less stringent rational basis test.","references":"• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 434 (1992) (holding that a state ban on write-in candidates is an ordinary burden subject to rational basis scrutiny).","rule":"Severe (discriminatory) electoral regulations are subject to strict scrutiny. But ordinary (nondiscriminatory) electoral regulations (eg, ban on write-in candidates) are subject to the less stringent rational basis test.","irac":{"issue":"Of the following, which is the best argument that a voter challenging the constitutionality of this ban on write-in candidates can make?","rule":"Severe (discriminatory) electoral regulations are subject to strict scrutiny. But ordinary (nondiscriminatory) electoral regulations (eg, ban on write-in candidates) are subject to the less stringent rational basis test.","analysis":"State-election restrictions\n\nRestriction Examples Test\n\nOrdinary\n• Voter registration Rational basis: challenger must show\n• Photo-ID restriction lacks rational relationship (nondiscriminatory) to legitimate state interest requirement\n• Disallowing write-in voting Severe\n• Poll tax Strict scrutiny: state must show\n• Disallowing third- restriction is necessary to achieve (discriminatory) compelling state interest party candidacies\n• Property-ownership requirement State electoral regulations impact an individual's fundamental right to vote and freely associate, so they can be challenged under the First Amendment or the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. The standard for evaluating such regulations under either amendment depends on the severity of the burden imposed. A court will apply either:\n• rational basis review – applies to ordinary (nondiscriminatory) burdens and requires the challenger to show that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny – applies to severe (discriminatory) burdens and requires the state to show that the regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest.\n\nHere, the state bans write-in candidates in both primary and general elections. The state instead provides a mechanism by which individuals who want to appear as candidates on the primary ballot, as well as groups that want to be classified as a party, can do so. Since this ban is an ordinary (nondiscriminatory) burden, it is subject to rational basis review— not strict scrutiny review (Choice C). Therefore, the best argument to challenge this ban is that the mechanism provided by the state does not provide for reasonable access to the ballot. (Choice A) The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits state and federal governments from abridging the right to vote on the basis of race, color, or previous condition of servitude. But since the state regulation here does not bar voting on any of these bases, this is not a strong argument.\n\n(Choice D) The fact that the state ban imposes a burden on the right of voters to select candidate of their choice does not, on its own, provide a strong argument to challenge the constitutionality of the ban. Instead, a challenger must also show that the ban does not satisfy rational basis scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer B: The mechanism provided by the state does not provide for reasonable access to the ballot."},"questionZh":"州禁止在初选和大选中投票给补记候选人。然而，国家提供了一种机制，希望作为候选人参加初选的个人以及希望被归类为政党的团体都可以这样做。对于质疑写入候选人禁令的合宪性的选民来说，以下哪一项是最好的论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"第十五修正案限制各州监管其选举的权力。","B":"国家提供的机制没有提供合理的投票机会。","C":"投票权是一项基本权利，因此国家禁令要受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。","D":"州禁令对选民选择自己选择的候选人的权利施加了负担。"},"explanationZh":"州选举限制\n\n限制示例测试\n\n普通\n• 选民登记 合理基础：挑战者必须表明\n• 照片身份限制与合法的国家利益要求缺乏理性关系（非歧视性）\n• 不允许写入投票 严重\n• 人头税 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：州政府必须表明\n• 禁止第三方限制对于获得（歧视性）令人信服的国家利益党候选人资格是必要的\n• 财产所有权要求 州选举法规影响个人投票和自由结社的基本权利，因此这些权利可能会受到第一修正案或第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的质疑。根据任何一项修正案评估此类法规的标准取决于所施加负担的严重程度。法院将适用以下任一方法：\n• 合理基础审查——适用于普通（非歧视）负担，并要求质疑者证明该法规与合法国家利益没有合理关系，或\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）——适用于严重的（歧视性）负担，并要求国家表明监管是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n在这里，该州禁止在初选和大选中提名候选人。相反，国家提供了一种机制，希望作为候选人参加初选的个人以及希望被归类为政党的团体都可以这样做。由于该禁令是一项普通（非歧视性）负担，因此需要接受合理基础审查，而不是严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（选项 C）。因此，质疑这一禁令的最佳论据是，国家提供的机制没有提供合理的投票机会。 （选项 A） 第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府因种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况而剥夺投票权。但由于这里的国家法规并不禁止基于任何这些基础进行投票，因此这并不是一个有力的论据。\n\n（选项 D）州禁令对选民选择自己选择的候选人的权利施加了负担，这一事实本身并不能提供强有力的论据来质疑该禁令的合宪性。相反，挑战者还必须证明该禁令不符合合理基础的审查。\n\n教育目标：严厉（歧视性）的选举法规受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但普通（非歧视性）选举法规（例如，禁止写入候选人）受到不太严格的合理基础测试。\n\n参考文献\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 434 (1992)（认为州对补录候选人的禁令是一项普通负担，需要经过合理基础审查）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"严厉的（歧视性的）选举法规受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但普通（非歧视性）选举法规（例如，禁止写入候选人）受到不太严格的合理基础测试。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于质疑写入候选人禁令的合宪性的选民来说，以下哪一项是最好的论据？","rule":"严厉的（歧视性的）选举法规受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但普通（非歧视性）选举法规（例如，禁止写入候选人）受到不太严格的合理基础测试。","analysis":"州选举限制\n\n限制示例测试\n\n普通\n• 选民登记 合理基础：挑战者必须表明\n• 照片身份限制与合法的国家利益要求缺乏理性关系（非歧视性）\n• 不允许写入投票 严重\n• 人头税 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：州政府必须表明\n• 禁止第三方限制对于获得（歧视性）令人信服的国家利益党候选人资格是必要的\n• 财产所有权要求 州选举法规影响个人投票和自由结社的基本权利，因此这些权利可能会受到第一修正案或第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的质疑。根据任何一项修正案评估此类法规的标准取决于所施加负担的严重程度。法院将适用以下任一方法：\n• 合理基础审查——适用于普通（非歧视）负担，并要求质疑者证明该法规与合法国家利益没有合理关系，或\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）——适用于严重的（歧视性）负担，并要求国家表明监管是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n在这里，该州禁止在初选和大选中提名候选人。相反，国家提供了一种机制，希望作为候选人参加初选的个人以及希望被归类为政党的团体都可以这样做。由于该禁令是一项普通（非歧视性）负担，因此需要接受合理基础审查，而不是严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（选项 C）。因此，质疑这一禁令的最佳论据是，国家提供的机制没有提供合理的投票机会。 （选项 A） 第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府因种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况而剥夺投票权。但由于这里的国家法规并不禁止基于任何这些基础进行投票，因此这并不是一个有力的论据。\n\n（选项 D）州禁令对选民选择自己选择的候选人的权利施加了负担，这一事实本身并不能提供强有力的论据来质疑该禁令的合宪性。相反，挑战者还必须证明该禁令不符合合理基础的审查。\n\n教育目标：严厉（歧视性）的选举法规受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但普通（非歧视性）选举法规（例如，禁止写入候选人）受到不太严格的合理基础测试。\n\n参考文献\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 434 (1992)（认为州对补录候选人的禁令是一项普通负担，需要经过合理基础审查）。","conclusion":"答案 B：国家提供的机制没有提供合理的投票机会。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"least restrictive means","ipa":"/lˈist ɹistɹˈɪktɪv mˈinz/","syllables":"least re·stric·tive means","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"限制最小的手段：实现政府目标而最少侵害权利的可行方案","contextualMeaning":"限制最小的手段：实现政府目标而最少侵害权利的可行方案","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A court will apply either: • rational basis review – applies to ordinary (nondiscriminatory) burdens and requires the challenger to show that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest or • strict scrutiny – applies to severe (discriminatory) burdens and requires the state to show that the regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest.","synonyms":["least restrictive agency","least restrictive way","least restrictive substance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":137,"sourceQuestionPage":410,"sourceQuestionPages":[410],"sourceExplanationPages":[411,412],"sourceAnswerPages":[411,412],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-138","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state court issued a gag order that prohibited the publication of information about a pending murder trial until the jury had been impaneled. The purpose of the order was to avoid pretrial publicity that would prejudice the criminal defendant's right to an impartial jury. Several newspapers that sought to report about the trial's pretrial events challenged the order, arguing that it was unconstitutional. Is the court's order likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the Sixth Amendment right to an impartial jury does not apply to state criminal prosecutions.","B":"No, because there are less restrictive means to guarantee the defendant's right to an impartial jury.","C":"Yes, because the court has a compelling interest in protecting the defendant's right to an impartial jury.","D":"Yes, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states plenary authority over state criminal prosecutions."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The First Amendment freedom of the press generally prohibits the government from restricting the right to publish lawfully obtained, truthful information about matters of public significance. A government action that abridges this right is presumptively unconstitutional and must survive strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that its action was the least restrictive means (ie, narrowly tailored) to achieve a compelling government interest. A gag order is a judicial order that prohibits the press from reporting about court proceedings, which abridges the freedom of the press. Here, the state court likely has a compelling interest in ensuring that the criminal defendant is afforded the right to an impartial jury. However, the court has less restrictive means to achieve this interest—eg, changing the trial's venue, postponing the trial, or intensely questioning prospective jurors during voir dire (Choice C). Therefore, the court's order cannot survive strict scrutiny and is likely unconstitutional. (Choice A) The Sixth Amendment guarantees the right to an impartial jury in federal criminal prosecutions and in state criminal prosecutions through the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including power over state criminal prosecutions. However, this power is not plenary (ie, absolute) because states must comply with other constitutional provisions.\n\nEducational objective: A government action that abridges the First Amendment freedom of the press (eg, a gag order) must survive strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its action was the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Neb. Press Ass'n v. Stuart, 427 U.S. 539, 563–64 (1976) (explaining that a court has other means short of a gag order to achieve its compelling government interest in ensuring a criminal defendant's right to a fair trial).","educationalObjective":"A government action that abridges the First Amendment freedom of the press (eg, a gag order) must survive strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its action was the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Neb. Press Ass'n v. Stuart, 427 U.S. 539, 563–64 (1976) (explaining that a court has other means short of a gag order to achieve its compelling government interest in ensuring a criminal defendant's right to a fair trial).","rule":"A government action that abridges the First Amendment freedom of the press (eg, a gag order) must survive strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its action was the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court's order likely constitutional?","rule":"A government action that abridges the First Amendment freedom of the press (eg, a gag order) must survive strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its action was the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment freedom of the press generally prohibits the government from restricting the right to publish lawfully obtained, truthful information about matters of public significance. A government action that abridges this right is presumptively unconstitutional and must survive strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that its action was the least restrictive means (ie, narrowly tailored) to achieve a compelling government interest. A gag order is a judicial order that prohibits the press from reporting about court proceedings, which abridges the freedom of the press. Here, the state court likely has a compelling interest in ensuring that the criminal defendant is afforded the right to an impartial jury. However, the court has less restrictive means to achieve this interest—eg, changing the trial's venue, postponing the trial, or intensely questioning prospective jurors during voir dire (Choice C). Therefore, the court's order cannot survive strict scrutiny and is likely unconstitutional. (Choice A) The Sixth Amendment guarantees the right to an impartial jury in federal criminal prosecutions and in state criminal prosecutions through the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice D) The Tenth Amendment reserves to the states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government, including power over state criminal prosecutions. However, this power is not plenary (ie, absolute) because states must comply with other constitutional provisions.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because there are less restrictive means to guarantee the defendant's right to an impartial jury."},"questionZh":"州法院发布了一项禁言令，禁止公布有关悬而未决的谋杀案审判的信息，直到陪审团选出为止。该命令的目的是避免审前宣传损害刑事被告获得公正陪审团审判的权利。几家试图报道审判预审事件的报纸对该命令提出质疑，认为该命令违宪。法院的命令可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为第六修正案要求公正陪审团的权利不适用于州刑事起诉。","B":"不，因为有较少的限制性手段来保证被告获得公正陪审团的权利。","C":"是的，因为法院迫切需要保护被告获得公正陪审团的权利。","D":"是的，因为第十修正案保留了各州对州刑事起诉的全部权力。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案的新闻自由一般禁止政府限制发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的权利。剥夺这一权利的政府行为被推定为违宪，必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这要求政府证明其行动是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段（即，严格定制的手段）。禁言令是一项司法命令，禁止媒体报道法庭诉讼，从而限制了新闻自由。在这里，州法院可能对确保刑事被告获得公正陪审团的权利有着迫切的兴趣。然而，法院实现这一利益的限制性手段较少，例如改变审判地点、推迟审判或在预审期间强烈质疑未来的陪审员（选项C）。因此，法院的命令无法经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且很可能违宪。 （选项A）第六修正案通过第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保障联邦刑事诉讼和州刑事诉讼中拥有公正陪审团的权利。 （选项 D）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括对州刑事起诉的权力。然而，这种权力不是全权（即绝对），因为各州必须遵守其他宪法规定。\n\n教育目标：限制第一修正案新闻自由的政府行为（例如禁言令）必须经受住严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明其行为是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n参考文献\n• Neb. Press Ass'n v. Stuart, 427 U.S. 539, 563–64 (1976)（解释说，除了禁言令之外，法院还有其他手段来实现其令人信服的政府利益，确保刑事被告获得公平审判的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"限制第一修正案新闻自由的政府行为（例如禁言令）必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明其行为是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院的命令可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"限制第一修正案新闻自由的政府行为（例如禁言令）必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明其行为是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。","analysis":"第一修正案的新闻自由一般禁止政府限制发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的权利。剥夺这一权利的政府行为被推定为违宪，必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这要求政府证明其行动是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段（即，严格定制的手段）。禁言令是一项司法命令，禁止媒体报道法庭诉讼，从而限制了新闻自由。在这里，州法院可能对确保刑事被告获得公正陪审团的权利有着迫切的兴趣。然而，法院实现这一利益的限制性手段较少，例如改变审判地点、推迟审判或在预审期间强烈质疑未来的陪审员（选项C）。因此，法院的命令无法经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且很可能违宪。 （选项A）第六修正案通过第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保障联邦刑事诉讼和州刑事诉讼中拥有公正陪审团的权利。 （选项 D）第十修正案向各州保留宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括对州刑事起诉的权力。然而，这种权力不是全权（即绝对），因为各州必须遵守其他宪法规定。\n\n教育目标：限制第一修正案新闻自由的政府行为（例如禁言令）必须经受住严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明其行为是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n参考文献\n• Neb. Press Ass'n v. Stuart, 427 U.S. 539, 563–64 (1976)（解释说，除了禁言令之外，法院还有其他手段来实现其令人信服的政府利益，确保刑事被告获得公平审判的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为有较少的限制性手段来保证被告获得公正陪审团的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"impartial","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈɑɹʃəl/","syllables":"im·par·tial","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 公平的, 不偏不倚的；[法] 公正无私的, 不偏袒的, 公平的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 公平的, 不偏不倚的；[法] 公正无私的, 不偏袒的, 公平的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"The purpose of the order was to avoid pretrial publicity that would prejudice the criminal defendant's right to an impartial jury.","synonyms":["unprejudiced"]},{"term":"gag","ipa":"/ɡˈæɡ/","syllables":"gag","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 箝口物, 箝制言论, 讨论终结；vt. 阻塞, 压制言论自由, 使窒息, 使呕吐, 插科打浑, 欺骗；vi. 窒息, 作呕, 欺骗, 插科打浑","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 箝口物, 箝制言论, 讨论终结；vt. 阻塞, 压制言论自由, 使窒息, 使呕吐, 插科打浑, 欺骗；vi. 窒息, 作呕, 欺骗, 插科打浑","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:gags/p:gagged/i:gagging/d:gagged/3:gags","sourceSentence":"A state court issued a gag order that prohibited the publication of information about a pending murder trial until the jury had been impaneled.","synonyms":["muzzle","joke","laugh","jest"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":138,"sourceQuestionPage":413,"sourceQuestionPages":[413],"sourceExplanationPages":[414],"sourceAnswerPages":[414],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-139","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"An environmental organization's stated mission is to support environmental causes. The organization's membership is generally open to the public, but its bylaws permit its officers to refuse membership to anyone who does not adhere to the organization's mission statement. In a recent state administrative proceeding, the organization opposed plans to begin mining operations in the mountains surrounding a small town. Its opposition prevented the mine from being opened on schedule. In an effort to force the organization to withdraw its opposition, certain residents of the town attended a meeting of the organization and tried to become members, but the officers refused to admit them. The residents sued the organization, claiming that the refusal to admit them was discriminatory and violated a local ordinance that prohibits any organization from discriminating on the basis of an individual's political views. The organization responded that the ordinance is unconstitutional as applied to its membership decisions. Are the residents likely to prevail in their claim?","choices":{"A":"No, because the membership policies of a private organization are not state action.","B":"No, because the organization's right to freedom of association allows it to refuse to admit potential members who do not adhere to its mission statement.","C":"Yes, because the action of the officers in refusing to admit the residents as members violates equal protection of the laws.","D":"Yes, because the ordinance serves the compelling interest of protecting the residents' free speech rights."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Freedom of association\n\nGeneral rule Infringing upon right to associate barred unless:\n\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Subversive Punishing member of subversive organization permitted if organization person:\n\n• is active member\n• knows of organization's illegal objectives and\n• specifically intends to further them Loyalty oath Requiring loyalty oath for public employment permitted unless:\n\n• overbroad – infringes on constitutionally protected behavior or\n• vague – reasonable person would not understand oath Forced inclusion Interfering with organization's discriminatory policy barred unless:\n\n• no significant burden on organization's mission and\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Electoral process Imposing restriction on electoral process:\n\n• ordinary – permitted if rationally related to legitimate interest\n• severe – barred unless necessary to achieve compelling interest The First Amendment freedom of association allows persons to form groups to express certain viewpoints. The government interferes with this right when it forces a group to accept members who would significantly burden the group's ability to express its views or carry out its mission—eg, forced inclusion of a homophobe in a gay-rights organization. Such interference is only justified if it is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest unrelated to the suppression of ideas. Here, residents allege that an environmental organization violated an ordinance prohibiting political-viewpoint discrimination when the organization refused to admit them. But the ordinance is unconstitutional as applied to the organization because it significantly burdens the organization's right to oppose the mining operation. And the ordinance is not necessary to achieve the government's compelling interest in protecting free speech (Choice D). Therefore, the residents' claim against the organization will likely fail.\n\n(Choice A) The Constitution—excluding the Thirteenth Amendment—only applies to state (ie, government) action. Since private organizations are not state actors, their membership policies need not comply with the Constitution. But since the ordinance under which the residents sued is state action, the ordinance must respect the organization's constitutional rights—including its freedom of association. (Choice C) Since the environmental organization is a private (not state) actor, its conduct is not subject to the Fourteenth Amendment's guarantee that individuals will be afforded the equal protection of the laws.\n\nEducational objective: Under the First Amendment freedom of association, the government cannot force a group to accept members who would significantly burden its views unless that interference is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Boy Scouts of Am. v. Dale, 530 U.S. 640, 659 (2000) (striking down a state law requiring the Boy Scouts to retain a gay scoutmaster since his presence seriously impacted the group's ability to advocate viewpoints).","educationalObjective":"Under the First Amendment freedom of association, the government cannot force a group to accept members who would significantly burden its views unless that interference is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Boy Scouts of Am. v. Dale, 530 U.S. 640, 659 (2000) (striking down a state law requiring the Boy Scouts to retain a gay scoutmaster since his presence seriously impacted the group's ability to advocate viewpoints).","rule":"Under the First Amendment freedom of association, the government cannot force a group to accept members who would significantly burden its views unless that interference is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Are the residents likely to prevail in their claim?","rule":"Under the First Amendment freedom of association, the government cannot force a group to accept members who would significantly burden its views unless that interference is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"Freedom of association\n\nGeneral rule Infringing upon right to associate barred unless:\n\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Subversive Punishing member of subversive organization permitted if organization person:\n\n• is active member\n• knows of organization's illegal objectives and\n• specifically intends to further them Loyalty oath Requiring loyalty oath for public employment permitted unless:\n\n• overbroad – infringes on constitutionally protected behavior or\n• vague – reasonable person would not understand oath Forced inclusion Interfering with organization's discriminatory policy barred unless:\n\n• no significant burden on organization's mission and\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Electoral process Imposing restriction on electoral process:\n\n• ordinary – permitted if rationally related to legitimate interest\n• severe – barred unless necessary to achieve compelling interest The First Amendment freedom of association allows persons to form groups to express certain viewpoints. The government interferes with this right when it forces a group to accept members who would significantly burden the group's ability to express its views or carry out its mission—eg, forced inclusion of a homophobe in a gay-rights organization. Such interference is only justified if it is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest unrelated to the suppression of ideas. Here, residents allege that an environmental organization violated an ordinance prohibiting political-viewpoint discrimination when the organization refused to admit them. But the ordinance is unconstitutional as applied to the organization because it significantly burdens the organization's right to oppose the mining operation. And the ordinance is not necessary to achieve the government's compelling interest in protecting free speech (Choice D). Therefore, the residents' claim against the organization will likely fail.\n\n(Choice A) The Constitution—excluding the Thirteenth Amendment—only applies to state (ie, government) action. Since private organizations are not state actors, their membership policies need not comply with the Constitution. But since the ordinance under which the residents sued is state action, the ordinance must respect the organization's constitutional rights—including its freedom of association. (Choice C) Since the environmental organization is a private (not state) actor, its conduct is not subject to the Fourteenth Amendment's guarantee that individuals will be afforded the equal protection of the laws.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the organization's right to freedom of association allows it to refuse to admit potential members who do not adhere to its mission statement."},"questionZh":"环境组织的既定使命是支持环境事业。该组织的会员资格一般向公众开放，但其章程允许其官员拒绝任何不遵守该组织使命宣言的人的会员资格。在最近的一次州行政诉讼中，该组织反对在一个小镇周围的山区开始采矿作业的计划。它的反对阻止了该矿如期开工。为了迫使该组织撤回反对意见，该镇的一些居民参加了该组织的会议并试图成为会员，但官员拒绝接纳他们。居民起诉该组织，声称拒绝接纳他们是歧视性的，并违反了当地禁止任何组织基于个人政治观点进行歧视的法令。该组织回应称，该条例应用于其会员决定是违宪的。居民的主张有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为私人组织的会员政策不是国家行为。","B":"不可以，因为该组织的结社自由权允许其拒绝接纳不遵守其使命宣言的潜在成员。","C":"是的，因为人员拒绝接纳居民为会员的行为，违反了平等保障的法律。","D":"是的，因为该条例符合保护居民言论自由权的迫切利益。"},"explanationZh":"结社自由\n\n一般规则 禁止侵犯结社权，除非：\n\n• 为实现引人注目的利益所必需的 颠覆性惩罚 允许颠覆性组织的成员，如果组织人员：\n\n• 是活跃会员\n• 了解组织的非法目标并且\n• 特别旨在促进这些目标 忠诚宣誓 允许要求公职人员宣誓忠诚，除非：\n\n• 过于宽泛——侵犯受宪法保护的行为或\n• 模糊——有理智的人不会理解誓言 强制纳入 禁止干涉组织的歧视性政策，除非：\n\n• 对组织的使命没有重大负担，并且\n• 实现引人注目的利益所必需的 选举过程 对选举过程施加限制：\n\n• 普通——如果与合法利益合理相关则允许\n• 严重——除非为了实现令人信服的利益而有必要，否则禁止。 第一修正案的结社自由允许人们组成团体来表达某些观点。当政府强迫一个团体接受那些会严重影响该团体表达其观点或执行其使命的能力的成员时，政府就干涉了这项权利——例如，强迫同性恋权利组织纳入恐同者。只有当有必要实现与压制思想无关的令人信服的政府利益时，这种干预才是合理的。在这里，居民指控环保组织拒绝接纳他们，从而违反了禁止政治观点歧视的条例。但该法令适用于该组织是违宪的，因为它严重损害了该组织反对采矿作业的权利。而且该法令对于实现政府保护言论自由的迫切利益而言并不是必要的（选项 D）。因此，居民对该组织的索赔很可能会失败。\n\n（选项 A） 宪法（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于国家（即政府）行为。由于私人组织不是国家行为者，其成员政策无需遵守宪法。但由于居民起诉所依据的法令是国家行为，因此该法令必须尊重该组织的宪法权利，包括其结社自由。 （选项C）由于环保组织是私人（而非国家）行为体，其行为不受第十四修正案关于个人受到法律平等保护的保证的约束。\n\n教育目标：根据第一修正案的结社自由，政府不能强迫一个团体接受会严重影响其观点的成员，除非这种干预是实现令人信服的政府利益所必需的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国童子军。 v. Dale, 530 U.S. 640, 659 (2000)（废除要求童子军保留一名同性恋童子军队长的州法律，因为他的存在严重影响了该组织倡导观点的能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据第一修正案的结社自由，政府不能强迫某个团体接受会严重影响其观点的成员，除非这种干预是实现令人信服的政府利益所必需的。","iracZh":{"issue":"居民的主张有可能获胜吗？","rule":"根据第一修正案的结社自由，政府不能强迫某个团体接受会严重影响其观点的成员，除非这种干预是实现令人信服的政府利益所必需的。","analysis":"结社自由\n\n一般规则 禁止侵犯结社权，除非：\n\n• 为实现引人注目的利益所必需的 颠覆性惩罚 允许颠覆性组织的成员，如果组织人员：\n\n• 是活跃会员\n• 了解组织的非法目标并且\n• 特别旨在促进这些目标 忠诚宣誓 允许要求公职人员宣誓忠诚，除非：\n\n• 过于宽泛——侵犯受宪法保护的行为或\n• 模糊——有理智的人不会理解誓言 强制纳入 禁止干涉组织的歧视性政策，除非：\n\n• 对组织的使命没有重大负担，并且\n• 实现引人注目的利益所必需的 选举过程 对选举过程施加限制：\n\n• 普通——如果与合法利益合理相关则允许\n• 严重——除非为了实现令人信服的利益而有必要，否则禁止。 第一修正案的结社自由允许人们组成团体来表达某些观点。当政府强迫一个团体接受那些会严重影响该团体表达其观点或执行其使命的能力的成员时，政府就干涉了这项权利——例如，强迫同性恋权利组织纳入恐同者。只有当有必要实现与压制思想无关的令人信服的政府利益时，这种干预才是合理的。在这里，居民指控环保组织拒绝接纳他们，从而违反了禁止政治观点歧视的条例。但该法令适用于该组织是违宪的，因为它严重损害了该组织反对采矿作业的权利。而且该法令对于实现政府保护言论自由的迫切利益而言并不是必要的（选项 D）。因此，居民对该组织的索赔很可能会失败。\n\n（选项 A） 宪法（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于国家（即政府）行为。由于私人组织不是国家行为者，其成员政策无需遵守宪法。但由于居民起诉所依据的法令是国家行为，因此该法令必须尊重该组织的宪法权利，包括其结社自由。 （选项C）由于环保组织是私人（而非国家）行为体，其行为不受第十四修正案关于个人受到法律平等保护的保证的约束。\n\n教育目标：根据第一修正案的结社自由，政府不能强迫一个团体接受会严重影响其观点的成员，除非这种干预是实现令人信服的政府利益所必需的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国童子军。 v. Dale, 530 U.S. 640, 659 (2000)（废除要求童子军保留一名同性恋童子军队长的州法律，因为他的存在严重影响了该组织倡导观点的能力）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为该组织的结社自由权允许其拒绝接纳不遵守其使命宣言的潜在成员。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"viewpoint discrimination","ipa":"/vjˈupˌɔɪnt dɪskɹˌɪmʌnˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"view·point dis·crim·i·na·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"观点歧视：因说话者对主题的立场而区别对待","contextualMeaning":"观点歧视：因说话者对主题的立场而区别对待","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Here, residents allege that an environmental organization violated an ordinance prohibiting political-viewpoint discrimination when the organization refused to admit them.","synonyms":["viewpoint favoritism","viewpoint favouritism","viewpoint secernment"]},{"term":"oath","ipa":"/ˈoʊθ/","syllables":"oath","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 誓约, 誓言, 咒骂；[法] 誓言, 誓约, 宣誓","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 誓约, 誓言, 咒骂；[法] 誓言, 誓约, 宣誓","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:oaths","sourceSentence":"Freedom of association General rule Infringing upon right to associate barred unless: • necessary to achieve compelling interest Subversive Punishing member of subversive organization permitted if organization person: • is active member • knows of organization's illegal objectives and • specifically intends to further them Loyalty oath Requiring loyalty oath for public employment permitted unless: • overbroad – infri","synonyms":["swearing","curse","curse word","expletive"]},{"term":"bylaws","ipa":"/bˈaɪlˌɔz/","syllables":"by·laws","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 章程；规章制度；细则（bylaw的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 章程；规章制度；细则（bylaw的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 bylaw；词形 0:bylaw/1:s","sourceSentence":"The organization's membership is generally open to the public, but its bylaws permit its officers to refuse membership to anyone who does not adhere to the organization's mission statement.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":139,"sourceQuestionPage":415,"sourceQuestionPages":[415],"sourceExplanationPages":[416,417],"sourceAnswerPages":[416,417],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-140","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A city ordinance makes the city building inspector responsible for ensuring that all buildings in that city are kept up to building code standards and requires the inspector to refer for prosecution all known building code violations. Another ordinance provides that the city building inspector may be discharged for \"good cause.\" The building inspector took a newspaper reporter through a number of run-down buildings in a slum neighborhood. After using various epithets and slurs to describe the occupants of these buildings, the building inspector stated to the reporter: \"I do not even try to get these buildings up to code or to have their owners prosecuted for code violations because if these buildings are repaired, the people who live in them will just wreck them again.\" The reporter published these statements in a story in the local newspaper. The building inspector admitted he made the statements. On the basis of these statements, the city council discharged the building inspector. Is the action of the city council constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the statements were lawful comments on a matter of public concern.","B":"No, because the statements were published in a newspaper that is protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","C":"Yes, because the building inspector is a government employee and a person holding such a position may not make public comments inconsistent with current governmental policy.","D":"Yes, because the statements demonstrate that the building inspector has an attitude toward a certain class of persons that interferes with the proper performance of the obligations of his job."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Acceptance of public employment does not eliminate an individual's right to free speech—but this right is not absolute. When a public employee is speaking as a private citizen, the government can limit that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech. And when the employee is speaking pursuant to official duties (ie, within the scope of employment), the government has even greater latitude to regulate that speech and First Amendment protections rarely apply. Here, the city building inspector was likely speaking as a private citizen since talking to a reporter presumably fell outside his official duties. But since the inspector's statements showed his lack of effort to get some buildings up to code, the city council's interest in ensuring that he properly perform his job outweighed his right to free speech. And if talking to reporters was an official duty, the city council's right to regulate that speech was even greater. Therefore, the city's decision to discharge him was constitutional. (Choice A) Comments on a matter of public concern (as seen here) are afforded greater First Amendment protection than those involving a private concern. But the city council still had \"good cause\" to fire the inspector since his statements demonstrated that he could not properly perform his job. (Choice B) The First Amendment (applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment) gives the media the right to publish information on matters of public concern. But the fact that the inspector's statements were published in a newspaper is irrelevant to determining whether the city council can fire him for those statements. (Choice C) A government employee can make public comments that are inconsistent with current government policy if those comments do not interfere with his/her ability to adequately perform official duties.\n\nEducational objective: When a public employee is speaking as a private citizen, the government can only restrict that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech. But when the employee is speaking pursuant to official duties, the government has greater latitude to regulate that speech.\n\nReferences\n• Pickering v. Bd. of Educ., 391 U.S. 563, 568 (1968) (explaining that a public employee's right to free speech as a private citizen must be balanced against the government's interest in efficient government function).","educationalObjective":"When a public employee is speaking as a private citizen, the government can only restrict that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech. But when the employee is speaking pursuant to official duties, the government has greater latitude to regulate that speech.","references":"• Pickering v. Bd. of Educ., 391 U.S. 563, 568 (1968) (explaining that a public employee's right to free speech as a private citizen must be balanced against the government's interest in efficient government function).","rule":"When a public employee is speaking as a private citizen, the government can only restrict that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech. But when the employee is speaking pursuant to official duties, the government has greater latitude to regulate that speech.","irac":{"issue":"Is the action of the city council constitutional?","rule":"When a public employee is speaking as a private citizen, the government can only restrict that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech. But when the employee is speaking pursuant to official duties, the government has greater latitude to regulate that speech.","analysis":"Acceptance of public employment does not eliminate an individual's right to free speech—but this right is not absolute. When a public employee is speaking as a private citizen, the government can limit that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech. And when the employee is speaking pursuant to official duties (ie, within the scope of employment), the government has even greater latitude to regulate that speech and First Amendment protections rarely apply. Here, the city building inspector was likely speaking as a private citizen since talking to a reporter presumably fell outside his official duties. But since the inspector's statements showed his lack of effort to get some buildings up to code, the city council's interest in ensuring that he properly perform his job outweighed his right to free speech. And if talking to reporters was an official duty, the city council's right to regulate that speech was even greater. Therefore, the city's decision to discharge him was constitutional. (Choice A) Comments on a matter of public concern (as seen here) are afforded greater First Amendment protection than those involving a private concern. But the city council still had \"good cause\" to fire the inspector since his statements demonstrated that he could not properly perform his job. (Choice B) The First Amendment (applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment) gives the media the right to publish information on matters of public concern. But the fact that the inspector's statements were published in a newspaper is irrelevant to determining whether the city council can fire him for those statements. (Choice C) A government employee can make public comments that are inconsistent with current government policy if those comments do not interfere with his/her ability to adequately perform official duties.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the statements demonstrate that the building inspector has an attitude toward a certain class of persons that interferes with the proper performance of the obligations of his job."},"questionZh":"一项城市法令规定城市建筑检查员负责确保该城市的所有建筑物均符合建筑规范标准，并要求检查员对所有已知的建筑规范违规行为进行起诉。另一项法令规定，城市建筑检查员可以因“正当理由”而被解雇。建筑检查员带着一名报纸记者参观了贫民窟附近的一些破旧建筑。在使用各种绰号和诽谤来描述这些建筑物的居住者后，建筑检查员对记者表示：“我什至不想让这些建筑物符合规范，也不想让它们的业主因违反规范而受到起诉，因为如果这些建筑物被修复，居住在其中的人们只会再次破坏它们。”记者在当地报纸的一篇报道中发表了这些声明。建筑检查员承认他曾说过这些话。根据这些陈述，市议会解除了建筑检查员的职务。市议会的行动是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这些言论是对公众关心的问题的合法评论。","B":"不，因为这些声明是在受第一修正案和第十四修正案保护的报纸上发表的。","C":"是的，因为验楼师是政府雇员，担任此职务的人不得发表与现行政府政策不符的公开评论。","D":"是的，因为这些陈述表明建筑检查员对某一类人的态度妨碍了其正确履行工作义务。"},"explanationZh":"接受公共就业并不会消除个人的言论自由权，但这项权利并不是绝对的。当一名公职人员以普通公民身份发言时，如果政府对有效政府职能的利益大于该雇员的言论自由权，政府可以限制其言论。当雇员根据公务（即在雇佣范围内）发表言论时，政府有更大的自由度来规范言论和第一修正案的保护很少适用。在这里，城市建筑检查员很可能以普通公民的身份发言，因为与记者交谈可能不属于他的公务范围。但由于检查员的声明表明他没有努力让一些建筑物符合规范，因此市议会对确保他正确履行工作的兴趣超过了他的言论自由权。如果与记者交谈是一项官方职责，那么市议会规范这种言论的权利就更大了。因此，市政府释放他的决定是符合宪法的。 （选项 A）对公众关心的问题（如此处所示）的评论比涉及私人问题的评论受到更大的第一修正案保护。但市议会仍然有“充分的理由”解雇这位检查员，因为他的言论表明他无法正常履行职责。 （选项 B） 第一修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）赋予媒体发布有关公众关注事项的信息的权利。但该检查员的言论发表在报纸上这一事实与决定市议会是否可以因这些言论而解雇他无关。 （选择 C）政府雇员可以发表与当前政府政策不一致的公开评论，只要这些评论不影响其充分履行公务的能力。\n\n教育目标：当一名公职人员以普通公民的身份发言时，只有在政府高效运作的利益大于该雇员的言论自由权的情况下，政府才能限制其言论。但当员工根据公务发表言论时，政府有更大的自由度来规范其言论。\n\n参考文献\n• 皮克林诉Bd。 of Educ., 391 U.S. 563, 568 (1968)（解释说公共雇员作为普通公民的言论自由权必须与政府有效履行政府职能的利益相平衡）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一名公职人员以普通公民的身份发表言论时，只有在政府高效运作的利益大于该雇员的言论自由权的情况下，政府才能限制其言论。但当员工根据公务发表言论时，政府有更大的自由度来规范其言论。","iracZh":{"issue":"市议会的行动是否符合宪法？","rule":"当一名公职人员以普通公民的身份发表言论时，只有在政府高效运作的利益大于该雇员的言论自由权的情况下，政府才能限制其言论。但当员工根据公务发表言论时，政府有更大的自由度来规范其言论。","analysis":"接受公共就业并不会消除个人的言论自由权，但这项权利并不是绝对的。当一名公职人员以普通公民身份发言时，如果政府对有效政府职能的利益大于该雇员的言论自由权，政府可以限制其言论。当雇员根据公务（即在雇佣范围内）发表言论时，政府有更大的自由度来规范言论和第一修正案的保护很少适用。在这里，城市建筑检查员很可能以普通公民的身份发言，因为与记者交谈可能不属于他的公务范围。但由于检查员的声明表明他没有努力让一些建筑物符合规范，因此市议会对确保他正确履行工作的兴趣超过了他的言论自由权。如果与记者交谈是一项官方职责，那么市议会规范这种言论的权利就更大了。因此，市政府释放他的决定是符合宪法的。 （选项 A）对公众关心的问题（如此处所示）的评论比涉及私人问题的评论受到更大的第一修正案保护。但市议会仍然有“充分的理由”解雇这位检查员，因为他的言论表明他无法正常履行职责。 （选项 B） 第一修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）赋予媒体发布有关公众关注事项的信息的权利。但该检查员的言论发表在报纸上这一事实与决定市议会是否可以因这些言论而解雇他无关。 （选择 C）政府雇员可以发表与当前政府政策不一致的公开评论，只要这些评论不影响其充分履行公务的能力。\n\n教育目标：当一名公职人员以普通公民的身份发言时，只有在政府高效运作的利益大于该雇员的言论自由权的情况下，政府才能限制其言论。但当员工根据公务发表言论时，政府有更大的自由度来规范其言论。\n\n参考文献\n• 皮克林诉Bd。 of Educ., 391 U.S. 563, 568 (1968)（解释说公共雇员作为普通公民的言论自由权必须与政府有效履行政府职能的利益相平衡）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为这些陈述表明建筑检查员对某一类人的态度妨碍了其正确履行工作义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-7fb9c70a7d583111"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":140,"sourceQuestionPage":418,"sourceQuestionPages":[418],"sourceExplanationPages":[419,420],"sourceAnswerPages":[419,420],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-141","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A city ordinance prohibited individuals from picketing in residential neighborhoods unless the picketing related to the neighborhood zoning requirements. This exception to the ordinance was adopted in response to local citizens' strong views about proposed rezoning of residential neighborhoods. A group that wished to picket in front of a business owner's home because of the business owner's employment practices challenged the ordinance as unconstitutional under the First Amendment. Will the group's challenge likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the ordinance is a content-neutral regulation of speech.","B":"No, because the ordinance regulates conduct rather than speech.","C":"Yes, because the ordinance irrationally discriminates between different types of protesters.","D":"Yes, because the ordinance is a content-based regulation of speech."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment's preeminent task is to protect the free flow of ideas, so content- based restrictions on speech are presumptively unconstitutional and subject to strict scrutiny. As a result, the government can only regulate speech based on what is said (ie, its message or ideas) by showing that the regulation is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest—a nearly impossible task. The ordinance here is a content-based regulation because it allows speech on one topic (neighborhood zoning requirements) but no other. And since it is highly unlikely that the ordinance can survive strict scrutiny, the group's challenge will likely prevail. (Choice A) Unlike content-based restrictions (as seen here), content-neutral regulations are constitutional if they pass intermediate scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the regulation (1) is narrowly tailored to achieve an important or substantial government interest and (2) leaves open alternative channels of communication.* *Content-neutral restrictions that likely satisfy intermediate scrutiny include those penalizing trespasses on private property and prohibiting protests on streets during rush- hour traffic. (Choice B) A person's conduct is protected by the First Amendment if it is expressive—ie, (1) the person intends to communicate a message and (2) the audience is likely to understand that message. And since the ordinance unjustifiably restricts the expressive aspect of picketing, it is likely unconstitutional. (Choice C) Had the ordinance regulated a nonpublic forum, rational basis scrutiny would require the group to show that the ordinance irrationally discriminates between different types of protesters. However, residential neighborhoods are public forums because they contain public streets and sidewalks.\n\nEducational objective: Since content-based restrictions on speech are heavily disfavored, they are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Playboy Entm't Group, 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000) (explaining that content-based restrictions on speech require the government to satisfy strict scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"Since content-based restrictions on speech are heavily disfavored, they are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• United States v. Playboy Entm't Group, 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000) (explaining that content-based restrictions on speech require the government to satisfy strict scrutiny).","rule":"Since content-based restrictions on speech are heavily disfavored, they are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Will the group's challenge likely prevail?","rule":"Since content-based restrictions on speech are heavily disfavored, they are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment's preeminent task is to protect the free flow of ideas, so content- based restrictions on speech are presumptively unconstitutional and subject to strict scrutiny. As a result, the government can only regulate speech based on what is said (ie, its message or ideas) by showing that the regulation is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest—a nearly impossible task. The ordinance here is a content-based regulation because it allows speech on one topic (neighborhood zoning requirements) but no other. And since it is highly unlikely that the ordinance can survive strict scrutiny, the group's challenge will likely prevail. (Choice A) Unlike content-based restrictions (as seen here), content-neutral regulations are constitutional if they pass intermediate scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the regulation (1) is narrowly tailored to achieve an important or substantial government interest and (2) leaves open alternative channels of communication.* *Content-neutral restrictions that likely satisfy intermediate scrutiny include those penalizing trespasses on private property and prohibiting protests on streets during rush- hour traffic. (Choice B) A person's conduct is protected by the First Amendment if it is expressive—ie, (1) the person intends to communicate a message and (2) the audience is likely to understand that message. And since the ordinance unjustifiably restricts the expressive aspect of picketing, it is likely unconstitutional. (Choice C) Had the ordinance regulated a nonpublic forum, rational basis scrutiny would require the group to show that the ordinance irrationally discriminates between different types of protesters. However, residential neighborhoods are public forums because they contain public streets and sidewalks.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the ordinance is a content-based regulation of speech."},"questionZh":"一项城市法令禁止个人在住宅区设置纠察线，除非纠察线与街区分区要求相关。该法令的这一例外是为了回应当地居民对拟议的住宅区重新分区的强烈意见。一群因企业主的雇佣行为而希望在企业主家门前举行纠察的团体对该法令提出质疑，认为该法令违反第一修正案。该组织的挑战可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该条例是内容中立的言论法规。","B":"不，因为该条例规范的是行为而不是言论。","C":"是的，因为该法令对不同类型的抗议者进行了不合理的歧视。","D":"是的，因为该条例是基于内容的言论管制。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案的首要任务是保护思想的自由流动，因此基于内容的言论限制被推定为违宪，并受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。因此，政府只能根据所说的内容（即其信息或想法）来规范言论，方法是表明该监管是必要的，并且是为实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的——这几乎是不可能的任务。这里的法令是一项基于内容的法规，因为它允许就一个主题（社区分区要求）发表言论，但不允许就其他主题发表言论。由于该法令不太可能通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此该组织的挑战很可能会获胜。 （选项 A）与基于内容的限制（如此处所见）不同，内容中立的法规如果通过了中间审查，即符合宪法，即政府证明该法规 (1) 是为实现重要或实质性的政府利益而专门制定的，并且 (2) 留有开放的替代沟通渠道。* *可能满足中间审查的内容中​​立限制包括惩罚侵入私人财产和禁止高峰时段交通期间在街道上抗议。 （选项 B）如果一个人的行为具有表达性，即（1）该人打算传达某种信息，并且（2）受众可能理解该信息，则该行为受到第一修正案的保护。由于该法令不合理地限制了纠察队的表达方式，因此它很可能是违宪的。 （选项 C）如果该法令规范了非公开论坛，则合理基础审查将要求该团体证明该法令对不同类型的抗议者进行非理性歧视。然而，住宅区是公共场所，因为它们包含公共街道和人行道。\n\n教育目标：由于基于内容的言论限制受到强烈反对，因此除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），否则它们是违宪的——即政府证明这种限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Playboy Entm't Group, 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000)（解释基于内容的言论限制要求政府满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"由于基于内容的言论限制受到严重反对，因此除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），否则它们是违宪的——即政府证明这种限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","iracZh":{"issue":"该组织的挑战可能会获胜吗？","rule":"由于基于内容的言论限制受到严重反对，因此除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），否则它们是违宪的——即政府证明这种限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","analysis":"第一修正案的首要任务是保护思想的自由流动，因此基于内容的言论限制被推定为违宪，并受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。因此，政府只能根据所说的内容（即其信息或想法）来规范言论，方法是表明该监管是必要的，并且是为实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的——这几乎是不可能的任务。这里的法令是一项基于内容的法规，因为它允许就一个主题（社区分区要求）发表言论，但不允许就其他主题发表言论。由于该法令不太可能通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此该组织的挑战很可能会获胜。 （选项 A）与基于内容的限制（如此处所见）不同，内容中立的法规如果通过了中间审查，即符合宪法，即政府证明该法规 (1) 是为实现重要或实质性的政府利益而专门制定的，并且 (2) 留有开放的替代沟通渠道。* *可能满足中间审查的内容中​​立限制包括惩罚侵入私人财产和禁止高峰时段交通期间在街道上抗议。 （选项 B）如果一个人的行为具有表达性，即（1）该人打算传达某种信息，并且（2）受众可能理解该信息，则该行为受到第一修正案的保护。由于该法令不合理地限制了纠察队的表达方式，因此它很可能是违宪的。 （选项 C）如果该法令规范了非公开论坛，则合理基础审查将要求该团体证明该法令对不同类型的抗议者进行非理性歧视。然而，住宅区是公共场所，因为它们包含公共街道和人行道。\n\n教育目标：由于基于内容的言论限制受到强烈反对，因此除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），否则它们是违宪的——即政府证明这种限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Playboy Entm't Group, 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000)（解释基于内容的言论限制要求政府满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该条例是基于内容的言论管制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"public forum","ipa":"/pˈəblɪk fˈɔɹəm/","syllables":"pub·lic fo·rum","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公共论坛：传统或指定用于公众表达的政府场所","contextualMeaning":"公共论坛：传统或指定用于公众表达的政府场所","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Had the ordinance regulated a nonpublic forum, rational basis scrutiny would require the group to show that the ordinance irrationally discriminates between different types of protesters.","synonyms":["public assembly","public meeting place"]},{"term":"nonpublic forum","ipa":"/nɑnpˈəblɪk fˈɔɹəm/","syllables":"non·pub·lic fo·rum","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"非公共论坛：并非按传统或指定方式向公众表达开放的政府财产","contextualMeaning":"非公共论坛：并非按传统或指定方式向公众表达开放的政府财产","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Had the ordinance regulated a nonpublic forum, rational basis scrutiny would require the group to show that the ordinance irrationally discriminates between different types of protesters.","synonyms":["nonpublic assembly","nonpublic meeting place"]},{"term":"protesters","ipa":"/pɹˈoʊtˌɛstɝz/","syllables":"pro·test·ers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 抗议者( protester的复数形式 )； 反对者； 拒绝者","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 抗议者( protester的复数形式 )； 反对者； 拒绝者","wordForm":"名词；原形 protester；词形 0:protester/1:s","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the ordinance irrationally discriminates between different types of protesters.","synonyms":["demonstrator","dissenter","dissident","objector"]},{"term":"penalizing","ipa":"/pˈinʌlˌaɪzɪŋ/","syllables":"pe·nal·iz·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 对…予以惩罚( penalize的现在分词 )； 使处于不利地位","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 对…予以惩罚( penalize的现在分词 )； 使处于不利地位","wordForm":"词；原形 penalize；词形 0:penalize/1:i","sourceSentence":"* *Content-neutral restrictions that likely satisfy intermediate scrutiny include those penalizing trespasses on private property and prohibiting protests on streets during rush- hour traffic.","synonyms":["punish","penalise"]},{"term":"expressive","ipa":"/ɪkspɹˈɛsɪv/","syllables":"ex·pres·sive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 表达的, 富于表情的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 表达的, 富于表情的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) A person's conduct is protected by the First Amendment if it is expressive—ie, (1) the person intends to communicate a message and (2) the audience is likely to understand that message.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"picketing","ipa":"/pˈɪkətɪŋ/","syllables":"pick·et·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[经] 罢工工人劝阻工人上班, 工人纠察线","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[经] 罢工工人劝阻工人上班, 工人纠察线","wordForm":"词；原形 pickete；词形 0:pickete/1:i/i:picketing","sourceSentence":"A city ordinance prohibited individuals from picketing in residential neighborhoods unless the picketing related to the neighborhood zoning requirements.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"picket","ipa":"/pˈɪkɪt/","syllables":"picket","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 桩, 尖桩, 警戒哨, 巡逻艇, 纠察队；vt. 围住, 警戒, 派...担任纠察；vi. 担任纠察","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 桩, 尖桩, 警戒哨, 巡逻艇, 纠察队；vt. 围住, 警戒, 派...担任纠察；vi. 担任纠察","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:pickets/d:picketted/i:picketing/p:picketed/3:pickets","sourceSentence":"A city ordinance prohibited individuals from picketing in residential neighborhoods unless the picketing related to the neighborhood zoning requirements.","synonyms":["piquet","pale","lookout","lookout man"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f734aa717a0f6f76"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":141,"sourceQuestionPage":421,"sourceQuestionPages":[421],"sourceExplanationPages":[422,423],"sourceAnswerPages":[422,423],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-142","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"In an effort to prevent quid pro quo corruption between donors and candidates for state elective office, a state enacted a law that limits the total amount an individual may contribute to individual candidates during an election cycle. A donor who was fined for violating this statute has argued that it is unconstitutional. Is the state law constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because political speech cannot be restricted.","B":"No, because the law is not the least restrictive means to serve a compelling state interest.","C":"Yes, because campaign contributions are not speech protected by the First Amendment.","D":"Yes, because the law is closely drawn to serve an important state interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"[Current-law update — answer unchanged] National Republican Senatorial Committee v. FEC (2026) requires rigorous closely drawn review and invalidated political-party coordinated-expenditure limits. It also reaffirmed unlimited independent expenditures and preserved the distinct treatment of direct contribution limits.\n\nCampaign-finance restrictions\n\nExamples Test\n\nContribution\n• Limit on amount of contribution to: Intermediate scrutiny restrictions – single candidate\n• closely – political candidates, parties drawn/substantially & action committees related to important combined government interest\n• Increase of limit on amount of contribution to candidate when self-financed opponent exceeds certain spending amount Expenditure\n• Limit on spending by: Strict scrutiny restrictions – corporations or unions in\n• necessary/least support of or opposition to restrictive means to candidate achieve compelling – candidate on his/her own government interest behalf The First Amendment protects political speech, which includes campaign contributions and expenditures (Choice C). Political speech is fundamental to a free and democratic society but may be restricted if the government satisfies the appropriate level of scrutiny (Choice A). A government restriction on campaign contributions must survive intermediate scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its restriction is closely drawn to serve (ie, substantially related to) an important government interest. Here, the state law limits the amount that an individual may contribute to a single candidate for state elective office during an election cycle. The state has an important interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption between donors and candidates.* And in Buckley v. Valeo, the U.S. Supreme Court held that limiting the amount of money an individual may donate to a single candidate is closely drawn to serve that interest. Therefore, the state law is constitutional. *Preventing quid pro quo corruption or the appearance of it is the only government interest that the U.S. Supreme Court has recognized is important enough to justify government restrictions on campaign contributions. (Choice B) A government restriction on campaign expenditures must satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, the government must show that its restriction is the least restrictive means to\n\nserve a compelling government interest. However, this law restricts campaign contributions, so the state need only satisfy intermediate scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment protects political speech, which includes campaign contributions and expenditures. A law that restricts campaign contributions is unconstitutional unless the government satisfies intermediate scrutiny by showing that the law is closely drawn to serve (ie, substantially related to) an important government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 29 (1976) (holding that a limit on the amount an individual may donate to a candidate is a constitutional means to serve the government's important interest in combating quid pro quo corruption).","educationalObjective":"Candidate-contribution limits receive rigorous “closely drawn” scrutiny and are valid only when proportionate, necessary, and narrowly tailored to preventing quid pro quo corruption or its appearance.","references":"• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 29 (1976) (holding that a limit on the amount an individual may donate to a candidate is a constitutional means to serve the government's important interest in combating quid pro quo corruption).","rule":"Candidate-contribution limits receive rigorous “closely drawn” scrutiny and are valid only when proportionate, necessary, and narrowly tailored to preventing quid pro quo corruption or its appearance.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state law constitutional?","rule":"Candidate-contribution limits receive rigorous “closely drawn” scrutiny and are valid only when proportionate, necessary, and narrowly tailored to preventing quid pro quo corruption or its appearance.","analysis":"Campaign-finance restrictions\n\nExamples Test\n\nContribution\n• Limit on amount of contribution to: Intermediate scrutiny restrictions – single candidate\n• closely – political candidates, parties drawn/substantially & action committees related to important combined government interest\n• Increase of limit on amount of contribution to candidate when self-financed opponent exceeds certain spending amount Expenditure\n• Limit on spending by: Strict scrutiny restrictions – corporations or unions in\n• necessary/least support of or opposition to restrictive means to candidate achieve compelling – candidate on his/her own government interest behalf The First Amendment protects political speech, which includes campaign contributions and expenditures (Choice C). Political speech is fundamental to a free and democratic society but may be restricted if the government satisfies the appropriate level of scrutiny (Choice A). A government restriction on campaign contributions must survive intermediate scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its restriction is closely drawn to serve (ie, substantially related to) an important government interest. Here, the state law limits the amount that an individual may contribute to a single candidate for state elective office during an election cycle. The state has an important interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption between donors and candidates.* And in Buckley v. Valeo, the U.S. Supreme Court held that limiting the amount of money an individual may donate to a single candidate is closely drawn to serve that interest. Therefore, the state law is constitutional. *Preventing quid pro quo corruption or the appearance of it is the only government interest that the U.S. Supreme Court has recognized is important enough to justify government restrictions on campaign contributions. (Choice B) A government restriction on campaign expenditures must satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, the government must show that its restriction is the least restrictive means to\n\nserve a compelling government interest. However, this law restricts campaign contributions, so the state need only satisfy intermediate scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the law is closely drawn to serve an important state interest."},"questionZh":"为了防止捐助者和州选举办公室候选人之间的交换腐败，州颁布了一项法律，限制个人在选举周期内向个别候选人捐款的总额。一名因违反该法规而被罚款的捐赠者辩称，该法规违宪。国家法律是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为政治言论不能受到限制。","B":"不，因为法律并不是服务于令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。","C":"是的，因为竞选捐款不属于受第一修正案保护的言论。","D":"是的，因为法律是紧密服务于重要的国家利益的。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法更新——答案不变】National Republican Senatorial Committee v. FEC（2026）要求严格适用“紧密契合”审查，并废除了政党协调支出限额；同时重申独立支出不受此类上限，并保留对直接捐款限额的不同处理。\n\n竞选资金限制\n\n示例测试\n\n贡献\n• 捐款金额限制： 中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）限制 – 单一候选人\n• 密切 - 与重要的综合政府利益相关的政治候选人、政党和行动委员会\n• 当自费对手超过一定支出金额时，提高候选人的捐款限额 支出\n• 支出限制： 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）限制——公司或工会\n• 必要/最少支持或反对限制性手段，使候选人达到令人信服的程度——候选人代表自己的政府利益 第一修正案保护政治言论，其中包括竞选捐款和支出（选项C）。政治言论是自由民主社会的基础，但如果政府满足适当程度的审查，政治言论可能会受到限制（选择 A）。政府对竞选捐款的限制必须通过中间审查，这要求政府证明其限制是为了服务于（即实质上相关）重要的政府利益。在这里，州法律限制了个人在选举周期内可以向州选举办公室的单个候选人捐款的金额。防止捐助者和候选人之间的交换腐败符合国家的重要利益。*在巴克利诉法雷奥案中，美国最高法院认为，限制个人向单个候选人捐赠的资金数额密切关注这一利益。因此，州法是宪法性的。 *防止交换式腐败或腐败的出现是美国最高法院承认的唯一政府利益，其重要性足以证明政府对竞选捐款的限制是合理的。 （选择B）政府对竞选支出的限制必须满足严格的审查，即政府必须表明其限制是对竞选支出限制最少的手段。\n\n服务于引人注目的政府利益。然而，这项法律限制了竞选捐款，因此国家只需满足中间审查即可。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护政治言论，包括竞选捐款和支出。限制竞选捐款的法律是违宪的，除非政府通过表明该法律紧密服务于（即实质上相关）重要的政府利益来满足中间审查的要求。\n\n参考文献\n• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 29 (1976)（认为限制个人向候选人捐赠的金额是符合政府打击交换条件腐败的重要利益的宪法手段）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"候选人捐款限额适用严格的“紧密契合”审查；只有在与防止权钱交易式腐败或其外观相称、必要并狭义量身定制时才有效。","iracZh":{"issue":"国家法律是否符合宪法？","rule":"候选人捐款限额适用严格的“紧密契合”审查；只有在与防止权钱交易式腐败或其外观相称、必要并狭义量身定制时才有效。","analysis":"竞选资金限制\n\n示例测试\n\n贡献\n• 捐款金额限制： 中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）限制 – 单一候选人\n• 密切 - 与重要的综合政府利益相关的政治候选人、政党和行动委员会\n• 当自费对手超过一定支出金额时，提高候选人的捐款限额 支出\n• 支出限制： 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）限制——公司或工会\n• 必要/最少支持或反对限制性手段，使候选人达到令人信服的程度——候选人代表自己的政府利益 第一修正案保护政治言论，其中包括竞选捐款和支出（选项C）。政治言论是自由民主社会的基础，但如果政府满足适当程度的审查，政治言论可能会受到限制（选择 A）。政府对竞选捐款的限制必须通过中间审查，这要求政府证明其限制是为了服务于（即实质上相关）重要的政府利益。在这里，州法律限制了个人在选举周期内可以向州选举办公室的单个候选人捐款的金额。防止捐助者和候选人之间的交换腐败符合国家的重要利益。*在巴克利诉法雷奥案中，美国最高法院认为，限制个人向单个候选人捐赠的资金数额密切关注这一利益。因此，州法是宪法性的。 *防止交换式腐败或腐败的出现是美国最高法院承认的唯一政府利益，其重要性足以证明政府对竞选捐款的限制是合理的。 （选择B）政府对竞选支出的限制必须满足严格的审查，即政府必须表明其限制是对竞选支出限制最少的手段。\n\n服务于引人注目的政府利益。然而，这项法律限制了竞选捐款，因此国家只需满足中间审查即可。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护政治言论，包括竞选捐款和支出。限制竞选捐款的法律是违宪的，除非政府通过表明该法律紧密服务于（即实质上相关）重要的政府利益来满足中间审查的要求。\n\n参考文献\n• Buckley v. Valeo, 424 U.S. 1, 29 (1976)（认为限制个人向候选人捐赠的金额是符合政府打击交换条件腐败的重要利益的宪法手段）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为法律是紧密服务于重要的国家利益的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-2026-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已纳入 2026 年 NRSC v. FEC；本题答案 D 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"National Republican Senatorial Committee v. FEC, 609 U.S. ___ (2026)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-621_h315.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contribution","ipa":"/kˌɑntɹʌbjˈuʃən/","syllables":"con·tri·bu·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 捐助, 捐助之物, 贡献；[经] 贡献, 捐款, 补助品","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 捐助, 捐助之物, 贡献；[经] 贡献, 捐款, 补助品","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:contributions","sourceSentence":"Yes, because campaign contributions are not speech protected by the First Amendment.","synonyms":["part","share","donation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":142,"sourceQuestionPage":424,"sourceQuestionPages":[424],"sourceExplanationPages":[425,426],"sourceAnswerPages":[425,426],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-143","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"To improve the quality of rental housing within its boundaries, a city proposed an ordinance requiring all new and existing rental-housing units to provide at least one full bathroom for each bedroom, plumbing and electrical hookups for a washer and dryer, and a covered parking space. A majority of the owners of existing rental housing in the city opposed the ordinance. They argued that it would dramatically decrease the number of low-income rental-housing units because owners would be unable to raise rents enough to recoup the investment required to comply. Without denying these contentions, the city enacted the ordinance. A plaintiff who owns low-income rental housing has sued the city, claiming only that the ordinance is unconstitutional on its face. Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action?","choices":{"A":"The city must demonstrate that the ordinance is necessary to serve a compelling state interest, because it adversely affects the fundamental right of rental-housing owners to use their property in the manner they deem most economically efficient.","B":"The city must demonstrate that the ordinance is necessary to serve a compelling state interest, because it will have a substantial and disproportionate negative impact on low-income persons.","C":"The plaintiff must demonstrate that the ordinance is not substantially related to an important state interest, because it requires some owners of rental housing to invest money that they will not be able to recoup from increased rents.","D":"The plaintiff must demonstrate that there is no rational relationship between the ordinance and any legitimate state interest, because the ordinance regulates economic activity of a type normally presumed to be within state regulatory authority."},"answer":"D","explanation":"All state and local laws must comply with the Fourteenth Amendment, including the substantive due process and equal protection clauses. The substantive due process clause applies when a law impacts all persons, while the equal protection clause applies when a law impacts a particular class of persons. And under either clause, the challenged law must survive the appropriate level of scrutiny:\n• rational basis scrutiny (default test) – requires the challenger to show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest\n\n• intermediate scrutiny (quasi-suspect class) – requires the state to show that the law is substantially related to an important state interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny (suspect class or fundamental right) – requires the state to show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling state interest.\n\nHere, the city ordinance requires all rental-housing units to include certain amenities. Although this may have a substantial and disproportionate negative impact on low-income persons, wealth-based classifications are not a suspect or quasi-suspect class (Choice B). And rental-housing owners do not have a fundamental right to use their property in any manner (Choice A). Instead, the ordinance regulates the type of economic activity normally presumed to be within a state's authority. Therefore, the plaintiff has the burden to satisfy rational basis scrutiny. (Choice C) The city would have to show that the ordinance is substantially related to an important government interest had the ordinance substantially impacted a quasi-suspect class.\n\nEducational objective: Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, most state and local laws are subject to rational basis review. This standard requires the challenger to demonstrate that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate state interest.\n\nReferences\n• Pennell v. San Jose, 485 U.S. 1, 13–14 (1988) (applying rational basis scrutiny to a price-control ordinance since it does not impact a protected class or a fundamental right).","educationalObjective":"Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, most state and local laws are subject to rational basis review. This standard requires the challenger to demonstrate that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate state interest.","references":"• Pennell v. San Jose, 485 U.S. 1, 13–14 (1988) (applying rational basis scrutiny to a price-control ordinance since it does not impact a protected class or a fundamental right).","rule":"Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, most state and local laws are subject to rational basis review. This standard requires the challenger to demonstrate that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate state interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action?","rule":"Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, most state and local laws are subject to rational basis review. This standard requires the challenger to demonstrate that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate state interest.","analysis":"All state and local laws must comply with the Fourteenth Amendment, including the substantive due process and equal protection clauses. The substantive due process clause applies when a law impacts all persons, while the equal protection clause applies when a law impacts a particular class of persons. And under either clause, the challenged law must survive the appropriate level of scrutiny:\n• rational basis scrutiny (default test) – requires the challenger to show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest\n\n• intermediate scrutiny (quasi-suspect class) – requires the state to show that the law is substantially related to an important state interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny (suspect class or fundamental right) – requires the state to show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling state interest.\n\nHere, the city ordinance requires all rental-housing units to include certain amenities. Although this may have a substantial and disproportionate negative impact on low-income persons, wealth-based classifications are not a suspect or quasi-suspect class (Choice B). And rental-housing owners do not have a fundamental right to use their property in any manner (Choice A). Instead, the ordinance regulates the type of economic activity normally presumed to be within a state's authority. Therefore, the plaintiff has the burden to satisfy rational basis scrutiny. (Choice C) The city would have to show that the ordinance is substantially related to an important government interest had the ordinance substantially impacted a quasi-suspect class.","conclusion":"Answer D: The plaintiff must demonstrate that there is no rational relationship between the ordinance and any legitimate state interest, because the ordinance regulates economic activity of a type normally presumed to be within state regulatory authority."},"questionZh":"为了提高辖区内出租房的质量，某城市提出了一项法令，要求所有新建和现有的出租房单元为每间卧室提供至少一间完整的浴室、洗衣机和烘干机的管道和电气连接，以及有顶棚的停车位。该市大多数现有出租房屋的业主反对该法令。他们认为，这将大大减少低收入出租房屋的数量，因为业主将无法提高租金足以收回遵守规定所需的投资。在不否认这些论点的情况下，该市颁布了该法令。一名拥有低收入出租房的原告已起诉该市，仅声称该法令从表面上看是违宪的。以下哪项最能说明此行动中的说服负担？","choicesZh":{"A":"该市必须证明该法令对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的，因为它对出租房屋业主以他们认为最经济有效的方式使用其财产的基本权利产生了不利影响。","B":"该市必须证明该法令对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的，因为它将对低收入人群产生巨大且不成比例的负面影响。","C":"原告必须证明该法令与重要的国家利益没有实质性关系，因为它要求一些出租房屋业主投入资金，而他们将无法从增加的租金中收回资金。","D":"原告必须证明该法令与任何合法的国家利益之间不存在合理关系，因为该法令监管的经济活动通常被认为属于国家监管机构的范围。"},"explanationZh":"所有州和地方法律都必须遵守第十四修正案，包括实质性正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。当法律影响所有人时，适用实质性正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）；当法律影响特定类别的人时，适用平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。根据任一条款，受到质疑的法律必须通过适当级别的审查：\n• 合理基础审查（违约测试）——要求挑战者证明法律与任何合法国家利益没有合理关系\n\n• 中间审查（准可疑类）——要求国家证明该法律与重要的国家利益有实质性关系或\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（可疑阶级或基本权利）——要求国家证明法律对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的。\n\n在这里，城市法令要求所有出租房屋单位都配备某些设施。尽管这可能对低收入人群产生重大且不成比例的负面影响，但基于财富的分类并不是可疑或准可疑类别（选项 B）。出租屋业主没有以任何方式使用其财产的基本权利（选择 A）。相反，该法令规范通常被认为属于州权力范围内的经济活动类型。因此，原告有责任满足合理的基础审查。 （选项 C）如果该法令对准嫌疑阶层产生重大影响，该市必须证明该法令与重要的政府利益密切相关。\n\n教育目标：根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），大多数州和地方法律都要接受合理的基础审查。该标准要求挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有理性关系。\n\n参考文献\n• Pennell v. San Jose, 485 U.S. 1, 13–14 (1988)（对价格控制法令进行合理基础审查，因为它不影响受保护阶层或基本权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），大多数州和地方法律都要接受合理的基础审查。该标准要求挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有理性关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能说明此行动中的说服负担？","rule":"根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），大多数州和地方法律都要接受合理的基础审查。该标准要求挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有理性关系。","analysis":"所有州和地方法律都必须遵守第十四修正案，包括实质性正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。当法律影响所有人时，适用实质性正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）；当法律影响特定类别的人时，适用平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。根据任一条款，受到质疑的法律必须通过适当级别的审查：\n• 合理基础审查（违约测试）——要求挑战者证明法律与任何合法国家利益没有合理关系\n\n• 中间审查（准可疑类）——要求国家证明该法律与重要的国家利益有实质性关系或\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（可疑阶级或基本权利）——要求国家证明法律对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的。\n\n在这里，城市法令要求所有出租房屋单位都配备某些设施。尽管这可能对低收入人群产生重大且不成比例的负面影响，但基于财富的分类并不是可疑或准可疑类别（选项 B）。出租屋业主没有以任何方式使用其财产的基本权利（选择 A）。相反，该法令规范通常被认为属于州权力范围内的经济活动类型。因此，原告有责任满足合理的基础审查。 （选项 C）如果该法令对准嫌疑阶层产生重大影响，该市必须证明该法令与重要的政府利益密切相关。\n\n教育目标：根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），大多数州和地方法律都要接受合理的基础审查。该标准要求挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有理性关系。\n\n参考文献\n• Pennell v. San Jose, 485 U.S. 1, 13–14 (1988)（对价格控制法令进行合理基础审查，因为它不影响受保护阶层或基本权利）。","conclusion":"答案 D：原告必须证明该法令与任何合法的国家利益之间不存在合理关系，因为该法令监管的经济活动通常被认为属于国家监管机构的范围。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0a9a97f020437e5f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":143,"sourceQuestionPage":427,"sourceQuestionPages":[427],"sourceExplanationPages":[428,429],"sourceAnswerPages":[428,429],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-144","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A man pleaded guilty in state court to dealing a controlled substance. He was sentenced to two years of probation and ordered to pay a $2,000 fine, the maximum monetary fine permissible for this offense. The state has now brought a civil forfeiture action in state court against the man's new automobile, which was seized when the man was arrested. The state claims that the man used the automobile to transport the controlled substance. The man, who had recently purchased the automobile for $50,000 with money he inherited from his mother's estate, believes that the civil forfeiture action violates his constitutional rights. What is the most obvious constitutional basis for the man to challenge the civil forfeiture action?","choices":{"A":"The Eighth Amendment's prohibition against excessive fines, as incorporated into the due process clause of the Fifth Amendment.","B":"The Eighth Amendment's prohibition against excessive fines, as incorporated into the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"The Fifth Amendment's prohibition against taking private property without just compensation, as incorporated into the due process clause of the Fifth Amendment.","D":"The Fifth Amendment's prohibition against taking private property without just compensation, as incorporated into the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The Bill of Rights is composed of the first 10 amendments to the U.S. Constitution. Prior to the ratification of the Civil War Amendments, the Bill of Rights only limited the power of the federal government. These protections, with limited exceptions, have since been incorporated into the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment and therefore apply to the states with equal force. In Timbs v. Indiana, the Supreme Court held that the Eighth Amendment ban on excessive fines applies to the states because it has been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. The Eighth Amendment ban limits the amount of payments, whether in cash or other property, that may be imposed as punishment for an offense. Since the maximum allowable fine for the man's offense is $2,000 but his car is worth $50,000, this protection is his most obvious basis to challenge the civil forfeiture action. (Choice A) The Fifth and Fourteenth Amendment due process clauses contain the same language. However, the Fifth Amendment applies to the federal government while the Fourteenth Amendment applies to the states. (Choices C & D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause—applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause—bars the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. However, the Supreme Court has consistently held that a government seizure of property for a criminal investigation or forfeiture action (as seen here) is not a Fifth Amendment taking.\n\nEducational objective: Most of the Bill of Rights protections, including the Eighth Amendment ban on excessive fines, apply with equal force to the states because they have been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause.\n\nReferences\n• Timbs v. Indiana, 139 S. Ct. 682, 687 (2019) (holding that the Eighth Amendment's ban on excessive fines applies to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause).","educationalObjective":"Most of the Bill of Rights protections, including the Eighth Amendment ban on excessive fines, apply with equal force to the states because they have been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause.","references":"• Timbs v. Indiana, 139 S. Ct. 682, 687 (2019) (holding that the Eighth Amendment's ban on excessive fines applies to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause).","rule":"Most of the Bill of Rights protections, including the Eighth Amendment ban on excessive fines, apply with equal force to the states because they have been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause.","irac":{"issue":"What is the most obvious constitutional basis for the man to challenge the civil forfeiture action?","rule":"Most of the Bill of Rights protections, including the Eighth Amendment ban on excessive fines, apply with equal force to the states because they have been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause.","analysis":"The Bill of Rights is composed of the first 10 amendments to the U.S. Constitution. Prior to the ratification of the Civil War Amendments, the Bill of Rights only limited the power of the federal government. These protections, with limited exceptions, have since been incorporated into the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment and therefore apply to the states with equal force. In Timbs v. Indiana, the Supreme Court held that the Eighth Amendment ban on excessive fines applies to the states because it has been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. The Eighth Amendment ban limits the amount of payments, whether in cash or other property, that may be imposed as punishment for an offense. Since the maximum allowable fine for the man's offense is $2,000 but his car is worth $50,000, this protection is his most obvious basis to challenge the civil forfeiture action. (Choice A) The Fifth and Fourteenth Amendment due process clauses contain the same language. However, the Fifth Amendment applies to the federal government while the Fourteenth Amendment applies to the states. (Choices C & D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause—applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause—bars the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. However, the Supreme Court has consistently held that a government seizure of property for a criminal investigation or forfeiture action (as seen here) is not a Fifth Amendment taking.","conclusion":"Answer B: The Eighth Amendment's prohibition against excessive fines, as incorporated into the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"一名男子在州法院承认贩卖受管制物质的罪名。他被判处两年缓刑，并处以 2,000 美元罚款，这是该罪行允许的最高罚款额。该州现已向州法院对该男子的新车提起民事没收诉讼，该车是在该男子被捕时被扣押的。该州声称该男子使用汽车来运输受管制物质。该男子最近用从母亲遗产中继承的钱以 5 万美元购买了这辆汽车，他认为民事没收行动侵犯了他的宪法权利。该男子挑战民事没收诉讼的最明显的宪法依据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"第八修正案禁止过度罚款，这一点已纳入第五修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。","B":"第八修正案禁止过度罚款，已纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","C":"第五修正案禁止在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私有财产，这一点已纳入第五修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。","D":"第五修正案禁止在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私有财产，这一点已纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。"},"explanationZh":"《权利法案》由美国宪法的前 10 项修正案组成。在批准《内战修正案》之前，《权利法案》仅限制联邦政府的权力。除有限的例外情况外，这些保护措施已被纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），因此对各州具有同等效力。在廷布斯诉印第安纳州案中，最高法院认为第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定适用于各州，因为它已被纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。第八修正案禁令限制了可作为对犯罪行为的惩罚而征收的付款金额，无论是现金还是其他财产。由于该男子罪行的最高允许罚款为 2,000 美元，但他的汽车价值 50,000 美元，因此这种保护是他挑战民事没收诉讼的最明显依据。 （选项 A）第五修正案和第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）包含相同的语言。然而，第五修正案适用于联邦政府，而第十四修正案适用于各州。 （选项 C 和 D）第五修正案征用条款（通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私有财产供公共使用。然而，最高法院一贯认为，政府因刑事调查或没收诉讼而扣押财产（如此处所示）并不是第五修正案的扣押。\n\n教育目标：《权利法案》的大部分保护措施，包括第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定，对各州具有同等效力，因为它们已纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。\n\n参考文献\n• Timbs 诉印第安纳州，139 S. Ct. 682, 687 (2019)（认为第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"大多数权利法案的保护措施，包括第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定，对各州具有同等效力，因为它们已被纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。","iracZh":{"issue":"该男子挑战民事没收诉讼的最明显的宪法依据是什么？","rule":"大多数权利法案的保护措施，包括第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定，对各州具有同等效力，因为它们已被纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。","analysis":"《权利法案》由美国宪法的前 10 项修正案组成。在批准《内战修正案》之前，《权利法案》仅限制联邦政府的权力。除有限的例外情况外，这些保护措施已被纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），因此对各州具有同等效力。在廷布斯诉印第安纳州案中，最高法院认为第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定适用于各州，因为它已被纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。第八修正案禁令限制了可作为对犯罪行为的惩罚而征收的付款金额，无论是现金还是其他财产。由于该男子罪行的最高允许罚款为 2,000 美元，但他的汽车价值 50,000 美元，因此这种保护是他挑战民事没收诉讼的最明显依据。 （选项 A）第五修正案和第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）包含相同的语言。然而，第五修正案适用于联邦政府，而第十四修正案适用于各州。 （选项 C 和 D）第五修正案征用条款（通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私有财产供公共使用。然而，最高法院一贯认为，政府因刑事调查或没收诉讼而扣押财产（如此处所示）并不是第五修正案的扣押。\n\n教育目标：《权利法案》的大部分保护措施，包括第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定，对各州具有同等效力，因为它们已纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。\n\n参考文献\n• Timbs 诉印第安纳州，139 S. Ct. 682, 687 (2019)（认为第八修正案禁止过度罚款的规定通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）。","conclusion":"答案 B：第八修正案禁止过度罚款，已纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-c65993ab5616905a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":144,"sourceQuestionPage":430,"sourceQuestionPages":[430],"sourceExplanationPages":[431,432],"sourceAnswerPages":[431,432],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-145","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A group of students at a state university's law school wished to debate the future of affirmative action in that state and at that law school. For this debate, the students requested the use of a meeting room in the law school that is available on a first-come, first- served basis for extracurricular student use. Speakers presenting all sides of the issue were scheduled to participate. The law school administration denied the use of its meeting room for this purpose solely because it believed that \"such a debate, even if balanced, would have a negative effect on the morale of the law school community and might cause friction among the students that would disrupt the institution's educational mission.\" Is the law school's denial of the use of its meeting room for this purpose likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the law school cannot demonstrate that its action was necessary to serve a compelling state interest.","B":"No, because the law school cannot demonstrate that its action was rationally related to a legitimate state interest.","C":"Yes, because the law school's only concern was the adverse effect of such a discussion of affirmative action on the immediate audience and the mission of the institution.","D":"Yes, because the law students do not have a right to use a state-owned law school facility for a meeting that is not organized and sponsored by the law school itself."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The First Amendment protects the right to free expression and encourages the free flow of ideas—even on government property. As a result, speech that occurs in a public forum cannot be regulated based on its message, subject, or ideas (ie, content-based restriction) unless that regulation meets strict scrutiny. This standard of constitutional review gives the government the nearly impossible burden of proving that the restriction is necessary to serve a compelling government interest. Here, a state-owned law school has a meeting room that is designated for extracurricular student use. A student group asked to use the room to debate affirmative action. However, the school denied this request because it feared that the content of the debate would negatively affect the school. As a result, the denial is subject to strict scrutiny. And since this is such a difficult standard to meet, the denial is likely unconstitutional. (Choice B) Rational basis scrutiny requires the challenger (eg, students) to demonstrate that the government's content-based restriction was not rationally related to any legitimate state interest and viewpoint neutral. But this standard of review only applies when the government restricts use of (1) a nonpublic forum or (2) a public forum for an undesignated purpose (eg, something other than extracurricular student use). (Choice C) The debate's effect on the immediate audience and the mission of the institution are important—but not compelling—concerns for the state-owned school. But even if they were compelling, the denial was not necessary to achieve those interests. (Choice D) The meeting room is a designated public forum available for extracurricular student use. Therefore, the students had the right to use the room for a meeting— regardless of whether it was organized or sponsored by the law school—for that limited purpose.\n\nEducational objective: The government cannot impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums unless it can satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, show that the restrictions are necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Widmar v. Vincent, 454 U.S. 263, 269–70 (1981) (holding that a state university's exclusion of a religious student group from using university facilities is a content- based restriction on speech subject to strict scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"The government cannot impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums unless it can satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, show that the restrictions are necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Widmar v. Vincent, 454 U.S. 263, 269–70 (1981) (holding that a state university's exclusion of a religious student group from using university facilities is a content- based restriction on speech subject to strict scrutiny).","rule":"The government cannot impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums unless it can satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, show that the restrictions are necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"A group of students at a state university's law school wished to debate the future of affirmative action in that state and at that law school. For this debate, the students requested the use of a meeting room in the law school that is available on a first-come, first- served basis for extracurricular student use. Speakers presenting all sides of the issue were scheduled to participate. The law school administration denied the use of its meeting room for this purpose solely because it believed that \"such a debate, even if balanced, would have a negative effect on the morale of the law school community and might cause friction among the students that would disrupt the institution's educational mission.\" Is the law school's denial of the use of its meeting room for this purpose likely constitutional?","rule":"The government cannot impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums unless it can satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, show that the restrictions are necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment protects the right to free expression and encourages the free flow of ideas—even on government property. As a result, speech that occurs in a public forum cannot be regulated based on its message, subject, or ideas (ie, content-based restriction) unless that regulation meets strict scrutiny. This standard of constitutional review gives the government the nearly impossible burden of proving that the restriction is necessary to serve a compelling government interest. Here, a state-owned law school has a meeting room that is designated for extracurricular student use. A student group asked to use the room to debate affirmative action. However, the school denied this request because it feared that the content of the debate would negatively affect the school. As a result, the denial is subject to strict scrutiny. And since this is such a difficult standard to meet, the denial is likely unconstitutional. (Choice B) Rational basis scrutiny requires the challenger (eg, students) to demonstrate that the government's content-based restriction was not rationally related to any legitimate state interest and viewpoint neutral. But this standard of review only applies when the government restricts use of (1) a nonpublic forum or (2) a public forum for an undesignated purpose (eg, something other than extracurricular student use). (Choice C) The debate's effect on the immediate audience and the mission of the institution are important—but not compelling—concerns for the state-owned school. But even if they were compelling, the denial was not necessary to achieve those interests. (Choice D) The meeting room is a designated public forum available for extracurricular student use. Therefore, the students had the right to use the room for a meeting— regardless of whether it was organized or sponsored by the law school—for that limited purpose.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the law school cannot demonstrate that its action was necessary to serve a compelling state interest."},"questionZh":"一所州立大学法学院的一群学生希望就该州和该法学院平权行动的未来进行辩论。在这次辩论中，学生们要求使用法学院的一间会议室，该会议室按照先到先得的原则供课外学生使用。代表该问题各个方面的发言人都计划参加。法学院管理部门拒绝将其会议室用于此目的，仅仅是因为它认为“这样的辩论，即使是平衡的，也会对法学院社区的士气产生负面影响，并可能在学生之间引起摩擦，从而破坏该机构的教育使命。”法学院拒绝为此目的使用其会议室是否可能符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为法学院无法证明其行动对于服务引人注目的国家利益是必要的。","B":"不，因为法学院无法证明其行为与合法的国家利益合理相关。","C":"是的，因为法学院唯一担心的是这种平权行动的讨论对直接受众和该机构的使命产生的不利影响。","D":"是的，因为法学院学生无权使用国有法学院设施参加非法学院本身组织和赞助的会议。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案保护言论自由权并鼓励思想自由流动——即使是在政府财产上。因此，公共论坛中发生的言论不能根据其信息、主题或想法（即基于内容的限制）进行监管，除非该监管符合严格的审查。这种宪法审查标准给政府带来了几乎不可能的负担，需要证明该限制对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的。这里，一所国有法学院设有一间会议室，专门供学生课外活动使用。一个学生团体要求使用这个房间来辩论平权行​​动。但校方因担心辩论内容会给学校带来负面影响而拒绝了这一要求。因此，否认会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。由于这是一个很难达到的标准，因此否认可能是违宪的。 （选择B）合理基础审查要求挑战者（例如学生）证明政府基于内容的限制与任何合法的国家利益和观点中立没有合理关系。但这一审查标准仅适用于政府限制使用 (1) 非公共论坛或 (2) 出于未指定目的（例如，学生课外用途以外的用途）的公共论坛。 （选项 C） 辩论对直接受众的影响和机构的使命对于公立学校来说是重要的但不是引人注目的关注点。但即使它们令人信服，否认也不是实现这些利益所必需的。 （选项D） 会议室是供课外学生使用的指定公共论坛。因此，学生有权使用该房间举行会议——无论会议是由法学院组织还是赞助——用于有限的目的。\n\n教育目标：政府不能对公共论坛上的言论施加基于内容的限制，除非它能够满足严格的审查，即表明这些限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• Widmar v. Vincent, 454 U.S. 263, 269–70 (1981)（认为州立大学禁止宗教学生团体使用大学设施是基于内容的言论限制，须受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"政府不能对公共论坛上的言论施加基于内容的限制，除非它能够满足严格的审查——即表明这些限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","iracZh":{"issue":"一所州立大学法学院的一群学生希望就该州和该法学院平权行动的未来进行辩论。在这次辩论中，学生们要求使用法学院的一间会议室，该会议室按照先到先得的原则供课外学生使用。代表该问题各个方面的发言人都计划参加。法学院管理部门拒绝将其会议室用于此目的，仅仅是因为它认为“这样的辩论，即使是平衡的，也会对法学院社区的士气产生负面影响，并可能在学生之间引起摩擦，从而破坏该机构的教育使命。”法学院拒绝为此目的使用其会议室是否可能符合宪法？","rule":"政府不能对公共论坛上的言论施加基于内容的限制，除非它能够满足严格的审查——即表明这些限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","analysis":"第一修正案保护言论自由权并鼓励思想自由流动——即使是在政府财产上。因此，公共论坛中发生的言论不能根据其信息、主题或想法（即基于内容的限制）进行监管，除非该监管符合严格的审查。这种宪法审查标准给政府带来了几乎不可能的负担，需要证明该限制对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的。这里，一所国有法学院设有一间会议室，专门供学生课外活动使用。一个学生团体要求使用这个房间来辩论平权行​​动。但校方因担心辩论内容会给学校带来负面影响而拒绝了这一要求。因此，否认会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。由于这是一个很难达到的标准，因此否认可能是违宪的。 （选择B）合理基础审查要求挑战者（例如学生）证明政府基于内容的限制与任何合法的国家利益和观点中立没有合理关系。但这一审查标准仅适用于政府限制使用 (1) 非公共论坛或 (2) 出于未指定目的（例如，学生课外用途以外的用途）的公共论坛。 （选项 C） 辩论对直接受众的影响和机构的使命对于公立学校来说是重要的但不是引人注目的关注点。但即使它们令人信服，否认也不是实现这些利益所必需的。 （选项D） 会议室是供课外学生使用的指定公共论坛。因此，学生有权使用该房间举行会议——无论会议是由法学院组织还是赞助——用于有限的目的。\n\n教育目标：政府不能对公共论坛上的言论施加基于内容的限制，除非它能够满足严格的审查，即表明这些限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• Widmar v. Vincent, 454 U.S. 263, 269–70 (1981)（认为州立大学禁止宗教学生团体使用大学设施是基于内容的言论限制，须受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为法学院无法证明其行动对于服务引人注目的国家利益是必要的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"affirmative","ipa":"/ʌfˈɝmətɪv/","syllables":"af·fir·ma·tive","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 肯定的, 赞成的, 积极的；n. 肯定语","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 肯定的, 赞成的, 积极的；n. 肯定语","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:affirmatives","sourceSentence":"A group of students at a state university's law school wished to debate the future of affirmative action in that state and at that law school.","synonyms":["affirmatory","approving","approbative","approbatory"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-4424e92eb0af0f5b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":145,"sourceQuestionPage":433,"sourceQuestionPages":[433],"sourceExplanationPages":[434,435],"sourceAnswerPages":[434,435],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-146","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"In an effort to counteract a steep increase in juvenile crime, a state enacted a law terminating the parental rights of any state resident whose child under 16 years of age is convicted of a violent crime in the state. The law directs the state juvenile court to enter a termination order in such a case after the parent has been afforded notice and an opportunity for a hearing at which the only relevant issues are the age of the child and whether the child has been convicted of a violent crime in the state. Is the state law constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the law is not narrowly tailored to serve a substantial state interest.","B":"No, because the law is not necessary to serve a compelling state interest.","C":"Yes, because a state's police power authorizes it to punish criminal behavior with appropriate sanctions.","D":"Yes, because the law is rationally related to a legitimate state interest."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Fundamental rights (First VIP)\n\n(strict scrutiny)\n\n• First Amendment freedoms\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy – Marriage / family – Parental rights – Sexual acts – Contraceptives State laws that substantially impact a fundamental right—including parents' right to direct the upbringing of their children—can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment based on either:\n• substantive due process – applies when a law affects all people (eg, everyone's fundamental parental rights) or\n\n• equal protection – applies when a law affects a specific class of persons (eg, parents with children under 16 years of age).\n\nFundamental rights are highly protected under both clauses, and any law that substantially impacts these rights is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny. This standard of constitutional review requires the state to perform the nearly impossible task of showing that the law is necessary to serve a compelling state interest. Here, the state law is subject to strict scrutiny because it terminates the fundamental parental rights of any resident whose child under 16 years of age is convicted of a violent crime. The law addresses the state's compelling interest in counteracting juvenile crime. But the law is not necessary to serve that interest because the state can do so with less restrictive measures—eg, creating diversion programs for at-risk youths. Therefore, the law is unconstitutional. (Choice A) There is no standard of constitutional review that requires a law to be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial state interest. (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment gives states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the police power to punish criminal behavior with appropriate sanctions. But these sanctions must comport with other constitutional provisions—including the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\n(Choice D) Most due process and equal protection challenges are subject to rational basis scrutiny, which requires the challenger to show that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate state interest. But when a law substantially impacts a fundamental right (as seen here), strict scrutiny is required.\n\nEducational objective: Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right (eg, parental rights) must satisfy strict scrutiny— ie, the law must be necessary to serve a compelling state interest.\n\nReferences\n• Meyer v. Nebraska, 262 U.S. 390, 399 (1923) (establishing parents' fundamental right to direct their children's upbringing).\n\n• Troxel v. Granville, 530 U.S. 57, 65–66 (2000) (plurality opinion) (explaining that parents have a fundamental right \"to make decisions concerning the care, custody, and control of their children\").","educationalObjective":"Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right (eg, parental rights) must satisfy strict scrutiny— ie, the law must be necessary to serve a compelling state interest.","references":"• Meyer v. Nebraska, 262 U.S. 390, 399 (1923) (establishing parents' fundamental right to direct their children's upbringing).\n\n• Troxel v. Granville, 530 U.S. 57, 65–66 (2000) (plurality opinion) (explaining that parents have a fundamental right \"to make decisions concerning the care, custody, and control of their children\").","rule":"Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right (eg, parental rights) must satisfy strict scrutiny— ie, the law must be necessary to serve a compelling state interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state law constitutional?","rule":"Under the Fourteenth Amendment due process and equal protection clauses, state laws that substantially impact a fundamental right (eg, parental rights) must satisfy strict scrutiny— ie, the law must be necessary to serve a compelling state interest.","analysis":"Fundamental rights (First VIP)\n\n(strict scrutiny)\n\n• First Amendment freedoms\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy – Marriage / family – Parental rights – Sexual acts – Contraceptives State laws that substantially impact a fundamental right—including parents' right to direct the upbringing of their children—can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment based on either:\n• substantive due process – applies when a law affects all people (eg, everyone's fundamental parental rights) or\n\n• equal protection – applies when a law affects a specific class of persons (eg, parents with children under 16 years of age).\n\nFundamental rights are highly protected under both clauses, and any law that substantially impacts these rights is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny. This standard of constitutional review requires the state to perform the nearly impossible task of showing that the law is necessary to serve a compelling state interest. Here, the state law is subject to strict scrutiny because it terminates the fundamental parental rights of any resident whose child under 16 years of age is convicted of a violent crime. The law addresses the state's compelling interest in counteracting juvenile crime. But the law is not necessary to serve that interest because the state can do so with less restrictive measures—eg, creating diversion programs for at-risk youths. Therefore, the law is unconstitutional. (Choice A) There is no standard of constitutional review that requires a law to be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial state interest. (Choice C) The Tenth Amendment gives states any power that the Constitution does not expressly grant to the federal government—including the police power to punish criminal behavior with appropriate sanctions. But these sanctions must comport with other constitutional provisions—including the Fourteenth Amendment.\n\n(Choice D) Most due process and equal protection challenges are subject to rational basis scrutiny, which requires the challenger to show that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate state interest. But when a law substantially impacts a fundamental right (as seen here), strict scrutiny is required.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the law is not necessary to serve a compelling state interest."},"questionZh":"为了遏制青少年犯罪率的急剧上升，某州颁布了一项法律，终止任何该州居民 16 岁以下的孩子在该州被判犯有暴力犯罪的父母权利。法律指示州少年法院在向家长发出通知并有机会举行听证会后，在此类案件中下达终止令，听证会上唯一相关的问题是孩子的年龄以及孩子是否在该州被判犯有暴力犯罪。国家法律是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为法律并不是狭隘地为服务重大国家利益而制定的。","B":"不，因为法律并不是服务于令人信服的国家利益所必需的。","C":"是的，因为一个国家的警察权力授权它以适当的制裁来惩罚犯罪行为。","D":"是的，因为法律与合法的国家利益合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"基本权利（第一VIP）\n\n（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））\n\n• 第一修正案的自由\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私 – 婚姻/家庭 – 父母权利 – 性行为 – 避孕药具 严重影响基本权利（包括父母指导子女抚养的权利）的州法律可能会根据第十四修正案基于以下任一理由受到质疑：\n• 实质性正当程序——适用于法律影响所有人的情况（例如，每个人的基本父母权利）或\n\n• 平等保护——适用于某项法律影响特定类别的人（例如，有 16 岁以下子女的父母）。\n\n这两个条款都高度保护基本权利，任何对这些权利产生重大影响的法律都是违宪的，除非它能经受住严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这一宪法审查标准要求国家执行几乎不可能完成的任务，即证明法律对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的。在这里，州法律受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因为它终止了任何 16 岁以下子女被判犯有暴力犯罪的居民的基本父母权利。该法律解决了国家在打击青少年犯罪方面的迫切利益。但法律没有必要服务于这种利益，因为国家可以通过限制较少的措施来做到这一点，例如为高危青少年制定转移计划。因此，该法律是违宪的。 （选项 A）不存在任何宪法审查标准要求法律必须狭隘地调整以服务于重大国家利益。 （选项 C）第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括警察通过适当制裁惩罚犯罪行为的权力。但这些制裁必须符合其他宪法条款——包括第十四修正案。\n\n（选项D）大多数正当程序和平等保护挑战都受到合理基础的审查，这要求挑战者证明法律与任何合法的国家利益没有理性关系。但是，当法律对一项基本权利产生重大影响时（如此处所示），就需要严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n教育目标：根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利（例如父母权利）产生重大影响的州法律必须经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即法律必须是服务于令人信服的州利益所必需的。\n\n参考文献\n• Meyer v. Nebraska, 262 U.S. 390, 399 (1923)（确立父母指导子女抚养的基本权利）。\n\n• Troxel v. Granville, 530 U.S. 57, 65–66 (2000)（多数意见）（解释父母拥有“就子女的照顾、监护和控制做出决定”的基本权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利（例如父母权利）产生重大影响的州法律必须经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即法律必须是服务于令人信服的州利益所必需的。","iracZh":{"issue":"国家法律是否符合宪法？","rule":"根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利（例如父母权利）产生重大影响的州法律必须经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即法律必须是服务于令人信服的州利益所必需的。","analysis":"基本权利（第一VIP）\n\n（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））\n\n• 第一修正案的自由\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私 – 婚姻/家庭 – 父母权利 – 性行为 – 避孕药具 严重影响基本权利（包括父母指导子女抚养的权利）的州法律可能会根据第十四修正案基于以下任一理由受到质疑：\n• 实质性正当程序——适用于法律影响所有人的情况（例如，每个人的基本父母权利）或\n\n• 平等保护——适用于某项法律影响特定类别的人（例如，有 16 岁以下子女的父母）。\n\n这两个条款都高度保护基本权利，任何对这些权利产生重大影响的法律都是违宪的，除非它能经受住严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这一宪法审查标准要求国家执行几乎不可能完成的任务，即证明法律对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的。在这里，州法律受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因为它终止了任何 16 岁以下子女被判犯有暴力犯罪的居民的基本父母权利。该法律解决了国家在打击青少年犯罪方面的迫切利益。但法律没有必要服务于这种利益，因为国家可以通过限制较少的措施来做到这一点，例如为高危青少年制定转移计划。因此，该法律是违宪的。 （选项 A）不存在任何宪法审查标准要求法律必须狭隘地调整以服务于重大国家利益。 （选项 C）第十修正案赋予各州宪法未明确授予联邦政府的任何权力，包括警察通过适当制裁惩罚犯罪行为的权力。但这些制裁必须符合其他宪法条款——包括第十四修正案。\n\n（选项D）大多数正当程序和平等保护挑战都受到合理基础的审查，这要求挑战者证明法律与任何合法的国家利益没有理性关系。但是，当法律对一项基本权利产生重大影响时（如此处所示），就需要严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n教育目标：根据第十四修正案的正当程序和平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利（例如父母权利）产生重大影响的州法律必须经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即法律必须是服务于令人信服的州利益所必需的。\n\n参考文献\n• Meyer v. Nebraska, 262 U.S. 390, 399 (1923)（确立父母指导子女抚养的基本权利）。\n\n• Troxel v. Granville, 530 U.S. 57, 65–66 (2000)（多数意见）（解释父母拥有“就子女的照顾、监护和控制做出决定”的基本权利）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为法律并不是服务于令人信服的国家利益所必需的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":146,"sourceQuestionPage":436,"sourceQuestionPages":[436],"sourceExplanationPages":[437,438],"sourceAnswerPages":[437,438],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-147","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Supremacy & Preemption","question":"A woman belonged to a subversive organization that advocated for the United States to preemptively attack certain foreign countries. The organization usually used leafleting and public speeches to advance this view, but it sometimes engaged in violent demonstrations against the embassies and consuls of those countries. Although the woman had never participated in a violent demonstration, she was criminally prosecuted for being a member of the organization. In her defense, the woman claimed that her association with the organization was protected by the First Amendment. Which of the following would the prosecution need to prove to overcome that defense?","choices":{"A":"The woman continued to provide material aid to the organization through the payment of dues after the violent demonstrations.","B":"The woman expressed public support of the organization after the violent demonstrations.","C":"The woman joined the organization with the specific intent of furthering its illegal activities.","D":"The woman provided material aid to the organization through the payment of dues before the violent demonstrations."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Freedom of association\n\nGeneral rule Infringing upon right to associate barred unless:\n\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Subversive Punishing member of subversive organization permitted if organization person:\n\n• is active member\n• knows of organization's illegal objectives and\n• specifically intends to further them Loyalty oath Requiring loyalty oath for public employment permitted unless:\n\n• overbroad – infringes on constitutionally protected behavior or\n• vague – reasonable person would not understand oath Forced inclusion Interfering with organization's discriminatory policy barred unless:\n\n• no significant burden on organization's mission and\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Electoral process Imposing restriction on electoral process:\n\n• ordinary – permitted if rationally related to legitimate interest\n• severe – barred unless necessary to achieve compelling interest The First Amendment protects against government interference with a person's right to associate with any group or organization. But since this right is not absolute, the government can punish (ie, criminally prosecute or deny public employment to) persons who:\n• are active members of a subversive organization\n• know of the organization's illegal objectives and\n• specifically intend to further those objectives. Therefore, to overcome the woman's freedom-of-association defense, the prosecution would have to prove that she joined the subversive organization with the specific intent of furthering its illegal activities.\n\n(Choices A & D) Payment of dues does not show that the woman was an active member of that organization since even passive (ie, nonparticipating) members pay dues. (Choice B) The woman cannot be convicted for merely expressing public support for the organization. But she could be convicted for intentionally furthering its illegal objectives.\n\nEducational objective: The government can interfere with the First Amendment freedom of association by punishing persons who (1) are active members of a subversive organization, (2) know of the organization's illegal objectives, and (3) specifically intend to further those objectives.\n\nReferences\n• Scales v. United States, 367 U.S. 203, 229 (1961) (explaining when a person can be criminally prosecuted for membership in a subversive organization).","educationalObjective":"The government can interfere with the First Amendment freedom of association by punishing persons who (1) are active members of a subversive organization, (2) know of the organization's illegal objectives, and (3) specifically intend to further those objectives.","references":"• Scales v. United States, 367 U.S. 203, 229 (1961) (explaining when a person can be criminally prosecuted for membership in a subversive organization).","rule":"The government can interfere with the First Amendment freedom of association by punishing persons who (1) are active members of a subversive organization, (2) know of the organization's illegal objectives, and (3) specifically intend to further those objectives.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would the prosecution need to prove to overcome that defense?","rule":"The government can interfere with the First Amendment freedom of association by punishing persons who (1) are active members of a subversive organization, (2) know of the organization's illegal objectives, and (3) specifically intend to further those objectives.","analysis":"Freedom of association\n\nGeneral rule Infringing upon right to associate barred unless:\n\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Subversive Punishing member of subversive organization permitted if organization person:\n\n• is active member\n• knows of organization's illegal objectives and\n• specifically intends to further them Loyalty oath Requiring loyalty oath for public employment permitted unless:\n\n• overbroad – infringes on constitutionally protected behavior or\n• vague – reasonable person would not understand oath Forced inclusion Interfering with organization's discriminatory policy barred unless:\n\n• no significant burden on organization's mission and\n• necessary to achieve compelling interest Electoral process Imposing restriction on electoral process:\n\n• ordinary – permitted if rationally related to legitimate interest\n• severe – barred unless necessary to achieve compelling interest The First Amendment protects against government interference with a person's right to associate with any group or organization. But since this right is not absolute, the government can punish (ie, criminally prosecute or deny public employment to) persons who:\n• are active members of a subversive organization\n• know of the organization's illegal objectives and\n• specifically intend to further those objectives. Therefore, to overcome the woman's freedom-of-association defense, the prosecution would have to prove that she joined the subversive organization with the specific intent of furthering its illegal activities.\n\n(Choices A & D) Payment of dues does not show that the woman was an active member of that organization since even passive (ie, nonparticipating) members pay dues. (Choice B) The woman cannot be convicted for merely expressing public support for the organization. But she could be convicted for intentionally furthering its illegal objectives.","conclusion":"Answer C: The woman joined the organization with the specific intent of furthering its illegal activities."},"questionZh":"一名妇女属于一个颠覆组织，该组织主张美国先发制人地攻击某些外国国家。该组织通常通过散发传单和公开演讲来宣扬这一观点，但有时也会针对这些国家的使馆和领事馆进行暴力示威。尽管该女子从未参加过暴力示威活动，但她因是该组织成员而受到刑事起诉。在她的辩护中，该妇女声称她与该组织的关系受到第一修正案的保护。控方需要证明以下哪一项才能推翻该辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"暴力示威后，该妇女继续通过缴纳会费的方式向该组织提供物质援助。","B":"暴力示威后，该女子公开表达了对该组织的支持。","C":"该妇女加入该组织的具体目的是促进其非法活动。","D":"该妇女在暴力示威前通过缴纳会费向该组织提供物质援助。"},"explanationZh":"结社自由\n\n一般规则 禁止侵犯结社权，除非：\n\n• 为实现引人注目的利益所必需的 颠覆性惩罚 允许颠覆性组织的成员，如果组织人员：\n\n• 是活跃会员\n• 了解组织的非法目标并且\n• 特别旨在促进这些目标 忠诚宣誓 允许要求公职人员宣誓忠诚，除非：\n\n• 过于宽泛——侵犯受宪法保护的行为或\n• 模糊——有理智的人不会理解誓言 强制纳入 禁止干涉组织的歧视性政策，除非：\n\n• 对组织的使命没有重大负担，并且\n• 实现引人注目的利益所必需的 选举过程 对选举过程施加限制：\n\n• 普通——如果与合法利益合理相关则允许\n• 严重——禁止，除非为实现令人信服的利益所必需。 第一修正案保护个人与任何团体或组织交往的权利免受政府干预。但由于这项权利不是绝对的，政府可以惩罚（即刑事起诉或拒绝公共就业）以下人员：\n• 是颠覆组织的活跃成员\n• 了解该组织的非法目标并\n• 特别打算推进这些目标。因此，为了克服该妇女的结社自由辩护，检方必须证明她加入颠覆组织的具体意图是促进其非法活动。\n\n（选择 A 和 D） 缴纳会费并不表明该妇女是该组织的积极成员，因为即使是被动（即非参与）成员也会缴纳会费。 （选择 B） 该妇女不能仅仅因为公开表达对该组织的支持而被定罪。但她可能会因故意推进其非法目标而被定罪。\n\n教育目标：政府可以通过惩罚以下人员来干扰第一修正案的结社自由：(1) 颠覆组织的积极成员，(2) 了解该组织的非法目标，以及 (3) 特别打算推进这些目标。\n\n参考文献\n• Scales v. United States, 367 U.S. 203, 229 (1961)（解释了一个人何时可以因加入颠覆组织而受到刑事起诉）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"政府可以通过惩罚以下人员来干扰第一修正案的结社自由：(1) 颠覆组织的积极成员，(2) 了解该组织的非法目标，以及 (3) 特别打算推进这些目标。","iracZh":{"issue":"控方需要证明以下哪一项才能推翻该辩护？","rule":"政府可以通过惩罚以下人员来干扰第一修正案的结社自由：(1) 颠覆组织的积极成员，(2) 了解该组织的非法目标，以及 (3) 特别打算推进这些目标。","analysis":"结社自由\n\n一般规则 禁止侵犯结社权，除非：\n\n• 为实现引人注目的利益所必需的 颠覆性惩罚 允许颠覆性组织的成员，如果组织人员：\n\n• 是活跃会员\n• 了解组织的非法目标并且\n• 特别旨在促进这些目标 忠诚宣誓 允许要求公职人员宣誓忠诚，除非：\n\n• 过于宽泛——侵犯受宪法保护的行为或\n• 模糊——有理智的人不会理解誓言 强制纳入 禁止干涉组织的歧视性政策，除非：\n\n• 对组织的使命没有重大负担，并且\n• 实现引人注目的利益所必需的 选举过程 对选举过程施加限制：\n\n• 普通——如果与合法利益合理相关则允许\n• 严重——禁止，除非为实现令人信服的利益所必需。 第一修正案保护个人与任何团体或组织交往的权利免受政府干预。但由于这项权利不是绝对的，政府可以惩罚（即刑事起诉或拒绝公共就业）以下人员：\n• 是颠覆组织的活跃成员\n• 了解该组织的非法目标并\n• 特别打算推进这些目标。因此，为了克服该妇女的结社自由辩护，检方必须证明她加入颠覆组织的具体意图是促进其非法活动。\n\n（选择 A 和 D） 缴纳会费并不表明该妇女是该组织的积极成员，因为即使是被动（即非参与）成员也会缴纳会费。 （选择 B） 该妇女不能仅仅因为公开表达对该组织的支持而被定罪。但她可能会因故意推进其非法目标而被定罪。\n\n教育目标：政府可以通过惩罚以下人员来干扰第一修正案的结社自由：(1) 颠覆组织的积极成员，(2) 了解该组织的非法目标，以及 (3) 特别打算推进这些目标。\n\n参考文献\n• Scales v. United States, 367 U.S. 203, 229 (1961)（解释了一个人何时可以因加入颠覆组织而受到刑事起诉）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该妇女加入该组织的具体目的是促进其非法活动。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"preemptively","ipa":"/pɹiˈɛmptɪvli/","syllables":"pre·emp·tively","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"先发制人地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：先发制人地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"A woman belonged to a subversive organization that advocated for the United States to preemptively attack certain foreign countries.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"criminally","ipa":"/kɹˈɪmənəli/","syllables":"crim·i·nally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 刑法上, 犯罪上","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[法] 刑法上, 犯罪上","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Although the woman had never participated in a violent demonstration, she was criminally prosecuted for being a member of the organization.","synonyms":["reprehensively"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":147,"sourceQuestionPage":439,"sourceQuestionPages":[439],"sourceExplanationPages":[440,441],"sourceAnswerPages":[440,441],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-148","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Privileges & Immunities","question":"According to a state law, state employees may be fired only \"for good cause.\" A woman who was both a resident and an employee of the state was summarily fired on the sole ground that she had notified federal officials that the state was not following federal rules governing the administration of certain federally funded state programs on which she worked. The state denied the woman's request for a hearing to allow her to contest the charge. There is no record of any other state employee having been terminated for this reason. In a suit to reinstate her employment, which of the following claims provides the LEAST support for the woman?","choices":{"A":"The state's firing of her unconstitutionally abridged her freedom of speech.","B":"The state's firing of her unconstitutionally denied her a privilege or immunity of state citizenship protected by Article IV.","C":"The state's firing of her violated the supremacy clause of Article VI, because it interfered with the enforcement of federal rules.","D":"The state's firing of her without affording her an opportunity for a hearing was an unconstitutional denial of procedural due process."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Privileges & immunities clauses\n\nProvision Applicability\n\nArticle IV\n• Discrimination against citizens of other states\n• Interference with right of state citizenship Fourteenth Amendment\n• Interference with right of national citizenship\n\nThe Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship. But this clause does not apply here since (1) the woman is a resident (ie, citizen) of the state that fired her and (2) public employment is not a right of state citizenship. Therefore, this clause provides the least support for the woman in a suit to reinstate her employment. (Choice A) The state fired the woman solely because she notified federal officials that the state was not complying with federal rules. As a result, that firing may have unconstitutionally abridged her right to free speech as a public employee. (Choice C) The Article VI supremacy clause renders state action void when federal law preempts it—eg, when state action interferes with the achievement of federal objectives. Here, the state's firing of the woman for notifying federal officials that the state was not following federal rules likely interfered with the enforcement of those rules in violation of the supremacy clause. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment right to procedural due process requires states to give persons notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard when depriving them of life, liberty, or property. And public employees who can only be fired for cause—like the woman—have a property interest in their jobs. Therefore, it was an unconstitutional denial of procedural due process to fire her without giving her an opportunity for a hearing.\n\nEducational objective: The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship.\n\nReferences\n• Paul v. Virginia, 75 U.S. 168, 180 (1869) (holding that the Article IV privileges and immunities clause only applies when a state discriminates against citizens of other states).","educationalObjective":"The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship.","references":"• Paul v. Virginia, 75 U.S. 168, 180 (1869) (holding that the Article IV privileges and immunities clause only applies when a state discriminates against citizens of other states).","rule":"The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit to reinstate her employment, which of the following claims provides the LEAST support for the woman?","rule":"The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship.","analysis":"Privileges & immunities clauses\n\nProvision Applicability\n\nArticle IV\n• Discrimination against citizens of other states\n• Interference with right of state citizenship Fourteenth Amendment\n• Interference with right of national citizenship\n\nThe Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship. But this clause does not apply here since (1) the woman is a resident (ie, citizen) of the state that fired her and (2) public employment is not a right of state citizenship. Therefore, this clause provides the least support for the woman in a suit to reinstate her employment. (Choice A) The state fired the woman solely because she notified federal officials that the state was not complying with federal rules. As a result, that firing may have unconstitutionally abridged her right to free speech as a public employee. (Choice C) The Article VI supremacy clause renders state action void when federal law preempts it—eg, when state action interferes with the achievement of federal objectives. Here, the state's firing of the woman for notifying federal officials that the state was not following federal rules likely interfered with the enforcement of those rules in violation of the supremacy clause. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment right to procedural due process requires states to give persons notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard when depriving them of life, liberty, or property. And public employees who can only be fired for cause—like the woman—have a property interest in their jobs. Therefore, it was an unconstitutional denial of procedural due process to fire her without giving her an opportunity for a hearing.","conclusion":"Answer B: The state's firing of her unconstitutionally denied her a privilege or immunity of state citizenship protected by Article IV."},"questionZh":"根据州法律，只有“出于正当理由”才可以解雇国家雇员。一名既是该州居民又是雇员的妇女被立即解雇，理由是她通知联邦官员，该州没有遵守管理她所从事的某些联邦资助的州项目的联邦规则。该州拒绝了该妇女要求举行听证会以对指控提出异议的请求。没有任何其他国家雇员因此而被解雇的记录。在恢复其就业的诉讼中，以下哪项主张为该妇女提供的支持最少？","choicesZh":{"A":"州政府违宪地解雇了她，剥夺了她的言论自由。","B":"国家违宪地解雇了她，剥夺了她受第四条保护的国家公民特权或豁免权。","C":"该州解雇她违反了第六条的至上条款，因为它干扰了联邦规则的执行。","D":"州政府在没有给她听证机会的情况下解雇她，这是违宪地剥夺了程序上的正当程序。"},"explanationZh":"特权和豁免条款\n\n规定的适用性\n\n第四条\n• 对其他国家公民的歧视\n• 干涉国家公民权 第十四修正案\n• 干涉公民权\n\n第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的国家公民权来歧视他们。但该条款在这里并不适用，因为 (1) 该妇女是解雇她所在州的居民（即公民），并且 (2) 公共就业不是该州公民的权利。因此，该条款为提起诉讼的女性恢复就业提供了最少的支持。 （选项 A）该州解雇该妇女只是因为她通知联邦官员该州没有遵守联邦规定。因此，这次解雇可能违宪地剥夺了她作为公职人员的言论自由权。 （选项C）当联邦法律优先于该条款时，例如，当国家行为（state action）干扰联邦目标的实现时，第六条至上条款使国家行为（state action）无效。在这里，该州因通知联邦官员该州没有遵守联邦规则而解雇该妇女，这可能会干扰这些规则的执行，从而违反了至上条款。 （选项 D）第十四修正案的程序正当程序权要求各州在剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产时给予其通知和有意义的表达意见的机会。而只能因故被解雇的公职人员——比如这位女性——在他们的工作中拥有财产权益。因此，在没有给她听证机会的情况下解雇她是违宪的，违反了程序上的正当程序。\n\n教育目标：第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的国家公民权利来歧视他们。\n\n参考文献\n• Paul v. Virginia, 75 U.S. 168, 180 (1869)（认为第四条特权和豁免条款仅适用于一个州歧视其他州公民的情况）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的国家公民权来歧视他们。","iracZh":{"issue":"在恢复其就业的诉讼中，以下哪项主张为该妇女提供的支持最少？","rule":"第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的国家公民权来歧视他们。","analysis":"特权和豁免条款\n\n规定的适用性\n\n第四条\n• 对其他国家公民的歧视\n• 干涉国家公民权 第十四修正案\n• 干涉公民权\n\n第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的国家公民权来歧视他们。但该条款在这里并不适用，因为 (1) 该妇女是解雇她所在州的居民（即公民），并且 (2) 公共就业不是该州公民的权利。因此，该条款为提起诉讼的女性恢复就业提供了最少的支持。 （选项 A）该州解雇该妇女只是因为她通知联邦官员该州没有遵守联邦规定。因此，这次解雇可能违宪地剥夺了她作为公职人员的言论自由权。 （选项C）当联邦法律优先于该条款时，例如，当国家行为（state action）干扰联邦目标的实现时，第六条至上条款使国家行为（state action）无效。在这里，该州因通知联邦官员该州没有遵守联邦规则而解雇该妇女，这可能会干扰这些规则的执行，从而违反了至上条款。 （选项 D）第十四修正案的程序正当程序权要求各州在剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产时给予其通知和有意义的表达意见的机会。而只能因故被解雇的公职人员——比如这位女性——在他们的工作中拥有财产权益。因此，在没有给她听证机会的情况下解雇她是违宪的，违反了程序上的正当程序。\n\n教育目标：第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的国家公民权利来歧视他们。\n\n参考文献\n• Paul v. Virginia, 75 U.S. 168, 180 (1869)（认为第四条特权和豁免条款仅适用于一个州歧视其他州公民的情况）。","conclusion":"答案 B：国家违宪地解雇了她，剥夺了她受第四条保护的国家公民特权或豁免权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":148,"sourceQuestionPage":442,"sourceQuestionPages":[442],"sourceExplanationPages":[443,444],"sourceAnswerPages":[443,444],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-149","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A generally applicable state statute requires an autopsy by the county coroner in all cases of death that are not obviously of natural causes. The purpose of this law is to ensure the discovery and prosecution of all illegal activity resulting in death. In the 50 years since its enactment, the statute has been consistently enforced. A married couple are sincere practicing members of a religion that maintains it is essential for a deceased person's body to be buried promptly and without any invasive procedures, including an autopsy. When the couple's son died of mysterious causes and an autopsy was scheduled, they filed an action in state court challenging the constitutionality of the state statute and seeking an injunction prohibiting the county coroner from performing an autopsy on their son's body. In this action, the couple claimed only that applying this statute in the circumstances of their son's death would violate their right to the free exercise of religion as guaranteed by the First and Fourteenth Amendments. Assume that no federal statutes are applicable. Is the state's autopsy statute constitutional as applied to the couple?","choices":{"A":"No, because it is not necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest.","B":"No, because it is not substantially related to an important state interest.","C":"Yes, because a dead individual is not a person protected by the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"Yes, because it is a generally applicable statute and is rationally related to a legitimate state purpose."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment, applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, protects an individual's right to freely exercise his/her sincerely held religious beliefs. Any government infringement on this right must satisfy the appropriate level of scrutiny, which depends on the type of burden imposed:\n• direct (intentionally targets religion) – the burden is presumptively invalid and the state must prove that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny)\n\n• incidental (generally applies to all) – the burden is presumptively valid and the challenger must prove that the burden is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest (ie, rational basis scrutiny)\n\nHere, the couple's sincere religious belief that bodies must be buried promptly and without any invasive procedures was burdened when the county coroner scheduled an autopsy of their son's body. But since a generally applicable state statute requires autopsies for all mysterious deaths like his, that burden is merely incidental. And since autopsies for suspicious deaths are rationally related to a legitimate state purpose—discovering and prosecuting illegal activity resulting in death—the statute is constitutional as applied to the couple. (Choice A) Had the state intentionally targeted the couple's religion, the state would have needed to prove that the statute was necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny). (Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause, laws that impact a quasi-suspect class must be substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny). But religion is not a quasi-suspect class, so this standard does not apply. (Choice C) A dead individual is not protected by the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. But the couple's challenge of the statute is based on their own constitutional rights—not those of their dead son.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise one's sincerely held religious beliefs. Direct burdens (which target religion) are almost always unconstitutional under strict scrutiny. But incidental burdens (which apply to all) are usually constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.\n\nReferences\n• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 885 (1990) (holding that a generally applicable regulation on conduct need not satisfy strict scrutiny to comply with the First Amendment free exercise clause).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise one's sincerely held religious beliefs. Direct burdens (which target religion) are almost always unconstitutional under strict scrutiny. But incidental burdens (which apply to all) are usually constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.","references":"• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 885 (1990) (holding that a generally applicable regulation on conduct need not satisfy strict scrutiny to comply with the First Amendment free exercise clause).","rule":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise one's sincerely held religious beliefs. Direct burdens (which target religion) are almost always unconstitutional under strict scrutiny. But incidental burdens (which apply to all) are usually constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state's autopsy statute constitutional as applied to the couple?","rule":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise one's sincerely held religious beliefs. Direct burdens (which target religion) are almost always unconstitutional under strict scrutiny. But incidental burdens (which apply to all) are usually constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.","analysis":"The First Amendment, applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, protects an individual's right to freely exercise his/her sincerely held religious beliefs. Any government infringement on this right must satisfy the appropriate level of scrutiny, which depends on the type of burden imposed:\n• direct (intentionally targets religion) – the burden is presumptively invalid and the state must prove that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny)\n\n• incidental (generally applies to all) – the burden is presumptively valid and the challenger must prove that the burden is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest (ie, rational basis scrutiny)\n\nHere, the couple's sincere religious belief that bodies must be buried promptly and without any invasive procedures was burdened when the county coroner scheduled an autopsy of their son's body. But since a generally applicable state statute requires autopsies for all mysterious deaths like his, that burden is merely incidental. And since autopsies for suspicious deaths are rationally related to a legitimate state purpose—discovering and prosecuting illegal activity resulting in death—the statute is constitutional as applied to the couple. (Choice A) Had the state intentionally targeted the couple's religion, the state would have needed to prove that the statute was necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny). (Choice B) Under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause, laws that impact a quasi-suspect class must be substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny). But religion is not a quasi-suspect class, so this standard does not apply. (Choice C) A dead individual is not protected by the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. But the couple's challenge of the statute is based on their own constitutional rights—not those of their dead son.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because it is a generally applicable statute and is rationally related to a legitimate state purpose."},"questionZh":"普遍适用的州法规要求县验尸官对所有非明显自然原因的死亡案件进行尸检。该法的目的是确保发现并起诉所有导致死亡的非法活动。该法规颁布50年来，一直得到贯彻执行。一对已婚夫妇是虔诚的宗教信徒，该宗教认为死者的尸体必须立即下葬，并且不进行任何侵入性程序，包括尸检。当这对夫妇的儿子死于神秘原因并安排尸检时，他们向州法院提起诉讼，质疑州法规的合宪性，并寻求禁令，禁止县验尸官对儿子的尸体进行尸检。在该诉讼中，这对夫妇仅声称，在他们儿子死亡的情况下适用该法规将侵犯第一修正案和第十四修正案所保障的他们自由行使宗教的权利。假设没有适用的联邦法规。适用于这对夫妇的州尸检法规是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为没有必要维护令人信服的国家利益。","B":"不会，因为它与重要的国家利益没有实质性关系。","C":"是的，因为死者不是受第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护的人。","D":"是的，因为它是一项普遍适用的法规，并且与合法的国家目的合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州，保护个人自由行使他/她真诚持有的宗教信仰的权利。政府对这一权利的任何侵犯都必须满足适当程度的审查，这取决于施加的负担类型：\n• 直接（故意针对宗教）——该负担被推定为无效，国家必须证明该负担对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））\n\n• 附带的（一般适用于所有人）——该负担被推定为有效，并且挑战者必须证明该负担与任何合法的国家利益不合理相关（即合理基础审查）\n\n在这里，当县验尸官安排对他们儿子的尸体进行尸检时，这对夫妇真诚的宗教信仰（即必须立即埋葬尸体且不进行任何侵入性程序）受到了影响。但由于普遍适用的州法规要求对所有像他这样的神秘死亡事件进行尸检，因此这种负担只是偶然的。由于对可疑死亡的尸检与合法的国家目的（发现和起诉导致死亡的非法活动）合理相关，因此适用于这对夫妇的法规是符合宪法的。 （选择A）如果国家故意针对这对夫妇的宗教信仰，国家将需要证明该法规对于维护令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。 （选项B）根据第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），影响准嫌疑阶层的法律必须与重要的国家利益实质相关（即中间审查）。但宗教并不是准嫌疑阶级，所以这个标准不适用。 （选项 C）死亡个人不受第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）的保护。但这对夫妇对该法规的质疑是基于他们自己的宪法权利，而不是他们死去儿子的权利。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担（针对宗教）几乎总是违宪的。但附带负担（适用于所有人）通常在合理基础的审查下是符合宪法的。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 885 (1990)（认为普遍适用的行为法规无需经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）即可遵守第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担（针对宗教）几乎总是违宪的。但附带负担（适用于所有人）通常在合理基础的审查下是符合宪法的。","iracZh":{"issue":"适用于这对夫妇的州尸检法规是否符合宪法？","rule":"第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担（针对宗教）几乎总是违宪的。但附带负担（适用于所有人）通常在合理基础的审查下是符合宪法的。","analysis":"第一修正案通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州，保护个人自由行使他/她真诚持有的宗教信仰的权利。政府对这一权利的任何侵犯都必须满足适当程度的审查，这取决于施加的负担类型：\n• 直接（故意针对宗教）——该负担被推定为无效，国家必须证明该负担对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））\n\n• 附带的（一般适用于所有人）——该负担被推定为有效，并且挑战者必须证明该负担与任何合法的国家利益不合理相关（即合理基础审查）\n\n在这里，当县验尸官安排对他们儿子的尸体进行尸检时，这对夫妇真诚的宗教信仰（即必须立即埋葬尸体且不进行任何侵入性程序）受到了影响。但由于普遍适用的州法规要求对所有像他这样的神秘死亡事件进行尸检，因此这种负担只是偶然的。由于对可疑死亡的尸检与合法的国家目的（发现和起诉导致死亡的非法活动）合理相关，因此适用于这对夫妇的法规是符合宪法的。 （选择A）如果国家故意针对这对夫妇的宗教信仰，国家将需要证明该法规对于维护令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。 （选项B）根据第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），影响准嫌疑阶层的法律必须与重要的国家利益实质相关（即中间审查）。但宗教并不是准嫌疑阶级，所以这个标准不适用。 （选项 C）死亡个人不受第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）的保护。但这对夫妇对该法规的质疑是基于他们自己的宪法权利，而不是他们死去儿子的权利。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，直接负担（针对宗教）几乎总是违宪的。但附带负担（适用于所有人）通常在合理基础的审查下是符合宪法的。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 885 (1990)（认为普遍适用的行为法规无需经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）即可遵守第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为它是一项普遍适用的法规，并且与合法的国家目的合理相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-713dc2d0de798834"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":149,"sourceQuestionPage":445,"sourceQuestionPages":[445],"sourceExplanationPages":[446,447],"sourceAnswerPages":[446,447],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-150","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state legislature enacted a statute to provide parental and student choice in education. The statute established a program that allocates taxpayer funds to private organizations that award scholarships to students for tuition at private or charter schools within the state. However, the statute expressly prohibits scholarship recipients from using the funds at any school controlled by a church, sect, or denomination. A group of parents whose children were awarded tuition assistance through the program sought to use the funds at a private, religious school. The state denied the parents' request pursuant to the statutory provision. The parents have filed suit in federal district court to obtain an injunction, challenging the constitutionality of the provision. Is the court likely to uphold the provision?","choices":{"A":"No, because the provision is an unconstitutional establishment of religion.","B":"No, because the provision unconstitutionally restricts the parents' right to freely exercise their religious beliefs.","C":"Yes, because the parents do not have standing.","D":"Yes, because the Tenth Amendment gives the state legislature plenary power to appropriate state funds in the manner that it deems most conducive to the welfare of its people."},"answer":"B","explanation":"[Current-law confirmation — answer unchanged] Espinoza, Carson, Mahmoud, and Catholic Charities reinforce that government may not exclude or classify religious participants because of their religious status or character absent a constitutionally sufficient justification.\n\nThe First Amendment free exercise clause bars governments from enacting laws or policies that impose burdens based on an individual's religious beliefs or conduct. The level of scrutiny applied to such laws and policies depends on whether that burden was:\n• direct (intentional) – triggering strict scrutiny, which invalidates a law unless the government proves that it is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• incidental (generally applicable) – triggering rational basis scrutiny, which upholds a law unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.\n\nHere, the statutory provision directly burdens religion because it intentionally prohibits scholarship recipients from using the funds at schools controlled by a church, sect, or denomination. Since the state has the nearly impossible task of satisfying strict scrutiny, this provision unconstitutionally restricts the parents' right to freely exercise their religious beliefs. As a result, the court is unlikely to uphold the provision. (Choice A) The First Amendment establishment clause prohibits the government from expressing a religious preference or otherwise violating the separation of church and state. The statutory provision does not violate the establishment clause because it applies equally to all religious schools and promotes the separation of church and state. (Choice C) The parents have standing since their right to direct the religious upbringing of their children was intentionally burdened (injury-in-fact) by the statutory provision (causation) and an injunction will provide relief (redressability). (Choice D) Although the Tenth Amendment gives states the power to establish laws for the welfare, health, and safety of their people, that power is not plenary (ie, absolute). Instead, the state must comply with the other provisions of the U.S. Constitution (eg, free exercise clause) when exercising this power.\n\nEducational objective: Direct burdens on the free exercise of religion are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990) (establishing the direct/incidental burden test for the free exercise clause).\n\n• Espinoza v. Mont. Dep't of Revenue, 140 S. Ct. 2246, 2261 (2020) (holding that a state cannot prohibit state-sponsored scholarships from being used at religious schools).","educationalObjective":"Direct burdens on the free exercise of religion are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990) (establishing the direct/incidental burden test for the free exercise clause).\n\n• Espinoza v. Mont. Dep't of Revenue, 140 S. Ct. 2246, 2261 (2020) (holding that a state cannot prohibit state-sponsored scholarships from being used at religious schools).","rule":"Direct burdens on the free exercise of religion are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to uphold the provision?","rule":"Direct burdens on the free exercise of religion are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the burden is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment free exercise clause bars governments from enacting laws or policies that impose burdens based on an individual's religious beliefs or conduct. The level of scrutiny applied to such laws and policies depends on whether that burden was:\n• direct (intentional) – triggering strict scrutiny, which invalidates a law unless the government proves that it is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• incidental (generally applicable) – triggering rational basis scrutiny, which upholds a law unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.\n\nHere, the statutory provision directly burdens religion because it intentionally prohibits scholarship recipients from using the funds at schools controlled by a church, sect, or denomination. Since the state has the nearly impossible task of satisfying strict scrutiny, this provision unconstitutionally restricts the parents' right to freely exercise their religious beliefs. As a result, the court is unlikely to uphold the provision. (Choice A) The First Amendment establishment clause prohibits the government from expressing a religious preference or otherwise violating the separation of church and state. The statutory provision does not violate the establishment clause because it applies equally to all religious schools and promotes the separation of church and state. (Choice C) The parents have standing since their right to direct the religious upbringing of their children was intentionally burdened (injury-in-fact) by the statutory provision (causation) and an injunction will provide relief (redressability). (Choice D) Although the Tenth Amendment gives states the power to establish laws for the welfare, health, and safety of their people, that power is not plenary (ie, absolute). Instead, the state must comply with the other provisions of the U.S. Constitution (eg, free exercise clause) when exercising this power.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the provision unconstitutionally restricts the parents' right to freely exercise their religious beliefs."},"questionZh":"州立法机关颁布了一项法规，为家长和学生提供教育选择。该法规制定了一项计划，将纳税人的资金分配给私人组织，这些组织向州内私立或特许学校的学生提供奖学金。然而，该法规明确禁止奖学金获得者在任何由教会、教派或教派控制的学校使用这些资金。一群孩子通过该计划获得学费援助的家长试图将这些资金用于一所私立宗教学校。国家根据法律规定驳回了父母的请求。父母已向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，要求获得禁令，质疑该条款的合宪性。法院有可能维持该条款吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该规定违反宪法建立宗教。","B":"不可以，因为该条款违宪地限制了父母自由行使宗教信仰的权利。","C":"是的，因为父母没有地位。","D":"是的，因为第十修正案赋予州立法机关以它认为最有利于人民福祉的方式拨款国家资金的全权。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法确认——答案不变】Espinoza、Carson、Mahmoud 与 Catholic Charities 进一步确认，政府原则上不得仅因参与者的宗教身份或性质而将其排除或区别分类。\n\n第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）禁止政府制定基于个人宗教信仰或行为施加负担的法律或政策。适用于此类法律和政策的审查程度取决于该负担是否：\n• 直接（有意）——引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这会使法律无效，除非政府证明有必要实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 偶然（普遍适用）——引发合理基础审查，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系，否则该法律将得到维护。\n\n在这里，法律规定直接给宗教带来负担，因为它故意禁止奖学金获得者在教会、教派或教派控制的学校使用资金。由于国家承担着几乎不可能完成的严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）任务，这一规定违宪地限制了父母自由行使宗教信仰的权利。因此，法院不太可能支持该条款。 （选项 A） 第一修正案的设立条款禁止政府表达宗教偏好或以其他方式违反政教分离。该法律规定并不违反建制条款，因为它平等地适用于所有宗教学校，并促进政教分离。 （选项C）父母有资格，因为他们指导子女宗教教养的权利受到法定条款（因果关系）的故意负担（事实上的伤害），而禁令将提供救济（可补救性）。 （选项D） 虽然第十修正案赋予各州为其人民的福利、健康和安全制定法律的权力，但该权力不是全权（即绝对）。相反，州在行使这一权力时必须遵守美国宪法的其他条款（例如宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause））。\n\n教育目标：对宗教自由行使的直接负担在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是无效的，这要求政府证明这种负担对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990)（为宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）建立直接/附带负担测试）。\n\n• 埃斯皮诺萨诉蒙。税务部，140 S. Ct. 2246, 2261 (2020)（认为国家不能禁止宗教学校使用国家资助的奖学金）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，对宗教自由行使的直接负担几乎总是无效的，这需要政府证明这种负担对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院有可能维持该条款吗？","rule":"在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，对宗教自由行使的直接负担几乎总是无效的，这需要政府证明这种负担对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","analysis":"第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）禁止政府制定基于个人宗教信仰或行为施加负担的法律或政策。适用于此类法律和政策的审查程度取决于该负担是否：\n• 直接（有意）——引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这会使法律无效，除非政府证明有必要实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 偶然（普遍适用）——引发合理基础审查，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系，否则该法律将得到维护。\n\n在这里，法律规定直接给宗教带来负担，因为它故意禁止奖学金获得者在教会、教派或教派控制的学校使用资金。由于国家承担着几乎不可能完成的严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）任务，这一规定违宪地限制了父母自由行使宗教信仰的权利。因此，法院不太可能支持该条款。 （选项 A） 第一修正案的设立条款禁止政府表达宗教偏好或以其他方式违反政教分离。该法律规定并不违反建制条款，因为它平等地适用于所有宗教学校，并促进政教分离。 （选项C）父母有资格，因为他们指导子女宗教教养的权利受到法定条款（因果关系）的故意负担（事实上的伤害），而禁令将提供救济（可补救性）。 （选项D） 虽然第十修正案赋予各州为其人民的福利、健康和安全制定法律的权力，但该权力不是全权（即绝对）。相反，州在行使这一权力时必须遵守美国宪法的其他条款（例如宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause））。\n\n教育目标：对宗教自由行使的直接负担在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是无效的，这要求政府证明这种负担对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–79 (1990)（为宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）建立直接/附带负担测试）。\n\n• 埃斯皮诺萨诉蒙。税务部，140 S. Ct. 2246, 2261 (2020)（认为国家不能禁止宗教学校使用国家资助的奖学金）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为该条款违宪地限制了父母自由行使宗教信仰的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-through-2026","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已结合 2025 年 Mahmoud 与 Catholic Charities 复核；原答案 B 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Mahmoud v. Taylor, 606 U.S. ___ (2025)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/24pdf/24-297_4f14.pdf"},{"title":"Catholic Charities Bureau v. Wisconsin LIRC, 605 U.S. 238 (2025)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/24pdf/24-154_2b82.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"denomination","ipa":"/dɪnˌɔmʌnˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"de·nom·i·na·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 名称, 教派, 面额；[经] 票面金额, 名称, (度量衡)","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 名称, 教派, 面额；[经] 票面金额, 名称, (度量衡)","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:denominations","sourceSentence":"However, the statute expressly prohibits scholarship recipients from using the funds at any school controlled by a church, sect, or denomination.","synonyms":["appellation","designation","appellative"]},{"term":"classify","ipa":"/klˈæsʌfˌaɪ/","syllables":"clas·sify","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 分类, 归类, 分等；[建] 分类, 分级, 分粒","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：vt. 分类, 归类, 分等；[建] 分类, 分级, 分粒","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:classified/i:classifying/3:classifies/p:classified","sourceSentence":"[Current-law confirmation — answer unchanged] Espinoza, Carson, Mahmoud, and Catholic Charities reinforce that government may not exclude or classify religious participants because of their religious status or character absent a constitutionally sufficient justification.","synonyms":["class","sort","assort","sort out"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-713dc2d0de798834"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":150,"sourceQuestionPage":448,"sourceQuestionPages":[448],"sourceExplanationPages":[449,450],"sourceAnswerPages":[449,450],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-151","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"An unconstrued state law prohibited the distribution within the state of \"seditious propaganda.\" The state prosecuted United States Post Office letter carriers under this law for delivering propaganda from a foreign country to state residents. Which of the following statements is an INACCURATE description of the state's law as applied to the letter carriers?","choices":{"A":"It is an unconstitutional bill of attainder.","B":"It is void for vagueness.","C":"It may not be applied to the letter carriers, because they are employees of a federal instrumentality carrying out an authorized function.","D":"It unconstitutionally abridges rights protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Prohibited legislation\n\nBill of attainder Prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment against clauses identified persons (or groups of persons) without a trial\n\nEx post facto Prohibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that: clauses\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof The Constitution bars the federal and state governments from enacting bills of attainder— ie, legislative acts that punish specified persons (or an easily identifiable group of persons) without a judicial trial. Here, the state law prohibits the distribution of seditious propaganda by all persons—not just specified letter carriers. And since the state prosecuted the letter carriers under this law, it afforded them a trial prior to imposing any punishment. Therefore, the law is not an unconstitutional bill of attainder as applied to the letter carriers. (Choice B) A law is void for vagueness if it fails to provide persons of ordinary intelligence with fair and reasonable notice of the prohibited conduct. Here, the law is unconstitutionally vague since it does not define \"seditious propaganda\" and the average person would not know what this term means. (Choice C) States cannot regulate the federal government unless Congress consents. As a result, federal employees are immune from state laws that interfere with their obligation to carry out authorized functions of their employment. And since U.S. Post Office letter carriers are authorized by federal law to deliver mail, they would be immune from the state law prohibiting the distribution of seditious propaganda. (Choice D) The First Amendment—applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment—protects individuals' right of expression, including the distribution of seditious propaganda. Seditious speech is unprotected and subject to punishment only when it is directed to incite imminent lawlessness and likely will do so. But since the state law prohibits the distribution of all seditious propaganda, it unconstitutionally abridges free expression.\n\nEducational objective: Bills of attainder are constitutionally prohibited legislative acts that punish specified persons—or an easily identifiable group of persons—without a trial. Therefore, laws that applies to all persons are not bills of attainder.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 10 (prohibits states from enacting bills of attainder).\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315 (1946) (explaining that the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder).","educationalObjective":"Bills of attainder are constitutionally prohibited legislative acts that punish specified persons—or an easily identifiable group of persons—without a trial. Therefore, laws that applies to all persons are not bills of attainder.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 10 (prohibits states from enacting bills of attainder).\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315 (1946) (explaining that the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder).","rule":"Bills of attainder are constitutionally prohibited legislative acts that punish specified persons—or an easily identifiable group of persons—without a trial. Therefore, laws that applies to all persons are not bills of attainder.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements is an INACCURATE description of the state's law as applied to the letter carriers?","rule":"Bills of attainder are constitutionally prohibited legislative acts that punish specified persons—or an easily identifiable group of persons—without a trial. Therefore, laws that applies to all persons are not bills of attainder.","analysis":"Prohibited legislation\n\nBill of attainder Prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment against clauses identified persons (or groups of persons) without a trial\n\nEx post facto Prohibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that: clauses\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof The Constitution bars the federal and state governments from enacting bills of attainder— ie, legislative acts that punish specified persons (or an easily identifiable group of persons) without a judicial trial. Here, the state law prohibits the distribution of seditious propaganda by all persons—not just specified letter carriers. And since the state prosecuted the letter carriers under this law, it afforded them a trial prior to imposing any punishment. Therefore, the law is not an unconstitutional bill of attainder as applied to the letter carriers. (Choice B) A law is void for vagueness if it fails to provide persons of ordinary intelligence with fair and reasonable notice of the prohibited conduct. Here, the law is unconstitutionally vague since it does not define \"seditious propaganda\" and the average person would not know what this term means. (Choice C) States cannot regulate the federal government unless Congress consents. As a result, federal employees are immune from state laws that interfere with their obligation to carry out authorized functions of their employment. And since U.S. Post Office letter carriers are authorized by federal law to deliver mail, they would be immune from the state law prohibiting the distribution of seditious propaganda. (Choice D) The First Amendment—applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment—protects individuals' right of expression, including the distribution of seditious propaganda. Seditious speech is unprotected and subject to punishment only when it is directed to incite imminent lawlessness and likely will do so. But since the state law prohibits the distribution of all seditious propaganda, it unconstitutionally abridges free expression.","conclusion":"Answer A: It is an unconstitutional bill of attainder."},"questionZh":"未经解释的州法律禁止在州内传播“煽动性宣传”。该州根据该法律起诉美国​​邮局信件承运人从外国向该州居民传递宣传品。以下哪项对适用于信件承运人的州法律的描述不准确？","choicesZh":{"A":"这是一项违宪的剥夺公民权法案。","B":"它因含糊不清而无效。","C":"它可能不适用于邮递员，因为他们是执行授权职能的联邦机构的雇员。","D":"它违宪地剥夺了第一修正案和第十四修正案保护的权利。"},"explanationZh":"禁止的立法\n\n剥夺公民权利法案 禁止在未经审判的情况下对特定个人（或群体）施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为\n\n事后禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法： 条款\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 宪法禁止联邦和州政府颁布剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判而惩罚特定人员（或易于识别的群体）的立法行为。在这里，州法律禁止所有人（而不仅仅是特定的邮递员）散布煽动性宣传品。由于国家根据该法律起诉信件携带者，因此在实施任何处罚之前都会对他们进行审判。因此，该法律并不是适用于邮递员的违宪剥夺公民权法案。 （选项 B）如果法律未能向普通智力人员提供有关禁止行为的公平合理的通知，则该法律因含糊不清而无效。在这里，法律是违宪的模糊的，因为它没有定义“煽动性宣传”，普通人不知道这个词的含义。 （选项 C）除非国会同意，否则各州不能监管联邦政府。因此，联邦雇员不受州法律的影响，这些法律干扰他们履行授权的就业职能的义务。由于美国邮局信件承运人经联邦法律授权可以投递邮件，因此他们将不受禁止散布煽动性宣传品的州法律的约束。 （选项 D） 第一修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）保护个人的言论权，包括散布煽动性宣传的权利。煽动性言论不受保护，只有在煽动迫在眉睫的违法行为并且很可能会这样做时才会受到惩罚。但由于州法律禁止传播所有煽动性宣传材料，它违宪地限制了言论自由。\n\n教育目标：剥夺公民权利法案是宪法禁止的立法行为，未经审判即惩罚特定的人或易于识别的群体。因此，适用于所有人的法律不是剥夺公民权法案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 10（禁止各州颁布剥夺公民权法案）。\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315 (1946)（解释宪法禁止剥夺公民权法案）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"剥夺公民权法案是宪法禁止的立法行为，未经审判即惩罚特定的人或易于识别的群体。因此，适用于所有人的法律不是剥夺公民权法案。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项对适用于信件承运人的州法律的描述不准确？","rule":"剥夺公民权法案是宪法禁止的立法行为，未经审判即惩罚特定的人或易于识别的群体。因此，适用于所有人的法律不是剥夺公民权法案。","analysis":"禁止的立法\n\n剥夺公民权利法案 禁止在未经审判的情况下对特定个人（或群体）施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为\n\n事后禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法： 条款\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 宪法禁止联邦和州政府颁布剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判而惩罚特定人员（或易于识别的群体）的立法行为。在这里，州法律禁止所有人（而不仅仅是特定的邮递员）散布煽动性宣传品。由于国家根据该法律起诉信件携带者，因此在实施任何处罚之前都会对他们进行审判。因此，该法律并不是适用于邮递员的违宪剥夺公民权法案。 （选项 B）如果法律未能向普通智力人员提供有关禁止行为的公平合理的通知，则该法律因含糊不清而无效。在这里，法律是违宪的模糊的，因为它没有定义“煽动性宣传”，普通人不知道这个词的含义。 （选项 C）除非国会同意，否则各州不能监管联邦政府。因此，联邦雇员不受州法律的影响，这些法律干扰他们履行授权的就业职能的义务。由于美国邮局信件承运人经联邦法律授权可以投递邮件，因此他们将不受禁止散布煽动性宣传品的州法律的约束。 （选项 D） 第一修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）保护个人的言论权，包括散布煽动性宣传的权利。煽动性言论不受保护，只有在煽动迫在眉睫的违法行为并且很可能会这样做时才会受到惩罚。但由于州法律禁止传播所有煽动性宣传材料，它违宪地限制了言论自由。\n\n教育目标：剥夺公民权利法案是宪法禁止的立法行为，未经审判即惩罚特定的人或易于识别的群体。因此，适用于所有人的法律不是剥夺公民权法案。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 10（禁止各州颁布剥夺公民权法案）。\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315 (1946)（解释宪法禁止剥夺公民权法案）。","conclusion":"答案 A：这是一项违宪的剥夺公民权法案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"vagueness","ipa":"/vˈeɪɡnɪs/","syllables":"vague·ness","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 含糊, 暧昧","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 含糊, 暧昧","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"It is void for vagueness.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":151,"sourceQuestionPage":451,"sourceQuestionPages":[451],"sourceExplanationPages":[452,453],"sourceAnswerPages":[452,453],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-152","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Second Amendment","question":"A city ordinance requires residents to keep firearms unloaded and disassembled while the firearms are located in their homes. The ordinance also requires residents to keep the disassembled firearm parts and any ammunition in separate safes. The purpose of the ordinance is to deter gun ownership by making it inconvenient to store firearms. A resident of the city who owns and keeps firearms in her home for self-defense has filed suit against the city in a federal district court, arguing that the ordinance is unconstitutional. Is the ordinance constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the ordinance interferes with the right to use firearms for self-defense in one's home.","B":"No, because the Second Amendment guarantees the absolute right to possess and use firearms.","C":"Yes, because the ordinance is rationally related to a legitimate government purpose.","D":"Yes, because the Second Amendment does not apply to municipal actions."},"answer":"A","explanation":"[Current-law update — answer unchanged] Bruen, Rahimi, Wolford, and Hemani use a text-and-history framework rather than means-end scrutiny. A rule that disables every home firearm for the stated purpose of deterring ownership directly contradicts Heller's core self-defense holding.\n\nThe Second Amendment generally prohibits government interference with an individual's right to possess and use firearms for a traditionally lawful purpose (eg, self-defense in one's home). This right is applicable to states and municipalities (eg, cities) through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause (Choice D).* However, this right is not absolute (Choice B). Instead, SCOTUS has stated that certain government restrictions on firearms are permissible, including:\n• banning unusually dangerous firearms (eg, grenades)\n• imposing conditions and qualifications on commercial sales of firearms\n• forbidding the possession of firearms by felons and mentally ill individuals and\n• prohibiting the open carry of firearms in certain areas (eg, schools) or concealed carry of firearms in public. Here, a city ordinance requires residents to keep firearms unloaded and disassembled while the firearms are in their homes. This restriction makes it virtually impossible for residents to use firearms for the traditionally lawful purpose of self-defense in their homes. That is because residents must reassemble and reload the firearm to use it, which means that residents would not be able to immediately defend themselves if needed. For that reason, and because the ordinance does not fall within the permitted government restrictions, the ordinance is unconstitutional. *Every constitutional right applies to states and municipalities except (1) the Third Amendment ban on quartering soldiers in homes, (2) the Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment for capital charges, and (3) the Seventh Amendment guarantee of a civil jury trial. (Choice C) SCOTUS has never determined whether rational basis or strict scrutiny review applies to restrictions on the Second Amendment right to bear arms. Instead, such restrictions must not interfere with a traditionally lawful purpose for possessing and using firearms.\n\nEducational objective: The Second Amendment guarantees individuals the right to possess and use firearms for traditionally lawful purposes like self-defense in the home. However, this right is not absolute, so certain government restrictions are permissible—eg, banning certain types of firearms, prohibiting possession of firearms in certain places. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nReferences\n• District of Columbia v. Heller, 554 U.S. 570, 635 (2008) (holding that the Second Amendment protects an individual's right to possess and use firearms for the traditionally lawful purpose of self-defense in the home).\n\n• McDonald v. City of Chicago, 561 U.S. 742, 791 (2010) (holding that the Second Amendment is applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause).","educationalObjective":"When the Second Amendment text covers the regulated conduct, the conduct is presumptively protected and the government must show that its restriction is consistent with the Nation's historical tradition of firearm regulation. The right centrally protects keeping an operable firearm in the home for self-defense.","references":"• District of Columbia v. Heller, 554 U.S. 570, 635 (2008) (holding that the Second Amendment protects an individual's right to possess and use firearms for the traditionally lawful purpose of self-defense in the home).\n\n• McDonald v. City of Chicago, 561 U.S. 742, 791 (2010) (holding that the Second Amendment is applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause).","rule":"When the Second Amendment text covers the regulated conduct, the conduct is presumptively protected and the government must show that its restriction is consistent with the Nation's historical tradition of firearm regulation. The right centrally protects keeping an operable firearm in the home for self-defense.","irac":{"issue":"Is the ordinance constitutional?","rule":"When the Second Amendment text covers the regulated conduct, the conduct is presumptively protected and the government must show that its restriction is consistent with the Nation's historical tradition of firearm regulation. The right centrally protects keeping an operable firearm in the home for self-defense.","analysis":"The Second Amendment generally prohibits government interference with an individual's right to possess and use firearms for a traditionally lawful purpose (eg, self-defense in one's home). This right is applicable to states and municipalities (eg, cities) through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause (Choice D).* However, this right is not absolute (Choice B). Instead, SCOTUS has stated that certain government restrictions on firearms are permissible, including:\n• banning unusually dangerous firearms (eg, grenades)\n• imposing conditions and qualifications on commercial sales of firearms\n• forbidding the possession of firearms by felons and mentally ill individuals and\n• prohibiting the open carry of firearms in certain areas (eg, schools) or concealed carry of firearms in public. Here, a city ordinance requires residents to keep firearms unloaded and disassembled while the firearms are in their homes. This restriction makes it virtually impossible for residents to use firearms for the traditionally lawful purpose of self-defense in their homes. That is because residents must reassemble and reload the firearm to use it, which means that residents would not be able to immediately defend themselves if needed. For that reason, and because the ordinance does not fall within the permitted government restrictions, the ordinance is unconstitutional. *Every constitutional right applies to states and municipalities except (1) the Third Amendment ban on quartering soldiers in homes, (2) the Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment for capital charges, and (3) the Seventh Amendment guarantee of a civil jury trial. (Choice C) SCOTUS has never determined whether rational basis or strict scrutiny review applies to restrictions on the Second Amendment right to bear arms. Instead, such restrictions must not interfere with a traditionally lawful purpose for possessing and using firearms.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the ordinance interferes with the right to use firearms for self-defense in one's home."},"questionZh":"一项城市法令要求居民在枪支在家中时将枪支卸下并拆卸。该法令还要求居民将拆卸的枪支零件和任何弹药存放在单独的保险箱中。该法令的目的是通过不方便储存枪支来阻止枪支拥有。该市一名居民在家中拥有并保留枪支用于自卫，已向联邦地区法院对该市提起诉讼，认为该法令违宪。该法令是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该法令干涉了在家中使用枪支进行自卫的权利。","B":"不，因为第二修正案保障拥有和使用枪支的绝对权利。","C":"是的，因为该条例与合法的政府目的合理相关。","D":"是的，因为第二修正案不适用于市政行为。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法更新——答案不变】Bruen、Rahimi、Wolford 与 Hemani 采用“文本与历史传统”框架，而非利益衡量审查。以阻止持枪为目的、使家中所有枪支均无法即时使用的规则，直接违反 Heller 对家庭自卫核心权利的认定。\n\n第二修正案一般禁止政府干涉个人出于传统合法目的（例如在家自卫）而拥有和使用枪支的权利。这项权利通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于州和直辖市（例如城市）（选择 D）。*但是，这项权利不是绝对的（选择 B）。相反，最高法院表示政府对枪支的某些限制是允许的，包括：\n• 禁止异常危险的枪支（例如手榴弹）\n• 对枪支商业销售施加条件和资格\n• 禁止重罪犯和精神病患者拥有枪支以及\n• 禁止在某些区域（例如学校）公开携带枪支或在公共场所隐蔽携带枪支。在这里，一项城市法令要求居民在枪支在家中时将枪支卸下并拆卸。这一限制使得居民几乎不可能在家中使用枪支来实现传统上合法的自卫目的。这是因为居民必须重新组装并重新装载枪支才能使用它，这意味着居民在需要时将无法立即自卫。因此，并且由于该法令不属于政府允许的限制范围，因此该法令违宪。 *每项宪法权利均适用于州和市，但 (1) 第三修正案禁止士兵在家中驻扎，(2) 第五修正案要求大陪审团对死刑指控提出起诉，以及 (3) 第七修正案保证民事陪审团审判。 （选项C） 最高法院从未确定对第二修正案携带武器权利的限制是否适用合理依据或严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。相反，此类限制不得妨碍拥有和使用枪支的传统合法目的。\n\n教育目标：第二修正案保障个人出于传统合法目的（例如家庭自卫）拥有和使用枪支的权利。然而，这项权利并不是绝对的，因此某些政府限制是允许的——例如，禁止某些类型的枪支、禁止在某些地方拥有枪支。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• District of Columbia v. Heller, 554 U.S. 570, 635 (2008)（认为第二修正案保护个人出于传统合法的家庭自卫目的而拥有和使用枪支的权利）。\n\n• McDonald 诉芝加哥市，561 U.S. 742, 791 (2010)（认为第二修正案通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当第二修正案文本涵盖被规制行为时，该行为推定受保护，政府须证明限制符合美国枪支规制的历史传统。该权利的核心包括在家中保有可操作枪支用于自卫。","iracZh":{"issue":"该法令是否符合宪法？","rule":"当第二修正案文本涵盖被规制行为时，该行为推定受保护，政府须证明限制符合美国枪支规制的历史传统。该权利的核心包括在家中保有可操作枪支用于自卫。","analysis":"第二修正案一般禁止政府干涉个人出于传统合法目的（例如在家自卫）而拥有和使用枪支的权利。这项权利通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于州和直辖市（例如城市）（选择 D）。*但是，这项权利不是绝对的（选择 B）。相反，最高法院表示政府对枪支的某些限制是允许的，包括：\n• 禁止异常危险的枪支（例如手榴弹）\n• 对枪支商业销售施加条件和资格\n• 禁止重罪犯和精神病患者拥有枪支以及\n• 禁止在某些区域（例如学校）公开携带枪支或在公共场所隐蔽携带枪支。在这里，一项城市法令要求居民在枪支在家中时将枪支卸下并拆卸。这一限制使得居民几乎不可能在家中使用枪支来实现传统上合法的自卫目的。这是因为居民必须重新组装并重新装载枪支才能使用它，这意味着居民在需要时将无法立即自卫。因此，并且由于该法令不属于政府允许的限制范围，因此该法令违宪。 *每项宪法权利均适用于州和市，但 (1) 第三修正案禁止士兵在家中驻扎，(2) 第五修正案要求大陪审团对死刑指控提出起诉，以及 (3) 第七修正案保证民事陪审团审判。 （选项C） 最高法院从未确定对第二修正案携带武器权利的限制是否适用合理依据或严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。相反，此类限制不得妨碍拥有和使用枪支的传统合法目的。\n\n教育目标：第二修正案保障个人出于传统合法目的（例如家庭自卫）拥有和使用枪支的权利。然而，这项权利并不是绝对的，因此某些政府限制是允许的——例如，禁止某些类型的枪支、禁止在某些地方拥有枪支。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• District of Columbia v. Heller, 554 U.S. 570, 635 (2008)（认为第二修正案保护个人出于传统合法的家庭自卫目的而拥有和使用枪支的权利）。\n\n• McDonald 诉芝加哥市，561 U.S. 742, 791 (2010)（认为第二修正案通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为该法令干涉了在家中使用枪支进行自卫的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-2026-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"以 Bruen—Rahimi—Wolford—Hemani 的文本与历史传统框架更新规则；原答案 A 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Wolford v. Lopez, 609 U.S. ___ (2026)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-1046_nmio.pdf"},{"title":"United States v. Hemani, 608 U.S. ___ (2026)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-1234_new_6k47.pdf"},{"title":"United States v. Rahimi, 602 U.S. 680 (2024)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/22-915_8o6b.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-a32eb870f26fcd34"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":152,"sourceQuestionPage":454,"sourceQuestionPages":[454],"sourceExplanationPages":[455,456],"sourceAnswerPages":[455,456],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-153","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Takings Clause","question":"A city filed eminent domain proceedings in order to obtain 40 beach houses fronting a particularly attractive stretch of shoreline. The city would pay the owners the fair market value of the properties. As part of an elaborate plan to increase the city's tourist trade and revive the local economy, the city planned to sell the beach houses to a company that would demolish the houses and build a luxury hotel in their place. The owners of the beach houses have challenged the city's exercise of eminent domain, contending only that the city's plan is unconstitutional. Will the owners of the beach houses be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a property owner can challenge an exercise of eminent domain only on the ground of the sufficiency of the compensation.","B":"No, because the planned sale to the private developer to increase the tourist trade qualifies as a public use.","C":"Yes, because a public entity cannot seize the property of one person in order to transfer that property intact to other private parties.","D":"Yes, because the city's action would deprive the owners of all economic use of their property."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The Fifth Amendment takings clause requires the government to pay just compensation (ie, fair market value) when exercising its power of eminent domain—ie, taking private property for public use. Public use is broadly defined as any use that is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose. Here, the city initiated eminent domain proceedings to seize 40 beachfront houses and plans to pay the owners the fair market value of their property. The city also plans to sell the houses to a company that will build a luxury hotel in their place. This planned sale qualifies as a public use because it is rationally related to the city's interest in increasing tourist trade and reviving the local economy. Therefore, the beach-house owners will likely fail. (Choice A) A property owner can challenge an exercise of eminent domain on two grounds: (1) that the compensation is insufficient and (2) that the taking is not for a public use. (Choice C) A public entity (eg, the city) can seize private property to transfer it—intact or otherwise—to other private parties if doing so would primarily benefit the public (eg, economic redevelopment). (Choice D) The government commits a regulatory taking when its actions deprive an owner of all economically beneficial uses of the property—even when the owner retains possession of that property. But any taking is constitutional when, as here, property is taken for public use and the owners receive just compensation.\n\nEducational objective: The takings clause requires the government to pay just compensation when it exercises its eminent domain powers by taking private property for public use. Public use encompasses any use that is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose (eg, economic redevelopment).\n\nReferences\n• Kelo v. City of New London, 545 U.S. 469, 488–89 (2005) (holding that a taking is for a public use when the government transfers private property to another private party as part of an economic redevelopment plan).\n\n• 26 Am. Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 44 (2019) (explaining that a taking is for a public use if it is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose).","educationalObjective":"The takings clause requires the government to pay just compensation when it exercises its eminent domain powers by taking private property for public use. Public use encompasses any use that is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose (eg, economic redevelopment).","references":"• Kelo v. City of New London, 545 U.S. 469, 488–89 (2005) (holding that a taking is for a public use when the government transfers private property to another private party as part of an economic redevelopment plan).\n\n• 26 Am. Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 44 (2019) (explaining that a taking is for a public use if it is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose).","rule":"The takings clause requires the government to pay just compensation when it exercises its eminent domain powers by taking private property for public use. Public use encompasses any use that is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose (eg, economic redevelopment).","irac":{"issue":"Will the owners of the beach houses be likely to prevail?","rule":"The takings clause requires the government to pay just compensation when it exercises its eminent domain powers by taking private property for public use. Public use encompasses any use that is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose (eg, economic redevelopment).","analysis":"The Fifth Amendment takings clause requires the government to pay just compensation (ie, fair market value) when exercising its power of eminent domain—ie, taking private property for public use. Public use is broadly defined as any use that is rationally related to a legitimate public purpose. Here, the city initiated eminent domain proceedings to seize 40 beachfront houses and plans to pay the owners the fair market value of their property. The city also plans to sell the houses to a company that will build a luxury hotel in their place. This planned sale qualifies as a public use because it is rationally related to the city's interest in increasing tourist trade and reviving the local economy. Therefore, the beach-house owners will likely fail. (Choice A) A property owner can challenge an exercise of eminent domain on two grounds: (1) that the compensation is insufficient and (2) that the taking is not for a public use. (Choice C) A public entity (eg, the city) can seize private property to transfer it—intact or otherwise—to other private parties if doing so would primarily benefit the public (eg, economic redevelopment). (Choice D) The government commits a regulatory taking when its actions deprive an owner of all economically beneficial uses of the property—even when the owner retains possession of that property. But any taking is constitutional when, as here, property is taken for public use and the owners receive just compensation.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the planned sale to the private developer to increase the tourist trade qualifies as a public use."},"questionZh":"某城市提起征用诉讼，以获得 40 栋面向特别有吸引力的海岸线的海滨别墅。市政府将向业主支付房产的公平市场价值。作为增加该市旅游业和振兴当地经济的精心计划的一部分，该市计划将海滨别墅出售给一家公司，该公司将拆除这些房屋并在其所在地建造一家豪华酒店。海滨别墅的业主对市政府征用权的行使提出质疑，仅认为该市的规划违宪。海滨别墅的业主会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为业主只能以补偿是否充足为由质疑征用权的行使。","B":"不，因为计划出售给私人开发商以增加旅游贸易符合公共用途的条件。","C":"是的，因为公共实体不能扣押一个人的财产，以便将该财产完好无损地转让给其他私人团体。","D":"是的，因为该市的行动将剥夺业主对其财产的所有经济用途。"},"explanationZh":"第五修正案征用条款要求政府在行使其征用权（即征用私有财产供公共使用）时支付公正的补偿（即公平的市场价值）。公共使用广义上定义为与合法公共目的合理相关的任何使用。在这里，该市启动了征用程序，没收了 40 栋海滨房屋，并计划向业主支付其房产的公平市场价值。该市还计划将这些房屋出售给一家公司，该公司将在其所在地建造一家豪华酒店。此次计划出售符合公共用途，因为它与该市增加旅游贸易和振兴当地经济的利益合理相关。因此，海滨别墅的业主很可能会失败。 （选择A）业主可以基于两个理由对征用权的行使提出质疑：（1）补偿不足；（2）征用不用于公共用途。 （选择C）如果这样做主要有利于公众（例如，经济重建），公共实体（例如，城市）可以扣押私有财产，并将其完整或以其他方式转让给其他私人团体。 （选项 D）当政府的行为剥夺了所有者对财产的所有经济利益使用时，即使所有者保留对该财产的占有，政府也会采取监管措施。但是，当财产被征用供公共使用并且所有者得到公正补偿时，任何征用都是符合宪法的。\n\n教育目标：征用条款要求政府在行使征用权、征用私有财产供公共使用时支付公正的补偿。公共用途包括与合法公共目的合理相关的任何用途（例如，经济重建）。\n\n参考文献\n• Kelo v. City of New London, 545 U.S. 469, 488-89 (2005)（认为当政府将私有财产转让给另一个私人方作为经济再开发计划的一部分时，征用属于公共用途）。\n\n• 上午26 点。尤尔。 2d 征用权 § 44 (2019)（解释说，如果一项征用与合法公共目的合理相关，则该征用属于公共用途）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"征用条款要求政府在行使征用权、征用私有财产供公共使用时支付公正的补偿。公共用途包括与合法公共目的合理相关的任何用途（例如，经济重建）。","iracZh":{"issue":"海滨别墅的业主会获胜吗？","rule":"征用条款要求政府在行使征用权、征用私有财产供公共使用时支付公正的补偿。公共用途包括与合法公共目的合理相关的任何用途（例如，经济重建）。","analysis":"第五修正案征用条款要求政府在行使其征用权（即征用私有财产供公共使用）时支付公正的补偿（即公平的市场价值）。公共使用广义上定义为与合法公共目的合理相关的任何使用。在这里，该市启动了征用程序，没收了 40 栋海滨房屋，并计划向业主支付其房产的公平市场价值。该市还计划将这些房屋出售给一家公司，该公司将在其所在地建造一家豪华酒店。此次计划出售符合公共用途，因为它与该市增加旅游贸易和振兴当地经济的利益合理相关。因此，海滨别墅的业主很可能会失败。 （选择A）业主可以基于两个理由对征用权的行使提出质疑：（1）补偿不足；（2）征用不用于公共用途。 （选择C）如果这样做主要有利于公众（例如，经济重建），公共实体（例如，城市）可以扣押私有财产，并将其完整或以其他方式转让给其他私人团体。 （选项 D）当政府的行为剥夺了所有者对财产的所有经济利益使用时，即使所有者保留对该财产的占有，政府也会采取监管措施。但是，当财产被征用供公共使用并且所有者得到公正补偿时，任何征用都是符合宪法的。\n\n教育目标：征用条款要求政府在行使征用权、征用私有财产供公共使用时支付公正的补偿。公共用途包括与合法公共目的合理相关的任何用途（例如，经济重建）。\n\n参考文献\n• Kelo v. City of New London, 545 U.S. 469, 488-89 (2005)（认为当政府将私有财产转让给另一个私人方作为经济再开发计划的一部分时，征用属于公共用途）。\n\n• 上午26 点。尤尔。 2d 征用权 § 44 (2019)（解释说，如果一项征用与合法公共目的合理相关，则该征用属于公共用途）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为计划出售给私人开发商以增加旅游贸易符合公共用途的条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"regulatory taking","ipa":"/ɹˈɛɡjʌlʌtˌɔɹi tˈeɪkɪŋ/","syllables":"reg·u·la·tory tak·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"管制性征收：规制虽未实际占有财产，却因影响过重而构成征收","contextualMeaning":"管制性征收：规制虽未实际占有财产，却因影响过重而构成征收","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) The government commits a regulatory taking when its actions deprive an owner of all economically beneficial uses of the property—even when the owner retains possession of that property.","synonyms":["regulatory pickings","regulatory fetching","regulatory winning"]},{"term":"eminent","ipa":"/ˈɛmənənt/","syllables":"em·i·nent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 显赫的, 杰出的, 有名的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 显赫的, 杰出的, 有名的","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"A city filed eminent domain proceedings in order to obtain 40 beach houses fronting a particularly attractive stretch of shoreline.","synonyms":["lofty","soaring","towering","high"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f56b1da57cb89266"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":153,"sourceQuestionPage":457,"sourceQuestionPages":[457],"sourceExplanationPages":[458,459],"sourceAnswerPages":[458,459],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-154","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A clerical employee of a city water department was responsible for sending out water bills to customers. His work in this respect had always been satisfactory. The employee's sister ran in a recent election against the incumbent mayor, but she lost. The employee had supported his sister in the election campaign. After the mayor found out about this, she fired the employee solely because his support for the sister indicated that he was \"disloyal\" to the mayor. The city's charter provides that \"all employees of the city work at the pleasure of the mayor.\" Is the mayor's action constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because public employees have a property interest in their employment, which gives them a right to a hearing prior to discharge.","B":"No, because the mayor's action violates the employee's right to freedom of expression and association.","C":"Yes, because the employee has no property interest in his job since the city charter provides that he holds the job \"at the pleasure of the mayor.\"","D":"Yes, because the mayor may require members of her administration to be politically loyal to her."},"answer":"B","explanation":"First Amendment freedoms\n\nExpression Right to communicate information & ideas through speech or conduct\n\nAssociation Right to form or participate in any group or organization\n\nPress Right to publish truthful information\n\nReligion Free exercise – right to practice religion or no religion without government interference\n\nEstablishment – prohibits government from favoring particular religion or religion generally\n\nThe First Amendment protects a person's (including a public employee's) right to freedom of expression and association. Political belief and association are the core of these protected activities. As a result, the government cannot condition public employment on a person's political views or affiliations unless these factors are essential to effective performance of the job in question (eg, policymaking position). Here, the mayor fired a city water department employee for \"disloyalty\" after the employee supported his sister in an election against the mayor. But since the employee was merely responsible for sending out water bills to customers, his political views and affiliations were irrelevant to performing his job. Therefore, the mayor's action is an unconstitutional violation of the employee's right to freedom of expression and association. (Choices A & C) Procedural due process guarantees persons a fair opportunity to be heard when the government deprives them of life, liberty, or property. And public employees have a property interest in their employment if they can only be fired for cause. But since this city's employees work \"at the pleasure of the mayor,\" they do not have a property interest in their jobs. Nevertheless, the mayor's action violates the First Amendment. (Choice D) The mayor may only require members of her administration to be politically loyal to her if that loyalty is essential to the effective performance of the job in question—eg, chief of staff (not clerical employee).\n\nEducational objective: The government cannot condition public employment on a person's political views or affiliations unless these factors are required for effective job performance.\n\nReferences\n• Rutan v. Republican Party, 497 U.S. 62, 75–76 (1990) (holding that the government cannot base employment-related decisions on political views or affiliations unless required for effective job performance).","educationalObjective":"The government cannot condition public employment on a person's political views or affiliations unless these factors are required for effective job performance.","references":"• Rutan v. Republican Party, 497 U.S. 62, 75–76 (1990) (holding that the government cannot base employment-related decisions on political views or affiliations unless required for effective job performance).","rule":"The government cannot condition public employment on a person's political views or affiliations unless these factors are required for effective job performance.","irac":{"issue":"A clerical employee of a city water department was responsible for sending out water bills to customers. His work in this respect had always been satisfactory. The employee's sister ran in a recent election against the incumbent mayor, but she lost. The employee had supported his sister in the election campaign. After the mayor found out about this, she fired the employee solely because his support for the sister indicated that he was \"disloyal\" to the mayor. The city's charter provides that \"all employees of the city work at the pleasure of the mayor.\" Is the mayor's action constitutional?","rule":"The government cannot condition public employment on a person's political views or affiliations unless these factors are required for effective job performance.","analysis":"First Amendment freedoms\n\nExpression Right to communicate information & ideas through speech or conduct\n\nAssociation Right to form or participate in any group or organization\n\nPress Right to publish truthful information\n\nReligion Free exercise – right to practice religion or no religion without government interference\n\nEstablishment – prohibits government from favoring particular religion or religion generally\n\nThe First Amendment protects a person's (including a public employee's) right to freedom of expression and association. Political belief and association are the core of these protected activities. As a result, the government cannot condition public employment on a person's political views or affiliations unless these factors are essential to effective performance of the job in question (eg, policymaking position). Here, the mayor fired a city water department employee for \"disloyalty\" after the employee supported his sister in an election against the mayor. But since the employee was merely responsible for sending out water bills to customers, his political views and affiliations were irrelevant to performing his job. Therefore, the mayor's action is an unconstitutional violation of the employee's right to freedom of expression and association. (Choices A & C) Procedural due process guarantees persons a fair opportunity to be heard when the government deprives them of life, liberty, or property. And public employees have a property interest in their employment if they can only be fired for cause. But since this city's employees work \"at the pleasure of the mayor,\" they do not have a property interest in their jobs. Nevertheless, the mayor's action violates the First Amendment. (Choice D) The mayor may only require members of her administration to be politically loyal to her if that loyalty is essential to the effective performance of the job in question—eg, chief of staff (not clerical employee).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the mayor's action violates the employee's right to freedom of expression and association."},"questionZh":"市水务部门的一名文员负责向客户发送水费单。他在这方面的工作一直令人满意。该雇员的姐姐在最近的一次选举中与现任市长竞选，但她失败了。该雇员在竞选活动中支持他的妹妹。市长发现此事后，解雇了这名员工，仅仅因为他对妹妹的支持表明他对市长“不忠”。该市的章​​程规定，“该市的所有雇员都在市长乐意的情况下工作”。市长的行为是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为公职人员在其工作中拥有财产权益，这使他们有权在解雇前参加听证会。","B":"不可以，因为市长的行为侵犯了雇员的言论和结社自由权。","C":"是的，因为该雇员在其工作中没有财产权益，因为城市宪章规定他“根据市长的意愿”担任该工作。","D":"是的，因为市长可能要求她的政府成员在政治上忠于她。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案的自由\n\n表达 通过言论或行为传达信息和想法的权利\n\n结社 组建或参加任何团体或组织的权利\n\n按权利发布真实信息\n\n宗教自由——在不受政府干预的情况下信奉宗教或不信奉宗教的权利\n\n建制——禁止政府偏袒特定宗教或普遍宗教\n\n第一修正案保护个人（包括公职人员）言论和结社自由的权利。政治信仰和结社是这些受保护活动的核心。因此，政府不能根据个人的政治观点或派别来决定公共就业，除非这些因素对于有效履行相关工作（例如决策职位）至关重要。在这里，市长以“不忠诚”为由解雇了一名市水务局员工，因为该员工支持他的妹妹反对市长的选举。但由于该员工仅负责向客户发送水费单，因此他的政治观点和派别与履行其工作无关。因此，市长的行为违宪侵犯了雇员的言论和结社自由权。 （选项 A 和 C）程序性正当程序保证人们在政府剥夺他们的生命、自由或财产时有公平的机会发表意见。公职人员如果只能因故被解雇，则在其就业中享有财产权益。但由于这座城市的雇员“按照市长的意愿”工作，他们的工作中没有财产权益。然而，市长的行为违反了第一修正案。 （选项 D） 市长可能只要求她的政府成员在政治上对她忠诚，如果这种忠诚对于有效履行相关工作至关重要，例如幕僚长（不是文员）。\n\n教育目标：政府不能根据个人的政治观点或派别来决定公共就业，除非这些因素是有效工作表现所必需的。\n\n参考文献\n• Rutan v.Republican Party, 497 U.S. 62, 75–76 (1990)（认为政府不能根据政治观点或关系做出与就业相关的决策，除非是为了有效的工作表现）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"政府不能根据一个人的政治观点或派别来决定公共就业，除非这些因素是有效工作表现所必需的。","iracZh":{"issue":"市水务部门的一名文员负责向客户发送水费单。他在这方面的工作一直令人满意。该雇员的姐姐最近在一次选举中与现任市长竞选，但她失败了。该雇员在竞选活动中支持他的妹妹。市长发现此事后，解雇了这名员工，仅仅因为他对妹妹的支持表明他对市长“不忠”。该市的章​​程规定，“该市的所有雇员都在市长乐意的情况下工作”。市长的行为是否符合宪法？","rule":"政府不能根据一个人的政治观点或派别来决定公共就业，除非这些因素是有效工作表现所必需的。","analysis":"第一修正案的自由\n\n表达 通过言论或行为传达信息和想法的权利\n\n结社 组建或参加任何团体或组织的权利\n\n按权利发布真实信息\n\n宗教自由——在不受政府干预的情况下信奉宗教或不信奉宗教的权利\n\n建制——禁止政府偏袒特定宗教或普遍宗教\n\n第一修正案保护个人（包括公职人员）言论和结社自由的权利。政治信仰和结社是这些受保护活动的核心。因此，政府不能根据个人的政治观点或派别来决定公共就业，除非这些因素对于有效履行相关工作（例如决策职位）至关重要。在这里，市长以“不忠诚”为由解雇了一名市水务局员工，因为该员工支持他的妹妹反对市长的选举。但由于该员工仅负责向客户发送水费单，因此他的政治观点和派别与履行其工作无关。因此，市长的行为违宪侵犯了雇员的言论和结社自由权。 （选项 A 和 C）程序性正当程序保证人们在政府剥夺他们的生命、自由或财产时有公平的机会发表意见。公职人员如果只能因故被解雇，则在其就业中享有财产权益。但由于这座城市的雇员“按照市长的意愿”工作，他们的工作中没有财产权益。然而，市长的行为违反了第一修正案。 （选项 D） 市长可能只要求她的政府成员在政治上对她忠诚，如果这种忠诚对于有效履行相关工作至关重要，例如幕僚长（不是文员）。\n\n教育目标：政府不能根据个人的政治观点或派别来决定公共就业，除非这些因素是有效工作表现所必需的。\n\n参考文献\n• Rutan v.Republican Party, 497 U.S. 62, 75–76 (1990)（认为政府不能根据政治观点或关系做出与就业相关的决策，除非是为了有效的工作表现）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为市长的行为侵犯了雇员的言论和结社自由权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":154,"sourceQuestionPage":460,"sourceQuestionPages":[460],"sourceExplanationPages":[461,462],"sourceAnswerPages":[461,462],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-155","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state law provides that only U.S. citizens may serve as jurors in the state courts of that state. A woman who is a lawful resident noncitizen and who has resided in the state for many years was summoned for jury duty in a state court. The woman's name was selected from a list of potential jurors that was compiled from a comprehensive list of local residents. She was disqualified from service solely because she is not a U.S. citizen. The woman has filed an action for a declaratory judgment that the state law is unconstitutional. Who should prevail in this action?","choices":{"A":"The state, because a state may limit to U.S. citizens functions that are an integral part of the process of self-government.","B":"The state, because jury service is a privilege, not a right, and therefore it is not a liberty interest protected by the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"The woman, because the Constitution gives Congress plenary power to make classifications with respect to noncitizens.","D":"The woman, because the state has not articulated a legitimate reason for prohibiting resident noncitizens from serving as jurors in the state's courts."},"answer":"A","explanation":"State laws that discriminate against lawful residents of the United States based on their citizenship can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. Since citizenship is a suspect class, such laws are generally subject to strict scrutiny review* and struck down. However, a growing exception exists for state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions that would allow (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion—eg, serving on a jury, voting, employment as a police officer or public school teacher. Under this political-function exception, such laws need only satisfy rational basis scrutiny and will be upheld unless the challenger proves that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest. Here, a state law discriminates against noncitizens by excluding them from jury service. And since jury service is a function that is an integral part of the process of self- government, the political-function exception applies. The law is rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in ensuring that only U.S. citizens have a direct effect on the functioning of the government (Choice D). Therefore, the state should prevail. *Because Congress has the power to regulate immigration, federal laws that discriminate based on citizenship are subject to rational basis review under the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment. (Choice B) The distinction between a privilege and a right is never relevant to a Fourteenth Amendment due process challenge. Additionally, the woman's claim will be analyzed under the equal protection clause since the challenged law denies jury service to some (but not all) persons. (Choice C) Congress has plenary power (ie, exclusive authority) to make immigration law and policy. But states can still make classifications based on citizenship so long as they are constitutional (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis scrutiny applies when such laws restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions allowing the (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion.\n\nReferences\n• Bernal v. Fainter, 467 U.S. 216, 219–20 (1984) (holding that a state law discriminating against resident noncitizens is subject to strict scrutiny unless the political-function exception applies).","educationalObjective":"State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis scrutiny applies when such laws restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions allowing the (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion.","references":"• Bernal v. Fainter, 467 U.S. 216, 219–20 (1984) (holding that a state law discriminating against resident noncitizens is subject to strict scrutiny unless the political-function exception applies).","rule":"State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis scrutiny applies when such laws restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions allowing the (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion.","irac":{"issue":"Who should prevail in this action?","rule":"State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis scrutiny applies when such laws restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions allowing the (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion.","analysis":"State laws that discriminate against lawful residents of the United States based on their citizenship can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. Since citizenship is a suspect class, such laws are generally subject to strict scrutiny review* and struck down. However, a growing exception exists for state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions that would allow (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion—eg, serving on a jury, voting, employment as a police officer or public school teacher. Under this political-function exception, such laws need only satisfy rational basis scrutiny and will be upheld unless the challenger proves that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest. Here, a state law discriminates against noncitizens by excluding them from jury service. And since jury service is a function that is an integral part of the process of self- government, the political-function exception applies. The law is rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in ensuring that only U.S. citizens have a direct effect on the functioning of the government (Choice D). Therefore, the state should prevail. *Because Congress has the power to regulate immigration, federal laws that discriminate based on citizenship are subject to rational basis review under the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment. (Choice B) The distinction between a privilege and a right is never relevant to a Fourteenth Amendment due process challenge. Additionally, the woman's claim will be analyzed under the equal protection clause since the challenged law denies jury service to some (but not all) persons. (Choice C) Congress has plenary power (ie, exclusive authority) to make immigration law and policy. But states can still make classifications based on citizenship so long as they are constitutional (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: The state, because a state may limit to U.S. citizens functions that are an integral part of the process of self-government."},"questionZh":"州法律规定，只有美国公民才能担任该州州法院的陪审员。一名在该州居住多年的合法居民非公民妇女被传唤到州法院担任陪审员。该女子的名字是从潜在陪审员名单中选出的，该名单是根据当地居民的综合名单编制的。她被取消服役资格仅仅是因为她不是美国公民。该妇女已提起诉讼，要求做出州法律违宪的宣告性判决。这场行动中谁应该占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"国家，因为国家可能限制美国公民行使自治过程中不可分割的一部分的职能。","B":"国家，因为陪审员服务是一种特权，而不是一种权利，因此它不是受第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护的自由利益。","C":"女人，因为宪法赋予国会对非公民进行分类的全权。","D":"这位女士，因为该州没有阐明禁止居民非公民担任州法院陪审员的合法理由。"},"explanationZh":"基于公民身份歧视美国合法居民的州法律可能会根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。由于公民身份是一个可疑的类别，此类法律通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）*并被废除。然而，限制非公民参与政府职能的州法律存在越来越多的例外情况，这些政府职能允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策，或（2）行使广泛的自由裁量权，例如担任陪审团、投票、担任警察或公立学校教师。在这种政治功能例外的情况下，此类法律只需要满足合理基础的审查，就会得到维持，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理关系。在这里，州法律将非公民排除在陪审员之外，从而歧视他们。由于陪审团服务是自治过程中不可或缺的一部分，因此政治职能例外。该法律与国家的合法利益合理相关，以确保只有美国公民才能对政府的运作产生直接影响（选项 D）。因此，国家应该占上风。 *由于国会有权监管移民，因此基于公民身份进行歧视的联邦法律应根据第五修正案的平等保护部分接​​受合理的审查。 （选项 B）特权和权利之间的区别与第十四修正案正当程序挑战无关。此外，由于受到质疑的法律拒绝为某些（但不是全部）人提供陪审服务，因此将根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）对妇女的主张进行分析。 （选项C）国会拥有制定移民法律和政策的全权（即专属权力）。但各州仍然可以根据公民身份进行分类，只要它们符合宪法（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，当此类法律限制非公民参与允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策或（2）行使广泛自由裁量权的政府职能时，则适用合理基础审查。\n\n参考文献\n• Bernal v. Fainter, 467 U.S. 216, 219-20 (1984)（认为歧视非公民居民的州法律应受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），除非适用政治职能例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，当此类法律限制非公民参与允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策或（2）行使广泛自由裁量权的政府职能时，则适用合理基础审查。","iracZh":{"issue":"这场行动中谁应该占上风？","rule":"基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，当此类法律限制非公民参与允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策或（2）行使广泛自由裁量权的政府职能时，则适用合理基础审查。","analysis":"基于公民身份歧视美国合法居民的州法律可能会根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。由于公民身份是一个可疑的类别，此类法律通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）*并被废除。然而，限制非公民参与政府职能的州法律存在越来越多的例外情况，这些政府职能允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策，或（2）行使广泛的自由裁量权，例如担任陪审团、投票、担任警察或公立学校教师。在这种政治功能例外的情况下，此类法律只需要满足合理基础的审查，就会得到维持，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理关系。在这里，州法律将非公民排除在陪审员之外，从而歧视他们。由于陪审团服务是自治过程中不可或缺的一部分，因此政治职能例外。该法律与国家的合法利益合理相关，以确保只有美国公民才能对政府的运作产生直接影响（选项 D）。因此，国家应该占上风。 *由于国会有权监管移民，因此基于公民身份进行歧视的联邦法律应根据第五修正案的平等保护部分接​​受合理的审查。 （选项 B）特权和权利之间的区别与第十四修正案正当程序挑战无关。此外，由于受到质疑的法律拒绝为某些（但不是全部）人提供陪审服务，因此将根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）对妇女的主张进行分析。 （选项C）国会拥有制定移民法律和政策的全权（即专属权力）。但各州仍然可以根据公民身份进行分类，只要它们符合宪法（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，当此类法律限制非公民参与允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策或（2）行使广泛自由裁量权的政府职能时，则适用合理基础审查。\n\n参考文献\n• Bernal v. Fainter, 467 U.S. 216, 219-20 (1984)（认为歧视非公民居民的州法律应受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），除非适用政治职能例外）。","conclusion":"答案 A：国家，因为国家可能限制美国公民行使自治过程中不可分割的一部分的职能。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0acbaed72d2e7de4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":155,"sourceQuestionPage":463,"sourceQuestionPages":[463],"sourceExplanationPages":[464,465],"sourceAnswerPages":[464,465],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-156","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state enacted a statute that prohibited children born outside of marriage from receiving welfare benefits that the state provides to children who live in households below the federal poverty line. The purpose of the statute was to encourage individuals to get married before having children. A man and his unmarried partner had a daughter out of wedlock. Before the man and his partner married, the partner died. The man's annual household income was below the federal poverty line, so he applied for welfare benefits on the daughter's behalf. The state agency overseeing the program denied the application based on the state statute. The man filed suit in a federal district court, alleging that the state statute violates the equal protection clause. Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in the suit?","choices":{"A":"The agency must demonstrate that the state statute is substantially related to an important state interest.","B":"The agency must demonstrate that the state statute is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling state interest.","C":"The man must demonstrate that the state statute is not substantially related to an important state interest.","D":"The man must demonstrate that the state statute is not the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling state interest."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A state law that discriminates against similarly situated persons can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. The standard under which a court will review this challenge depends on the alleged discrimination. A law that discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (eg, nonmarital birth) must satisfy intermediate scrutiny. This imposes a difficult burden on the state to prove that the law is substantially related to an important state interest. Here, a state statute prohibits children born outside of marriage from receiving welfare benefits that the state provides to children who live in households below the federal poverty line. Since this statute discriminates against nonmarital children, the state agency overseeing the welfare program (not the man) has the burden to satisfy intermediate scrutiny (Choice C). Therefore, the agency must demonstrate that the state statute is substantially related to an important state interest. (Choices B & D) Strict scrutiny applies when a state law discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right. This highest level of scrutiny places the burden on the state (not the challenger) to prove that the law is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling state interest. But since the state statute here discriminates against a quasi-suspect class, intermediate scrutiny applies.\n\nEducational objective: A state law that discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (eg, nonmarital birth) must satisfy intermediate scrutiny, which imposes the burden on the state to prove that the law is substantially related to an important state interest.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Clark v. Jeter, 486 U.S. 456, 461 (1988) (holding that a law discriminating against nonmarital children is subject to intermediate scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"A state law that discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (eg, nonmarital birth) must satisfy intermediate scrutiny, which imposes the burden on the state to prove that the law is substantially related to an important state interest.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Clark v. Jeter, 486 U.S. 456, 461 (1988) (holding that a law discriminating against nonmarital children is subject to intermediate scrutiny).","rule":"A state law that discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (eg, nonmarital birth) must satisfy intermediate scrutiny, which imposes the burden on the state to prove that the law is substantially related to an important state interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in the suit?","rule":"A state law that discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (eg, nonmarital birth) must satisfy intermediate scrutiny, which imposes the burden on the state to prove that the law is substantially related to an important state interest.","analysis":"A state law that discriminates against similarly situated persons can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. The standard under which a court will review this challenge depends on the alleged discrimination. A law that discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (eg, nonmarital birth) must satisfy intermediate scrutiny. This imposes a difficult burden on the state to prove that the law is substantially related to an important state interest. Here, a state statute prohibits children born outside of marriage from receiving welfare benefits that the state provides to children who live in households below the federal poverty line. Since this statute discriminates against nonmarital children, the state agency overseeing the welfare program (not the man) has the burden to satisfy intermediate scrutiny (Choice C). Therefore, the agency must demonstrate that the state statute is substantially related to an important state interest. (Choices B & D) Strict scrutiny applies when a state law discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right. This highest level of scrutiny places the burden on the state (not the challenger) to prove that the law is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling state interest. But since the state statute here discriminates against a quasi-suspect class, intermediate scrutiny applies.","conclusion":"Answer A: The agency must demonstrate that the state statute is substantially related to an important state interest."},"questionZh":"某州颁布了一项法规，禁止非婚生儿童领取该州向联邦贫困线以下家庭的儿童提供的福利。该法规的目的是鼓励个人先结婚再生育。一个男人和他的未婚伴侣有一个未婚生的女儿。在该男子和他的伴侣结婚之前，伴侣就去世了。该男子的家庭年收入低于联邦贫困线，因此他代表女儿申请福利金。监督该计划的州机构根据州法规拒绝了该申请。该男子向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，指控州法规违反了平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。以下哪项最能说明诉讼中的说服责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"该机构必须证明该州法规与重要的国家利益密切相关。","B":"该机构必须证明州法规是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。","C":"该人必须证明该州法规与重要的国家利益没有实质性关系。","D":"该人必须证明，国家法规并不是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。"},"explanationZh":"根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），歧视处境相似的人的州法律可能会受到质疑。法院审查这一质疑的标准取决于所指控的歧视。歧视准嫌疑阶层（例如非婚生育）的法律必须通过中间审查。这给国家带来了证明该法律与重要国家利益实质相关的困难负担。在这里，州法规禁止非婚生儿童领取州为生活在联邦贫困线以下家庭的儿童提供的福利。由于该法规歧视非婚子女，因此监督福利计划的国家机构（而不是男性）有责任满足中间审查（选项 C）。因此，该机构必须证明该州法规与重要的国家利益密切相关。 （选择 B 和 D）当州法律歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利时，适用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这种最高级别的审查使国家（而不是挑战者）有责任证明法律是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。但由于这里的州法规歧视准嫌疑阶层，因此需要进行中间审查。\n\n教育目标：歧视准嫌疑阶层（例如非婚生育）的州法律必须满足中间审查，这使州有责任证明该法律与重要的国家利益实质相关。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Clark v. Jeter, 486 U.S. 456, 461 (1988)（认为歧视非婚生子女的法律应接受中间审查）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"歧视准嫌疑阶层（例如非婚生育）的州法律必须满足中间审查，这使州有责任证明该法律与重要的国家利益实质相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能说明诉讼中的说服责任？","rule":"歧视准嫌疑阶层（例如非婚生育）的州法律必须满足中间审查，这使州有责任证明该法律与重要的国家利益实质相关。","analysis":"根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），歧视处境相似的人的州法律可能会受到质疑。法院审查这一质疑的标准取决于所指控的歧视。歧视准嫌疑阶层（例如非婚生育）的法律必须通过中间审查。这给国家带来了证明该法律与重要国家利益实质相关的困难负担。在这里，州法规禁止非婚生儿童领取州为生活在联邦贫困线以下家庭的儿童提供的福利。由于该法规歧视非婚子女，因此监督福利计划的国家机构（而不是男性）有责任满足中间审查（选项 C）。因此，该机构必须证明该州法规与重要的国家利益密切相关。 （选择 B 和 D）当州法律歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利时，适用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这种最高级别的审查使国家（而不是挑战者）有责任证明法律是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。但由于这里的州法规歧视准嫌疑阶层，因此需要进行中间审查。\n\n教育目标：歧视准嫌疑阶层（例如非婚生育）的州法律必须满足中间审查，这使州有责任证明该法律与重要的国家利益实质相关。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Clark v. Jeter, 486 U.S. 456, 461 (1988)（认为歧视非婚生子女的法律应接受中间审查）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该机构必须证明该州法规与重要的国家利益密切相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-bfe47b1d10f919a6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":156,"sourceQuestionPage":466,"sourceQuestionPages":[466],"sourceExplanationPages":[467,468],"sourceAnswerPages":[467,468],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-157","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A federal criminal law imposes penalties for the intentional publication of communications by individuals who know or have reason to know that the communications were unlawfully recorded. A teachers' union and school board were engaged in contentious negotiations over a collective-bargaining agreement. One of the members of the teachers' union unlawfully placed a wiretap on the president of the school board's telephone. The teachers' union member anonymously sent the recordings of several of the president's phone conversations about the negotiations to a local radio talk show host. The talk show host received the recordings in a box with no return address, sender information, or label. After listening to the recordings, the talk show host played them on her show. In the conversations, the president made several derogatory and embarrassing remarks. A prosecutor subsequently filed charges against the radio talk show host for playing the recordings on her show in violation of the federal law. The radio talk show host claims that the federal law is unconstitutional. Is the prosecution of the radio talk show host under the federal law likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal law is a prior restraint.","B":"No, because the host did not unlawfully obtain the recordings and does not know who did.","C":"Yes, because private phone conversations are not a matter of public concern.","D":"Yes, because the host knew that the recordings were unlawfully obtained."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The First Amendment shields the media from criminal and civil liability for publishing lawfully obtained private facts (eg, crime victim's identity) and other truthful information involving matters of public concern (ie, newsworthy events). This amendment also shields the media from liability for publishing truthful information that is unlawfully obtained by a third party if:\n• the information involves a matter of public concern and\n• the publisher neither obtained it unlawfully nor knows who did. Here, the federal government prosecuted the radio talk show host for publishing the recordings of the school board president's phone conversations, which were unlawfully obtained by a member of the teachers' union. The conversations involve a matter of public concern since they relate to the ongoing collective-bargaining negotiations. Additionally, the host did not unlawfully obtain the recordings herself and did not know the member who anonymously sent them to her. Therefore, the prosecution is likely unconstitutional. (Choice A) A prior restraint is a government action (eg, an injunction) that prohibits a publication before it occurs. Since the federal law here penalizes a publication after it occurs, it is not a prior restraint. (Choice C) The phone conversations are a matter of public concern because public and private statements about collective-bargaining negotiations between a school board and a teachers' union are newsworthy. (Choice D) The host may have had reason to believe that the recordings were unlawfully obtained. However, she did not unlawfully obtain them herself or know who did because they were sent in box with no return address, sender information, or label. Therefore, she had the right to publish the information without punishment.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment shields the media from liability for publishing truthful information that was unlawfully obtained by a third party if (1) the information involves a matter of public concern and (2) the publisher neither obtained it unlawfully nor knows who did.\n\nReferences\n• Bartnicki v. Vopper, 532 U.S. 514, 535 (2001) (holding that the First Amendment prevents liability for the publication of truthful information about a matter of public concern unlawfully obtained by an unknown third party).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment shields the media from liability for publishing truthful information that was unlawfully obtained by a third party if (1) the information involves a matter of public concern and (2) the publisher neither obtained it unlawfully nor knows who did.","references":"• Bartnicki v. Vopper, 532 U.S. 514, 535 (2001) (holding that the First Amendment prevents liability for the publication of truthful information about a matter of public concern unlawfully obtained by an unknown third party).","rule":"The First Amendment shields the media from liability for publishing truthful information that was unlawfully obtained by a third party if (1) the information involves a matter of public concern and (2) the publisher neither obtained it unlawfully nor knows who did.","irac":{"issue":"Is the prosecution of the radio talk show host under the federal law likely constitutional?","rule":"The First Amendment shields the media from liability for publishing truthful information that was unlawfully obtained by a third party if (1) the information involves a matter of public concern and (2) the publisher neither obtained it unlawfully nor knows who did.","analysis":"The First Amendment shields the media from criminal and civil liability for publishing lawfully obtained private facts (eg, crime victim's identity) and other truthful information involving matters of public concern (ie, newsworthy events). This amendment also shields the media from liability for publishing truthful information that is unlawfully obtained by a third party if:\n• the information involves a matter of public concern and\n• the publisher neither obtained it unlawfully nor knows who did. Here, the federal government prosecuted the radio talk show host for publishing the recordings of the school board president's phone conversations, which were unlawfully obtained by a member of the teachers' union. The conversations involve a matter of public concern since they relate to the ongoing collective-bargaining negotiations. Additionally, the host did not unlawfully obtain the recordings herself and did not know the member who anonymously sent them to her. Therefore, the prosecution is likely unconstitutional. (Choice A) A prior restraint is a government action (eg, an injunction) that prohibits a publication before it occurs. Since the federal law here penalizes a publication after it occurs, it is not a prior restraint. (Choice C) The phone conversations are a matter of public concern because public and private statements about collective-bargaining negotiations between a school board and a teachers' union are newsworthy. (Choice D) The host may have had reason to believe that the recordings were unlawfully obtained. However, she did not unlawfully obtain them herself or know who did because they were sent in box with no return address, sender information, or label. Therefore, she had the right to publish the information without punishment.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the host did not unlawfully obtain the recordings and does not know who did."},"questionZh":"联邦刑法对明知或有理由知道通信被非法记录的个人故意发布通信的行为实施处罚。教师工会和学校董事会就集体谈判协议进行了有争议的谈判。教师工会的一名成员非法窃听学校董事会主席的电话。教师工会成员匿名将总统几次有关谈判的电话录音发送给当地电台脱口秀主持人。脱口秀主持人收到的录音盒里没有回邮地址、发件人信息或标签。听完录音后，脱口秀主持人在她的节目中播放了这些录音。在谈话中，总统发表了一些贬损和令人尴尬的言论。一名检察官随后对电台脱口秀主持人提出指控，称其在节目中播放录音违反了联邦法律。广播脱口秀主持人声称联邦法律违宪。根据联邦法律起诉广播脱口秀主持人是否可能符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为联邦法律是事先限制。","B":"不会，因为主持人没有非法获取录音，也不知道是谁非法获取的。","C":"是的，因为私人电话交谈不是公众关心的问题。","D":"是的，因为主持人知道录音是非法获取的。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案保护媒体免于发布合法获得的私人事实（例如犯罪受害者的身份）和涉及公众关注事项（即有新闻价值的事件）的其他真实信息的刑事和民事责任。该修正案还使媒体在以下情况下免于发布第三方非法获得的真实信息：\n• 该信息涉及公众关心的问题并且\n• 出版商既没有非法获得它，也不知道是谁非法获得的。在这里，联邦政府起诉广播脱口秀主持人公布学校董事会主席的电话录音，这些录音是由教师工会成员非法获得的。这些对话涉及公众关注的问题，因为它们涉及正在进行的集体谈判。此外，主持人本人并非非法获取录音，也不认识匿名发送给她的成员。因此，起诉很可能是违宪的。 （选择 A） 事先限制是指在出版物发生之前禁止出版物的政府行为（例如禁令）。由于联邦法律在出版物发生后对其进行处罚，因此这不是事先限制。 （选项C） 电话谈话是公众关注的问题，因为有关学校董事会和教师工会之间集体谈判的公开和私下声明具有新闻价值。 （选项D）主持人可能有理由相信录音是非法获取的。然而，她并没有自己非法获取这些邮件，也不知道是谁非法获取的，因为这些邮件是用盒子寄出的，没有回邮地址、发件人信息或标签。因此，她有权公开该信息，而不受处罚。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护媒体在发布第三方非法获得的真实信息时不承担责任，如果（1）该信息涉及公众关注的问题，并且（2）发布者既没有非法获得也不知道是谁非法获得的。\n\n参考文献\n• Bartnicki v. Vopper, 532 U.S. 514, 535 (2001)（认为第一修正案禁止发布有关未知第三方非法获得的公众关注事项的真实信息的责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案保护媒体免于发布第三方非法获得的真实信息的责任，如果（1）该信息涉及公众关注的问题，并且（2）发布者既没有非法获得也不知道是谁非法获得的。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据联邦法律起诉广播脱口秀主持人是否可能符合宪法？","rule":"第一修正案保护媒体免于发布第三方非法获得的真实信息的责任，如果（1）该信息涉及公众关注的问题，并且（2）发布者既没有非法获得也不知道是谁非法获得的。","analysis":"第一修正案保护媒体免于发布合法获得的私人事实（例如犯罪受害者的身份）和涉及公众关注事项（即有新闻价值的事件）的其他真实信息的刑事和民事责任。该修正案还使媒体在以下情况下免于发布第三方非法获得的真实信息：\n• 该信息涉及公众关心的问题并且\n• 出版商既没有非法获得它，也不知道是谁非法获得的。在这里，联邦政府起诉广播脱口秀主持人公布学校董事会主席的电话录音，这些录音是由教师工会成员非法获得的。这些对话涉及公众关注的问题，因为它们涉及正在进行的集体谈判。此外，主持人本人并非非法获取录音，也不认识匿名发送给她的成员。因此，起诉很可能是违宪的。 （选择 A） 事先限制是指在出版物发生之前禁止出版物的政府行为（例如禁令）。由于联邦法律在出版物发生后对其进行处罚，因此这不是事先限制。 （选项C） 电话谈话是公众关注的问题，因为有关学校董事会和教师工会之间集体谈判的公开和私下声明具有新闻价值。 （选项D）主持人可能有理由相信录音是非法获取的。然而，她并没有自己非法获取这些邮件，也不知道是谁非法获取的，因为这些邮件是用盒子寄出的，没有回邮地址、发件人信息或标签。因此，她有权公开该信息，而不受处罚。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护媒体在发布第三方非法获得的真实信息时不承担责任，如果（1）该信息涉及公众关注的问题，并且（2）发布者既没有非法获得也不知道是谁非法获得的。\n\n参考文献\n• Bartnicki v. Vopper, 532 U.S. 514, 535 (2001)（认为第一修正案禁止发布有关未知第三方非法获得的公众关注事项的真实信息的责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为主持人没有非法获取录音，也不知道是谁非法获取的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"prior restraint","ipa":"/pɹˈaɪɝ ɹɪstɹˈeɪnt/","syllables":"prior re·straint","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"事先限制：在言论发表前实施的政府禁令或许可限制","contextualMeaning":"事先限制：在言论发表前实施的政府禁令或许可限制","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the federal law is a prior restraint.","synonyms":["prepublication restriction","advance censorship"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":157,"sourceQuestionPage":469,"sourceQuestionPages":[469],"sourceExplanationPages":[470,471],"sourceAnswerPages":[470,471],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-158","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A federal law prohibits individuals and corporations from making independent expenditures that support or oppose specific candidates for federal elective office. The purpose of the law is to prevent quid pro quo corruption in the federal government. A steel manufacturer broadcast a television ad in support of a federal congressional candidate who favors placing tariffs on imported steel. The manufacturer has filed an action in federal district court that challenges the law's constitutionality. Should the court uphold the law's constitutionality?","choices":{"A":"No, because political speech cannot be banned or restricted as a categorical matter.","B":"No, because the law is not the least restrictive means to achieve the federal government's compelling interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption.","C":"Yes, because campaign expenditures are not speech protected by the First Amendment.","D":"Yes, because the law is closely drawn to serving the federal government's important interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption."},"answer":"B","explanation":"[Current-law update — answer unchanged] National Republican Senatorial Committee v. FEC (2026) requires rigorous closely drawn review and invalidated political-party coordinated-expenditure limits. It also reaffirmed unlimited independent expenditures and preserved the distinct treatment of direct contribution limits.\n\nCampaign-finance restrictions\n\nExamples Test\n\nContribution\n• Limit on amount of contribution to: Intermediate scrutiny restrictions – single candidate\n• closely – political candidates, parties drawn/substantially & action committees related to important combined government interest\n• Increase of limit on amount of contribution to candidate when self-financed opponent exceeds certain spending amount Expenditure\n• Limit on spending by: Strict scrutiny restrictions – corporations or unions in\n• necessary/least support of or opposition to restrictive means to candidate achieve compelling – candidate on his/her own government interest behalf Political speech, including campaign contributions and expenditures, is a core right protected by the First Amendment (Choice C). However, this category of speech is still subject to government restrictions that satisfy the appropriate level of scrutiny (Choice A). Restrictions on campaign expenditures impose a substantial burden on political speech and must therefore survive strict scrutiny. This gives the government the nearly impossible burden of proving that its restriction is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the federal law prohibits individuals and corporations from making independent campaign expenditures that support or oppose a specific candidate for federal elective office. The government likely has a compelling interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption. However, this prohibition is not the least restrictive means to achieve that interest. For example, the government can require that the identity of the individual or corporation expending the money be disclosed. Therefore, the law is unconstitutional and the court should not uphold it.* *Government restrictions that limit how much a candidate can spend on his/her own behalf are also unconstitutional. That is because the government's interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption or the appearance of it are absent when a candidate spends his/her own money.\n\n(Choice D) A government restriction on campaign contributions must satisfy intermediate scrutiny—ie, the government must show that its restriction is closely drawn to serve an important government interest. However, this law restricts campaign expenditures, so the federal government must satisfy the more exacting strict scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment protects political speech, including campaign contributions and expenditures. A government restriction on campaign expenditures is unconstitutional unless the government satisfies strict scrutiny by showing that the restriction is the least restrictive means to serve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Citizens United v. Fed. Election Comm'n, 558 U.S. 310, 360–61 (2010) (holding that limits on independent campaign expenditures made by corporations violate the First Amendment and are unconstitutional).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 489 (2020) (explaining the constitutionality of campaign finance regulations).","educationalObjective":"Independent campaign expenditures are core political speech and may not be capped to prevent quid pro quo corruption. In 2026, the Court also invalidated limits on political-party coordinated expenditures under rigorous closely drawn scrutiny.","references":"• Citizens United v. Fed. Election Comm'n, 558 U.S. 310, 360–61 (2010) (holding that limits on independent campaign expenditures made by corporations violate the First Amendment and are unconstitutional).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 489 (2020) (explaining the constitutionality of campaign finance regulations).","rule":"Independent campaign expenditures are core political speech and may not be capped to prevent quid pro quo corruption. In 2026, the Court also invalidated limits on political-party coordinated expenditures under rigorous closely drawn scrutiny.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court uphold the law's constitutionality?","rule":"Independent campaign expenditures are core political speech and may not be capped to prevent quid pro quo corruption. In 2026, the Court also invalidated limits on political-party coordinated expenditures under rigorous closely drawn scrutiny.","analysis":"Campaign-finance restrictions\n\nExamples Test\n\nContribution\n• Limit on amount of contribution to: Intermediate scrutiny restrictions – single candidate\n• closely – political candidates, parties drawn/substantially & action committees related to important combined government interest\n• Increase of limit on amount of contribution to candidate when self-financed opponent exceeds certain spending amount Expenditure\n• Limit on spending by: Strict scrutiny restrictions – corporations or unions in\n• necessary/least support of or opposition to restrictive means to candidate achieve compelling – candidate on his/her own government interest behalf Political speech, including campaign contributions and expenditures, is a core right protected by the First Amendment (Choice C). However, this category of speech is still subject to government restrictions that satisfy the appropriate level of scrutiny (Choice A). Restrictions on campaign expenditures impose a substantial burden on political speech and must therefore survive strict scrutiny. This gives the government the nearly impossible burden of proving that its restriction is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the federal law prohibits individuals and corporations from making independent campaign expenditures that support or oppose a specific candidate for federal elective office. The government likely has a compelling interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption. However, this prohibition is not the least restrictive means to achieve that interest. For example, the government can require that the identity of the individual or corporation expending the money be disclosed. Therefore, the law is unconstitutional and the court should not uphold it.* *Government restrictions that limit how much a candidate can spend on his/her own behalf are also unconstitutional. That is because the government's interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption or the appearance of it are absent when a candidate spends his/her own money.\n\n(Choice D) A government restriction on campaign contributions must satisfy intermediate scrutiny—ie, the government must show that its restriction is closely drawn to serve an important government interest. However, this law restricts campaign expenditures, so the federal government must satisfy the more exacting strict scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the law is not the least restrictive means to achieve the federal government's compelling interest in preventing quid pro quo corruption."},"questionZh":"联邦法律禁止个人和公司进行独立支出来支持或反对联邦选举办公室的特定候选人。该法律的目的是防止联邦政府中的交换式腐败。一家钢铁制造商播放电视广告，支持一位赞成对进口钢铁征收关税的联邦国会候选人。该制造商已向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，质疑该法律的合宪性。法院是否应该维护法律的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为政治言论不能被绝对禁止或限制。","B":"不，因为法律并不是实现联邦政府防止交换腐败的迫切利益的限制最少的手段。","C":"是的，因为竞选支出不受第一修正案的言论保护。","D":"是的，因为该法律密切关注服务于联邦政府防止交换腐败的重要利益。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法更新——答案不变】National Republican Senatorial Committee v. FEC（2026）要求严格适用“紧密契合”审查，并废除了政党协调支出限额；同时重申独立支出不受此类上限，并保留对直接捐款限额的不同处理。\n\n竞选资金限制\n\n示例测试\n\n贡献\n• 捐款金额限制： 中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）限制 – 单一候选人\n• 密切 - 与重要的综合政府利益相关的政治候选人、政党和行动委员会\n• 当自费对手超过一定支出金额时，提高候选人的捐款限额 支出\n• 支出限制： 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）限制——公司或工会\n• 必要/最少支持或反对的限制性手段使候选人达到令人信服的程度——候选人代表他/她自己的政府利益 政治言论，包括竞选捐款和支出，是受第一修正案保护的核心权利（选项C）。然而，此类言论仍然受到满足适当审查级别的政府限制（选择 A）。对竞选支出的限制给政治言论带来了沉重负担，因此必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这给政府带来了几乎不可能的负担，要证明其限制是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。在这里，联邦法律禁止个人和公司进行独立的竞选支出来支持或反对联邦选举办公室的特定候选人。政府可能对防止交换式腐败有着强烈的兴趣。然而，这一禁令并不是实现这一利益的最严格的限制手段。例如，政府可以要求披露花钱的个人或公司的身份。因此，该法律违宪，法院不应维持该法律。* *政府限制候选人可以代表自己花多少钱的限制也是违宪的。这是因为，当候选人花自己的钱时，政府就没有兴趣防止交换性腐败或出现腐败。\n\n（选项D）政府对竞选捐款的限制必须满足中间审查，即政府必须表明其限制是为了服务重要的政府利益。然而，这项法律限制了竞选支出，因此联邦政府必须满足更严格的审查。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护政治言论，包括竞选捐款和支出。政府对竞选支出的限制是违宪的，除非政府通过证明该限制是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段来满足严格的审查。\n\n参考文献\n• 公民联合诉美联储。 Election Comm'n, 558 U.S. 310, 360–61 (2010)（认为对公司独立竞选支出的限制违反了第一修正案并且违宪）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 489 条 (2020)（解释竞选财务法规的合宪性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"独立竞选支出属于核心政治言论，不能以防止权钱交易式腐败为由设置上限。2026 年最高法院又在严格的“紧密契合”审查下废除了政党协调支出限额。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该维护法律的合宪性？","rule":"独立竞选支出属于核心政治言论，不能以防止权钱交易式腐败为由设置上限。2026 年最高法院又在严格的“紧密契合”审查下废除了政党协调支出限额。","analysis":"竞选资金限制\n\n示例测试\n\n贡献\n• 捐款金额限制： 中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）限制 – 单一候选人\n• 密切 - 与重要的综合政府利益相关的政治候选人、政党和行动委员会\n• 当自费对手超过一定支出金额时，提高候选人的捐款限额 支出\n• 支出限制： 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）限制——公司或工会\n• 必要/最少支持或反对的限制性手段使候选人达到令人信服的程度——候选人代表他/她自己的政府利益 政治言论，包括竞选捐款和支出，是受第一修正案保护的核心权利（选项C）。然而，此类言论仍然受到满足适当审查级别的政府限制（选择 A）。对竞选支出的限制给政治言论带来了沉重负担，因此必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这给政府带来了几乎不可能的负担，要证明其限制是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。在这里，联邦法律禁止个人和公司进行独立的竞选支出来支持或反对联邦选举办公室的特定候选人。政府可能对防止交换式腐败有着强烈的兴趣。然而，这一禁令并不是实现这一利益的最严格的限制手段。例如，政府可以要求披露花钱的个人或公司的身份。因此，该法律违宪，法院不应维持该法律。* *政府限制候选人可以代表自己花多少钱的限制也是违宪的。这是因为，当候选人花自己的钱时，政府就没有兴趣防止交换性腐败或出现腐败。\n\n（选项D）政府对竞选捐款的限制必须满足中间审查，即政府必须表明其限制是为了服务重要的政府利益。然而，这项法律限制了竞选支出，因此联邦政府必须满足更严格的审查。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护政治言论，包括竞选捐款和支出。政府对竞选支出的限制是违宪的，除非政府通过证明该限制是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段来满足严格的审查。\n\n参考文献\n• 公民联合诉美联储。 Election Comm'n, 558 U.S. 310, 360–61 (2010)（认为对公司独立竞选支出的限制违反了第一修正案并且违宪）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 489 条 (2020)（解释竞选财务法规的合宪性）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为法律并不是实现联邦政府防止交换腐败的迫切利益的限制最少的手段。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-2026-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已纳入 2026 年 NRSC v. FEC；本题答案 B 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"National Republican Senatorial Committee v. FEC, 609 U.S. ___ (2026)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-621_h315.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":158,"sourceQuestionPage":472,"sourceQuestionPages":[472],"sourceExplanationPages":[473,474],"sourceAnswerPages":[473,474],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-159","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Voting Rights","question":"Twenty percent of a city's residents are members of minority racial groups. These residents are evenly distributed among the many different residential areas of the city. The five city council members of the city are elected from five single-member electoral districts that are nearly equally populated. No member of a minority racial group has ever been elected to the city council. A group of citizens who are members of minority racial groups files suit in federal district court seeking a declaratory judgment that the single-member districts in the city are unconstitutional. The group claims that the single-member districting system in that city diminishes the ability of voters who are members of minority racial groups to affect the outcome of city elections. The group seeks an order from the court forcing the city to adopt an at-large election system in which the five candidates with the greatest vote totals would be elected to the city council. No state or federal statutes are applicable to the resolution of this suit. Which of the following constitutional provisions provides the most obvious basis for the group's claim in this suit?","choices":{"A":"The due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"The Fifteenth Amendment.","C":"The privileges or immunities clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"The Thirteenth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"[Current-law update — answer unchanged] Louisiana v. Callais (2026) confirms that the Fifteenth Amendment reaches intentional racial discrimination, not disparate impact alone. The Fifteenth Amendment remains the most relevant listed constitutional basis, though plaintiffs would still need to prove discriminatory purpose to prevail.\n\nVoting protections\n\n14th Prohibits substantial impairment of voting rights Amendment\n\n15th Prohibits denial or abridgment of voting rights based on race, color, Amendment previous servitude\n\n19th Prohibits denial or abridgment of voting rights based on sex Amendment\n\n24th Abolishes poll taxes Amendment\n\n26th Establishes right to vote at 18 years old Amendment\n\nThe Fifteenth Amendment prohibits state and federal governments from abridging the right to vote—which includes the right to have that vote meaningfully counted—on the basis of race, color, or previous condition of servitude. As a result, the Fifteenth Amendment provides a basis to challenge both:\n• direct limitations on the ability to register to vote or cast a ballot (eg, only whites are allowed to vote) and\n\n• dilutions of voting power (eg, districting system purposely limits minority voters' ability to affect election outcomes).\n\nHere, the group of citizens is challenging the city's districting system on the ground that it diminishes the ability of racial minority voters to affect the outcome of city elections. Since this alleges dilution of voting power based on race, the Fifteenth Amendment provides the most obvious basis for the group's claim. (Choice A) The group could base its claim on the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, which prohibits states from infringing upon a life, liberty, or property interest (eg, the right to vote) without adequate justification. But since the Fifteenth Amendment specifically prohibits a denial of voting rights based on race (as alleged here), it provides a more obvious basis for the group's claim. (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from interfering with the very limited rights of national citizenship—eg, the right to vote for national offices. But since the group's claim concerns city council elections, no right of national citizenship is at stake.\n\n(Choice D) The Thirteenth Amendment prohibits both government and private actors from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude—neither of which is at issue here.\n\nEducational objective: The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits state and federal governments from abridging the right to vote—by direct limitation or diluting voting power—on the basis of race, color, or previous condition of servitude.\n\nReferences\n• Allen v. State Bd. of Elections, 393 U.S. 544, 569 (1969) (explaining that the \"right to vote can be affected by a dilution of voting power as well as by an absolute prohibition on casting a ballot\").","educationalObjective":"The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits federal or state action intentionally denying or abridging voting rights on account of race, color, or previous servitude. Vote dilution can be challenged under that Amendment, but current doctrine requires discriminatory purpose, not disparate impact alone.","references":"• Allen v. State Bd. of Elections, 393 U.S. 544, 569 (1969) (explaining that the \"right to vote can be affected by a dilution of voting power as well as by an absolute prohibition on casting a ballot\").","rule":"The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits federal or state action intentionally denying or abridging voting rights on account of race, color, or previous servitude. Vote dilution can be challenged under that Amendment, but current doctrine requires discriminatory purpose, not disparate impact alone.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following constitutional provisions provides the most obvious basis for the group's claim in this suit?","rule":"The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits federal or state action intentionally denying or abridging voting rights on account of race, color, or previous servitude. Vote dilution can be challenged under that Amendment, but current doctrine requires discriminatory purpose, not disparate impact alone.","analysis":"Voting protections\n\n14th Prohibits substantial impairment of voting rights Amendment\n\n15th Prohibits denial or abridgment of voting rights based on race, color, Amendment previous servitude\n\n19th Prohibits denial or abridgment of voting rights based on sex Amendment\n\n24th Abolishes poll taxes Amendment\n\n26th Establishes right to vote at 18 years old Amendment\n\nThe Fifteenth Amendment prohibits state and federal governments from abridging the right to vote—which includes the right to have that vote meaningfully counted—on the basis of race, color, or previous condition of servitude. As a result, the Fifteenth Amendment provides a basis to challenge both:\n• direct limitations on the ability to register to vote or cast a ballot (eg, only whites are allowed to vote) and\n\n• dilutions of voting power (eg, districting system purposely limits minority voters' ability to affect election outcomes).\n\nHere, the group of citizens is challenging the city's districting system on the ground that it diminishes the ability of racial minority voters to affect the outcome of city elections. Since this alleges dilution of voting power based on race, the Fifteenth Amendment provides the most obvious basis for the group's claim. (Choice A) The group could base its claim on the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, which prohibits states from infringing upon a life, liberty, or property interest (eg, the right to vote) without adequate justification. But since the Fifteenth Amendment specifically prohibits a denial of voting rights based on race (as alleged here), it provides a more obvious basis for the group's claim. (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from interfering with the very limited rights of national citizenship—eg, the right to vote for national offices. But since the group's claim concerns city council elections, no right of national citizenship is at stake.\n\n(Choice D) The Thirteenth Amendment prohibits both government and private actors from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude—neither of which is at issue here.","conclusion":"Answer B: The Fifteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"一个城市百分之二十的居民是少数族裔群体的成员。这些居民均匀分布在城市的许多不同的住宅区中。该市的五名市议会成员是从人口几乎相等的五个单一成员选区选出的。从未有少数族裔成员当选为市议会成员。一群少数族裔公民向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，寻求宣告性判决，判定该市的单一成员选区违宪。该组织声称，该城市的单一选区制度削弱了少数族裔选民影响城市选举结果的能力。该组织寻求法院下令，迫使该市采用普选制度，其中得票最多的五名候选人将被选入市议会。没有任何州或联邦法规适用于本诉讼的解决。以下哪项宪法条款为该集团在本次诉讼中的主张提供了最明显的依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","B":"第十五修正案。","C":"第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款。","D":"第十三修正案。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法更新——答案不变】Louisiana v. Callais（2026）确认，第十五修正案针对故意种族歧视，而非仅有差别影响。就所列选项而言，第十五修正案仍是最直接的宪法依据，但原告要胜诉仍须证明歧视目的。\n\n投票保护\n\n第十四条 禁止实质性损害投票权修正案\n\n第 15 条 禁止基于种族、肤色拒绝或削减投票权，修正先前的奴役情况\n\n第十九条 禁止基于性别拒绝或剥夺投票权修正案\n\n24日废除人头税修正案\n\n第26条规定18岁有投票权修正案\n\n第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府因种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况而剥夺投票权，其中包括有意义地计算选票的权利。因此，第十五修正案提供了挑战以下两者的基础：\n• 对登记投票或投票的能力的直接限制（例如，只允许白人投票）以及\n\n• 投票权的稀释（例如，选区制度有意限制少数选民影响选举结果的能力）。\n\n在这里，公民团体正在挑战城市的选区制度，理由是它削弱了少数族裔选民影响城市选举结果的能力。由于这声称基于种族的投票权被稀释，第十五修正案为该组织的主张提供了最明显的基础。 （选项A）该团体可以根据第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）提出其主张，该条款禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下侵犯生命、自由或财产利益（例如投票权）。但由于第十五修正案明确禁止基于种族而剥夺投票权（如此处所称），因此它为该组织的主张提供了更明显的基础。 （选项C） 第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州干涉国家公民的非常有限的权利，例如投票选举国家公职的权利。但由于该组织的主张涉及市议会选举，因此国民公民权不会受到威胁。\n\n（选项 D）第十三修正案禁止政府和私人行为者从事奴役或非自愿奴役——这两者都不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府基于种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况，通过直接限制或稀释投票权来剥夺投票权。\n\n参考文献\n• Allen 诉State Bd。 of Elections，393 U.S. 544, 569 (1969)（解释说“投票权可能会受到投票权稀释以及绝对禁止投票的影响”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十五修正案禁止联邦或州基于种族、肤色或曾受奴役身份，故意剥夺或削弱投票权。选票稀释可以据此挑战，但现行规则要求歧视目的，仅有差别影响并不足够。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项宪法条款为该集团在本次诉讼中的主张提供了最明显的依据？","rule":"第十五修正案禁止联邦或州基于种族、肤色或曾受奴役身份，故意剥夺或削弱投票权。选票稀释可以据此挑战，但现行规则要求歧视目的，仅有差别影响并不足够。","analysis":"投票保护\n\n第十四条 禁止实质性损害投票权修正案\n\n第 15 条 禁止基于种族、肤色拒绝或削减投票权，修正先前的奴役情况\n\n第十九条 禁止基于性别拒绝或剥夺投票权修正案\n\n24日废除人头税修正案\n\n第26条规定18岁有投票权修正案\n\n第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府因种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况而剥夺投票权，其中包括有意义地计算选票的权利。因此，第十五修正案提供了挑战以下两者的基础：\n• 对登记投票或投票的能力的直接限制（例如，只允许白人投票）以及\n\n• 投票权的稀释（例如，选区制度有意限制少数选民影响选举结果的能力）。\n\n在这里，公民团体正在挑战城市的选区制度，理由是它削弱了少数族裔选民影响城市选举结果的能力。由于这声称基于种族的投票权被稀释，第十五修正案为该组织的主张提供了最明显的基础。 （选项A）该团体可以根据第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）提出其主张，该条款禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下侵犯生命、自由或财产利益（例如投票权）。但由于第十五修正案明确禁止基于种族而剥夺投票权（如此处所称），因此它为该组织的主张提供了更明显的基础。 （选项C） 第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州干涉国家公民的非常有限的权利，例如投票选举国家公职的权利。但由于该组织的主张涉及市议会选举，因此国民公民权不会受到威胁。\n\n（选项 D）第十三修正案禁止政府和私人行为者从事奴役或非自愿奴役——这两者都不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府基于种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况，通过直接限制或稀释投票权来剥夺投票权。\n\n参考文献\n• Allen 诉State Bd。 of Elections，393 U.S. 544, 569 (1969)（解释说“投票权可能会受到投票权稀释以及绝对禁止投票的影响”）。","conclusion":"答案 B：第十五修正案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-2026-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"增加 Callais（2026）关于歧视目的的要求；本题只问最直接的宪法依据，原答案 B 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Louisiana v. Callais, 608 U.S. ___ (2026)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-109_new_jifl.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"districting","ipa":"/dˈɪstɹɪktɪnɡ/","syllables":"dis·trict·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 分区；空间聚合；v. 分区（district的ing形式）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 分区；空间聚合；v. 分区（district的ing形式）","wordForm":"词；词形 1:i/0:district","sourceSentence":"The group claims that the single-member districting system in that city diminishes the ability of voters who are members of minority racial groups to affect the outcome of city elections.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"dilution","ipa":"/daɪlˈuʃən/","syllables":"di·lu·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 冲淡, 淡度, 稀度, 稀释物, 冲淡物, 稀释；[计] 稀释度的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 冲淡, 淡度, 稀度, 稀释物, 冲淡物, 稀释；[计] 稀释度的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:dilutions","sourceSentence":"Vote dilution can be challenged under that Amendment, but current doctrine requires discriminatory purpose, not disparate impact alone.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":159,"sourceQuestionPage":475,"sourceQuestionPages":[475],"sourceExplanationPages":[476,477],"sourceAnswerPages":[476,477],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-160","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A federal law requires cable television providers to carry local broadcast television stations as part of the television programming they provide to subscribers. The purpose of the federal law is to preserve the economic viability of local broadcast television, which has suffered a significant decline in viewers and revenue due to the increased demand for cable television. A cable television provider that refuses to carry local broadcast television stations has filed an action in a federal district court that challenges the federal law. The provider argues that the law is unconstitutional. Is this federal law constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal government cannot regulate the content of cable television.","B":"No, because there are less restrictive means to preserve the economic viability of local broadcast television stations.","C":"Yes, because the cable television provider cannot show that the federal law does not directly advance an important government interest.","D":"Yes, because the federal law preserves the economic viability of local broadcast television without being substantially more restrictive than necessary."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely communicate information and ideas, including those transmitted by cable television. As a result, a government regulation of cable television is subject to strict scrutiny if it is content-based—ie, targets the message being conveyed. However, a regulation is subject to less strenuous intermediate scrutiny if it is content-neutral—ie, targets the manner of operations. Intermediate scrutiny requires the government to prove that its regulation:\n• directly advances an important government interest and\n• is not substantially more restrictive than necessary to serve that interest. Here, the federal law requires cable providers to carry local broadcast television stations. This requirement is content-neutral since it regulates the providers' manner of operations—not the content of their programming. SCOTUS has held that Congress has an important interest in preserving the economic viability of local broadcast television stations. And since the federal law directly advances this interest without being substantially more restrictive than necessary, the law satisfies intermediate scrutiny and is constitutional. (Choice A) The federal government can regulate the content of cable television if the regulation satisfies strict scrutiny. Additionally, the federal law here does not regulate the content of cable television but is merely a content-neutral regulation. (Choice B) There may be less restrictive means available to preserve the economic viability of local broadcast television stations (eg, providing them with subsidies). However, intermediate scrutiny only requires that the regulation not be substantially more restrictive than necessary to achieve an important government interest. (Choice C) Intermediate scrutiny places the burden on the government, not the challenger, to show that its content-neutral regulation directly advances an important government interest.\n\nEducational objective: Content-neutral regulations of speech, including speech transmitted by cable television, are constitutional if they satisfy intermediate scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation (1) directly advances an important government interest and (2) is not substantially more restrictive than necessary to serve that interest. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\nReferences\n• Turner Broad. Sys., Inc. v. Fed. Commc'ns Comm'n, 512 U.S. 622, 662 (1994) (holding that a federal law requiring cable providers to carry local broadcast television is a content-neutral regulation subject to intermediate scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"Content-neutral regulations of speech, including speech transmitted by cable television, are constitutional if they satisfy intermediate scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation (1) directly advances an important government interest and (2) is not substantially more restrictive than necessary to serve that interest. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","references":"• Turner Broad. Sys., Inc. v. Fed. Commc'ns Comm'n, 512 U.S. 622, 662 (1994) (holding that a federal law requiring cable providers to carry local broadcast television is a content-neutral regulation subject to intermediate scrutiny).","rule":"Content-neutral regulations of speech, including speech transmitted by cable television, are constitutional if they satisfy intermediate scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation (1) directly advances an important government interest and (2) is not substantially more restrictive than necessary to serve that interest. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","irac":{"issue":"Is this federal law constitutional?","rule":"Content-neutral regulations of speech, including speech transmitted by cable television, are constitutional if they satisfy intermediate scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation (1) directly advances an important government interest and (2) is not substantially more restrictive than necessary to serve that interest. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","analysis":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely communicate information and ideas, including those transmitted by cable television. As a result, a government regulation of cable television is subject to strict scrutiny if it is content-based—ie, targets the message being conveyed. However, a regulation is subject to less strenuous intermediate scrutiny if it is content-neutral—ie, targets the manner of operations. Intermediate scrutiny requires the government to prove that its regulation:\n• directly advances an important government interest and\n• is not substantially more restrictive than necessary to serve that interest. Here, the federal law requires cable providers to carry local broadcast television stations. This requirement is content-neutral since it regulates the providers' manner of operations—not the content of their programming. SCOTUS has held that Congress has an important interest in preserving the economic viability of local broadcast television stations. And since the federal law directly advances this interest without being substantially more restrictive than necessary, the law satisfies intermediate scrutiny and is constitutional. (Choice A) The federal government can regulate the content of cable television if the regulation satisfies strict scrutiny. Additionally, the federal law here does not regulate the content of cable television but is merely a content-neutral regulation. (Choice B) There may be less restrictive means available to preserve the economic viability of local broadcast television stations (eg, providing them with subsidies). However, intermediate scrutiny only requires that the regulation not be substantially more restrictive than necessary to achieve an important government interest. (Choice C) Intermediate scrutiny places the burden on the government, not the challenger, to show that its content-neutral regulation directly advances an important government interest.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the federal law preserves the economic viability of local broadcast television without being substantially more restrictive than necessary."},"questionZh":"联邦法律要求有线电视提供商将当地广播电视台作为其向订户提供的电视节目的一部分。联邦法律的目的是维护当地广播电视的经济活力，由于有线电视需求的增加，当地广播电视的观众和收入大幅下降。一家拒绝转播当地广播电视台的有线电视提供商已向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，质疑联邦法律。提供者辩称该法律违宪。这项联邦法律符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为联邦政府无法监管有线电视的内容。","B":"不会，因为保持地方广播电视台经济生存能力的限制性措施较少。","C":"是的，因为有线电视提供商无法证明联邦法律没有直接促进重要的政府利益。","D":"是的，因为联邦法律保留了地方广播电视的经济可行性，但没有过多限制。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案保护自由传播信息和思想的权利，包括通过有线电视传播的信息和思想。因此，政府对有线电视的监管如果是基于内容的，即针对所传达的信息，就会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。然而，如果一项法规是内容中立的，即针对运营方式，则该法规将受到不太严格的中间审查。中间审查要求政府证明其监管：\n• 直接促进重要的政府利益并且\n• 没有比满足该利益所需的限制更多。在这里，联邦法律要求有线电视提供商运营当地广播电视台。这一要求是内容中立的，因为它规范了提供商的运营方式，而不是其节目内容。最高法院认为，维护地方广播电视台的经济生存能力符合国会的重要利益。由于联邦法律直接促进了这一利益，而没有过多限制，因此该法律满足中间审查并且符合宪法。 （选项 A）如果法规满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），联邦政府可以对有线电视内容进行监管。此外，这里的联邦法律并不规范有线电视的内容，而仅仅是内容中立的规定。 （选择B） 可能存在限制性较少的手段来保持地方广播电视台的经济生存能力（例如，向其提供补贴）。然而，中间审查仅要求监管的限制性不得超过实现重要政府利益所需的限制性。 （选项C）中间审查将负担置于政府身上，而不是挑战者身上，以表明其内容中立的监管直接促进了重要的政府利益。\n\n教育目标：内容中立的言论法规，包括通过有线电视传输的言论，如果满足中间审查，则符合宪法。这要求政府证明该法规 (1) 直接促进了重要的政府利益，并且 (2) 的限制性并未超出服务该利益所需的程度。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• 特纳·布罗德。 Sys., Inc. 诉 Fed。 Commc'ns Comm'n, 512 U.S. 622, 662 (1994)（认为要求有线电视提供商提供本地广播电视的联邦法律是一项内容中立的法规，须接受中间审查）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"内容中立的言论法规，包括通过有线电视传输的言论，如果满足中间审查，就是符合宪法的。这要求政府证明该法规 (1) 直接促进了重要的政府利益，并且 (2) 的限制性并未超出服务该利益所需的程度。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。","iracZh":{"issue":"这项联邦法律符合宪法吗？","rule":"内容中立的言论法规，包括通过有线电视传输的言论，如果满足中间审查，就是符合宪法的。这要求政府证明该法规 (1) 直接促进了重要的政府利益，并且 (2) 的限制性并未超出服务该利益所需的程度。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。","analysis":"第一修正案保护自由传播信息和思想的权利，包括通过有线电视传播的信息和思想。因此，政府对有线电视的监管如果是基于内容的，即针对所传达的信息，就会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。然而，如果一项法规是内容中立的，即针对运营方式，则该法规将受到不太严格的中间审查。中间审查要求政府证明其监管：\n• 直接促进重要的政府利益并且\n• 没有比满足该利益所需的限制更多。在这里，联邦法律要求有线电视提供商运营当地广播电视台。这一要求是内容中立的，因为它规范了提供商的运营方式，而不是其节目内容。最高法院认为，维护地方广播电视台的经济生存能力符合国会的重要利益。由于联邦法律直接促进了这一利益，而没有过多限制，因此该法律满足中间审查并且符合宪法。 （选项 A）如果法规满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），联邦政府可以对有线电视内容进行监管。此外，这里的联邦法律并不规范有线电视的内容，而仅仅是内容中立的规定。 （选择B） 可能存在限制性较少的手段来保持地方广播电视台的经济生存能力（例如，向其提供补贴）。然而，中间审查仅要求监管的限制性不得超过实现重要政府利益所需的限制性。 （选项C）中间审查将负担置于政府身上，而不是挑战者身上，以表明其内容中立的监管直接促进了重要的政府利益。\n\n教育目标：内容中立的言论法规，包括通过有线电视传输的言论，如果满足中间审查，则符合宪法。这要求政府证明该法规 (1) 直接促进了重要的政府利益，并且 (2) 的限制性并未超出服务该利益所需的程度。 SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n参考文献\n• 特纳·布罗德。 Sys., Inc. 诉 Fed。 Commc'ns Comm'n, 512 U.S. 622, 662 (1994)（认为要求有线电视提供商提供本地广播电视的联邦法律是一项内容中立的法规，须接受中间审查）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为联邦法律保留了地方广播电视的经济可行性，但没有过多限制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-58c77a48304d1e8c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":160,"sourceQuestionPage":478,"sourceQuestionPages":[478],"sourceExplanationPages":[479,480],"sourceAnswerPages":[479,480],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-161","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A professional motorcycle rider put on a performance in a privately owned stadium during which he leaped his motorcycle over 21 automobiles. Spectators were charged $5 each to view the jump and were prohibited from using cameras. However, the local television station filmed the whole event from within the stadium without the rider's knowledge or consent and showed the film in its entirety on the evening newscast that day. The rider thereafter brought suit to recover damages from the station for the admittedly unauthorized filming and broadcasting of the performance. The television station raised only constitutional defenses. How should the court rule on the rider's suit?","choices":{"A":"Find the station liable, because its action deprives the rider of his property without due process.","B":"Find the station liable, because the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not deprive an entertainer of the commercial value of his or her performances.","C":"Hold against the rider, because news broadcasts are absolutely privileged under the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","D":"Hold against the rider, because the First and Fourteenth Amendments authorize press coverage of newsworthy entertainment events."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The First Amendment—applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment— protects the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance (ie, newsworthy events). But this right is not unlimited and must be balanced against the right to publicity—ie, the right to control or commercially benefit from one's own name, likeness, or other intimately associated qualities. Here, the station published the rider's entire performance—a newsworthy event—on its evening newscast without the rider's consent. That newscast deprived the rider of the commercial value of his performance since people are less likely to pay to see what they can now view for free. Therefore, the First and Fourteenth Amendments offer the station no protection, and the court should find the station liable. (Choice A) The Fourteenth Amendment prohibits the government from depriving an individual of life, liberty, or property without due process. But since the television station is a private entity, it is not required to provide due process. (Choice C) Some news broadcasts are absolutely privileged under the First and Fourteenth Amendments—eg, shows broadcast with the entertainer's consent—and shield the broadcaster from liability for interfering with another's right to publicity (ie, invasion of privacy). But no absolute privilege applies to the newscast at issue here. (Choice D) The First and Fourteenth Amendments authorize some press coverage of newsworthy entertainment events. But they do not authorize the unapproved broadcasting of an entire event because that would seriously infringe upon the entertainer's right to publicity.\n\nEducational objective: There is a First Amendment right to report on newsworthy events. But there is no right to publish an entertainer's entire act without his/her consent since that would seriously infringe upon the entertainer's right to publicity.\n\nReferences\n• Zacchini v. Scripps-Howard Broad. Co., 433 U.S. 562, 578 (1977) (holding that the press does not have a First Amendment right to broadcast the entirety of a private individual's performance).","educationalObjective":"There is a First Amendment right to report on newsworthy events. But there is no right to publish an entertainer's entire act without his/her consent since that would seriously infringe upon the entertainer's right to publicity.","references":"• Zacchini v. Scripps-Howard Broad. Co., 433 U.S. 562, 578 (1977) (holding that the press does not have a First Amendment right to broadcast the entirety of a private individual's performance).","rule":"There is a First Amendment right to report on newsworthy events. But there is no right to publish an entertainer's entire act without his/her consent since that would seriously infringe upon the entertainer's right to publicity.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule on the rider's suit?","rule":"There is a First Amendment right to report on newsworthy events. But there is no right to publish an entertainer's entire act without his/her consent since that would seriously infringe upon the entertainer's right to publicity.","analysis":"The First Amendment—applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment— protects the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance (ie, newsworthy events). But this right is not unlimited and must be balanced against the right to publicity—ie, the right to control or commercially benefit from one's own name, likeness, or other intimately associated qualities. Here, the station published the rider's entire performance—a newsworthy event—on its evening newscast without the rider's consent. That newscast deprived the rider of the commercial value of his performance since people are less likely to pay to see what they can now view for free. Therefore, the First and Fourteenth Amendments offer the station no protection, and the court should find the station liable. (Choice A) The Fourteenth Amendment prohibits the government from depriving an individual of life, liberty, or property without due process. But since the television station is a private entity, it is not required to provide due process. (Choice C) Some news broadcasts are absolutely privileged under the First and Fourteenth Amendments—eg, shows broadcast with the entertainer's consent—and shield the broadcaster from liability for interfering with another's right to publicity (ie, invasion of privacy). But no absolute privilege applies to the newscast at issue here. (Choice D) The First and Fourteenth Amendments authorize some press coverage of newsworthy entertainment events. But they do not authorize the unapproved broadcasting of an entire event because that would seriously infringe upon the entertainer's right to publicity.","conclusion":"Answer B: Find the station liable, because the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not deprive an entertainer of the commercial value of his or her performances."},"questionZh":"一名职业摩托车骑手在一个私人体育场进行表演，他驾驶摩托车跳过了 21 辆汽车。观看跳台表演的观众每人需支付 5 美元，并且禁止使用相机。然而，当地电视台在骑手不知情或未同意的情况下，在体育场内拍摄了整个比赛过程，并在当天晚间新闻联播中完整播放了该影片。随后，该骑手向电视台提起诉讼，要求电视台赔偿其未经授权拍摄和播放表演的损失。电视台只提出了宪法辩护。法院该如何审理骑手的诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"认定该电台负有责任，因为其行为未经正当程序剥夺了乘客的财产。","B":"要求电视台承担责任，因为第一修正案和第十四修正案并没有剥夺艺人表演的商业价值。","C":"反对附加条款，因为根据第一修正案和第十四修正案，新闻广播享有绝对特权。","D":"反对附加条款，因为第一修正案和第十四修正案授权媒体报道有新闻价值的娱乐活动。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）保护发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的事件（即有新闻价值的事件）的真实信息的权利。但这种权利并不是无限的，必须与公开权相平衡——即控制自己的姓名、肖像或其他密切相关的品质或从中获得商业利益的权利。在这里，电视台在未经骑手同意的情况下，在晚间新闻广播中公布了骑手的整个表演（具有新闻价值的事件）。该新闻广播剥夺了骑手表演的商业价值，因为人们不太可能付费观看他们现在可以免费观看的内容。因此，第一修正案和第十四修正案没有为电视台提供任何保护，法院应认定电视台承担责任。 （选项 A） 第十四修正案禁止政府未经正当程序剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产。但由于电视台是私人实体，因此不需要提供正当程序。 （选项C） 根据第一修正案和第十四修正案，某些新闻广播享有绝对特权——例如，经演艺人员同意而播出的节目——并且使广播公司免于因干扰他人的公开权（即侵犯隐私）而承担责任。但这里所讨论的新闻广播没有绝对的特权。 （选项 D） 第一修正案和第十四修正案授权媒体对有新闻价值的娱乐事件进行报道。但他们不会授权未经批准转播整个活动，因为这会严重侵犯艺人的公开权。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案赋予报道具有新闻价值事件的权利。但未经艺人同意，无权将艺人的全部表演公开，因为这将严重侵犯艺人的公开权。\n\n参考文献\n• Zacchini 诉Scripps-Howard Broad。 Co., 433 U.S. 562, 578 (1977)（认为媒体没有第一修正案权利来广播私人个人的全部表演）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案赋予报道有新闻价值事件的权利。但未经艺人同意，无权将艺人的全部表演公开，因为这将严重侵犯艺人的公开权。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院该如何审理骑手的诉讼？","rule":"第一修正案赋予报道有新闻价值事件的权利。但未经艺人同意，无权将艺人的全部表演公开，因为这将严重侵犯艺人的公开权。","analysis":"第一修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）保护发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的事件（即有新闻价值的事件）的真实信息的权利。但这种权利并不是无限的，必须与公开权相平衡——即控制自己的姓名、肖像或其他密切相关的品质或从中获得商业利益的权利。在这里，电视台在未经骑手同意的情况下，在晚间新闻广播中公布了骑手的整个表演（具有新闻价值的事件）。该新闻广播剥夺了骑手表演的商业价值，因为人们不太可能付费观看他们现在可以免费观看的内容。因此，第一修正案和第十四修正案没有为电视台提供任何保护，法院应认定电视台承担责任。 （选项 A） 第十四修正案禁止政府未经正当程序剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产。但由于电视台是私人实体，因此不需要提供正当程序。 （选项C） 根据第一修正案和第十四修正案，某些新闻广播享有绝对特权——例如，经演艺人员同意而播出的节目——并且使广播公司免于因干扰他人的公开权（即侵犯隐私）而承担责任。但这里所讨论的新闻广播没有绝对的特权。 （选项 D） 第一修正案和第十四修正案授权媒体对有新闻价值的娱乐事件进行报道。但他们不会授权未经批准转播整个活动，因为这会严重侵犯艺人的公开权。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案赋予报道具有新闻价值事件的权利。但未经艺人同意，无权将艺人的全部表演公开，因为这将严重侵犯艺人的公开权。\n\n参考文献\n• Zacchini 诉Scripps-Howard Broad。 Co., 433 U.S. 562, 578 (1977)（认为媒体没有第一修正案权利来广播私人个人的全部表演）。","conclusion":"答案 B：要求电视台承担责任，因为第一修正案和第十四修正案并没有剥夺艺人表演的商业价值。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"rider","ipa":"/ɹˈaɪdɝ/","syllables":"rider","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 骑手, (文件后的)附件, 扶手；[化] 游码","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 骑手, (文件后的)附件, 扶手；[化] 游码","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:riders","sourceSentence":"A professional motorcycle rider put on a performance in a privately owned stadium during which he leaped his motorcycle over 21 automobiles.","synonyms":["passenger"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":161,"sourceQuestionPage":481,"sourceQuestionPages":[481],"sourceExplanationPages":[482],"sourceAnswerPages":[482],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-162","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"A state statute declares that after five years of continuous service in their positions all state employees, including faculty members at the state university, are entitled to retain their positions during \"good behavior.\" The statute also contains a number of procedural provisions. Any state employee who is dismissed after that five-year period must be given reasons for the dismissal before it takes effect. In addition, such an employee must, upon request, be granted a post-dismissal hearing before an administrative board to seek reinstatement and back pay. The statute precludes any other hearing or opportunity to respond to the charges. That post-dismissal hearing must occur within six months after the dismissal takes effect. The burden of proof at such a hearing is on the state, and the board may uphold the dismissal only if it is supported by a preponderance of the evidence. An employee who is dissatisfied with a decision of the board after a hearing may appeal its decision to the state courts. The provisions of this statute are inseverable. A teacher who had been employed continuously for seven years as a faculty member at the state university was dismissed. A week before the dismissal took effect, she was informed that she was being dismissed because of a charge that she accepted a bribe from a student in return for raising the student's final grade in her course. At that time, she requested an immediate hearing to contest the propriety of her dismissal. Three months after her dismissal, she was granted a hearing before the state administrative board. The board upheld her dismissal, finding that the charge against her was supported by a preponderance of the evidence presented at the hearing. The teacher did not appeal the decision of the state administrative board to the state courts. Instead, she sought a declaratory judgment in federal district court to the effect that the state statute prescribing the procedures for her dismissal violates the United States Constitution. In this case, how should the federal district court rule?","choices":{"A":"Dismiss the suit, because a claim that a state statute is unconstitutional is not ripe for adjudication by a federal court until all judicial remedies in state courts provided for by state law have been exhausted.","B":"Hold that the statute is constitutional, because the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment entitles state employees who have a right to their jobs during good behavior only to a statement of reasons for their dismissal and an opportunity for a post-dismissal hearing.","C":"Hold that the statute is unconstitutional, because a state may not ordinarily deprive an employee of a property interest in a job without giving the employee an opportunity for some kind of a pre-dismissal hearing to respond to the charges against that employee.","D":"Hold that the statute is unconstitutional, because the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment requires a state to demonstrate beyond a reasonable doubt the facts constituting good cause for termination of a state employee."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Deprivation of public employment\n\nType Due process requirements\n\nTermination\n• Notice of alleged misconduct\n• Pre-termination opportunity to respond to allegation (serious)\n• Post-termination evidentiary hearing Suspension without pay\n• Notice of alleged misconduct\n• Prompt post-suspension evidentiary hearing (less serious)\n\nWhen a state attempts to deprive an individual of life, liberty, or property, the Fourteenth Amendment requires that the state provide procedural due process. To determine the process due, courts balance the government's interest against the individual's interest. And since a public employee who can only be terminated for cause has a property interest in such employment—and termination is a serious deprivation of that interest—due process requires that the employee receive:\n• notice of his/her alleged misconduct\n• a pre-termination opportunity to respond to that allegation and\n• a post-termination evidentiary hearing to determine if the termination was warranted. Here, the state-university teacher had a property interest in her job because she was entitled to retain her position during \"good behavior\" and could only be terminated for cause. The university provided the teacher with notice and a post-termination evidentiary hearing. But since the statute precludes any other hearing or opportunity to respond to the charge, she did not have a pre-termination opportunity to respond. Therefore, the court should hold that the statute violates due process (Choice B). (Choice A) Under the doctrine of administrative remedies, a constitutional claim is not ripe for adjudication until the plaintiff has exhausted all administrative remedies provided by law. But this doctrine does not require plaintiffs to exhaust all state judicial remedies before proceeding to federal court. (Choice D) Due process does not require a state to prove good cause for terminating an employee. And even if a state chooses to require good cause, due process does not require the state to prove it beyond a reasonable doubt.\n\nEducational objective: When a public employee can only be fired for cause, procedural due process generally requires that the employee receive (1) notice of any alleged misconduct, (2) a pre-\n\ntermination hearing to respond to the allegation, and (3) a post-termination evidentiary hearing to determine if the dismissal was warranted.\n\nReferences\n• Cleveland Bd. of Educ. v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 542–46 (1985) (explaining the process due when the government terminates a public employee who can only be fired for cause).","educationalObjective":"When a public employee can only be fired for cause, procedural due process generally requires that the employee receive (1) notice of any alleged misconduct, (2) a pre-\n\ntermination hearing to respond to the allegation, and (3) a post-termination evidentiary hearing to determine if the dismissal was warranted.","references":"• Cleveland Bd. of Educ. v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 542–46 (1985) (explaining the process due when the government terminates a public employee who can only be fired for cause).","rule":"When a public employee can only be fired for cause, procedural due process generally requires that the employee receive (1) notice of any alleged misconduct, (2) a pre-\n\ntermination hearing to respond to the allegation, and (3) a post-termination evidentiary hearing to determine if the dismissal was warranted.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, how should the federal district court rule?","rule":"When a public employee can only be fired for cause, procedural due process generally requires that the employee receive (1) notice of any alleged misconduct, (2) a pre-\n\ntermination hearing to respond to the allegation, and (3) a post-termination evidentiary hearing to determine if the dismissal was warranted.","analysis":"Deprivation of public employment\n\nType Due process requirements\n\nTermination\n• Notice of alleged misconduct\n• Pre-termination opportunity to respond to allegation (serious)\n• Post-termination evidentiary hearing Suspension without pay\n• Notice of alleged misconduct\n• Prompt post-suspension evidentiary hearing (less serious)\n\nWhen a state attempts to deprive an individual of life, liberty, or property, the Fourteenth Amendment requires that the state provide procedural due process. To determine the process due, courts balance the government's interest against the individual's interest. And since a public employee who can only be terminated for cause has a property interest in such employment—and termination is a serious deprivation of that interest—due process requires that the employee receive:\n• notice of his/her alleged misconduct\n• a pre-termination opportunity to respond to that allegation and\n• a post-termination evidentiary hearing to determine if the termination was warranted. Here, the state-university teacher had a property interest in her job because she was entitled to retain her position during \"good behavior\" and could only be terminated for cause. The university provided the teacher with notice and a post-termination evidentiary hearing. But since the statute precludes any other hearing or opportunity to respond to the charge, she did not have a pre-termination opportunity to respond. Therefore, the court should hold that the statute violates due process (Choice B). (Choice A) Under the doctrine of administrative remedies, a constitutional claim is not ripe for adjudication until the plaintiff has exhausted all administrative remedies provided by law. But this doctrine does not require plaintiffs to exhaust all state judicial remedies before proceeding to federal court. (Choice D) Due process does not require a state to prove good cause for terminating an employee. And even if a state chooses to require good cause, due process does not require the state to prove it beyond a reasonable doubt.","conclusion":"Answer C: Hold that the statute is unconstitutional, because a state may not ordinarily deprive an employee of a property interest in a job without giving the employee an opportunity for some kind of a pre-dismissal hearing to respond to the charges against that employee."},"questionZh":"一项州法规规定，在其职位上连续服务五年后，所有州雇员，包括州立大学的教职人员，都有权在“表现良好”的情况下保留其职位。该法规还包含一些程序性规定。任何在五年期限后被解雇的国家雇员都必须在解雇生效前给出解雇理由。此外，此类雇员必须根据要求获得行政委员会举行解雇后听证会的许可，以寻求复职和欠薪。该法规排除了任何其他听证会或对指控作出回应的机会。解雇后听证会必须在解雇生效后六个月内举行。在此类听证会上，州政府负有举证责任，只有在得到大量证据支持的情况下，委员会才可以维持驳回决定。听证会后对董事会决定不满意的员工可以向州法院提出上诉。本法规的规定是不可分割的。一名在州立大学连续任教七年的教师被解雇。解雇生效前一周，她被告知被解雇，因为她被指控接受一名学生的贿赂，以换取提高该学生课程的期末成绩。当时，她要求立即举行听证会，对解雇她的行为是否恰当提出质疑。被解雇三个月后，她获准在国家行政委员会举行听证会。委员会维持了对她的解雇，认为对她的指控得到了听证会上提供的大量证据的支持。该教师没有向州法院上诉州行政委员会的决定。相反，她向联邦地区法院寻求宣告性判决，认为规定解雇她程序的州法规违反了美国宪法。对于这种情况，联邦地方法院该如何裁决呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回诉讼，因为在用尽州法律规定的州法院的所有司法补救措施之前，联邦法院裁决州法规违宪的时机还不成熟。","B":"认为该法规符合宪法，因为第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）赋予在行为良好期间有权继续工作的国家雇员仅有权获得解雇理由声明以及解雇后听证会的机会。","C":"认为该法规违宪，因为州通常不得剥夺雇员在工作中的财产权益，而不给予雇员举行某种解雇前听证会以回应针对该雇员的指控的机会。","D":"认为该法规违宪，因为第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求州必须毫无合理怀疑地证明构成解雇州雇员的正当理由的事实。"},"explanationZh":"剥夺公共就业机会\n\n类型 正当程序要求\n\n终止\n• 涉嫌不当行为的通知\n• 解雇前回应指控的机会（严重）\n• 解雇后证据听证会 无薪停职\n• 涉嫌不当行为的通知\n• 暂停后立即举行证据听证会（不太严重）\n\n当一个州试图剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产时，第十四修正案要求该州提供程序上的正当程序。为了确定适当的程序，法院会平衡政府的利益与个人的利益。由于只能因故被解雇的公职人员在此类雇佣关系中拥有财产利益，而解雇是对该利益的严重剥夺，因此正当程序要求该雇员获得：\n• 关于他/她涉嫌不当行为的通知\n• 终止合同前有机会回应该指控，并且\n• 终止后证据听证会以确定终止是否合理。在这里，国立大学的老师在她的工作中拥有财产权益，因为她有权在“表现良好”期间保留自己的职位，并且只能因故被解雇。大学向该老师发出了通知并举行了终止后证据听证会。但由于该法规排除了任何其他听证会或对指控做出回应的机会，因此她没有在终止合同前做出回应的机会。因此，法院应认定该法规违反正当程序（选项B）。 （选项A）根据行政救济原则，在原告用尽法律规定的所有行政救济之前，宪法诉讼的裁决时机并不成熟。但这一原则并不要求原告在向联邦法院提起诉讼之前用尽所有州司法补救措施。 （选择 D）正当程序不要求州政府证明解雇雇员的充分理由。即使一个州选择要求有正当理由，正当程序也不要求该州证明这一点是毫无疑问的。\n\n教育目标：当公职人员只能因故被解雇时，程序正当程序通常要求该雇员收到 (1) 任何涉嫌不当行为的通知，(2) 预\n\n解雇听证会以回应指控，以及 (3) 解雇后证据听证会以确定解雇是否合理。\n\n参考文献\n• 克利夫兰Bd。教育的。 v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 542–46 (1985)（解释了政府解雇只能因故被解雇的公职人员时应采取的程序）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当公职人员只能因故被解雇时，程序正当程序通常要求该雇员收到 (1) 任何涉嫌不当行为的通知，(2)\n\n解雇听证会以回应指控，以及 (3) 解雇后证据听证会以确定解雇是否合理。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于这种情况，联邦地方法院该如何裁决呢？","rule":"当公职人员只能因故被解雇时，程序正当程序通常要求该雇员收到 (1) 任何涉嫌不当行为的通知，(2)\n\n解雇听证会以回应指控，以及 (3) 解雇后证据听证会以确定解雇是否合理。","analysis":"剥夺公共就业机会\n\n类型 正当程序要求\n\n终止\n• 涉嫌不当行为的通知\n• 解雇前回应指控的机会（严重）\n• 解雇后证据听证会 无薪停职\n• 涉嫌不当行为的通知\n• 暂停后立即举行证据听证会（不太严重）\n\n当一个州试图剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产时，第十四修正案要求该州提供程序上的正当程序。为了确定适当的程序，法院会平衡政府的利益与个人的利益。由于只能因故被解雇的公职人员在此类雇佣关系中拥有财产利益，而解雇是对该利益的严重剥夺，因此正当程序要求该雇员获得：\n• 关于他/她涉嫌不当行为的通知\n• 终止合同前有机会回应该指控，并且\n• 终止后证据听证会以确定终止是否合理。在这里，国立大学的老师在她的工作中拥有财产权益，因为她有权在“表现良好”期间保留自己的职位，并且只能因故被解雇。大学向该老师发出了通知并举行了终止后证据听证会。但由于该法规排除了任何其他听证会或对指控做出回应的机会，因此她没有在终止合同前做出回应的机会。因此，法院应认定该法规违反正当程序（选项B）。 （选项A）根据行政救济原则，在原告用尽法律规定的所有行政救济之前，宪法诉讼的裁决时机并不成熟。但这一原则并不要求原告在向联邦法院提起诉讼之前用尽所有州司法补救措施。 （选择 D）正当程序不要求州政府证明解雇雇员的充分理由。即使一个州选择要求有正当理由，正当程序也不要求该州证明这一点是毫无疑问的。\n\n教育目标：当公职人员只能因故被解雇时，程序正当程序通常要求该雇员收到 (1) 任何涉嫌不当行为的通知，(2) 预\n\n解雇听证会以回应指控，以及 (3) 解雇后证据听证会以确定解雇是否合理。\n\n参考文献\n• 克利夫兰Bd。教育的。 v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 542–46 (1985)（解释了政府解雇只能因故被解雇的公职人员时应采取的程序）。","conclusion":"答案 C：认为该法规违宪，因为州通常不得剥夺雇员在工作中的财产权益，而不给予雇员举行某种解雇前听证会以回应针对该雇员的指控的机会。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"allegation","ipa":"/ˌælʌɡˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"al·le·ga·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 断言, 主张, 申辩；[法] 声明, 事实陈述, 断言","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 断言, 主张, 申辩；[法] 声明, 事实陈述, 断言","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:allegations","sourceSentence":"When a public employee can only be fired for cause, procedural due process generally requires that the employee receive (1) notice of any alleged misconduct, (2) a pre- termination hearing to respond to the allegation, and (3) a post-termination evidentiary hearing to determine if the dismissal was warranted.","synonyms":["allegement"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":162,"sourceQuestionPage":483,"sourceQuestionPages":[483],"sourceExplanationPages":[484,485],"sourceAnswerPages":[484,485],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-163","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"In order to foster an environment conducive to learning, a school board enacted a dress code that prohibited all public high school students from wearing in school shorts cut above the knee. Because female students at the school considered it unfashionable to wear shorts cut at or below the knee, they no longer wore shorts to school. On the other hand, male students at the school regularly wore shorts cut at or below the knee because they considered such shorts to be fashionable. Female students sued to challenge the constitutionality of the dress code on the ground that it denied them the equal protection of the laws. Should the court uphold the dress code?","choices":{"A":"No, because the dress code is not necessary to further a compelling state interest.","B":"No, because the dress code is not substantially related to an important state interest.","C":"Yes, because the dress code is narrowly tailored to further an important state interest.","D":"Yes, because the dress code is rationally related to a legitimate state interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Types of discrimination\n\nType Description Proof of intent\n\nFacially\n• Language of law\n• Discriminatory intent discriminatory distinguishes between presumed classes of persons\n• Eg, law states \"all state employees must be male\" Discriminatory\n• Facially neutral law\n• Purposeful application applied differently to discrimination different classes of persons\n• Eg, female applicants\n• Eg, state hires only male equally qualified applicants Discriminatory\n• Law neutral on its face & in\n• Purposeful impact its application, but discrimination disproportionately impacts\n• Eg, legislative history particular class shows intent to exclude\n• Eg, 6' height requirement female applicants mostly affects female applicants Discriminatory government regulations are permissible so long as they withstand equal protection review. The default standard is rational basis review, which requires the challenging party to show that the regulation is not rationally related to any legitimate government interest. But a heightened standard of review applies when the challenger proves that the government intentionally discriminated against a quasi-suspect class or suspect class (or substantially impacted a fundamental right). Here, the dress code is not facially discriminatory since it applies to \"all public high school students.\" Nor is it discriminatory in its application since the dress code is applied equally to male and female students. And though it has a discriminatory impact on female students, there is no evidence that the school board intentionally targeted that quasi-suspect class. Therefore, the court will apply rational basis review and uphold the dress code since it is rationally related to the school's legitimate interest in fostering an environment conducive to learning. (Choice A) Proof that a regulation is necessary to further a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny) is required when the regulation intentionally discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right—neither of which is implicated here.\n\n(Choice B) A regulation must be substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny) when the regulation intentionally discriminates against a quasi- suspect class (ie, sex/gender, legitimacy). Here, the dress code may discriminate based on gender, but that discrimination was not intentional. (Choice C) There is no level of scrutiny requiring that a regulation be narrowly tailored to further an important state interest.\n\nEducational objective: A law that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its impact—against a quasi- suspect class or suspect class will only be subject to heightened scrutiny if the government engaged in intentional discrimination.\n\nReferences\n• Washington v. Davis, 426 U.S. 229, 242 (1976) (laws that disproportionately impact a protected class are unconstitutional if the government acted intentionally).","educationalObjective":"A law that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its impact—against a quasi- suspect class or suspect class will only be subject to heightened scrutiny if the government engaged in intentional discrimination.","references":"• Washington v. Davis, 426 U.S. 229, 242 (1976) (laws that disproportionately impact a protected class are unconstitutional if the government acted intentionally).","rule":"A law that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its impact—against a quasi- suspect class or suspect class will only be subject to heightened scrutiny if the government engaged in intentional discrimination.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court uphold the dress code?","rule":"A law that discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its impact—against a quasi- suspect class or suspect class will only be subject to heightened scrutiny if the government engaged in intentional discrimination.","analysis":"Types of discrimination\n\nType Description Proof of intent\n\nFacially\n• Language of law\n• Discriminatory intent discriminatory distinguishes between presumed classes of persons\n• Eg, law states \"all state employees must be male\" Discriminatory\n• Facially neutral law\n• Purposeful application applied differently to discrimination different classes of persons\n• Eg, female applicants\n• Eg, state hires only male equally qualified applicants Discriminatory\n• Law neutral on its face & in\n• Purposeful impact its application, but discrimination disproportionately impacts\n• Eg, legislative history particular class shows intent to exclude\n• Eg, 6' height requirement female applicants mostly affects female applicants Discriminatory government regulations are permissible so long as they withstand equal protection review. The default standard is rational basis review, which requires the challenging party to show that the regulation is not rationally related to any legitimate government interest. But a heightened standard of review applies when the challenger proves that the government intentionally discriminated against a quasi-suspect class or suspect class (or substantially impacted a fundamental right). Here, the dress code is not facially discriminatory since it applies to \"all public high school students.\" Nor is it discriminatory in its application since the dress code is applied equally to male and female students. And though it has a discriminatory impact on female students, there is no evidence that the school board intentionally targeted that quasi-suspect class. Therefore, the court will apply rational basis review and uphold the dress code since it is rationally related to the school's legitimate interest in fostering an environment conducive to learning. (Choice A) Proof that a regulation is necessary to further a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny) is required when the regulation intentionally discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right—neither of which is implicated here.\n\n(Choice B) A regulation must be substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny) when the regulation intentionally discriminates against a quasi- suspect class (ie, sex/gender, legitimacy). Here, the dress code may discriminate based on gender, but that discrimination was not intentional. (Choice C) There is no level of scrutiny requiring that a regulation be narrowly tailored to further an important state interest.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the dress code is rationally related to a legitimate state interest."},"questionZh":"为了营造一个有利于学习的环境，学校董事会颁布了一项着装规定，禁止所有公立高中学生穿着膝盖以上的学校短裤。由于学校里的女学生认为穿膝盖以下的短裤不时尚，所以她们不再穿短裤上学。另一方面，学校的男学生经常穿膝盖或膝盖以下的短裤，因为他们认为这样的短裤很时尚。女学生提起诉讼，质疑着装规范的合宪性，理由是它剥夺了她们受到法律的平等保护。法庭应该遵守着装规定吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为着装规范对于促进引人注目的国家利益来说并不是必要的。","B":"不，因为着装要求与重要的国家利益没有实质性关系。","C":"是的，因为着装规范是为了促进重要的国家利益而专门制定的。","D":"是的，因为着装规范与合法的国家利益合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"歧视的类型\n\n类型 描述 意图证明\n\n面部\n• 法律语言\n• 歧视性意图 歧视性地区分了假定的人群类别\n• 例如，法律规定“所有国家雇员必须是男性” 歧视\n• 面部中立法\n• 有目的地应用不同的方式来歧视不同类别的人\n• 例如，女性申请人\n• 例如，国家只雇用同等资格的男性申请人 歧视\n• 表面上和实质上法律中立\n• 有目的地影响其应用，但歧视影响不成比例\n• 例如，特定类别的立法历史显示出排除的意图\n• 例如，6 英尺身高要求女性申请人对女性申请人的影响最大。 只要能够经受平等保护审查，歧视性的政府法规是允许的。默认标准是合理基础审查，要求质疑方证明该监管与任何合法政府利益不合理相关。但是，当挑战者证明政府故意歧视准可疑阶层或可疑阶层（或严重影响基本权利）时，适用更高的审查标准。在这里，着装要求并不存在面部歧视，因为它适用于“所有公立高中学生”。它的应用也不存在歧视，因为着装要求对男女学生同样适用。尽管这对女学生有歧视性影响，但没有证据表明学校董事会有意针对那个准可疑班级。因此，法院将进行合理的基础审查并维持着装规范，因为它与学校营造有利于学习的环境的合法利益合理相关。 （选择A）当法规故意歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利时（此处未涉及这两种情况），需要证明法规对于促进令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。\n\n（选择B）当法规故意歧视准嫌疑阶层（即性别、合法性）时，该法规必须与重要的国家利益（即中间审查）实质相关。在这里，着装规范可能存在性别歧视，但这种歧视并不是故意的。 （选项 C）不存在任何程度的审查要求严格制定监管措施以促进重要的国家利益。\n\n教育目标：如果政府故意歧视，一项在表面、应用或影响上歧视准嫌疑阶级或嫌疑阶级的法律只会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n参考文献\n• Washington v. Davis, 426 U.S. 229, 242 (1976)（如果政府故意行事，对受保护阶层产生不成比例影响的法律是违宪的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一项在表面、应用或影响上歧视准嫌疑阶级或嫌疑阶级的法律只有在政府故意歧视的情况下才会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭应该遵守着装规定吗？","rule":"一项在表面、应用或影响上歧视准嫌疑阶级或嫌疑阶级的法律只有在政府故意歧视的情况下才会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。","analysis":"歧视的类型\n\n类型 描述 意图证明\n\n面部\n• 法律语言\n• 歧视性意图 歧视性地区分了假定的人群类别\n• 例如，法律规定“所有国家雇员必须是男性” 歧视\n• 面部中立法\n• 有目的地应用不同的方式来歧视不同类别的人\n• 例如，女性申请人\n• 例如，国家只雇用同等资格的男性申请人 歧视\n• 表面上和实质上法律中立\n• 有目的地影响其应用，但歧视影响不成比例\n• 例如，特定类别的立法历史显示出排除的意图\n• 例如，6 英尺身高要求女性申请人对女性申请人的影响最大。 只要能够经受平等保护审查，歧视性的政府法规是允许的。默认标准是合理基础审查，要求质疑方证明该监管与任何合法政府利益不合理相关。但是，当挑战者证明政府故意歧视准可疑阶层或可疑阶层（或严重影响基本权利）时，适用更高的审查标准。在这里，着装要求并不存在面部歧视，因为它适用于“所有公立高中学生”。它的应用也不存在歧视，因为着装要求对男女学生同样适用。尽管这对女学生有歧视性影响，但没有证据表明学校董事会有意针对那个准可疑班级。因此，法院将进行合理的基础审查并维持着装规范，因为它与学校营造有利于学习的环境的合法利益合理相关。 （选择A）当法规故意歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利时（此处未涉及这两种情况），需要证明法规对于促进令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。\n\n（选择B）当法规故意歧视准嫌疑阶层（即性别、合法性）时，该法规必须与重要的国家利益（即中间审查）实质相关。在这里，着装规范可能存在性别歧视，但这种歧视并不是故意的。 （选项 C）不存在任何程度的审查要求严格制定监管措施以促进重要的国家利益。\n\n教育目标：如果政府故意歧视，一项在表面、应用或影响上歧视准嫌疑阶级或嫌疑阶级的法律只会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n参考文献\n• Washington v. Davis, 426 U.S. 229, 242 (1976)（如果政府故意行事，对受保护阶层产生不成比例影响的法律是违宪的）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为着装规范与合法的国家利益合理相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":163,"sourceQuestionPage":486,"sourceQuestionPages":[486],"sourceExplanationPages":[487,488],"sourceAnswerPages":[487,488],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-164","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A city passed an ordinance requiring individuals to obtain a license in order to care for children under the age of 12 for pay. City officials who promoted the ordinance said that the licensing process would ensure that child-care workers were adequately regulated for the health and safety of the city's children. To receive a license, the ordinance requires an individual to complete 10 hours of instruction in child care, undergo a background check, and pay a $100 fee. The ordinance affects women disproportionately to men, because female child-care workers far outnumber male child-care workers in the city. Is the ordinance constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it has a disparate impact on women without a showing that the ordinance is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.","B":"No, because it infringes on the freedom of contract without a compelling government interest.","C":"Yes, because any burden it imposes is clearly outweighed by an important government objective.","D":"Yes, because it is rationally related to a legitimate government objective."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Discriminatory laws challenged under the equal protection clause are generally subject to rational basis scrutiny, which requires that a law be rationally related to a legitimate government objective. But heightened scrutiny is used when a law (1) substantially impacts a fundamental right or (2) intentionally discriminates against a suspect or quasi- suspect class. Intentional discrimination can be seen on a law's:\n• face – the language of the law distinguishes between different classes (intent presumed)\n• application – a facially neutral law is purposefully applied differently to different classes or\n• motive – an otherwise neutral law was enacted to disproportionately impact a protected class. Here, although the ordinance disproportionately affects women (quasi-suspect class), it is not subject to heightened scrutiny since there is no evidence that this discrimination was intentional. That is because the ordinance:\n• requires that all child-care workers obtain a license (no facial discrimination)\n• is applied equally to men and women (no discriminatory application) and\n• was not enacted to target women (no discriminatory motive). And since the ordinance is rationally related to the government's legitimate interest in the health and safety of children, it is constitutional. (Choice A) The city would have had to prove that the ordinance was necessary to serve a compelling government interest (ie, strict scrutiny) had it substantially impacted a fundamental right or intentionally discriminated against a suspect class. Since the ordinance does neither, it need only survive rational basis review. (Choice B) The freedom of contract is protected by the contracts clause, which restricts state and local legislatures from impairing existing contracts. Since the ordinance affects the ability to enter into future contracts for child-care services, it does not violate this clause. (Choice C) Had the ordinance intentionally discriminated against women, the city would have had to prove that the ordinance is substantially related to an important government objective (ie, intermediate scrutiny)—not that this objective clearly outweighs any burden the ordinance imposes.\n\nEducational objective: Heightened equal protection review is appropriate when a law intentionally discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its motive—against a quasi-suspect or suspect class.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Pers. Adm'r of Mass. v. Feeney, 442 U.S. 256, 274 (1979) (explaining that a neutral law's disparate impact on a quasi-suspect or suspect class, standing alone, is insufficient to require heightened scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"Heightened equal protection review is appropriate when a law intentionally discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its motive—against a quasi-suspect or suspect class.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Pers. Adm'r of Mass. v. Feeney, 442 U.S. 256, 274 (1979) (explaining that a neutral law's disparate impact on a quasi-suspect or suspect class, standing alone, is insufficient to require heightened scrutiny).","rule":"Heightened equal protection review is appropriate when a law intentionally discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its motive—against a quasi-suspect or suspect class.","irac":{"issue":"Is the ordinance constitutional?","rule":"Heightened equal protection review is appropriate when a law intentionally discriminates—on its face, in its application, or in its motive—against a quasi-suspect or suspect class.","analysis":"Discriminatory laws challenged under the equal protection clause are generally subject to rational basis scrutiny, which requires that a law be rationally related to a legitimate government objective. But heightened scrutiny is used when a law (1) substantially impacts a fundamental right or (2) intentionally discriminates against a suspect or quasi- suspect class. Intentional discrimination can be seen on a law's:\n• face – the language of the law distinguishes between different classes (intent presumed)\n• application – a facially neutral law is purposefully applied differently to different classes or\n• motive – an otherwise neutral law was enacted to disproportionately impact a protected class. Here, although the ordinance disproportionately affects women (quasi-suspect class), it is not subject to heightened scrutiny since there is no evidence that this discrimination was intentional. That is because the ordinance:\n• requires that all child-care workers obtain a license (no facial discrimination)\n• is applied equally to men and women (no discriminatory application) and\n• was not enacted to target women (no discriminatory motive). And since the ordinance is rationally related to the government's legitimate interest in the health and safety of children, it is constitutional. (Choice A) The city would have had to prove that the ordinance was necessary to serve a compelling government interest (ie, strict scrutiny) had it substantially impacted a fundamental right or intentionally discriminated against a suspect class. Since the ordinance does neither, it need only survive rational basis review. (Choice B) The freedom of contract is protected by the contracts clause, which restricts state and local legislatures from impairing existing contracts. Since the ordinance affects the ability to enter into future contracts for child-care services, it does not violate this clause. (Choice C) Had the ordinance intentionally discriminated against women, the city would have had to prove that the ordinance is substantially related to an important government objective (ie, intermediate scrutiny)—not that this objective clearly outweighs any burden the ordinance imposes.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because it is rationally related to a legitimate government objective."},"questionZh":"某城市通过了一项法令，要求个人必须获得许可证才能有偿照顾 12 岁以下儿童。推动该法令的市政府官员表示，许可程序将确保儿童保育工作者受到充分监管，以保障该市儿童的健康和安全。该法令要求个人完成 10 小时的儿童保育指导、接受背景调查并支付 100 美元的费用，才能获得执照。该法令对女性的影响与男性不成比例，因为该市女性保育员的数量远远多于男性保育员。该法令是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为它对女性有不同的影响，但没有表明该法令对于服务政府的迫切利益是必要的。","B":"不，因为它侵犯了合同自由，而没有令人信服的政府利益。","C":"是的，因为它所带来的任何负担显然都被重要的政府目标所抵消。","D":"是的，因为它与合法的政府目标合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑的歧视性法律通常会受到合理基础的审查，这要求法律与合法的政府目标合理相关。但是，当法律（1）严重影响基本权利或（2）故意歧视嫌疑人或准嫌疑人时，就会进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。故意歧视可见于法律：\n• 面子——法律语言区分不同阶级（推测意图）\n• 应用——表面中立的法律有目的地以不同的方式适用于不同的阶级或群体。\n• 动机——颁布原本中立的法律是为了对受保护阶层产生不成比例的影响。在这里，尽管该法令对女性（准嫌疑阶层）的影响不成比例，但由于没有证据表明这种歧视是故意的，因此并未受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。那是因为该法令：\n• 要求所有儿童保育人员获得执照（禁止面部歧视）\n• 对男性和女性同样适用（无歧视性适用）并且\n• 颁布并非针对女性（无歧视动机）。由于该法令与政府对儿童健康和安全的合法利益合理相关，因此它是符合宪法的。 （选项A）如果该法令严重影响了基本权利或故意歧视可疑阶层，则该市必须证明该法令对于服务于令人信服的政府利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。由于该法令两者都没有，因此它只需要经过合理的基础审查即可通过。 （选项B） 合同自由受到合同条款的保护，该条款限制州和地方立法机关损害现有合同。由于该法令影响未来签订儿童保育服务合同的能力，因此它并不违反本条款。 （选项C）如果该法令故意歧视妇女，该市将必须证明该法令与重要的政府目标（即中间审查）有实质性关系，而不是该目标明显超过该法令施加的任何负担。\n\n教育目标：当一项法律在其表面、适用或动机上故意歧视准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层时，加强平等保护审查是适当的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• 个人。 Adm'r of Mass. v. Feeney, 442 U.S. 256, 274 (1979)（解释说，中立法律对准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层的不同影响，单独存在，不足以要求加强审查）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一项法律在其表面、适用或动机上故意歧视准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层时，加强平等保护审查是适当的。","iracZh":{"issue":"该法令是否符合宪法？","rule":"当一项法律在其表面、适用或动机上故意歧视准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层时，加强平等保护审查是适当的。","analysis":"根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑的歧视性法律通常会受到合理基础的审查，这要求法律与合法的政府目标合理相关。但是，当法律（1）严重影响基本权利或（2）故意歧视嫌疑人或准嫌疑人时，就会进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。故意歧视可见于法律：\n• 面子——法律语言区分不同阶级（推测意图）\n• 应用——表面中立的法律有目的地以不同的方式适用于不同的阶级或群体。\n• 动机——颁布原本中立的法律是为了对受保护阶层产生不成比例的影响。在这里，尽管该法令对女性（准嫌疑阶层）的影响不成比例，但由于没有证据表明这种歧视是故意的，因此并未受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。那是因为该法令：\n• 要求所有儿童保育人员获得执照（禁止面部歧视）\n• 对男性和女性同样适用（无歧视性适用）并且\n• 颁布并非针对女性（无歧视动机）。由于该法令与政府对儿童健康和安全的合法利益合理相关，因此它是符合宪法的。 （选项A）如果该法令严重影响了基本权利或故意歧视可疑阶层，则该市必须证明该法令对于服务于令人信服的政府利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。由于该法令两者都没有，因此它只需要经过合理的基础审查即可通过。 （选项B） 合同自由受到合同条款的保护，该条款限制州和地方立法机关损害现有合同。由于该法令影响未来签订儿童保育服务合同的能力，因此它并不违反本条款。 （选项C）如果该法令故意歧视妇女，该市将必须证明该法令与重要的政府目标（即中间审查）有实质性关系，而不是该目标明显超过该法令施加的任何负担。\n\n教育目标：当一项法律在其表面、适用或动机上故意歧视准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层时，加强平等保护审查是适当的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• 个人。 Adm'r of Mass. v. Feeney, 442 U.S. 256, 274 (1979)（解释说，中立法律对准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层的不同影响，单独存在，不足以要求加强审查）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为它与合法的政府目标合理相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-c12a6454a83fe33d"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":164,"sourceQuestionPage":489,"sourceQuestionPages":[489],"sourceExplanationPages":[490,491],"sourceAnswerPages":[490,491],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-165","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A defendant was on trial for a highly publicized capital murder in a federal district court. The court issued an order that prohibited public attendance during voir dire to protect the prospective jurors' privacy, especially concerning sensitive issues. The court issued findings on the record and concluded that public attendance during voir dire would prevent the prospective jurors from answering sensitive questions without suffering personal embarrassment. Several television networks that are televising the trial have challenged the order, arguing that it is unconstitutional. Is the court's order likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the First Amendment guarantees the press and the public the right to attend criminal trials.","B":"No, because there are less restrictive means to protect the privacy of the prospective jurors.","C":"Yes, because the court has an interest in protecting prospective jurors from personal embarrassment.","D":"Yes, because the court-issued findings on the record warranted closing the proceedings to the public."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The First Amendment generally guarantees the press and the public the right to attend every stage of a criminal trial, including voir dire.* However, this right is not absolute, and a court can order that trial proceedings be closed if the court's findings on the record demonstrate that the closure satisfies strict scrutiny (Choice A). This requires the court to show that the closure is the least restrictive means to serve a compelling government interest. Here, the court prohibited the public from attending voir dire in the defendant's murder trial to protect the prospective jurors from personal embarrassment. This promotes the compelling interests of encouraging the prospective jurors to speak frankly about sensitive issues and protecting their privacy—but less restrictive means were available to serve these interests (Choice C). For example, the court could have allowed prospective jurors to answer any sensitive questions outside the presence of the public. Therefore, the order is likely unconstitutional. *The Supreme Court has not conclusively ruled whether the First Amendment guarantees the press and the public the right to attend civil trials. (Choice D) The court-issued findings on the record did not warrant closing the proceedings to the public because these findings did not demonstrate that the closure was the least restrictive means to protect the prospective jurors' privacy.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment generally guarantees the press and the public the right to attend criminal trials. However, criminal trial proceedings may be closed when the court demonstrates that the closure satisfies strict scrutiny—ie, that it is the least restrictive means to serve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Press-Enter. Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 464 U.S. 501, 511 (1984) (holding that closing all voir dire proceedings in a criminal trial to the public and press is unconstitutional).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment generally guarantees the press and the public the right to attend criminal trials. However, criminal trial proceedings may be closed when the court demonstrates that the closure satisfies strict scrutiny—ie, that it is the least restrictive means to serve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Press-Enter. Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 464 U.S. 501, 511 (1984) (holding that closing all voir dire proceedings in a criminal trial to the public and press is unconstitutional).","rule":"The First Amendment generally guarantees the press and the public the right to attend criminal trials. However, criminal trial proceedings may be closed when the court demonstrates that the closure satisfies strict scrutiny—ie, that it is the least restrictive means to serve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court's order likely constitutional?","rule":"The First Amendment generally guarantees the press and the public the right to attend criminal trials. However, criminal trial proceedings may be closed when the court demonstrates that the closure satisfies strict scrutiny—ie, that it is the least restrictive means to serve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment generally guarantees the press and the public the right to attend every stage of a criminal trial, including voir dire.* However, this right is not absolute, and a court can order that trial proceedings be closed if the court's findings on the record demonstrate that the closure satisfies strict scrutiny (Choice A). This requires the court to show that the closure is the least restrictive means to serve a compelling government interest. Here, the court prohibited the public from attending voir dire in the defendant's murder trial to protect the prospective jurors from personal embarrassment. This promotes the compelling interests of encouraging the prospective jurors to speak frankly about sensitive issues and protecting their privacy—but less restrictive means were available to serve these interests (Choice C). For example, the court could have allowed prospective jurors to answer any sensitive questions outside the presence of the public. Therefore, the order is likely unconstitutional. *The Supreme Court has not conclusively ruled whether the First Amendment guarantees the press and the public the right to attend civil trials. (Choice D) The court-issued findings on the record did not warrant closing the proceedings to the public because these findings did not demonstrate that the closure was the least restrictive means to protect the prospective jurors' privacy.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because there are less restrictive means to protect the privacy of the prospective jurors."},"questionZh":"一名被告因一起广为人知的死刑谋杀案在联邦地区法院受审。法院发布命令，禁止公众在预审期间出席，以保护潜在陪审员的隐私，尤其是涉及敏感问题的隐私。法院公布了记录在案的调查结果，并得出结论，在预审期间公众出席将阻止未来的陪审员回答敏感问题，而不会遭受个人尴尬。几家正在直播审判的电视网络对该命令提出质疑，认为该命令违宪。法院的命令可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为第一修正案保障新闻界和公众参加刑事审判的权利。","B":"不会，因为保护潜在陪审员隐私的限制性措施较少。","C":"是的，因为法院有兴趣保护未来的陪审员免受个人尴尬。","D":"是的，因为法院发布的记录在案的调查结果有必要向公众结束诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案一般保障新闻界和公众参与刑事审判各个阶段的权利，包括预审。*然而，这项权利不是绝对的，如果法院记录在案的调查结果表明结束审判程序符合严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），法院可以下令结束审判程序（选择 A）。这要求法院证明关闭是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。在这里，法院禁止公众旁听被告谋杀案的审判，以保护未来的陪审员免受个人尴尬。这促进了鼓励潜在陪审员坦诚谈论敏感问题并保护他们的隐私的迫切利益，但可以使用较少限制的手段来服务这些利益（选项 C）。例如，法院本可以允许未来的陪审员在公众不在场的情况下回答任何敏感问题。因此，该命令很可能违宪。 *最高法院尚未最终裁定第一修正案是否保障新闻界和公众参加民事审判的权利。 （选项 D） 法院发布的记录在案的调查结果并不保证向公众关闭诉讼程序，因为这些调查结果并没有表明关闭程序是保护未来陪审员隐私的限制性最小的手段。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案总体上保障新闻界和公众参加刑事审判的权利。然而，当法院证明关闭符合严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（即，这是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段）时，刑事审判程序可以关闭。\n\n参考文献\n• 按Enter 键。公司诉超级。 CT。加州，464 U.S. 501, 511 (1984)（认为在刑事审判中向公众和媒体关闭所有预审程序是违宪的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案总体上保障新闻界和公众参加刑事审判的权利。然而，当法院证明关闭符合严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（即，这是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段）时，刑事审判程序可以关闭。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院的命令可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"第一修正案总体上保障新闻界和公众参加刑事审判的权利。然而，当法院证明关闭符合严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（即，这是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段）时，刑事审判程序可以关闭。","analysis":"第一修正案一般保障新闻界和公众参与刑事审判各个阶段的权利，包括预审。*然而，这项权利不是绝对的，如果法院记录在案的调查结果表明结束审判程序符合严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），法院可以下令结束审判程序（选择 A）。这要求法院证明关闭是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。在这里，法院禁止公众旁听被告谋杀案的审判，以保护未来的陪审员免受个人尴尬。这促进了鼓励潜在陪审员坦诚谈论敏感问题并保护他们的隐私的迫切利益，但可以使用较少限制的手段来服务这些利益（选项 C）。例如，法院本可以允许未来的陪审员在公众不在场的情况下回答任何敏感问题。因此，该命令很可能违宪。 *最高法院尚未最终裁定第一修正案是否保障新闻界和公众参加民事审判的权利。 （选项 D） 法院发布的记录在案的调查结果并不保证向公众关闭诉讼程序，因为这些调查结果并没有表明关闭程序是保护未来陪审员隐私的限制性最小的手段。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案总体上保障新闻界和公众参加刑事审判的权利。然而，当法院证明关闭符合严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（即，这是服务于令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段）时，刑事审判程序可以关闭。\n\n参考文献\n• 按Enter 键。公司诉超级。 CT。加州，464 U.S. 501, 511 (1984)（认为在刑事审判中向公众和媒体关闭所有预审程序是违宪的）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为保护潜在陪审员隐私的限制性措施较少。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-fecfe5a85f7b13cf"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":165,"sourceQuestionPage":492,"sourceQuestionPages":[492],"sourceExplanationPages":[493,494],"sourceAnswerPages":[493,494],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-166","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A state law imposes penalties for \"any public statement containing false or misleading information about a service or product.\" An airline falsely claimed in an advertisement that its competitor had an inferior safety record. The claim was based on erroneous information, found on the website of a nonprofit consumer advocacy group, that the airline assumed to be true. The airline was charged under the state law for making a false statement. No federal statute applies. Which of the following best supports the airline in a defense based on the First Amendment?","choices":{"A":"Its statement about the safety record was made without malice.","B":"Its statement about the safety record was protected noncommercial speech.","C":"The state law is a prior restraint.","D":"The state law is overbroad."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Constitutional challenges\n\nFacial\n• No application of law would be constitutional\n• Strike down law in its entirety As applied\n• Generally valid law is unconstitutional as to challenger\n• Overturn application of law against challenger The state law cannot be challenged as applied to the airline's false advertisement because the First Amendment does not protect commercial speech that is (1) false or misleading or (2) concerns an unlawful activity. But the airline can still use the First Amendment to challenge the law on its face based on the overbreadth doctrine. Under this doctrine, a law is unenforceable against anyone—including those engaged in unprotected speech—if it restricts a substantial amount of constitutionally protected speech. Here, the law penalizes \"any public statement containing false or misleading information about a service or product.\" And though this encompasses unprotected commercial speech, it also impacts a substantial amount of protected speech—eg, false or misleading noncommercial speech. Therefore, the airline's best defense is that the law is overbroad. (Choice A) Defamation of a public official or figure (eg, the airline's competitor) requires proof that the speaker acted with malice—ie, knowingly or recklessly disregarded the falsity of its published statement. And though the airline's lack of malice may show that its advertisement was not defamatory, the false advertisement is still unprotected speech. Therefore, this evidence would not aid the airline's defense. (Choice B) The airline's claim about the competitor's safety record was commercial speech because it was used in an advertisement—a type of economically-oriented expression. And it was unprotected commercial speech because it contained false information. (Choice C) A prior restraint is a government action (eg, an injunction) that prohibits speech before it occurs. But the state law is not a prior restraint since it imposes penalties after speech occurs.\n\nEducational objective: Under the overbreadth doctrine, a law is unenforceable against anyone—including those engaged in unprotected speech—if it restricts a substantial amount of constitutionally protected speech.\n\nReferences\n• Houston v. Hill, 482 U.S. 451, 458–59 (1987) (explaining the overbreadth doctrine).","educationalObjective":"Under the overbreadth doctrine, a law is unenforceable against anyone—including those engaged in unprotected speech—if it restricts a substantial amount of constitutionally protected speech.","references":"• Houston v. Hill, 482 U.S. 451, 458–59 (1987) (explaining the overbreadth doctrine).","rule":"Under the overbreadth doctrine, a law is unenforceable against anyone—including those engaged in unprotected speech—if it restricts a substantial amount of constitutionally protected speech.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best supports the airline in a defense based on the First Amendment?","rule":"Under the overbreadth doctrine, a law is unenforceable against anyone—including those engaged in unprotected speech—if it restricts a substantial amount of constitutionally protected speech.","analysis":"Constitutional challenges\n\nFacial\n• No application of law would be constitutional\n• Strike down law in its entirety As applied\n• Generally valid law is unconstitutional as to challenger\n• Overturn application of law against challenger The state law cannot be challenged as applied to the airline's false advertisement because the First Amendment does not protect commercial speech that is (1) false or misleading or (2) concerns an unlawful activity. But the airline can still use the First Amendment to challenge the law on its face based on the overbreadth doctrine. Under this doctrine, a law is unenforceable against anyone—including those engaged in unprotected speech—if it restricts a substantial amount of constitutionally protected speech. Here, the law penalizes \"any public statement containing false or misleading information about a service or product.\" And though this encompasses unprotected commercial speech, it also impacts a substantial amount of protected speech—eg, false or misleading noncommercial speech. Therefore, the airline's best defense is that the law is overbroad. (Choice A) Defamation of a public official or figure (eg, the airline's competitor) requires proof that the speaker acted with malice—ie, knowingly or recklessly disregarded the falsity of its published statement. And though the airline's lack of malice may show that its advertisement was not defamatory, the false advertisement is still unprotected speech. Therefore, this evidence would not aid the airline's defense. (Choice B) The airline's claim about the competitor's safety record was commercial speech because it was used in an advertisement—a type of economically-oriented expression. And it was unprotected commercial speech because it contained false information. (Choice C) A prior restraint is a government action (eg, an injunction) that prohibits speech before it occurs. But the state law is not a prior restraint since it imposes penalties after speech occurs.","conclusion":"Answer D: The state law is overbroad."},"questionZh":"州法律对“任何包含有关服务或产品的虚假或误导性信息的公开声明”进行处罚。一家航空公司在广告中谎称其竞争对手的安全记录较差。该说法是基于在非营利消费者权益组织网站上发现的错误信息，而航空公司却认为这些信息是真实的。该航空公司因做出虚假陈述而根据州法律受到指控。没有适用的联邦法规。以下哪项最能支持航空公司根据第一修正案进行辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"它关于安全记录的声明是没有恶意的。","B":"其有关安全记录的声明是受保护的非商业言论。","C":"州法律是一种优先限制。","D":"州法律过于宽泛。"},"explanationZh":"宪法挑战\n\n面部护理\n• 任何法律的适用都不符合宪法\n• 完整的罢免法 所适用的\n• 对于挑战者而言，普遍有效的法律是违宪的\n• 推翻针对质疑者的法律适用 州法律适用于航空公司的虚假广告，因此不能受到质疑，因为第一修正案不保护 (1) 虚假或误导性的或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论。但航空公司仍然可以根据超宽原则，利用第一修正案来挑战法律的表面。根据这一原则，如果一项法律限制了大量受宪法保护的言论，那么该法律就无法针对任何人（包括那些从事不受保护言论的人）执行。在这里，法律惩罚“任何包含有关服务或产品的虚假或误导性信息的公开声明”。尽管这包括不受保护的商业言论，但它也影响了大量受保护的言论，例如虚假或误导性的非商业言论。因此，航空公司最好的辩护是法律过于宽泛。 （选择A）诽谤公职人员或人物（例如航空公司的竞争对手）需要证据证明说话者的行为具有恶意，即故意或鲁莽地忽视其所发表声明的虚假性。尽管航空公司没有恶意可能表明其广告没有诽谤性，但虚假广告仍然是不受保护的言论。因此，该证据无助于航空公司的辩护。 （选项B） 航空公司关于竞争对手安全记录的说法属于商业言论，因为它是在广告中使用的，是一种以经济为导向的表达方式。这是不受保护的商业言论，因为它包含虚假信息。 （选项C） 事前限制是指在言论发生之前禁止言论的政府行为（例如禁令）。但州法律并不是事前限制，因为它是在言论发生后才施加处罚。\n\n教育目标：根据过度宽泛原则，如果一项法律限制了大量受宪法保护的言论，那么该法律就无法针对任何人（包括那些从事不受保护言论的人）执行。\n\n参考文献\n• Houston v. Hill, 482 U.S. 451, 458–59 (1987)（解释过度宽度原则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据过度广度原则，如果一项法律限制了大量受宪法保护的言论，那么该法律就无法针对任何人（包括那些从事不受保护言论的人）执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能支持航空公司根据第一修正案进行辩护？","rule":"根据过度广度原则，如果一项法律限制了大量受宪法保护的言论，那么该法律就无法针对任何人（包括那些从事不受保护言论的人）执行。","analysis":"宪法挑战\n\n面部护理\n• 任何法律的适用都不符合宪法\n• 完整的罢免法 所适用的\n• 对于挑战者而言，普遍有效的法律是违宪的\n• 推翻针对质疑者的法律适用 州法律适用于航空公司的虚假广告，因此不能受到质疑，因为第一修正案不保护 (1) 虚假或误导性的或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论。但航空公司仍然可以根据超宽原则，利用第一修正案来挑战法律的表面。根据这一原则，如果一项法律限制了大量受宪法保护的言论，那么该法律就无法针对任何人（包括那些从事不受保护言论的人）执行。在这里，法律惩罚“任何包含有关服务或产品的虚假或误导性信息的公开声明”。尽管这包括不受保护的商业言论，但它也影响了大量受保护的言论，例如虚假或误导性的非商业言论。因此，航空公司最好的辩护是法律过于宽泛。 （选择A）诽谤公职人员或人物（例如航空公司的竞争对手）需要证据证明说话者的行为具有恶意，即故意或鲁莽地忽视其所发表声明的虚假性。尽管航空公司没有恶意可能表明其广告没有诽谤性，但虚假广告仍然是不受保护的言论。因此，该证据无助于航空公司的辩护。 （选项B） 航空公司关于竞争对手安全记录的说法属于商业言论，因为它是在广告中使用的，是一种以经济为导向的表达方式。这是不受保护的商业言论，因为它包含虚假信息。 （选项C） 事前限制是指在言论发生之前禁止言论的政府行为（例如禁令）。但州法律并不是事前限制，因为它是在言论发生后才施加处罚。\n\n教育目标：根据过度宽泛原则，如果一项法律限制了大量受宪法保护的言论，那么该法律就无法针对任何人（包括那些从事不受保护言论的人）执行。\n\n参考文献\n• Houston v. Hill, 482 U.S. 451, 458–59 (1987)（解释过度宽度原则）。","conclusion":"答案 D：州法律过于宽泛。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"advocacy","ipa":"/ˈædvəkəsi/","syllables":"ad·vo·cacy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 拥护, 支持, 鼓吹, 辩护, 辩护术；[法] 辩护, 拥护, 提倡","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 拥护, 支持, 鼓吹, 辩护, 辩护术；[法] 辩护, 拥护, 提倡","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"The claim was based on erroneous information, found on the website of a nonprofit consumer advocacy group, that the airline assumed to be true.","synonyms":["protagonism"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":166,"sourceQuestionPage":495,"sourceQuestionPages":[495],"sourceExplanationPages":[496,497],"sourceAnswerPages":[496,497],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-167","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A protester entered an IRS office during business hours. He denounced the income tax and set fire to pages from his copy of the Internal Revenue Code. The fire was extinguished before it caused any other damage. The protester was arrested and charged with violating a state law that prohibited igniting a fire in a public building. He claimed that his prosecution was unconstitutional under the First Amendment. May the protester constitutionally be convicted?","choices":{"A":"No, because he was exercising his right to freedom of speech by burning a copy of the code.","B":"No, because the copy of the code belonged to him, and thus burning it did not infringe upon a legitimate government interest.","C":"Yes, because the burning of the code was conduct rather than speech.","D":"Yes, because the state law is narrowly drawn to further a substantial government interest in prohibiting the noncommunicative aspects of the act in question."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment protects all forms of expression from unjustified government interference. This includes expressive conduct—ie, behavior that is intended to convey a particular message that is likely to be understood by the audience. To ensure such protection, regulations based solely on the message being communicated (ie, content-based restrictions) are subject to strict scrutiny and usually invalid. But content-neutral regulations are valid so long as they are narrowly tailored to further a substantial government interest. Here, the protester's burning of the tax code was protected expressive conduct since it was intended to denounce the IRS and that message would likely be understood by viewers (Choice C). But the law banning fires in public buildings is content-neutral since all fires are prohibited—regardless of any message being conveyed. And since the law is a narrowly drawn means of furthering the state's substantial interest in preventing fires in public buildings, the protester can constitutionally be convicted of violating it (Choice A). (Choice B) The state's legitimate (and substantial) interest in preventing fires in public buildings applies regardless of who owns the materials being burned.\n\nEducational objective: Content-based restrictions on expression (including expressive conduct) are strictly scrutinized and usually invalid. But content-neutral restrictions are valid if they are narrowly tailored to further a substantial government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Clark v. Cmty. for Creative Non-Violence, 468 U.S. 288, 294 (1984) (setting forth the test for determining whether the government's regulation of expressive conduct is valid).","educationalObjective":"Content-based restrictions on expression (including expressive conduct) are strictly scrutinized and usually invalid. But content-neutral restrictions are valid if they are narrowly tailored to further a substantial government interest.","references":"• Clark v. Cmty. for Creative Non-Violence, 468 U.S. 288, 294 (1984) (setting forth the test for determining whether the government's regulation of expressive conduct is valid).","rule":"Content-based restrictions on expression (including expressive conduct) are strictly scrutinized and usually invalid. But content-neutral restrictions are valid if they are narrowly tailored to further a substantial government interest.","irac":{"issue":"May the protester constitutionally be convicted?","rule":"Content-based restrictions on expression (including expressive conduct) are strictly scrutinized and usually invalid. But content-neutral restrictions are valid if they are narrowly tailored to further a substantial government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment protects all forms of expression from unjustified government interference. This includes expressive conduct—ie, behavior that is intended to convey a particular message that is likely to be understood by the audience. To ensure such protection, regulations based solely on the message being communicated (ie, content-based restrictions) are subject to strict scrutiny and usually invalid. But content-neutral regulations are valid so long as they are narrowly tailored to further a substantial government interest. Here, the protester's burning of the tax code was protected expressive conduct since it was intended to denounce the IRS and that message would likely be understood by viewers (Choice C). But the law banning fires in public buildings is content-neutral since all fires are prohibited—regardless of any message being conveyed. And since the law is a narrowly drawn means of furthering the state's substantial interest in preventing fires in public buildings, the protester can constitutionally be convicted of violating it (Choice A). (Choice B) The state's legitimate (and substantial) interest in preventing fires in public buildings applies regardless of who owns the materials being burned.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the state law is narrowly drawn to further a substantial government interest in prohibiting the noncommunicative aspects of the act in question."},"questionZh":"一名抗议者在工作时间进入国税局办公室。他谴责所得税并烧毁了他的《国内税收法》副本。大火在造成任何其他损害之前就被扑灭了。抗议者被捕并被指控违反禁止在公共建筑内纵火的州法律。他声称根据第一修正案，对他的起诉违反宪法。抗议者可以根据宪法被定罪吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为他通过烧毁代码副本来行使言论自由权。","B":"不，因为代码的副本属于他，因此焚烧它并没有侵犯合法的政府利益。","C":"是的，因为烧毁代码是行为而不是言论。","D":"是的，因为州法律的狭隘目的是为了进一步促进政府的重大利益，禁止相关行为的非交流方面。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案保护所有形式的表达免受政府不公正的干预。这包括表达行为，即旨在传达受众可能理解的特定信息的行为。为了确保这种保护，仅基于所传达的消息的法规（即基于内容的限制）受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且通常无效。但只要内容中立的法规是为了促进政府的重大利益而专门制定的，那么它们就是有效的。在这里，抗议者焚烧税法的行为受到保护，因为其目的是谴责国税局，并且观众可能会理解这一信息（选项 C）。但禁止在公共建筑内生火的法律在内容上是中立的，因为所有的火灾都是被禁止的——无论传达的是什么信息。由于该法律是一种狭隘的手段，旨在促进国家在预防公共建筑火灾方面的重大利益，因此抗议者可以根据宪法被定罪为违反该法律（选择 A）。 （选择 B）无论谁拥有被燃烧的材料，国家在预防公共建筑火灾方面的合法（和实质性）利益都适用。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的表达限制（包括表达行为）受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），通常是无效的。但如果内容中立的限制是为了促进政府的重大利益而专门制定的，那么它们就是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 克拉克诉Cmty。对于创造性非暴力，468 U.S. 288, 294 (1984)（提出了确定政府对表达行为的监管是否有效的测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于内容的表达限制（包括表达行为）受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），通常是无效的。但如果内容中立的限制是为了促进政府的重大利益而专门制定的，那么它们就是有效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"抗议者可以根据宪法被定罪吗？","rule":"基于内容的表达限制（包括表达行为）受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），通常是无效的。但如果内容中立的限制是为了促进政府的重大利益而专门制定的，那么它们就是有效的。","analysis":"第一修正案保护所有形式的表达免受政府不公正的干预。这包括表达行为，即旨在传达受众可能理解的特定信息的行为。为了确保这种保护，仅基于所传达的消息的法规（即基于内容的限制）受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且通常无效。但只要内容中立的法规是为了促进政府的重大利益而专门制定的，那么它们就是有效的。在这里，抗议者焚烧税法的行为受到保护，因为其目的是谴责国税局，并且观众可能会理解这一信息（选项 C）。但禁止在公共建筑内生火的法律在内容上是中立的，因为所有的火灾都是被禁止的——无论传达的是什么信息。由于该法律是一种狭隘的手段，旨在促进国家在预防公共建筑火灾方面的重大利益，因此抗议者可以根据宪法被定罪为违反该法律（选择 A）。 （选择 B）无论谁拥有被燃烧的材料，国家在预防公共建筑火灾方面的合法（和实质性）利益都适用。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的表达限制（包括表达行为）受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），通常是无效的。但如果内容中立的限制是为了促进政府的重大利益而专门制定的，那么它们就是有效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 克拉克诉Cmty。对于创造性非暴力，468 U.S. 288, 294 (1984)（提出了确定政府对表达行为的监管是否有效的测试）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为州法律的狭隘目的是为了进一步促进政府的重大利益，禁止相关行为的非交流方面。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-58c77a48304d1e8c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":167,"sourceQuestionPage":498,"sourceQuestionPages":[498],"sourceExplanationPages":[499,500],"sourceAnswerPages":[499,500],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-168","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A public high school has had a very high rate of pregnancy among its students. In order to assist students who keep their babies to complete high school, the school has established an infant day-care center for children of its students, and also offers classes in child care. Because the child-care classes are always overcrowded, the school limits admission to those classes solely to students who are the mothers of babies in the infant day-care center. A male student at the high school has legal custody of his infant son. The school provides care for his son in its infant day-care center, but will not allow the student to enroll in the child-care classes. He brings suit against the school challenging, on constitutional grounds, his exclusion from the child-care classes. Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this case?","choices":{"A":"The male student must demonstrate that the admission requirement is not as narrowly drawn as possible to achieve a substantial governmental interest.","B":"The male student must demonstrate that the admission requirement is not rationally related to a legitimate governmental interest.","C":"The school must demonstrate that the admission policy is substantially related to an important governmental interest.","D":"The school must demonstrate that the admission policy is the least restrictive means by which to achieve a compelling governmental interest."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A government action that treats similarly situated persons differently may be challenged under the equal protection clause. The action will only be deemed constitutional if there is adequate justification for the alleged discrimination. A law that discriminates based on gender or nonmarital birth (ie, quasi-suspect classes) is presumptively invalid unless it survives intermediate scrutiny—ie, the government demonstrates that the law is substantially related to an important government interest. Here, a public high school offers child-care classes to students with children (government action). But enrollment is limited to female students with children in the school's infant day-care center (gender discrimination). And since the male student was excluded from these classes, the admission policy will only be found constitutional if the school meets its burden of persuasion under intermediate scrutiny. (Choice A) The male student would have had the burden to prove that there was no adequate justification for the admission policy if rational basis review applied. And there is no burden of persuasion that requires proof that a government action be as narrowly drawn as possible to achieve a substantial government interest. (Choice B) Generally, a challenger must prove that the government action does not rationally relate to a legitimate government interest (ie, rational basis review). But this burden of persuasion does not apply when the government action impacts a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class (as seen here). (Choice D) The school would have had to demonstrate that the admission policy is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest (ie, strict scrutiny) had the policy impacted a fundamental right or suspect class. But here, the policy affected a quasi-suspect class (gender).\n\nEducational objective: A government action challenged under the equal protection clause is subject to intermediate scrutiny if it discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (ie, sex/gender, nonmarital children). Intermediate scrutiny requires the government to prove that its action is substantially related to an important government interest.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Craig v. Boren, 429 U.S. 190, 197 (1976) (holding that state discrimination based on sex/gender places the burden on the state to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest).","educationalObjective":"A government action challenged under the equal protection clause is subject to intermediate scrutiny if it discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (ie, sex/gender, nonmarital children). Intermediate scrutiny requires the government to prove that its action is substantially related to an important government interest.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Craig v. Boren, 429 U.S. 190, 197 (1976) (holding that state discrimination based on sex/gender places the burden on the state to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest).","rule":"A government action challenged under the equal protection clause is subject to intermediate scrutiny if it discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (ie, sex/gender, nonmarital children). Intermediate scrutiny requires the government to prove that its action is substantially related to an important government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this case?","rule":"A government action challenged under the equal protection clause is subject to intermediate scrutiny if it discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (ie, sex/gender, nonmarital children). Intermediate scrutiny requires the government to prove that its action is substantially related to an important government interest.","analysis":"A government action that treats similarly situated persons differently may be challenged under the equal protection clause. The action will only be deemed constitutional if there is adequate justification for the alleged discrimination. A law that discriminates based on gender or nonmarital birth (ie, quasi-suspect classes) is presumptively invalid unless it survives intermediate scrutiny—ie, the government demonstrates that the law is substantially related to an important government interest. Here, a public high school offers child-care classes to students with children (government action). But enrollment is limited to female students with children in the school's infant day-care center (gender discrimination). And since the male student was excluded from these classes, the admission policy will only be found constitutional if the school meets its burden of persuasion under intermediate scrutiny. (Choice A) The male student would have had the burden to prove that there was no adequate justification for the admission policy if rational basis review applied. And there is no burden of persuasion that requires proof that a government action be as narrowly drawn as possible to achieve a substantial government interest. (Choice B) Generally, a challenger must prove that the government action does not rationally relate to a legitimate government interest (ie, rational basis review). But this burden of persuasion does not apply when the government action impacts a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class (as seen here). (Choice D) The school would have had to demonstrate that the admission policy is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest (ie, strict scrutiny) had the policy impacted a fundamental right or suspect class. But here, the policy affected a quasi-suspect class (gender).","conclusion":"Answer C: The school must demonstrate that the admission policy is substantially related to an important governmental interest."},"questionZh":"一所公立高中的学生怀孕率非常高。为了帮助带娃的学生完成高中学业，学校为学生的孩子设立了婴儿日托中心，并开设了幼儿保育课程。由于托儿班总是人满为患，学校只允许婴儿日托中心的婴儿母亲学生进入这些班。一名高中男学生拥有其年幼儿子的合法监护权。学校在婴儿日托中心为他的儿子提供照顾，但不允许学生参加托儿班。他对学校提起诉讼，以宪法为由质疑学校将他排除在儿童保育班之外。以下哪项最能说明本案中的说服负担？","choicesZh":{"A":"男学生必须证明入学要求并非为了实现重大政府利益而尽可能狭隘。","B":"男学生必须证明入学要求与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。","C":"学校必须证明招生政策与重要的政府利益密切相关。","D":"学校必须证明招生政策是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。"},"explanationZh":"对处境相似的人采取不同对待的政府行为可能会根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。只有在所指控的歧视有充分理由的情况下，该行动才会被视为符合宪法。一项基于性别或非婚生育（即准可疑阶层）歧视的法律被推定为无效，除非它通过了中间审查——即政府证明该法律与重要的政府利益密切相关。在这里，一所公立高中为有孩子的学生提供儿童保育课程（政府行动）。但入学仅限于学校婴儿日托中心有孩子的女学生（性别歧视）。由于男学生被排除在这些班级之外，只有学校在中间审查下履行其说服责任，招生政策才会被认为符合宪法。 （选项A）如果采用合理基础审查，男学生将有责任证明录取政策没有充分的理由。而且不存在需要证明政府行动是为了实现重大政府利益而尽可能缩小范围的说服负担。 （选择B） 一般而言，挑战者必须证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有合理关联（即合理基础审查）。但当政府行为影响基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层时（如此处所示），这种说服负担并不适用。 （选项D）如果招生政策影响到基本权利或可疑阶层，学校必须证明该招生政策是实现令人信服的政府利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））的限制性最小的手段。但在这里，该政策影响的是准可疑阶层（性别）。\n\n教育目标：根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑的政府行为如果歧视准可疑阶层（即性/性别、非婚子女），则将受到中间审查。中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）要求政府证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Craig v. Boren, 429 U.S. 190, 197 (1976)（认为基于性/性别的国家歧视使国家有责任证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑的政府行为歧视准嫌疑群体（即性/性别、非婚子女），则该行为将受到中间审查。中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）要求政府证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能说明本案中的说服负担？","rule":"如果根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑的政府行为歧视准嫌疑群体（即性/性别、非婚子女），则该行为将受到中间审查。中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）要求政府证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。","analysis":"对处境相似的人采取不同对待的政府行为可能会根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。只有在所指控的歧视有充分理由的情况下，该行动才会被视为符合宪法。一项基于性别或非婚生育（即准可疑阶层）歧视的法律被推定为无效，除非它通过了中间审查——即政府证明该法律与重要的政府利益密切相关。在这里，一所公立高中为有孩子的学生提供儿童保育课程（政府行动）。但入学仅限于学校婴儿日托中心有孩子的女学生（性别歧视）。由于男学生被排除在这些班级之外，只有学校在中间审查下履行其说服责任，招生政策才会被认为符合宪法。 （选项A）如果采用合理基础审查，男学生将有责任证明录取政策没有充分的理由。而且不存在需要证明政府行动是为了实现重大政府利益而尽可能缩小范围的说服负担。 （选择B） 一般而言，挑战者必须证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有合理关联（即合理基础审查）。但当政府行为影响基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层时（如此处所示），这种说服负担并不适用。 （选项D）如果招生政策影响到基本权利或可疑阶层，学校必须证明该招生政策是实现令人信服的政府利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））的限制性最小的手段。但在这里，该政策影响的是准可疑阶层（性别）。\n\n教育目标：根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑的政府行为如果歧视准可疑阶层（即性/性别、非婚子女），则将受到中间审查。中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）要求政府证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Craig v. Boren, 429 U.S. 190, 197 (1976)（认为基于性/性别的国家歧视使国家有责任证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关）。","conclusion":"答案 C：学校必须证明招生政策与重要的政府利益密切相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"important governmental interest","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈɔɹtənt ɡˈʌvɝmˌɛntəl ˈɪntɹəst/","syllables":"im·por·tant gov·ern·men·tal in·ter·est","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"重要政府利益：中度审查所要求的重要公共目标","contextualMeaning":"重要政府利益：中度审查所要求的重要公共目标","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The school must demonstrate that the admission policy is substantially related to an important governmental interest.","synonyms":["important governmental pastime","important governmental pursuit","important governmental sake"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-d2769e78e372c137"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":168,"sourceQuestionPage":501,"sourceQuestionPages":[501],"sourceExplanationPages":[502,503],"sourceAnswerPages":[502,503],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-169","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A city enacted an ordinance banning from its public sidewalks all machines dispensing publications consisting wholly of commercial advertisements. The ordinance was enacted because of a concern about the adverse aesthetic effects of litter from publications distributed on the public sidewalks and streets. However, the city continued to allow machines dispensing other types of publications on the public sidewalks. As a result of the ordinance, 30 of the 300 sidewalk machines that were dispensing publications in the city were removed. Is this ordinance constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it does not constitute the least restrictive means with which to protect the aesthetics of the city's sidewalks and streets.","B":"No, because there is not a reasonable fit between the city's legitimate interest in preserving the aesthetics of its sidewalks and streets and the means it chose to advance that interest.","C":"Yes, because regulations of commercial speech are subject only to the requirement that they be rationally related to a legitimate state goal, and that requirement is satisfied here.","D":"Yes, because the city has a compelling interest in protecting the aesthetics of its sidewalks and streets, and such a ban is necessary to vindicate this interest."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Commercial speech is protected by the First Amendment so long as it concerns lawful activity and is not false or misleading. As a result, the government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the government proves that its regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. The second element is met if there is a reasonable fit between the means chosen and the interest served. Here, a city ordinance banned all sidewalk machines dispensing solely commercial advertisements. The ordinance directly advances the city's legitimate (and likely substantial) interest in preserving the aesthetics of its sidewalks and streets. But there is no reasonable fit between that interest and the means chosen to advance it since only 30 of the 300 machines were removed and there is no indication that the advertisements generate more litter than other publications. Therefore, the ordinance is unconstitutional. (Choice A) In the context of commercial speech, \"no more extensive than necessary\" does not mean the least restrictive means available. Instead, it means that there must be a reasonable fit between the relevant government interest and the means chosen to achieve it. And since there is no reasonable fit here, the ordinance is invalid. (Choice C) Rational basis review—which merely requires that a government regulation be rationally related to a legitimate government interest—does not apply to commercial speech. (Choice D) Strict scrutiny would require the city to prove that its ordinance is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. But this stringent standard of review does not apply here. And if it did, the ordinance would fail since it cannot even meet the lower standard applicable to commercial speech.\n\nEducational objective: Government regulations of protected commercial speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) not be more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. The second element is met if there is a reasonable fit between the government interest and the means chosen to achieve it.\n\nReferences\n• Bd. of Trs. of State Univ. of N. Y. v. Fox, 492 U.S. 469, 480 (1989) (explaining that government restrictions on protected commercial speech must have a reasonable fit between means and end).\n\n• Cincinnati v. Discovery Network, 507 U.S. 410, 424–25 (1993) (finding that there was no reasonable fit between city's ban only of commercial news racks and city's interest in sidewalk aesthetics).","educationalObjective":"Government regulations of protected commercial speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) not be more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. The second element is met if there is a reasonable fit between the government interest and the means chosen to achieve it.","references":"• Bd. of Trs. of State Univ. of N. Y. v. Fox, 492 U.S. 469, 480 (1989) (explaining that government restrictions on protected commercial speech must have a reasonable fit between means and end).\n\n• Cincinnati v. Discovery Network, 507 U.S. 410, 424–25 (1993) (finding that there was no reasonable fit between city's ban only of commercial news racks and city's interest in sidewalk aesthetics).","rule":"Government regulations of protected commercial speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) not be more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. The second element is met if there is a reasonable fit between the government interest and the means chosen to achieve it.","irac":{"issue":"Is this ordinance constitutional?","rule":"Government regulations of protected commercial speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) not be more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. The second element is met if there is a reasonable fit between the government interest and the means chosen to achieve it.","analysis":"Commercial speech is protected by the First Amendment so long as it concerns lawful activity and is not false or misleading. As a result, the government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the government proves that its regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is not more extensive than necessary to serve that interest. The second element is met if there is a reasonable fit between the means chosen and the interest served. Here, a city ordinance banned all sidewalk machines dispensing solely commercial advertisements. The ordinance directly advances the city's legitimate (and likely substantial) interest in preserving the aesthetics of its sidewalks and streets. But there is no reasonable fit between that interest and the means chosen to advance it since only 30 of the 300 machines were removed and there is no indication that the advertisements generate more litter than other publications. Therefore, the ordinance is unconstitutional. (Choice A) In the context of commercial speech, \"no more extensive than necessary\" does not mean the least restrictive means available. Instead, it means that there must be a reasonable fit between the relevant government interest and the means chosen to achieve it. And since there is no reasonable fit here, the ordinance is invalid. (Choice C) Rational basis review—which merely requires that a government regulation be rationally related to a legitimate government interest—does not apply to commercial speech. (Choice D) Strict scrutiny would require the city to prove that its ordinance is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. But this stringent standard of review does not apply here. And if it did, the ordinance would fail since it cannot even meet the lower standard applicable to commercial speech.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because there is not a reasonable fit between the city's legitimate interest in preserving the aesthetics of its sidewalks and streets and the means it chose to advance that interest."},"questionZh":"某城市颁布了一项法令，禁止在其公共人行道上使用所有分发完全由商业广告组成的出版物的机器。颁布该法令的原因是担心公共人行道和街道上散发的出版物垃圾对美观造成不良影响。然而，该市继续允许机器在公共人行道上分发其他类型的出版物。该法令颁布后，该市 300 台人行道上分发出版物的机器中，有 30 台被拆除。该法令是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为它不构成保护城市人行道和街道美观的最小限制手段。","B":"不，因为城市保护人行道和街道美观的合法利益与其选择促进这种利益的手段之间不存在合理的匹配。","C":"是的，因为商业言论的监管仅受其与合法国家目标合理相关的要求的约束，而这一要求在这里得到了满足。","D":"是的，因为这座城市对保护人行道和街道的美观有着强烈的兴趣，而这样的禁令对于维护这种利益是必要的。"},"explanationZh":"只要涉及合法活动并且不存在虚假或误导性，商业言论就受到第一修正案的保护。因此，只有当政府证明其监管（1）直接促进重大政府利益并且（2）范围不超过服务该利益所需的范围时，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论。如果所选择的手段和所服务的利益之间存在合理的匹配，则满足第二个要素。在这里，一项城市法令禁止所有人行道机器仅投放商业广告。该法令直接促进了城市在保护人行道和街道美观方面的合法（并且可能是实质性的）利益。但这种兴趣与所选择的推进手段之间并没有合理的契合，因为 300 台机器中只有 30 台被拆除，而且没有迹象表明这些广告比其他出版物产生更多的垃圾。因此，该法令是违宪的。 （选项A） 在商业演讲中，“不超过必要的范围”并不意味着可用的限制性最少的手段。相反，它意味着相关政府利益和实现该利益所选择的手段之间必须合理匹配。由于这里没有合理的配合，该条例无效。 （选项C）合理基础审查——仅要求政府监管与合法政府利益合理相关——不适用于商业言论。 （选项 D）严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）将要求该市证明其法令对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。但这种严格的审查标准在这里并不适用。如果确实如此，该条例就会失败，因为它甚至无法满足适用于商业言论的较低标准。\n\n教育目标：政府对受保护商业言论的法规必须 (1) 直接促进重大政府利益，并且 (2) 范围不得超过服务该利益所需的范围。如果政府利益和实现该利益所选择的手段之间存在合理的匹配，那么第二个要素就得到满足。\n\n参考文献\n• Bd。 Trs。州立大学。 N. Y. v. Fox, 492 U.S. 469, 480 (1989)（解释说政府对受保护的商业言论的限制必须在手段和目的之间合理匹配）。\n\n• 辛辛那提诉探索网络 (Cincinnati v. Discovery Network)，507 U.S. 410, 424–25 (1993)（发现城市仅禁止商业报刊架与城市对人行道美学的兴趣之间不存在合理契合）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"受保护的商业言论的政府法规必须 (1) 直接促进重大政府利益，并且 (2) 范围不得超过服务该利益所需的范围。如果政府利益和实现该利益所选择的手段之间存在合理的匹配，那么第二个要素就得到满足。","iracZh":{"issue":"该法令是否符合宪法？","rule":"受保护的商业言论的政府法规必须 (1) 直接促进重大政府利益，并且 (2) 范围不得超过服务该利益所需的范围。如果政府利益和实现该利益所选择的手段之间存在合理的匹配，那么第二个要素就得到满足。","analysis":"只要涉及合法活动并且不存在虚假或误导性，商业言论就受到第一修正案的保护。因此，只有当政府证明其监管（1）直接促进重大政府利益并且（2）范围不超过服务该利益所需的范围时，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论。如果所选择的手段和所服务的利益之间存在合理的匹配，则满足第二个要素。在这里，一项城市法令禁止所有人行道机器仅投放商业广告。该法令直接促进了城市在保护人行道和街道美观方面的合法（并且可能是实质性的）利益。但这种兴趣与所选择的推进手段之间并没有合理的契合，因为 300 台机器中只有 30 台被拆除，而且没有迹象表明这些广告比其他出版物产生更多的垃圾。因此，该法令是违宪的。 （选项A） 在商业演讲中，“不超过必要的范围”并不意味着可用的限制性最少的手段。相反，它意味着相关政府利益和实现该利益所选择的手段之间必须合理匹配。由于这里没有合理的配合，该条例无效。 （选项C）合理基础审查——仅要求政府监管与合法政府利益合理相关——不适用于商业言论。 （选项 D）严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）将要求该市证明其法令对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。但这种严格的审查标准在这里并不适用。如果确实如此，该条例就会失败，因为它甚至无法满足适用于商业言论的较低标准。\n\n教育目标：政府对受保护商业言论的法规必须 (1) 直接促进重大政府利益，并且 (2) 范围不得超过服务该利益所需的范围。如果政府利益和实现该利益所选择的手段之间存在合理的匹配，那么第二个要素就得到满足。\n\n参考文献\n• Bd。 Trs。州立大学。 N. Y. v. Fox, 492 U.S. 469, 480 (1989)（解释说政府对受保护的商业言论的限制必须在手段和目的之间合理匹配）。\n\n• 辛辛那提诉探索网络 (Cincinnati v. Discovery Network)，507 U.S. 410, 424–25 (1993)（发现城市仅禁止商业报刊架与城市对人行道美学的兴趣之间不存在合理契合）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为城市保护人行道和街道美观的合法利益与其选择促进这种利益的手段之间不存在合理的匹配。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-6b7a858f57441c17"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":169,"sourceQuestionPage":504,"sourceQuestionPages":[504],"sourceExplanationPages":[505,506],"sourceAnswerPages":[505,506],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-170","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"A city's personnel handbook contains all of that city's personnel policies. One section of the handbook states that \"where feasible and practicable supervisors are encouraged to follow the procedures specified in this handbook before discharging a city employee.\" Those specified procedures include a communication to the employee of the reasons for the contemplated discharge and an opportunity for a pre-termination trial-type hearing at which the employee may challenge those reasons. After a year of service, the secretary to the city council was discharged without having received any communication of reasons for her contemplated discharge and without having received an opportunity for a pre-termination trial-type hearing. The former secretary files suit in federal district court to challenge her discharge solely on constitutional grounds. Which of the following best describes the initial burden of persuasion in that suit?","choices":{"A":"The city council must demonstrate that its personnel handbook created no constitutionally protected interest in city employment or in the procedures by which such employment is terminated.","B":"The city council must demonstrate that the former secretary's termination was for good cause.","C":"The former secretary must demonstrate that she reasonably believed that she could work for the city for as long as she wished.","D":"The former secretary must demonstrate that state law creates a constitutionally protected interest in her employment or in the procedures by which her employment is terminated."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The former secretary's claim raises the Fourteenth Amendment right to procedural due process, which requires states and municipalities to follow certain procedures when they intentionally deprive a person of life, liberty, or property. These procedures include giving persons:\n• notice of the charges, proceedings, or other actions against them and\n• a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker. In a due process claim, the challenging party has the initial burden of showing that he/she has a protected life, liberty, or property interest at stake. Therefore, the former secretary must demonstrate that state law created a constitutionally protected interest in her employment or the procedures by which she was terminated (Choice A). (Choice B) The city council would only need to demonstrate that the former secretary's termination was for good cause if she first demonstrates that she has a constitutionally protected interest in her employment. (Choice C) Public employees have a property interest in their jobs if they (1) have an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receive assurances of continued employment. But the former secretary's reasonable belief that she could work for the city as long as she wished would not create this interest.\n\nEducational objective: Procedural due process requires the government to follow certain procedures when it intentionally deprives a person of life, liberty, or property. And in a due process claim, the challenger has the initial burden of showing that he/she has a protected interest at stake.\n\nReferences\n• Cleveland Bd. of Educ. v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 538 (1985) (holding that a state cannot terminate an employee without due process when the employee has a property right in his/her employment).","educationalObjective":"Procedural due process requires the government to follow certain procedures when it intentionally deprives a person of life, liberty, or property. And in a due process claim, the challenger has the initial burden of showing that he/she has a protected interest at stake.","references":"• Cleveland Bd. of Educ. v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 538 (1985) (holding that a state cannot terminate an employee without due process when the employee has a property right in his/her employment).","rule":"Procedural due process requires the government to follow certain procedures when it intentionally deprives a person of life, liberty, or property. And in a due process claim, the challenger has the initial burden of showing that he/she has a protected interest at stake.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best describes the initial burden of persuasion in that suit?","rule":"Procedural due process requires the government to follow certain procedures when it intentionally deprives a person of life, liberty, or property. And in a due process claim, the challenger has the initial burden of showing that he/she has a protected interest at stake.","analysis":"The former secretary's claim raises the Fourteenth Amendment right to procedural due process, which requires states and municipalities to follow certain procedures when they intentionally deprive a person of life, liberty, or property. These procedures include giving persons:\n• notice of the charges, proceedings, or other actions against them and\n• a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker. In a due process claim, the challenging party has the initial burden of showing that he/she has a protected life, liberty, or property interest at stake. Therefore, the former secretary must demonstrate that state law created a constitutionally protected interest in her employment or the procedures by which she was terminated (Choice A). (Choice B) The city council would only need to demonstrate that the former secretary's termination was for good cause if she first demonstrates that she has a constitutionally protected interest in her employment. (Choice C) Public employees have a property interest in their jobs if they (1) have an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receive assurances of continued employment. But the former secretary's reasonable belief that she could work for the city as long as she wished would not create this interest.","conclusion":"Answer D: The former secretary must demonstrate that state law creates a constitutionally protected interest in her employment or in the procedures by which her employment is terminated."},"questionZh":"一个城市的人事手册包含该城市的所有人事政策。该手册的一部分指出，“在可行和可行的情况下，鼓励主管在解雇城市雇员之前遵循本手册中规定的程序。”这些规定的程序包括向雇员传达预期解雇的原因，以及举行解雇前审判型听证会的机会，雇员可以在听证会上质疑这些理由。服务一年后，市议会秘书在没有收到任何关于她打算解雇的原因的通知的情况下被解雇，也没有获得终止合同前审判类型听证会的机会。这位前秘书向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，质疑仅以宪法为由将她解雇。以下哪一项最能描述该诉讼中最初的说服负担？","choicesZh":{"A":"市议会必须证明其人事手册在城市就业或终止此类就业的程序中没有产生受宪法保护的利益。","B":"市议会必须证明前任秘书长的解雇是有充分理由的。","C":"前秘书必须证明她有理由相信自己可以为这座城市工作多久，只要她愿意。","D":"前任秘书必须证明州法律在她的就业或终止其就业的程序中创造了受宪法保护的利益。"},"explanationZh":"这位前国务卿的主张提出了第十四修正案的程序正当程序权，该权利要求州和市政府在故意剥夺一个人的生命、自由或财产时遵循某些程序。这些程序包括向人员提供：\n• 针对他们的指控、诉讼或其他行动的通知以及\n• 中立决策者发表意见的有意义的机会。在正当程序索赔中，质疑方首先有责任证明他/她的生命、自由或财产利益受到保护。因此，前秘书必须证明州法律在她的就业或解雇她的程序中创造了受宪法保护的利益（选择 A）。 （选择 B）如果前秘书首先证明她在就业中拥有受宪法保护的利益，市议会只需证明前秘书的解雇是有充分理由的。 （选择 C）公职人员在其工作中拥有财产权益，如果他们 (1) 有持续的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证。但前秘书合理地相信，只要她愿意，她就可以为这座城市工作，但这并不会产生这种兴趣。\n\n教育目标：程序正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产时遵循一定的程序。在正当程序索赔中，挑战者首先有责任证明他/她的利益受到保护。\n\n参考文献\n• 克利夫兰Bd。教育的。 v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 538 (1985)（认为当雇员在其雇佣关系中拥有财产权时，州不得未经正当程序解雇雇员）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"程序正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产时遵循一定的程序。在正当程序索赔中，挑战者首先有责任证明他/她的利益受到保护。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项最能描述该诉讼中最初的说服负担？","rule":"程序正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产时遵循一定的程序。在正当程序索赔中，挑战者首先有责任证明他/她的利益受到保护。","analysis":"这位前国务卿的主张提出了第十四修正案的程序正当程序权，该权利要求州和市政府在故意剥夺一个人的生命、自由或财产时遵循某些程序。这些程序包括向人员提供：\n• 针对他们的指控、诉讼或其他行动的通知以及\n• 中立决策者发表意见的有意义的机会。在正当程序索赔中，质疑方首先有责任证明他/她的生命、自由或财产利益受到保护。因此，前秘书必须证明州法律在她的就业或解雇她的程序中创造了受宪法保护的利益（选择 A）。 （选择 B）如果前秘书首先证明她在就业中拥有受宪法保护的利益，市议会只需证明前秘书的解雇是有充分理由的。 （选择 C）公职人员在其工作中拥有财产权益，如果他们 (1) 有持续的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证。但前秘书合理地相信，只要她愿意，她就可以为这座城市工作，但这并不会产生这种兴趣。\n\n教育目标：程序正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产时遵循一定的程序。在正当程序索赔中，挑战者首先有责任证明他/她的利益受到保护。\n\n参考文献\n• 克利夫兰Bd。教育的。 v. Loudermill, 470 U.S. 532, 538 (1985)（认为当雇员在其雇佣关系中拥有财产权时，州不得未经正当程序解雇雇员）。","conclusion":"答案 D：前任秘书必须证明州法律在她的就业或终止其就业的程序中创造了受宪法保护的利益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-c4dba56348078ce8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":170,"sourceQuestionPage":507,"sourceQuestionPages":[507],"sourceExplanationPages":[508,509],"sourceAnswerPages":[508,509],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-171","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A clerical worker has been employed for the past two years in a permanent position in the county public records office in a state. The clerk has been responsible for copying and filing records of real estate transactions in that office. The clerk works in a nonpublic part of the office and has no contact with members of the public. However, state law provides that all real estate records in that office are to be made available for public inspection. On the day an attempted assassination of the state's governor was reported on the radio, the clerk remarked to a coworker, \"Our governor is such an evil man, I am sorry they did not get him.\" The coworker reported this remark to their employer, the county recorder. After the clerk admitted making the remark, the county recorder dismissed him stating that \"there is no room in this office for a person who hates the governor so much.\" The clerk sued for reinstatement and back pay. His only claim is that the dismissal violated his constitutional rights. What will the court likely hold?","choices":{"A":"The case is nonjusticiable, because public employment is a privilege rather than a right and, therefore, the clerk lacked standing to bring this suit.","B":"The dismissal was constitutional, because the compelling interest of the state in having loyal and supportive employees outweighs the interest of any state employee in his or her job or in free speech on a matter of public concern.","C":"The dismissal was unconstitutional, because in light of the clerk's particular employment duties, his right to express himself on a matter of public concern outweighed any legitimate interest the state might have had in discharging him.","D":"The dismissal was unconstitutional, because it constitutes a taking without just compensation of the clerk's property interest in his permanent position with the county."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The First Amendment right to free speech, applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, does not extend to public employees speaking pursuant to their official duties. Therefore, the government can freely discipline or terminate employees for the content of their employment-related speech. But since this right does extend to public employees speaking as private citizens, the government can only regulate the content of that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's free speech right. Here, the clerk was fired for remarking that he was sorry that the governor had not been assassinated. But the clerk spoke as a private citizen because his comment was made to a single coworker and was unrelated to his job duties—copying and filing real estate records with no public interaction. And since there is no indication that his comment impeded government efficiency, his right to express himself on this matter of public concern outweighs any legitimate state interest in discharging him. Therefore, the dismissal will likely be held unconstitutional. (Choice A) To have a justiciable claim, a plaintiff must have standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact (unemployment) caused by the challenged action (termination) and redressable by the remedy sought (reinstatement and back pay). The fact that public employment is a privilege rather than a right is irrelevant. (Choice B) The state's compelling interest in loyal and supportive employees does not outweigh a government employee's right to free speech on a matter of public concern (as seen here). But the state's interest will always outweigh an employee's right to free speech on a matter of private concern (eg, co-worker grievances). (Choice D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. Under this clause, private property includes real property and personal property—but not the clerk's property interest in continuing public employment.\n\nEducational objective: The government may regulate private speech by a public employee if the government's legitimate interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech.\n\nReferences\n• Rankin v. McPherson, 483 U.S. 378, 388–90 (1987) (discussing when a government employee can be punished for speaking as a private citizen).","educationalObjective":"The government may regulate private speech by a public employee if the government's legitimate interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech.","references":"• Rankin v. McPherson, 483 U.S. 378, 388–90 (1987) (discussing when a government employee can be punished for speaking as a private citizen).","rule":"The government may regulate private speech by a public employee if the government's legitimate interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech.","irac":{"issue":"What will the court likely hold?","rule":"The government may regulate private speech by a public employee if the government's legitimate interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's right to free speech.","analysis":"The First Amendment right to free speech, applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, does not extend to public employees speaking pursuant to their official duties. Therefore, the government can freely discipline or terminate employees for the content of their employment-related speech. But since this right does extend to public employees speaking as private citizens, the government can only regulate the content of that speech if its interest in efficient government function outweighs the employee's free speech right. Here, the clerk was fired for remarking that he was sorry that the governor had not been assassinated. But the clerk spoke as a private citizen because his comment was made to a single coworker and was unrelated to his job duties—copying and filing real estate records with no public interaction. And since there is no indication that his comment impeded government efficiency, his right to express himself on this matter of public concern outweighs any legitimate state interest in discharging him. Therefore, the dismissal will likely be held unconstitutional. (Choice A) To have a justiciable claim, a plaintiff must have standing—ie, suffered an injury-in-fact (unemployment) caused by the challenged action (termination) and redressable by the remedy sought (reinstatement and back pay). The fact that public employment is a privilege rather than a right is irrelevant. (Choice B) The state's compelling interest in loyal and supportive employees does not outweigh a government employee's right to free speech on a matter of public concern (as seen here). But the state's interest will always outweigh an employee's right to free speech on a matter of private concern (eg, co-worker grievances). (Choice D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. Under this clause, private property includes real property and personal property—but not the clerk's property interest in continuing public employment.","conclusion":"Answer C: The dismissal was unconstitutional, because in light of the clerk's particular employment duties, his right to express himself on a matter of public concern outweighed any legitimate interest the state might have had in discharging him."},"questionZh":"过去两年，一名文员在某州的县公共档案办公室担任长期职位。该职员负责复制和归档该办公室的房地产交易记录。该文员在办公室的非公共部分工作，与公众没有接触。然而，州法律规定该办公室的所有房地产记录均可供公众查阅。那天，广播电台报道了该州州长被刺杀的事件，这名职员对一位同事说道：“我们的州长是一个如此邪恶的人，我很遗憾他们没有抓到他。”这名同事向他们的雇主——县记录员报告了这一言论。这名书记员承认自己说过这句话后，县记录员驳回了他，称“这个办公室容不下一个如此憎恨州长的人”。该职员提起诉讼，要求恢复原职并支付欠薪。他唯一的主张是解雇侵犯了他的宪法权利。法庭可能会做出什么判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"该案不可审理，因为公共就业是一种特权而不是一项权利，因此，该书记员没有资格提起诉讼。","B":"这次解雇是符合宪法的，因为国家在拥有忠诚和支持性雇员方面的迫切利益超过了任何国家雇员对其工作或公众关心的问题的言论自由的利益。","C":"解雇是违宪的，因为鉴于该职员的特殊雇佣职责，他在公众关心的问题上表达意见的权利超过了国家解雇他可能拥有的任何合法利益。","D":"解雇是违宪的，因为这构成了对书记官在县内永久职位的财产权益的不公正补偿的剥夺。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案的言论自由权通过第十四修正案适用于各州，但并不适用于公职人员根据其公务发表言论。因此，政府可以自由地对雇员的与雇佣相关的言论内容进行纪律处分或解雇。但由于这项权利确实适用于作为私人公民发表言论的公职人员，因此，只有当政府对有效政府职能的利益大于雇员的言论自由权时，政府才能规范言论内容。在这里，一名职员因对州长没有被暗杀感到遗憾而被解雇。但这名职员是以普通公民的身份发表言论的，因为他的评论是向一名同事发表的，与他的工作职责无关——在没有公开互动的情况下复制和归档房地产记录。由于没有迹象表明他的言论妨碍了政府效率，因此他在公众关注的问题上表达意见的权利高于解雇他的任何合法国家利益。因此，解职很可能会被视为违宪。 （选择 A）要获得可审理的索赔，原告必须具有诉讼资格，即因受质疑的行为（终止）而遭受事实上的伤害（失业），并可通过寻求的补救措施（恢复原状和欠薪）进行补救。公共就业是一种特权而不是权利这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项 B）国家对忠诚和支持性雇员的强烈兴趣并不超过政府雇员就公众关心的问题发表言论自由的权利（如此处所示）。但国家利益永远高于员工在私人问题上的言论自由权（例如，同事的不满）。 （选项D） 第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私人财产供公共使用。根据该条款，私有财产包括不动产和个人财产，但不包括职员在继续公共就业中的财产权益。\n\n教育目标：如果政府有效履行政府职能的合法利益高于雇员的言论自由权，则政府可以规范公职人员的私人言论。\n\n参考文献\n• Rankin v. McPherson, 483 U.S. 378, 388–90 (1987)（讨论政府雇员何时会因为以普通公民身份发言而受到惩罚）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果政府有效履行政府职能的合法利益高于雇员的言论自由权，则政府可以规范公职人员的私人言论。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭可能会做出什么判决？","rule":"如果政府有效履行政府职能的合法利益高于雇员的言论自由权，则政府可以规范公职人员的私人言论。","analysis":"第一修正案的言论自由权通过第十四修正案适用于各州，但并不适用于公职人员根据其公务发表言论。因此，政府可以自由地对雇员的与雇佣相关的言论内容进行纪律处分或解雇。但由于这项权利确实适用于作为私人公民发表言论的公职人员，因此，只有当政府对有效政府职能的利益大于雇员的言论自由权时，政府才能规范言论内容。在这里，一名职员因对州长没有被暗杀感到遗憾而被解雇。但这名职员是以普通公民的身份发表言论的，因为他的评论是向一名同事发表的，与他的工作职责无关——在没有公开互动的情况下复制和归档房地产记录。由于没有迹象表明他的言论妨碍了政府效率，因此他在公众关注的问题上表达意见的权利高于解雇他的任何合法国家利益。因此，解职很可能会被视为违宪。 （选择 A）要获得可审理的索赔，原告必须具有诉讼资格，即因受质疑的行为（终止）而遭受事实上的伤害（失业），并可通过寻求的补救措施（恢复原状和欠薪）进行补救。公共就业是一种特权而不是权利这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项 B）国家对忠诚和支持性雇员的强烈兴趣并不超过政府雇员就公众关心的问题发表言论自由的权利（如此处所示）。但国家利益永远高于员工在私人问题上的言论自由权（例如，同事的不满）。 （选项D） 第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私人财产供公共使用。根据该条款，私有财产包括不动产和个人财产，但不包括职员在继续公共就业中的财产权益。\n\n教育目标：如果政府有效履行政府职能的合法利益高于雇员的言论自由权，则政府可以规范公职人员的私人言论。\n\n参考文献\n• Rankin v. McPherson, 483 U.S. 378, 388–90 (1987)（讨论政府雇员何时会因为以普通公民身份发言而受到惩罚）。","conclusion":"答案 C：解雇是违宪的，因为鉴于该职员的特殊雇佣职责，他在公众关心的问题上表达意见的权利超过了国家解雇他可能拥有的任何合法利益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-7fb9c70a7d583111"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":171,"sourceQuestionPage":510,"sourceQuestionPages":[510],"sourceExplanationPages":[511,512],"sourceAnswerPages":[511,512],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-172","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A state statute provided state monetary grants to private dance, theater, and opera groups located in that state. The statute required recipients of such grants to use the granted monies for the acquisition, construction, and maintenance of appropriate facilities for the public performance of their performing arts. The last section of the statute conditioned the award of each such grant on the recipient's agreement to refrain from all kinds of political lobbying calculated to secure additional tax support for the performing arts. Which of the following provides the strongest constitutional basis to attack the validity of the last section of the statute?","choices":{"A":"Commerce clause.","B":"Fifth Amendment.","C":"First and Fourteenth Amendments.","D":"Obligation of contracts clause."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Conditions on government funding\n\nFunded Government can restrict use \"Grant monies must be used for the activities of funds to authorized acquisition, construction, and maintenance purposes of appropriate performance facilities\"\n\nFunding Government cannot restrict \"Recipient must agree to refrain from all recipients protected conduct unrelated kinds of political lobbying calculated to to funding's purpose secure additional tax support for the performing arts\"\n\nThe last section of the statute conditions state funding on a recipient's agreement to refrain from certain political lobbying efforts. Since this condition interferes with the First Amendment right to free expression—which is applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment—the section can be challenged on that basis. But due to the government's countervailing right to decide how to spend its funds, the success of this attack depends on whether the condition is placed on:\n• funded activities – in which case, the government can insist that the funds be used for authorized purposes or\n\n• funding recipients – in which case, the government cannot restrict the recipient's right to engage in protected conduct that falls outside the funding's purpose or scope (as seen here).\n\nPolitical lobbying is protected First Amendment conduct that is unrelated to the activities— acquiring, constructing, and maintaining performing arts facilities—that are funded by the statute. And since the statute conditions the award of such grants on the recipient's agreement to refrain from such conduct, the First and Fourteenth Amendments provide the strongest constitutional basis to attack this section of the statute. (Choice A) The commerce clause bars states from discriminating against interstate commerce by favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests unless the state acts as a market participant (eg, by providing grants—as seen here). Therefore, this is a weak basis to attack the last section's constitutionality. (Choice B) The Fifth Amendment does not, by itself, provide a basis to attack the last section since the Fifth Amendment only applies to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice D) The contracts clause restricts state legislatures' ability to substantially impair existing contracts. But since no existing contracts are at issue here, this clause does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: The government can condition funding on the promise that the funds be used for a particular purpose. But it cannot condition funding on a recipient's promise to refrain from engaging in protected First Amendment conduct if that conduct is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.\n\nReferences\n• Agency for Int'l Dev. v. All. for Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013) (holding that a state funding program violated the First Amendment by conditioning funds on requirements unrelated to the program's purpose).\n\n• Rust v. Sullivan, 500 U.S. 173, 192–200 (1991) (upholding a federal funding program that limited fund recipients' ability to provide abortion-related services that were outside the program's scope).","educationalObjective":"The government can condition funding on the promise that the funds be used for a particular purpose. But it cannot condition funding on a recipient's promise to refrain from engaging in protected First Amendment conduct if that conduct is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.","references":"• Agency for Int'l Dev. v. All. for Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013) (holding that a state funding program violated the First Amendment by conditioning funds on requirements unrelated to the program's purpose).\n\n• Rust v. Sullivan, 500 U.S. 173, 192–200 (1991) (upholding a federal funding program that limited fund recipients' ability to provide abortion-related services that were outside the program's scope).","rule":"The government can condition funding on the promise that the funds be used for a particular purpose. But it cannot condition funding on a recipient's promise to refrain from engaging in protected First Amendment conduct if that conduct is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following provides the strongest constitutional basis to attack the validity of the last section of the statute?","rule":"The government can condition funding on the promise that the funds be used for a particular purpose. But it cannot condition funding on a recipient's promise to refrain from engaging in protected First Amendment conduct if that conduct is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.","analysis":"Conditions on government funding\n\nFunded Government can restrict use \"Grant monies must be used for the activities of funds to authorized acquisition, construction, and maintenance purposes of appropriate performance facilities\"\n\nFunding Government cannot restrict \"Recipient must agree to refrain from all recipients protected conduct unrelated kinds of political lobbying calculated to to funding's purpose secure additional tax support for the performing arts\"\n\nThe last section of the statute conditions state funding on a recipient's agreement to refrain from certain political lobbying efforts. Since this condition interferes with the First Amendment right to free expression—which is applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment—the section can be challenged on that basis. But due to the government's countervailing right to decide how to spend its funds, the success of this attack depends on whether the condition is placed on:\n• funded activities – in which case, the government can insist that the funds be used for authorized purposes or\n\n• funding recipients – in which case, the government cannot restrict the recipient's right to engage in protected conduct that falls outside the funding's purpose or scope (as seen here).\n\nPolitical lobbying is protected First Amendment conduct that is unrelated to the activities— acquiring, constructing, and maintaining performing arts facilities—that are funded by the statute. And since the statute conditions the award of such grants on the recipient's agreement to refrain from such conduct, the First and Fourteenth Amendments provide the strongest constitutional basis to attack this section of the statute. (Choice A) The commerce clause bars states from discriminating against interstate commerce by favoring in-state over out-of-state economic interests unless the state acts as a market participant (eg, by providing grants—as seen here). Therefore, this is a weak basis to attack the last section's constitutionality. (Choice B) The Fifth Amendment does not, by itself, provide a basis to attack the last section since the Fifth Amendment only applies to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice D) The contracts clause restricts state legislatures' ability to substantially impair existing contracts. But since no existing contracts are at issue here, this clause does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer C: First and Fourteenth Amendments."},"questionZh":"州法规向该州的私人舞蹈、戏剧和歌剧团体提供国家财政补助。该法规要求此类赠款的接受者将赠款用于购买、建造和维护适当的设施，以便公开表演其表演艺术。该法规的最后一部分规定，每笔此类赠款的授予均以受赠者同意不进行旨在为表演艺术提供额外税收支持的各种政治游说为条件。以下哪项为攻击该法规最后一节的有效性提供了最有力的宪法依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。","B":"第五修正案。","C":"第一和第十四修正案。","D":"合同义务条款。"},"explanationZh":"政府资助的条件\n\n受资助的政府可以限制使用“赠款必须用于资金活动，以授权购买、建设和维护适当的表演设施”\n\n资助政府不能限制“受资助者必须同意避免所有受保护的受资助者进行与资助目的无关的政治游说活动，以确保对表演艺术的额外税收支持”\n\n该法规的最后一部分规定，受资助者同意不进行某些政治游说活动，才能获得资助。由于这一条件干扰了第一修正案的言论自由权（该权利通过第十四修正案适用于各州），因此可以在此基础上对本节提出质疑。但由于政府拥有决定如何使用其资金的反补贴权，这次攻击的成功取决于是否满足以下条件：\n• 资助的活动——在这种情况下，政府可以坚持将资金用于授权目的或\n\n• 资金接受者——在这种情况下，政府不能限制接受者参与超出资金目的或范围的受保护行为的权利（如此处所示）。\n\n政治游说是受第一修正案保护的行为，与该法规资助的活动（获取、建造和维护表演艺术设施）无关。由于该法规规定授予此类赠款的条件是接受者同意不采取此类行为，因此第一修正案和第十四修正案为攻击该法规的这一部分提供了最有力的宪法依据。 （选项A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）禁止各州通过偏袒州内而非州外的经济利益来歧视州际商业，除非该州充当市场参与者（例如，通过提供赠款——如此处所示）。因此，这是攻击最后一节合宪性的薄弱基础。 （选项B）第五修正案本身并没有提供攻击最后一节的基础，因为第五修正案仅适用于第十四修正案之前的各州。 （选项 D） 合同条款限制州立法机关实质性损害现有合同的能力。但由于此处不涉及任何现有合同，因此该条款不适用。\n\n教育目标：政府可以以承诺将资金用于特定目的为条件提供资助。但它不能以受助人承诺不从事受第一修正案保护的行为为条件，如果该行为与资助的目的或范围无关。\n\n参考文献\n• 国际开发机构。 v.全部。 Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013)（认为州资助计划违反了第一修正案，因为资金条件与计划目的无关）。\n\n• Rust v. Sullivan, 500 U.S. 173, 192–200 (1991)（支持联邦资助计划，该计划限制资金接受者提供超出该计划范围的堕胎相关服务的能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"政府可以以资金用于特定目的的承诺为条件提供资金。但它不能以受助人承诺不从事受第一修正案保护的行为为条件，如果该行为与资助的目的或范围无关。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项为攻击该法规最后一节的有效性提供了最有力的宪法依据？","rule":"政府可以以资金用于特定目的的承诺为条件提供资金。但它不能以受助人承诺不从事受第一修正案保护的行为为条件，如果该行为与资助的目的或范围无关。","analysis":"政府资助的条件\n\n受资助的政府可以限制使用“赠款必须用于资金活动，以授权购买、建设和维护适当的表演设施”\n\n资助政府不能限制“受资助者必须同意避免所有受保护的受资助者进行与资助目的无关的政治游说活动，以确保对表演艺术的额外税收支持”\n\n该法规的最后一部分规定，受资助者同意不进行某些政治游说活动，才能获得资助。由于这一条件干扰了第一修正案的言论自由权（该权利通过第十四修正案适用于各州），因此可以在此基础上对本节提出质疑。但由于政府拥有决定如何使用其资金的反补贴权，这次攻击的成功取决于是否满足以下条件：\n• 资助的活动——在这种情况下，政府可以坚持将资金用于授权目的或\n\n• 资金接受者——在这种情况下，政府不能限制接受者参与超出资金目的或范围的受保护行为的权利（如此处所示）。\n\n政治游说是受第一修正案保护的行为，与该法规资助的活动（获取、建造和维护表演艺术设施）无关。由于该法规规定授予此类赠款的条件是接受者同意不采取此类行为，因此第一修正案和第十四修正案为攻击该法规的这一部分提供了最有力的宪法依据。 （选项A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）禁止各州通过偏袒州内而非州外的经济利益来歧视州际商业，除非该州充当市场参与者（例如，通过提供赠款——如此处所示）。因此，这是攻击最后一节合宪性的薄弱基础。 （选项B）第五修正案本身并没有提供攻击最后一节的基础，因为第五修正案仅适用于第十四修正案之前的各州。 （选项 D） 合同条款限制州立法机关实质性损害现有合同的能力。但由于此处不涉及任何现有合同，因此该条款不适用。\n\n教育目标：政府可以以承诺将资金用于特定目的为条件提供资助。但它不能以受助人承诺不从事受第一修正案保护的行为为条件，如果该行为与资助的目的或范围无关。\n\n参考文献\n• 国际开发机构。 v.全部。 Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013)（认为州资助计划违反了第一修正案，因为资金条件与计划目的无关）。\n\n• Rust v. Sullivan, 500 U.S. 173, 192–200 (1991)（支持联邦资助计划，该计划限制资金接受者提供超出该计划范围的堕胎相关服务的能力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：第一和第十四修正案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":172,"sourceQuestionPage":513,"sourceQuestionPages":[513],"sourceExplanationPages":[514,515],"sourceAnswerPages":[514,515],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-173","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Racial Gerrymandering","question":"The constitution of State X authorizes a five-member state reapportionment board to redraw state legislative districts every ten years. In the last state legislative reapportionment, the board, by a unanimous vote, divided a greater metropolitan area composed of a city and several contiguous townships into three equally populated state legislative districts. As a result of that districting, 40% of the area's total black population resided in one of those districts, 45% of the area's total black population resided in the second of those districts, and 15% resided in the third district. A black resident of the greater metropolitan area is a registered voter. He filed suit in the appropriate court against the members of the state reapportionment board, seeking declaratory and injunctive relief that would require the boundary lines of the state legislative districts in the greater metropolitan area to be redrawn. His only claim is that the current apportionment violates the Fifteenth Amendment and the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment because it improperly dilutes the voting power of blacks who reside in that area. If no federal statute is applicable, which of the following facts, if proven, would most strongly support the validity of the action of the state reapportionment board?","choices":{"A":"Although the rate of voter registration among blacks is below that of voter registration among whites in the greater metropolitan area, two minority-preferred legislators were elected from that area during the last 15 years.","B":"In drawing the current district lines, the reapportionment board precisely complied with state constitutional requirements that state legislative districts be compact and follow political subdivision boundaries to the maximum extent feasible.","C":"The reapportionment board was composed of three white members and two black members, and the black board members were satisfied that the plan did not improperly dilute the voting power of blacks residing in that area.","D":"The total black population of the greater metropolitan area amounts to only 15% of the population that is required to comprise a single legislative district."},"answer":"B","explanation":"[Current-law update — answer unchanged] Louisiana v. Callais (2026) tightened racial-districting doctrine and the §2 framework. Choice B remains strongest because precise compliance with compactness and political-boundary requirements supplies a race-neutral explanation for the map.\n\nThe Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause ensures that each citizen is given equal voting power (ie, one person, one vote) by requiring state legislative districts to have largely equal populations. And the Fifteenth Amendment ensures that the right to vote is not abridged on account of race. Therefore, race cannot be the predominant factor used to draw boundary lines for state legislative districts. If it is, the boundary lines will be strictly scrutinized and likely deemed unconstitutional. Here, the state reapportionment board created three equally populated state legislative districts. These districts have varying black populations and may improperly dilute the voting power of blacks who reside in those areas. But race would not be the predominant factor behind redistricting if the board drew these boundaries to comply with state constitutional requirements that districts be compact and follow political subdivision boundaries. In that case, the board's action would be valid. (Choice A) The Voting Rights Act (not the Constitution) requires that jurisdictions maintain a minority's ability to elect preferred candidates. But the question stipulates that this federal statute does not apply. And even if it did, the prior election of minority- preferred legislators could invalidate the board's action if the redistricting impaired black voters' ability to continue electing these candidates. But the rate of voter registration is irrelevant. (Choice C) Had the board been predominantly motivated by the improper dilution of minority voting power (ie, race), neither its composition nor its black members' satisfaction with the plan could validate the redistricting. But an argument that the board was motivated by other, non-racial factors would support its redistricting action. (Choice D) Although the metropolitan area's black population is only 15% of the population required for a legislative district, the validity of a redistricting plan is not impacted by how the area's minority population compares to the population required for a district. Therefore, this fact has no bearing on the validity of the board's action.\n\nEducational objective: Race cannot be the predominant factor used to draw boundary lines for state legislative districts. And if it is, those boundary lines will be subject to strict scrutiny and likely invalidated on equal protection grounds.\n\nReferences\n• Miller v. Johnson, 515 U.S. 900, 916 (1995) (explaining that the use of race as a predominant factor in drawing state legislative districts should be strictly scrutinized).","educationalObjective":"Intentional governmental use of race in districting triggers strict scrutiny. Compliance with Voting Rights Act §2 can be compelling only when §2, as currently construed, actually requires the race-conscious district; traditional race-neutral criteria remain powerful evidence that a map was not drawn for a racial purpose.","references":"• Miller v. Johnson, 515 U.S. 900, 916 (1995) (explaining that the use of race as a predominant factor in drawing state legislative districts should be strictly scrutinized).","rule":"Intentional governmental use of race in districting triggers strict scrutiny. Compliance with Voting Rights Act §2 can be compelling only when §2, as currently construed, actually requires the race-conscious district; traditional race-neutral criteria remain powerful evidence that a map was not drawn for a racial purpose.","irac":{"issue":"If no federal statute is applicable, which of the following facts, if proven, would most strongly support the validity of the action of the state reapportionment board?","rule":"Intentional governmental use of race in districting triggers strict scrutiny. Compliance with Voting Rights Act §2 can be compelling only when §2, as currently construed, actually requires the race-conscious district; traditional race-neutral criteria remain powerful evidence that a map was not drawn for a racial purpose.","analysis":"The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause ensures that each citizen is given equal voting power (ie, one person, one vote) by requiring state legislative districts to have largely equal populations. And the Fifteenth Amendment ensures that the right to vote is not abridged on account of race. Therefore, race cannot be the predominant factor used to draw boundary lines for state legislative districts. If it is, the boundary lines will be strictly scrutinized and likely deemed unconstitutional. Here, the state reapportionment board created three equally populated state legislative districts. These districts have varying black populations and may improperly dilute the voting power of blacks who reside in those areas. But race would not be the predominant factor behind redistricting if the board drew these boundaries to comply with state constitutional requirements that districts be compact and follow political subdivision boundaries. In that case, the board's action would be valid. (Choice A) The Voting Rights Act (not the Constitution) requires that jurisdictions maintain a minority's ability to elect preferred candidates. But the question stipulates that this federal statute does not apply. And even if it did, the prior election of minority- preferred legislators could invalidate the board's action if the redistricting impaired black voters' ability to continue electing these candidates. But the rate of voter registration is irrelevant. (Choice C) Had the board been predominantly motivated by the improper dilution of minority voting power (ie, race), neither its composition nor its black members' satisfaction with the plan could validate the redistricting. But an argument that the board was motivated by other, non-racial factors would support its redistricting action. (Choice D) Although the metropolitan area's black population is only 15% of the population required for a legislative district, the validity of a redistricting plan is not impacted by how the area's minority population compares to the population required for a district. Therefore, this fact has no bearing on the validity of the board's action.","conclusion":"Answer B: In drawing the current district lines, the reapportionment board precisely complied with state constitutional requirements that state legislative districts be compact and follow political subdivision boundaries to the maximum extent feasible."},"questionZh":"X州宪法授权由五名成员组成的州重新分配委员会每十年重新划定州立法选区。在上一次州立法重新分配中，委员会一致投票将由一个城市和几个相邻乡镇组成的大都市区划分为三个人口相等的州立法区。这一选区的结果是，该地区黑人总人口的 40% 居住在其中一个选区，该地区黑人总人口的 45% 居住在第二个选区，15% 居住在第三个选区。大都市区的黑人居民是登记选民。他向适当的法院对州重新分配委员会的成员提起诉讼，寻求宣告性和禁令性救济，要求重新划定大都市区的州立法区的边界线。他唯一的主张是，目前的分配违反了第十五修正案和第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它不当地削弱了居住在该地区的黑人的投票权。如果没有适用的联邦法规，以下哪项事实如果得到证实，将最有力地支持州重新分配委员会行动的有效性？","choicesZh":{"A":"尽管在大都市区黑人选民登记率低于白人选民登记率，但过去15年中有两名少数族裔优先的立法者从该地区选出。","B":"在划定目前的选区界线时，重新分配委员会严格遵守了州宪法的要求，即州立法选区要紧凑，并在最大可行的范围内遵循政治分区边界。","C":"重新分配委员会由三名白人成员和两名黑人成员组成，黑人委员会成员对该计划没有不当稀释居住在该地区的黑人的投票权感到满意。","D":"大都市区的黑人总人口仅占组成一个立法选区所需人口的 15%。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法更新——答案不变】Louisiana v. Callais（2026）收紧了种族选区划分及《投票权法》第 2 条框架。选项 B 仍最有力，因为严格遵守紧凑性与政治分区边界要求，为地图提供了种族中立解释。\n\n第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）通过要求州立法选区人口基本平等的方式确保每个公民享有平等的投票权（即一人一票）。第十五修正案确保投票权不会因种族而被剥夺。因此，种族不能成为划定州立法选区边界的主要因素。如果是的话，边界线将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并可能被视为违宪。在这里，州重新分配委员会创建了三个人口相等的州立法选区。这些地区的黑人人口各不相同，可能会不当削弱居住在这些地区的黑人的投票权。但如果委员会划定这些边界是为了遵守州宪法要求选区紧凑并遵循政治分区边界的要求，那么种族就不会成为重新划分选区的主要因素。在这种情况下，董事会的行动将是有效的。 （选项 A）《投票权法》（而非《宪法》）要求司法管辖区保持少数派选举首选候选人的能力。但问题规定该联邦法规不适用。即使确实如此，如果选区重新划分损害了黑人选民继续选举这些候选人的能力，那么先前选举少数族裔优先的立法者可能会使委员会的行动无效。但选民登记率无关紧要。 （选项C） 如果董事会的主要动机是少数族裔投票权的不当稀释（即种族），那么其组成和黑人成员对计划的满意度都无法验证选区重新划分的有效性。但如果有人认为委员会是受到其他非种族因素的推动，那么就会支持其重新划分选区的行动。 （选项 D） 尽管大都市区的黑人人口仅占立法选区所需人口的 15%，但选区重划计划的有效性并不受该地区少数民族人口与选区所需人口相比的影响。因此，这一事实与董事会行动的有效性无关。\n\n教育目标：种族不能成为划定州立法选区边界的主要因素。如果是这样，这些边界线将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并可能因平等保护而失效。\n\n参考文献\n• Miller v. Johnson, 515 U.S. 900, 916 (1995)（解释说，应严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）将种族作为划定州立法选区的主要因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"政府在划分选区时有意使用种族因素会触发严格审查。只有依现行解释的《投票权法》第 2 条确实要求采用种族考量选区时，遵守该法才可能构成重大利益；紧凑性及政治分区边界等传统种族中立标准，仍是地图并非出于种族目的的重要证据。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果没有适用的联邦法规，以下哪项事实如果得到证实，将最有力地支持州重新分配委员会行动的有效性？","rule":"政府在划分选区时有意使用种族因素会触发严格审查。只有依现行解释的《投票权法》第 2 条确实要求采用种族考量选区时，遵守该法才可能构成重大利益；紧凑性及政治分区边界等传统种族中立标准，仍是地图并非出于种族目的的重要证据。","analysis":"第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）通过要求州立法选区人口基本平等的方式确保每个公民享有平等的投票权（即一人一票）。第十五修正案确保投票权不会因种族而被剥夺。因此，种族不能成为划定州立法选区边界的主要因素。如果是的话，边界线将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并可能被视为违宪。在这里，州重新分配委员会创建了三个人口相等的州立法选区。这些地区的黑人人口各不相同，可能会不当削弱居住在这些地区的黑人的投票权。但如果委员会划定这些边界是为了遵守州宪法要求选区紧凑并遵循政治分区边界的要求，那么种族就不会成为重新划分选区的主要因素。在这种情况下，董事会的行动将是有效的。 （选项 A）《投票权法》（而非《宪法》）要求司法管辖区保持少数派选举首选候选人的能力。但问题规定该联邦法规不适用。即使确实如此，如果选区重新划分损害了黑人选民继续选举这些候选人的能力，那么先前选举少数族裔优先的立法者可能会使委员会的行动无效。但选民登记率无关紧要。 （选项C） 如果董事会的主要动机是少数族裔投票权的不当稀释（即种族），那么其组成和黑人成员对计划的满意度都无法验证选区重新划分的有效性。但如果有人认为委员会是受到其他非种族因素的推动，那么就会支持其重新划分选区的行动。 （选项 D） 尽管大都市区的黑人人口仅占立法选区所需人口的 15%，但选区重划计划的有效性并不受该地区少数民族人口与选区所需人口相比的影响。因此，这一事实与董事会行动的有效性无关。\n\n教育目标：种族不能成为划定州立法选区边界的主要因素。如果是这样，这些边界线将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并可能因平等保护而失效。\n\n参考文献\n• Miller v. Johnson, 515 U.S. 900, 916 (1995)（解释说，应严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）将种族作为划定州立法选区的主要因素）。","conclusion":"答案 B：在划定目前的选区界线时，重新分配委员会严格遵守了州宪法的要求，即州立法选区要紧凑，并在最大可行的范围内遵循政治分区边界。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-2026-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"依据 Callais（2026）更新种族选区划分规则；选项 B 仍为最强支持事实。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Louisiana v. Callais, 608 U.S. ___ (2026)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/24-109_new_jifl.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"satisfaction","ipa":"/sˌætʌsfˈækʃən/","syllables":"sat·is·fac·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 满足, 满意, 快事, 赔偿, 赎罪, 报仇的机会；[经] 偿还, 赎回","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 满足, 满意, 快事, 赔偿, 赎罪, 报仇的机会；[经] 偿还, 赎回","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:satisfactions","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) Had the board been predominantly motivated by the improper dilution of minority voting power (ie, race), neither its composition nor its black members' satisfaction with the plan could validate the redistricting.","synonyms":["atonement","expiation","gratification"]},{"term":"compactness","ipa":"/kʌmpˈæktnəs/","syllables":"com·pact·ness","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 紧密, 紧密度, 简洁, 坚实；[计] 紧致性, 压缩性","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 紧密, 紧密度, 简洁, 坚实；[计] 紧致性, 压缩性","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Choice B remains strongest because precise compliance with compactness and political-boundary requirements supplies a race-neutral explanation for the map.","synonyms":["concentration","density","denseness","tightness"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-ab51d7bd4acb37ff"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":173,"sourceQuestionPage":516,"sourceQuestionPages":[516],"sourceExplanationPages":[517,518],"sourceAnswerPages":[517,518],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-174","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"The open-air amphitheater in a city park has been utilized for concerts and other entertainment programs. Until this year, each of the groups performing in that city facility was allowed to make its own arrangements for sound equipment and sound technicians. After recurring complaints from occupants of residential buildings adjacent to the city park about intrusive noise from some performances held in the amphitheater, the city council passed an ordinance establishing city control over all sound amplification at all programs held there. The ordinance provided that the city's parks department would be the sole provider in the amphitheater of sound amplification equipment and of the technicians to operate the equipment \"to ensure a proper balance between the quality of the sound at such performances and respect for the privacy of nearby residential neighbors.\" Which of the following standards should a court use to determine the constitutionality on its face of this content-neutral ordinance?","choices":{"A":"The ordinance is narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and does not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of expression.","B":"The ordinance is rationally related to a legitimate government interest and does not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of expression.","C":"The ordinance is rationally related to a legitimate government interest and restricts the expressive rights involved no more than is reasonable under the circumstances.","D":"The ordinance is substantially related to a legitimate governmental interest and restricts the expressive rights involved no more than is reasonable in light of the surrounding circumstances."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The First Amendment primarily protects speech against content-based regulations (ie, restrictions on what is said). And though the government has more latitude to impose content-neutral regulations (ie, time, place, or manner restrictions), such regulations are still subject to constitutional review. And in a public forum (as seen here), the government may only impose time, place, or manner restrictions if they:\n• are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and\n• leave open ample alternative channels of communication. Here, the city ordinance regulates the manner of performances in the city park by requiring all performers to use city-provided sound equipment and technicians. This content-neutral ordinance is only valid if it is narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and does not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of expression. Therefore, the court should use this standard to determine the ordinance's constitutionality (Choices B, C, & D).\n\nEducational objective: In a public forum, the government's time, place, or manner restrictions on speech must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.\n\nReferences\n• Ward v. Rock Against Racism, 491 U.S. 781, 791 (1989) (setting forth the test for determining the validity of government restrictions on the time, place, or manner of protected speech in a public forum).","educationalObjective":"In a public forum, the government's time, place, or manner restrictions on speech must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","references":"• Ward v. Rock Against Racism, 491 U.S. 781, 791 (1989) (setting forth the test for determining the validity of government restrictions on the time, place, or manner of protected speech in a public forum).","rule":"In a public forum, the government's time, place, or manner restrictions on speech must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","irac":{"issue":"The open-air amphitheater in a city park has been utilized for concerts and other entertainment programs. Until this year, each of the groups performing in that city facility was allowed to make its own arrangements for sound equipment and sound technicians. After recurring complaints from occupants of residential buildings adjacent to the city park about intrusive noise from some performances held in the amphitheater, the city council passed an ordinance establishing city control over all sound amplification at all programs held there. The ordinance provided that the city's parks department would be the sole provider in the amphitheater of sound amplification equipment and of the technicians to operate the equipment \"to ensure a proper balance between the quality of the sound at such performances and respect for the privacy of nearby residential neighbors.\" Which of the following standards should a court use to determine the constitutionality on its face of this content-neutral ordinance?","rule":"In a public forum, the government's time, place, or manner restrictions on speech must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","analysis":"The First Amendment primarily protects speech against content-based regulations (ie, restrictions on what is said). And though the government has more latitude to impose content-neutral regulations (ie, time, place, or manner restrictions), such regulations are still subject to constitutional review. And in a public forum (as seen here), the government may only impose time, place, or manner restrictions if they:\n• are narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and\n• leave open ample alternative channels of communication. Here, the city ordinance regulates the manner of performances in the city park by requiring all performers to use city-provided sound equipment and technicians. This content-neutral ordinance is only valid if it is narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and does not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of expression. Therefore, the court should use this standard to determine the ordinance's constitutionality (Choices B, C, & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: The ordinance is narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and does not unreasonably limit alternative avenues of expression."},"questionZh":"城市公园的露天圆形剧场被用来举办音乐会和其他娱乐节目。直到今年，在该城市设施中表演的每个团体都可以自行安排音响设备和音响技术人员。在城市公园附近的住宅楼住户多次抱怨圆形剧场举行的一些表演产生的噪音后，市议会通过了一项法令，规定城市对在那里举行的所有节目的所有扩声进行控制。该法令规定，市公园部门将是露天剧场扩声设备和操作该设备的技术人员的唯一提供者，“以确保此类表演的声音质量与尊重附近住宅邻居隐私之间的适当平衡”。法院应使用以下哪个标准来确定该内容中立法令的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法令旨在服务于政府的重大利益，并且不会无理限制其他表达途径。","B":"该法令与合法的政府利益合理相关，并且不会无理限制其他表达途径。","C":"该法令与合法的政府利益合理相关，并且对所涉及的表达权的限制不超过该情况下合理的范围。","D":"该法令与合法的政府利益密切相关，并且对所涉及的表达权利的限制不超过根据周围情况合理的范围。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案主要保护言论不受基于内容的监管（即对言论内容的限制）。尽管政府有更大的自由度来实施内容中立的法规（即时间、地点或方式限制），但此类法规仍需接受宪法审查。在公共论坛中（如此处所示），政府只能在以下情况下施加时间、地点或方式限制：\n• 专门为服务重大政府利益而定制，并且\n• 保留充足的替代沟通渠道。在这里，城市法令规范了城市公园的表演方式，要求所有表演者使用城市提供的音响设备和技术人员。这项内容中立的法令只有在专门服务于政府的重大利益并且不会不合理地限制其他表达途径的情况下才有效。因此，法院应使用这一标准来确定该法令的合宪性（选项B、C和D）。\n\n教育目标：在公共论坛中，政府对言论的时间、地点或方式限制必须（1）严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益；（2）留出充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n参考文献\n• Ward v. Rock Against Racism, 491 U.S. 781, 791 (1989)（提出了确定政府对公共论坛中受保护言论的时间、地点或方式的限制有效性的测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在公共论坛上，政府对言论的时间、地点或方式限制必须（1）严格限制以服务于政府的重大利益；（2）留出充足的替代沟通渠道。","iracZh":{"issue":"城市公园的露天圆形剧场被用来举办音乐会和其他娱乐节目。直到今年，在该城市设施中表演的每个团体都可以自行安排音响设备和音响技术人员。在城市公园附近的住宅楼住户多次抱怨圆形剧场举行的一些表演产生的噪音后，市议会通过了一项法令，规定城市对在那里举行的所有节目的所有扩声进行控制。该法令规定，市公园部门将是露天剧场扩声设备和操作该设备的技术人员的唯一提供者，“以确保此类表演的声音质量与尊重附近住宅邻居隐私之间的适当平衡”。法院应使用以下哪个标准来确定该内容中立法令的合宪性？","rule":"在公共论坛上，政府对言论的时间、地点或方式限制必须（1）严格限制以服务于政府的重大利益；（2）留出充足的替代沟通渠道。","analysis":"第一修正案主要保护言论不受基于内容的监管（即对言论内容的限制）。尽管政府有更大的自由度来实施内容中立的法规（即时间、地点或方式限制），但此类法规仍需接受宪法审查。在公共论坛中（如此处所示），政府只能在以下情况下施加时间、地点或方式限制：\n• 专门为服务重大政府利益而定制，并且\n• 保留充足的替代沟通渠道。在这里，城市法令规范了城市公园的表演方式，要求所有表演者使用城市提供的音响设备和技术人员。这项内容中立的法令只有在专门服务于政府的重大利益并且不会不合理地限制其他表达途径的情况下才有效。因此，法院应使用这一标准来确定该法令的合宪性（选项B、C和D）。\n\n教育目标：在公共论坛中，政府对言论的时间、地点或方式限制必须（1）严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益；（2）留出充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n参考文献\n• Ward v. Rock Against Racism, 491 U.S. 781, 791 (1989)（提出了确定政府对公共论坛中受保护言论的时间、地点或方式的限制有效性的测试）。","conclusion":"答案 A：该法令旨在服务于政府的重大利益，并且不会无理限制其他表达途径。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-e5bbe5fc0dddbafd"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":174,"sourceQuestionPage":519,"sourceQuestionPages":[519],"sourceExplanationPages":[520,521],"sourceAnswerPages":[520,521],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-175","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state criminal law prohibits the publication of any description of the details of the execution of any prisoner who is sentenced to death by the courts of the state. Although the law allows a specified number of reporters to observe an execution, only the prison warden's official statement that the prisoner was \"executed as provided by law\" at a certain time and date may be published. The purpose of the law is to protect the public, particularly children, from the details of executions. After a particular execution was mishandled, causing the prisoner to suffer, a newspaper in the state published a story describing the event in detail. The story was written by the newspaper's reporter, who was permitted to observe the execution but did not promise prison officials that he would report only the warden's official statement. A prosecutor subsequently filed charges against the newspaper for publishing the details of the execution in violation of the state law. Is this prosecution constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the prosecution seeks to punish the publication of lawfully obtained, truthful information about a matter of public significance, without adequate justification.","B":"No, because the reporter did not promise prison officials that he would report only the warden's official statement about the execution.","C":"Yes, because publication of the details of such events might cause psychological damage to some children.","D":"Yes, because the newspaper should have brought an action to test the validity of the law before publishing the reporter's story."},"answer":"A","explanation":"First Amendment freedoms\n\nExpression Right to communicate information & ideas through speech or conduct\n\nAssociation Right to form or participate in any group or organization\n\nPress Right to publish truthful information\n\nReligion Free exercise – right to practice religion or no religion without government interference\n\nEstablishment – prohibits government from favoring particular religion or religion generally\n\nThe First Amendment freedom of the press prohibits the government from unduly restricting the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance. Any government actions that abridge this right must satisfy strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its actions were narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the state prosecuted a newspaper for publishing the truthful details of a mishandled execution that its reporter was permitted to observe. The state may have a compelling interest in protecting the public, particularly children, from the details of executions. But this interest could be achieved by less restrictive means—eg, by requiring a graphic-content warning (Choice C). And since the prosecution lacks adequate justification to survive strict scrutiny, it is unconstitutional. (Choice B) The prosecution would still be unconstitutional had the reporter promised prison officials that he would report only the warden's official statement about the execution. That is because the published information about the execution was lawfully obtained, truthful, and significant to the public. (Choice D) The newspaper did not need to bring an action to test the validity of the law before publishing the reporter's story. Instead, it can challenge the law's validity in its defense against this prosecution.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment freedom of the press protects the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance. If the government seeks to abridge this right, it must satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, prove that its actions are narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Smith v. Daily Mail Pub. Co., 443 U.S. 97, 103 (1979) (holding that the government must satisfy strict scrutiny to justifiably punish the publication of lawfully obtained, truthful information about a matter of public significance).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment freedom of the press protects the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance. If the government seeks to abridge this right, it must satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, prove that its actions are narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Smith v. Daily Mail Pub. Co., 443 U.S. 97, 103 (1979) (holding that the government must satisfy strict scrutiny to justifiably punish the publication of lawfully obtained, truthful information about a matter of public significance).","rule":"The First Amendment freedom of the press protects the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance. If the government seeks to abridge this right, it must satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, prove that its actions are narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is this prosecution constitutional?","rule":"The First Amendment freedom of the press protects the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance. If the government seeks to abridge this right, it must satisfy strict scrutiny—ie, prove that its actions are narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"First Amendment freedoms\n\nExpression Right to communicate information & ideas through speech or conduct\n\nAssociation Right to form or participate in any group or organization\n\nPress Right to publish truthful information\n\nReligion Free exercise – right to practice religion or no religion without government interference\n\nEstablishment – prohibits government from favoring particular religion or religion generally\n\nThe First Amendment freedom of the press prohibits the government from unduly restricting the right to publish lawfully obtained and truthful information about matters of public significance. Any government actions that abridge this right must satisfy strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that its actions were narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the state prosecuted a newspaper for publishing the truthful details of a mishandled execution that its reporter was permitted to observe. The state may have a compelling interest in protecting the public, particularly children, from the details of executions. But this interest could be achieved by less restrictive means—eg, by requiring a graphic-content warning (Choice C). And since the prosecution lacks adequate justification to survive strict scrutiny, it is unconstitutional. (Choice B) The prosecution would still be unconstitutional had the reporter promised prison officials that he would report only the warden's official statement about the execution. That is because the published information about the execution was lawfully obtained, truthful, and significant to the public. (Choice D) The newspaper did not need to bring an action to test the validity of the law before publishing the reporter's story. Instead, it can challenge the law's validity in its defense against this prosecution.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the prosecution seeks to punish the publication of lawfully obtained, truthful information about a matter of public significance, without adequate justification."},"questionZh":"州刑法禁止公布该州法院判处死刑的任何囚犯的处决细节的任何描述。尽管法律允许一定数量的记者旁听处决，但只有监狱长关于囚犯在特定时间和日期“依法被处决”的官方声明才可以发布。该法律的目的是保护公众，特别是儿童，免受处决细节的影响。在一次处决处理不当，导致囚犯遭受痛苦之后，该州的一家报纸发表了一篇详细描述该事件的报道。这篇报道是由该报记者撰写的，他被允许观察行刑，但没有向监狱官员承诺他只会报道典狱长的官方声明。检察官随后对该报提出指控，称该报违反州法律发布了处决细节。这次起诉是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为检方寻求惩罚在没有充分理由的情况下公开合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的行为。","B":"不，因为记者没有向监狱官员承诺他只会报道监狱长关于处决的官方声明。","C":"是的，因为公布此类事件的细节可能会对一些孩子造成心理伤害。","D":"是的，因为报纸在发表记者的报道之前应该提起诉讼来检验法律的有效性。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案的自由\n\n表达 通过言论或行为传达信息和想法的权利\n\n结社 组建或参加任何团体或组织的权利\n\n按权利发布真实信息\n\n宗教自由——在不受政府干预的情况下信奉宗教或不信奉宗教的权利\n\n建制——禁止政府偏袒特定宗教或普遍宗教\n\n第一修正案的新闻自由禁止政府不适当地限制发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的权利。任何削弱这一权利的政府行为都必须经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明其行为是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的（即限制性最少的手段）。在这里，该州起诉了一家报纸，因为该报纸刊登了其记者被允许观察的一次处理不当的处决的真实细节。保护公众，特别是儿童，免受处决细节的影响，可能符合国家的迫切利益。但这种兴趣可以通过限制较少的方式来实现——例如，通过要求图形内容警告（选择C）。而且由于检方缺乏足够的理由来经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此它是违宪的。 （选项B） 如果记者向监狱官员承诺他只会报道监狱长关于处决的官方声明，那么起诉仍然是违宪的。因为所公开的执行死刑信息是合法获取的、真实的、对公众具有重要意义的。 （选项D） 报纸在发表记者的报道之前不需要提起诉讼来检验法律的有效性。相反，它可以质疑法律在针对此次起诉的辩护中的有效性。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案的新闻自由保护出版合法获得的有关具有公共意义的事件的真实信息的权利。如果政府试图削弱这项权利，它就必须满足严格的审查，即证明其行动是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。\n\n参考文献\n• 史密斯诉《每日邮报》杂志。 Co., 443 U.S. 97, 103 (1979)（认为政府必须满足严格的审查，以合理地惩罚公开合法获得的有关公共重大事项的真实信息）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案的新闻自由保护发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的权利。如果政府试图削弱这项权利，它就必须满足严格的审查，即证明其行动是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。","iracZh":{"issue":"这次起诉是否符合宪法？","rule":"第一修正案的新闻自由保护发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的权利。如果政府试图削弱这项权利，它就必须满足严格的审查，即证明其行动是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。","analysis":"第一修正案的自由\n\n表达 通过言论或行为传达信息和想法的权利\n\n结社 组建或参加任何团体或组织的权利\n\n按权利发布真实信息\n\n宗教自由——在不受政府干预的情况下信奉宗教或不信奉宗教的权利\n\n建制——禁止政府偏袒特定宗教或普遍宗教\n\n第一修正案的新闻自由禁止政府不适当地限制发布合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的权利。任何削弱这一权利的政府行为都必须经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明其行为是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的（即限制性最少的手段）。在这里，该州起诉了一家报纸，因为该报纸刊登了其记者被允许观察的一次处理不当的处决的真实细节。保护公众，特别是儿童，免受处决细节的影响，可能符合国家的迫切利益。但这种兴趣可以通过限制较少的方式来实现——例如，通过要求图形内容警告（选择C）。而且由于检方缺乏足够的理由来经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此它是违宪的。 （选项B） 如果记者向监狱官员承诺他只会报道监狱长关于处决的官方声明，那么起诉仍然是违宪的。因为所公开的执行死刑信息是合法获取的、真实的、对公众具有重要意义的。 （选项D） 报纸在发表记者的报道之前不需要提起诉讼来检验法律的有效性。相反，它可以质疑法律在针对此次起诉的辩护中的有效性。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案的新闻自由保护出版合法获得的有关具有公共意义的事件的真实信息的权利。如果政府试图削弱这项权利，它就必须满足严格的审查，即证明其行动是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。\n\n参考文献\n• 史密斯诉《每日邮报》杂志。 Co., 443 U.S. 97, 103 (1979)（认为政府必须满足严格的审查，以合理地惩罚公开合法获得的有关公共重大事项的真实信息）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为检方寻求惩罚在没有充分理由的情况下公开合法获得的有关具有公共意义的真实信息的行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":175,"sourceQuestionPage":522,"sourceQuestionPages":[522],"sourceExplanationPages":[523,524],"sourceAnswerPages":[523,524],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-176","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"Congress enacted a statute that made it illegal for \"any employee, without the consent of his or her employer, to post on the Internet any information concerning the employer.\" The purpose of the statute was to prevent employees from revealing their employers' trade secrets. Is the statute constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it is not narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest.","B":"No, because it targets a particular medium of communication for special regulation.","C":"Yes, because it leaves open ample alternative channels of communication.","D":"Yes, because it prevents employees from engaging in unethical conduct."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The First Amendment free speech clause prohibits the government from unduly restricting an individual's right to freely communicate information and ideas. Restrictions based on the content of speech (ie, its message or ideas) are presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if they survive strict judicial scrutiny. Under this standard of review, the government has the nearly impossible task of proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to further a compelling government interest. Here, the statute targets speech based on its content since the statute prohibits employees from posting information concerning their employer. And though the government may have a compelling interest in preventing employees from revealing trade secrets, the statute is not necessary or narrowly tailored to further that interest. That is because less restrictive means could be used to protect trade secrets—eg, barring employees from posting information concerning their employers' trade secrets. As a result, the statute is unconstitutional. (Choice B) A speech regulation does not violate the First Amendment simply because it targets a particular medium of communication (eg, Internet postings—as seen here).* *In Packingham v. North Carolina, the Supreme Court equated the Internet to public streets and parks in dicta but never expressly held that the Internet is a public forum. Several cases dealing with this issue are currently being litigated in lower courts. (Choice C) The availability of alternative channels of communication is one factor in evaluating content-neutral speech restrictions (ie, time, place, or manner restrictions). But since this statute is a content-based restriction, the fact that it leaves open channels of communication other than the Internet is irrelevant. (Choice D) The statute may prevent employees from engaging in unethical conduct (eg, revealing trade secrets). But regardless, it is unconstitutional because it cannot survive strict scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: Content-based restrictions on speech are presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government must prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Playboy Entm't Grp., 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000) (explaining that content-based restrictions on speech require the government to satisfy strict scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"Content-based restrictions on speech are presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government must prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• United States v. Playboy Entm't Grp., 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000) (explaining that content-based restrictions on speech require the government to satisfy strict scrutiny).","rule":"Content-based restrictions on speech are presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government must prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the statute constitutional?","rule":"Content-based restrictions on speech are presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if they survive strict scrutiny—ie, the government must prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment free speech clause prohibits the government from unduly restricting an individual's right to freely communicate information and ideas. Restrictions based on the content of speech (ie, its message or ideas) are presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if they survive strict judicial scrutiny. Under this standard of review, the government has the nearly impossible task of proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to further a compelling government interest. Here, the statute targets speech based on its content since the statute prohibits employees from posting information concerning their employer. And though the government may have a compelling interest in preventing employees from revealing trade secrets, the statute is not necessary or narrowly tailored to further that interest. That is because less restrictive means could be used to protect trade secrets—eg, barring employees from posting information concerning their employers' trade secrets. As a result, the statute is unconstitutional. (Choice B) A speech regulation does not violate the First Amendment simply because it targets a particular medium of communication (eg, Internet postings—as seen here).* *In Packingham v. North Carolina, the Supreme Court equated the Internet to public streets and parks in dicta but never expressly held that the Internet is a public forum. Several cases dealing with this issue are currently being litigated in lower courts. (Choice C) The availability of alternative channels of communication is one factor in evaluating content-neutral speech restrictions (ie, time, place, or manner restrictions). But since this statute is a content-based restriction, the fact that it leaves open channels of communication other than the Internet is irrelevant. (Choice D) The statute may prevent employees from engaging in unethical conduct (eg, revealing trade secrets). But regardless, it is unconstitutional because it cannot survive strict scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because it is not narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项法规，规定“任何雇员未经雇主同意，在互联网上发布有关雇主的任何信息”都是非法的。该法规的目的是防止雇员泄露雇主的商业秘密。该法规是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为它并不是专门为了促进令人信服的政府利益而定制的。","B":"不，因为它针对特定的通信媒介进行特殊监管。","C":"是的，因为它留下了充足的替代沟通渠道。","D":"是的，因为它可以防止员工从事不道德行为。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案言论自由条款禁止政府不适当地限制个人自由交流信息和思想的权利。基于言论内容（即其信息或想法）的限制被推定为无效，并且只有在经过严格的司法审查后才会得到维持。在这种审查标准下，政府面临着几乎不可能完成的任务，即证明限制是必要的，并且是严格定制的（即限制最少的手段），以促进令人信服的政府利益。在这里，该法规根据其内容来针对言论，因为该法规禁止雇员发布有关其雇主的信息。尽管政府在阻止员工泄露商业秘密方面可能有迫切的利益，但该法规没有必要或专门为促进这种利益而制定。这是因为可以使用限制性较小的手段来保护商业秘密，例如，禁止员工发布有关其雇主商业秘密的信息。因此，该法规违宪。 （选项 B）言论管制并不违反第一修正案，仅仅因为它针对特定的通信媒介（例如，互联网发帖——如此处所示）。* *在 Packingham v. North Carolina 案中，最高法院在口述中将互联网等同于公共街道和公园，但从未明确认为互联网是一个公共论坛。目前，涉及该问题的几起案件正在下级法院提起诉讼。 （选项C） 替代通信渠道的可用性是评估内容中立的言论限制（即时间、地点或方式限制）的因素之一。但由于该法规是基于内容的限制，因此它留下互联网以外的开放沟通渠道这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项D） 法规可以防止员工从事不道德行为（例如泄露商业秘密）。但无论如何，它都是违宪的，因为它无法经受严格的审查。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论限制被推定为无效，并且只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才会得到维持，即政府必须证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Playboy Entm't Grp., 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000)（解释基于内容的言论限制要求政府满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于内容的言论限制被推定为无效，只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才会得到维持——即政府必须证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","iracZh":{"issue":"该法规是否符合宪法？","rule":"基于内容的言论限制被推定为无效，只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才会得到维持——即政府必须证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","analysis":"第一修正案言论自由条款禁止政府不适当地限制个人自由交流信息和思想的权利。基于言论内容（即其信息或想法）的限制被推定为无效，并且只有在经过严格的司法审查后才会得到维持。在这种审查标准下，政府面临着几乎不可能完成的任务，即证明限制是必要的，并且是严格定制的（即限制最少的手段），以促进令人信服的政府利益。在这里，该法规根据其内容来针对言论，因为该法规禁止雇员发布有关其雇主的信息。尽管政府在阻止员工泄露商业秘密方面可能有迫切的利益，但该法规没有必要或专门为促进这种利益而制定。这是因为可以使用限制性较小的手段来保护商业秘密，例如，禁止员工发布有关其雇主商业秘密的信息。因此，该法规违宪。 （选项 B）言论管制并不违反第一修正案，仅仅因为它针对特定的通信媒介（例如，互联网发帖——如此处所示）。* *在 Packingham v. North Carolina 案中，最高法院在口述中将互联网等同于公共街道和公园，但从未明确认为互联网是一个公共论坛。目前，涉及该问题的几起案件正在下级法院提起诉讼。 （选项C） 替代通信渠道的可用性是评估内容中立的言论限制（即时间、地点或方式限制）的因素之一。但由于该法规是基于内容的限制，因此它留下互联网以外的开放沟通渠道这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项D） 法规可以防止员工从事不道德行为（例如泄露商业秘密）。但无论如何，它都是违宪的，因为它无法经受严格的审查。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论限制被推定为无效，并且只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才会得到维持，即政府必须证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Playboy Entm't Grp., 529 U.S. 803, 816–17 (2000)（解释基于内容的言论限制要求政府满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为它并不是专门为了促进令人信服的政府利益而定制的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"dicta","ipa":"/dˈɪktɑ/","syllables":"dicta","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 格言；权威的断言（dictum的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 格言；权威的断言（dictum的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 dictum；词形 0:dictum/1:s","sourceSentence":"North Carolina, the Supreme Court equated the Internet to public streets and parks in dicta but never expressly held that the Internet is a public forum.","synonyms":["pronouncement","say-so","obiter dictum"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f734aa717a0f6f76"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":176,"sourceQuestionPage":525,"sourceQuestionPages":[525],"sourceExplanationPages":[526,527],"sourceAnswerPages":[526,527],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-177","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state's department of education routinely accredits public and private schools, licenses their teachers, sets strict curriculum guidelines, and provides funds for busses and secular extracurricular activities. A private high school in the state receives all of these benefits and enforces a strict \"whites only\" admissions policy. A group of parents whose children were denied admission to the private school solely because they are minorities brought suit in federal court. The parents sought to enjoin the private school from continuing its admissions policy on the ground that it violates the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. The private school's sole defense is that it is not bound by this constitutional provision. What is the parents' strongest argument that the private school must comply with the equal protection clause?","choices":{"A":"Education is a traditional public function.","B":"Racial segregation is not allowed in any school.","C":"The private school employs teachers licensed by the state.","D":"The private school is subject to pervasive state regulation and support."},"answer":"D","explanation":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control Constitutional claims must typically be asserted against government entities—not private actors.* But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor can be treated as a government entity and similarly bound by the Constitution when the government is pervasively intertwined in the control or management of the private actor. Here, the parents sought to enjoin the private school from continuing its racially segregated admissions policy on the ground that it violates the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. Therefore, the strongest argument that the private school must comply with this clause is that the school is subject to pervasive state regulation and support—eg, through state accreditation, teacher licensing, curriculum guidelines, funding for busses and secular extracurricular activities. *The Thirteenth Amendment's prohibition against slavery is the only constitutional provision that directly applies to private conduct. (Choice A) Private entities can be treated as state actors if they perform a function that has traditionally and exclusively been performed by the state. Although education is a public function, it has long been undertaken by private entities—not just the state. Therefore, the private school will not be treated as a state actor on this basis. (Choice B) States are constitutionally obligated to eliminate segregation in all public schools within their borders. However, racial segregation is allowed in private schools that do not have significant government involvement. (Choice C) The fact that the private school employs state-licensed teachers does not, by itself, require the school to comply with the equal protection clause. That is because the Supreme Court held in Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis that issuing a license—without more— does not amount to significant government involvement.\n\nEducational objective: The Constitution generally applies to government (not private) entities. But under the state-action doctrine, a private entity will be bound by the Constitution when the government is so pervasively intertwined in its control or management that the private entity is essentially a government actor.\n\nReferences\n• Brentwood Acad. v. Tenn. Secondary Sch. Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 298–302 (2001) (recognizing that a private association's regulatory activity amounted to state action due to the state's pervasive entwinement).\n\n• Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis, 407 U.S. 163, 176–77 (1972) (explaining that mere licensure does not amount to significant government involvement).","educationalObjective":"The Constitution generally applies to government (not private) entities. But under the state-action doctrine, a private entity will be bound by the Constitution when the government is so pervasively intertwined in its control or management that the private entity is essentially a government actor.","references":"• Brentwood Acad. v. Tenn. Secondary Sch. Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 298–302 (2001) (recognizing that a private association's regulatory activity amounted to state action due to the state's pervasive entwinement).\n\n• Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis, 407 U.S. 163, 176–77 (1972) (explaining that mere licensure does not amount to significant government involvement).","rule":"The Constitution generally applies to government (not private) entities. But under the state-action doctrine, a private entity will be bound by the Constitution when the government is so pervasively intertwined in its control or management that the private entity is essentially a government actor.","irac":{"issue":"What is the parents' strongest argument that the private school must comply with the equal protection clause?","rule":"The Constitution generally applies to government (not private) entities. But under the state-action doctrine, a private entity will be bound by the Constitution when the government is so pervasively intertwined in its control or management that the private entity is essentially a government actor.","analysis":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control Constitutional claims must typically be asserted against government entities—not private actors.* But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor can be treated as a government entity and similarly bound by the Constitution when the government is pervasively intertwined in the control or management of the private actor. Here, the parents sought to enjoin the private school from continuing its racially segregated admissions policy on the ground that it violates the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. Therefore, the strongest argument that the private school must comply with this clause is that the school is subject to pervasive state regulation and support—eg, through state accreditation, teacher licensing, curriculum guidelines, funding for busses and secular extracurricular activities. *The Thirteenth Amendment's prohibition against slavery is the only constitutional provision that directly applies to private conduct. (Choice A) Private entities can be treated as state actors if they perform a function that has traditionally and exclusively been performed by the state. Although education is a public function, it has long been undertaken by private entities—not just the state. Therefore, the private school will not be treated as a state actor on this basis. (Choice B) States are constitutionally obligated to eliminate segregation in all public schools within their borders. However, racial segregation is allowed in private schools that do not have significant government involvement. (Choice C) The fact that the private school employs state-licensed teachers does not, by itself, require the school to comply with the equal protection clause. That is because the Supreme Court held in Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis that issuing a license—without more— does not amount to significant government involvement.","conclusion":"Answer D: The private school is subject to pervasive state regulation and support."},"questionZh":"州教育部门定期对公立和私立学校进行认证，向其教师颁发执照，制定严格的课程指南，并为校车和世俗课外活动提供资金。该州的一所私立高中享有所有这些福利，并执行严格的“仅限白人”招生政策。一群孩子仅仅因为是少数族裔而被拒绝进入私立学校的家长向联邦法院提起诉讼。家长们试图禁止私立学校继续执行其招生政策，理由是该政策违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。私立学校的唯一辩护是它不受这一宪法条款的约束。家长认为私立学校必须遵守平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的最强烈论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"教育是一项传统的公共职能。","B":"任何学校都不允许种族隔离。","C":"私立学校聘用经国家许可的教师。","D":"私立学校受到国家的普遍监管和支持。"},"explanationZh":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍卷入私人实体的管理或控制 宪法主张通常必须针对政府实体而非私人行为者提出。*但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当政府普遍卷入对私人行为者的控制或管理时，私人行为者可以被视为政府实体并同样受到宪法的约束。在此，家长们试图禁止私立学校继续实行种族隔离的招生政策，理由是该政策违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。因此，私立学校必须遵守这一条款的最有力论据是，学校受到普遍的国家监管和支持——例如，通过国家认证、教师执照、课程指南、校车资助和世俗课外活动。 *第十三修正案禁止奴隶制是唯一直接适用于私人行为的宪法条款。 （选择 A）如果私人实体履行传统上完全由国家履行的职能，则可以将其视为国家行为者。尽管教育是一项公共职能，但它长期以来都是由私人实体——而不仅仅是国家——承担的。因此，私立学校不会据此被视为国家行为者。 （选择 B） 根据宪法，各州有义务消除其境内所有公立学校的种族隔离。然而，在没有政府大力参与的私立学校中，种族隔离是允许的。 （选项C）私立学校聘用国家许可教师这一事实本身并不要求学校遵守平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。这是因为最高法院在 Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis 案中裁定，颁发许可证（无需更多）并不构成政府的重大参与。\n\n教育目标：宪法一般适用于政府（而非私人）实体。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当政府的控制或管理如此普遍，以至于私人实体本质上是政府行为者时，私人实体将受到宪法的约束。\n\n参考文献\n• 布伦特伍德学院。诉田纳西州中学。 Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 298–302 (2001)（认识到由于国家的普遍介入，私人协会的监管活动相当于国家行为（state action））。\n\n• Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis, 407 U.S. 163, 176–77 (1972)（解释说仅仅获得许可并不等于政府的重大参与）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"宪法一般适用于政府（而非私人）实体。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当政府的控制或管理如此普遍，以至于私人实体本质上是政府行为者时，私人实体将受到宪法的约束。","iracZh":{"issue":"家长认为私立学校必须遵守平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的最强烈论点是什么？","rule":"宪法一般适用于政府（而非私人）实体。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当政府的控制或管理如此普遍，以至于私人实体本质上是政府行为者时，私人实体将受到宪法的约束。","analysis":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍卷入私人实体的管理或控制 宪法主张通常必须针对政府实体而非私人行为者提出。*但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当政府普遍卷入对私人行为者的控制或管理时，私人行为者可以被视为政府实体并同样受到宪法的约束。在此，家长们试图禁止私立学校继续实行种族隔离的招生政策，理由是该政策违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。因此，私立学校必须遵守这一条款的最有力论据是，学校受到普遍的国家监管和支持——例如，通过国家认证、教师执照、课程指南、校车资助和世俗课外活动。 *第十三修正案禁止奴隶制是唯一直接适用于私人行为的宪法条款。 （选择 A）如果私人实体履行传统上完全由国家履行的职能，则可以将其视为国家行为者。尽管教育是一项公共职能，但它长期以来都是由私人实体——而不仅仅是国家——承担的。因此，私立学校不会据此被视为国家行为者。 （选择 B） 根据宪法，各州有义务消除其境内所有公立学校的种族隔离。然而，在没有政府大力参与的私立学校中，种族隔离是允许的。 （选项C）私立学校聘用国家许可教师这一事实本身并不要求学校遵守平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。这是因为最高法院在 Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis 案中裁定，颁发许可证（无需更多）并不构成政府的重大参与。\n\n教育目标：宪法一般适用于政府（而非私人）实体。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当政府的控制或管理如此普遍，以至于私人实体本质上是政府行为者时，私人实体将受到宪法的约束。\n\n参考文献\n• 布伦特伍德学院。诉田纳西州中学。 Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 298–302 (2001)（认识到由于国家的普遍介入，私人协会的监管活动相当于国家行为（state action））。\n\n• Moose Lodge No. 107 v. Irvis, 407 U.S. 163, 176–77 (1972)（解释说仅仅获得许可并不等于政府的重大参与）。","conclusion":"答案 D：私立学校受到国家的普遍监管和支持。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"racially","ipa":"/ɹˈeɪʃəli/","syllables":"racially","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 按人种, 人种上","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 按人种, 人种上","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Here, the parents sought to enjoin the private school from continuing its racially segregated admissions policy on the ground that it violates the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"secular","ipa":"/sˈɛkjəlɝ/","syllables":"sec·u·lar","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 修道院外的教士；a. 世俗的, 现世的, 长期的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 修道院外的教士；a. 世俗的, 现世的, 长期的","wordForm":"形容词；词形 s:seculars","sourceSentence":"A state's department of education routinely accredits public and private schools, licenses their teachers, sets strict curriculum guidelines, and provides funds for busses and secular extracurricular activities.","synonyms":["layman","layperson","profane","laic"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":177,"sourceQuestionPage":528,"sourceQuestionPages":[528],"sourceExplanationPages":[529,530],"sourceAnswerPages":[529,530],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-178","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A city had a severe traffic problem on its streets. As a result, it enacted an ordinance prohibiting all sales to the public of food or other items by persons selling directly from trucks, cars, or other vehicles located on city streets. The ordinance included an inseverable grandfather provision exempting from its prohibition vendors who, for 20 years or more, have continuously sold food or other items from such vehicles located on the city's streets. An ice cream truck is the only food vendor that qualifies for this exemption. A frozen yogurt vendor is similar to the ice cream truck, but the yogurt vendor has only been selling to the public directly from trucks located on the city's streets for the past ten years. The yogurt vendor has filed suit in an appropriate federal district court to enjoin enforcement of this ordinance on the ground that it denies the yogurt vendor equal protection of the laws. How is the court likely to rule?","choices":{"A":"The ordinance is constitutional, because it is narrowly tailored to implement the city's compelling interest in reducing traffic congestion and, therefore, satisfies strict scrutiny.","B":"The ordinance is constitutional, because its validity is governed by the rational basis test, and courts consistently defer to the objectives embodied in such legislation if they are even plausibly justifiable.","C":"The ordinance is unconstitutional, because economic benefits or burdens imposed by legislatures through grandfather provisions have consistently been declared invalid by courts as per se equal protection violations.","D":"The ordinance is unconstitutional, because the nexus between the legitimate purpose of the ordinance and the conduct it prohibits is so tenuous and the ordinance is so underinclusive that it fails to satisfy the substantial relationship test."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives Equal protection challenges to discriminatory laws are generally reviewed under the rational basis test. This test requires the challenger to show that the law has no rational relationship to any legitimate government interest. Most laws subject to rational basis review are upheld because the court will give extreme deference to a legislature's right to define the law's objectives. And if those objectives are unclear, the law will still be upheld if the court can conceive of anyplausible justification for the law. As a result, the court will likely rule that the ordinance is constitutional.\n\n(Choice A) Laws are only subject to strict scrutiny if they substantially impact a fundamental right or suspect class. Since the ordinance discriminates based on a vendor's years of operation, this heightened level of scrutiny does not apply. But if it did, the ordinance would fail strict scrutiny since the city's interest in reducing traffic can be achieved by narrower or less restrictive means (eg, limiting vendors' hours of operation). (Choice C) There are no per se (ie, automatic) equal protection violations. Instead, all equal protection challenges must undergo the appropriate review—strict scrutiny, intermediate scrutiny, or rational basis. (Choice D) The substantial relationship test (ie, intermediate scrutiny) does not apply here since the ordinance does not substantially impact a quasi-suspect class. But if this test did apply, it would require a substantial nexus between the ordinance and an important (not merely legitimate) government interest.\n\nEducational objective: When reviewing an equal protection challenge under the rational basis test, the court will give extreme deference to a legislature's right to define a law's objectives. And if those objectives are unclear, the law will be upheld if the court can conceive of any plausible justification for the law.\n\nReferences\n• New Orleans v. Dukes, 427 U.S. 297, 305 (1976) (upholding a city ordinance containing a grandfather clause that discriminates against vendors that have been in operation for less than eight years).","educationalObjective":"When reviewing an equal protection challenge under the rational basis test, the court will give extreme deference to a legislature's right to define a law's objectives. And if those objectives are unclear, the law will be upheld if the court can conceive of any plausible justification for the law.","references":"• New Orleans v. Dukes, 427 U.S. 297, 305 (1976) (upholding a city ordinance containing a grandfather clause that discriminates against vendors that have been in operation for less than eight years).","rule":"When reviewing an equal protection challenge under the rational basis test, the court will give extreme deference to a legislature's right to define a law's objectives. And if those objectives are unclear, the law will be upheld if the court can conceive of any plausible justification for the law.","irac":{"issue":"How is the court likely to rule?","rule":"When reviewing an equal protection challenge under the rational basis test, the court will give extreme deference to a legislature's right to define a law's objectives. And if those objectives are unclear, the law will be upheld if the court can conceive of any plausible justification for the law.","analysis":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives Equal protection challenges to discriminatory laws are generally reviewed under the rational basis test. This test requires the challenger to show that the law has no rational relationship to any legitimate government interest. Most laws subject to rational basis review are upheld because the court will give extreme deference to a legislature's right to define the law's objectives. And if those objectives are unclear, the law will still be upheld if the court can conceive of anyplausible justification for the law. As a result, the court will likely rule that the ordinance is constitutional.\n\n(Choice A) Laws are only subject to strict scrutiny if they substantially impact a fundamental right or suspect class. Since the ordinance discriminates based on a vendor's years of operation, this heightened level of scrutiny does not apply. But if it did, the ordinance would fail strict scrutiny since the city's interest in reducing traffic can be achieved by narrower or less restrictive means (eg, limiting vendors' hours of operation). (Choice C) There are no per se (ie, automatic) equal protection violations. Instead, all equal protection challenges must undergo the appropriate review—strict scrutiny, intermediate scrutiny, or rational basis. (Choice D) The substantial relationship test (ie, intermediate scrutiny) does not apply here since the ordinance does not substantially impact a quasi-suspect class. But if this test did apply, it would require a substantial nexus between the ordinance and an important (not merely legitimate) government interest.","conclusion":"Answer B: The ordinance is constitutional, because its validity is governed by the rational basis test, and courts consistently defer to the objectives embodied in such legislation if they are even plausibly justifiable."},"questionZh":"一座城市的街道上存在严重的交通问题。因此，它颁布了一项法令，禁止直接从城市街道上的卡车、汽车或其他车辆销售的人员向公众销售食品或其他物品。该法令包括一项不可分割的祖父条款，豁免那些在 20 年或以上连续通过位于城市街道上的此类车辆出售食品或其他物品的商贩。冰淇淋车是唯一有资格获得此项豁免的食品供应商。冷冻酸奶摊贩与冰淇淋车类似，但过去十年来，酸奶摊贩仅通过位于城市街道上的卡车直接向公众销售。酸奶供应商已向适当的联邦地区法院提起诉讼，要求禁止执行该法令，理由是该法令剥夺了酸奶供应商受到法律的平等保护。法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法令是宪法性的，因为它是专门为了实现该市在减少交通拥堵方面的迫切利益而制定的，因此能够满足严格的审查。","B":"该法令是宪法性的，因为它的有效性是由合理基础测试决定的，法院一贯尊重此类立法所体现的目标，即使这些目标看似合理。","C":"该法令是违宪的，因为立法机关通过祖父条款施加的经济利益或负担一直被法院宣布为无效，因为其本身违反了平等保护规定。","D":"该法令是违宪的，因为该法令的合法目的与其所禁止的行为之间的联系非常脆弱，并且该法令的包容性非常不足，以至于无法满足实质性关系测试。"},"explanationZh":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 对歧视性法律的平等保护挑战通常根据合理基础测试进行审查。该测试要求挑战者证明法律与任何合法的政府利益没有理性关系。大多数接受合理基础审查的法律都会得到维持，因为法院将极大尊重立法机关定义法律目标的权利。如果这些目标不明确，如果法院能够为该法律找到任何合理的理由，该法律仍将得到维护。因此，法院可能会裁定该法令符合宪法。\n\n（选择 A）只有当法律对基本权利或可疑类别产生重大影响时，法律才会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。由于该法令根据供应商的经营年限进行歧视，因此这种严格的审查并不适用。但如果确实如此，该条例将无法通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因为该市减少交通的利益可以通过更窄或更少限制的手段（例如，限制摊贩的营业时间）来实现。 （选项C）本身不存在（即自动的）平等保护违规行为。相反，所有平等保护挑战都必须经过适当的审查——严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）、中间审查或合理基础。 （选项D）实质性关系测试（即中间审查）在此不适用，因为该条例不会对准嫌疑类别产生实质性影响。但如果这一测试确实适用，则需要该法令与重要的（不仅仅是合法的）政府利益之间存在实质性联系。\n\n教育目标：在合理基础测试下审查平等保护挑战时，法院将极大尊重立法机关定义法律目标的权利。如果这些目标不明确，如果法院能够为该法律找到任何合理的理由，该法律就会得到维护。\n\n参考文献\n• New Orleans v. Dukes, 427 U.S. 297, 305 (1976)（维护包含祖父条款的城市法令，该条款歧视经营时间不到八年的供应商）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在合理基础测试下审查平等保护质疑时，法院将极度尊重立法机关定义法律目标的权利。如果这些目标不明确，如果法院能够为该法律找到任何合理的理由，该法律就会得到维护。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"在合理基础测试下审查平等保护质疑时，法院将极度尊重立法机关定义法律目标的权利。如果这些目标不明确，如果法院能够为该法律找到任何合理的理由，该法律就会得到维护。","analysis":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 对歧视性法律的平等保护挑战通常根据合理基础测试进行审查。该测试要求挑战者证明法律与任何合法的政府利益没有理性关系。大多数接受合理基础审查的法律都会得到维持，因为法院将极大尊重立法机关定义法律目标的权利。如果这些目标不明确，如果法院能够为该法律找到任何合理的理由，该法律仍将得到维护。因此，法院可能会裁定该法令符合宪法。\n\n（选择 A）只有当法律对基本权利或可疑类别产生重大影响时，法律才会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。由于该法令根据供应商的经营年限进行歧视，因此这种严格的审查并不适用。但如果确实如此，该条例将无法通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因为该市减少交通的利益可以通过更窄或更少限制的手段（例如，限制摊贩的营业时间）来实现。 （选项C）本身不存在（即自动的）平等保护违规行为。相反，所有平等保护挑战都必须经过适当的审查——严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）、中间审查或合理基础。 （选项D）实质性关系测试（即中间审查）在此不适用，因为该条例不会对准嫌疑类别产生实质性影响。但如果这一测试确实适用，则需要该法令与重要的（不仅仅是合法的）政府利益之间存在实质性联系。\n\n教育目标：在合理基础测试下审查平等保护挑战时，法院将极大尊重立法机关定义法律目标的权利。如果这些目标不明确，如果法院能够为该法律找到任何合理的理由，该法律就会得到维护。\n\n参考文献\n• New Orleans v. Dukes, 427 U.S. 297, 305 (1976)（维护包含祖父条款的城市法令，该条款歧视经营时间不到八年的供应商）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该法令是宪法性的，因为它的有效性是由合理基础测试决定的，法院一贯尊重此类立法所体现的目标，即使这些目标看似合理。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":178,"sourceQuestionPage":531,"sourceQuestionPages":[531],"sourceExplanationPages":[532,533],"sourceAnswerPages":[532,533],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-179","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"In response to consumer complaints across the country, Congress enacted a statute prohibiting private parties from making automated telephone calls that deliver a recorded message (robocalls). A severable provision of the statute permits robocalls solely to collect a debt owed to or guaranteed by the federal government. An organization that wishes to make robocalls to solicit political donations challenged the statutory provision authorizing robocalls to collect federal government debt as unconstitutional under the First Amendment. Will the organization's challenge likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the provision is a content-neutral speech restriction.","B":"No, because the provision leaves open ample alternative channels of communication.","C":"Yes, because the provision is a content-based speech restriction.","D":"Yes, because the provision targets a particular form of communication for specific regulation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Speech regulations\n\nType Definition Test\n\nContent-\n• Facial discrimination Strict scrutiny based\n• Primary purpose targets\n• Necessary & narrowly tailored message, subject, or idea to achieve compelling interest Content-\n• Regulates time, place, or Intermediate scrutiny neutral manner of speech\n• Narrowly tailored to achieve important interest\n• Alternative avenues of communication remain The First Amendment free speech clause is designed to protect the free flow of information and ideas from undue government restrictions. A content-based restriction exists when the government treats speech differently based on its message or subject matter. Such a restriction is presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if it survives strict scrutiny. This level of scrutiny requires the government to satisfy the nearly impossible task of proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest. Here, the congressional statute prohibiting robocalls by private parties contains a severable provision* that permits such calls to collect a debt owed to or guaranteed by the federal government. This provision is a content-based speech restriction because it allows speech on one subject (government debt collection) but no others (eg, political donation solicitation). Since it is highly unlikely that the government can justify the provision under strict scrutiny, the organization's challenge will likely prevail. *A statutory provision that is held unconstitutional does not affect the validity of the rest of the statute when, as here, it can be severed from the rest of the law. (Choices A & B) Content-neutral speech restrictions regulate the time, place, or manner of speech (eg, no robocalls after 5:00 PM). These restrictions are constitutional if they satisfy intermediate scrutiny, which requires that alternative channels of communication remain open. But since the statutory provision here is a content-based restriction, the fact that it leaves open such channels is irrelevant. (Choice D) A speech regulation does not violate the First Amendment simply because it specifically targets a particular form of communication (eg, robocalls—as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A content-based speech restriction exists when the government singles out messages or subject matter for different treatment. Such restrictions will only be upheld if the government satisfies strict scrutiny by proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Barr v. Am. Ass'n of Political Consultants, Inc., 140 S. Ct. 2335, 2347 (2020) (holding that a provision allowing robocalls to collect government debt but barring all others is a content-based restriction that cannot satisfy strict scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"A content-based speech restriction exists when the government singles out messages or subject matter for different treatment. Such restrictions will only be upheld if the government satisfies strict scrutiny by proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest.","references":"• Barr v. Am. Ass'n of Political Consultants, Inc., 140 S. Ct. 2335, 2347 (2020) (holding that a provision allowing robocalls to collect government debt but barring all others is a content-based restriction that cannot satisfy strict scrutiny).","rule":"A content-based speech restriction exists when the government singles out messages or subject matter for different treatment. Such restrictions will only be upheld if the government satisfies strict scrutiny by proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Will the organization's challenge likely prevail?","rule":"A content-based speech restriction exists when the government singles out messages or subject matter for different treatment. Such restrictions will only be upheld if the government satisfies strict scrutiny by proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest.","analysis":"Speech regulations\n\nType Definition Test\n\nContent-\n• Facial discrimination Strict scrutiny based\n• Primary purpose targets\n• Necessary & narrowly tailored message, subject, or idea to achieve compelling interest Content-\n• Regulates time, place, or Intermediate scrutiny neutral manner of speech\n• Narrowly tailored to achieve important interest\n• Alternative avenues of communication remain The First Amendment free speech clause is designed to protect the free flow of information and ideas from undue government restrictions. A content-based restriction exists when the government treats speech differently based on its message or subject matter. Such a restriction is presumptively invalid and will only be upheld if it survives strict scrutiny. This level of scrutiny requires the government to satisfy the nearly impossible task of proving that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to further a compelling government interest. Here, the congressional statute prohibiting robocalls by private parties contains a severable provision* that permits such calls to collect a debt owed to or guaranteed by the federal government. This provision is a content-based speech restriction because it allows speech on one subject (government debt collection) but no others (eg, political donation solicitation). Since it is highly unlikely that the government can justify the provision under strict scrutiny, the organization's challenge will likely prevail. *A statutory provision that is held unconstitutional does not affect the validity of the rest of the statute when, as here, it can be severed from the rest of the law. (Choices A & B) Content-neutral speech restrictions regulate the time, place, or manner of speech (eg, no robocalls after 5:00 PM). These restrictions are constitutional if they satisfy intermediate scrutiny, which requires that alternative channels of communication remain open. But since the statutory provision here is a content-based restriction, the fact that it leaves open such channels is irrelevant. (Choice D) A speech regulation does not violate the First Amendment simply because it specifically targets a particular form of communication (eg, robocalls—as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the provision is a content-based speech restriction."},"questionZh":"为了回应全国各地的消费者投诉，国会颁布了一项法规，禁止私人团体拨打传送录音信息的自动电话（机器人电话）。该法规的一项单独条款允许机器人电话仅用于收取联邦政府欠下或由联邦政府担保的债务。一个希望通过自动电话募集政治捐款的组织对授权自动电话收取联邦政府债务的法定条款提出质疑，认为根据第一修正案，该条款违宪。该组织的挑战可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该规定是内容中立的言论限制。","B":"不，因为该条款留下了充足的替代沟通渠道。","C":"是的，因为该规定是基于内容的言论限制。","D":"是的，因为该条款针对的是特定监管的特定通信形式。"},"explanationZh":"言论规定\n\n类型定义测试\n\n内容-\n• 面部歧视 基于严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）\n• 主要目的目标\n• 必要且精心定制的信息、主题或想法，以实现引人注目的兴趣 内容-\n• 规定时间、地点或中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）中立的言论方式\n• 为实现重要利益而进行的狭隘定制\n• 保留替代沟通途径 第一修正案言论自由条款旨在保护信息和思想的自由流动，使其免受政府不当限制。当政府根据言论的信息或主题区别对待言论时，就会存在基于内容的限制。这种限制被推定为无效，只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才会得到维持。这种程度的审查要求政府完成几乎不可能完成的任务，即证明限制是必要的，并且是为了进一步实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。在这里，禁止私人团体拨打机器人电话的国会法规包含一项重要条款*，允许此类电话收取联邦政府欠下或由联邦政府担保的债务。该规定是基于内容的言论限制，因为它允许就一个主题（政府收债）发表言论，但不允许就其他主题（例如政治捐款募集）发表言论。由于政府不太可能在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下证明该规定的合理性，因此该组织的挑战很可能会占上风。 *被认为违宪的法律条款不会影响法律其余部分的有效性，就像这里一样，它可以与法律的其余部分分开。 （选项 A 和 B）内容中立的言论限制规定言论的时间、地点或方式（例如，下午 5:00 后禁止自动呼叫）。如果这些限制满足中间审查，则它们是符合宪法的，中间审查要求替代沟通渠道保持开放。但由于此处的法律规定是基于内容的限制，因此它是否开放此类渠道这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项 D） 言论管制并不违反第一修正案，仅仅因为它专门针对特定形式的通信（例如，机器人呼叫 - 如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当政府挑选信息或主题进行不同处理时，就会存在基于内容的言论限制。只有当政府通过证明限制是必要的并且是专门为了进一步实现令人信服的政府利益而满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下，此类限制才会得到维持。\n\n参考文献\n• 巴尔诉Am。政治顾问协会，140 S. Ct. 2335, 2347 (2020)（认为允许机器人电话收取政府债务但禁止所有其他条款的条款是基于内容的限制，无法满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当政府挑选信息或主题进行不同处理时，就会存在基于内容的言论限制。只有当政府通过证明限制是必要的并且是专门为了进一步实现令人信服的政府利益而满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下，此类限制才会得到维持。","iracZh":{"issue":"该组织的挑战可能会获胜吗？","rule":"当政府挑选信息或主题进行不同处理时，就会存在基于内容的言论限制。只有当政府通过证明限制是必要的并且是专门为了进一步实现令人信服的政府利益而满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下，此类限制才会得到维持。","analysis":"言论规定\n\n类型定义测试\n\n内容-\n• 面部歧视 基于严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）\n• 主要目的目标\n• 必要且精心定制的信息、主题或想法，以实现引人注目的兴趣 内容-\n• 规定时间、地点或中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）中立的言论方式\n• 为实现重要利益而进行的狭隘定制\n• 保留替代沟通途径 第一修正案言论自由条款旨在保护信息和思想的自由流动，使其免受政府不当限制。当政府根据言论的信息或主题区别对待言论时，就会存在基于内容的限制。这种限制被推定为无效，只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才会得到维持。这种程度的审查要求政府完成几乎不可能完成的任务，即证明限制是必要的，并且是为了进一步实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。在这里，禁止私人团体拨打机器人电话的国会法规包含一项重要条款*，允许此类电话收取联邦政府欠下或由联邦政府担保的债务。该规定是基于内容的言论限制，因为它允许就一个主题（政府收债）发表言论，但不允许就其他主题（例如政治捐款募集）发表言论。由于政府不太可能在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下证明该规定的合理性，因此该组织的挑战很可能会占上风。 *被认为违宪的法律条款不会影响法律其余部分的有效性，就像这里一样，它可以与法律的其余部分分开。 （选项 A 和 B）内容中立的言论限制规定言论的时间、地点或方式（例如，下午 5:00 后禁止自动呼叫）。如果这些限制满足中间审查，则它们是符合宪法的，中间审查要求替代沟通渠道保持开放。但由于此处的法律规定是基于内容的限制，因此它是否开放此类渠道这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项 D） 言论管制并不违反第一修正案，仅仅因为它专门针对特定形式的通信（例如，机器人呼叫 - 如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当政府挑选信息或主题进行不同处理时，就会存在基于内容的言论限制。只有当政府通过证明限制是必要的并且是专门为了进一步实现令人信服的政府利益而满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下，此类限制才会得到维持。\n\n参考文献\n• 巴尔诉Am。政治顾问协会，140 S. Ct. 2335, 2347 (2020)（认为允许机器人电话收取政府债务但禁止所有其他条款的条款是基于内容的限制，无法满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为该规定是基于内容的言论限制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"solicitation","ipa":"/sʌlˌɪsɪtˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"so·lic·i·ta·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 恳求, 恳请, 教唆, 诱惑","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 恳求, 恳请, 教唆, 诱惑","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:solicitations","sourceSentence":"This provision is a content-based speech restriction because it allows speech on one subject (government debt collection) but no others (eg, political donation solicitation).","synonyms":["allurement","appeal","collection","ingathering"]},{"term":"solicit","ipa":"/sʌlˈɪsɪt/","syllables":"so·licit","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 请求, 乞求, 招揽, 征求","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：v. 请求, 乞求, 招揽, 征求","wordForm":"动词；词形 i:soliciting/d:solicited/p:solicited/3:solicits","sourceSentence":"An organization that wishes to make robocalls to solicit political donations challenged the statutory provision authorizing robocalls to collect federal government debt as unconstitutional under the First Amendment.","synonyms":["hook","accost","beg","tap"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":179,"sourceQuestionPage":534,"sourceQuestionPages":[534],"sourceExplanationPages":[535,536],"sourceAnswerPages":[535,536],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-180","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A city ordinance requires every taxicab operator in the city to have a license and permits revocation of that license only for \"good cause.\" The ordinance conditions the issuance of such a license on an agreement that the licensee \"not display in or on his or her vehicle any bumper sticker or other placard or sign favoring a particular candidate for any elected municipal office.\" The ordinance imposes this condition to prevent the possible imputation of the views of taxicab licensees onto the city council, and it states that any licensee who violates this condition shall have his or her license revoked. A driver who holds a taxicab operator's license decorates his cab with bumper stickers and other signs favoring specified candidates in a forthcoming election for municipal offices. A proceeding is initiated against him to revoke his taxicab operator's license on the sole basis of that admitted conduct. In this proceeding, does the driver have a meritorious constitutional defense?","choices":{"A":"No, because a taxicab operator's license is a privilege and not a right and, therefore, is not protected by the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"No, because he accepted the license with knowledge of the condition and, therefore, has no standing to contest it.","C":"Yes, because such a proceeding threatens the driver with a taking of property, his license, without just compensation.","D":"Yes, because the condition imposed on taxicab operators' licenses restricts political speech based wholly on its content, without any adequate governmental justification."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The city ordinance infringes upon the First Amendment right to free expression by conditioning the issuance of a license on the driver's promise not to display signage favoring political candidates. The ordinance regulates speech based on its content (ie, message, subject, or ideas), so it is presumptively invalid and only enforceable if it survives strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the ordinance is necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the city may have a compelling interest in avoiding the imputation of drivers' views onto the city council. But since that interest can be achieved through less restrictive means—eg, by requiring drivers to display a disclaimer—the ordinance cannot survive strict scrutiny. As a result, the driver has a meritorious constitutional defense against his license being revoked based solely on his violation of this invalid ordinance. (Choice A) The Supreme Court no longer distinguishes between a privilege and a right when considering challenges under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. (Choice B) The driver did not waive his constitutional right to engage in political speech by accepting the license with knowledge of the signage condition. Therefore, the driver does have standing to contest the revocation of his license. (Choice C) The Fifth Amendment takings clause only requires the payment of just compensation when the government takes private property for public use, so it does not apply here.\n\nEducational objective: Content-based speech regulations must pass strict judicial scrutiny. This requires proof that the regulation is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","educationalObjective":"Content-based speech regulations must pass strict judicial scrutiny. This requires proof that the regulation is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"","rule":"Content-based speech regulations must pass strict judicial scrutiny. This requires proof that the regulation is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"In this proceeding, does the driver have a meritorious constitutional defense?","rule":"Content-based speech regulations must pass strict judicial scrutiny. This requires proof that the regulation is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The city ordinance infringes upon the First Amendment right to free expression by conditioning the issuance of a license on the driver's promise not to display signage favoring political candidates. The ordinance regulates speech based on its content (ie, message, subject, or ideas), so it is presumptively invalid and only enforceable if it survives strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the ordinance is necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the city may have a compelling interest in avoiding the imputation of drivers' views onto the city council. But since that interest can be achieved through less restrictive means—eg, by requiring drivers to display a disclaimer—the ordinance cannot survive strict scrutiny. As a result, the driver has a meritorious constitutional defense against his license being revoked based solely on his violation of this invalid ordinance. (Choice A) The Supreme Court no longer distinguishes between a privilege and a right when considering challenges under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. (Choice B) The driver did not waive his constitutional right to engage in political speech by accepting the license with knowledge of the signage condition. Therefore, the driver does have standing to contest the revocation of his license. (Choice C) The Fifth Amendment takings clause only requires the payment of just compensation when the government takes private property for public use, so it does not apply here.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the condition imposed on taxicab operators' licenses restricts political speech based wholly on its content, without any adequate governmental justification."},"questionZh":"一项城市法令要求该市的每个出租车运营商都必须持有许可证，并且仅允许出于“正当理由”撤销该许可证。该条例规定，颁发此类许可证的条件是，被许可人“不得在其车辆内或车辆上展示任何有利于任何民选市政府办公室特定候选人的保险杠贴纸或其他标语牌或标志”。该法令规定了这一条件，以防止出租车执照持有人的意见可能被归咎于市议会，并规定任何违反此条件的执照持有人将被吊销执照。一名持有出租车经营执照的司机在他的出租车上贴满了保险杠贴纸和其他标志，以支持即将举行的市政办公室选举中的特定候选人。仅仅因为他承认的行为，就对他提起诉讼，吊销其出租车运营商执照。在这个诉讼中，司机是否有立功的宪法辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为出租车运营商的执照是一种特权，而不是一项权利，因此不受第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）的保护。","B":"不，因为他在了解该条件的情况下接受了许可，因此没有资格提出异议。","C":"是的，因为这样的诉讼会威胁司机没收财产，即他的驾照，而不给予公正的赔偿。","D":"是的，因为对出租车运营商执照施加的条件完全根据其内容限制政治言论，而没有任何充分的政府理由。"},"explanationZh":"该市法令以司机承诺不展示有利于政治候选人的标牌为条件，侵犯了第一修正案的言论自由权。该法令根据其内容（即信息、主题或想法）对言论进行监管，因此该法令被推定为无效，只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才可执行。这要求政府证明该法令是必要的，并且是严格定制的（即限制最少的手段），以实现令人信服的政府利益。在这里，该市可能迫切希望避免将司机的观点归咎于市议会。但由于这种利益可以通过限制较少的方式来实现——例如，通过要求司机出示免责声明——该条例无法经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。因此，司机有充分的宪法辩护理由，可以防止仅仅因为违反了这项无效法令而吊销他的驾照。 （选项 A）最高法院在考虑第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）下的质疑时不再区分特权和权利。 （选项 B） 司机在了解标志条件的情况下接受驾照，并没有放弃其参与政治言论的宪法权利。因此，该司机确实有资格对吊销驾照提出异议。 （选项C）第五修正案征用条款仅要求政府在征用私人财产供公共使用时给予合理补偿，因此在此不适用。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论法规必须经过严格的司法审查。这需要证明监管是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于内容的言论监管必须经过严格的司法审查。这需要证明监管是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这个诉讼中，司机是否有立功的宪法辩护？","rule":"基于内容的言论监管必须经过严格的司法审查。这需要证明监管是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","analysis":"该市法令以司机承诺不展示有利于政治候选人的标牌为条件，侵犯了第一修正案的言论自由权。该法令根据其内容（即信息、主题或想法）对言论进行监管，因此该法令被推定为无效，只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才可执行。这要求政府证明该法令是必要的，并且是严格定制的（即限制最少的手段），以实现令人信服的政府利益。在这里，该市可能迫切希望避免将司机的观点归咎于市议会。但由于这种利益可以通过限制较少的方式来实现——例如，通过要求司机出示免责声明——该条例无法经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。因此，司机有充分的宪法辩护理由，可以防止仅仅因为违反了这项无效法令而吊销他的驾照。 （选项 A）最高法院在考虑第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）下的质疑时不再区分特权和权利。 （选项 B） 司机在了解标志条件的情况下接受驾照，并没有放弃其参与政治言论的宪法权利。因此，该司机确实有资格对吊销驾照提出异议。 （选项C）第五修正案征用条款仅要求政府在征用私人财产供公共使用时给予合理补偿，因此在此不适用。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论法规必须经过严格的司法审查。这需要证明监管是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而专门制定的。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为对出租车运营商执照施加的条件完全根据其内容限制政治言论，而没有任何充分的政府理由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-58c77a48304d1e8c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":180,"sourceQuestionPage":537,"sourceQuestionPages":[537],"sourceExplanationPages":[538,539],"sourceAnswerPages":[538,539],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-181","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"To encourage the growth of its population, a state established a program that awarded $1,000 to the parents of each child born within the state, provided that at the time of the child's birth the mother and father of the newborn were citizens of the United States. A married couple, noncitizens who are permanent residents of the United States, have resided in the state for three years. When their first child was born two months ago, they applied for and were denied the $1,000 award by state officials on the sole ground that they are not citizens of the United States. The couple filed suit in federal court contending that their exclusion from the award program was unconstitutional. Assume no federal statute addresses this question. In this case, how should the court rule as to the constitutionality of the state's exclusion of noncitizens from the award program?","choices":{"A":"The exclusion is constitutional, because the state has a legitimate interest in encouraging the growth of its population, and a rational legislature could believe that families in which both parents are United States citizens are more likely to stay in the state and contribute to its future prosperity than those in which one or both of the parents are noncitizens.","B":"The exclusion is constitutional, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states plenary authority over the spending of state funds.","C":"The exclusion is unconstitutional, because state classifications based on citizenship are impermissible unless explicitly authorized by an act of Congress.","D":"The exclusion is unconstitutional, because strict scrutiny governs judicial review of such state classifications based on citizenship, and the state cannot demonstrate that this classification is necessary to advance a compelling state interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Discriminatory state action can be challenged on Fourteenth Amendment equal protection grounds. When such action discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right, it must survive strict scrutiny—the most stringent standard of review. This requires the state to prove that its action is necessary to advance a compelling state interest. And since this is a nearly impossible standard to meet, state actions subject to strict scrutiny are almost always held unconstitutional. Here, the state program discriminates against parents who are resident noncitizens by denying them the $1,000 award given to other parents of newborns. Since citizenship is a suspect class, strict scrutiny applies. And since the state's interest in promoting population growth could be achieved without excluding resident noncitizens from its award program, the state cannot demonstrate that its actions were necessary to advance a compelling state interest. As a result, the court should find the state's exclusion of noncitizens from the award program unconstitutional. (Choice A) Under the rational basis test (the least stringent standard of review), government actions are constitutional as long as they are rationally related to any legitimate state interest. But this test only applies when the government action does not impact a quasi-suspect class, suspect class, or fundamental right. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment gives the states any powers not given to the federal government. But states must comply with other constitutional provisions like the equal protection clause when exercising those powers. (Choice C) Congress cannot authorize state classifications based on citizenship. Such classifications must survive strict judicial scrutiny instead.\n\nEducational objective: State actions that discriminate against a suspect class or substantially impact a fundamental right are subject to strict scrutiny. Under this most stringent standard of review, such actions are unconstitutional unless the state can prove that they are necessary to advance a compelling state interest.\n\nReferences\n• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 7 (1977) (explaining that state classifications based on citizenship are inherently suspect and subject to strict scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"State actions that discriminate against a suspect class or substantially impact a fundamental right are subject to strict scrutiny. Under this most stringent standard of review, such actions are unconstitutional unless the state can prove that they are necessary to advance a compelling state interest.","references":"• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 7 (1977) (explaining that state classifications based on citizenship are inherently suspect and subject to strict scrutiny).","rule":"State actions that discriminate against a suspect class or substantially impact a fundamental right are subject to strict scrutiny. Under this most stringent standard of review, such actions are unconstitutional unless the state can prove that they are necessary to advance a compelling state interest.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, how should the court rule as to the constitutionality of the state's exclusion of noncitizens from the award program?","rule":"State actions that discriminate against a suspect class or substantially impact a fundamental right are subject to strict scrutiny. Under this most stringent standard of review, such actions are unconstitutional unless the state can prove that they are necessary to advance a compelling state interest.","analysis":"Discriminatory state action can be challenged on Fourteenth Amendment equal protection grounds. When such action discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right, it must survive strict scrutiny—the most stringent standard of review. This requires the state to prove that its action is necessary to advance a compelling state interest. And since this is a nearly impossible standard to meet, state actions subject to strict scrutiny are almost always held unconstitutional. Here, the state program discriminates against parents who are resident noncitizens by denying them the $1,000 award given to other parents of newborns. Since citizenship is a suspect class, strict scrutiny applies. And since the state's interest in promoting population growth could be achieved without excluding resident noncitizens from its award program, the state cannot demonstrate that its actions were necessary to advance a compelling state interest. As a result, the court should find the state's exclusion of noncitizens from the award program unconstitutional. (Choice A) Under the rational basis test (the least stringent standard of review), government actions are constitutional as long as they are rationally related to any legitimate state interest. But this test only applies when the government action does not impact a quasi-suspect class, suspect class, or fundamental right. (Choice B) The Tenth Amendment gives the states any powers not given to the federal government. But states must comply with other constitutional provisions like the equal protection clause when exercising those powers. (Choice C) Congress cannot authorize state classifications based on citizenship. Such classifications must survive strict judicial scrutiny instead.","conclusion":"Answer D: The exclusion is unconstitutional, because strict scrutiny governs judicial review of such state classifications based on citizenship, and the state cannot demonstrate that this classification is necessary to advance a compelling state interest."},"questionZh":"为了鼓励人口增长，各州制定了一项计划，向在该州出生的每个孩子的父母奖励 1,000 美元，前提是孩子出生时，新生儿的父母都是美国公民。一对已婚夫妇是美国永久居民的非公民，已在该州居住三年。两个月前，当他们的第一个孩子出生时，他们申请了 1000 美元的奖励，但被州官员拒绝，理由是他们不是美国公民。这对夫妇向联邦法院提起诉讼，声称将他们排除在奖励计划之外是违宪的。假设没有联邦法规解决这个问题。在这种情况下，法院应如何裁定国家将非公民排除在奖励计划之外的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"这种排除是符合宪法的，因为国家在鼓励人口增长方面拥有合法利益，并且理性的立法机关可能会认为，父母双方都是美国公民的家庭比父母一方或双方都是非公民的家庭更有可能留在该州并为其未来的繁荣做出贡献。","B":"这种排除是符合宪法的，因为第十修正案保留了各州对州资金支出的全权权力。","C":"这种排除是违宪的，因为除非得到国会法案的明确授权，否则基于公民身份的国家分类是不允许的。","D":"这种排除是违宪的，因为对这种基于公民身份的国家分类的司法审查受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且国家无法证明这种分类对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。"},"explanationZh":"可以根据第十四修正案的平等保护理由对歧视性国家行为提出质疑。当此类行为歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利时，它必须经受住严格的审查——最严格的审查标准。这要求国家证明其行动对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。由于这几乎是不可能达到的标准，因此受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的国家行为几乎总是被认为是违宪的。在这里，该州计划歧视非公民居民的父母，拒绝向其他新生儿父母发放 1,000 美元的奖励。由于公民身份是一个可疑的类别，因此需要进行严格的审查。而且，由于国家促进人口增长的利益可以在不将居民非公民排除在其奖励计划之外的情况下实现，因此国家无法证明其行动对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。因此，法院应认定该州将非公民排除在奖励计划之外是违宪的。 （选项A）根据合理基础测试（最不严格的审查标准），政府行为只要与任何合法的国家利益合理相关，就符合宪法。但这一测试仅适用于政府行为不影响准可疑阶层、可疑阶层或基本权利的情况。 （选项 B）第十修正案赋予各州联邦政府未赋予的任何权力。但各州在行使这些权力时必须遵守其他宪法条款，例如平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。 （选项 C）国会不能授权根据公民身份进行州分类。这种分类必须经过严格的司法审查。\n\n教育目标：歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利的国家行为将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。根据这种最严格的审查标准，此类行为是违宪的，除非国家能够证明这些行为对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 7 (1977)（解释说基于公民身份的国家分类本质上是可疑的，并且受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利的国家行为将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。根据这种最严格的审查标准，此类行为是违宪的，除非国家能够证明这些行为对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种情况下，法院应如何裁定国家将非公民排除在奖励计划之外的合宪性？","rule":"歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利的国家行为将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。根据这种最严格的审查标准，此类行为是违宪的，除非国家能够证明这些行为对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。","analysis":"可以根据第十四修正案的平等保护理由对歧视性国家行为提出质疑。当此类行为歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利时，它必须经受住严格的审查——最严格的审查标准。这要求国家证明其行动对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。由于这几乎是不可能达到的标准，因此受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的国家行为几乎总是被认为是违宪的。在这里，该州计划歧视非公民居民的父母，拒绝向其他新生儿父母发放 1,000 美元的奖励。由于公民身份是一个可疑的类别，因此需要进行严格的审查。而且，由于国家促进人口增长的利益可以在不将居民非公民排除在其奖励计划之外的情况下实现，因此国家无法证明其行动对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。因此，法院应认定该州将非公民排除在奖励计划之外是违宪的。 （选项A）根据合理基础测试（最不严格的审查标准），政府行为只要与任何合法的国家利益合理相关，就符合宪法。但这一测试仅适用于政府行为不影响准可疑阶层、可疑阶层或基本权利的情况。 （选项 B）第十修正案赋予各州联邦政府未赋予的任何权力。但各州在行使这些权力时必须遵守其他宪法条款，例如平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。 （选项 C）国会不能授权根据公民身份进行州分类。这种分类必须经过严格的司法审查。\n\n教育目标：歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利的国家行为将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。根据这种最严格的审查标准，此类行为是违宪的，除非国家能够证明这些行为对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 7 (1977)（解释说基于公民身份的国家分类本质上是可疑的，并且受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 D：这种排除是违宪的，因为对这种基于公民身份的国家分类的司法审查受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且国家无法证明这种分类对于促进令人信服的国家利益是必要的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-07528abbab3c00cf"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":181,"sourceQuestionPage":540,"sourceQuestionPages":[540],"sourceExplanationPages":[541,542],"sourceAnswerPages":[541,542],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-182","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Establishment Clause","question":"The governor of a state proposes to place a Christmas nativity scene, the components of which would be permanently donated to the state by private citizens, in the state capitol building rotunda where the state legislature meets annually. The governor further proposes to display this state-owned nativity scene annually from December 1 to December 31, next to permanent displays that depict the various products manufactured in the state. The governor's proposal is supported by all members of both houses of the legislature. If the governor's proposed nativity-scene display is challenged in a lawsuit on establishment clause grounds, will the court likely find it constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the components of the nativity scene would be owned by the state rather than by private persons.","B":"No, because the prominent state-owned devotional display is not shown to fit a historically accepted tradition and is not presented as part of an inclusive secular holiday display.","C":"Yes, because the components of the nativity scene would be donated to the state by private citizens rather than purchased with state funds.","D":"Yes, because the nativity scene would be displayed alongside an exhibit of various products manufactured in the state."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022) abandoned Lemon and the endorsement test. The governing inquiry asks whether government conduct is consistent with historical practices and understandings of the Establishment Clause. Longstanding or inclusive holiday displays may be permissible, and state ownership alone is not dispositive. Here, however, the proposed display is a state-owned devotional nativity scene placed prominently in the seat of the legislature, with no stated longstanding tradition and no secular holiday setting; unrelated exhibits of state-manufactured products do not supply that setting. On these exam facts, the stronger conclusion is that the display resembles official promotion of Christianity without a demonstrated historical analogue, so revised Choice B is best.\n\n(Choice A) Government ownership is relevant but not independently decisive.\n\n(Choice C) Private donation does not convert the later state-owned display into private speech.\n\n(Choice D) Unrelated product exhibits do not establish an inclusive holiday context.\n\nEducational objective: Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause questions are decided by reference to historical practices and understandings, including traditional hallmarks such as governmental coercion, denominational preference, and practices comparable to an established church.\n\nReferences\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507, 535–36 (2022).\n• American Legion v. American Humanist Ass'n, 588 U.S. 29 (2019).\n• Lynch v. Donnelly, 465 U.S. 668 (1984).","educationalObjective":"Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause questions are decided by reference to historical practices and understandings, including traditional hallmarks such as governmental coercion, denominational preference, and practices comparable to an established church.","references":"• Lynch v. Donnelly, 465 U.S. 668, 692–93 (1984) (finding that a government- sponsored nativity scene surrounded by secular holiday displays is not a government endorsement of religion).\n\n• County of Allegheny v. ACLU, 492 U.S. 573, 598–601 (1989) (holding that a government-sponsored nativity scene located on prominent government property and not surrounded by secular holiday displays is unconstitutional).","rule":"Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause questions are decided by reference to historical practices and understandings, including traditional hallmarks such as governmental coercion, denominational preference, and practices comparable to an established church.","irac":{"issue":"If the governor's proposed nativity-scene display is challenged in a lawsuit on establishment clause grounds, will the court likely find it constitutional?","rule":"Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause questions are decided by reference to historical practices and understandings, including traditional hallmarks such as governmental coercion, denominational preference, and practices comparable to an established church.","analysis":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022) abandoned Lemon and the endorsement test. The governing inquiry asks whether government conduct is consistent with historical practices and understandings of the Establishment Clause. Longstanding or inclusive holiday displays may be permissible, and state ownership alone is not dispositive. Here, however, the proposed display is a state-owned devotional nativity scene placed prominently in the seat of the legislature, with no stated longstanding tradition and no secular holiday setting; unrelated exhibits of state-manufactured products do not supply that setting. On these exam facts, the stronger conclusion is that the display resembles official promotion of Christianity without a demonstrated historical analogue, so revised Choice B is best.\n\n(Choice A) Government ownership is relevant but not independently decisive.\n\n(Choice C) Private donation does not convert the later state-owned display into private speech.\n\n(Choice D) Unrelated product exhibits do not establish an inclusive holiday context.\n\nEducational objective: Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause questions are decided by reference to historical practices and understandings, including traditional hallmarks such as governmental coercion, denominational preference, and practices comparable to an established church.\n\nReferences\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507, 535–36 (2022).\n• American Legion v. American Humanist Ass'n, 588 U.S. 29 (2019).\n• Lynch v. Donnelly, 465 U.S. 668 (1984).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the prominent state-owned devotional display is not shown to fit a historically accepted tradition and is not presented as part of an inclusive secular holiday display."},"questionZh":"某州州长提议在每年州议会开会的州议会大厦圆形大厅中放置一个圣诞耶稣诞生场景，其组成部分将由私人永久捐赠给该州。州长还提议每年 12 月 1 日至 12 月 31 日期间展示这个国有的耶稣诞生场景，旁边还有描绘该州制造的各种产品的永久展示。州长的提议得到了立法机关参众两院全体议员的支持。如果州长提议的耶稣诞生场景展示在诉讼中因建制条款受到质疑，法院可能会认为其符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为耶稣诞生场景的组成部分将归国家而不是私人所有。","B":"不，因为这一位于显著政府场所、由州所有的宗教性展示，并未显示其属于历史上可接受的传统，也不是包容性的世俗节日展示的一部分。","C":"是的，因为耶稣诞生场景的组成部分将由私人公民捐赠给国家，而不是用国家资金购买。","D":"是的，因为耶稣诞生的场景将与该州制造的各种产品一起展出。"},"explanationZh":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District（2022）已经放弃 Lemon 测试与“政府背书”测试。现行问题是：政府行为是否符合政教分离条款的历史实践与理解。历史悠久或具有包容性节日背景的宗教展示可能合宪，州所有本身也不是决定性因素。可是，本题拟展示的是由州所有、放在州立法机关显著位置的宗教性耶稣诞生场景；题目没有给出长期历史传统，也没有世俗节日布置，旁边不相关的州产品展览不能补足这一背景。按本题给定事实，更强的结论是该展示近似政府正式推崇基督教，且缺乏已证明的历史类比，因此修订后的选项 B 最佳。\n\n（选项 A）政府所有权有关联，但不能单独决定违宪。\n\n（选项 C）私人捐赠不会使后来由州所有和展示的内容转化为私人言论。\n\n（选项 D）不相关的产品展览不能建立包容性的节日语境。\n\n教育目标：Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题须参照历史实践与理解判断，重点包括政府强迫、宗派偏袒及类似国教制度的传统特征。\n\n参考：Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507（2022）；American Legion v. American Humanist Ass’n, 588 U.S. 29（2019）；Lynch v. Donnelly, 465 U.S. 668（1984）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题须参照历史实践与理解判断，重点包括政府强迫、宗派偏袒及类似国教制度的传统特征。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果州长提议的耶稣诞生场景展示在诉讼中因建制条款受到质疑，法院可能会认为其符合宪法吗？","rule":"Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题须参照历史实践与理解判断，重点包括政府强迫、宗派偏袒及类似国教制度的传统特征。","analysis":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District（2022）已经放弃 Lemon 测试与“政府背书”测试。现行问题是：政府行为是否符合政教分离条款的历史实践与理解。历史悠久或具有包容性节日背景的宗教展示可能合宪，州所有本身也不是决定性因素。可是，本题拟展示的是由州所有、放在州立法机关显著位置的宗教性耶稣诞生场景；题目没有给出长期历史传统，也没有世俗节日布置，旁边不相关的州产品展览不能补足这一背景。按本题给定事实，更强的结论是该展示近似政府正式推崇基督教，且缺乏已证明的历史类比，因此修订后的选项 B 最佳。\n\n（选项 A）政府所有权有关联，但不能单独决定违宪。\n\n（选项 C）私人捐赠不会使后来由州所有和展示的内容转化为私人言论。\n\n（选项 D）不相关的产品展览不能建立包容性的节日语境。\n\n教育目标：Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题须参照历史实践与理解判断，重点包括政府强迫、宗派偏袒及类似国教制度的传统特征。\n\n参考：Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507（2022）；American Legion v. American Humanist Ass’n, 588 U.S. 29（2019）；Lynch v. Donnelly, 465 U.S. 668（1984）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为这一位于显著政府场所、由州所有的宗教性展示，并未显示其属于历史上可接受的传统，也不是包容性的世俗节日展示的一部分。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"corrected-current-framework","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"原解析采用已被放弃的 endorsement test；已改用 Kennedy 的历史实践与理解框架，并修订选项 B 及中英文解析。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507 (2022)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/21-418_i425.pdf"},{"title":"Constitution Annotated — Abandonment of the Lemon Test","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/amdt1-3-7-1/ALDE_00013088/"}],"originalAnswer":"B","revisedAnswer":"B"},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"denominational","ipa":"/dɪnˌɔmʌnˈeɪʃənəl/","syllables":"de·nom·i·na·tional","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 宗派的, 教派的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 宗派的, 教派的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"Establishment Clause questions are decided by reference to historical practices and understandings, including traditional hallmarks such as governmental coercion, denominational preference, and practices comparable to an established church.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"devotional","ipa":"/dɪvˈoʊʃənəl/","syllables":"de·vo·tional","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 忠诚的, 信仰的, 虔诚的, 祷告的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 忠诚的, 信仰的, 虔诚的, 祷告的","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"No, because the prominent state-owned devotional display is not shown to fit a historically accepted tradition and is not presented as part of an inclusive secular holiday display.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-2aef9cec4af97f49"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":182,"sourceQuestionPage":543,"sourceQuestionPages":[543],"sourceExplanationPages":[544,545],"sourceAnswerPages":[544,545],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-183","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A city owned and operated a municipal bus system. The city sold space on its buses for the posting of placards. Under the relevant city ordinance, the administrator of the bus system had sole discretion to decide which placards could be posted on the buses, and the administrator's decision was final. Although most of the placards that appeared on city buses were commercial advertisements, the administrator had often sold space on the buses for placards promoting various political, charitable, and religious causes. After a circus bought space on the buses for placards advertising its upcoming performances, an animal rights organization asked to buy space for a placard with photographs showing the mistreatment of animals in circus shows. The administrator denied the organization's request, stating that the proposed placard would be offensive to the circus, which had paid a substantial sum to place its placards on the buses, and that a circus employee had told her that none of the photographs on the organization's placard depicted animals belonging to this particular circus. The organization sued the administrator in an appropriate court for a declaration that her denial of the organization's request for placard space for the reasons she gave violated the First Amendment as made applicable to the states by the Fourteenth Amendment. Is the organization likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a public official may not allow the use of public facilities for the propagation of a message that he or she believes may create a false or misleading impression.","B":"No, because the administrator's denial of space to the organization was a reasonable time, place, and manner restriction of speech.","C":"Yes, because a public official may not refuse to allow the use of any public facility to publish a message dealing with an issue of public concern.","D":"Yes, because a public official may not refuse to permit the dissemination of a message in a public forum solely on the basis of its content unless that denial is necessary to serve a compelling government interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Forum-based regulation of speech\n\nTypes Definition Regulations\n\nTraditional public Public property historically used for, or Content-based regulations forums associated with, public speech (eg, prohibited streets, sidewalks, parks)\n\nDesignated Public property opened for specific (limited) public types of expression (eg, school forums classrooms, civic auditoriums)\n\nNonpublic forums All other public property Content-based regulations permitted if viewpoint- neutral\n\nThe First Amendment prohibits the government from engaging in content-based discrimination against different categories of speech in public forums. Therefore, government censorship of speech based solely on its message (or what is said) will only be upheld if the government can satisfy strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that its actions are necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the administrator for the municipal bus system denied the animal rights organization's request to buy space for placards with photos of mistreated circus animals. Her stated reasoning was that the content of the placard would offend the circus and the photos therein did not depict animals belonging to that circus. Since this constitutes content-based discrimination that is unlikely to pass strict scrutiny, the organization will likely prevail. (Choice A) The government can limit the use of public facilities for commercial speech (eg, an advertisement placard) that creates a false or misleading impression since that speech is not protected by the First Amendment. But it cannot limit the use of public facilities for noncommercial speech—even if it is false or misleading—since that amounts to a content- based restriction subject to strict scrutiny. (Choice B) The administrator's denial of space for bus placards was based on the content—not the time, place, or manner—of the organization's message. However, reasonable time, place, and manner restrictions are permitted in designated public forums. Here, the placard spaces likely qualify as designated public forums because the city opened this public property for expressive use—including political, charitable, religious, and commercial content.*\n\n*In contrast, the bus likely would have been a nonpublic forum had the city permitted only commercial content or put comprehensive, restrictive guidelines in place for approving noncommercial content. (Choice C) A public official engages in content-based regulation by refusing to allow the use of a public facility to publish a message that addresses a public concern. But this refusal is permitted in nonpublic forums so long as it is viewpoint-neutral—ie, denies discussion on all sides of the issue.\n\nEducational objective: The government can only impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums if the government can satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, prove that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Lehman v. City of Shaker Heights, 418 U.S. 298, 303–04 (1974) (holding that advertisement placards on a city-owned transit system are a nonpublic forum when the city permits commercial content only).\n\n• N.Y. Magazine v. Metro. Transp. Auth., 136 F.3d 128, 130 (2d Cir. 1998) (holding that a designated public forum exists when a city permits both noncommercial and commercial content on city-owned transit systems).","educationalObjective":"The government can only impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums if the government can satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, prove that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Lehman v. City of Shaker Heights, 418 U.S. 298, 303–04 (1974) (holding that advertisement placards on a city-owned transit system are a nonpublic forum when the city permits commercial content only).\n\n• N.Y. Magazine v. Metro. Transp. Auth., 136 F.3d 128, 130 (2d Cir. 1998) (holding that a designated public forum exists when a city permits both noncommercial and commercial content on city-owned transit systems).","rule":"The government can only impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums if the government can satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, prove that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the organization likely to prevail?","rule":"The government can only impose content-based restrictions on speech in public forums if the government can satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, prove that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"Forum-based regulation of speech\n\nTypes Definition Regulations\n\nTraditional public Public property historically used for, or Content-based regulations forums associated with, public speech (eg, prohibited streets, sidewalks, parks)\n\nDesignated Public property opened for specific (limited) public types of expression (eg, school forums classrooms, civic auditoriums)\n\nNonpublic forums All other public property Content-based regulations permitted if viewpoint- neutral\n\nThe First Amendment prohibits the government from engaging in content-based discrimination against different categories of speech in public forums. Therefore, government censorship of speech based solely on its message (or what is said) will only be upheld if the government can satisfy strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that its actions are necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the administrator for the municipal bus system denied the animal rights organization's request to buy space for placards with photos of mistreated circus animals. Her stated reasoning was that the content of the placard would offend the circus and the photos therein did not depict animals belonging to that circus. Since this constitutes content-based discrimination that is unlikely to pass strict scrutiny, the organization will likely prevail. (Choice A) The government can limit the use of public facilities for commercial speech (eg, an advertisement placard) that creates a false or misleading impression since that speech is not protected by the First Amendment. But it cannot limit the use of public facilities for noncommercial speech—even if it is false or misleading—since that amounts to a content- based restriction subject to strict scrutiny. (Choice B) The administrator's denial of space for bus placards was based on the content—not the time, place, or manner—of the organization's message. However, reasonable time, place, and manner restrictions are permitted in designated public forums. Here, the placard spaces likely qualify as designated public forums because the city opened this public property for expressive use—including political, charitable, religious, and commercial content.*\n\n*In contrast, the bus likely would have been a nonpublic forum had the city permitted only commercial content or put comprehensive, restrictive guidelines in place for approving noncommercial content. (Choice C) A public official engages in content-based regulation by refusing to allow the use of a public facility to publish a message that addresses a public concern. But this refusal is permitted in nonpublic forums so long as it is viewpoint-neutral—ie, denies discussion on all sides of the issue.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because a public official may not refuse to permit the dissemination of a message in a public forum solely on the basis of its content unless that denial is necessary to serve a compelling government interest."},"questionZh":"某个城市拥有并运营市政公交系统。该市出售公交车上的空间来张贴标语牌。根据相关城市法令，公交车系统管理员可自行决定哪些标牌可以张贴在公交车上，且管理员的决定为最终决定。尽管城市公交车上出现的大多数标语牌都是商业广告，但管理人员经常在公交车上出售空间，用于宣传各种政治、慈善和宗教事业的标语牌。一家马戏团在巴士上购买了空间来放置即将举行的表演的标语牌，随后，一个动物权利组织要求购买空间来放置标语牌，上面的照片显示了马戏团表演中虐待动物的情况。管理员拒绝了该组织的请求，称拟议的标语牌会冒犯马戏团，因为马戏团已经支付了一大笔钱将其标语牌放置在公共汽车上，并且一名马戏团员工告诉她，该组织标语牌上的照片都没有描绘属于这个特定马戏团的动物。该组织在适当的法院起诉管理员，要求其声明她以她给出的理由拒绝该组织的标语牌空间请求违反了第一修正案，该修正案通过第十四修正案适用于各州。该组织有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为公职人员可能不允许使用公共设施来传播他或她认为可能会造成虚假或误导性印象的信息。","B":"不，因为管理员对组织空间的剥夺是合理的时间、地点和方式的言论限制。","C":"是的，因为公职人员不得拒绝允许使用任何公共设施发布涉及公众关注问题的消息。","D":"是的，因为公职人员不得仅因其内容而拒绝允许在公共论坛上传播消息，除非为了实现令人信服的政府利益而必须拒绝该消息。"},"explanationZh":"基于论坛的言论监管\n\n类型 定义 规定\n\n传统公共 历史上用于公共言论的公共财产或与公共言论相关的基于内容的法规论坛（例如禁止的街道、人行道、公园）\n\n为特定（有限）公共表达类型开放的指定公共财产（例如学校论坛教室、公民礼堂）\n\n非公共论坛 所有其他公共财产 如果观点中立，则允许基于内容的法规\n\n第一修正案禁止政府对公共论坛上不同类别的言论进行基于内容的歧视。因此，只有在政府能够满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下，政府仅根据其信息（或所说内容）进行的言论审查才会得到维持。这要求政府证明其行动是必要的，并且是针对实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。在这里，市政公交系统的管理员拒绝了动物权利组织购买带有虐待马戏动物照片的标语牌的空间的请求。她声称的理由是标语牌的内容会冒犯马戏团，而且其中的照片没有描绘属于该马戏团的动物。由于这构成基于内容的歧视，不太可能通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此该组织很可能会获胜。 （选择A）政府可以限制使用公共设施进行商业言论（例如广告牌），因为这种言论不受第一修正案的保护，从而造成虚假或误导性印象。但它不能限制使用公共设施进行非商业言论——即使它是虚假或误导性的——因为这相当于基于内容的限制，需要受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。 （选项 B） 管理员拒绝为公交车标语牌提供空间是基于组织消息的内容，而不是时间、地点或方式。但指定公共论坛允许合理的时间、地点和方式限制。在这里，标语牌空间可能有资格作为指定的公共论坛，因为该市开放了这一公共财产用于表达用途，包括政治、慈善、宗教和商业内容。*\n\n*相比之下，如果该市只允许商业内容或制定全面的限制性准则来批准非商业内容，那么公交车很可能会成为一个非公共论坛。 （选项 C）公职人员通过拒绝允许使用公共设施发布解决公众关切的消息来参与基于内容的监管。但这种拒绝在非公开论坛中是允许的，只要它是观点中立的——即否认问题各方面的讨论。\n\n教育目标：如果政府能够满足严格的审查测试，即证明该限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，那么政府只能对公共论坛上的言论施加基于内容的限制。\n\n参考文献\n• Lehman v. City of Shaker Heights, 418 U.S. 298, 303–04 (1974)（认为当城市只允许商业内容时，城市拥有的交通系统上的广告牌属于非公共论坛）。\n\n• 纽约杂志诉Metro。运输。 Auth., 136 F.3d 128, 130 (2d Cir. 1998)（认为当一个城市允许在其拥有的交通系统上发布非商业和商业内容时，就存在指定的公共论坛）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果政府能够满足严格的审查测试，即证明该限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，那么政府只能对公共论坛上的言论实施基于内容的限制。","iracZh":{"issue":"该组织有可能获胜吗？","rule":"如果政府能够满足严格的审查测试，即证明该限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，那么政府只能对公共论坛上的言论实施基于内容的限制。","analysis":"基于论坛的言论监管\n\n类型 定义 规定\n\n传统公共 历史上用于公共言论的公共财产或与公共言论相关的基于内容的法规论坛（例如禁止的街道、人行道、公园）\n\n为特定（有限）公共表达类型开放的指定公共财产（例如学校论坛教室、公民礼堂）\n\n非公共论坛 所有其他公共财产 如果观点中立，则允许基于内容的法规\n\n第一修正案禁止政府对公共论坛上不同类别的言论进行基于内容的歧视。因此，只有在政府能够满足严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下，政府仅根据其信息（或所说内容）进行的言论审查才会得到维持。这要求政府证明其行动是必要的，并且是针对实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。在这里，市政公交系统的管理员拒绝了动物权利组织购买带有虐待马戏动物照片的标语牌的空间的请求。她声称的理由是标语牌的内容会冒犯马戏团，而且其中的照片没有描绘属于该马戏团的动物。由于这构成基于内容的歧视，不太可能通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此该组织很可能会获胜。 （选择A）政府可以限制使用公共设施进行商业言论（例如广告牌），因为这种言论不受第一修正案的保护，从而造成虚假或误导性印象。但它不能限制使用公共设施进行非商业言论——即使它是虚假或误导性的——因为这相当于基于内容的限制，需要受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。 （选项 B） 管理员拒绝为公交车标语牌提供空间是基于组织消息的内容，而不是时间、地点或方式。但指定公共论坛允许合理的时间、地点和方式限制。在这里，标语牌空间可能有资格作为指定的公共论坛，因为该市开放了这一公共财产用于表达用途，包括政治、慈善、宗教和商业内容。*\n\n*相比之下，如果该市只允许商业内容或制定全面的限制性准则来批准非商业内容，那么公交车很可能会成为一个非公共论坛。 （选项 C）公职人员通过拒绝允许使用公共设施发布解决公众关切的消息来参与基于内容的监管。但这种拒绝在非公开论坛中是允许的，只要它是观点中立的——即否认问题各方面的讨论。\n\n教育目标：如果政府能够满足严格的审查测试，即证明该限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，那么政府只能对公共论坛上的言论施加基于内容的限制。\n\n参考文献\n• Lehman v. City of Shaker Heights, 418 U.S. 298, 303–04 (1974)（认为当城市只允许商业内容时，城市拥有的交通系统上的广告牌属于非公共论坛）。\n\n• 纽约杂志诉Metro。运输。 Auth., 136 F.3d 128, 130 (2d Cir. 1998)（认为当一个城市允许在其拥有的交通系统上发布非商业和商业内容时，就存在指定的公共论坛）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为公职人员不得仅因其内容而拒绝允许在公共论坛上传播消息，除非为了实现令人信服的政府利益而必须拒绝该消息。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"censorship","ipa":"/sˈɛnsɝʃˌɪp/","syllables":"cen·sor·ship","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 检查制度；[医] 督察, 监察","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 检查制度；[医] 督察, 监察","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Therefore, government censorship of speech based solely on its message (or what is said) will only be upheld if the government can satisfy strict scrutiny.","synonyms":["censoring","security review"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":183,"sourceQuestionPage":546,"sourceQuestionPages":[546],"sourceExplanationPages":[547,548],"sourceAnswerPages":[547,548],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-184","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state law imposed a generally applicable sales tax payable by the vendor. That law exempted from its provisions the sale of \"all magazines, periodicals, newspapers, and books.\" In order to raise additional revenue, the state legislature eliminated that broad exemption and substituted a narrower exemption. The new, narrower exemption excluded from the state sales tax only the sale of those \"magazines, periodicals, newspapers, and books that are published or distributed by a state-recognized religious faith and that consist wholly of writings sacred to such a religious faith.\" A magazine devoted to history and politics paid under protest the sales tax due on its sales according to the amended sales tax law. The magazine then filed suit against the state in an appropriate state court for a refund of the sales taxes paid. It contended that the state's elimination of the earlier, broader exemption and adoption of the new, narrower exemption restricted to sacred writings of state-recognized religious faiths violates the First and Fourteenth Amendments to the Constitution. In this case, how will the court likely rule?","choices":{"A":"The Eleventh Amendment bars the state court from exercising jurisdiction over this suit in the absence of a state law expressly waiving the state's immunity.","B":"The magazine lacks standing to sue for a refund of sales taxes imposed by a generally applicable state law because Article III of the Constitution precludes taxpayers from bringing such suits.","C":"The new, narrower exemption from the state sales tax law violates the establishment clause of the First and Fourteenth Amendments by granting preferential state support to religious faiths recognized by the state.","D":"The new, narrower exemption from the state sales tax law violates the freedom of the press guaranteed by the First and Fourteenth Amendments because it imposes a prior restraint on nonreligious publications that are required to pay the tax."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Establishment clause tests\n\nTest Requirements Applicability\n\nHistorical*\n• Longstanding tradition & Default historical foundation Endorsement\n• No appearance that government Public displays or monuments endorses religion (eg, holiday displays)\n\nCoercion\n• No forced conformity with Public schools (eg, convocation religious belief or practice prayer)\n\n*Courts may apply strict scrutiny if government directly benefits/burdens religion.\n\nGovernment benefits to religious organizations must comply with the First Amendment establishment clause, which applies to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment. Challenges brought under this clause are generally reviewed under the historical test. However, courts may forego this test and apply the more stringent strict scrutiny test when the law directly benefits or burdens a particular religion. Under strict scrutiny, the law is invalid unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the new sales tax exemption applies only to sacred writings published or distributed by religious faiths recognized by the state. This exemption directly benefits state- recognized religions and burdens unrecognized religions, so strict scrutiny applies. And since it is unlikely that the state will be able to show that granting preferential state support to state-recognized religious faiths is necessary to achieve a compelling interest, the court will likely rule that the exemption violates the establishment clause. (Choice A) The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties (and foreign governments) from suing a state in federal court without the state's express consent. But since the magazine sued in state court, the Eleventh Amendment does not apply. (Choice B) Article III's standing requirement only applies to federal courts. But even if it applied to state courts, taxpayers do have standing to sue the government for sales tax refunds or any other issue regarding taxes owed if that injury is redressable by the remedy sought. (Choice D) Prior restraints are a form of government censorship that bans publication before it occurs. As a result, they are unconstitutional absent exceptional circumstances (eg, national security). Here, the tax exemption is not a prior restraint since it does not prohibit publication.\n\nEducational objective: Establishment clause challenges are generally reviewed under the historical test. But strict scrutiny can be used instead when a law directly benefits or burdens a particular religion. In that case, the law is invalid unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Texas Monthly, Inc. v. Bullock, 489 U.S. 1, 14–15, 20 (1989) (holding that preferential tax exemptions solely for religious groups violate the establishment clause).\n\n• Larson v. Valente, 456 U.S. 228, 246 (1982) (explaining that strict scrutiny applies to laws that aid or prefer one religion).","educationalObjective":"Establishment clause challenges are generally reviewed under the historical test. But strict scrutiny can be used instead when a law directly benefits or burdens a particular religion. In that case, the law is invalid unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Texas Monthly, Inc. v. Bullock, 489 U.S. 1, 14–15, 20 (1989) (holding that preferential tax exemptions solely for religious groups violate the establishment clause).\n\n• Larson v. Valente, 456 U.S. 228, 246 (1982) (explaining that strict scrutiny applies to laws that aid or prefer one religion).","rule":"Establishment clause challenges are generally reviewed under the historical test. But strict scrutiny can be used instead when a law directly benefits or burdens a particular religion. In that case, the law is invalid unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, how will the court likely rule?","rule":"Establishment clause challenges are generally reviewed under the historical test. But strict scrutiny can be used instead when a law directly benefits or burdens a particular religion. In that case, the law is invalid unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"Establishment clause tests\n\nTest Requirements Applicability\n\nHistorical*\n• Longstanding tradition & Default historical foundation Endorsement\n• No appearance that government Public displays or monuments endorses religion (eg, holiday displays)\n\nCoercion\n• No forced conformity with Public schools (eg, convocation religious belief or practice prayer)\n\n*Courts may apply strict scrutiny if government directly benefits/burdens religion.\n\nGovernment benefits to religious organizations must comply with the First Amendment establishment clause, which applies to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment. Challenges brought under this clause are generally reviewed under the historical test. However, courts may forego this test and apply the more stringent strict scrutiny test when the law directly benefits or burdens a particular religion. Under strict scrutiny, the law is invalid unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the new sales tax exemption applies only to sacred writings published or distributed by religious faiths recognized by the state. This exemption directly benefits state- recognized religions and burdens unrecognized religions, so strict scrutiny applies. And since it is unlikely that the state will be able to show that granting preferential state support to state-recognized religious faiths is necessary to achieve a compelling interest, the court will likely rule that the exemption violates the establishment clause. (Choice A) The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties (and foreign governments) from suing a state in federal court without the state's express consent. But since the magazine sued in state court, the Eleventh Amendment does not apply. (Choice B) Article III's standing requirement only applies to federal courts. But even if it applied to state courts, taxpayers do have standing to sue the government for sales tax refunds or any other issue regarding taxes owed if that injury is redressable by the remedy sought. (Choice D) Prior restraints are a form of government censorship that bans publication before it occurs. As a result, they are unconstitutional absent exceptional circumstances (eg, national security). Here, the tax exemption is not a prior restraint since it does not prohibit publication.","conclusion":"Answer C: The new, narrower exemption from the state sales tax law violates the establishment clause of the First and Fourteenth Amendments by granting preferential state support to religious faiths recognized by the state."},"questionZh":"州法律规定供应商应缴纳普遍适用的销售税。该法律免除了“所有杂志、期刊、报纸和书籍”销售的约束。为了增加额外收入，州立法机构取消了广泛的豁免，取而代之的是范围更窄的豁免。新的、范围更窄的豁免仅将那些“由国家认可的宗教信仰出版或发行的、完全由该宗教信仰神圣的著作组成的杂志、期刊、报纸和书籍”的销售排除在国家销售税之外。一家致力于历史和政治的杂志根据修订后的销售税法缴纳了销售税，以抗议其销售。该杂志随后向相应的州法院提起诉讼，要求退还已缴纳的销售税。它认为，该州取消了先前的、更广泛的豁免，并采用了仅限于国家承认的宗教信仰的神圣著作的新的、更窄的豁免，违反了宪法第一修正案和第十四修正案。在这种情况下，法院可能会如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"第十一条修正案禁止州法院在没有州法律明确放弃州豁免权的情况下对本诉讼行使管辖权。","B":"该杂志没有资格起诉要求退还普遍适用的州法律征收的销售税，因为宪法第三条禁止纳税人提起此类诉讼。","C":"新的、范围更窄的州销售税法豁免条款向国家承认的宗教信仰提供国家优惠支持，违反了第一修正案和第十四修正案的设立条款。","D":"新的、范围更窄的州销售税法豁免违反了第一修正案和第十四修正案所保障的新闻自由，因为它对需要纳税的非宗教出版物施加了事先限制。"},"explanationZh":"设立条款测试\n\n测试要求的适用性\n\n历史*\n• 悠久传统&默认历史基础背书\n• 政府公共展示或纪念碑不支持宗教（例如节日展示）\n\n强制\n• 不得强迫遵守公立学校规定（例如，召集宗教信仰或进行祈祷）\n\n*如果政府直接使宗教受益/加重宗教负担，法院可能会进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n政府向宗教组织提供的福利必须符合第一修正案的设立条款，该条款通过第十四修正案适用于各州。该条款带来的挑战通常会经过历史检验。然而，当法律直接有利于特定宗教或给特定宗教带来负担时，法院可能会放弃这一测试，并适用更严格的严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）测试。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则该法律无效。在这里，新的销售税豁免仅适用于国家认可的宗教信仰出版或发行的神圣著作。这种豁免直接有利于国家承认的宗教，却给未得到承认的宗教带来负担，因此需要严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。而且，由于国家不太可能证明向国家承认的宗教信仰提供优惠的国家支持对于实现令人信服的利益是必要的，因此法院可能会裁定豁免违反了确立条款。 （选项 A）第十一修正案禁止私人团体（和外国政府）未经州政府明确同意，不得在联邦法院起诉州政府。但由于该杂志在州法院提起诉讼，第十一修正案并不适用。 （选择B） 第三条的常设要求仅适用于联邦法院。但即使它适用于州法院，纳税人也确实有资格起诉政府，要求退还销售税或任何其他有关所欠税款的问题，如果所寻求的补救措施可以弥补这种损害。 （选项 D）事前限制是政府审查的一种形式，在发布之前禁止发布。因此，如果没有特殊情况（例如国家安全），它们就是违宪的。在这里，免税并不是一种事先限制，因为它并不禁止出版。\n\n教育目标：建制条款的挑战通常在历史检验下进行审查。但是，当法律直接使特定宗教受益或给特定宗教带来负担时，可以采用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这种情况下，除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则该法律无效。\n\n参考文献\n• Texas Monthly, Inc. v. Bullock, 489 U.S. 1, 14–15, 20 (1989)（认为仅针对宗教团体的优惠免税违反了建国条款）。\n\n• Larson v. Valente, 456 U.S. 228, 246 (1982)（解释说严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）适用于支持或偏好某一宗教的法律）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"建制条款的挑战通常会根据历史检验进行审查。但是，当法律直接使特定宗教受益或给特定宗教带来负担时，可以采用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这种情况下，除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则该法律无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种情况下，法院可能会如何判决？","rule":"建制条款的挑战通常会根据历史检验进行审查。但是，当法律直接使特定宗教受益或给特定宗教带来负担时，可以采用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这种情况下，除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则该法律无效。","analysis":"设立条款测试\n\n测试要求的适用性\n\n历史*\n• 悠久传统&默认历史基础背书\n• 政府公共展示或纪念碑不支持宗教（例如节日展示）\n\n强制\n• 不得强迫遵守公立学校规定（例如，召集宗教信仰或进行祈祷）\n\n*如果政府直接使宗教受益/加重宗教负担，法院可能会进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n政府向宗教组织提供的福利必须符合第一修正案的设立条款，该条款通过第十四修正案适用于各州。该条款带来的挑战通常会经过历史检验。然而，当法律直接有利于特定宗教或给特定宗教带来负担时，法院可能会放弃这一测试，并适用更严格的严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）测试。在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则该法律无效。在这里，新的销售税豁免仅适用于国家认可的宗教信仰出版或发行的神圣著作。这种豁免直接有利于国家承认的宗教，却给未得到承认的宗教带来负担，因此需要严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。而且，由于国家不太可能证明向国家承认的宗教信仰提供优惠的国家支持对于实现令人信服的利益是必要的，因此法院可能会裁定豁免违反了确立条款。 （选项 A）第十一修正案禁止私人团体（和外国政府）未经州政府明确同意，不得在联邦法院起诉州政府。但由于该杂志在州法院提起诉讼，第十一修正案并不适用。 （选择B） 第三条的常设要求仅适用于联邦法院。但即使它适用于州法院，纳税人也确实有资格起诉政府，要求退还销售税或任何其他有关所欠税款的问题，如果所寻求的补救措施可以弥补这种损害。 （选项 D）事前限制是政府审查的一种形式，在发布之前禁止发布。因此，如果没有特殊情况（例如国家安全），它们就是违宪的。在这里，免税并不是一种事先限制，因为它并不禁止出版。\n\n教育目标：建制条款的挑战通常在历史检验下进行审查。但是，当法律直接使特定宗教受益或给特定宗教带来负担时，可以采用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这种情况下，除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则该法律无效。\n\n参考文献\n• Texas Monthly, Inc. v. Bullock, 489 U.S. 1, 14–15, 20 (1989)（认为仅针对宗教团体的优惠免税违反了建国条款）。\n\n• Larson v. Valente, 456 U.S. 228, 246 (1982)（解释说严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）适用于支持或偏好某一宗教的法律）。","conclusion":"答案 C：新的、范围更窄的州销售税法豁免条款向国家承认的宗教信仰提供国家优惠支持，违反了第一修正案和第十四修正案的设立条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"waiving","ipa":"/wˈeɪvɪŋ/","syllables":"waiv·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 放弃","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[法] 放弃","wordForm":"词；原形 waive；词形 0:waive/1:i","sourceSentence":"The Eleventh Amendment bars the state court from exercising jurisdiction over this suit in the absence of a state law expressly waiving the state's immunity.","synonyms":["forfeit","give up","throw overboard","forgo"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":184,"sourceQuestionPage":549,"sourceQuestionPages":[549],"sourceExplanationPages":[550,551],"sourceAnswerPages":[550,551],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-185","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A city ordinance created a three-member zoning board, which is responsible for approving the location of all entertainment venues in the city. In an inseverable provision, the ordinance requires that one member of the board be a representative of the local council of churches. A minister was appointed to the board to represent the local council of churches. During the minister's tenure, the board denied a company permission to open a nightclub in a particular location solely because it would be so close to an existing church that it might disturb the church's operations. The company has challenged the ordinance and its application in federal court on constitutional grounds. Is the company likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the existing church has a right to have its vested property interests protected.","B":"No, because the minister was only one of three votes and, therefore, could not dictate the decision of the zoning board.","C":"Yes, because, as applied, the ordinance denied the company the equal protection of the laws by irrationally discriminating against its particular type of business.","D":"Yes, because the requirement that the zoning board include a representative of the local council of churches violates the First and Fourteenth Amendments."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A law that benefits or burdens religion can be challenged under the First Amendment establishment clause, as applied to states and municipalities through the Fourteenth Amendment. This clause places a wall of separation between church and state. But this wall contains gates that can be opened if the government passes the appropriate test. The busiest gate—the historical test—opens when a law has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution. Here, an ordinance requires that one member of the zoning board be a representative of the local council of churches. However, guaranteeing religious institutions positions on government boards and committees has historically not been permitted under the original meaning of the Constitution. Since the ordinance requires membership by a church representative on the zoning board, the gates of the establishment-clause wall should remain closed. Therefore, the company will likely prevail. (Choice A) Although the church has a vested interest in its property, this interest can only be protected by constitutional means—eg, religiously neutral zoning decisions. (Choice B) The minister had only one of three votes and could not dictate the zoning board's decision. But the provision granting him a spot on the board is still unconstitutional as violative of the establishment clause. (Choice C) The equal protection clause can be used to invalidate a law that is applied in a discriminatory manner. But since there is no evidence that similarly situated businesses were permitted to open near a church, there is no proof of discrimination against the company. As a result, it is unlikely to prevail on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: The historical test allows the government to pass through the First Amendment establishment-clause wall separating church and state when the government action has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.\n\nReferences\n• Larkin v. Grendel's Den, 459 U.S. 116, 125–27 (1982) (holding that a law that delegates or shares important government powers with a religious institution violates the First Amendment establishment clause).\n\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School Dist., 142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022) (holding that challenges based on the establishment clause \"must be interpreted by reference to historical practices and understandings\").","educationalObjective":"The historical test allows the government to pass through the First Amendment establishment-clause wall separating church and state when the government action has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.","references":"• Larkin v. Grendel's Den, 459 U.S. 116, 125–27 (1982) (holding that a law that delegates or shares important government powers with a religious institution violates the First Amendment establishment clause).\n\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School Dist., 142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022) (holding that challenges based on the establishment clause \"must be interpreted by reference to historical practices and understandings\").","rule":"The historical test allows the government to pass through the First Amendment establishment-clause wall separating church and state when the government action has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.","irac":{"issue":"Is the company likely to prevail?","rule":"The historical test allows the government to pass through the First Amendment establishment-clause wall separating church and state when the government action has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution.","analysis":"A law that benefits or burdens religion can be challenged under the First Amendment establishment clause, as applied to states and municipalities through the Fourteenth Amendment. This clause places a wall of separation between church and state. But this wall contains gates that can be opened if the government passes the appropriate test. The busiest gate—the historical test—opens when a law has historically been permitted in accordance with the original meaning of the Constitution. Here, an ordinance requires that one member of the zoning board be a representative of the local council of churches. However, guaranteeing religious institutions positions on government boards and committees has historically not been permitted under the original meaning of the Constitution. Since the ordinance requires membership by a church representative on the zoning board, the gates of the establishment-clause wall should remain closed. Therefore, the company will likely prevail. (Choice A) Although the church has a vested interest in its property, this interest can only be protected by constitutional means—eg, religiously neutral zoning decisions. (Choice B) The minister had only one of three votes and could not dictate the zoning board's decision. But the provision granting him a spot on the board is still unconstitutional as violative of the establishment clause. (Choice C) The equal protection clause can be used to invalidate a law that is applied in a discriminatory manner. But since there is no evidence that similarly situated businesses were permitted to open near a church, there is no proof of discrimination against the company. As a result, it is unlikely to prevail on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the requirement that the zoning board include a representative of the local council of churches violates the First and Fourteenth Amendments."},"questionZh":"一项城市法令设立了一个三人分区委员会，负责批准该市所有娱乐场所的位置。在一项不可分割的条款中，该法令要求董事会的一名成员是当地教会理事会的代表。一名牧师被任命为董事会成员，代表当地教会理事会。在部长任职期间，董事会拒绝一家公司在特定地点开设夜总会，仅仅是因为该地点距离现有教堂太近，可能会干扰教堂的运营。该公司已基于宪法理由对该法令及其在联邦法院的适用提出质疑。公司有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为现有教会有权保护其既得财产利益。","B":"不可以，因为部长只有三票之一，因此不能决定分区委员会的决定。","C":"是的，因为根据应用，该条例通过不合理地歧视其特定类型的业务，剥夺了公司受到法律的平等保护。","D":"是的，因为分区委员会包括地方教会理事会代表的要求违反了第一修正案和第十四修正案。"},"explanationZh":"有利于宗教或给宗教带来负担的法律可以根据第一修正案设立条款受到质疑，该条款通过第十四修正案适用于州和市。该条款在教会和国家之间设置了一堵隔离墙。但是，如果政府通过适当的测试，这堵墙就可以打开。最繁忙的大门——历史考验——在一项法律根据宪法的原意历史性地获得批准时打开。在这里，一项法令要求分区委员会的一名成员必须是当地教会理事会的代表。然而，根据宪法的原意，历史上并没有允许保障宗教机构在政府理事会和委员会中的地位。由于该法令要求教会代表成为分区委员会的成员，因此建制条款墙的大门应保持关闭。因此，该公司很可能会获胜。 （选项A）虽然教会对其财产拥有既得利益，但这种利益只能通过宪法手段来保护——例如，宗教中立的分区决定。 （选择 B） 部长只有三票中的一票，不能决定分区委员会的决定。但授予他在董事会席位的条款仍然违宪，因为违反了建制条款。 （选项C）平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）可用于使以歧视性方式适用的法律无效。但由于没有证据表明类似情况的企业被允许在教堂附近开业，因此没有证据表明该公司存在歧视。因此，在此基础上它不太可能占上风。\n\n教育目标：历史检验允许政府在历史上根据宪法的本意被允许的情况下，通过第一修正案建制条款隔离教会和国家的墙。\n\n参考文献\n• Larkin v. Grendel's Den, 459 U.S. 116, 125–27 (1982)（认为向宗教机构委托或分享重要政府权力的法律违反了第一修正案的设立条款）。\n\n• 肯尼迪诉布雷默顿学区，142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022)（认为基于成立条款的质疑“必须参考历史实践和理解来解释”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"历史检验允许政府在历史上根据宪法的原始含义允许政府行为时，通过第一修正案建国条款隔离教会和国家的墙。","iracZh":{"issue":"公司有可能获胜吗？","rule":"历史检验允许政府在历史上根据宪法的原始含义允许政府行为时，通过第一修正案建国条款隔离教会和国家的墙。","analysis":"有利于宗教或给宗教带来负担的法律可以根据第一修正案设立条款受到质疑，该条款通过第十四修正案适用于州和市。该条款在教会和国家之间设置了一堵隔离墙。但是，如果政府通过适当的测试，这堵墙就可以打开。最繁忙的大门——历史考验——在一项法律根据宪法的原意历史性地获得批准时打开。在这里，一项法令要求分区委员会的一名成员必须是当地教会理事会的代表。然而，根据宪法的原意，历史上并没有允许保障宗教机构在政府理事会和委员会中的地位。由于该法令要求教会代表成为分区委员会的成员，因此建制条款墙的大门应保持关闭。因此，该公司很可能会获胜。 （选项A）虽然教会对其财产拥有既得利益，但这种利益只能通过宪法手段来保护——例如，宗教中立的分区决定。 （选择 B） 部长只有三票中的一票，不能决定分区委员会的决定。但授予他在董事会席位的条款仍然违宪，因为违反了建制条款。 （选项C）平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）可用于使以歧视性方式适用的法律无效。但由于没有证据表明类似情况的企业被允许在教堂附近开业，因此没有证据表明该公司存在歧视。因此，在此基础上它不太可能占上风。\n\n教育目标：历史检验允许政府在历史上根据宪法的本意被允许的情况下，通过第一修正案建制条款隔离教会和国家的墙。\n\n参考文献\n• Larkin v. Grendel's Den, 459 U.S. 116, 125–27 (1982)（认为向宗教机构委托或分享重要政府权力的法律违反了第一修正案的设立条款）。\n\n• 肯尼迪诉布雷默顿学区，142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022)（认为基于成立条款的质疑“必须参考历史实践和理解来解释”）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为分区委员会包括地方教会理事会代表的要求违反了第一修正案和第十四修正案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-3b705c40dfe53746"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":185,"sourceQuestionPage":552,"sourceQuestionPages":[552],"sourceExplanationPages":[553,554],"sourceAnswerPages":[553,554],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-186","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"A doctor who was licensed to practice medicine in a particular state was convicted in state court of improperly distributing specified drugs by writing prescriptions for fictitious persons. Under state law, such an abuse of the prescription-writing privilege requires revocation of a doctor's license. After it received an official notification of the doctor's conviction, the state medical board revoked the doctor's license without affording him any opportunity for a hearing. The doctor has sued the board in state court to set aside the revocation, alleging deprivation of property without due process of law because the board did not provide an opportunity for a trial-type hearing before revoking the license. The doctor does not deny the conviction or the factual basis for it. Which of the following is the strongest argument in support of the state medical board?","choices":{"A":"A doctor's license to practice is a privilege, not a right, and therefore is not property within the meaning of the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"Due process requires a balancing of interests, and the state's interest in preventing drug abuse outweighs the doctor's interest in the particular procedure followed in the disciplinary proceeding.","C":"The adjudicative facts necessary to revoke the doctor's license were determined in the criminal trial, and therefore due process does not require any further trial-type hearing.","D":"The licensing board was required to summarily revoke the doctor's license because Article IV, Section 1, of the Constitution requires the licensing board to give full faith and credit to the doctor's criminal conviction."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Procedural due process requires that the government provide reasonable notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard before a neutral decision-maker when depriving persons of life, liberty, or property. To determine whether the government provided due process, courts balance three factors:\n• the private interest affected by the deprivation\n\n• the risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest through current procedures and the probable value of additional or substitute procedural safeguards and\n\n• the government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail.\n\nHere, the doctor's property interest in his medical license is likely outweighed by the state's interest in preventing drug abuse—particularly since additional or substitute proceedings would fiscally and administratively burden the medical board. And the risk of erroneously depriving the doctor of his license is low given the protections afforded in his related criminal proceeding. Therefore, the board's strongest argument is that the facts necessary to revoke the doctor's license were determined in the criminal trial, so no further trial-type hearing is required. (Choice A) Due process protections historically extended to rights—not privileges. But this distinction has been abandoned, and such protections extend to any legitimate life, liberty, or property interest. (Choice B) Due process does require a balancing of interests. But it weighs the state's interest in preventing drug abuse against the doctor's interest in his license (not the board's particular procedures) and the risks of current procedures and value of alternative safeguards. (Choice D) Under the full faith and credit clause, a valid judgment issued in one state must be recognized in every other state. This clause does not apply here since the doctor's conviction and license revocation occurred in the same state. But even if it did, the state need only honor the criminal conviction—not revoke the doctor's license.\n\nEducational objective: Procedural due process is assessed by weighing three factors: (1) the private interest affected, (2) the risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest and probable value of other procedural safeguards, and (3) the government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail.\n\nReferences\n• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976) (listing the three factors used to assess compliance with procedural due process).","educationalObjective":"Procedural due process is assessed by weighing three factors: (1) the private interest affected, (2) the risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest and probable value of other procedural safeguards, and (3) the government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail.","references":"• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976) (listing the three factors used to assess compliance with procedural due process).","rule":"Procedural due process is assessed by weighing three factors: (1) the private interest affected, (2) the risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest and probable value of other procedural safeguards, and (3) the government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest argument in support of the state medical board?","rule":"Procedural due process is assessed by weighing three factors: (1) the private interest affected, (2) the risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest and probable value of other procedural safeguards, and (3) the government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail.","analysis":"Procedural due process requires that the government provide reasonable notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard before a neutral decision-maker when depriving persons of life, liberty, or property. To determine whether the government provided due process, courts balance three factors:\n• the private interest affected by the deprivation\n\n• the risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest through current procedures and the probable value of additional or substitute procedural safeguards and\n\n• the government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail.\n\nHere, the doctor's property interest in his medical license is likely outweighed by the state's interest in preventing drug abuse—particularly since additional or substitute proceedings would fiscally and administratively burden the medical board. And the risk of erroneously depriving the doctor of his license is low given the protections afforded in his related criminal proceeding. Therefore, the board's strongest argument is that the facts necessary to revoke the doctor's license were determined in the criminal trial, so no further trial-type hearing is required. (Choice A) Due process protections historically extended to rights—not privileges. But this distinction has been abandoned, and such protections extend to any legitimate life, liberty, or property interest. (Choice B) Due process does require a balancing of interests. But it weighs the state's interest in preventing drug abuse against the doctor's interest in his license (not the board's particular procedures) and the risks of current procedures and value of alternative safeguards. (Choice D) Under the full faith and credit clause, a valid judgment issued in one state must be recognized in every other state. This clause does not apply here since the doctor's conviction and license revocation occurred in the same state. But even if it did, the state need only honor the criminal conviction—not revoke the doctor's license.","conclusion":"Answer C: The adjudicative facts necessary to revoke the doctor's license were determined in the criminal trial, and therefore due process does not require any further trial-type hearing."},"questionZh":"一名在特定州获得行医许可的医生因通过为虚构人物开处方不当分发特定药物而被州法院定罪。根据州法律，这种滥用处方权的行为需要吊销医生执照。在收到该医生定罪的正式通知后，州医疗委员会吊销了该医生的执照，并且没有给他任何听证会的机会。该医生已向州法院起诉该委员会，要求撤销该执照，指控该委员会未经正当法律程序就剥夺了财产，因为该委员会在吊销执照之前没有提供审判型听证会的机会。医生并不否认定罪或其事实依据。以下哪一项是支持州医疗委员会最有力的论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"医生的执业执照是一种特权，而不是一项权利，因此不属于第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）含义内的财产。","B":"正当程序需要利益平衡，国家在防止药物滥用方面的利益超过了医生在纪律程序中遵循的特定程序的利益。","C":"吊销医生执照所需的裁判事实已在刑事审判中确定，因此正当程序不需要任何进一步的审判式听证会。","D":"执照颁发委员会被要求立即吊销该医生的执照，因为《宪法》第四条第一款要求执照颁发委员会对医生的刑事定罪给予充分的信任和信任。"},"explanationZh":"程序正当程序要求政府在剥夺他人生命、自由或财产时提供合理的通知和有意义的机会，以便在中立决策者面前发表意见。为了确定政府是否提供了正当程序，法院会权衡三个因素：\n• 受剥夺影响的私人利益\n\n• 通过现行程序错误剥夺该利益的风险以及额外或替代程序保障措施的可能价值；\n\n• 政府的利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担。\n\n在这里，医生在其医疗执照中的财产利益可能会被国家防止药物滥用的利益所压倒——特别是因为额外或替代程序会给医疗委员会带来财政和行政负担。鉴于相关刑事诉讼中提供的保护，错误地剥夺该医生执照的风险很低。因此，该委员会最有力的论点是，吊销医生执照所需的事实已在刑事审判中确定，因此不需要进一步进行审判式听证会。 （选项 A） 正当程序保护历来延伸至权利，而非特权。但这种区别已被放弃，这种保护延伸到任何合法的生命、自由或财产利益。 （选择 B） 正当程序确实需要利益平衡。但它权衡了国家防止药物滥用的利益与医生对其执照的利益（而不是委员会的特定程序）以及当前程序的风险和替代保障措施的价值。 （选项D）根据完整的信用条款，在一个州做出的有效判决必须在所有其他州得到承认。该条款不适用于此处，因为医生的定罪和执照吊销发生在同一州。但即使确实如此，国家也只需遵守刑事定罪，而不是吊销医生执照。\n\n教育目标：通过权衡三个因素来评估程序正当程序：(1) 受影响的私人利益，(2) 错误剥夺该利益的风险和其他程序保障措施的可能价值，以及 (3) 政府利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担。\n\n参考文献\n• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976)（列出了用于评估遵守程序正当程序的三个因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"程序正当程序通过权衡三个因素来评估：(1) 受影响的私人利益，(2) 错误剥夺该利益的风险和其他程序保障措施的可能价值，以及 (3) 政府利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是支持州医疗委员会最有力的论据？","rule":"程序正当程序通过权衡三个因素来评估：(1) 受影响的私人利益，(2) 错误剥夺该利益的风险和其他程序保障措施的可能价值，以及 (3) 政府利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担。","analysis":"程序正当程序要求政府在剥夺他人生命、自由或财产时提供合理的通知和有意义的机会，以便在中立决策者面前发表意见。为了确定政府是否提供了正当程序，法院会权衡三个因素：\n• 受剥夺影响的私人利益\n\n• 通过现行程序错误剥夺该利益的风险以及额外或替代程序保障措施的可能价值；\n\n• 政府的利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担。\n\n在这里，医生在其医疗执照中的财产利益可能会被国家防止药物滥用的利益所压倒——特别是因为额外或替代程序会给医疗委员会带来财政和行政负担。鉴于相关刑事诉讼中提供的保护，错误地剥夺该医生执照的风险很低。因此，该委员会最有力的论点是，吊销医生执照所需的事实已在刑事审判中确定，因此不需要进一步进行审判式听证会。 （选项 A） 正当程序保护历来延伸至权利，而非特权。但这种区别已被放弃，这种保护延伸到任何合法的生命、自由或财产利益。 （选择 B） 正当程序确实需要利益平衡。但它权衡了国家防止药物滥用的利益与医生对其执照的利益（而不是委员会的特定程序）以及当前程序的风险和替代保障措施的价值。 （选项D）根据完整的信用条款，在一个州做出的有效判决必须在所有其他州得到承认。该条款不适用于此处，因为医生的定罪和执照吊销发生在同一州。但即使确实如此，国家也只需遵守刑事定罪，而不是吊销医生执照。\n\n教育目标：通过权衡三个因素来评估程序正当程序：(1) 受影响的私人利益，(2) 错误剥夺该利益的风险和其他程序保障措施的可能价值，以及 (3) 政府利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担。\n\n参考文献\n• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976)（列出了用于评估遵守程序正当程序的三个因素）。","conclusion":"答案 C：吊销医生执照所需的裁判事实已在刑事审判中确定，因此正当程序不需要任何进一步的审判式听证会。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"adjudicative","ipa":"/ə'dʒu:dikeitiv/","syllables":"ad·ju·dica·tive","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. 判决的, 宣告的；[法] 判决的, 审判的, 裁定的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：a. 判决的, 宣告的；[法] 判决的, 审判的, 裁定的","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"The adjudicative facts necessary to revoke the doctor's license were determined in the criminal trial, and therefore due process does not require any further trial-type hearing.","synonyms":["adjudicatory"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-5c18e68f38dc289f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":186,"sourceQuestionPage":555,"sourceQuestionPages":[555],"sourceExplanationPages":[556,557],"sourceAnswerPages":[556,557],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-187","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state statute prohibits any retailer of books, magazines, pictures, or posters from \"publicly displaying or selling to any person any material that may be harmful to minors because of the violent or sexually explicit nature of its pictorial content.\" Violation of this statute is a misdemeanor. A corner store displays publicly and sells magazines containing violent and sexually explicit pictures. The owner of this store is prosecuted under the above statute for these actions. In defending against this prosecution in a state trial court, which of the following is the best defense for the corner store?","choices":{"A":"Equal protection of the laws, because the statute irrationally distinguishes between violent and sexually explicit pictorial material that may harm minors and such material that may harm only adults.","B":"Equal protection of the laws, because the statute irrationally treats violent and sexually explicit material that is pictorial differently from such material that is composed wholly of printed words.","C":"First Amendment as it is incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment, because a state may not prohibit the sale of violent or sexually explicit material in the absence of proof that the material is utterly without any redeeming value in the marketplace of ideas.","D":"First Amendment as it is incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment, because the statute is excessively vague and overbroad."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment free speech clause (applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment) protects the free flow of ideas with limited exceptions. A law that infringes upon this right can be challenged on its face and struck down in its entirety under either:\n• the overbreadth doctrine – renders laws unconstitutional if they restrict a substantial amount of protected speech or\n\n• the vagueness doctrine – renders laws unconstitutional if ordinary persons would have to guess the law's meaning because the law fails to provide notice of what speech is prohibited.\n\nHere, the state statute regulates speech by prohibiting retailers from publicly displaying or selling any material containing violent or sexually explicit pictures that may be harmful to minors. Although some violent or sexually explicit speech is not protected by the First Amendment, a substantial amount is. Therefore, the overbreadth doctrine is a defense against the enforcement of this statute. And since this statute also requires ordinary persons to guess as to which materials may be harmful to minors, the vagueness doctrine is another valid defense. (Choices A & B) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause prohibits states from treating similarly situated persons (or classes of persons) differently without adequate justification. But here, there is no evidence that this law is being applied in a discriminatory manner—eg, had corner store owners, but no other retailers, been prosecuted under the statute. Therefore, an equal protection defense would likely fail. (Choice C) A state may prohibit the sale of violent or sexually explicit material if (1) it falls outside of the First Amendment's protections—eg, material that incites crime or is obscene under the Miller test—or (2) this content-based restriction passes strict scrutiny. Neither requires proof that the material is utterly without any redeeming value in the marketplace of ideas.\n\nEducational objective: A law that interferes with the freedom of speech will be struck down on its face if it restricts a substantial amount of protected speech (ie, overbroad) OR fails to give ordinary persons notice of the prohibited speech (ie, unduly vague).\n\nReferences\n• Watchtower Bible &amp; Tract Soc'y of N.Y., Inc. v. Vill. of Stratton, 536 U.S. 150, 165 (2002) (finding an ordinance overbroad since it prohibits all door-to-door, not just commercial, canvassing without a license).\n\n• Gentile v. State Bar of Nev., 501 U.S. 1030, 1057–58 (1991) (striking down a law for vagueness since it tends to mislead attorneys about potential punishment for pretrial publicity of clients' criminal defenses).","educationalObjective":"A law that interferes with the freedom of speech will be struck down on its face if it restricts a substantial amount of protected speech (ie, overbroad) OR fails to give ordinary persons notice of the prohibited speech (ie, unduly vague).","references":"• Watchtower Bible &amp; Tract Soc'y of N.Y., Inc. v. Vill. of Stratton, 536 U.S. 150, 165 (2002) (finding an ordinance overbroad since it prohibits all door-to-door, not just commercial, canvassing without a license).\n\n• Gentile v. State Bar of Nev., 501 U.S. 1030, 1057–58 (1991) (striking down a law for vagueness since it tends to mislead attorneys about potential punishment for pretrial publicity of clients' criminal defenses).","rule":"A law that interferes with the freedom of speech will be struck down on its face if it restricts a substantial amount of protected speech (ie, overbroad) OR fails to give ordinary persons notice of the prohibited speech (ie, unduly vague).","irac":{"issue":"In defending against this prosecution in a state trial court, which of the following is the best defense for the corner store?","rule":"A law that interferes with the freedom of speech will be struck down on its face if it restricts a substantial amount of protected speech (ie, overbroad) OR fails to give ordinary persons notice of the prohibited speech (ie, unduly vague).","analysis":"The First Amendment free speech clause (applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment) protects the free flow of ideas with limited exceptions. A law that infringes upon this right can be challenged on its face and struck down in its entirety under either:\n• the overbreadth doctrine – renders laws unconstitutional if they restrict a substantial amount of protected speech or\n\n• the vagueness doctrine – renders laws unconstitutional if ordinary persons would have to guess the law's meaning because the law fails to provide notice of what speech is prohibited.\n\nHere, the state statute regulates speech by prohibiting retailers from publicly displaying or selling any material containing violent or sexually explicit pictures that may be harmful to minors. Although some violent or sexually explicit speech is not protected by the First Amendment, a substantial amount is. Therefore, the overbreadth doctrine is a defense against the enforcement of this statute. And since this statute also requires ordinary persons to guess as to which materials may be harmful to minors, the vagueness doctrine is another valid defense. (Choices A & B) The Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause prohibits states from treating similarly situated persons (or classes of persons) differently without adequate justification. But here, there is no evidence that this law is being applied in a discriminatory manner—eg, had corner store owners, but no other retailers, been prosecuted under the statute. Therefore, an equal protection defense would likely fail. (Choice C) A state may prohibit the sale of violent or sexually explicit material if (1) it falls outside of the First Amendment's protections—eg, material that incites crime or is obscene under the Miller test—or (2) this content-based restriction passes strict scrutiny. Neither requires proof that the material is utterly without any redeeming value in the marketplace of ideas.","conclusion":"Answer D: First Amendment as it is incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment, because the statute is excessively vague and overbroad."},"questionZh":"州法规禁止任何书籍、杂志、图片或海报零售商“向任何人公开展示或出售任何可能因其图片内容的暴力或色情性质而对未成年人有害的材料”。违反本法规属于轻罪。街角商店公开展示并出售包含暴力和色情图片的杂志。该商店的老板因这些行为根据上述法律被起诉。在州初审法院针对这一起诉进行辩护时，以下哪项是街角商店的最佳辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"法律的平等保护，因为该法规不合理地将可能伤害未成年人的暴力和色情图片材料与可能仅伤害成年人的材料区分开来。","B":"法律的平等保护，因为该法规不合理地对待暴力和露骨的图片材料与完全由印刷文字组成的材料。","C":"第一修正案并入第十四修正案，因为在没有证据证明材料在思想市场上完全没有任何救赎价值的情况下，州不得禁止销售暴力或色情材料。","D":"第一修正案被纳入第十四修正案，因为该法规过于模糊和过于宽泛。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案的言论自由条款（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）保护思想的自由流动，但有有限的例外。侵犯这项权利的法律可以在以下任一情况下受到表面质疑并被彻底废除：\n• 过度范围原则——如果法律限制大量受保护的言论或内容，则该法律违宪。\n\n• 模糊性原则——如果普通人因法律未能提供有关禁止言论的通知而不得不猜测法律的含义，则该法律将违宪。\n\n在这里，州法规通过禁止零售商公开展示或销售任何包含可能对未成年人有害的暴力或色情图片的材料来规范言论。尽管一些暴力或露骨的言论不受第一修正案的保护，但很大一部分是受到保护的。因此，过度宽泛原则是对该法规执行的一种辩护。由于该法规还要求普通人猜测哪些材料可能对未成年人有害，因此模糊性原则是另一种有效的辩护。 （选项 A 和 B） 第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下以不同方式对待处境相似的人（或类别的人）。但在这里，没有证据表明该法律是以歧视性方式适用的——例如，街角商店的老板，但没有其他零售商，根据该法规受到起诉。因此，平等保护防御很可能会失败。 （选项 C）如果 (1) 不属于第一修正案的保护范围（例如，根据米勒测试煽动犯罪或淫秽的材料），或者 (2) 这种基于内容的限制通过了严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），则州可以禁止销售暴力或露骨色情材料。两者都不需要证明这些材料在思想市场上完全没有任何救赎价值。\n\n教育目标：如果一项干涉言论自由的法律限制大量受保护的言论（即过于宽泛）或未能向普通人告知禁止的言论（即过于含糊），那么该法律将被彻底推翻。\n\n参考文献\n• Watchtower Bible & Tract Soc'y of N.Y., Inc. 诉 Vill。 of Stratton, 536 U.S. 150, 165 (2002)（认为该法令过于宽泛，因为它禁止所有上门拉票，而不仅仅是商业拉票）。\n\n• Gentile v. State Bar of Nev., 501 U.S. 1030, 1057–58 (1991)（驳回法律的含糊之处，因为它往往会误导律师，让他们相信审前公开客户刑事辩护可能会受到惩罚）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一项干涉言论自由的法律，如果它限制大量受保护的言论（即过于宽泛）或未能向普通人告知禁止的言论（即过于含糊），那么它就会被彻底打倒。","iracZh":{"issue":"在州初审法院针对这一起诉进行辩护时，以下哪项是街角商店的最佳辩护？","rule":"一项干涉言论自由的法律，如果它限制大量受保护的言论（即过于宽泛）或未能向普通人告知禁止的言论（即过于含糊），那么它就会被彻底打倒。","analysis":"第一修正案的言论自由条款（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）保护思想的自由流动，但有有限的例外。侵犯这项权利的法律可以在以下任一情况下受到表面质疑并被彻底废除：\n• 过度范围原则——如果法律限制大量受保护的言论或内容，则该法律违宪。\n\n• 模糊性原则——如果普通人因法律未能提供有关禁止言论的通知而不得不猜测法律的含义，则该法律将违宪。\n\n在这里，州法规通过禁止零售商公开展示或销售任何包含可能对未成年人有害的暴力或色情图片的材料来规范言论。尽管一些暴力或露骨的言论不受第一修正案的保护，但很大一部分是受到保护的。因此，过度宽泛原则是对该法规执行的一种辩护。由于该法规还要求普通人猜测哪些材料可能对未成年人有害，因此模糊性原则是另一种有效的辩护。 （选项 A 和 B） 第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下以不同方式对待处境相似的人（或类别的人）。但在这里，没有证据表明该法律是以歧视性方式适用的——例如，街角商店的老板，但没有其他零售商，根据该法规受到起诉。因此，平等保护防御很可能会失败。 （选项 C）如果 (1) 不属于第一修正案的保护范围（例如，根据米勒测试煽动犯罪或淫秽的材料），或者 (2) 这种基于内容的限制通过了严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），则州可以禁止销售暴力或露骨色情材料。两者都不需要证明这些材料在思想市场上完全没有任何救赎价值。\n\n教育目标：如果一项干涉言论自由的法律限制大量受保护的言论（即过于宽泛）或未能向普通人告知禁止的言论（即过于含糊），那么该法律将被彻底推翻。\n\n参考文献\n• Watchtower Bible & Tract Soc'y of N.Y., Inc. 诉 Vill。 of Stratton, 536 U.S. 150, 165 (2002)（认为该法令过于宽泛，因为它禁止所有上门拉票，而不仅仅是商业拉票）。\n\n• Gentile v. State Bar of Nev., 501 U.S. 1030, 1057–58 (1991)（驳回法律的含糊之处，因为它往往会误导律师，让他们相信审前公开客户刑事辩护可能会受到惩罚）。","conclusion":"答案 D：第一修正案被纳入第十四修正案，因为该法规过于模糊和过于宽泛。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"vagueness doctrine","ipa":"/vˈeɪɡnɪs dˈɑktɹən/","syllables":"vague·ness doc·trine","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"模糊性原则：法律必须给予普通人公平通知并限制任意执法","contextualMeaning":"模糊性原则：法律必须给予普通人公平通知并限制任意执法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A law that infringes upon this right can be challenged on its face and struck down in its entirety under either: • the overbreadth doctrine – renders laws unconstitutional if they restrict a substantial amount of protected speech or • the vagueness doctrine – renders laws unconstitutional if ordinary persons would have to guess the law's meaning because the law fails to provide notice of what speech is prohibited.","synonyms":["vagueness philosophy","vagueness philosophical system","vagueness school of thought"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f8b06ca86942d2af"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":187,"sourceQuestionPage":558,"sourceQuestionPages":[558],"sourceExplanationPages":[559,560],"sourceAnswerPages":[559,560],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-188","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Takings Clause","question":"A purchaser bought land in the mountain foothills just outside a resort town and planned to build a housing development there. Soon thereafter, the county in which the land was located unexpectedly adopted a regulation that, for the first time, prohibited all construction in several foothill and mountain areas, including the area of the purchaser's property. The purpose of the county's regulation was \"to conserve for future generations the unique natural wildlife and plant habitats\" in the mountain areas. Since the adoption of the regulation, the purchaser has been unable to lease or sell the property at any price. Several realtors have advised the purchaser that his property is now worthless. The purchaser has sued the county, claiming that the regulation has effected a taking of the purchaser's property and that the county therefore owes the purchaser just compensation. Is the court likely to rule in favor of the purchaser?","choices":{"A":"No, because the county did not take title to the property from the purchaser.","B":"No, because the regulation has not caused or authorized any uninvited physical invasion or intrusion onto the property.","C":"Yes, because the conservation objective of the county ordinance is not sufficiently compelling to justify the substantial diminution in the property value.","D":"Yes, because the effect of the county's regulation is to deny the purchaser's investment- backed expectation and essentially all economically beneficial use of the property."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nA taking occurs when the government:\n• physically possesses or invades private property (ie, physical taking) or\n\n• deprives the owner of all economically beneficial use of the property but the owner retains possession (ie, regulatory taking) (Choices A & B).\n\nAnd under the Fifth Amendment—as applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment—a taking must be for a public use, and the government must justly compensate the owner for the property's fair market value. Here, the county's regulation prohibits all construction on the purchaser's land. This amounted to a regulatory taking because it denied the purchaser (1) his investment-backed expectation for the planned housing development and (2) essentially all economically beneficial use of the property. And since the property was rendered worthless, the court will likely rule that the purchaser is entitled to just compensation. (Choice C) The ordinance's objective to conserve natural wildlife and plant habitats may be compelling. But the county can only justify the substantial diminution in the purchaser's property value by providing him just compensation.\n\nEducational objective: A Fifth Amendment taking occurs when (1) the government physically possesses or invades private property or (2) the owner retains possession but loses all economically beneficial use of the property due to government action.\n\nReferences\n• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992) (holding that a deprivation of all economically beneficial uses of property constitutes a taking).\n\n• 26 Am Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 12 (2019) (explaining a deprivation of all economically beneficial uses of property).","educationalObjective":"A Fifth Amendment taking occurs when (1) the government physically possesses or invades private property or (2) the owner retains possession but loses all economically beneficial use of the property due to government action.","references":"• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992) (holding that a deprivation of all economically beneficial uses of property constitutes a taking).\n\n• 26 Am Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 12 (2019) (explaining a deprivation of all economically beneficial uses of property).","rule":"A Fifth Amendment taking occurs when (1) the government physically possesses or invades private property or (2) the owner retains possession but loses all economically beneficial use of the property due to government action.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to rule in favor of the purchaser?","rule":"A Fifth Amendment taking occurs when (1) the government physically possesses or invades private property or (2) the owner retains possession but loses all economically beneficial use of the property due to government action.","analysis":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nA taking occurs when the government:\n• physically possesses or invades private property (ie, physical taking) or\n\n• deprives the owner of all economically beneficial use of the property but the owner retains possession (ie, regulatory taking) (Choices A & B).\n\nAnd under the Fifth Amendment—as applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment—a taking must be for a public use, and the government must justly compensate the owner for the property's fair market value. Here, the county's regulation prohibits all construction on the purchaser's land. This amounted to a regulatory taking because it denied the purchaser (1) his investment-backed expectation for the planned housing development and (2) essentially all economically beneficial use of the property. And since the property was rendered worthless, the court will likely rule that the purchaser is entitled to just compensation. (Choice C) The ordinance's objective to conserve natural wildlife and plant habitats may be compelling. But the county can only justify the substantial diminution in the purchaser's property value by providing him just compensation.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the effect of the county's regulation is to deny the purchaser's investment- backed expectation and essentially all economically beneficial use of the property."},"questionZh":"一位买家在一个度假小镇外的山麓购买了一块土地，并计划在那里建造一个住宅开发项目。此后不久，该地块所在县出人意料地出台了一项规定，首次禁止在包括购买者房产区域在内的多个山麓山区进行任何建设活动。该县制定法规的目的是“为子孙后代保护山区独特的自然野生动物和植物栖息地”。自该规定通过以来，买方一直无法以任何价格出租或出售该房产。几位房地产经纪人告诉买家，他的房产现在一文不值。购买者已起诉该县，声称该规定已对购买者的财产进行了没收，因此该县应对购买者进行公正的赔偿。法院可能做出有利于买方的裁决吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为县政府没有从购买者手中取得该房产的所有权。","B":"不会，因为该法规并未导致或授权任何不请自来的物理入侵或侵入财产。","C":"是的，因为县法令的保护目标不足以证明财产价值的大幅下降是合理的。","D":"是的，因为该县的法规的作用是否认购买者的投资支持期望以及基本上所有对财产的经济利益使用。"},"explanationZh":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当政府出现以下情况时，就会发生征用：\n• 实际拥有或侵犯私有财产（即实际夺取）或\n\n• 剥夺所有者对财产的所有经济利益使用，但所有者保留占有（即监管占用）（选择A 和B）。\n\n根据第五修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州），征用必须用于公共用途，政府必须按财产的公平市场价值公正地补偿所有者。在这里，该县的规定禁止在购买者的土地上进行任何建设。这相当于监管征收，因为它否定了购买者（1）对计划中的住房开发的投资支持期望，以及（2）基本上对财产的所有经济利益使用。由于该财产已变得毫无价值，法院可能会裁定购买者有权获得公正的赔偿。 （选项 C）该法令保护自然野生动物和植物栖息地的目标可能令人信服。但县政府只能通过向购买者提供公正的补偿来证明购买者财产价值的大幅减少是合理的。\n\n教育目标：当（1）政府实际拥有或侵犯私人财产或（2）所有者保留占有但由于政府行为而失去对该财产的所有经济利益使用时，第五修正案就会生效。\n\n参考文献\n• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992)（认为剥夺财产的所有经济利益用途构成征用）。\n\n• 26 日上午2d 征用权 § 12 (2019)（解释了对财产的所有经济利益用途的剥夺）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当（1）政府实际拥有或侵犯私人财产或（2）所有者保留占有但由于政府行为而失去对该财产的所有经济利益使用时，第五修正案就会生效。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能做出有利于买方的裁决吗？","rule":"当（1）政府实际拥有或侵犯私人财产或（2）所有者保留占有但由于政府行为而失去对该财产的所有经济利益使用时，第五修正案就会生效。","analysis":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当政府出现以下情况时，就会发生征用：\n• 实际拥有或侵犯私有财产（即实际夺取）或\n\n• 剥夺所有者对财产的所有经济利益使用，但所有者保留占有（即监管占用）（选择A 和B）。\n\n根据第五修正案（通过第十四修正案适用于各州），征用必须用于公共用途，政府必须按财产的公平市场价值公正地补偿所有者。在这里，该县的规定禁止在购买者的土地上进行任何建设。这相当于监管征收，因为它否定了购买者（1）对计划中的住房开发的投资支持期望，以及（2）基本上对财产的所有经济利益使用。由于该财产已变得毫无价值，法院可能会裁定购买者有权获得公正的赔偿。 （选项 C）该法令保护自然野生动物和植物栖息地的目标可能令人信服。但县政府只能通过向购买者提供公正的补偿来证明购买者财产价值的大幅减少是合理的。\n\n教育目标：当（1）政府实际拥有或侵犯私人财产或（2）所有者保留占有但由于政府行为而失去对该财产的所有经济利益使用时，第五修正案就会生效。\n\n参考文献\n• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992)（认为剥夺财产的所有经济利益用途构成征用）。\n\n• 26 日上午2d 征用权 § 12 (2019)（解释了对财产的所有经济利益用途的剥夺）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该县的法规的作用是否认购买者的投资支持期望以及基本上所有对财产的经济利益使用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"physical taking","ipa":"/fˈɪzɪkəl tˈeɪkɪŋ/","syllables":"phys·i·cal tak·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"物理征收：政府实际占有或永久侵入私人财产","contextualMeaning":"物理征收：政府实际占有或永久侵入私人财产","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Fifth Amendment takings Type Attributes Physical • Possession or permanent physical invasion of property Regulatory • Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses • Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction • Condition to obtain building/development permit Destruction • Destruction of property or property int","synonyms":["physical pickings","physical fetching","physical winning"]},{"term":"easements","ipa":"/ˈizmənt/","syllables":"ease·ments","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 地役权（easement的复数）；便利","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 地役权（easement的复数）；便利","wordForm":"名词；原形 easement；词形 0:easement/1:s","sourceSentence":"Fifth Amendment takings Type Attributes Physical • Possession or permanent physical invasion of property Regulatory • Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses • Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction • Condition to obtain building/development permit Destruction • Destruction of property or property int","synonyms":["easing","alleviation","relief"]},{"term":"liens","ipa":"/lˈinz/","syllables":"liens","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. <法>留置权, 扣押权( lien的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. <法>留置权, 扣押权( lien的复数形式 )","wordForm":"名词；原形 lien；词形 0:lien/1:s","sourceSentence":"Fifth Amendment takings Type Attributes Physical • Possession or permanent physical invasion of property Regulatory • Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses • Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction • Condition to obtain building/development permit Destruction • Destruction of property or property int","synonyms":["spleen"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":188,"sourceQuestionPage":561,"sourceQuestionPages":[561],"sourceExplanationPages":[562,563],"sourceAnswerPages":[562,563],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-189","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Establishment Clause","question":"A state law required vacant public school buildings to be sold at auction to the highest bidder. A church in a city located in the state wanted to acquire larger facilities for its school. The city's school board proposed a new state law that would authorize it to sell a vacant public school building, at a price below its fair market value, to the church for use as its school. If enacted, would this law be constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because a statute specially authorizing the sale of the building without competitive bidding would violate the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"No, because the transfer of the building to the church under these circumstances would constitute an establishment of religion prohibited by the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","C":"Yes, because surplus government property is not subject to the limitations imposed by the establishment clause as incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"Yes, because the law would not primarily or substantially support the religious activities of the church."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022) abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. The current inquiry looks to historical practices and understandings of the Establishment Clause. Although government may include religious organizations in neutral, generally available benefit programs, this proposed law is different: it specially authorizes a below-market transfer of valuable public property to one church, outside the State's ordinary highest-bidder rule. That preferential subsidy resembles the governmental financial support of a favored church that the Establishment Clause historically forbids. The transfer would therefore be unconstitutional, and Choice B remains best.\n\n(Choice A) The special departure from competitive bidding is relevant evidence of preferential support, but the Equal Protection Clause is not the most direct ground identified by these facts.\n\n(Choice C) Surplus public property remains subject to the Religion Clauses.\n\n(Choice D) The former Lemon inquiry into a law's primary effect is no longer the governing test; in any event, a church-specific below-market conveyance supplies direct material support.\n\nEducational objective: Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause challenges are assessed by historical practices and understandings. Government financial or property support that specially favors a church, rather than operating through a neutral generally available program, presents a core establishment concern.\n\nReferences\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507, 535–36 (2022).\n• Constitution Annotated, Overview of Financial Assistance to Religion.","educationalObjective":"Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause challenges are assessed by historical practices and understandings. Government financial or property support that specially favors a church, rather than operating through a neutral generally available program, presents a core establishment concern.","references":"• Larson v. Valente, 456 U.S. 228, 246 (1982) (explaining that strict scrutiny applies to laws that aid or prefer one religion).\n\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School Dist., 142 S. Ct. 2407, 2428 (2022) (holding that challenges based on the establishment clause \"must be interpreted by reference to historical practices and understandings\").","rule":"Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause challenges are assessed by historical practices and understandings. Government financial or property support that specially favors a church, rather than operating through a neutral generally available program, presents a core establishment concern.","irac":{"issue":"If enacted, would this law be constitutional?","rule":"Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause challenges are assessed by historical practices and understandings. Government financial or property support that specially favors a church, rather than operating through a neutral generally available program, presents a core establishment concern.","analysis":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022) abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. The current inquiry looks to historical practices and understandings of the Establishment Clause. Although government may include religious organizations in neutral, generally available benefit programs, this proposed law is different: it specially authorizes a below-market transfer of valuable public property to one church, outside the State's ordinary highest-bidder rule. That preferential subsidy resembles the governmental financial support of a favored church that the Establishment Clause historically forbids. The transfer would therefore be unconstitutional, and Choice B remains best.\n\n(Choice A) The special departure from competitive bidding is relevant evidence of preferential support, but the Equal Protection Clause is not the most direct ground identified by these facts.\n\n(Choice C) Surplus public property remains subject to the Religion Clauses.\n\n(Choice D) The former Lemon inquiry into a law's primary effect is no longer the governing test; in any event, a church-specific below-market conveyance supplies direct material support.\n\nEducational objective: Kennedy v. Bremerton abandoned the Lemon and endorsement tests. Establishment Clause challenges are assessed by historical practices and understandings. Government financial or property support that specially favors a church, rather than operating through a neutral generally available program, presents a core establishment concern.\n\nReferences\n• Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507, 535–36 (2022).\n• Constitution Annotated, Overview of Financial Assistance to Religion.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the transfer of the building to the church under these circumstances would constitute an establishment of religion prohibited by the First and Fourteenth Amendments."},"questionZh":"州法律要求空置的公立学校建筑必须通过拍卖给出价最高者。该州一座城市的一座教堂希望为其学校购买更大的设施。该市学校董事会提出了一项新的州法律，授权其以低于公平市场价值的价格将一栋空置的公立学校建筑出售给教堂用作学校。如果颁布，这项法律是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为专门授权在不进行竞标的情况下出售建筑物的法规将违反第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。","B":"不可以，因为在这种情况下将建筑物移交给教堂将构成第一修正案和第十四修正案所禁止的宗教信仰。","C":"是的，因为剩余政府财产不受第十四修正案中的设立条款的限制。","D":"是的，因为法律不会主要或实质上支持教会的宗教活动。"},"explanationZh":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District（2022）已放弃 Lemon 测试与“政府背书”测试。现行判断应参照政教分离条款的历史实践与理解。政府可以让宗教组织平等参加中立且普遍开放的福利项目，但本题并非如此：拟议法律绕过州通常的“价高者得”规则，专门授权以低于市场价的价格向某一教会转让有价值的公共财产。这种特别补贴类似政教分离条款在历史上所禁止的政府对受偏爱教会的物质支持。因此，该转让违宪，选项 B 仍为最佳答案。\n\n（选项 A）偏离公开竞价可证明政府给予特别支持，但依本题事实，平等保护并非最直接的宪法依据。\n\n（选项 C）剩余公共财产仍受宗教条款限制。\n\n（选项 D）原 Lemon 测试的“主要效果”分析已不是现行标准；而且，教会专属的低价转让本身就是直接物质支持。\n\n教育目标：Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题应参照历史实践与理解判断。政府若并非通过中立、普遍开放的项目，而是以资金或财产特别优待某一教会，会触及国教制度的核心疑虑。\n\n参考：Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507（2022）；Constitution Annotated，Overview of Financial Assistance to Religion。","educationalObjectiveZh":"Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题应参照历史实践与理解判断。政府若并非通过中立、普遍开放的项目，而是以资金或财产特别优待某一教会，会触及国教制度的核心疑虑。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果颁布，这项法律是否符合宪法？","rule":"Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题应参照历史实践与理解判断。政府若并非通过中立、普遍开放的项目，而是以资金或财产特别优待某一教会，会触及国教制度的核心疑虑。","analysis":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District（2022）已放弃 Lemon 测试与“政府背书”测试。现行判断应参照政教分离条款的历史实践与理解。政府可以让宗教组织平等参加中立且普遍开放的福利项目，但本题并非如此：拟议法律绕过州通常的“价高者得”规则，专门授权以低于市场价的价格向某一教会转让有价值的公共财产。这种特别补贴类似政教分离条款在历史上所禁止的政府对受偏爱教会的物质支持。因此，该转让违宪，选项 B 仍为最佳答案。\n\n（选项 A）偏离公开竞价可证明政府给予特别支持，但依本题事实，平等保护并非最直接的宪法依据。\n\n（选项 C）剩余公共财产仍受宗教条款限制。\n\n（选项 D）原 Lemon 测试的“主要效果”分析已不是现行标准；而且，教会专属的低价转让本身就是直接物质支持。\n\n教育目标：Kennedy v. Bremerton 已放弃 Lemon 与“政府背书”测试。政教分离条款问题应参照历史实践与理解判断。政府若并非通过中立、普遍开放的项目，而是以资金或财产特别优待某一教会，会触及国教制度的核心疑虑。\n\n参考：Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507（2022）；Constitution Annotated，Overview of Financial Assistance to Religion。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为在这种情况下将建筑物移交给教堂将构成第一修正案和第十四修正案所禁止的宗教信仰。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"corrected-current-framework","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"原解析仍混用 Lemon/endorsement 表述；已改用 Kennedy 的历史实践框架及现行宗教财政援助原则，答案 B 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District, 597 U.S. 507 (2022)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/21-418_i425.pdf"},{"title":"Constitution Annotated — Overview of Financial Assistance to Religion","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/browse/essay/amdt1-3-4-1/ALDE_00013074/"}],"originalAnswer":"B","revisedAnswer":"B"},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":189,"sourceQuestionPage":564,"sourceQuestionPages":[564],"sourceExplanationPages":[565,566],"sourceAnswerPages":[565,566],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-190","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A state initiated a criminal prosecution against the owner of a video store for selling a video that consisted entirely of pictures of nude sunbathers on a beach in a foreign country where nude public sunbathing is common. The state charged that selling the video violated its anti-obscenity law. The store owner defended on the ground that the prosecution violated his constitutional right to freedom of speech. Should the store owner prevail in this defense?","choices":{"A":"No, because the store owner is engaged in the commercial sale of the video, which is not protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","B":"No, because the video consists entirely of portrayals of nudity, appeals to the prurient interest of viewers, and lacks serious social value as a whole.","C":"Yes, because mere portrayals of nudity are insufficient to justify a finding that the video is obscene as a matter of constitutional law.","D":"Yes, because the portrayals of nudity occurred outside the United States, and therefore the state lacks a compelling interest in applying its anti-obscenity law to the sale of the video."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Unprotected speech\n\n• Fighting words/threats\n• Inciting crime\n• Defamation\n• Obscenity Mnemonic: FIDO\n\nThe First Amendment free speech clause generally prohibits the government from regulating speech based on its content (ie, what is being said). Such content-based regulations are usually subject to strict scrutiny. But certain categories of speech—like obscenity—are not protected and can be freely regulated by the government. Under the Millertest, speech is considered obscene when it:\n• appeals to the prurient interest (ie, a shameful or morbid interest in sexual matters)\n• depicts sexual conduct in a patently offensive way and\n• lacks serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value. The first two elements of this test are evaluated under contemporary community standards, while the third element is evaluated under a national standard. But mere portrayals of nudity—as seen in the store owner's video—are never obscene (Choice B). As a result, this content-based restriction is invalid unless it survives strict scrutiny. This requires the state to prove that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. And since this is a nearly impossible task, the store owner should prevail. (Choice A) The government can freely regulate the production, sale, and distribution of obscene materials. But since the video is not obscene, commercial sale of the video is protected by the First Amendment (as applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment). (Choice D) The location of the video itself is relevant since it affects whether the state has jurisdiction to prosecute the store owner. But where the nudity in that video occurred is irrelevant and does not affect the state's interest in applying the anti-obscenity law.\n\nEducational objective: Obscenity is not protected by the free speech clause. And under the three-part Miller test for evaluating obscenity, mere portrayals of nudity are never obscene.\n\nReferences\n• Miller v. California, 413 U.S. 15, 24 (1973) (setting forth the three-prong obscenity test).\n\n• Erznoznik v. Jacksonville, 422 U.S. 205, 213–14 (1975) (holding that mere nudity is not obscene).","educationalObjective":"Obscenity is not protected by the free speech clause. And under the three-part Miller test for evaluating obscenity, mere portrayals of nudity are never obscene.","references":"• Miller v. California, 413 U.S. 15, 24 (1973) (setting forth the three-prong obscenity test).\n\n• Erznoznik v. Jacksonville, 422 U.S. 205, 213–14 (1975) (holding that mere nudity is not obscene).","rule":"Obscenity is not protected by the free speech clause. And under the three-part Miller test for evaluating obscenity, mere portrayals of nudity are never obscene.","irac":{"issue":"Should the store owner prevail in this defense?","rule":"Obscenity is not protected by the free speech clause. And under the three-part Miller test for evaluating obscenity, mere portrayals of nudity are never obscene.","analysis":"Unprotected speech\n\n• Fighting words/threats\n• Inciting crime\n• Defamation\n• Obscenity Mnemonic: FIDO\n\nThe First Amendment free speech clause generally prohibits the government from regulating speech based on its content (ie, what is being said). Such content-based regulations are usually subject to strict scrutiny. But certain categories of speech—like obscenity—are not protected and can be freely regulated by the government. Under the Millertest, speech is considered obscene when it:\n• appeals to the prurient interest (ie, a shameful or morbid interest in sexual matters)\n• depicts sexual conduct in a patently offensive way and\n• lacks serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value. The first two elements of this test are evaluated under contemporary community standards, while the third element is evaluated under a national standard. But mere portrayals of nudity—as seen in the store owner's video—are never obscene (Choice B). As a result, this content-based restriction is invalid unless it survives strict scrutiny. This requires the state to prove that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. And since this is a nearly impossible task, the store owner should prevail. (Choice A) The government can freely regulate the production, sale, and distribution of obscene materials. But since the video is not obscene, commercial sale of the video is protected by the First Amendment (as applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment). (Choice D) The location of the video itself is relevant since it affects whether the state has jurisdiction to prosecute the store owner. But where the nudity in that video occurred is irrelevant and does not affect the state's interest in applying the anti-obscenity law.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because mere portrayals of nudity are insufficient to justify a finding that the video is obscene as a matter of constitutional law."},"questionZh":"某州对一家音像店老板提起刑事诉讼，原因是其在外国公共场所裸体日光浴很常见的情况下，出售了完全由裸体日光浴者在海滩上的照片组成的视频。该州指控出售该视频违反了反淫秽法。该店老板辩称，检方侵犯了他的宪法言论自由权。店主是否应该在这场辩护中占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为店主从事视频的商业销售，不受第一修正案和第十四修正案的保护。","B":"不会，因为该视频完全由裸体描绘组成，迎合了观众的淫欲，整体上缺乏严肃的社会价值。","C":"是的，因为仅仅对裸体的描绘不足以证明该视频作为宪法问题是淫秽的。","D":"是的，因为裸体的描绘发生在美国境外，因此该州缺乏将其反淫秽法应用于视频销售的强烈兴趣。"},"explanationZh":"不受保护的言论\n\n• 对抗言语/威胁\n• 煽动犯罪\n• 诽谤\n• 淫秽助记符：FIDO\n\n第一修正案言论自由条款一般禁止政府根据言论内容（即所说的内容）对其进行监管。此类基于内容的法规通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但某些类别的言论（例如淫秽言论）不受保护，可以由政府自由监管。根据米勒测试，以下言论被认为是淫秽的：\n• 诉诸淫欲（即对性问题的可耻或病态的兴趣）\n• 以明显令人反感的方式描述性行为，并且\n• 缺乏严肃的文学、艺术、政治或科学价值。该测试的前两个要素根据当代社区标准进行评估，而第三个要素则根据国家标准进行评估。但单纯的裸体描绘——如店主的视频中所见——绝不是淫秽的（选项 B）。因此，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），否则这种基于内容的限制是无效的。这要求国家证明该限制对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。由于这几乎是不可能的任务，所以店主应该占上风。 （选项A）政府可以自由监管淫秽物品的生产、销售和传播。但由于该视频不是淫秽视频，因此该视频的商业销售受到第一修正案的保护（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）。 （选项D）视频本身的位置是相关的，因为它影响到国家是否有管辖权起诉店主。但该视频中的裸露​​内容发生在哪里无关紧要，也不影响国家适用反淫秽法的利益。\n\n教育目标：淫秽内容不受言论自由条款的保护。根据评估淫秽的三部分米勒测试，单纯的裸体描绘绝不是淫秽的。\n\n参考文献\n• Miller v. California, 413 U.S. 15, 24 (1973)（提出了三管齐下的淫秽测试）。\n\n• Erznoznik v. Jacksonville, 422 U.S. 205, 213–14 (1975)（认为单纯的裸体并不构成淫秽）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"淫秽内容不受言论自由条款的保护。根据评估淫秽的三部分米勒测试，单纯的裸体描绘绝不是淫秽的。","iracZh":{"issue":"店主是否应该在这场辩护中占上风？","rule":"淫秽内容不受言论自由条款的保护。根据评估淫秽的三部分米勒测试，单纯的裸体描绘绝不是淫秽的。","analysis":"不受保护的言论\n\n• 对抗言语/威胁\n• 煽动犯罪\n• 诽谤\n• 淫秽助记符：FIDO\n\n第一修正案言论自由条款一般禁止政府根据言论内容（即所说的内容）对其进行监管。此类基于内容的法规通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但某些类别的言论（例如淫秽言论）不受保护，可以由政府自由监管。根据米勒测试，以下言论被认为是淫秽的：\n• 诉诸淫欲（即对性问题的可耻或病态的兴趣）\n• 以明显令人反感的方式描述性行为，并且\n• 缺乏严肃的文学、艺术、政治或科学价值。该测试的前两个要素根据当代社区标准进行评估，而第三个要素则根据国家标准进行评估。但单纯的裸体描绘——如店主的视频中所见——绝不是淫秽的（选项 B）。因此，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），否则这种基于内容的限制是无效的。这要求国家证明该限制对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。由于这几乎是不可能的任务，所以店主应该占上风。 （选项A）政府可以自由监管淫秽物品的生产、销售和传播。但由于该视频不是淫秽视频，因此该视频的商业销售受到第一修正案的保护（通过第十四修正案适用于各州）。 （选项D）视频本身的位置是相关的，因为它影响到国家是否有管辖权起诉店主。但该视频中的裸露​​内容发生在哪里无关紧要，也不影响国家适用反淫秽法的利益。\n\n教育目标：淫秽内容不受言论自由条款的保护。根据评估淫秽的三部分米勒测试，单纯的裸体描绘绝不是淫秽的。\n\n参考文献\n• Miller v. California, 413 U.S. 15, 24 (1973)（提出了三管齐下的淫秽测试）。\n\n• Erznoznik v. Jacksonville, 422 U.S. 205, 213–14 (1975)（认为单纯的裸体并不构成淫秽）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为仅仅对裸体的描绘不足以证明该视频作为宪法问题是淫秽的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":190,"sourceQuestionPage":567,"sourceQuestionPages":[567],"sourceExplanationPages":[568,569],"sourceAnswerPages":[568,569],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-191","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A public university requires students to pay a student-activity fee that funds the activities of various student organizations. The university allocates these funds to student organizations without regard to their views. A student organization received student-activity fee funds to hold a float parade. The organization rejected a student's float submission because the views espoused by the float were contrary to the organization's and parade's message. The student appealed the organization's decision to the university, but the university upheld the rejection of his submission. The student has filed an action against the university, arguing that the university's decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the student's submission and the university's mandatory student-activity fee violate the First Amendment. Are the university's actions constitutional?","choices":{"A":"The decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the student's submission and the mandatory student-activity fee are constitutional.","B":"The decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the student's submission and the mandatory student-activity fee are unconstitutional.","C":"The decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the student's submission is constitutional but the mandatory student-activity fee is unconstitutional.","D":"The decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the student's submission is unconstitutional but the mandatory student-activity fee is constitutional."},"answer":"A","explanation":"First Amendment freedom not to speak\n\nType Examples\n\nImpermissible\n• Forcing students to salute flag or recite Pledge of Allegiance compulsion\n• Punishing motorists for covering state motto on license plates\n• Requiring private groups to include opposing viewpoints in their events\n• Requiring nonunion employees to pay public-sector union dues Permissible\n• Forcing public university students to pay activity fee if funds compulsion allocated in viewpoint-neutral manner\n• Requiring public shopping center to permit speech on property if speech not dictated by state & not identified with center's owner\n• Requiring lawyers to join bar association & pay dues to practice law The First Amendment protects individuals' freedom to speak and their freedom not to speak. This means that the government and its subsidiaries (eg, public universities) cannot compel individuals to endorse or include viewpoints with which they disagree. Here, the student organization had the right to reject the student's float submission, and the university could not force the organization to accept that submission. Therefore, the university's decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the submission is constitutional (Choices B & D). Additionally, the First Amendment generally prohibits the government and its subsidiaries from compelling individuals to financially support private speech with which they disagree.* However, there are limited exceptions to this rule, including that a public university can force its students to pay a student-activity fee if the funds are allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner. Since the public university here allocates funds to student organizations without regard to their views, the mandatory student-activity fee is constitutional (Choices B & C). *Individuals can be compelled to subsidize government speech through taxes because the First Amendment does not prohibit the government from using taxpayer funds to advocate for or defend its own policies or viewpoints.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment protects the right to speak and not to speak. This means that the government cannot compel individuals to endorse, include, or financially support private speech with which they disagree. However, a public university can compel its students to pay an activity fee if the funds are allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner.\n\nReferences\n• Hurley v. Irish-Am. Gay, Lesbian & Bisexual Grp. of Bos., 515 U.S. 557, 581 (1995) (holding that a state may not require private parade organizers to include messages they oppose in the parade).\n\n• Bd. of Regents of Univ. of Wisconsin Sys. v. Southworth, 529 U.S. 217, 234 (2000) (holding that a public university's mandatory student-activity fee is constitutional if the fee is allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law §§ 471–73 (2020) (explaining when the First Amendment prohibits government-compelled speech).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment protects the right to speak and not to speak. This means that the government cannot compel individuals to endorse, include, or financially support private speech with which they disagree. However, a public university can compel its students to pay an activity fee if the funds are allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner.","references":"• Hurley v. Irish-Am. Gay, Lesbian & Bisexual Grp. of Bos., 515 U.S. 557, 581 (1995) (holding that a state may not require private parade organizers to include messages they oppose in the parade).\n\n• Bd. of Regents of Univ. of Wisconsin Sys. v. Southworth, 529 U.S. 217, 234 (2000) (holding that a public university's mandatory student-activity fee is constitutional if the fee is allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner).\n\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law §§ 471–73 (2020) (explaining when the First Amendment prohibits government-compelled speech).","rule":"The First Amendment protects the right to speak and not to speak. This means that the government cannot compel individuals to endorse, include, or financially support private speech with which they disagree. However, a public university can compel its students to pay an activity fee if the funds are allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner.","irac":{"issue":"Are the university's actions constitutional?","rule":"The First Amendment protects the right to speak and not to speak. This means that the government cannot compel individuals to endorse, include, or financially support private speech with which they disagree. However, a public university can compel its students to pay an activity fee if the funds are allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner.","analysis":"First Amendment freedom not to speak\n\nType Examples\n\nImpermissible\n• Forcing students to salute flag or recite Pledge of Allegiance compulsion\n• Punishing motorists for covering state motto on license plates\n• Requiring private groups to include opposing viewpoints in their events\n• Requiring nonunion employees to pay public-sector union dues Permissible\n• Forcing public university students to pay activity fee if funds compulsion allocated in viewpoint-neutral manner\n• Requiring public shopping center to permit speech on property if speech not dictated by state & not identified with center's owner\n• Requiring lawyers to join bar association & pay dues to practice law The First Amendment protects individuals' freedom to speak and their freedom not to speak. This means that the government and its subsidiaries (eg, public universities) cannot compel individuals to endorse or include viewpoints with which they disagree. Here, the student organization had the right to reject the student's float submission, and the university could not force the organization to accept that submission. Therefore, the university's decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the submission is constitutional (Choices B & D). Additionally, the First Amendment generally prohibits the government and its subsidiaries from compelling individuals to financially support private speech with which they disagree.* However, there are limited exceptions to this rule, including that a public university can force its students to pay a student-activity fee if the funds are allocated in a viewpoint-neutral manner. Since the public university here allocates funds to student organizations without regard to their views, the mandatory student-activity fee is constitutional (Choices B & C). *Individuals can be compelled to subsidize government speech through taxes because the First Amendment does not prohibit the government from using taxpayer funds to advocate for or defend its own policies or viewpoints.","conclusion":"Answer A: The decision to uphold the organization's rejection of the student's submission and the mandatory student-activity fee are constitutional."},"questionZh":"公立大学要求学生缴纳学生活动费，用于资助各种学生组织的活动。大学将这些资金分配给学生组织，而不考虑他们的意见。一个学生组织收到了学生活动费资金来举办花车游行。该组织拒绝了学生提交的花车，因为花车所拥护的观点与该组织和游行的信息相反。该学生对该组织的决定向大学提出上诉，但大学维持了拒绝他提交的申请的决定。该学生已对大学提起诉讼，认为大学维持该组织拒绝学生提交材料的决定以及大学的强制性学生活动费违反了第一修正案。大学的行为是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持该组织拒绝学生提交材料和强制性学生活动费的决定是符合宪法的。","B":"维持该组织拒绝学生提交材料和强制性学生活动费的决定是违宪的。","C":"支持该组织拒绝学生提交材料的决定是符合宪法的，但强制性的学生活动费是违宪的。","D":"支持该组织拒绝学生提交材料的决定是违宪的，但强制性的学生活动费是符合宪法的。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案不言论自由\n\n类型示例\n\n不允许的\n• 强迫学生向国旗敬礼或背诵效忠誓词\n• 惩罚在车牌上覆盖国家座右铭的驾车者\n• 要求私人团体在其活动中包含相反的观点\n• 要求非工会雇员缴纳公共部门工会会费 允许\n• 如果资金以观点中立的方式强制分配，则强迫公立大学学生支付活动费\n• 要求公共购物中心允许在财产上发表言论，如果言论不是由州规定且未由中心所有者确定的\n• 要求律师加入律师协会并缴纳律师执业费 第一修正案保护个人言论自由和不言论自由。这意味着政府及其附属机构（例如公立大学）不能强迫个人认可或包含他们不同意的观点。在这里，学生组织有权拒绝学生的浮动提交，大学不能强迫该组织接受该提交。因此，大学决定维持该组织拒绝提交的决定是符合宪法的（选择B＆D）。此外，第一修正案一般禁止政府及其附属机构强迫个人在经济上支持他们不同意的私人言论。*然而，这一规则也有有限的例外，包括如果资金以观点中立的方式分配，公立大学可以强迫其学生支付学生活动费。由于这里的公立大学在向学生组织分配资金时不考虑他们的意见，因此强制性的学生活动费是符合宪法的（选项B和C）。 *个人可能被迫通过税收补贴政府言论，因为第一修正案并不禁止政府使用纳税人的资金来倡导或捍卫自己的政策或观点。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护言论权和不言论权。这意味着政府不能强迫个人认可、纳入或在经济上支持他们不同意的私人言论。然而，如果资金以观点中立的方式分配，公立大学可以强迫学生支付活动费。\n\n参考文献\n• 赫尔利诉爱尔兰裔美国人案。男同性恋、女同性恋和双性恋组。 of Bos., 515 U.S. 557, 581 (1995)（认为国家不得要求私人游行组织者在游行中包含他们反对的信息）。\n\n• Bd。大学董事会。威斯康星州系统。 v. Southworth, 529 U.S. 217, 234 (2000)（认为公立大学的强制性学生活动费如果以观点中立的方式分配，则符合宪法）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 §§ 471–73 (2020)（解释第一修正案何时禁止政府强迫言论）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案保护言论权和不言论权。这意味着政府不能强迫个人认可、纳入或在经济上支持他们不同意的私人言论。然而，如果资金以观点中立的方式分配，公立大学可以强迫学生支付活动费。","iracZh":{"issue":"大学的行为是否符合宪法？","rule":"第一修正案保护言论权和不言论权。这意味着政府不能强迫个人认可、纳入或在经济上支持他们不同意的私人言论。然而，如果资金以观点中立的方式分配，公立大学可以强迫学生支付活动费。","analysis":"第一修正案不言论自由\n\n类型示例\n\n不允许的\n• 强迫学生向国旗敬礼或背诵效忠誓词\n• 惩罚在车牌上覆盖国家座右铭的驾车者\n• 要求私人团体在其活动中包含相反的观点\n• 要求非工会雇员缴纳公共部门工会会费 允许\n• 如果资金以观点中立的方式强制分配，则强迫公立大学学生支付活动费\n• 要求公共购物中心允许在财产上发表言论，如果言论不是由州规定且未由中心所有者确定的\n• 要求律师加入律师协会并缴纳律师执业费 第一修正案保护个人言论自由和不言论自由。这意味着政府及其附属机构（例如公立大学）不能强迫个人认可或包含他们不同意的观点。在这里，学生组织有权拒绝学生的浮动提交，大学不能强迫该组织接受该提交。因此，大学决定维持该组织拒绝提交的决定是符合宪法的（选择B＆D）。此外，第一修正案一般禁止政府及其附属机构强迫个人在经济上支持他们不同意的私人言论。*然而，这一规则也有有限的例外，包括如果资金以观点中立的方式分配，公立大学可以强迫其学生支付学生活动费。由于这里的公立大学在向学生组织分配资金时不考虑他们的意见，因此强制性的学生活动费是符合宪法的（选项B和C）。 *个人可能被迫通过税收补贴政府言论，因为第一修正案并不禁止政府使用纳税人的资金来倡导或捍卫自己的政策或观点。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护言论权和不言论权。这意味着政府不能强迫个人认可、纳入或在经济上支持他们不同意的私人言论。然而，如果资金以观点中立的方式分配，公立大学可以强迫学生支付活动费。\n\n参考文献\n• 赫尔利诉爱尔兰裔美国人案。男同性恋、女同性恋和双性恋组。 of Bos., 515 U.S. 557, 581 (1995)（认为国家不得要求私人游行组织者在游行中包含他们反对的信息）。\n\n• Bd。大学董事会。威斯康星州系统。 v. Southworth, 529 U.S. 217, 234 (2000)（认为公立大学的强制性学生活动费如果以观点中立的方式分配，则符合宪法）。\n\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 §§ 471–73 (2020)（解释第一修正案何时禁止政府强迫言论）。","conclusion":"答案 A：维持该组织拒绝学生提交材料和强制性学生活动费的决定是符合宪法的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"submission","ipa":"/sʌbmˈɪʃən/","syllables":"sub·mis·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 屈服, 服从, 谦恭, 呈递, 提交, 意见；[经] 提交, 提出, 提交公断","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 屈服, 服从, 谦恭, 呈递, 提交, 意见；[经] 提交, 提出, 提交公断","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:submissions","sourceSentence":"The organization rejected a student's float submission because the views espoused by the float were contrary to the organization's and parade's message.","synonyms":["compliance","entry","meekness"]},{"term":"lawyers","ipa":"/lˈɔjɝz/","syllables":"lawyers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 律师（lawyer的复数）","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 律师（lawyer的复数）","wordForm":"词；原形 lawyer；词形 0:lawyer/1:s","sourceSentence":"First Amendment freedom not to speak Type Examples Impermissible • Forcing students to salute flag or recite Pledge of Allegiance compulsion • Punishing motorists for covering state motto on license plates • Requiring private groups to include opposing viewpoints in their events • Requiring nonunion employees to pay public-sector union dues Permissible • Forcing public university students to pay activity fee if funds","synonyms":["attorney"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":191,"sourceQuestionPage":570,"sourceQuestionPages":[570],"sourceExplanationPages":[571,572],"sourceAnswerPages":[571,572],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-192","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection — Race-Based Remedial Measures","question":"An independent municipal water-supply district was incorporated under the applicable laws of a state. The district was created solely to supply water to an entirely new community in a recently developed area of the state. That new community is racially, ethnically, and socioeconomically diverse, and the community has never engaged in any discrimination against members of minority groups. The five-member elected governing board of the newly created water-supply district contains two persons who are members of racial minority groups. At its first meeting, the governing board adopted a rule unqualifiedly setting aside 25% of all positions on the staff of the water-supply district and 25% of all contracts to be awarded by the district to members of racial minority groups. The purpose of the rule was \"to help redress the historical discrimination against these groups in this country and to help them achieve economic parity with other groups in our society.\" Assume that no federal statute applies. In a suit by appropriate parties challenging the constitutionality of these set-asides, what is the most appropriate ruling based on applicable United States Supreme Court precedent?","choices":{"A":"The set-asides are constitutional, because the function and activities of the water- supply district are of a proprietary nature rather than a governmental nature and, therefore, are not subject to the usual requirements of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"The set-asides are constitutional, because they would assure members of racial minority groups the equal protection of the laws.","C":"The set-asides are unconstitutional, because they would deny other potential employees or potential contractors the equal protection of the laws.","D":"The set-asides are unconstitutional, because they would impermissibly impair the right to contract of other potential employees or potential contractors."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Every governmental racial classification is subject to strict scrutiny. Under City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co., a public employer or contracting authority may use a narrowly tailored race-conscious remedy only when it has a strong evidentiary basis for addressing its own specific past discrimination; generalized societal discrimination is insufficient. Students for Fair Admissions (2023) also rejected the former Grutter diversity rationale for race-based university admissions, so that obsolete line from the source chart must not be used.\n\nThis new water district has no history of discrimination. Its fixed 25% employment and contracting set-asides seek only to redress discrimination throughout the country. That generalized objective is not a compelling interest, and the unqualified quotas are not narrowly tailored. The set-asides therefore violate equal protection (Choice C).\n\n(Choice A) A municipal district exercising public authority is a state actor even when its operations have proprietary features.\n\n(Choice B) Benign motive does not exempt racial classifications from strict scrutiny.\n\n(Choice D) The Contracts Clause concerns state impairment of existing contractual obligations; equal protection is the applicable ground here.\n\nEducational objective: All governmental racial classifications receive strict scrutiny. Remediating specific, identified past discrimination by the government itself can be compelling, but remedying generalized societal discrimination is not. Students for Fair Admissions rejected the former Grutter diversity rationale for race-based admissions; that obsolete rationale is not applicable to public employment or contracting set-asides.\n\nReferences\n• Students for Fair Admissions, Inc. v. President & Fellows of Harvard College, 600 U.S. 181, 206–25 (2023).\n• City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co., 488 U.S. 469 (1989).","educationalObjective":"All governmental racial classifications receive strict scrutiny. Remediating specific, identified past discrimination by the government itself can be compelling, but remedying generalized societal discrimination is not. Students for Fair Admissions rejected the former Grutter diversity rationale for race-based admissions; that obsolete rationale is not applicable to public employment or contracting set-asides.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co., 488 U.S. 469, 504 (1989) (holding that racially-based set-asides for government employment or contracts must satisfy strict scrutiny).","rule":"All governmental racial classifications receive strict scrutiny. Remediating specific, identified past discrimination by the government itself can be compelling, but remedying generalized societal discrimination is not. Students for Fair Admissions rejected the former Grutter diversity rationale for race-based admissions; that obsolete rationale is not applicable to public employment or contracting set-asides.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by appropriate parties challenging the constitutionality of these set-asides, what is the most appropriate ruling based on applicable United States Supreme Court precedent?","rule":"All governmental racial classifications receive strict scrutiny. Remediating specific, identified past discrimination by the government itself can be compelling, but remedying generalized societal discrimination is not. Students for Fair Admissions rejected the former Grutter diversity rationale for race-based admissions; that obsolete rationale is not applicable to public employment or contracting set-asides.","analysis":"Every governmental racial classification is subject to strict scrutiny. Under City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co., a public employer or contracting authority may use a narrowly tailored race-conscious remedy only when it has a strong evidentiary basis for addressing its own specific past discrimination; generalized societal discrimination is insufficient. Students for Fair Admissions (2023) also rejected the former Grutter diversity rationale for race-based university admissions, so that obsolete line from the source chart must not be used.\n\nThis new water district has no history of discrimination. Its fixed 25% employment and contracting set-asides seek only to redress discrimination throughout the country. That generalized objective is not a compelling interest, and the unqualified quotas are not narrowly tailored. The set-asides therefore violate equal protection (Choice C).\n\n(Choice A) A municipal district exercising public authority is a state actor even when its operations have proprietary features.\n\n(Choice B) Benign motive does not exempt racial classifications from strict scrutiny.\n\n(Choice D) The Contracts Clause concerns state impairment of existing contractual obligations; equal protection is the applicable ground here.\n\nEducational objective: All governmental racial classifications receive strict scrutiny. Remediating specific, identified past discrimination by the government itself can be compelling, but remedying generalized societal discrimination is not. Students for Fair Admissions rejected the former Grutter diversity rationale for race-based admissions; that obsolete rationale is not applicable to public employment or contracting set-asides.\n\nReferences\n• Students for Fair Admissions, Inc. v. President & Fellows of Harvard College, 600 U.S. 181, 206–25 (2023).\n• City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co., 488 U.S. 469 (1989).","conclusion":"Answer C: The set-asides are unconstitutional, because they would deny other potential employees or potential contractors the equal protection of the laws."},"questionZh":"独立的市政供水区根据州的适用法律成立。该地区的创建只是为了向该州最近开发的地区的一个全新社区供水。这个新社区在种族、民族和社会经济上是多元化的，并且该社区从未对少数群体成员进行任何歧视。新成立的供水区由五名选举产生的理事会成员组成，其中有两名少数族裔成员。在第一次会议上，理事会无条件地通过了一项规则，将供水区工作人员所有职位的 25% 以及该区授予的所有合同的 25% 预留给少数族裔成员。该规则的目的是“帮助纠正这个国家对这些群体的历史歧视，并帮助他们实现与我们社会其他群体的经济平等。”假设没有适用的联邦法规。在有关各方质疑这些保留的合宪性的诉讼中，根据适用的美国最高法院先例，最合适的裁决是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"这些预留是符合宪法的，因为供水区的功能和活动具有专有性质，而不是政府性质，因此不受第十四修正案的通常要求的约束。","B":"这些规定是符合宪法的，因为它们将确保少数族裔群体的成员受到法律的平等保护。","C":"这种预留是违宪的，因为它们会剥夺其他潜在雇员或潜在承包商受到法律的平等保护。","D":"这些预留是违宪的，因为它们会不允许地损害其他潜在雇员或潜在承包商的合同权。"},"explanationZh":"政府采用任何种族分类，都必须通过严格审查。依 City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co.，公共雇主或政府采购机关只有在有充分证据证明需要纠正其自身具体既往歧视时，才可能采用狭义量身定制的种族补救措施；一般性的社会歧视不足。Students for Fair Admissions（2023）还否定了 Grutter 曾认可的高校招生多元化理由，因此原资料图表中的该旧规则不得继续使用。\n\n本题新设供水区从未有歧视历史。固定保留 25% 职位与合同，只是为了纠正全国范围的一般历史歧视。该概括性目标不是重大政府利益，且无条件配额也并非狭义量身定制。因此，这些保留名额违反平等保护（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）市政供水区行使公共权力，即使活动带有经营性质，仍属于国家行为者。\n\n（选项 B）善意动机不会使种族分类免于严格审查。\n\n（选项 D）合同条款针对州损害既有合同义务；本题直接适用的是平等保护。\n\n教育目标：一切政府种族分类均适用严格审查。纠正该政府主体自身具体、已查明的既往歧视，可能构成重大利益；纠正一般社会歧视则不构成。Students for Fair Admissions 已否定 Grutter 曾认可的高校招生多元化理由，该旧理由也不能支持公共就业或政府合同配额。\n\n参考：Students for Fair Admissions, Inc. v. President & Fellows of Harvard College, 600 U.S. 181（2023）；City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co., 488 U.S. 469（1989）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一切政府种族分类均适用严格审查。纠正该政府主体自身具体、已查明的既往歧视，可能构成重大利益；纠正一般社会歧视则不构成。Students for Fair Admissions 已否定 Grutter 曾认可的高校招生多元化理由，该旧理由也不能支持公共就业或政府合同配额。","iracZh":{"issue":"在有关各方质疑这些保留的合宪性的诉讼中，根据适用的美国最高法院先例，最合适的裁决是什么？","rule":"一切政府种族分类均适用严格审查。纠正该政府主体自身具体、已查明的既往歧视，可能构成重大利益；纠正一般社会歧视则不构成。Students for Fair Admissions 已否定 Grutter 曾认可的高校招生多元化理由，该旧理由也不能支持公共就业或政府合同配额。","analysis":"政府采用任何种族分类，都必须通过严格审查。依 City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co.，公共雇主或政府采购机关只有在有充分证据证明需要纠正其自身具体既往歧视时，才可能采用狭义量身定制的种族补救措施；一般性的社会歧视不足。Students for Fair Admissions（2023）还否定了 Grutter 曾认可的高校招生多元化理由，因此原资料图表中的该旧规则不得继续使用。\n\n本题新设供水区从未有歧视历史。固定保留 25% 职位与合同，只是为了纠正全国范围的一般历史歧视。该概括性目标不是重大政府利益，且无条件配额也并非狭义量身定制。因此，这些保留名额违反平等保护（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）市政供水区行使公共权力，即使活动带有经营性质，仍属于国家行为者。\n\n（选项 B）善意动机不会使种族分类免于严格审查。\n\n（选项 D）合同条款针对州损害既有合同义务；本题直接适用的是平等保护。\n\n教育目标：一切政府种族分类均适用严格审查。纠正该政府主体自身具体、已查明的既往歧视，可能构成重大利益；纠正一般社会歧视则不构成。Students for Fair Admissions 已否定 Grutter 曾认可的高校招生多元化理由，该旧理由也不能支持公共就业或政府合同配额。\n\n参考：Students for Fair Admissions, Inc. v. President & Fellows of Harvard College, 600 U.S. 181（2023）；City of Richmond v. J.A. Croson Co., 488 U.S. 469（1989）。","conclusion":"答案 C：这种预留是违宪的，因为它们会剥夺其他潜在雇员或潜在承包商受到法律的平等保护。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"updated-rule-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"删除原图表中已失效的 Grutter 高校招生多元化规则，并纳入 Students for Fair Admissions；Croson 仍控制本题，原答案 C 不变。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"},{"title":"Students for Fair Admissions, Inc. v. President & Fellows of Harvard College, 600 U.S. 181 (2023)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/22pdf/20-1199_hgdj.pdf"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"remediating","ipa":"/ɹimˈidiət/","syllables":"re·me·di·at·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"(remediate 的现在分词) adj. (= remedial)补救的； 治疗的； 矫正的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：(remediate 的现在分词) adj. (= remedial)补救的； 治疗的； 矫正的","wordForm":"词；原形 remediate；词形 0:remediate/1:i","sourceSentence":"Remediating specific, identified past discrimination by the government itself can be compelling, but remedying generalized societal discrimination is not.","synonyms":["rectify","remedy","repair","amend"]},{"term":"redress","ipa":"/ɹɪdɹˈɛs/","syllables":"re·dress","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 赔偿, 补救, 矫正；vt. 纠正, 赔偿, 救济","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 赔偿, 补救, 矫正；vt. 纠正, 赔偿, 救济","wordForm":"名词；词形 i:redressing/d:redressed/3:redresses/p:redressed","sourceSentence":"The purpose of the rule was \"to help redress the historical discrimination against these groups in this country and to help them achieve economic parity with other groups in our society.","synonyms":["remedy","remediation","damages","amends"]},{"term":"quotas","ipa":"/kwˈoʊtəz/","syllables":"quo·tas","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. （正式限定的）定量( quota的复数形式 )； 定额； 指标","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. （正式限定的）定量( quota的复数形式 )； 定额； 指标","wordForm":"名词；原形 quota；词形 0:quota/1:s","sourceSentence":"That generalized objective is not a compelling interest, and the unqualified quotas are not narrowly tailored.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":192,"sourceQuestionPage":573,"sourceQuestionPages":[573],"sourceExplanationPages":[574,575],"sourceAnswerPages":[574,575],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-193","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A state legislature received complaints from traffic accident victims who, in the days immediately following their accidents, had received unwelcome and occasionally misleading telephone calls on behalf of medical care providers. The callers warned of the risks of not obtaining prompt medical evaluations to detect injuries resulting from accidents and offered free examinations to determine whether the victims had suffered any compensable injuries. In response to these complaints, the legislature enacted a law prohibiting medical care providers from soliciting any accident victim by telephone within 30 days of his or her accident. Which of the following arguments would be most helpful to the state in defending the constitutionality of the law?","choices":{"A":"Because the commercial speech that is the subject of the law includes some speech that is misleading, the First Amendment does not limit the state's power to regulate that speech.","B":"Because the law regulates only commercial speech, the state need only demonstrate that the restriction is rationally related to achieving the state's legitimate interests in protecting the privacy of accident victims and in regulating the medical profession.","C":"The law is a reasonable time, place, and manner regulation.","D":"The state has substantial interests in protecting the privacy of accident victims and in regulating the practice of medical care providers, and the law is narrowly tailored to achieve the state's objectives."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity does not fall under First Amendment protection and can be freely regulated by the government. But all other commercial speech is entitled to an intermediate level of First Amendment protection. This means that the government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the government proves that its regulation directly advances a substantial government interest and the regulation is narrowly tailored to achieve that interest. Here, the state law prohibits medical care providers from soliciting accident victims by telephone within 30 days of the accident. The phone calls are protected commercial speech since they are only occasionally misleading (ie, most are truthful) and do not concern unlawful activity (Choice A). As a result, the state's best argument is that it has substantial interests in protecting the privacy of accident victims and in regulating the practice of medical care providers, and the law is narrowly tailored to achieve those objectives (Choice B). (Choice C) The law is not a time, place, and manner regulation (ie, content-neutral restriction) since it prohibits phone calls based on what is being said (ie, content-based restriction).\n\nEducational objective: Commercial speech that is not false or misleading and does not concern unlawful activity receives First Amendment protection. And any government regulation of that speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) be narrowly tailored to achieve that interest.\n\nReferences\n• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980) (providing the test for determining whether the government's regulation of protected commercial speech is constitutional).","educationalObjective":"Commercial speech that is not false or misleading and does not concern unlawful activity receives First Amendment protection. And any government regulation of that speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) be narrowly tailored to achieve that interest.","references":"• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980) (providing the test for determining whether the government's regulation of protected commercial speech is constitutional).","rule":"Commercial speech that is not false or misleading and does not concern unlawful activity receives First Amendment protection. And any government regulation of that speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) be narrowly tailored to achieve that interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments would be most helpful to the state in defending the constitutionality of the law?","rule":"Commercial speech that is not false or misleading and does not concern unlawful activity receives First Amendment protection. And any government regulation of that speech must (1) directly advance a substantial government interest and (2) be narrowly tailored to achieve that interest.","analysis":"Commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity does not fall under First Amendment protection and can be freely regulated by the government. But all other commercial speech is entitled to an intermediate level of First Amendment protection. This means that the government can only regulate protected commercial speech if the government proves that its regulation directly advances a substantial government interest and the regulation is narrowly tailored to achieve that interest. Here, the state law prohibits medical care providers from soliciting accident victims by telephone within 30 days of the accident. The phone calls are protected commercial speech since they are only occasionally misleading (ie, most are truthful) and do not concern unlawful activity (Choice A). As a result, the state's best argument is that it has substantial interests in protecting the privacy of accident victims and in regulating the practice of medical care providers, and the law is narrowly tailored to achieve those objectives (Choice B). (Choice C) The law is not a time, place, and manner regulation (ie, content-neutral restriction) since it prohibits phone calls based on what is being said (ie, content-based restriction).","conclusion":"Answer D: The state has substantial interests in protecting the privacy of accident victims and in regulating the practice of medical care providers, and the law is narrowly tailored to achieve the state's objectives."},"questionZh":"州立法机构收到了交通事故受害者的投诉，他们在事故发生后的几天内接到了代表医疗保健提供者的不受欢迎且偶尔具有误导性的电话。打电话的人警告说，如果不及时进行医疗评估来发现事故造成的伤害，会存在风险，并提供免费检查以确定受害者是否受到任何可赔偿的伤害。针对这些投诉，立法机关颁布了一项法律，禁止医疗服务提供者在事故发生后 30 天内通过电话向任何事故受害者寻求帮助。以下哪项论点最有利于国家捍卫法律的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于作为法律主体的商业言论包含一些误导性言论，因此第一修正案并不限制州监管该言论的权力。","B":"由于法律仅规范商业言论，国家只需证明该限制与实现国家在保护事故受害者隐私和规范医疗行业方面的合法利益合理相关。","C":"法律是合理的时间、地点和方式的规定。","D":"国家在保护事故受害者的隐私和规范医疗保健提供者的实践方面拥有重大利益，并且该法律是专门为实现国家目标而制定的。"},"explanationZh":"(1) 虚假或误导性的或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论不受第一修正案的保护，可以由政府自由监管。但所有其他商业言论都有权获得第一修正案的中等程度的保护。这意味着，只有当政府证明其监管直接促进了政府的重大利益并且该监管是专门为实现该利益而制定的时，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论。在此，州法律禁止医疗保健提供者在事故发生后 30 天内通过电话向事故受害者寻求帮助。这些电话是受保护的商业言论，因为它们只是偶尔具有误导性（即大多数是真实的）并且不涉及非法活动（选择 A）。因此，国家最好的论据是，它在保护事故受害者的隐私和规范医疗保健提供者的实践方面拥有重大利益，并且法律是专门为实现这些目标而制定的（选择 B）。 （选项C） 法律不是时间、地点和方式的规定（即内容中立的限制），因为它禁止基于所说内容的电话呼叫（即基于内容的限制）。\n\n教育目标：不虚假或误导性且不涉及非法活动的商业言论受到第一修正案的保护。政府对该言论的任何监管都必须（1）直接促进重大政府利益，并且（2）针对实现该利益进行严格调整。\n\n参考文献\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980)（提供了确定政府对受保护商业言论的监管是否符合宪法的测试）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"不虚假或误导性且不涉及非法活动的商业言论受到第一修正案的保护。政府对该言论的任何监管都必须（1）直接促进重大政府利益，并且（2）针对实现该利益进行严格调整。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项论点最有利于国家捍卫法律的合宪性？","rule":"不虚假或误导性且不涉及非法活动的商业言论受到第一修正案的保护。政府对该言论的任何监管都必须（1）直接促进重大政府利益，并且（2）针对实现该利益进行严格调整。","analysis":"(1) 虚假或误导性的或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论不受第一修正案的保护，可以由政府自由监管。但所有其他商业言论都有权获得第一修正案的中等程度的保护。这意味着，只有当政府证明其监管直接促进了政府的重大利益并且该监管是专门为实现该利益而制定的时，政府才能监管受保护的商业言论。在此，州法律禁止医疗保健提供者在事故发生后 30 天内通过电话向事故受害者寻求帮助。这些电话是受保护的商业言论，因为它们只是偶尔具有误导性（即大多数是真实的）并且不涉及非法活动（选择 A）。因此，国家最好的论据是，它在保护事故受害者的隐私和规范医疗保健提供者的实践方面拥有重大利益，并且法律是专门为实现这些目标而制定的（选择 B）。 （选项C） 法律不是时间、地点和方式的规定（即内容中立的限制），因为它禁止基于所说内容的电话呼叫（即基于内容的限制）。\n\n教育目标：不虚假或误导性且不涉及非法活动的商业言论受到第一修正案的保护。政府对该言论的任何监管都必须（1）直接促进重大政府利益，并且（2）针对实现该利益进行严格调整。\n\n参考文献\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 564 (1980)（提供了确定政府对受保护商业言论的监管是否符合宪法的测试）。","conclusion":"答案 D：国家在保护事故受害者的隐私和规范医疗保健提供者的实践方面拥有重大利益，并且该法律是专门为实现国家目标而制定的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-58c77a48304d1e8c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":193,"sourceQuestionPage":576,"sourceQuestionPages":[576],"sourceExplanationPages":[577,578],"sourceAnswerPages":[577,578],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-194","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"Residents of a city complained that brightly colored signs detracted from the character of the city's historic district and distracted motorists trying to navigate its narrow streets. In response, the city council enacted an ordinance requiring any \"sign or visual display\" visible on the streets of the historic district to be black and white and to be no more than four feet long or wide. A political party wanted to hang a six-foot-long red, white, and blue political banner in front of a building in the historic district. The party filed suit to challenge the constitutionality of the sign ordinance as applied to the display of its banner. Which of the following would be the most useful argument for the political party?","choices":{"A":"The ordinance effectively favors some categories of speech over others.","B":"The ordinance imposes a prior restraint on political expression.","C":"The ordinance is not narrowly tailored to further an important government interest, nor does it leave open alternative channels of communication.","D":"The ordinance is not the least restrictive means of promoting a compelling government interest."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Laws that restrict an individual's right to freely communicate information and ideas through speech or conduct can be challenged under the First Amendment. The level of scrutiny applied to such challenges depends on whether the law is either:\n• content-based – in which case, strict scrutiny applies and the law is unconstitutional unless the government can show that it is necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• content-neutral – in which case, intermediate scrutiny applies and the law is permissible so long as the government can show that it is narrowly tailored to achieve an important government interest and leaves open alternative channels of communication.\n\nThe ordinance here is content-neutral since it applies equally to all signs and visual displays irrespective of their message, so strict scrutiny does not apply (Choices A & D). As a result, the political party's most useful argument is that the ordinance cannot survive intermediate scrutiny. (Choice B) A prior restraint is a highly disfavored government action that prohibits speech before it even occurs (eg, injunction, permitting process). The ordinance here is not a prior restraint because it does not require government approval before signs can be posted— they must merely comply with the ordinance.\n\nEducational objective: Content-based speech regulations must be necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest (strict scrutiny). In contrast, content-neutral regulations need only be narrowly tailored to achieve an important government interest and leave open alternative channels of communication (intermediate scrutiny).\n\nReferences\n• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 480 (2019) (level of scrutiny applied to content-based and content-neutral restrictions).","educationalObjective":"Content-based speech regulations must be necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest (strict scrutiny). In contrast, content-neutral regulations need only be narrowly tailored to achieve an important government interest and leave open alternative channels of communication (intermediate scrutiny).","references":"• 16A Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 480 (2019) (level of scrutiny applied to content-based and content-neutral restrictions).","rule":"Content-based speech regulations must be necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest (strict scrutiny). In contrast, content-neutral regulations need only be narrowly tailored to achieve an important government interest and leave open alternative channels of communication (intermediate scrutiny).","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be the most useful argument for the political party?","rule":"Content-based speech regulations must be necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest (strict scrutiny). In contrast, content-neutral regulations need only be narrowly tailored to achieve an important government interest and leave open alternative channels of communication (intermediate scrutiny).","analysis":"Laws that restrict an individual's right to freely communicate information and ideas through speech or conduct can be challenged under the First Amendment. The level of scrutiny applied to such challenges depends on whether the law is either:\n• content-based – in which case, strict scrutiny applies and the law is unconstitutional unless the government can show that it is necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• content-neutral – in which case, intermediate scrutiny applies and the law is permissible so long as the government can show that it is narrowly tailored to achieve an important government interest and leaves open alternative channels of communication.\n\nThe ordinance here is content-neutral since it applies equally to all signs and visual displays irrespective of their message, so strict scrutiny does not apply (Choices A & D). As a result, the political party's most useful argument is that the ordinance cannot survive intermediate scrutiny. (Choice B) A prior restraint is a highly disfavored government action that prohibits speech before it even occurs (eg, injunction, permitting process). The ordinance here is not a prior restraint because it does not require government approval before signs can be posted— they must merely comply with the ordinance.","conclusion":"Answer C: The ordinance is not narrowly tailored to further an important government interest, nor does it leave open alternative channels of communication."},"questionZh":"一座城市的居民抱怨说，色彩鲜艳的标志有损于该市历史街区的特色，并分散了试图在狭窄街道上行驶的驾车者的注意力。作为回应，市议会颁布了一项法令，要求历史街区街道上可见的任何“标志或视觉展示”必须是黑白的，并且长或宽不超过四英尺。一个政党想要在历史街区的一栋建筑前悬挂一面六英尺长的红、白、蓝政治横幅。该党提起诉讼，质疑适用于其旗帜展示的标志条例的合宪性。以下哪一项对政党来说是最有用的论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法令实际上比其他言论更倾向于某些类别的言论。","B":"该法令对政治言论施加了事先限制。","C":"该法令并不是狭隘地为了促进重要的政府利益而制定的，也没有留下开放的替代沟通渠道。","D":"该法令并不是促进令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。"},"explanationZh":"限制个人通过言论或行为自由交流信息和思想的权利的法律可以根据第一修正案受到质疑。适用于此类挑战的审查程度取决于法律是否：\n• 基于内容——在这种情况下，需要进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且该法律是违宪的，除非政府能够证明该法律是必要的且经过严格定制（即限制性最少的手段）以实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 内容中立——在这种情况下，适用中间审查，并且只要政府能够表明该法律是专门为实现重要的政府利益而制定的，并留有开放的替代沟通渠道，该法律就是允许的。\n\n这里的法令是内容中立的，因为它同样适用于所有标志和视觉展示，无论其信息如何，因此严格的审查不适用（选择 A 和 D）。因此，该政党最有用的论据是该法令无法通过中间审查。 （选择 B） 事先限制是一种非常不受欢迎的政府行为，它在言论发生之前就禁止言论（例如，禁令、许可程序）。这里的法令不是事先限制，因为在张贴标志之前不需要政府批准——他们只需遵守该法令即可。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论法规必须是必要的且经过严格定制，以实现令人信服的政府利益（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。相比之下，内容中立的法规只需要进行狭隘的调整即可实现重要的政府利益，并留下开放的替代沟通渠道（中间审查）。\n\n参考文献\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 480 条 (2019)（适用于基于内容和内容中立的限制的审查级别）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于内容的言论法规必须是必要的，并且是严格定制的，以实现令人信服的政府利益（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。相比之下，内容中立的法规只需要进行狭隘的调整即可实现重要的政府利益，并留下开放的替代沟通渠道（中间审查）。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项对政党来说是最有用的论据？","rule":"基于内容的言论法规必须是必要的，并且是严格定制的，以实现令人信服的政府利益（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。相比之下，内容中立的法规只需要进行狭隘的调整即可实现重要的政府利益，并留下开放的替代沟通渠道（中间审查）。","analysis":"限制个人通过言论或行为自由交流信息和思想的权利的法律可以根据第一修正案受到质疑。适用于此类挑战的审查程度取决于法律是否：\n• 基于内容——在这种情况下，需要进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且该法律是违宪的，除非政府能够证明该法律是必要的且经过严格定制（即限制性最少的手段）以实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 内容中立——在这种情况下，适用中间审查，并且只要政府能够表明该法律是专门为实现重要的政府利益而制定的，并留有开放的替代沟通渠道，该法律就是允许的。\n\n这里的法令是内容中立的，因为它同样适用于所有标志和视觉展示，无论其信息如何，因此严格的审查不适用（选择 A 和 D）。因此，该政党最有用的论据是该法令无法通过中间审查。 （选择 B） 事先限制是一种非常不受欢迎的政府行为，它在言论发生之前就禁止言论（例如，禁令、许可程序）。这里的法令不是事先限制，因为在张贴标志之前不需要政府批准——他们只需遵守该法令即可。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论法规必须是必要的且经过严格定制，以实现令人信服的政府利益（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。相比之下，内容中立的法规只需要进行狭隘的调整即可实现重要的政府利益，并留下开放的替代沟通渠道（中间审查）。\n\n参考文献\n• 16A 上午。尤尔。 2d 《宪法》第 480 条 (2019)（适用于基于内容和内容中立的限制的审查级别）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该法令并不是狭隘地为了促进重要的政府利益而制定的，也没有留下开放的替代沟通渠道。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f734aa717a0f6f76"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":194,"sourceQuestionPage":579,"sourceQuestionPages":[579],"sourceExplanationPages":[580,581],"sourceAnswerPages":[580,581],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-195","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state statute provides financial aid for tuition, books, and other academic materials to students who are accepted into one of the state's universities. The state statute lists several requirements for a student to receive this financial aid. One of the requirements is that the student be a citizen of the United States or a resident noncitizen who has applied for citizenship. A teenager was accepted into one of the state's universities and applied for financial aid pursuant to the state statute. However, his application was denied solely because he is a resident noncitizen who has not applied for United States citizenship. The teenager filed suit in federal court to challenge the constitutionality of the state statute's provision relating to United States citizenship. Will the federal court likely find the provision to be constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the state likely cannot show that the provision is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","B":"No, because this type of arbitrary classification is barred by the Article IV privileges and immunities clause.","C":"Yes, because the Fourteenth Amendment does not apply to resident noncitizens.","D":"Yes, because the provision is reasonably related to a legitimate state interest."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The state statutory provision is discriminatory since it grants tuition assistance to U.S. citizens and resident noncitizens who have applied for citizenship—but not resident noncitizens who have not sought citizenship. As a result, it can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. The court will impose strict scrutiny since citizenship is a suspect class (Choice C). Under this test, the state bears the nearly impossible burden of proving that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. As a result, this statutory provision— like most laws subject to strict scrutiny—is likely unconstitutional. (Choice B) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from creating arbitrary classifications that discriminate against citizens of other states. But noncitizens (and corporations) are not protected by this clause. (Choice D) The statutory provision would be valid under the rational basis test if it was reasonably related to a legitimate state interest. But this minimal scrutiny only applies to laws that do not discriminate against a suspect or quasi-suspect class or substantially impact a fundamental right.\n\nEducational objective: Laws that discriminate against a suspect class are subject to strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Since this standard is nearly impossible to meet, these laws are almost always struck down.\n\nReferences\n• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1977) (applying strict scrutiny to invalidate a state statute that denied tuition assistance to resident noncitizens not seeking citizenship).","educationalObjective":"Laws that discriminate against a suspect class are subject to strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Since this standard is nearly impossible to meet, these laws are almost always struck down.","references":"• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1977) (applying strict scrutiny to invalidate a state statute that denied tuition assistance to resident noncitizens not seeking citizenship).","rule":"Laws that discriminate against a suspect class are subject to strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Since this standard is nearly impossible to meet, these laws are almost always struck down.","irac":{"issue":"Will the federal court likely find the provision to be constitutional?","rule":"Laws that discriminate against a suspect class are subject to strict scrutiny, which requires the government to prove that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Since this standard is nearly impossible to meet, these laws are almost always struck down.","analysis":"The state statutory provision is discriminatory since it grants tuition assistance to U.S. citizens and resident noncitizens who have applied for citizenship—but not resident noncitizens who have not sought citizenship. As a result, it can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. The court will impose strict scrutiny since citizenship is a suspect class (Choice C). Under this test, the state bears the nearly impossible burden of proving that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. As a result, this statutory provision— like most laws subject to strict scrutiny—is likely unconstitutional. (Choice B) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from creating arbitrary classifications that discriminate against citizens of other states. But noncitizens (and corporations) are not protected by this clause. (Choice D) The statutory provision would be valid under the rational basis test if it was reasonably related to a legitimate state interest. But this minimal scrutiny only applies to laws that do not discriminate against a suspect or quasi-suspect class or substantially impact a fundamental right.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the state likely cannot show that the provision is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest."},"questionZh":"州法规为被该州大学录取的学生提供学费、书籍和其他学术材料的经济援助。州法规列出了学生获得此项经济援助的几项要求。其中一项要求是学生必须是美国公民或已申请公民身份的非公民居民。一名青少年被该州一所大学录取，并根据州法规申请经济援助。然而，他的申请被拒绝仅仅是因为他是一名居民非公民，尚未申请美国公民身份。该少年向联邦法院提起诉讼，质疑州法规中有关美国公民身份的条款是否符合宪法。联邦法院可能会认为该条款符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国家可能无法证明该条款对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","B":"不可以，因为这种任意分类受到第四条特权和豁免条款的禁止。","C":"是的，因为第十四修正案不适用于居民非公民。","D":"是的，因为该条款与合法的国家利益合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"该州的法律规定具有歧视性，因为它向美国公民和已申请公民身份的居民非公民提供学费援助，但不向未寻求公民身份的居民非公民提供学费援助。因此，它可以根据第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。由于公民身份属于可疑类别（选项 C），因此法院将进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这一检验下，国家承担着几乎不可能的责任来证明法律对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。因此，这一法定条款——像大多数受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的法律一样——很可能违宪。 （选项 B） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止各国制定歧视其他国家公民的任意分类。但非公民（和公司）不受本条款的保护。 （选项D）如果该法定条款与合法的国家利益合理相关，则该法定条款在合理基础测试下将是有效的。但这种最低限度的审查仅适用于不歧视嫌疑人或准嫌疑人阶层或不严重影响基本权利的法律。\n\n教育目标：歧视可疑阶层的法律受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。由于这一标准几乎不可能达到，因此这些法律几乎总是被废除。\n\n参考文献\n• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1977)（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），使拒绝向不寻求公民身份的非公民居民提供学费援助的州法规无效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"歧视可疑阶层的法律受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。由于这一标准几乎不可能达到，因此这些法律几乎总是被废除。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法院可能会认为该条款符合宪法吗？","rule":"歧视可疑阶层的法律受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。由于这一标准几乎不可能达到，因此这些法律几乎总是被废除。","analysis":"该州的法律规定具有歧视性，因为它向美国公民和已申请公民身份的居民非公民提供学费援助，但不向未寻求公民身份的居民非公民提供学费援助。因此，它可以根据第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。由于公民身份属于可疑类别（选项 C），因此法院将进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这一检验下，国家承担着几乎不可能的责任来证明法律对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。因此，这一法定条款——像大多数受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的法律一样——很可能违宪。 （选项 B） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止各国制定歧视其他国家公民的任意分类。但非公民（和公司）不受本条款的保护。 （选项D）如果该法定条款与合法的国家利益合理相关，则该法定条款在合理基础测试下将是有效的。但这种最低限度的审查仅适用于不歧视嫌疑人或准嫌疑人阶层或不严重影响基本权利的法律。\n\n教育目标：歧视可疑阶层的法律受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。由于这一标准几乎不可能达到，因此这些法律几乎总是被废除。\n\n参考文献\n• Nyquist v. Mauclet, 432 U.S. 1, 8–9 (1977)（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），使拒绝向不寻求公民身份的非公民居民提供学费援助的州法规无效）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为国家可能无法证明该条款对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-07528abbab3c00cf"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":195,"sourceQuestionPage":582,"sourceQuestionPages":[582],"sourceExplanationPages":[583,584],"sourceAnswerPages":[583,584],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-196","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Establishment Clause","question":"Several public high school students asked the superintendent of their public school district whether the minister of a local church could deliver an interdenominational prayer at their graduation ceremony in the school auditorium. None of the students or their guests at graduation would be required to pray while the minister delivered the prayer. Would the minister's delivery of such a prayer at the public high school graduation be constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because it would be an unconstitutional establishment of religion.","B":"No, because it would deny attendees who are not members of the minister's denomination the right to freely exercise their religion.","C":"Yes, because none of the students or their guests would be required to pray at the graduation ceremony.","D":"Yes, because the idea for the prayer originated with the students and not with school officials."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Establishment clause tests\n\nTest Requirements Applicability\n\nHistorical*\n• Longstanding tradition & Default historical foundation Endorsement\n• No appearance that government Public displays or monuments endorses religion (eg, holiday displays)\n\nCoercion\n• No forced conformity with Public schools (eg, convocation religious belief or practice prayer)\n\n*Courts may apply strict scrutiny if government directly benefits/burdens religion.\n\nThe First Amendment establishment clause prohibits the government from expressing a religious preference or otherwise violating the separation of church and state. In Lee v. Weisman, the Supreme Court held that school-sponsored prayer must be reviewed under the coercion test, which considers whether the government forced participation in a religious exercise. Since elementary and secondary school students are more susceptible to social and peer pressure than adults, school-sponsored prayer in these public schools is inherently coercive—even when participation is voluntary (Choice C). As a result, the minister's delivery of a prayer at the public high school graduation would be an unconstitutional establishment of religion. (Choice B) Since participation would be voluntary, the minister's prayer would not deny attendees the right to freely exercise their own religion. But the prayer would still violate the establishment clause—despite being interdenominational—because it would show government preference for religion in general. (Choice D) Although the graduation prayer was the students' idea, the school still must abide by the Constitution if it approves that idea and sponsors the prayer.\n\nEducational objective: Under the coercion test, school-sponsored prayer in public schools violates the establishment clause even when participation is voluntary.\n\nReferences\n• Lee v. Weisman, 505 U.S. 577, 592–93 (1992) (holding that an interdenominational prayer at middle school graduation ceremony coerces religious participation in violation of the establishment clause).","educationalObjective":"Under the coercion test, school-sponsored prayer in public schools violates the establishment clause even when participation is voluntary.","references":"• Lee v. Weisman, 505 U.S. 577, 592–93 (1992) (holding that an interdenominational prayer at middle school graduation ceremony coerces religious participation in violation of the establishment clause).","rule":"Under the coercion test, school-sponsored prayer in public schools violates the establishment clause even when participation is voluntary.","irac":{"issue":"Would the minister's delivery of such a prayer at the public high school graduation be constitutional?","rule":"Under the coercion test, school-sponsored prayer in public schools violates the establishment clause even when participation is voluntary.","analysis":"Establishment clause tests\n\nTest Requirements Applicability\n\nHistorical*\n• Longstanding tradition & Default historical foundation Endorsement\n• No appearance that government Public displays or monuments endorses religion (eg, holiday displays)\n\nCoercion\n• No forced conformity with Public schools (eg, convocation religious belief or practice prayer)\n\n*Courts may apply strict scrutiny if government directly benefits/burdens religion.\n\nThe First Amendment establishment clause prohibits the government from expressing a religious preference or otherwise violating the separation of church and state. In Lee v. Weisman, the Supreme Court held that school-sponsored prayer must be reviewed under the coercion test, which considers whether the government forced participation in a religious exercise. Since elementary and secondary school students are more susceptible to social and peer pressure than adults, school-sponsored prayer in these public schools is inherently coercive—even when participation is voluntary (Choice C). As a result, the minister's delivery of a prayer at the public high school graduation would be an unconstitutional establishment of religion. (Choice B) Since participation would be voluntary, the minister's prayer would not deny attendees the right to freely exercise their own religion. But the prayer would still violate the establishment clause—despite being interdenominational—because it would show government preference for religion in general. (Choice D) Although the graduation prayer was the students' idea, the school still must abide by the Constitution if it approves that idea and sponsors the prayer.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because it would be an unconstitutional establishment of religion."},"questionZh":"几名公立高中学生询问公立学区的负责人，当地教会的牧师是否可以在学校礼堂举行的毕业典礼上进行跨教派祈祷。当牧师进行祈祷时，毕业典礼上的学生或他们的客人都不需要祈祷。部长在公立高中毕业典礼上进行这样的祈祷是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这将是违宪的宗教建立。","B":"不可以，因为这会剥夺非部长教派成员的与会者自由行使宗教信仰的权利。","C":"是的，因为学生或他们的客人都不需要在毕业典礼上祈祷。","D":"是的，因为祈祷的想法源于学生而不是学校官员。"},"explanationZh":"设立条款测试\n\n测试要求的适用性\n\n历史*\n• 悠久传统&默认历史基础背书\n• 政府公共展示或纪念碑不支持宗教（例如节日展示）\n\n强制\n• 不得强迫遵守公立学校规定（例如，召集宗教信仰或进行祈祷）\n\n*如果政府直接使宗教受益/加重宗教负担，法院可能会进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n第一修正案的设立条款禁止政府表达宗教偏好或以其他方式违反政教分离。在李诉韦斯曼案中，最高法院认为，学校资助的祈祷必须根据强制测试进行审查，该测试考虑政府是否强迫参加宗教活动。由于小学生和中学生比成年人更容易受到社会和同伴压力的影响，因此这些公立学校中学校资助的祈祷本质上是强制性的——即使参与是自愿的（选项C）。因此，牧师在公立高中毕业典礼上进行祈祷将构成违宪的宗教确立。 （选项 B） 由于参与是自愿的，牧师的祈祷不会剥夺参加者自由信奉自己宗教的权利。但祈祷仍然会违反建制条款——尽管是跨教派的——因为它会显示出政府对宗教的总体偏好。 （选项D）虽然毕业祈祷是学生的想法，但学校如果同意这个想法并赞助祈祷，仍然必须遵守章程。\n\n教育目标：根据强制测试，公立学校由学校资助的祈祷违反了既定条款，即使参与是自愿的。\n\n参考文献\n• Lee v. Weisman, 505 U.S. 577, 592–93 (1992)（认为中学毕业典礼上的跨教派祈祷强迫宗教参与，违反了建制条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据强制测试，公立学校由学校资助的祈祷活动违反了设立条款，即使参与是自愿的。","iracZh":{"issue":"部长在公立高中毕业典礼上进行这样的祈祷是否符合宪法？","rule":"根据强制测试，公立学校由学校资助的祈祷活动违反了设立条款，即使参与是自愿的。","analysis":"设立条款测试\n\n测试要求的适用性\n\n历史*\n• 悠久传统&默认历史基础背书\n• 政府公共展示或纪念碑不支持宗教（例如节日展示）\n\n强制\n• 不得强迫遵守公立学校规定（例如，召集宗教信仰或进行祈祷）\n\n*如果政府直接使宗教受益/加重宗教负担，法院可能会进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n第一修正案的设立条款禁止政府表达宗教偏好或以其他方式违反政教分离。在李诉韦斯曼案中，最高法院认为，学校资助的祈祷必须根据强制测试进行审查，该测试考虑政府是否强迫参加宗教活动。由于小学生和中学生比成年人更容易受到社会和同伴压力的影响，因此这些公立学校中学校资助的祈祷本质上是强制性的——即使参与是自愿的（选项C）。因此，牧师在公立高中毕业典礼上进行祈祷将构成违宪的宗教确立。 （选项 B） 由于参与是自愿的，牧师的祈祷不会剥夺参加者自由信奉自己宗教的权利。但祈祷仍然会违反建制条款——尽管是跨教派的——因为它会显示出政府对宗教的总体偏好。 （选项D）虽然毕业祈祷是学生的想法，但学校如果同意这个想法并赞助祈祷，仍然必须遵守章程。\n\n教育目标：根据强制测试，公立学校由学校资助的祈祷违反了既定条款，即使参与是自愿的。\n\n参考文献\n• Lee v. Weisman, 505 U.S. 577, 592–93 (1992)（认为中学毕业典礼上的跨教派祈祷强迫宗教参与，违反了建制条款）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为这将是违宪的宗教建立。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":196,"sourceQuestionPage":585,"sourceQuestionPages":[585],"sourceExplanationPages":[586,587],"sourceAnswerPages":[586,587],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-197","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"Congress recently enacted a statute creating a program that made federal loans available to family farmers who had been unable to obtain loans from private lenders. Congress appropriated a fixed sum of money to fund loans made pursuant to the program and gave a designated federal agency discretion to decide which applicants were to receive the loans. Two weeks after the program was established, a family farmer applied to the agency for a loan. Agency officials promptly reviewed her application and summarily denied it. The farmer has sued the agency in federal district court, claiming only that the denial of her application without the opportunity for a hearing violated the due process clause of the Fifth Amendment. The farmer claims that she could have proved at such a hearing that without the federal loan it would be necessary for her to sell her farm. Should the court uphold the agency's decision?","choices":{"A":"No, because due process requires federal agencies to provide a hearing before making any factual determination that adversely affects an identified individual based on his or her particular circumstances.","B":"No, because the denial of a loan may deprive the farmer of an established liberty interest to pursue her chosen occupation.","C":"Yes, because the applicable statute gives the farmer no legitimate claim of entitlement to receive a loan.","D":"Yes, because the spending clause of Article I, Section 8, gives Congress plenary power to control the distribution of appropriated funds in any manner it wishes."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Due process rights\n\nRight Applicability Examples\n\nLife Government deprives person of life\n• Execution\n\nLiberty Government significantly restrains person's\n• Incarceration physical freedom, exercise of fundamental\n• Commitment to mental rights, or freedom of choice institution\n• Loss of parental rights Property Government deprives benefit from\n• Suspension from public person legally entitled to receive it school\n• Termination of welfare benefits\n• Loss of public employment (not at will) The Fifth Amendment due process clause requires that the federal government provide an individual with notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard when it deprives that individual of life, liberty, or property. Under this clause, a property interest exists when an individual is legally entitled to a benefit—but mere anticipation of (or need for) a benefit does not create a protected interest. Here, the farmer applied for a federal loan under the newly enacted statute. But she was not legally entitled to the loan because the statute gave a federal agency the discretion to decide which applicants would receive loans. And since her need for a loan was not an interest protected by the due process clause, the agency was not obligated to provide her with an opportunity for a hearing. Therefore, the agency's decision should be upheld. (Choice A) Due process only requires a hearing when the government deprives an individual of a protected life, liberty, or property interest (not seen here). (Choice B) Pursuing one's chosen occupation is not a liberty interest protected by the due process clause. (Choice D) Although Article I, Section 8 gives Congress broad authority to control the distribution of appropriated funds, that authority is subject to many constitutional limitations—including the Fifth Amendment due process clause.\n\nEducational objective: Fifth Amendment due process protections are triggered when the federal government deprives an individual of life, liberty, or property. An individual has a protected property interest when he/she is legally entitled to (not merely anticipating or needing) a benefit.\n\nReferences\n• Bd. of Regents of State Colls. v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 577 (1972) (defining property interests protected by procedural due process).","educationalObjective":"Fifth Amendment due process protections are triggered when the federal government deprives an individual of life, liberty, or property. An individual has a protected property interest when he/she is legally entitled to (not merely anticipating or needing) a benefit.","references":"• Bd. of Regents of State Colls. v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 577 (1972) (defining property interests protected by procedural due process).","rule":"Fifth Amendment due process protections are triggered when the federal government deprives an individual of life, liberty, or property. An individual has a protected property interest when he/she is legally entitled to (not merely anticipating or needing) a benefit.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court uphold the agency's decision?","rule":"Fifth Amendment due process protections are triggered when the federal government deprives an individual of life, liberty, or property. An individual has a protected property interest when he/she is legally entitled to (not merely anticipating or needing) a benefit.","analysis":"Due process rights\n\nRight Applicability Examples\n\nLife Government deprives person of life\n• Execution\n\nLiberty Government significantly restrains person's\n• Incarceration physical freedom, exercise of fundamental\n• Commitment to mental rights, or freedom of choice institution\n• Loss of parental rights Property Government deprives benefit from\n• Suspension from public person legally entitled to receive it school\n• Termination of welfare benefits\n• Loss of public employment (not at will) The Fifth Amendment due process clause requires that the federal government provide an individual with notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard when it deprives that individual of life, liberty, or property. Under this clause, a property interest exists when an individual is legally entitled to a benefit—but mere anticipation of (or need for) a benefit does not create a protected interest. Here, the farmer applied for a federal loan under the newly enacted statute. But she was not legally entitled to the loan because the statute gave a federal agency the discretion to decide which applicants would receive loans. And since her need for a loan was not an interest protected by the due process clause, the agency was not obligated to provide her with an opportunity for a hearing. Therefore, the agency's decision should be upheld. (Choice A) Due process only requires a hearing when the government deprives an individual of a protected life, liberty, or property interest (not seen here). (Choice B) Pursuing one's chosen occupation is not a liberty interest protected by the due process clause. (Choice D) Although Article I, Section 8 gives Congress broad authority to control the distribution of appropriated funds, that authority is subject to many constitutional limitations—including the Fifth Amendment due process clause.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the applicable statute gives the farmer no legitimate claim of entitlement to receive a loan."},"questionZh":"国会最近颁布了一项法规，制定了一项计划，向无法从私人贷款机构获得贷款的家庭农民提供联邦贷款。国会拨款固定数额的资金来资助根据该计划发放的贷款，并赋予指定的联邦机构自由裁量权来决定哪些申请人将获得贷款。该计划建立两周后，一位家庭农民向该机构申请贷款。该机构官员立即审​​查了她的申请并立即予以拒绝。该农民已向联邦地区法院起诉该机构，仅声称在没有听证机会的情况下拒绝她的申请违反了第五修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。这位农民声称，她可以在这样的听证会上证明，如果没有联邦贷款，她就必须出售自己的农场。法院是否应该维持该机构的决定？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为正当程序要求联邦机构在根据特定个人的具体情况作出任何对已识别个人产生不利影响的事实决定之前举行听证会。","B":"不可以，因为拒绝贷款可能会剥夺农民从事其所选职业的既定自由利益。","C":"是的，因为适用的法规没有赋予农民获得贷款的合法权利。","D":"是的，因为第一条第 8 款的支出条款赋予国会全权以任何其希望的方式控制拨款的分配。"},"explanationZh":"正当程序权利\n\n正确的适用性示例\n\n生命政府剥夺人的生命\n• 执行\n\n自由政府极大地限制个人的\n• 监禁期间的人身自由、行使基本权利\n• 对精神权利或选择制度自由的承诺\n• 失去父母权利 财产政府剥夺了以下权利：\n• 暂停合法有权上学的公众资格\n• 福利金的终止\n• 失去公共就业（非自愿） 第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求联邦政府在剥夺个人生命、自由或财产时向其提供通知和有意义的表达意见的机会。根据本条款，当个人合法地有权获得某种利益时，就存在财产权益，但仅仅预期（或需要）某种利益并不会产生受保护的利益。在这里，农民根据新颁布的法规申请了联邦贷款。但她在法律上没有资格获得贷款，因为该法规赋予联邦机构自由裁量权来决定哪些申请人将获得贷款。由于她的贷款需求不受正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护，因此该机构没有义务为她提供听证会的机会。因此，应当维持该机构的决定。 （选择 A）正当程序仅在政府剥夺个人受保护的生命、自由或财产权益时才需要举行听证会（此处未显示）。 （选择B） 追求自己选择的职业不是受正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护的自由利益。 （选项 D）虽然第一条第 8 节赋予国会广泛的权力来控制拨款的分配，但该权力受到许多宪法限制，包括第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。\n\n教育目标：当联邦政府剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产时，就会触发第五修正案的正当程序保护。当一个人合法地有权（不仅仅是预期或需要）某种利益时，他/她就拥有受保护的财产利益。\n\n参考文献\n• Bd。州立大学摄政王。 v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 577 (1972)（定义受程序性正当程序保护的财产利益）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当联邦政府剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产时，第五修正案的正当程序保护就会被触发。当一个人合法地有权（不仅仅是预期或需要）某种利益时，他/她就拥有受保护的财产利益。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该维持该机构的决定？","rule":"当联邦政府剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产时，第五修正案的正当程序保护就会被触发。当一个人合法地有权（不仅仅是预期或需要）某种利益时，他/她就拥有受保护的财产利益。","analysis":"正当程序权利\n\n正确的适用性示例\n\n生命政府剥夺人的生命\n• 执行\n\n自由政府极大地限制个人的\n• 监禁期间的人身自由、行使基本权利\n• 对精神权利或选择制度自由的承诺\n• 失去父母权利 财产政府剥夺了以下权利：\n• 暂停合法有权上学的公众资格\n• 福利金的终止\n• 失去公共就业（非自愿） 第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求联邦政府在剥夺个人生命、自由或财产时向其提供通知和有意义的表达意见的机会。根据本条款，当个人合法地有权获得某种利益时，就存在财产权益，但仅仅预期（或需要）某种利益并不会产生受保护的利益。在这里，农民根据新颁布的法规申请了联邦贷款。但她在法律上没有资格获得贷款，因为该法规赋予联邦机构自由裁量权来决定哪些申请人将获得贷款。由于她的贷款需求不受正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护，因此该机构没有义务为她提供听证会的机会。因此，应当维持该机构的决定。 （选择 A）正当程序仅在政府剥夺个人受保护的生命、自由或财产权益时才需要举行听证会（此处未显示）。 （选择B） 追求自己选择的职业不是受正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）保护的自由利益。 （选项 D）虽然第一条第 8 节赋予国会广泛的权力来控制拨款的分配，但该权力受到许多宪法限制，包括第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。\n\n教育目标：当联邦政府剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产时，就会触发第五修正案的正当程序保护。当一个人合法地有权（不仅仅是预期或需要）某种利益时，他/她就拥有受保护的财产利益。\n\n参考文献\n• Bd。州立大学摄政王。 v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 577 (1972)（定义受程序性正当程序保护的财产利益）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为适用的法规没有赋予农民获得贷款的合法权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"entitlement","ipa":"/ɛntˈaɪtəlmənt/","syllables":"en·ti·tle·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 权利；[法] 权利","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 权利；[法] 权利","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:entitlements","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the applicable statute gives the farmer no legitimate claim of entitlement to receive a loan.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"legally","ipa":"/lˈiɡəli/","syllables":"legally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 法律上, 合法地；[法] 法律上, 合法地, 法定地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 法律上, 合法地；[法] 法律上, 合法地, 法定地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"An individual has a protected property interest when he/she is legally entitled to (not merely anticipating or needing) a benefit.","synonyms":["lawfully","de jure"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":197,"sourceQuestionPage":588,"sourceQuestionPages":[588],"sourceExplanationPages":[589,590],"sourceAnswerPages":[589,590],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-198","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"In one state, certain advanced diagnostic medical technologies were located only in hospitals, where they provided a major source of revenue. In many other states, such technologies were also available at \"diagnostic centers\" that were not affiliated with hospitals. A group of physicians announced its plan to immediately open in the state a diagnostic center that would not be affiliated with a hospital. The state hospital association argued to the state legislature that only hospitals could reliably handle advanced diagnostic medical technologies. The legislature then enacted a law prohibiting the operation in the state of diagnostic centers that were not affiliated with hospitals. The group of physicians filed suit challenging the constitutionality of the state law. What action should the court take?","choices":{"A":"Dismiss the suit without reaching the merits, because the suit is not ripe.","B":"Invalidate the law, because it imposes an undue burden on access to medical services in the state.","C":"Uphold the law, because the legislature could rationally believe that diagnostic centers not affiliated with hospitals would be less reliable than hospitals.","D":"Uphold the law, because the provision of medical services is traditionally a matter of legitimate local concern that states have unreviewable authority to regulate."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The constitutionality of a discriminatory law can be challenged under the equal protection clause. Such challenges are generally reviewed under the rational basis test, which requires that the law be rationally related to any legitimate state interest. And since any reasonable, conceivable purpose that may have motivated the legislature to pass the law will satisfy this test, laws subject to the rational basis test are usually upheld. Here, a state law discriminates against physicians who wish to operate an unaffiliated diagnostic center by requiring that diagnostic centers be affiliated with hospitals. Since the legislature could rationally believe that unaffiliated diagnostic centers would be less reliable than hospitals, the law is rationally related to a legitimate state interest—providing safe medical services. Therefore, the law satisfies the rational basis test and should be upheld. (Choice A) A court will dismiss a suit without reaching the merits if it is not ripe—ie, if the plaintiff has yet to suffer actual harm or an imminent threat thereof. But since this law threatens the physicians' immediate plan to open a diagnostic center in the state, their suit is ripe for litigation. (Choice B) Discriminatory laws that unduly burden a fundamental right are presumptively invalid and reviewed under strict scrutiny, which requires that the law be necessary to serve a compelling state interest. But access to medical services is not a fundamental right, so this law need only survive rational basis scrutiny. (Choice D) State laws and regulations are always subject to constitutional limits, so the state does not have the unreviewable authority to regulate the provision of medical services—even if it is traditionally a matter of legitimate local concern.\n\nEducational objective: Under the equal protection clause, discriminatory laws are generally reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the law must be rationally related to any legitimate state interest. And any reasonable, conceivable purpose that may have motivated the legislature in passing the law will satisfy this test.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Heller v. Doe, 509 U.S. 312, 319–20 (1993) (explaining that any reasonable, conceivable legislative purpose will satisfy rational basis scrutiny).\n\n• Barsky v. Board of Regents, 347 U.S. 442, 449 (1954) (finding that a state has broad power to regulate the health profession).","educationalObjective":"Under the equal protection clause, discriminatory laws are generally reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the law must be rationally related to any legitimate state interest. And any reasonable, conceivable purpose that may have motivated the legislature in passing the law will satisfy this test.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Heller v. Doe, 509 U.S. 312, 319–20 (1993) (explaining that any reasonable, conceivable legislative purpose will satisfy rational basis scrutiny).\n\n• Barsky v. Board of Regents, 347 U.S. 442, 449 (1954) (finding that a state has broad power to regulate the health profession).","rule":"Under the equal protection clause, discriminatory laws are generally reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the law must be rationally related to any legitimate state interest. And any reasonable, conceivable purpose that may have motivated the legislature in passing the law will satisfy this test.","irac":{"issue":"What action should the court take?","rule":"Under the equal protection clause, discriminatory laws are generally reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the law must be rationally related to any legitimate state interest. And any reasonable, conceivable purpose that may have motivated the legislature in passing the law will satisfy this test.","analysis":"The constitutionality of a discriminatory law can be challenged under the equal protection clause. Such challenges are generally reviewed under the rational basis test, which requires that the law be rationally related to any legitimate state interest. And since any reasonable, conceivable purpose that may have motivated the legislature to pass the law will satisfy this test, laws subject to the rational basis test are usually upheld. Here, a state law discriminates against physicians who wish to operate an unaffiliated diagnostic center by requiring that diagnostic centers be affiliated with hospitals. Since the legislature could rationally believe that unaffiliated diagnostic centers would be less reliable than hospitals, the law is rationally related to a legitimate state interest—providing safe medical services. Therefore, the law satisfies the rational basis test and should be upheld. (Choice A) A court will dismiss a suit without reaching the merits if it is not ripe—ie, if the plaintiff has yet to suffer actual harm or an imminent threat thereof. But since this law threatens the physicians' immediate plan to open a diagnostic center in the state, their suit is ripe for litigation. (Choice B) Discriminatory laws that unduly burden a fundamental right are presumptively invalid and reviewed under strict scrutiny, which requires that the law be necessary to serve a compelling state interest. But access to medical services is not a fundamental right, so this law need only survive rational basis scrutiny. (Choice D) State laws and regulations are always subject to constitutional limits, so the state does not have the unreviewable authority to regulate the provision of medical services—even if it is traditionally a matter of legitimate local concern.","conclusion":"Answer C: Uphold the law, because the legislature could rationally believe that diagnostic centers not affiliated with hospitals would be less reliable than hospitals."},"questionZh":"在一个州，某些先进的诊断医疗技术只存在于医院，而它们是医院的主要收入来源。在许多其他州，不隶属于医院的“诊断中心”也可以使用此类技术。一群医生宣布计划立即在该州开设一家不隶属于医院的诊断中心。州医院协会向州立法机关辩称，只有医院才能可靠地处理先进的诊断医疗技术。立法机关随后颁布了一项法律，禁止在该州运营不隶属于医院的诊断中心。这群医生提起诉讼，质疑州法律的合宪性。法院应该采取什么行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"在未达到案情实质的情况下驳回诉讼，因为诉讼时机尚不成熟。","B":"该法律无效，因为它给该州获得医疗服务带来了不应有的负担。","C":"维护法律，因为立法机关可以理性地认为不隶属于医院的诊断中心不如医院可靠。","D":"遵守法律，因为传统上，医疗服务的提供是当地合法关注的问题，各州拥有不可审查的监管权力。"},"explanationZh":"歧视性法律的合宪性可以根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。此类挑战通常会根据合理基础测试进行审查，这要求法律与任何合法的国家利益合理相关。由于任何可能促使立法机关通过法律的合理的、可想象的目的都将满足这一测试，因此接受合理基础测试的法律通常会得到维护。在这里，州法律要求诊断中心隶属于医院，从而歧视希望经营独立诊断中心的医生。由于立法机关可以合理地认为独立的诊断中心不如医院可靠，因此该法律合理地与合法的国家利益（提供安全的医疗服务）相关。因此，该法律满足合合理基础检验，应予维持。 （选择A）如果时机不成熟，即原告尚未遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的威胁，法院将在未确定案情实质的情况下驳回诉讼。但由于这项法律威胁到医生在该州开设诊断中心的近期计划，他们的诉讼时机已经成熟。 （选择 B）过度加重基本权利负担的歧视性法律被推定为无效，并经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求该法律对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的。但获得医疗服务并不是一项基本权利，因此该法律只需经过合理基础的审查即可。 （选项 D）州法律和法规始终受到宪法限制，因此州没有不可审查的权力来规范医疗服务的提供，即使这传统上是当地合法关注的问题。\n\n教育目标：在平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）下，歧视性法律通常在合理基础测试下进行审查，即法律必须与任何合法的国家利益合理相关。任何可能促使立法机关通过法律的合理、可想象的目的都将满足这一测试。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Heller v. Doe, 509 U.S. 312, 319–20 (1993)（解释说任何合理的、可想象的立法目的都将满足合理基础审查）。\n\n• Barsky v. Board of Regents, 347 U.S. 442, 449 (1954)（裁定国家拥有监管医疗行业的广泛权力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）下，歧视性法律通常根据合理基础测试进行审查，即法律必须与任何合法的国家利益合理相关。任何可能促使立法机关通过法律的合理、可想象的目的都将满足这一测试。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该采取什么行动？","rule":"在平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）下，歧视性法律通常根据合理基础测试进行审查，即法律必须与任何合法的国家利益合理相关。任何可能促使立法机关通过法律的合理、可想象的目的都将满足这一测试。","analysis":"歧视性法律的合宪性可以根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。此类挑战通常会根据合理基础测试进行审查，这要求法律与任何合法的国家利益合理相关。由于任何可能促使立法机关通过法律的合理的、可想象的目的都将满足这一测试，因此接受合理基础测试的法律通常会得到维护。在这里，州法律要求诊断中心隶属于医院，从而歧视希望经营独立诊断中心的医生。由于立法机关可以合理地认为独立的诊断中心不如医院可靠，因此该法律合理地与合法的国家利益（提供安全的医疗服务）相关。因此，该法律满足合合理基础检验，应予维持。 （选择A）如果时机不成熟，即原告尚未遭受实际伤害或迫在眉睫的威胁，法院将在未确定案情实质的情况下驳回诉讼。但由于这项法律威胁到医生在该州开设诊断中心的近期计划，他们的诉讼时机已经成熟。 （选择 B）过度加重基本权利负担的歧视性法律被推定为无效，并经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这要求该法律对于服务于令人信服的国家利益是必要的。但获得医疗服务并不是一项基本权利，因此该法律只需经过合理基础的审查即可。 （选项 D）州法律和法规始终受到宪法限制，因此州没有不可审查的权力来规范医疗服务的提供，即使这传统上是当地合法关注的问题。\n\n教育目标：在平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）下，歧视性法律通常在合理基础测试下进行审查，即法律必须与任何合法的国家利益合理相关。任何可能促使立法机关通过法律的合理、可想象的目的都将满足这一测试。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Heller v. Doe, 509 U.S. 312, 319–20 (1993)（解释说任何合理的、可想象的立法目的都将满足合理基础审查）。\n\n• Barsky v. Board of Regents, 347 U.S. 442, 449 (1954)（裁定国家拥有监管医疗行业的广泛权力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：维护法律，因为立法机关可以理性地认为不隶属于医院的诊断中心不如医院可靠。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-bfe47b1d10f919a6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":198,"sourceQuestionPage":591,"sourceQuestionPages":[591],"sourceExplanationPages":[592,593],"sourceAnswerPages":[592,593],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-199","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state statute requires all workers' compensation actions filed in state court to be decided by a judge. A woman brought a workers' compensation action in state court against her employer for injuries she sustained at work. The woman demanded a jury trial, but the court denied her request pursuant to the state statute. Is the state statute likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because denying a jury trial only in workers' compensation actions violates equal protection of the laws.","B":"No, because the Seventh Amendment guarantees the woman a jury trial.","C":"No, because the Sixth Amendment requires a jury to decide the outcome of the case.","D":"Yes."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The first 10 amendments to the U.S. Constitution (ie, the Bill of Rights) protect individuals from government overreach. These protections limit the power of the federal government, and most apply to the states with equal force through incorporation into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause—with three exceptions:\n• The Third Amendment prohibition against the government quartering soldiers in individuals' homes\n• The Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment to charge a person with a capital offense\n• The Seventh Amendment guarantee of a jury trial in civil cases* Here, the state court denied the woman's jury trial demand pursuant to a state statute requiring judges to decide the outcome of workers' compensation suits. Since the right to a jury trial in civil cases has not been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, this right does not apply in state court. As a result, the woman has no right to a jury trial, and the statute is likely constitutional (Choice B). *The Supreme Court incorporates a protection under the Bill of Rights into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause if the protection is fundamental to the scheme of ordered liberty or deeply rooted in the history and tradition of the United States. (Choice A) The statute implicates the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause because it treats workers' compensation claimants differently than other civil claimants. However, the statute does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, so rational basis scrutiny applies. Since this low level of scrutiny is easily met, the statute likely complies with the equal protection clause. (Choice C) The Sixth Amendment right to a jury trial attaches to any criminal proceeding in which the defendant could be sentenced to more than six months in prison. But since the woman filed a civil action, this right does not apply here.\n\nEducational objective: Every Bill of Rights protection applies to the states except (1) the Third Amendment prohibition against the government quartering soldiers in individuals' homes, (2) the Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment for capital offenses, and (3) the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial in civil cases.\n\nReferences\n• Minneapolis & St. Louis R.R. Co. v. Bombolis, 241 U.S. 211, 217–18 (1916) (explaining that the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial in civil cases does not apply to the states).\n\n• McDonald v. City of Chi., 561 U.S. 742, 765 n.13 (2010) (reaffirming that the Seventh Amendment guarantee of a civil jury trial has not been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause).","educationalObjective":"Every Bill of Rights protection applies to the states except (1) the Third Amendment prohibition against the government quartering soldiers in individuals' homes, (2) the Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment for capital offenses, and (3) the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial in civil cases.","references":"• Minneapolis & St. Louis R.R. Co. v. Bombolis, 241 U.S. 211, 217–18 (1916) (explaining that the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial in civil cases does not apply to the states).\n\n• McDonald v. City of Chi., 561 U.S. 742, 765 n.13 (2010) (reaffirming that the Seventh Amendment guarantee of a civil jury trial has not been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause).","rule":"Every Bill of Rights protection applies to the states except (1) the Third Amendment prohibition against the government quartering soldiers in individuals' homes, (2) the Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment for capital offenses, and (3) the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial in civil cases.","irac":{"issue":"Is the state statute likely constitutional?","rule":"Every Bill of Rights protection applies to the states except (1) the Third Amendment prohibition against the government quartering soldiers in individuals' homes, (2) the Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment for capital offenses, and (3) the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial in civil cases.","analysis":"The first 10 amendments to the U.S. Constitution (ie, the Bill of Rights) protect individuals from government overreach. These protections limit the power of the federal government, and most apply to the states with equal force through incorporation into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause—with three exceptions:\n• The Third Amendment prohibition against the government quartering soldiers in individuals' homes\n• The Fifth Amendment requirement of a grand jury indictment to charge a person with a capital offense\n• The Seventh Amendment guarantee of a jury trial in civil cases* Here, the state court denied the woman's jury trial demand pursuant to a state statute requiring judges to decide the outcome of workers' compensation suits. Since the right to a jury trial in civil cases has not been incorporated into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause, this right does not apply in state court. As a result, the woman has no right to a jury trial, and the statute is likely constitutional (Choice B). *The Supreme Court incorporates a protection under the Bill of Rights into the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause if the protection is fundamental to the scheme of ordered liberty or deeply rooted in the history and tradition of the United States. (Choice A) The statute implicates the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause because it treats workers' compensation claimants differently than other civil claimants. However, the statute does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, so rational basis scrutiny applies. Since this low level of scrutiny is easily met, the statute likely complies with the equal protection clause. (Choice C) The Sixth Amendment right to a jury trial attaches to any criminal proceeding in which the defendant could be sentenced to more than six months in prison. But since the woman filed a civil action, this right does not apply here.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes."},"questionZh":"州法规要求向州法院提起的所有工人赔偿诉讼均须由法官裁决。一名妇女因在工作中受伤而向州法院提起工伤赔偿诉讼。该女子要求陪审团审判，但法院根据州法规拒绝了她的请求。州法令可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为仅在工人赔偿诉讼中拒绝陪审团审判违反了法律的平等保护。","B":"不，因为第七修正案保证该妇女接受陪审团审判。","C":"不可以，因为第六修正案要求陪审团来决定案件的结果。","D":"是的。"},"explanationZh":"美国宪法的前 10 项修正案（即《权利法案》）保护个人免受政府过度干预。这些保护措施限制了联邦政府的权力，并且大多数通过纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）而适用于具有同等效力的各州，但有以下三个例外：\n• 第三修正案禁止政府将士兵安置在个人家中\n• 第五修正案要求大陪审团起诉某人犯有死刑\n• 第七修正案保证民事案件中陪审团审判* 在此，州法院根据要求法官决定工人赔偿诉讼结果的州法规，拒绝了该妇女的陪审团审判要求。由于民事案件中陪审团审判的权利尚未纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中，因此该权利不适用于州法院。因此，该妇女无权接受陪审团审判，并且该法规很可能符合宪法（选择 B）。 *如果《权利法案》规定的保护对于有序自由计划至关重要或深深植根于美国的历史和传统，则最高法院会将该保护纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。 （选项A）该法规涉及第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它对待工人赔偿索赔人与其他民事索赔人的待遇不同。然而，该法规不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，因此适用合理基础审查。由于这种低水平的审查很容易满足，因此该法规可能符合平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。 （选项 C）第六修正案规定陪审团审判的权利适用于被告可能被判处六个月以上监禁的任何刑事诉讼。但由于该女子提起了民事诉讼，这项权利在此不适用。\n\n教育目标：每项权利保护法案均适用于各州，但以下情况除外：(1) 第三修正案禁止政府在个人家中驻扎士兵；(2) 第五修正案要求大陪审团起诉死刑罪；(3) 第七修正案在民事案件中接受陪审团审判的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• Minneapolis & St. Louis R.R. Co. v. Bombolis, 241 U.S. 211, 217–18 (1916)（解释第七修正案民事案件陪审团审判的权利不适用于各州）。\n\n• McDonald v. City of Chi., 561 U.S. 742, 765 n.13 (2010)（重申第七修正案对民事陪审团审判的保证尚未纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"每一项权利保护法案都适用于各州，但以下情况除外：(1) 第三修正案禁止政府在个人家中驻扎士兵；(2) 第五修正案要求大陪审团对死刑罪提起公诉；(3) 第七修正案在民事案件中接受陪审团审判的权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"州法令可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"每一项权利保护法案都适用于各州，但以下情况除外：(1) 第三修正案禁止政府在个人家中驻扎士兵；(2) 第五修正案要求大陪审团对死刑罪提起公诉；(3) 第七修正案在民事案件中接受陪审团审判的权利。","analysis":"美国宪法的前 10 项修正案（即《权利法案》）保护个人免受政府过度干预。这些保护措施限制了联邦政府的权力，并且大多数通过纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）而适用于具有同等效力的各州，但有以下三个例外：\n• 第三修正案禁止政府将士兵安置在个人家中\n• 第五修正案要求大陪审团起诉某人犯有死刑\n• 第七修正案保证民事案件中陪审团审判* 在此，州法院根据要求法官决定工人赔偿诉讼结果的州法规，拒绝了该妇女的陪审团审判要求。由于民事案件中陪审团审判的权利尚未纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中，因此该权利不适用于州法院。因此，该妇女无权接受陪审团审判，并且该法规很可能符合宪法（选择 B）。 *如果《权利法案》规定的保护对于有序自由计划至关重要或深深植根于美国的历史和传统，则最高法院会将该保护纳入第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中。 （选项A）该法规涉及第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它对待工人赔偿索赔人与其他民事索赔人的待遇不同。然而，该法规不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，因此适用合理基础审查。由于这种低水平的审查很容易满足，因此该法规可能符合平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。 （选项 C）第六修正案规定陪审团审判的权利适用于被告可能被判处六个月以上监禁的任何刑事诉讼。但由于该女子提起了民事诉讼，这项权利在此不适用。\n\n教育目标：每项权利保护法案均适用于各州，但以下情况除外：(1) 第三修正案禁止政府在个人家中驻扎士兵；(2) 第五修正案要求大陪审团起诉死刑罪；(3) 第七修正案在民事案件中接受陪审团审判的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• Minneapolis & St. Louis R.R. Co. v. Bombolis, 241 U.S. 211, 217–18 (1916)（解释第七修正案民事案件陪审团审判的权利不适用于各州）。\n\n• McDonald v. City of Chi., 561 U.S. 742, 765 n.13 (2010)（重申第七修正案对民事陪审团审判的保证尚未纳入第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"claimants","ipa":"/klˈeɪmənts/","syllables":"claimants","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 申请者, 要求者( claimant的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 申请者, 要求者( claimant的复数形式 )","wordForm":"名词；原形 claimant；词形 0:claimant/1:s","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) The statute implicates the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause because it treats workers' compensation claimants differently than other civil claimants.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-c65993ab5616905a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":199,"sourceQuestionPage":594,"sourceQuestionPages":[594],"sourceExplanationPages":[595,596],"sourceAnswerPages":[595,596],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-200","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state has a statute providing that an unsuccessful candidate in a primary election for a party's nomination for elected public office may not become a candidate for the same office at the following general election by nominating petition or by write-in votes. A woman sought her party's nomination for governor in the primary election. After losing in the primary, the woman filed nominating petitions containing the requisite number of signatures to become a candidate for the office of governor in the following general election. The chief elections officer of the state refused to certify the woman's petitions solely because of the above statute. The woman then filed suit in federal district court challenging the constitutionality of this statute. If the court holds that the statute constitutes a severe restriction, which of the following is the proper burden of persuasion in this suit?","choices":{"A":"The state must demonstrate that the statute is rationally related to a legitimate state interest.","B":"The state must demonstrate that the statute is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest.","C":"The woman must demonstrate that the statute is not necessary to achieve a compelling state interest.","D":"The woman must demonstrate that the statute is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest."},"answer":"B","explanation":"State-election restrictions\n\nRestriction Examples Test\n\nOrdinary\n• Voter registration Rational basis: challenger must show\n• Photo-ID restriction lacks rational relationship (nondiscriminatory) to legitimate state interest requirement\n• Disallowing write-in voting Severe\n• Poll tax Strict scrutiny: state must show\n• Disallowing third- restriction is necessary to achieve (discriminatory) compelling state interest party candidacies\n• Property-ownership requirement States may regulate their own elections by requiring voters and candidates to abide by certain requirements. But these regulations must comply with the First Amendment and the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause, which together secure an individual's right to vote and freely associate. If the electoral regulation is challenged under either constitutional provision, the standard for evaluating that regulation depends on the severity of the burden imposed. A court will apply either:\n• rational basis review – applies to ordinary (nondiscriminatory) burdens and requires the challenger to prove that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny – applies to severe (discriminatory) burdens and requires the state to prove that its regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest.\n\nHere, a state statute prohibits a loser of a primary election from running for the same public office in the general election (ie, \"sore-loser law\"). This infringed upon the woman's right to access the ballot as a political candidate (ie, her right to vote and freely associate). And if the court holds that the statute constitutes a severe restriction on that right, then the state must demonstrate that the statute is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest. (Choices A & D) Rational basis review places the burden of persuasion on the challenging party—not the state—when ordinary electoral regulations are involved. (Choice C) Strict scrutiny places the burden of persuasion on the state to demonstrate that a law is necessary (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling state interest.\n\nEducational objective: When an electoral regulation imposes an ordinary burden, the challenging party must prove that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest (ie, rational basis test). But if the burden is severe, the state must show that the regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny).\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. I (freedom of association).\n\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992) (explaining that the level of scrutiny of an election law depends on the severity of the restriction).","educationalObjective":"When an electoral regulation imposes an ordinary burden, the challenging party must prove that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest (ie, rational basis test). But if the burden is severe, the state must show that the regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny).","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. I (freedom of association).\n\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992) (explaining that the level of scrutiny of an election law depends on the severity of the restriction).","rule":"When an electoral regulation imposes an ordinary burden, the challenging party must prove that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest (ie, rational basis test). But if the burden is severe, the state must show that the regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny).","irac":{"issue":"If the court holds that the statute constitutes a severe restriction, which of the following is the proper burden of persuasion in this suit?","rule":"When an electoral regulation imposes an ordinary burden, the challenging party must prove that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest (ie, rational basis test). But if the burden is severe, the state must show that the regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny).","analysis":"State-election restrictions\n\nRestriction Examples Test\n\nOrdinary\n• Voter registration Rational basis: challenger must show\n• Photo-ID restriction lacks rational relationship (nondiscriminatory) to legitimate state interest requirement\n• Disallowing write-in voting Severe\n• Poll tax Strict scrutiny: state must show\n• Disallowing third- restriction is necessary to achieve (discriminatory) compelling state interest party candidacies\n• Property-ownership requirement States may regulate their own elections by requiring voters and candidates to abide by certain requirements. But these regulations must comply with the First Amendment and the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause, which together secure an individual's right to vote and freely associate. If the electoral regulation is challenged under either constitutional provision, the standard for evaluating that regulation depends on the severity of the burden imposed. A court will apply either:\n• rational basis review – applies to ordinary (nondiscriminatory) burdens and requires the challenger to prove that the regulation is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny – applies to severe (discriminatory) burdens and requires the state to prove that its regulation is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest.\n\nHere, a state statute prohibits a loser of a primary election from running for the same public office in the general election (ie, \"sore-loser law\"). This infringed upon the woman's right to access the ballot as a political candidate (ie, her right to vote and freely associate). And if the court holds that the statute constitutes a severe restriction on that right, then the state must demonstrate that the statute is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest. (Choices A & D) Rational basis review places the burden of persuasion on the challenging party—not the state—when ordinary electoral regulations are involved. (Choice C) Strict scrutiny places the burden of persuasion on the state to demonstrate that a law is necessary (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling state interest.","conclusion":"Answer B: The state must demonstrate that the statute is the least restrictive means of achieving a compelling state interest."},"questionZh":"州有一项法规规定，在公职政党提名初选中落选的候选人不得通过提名请愿或记名投票的方式成为下一次大选中同一职位的候选人。一名妇女在初选中寻求本党州长提名。在初选失败后，该妇女提交了包含所需数量签名的提名请愿书，以便在接下来的大选中成为州长职位的候选人。该州首席选举官仅仅因为上述法规而拒绝认证该妇女的请愿书。该女子随后向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，质疑该法规的合宪性。如果法院认为该法规构成严格限制，则以下哪项是本案中适当的说服责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"国家必须证明该法规与合法的国家利益合理相关。","B":"国家必须证明该法规是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。","C":"该妇女必须证明该法规对于实现令人信服的国家利益并不是必要的。","D":"该妇女必须证明该法规与合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。"},"explanationZh":"州选举限制\n\n限制示例测试\n\n普通\n• 选民登记 合理基础：挑战者必须表明\n• 照片身份限制与合法的国家利益要求缺乏理性关系（非歧视性）\n• 不允许写入投票 严重\n• 人头税 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：州政府必须表明\n• 禁止第三方限制对于获得（歧视性）令人信服的国家利益党候选人资格是必要的\n• 财产所有权要求 各州可以通过要求选民和候选人遵守某些要求来规范自己的选举。但这些规定必须符合第一修正案和第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），它们共同保障个人的投票权和自由结社权。如果选举法规受到任何宪法条款的质疑，评估该法规的标准取决于所施加负担的严重程度。法院将适用以下任一方法：\n• 合理基础审查——适用于普通（非歧视）负担，并要求质疑者证明该法规与合法国家利益不合理相关，或\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）——适用于严重的（歧视性）负担，并要求国家证明其监管是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n在这里，州法规禁止初选失败者在大选中竞选同一公职（即“失败者法”）。这侵犯了妇女作为政治候选人的投票权（即投票权和自由结社权）。如果法院认为该法规对该权利构成了严格限制，那么国家必须证明该法规是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。 （选择 A 和 D）当涉及普通选举法规时，合理基础审查将说服的责任置于挑战方（而不是国家）身上。 （选择C） 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）将说服的责任置于国家身上，以证明法律对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即限制性最小的手段）。\n\n教育目标：当选举法规施加普通负担时，质疑方必须证明该法规与合法的国家利益不合理相关（即合理基础测试）。但如果负担很严重，国家必须证明监管是实现令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））的限制最少的手段。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。我（结社自由）。\n\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992)（解释说选举法的审查程度取决于限制的严重程度）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当选举法规施加普通负担时，质疑方必须证明该法规与合法的国家利益不合理相关（即合理基础测试）。但如果负担很严重，国家必须证明监管是实现令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））的限制最少的手段。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院认为该法规构成严格限制，则以下哪项是本案中适当的说服责任？","rule":"当选举法规施加普通负担时，质疑方必须证明该法规与合法的国家利益不合理相关（即合理基础测试）。但如果负担很严重，国家必须证明监管是实现令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））的限制最少的手段。","analysis":"州选举限制\n\n限制示例测试\n\n普通\n• 选民登记 合理基础：挑战者必须表明\n• 照片身份限制与合法的国家利益要求缺乏理性关系（非歧视性）\n• 不允许写入投票 严重\n• 人头税 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：州政府必须表明\n• 禁止第三方限制对于获得（歧视性）令人信服的国家利益党候选人资格是必要的\n• 财产所有权要求 各州可以通过要求选民和候选人遵守某些要求来规范自己的选举。但这些规定必须符合第一修正案和第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），它们共同保障个人的投票权和自由结社权。如果选举法规受到任何宪法条款的质疑，评估该法规的标准取决于所施加负担的严重程度。法院将适用以下任一方法：\n• 合理基础审查——适用于普通（非歧视）负担，并要求质疑者证明该法规与合法国家利益不合理相关，或\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）——适用于严重的（歧视性）负担，并要求国家证明其监管是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n在这里，州法规禁止初选失败者在大选中竞选同一公职（即“失败者法”）。这侵犯了妇女作为政治候选人的投票权（即投票权和自由结社权）。如果法院认为该法规对该权利构成了严格限制，那么国家必须证明该法规是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。 （选择 A 和 D）当涉及普通选举法规时，合理基础审查将说服的责任置于挑战方（而不是国家）身上。 （选择C） 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）将说服的责任置于国家身上，以证明法律对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即限制性最小的手段）。\n\n教育目标：当选举法规施加普通负担时，质疑方必须证明该法规与合法的国家利益不合理相关（即合理基础测试）。但如果负担很严重，国家必须证明监管是实现令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））的限制最少的手段。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。我（结社自由）。\n\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992)（解释说选举法的审查程度取决于限制的严重程度）。","conclusion":"答案 B：国家必须证明该法规是实现令人信服的国家利益的限制最少的手段。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":200,"sourceQuestionPage":597,"sourceQuestionPages":[597],"sourceExplanationPages":[598,599],"sourceAnswerPages":[598,599],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-201","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"A state legislature enacted a statute that allowed executors to claim a tax deduction for the sale of securities that were purchased by an estate after the decedent's death. After the statute was enacted, the state suffered a significant decrease in revenue because the number of estates that claimed the tax deduction was greater than originally projected. To recover this lost revenue, the legislature amended the statute to prevent an estate from claiming the deduction unless the decedent owned the securities prior to death. The amendment was made retroactive so that its effective date was when the statute was initially enacted. Before the amendment was enacted, an executor sold securities that he had purchased for a decedent's estate after her death. The executor attempted to claim the tax deduction on the estate's tax returns but the state agency that processes tax returns denied his claim even though the estate had purchased the securities before the amendment was enacted. The executor has filed suit against the agency to challenge the constitutionality of the retroactive application of the amendment. Is the executor's suit likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the retroactive application of the amendment was necessary to achieve a compelling government interest.","B":"No, because the retroactive application of the amendment was rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","C":"Yes, because the amendment constitutes a bill of attainder.","D":"Yes, because the amendment violates the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The Fourteenth Amendment substantive due process clause prevents states from depriving persons of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. Civil laws that retroactively impair an ordinary right—eg, the right to claim a tax deduction—must undergo rational basis scrutiny. Under this test, the law is presumed valid until the challenger shows that the law's retroactive application has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest. Here, the executor challenged the amendment's retroactive application since it prevented the estate from claiming the tax deduction on its tax returns, which is an ordinary right. But since the state suffered a significant decline in revenue after the statute was enacted, the retroactive application of the amendment was rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in recovering the lost revenue. This means that the amendment does not violate the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause (Choice D). Therefore, the executor's suit is unlikely to succeed. (Choice A) The retroactive application of a law that affects a fundamental right (eg, voting) is subject to strict scrutiny—ie, the government must prove that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. But here, the amendment's retroactive application affects an ordinary right and is therefore subject to rational basis scrutiny. (Choice C) A bill of attainder is a legislative act that punishes a specified person or group without a judicial trial. But since the amendment applies to all persons who purchase securities, it is not a bill of attainder. Additionally, the amendment seeks to increase the state's revenue—not punish individuals..\n\nEducational objective: Laws that retroactively impair an ordinary right—eg, the right to claim a tax deduction— can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny and generally are presumed valid.","educationalObjective":"Laws that retroactively impair an ordinary right—eg, the right to claim a tax deduction— can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny and generally are presumed valid.","references":"","rule":"Laws that retroactively impair an ordinary right—eg, the right to claim a tax deduction— can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny and generally are presumed valid.","irac":{"issue":"Is the executor's suit likely to succeed?","rule":"Laws that retroactively impair an ordinary right—eg, the right to claim a tax deduction— can be challenged on substantive due process grounds. Such laws are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny and generally are presumed valid.","analysis":"The Fourteenth Amendment substantive due process clause prevents states from depriving persons of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. Civil laws that retroactively impair an ordinary right—eg, the right to claim a tax deduction—must undergo rational basis scrutiny. Under this test, the law is presumed valid until the challenger shows that the law's retroactive application has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest. Here, the executor challenged the amendment's retroactive application since it prevented the estate from claiming the tax deduction on its tax returns, which is an ordinary right. But since the state suffered a significant decline in revenue after the statute was enacted, the retroactive application of the amendment was rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in recovering the lost revenue. This means that the amendment does not violate the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause (Choice D). Therefore, the executor's suit is unlikely to succeed. (Choice A) The retroactive application of a law that affects a fundamental right (eg, voting) is subject to strict scrutiny—ie, the government must prove that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. But here, the amendment's retroactive application affects an ordinary right and is therefore subject to rational basis scrutiny. (Choice C) A bill of attainder is a legislative act that punishes a specified person or group without a judicial trial. But since the amendment applies to all persons who purchase securities, it is not a bill of attainder. Additionally, the amendment seeks to increase the state's revenue—not punish individuals..","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the retroactive application of the amendment was rationally related to a legitimate government interest."},"questionZh":"州立法机关颁布了一项法规，允许遗嘱执行人对死者去世后遗产购买的证券的销售申请减税。该法规颁布后，由于申请减税的庄园数量比最初预计的要多，该州的收入大幅减少。为了弥补这一损失的收入，立法机关修改了法规，以防止遗产要求扣除，除非死者在死亡前拥有证券。该修正案具有追溯力，其生效日期为该法规最初颁布时。在修正案颁布之前，一名遗嘱执行人出售了他在死者去世后为她的遗产购买的证券。遗嘱执行人试图在遗产的纳税申报表上申请税收减免，但处理纳税申报表的国家机构拒绝了他的要求，尽管遗产在修正案颁布之前已经购买了这些证券。执行者已对该机构提起诉讼，质疑修正案追溯适用的合宪性。遗嘱执行人的诉讼有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为修正案的追溯适用对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","B":"不，因为修正案的追溯适用与合法的政府利益合理相关。","C":"是的，因为该修正案构成剥夺公民权法案。","D":"是的，因为该修正案违反了第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。"},"explanationZh":"第十四修正案的实质性正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺他人的生命、自由或财产。追溯损害普通权利（例如要求减税的权利）的民法必须经过合理的基础审查。根据这一测试，在挑战者证明该法律的追溯适用与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系之前，该法律被视为有效。在这里，遗嘱执行人对修正案的追溯适用提出质疑，因为它阻止了遗产在其纳税申报表上要求税收减免，这是一项普通权利。但由于该法颁布后国家收入大幅下降，因此修正案的追溯适用与国家收回损失收入的合法利益存在合理关系。这意味着该修正案不违反第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）（选项D）。因此，执行人的诉讼不太可能成功。 （选择A）影响基本权利（例如投票）的法律的追溯适用受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即政府必须证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。但在这里，修正案的追溯适用影响了普通权利，因此受到合理依据的审查。 （选项C） 剥夺公民权法案是一种未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为。但由于该修正案适用于所有购买证券的人，因此它不是剥夺公民权法案。此外，该修正案旨在增加国家收入，而不是惩罚个人。\n\n教育目标：追溯性损害普通权利（例如要求减税的权利）的法律可以基于实质性正当程序理由受到质疑。此类法律经过合理的审查，通常被认为是有效的。","educationalObjectiveZh":"追溯性损害普通权利（例如要求减税的权利）的法律可以基于实质性正当程序理由受到质疑。此类法律经过合理的审查，通常被认为是有效的。","iracZh":{"issue":"遗嘱执行人的诉讼有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"追溯性损害普通权利（例如要求减税的权利）的法律可以基于实质性正当程序理由受到质疑。此类法律经过合理的审查，通常被认为是有效的。","analysis":"第十四修正案的实质性正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺他人的生命、自由或财产。追溯损害普通权利（例如要求减税的权利）的民法必须经过合理的基础审查。根据这一测试，在挑战者证明该法律的追溯适用与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系之前，该法律被视为有效。在这里，遗嘱执行人对修正案的追溯适用提出质疑，因为它阻止了遗产在其纳税申报表上要求税收减免，这是一项普通权利。但由于该法颁布后国家收入大幅下降，因此修正案的追溯适用与国家收回损失收入的合法利益存在合理关系。这意味着该修正案不违反第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）（选项D）。因此，执行人的诉讼不太可能成功。 （选择A）影响基本权利（例如投票）的法律的追溯适用受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），即政府必须证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。但在这里，修正案的追溯适用影响了普通权利，因此受到合理依据的审查。 （选项C） 剥夺公民权法案是一种未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为。但由于该修正案适用于所有购买证券的人，因此它不是剥夺公民权法案。此外，该修正案旨在增加国家收入，而不是惩罚个人。\n\n教育目标：追溯性损害普通权利（例如要求减税的权利）的法律可以基于实质性正当程序理由受到质疑。此类法律经过合理的审查，通常被认为是有效的。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为修正案的追溯适用与合法的政府利益合理相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"retroactively","ipa":"/ɹˌɛtɹoʊˈæktɪvli/","syllables":"retroac·tively","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 逆动地, 追溯地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：adv. 逆动地, 追溯地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Laws that retroactively impair an ordinary right—eg, the right to claim a tax deduction— can be challenged on substantive due process grounds.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-268b9f4e798dce2e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":201,"sourceQuestionPage":600,"sourceQuestionPages":[600],"sourceExplanationPages":[601,602],"sourceAnswerPages":[601,602],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-202","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"The legislature of State B enacted a statute requiring that all law enforcement officers in that state be United States citizens. A lawfully admitted noncitizen became a permanent resident five years before the enactment of this statute. He sought employment as a forensic pathologist in the state coroner's office. He was denied that job solely because he was not a citizen. The resident noncitizen thereupon brought suit in federal district court against appropriate state officials seeking to invalidate this citizenship requirement on federal constitutional grounds. Which of the following is the strongest ground upon which to attack this citizenship requirement?","choices":{"A":"The requirement constitutes an ex post facto law as to previously admitted noncitizens.","B":"The requirement denies noncitizens equal protection of the laws guaranteed by the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"The requirement denies noncitizens the right to employment in violation of the privileges or immunities clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"The requirement deprives noncitizens of a fundamental right to employment without the due process of law under the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Fourteenth Amendment protections\n\nClause Applicability\n\nEqual protection Discriminatory treatment of similarly situated people\n\nDue process Substantive – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification\n\nProcedural – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate process\n\nPrivileges or Interference with rights of national citizenship immunities*\n\n*Rarely a correct answer.\n\nCitizenship requirements (as seen here) are discriminatory since they deny resident noncitizens benefits or opportunities that are generally available to U.S. citizens. As a result, laws with this requirement can be attacked on equal protection grounds. And since citizenship is a suspect class (triggering strict scrutiny review), these laws are rarely upheld. (Choice A) An ex post facto law typically refers to a criminal statute that retroactively imposes punishment. Although federal and state governments are barred from passing such laws, State B's statute is civil (not criminal) in nature and has no retroactive effect—ie, there is no requirement that noncitizens currently holding law enforcement positions be terminated. (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause only protects citizens (not noncitizens or corporations) from unreasonable state interference with the narrowly defined rights of national citizenship. (Choice D) A state law that impacts a fundamental right can be challenged on Fourteenth Amendment due process (or equal protection) grounds. But since employment is not a fundamental right, State B's citizenship requirement cannot be attacked on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: Citizenship requirements are discriminatory since they deny resident noncitizens benefits or opportunities that are generally available to citizens. As a result, they are strictly scrutinized (and rarely upheld) on equal protection grounds.\n\nReferences\n• Cabell v. Chavez-Salido, 454 U.S. 432, 437 (1982) (explaining that a resident noncitizen can challenge citizenship requirement for public employment).","educationalObjective":"Citizenship requirements are discriminatory since they deny resident noncitizens benefits or opportunities that are generally available to citizens. As a result, they are strictly scrutinized (and rarely upheld) on equal protection grounds.","references":"• Cabell v. Chavez-Salido, 454 U.S. 432, 437 (1982) (explaining that a resident noncitizen can challenge citizenship requirement for public employment).","rule":"Citizenship requirements are discriminatory since they deny resident noncitizens benefits or opportunities that are generally available to citizens. As a result, they are strictly scrutinized (and rarely upheld) on equal protection grounds.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest ground upon which to attack this citizenship requirement?","rule":"Citizenship requirements are discriminatory since they deny resident noncitizens benefits or opportunities that are generally available to citizens. As a result, they are strictly scrutinized (and rarely upheld) on equal protection grounds.","analysis":"Fourteenth Amendment protections\n\nClause Applicability\n\nEqual protection Discriminatory treatment of similarly situated people\n\nDue process Substantive – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification\n\nProcedural – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate process\n\nPrivileges or Interference with rights of national citizenship immunities*\n\n*Rarely a correct answer.\n\nCitizenship requirements (as seen here) are discriminatory since they deny resident noncitizens benefits or opportunities that are generally available to U.S. citizens. As a result, laws with this requirement can be attacked on equal protection grounds. And since citizenship is a suspect class (triggering strict scrutiny review), these laws are rarely upheld. (Choice A) An ex post facto law typically refers to a criminal statute that retroactively imposes punishment. Although federal and state governments are barred from passing such laws, State B's statute is civil (not criminal) in nature and has no retroactive effect—ie, there is no requirement that noncitizens currently holding law enforcement positions be terminated. (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause only protects citizens (not noncitizens or corporations) from unreasonable state interference with the narrowly defined rights of national citizenship. (Choice D) A state law that impacts a fundamental right can be challenged on Fourteenth Amendment due process (or equal protection) grounds. But since employment is not a fundamental right, State B's citizenship requirement cannot be attacked on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer B: The requirement denies noncitizens equal protection of the laws guaranteed by the Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"B 州立法机关颁布了一项法规，要求该州的所有执法人员都是美国公民。合法承认的非公民在本法颁布五年前成为永久居民。他在州验尸官办公室寻求法医病理学家的职位。他被拒绝这份工作只是因为他不是公民。该居民非公民随即向联邦地区法院提起诉讼，控告有关州官员，试图以联邦宪法为由废除这一公民身份要求。以下哪一项是攻击这一公民身份要求的最有力的依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"该要求构成了针对先前接纳的非公民的事后法律。","B":"该要求否认非公民受到第十四修正案所保障的法律的平等保护。","C":"该要求剥夺了非公民的就业权，违反了第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款。","D":"该要求剥夺了非公民未经第十四修正案规定的正当法律程序的基本就业权利。"},"explanationZh":"第十四修正案的保护\n\n条款适用性\n\n平等保护 对处境相似的人的歧视性待遇\n\n正当程序 实质性 – 在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n程序性——未经充分程序剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n特权或干扰国家公民豁免权*\n\n*很少有正确答案。\n\n公民身份要求（如此处所示）具有歧视性，因为它们剥夺了美国公民通常可以获得的非公民居民的福利或机会。因此，具有此要求的法律可能会因平等保护而受到攻击。由于公民身份是一个可疑的类别（引发严格的审查），这些法律很少得到维护。 （选项A）事后法律通常是指追溯性地施加惩罚的刑事法规。尽管联邦和州政府被禁止通过此类法律，但 B 州的法规本质上是民事（而非刑事）且不具有追溯力，即不要求终止目前担任执法职务的非公民。 （选项C）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款仅保护公民（而非非公民或公司）免受国家对狭义公民权利的不合理干涉。 （选项 D）影响基本权利的州法律可以根据第十四修正案的正当程序（或平等保护）理由受到质疑。但由于就业不是一项基本权利，因此不能以此为依据攻击B国的公民身份要求。\n\n教育目标：公民身份要求具有歧视性，因为它们剥夺了居民非公民普遍享有的福利或机会。因此，它们在平等保护的基础上受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（并且很少得到维护）。\n\n参考文献\n• Cabell v. Chavez-Salido, 454 U.S. 432, 437 (1982)（解释居民非公民可以质疑公共就业的公民身份要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"公民身份要求具有歧视性，因为它们剥夺了非公民居民通常享有的福利或机会。因此，它们在平等保护的基础上受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（并且很少得到维护）。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是攻击这一公民身份要求的最有力的依据？","rule":"公民身份要求具有歧视性，因为它们剥夺了非公民居民通常享有的福利或机会。因此，它们在平等保护的基础上受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（并且很少得到维护）。","analysis":"第十四修正案的保护\n\n条款适用性\n\n平等保护 对处境相似的人的歧视性待遇\n\n正当程序 实质性 – 在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n程序性——未经充分程序剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n特权或干扰国家公民豁免权*\n\n*很少有正确答案。\n\n公民身份要求（如此处所示）具有歧视性，因为它们剥夺了美国公民通常可以获得的非公民居民的福利或机会。因此，具有此要求的法律可能会因平等保护而受到攻击。由于公民身份是一个可疑的类别（引发严格的审查），这些法律很少得到维护。 （选项A）事后法律通常是指追溯性地施加惩罚的刑事法规。尽管联邦和州政府被禁止通过此类法律，但 B 州的法规本质上是民事（而非刑事）且不具有追溯力，即不要求终止目前担任执法职务的非公民。 （选项C）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款仅保护公民（而非非公民或公司）免受国家对狭义公民权利的不合理干涉。 （选项 D）影响基本权利的州法律可以根据第十四修正案的正当程序（或平等保护）理由受到质疑。但由于就业不是一项基本权利，因此不能以此为依据攻击B国的公民身份要求。\n\n教育目标：公民身份要求具有歧视性，因为它们剥夺了居民非公民普遍享有的福利或机会。因此，它们在平等保护的基础上受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（并且很少得到维护）。\n\n参考文献\n• Cabell v. Chavez-Salido, 454 U.S. 432, 437 (1982)（解释居民非公民可以质疑公共就业的公民身份要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该要求否认非公民受到第十四修正案所保障的法律的平等保护。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"ex post facto law","ipa":"/ˈɛks pˈoʊst fˈæktoʊ lˈɔ/","syllables":"ex post facto law","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"事后法：追溯性加重刑事责任或刑罚的法律","contextualMeaning":"事后法：追溯性加重刑事责任或刑罚的法律","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The requirement constitutes an ex post facto law as to previously admitted noncitizens.","synonyms":["ex post facto practice of law","ex post facto natural law","ex post facto law of nature"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":202,"sourceQuestionPage":603,"sourceQuestionPages":[603],"sourceExplanationPages":[604,605],"sourceAnswerPages":[604,605],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-203","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A federal statute prohibits websites from displaying sexually explicit material on their homepages. The purpose of the statute is to prevent minors from easily accessing such material. Several website owners who have been prosecuted for violating the statute have defended on the ground that the prosecution is unconstitutional. Are the website owners likely to prevail in this defense?","choices":{"A":"No, because sexually explicit materials are not protected speech.","B":"No, because the government has extensive power to regulate the Internet.","C":"Yes, because the government may not punish the broadcast of sexually explicit material.","D":"Yes, because there are less restrictive means available to protect children from harmful material."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment free speech clause protects the right to freely communicate information and ideas—even through the Internet. To ensure such protection, a content- based restriction on speech is presumptively unconstitutional and only enforceable if it survives strict scrutiny. This means that the government can only restrict speech based on what is being said (ie, its message or ideas) if it can prove that the restriction is the least restrictive means (ie, narrowly tailored) to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the federal statute targets speech based on its content by prohibiting websites from displaying sexually explicit material on their homepages. The government may have a compelling interest in preventing minors from easily accessing such material. However, less restrictive means are available to achieve this interest—eg, requiring sexually explicit materials to be identified so parents can limit what their children can access. Therefore, the statute cannot survive strict scrutiny, and the prosecution of the website owners is likely unconstitutional. (Choice A) The First Amendment does not protect certain forms of speech, including obscenity. Sexually explicit materials are only considered obscene, and fall outside the First Amendment's protections, in the rare instance when they satisfy the Miller test (not seen here). (Choice B) The government does have power to regulate the Internet, but it must comply with constitutional provisions such as the First Amendment when doing so. (Choice C) The government may punish radio and television stations for broadcasting sexual and offensive speech over the airwaves. That is because the government has extensive power to regulate the broadcast spectrum, which has a limited number of frequencies.\n\nEducational objective: Content-based government regulations of speech—even speech communicated via the Internet—must survive strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Reno v. Am. Civil Lib. Union, 521 U.S. 844, 879 (1997) (holding that the compelling government interest of protecting minors from sexually explicit materials on the Internet can be achieved by means other than a ban).","educationalObjective":"Content-based government regulations of speech—even speech communicated via the Internet—must survive strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Reno v. Am. Civil Lib. Union, 521 U.S. 844, 879 (1997) (holding that the compelling government interest of protecting minors from sexually explicit materials on the Internet can be achieved by means other than a ban).","rule":"Content-based government regulations of speech—even speech communicated via the Internet—must survive strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Are the website owners likely to prevail in this defense?","rule":"Content-based government regulations of speech—even speech communicated via the Internet—must survive strict scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the regulation is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment free speech clause protects the right to freely communicate information and ideas—even through the Internet. To ensure such protection, a content- based restriction on speech is presumptively unconstitutional and only enforceable if it survives strict scrutiny. This means that the government can only restrict speech based on what is being said (ie, its message or ideas) if it can prove that the restriction is the least restrictive means (ie, narrowly tailored) to achieve a compelling government interest. Here, the federal statute targets speech based on its content by prohibiting websites from displaying sexually explicit material on their homepages. The government may have a compelling interest in preventing minors from easily accessing such material. However, less restrictive means are available to achieve this interest—eg, requiring sexually explicit materials to be identified so parents can limit what their children can access. Therefore, the statute cannot survive strict scrutiny, and the prosecution of the website owners is likely unconstitutional. (Choice A) The First Amendment does not protect certain forms of speech, including obscenity. Sexually explicit materials are only considered obscene, and fall outside the First Amendment's protections, in the rare instance when they satisfy the Miller test (not seen here). (Choice B) The government does have power to regulate the Internet, but it must comply with constitutional provisions such as the First Amendment when doing so. (Choice C) The government may punish radio and television stations for broadcasting sexual and offensive speech over the airwaves. That is because the government has extensive power to regulate the broadcast spectrum, which has a limited number of frequencies.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because there are less restrictive means available to protect children from harmful material."},"questionZh":"联邦法规禁止网站在其主页上显示露骨的色情内容。该法规的目的是防止未成年人轻易获取此类材料。几位因违反该法而被起诉的网站所有者均以起诉违宪为由进行辩护。网站所有者是否有可能在这场辩护中获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为露骨的性内容不属于受保护的言论。","B":"不，因为政府拥有广泛的权力来监管互联网。","C":"是的，因为政府可能不会惩罚露骨色情材料的广播。","D":"是的，因为保护儿童免受有害物质侵害的限制性措施较少。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案言论自由条款保护自由交流信息和思想的权利——即使是通过互联网。为了确保这种保护，基于内容的言论限制被推定为违宪，并且只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才能强制执行。这意味着，如果政府能够证明限制是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制性最小的手段（即，狭隘的定制），那么政府只能根据所说的内容（即其信息或想法）来限制言论。在这里，联邦法规通过禁止网站在其主页上显示露骨的色情材料来针对基于内容的言论。政府可能迫切需要阻止未成年人轻易获取此类材料。然而，可以使用限制较少的手段来实现这种兴趣，例如，要求识别露骨的色情材料，以便父母可以限制孩子可以访问的内容。因此，该法规无法经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），对网站所有者的起诉很可能是违宪的。 （选项 A）第一修正案不保护某些形式的言论，包括淫秽言论。露骨的色情材料仅在满足米勒测试的极少数情况下才被视为淫秽，并且不受第一修正案的保护（此处未看到）。 （选项B）政府确实有权力监管互联网，但这样做时必须遵守第一修正案等宪法规定。 （选项C）政府可以惩罚广播电台和电视台通过广播广播性和攻击性言论。这是因为政府拥有广泛的权力来监管广播频谱，而广播频谱的频率数量有限。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的政府言论法规（即使是通过互联网传播的言论）必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这就要求政府证明监管是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n参考文献\n• 里诺诉Am。民事图书馆。 Union, 521 U.S. 844, 879 (1997)（认为保护未成年人免受互联网上色情内容侵害的政府利益可以通过禁令以外的方式实现）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于内容的政府言论监管——即使是通过互联网传播的言论——必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这就要求政府证明监管是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。","iracZh":{"issue":"网站所有者是否有可能在这场辩护中获胜？","rule":"基于内容的政府言论监管——即使是通过互联网传播的言论——必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这就要求政府证明监管是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。","analysis":"第一修正案言论自由条款保护自由交流信息和思想的权利——即使是通过互联网。为了确保这种保护，基于内容的言论限制被推定为违宪，并且只有在经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）的情况下才能强制执行。这意味着，如果政府能够证明限制是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制性最小的手段（即，狭隘的定制），那么政府只能根据所说的内容（即其信息或想法）来限制言论。在这里，联邦法规通过禁止网站在其主页上显示露骨的色情材料来针对基于内容的言论。政府可能迫切需要阻止未成年人轻易获取此类材料。然而，可以使用限制较少的手段来实现这种兴趣，例如，要求识别露骨的色情材料，以便父母可以限制孩子可以访问的内容。因此，该法规无法经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），对网站所有者的起诉很可能是违宪的。 （选项 A）第一修正案不保护某些形式的言论，包括淫秽言论。露骨的色情材料仅在满足米勒测试的极少数情况下才被视为淫秽，并且不受第一修正案的保护（此处未看到）。 （选项B）政府确实有权力监管互联网，但这样做时必须遵守第一修正案等宪法规定。 （选项C）政府可以惩罚广播电台和电视台通过广播广播性和攻击性言论。这是因为政府拥有广泛的权力来监管广播频谱，而广播频谱的频率数量有限。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的政府言论法规（即使是通过互联网传播的言论）必须经受严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这就要求政府证明监管是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。\n\n参考文献\n• 里诺诉Am。民事图书馆。 Union, 521 U.S. 844, 879 (1997)（认为保护未成年人免受互联网上色情内容侵害的政府利益可以通过禁令以外的方式实现）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为保护儿童免受有害物质侵害的限制性措施较少。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-58c77a48304d1e8c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":203,"sourceQuestionPage":606,"sourceQuestionPages":[606],"sourceExplanationPages":[607,608],"sourceAnswerPages":[607,608],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-204","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"To preserve the appearance and accessibility of its capitol building, a state enacted a law prohibiting \"the display of any sign on any portion of the public sidewalk surrounding\" the building. A group of five demonstrators who wanted to protest inadequate state funding for children's services applied for a permit to march single file on the sidewalk surrounding the capitol building. Each demonstrator planned to carry a two-foot-square sign that would read, \"Our lawmakers do not care about our children.\" The group's permit application was denied pursuant to the state law, and the group has filed an action challenging the law's constitutionality. Should the court uphold the law's constitutionality?","choices":{"A":"No, because even though the sidewalk at issue is not a public forum, the prohibition against the display of signs is more restrictive than needed to serve a legitimate government interest.","B":"No, because the sidewalk at issue is a public forum, and the prohibition against the display of signs is not narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest.","C":"Yes, because even though the sidewalk at issue is a public forum, the prohibition against the display of signs is necessary to serve a compelling public interest.","D":"Yes, because the sidewalk at issue is not a public forum, and the prohibition against the display of signs is reasonable."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely communicate information and ideas through speech or conduct—even on government property. But the government can impose reasonable time, place, or manner restrictions (ie, content-neutral regulations) on speech that occurs in a public forum so long as those restrictions pass intermediate scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the restriction:\n• is narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and\n• leaves open ample alternative channels of communication. Here, the group's permit application was denied pursuant to a content-neutral law that prohibits the display of any sign on the sidewalk surrounding the capitol building—a public forum (Choices A & D). And though the state has a substantial interest in preserving the appearance and accessibility of its capitol building, the law is not narrowly tailored to serve that interest. That is because alternative measures that burden substantially less speech could be used effectively—eg, prohibiting signs near the entrance. Therefore, the law is unconstitutional. (Choice C) Content-based restrictions on speech that occurs in a public forum must be necessary and narrowly tailored to serve a compelling government interest (ie, strict scrutiny). But this law is content-neutral.\n\nEducational objective: Content-neutral restrictions on the time, place, or manner of speech in a public forum must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.\n\nReferences\n• Frisby v. Schultz, 487 U.S. 474, 481 (1988) (setting forth the test for determining whether the government's regulation of the time, place, or manner of expression in a public forum is valid).","educationalObjective":"Content-neutral restrictions on the time, place, or manner of speech in a public forum must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","references":"• Frisby v. Schultz, 487 U.S. 474, 481 (1988) (setting forth the test for determining whether the government's regulation of the time, place, or manner of expression in a public forum is valid).","rule":"Content-neutral restrictions on the time, place, or manner of speech in a public forum must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court uphold the law's constitutionality?","rule":"Content-neutral restrictions on the time, place, or manner of speech in a public forum must (1) be narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","analysis":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely communicate information and ideas through speech or conduct—even on government property. But the government can impose reasonable time, place, or manner restrictions (ie, content-neutral regulations) on speech that occurs in a public forum so long as those restrictions pass intermediate scrutiny. This requires the government to prove that the restriction:\n• is narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and\n• leaves open ample alternative channels of communication. Here, the group's permit application was denied pursuant to a content-neutral law that prohibits the display of any sign on the sidewalk surrounding the capitol building—a public forum (Choices A & D). And though the state has a substantial interest in preserving the appearance and accessibility of its capitol building, the law is not narrowly tailored to serve that interest. That is because alternative measures that burden substantially less speech could be used effectively—eg, prohibiting signs near the entrance. Therefore, the law is unconstitutional. (Choice C) Content-based restrictions on speech that occurs in a public forum must be necessary and narrowly tailored to serve a compelling government interest (ie, strict scrutiny). But this law is content-neutral.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the sidewalk at issue is a public forum, and the prohibition against the display of signs is not narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest."},"questionZh":"为了保护国会大厦的外观和可达性，一个州颁布了一项法律，禁止“在建筑物周围的公共人行道的任何部分展示任何标志”。一群五名示威者想要抗议国家对儿童服务的资金不足，他们申请了在国会大厦周围的人行道上排成一列游行的许可证。每个示威者都计划举着一个两英尺见方的标语，上面写着：“我们的立法者不关心我们的孩子。”该组织的许可申请根据州法律被拒绝，该组织已提起诉讼质疑该法律的合宪性。法院是否应该维护法律的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为即使所涉及的人行道不是公共论坛，但禁止展示标志的限制性超出了服务合法政府利益所需的限制性。","B":"不，因为所涉及的人行道是一个公共论坛，禁止展示标志并不是狭隘地为了服务于政府的重大利益而设计的。","C":"是的，因为尽管所涉及的人行道是一个公共论坛，但为了实现引人注目的公共利益，禁止展示标志是必要的。","D":"是的，因为涉及的人行道不是公共论坛，禁止展示标志是合理的。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案保护通过言论或行为自由交流信息和思想的权利——即使是在政府财产上。但政府可以对公共论坛上的言论施加合理的时间、地点或方式限制（即内容中立的规定），只要这些限制通过中间审查即可。这需要政府证明该限制：\n• 专门为服务重大政府利益而定制，并且\n• 留有充足的替代沟通渠道。在这里，根据内容中立的法律，该团体的许可申请被拒绝，该法律禁止在国会大厦周围的人行道上展示任何标志——公共论坛（选择A和D）。尽管该州在保护其国会大厦的外观和可达性方面有着重大利益，但该法律并不是专门为服务于这一利益而制定的。这是因为可以有效地使用显着减少言论负担的替代措施，例如禁止在入口附近设置标志。因此，该法律是违宪的。 （选择C）对公共论坛中发生的言论进行基于内容的限制必须是必要的，并且是严格定制的，以服务于令人信服的政府利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。但该法律是内容中立的。\n\n教育目标：对公共论坛中的时间、地点或言论方式进行内容中立的限制必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益；(2) 留出充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n参考文献\n• Frisby v. Schultz, 487 U.S. 474, 481 (1988)（规定了确定政府对公共论坛表达时间、地点或方式的规定是否有效的检验）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对公共论坛中的时间、地点或言论方式的内容中立的限制必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益；(2) 留出充足的替代沟通渠道。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该维护法律的合宪性？","rule":"对公共论坛中的时间、地点或言论方式的内容中立的限制必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益；(2) 留出充足的替代沟通渠道。","analysis":"第一修正案保护通过言论或行为自由交流信息和思想的权利——即使是在政府财产上。但政府可以对公共论坛上的言论施加合理的时间、地点或方式限制（即内容中立的规定），只要这些限制通过中间审查即可。这需要政府证明该限制：\n• 专门为服务重大政府利益而定制，并且\n• 留有充足的替代沟通渠道。在这里，根据内容中立的法律，该团体的许可申请被拒绝，该法律禁止在国会大厦周围的人行道上展示任何标志——公共论坛（选择A和D）。尽管该州在保护其国会大厦的外观和可达性方面有着重大利益，但该法律并不是专门为服务于这一利益而制定的。这是因为可以有效地使用显着减少言论负担的替代措施，例如禁止在入口附近设置标志。因此，该法律是违宪的。 （选择C）对公共论坛中发生的言论进行基于内容的限制必须是必要的，并且是严格定制的，以服务于令人信服的政府利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。但该法律是内容中立的。\n\n教育目标：对公共论坛中的时间、地点或言论方式进行内容中立的限制必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益；(2) 留出充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n参考文献\n• Frisby v. Schultz, 487 U.S. 474, 481 (1988)（规定了确定政府对公共论坛表达时间、地点或方式的规定是否有效的检验）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为所涉及的人行道是一个公共论坛，禁止展示标志并不是狭隘地为了服务于政府的重大利益而设计的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"lawmakers","ipa":"/lˈɔmˌeɪkɝz/","syllables":"law·mak·ers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 立法者( lawmaker的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 立法者( lawmaker的复数形式 )","wordForm":"名词；原形 lawmaker；词形 0:lawmaker/1:s","sourceSentence":"Each demonstrator planned to carry a two-foot-square sign that would read, \"Our lawmakers do not care about our children.","synonyms":["lawgiver"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-e5bbe5fc0dddbafd"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":204,"sourceQuestionPage":609,"sourceQuestionPages":[609],"sourceExplanationPages":[610,611],"sourceAnswerPages":[610,611],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-205","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"The United States government demonstrated that terrorist attacks involving commercial airliners were perpetrated exclusively by individuals of one particular race. In response, Congress enacted a statute imposing stringent new airport and airline security measures only on individuals of that race seeking to board airplanes in the United States. Which of the following provides the best ground for challenging the constitutionality of this statute?","choices":{"A":"The commerce clause of Article I, Section 8.","B":"The due process clause of the Fifth Amendment.","C":"The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV.","D":"The privileges or immunities clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A law that treats similarly situated persons differently may violate the constitutional principle of equal protection, which applies to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause. This is especially likely when the law intentionally discriminates against a suspect class (eg, race). That is because the government must clear the highest hurdle—strict scrutiny—to justify such a law. And since this federal statute intentionally targets a particular race, due process provides the best ground to challenge the statute. *The equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment applies only to states and localities—not the federal government. (Choice A) The commerce clause grants Congress broad plenary power to regulate the instrumentalities of interstate commerce—eg, planes, trains, automobiles. Therefore, this clause supports Congress's authority to regulate airports and airlines. (Choice C) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states. But it does not apply to the federal government. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause applies to states— not the federal government—and bars them from depriving individuals of the very limited rights of national citizenship.\n\nEducational objective: Equal protection principles apply to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause. And under this clause, federal laws that intentionally discriminate against a suspect class (eg, race) are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.\n\nReferences\n• Bolling v. Sharpe, 347 U.S. 497, 499–500 (1954) (holding that equal protection principles apply to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause).","educationalObjective":"Equal protection principles apply to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause. And under this clause, federal laws that intentionally discriminate against a suspect class (eg, race) are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.","references":"• Bolling v. Sharpe, 347 U.S. 497, 499–500 (1954) (holding that equal protection principles apply to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause).","rule":"Equal protection principles apply to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause. And under this clause, federal laws that intentionally discriminate against a suspect class (eg, race) are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following provides the best ground for challenging the constitutionality of this statute?","rule":"Equal protection principles apply to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause. And under this clause, federal laws that intentionally discriminate against a suspect class (eg, race) are almost always invalidated under strict scrutiny.","analysis":"A law that treats similarly situated persons differently may violate the constitutional principle of equal protection, which applies to the federal government through the Fifth Amendment due process clause. This is especially likely when the law intentionally discriminates against a suspect class (eg, race). That is because the government must clear the highest hurdle—strict scrutiny—to justify such a law. And since this federal statute intentionally targets a particular race, due process provides the best ground to challenge the statute. *The equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment applies only to states and localities—not the federal government. (Choice A) The commerce clause grants Congress broad plenary power to regulate the instrumentalities of interstate commerce—eg, planes, trains, automobiles. Therefore, this clause supports Congress's authority to regulate airports and airlines. (Choice C) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states. But it does not apply to the federal government. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause applies to states— not the federal government—and bars them from depriving individuals of the very limited rights of national citizenship.","conclusion":"Answer B: The due process clause of the Fifth Amendment."},"questionZh":"美国政府证明，涉及商业航班的恐怖袭击完全是由某一特定种族的个人实施的。作为回应，国会颁布了一项法规，仅对寻求在美国登机的该种族个人实施严格的新机场和航空公司安全措施。以下哪一项为质疑该法规的合宪性提供了最佳依据？","choicesZh":{"A":"第一条第 8 节的商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）。","B":"第五修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","C":"第四条的特权和豁免条款。","D":"第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款。"},"explanationZh":"对处境相似的人区别对待的法律可能违反平等保护的宪法原则，该原则通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于联邦政府。当法律故意歧视可疑阶层（例如种族）时，这种情况尤其可能发生。这是因为政府必须清除最高的障碍——严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）——才能证明这样一项法律的合理性。由于这项联邦法规有意针对特定种族，因此正当程序为挑战该法规提供了最佳基础。 *第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）仅适用于州和地方，不适用于联邦政府。 （选项A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的全权来监管州际商业工具——例如飞机、火车、汽车。因此，该条款支持国会监管机场和航空公司的权力。 （选项C） 第四条特权和豁免条款一般禁止国家歧视其他国家的公民。但它不适用于联邦政府。 （选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款适用于各州，而不是联邦政府，并禁止它们剥夺个人非常有限的国家公民权利。\n\n教育目标：平等保护原则通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于联邦政府。根据该条款，故意歧视可疑阶层（例如种族）的联邦法律在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是无效。\n\n参考文献\n• Bolling v. Sharpe, 347 U.S. 497, 499–500 (1954)（认为平等保护原则通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于联邦政府）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），平等保护原则适用于联邦政府。根据该条款，故意歧视可疑阶层（例如种族）的联邦法律在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项为质疑该法规的合宪性提供了最佳依据？","rule":"通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause），平等保护原则适用于联邦政府。根据该条款，故意歧视可疑阶层（例如种族）的联邦法律在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是无效。","analysis":"对处境相似的人区别对待的法律可能违反平等保护的宪法原则，该原则通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于联邦政府。当法律故意歧视可疑阶层（例如种族）时，这种情况尤其可能发生。这是因为政府必须清除最高的障碍——严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）——才能证明这样一项法律的合理性。由于这项联邦法规有意针对特定种族，因此正当程序为挑战该法规提供了最佳基础。 *第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）仅适用于州和地方，不适用于联邦政府。 （选项A） 商业条款（Commerce Clause）（Commerce Clause）赋予国会广泛的全权来监管州际商业工具——例如飞机、火车、汽车。因此，该条款支持国会监管机场和航空公司的权力。 （选项C） 第四条特权和豁免条款一般禁止国家歧视其他国家的公民。但它不适用于联邦政府。 （选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款适用于各州，而不是联邦政府，并禁止它们剥夺个人非常有限的国家公民权利。\n\n教育目标：平等保护原则通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于联邦政府。根据该条款，故意歧视可疑阶层（例如种族）的联邦法律在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下几乎总是无效。\n\n参考文献\n• Bolling v. Sharpe, 347 U.S. 497, 499–500 (1954)（认为平等保护原则通过第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于联邦政府）。","conclusion":"答案 B：第五修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-b2efd6b666e25885"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":205,"sourceQuestionPage":612,"sourceQuestionPages":[612],"sourceExplanationPages":[613,614],"sourceAnswerPages":[613,614],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-206","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A federal statute provides health care benefits to U.S. citizens but excludes noncitizens. A group of resident noncitizens who were denied health care benefits pursuant to the statute brought a suit in federal court challenging the statute on constitutional grounds. The group specifically claims that the statute violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment due process clause. Is the federal statute likely constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the statute is not necessary to achieve an important government interest.","B":"No, because the statute is not substantially related to an important government interest.","C":"Yes, because the statute is necessary to achieve a legitimate government interest.","D":"Yes, because the statute is rationally related to a legitimate government interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Congress has plenary (ie, exclusive) authority over immigration and naturalization, so federal laws based on citizenship are presumptively constitutional. To rebut this presumption and show that such a law violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment, the challenger must show that the law is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest—ie, that the law is arbitrary or unreasonable. Here, the federal statute provides health care benefits to U.S. citizens but excludes noncitizens. The U.S. Supreme Court has repeatedly held that Congress has a legitimate interest in providing benefits only to citizens because U.S. citizens have closer ties to the U.S. than noncitizens. Since the federal statute is rationally related to this interest, the statute is likely constitutional and the group of resident noncitizens is unlikely to prevail. (Choices A & C) There is no level of scrutiny that requires the government to show that its statute is not necessary to achieve an important government interest or is necessary to achieve a legitimate government interest. Instead, strict scrutiny (not applicable here) requires the government to show that its statute is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. (Choice B) Intermediate scrutiny requires a government to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest when such an action discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: The equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment requires federal laws based on citizenship to satisfy rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny places the burden on the challenger to prove that the law is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 4 (federal immigration and naturalization power).\n\n• Mathews v. Diaz, 426 U.S. 67, 79–80 (1976) (explaining that Congress may provide benefits to citizens and exclude noncitizens).","educationalObjective":"The equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment requires federal laws based on citizenship to satisfy rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny places the burden on the challenger to prove that the law is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 8, cl. 4 (federal immigration and naturalization power).\n\n• Mathews v. Diaz, 426 U.S. 67, 79–80 (1976) (explaining that Congress may provide benefits to citizens and exclude noncitizens).","rule":"The equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment requires federal laws based on citizenship to satisfy rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny places the burden on the challenger to prove that the law is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the federal statute likely constitutional?","rule":"The equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment requires federal laws based on citizenship to satisfy rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny places the burden on the challenger to prove that the law is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","analysis":"Congress has plenary (ie, exclusive) authority over immigration and naturalization, so federal laws based on citizenship are presumptively constitutional. To rebut this presumption and show that such a law violates the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment, the challenger must show that the law is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest—ie, that the law is arbitrary or unreasonable. Here, the federal statute provides health care benefits to U.S. citizens but excludes noncitizens. The U.S. Supreme Court has repeatedly held that Congress has a legitimate interest in providing benefits only to citizens because U.S. citizens have closer ties to the U.S. than noncitizens. Since the federal statute is rationally related to this interest, the statute is likely constitutional and the group of resident noncitizens is unlikely to prevail. (Choices A & C) There is no level of scrutiny that requires the government to show that its statute is not necessary to achieve an important government interest or is necessary to achieve a legitimate government interest. Instead, strict scrutiny (not applicable here) requires the government to show that its statute is necessary to achieve a compelling state interest. (Choice B) Intermediate scrutiny requires a government to show that its action is substantially related to an important government interest when such an action discriminates against a quasi-suspect class (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the statute is rationally related to a legitimate government interest."},"questionZh":"联邦法规向美国公民提供医疗保健福利，但不包括非公民。一群根据该法规被剥夺医疗保健福利的居民非公民向联邦法院提起诉讼，以宪法为由质疑该法规。该组织特别声称该法规违反了第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中的平等保护部分。联邦法规可能符合宪法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该法规并不是实现重要政府利益所必需的。","B":"不，因为该法规与重要的政府利益没有实质性关系。","C":"是的，因为该法规对于实现合法的政府利益是必要的。","D":"是的，因为该法规与合法的政府利益合理相关。"},"explanationZh":"国会对移民和入籍拥有全权（即排他性）权力，因此基于公民身份的联邦法律被推定为符合宪法。为了反驳这一推定并表明这样的法律违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分，挑战者必须证明该法律与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系，即该法律是任意的或不合理的。在这里，联邦法规向美国公民提供医疗保健福利，但不包括非公民。美国最高法院一再认为国会只向公民提供福利具有合法利益，因为美国公民比非公民与美国的联系更紧密。由于联邦法规与这一利益合理相关，因此该法规很可能符合宪法，而居民非公民群体不太可能占上风。 （选项 A 和 C）没有任何级别的审查要求政府证明其法规对于实现重要的政府利益不是必要的，或者对于实现合法的政府利益是必要的。相反，严格的审查（此处不适用）要求政府证明其法规对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。 （选择B）中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）要求政府证明，当其行为歧视准可疑阶层（此处未看到）时，其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。\n\n教育目标：第五修正案的平等保护部分要求基于公民身份的联邦法律满足合理基础的审查。这种程度的审查让挑战者有责任证明该法律与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 4（联邦移民和入籍权）。\n\n• Mathews v. Diaz, 426 U.S. 67, 79–80 (1976)（解释国会可以向公民提供福利并排除非公民）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第五修正案的平等保护部分要求基于公民身份的联邦法律满足合理基础的审查。这种程度的审查让挑战者有责任证明该法律与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"联邦法规可能符合宪法吗？","rule":"第五修正案的平等保护部分要求基于公民身份的联邦法律满足合理基础的审查。这种程度的审查让挑战者有责任证明该法律与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。","analysis":"国会对移民和入籍拥有全权（即排他性）权力，因此基于公民身份的联邦法律被推定为符合宪法。为了反驳这一推定并表明这样的法律违反了第五修正案的平等保护部分，挑战者必须证明该法律与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系，即该法律是任意的或不合理的。在这里，联邦法规向美国公民提供医疗保健福利，但不包括非公民。美国最高法院一再认为国会只向公民提供福利具有合法利益，因为美国公民比非公民与美国的联系更紧密。由于联邦法规与这一利益合理相关，因此该法规很可能符合宪法，而居民非公民群体不太可能占上风。 （选项 A 和 C）没有任何级别的审查要求政府证明其法规对于实现重要的政府利益不是必要的，或者对于实现合法的政府利益是必要的。相反，严格的审查（此处不适用）要求政府证明其法规对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的。 （选择B）中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）要求政府证明，当其行为歧视准可疑阶层（此处未看到）时，其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。\n\n教育目标：第五修正案的平等保护部分要求基于公民身份的联邦法律满足合理基础的审查。这种程度的审查让挑战者有责任证明该法律与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 8，cl。 4（联邦移民和入籍权）。\n\n• Mathews v. Diaz, 426 U.S. 67, 79–80 (1976)（解释国会可以向公民提供福利并排除非公民）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该法规与合法的政府利益合理相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0acbaed72d2e7de4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":206,"sourceQuestionPage":615,"sourceQuestionPages":[615],"sourceExplanationPages":[616,617],"sourceAnswerPages":[616,617],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-207","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state wanted to prevent its only major league baseball team, which was privately owned and operated, from moving to a rival state. After a heated political debate in the legislature, the state enacted legislation providing for a one-time grant of $10 million in state funds to the team to cover part of the projected income losses the team would suffer during the next five years if it remained in that state. The legislation required that the team remain in the state for at least ten years if it accepted the grant. After accepting the grant, the team owners decided to build a new $150 million stadium in the state. As plans for the construction of the new stadium proceeded, it became evident that all of the contractors and subcontractors would be white males, and that they had been chosen by the team owners without any public bids because these contractors and subcontractors had successfully built the only other new baseball stadium in the region. Several contractors who were females or members of minority racial groups filed suit against the team owners in federal district court to compel public solicitation of bids for the construction of the team's new stadium on an equal opportunity basis, and to enjoin construction of the stadium until compliance was ensured. Their only claim was that the contracting practices of the team owners denied them the equal protection of the laws in violation of the Fourteenth Amendment. In this suit, what will the court probably rule?","choices":{"A":"In the absence of additional evidence of state involvement in the operations or decisions of the team owners, a one-time grant of state monies to them is insufficient to warrant treating their actions as subject to the limitations of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"The intense public preoccupation with the activities of major league baseball teams coupled with the fact that baseball is considered a national pastime is sufficient to justify application of the Fourteenth Amendment to the activities of major league teams.","C":"The issues presented by this case are nonjusticiable political questions because there is a lack of judicially manageable standards to resolve them and the court is likely to be deeply involved in partisan politics.","D":"The nexus between the actions of the team owners and the one-time grant of monies to them by the state is sufficiently substantial to subject their actions to the limitations of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control The Constitution generally limits government actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is considered a government actor—and similarly bound by the Constitution—when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Therefore, the contractors can only challenge the private baseball team owners' discriminatory contracting practices on equal protection grounds if the state-action doctrine applies. Here, the team owners are not performing a traditional and exclusive government function by building a baseball stadium. And though they received a one-time grant of state funds, government funding—however substantial—is insufficient to convert private conduct into state action (Choice D). Therefore, in the absence of additional evidence of significant state involvement in the team owners' operations or decisions, the court will probably rule that the team owners' actions are not subject to the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice B) Although major league baseball is a national pastime that intensely preoccupies the public, it is not a traditional government function that would trigger the state-action doctrine. (Choice C) The political-question doctrine bars federal courts from hearing cases that present issues that (1) are reserved for the executive or legislative branches or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since courts often resolve equal protection claims involving discriminatory executive or legislative policies—without becoming involved in partisan politics—this doctrine does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: The Constitution generally applies to government actors—not private actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor will be treated as a government actor when (1) the\n\nprivate actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.\n\nReferences\n• Brentwood Acad. v. Tenn. Secondary Sch. Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 296–98 (2001) (listing scenarios in which the state-action doctrine is applied).","educationalObjective":"The Constitution generally applies to government actors—not private actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor will be treated as a government actor when (1) the\n\nprivate actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.","references":"• Brentwood Acad. v. Tenn. Secondary Sch. Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 296–98 (2001) (listing scenarios in which the state-action doctrine is applied).","rule":"The Constitution generally applies to government actors—not private actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor will be treated as a government actor when (1) the\n\nprivate actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.","irac":{"issue":"In this suit, what will the court probably rule?","rule":"The Constitution generally applies to government actors—not private actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor will be treated as a government actor when (1) the\n\nprivate actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.","analysis":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control The Constitution generally limits government actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is considered a government actor—and similarly bound by the Constitution—when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Therefore, the contractors can only challenge the private baseball team owners' discriminatory contracting practices on equal protection grounds if the state-action doctrine applies. Here, the team owners are not performing a traditional and exclusive government function by building a baseball stadium. And though they received a one-time grant of state funds, government funding—however substantial—is insufficient to convert private conduct into state action (Choice D). Therefore, in the absence of additional evidence of significant state involvement in the team owners' operations or decisions, the court will probably rule that the team owners' actions are not subject to the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice B) Although major league baseball is a national pastime that intensely preoccupies the public, it is not a traditional government function that would trigger the state-action doctrine. (Choice C) The political-question doctrine bars federal courts from hearing cases that present issues that (1) are reserved for the executive or legislative branches or (2) lack judicially manageable standards for resolution. But since courts often resolve equal protection claims involving discriminatory executive or legislative policies—without becoming involved in partisan politics—this doctrine does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer A: In the absence of additional evidence of state involvement in the operations or decisions of the team owners, a one-time grant of state monies to them is insufficient to warrant treating their actions as subject to the limitations of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"一个州希望阻止其唯一的私人拥有和经营的大联盟棒球队搬到竞争对手的州。经过立法机关激烈的政治辩论后，该州颁布了一项立法，规定向该球队一次性拨款 1000 万美元的州资金，以弥补该球队如果留在该州，未来五年将遭受的部分收入损失。立法要求，如果团队接受资助，则必须在该州停留至少十年。接受拨款后，球队老板决定在该州建造一座耗资 1.5 亿美元的新体育场。随着新体育场建设计划的推进，很明显，所有承包商和分包商都将是白人男性，而且他们是由球队老板在没有任何公开招标的情况下选择的，因为这些承包商和分包商成功建造了该地区唯一的另一个新棒球场。几名女性或少数族裔承包商向联邦地区法院对球队所有者提起诉讼，要求在机会均等的基础上对球队新体育场的建设进行公开招标，并禁止体育场的建设，直到确保合规为止。他们唯一的主张是，球队老板的承包做法剥夺了他们受到法律的平等保护，违反了第十四修正案。在这起诉讼中，法院可能会做出什么裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"在没有更多证据证明国家参与球队所有者的运营或决策的情况下，一次性向他们提供国家资金不足以保证他们的行为受到第十四修正案的限制。","B":"公众对大联盟棒球队活动的强烈关注，加上棒球被视为全国性消遣的事实，足以证明第十四修正案适用于大联盟球队的活动。","C":"本案提出的问题是不可由司法管辖的政治问题，因为缺乏司法上可管理的标准来解决这些问题，而且法院很可能深深卷入党派政治。","D":"球队老板的行为与国家一次性向他们提供资金之间的联系足以使他们的行为受到第十四修正案的限制。"},"explanationZh":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍参与私人实体的管理或控制 宪法通常限制政府行为者。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者，并同样受到宪法的约束。因此，只有在国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用的情况下，承包商才能以平等保护为由质疑私人棒球队所有者的歧视性承包做法。在这里，球队所有者并没有通过建造棒球场来履行传统的、排他性的政府职能。尽管他们获得了国家资金的一次性补助，但政府资金——无论数额如何——不足以将私人行为转化为国家行为（state action）（选择D）。因此，在没有更多证据表明国家大量参与球队老板的运营或决策的情况下，法院可能会裁定球队老板的行为不受第十四修正案的约束。 （选项 B） 尽管美国职业棒球大联盟是一项深受公众关注的全国性消遣活动，但它并不是触发国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）的传统政府职能。 （选项 C）政治问题原则禁止联邦法院审理以下问题的案件：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于法院经常解决涉及歧视性行政或立法政策的平等保护主张，而不会卷入党派政治，因此这一原则并不适用。\n\n教育目标：宪法一般适用于政府行为者，而不是私人行为者。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行为者将被视为政府行为者：\n\n私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或者 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。\n\n参考文献\n• 布伦特伍德学院。诉田纳西州中学。 Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 296–98 (2001)（列出应用国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）的情景）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"宪法一般适用于政府行为者，而不是私人行为者。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行为者将被视为政府行为者：\n\n私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或者 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这起诉讼中，法院可能会做出什么裁决？","rule":"宪法一般适用于政府行为者，而不是私人行为者。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行为者将被视为政府行为者：\n\n私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或者 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。","analysis":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍参与私人实体的管理或控制 宪法通常限制政府行为者。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者，并同样受到宪法的约束。因此，只有在国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用的情况下，承包商才能以平等保护为由质疑私人棒球队所有者的歧视性承包做法。在这里，球队所有者并没有通过建造棒球场来履行传统的、排他性的政府职能。尽管他们获得了国家资金的一次性补助，但政府资金——无论数额如何——不足以将私人行为转化为国家行为（state action）（选择D）。因此，在没有更多证据表明国家大量参与球队老板的运营或决策的情况下，法院可能会裁定球队老板的行为不受第十四修正案的约束。 （选项 B） 尽管美国职业棒球大联盟是一项深受公众关注的全国性消遣活动，但它并不是触发国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）的传统政府职能。 （选项 C）政治问题原则禁止联邦法院审理以下问题的案件：(1) 保留给行政或立法部门，或 (2) 缺乏司法上可管理的解决标准。但由于法院经常解决涉及歧视性行政或立法政策的平等保护主张，而不会卷入党派政治，因此这一原则并不适用。\n\n教育目标：宪法一般适用于政府行为者，而不是私人行为者。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行为者将被视为政府行为者：\n\n私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或者 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。\n\n参考文献\n• 布伦特伍德学院。诉田纳西州中学。 Athletic Ass'n, 531 U.S. 288, 296–98 (2001)（列出应用国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）的情景）。","conclusion":"答案 A：在没有更多证据证明国家参与球队所有者的运营或决策的情况下，一次性向他们提供国家资金不足以保证他们的行为受到第十四修正案的限制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":207,"sourceQuestionPage":618,"sourceQuestionPages":[618],"sourceExplanationPages":[619,620],"sourceAnswerPages":[619,620],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-208","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Taxing & Spending Powers","question":"Congress enacted a federal statute that awards funds to nongovernmental organizations in the United States and around the world to combat the spread of a deadly airborne virus. The statute requires that the funds be used strictly for research and testing related to the virus. The last section of the statute conditions the award of the funds on the recipient's agreement to publicly announce its support for free-trade policies. A U.S. nongovernmental organization that is headquartered abroad qualifies for funding under the federal statute. The organization does not want to publicly support free-trade policies because it believes that such support will alienate certain foreign governments with which the organization works. As a result, the organization has filed suit in federal court, seeking a declaratory judgment that the statute's condition requiring the announcement of support for free-trade policies compels speech and is therefore unconstitutional as applied to it. How should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"Declare that the condition is constitutional, because Congress may compel speech by imposing conditions on the receipt of federal funds.","B":"Declare that the condition is constitutional, because the organization is headquartered abroad and therefore lacks constitutional protections.","C":"Declare that the condition is unconstitutional, because Congress cannot impose conditions on the receipt of federal funds.","D":"Declare that the condition is unconstitutional, because the condition infringes upon the organization's First Amendment right to free expression."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Conditions on government funding\n\nFunded Government can restrict use \"Grant monies must be used for the activities of funds to authorized acquisition, construction, and maintenance purposes of appropriate performance facilities\"\n\nFunding Government cannot restrict \"Recipient must agree to refrain from all recipients protected conduct unrelated kinds of political lobbying calculated to to funding's purpose secure additional tax support for the performing arts\"\n\nThe taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to spend federal revenue for the general welfare. Congress may place conditions on the receipt of federal funds so long as those conditions comply with the U.S. Constitution—including the First Amendment right to free expression (Choice C). The success of a First Amendment challenge to a conditional spending provision depends on whether the condition is placed on:\n• funded activities – in which case the government can insist that the funds be used for authorized purposes (eg, requiring that funds be used only for research and testing) or\n\n• funding recipients – in which case the government cannot restrict the recipient's right to engage in constitutionally protected conduct that falls outside the funding's purpose or scope (Choice A).\n\nHere, the federal statute places a condition on funding recipients by requiring them to publicly support a policy promoting free trade. This falls outside of the funding's purpose and scope to combat the virus and infringes upon the U.S. organization's First Amendment right to free expression. Therefore, this requirement is unconstitutional as applied to the organization. (Choice B) A constitutional challenge can be raised by a U.S. citizen located anywhere in the world or a foreign citizen located within U.S. territories—no one else possesses rights under the Constitution. Therefore, this U.S. organization headquartered abroad is protected by the Constitution.\n\nEducational objective: Congress can insist that federal funds be used for an authorized purpose, but it cannot restrict a funding recipient's right to engage in constitutionally protected conduct that is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.\n\nReferences\n• Agency for Int'l Dev. v. All. for Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013) (holding that a federal funding program violated the First Amendment by conditioning funds on requirements unrelated to the program's purpose).\n\n• Agency for Int'l Dev. v. All. for Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 140 S. Ct. 2082, 2086 (2020) (\"Foreign citizens outside U.S. territory do not possess rights under the U.S. Constitution\").","educationalObjective":"Congress can insist that federal funds be used for an authorized purpose, but it cannot restrict a funding recipient's right to engage in constitutionally protected conduct that is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.","references":"• Agency for Int'l Dev. v. All. for Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013) (holding that a federal funding program violated the First Amendment by conditioning funds on requirements unrelated to the program's purpose).\n\n• Agency for Int'l Dev. v. All. for Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 140 S. Ct. 2082, 2086 (2020) (\"Foreign citizens outside U.S. territory do not possess rights under the U.S. Constitution\").","rule":"Congress can insist that federal funds be used for an authorized purpose, but it cannot restrict a funding recipient's right to engage in constitutionally protected conduct that is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule?","rule":"Congress can insist that federal funds be used for an authorized purpose, but it cannot restrict a funding recipient's right to engage in constitutionally protected conduct that is unrelated to the funding's purpose or scope.","analysis":"Conditions on government funding\n\nFunded Government can restrict use \"Grant monies must be used for the activities of funds to authorized acquisition, construction, and maintenance purposes of appropriate performance facilities\"\n\nFunding Government cannot restrict \"Recipient must agree to refrain from all recipients protected conduct unrelated kinds of political lobbying calculated to to funding's purpose secure additional tax support for the performing arts\"\n\nThe taxing and spending clause gives Congress broad power to spend federal revenue for the general welfare. Congress may place conditions on the receipt of federal funds so long as those conditions comply with the U.S. Constitution—including the First Amendment right to free expression (Choice C). The success of a First Amendment challenge to a conditional spending provision depends on whether the condition is placed on:\n• funded activities – in which case the government can insist that the funds be used for authorized purposes (eg, requiring that funds be used only for research and testing) or\n\n• funding recipients – in which case the government cannot restrict the recipient's right to engage in constitutionally protected conduct that falls outside the funding's purpose or scope (Choice A).\n\nHere, the federal statute places a condition on funding recipients by requiring them to publicly support a policy promoting free trade. This falls outside of the funding's purpose and scope to combat the virus and infringes upon the U.S. organization's First Amendment right to free expression. Therefore, this requirement is unconstitutional as applied to the organization. (Choice B) A constitutional challenge can be raised by a U.S. citizen located anywhere in the world or a foreign citizen located within U.S. territories—no one else possesses rights under the Constitution. Therefore, this U.S. organization headquartered abroad is protected by the Constitution.","conclusion":"Answer D: Declare that the condition is unconstitutional, because the condition infringes upon the organization's First Amendment right to free expression."},"questionZh":"国会颁布了一项联邦法规，向美国和世界各地的非政府组织提供资金，以对抗致命的空气传播病毒的传播。法规要求资金严格用于与病毒相关的研究和检测。法规的最后一部分规定，在接受者同意公开宣布其对自由贸易政策的支持的情况下授予资金。总部位于国外的美国非政府组织有资格根据联邦法规获得资助。该组织不想公开支持自由贸易政策，因为它认为这种支持会疏远与该组织合作的某些外国政府。因此，该组织已向联邦法院提起诉讼，寻求宣告性判决，认定该法规中要求宣布支持自由贸易政策的条件强制言论，因此适用于该法规是违宪的。法院应该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"宣布该条件符合宪法，因为国会可以通过对联邦资金的接收施加条件来强迫言论。","B":"声明该条件符合宪法，因为该组织总部设在国外，因此缺乏宪法保护。","C":"宣布该条件违宪，因为国会不能对联邦资金的接收施加条件。","D":"宣布该条件违宪，因为该条件侵犯了该组织的第一修正案自由表达权。"},"explanationZh":"政府资助的条件\n\n受资助的政府可以限制使用“赠款必须用于资金活动，以授权购买、建设和维护适当的表演设施”\n\n资助政府不能限制“受资助者必须同意避免所有受保护的受资助者进行与资助目的无关的政治游说活动，以确保对表演艺术的额外税收支持”\n\n税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力，可以将联邦收入用于公共福利。国会可以对联邦资金的接收设定条件，只要这些条件符合美国宪法，包括第一修正案的言论自由权（选项 C）。第一修正案对有条件支出条款的质疑能否成功取决于该条件是否满足：\n• 资助的活动——在这种情况下，政府可以坚持将资金用于授权目的（例如，要求资金仅用于研究和测试）或\n\n• 资助接受者——在这种情况下，政府不能限制接受者参与超出资助目的或范围的受宪法保护的行为的权利（选择A）。\n\n在这里，联邦法规对资助者设定了一个条件，要求他们公开支持促进自由贸易的政策。这不属于抗击病毒的资金目的和范围，并且侵犯了美国组织第一修正案的言论自由权。因此，这一要求应用于该组织是违宪的。 （选项 B）位于世界任何地方的美国公民或位于美国领土内的外国公民都可以提出宪法质疑——其他人都不拥有宪法规定的权利。因此，这个总部设在国外的美国组织受到宪法的保护。\n\n教育目标：国会可以坚持联邦资金用于授权目的，但不能限制资金接受者从事与资金目的或范围无关的受宪法保护的行为的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• 国际开发机构。 v.全部。 Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013)（认为联邦资助计划违反了第一修正案，因为资金条件与计划目的无关）。\n\n• 国际开发机构。 v.全部。 Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 140 S. Ct. 2082, 2086 (2020)（“美国境外的外国公民不享有美国宪法规定的权利”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"国会可以坚持联邦资金用于授权目的，但不能限制资金接受者从事与资金目的或范围无关的受宪法保护的行为的权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该如何判决？","rule":"国会可以坚持联邦资金用于授权目的，但不能限制资金接受者从事与资金目的或范围无关的受宪法保护的行为的权利。","analysis":"政府资助的条件\n\n受资助的政府可以限制使用“赠款必须用于资金活动，以授权购买、建设和维护适当的表演设施”\n\n资助政府不能限制“受资助者必须同意避免所有受保护的受资助者进行与资助目的无关的政治游说活动，以确保对表演艺术的额外税收支持”\n\n税收和支出条款赋予国会广泛的权力，可以将联邦收入用于公共福利。国会可以对联邦资金的接收设定条件，只要这些条件符合美国宪法，包括第一修正案的言论自由权（选项 C）。第一修正案对有条件支出条款的质疑能否成功取决于该条件是否满足：\n• 资助的活动——在这种情况下，政府可以坚持将资金用于授权目的（例如，要求资金仅用于研究和测试）或\n\n• 资助接受者——在这种情况下，政府不能限制接受者参与超出资助目的或范围的受宪法保护的行为的权利（选择A）。\n\n在这里，联邦法规对资助者设定了一个条件，要求他们公开支持促进自由贸易的政策。这不属于抗击病毒的资金目的和范围，并且侵犯了美国组织第一修正案的言论自由权。因此，这一要求应用于该组织是违宪的。 （选项 B）位于世界任何地方的美国公民或位于美国领土内的外国公民都可以提出宪法质疑——其他人都不拥有宪法规定的权利。因此，这个总部设在国外的美国组织受到宪法的保护。\n\n教育目标：国会可以坚持联邦资金用于授权目的，但不能限制资金接受者从事与资金目的或范围无关的受宪法保护的行为的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• 国际开发机构。 v.全部。 Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 570 U.S. 205, 221 (2013)（认为联邦资助计划违反了第一修正案，因为资金条件与计划目的无关）。\n\n• 国际开发机构。 v.全部。 Open Soc'y Int'l, Inc., 140 S. Ct. 2082, 2086 (2020)（“美国境外的外国公民不享有美国宪法规定的权利”）。","conclusion":"答案 D：宣布该条件违宪，因为该条件侵犯了该组织的第一修正案自由表达权。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":208,"sourceQuestionPage":621,"sourceQuestionPages":[621],"sourceExplanationPages":[622,623],"sourceAnswerPages":[622,623],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-209","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"A state statute permits the termination of parental rights when the state proves by a preponderance of the evidence that a child has been a victim of neglect. A state agency initiated a proceeding in state court to terminate a woman's parental rights based on allegations that she repeatedly abused her child. The agency provided the woman with adequate notice of the hearing. At the hearing, the attorneys for the agency and the woman presented evidence, examined witnesses, and made opening and closing statements. The court found by a preponderance of the evidence that the child was a victim of neglect and entered an order terminating the woman's parental rights. The woman has appealed the court's order, alleging that the termination of her parental rights was unconstitutional. How should the appellate court rule in this case?","choices":{"A":"Affirm the order, because a court can terminate parental rights by using a preponderance-of-the-evidence burden of proof.","B":"Affirm the order, because the state's interest in the child's welfare outweighs the woman's interest in the burden of proof used at the proceeding.","C":"Reverse the order, because the statute denies the woman a right of national citizenship.","D":"Reverse the order, because the statute violates due process."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A state must provide procedural due process before depriving an individual of a life, liberty, or property interest (eg, parental rights). This requires that the government provide reasonable notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard before a neutral decision-maker—as was provided to the woman here. Procedural due process also requires that the proper burden of proof be applied at such hearings. To determine the proper burden (and any other process due), the court must balance three factors:\n• The private interest affected by the deprivation\n\n• The risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest through current procedures and the probable value of additional or substitute procedural safeguards\n\n• The government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail\n\nHere, the woman has an interest in maintaining her parental rights, while the state has an interest in ensuring the child's welfare. However, there is a high risk of erroneously terminating parental rights since the state need only prove its case by a preponderance of the evidence. This risk can be mitigated by requiring the state to satisfy a higher burden of proof (eg, clear and convincing evidence). Therefore, the statute violates due process, and the appellate court should reverse the order terminating the woman's parental rights (Choices A & B). (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from denying citizens a right of national citizenship, which does not include parental rights.\n\nEducational objective: A state seeking to terminate parental rights must prove its case by at least clear and convincing evidence to comply with procedural due process.\n\nReferences\n• Santosky v. Kramer, 455 U.S. 745, 768–69 (1982) (holding that the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires states to prove their case by at least clear and convincing evidence when terminating parental rights).\n\n• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976) (listing the three factors used to assess compliance with procedural due process).","educationalObjective":"A state seeking to terminate parental rights must prove its case by at least clear and convincing evidence to comply with procedural due process.","references":"• Santosky v. Kramer, 455 U.S. 745, 768–69 (1982) (holding that the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires states to prove their case by at least clear and convincing evidence when terminating parental rights).\n\n• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976) (listing the three factors used to assess compliance with procedural due process).","rule":"A state seeking to terminate parental rights must prove its case by at least clear and convincing evidence to comply with procedural due process.","irac":{"issue":"How should the appellate court rule in this case?","rule":"A state seeking to terminate parental rights must prove its case by at least clear and convincing evidence to comply with procedural due process.","analysis":"A state must provide procedural due process before depriving an individual of a life, liberty, or property interest (eg, parental rights). This requires that the government provide reasonable notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard before a neutral decision-maker—as was provided to the woman here. Procedural due process also requires that the proper burden of proof be applied at such hearings. To determine the proper burden (and any other process due), the court must balance three factors:\n• The private interest affected by the deprivation\n\n• The risk of erroneous deprivation of that interest through current procedures and the probable value of additional or substitute procedural safeguards\n\n• The government's interest, including the fiscal and administrative burdens that other safeguards would entail\n\nHere, the woman has an interest in maintaining her parental rights, while the state has an interest in ensuring the child's welfare. However, there is a high risk of erroneously terminating parental rights since the state need only prove its case by a preponderance of the evidence. This risk can be mitigated by requiring the state to satisfy a higher burden of proof (eg, clear and convincing evidence). Therefore, the statute violates due process, and the appellate court should reverse the order terminating the woman's parental rights (Choices A & B). (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from denying citizens a right of national citizenship, which does not include parental rights.","conclusion":"Answer D: Reverse the order, because the statute violates due process."},"questionZh":"当州通过大量证据证明儿童是忽视的受害者时，州法规允许终止父母权利。一个国家机构基于一名妇女多次虐待孩子的指控，向州法院提起诉讼，终止她的父母权利。该机构向该妇女提供了听证会的充分通知。在听证会上，该机构的律师和该女子出示了证据，询问了证人，并做了开场陈词和结案陈词。法院根据大量证据认定，孩子是疏忽照顾的受害者，并下令终止该妇女的父母权利。该妇女对法院的命令提出上诉，声称终止她的父母权利是违宪的。上诉法院对此案应如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"确认该命令，因为法院可以通过使用优势证据的举证责任来终止父母权利。","B":"确认该命令，因为国家对儿童福利的利益超过了妇女对诉讼中使用的举证责任的利益。","C":"颠倒顺序，因为该法规剥夺了妇女的公民权。","D":"颠倒顺序，因为该法规违反了正当程序。"},"explanationZh":"国家在剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产利益（例如父母权利）之前必须提供程序性正当程序。这要求政府提供合理的通知和在中立决策者面前发表意见的有意义的机会——就像这里向这位女士提供的那样。程序正当程序还要求在此类听证会上适用适当的举证责任。为了确定适当的负担（以及任何其他应有的程序），法院必须平衡三个因素：\n• 受剥夺影响的私人利益\n\n• 通过现行程序错误剥夺该利益的风险以及额外或替代程序保障措施的可能价值\n\n• 政府的利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担\n\n在这里，妇女的利益在于维护其父母的权利，而国家的利益在于确保孩子的福利。然而，错误终止父母权利的风险很高，因为国家只需通过大量证据来证明其案件。这种风险可以通过要求国家履行更高的举证责任（例如，明确且令人信服的证据）来减轻。因此，该法规违反了正当程序，上诉法院应撤销终止妇女父母权利的命令（选择A和B）。 （选项C） 第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州剥夺公民的国家公民权，其中不包括父母权利。\n\n教育目标：寻求终止父母权利的国家必须至少通过明确且令人信服的证据来证明其案件，以遵守程序正当程序。\n\n参考文献\n• Santosky v. Kramer, 455 U.S. 745, 768–69 (1982)（认为第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求各州在终止父母权利时至少通过明确且令人信服的证据来证明其案件）。\n\n• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976)（列出了用于评估遵守程序正当程序的三个因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"寻求终止父母权利的国家必须至少通过明确且令人信服的证据来证明其案件，以遵守程序上的正当程序。","iracZh":{"issue":"上诉法院对此案应如何判决？","rule":"寻求终止父母权利的国家必须至少通过明确且令人信服的证据来证明其案件，以遵守程序上的正当程序。","analysis":"国家在剥夺个人的生命、自由或财产利益（例如父母权利）之前必须提供程序性正当程序。这要求政府提供合理的通知和在中立决策者面前发表意见的有意义的机会——就像这里向这位女士提供的那样。程序正当程序还要求在此类听证会上适用适当的举证责任。为了确定适当的负担（以及任何其他应有的程序），法院必须平衡三个因素：\n• 受剥夺影响的私人利益\n\n• 通过现行程序错误剥夺该利益的风险以及额外或替代程序保障措施的可能价值\n\n• 政府的利益，包括其他保障措施可能带来的财政和行政负担\n\n在这里，妇女的利益在于维护其父母的权利，而国家的利益在于确保孩子的福利。然而，错误终止父母权利的风险很高，因为国家只需通过大量证据来证明其案件。这种风险可以通过要求国家履行更高的举证责任（例如，明确且令人信服的证据）来减轻。因此，该法规违反了正当程序，上诉法院应撤销终止妇女父母权利的命令（选择A和B）。 （选项C） 第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州剥夺公民的国家公民权，其中不包括父母权利。\n\n教育目标：寻求终止父母权利的国家必须至少通过明确且令人信服的证据来证明其案件，以遵守程序正当程序。\n\n参考文献\n• Santosky v. Kramer, 455 U.S. 745, 768–69 (1982)（认为第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求各州在终止父母权利时至少通过明确且令人信服的证据来证明其案件）。\n\n• Mathews v. Eldridge, 424 U.S. 319, 335 (1976)（列出了用于评估遵守程序正当程序的三个因素）。","conclusion":"答案 D：颠倒顺序，因为该法规违反了正当程序。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-563a310f46b35ac6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":209,"sourceQuestionPage":624,"sourceQuestionPages":[624],"sourceExplanationPages":[625,626],"sourceAnswerPages":[625,626],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-210","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state barber licensing statute provides that the Barber Licensing Board may revoke a barber license if it finds that a licensee has used his or her business premises for an illegal purpose. A licensed barber in the state was arrested by federal narcotics enforcement agents on a charge of selling cocaine in his barbershop in violation of federal laws. However, the local United States Attorney declined to prosecute, and the charges were dropped. Nevertheless, the Barber Licensing Board commenced a proceeding against the barber to revoke his license on the ground that he used his business premises for illegal sales of cocaine. At a subsequent hearing before the board, the only evidence against the barber was affidavits by unnamed informants, who were not present or available for cross- examination. Their affidavits stated that they purchased cocaine from the barber in his barbershop. Based solely on this evidence, the board found that the barber used his business premises for an illegal purpose and ordered his license revoked. In a suit to have this revocation set aside, what is the barber's best constitutional argument?","choices":{"A":"Article III requires a penalty of the kind imposed on the barber to be imposed by a court rather than an administrative agency.","B":"The administrative license revocation proceeding was invalid because it denied full faith and credit to the dismissal of the criminal charges by the United States Attorney.","C":"The barber's inability to cross-examine his accusers denied him a fair hearing and caused him to be deprived of his barber license without due process of law.","D":"The existence of federal laws penalizing the illegal sale of cocaine preempts state action relating to drug trafficking of the kind involved in the barber's case."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A person has a property interest in a benefit that is conferred by state law (eg, professional license, welfare benefits). The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause safeguards this interest by requiring states to follow certain procedures before intentionally depriving persons thereof. This typically includes providing persons with:\n• adequate notice of the charges or proceedings against them and\n\n• a meaningful opportunity to be heard—ie, to confront adverse witnesses and present arguments—before a neutral decision-maker.\n\nHere, the Barber Licensing Board commenced a proceeding to revoke the barber's license. The only evidence presented against the barber was affidavits by unnamed informants, who were not present or available for cross-examination. Therefore, the barber's best argument to have his revocation set aside is that his inability to confront his accusers denied him a fair hearing and due process of law. (Choice A) Article III limits the power of the federal courts. It does not apply to state courts or any other state proceedings (as seen here). (Choice B) The full faith and credit clause requires states to recognize valid judgments issued by other states—not charges dismissed by the United States Attorney (or other states). (Choice D) Under the Article IV supremacy clause, a state law is void if it is preempted by federal law. But federal laws penalizing the illegal sale of cocaine do not preempt state action relating to drug trafficking because these laws are not in conflict.\n\nEducational objective: Due process requires that the government provide persons with (1) adequate notice and (2) a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker—and to confront adverse witnesses—before intentionally depriving them of life, liberty, or property.\n\nReferences\n• Willner v. Comm. on Character & Fitness, 373 U.S. 96, 103 (1963) (explaining that \"procedural due process often requires confrontation and cross-examination of those whose word deprives a person of his livelihood\").","educationalObjective":"Due process requires that the government provide persons with (1) adequate notice and (2) a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker—and to confront adverse witnesses—before intentionally depriving them of life, liberty, or property.","references":"• Willner v. Comm. on Character & Fitness, 373 U.S. 96, 103 (1963) (explaining that \"procedural due process often requires confrontation and cross-examination of those whose word deprives a person of his livelihood\").","rule":"Due process requires that the government provide persons with (1) adequate notice and (2) a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker—and to confront adverse witnesses—before intentionally depriving them of life, liberty, or property.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit to have this revocation set aside, what is the barber's best constitutional argument?","rule":"Due process requires that the government provide persons with (1) adequate notice and (2) a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker—and to confront adverse witnesses—before intentionally depriving them of life, liberty, or property.","analysis":"A person has a property interest in a benefit that is conferred by state law (eg, professional license, welfare benefits). The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause safeguards this interest by requiring states to follow certain procedures before intentionally depriving persons thereof. This typically includes providing persons with:\n• adequate notice of the charges or proceedings against them and\n\n• a meaningful opportunity to be heard—ie, to confront adverse witnesses and present arguments—before a neutral decision-maker.\n\nHere, the Barber Licensing Board commenced a proceeding to revoke the barber's license. The only evidence presented against the barber was affidavits by unnamed informants, who were not present or available for cross-examination. Therefore, the barber's best argument to have his revocation set aside is that his inability to confront his accusers denied him a fair hearing and due process of law. (Choice A) Article III limits the power of the federal courts. It does not apply to state courts or any other state proceedings (as seen here). (Choice B) The full faith and credit clause requires states to recognize valid judgments issued by other states—not charges dismissed by the United States Attorney (or other states). (Choice D) Under the Article IV supremacy clause, a state law is void if it is preempted by federal law. But federal laws penalizing the illegal sale of cocaine do not preempt state action relating to drug trafficking because these laws are not in conflict.","conclusion":"Answer C: The barber's inability to cross-examine his accusers denied him a fair hearing and caused him to be deprived of his barber license without due process of law."},"questionZh":"州理发师执照法规规定，如果理发师执照委员会发现持照人将其营业场所用于非法目的，则可以吊销理发师执照。该州一名持照理发师因违反联邦法律在理发店出售可卡因而被联邦缉毒执法人员逮捕。然而，当地美国检察官拒绝起诉，指控也被撤销。尽管如此，理发师执照委员会对理发师提起诉讼，吊销其执照，理由是他利用其营业场所非法销售可卡因。在委员会随后举行的听证会上，对理发师不利的唯一证据是未透露姓名的线人的宣誓书，这些线人没有出席或无法接受盘问。他们的宣誓书称，他们从理发店的理发师那里购买了可卡因。仅根据这些证据，委员会就认定该理发师将其营业场所用于非法目的，并下令吊销其执照。在要求撤销这项撤销的诉讼中，理发师最好的宪法论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"第三条要求对理发师施加的处罚应由法院而不是行政机构实施。","B":"行政许可撤销程序无效，因为它否认了美国检察官驳回刑事指控的充分信任和信誉。","C":"理发师无法盘问原告，使他无法获得公正的听证会，并导致他在未经正当法律程序的情况下被剥夺了理发师执照。","D":"惩罚非法销售可卡因的联邦法律的存在先于国家对理发师案件中涉及的毒品贩运采取行动。"},"explanationZh":"一个人对州法律赋予的福利（例如专业执照、福利）拥有财产权益。第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）通过要求各州在故意剥夺个人利益之前遵循某些程序来保护这一利益。这通常包括向人们提供：\n• 对他们的指控或诉讼有充分的通知，并且\n\n• 在中立决策者面前表达意见的有意义的机会，即面对不利证人并提出论点。\n\n在此，理发师执照委员会提起诉讼，吊销该理发师的执照。针对理发师的唯一证据是未透露姓名的线人的宣誓书，这些线人没有在场，也没有接受盘问。因此，理发师要求撤销撤销的最佳理由是，他无法与原告对峙，导致他无法获得公平的听证会和正当的法律程序。 （选项 A） 第三条限制联邦法院的权力。它不适用于州法院或任何其他州诉讼程序（如此处所示）。 （选项 B）完整的信用条款要求各州承认其他州做出的有效判决，而不是美国检察官（或其他州）驳回的指控。 （选项D）根据第四条至上条款，如果州法律被联邦法律取代，则该州法律无效。但惩罚非法销售可卡因的联邦法律并不能优先于与贩毒有关的州行动，因为这些法律并不冲突。\n\n教育目标：正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产之前，向他们提供 (1) 充分的通知，以及 (2) 一个有意义的机会，让中立决策者发表意见，并与不利证人对峙。\n\n参考文献\n• 威尔纳诉Comm。 on Character & Fitness, 373 U.S. 96, 103 (1963)（解释说“程序上的正当程序通常需要对那些言语剥夺某人生计的人进行对抗和盘问”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产之前，向他们提供 (1) 充分的通知，以及 (2) 一个有意义的机会，让中立决策者发表意见，并与不利证人对峙。","iracZh":{"issue":"在要求撤销这项撤销的诉讼中，理发师最好的宪法论据是什么？","rule":"正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产之前，向他们提供 (1) 充分的通知，以及 (2) 一个有意义的机会，让中立决策者发表意见，并与不利证人对峙。","analysis":"一个人对州法律赋予的福利（例如专业执照、福利）拥有财产权益。第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）通过要求各州在故意剥夺个人利益之前遵循某些程序来保护这一利益。这通常包括向人们提供：\n• 对他们的指控或诉讼有充分的通知，并且\n\n• 在中立决策者面前表达意见的有意义的机会，即面对不利证人并提出论点。\n\n在此，理发师执照委员会提起诉讼，吊销该理发师的执照。针对理发师的唯一证据是未透露姓名的线人的宣誓书，这些线人没有在场，也没有接受盘问。因此，理发师要求撤销撤销的最佳理由是，他无法与原告对峙，导致他无法获得公平的听证会和正当的法律程序。 （选项 A） 第三条限制联邦法院的权力。它不适用于州法院或任何其他州诉讼程序（如此处所示）。 （选项 B）完整的信用条款要求各州承认其他州做出的有效判决，而不是美国检察官（或其他州）驳回的指控。 （选项D）根据第四条至上条款，如果州法律被联邦法律取代，则该州法律无效。但惩罚非法销售可卡因的联邦法律并不能优先于与贩毒有关的州行动，因为这些法律并不冲突。\n\n教育目标：正当程序要求政府在故意剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产之前，向他们提供 (1) 充分的通知，以及 (2) 一个有意义的机会，让中立决策者发表意见，并与不利证人对峙。\n\n参考文献\n• 威尔纳诉Comm。 on Character & Fitness, 373 U.S. 96, 103 (1963)（解释说“程序上的正当程序通常需要对那些言语剥夺某人生计的人进行对抗和盘问”）。","conclusion":"答案 C：理发师无法盘问原告，使他无法获得公正的听证会，并导致他在未经正当法律程序的情况下被剥夺了理发师执照。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"affidavits","ipa":"/ˌæfɪdˈeɪvɪts/","syllables":"af·fi·davits","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. <律>宣誓书, （经陈述者宣誓在法律上可采作证据的）书面陈述( affidavit的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. <律>宣誓书, （经陈述者宣誓在法律上可采作证据的）书面陈述( affidavit的复数形式 )","wordForm":"名词；原形 affidavit；词形 0:affidavit/1:s","sourceSentence":"At a subsequent hearing before the board, the only evidence against the barber was affidavits by unnamed informants, who were not present or available for cross- examination.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-c4dba56348078ce8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":210,"sourceQuestionPage":627,"sourceQuestionPages":[627],"sourceExplanationPages":[628,629],"sourceAnswerPages":[628,629],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-211","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A state adopted a rule denying admission to its bar to anyone who was currently or had previously been a member of a subversive group. The state's bar-application form was modified to ask applicants whether they were or had previously been members of any subversive organization. An applicant refused to answer the question and was denied bar admission on that basis. The applicant challenged the decision, arguing that the question infringed upon his freedom of association. Is the applicant likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because membership in a subversive group constitutes endorsement of the group's illegal activities.","B":"No, because the Constitution does not apply to the bar.","C":"Yes, because denying bar admission based on any association with a subversive organization violates the First Amendment.","D":"Yes, because inquiring into an applicant's association with a subversive organization to deny bar admission violates the First Amendment."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment, as applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, protects the right to freely associate with any group or organization—including subversive organizations. As a result, the government cannot inquire about a person's associations for the sole purpose of withholding a right or benefit because of that person's beliefs. Instead, the government must show that the inquiry is necessary (ie, the least restrictive means) to protect a legitimate state interest. Here, an applicant was denied bar admission for refusing to answer whether he was or had been a member of any subversive organization. This question (and the associated rule) relates to the state's legitimate interest in ensuring that applicants have the requisite character and professional competence to practice law. But since that interest can be protected through less-restrictive means—eg, requiring a list of references and other personal/professional information—the question infringed upon the applicant's freedom of association. As a result, he is likely to prevail. (Choice A) Membership in a subversive group does not necessarily constitute endorsement of the group's illegal activities since the individual may have joined for other, nonsubversive reasons. (Choice B) The Constitution (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) applies only to government action. The rule at issue here constitutes government action since it was adopted by the state. Therefore, the Constitution applies. (Choice C) The government may punish individuals (eg, by denying bar admission) who are active members of subversive organizations and have the specific intent to further the organizations' illegal activities.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment prohibits the government from inquiring into a person's associations unless that inquiry is necessary to protect a legitimate state interest.\n\nReferences\n• Baird v. State Bar of Ariz., 401 U.S. 1, 6–7 (1971) (holding that a state can only inquire about a person's associations if it is necessary to serve a legitimate state interest).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment prohibits the government from inquiring into a person's associations unless that inquiry is necessary to protect a legitimate state interest.","references":"• Baird v. State Bar of Ariz., 401 U.S. 1, 6–7 (1971) (holding that a state can only inquire about a person's associations if it is necessary to serve a legitimate state interest).","rule":"The First Amendment prohibits the government from inquiring into a person's associations unless that inquiry is necessary to protect a legitimate state interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the applicant likely to prevail?","rule":"The First Amendment prohibits the government from inquiring into a person's associations unless that inquiry is necessary to protect a legitimate state interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment, as applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, protects the right to freely associate with any group or organization—including subversive organizations. As a result, the government cannot inquire about a person's associations for the sole purpose of withholding a right or benefit because of that person's beliefs. Instead, the government must show that the inquiry is necessary (ie, the least restrictive means) to protect a legitimate state interest. Here, an applicant was denied bar admission for refusing to answer whether he was or had been a member of any subversive organization. This question (and the associated rule) relates to the state's legitimate interest in ensuring that applicants have the requisite character and professional competence to practice law. But since that interest can be protected through less-restrictive means—eg, requiring a list of references and other personal/professional information—the question infringed upon the applicant's freedom of association. As a result, he is likely to prevail. (Choice A) Membership in a subversive group does not necessarily constitute endorsement of the group's illegal activities since the individual may have joined for other, nonsubversive reasons. (Choice B) The Constitution (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) applies only to government action. The rule at issue here constitutes government action since it was adopted by the state. Therefore, the Constitution applies. (Choice C) The government may punish individuals (eg, by denying bar admission) who are active members of subversive organizations and have the specific intent to further the organizations' illegal activities.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because inquiring into an applicant's association with a subversive organization to deny bar admission violates the First Amendment."},"questionZh":"某州制定了一项规则，拒绝任何目前或曾经是颠覆组织成员的人进入该州的酒吧。该州的律师资格申请表经过修改，询问申请人是否是或曾经是任何颠覆组织的成员。一名申请人拒绝回答这个问题，并因此被拒绝接受律师资格。申请人对该决定提出质疑，认为该问题侵犯了他的结社自由。申请人有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为加入颠覆组织就构成对该组织非法活动的认可。","B":"不，因为宪法不适用于律师。","C":"是的，因为基于与颠覆组织的任何联系而拒绝律师进入违反了第一修正案。","D":"是的，因为通过调查申请人与颠覆组织的关系来拒绝律师资格违反了第一修正案。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案通过第十四修正案适用于各州，保护自由与任何团体或组织（包括颠覆组织）结社的权利。因此，政府不能仅出于因某人的信仰而扣留某项权利或利益的目的而询问该人的社团情况。相反，政府必须证明调查对于保护合法的国家利益是必要的（即限制性最小的手段）。在这里，一名申请人因拒绝回答他是否是或曾经是任何颠覆组织的成员而被拒绝进入律师资格。这个问题（以及相关规则）关系到国家确保申请人具备从事法律工作所需的品格和专业能力的合法利益。但由于这种利益可以通过限制较少的方式得到保护——例如，要求提供一份推荐信和其他个人/专业信息的清单——这个问题侵犯了申请人的结社自由。结果，他很可能获胜。 （选项 A） 加入颠覆性团体并不一定构成对该团体非法活动的认可，因为个人可能出于其他非颠覆性原因加入。 （选项B）宪法（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为。这里所讨论的规则自被国家通过以来就构成了政府行为。因此，适用宪法。 （选项C）政府可以惩罚那些是颠覆组织的积极成员并有特定意图推动该组织非法活动的个人（例如，通过拒绝律师准入）。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案禁止政府调查某人的社团，除非该调查对于保护合法的国家利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• Baird v. State Bar of Ariz., 401 U.S. 1, 6–7 (1971)（认为州只有在有必要服务于合法的国家利益时才可以询问某人的联系）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案禁止政府调查某人的社团，除非该调查是保护合法国家利益所必需的。","iracZh":{"issue":"申请人有可能获胜吗？","rule":"第一修正案禁止政府调查某人的社团，除非该调查是保护合法国家利益所必需的。","analysis":"第一修正案通过第十四修正案适用于各州，保护自由与任何团体或组织（包括颠覆组织）结社的权利。因此，政府不能仅出于因某人的信仰而扣留某项权利或利益的目的而询问该人的社团情况。相反，政府必须证明调查对于保护合法的国家利益是必要的（即限制性最小的手段）。在这里，一名申请人因拒绝回答他是否是或曾经是任何颠覆组织的成员而被拒绝进入律师资格。这个问题（以及相关规则）关系到国家确保申请人具备从事法律工作所需的品格和专业能力的合法利益。但由于这种利益可以通过限制较少的方式得到保护——例如，要求提供一份推荐信和其他个人/专业信息的清单——这个问题侵犯了申请人的结社自由。结果，他很可能获胜。 （选项 A） 加入颠覆性团体并不一定构成对该团体非法活动的认可，因为个人可能出于其他非颠覆性原因加入。 （选项B）宪法（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为。这里所讨论的规则自被国家通过以来就构成了政府行为。因此，适用宪法。 （选项C）政府可以惩罚那些是颠覆组织的积极成员并有特定意图推动该组织非法活动的个人（例如，通过拒绝律师准入）。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案禁止政府调查某人的社团，除非该调查对于保护合法的国家利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• Baird v. State Bar of Ariz., 401 U.S. 1, 6–7 (1971)（认为州只有在有必要服务于合法的国家利益时才可以询问某人的联系）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为通过调查申请人与颠覆组织的关系来拒绝律师资格违反了第一修正案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-37b4ad4ef0422533"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":211,"sourceQuestionPage":630,"sourceQuestionPages":[630],"sourceExplanationPages":[631,632],"sourceAnswerPages":[631,632],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-212","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A company wanted to expand the size of the building it owned that housed the company's supermarket by adding space for a coffeehouse. The company's building was located in the center of five acres of land owned by the company and wholly devoted to parking for its supermarket customers. City officials refused to grant a required building permit for the coffeehouse addition unless the company established in its store a childcare center that would take up space at least equal to the size of the proposed coffeehouse addition, which was to be 20% of the existing building. The city officials' action was authorized by provisions of the applicable zoning ordinance. In a suit filed in state court against the appropriate city officials, the company challenged this childcare-center requirement solely on constitutional grounds. The lower court upheld the requirement even though the city officials presented no evidence and made no findings to justify it other than a general assertion that there was a shortage of childcare facilities in the city. The company appealed. How should the appellate court rule on the constitutionality of the requirement imposed by the city on the issuance of this building permit?","choices":{"A":"The requirement is constitutional, because the burden was on the company to demonstrate that there was no rational relationship between this requirement and a legitimate governmental interest, and the company could not do so because the requirement is reasonably related to improving the lives of families and children residing in the city.","B":"The requirement is constitutional, because the burden was on the company to demonstrate that this requirement was not necessary to vindicate a compelling governmental interest, and the company could not do so on these facts.","C":"The requirement is unconstitutional, because the burden was on the city to demonstrate a rough proportionality between this requirement and the impact of the company's proposed action on the community, and the city failed to do so.","D":"The requirement is unconstitutional, because the burden was on the city to demonstrate that this requirement was necessary to vindicate a compelling governmental interest, and the city failed to meet its burden under that standard."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nAn exaction occurs when a local government conditions the issuance of a building or development permit on a landowner's promise to dedicate part of the property for public use. And an exaction amounts to a Fifth Amendment taking requiring just compensation unless the government establishes:\n• an essential nexus – the imposed condition substantially advances a legitimate government interest and\n\n• rough proportionality – individualized and quantifiable findings show that the proposed development's impact on the community is roughly proportional to the imposed condition's impact on the landowner.\n\nHere, the city conditioned the grant of a building permit on the company's promise to establish a childcare center in its store without just compensation. This condition substantially advances its legitimate interest in alleviating the shortage of childcare facilities. But the city failed to present any evidence to establish a rough proportionality between that condition and the impact of the company's proposed action on the community. Therefore, the appellate court should rule that this exaction amounts to an unconstitutional taking. (Choice A) The city had the burden to demonstrate that the imposed condition substantially advances the city's legitimate interest in expanding childcare facilities. (Choices B & D) Neither the company nor the city had the burden to demonstrate that the childcare-center requirement was necessary to vindicate a compelling government interest.\n\nEducational objective: An exaction is a Fifth Amendment taking unless the government shows: (1) an essential nexus between the imposed condition and a legitimate government interest and (2) a rough proportionality between the condition's impact on the landowner and the proposed development's impact on the community.\n\nReferences\n• Nollan v. Cal. Coastal Comm'n., 483 U.S. 825, 837 (1987) (explaining the essential nexus requirement).\n\n• Dolan v. City of Tigard, 512 U.S. 374, 391 (1994) (requiring the government to provide individualized findings to show a rough proportionality between the exaction and the projected impact of the development).","educationalObjective":"An exaction is a Fifth Amendment taking unless the government shows: (1) an essential nexus between the imposed condition and a legitimate government interest and (2) a rough proportionality between the condition's impact on the landowner and the proposed development's impact on the community.","references":"• Nollan v. Cal. Coastal Comm'n., 483 U.S. 825, 837 (1987) (explaining the essential nexus requirement).\n\n• Dolan v. City of Tigard, 512 U.S. 374, 391 (1994) (requiring the government to provide individualized findings to show a rough proportionality between the exaction and the projected impact of the development).","rule":"An exaction is a Fifth Amendment taking unless the government shows: (1) an essential nexus between the imposed condition and a legitimate government interest and (2) a rough proportionality between the condition's impact on the landowner and the proposed development's impact on the community.","irac":{"issue":"How should the appellate court rule on the constitutionality of the requirement imposed by the city on the issuance of this building permit?","rule":"An exaction is a Fifth Amendment taking unless the government shows: (1) an essential nexus between the imposed condition and a legitimate government interest and (2) a rough proportionality between the condition's impact on the landowner and the proposed development's impact on the community.","analysis":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nAn exaction occurs when a local government conditions the issuance of a building or development permit on a landowner's promise to dedicate part of the property for public use. And an exaction amounts to a Fifth Amendment taking requiring just compensation unless the government establishes:\n• an essential nexus – the imposed condition substantially advances a legitimate government interest and\n\n• rough proportionality – individualized and quantifiable findings show that the proposed development's impact on the community is roughly proportional to the imposed condition's impact on the landowner.\n\nHere, the city conditioned the grant of a building permit on the company's promise to establish a childcare center in its store without just compensation. This condition substantially advances its legitimate interest in alleviating the shortage of childcare facilities. But the city failed to present any evidence to establish a rough proportionality between that condition and the impact of the company's proposed action on the community. Therefore, the appellate court should rule that this exaction amounts to an unconstitutional taking. (Choice A) The city had the burden to demonstrate that the imposed condition substantially advances the city's legitimate interest in expanding childcare facilities. (Choices B & D) Neither the company nor the city had the burden to demonstrate that the childcare-center requirement was necessary to vindicate a compelling government interest.","conclusion":"Answer C: The requirement is unconstitutional, because the burden was on the city to demonstrate a rough proportionality between this requirement and the impact of the company's proposed action on the community, and the city failed to do so."},"questionZh":"一家公司希望通过增加咖啡馆的空间来扩大其拥有的超市大楼的规模。该公司的大楼位于该公司拥有的五英亩土地的中心，完全用于为其超市客户提供停车场。市政府官员拒绝授予咖啡馆扩建所需的建筑许可证，除非该公司在其商店内建立一个儿童保育中心，该中心所占用的空间至少等于拟议咖啡馆扩建的面积，即现有建筑的 20%。市政府官员的行动得到了适用分区条例规定的授权。在州法院针对相关市政府官员提起的诉讼中，该公司仅以宪法为由对这一儿童保育中心的要求提出质疑。下级法院维持了这一要求，尽管市政府官员没有提供任何证据，也没有做出任何调查结果来证明这一要求，只是普遍声称该市缺乏托儿设施。该公司提出上诉。上诉法院应如何裁定市政府颁发建筑许可证的要求是否符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"该要求是符合宪法的，因为公司有责任证明该要求与合法的政府利益之间不存在合理关系，并且该公司不能这样做，因为该要求与改善居住在城市的家庭和儿童的生活合理相关。","B":"该要求是符合宪法的，因为公司有责任证明该要求对于维护令人信服的政府利益而言不是必要的，并且公司不能根据这些事实这样做。","C":"该要求是违宪的，因为该市有责任证明该要求与公司拟议行动对社区的影响之间的大致比例，但该市未能这样做。","D":"该要求是违宪的，因为该市有责任证明该要求对于维护令人信服的政府利益是必要的，而该市未能满足该标准下的负担。"},"explanationZh":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当地方政府以土地所有者承诺将部分财产供公共使用为条件发放建筑或开发许可证时，就会发生勒索行为。征收相当于第五修正案要求公正的赔偿，除非政府规定：\n• 重要的联系——强加的条件大大促进了合法的政府利益，并且\n\n• 大致相称性——个性化和可量化的研究结果表明，拟议的开发项目对社区的影响与所施加的条件对土地所有者的影响大致成正比。\n\n在这里，市政府授予建筑许可证的条件是该公司承诺在其商店内建立一个儿童保育中心，而无需给予任何补偿。这一条件大大提高了其在缓解儿童保育设施短缺方面的合法利益。但该市未能提供任何证据来证明这种情况与该公司拟议行动对社区的影响之间存在大致的比例。因此，上诉法院应裁定这种征收行为构成违宪征收。 （选择 A） 市政府有责任证明所施加的条件极大地促进了市政府扩大托儿设施的合法利益。 （选择 B 和 D）无论是公司还是市政府都没有责任证明托儿中心的要求对于维护令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n教育目标：征收属于第五修正案，除非政府表明：(1) 强加的条件与合法的政府利益之间存在本质联系；(2) 该条件对土地所有者的影响与拟议的开发对社区的影响之间大致成比例。\n\n参考文献\n• 诺兰诉卡尔。 Coastal Comm'n., 483 U.S. 825, 837 (1987)（解释基本联系要求）。\n\n• Dolan v. City of Tigard, 512 U.S. 374, 391 (1994)（要求政府提供个性化的调查结果，以显示勒索和开发项目的预计影响之间的大致比例）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"征收属于第五修正案，除非政府表明：(1) 强加的条件与合法的政府利益之间存在本质联系；(2) 该条件对土地所有者的影响与拟议开发项目对社区的影响之间大致成比例。","iracZh":{"issue":"上诉法院应如何裁定市政府颁发建筑许可证的要求是否符合宪法？","rule":"征收属于第五修正案，除非政府表明：(1) 强加的条件与合法的政府利益之间存在本质联系；(2) 该条件对土地所有者的影响与拟议开发项目对社区的影响之间大致成比例。","analysis":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当地方政府以土地所有者承诺将部分财产供公共使用为条件发放建筑或开发许可证时，就会发生勒索行为。征收相当于第五修正案要求公正的赔偿，除非政府规定：\n• 重要的联系——强加的条件大大促进了合法的政府利益，并且\n\n• 大致相称性——个性化和可量化的研究结果表明，拟议的开发项目对社区的影响与所施加的条件对土地所有者的影响大致成正比。\n\n在这里，市政府授予建筑许可证的条件是该公司承诺在其商店内建立一个儿童保育中心，而无需给予任何补偿。这一条件大大提高了其在缓解儿童保育设施短缺方面的合法利益。但该市未能提供任何证据来证明这种情况与该公司拟议行动对社区的影响之间存在大致的比例。因此，上诉法院应裁定这种征收行为构成违宪征收。 （选择 A） 市政府有责任证明所施加的条件极大地促进了市政府扩大托儿设施的合法利益。 （选择 B 和 D）无论是公司还是市政府都没有责任证明托儿中心的要求对于维护令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n教育目标：征收属于第五修正案，除非政府表明：(1) 强加的条件与合法的政府利益之间存在本质联系；(2) 该条件对土地所有者的影响与拟议的开发对社区的影响之间大致成比例。\n\n参考文献\n• 诺兰诉卡尔。 Coastal Comm'n., 483 U.S. 825, 837 (1987)（解释基本联系要求）。\n\n• Dolan v. City of Tigard, 512 U.S. 374, 391 (1994)（要求政府提供个性化的调查结果，以显示勒索和开发项目的预计影响之间的大致比例）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该要求是违宪的，因为该市有责任证明该要求与公司拟议行动对社区的影响之间的大致比例，但该市未能这样做。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"landowner","ipa":"/lˈændˌoʊnɝ/","syllables":"landowner","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 地主；[法] 土地所有人, 地主","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 地主；[法] 土地所有人, 地主","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:landowners","sourceSentence":"An exaction is a Fifth Amendment taking unless the government shows: (1) an essential nexus between the imposed condition and a legitimate government interest and (2) a rough proportionality between the condition's impact on the landowner and the proposed development's impact on the community.","synonyms":["landholder","property owner"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":212,"sourceQuestionPage":633,"sourceQuestionPages":[633],"sourceExplanationPages":[634,635],"sourceAnswerPages":[634,635],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-213","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Contracts Clause","question":"A state in which several popular ski resorts are located has enacted a law requiring state certification of ski instructors. Under the law, applicants for certification must attend a monthlong course, pass a test, and pay a fee of $1,000. The stated purpose of the law is to \"promote uniformity in the methods of ski instruction at the state's ski areas.\" Shortly before the law's enactment, when the state did not require certification of ski instructors, a woman moved to the state intending to find employment as a ski instructor. The woman had been a ski instructor in another state for many years. But since her move, even though ski resorts in the state have found her to be highly qualified, no resort will offer her a contract for employment as a ski instructor because she has not received the required state certification. As applied to the woman, does the state certification requirement constitute a violation of the contracts clause?","choices":{"A":"No, because her ability to contract for employment with the state is not being impaired.","B":"No, because she has no existing contract that is being impaired.","C":"Yes, because as applied to an otherwise qualified ski instructor, the requirement is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","D":"Yes, because the requirement substantially impairs her ability to contract for employment in the absence of an important government objective."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The contracts clause restricts state laws—not court decisions or federal legislation—that substantially impair existing contracts. Therefore, this clause does not apply to future contracts. Here, the state law interferes with the woman's ability to obtain an employment contract as a ski instructor. But since she has not been hired by a ski resort, the law does not impair any existing contract. Therefore, the state certification requirement does not violate the contracts clause as applied to the woman. (Choice A) The contracts clause applies to private contracts (ie, agreements with non- government parties) and public contracts (ie, agreements with government entities). But the woman's ability to contract for employment with the state is irrelevant since this clause only protects existing contracts. (Choices C & D) The contracts clause would only apply if the woman—qualified or not— had an existing contract. And had one existed, a substantial impairment of that contract would only be upheld if it was reasonable and narrowly tailored (not just rationally related) to an important and legitimate government interest (here, promoting uniformity in ski- instruction methods in the state).\n\nEducational objective: The contracts clause restricts state laws that substantially impair existing (not future) contracts.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 10 (contracts clause).\n\n• Gen. Motors Corp. v. Romein, 503 U.S. 181,186 (1992) (explaining that the contracts clause only applies when a state law substantially impairs an existing contractual relationship).","educationalObjective":"The contracts clause restricts state laws that substantially impair existing (not future) contracts.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 10 (contracts clause).\n\n• Gen. Motors Corp. v. Romein, 503 U.S. 181,186 (1992) (explaining that the contracts clause only applies when a state law substantially impairs an existing contractual relationship).","rule":"The contracts clause restricts state laws that substantially impair existing (not future) contracts.","irac":{"issue":"As applied to the woman, does the state certification requirement constitute a violation of the contracts clause?","rule":"The contracts clause restricts state laws that substantially impair existing (not future) contracts.","analysis":"The contracts clause restricts state laws—not court decisions or federal legislation—that substantially impair existing contracts. Therefore, this clause does not apply to future contracts. Here, the state law interferes with the woman's ability to obtain an employment contract as a ski instructor. But since she has not been hired by a ski resort, the law does not impair any existing contract. Therefore, the state certification requirement does not violate the contracts clause as applied to the woman. (Choice A) The contracts clause applies to private contracts (ie, agreements with non- government parties) and public contracts (ie, agreements with government entities). But the woman's ability to contract for employment with the state is irrelevant since this clause only protects existing contracts. (Choices C & D) The contracts clause would only apply if the woman—qualified or not— had an existing contract. And had one existed, a substantial impairment of that contract would only be upheld if it was reasonable and narrowly tailored (not just rationally related) to an important and legitimate government interest (here, promoting uniformity in ski- instruction methods in the state).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because she has no existing contract that is being impaired."},"questionZh":"几个受欢迎的滑雪胜地所在的州颁布了一项法律，要求滑雪教练获得州认证。根据法律规定，认证申请人必须参加为期一个月的课程、通过测试并支付 1,000 美元的费用。该法律的既定目的是“促进该州滑雪场滑雪教学方法的统一”。在该法律颁布前不久，当该州不要求滑雪教练认证时，一名妇女搬到该州，打算寻找滑雪教练的工作。该女子多年来一直在另一个州担任滑雪教练。但自从她搬到这里后，尽管该州的滑雪场发现她资质很高，但没有一家滑雪场愿意为她提供滑雪教练的雇佣合同，因为她没有获得所需的州认证。对于女性来说，国家认证要求是否构成违反合同条款？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为她与国家签订就业合同的能力并未受到损害。","B":"不，因为她没有现有的合同受到损害。","C":"是的，因为适用于其他合格的滑雪教练时，该要求与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。","D":"是的，因为在没有重要的政府目标的情况下，这一要求会严重损害她签订就业合同的能力。"},"explanationZh":"合同条款限制严重损害现有合同的州法律，而不是法院判决或联邦立法。因此，该条款不适用于未来的合同。在这里，州法律干扰了妇女获得滑雪教练就业合同的能力。但由于她并未受雇于滑雪场，因此法律不会损害任何现有合同。因此，国家认证要求并不违反适用于妇女的合同条款。 （选择A） 合同条款适用于私人合同（即与非政府方的协议）和公共合同（即与政府实体的协议）。但妇女与国家签订就业合同的能力无关紧要，因为该条款仅保护现有合同。 （选项 C 和 D）合同条款仅适用于该妇女（无论是否合格）已有合同的情况。而且，如果存在这样的合同，只有在合理且狭隘地针对重要且合法的政府利益（而不仅仅是合理相关）（此处是促进该州滑雪教学方法的统一）的情况下，才会维持对该合同的重大损害。\n\n教育目标：合同条款限制严重损害现有（而非未来）合同的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 10（合同条款）。\n\n• Gen. Motors Corp. v. Romein，503 U.S. 181,186 (1992)（解释说合同条款仅在州法律严重损害现有合同关系时适用）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同条款限制严重损害现有（而非未来）合同的州法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于女性来说，国家认证要求是否构成违反合同条款？","rule":"合同条款限制严重损害现有（而非未来）合同的州法律。","analysis":"合同条款限制严重损害现有合同的州法律，而不是法院判决或联邦立法。因此，该条款不适用于未来的合同。在这里，州法律干扰了妇女获得滑雪教练就业合同的能力。但由于她并未受雇于滑雪场，因此法律不会损害任何现有合同。因此，国家认证要求并不违反适用于妇女的合同条款。 （选择A） 合同条款适用于私人合同（即与非政府方的协议）和公共合同（即与政府实体的协议）。但妇女与国家签订就业合同的能力无关紧要，因为该条款仅保护现有合同。 （选项 C 和 D）合同条款仅适用于该妇女（无论是否合格）已有合同的情况。而且，如果存在这样的合同，只有在合理且狭隘地针对重要且合法的政府利益（而不仅仅是合理相关）（此处是促进该州滑雪教学方法的统一）的情况下，才会维持对该合同的重大损害。\n\n教育目标：合同条款限制严重损害现有（而非未来）合同的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 10（合同条款）。\n\n• Gen. Motors Corp. v. Romein，503 U.S. 181,186 (1992)（解释说合同条款仅在州法律严重损害现有合同关系时适用）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为她没有现有的合同受到损害。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f1383f7788560e9b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":213,"sourceQuestionPage":636,"sourceQuestionPages":[636],"sourceExplanationPages":[637,638],"sourceAnswerPages":[637,638],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-214","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Takings Clause","question":"A company owned a large tract of land that contained coal deposits that the company intended to mine. The company acquired mining equipment and began to plan its mining operations. Just as the company was about to begin mining, Congress enacted a statute that imposed a number of new environmental regulations and land-reclamation requirements on all mining operations within the United States. The statute made the company's planned mining operations economically infeasible. As a result, the company sold the tract of land to a farmer. While the sale price allowed the company to recover its original investment in the land, it did not cover the additional cost of the mining equipment the company had purchased or the profits it had expected to earn from its mining operations on the land. In an action filed against the appropriate federal official, the company claims that the statute effected a taking of its property for which it is entitled to just compensation in an amount equal to the cost of the mining equipment it purchased and the profits it expected to earn from its mining operations on the land. Which of the following is the most appropriate result in the action?","choices":{"A":"The company should prevail on its claims for the cost of the mining equipment and for its lost profits.","B":"The company should prevail on its claim for the cost of the mining equipment, but not for its lost profits.","C":"The company should prevail on its claim for lost profits, but not for the cost of the mining equipment.","D":"The company should not prevail on its claim for the cost of the mining equipment or for its lost profits."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nA regulatory taking occurs when the government substantially restricts (or destroys all economically beneficial) use of private property—including land and personal property. A substantial restriction is evaluated under the totality of the circumstances, including:\n• the character or nature of the government action (ie, public benefit)\n• the severity of the economic impact on the property and\n• the interference with the owner's reasonable investment-backed expectations. And under the Fifth Amendment, the government can only commit a taking if it provides the owner just compensation—ie, the fair market value of the property at the time of the taking. Here, the federal statute did not effect a taking of the company's land because (1) it is still economically viable as farmland and (2) the company recouped its original investment through the sale to the farmer. Nor did the statute effect a taking of the mining equipment because it can be used on other land or sold. Therefore, the company is not entitled to just compensation for the land or mining equipment. But even if a taking had occurred, the company could only recover the fair market value of the taken property—not its lost profits (Choices A, B, & C).\n\nEducational objective: A regulatory taking occurs when the government substantially restricts (or destroys all economically beneficial) use of private property—including land and personal property. But this only entitles the owner to just compensation in the amount of the property's fair market value at the time of the taking—not lost profits.\n\nReferences\n• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992) (holding that a deprivation of all economically beneficial uses of property constitutes a taking).\n\n• Penn Cent. Transp. Co. v. New York City, 438 U.S. 104, 124 (1978) (establishing the factors to consider when determining if a regulation constitutes a taking).\n\n• 26 Am. Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 14 (2019) (explaining the Penn Central factors).","educationalObjective":"A regulatory taking occurs when the government substantially restricts (or destroys all economically beneficial) use of private property—including land and personal property. But this only entitles the owner to just compensation in the amount of the property's fair market value at the time of the taking—not lost profits.","references":"• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992) (holding that a deprivation of all economically beneficial uses of property constitutes a taking).\n\n• Penn Cent. Transp. Co. v. New York City, 438 U.S. 104, 124 (1978) (establishing the factors to consider when determining if a regulation constitutes a taking).\n\n• 26 Am. Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 14 (2019) (explaining the Penn Central factors).","rule":"A regulatory taking occurs when the government substantially restricts (or destroys all economically beneficial) use of private property—including land and personal property. But this only entitles the owner to just compensation in the amount of the property's fair market value at the time of the taking—not lost profits.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the most appropriate result in the action?","rule":"A regulatory taking occurs when the government substantially restricts (or destroys all economically beneficial) use of private property—including land and personal property. But this only entitles the owner to just compensation in the amount of the property's fair market value at the time of the taking—not lost profits.","analysis":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nA regulatory taking occurs when the government substantially restricts (or destroys all economically beneficial) use of private property—including land and personal property. A substantial restriction is evaluated under the totality of the circumstances, including:\n• the character or nature of the government action (ie, public benefit)\n• the severity of the economic impact on the property and\n• the interference with the owner's reasonable investment-backed expectations. And under the Fifth Amendment, the government can only commit a taking if it provides the owner just compensation—ie, the fair market value of the property at the time of the taking. Here, the federal statute did not effect a taking of the company's land because (1) it is still economically viable as farmland and (2) the company recouped its original investment through the sale to the farmer. Nor did the statute effect a taking of the mining equipment because it can be used on other land or sold. Therefore, the company is not entitled to just compensation for the land or mining equipment. But even if a taking had occurred, the company could only recover the fair market value of the taken property—not its lost profits (Choices A, B, & C).","conclusion":"Answer D: The company should not prevail on its claim for the cost of the mining equipment or for its lost profits."},"questionZh":"一家公司拥有一大片土地，其中含有该公司打算开采的煤矿。该公司购买了采矿设备并开始规划其采矿业务。正当该公司即将开始采矿时，国会颁布了一项法规，对美国境内的所有采矿作业实施了一系列新的环境法规和土地复垦要求。该法规使该公司计划的采矿作业在经济上不可行。结果，该公司将这块土地卖给了一位农民。虽然出售价格使该公司能够收回对该土地的原始投资，但它并没有涵盖该公司购买的采矿设备的额外成本或预计从该土地上的采矿业务中赚取的利润。在针对相关联邦官员的诉讼中，该公司声称，该法规影响了其财产的征收，因此该公司有权获得相当于其购买采矿设备成本和预期从该土地上采矿作业赚取的利润的赔偿。以下哪项是该行动最合适的结果？","choicesZh":{"A":"该公司对采矿设备成本和利润损失的索赔应胜诉。","B":"该公司对于采矿设备成本的索赔应胜诉，但其利润损失不应胜诉。","C":"该公司的利润损失索赔应胜诉，但采矿设备成本的索赔不应胜诉。","D":"该公司不应就采矿设备成本或利润损失提出索赔。"},"explanationZh":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当政府大幅限制（或破坏所有经济上有利的）私有财产（包括土地和个人财产）的使用时，就会发生监管征用。实质性限制是根据总体情况进行评估的，包括：\n• 政府行为的特征或性质（即公共利益）\n• 对财产的经济影响的严重程度以及\n• 干扰所有者的合理投资预期。根据第五修正案，政府只有在向所有者提供公正补偿（即征收时财产的公平市场价值）的情况下才能实施征收。在这里，联邦法规没有影响公司土地的征用，因为（1）它作为农田在经济上仍然可行，（2）公司通过出售给农民收回了其原始投资。该法规也不会影响采矿设备的征用，因为它可以在其他土地上使用或出售。因此，该公司无权就土地或采矿设备获得公正的补偿。但即使发生了征用，公司也只能收回被征用财产的公平市场价值，而不能收回其利润损失（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：当政府大幅限制（或破坏所有经济上有利的）私有财产（包括土地和个人财产）的使用时，就会发生监管征收。但这仅使业主有权获得按财产收取时的公平市场价值进行的补偿，而不是利润损失。\n\n参考文献\n• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992)（认为剥夺财产的所有经济利益用途构成征用）。\n\n• 宾夕法尼亚分公司。运输。 Co. v. New York City, 438 U.S. 104, 124 (1978)（确定了确定某项法规是否构成征收时要考虑的因素）。\n\n• 上午26 点。尤尔。 2d 征用权 § 14 (2019)（解释宾夕法尼亚大学中心因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当政府大幅限制（或破坏所有经济上有利的）私有财产（包括土地和个人财产）的使用时，就会发生监管征用。但这仅使业主有权获得按财产收取时的公平市场价值进行的补偿，而不是利润损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项是该行动最合适的结果？","rule":"当政府大幅限制（或破坏所有经济上有利的）私有财产（包括土地和个人财产）的使用时，就会发生监管征用。但这仅使业主有权获得按财产收取时的公平市场价值进行的补偿，而不是利润损失。","analysis":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当政府大幅限制（或破坏所有经济上有利的）私有财产（包括土地和个人财产）的使用时，就会发生监管征用。实质性限制是根据总体情况进行评估的，包括：\n• 政府行为的特征或性质（即公共利益）\n• 对财产的经济影响的严重程度以及\n• 干扰所有者的合理投资预期。根据第五修正案，政府只有在向所有者提供公正补偿（即征收时财产的公平市场价值）的情况下才能实施征收。在这里，联邦法规没有影响公司土地的征用，因为（1）它作为农田在经济上仍然可行，（2）公司通过出售给农民收回了其原始投资。该法规也不会影响采矿设备的征用，因为它可以在其他土地上使用或出售。因此，该公司无权就土地或采矿设备获得公正的补偿。但即使发生了征用，公司也只能收回被征用财产的公平市场价值，而不能收回其利润损失（选项 A、B 和 C）。\n\n教育目标：当政府大幅限制（或破坏所有经济上有利的）私有财产（包括土地和个人财产）的使用时，就会发生监管征收。但这仅使业主有权获得按财产收取时的公平市场价值进行的补偿，而不是利润损失。\n\n参考文献\n• Lucas v. S.C. Coastal Council, 505 U.S. 1003, 1019 (1992)（认为剥夺财产的所有经济利益用途构成征用）。\n\n• 宾夕法尼亚分公司。运输。 Co. v. New York City, 438 U.S. 104, 124 (1978)（确定了确定某项法规是否构成征收时要考虑的因素）。\n\n• 上午26 点。尤尔。 2d 征用权 § 14 (2019)（解释宾夕法尼亚大学中心因素）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该公司不应就采矿设备成本或利润损失提出索赔。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":214,"sourceQuestionPage":639,"sourceQuestionPages":[639],"sourceExplanationPages":[640,641],"sourceAnswerPages":[640,641],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-215","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A city owns and operates a large public auditorium. It leases the auditorium to any group that wishes to use it for a meeting, lecture, concert, or contest. Each user must post a damage deposit and pay rent, which is calculated only for the actual time the building is used by the lessee. Reservations are made on a first-come, first-served basis. A private organization that permits only males to serve in its highest offices rented the auditorium for its national convention. The organization planned to install its new officers at that convention. It broadly publicized the event, inviting members of the general public to attend the installation ceremony at the city auditorium. No statute or administrative rule prohibits the organization from restricting its highest offices to men. An appropriate plaintiff sues the private organization seeking to enjoin it from using the city auditorium for the installation of its new officers. The sole claim of the plaintiff is that the use of this auditorium by the organization for the installation ceremony violates the Fourteenth Amendment because the organization disqualifies women from serving in its highest offices. Will the plaintiff prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the freedom of association protected by the Fourteenth Amendment prohibits the city from interfering in any way with the organization's use of city facilities.","B":"No, because this organization is not a state actor and, therefore, its activities are not subject to the provisions of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"Yes, because the Fourteenth Amendment prohibits such an organization from discriminating against women in any of its activities to which it has invited members of the general public.","D":"Yes, because the organization's use of the city auditorium for this purpose subjects its conduct to the provisions of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The Constitution (except the Thirteenth Amendment) applies to government actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is treated as a government actor—and similarly bound by the Constitution—when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Significant involvement exists when the government:\n• has a mutually beneficial relationship with the private actor (eg, joint venture)\n\n• creates a nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, through a police officer acting under color of law) or\n\n• is pervasively intertwined in the private actor's management or control.\n\nHere, the private organization is not performing a traditional and exclusive government function because private groups have historically used city auditoriums for conventions (Choice D). And merely leasing the auditorium does not affirmatively facilitate the organization's actions or entwine the city in the organization's management. Nor does it create a mutually beneficial relationship since the city provides uniform access to any group at the same rate on a first-come, first-served basis. Therefore, the organization is not a state actor subject to the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice A) The First Amendment—applicable to states and municipalities through the Fourteenth Amendment—prohibits some (but not all) government interference with the freedom of association. (Choice C) The fact that the private organization invited members of the general public to its activities does not make it a government actor subject to the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause.\n\nEducational objective: Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are considered government actors and bound by the Constitution when (1) they perform a traditional government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in their activities—eg, more than providing access to government services or facilities.\n\nReferences\n• Gilmore v. Montgomery, 417 U.S. 556, 574 (1974) (explaining that the mere private use of government facilities does not constitute significant state involvement under the state-action doctrine).","educationalObjective":"Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are considered government actors and bound by the Constitution when (1) they perform a traditional government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in their activities—eg, more than providing access to government services or facilities.","references":"• Gilmore v. Montgomery, 417 U.S. 556, 574 (1974) (explaining that the mere private use of government facilities does not constitute significant state involvement under the state-action doctrine).","rule":"Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are considered government actors and bound by the Constitution when (1) they perform a traditional government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in their activities—eg, more than providing access to government services or facilities.","irac":{"issue":"Will the plaintiff prevail?","rule":"Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are considered government actors and bound by the Constitution when (1) they perform a traditional government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in their activities—eg, more than providing access to government services or facilities.","analysis":"The Constitution (except the Thirteenth Amendment) applies to government actors. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is treated as a government actor—and similarly bound by the Constitution—when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Significant involvement exists when the government:\n• has a mutually beneficial relationship with the private actor (eg, joint venture)\n\n• creates a nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, through a police officer acting under color of law) or\n\n• is pervasively intertwined in the private actor's management or control.\n\nHere, the private organization is not performing a traditional and exclusive government function because private groups have historically used city auditoriums for conventions (Choice D). And merely leasing the auditorium does not affirmatively facilitate the organization's actions or entwine the city in the organization's management. Nor does it create a mutually beneficial relationship since the city provides uniform access to any group at the same rate on a first-come, first-served basis. Therefore, the organization is not a state actor subject to the Fourteenth Amendment. (Choice A) The First Amendment—applicable to states and municipalities through the Fourteenth Amendment—prohibits some (but not all) government interference with the freedom of association. (Choice C) The fact that the private organization invited members of the general public to its activities does not make it a government actor subject to the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because this organization is not a state actor and, therefore, its activities are not subject to the provisions of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"一个城市拥有并经营一个大型公共礼堂。它将礼堂出租给任何希望将其用于会议、讲座、音乐会或比赛的团体。每个使用者必须缴纳损坏押金并支付租金，租金仅根据承租人实际使用建筑物的时间计算。预订遵循先到先得的原则。一个只允许男性担任最高职位的私人组织租用了礼堂举行全国代表大会。该组织计划在该大会上任命新的官员。它广泛宣传了这一活动，邀请公众参加在市礼堂举行的安装仪式。没有任何法规或行政规则禁止该组织将其最高职位限制为男性。适当的原告起诉该私人组织，试图禁止其使用市礼堂来安置新官员。原告的唯一主张是，该组织使用该礼堂举行就职仪式违反了第十四修正案，因为该组织取消了女性担任最高职位的资格。原告会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为受第十四修正案保护的结社自由禁止城市以任何方式干预该组织对城市设施的使用。","B":"不可以，因为该组织不是国家行为体，因此其活动不受第十四修正案的规定约束。","C":"是的，因为第十四修正案禁止此类组织在其邀请公众参加的任何活动中歧视妇女。","D":"是的，因为该组织为此目的使用市礼堂的行为使其行为受到第十四修正案的规定的约束。"},"explanationZh":"宪法（第十三修正案除外）适用于政府行为者。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者，并同样受到宪法的约束。当政府出现以下情况时，就会产生重大参与：\n• 与私人参与者有互惠互利的关系（例如合资企业）\n\n• 通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，通过以法律名义行事的警察）建立联系，或\n\n• 普遍与私人行为者的管理或控制交织在一起。\n\n在这里，私人组织并没有履行传统的、排他性的政府职能，因为私人团体历来使用城市礼堂举办会议（选项 D）。仅仅租赁礼堂并不能肯定地促进该组织的行动或将城市纳入该组织的管理。它也不会建立互惠互利的关系，因为该城市按照先到先得的原则以相同的价格向任何群体提供统一的访问权限。因此，该组织不是受第十四修正案约束的国家行为者。 （选项 A）第一修正案——通过第十四修正案适用于州和市——禁止部分（但不是全部）政府干预结社自由。 （选项 C）私人组织邀请公众参加其活动这一事实并不意味着它是受第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）约束的政府行为者。\n\n教育目标：根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当（1）私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与其活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束——例如，不仅仅是提供获得政府服务或设施的机会。\n\n参考文献\n• Gilmore v. Montgomery, 417 U.S. 556, 574 (1974)（解释说，根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），仅仅私人使用政府设施并不构成国家的重大参与）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当（1）私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或（2）政府大力参与其活动（例如，不仅仅是提供政府服务或设施）时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。","iracZh":{"issue":"原告会胜诉吗？","rule":"根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当（1）私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或（2）政府大力参与其活动（例如，不仅仅是提供政府服务或设施）时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。","analysis":"宪法（第十三修正案除外）适用于政府行为者。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者，并同样受到宪法的约束。当政府出现以下情况时，就会产生重大参与：\n• 与私人参与者有互惠互利的关系（例如合资企业）\n\n• 通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，通过以法律名义行事的警察）建立联系，或\n\n• 普遍与私人行为者的管理或控制交织在一起。\n\n在这里，私人组织并没有履行传统的、排他性的政府职能，因为私人团体历来使用城市礼堂举办会议（选项 D）。仅仅租赁礼堂并不能肯定地促进该组织的行动或将城市纳入该组织的管理。它也不会建立互惠互利的关系，因为该城市按照先到先得的原则以相同的价格向任何群体提供统一的访问权限。因此，该组织不是受第十四修正案约束的国家行为者。 （选项 A）第一修正案——通过第十四修正案适用于州和市——禁止部分（但不是全部）政府干预结社自由。 （选项 C）私人组织邀请公众参加其活动这一事实并不意味着它是受第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）约束的政府行为者。\n\n教育目标：根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当（1）私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与其活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束——例如，不仅仅是提供获得政府服务或设施的机会。\n\n参考文献\n• Gilmore v. Montgomery, 417 U.S. 556, 574 (1974)（解释说，根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），仅仅私人使用政府设施并不构成国家的重大参与）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为该组织不是国家行为体，因此其活动不受第十四修正案的规定约束。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-dfb752435e8daf8e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":215,"sourceQuestionPage":642,"sourceQuestionPages":[642],"sourceExplanationPages":[643,644],"sourceAnswerPages":[643,644],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-216","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"Members of a religious group believe in Lucifer as their Supreme Being. The members of this group meet once a year on top of a mountain, located in a national park, to hold an overnight encampment and a midnight dance around a large campfire. They believe that this overnight encampment and ritual are required by Lucifer to be held on the top of that mountain. U.S. National Park Service rules that have been consistently enforced prohibit all overnight camping and all campfires on the mountain because of the very great dangers overnight camping and campfires would pose in that particular location. As a result, the park superintendent denied a request by the religious group for a permit to conduct these activities on top of the mountain. The park superintendent, who was known to be violently opposed to cults and other unconventional groups, had previously issued permits to conventional religious groups to conduct sunrise services in other areas of that national park. The religious group brought suit in federal court against the U.S. National Park Service and the superintendent of the park to compel issuance of the requested permit. As a matter of constitutional law, was the denial of the permit valid?","choices":{"A":"No, because the free exercise clause of the First Amendment prohibits the Park Service from knowingly interfering with religious conduct.","B":"No, because these facts demonstrate that the action of the Park Service purposefully and invidiously discriminated against the religious group.","C":"Yes, because religiously motivated conduct may be subjected to nondiscriminatory time, place, and manner restrictions that advance important public interests.","D":"Yes, because the establishment clause of the First Amendment prohibits the holding of religious ceremonies on federal land."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The permit denial restricts the religious group's right to free expression by preventing its religiously motivated conduct. However, the permit was denied because of a content- neutral (ie, nondiscriminatory) rule prohibiting all overnight camping and campfires on the mountain—not because of the content of the group's religious conduct. As a result, this time, place, or manner restriction is valid under intermediate scrutiny if it:\n• is narrowly tailored (ie, no greater than necessary) to serve a substantial or important government interest and\n\n• leaves open ample alternative channels of communication.\n\nThe park has an important interest in avoiding the \"very great dangers\" posed by overnight camping and campfires. And the park rule is narrowly tailored to that interest since (1) park safety would be compromised by allowing the prohibited activities and (2) there is no evidence that the group's conduct poses no such safety threat. The rule also provides alternative channels of communication since the group can conduct religious rituals at other times and places. Therefore, this speech restriction is valid. The permit denial also interferes with the group's right to free exercise of religion, which largely prohibits intentional government interference with religion (Choice A). But an incidental interference is permissible if it is rationally related to a legitimate government interest. Here, the permit was denied under a generally applicable park rule (not the superintendent's beliefs), so the religious interference was incidental. And since the rule is rationally related to a legitimate interest in park safety, the permit denial complied with this right (Choice B). (Choice D) The establishment clause prohibits the government from favoring a particular religion or religion generally. Therefore, religious ceremonies on federal land are permissible so long as the government remains neutral toward religion.\n\nEducational objective: First Amendment free speech protections extend to religious conduct. But the government can impose content-neutral restrictions on such conduct so long as they (1) are narrowly tailored to serve an important government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.\n\nReferences\n• Clark v. Cmty. for Creative Non-Violence, 468 U.S. 288, 298–99 (1984) (holding that the denial of a camping permit for a demonstration is a constitutional time, place, and manner restriction on speech).","educationalObjective":"First Amendment free speech protections extend to religious conduct. But the government can impose content-neutral restrictions on such conduct so long as they (1) are narrowly tailored to serve an important government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","references":"• Clark v. Cmty. for Creative Non-Violence, 468 U.S. 288, 298–99 (1984) (holding that the denial of a camping permit for a demonstration is a constitutional time, place, and manner restriction on speech).","rule":"First Amendment free speech protections extend to religious conduct. But the government can impose content-neutral restrictions on such conduct so long as they (1) are narrowly tailored to serve an important government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","irac":{"issue":"As a matter of constitutional law, was the denial of the permit valid?","rule":"First Amendment free speech protections extend to religious conduct. But the government can impose content-neutral restrictions on such conduct so long as they (1) are narrowly tailored to serve an important government interest and (2) leave open ample alternative channels of communication.","analysis":"The permit denial restricts the religious group's right to free expression by preventing its religiously motivated conduct. However, the permit was denied because of a content- neutral (ie, nondiscriminatory) rule prohibiting all overnight camping and campfires on the mountain—not because of the content of the group's religious conduct. As a result, this time, place, or manner restriction is valid under intermediate scrutiny if it:\n• is narrowly tailored (ie, no greater than necessary) to serve a substantial or important government interest and\n\n• leaves open ample alternative channels of communication.\n\nThe park has an important interest in avoiding the \"very great dangers\" posed by overnight camping and campfires. And the park rule is narrowly tailored to that interest since (1) park safety would be compromised by allowing the prohibited activities and (2) there is no evidence that the group's conduct poses no such safety threat. The rule also provides alternative channels of communication since the group can conduct religious rituals at other times and places. Therefore, this speech restriction is valid. The permit denial also interferes with the group's right to free exercise of religion, which largely prohibits intentional government interference with religion (Choice A). But an incidental interference is permissible if it is rationally related to a legitimate government interest. Here, the permit was denied under a generally applicable park rule (not the superintendent's beliefs), so the religious interference was incidental. And since the rule is rationally related to a legitimate interest in park safety, the permit denial complied with this right (Choice B). (Choice D) The establishment clause prohibits the government from favoring a particular religion or religion generally. Therefore, religious ceremonies on federal land are permissible so long as the government remains neutral toward religion.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because religiously motivated conduct may be subjected to nondiscriminatory time, place, and manner restrictions that advance important public interests."},"questionZh":"宗教团体的成员相信路西法是他们的至高无上的存在。该团体的成员每年在国家公园的山顶聚会一次，举行过夜露营和围绕大型篝火举行午夜舞会。他们认为，这种过夜的营地和仪式是路西法要求在那座山的山顶举行的。美国国家公园管理局一贯执行的规定禁止在山上过夜露营和篝火，因为过夜露营和篝火会在该特定地点造成非常大的危险。结果，公园管理员拒绝了该宗教团体要求获得在山顶进行这些活动的许可的请求。众所周知，公园管理员强烈反对邪教和其他非传统团体，此前他曾向传统宗教团体颁发许可证，允许他们在该国家公园的其他地区进行日出服务。该宗教团体向联邦法院起诉美国国家公园管理局和公园管理员，要求强制发放所请求的许可证。作为宪法问题，拒绝许可是否有效？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为第一修正案的宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）禁止公园管理局故意干预宗教行为。","B":"不，因为这些事实表明公园管理局的行为是故意且令人不快的歧视宗教团体。","C":"是的，因为出于宗教动机的行为可能会受到促进重要公共利益的非歧视性时间、地点和方式限制。","D":"是的，因为第一修正案的建国条款禁止在联邦土地上举行宗教仪式。"},"explanationZh":"拒绝许可限制了宗教团体的言论自由权，阻止了其出于宗教动机的行为。然而，许可证被拒绝是因为内容中立（即非歧视）规则禁止在山上过夜露营和篝火，而不是因为该团体的宗教行为内容。因此，如果满足以下条件，则该时间、地点或方式限制在中间审查下是有效的：\n• 是为服务实质性或重要的政府利益而专门定制的（即，不超过必要的范围）\n\n• 留有充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n该公园对避免过夜露营和篝火造成的“非常大的危险”有着重要的兴趣。公园规则是针对这种利益而制定的，因为（1）公园安全会因允许禁止的活动而受到损害，以及（2）没有证据表明该团体的行为不会构成此类安全威胁。该规则还提供了替代的沟通渠道，因为该团体可以在其他时间和地点进行宗教仪式。因此，这一言论限制是有效的。拒绝许可还干扰了该团体自由行使宗教信仰的权利，这在很大程度上禁止政府故意干预宗教（选择 A）。但如果附带的干预与合法的政府利益合理相关，则是允许的。在这里，根据普遍适用的公园规则（而不是管理者的信仰），许可证被拒绝，因此宗教干扰是偶然的。由于该规则与公园安全的合法利益合理相关，因此拒绝许可符合这一权利（选择 B）。 （选项D） 建国条款禁止政府偏袒某一特定宗教或普遍宗教。因此，只要政府对宗教保持中立，就允许在联邦土地上举行宗教仪式。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案对言论自由的保护延伸至宗教行为。但政府可以对此类行为施加内容中立的限制，只要它们（1）专门为服务重要的政府利益而设计，并且（2）保留充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n参考文献\n• 克拉克诉Cmty。 for Creative Non-Violence, 468 U.S. 288, 298-99 (1984)（认为拒绝示威露营许可证是宪法对言论时间、地点和方式的限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案对言论自由的保护延伸到宗教行为。但政府可以对此类行为施加内容中立的限制，只要它们（1）专门为服务重要的政府利益而设计，并且（2）保留充足的替代沟通渠道。","iracZh":{"issue":"作为宪法问题，拒绝许可是否有效？","rule":"第一修正案对言论自由的保护延伸到宗教行为。但政府可以对此类行为施加内容中立的限制，只要它们（1）专门为服务重要的政府利益而设计，并且（2）保留充足的替代沟通渠道。","analysis":"拒绝许可限制了宗教团体的言论自由权，阻止了其出于宗教动机的行为。然而，许可证被拒绝是因为内容中立（即非歧视）规则禁止在山上过夜露营和篝火，而不是因为该团体的宗教行为内容。因此，如果满足以下条件，则该时间、地点或方式限制在中间审查下是有效的：\n• 是为服务实质性或重要的政府利益而专门定制的（即，不超过必要的范围）\n\n• 留有充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n该公园对避免过夜露营和篝火造成的“非常大的危险”有着重要的兴趣。公园规则是针对这种利益而制定的，因为（1）公园安全会因允许禁止的活动而受到损害，以及（2）没有证据表明该团体的行为不会构成此类安全威胁。该规则还提供了替代的沟通渠道，因为该团体可以在其他时间和地点进行宗教仪式。因此，这一言论限制是有效的。拒绝许可还干扰了该团体自由行使宗教信仰的权利，这在很大程度上禁止政府故意干预宗教（选择 A）。但如果附带的干预与合法的政府利益合理相关，则是允许的。在这里，根据普遍适用的公园规则（而不是管理者的信仰），许可证被拒绝，因此宗教干扰是偶然的。由于该规则与公园安全的合法利益合理相关，因此拒绝许可符合这一权利（选择 B）。 （选项D） 建国条款禁止政府偏袒某一特定宗教或普遍宗教。因此，只要政府对宗教保持中立，就允许在联邦土地上举行宗教仪式。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案对言论自由的保护延伸至宗教行为。但政府可以对此类行为施加内容中立的限制，只要它们（1）专门为服务重要的政府利益而设计，并且（2）保留充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n参考文献\n• 克拉克诉Cmty。 for Creative Non-Violence, 468 U.S. 288, 298-99 (1984)（认为拒绝示威露营许可证是宪法对言论时间、地点和方式的限制）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为出于宗教动机的行为可能会受到促进重要公共利益的非歧视性时间、地点和方式限制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"ritual","ipa":"/ɹˈɪtʃuəl/","syllables":"rit·ual","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 仪式, 典礼, 宗教仪式；a. 仪式的, 依仪式进行的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 仪式, 典礼, 宗教仪式；a. 仪式的, 依仪式进行的","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:rituals","sourceSentence":"They believe that this overnight encampment and ritual are required by Lucifer to be held on the top of that mountain.","synonyms":["rite"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f734aa717a0f6f76"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":216,"sourceQuestionPage":645,"sourceQuestionPages":[645],"sourceExplanationPages":[646,647],"sourceAnswerPages":[646,647],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-217","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state administrative agency employs individuals on a permanent and seasonal basis. Some seasonal employees are rehired at the end of their work period, but most are not. On rare and random occasions, the agency has given seasonal employees who were not rehired a statement of reasons and an opportunity for a hearing. No statute or agency rule requires a statement of reasons or an opportunity for a hearing. A man who was hired as a seasonal employee of the agency was not rehired at the end of his work period. The man was not given a statement of reasons or an opportunity for a hearing. The man sued the agency in federal court, requesting a statement of reasons and a hearing. Will the man's suit likely succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man has no property interest entitling him to procedural due process.","B":"No, because the Tenth Amendment reserves to the states the power to set the conditions of state employment.","C":"Yes, because the agency's procedure constituted a bill of attainder.","D":"Yes, because the agency's procedure denied the man the equal protection of the laws."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The man's claim invokes the Fourteenth Amendment right to procedural due process, which requires states to follow certain procedures when they intentionally deprive a person of life, liberty, or property. These procedures include giving persons:\n• notice of the charges, proceedings, or other actions against them and\n• a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker. Public employees are only entitled to these procedures if they have a property interest in their employment. This exists when the employee (1) has an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receives assurances of continued employment. But since none of these conditions apply to a seasonal (ie, probationary) public employee, the man had no property interest entitling him to due process. Therefore, his suit requesting a statement of reasons and a hearing will likely fail. (Choice B) Under the Tenth Amendment, all powers not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution—including the power to set the conditions of state employment—are reserved to the states. But when exercising such powers, states must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, procedural due process). (Choice C) A bill of attainder is a legislative act that punishes a specified person or group without a judicial trial. But since the agency's procedure is applied against random persons, it is not a bill of attainder. (Choice D) The equal protection clause prohibits the government from treating similarly situated people differently—even unintentionally—without adequate justification. But when, as here, the government does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, its actions will likely be upheld under rational basis scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: Public employees have a protected property interest in their jobs if they (1) have an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receive assurances of continued employment. Therefore, seasonal (ie, probationary) public employees can be terminated without due process.\n\nReferences\n• Bd. of Regents of State Colls. v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 576–77 (1972) (explaining when public employees have a protected interest in continued employment).","educationalObjective":"Public employees have a protected property interest in their jobs if they (1) have an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receive assurances of continued employment. Therefore, seasonal (ie, probationary) public employees can be terminated without due process.","references":"• Bd. of Regents of State Colls. v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 576–77 (1972) (explaining when public employees have a protected interest in continued employment).","rule":"Public employees have a protected property interest in their jobs if they (1) have an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receive assurances of continued employment. Therefore, seasonal (ie, probationary) public employees can be terminated without due process.","irac":{"issue":"Will the man's suit likely succeed?","rule":"Public employees have a protected property interest in their jobs if they (1) have an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receive assurances of continued employment. Therefore, seasonal (ie, probationary) public employees can be terminated without due process.","analysis":"The man's claim invokes the Fourteenth Amendment right to procedural due process, which requires states to follow certain procedures when they intentionally deprive a person of life, liberty, or property. These procedures include giving persons:\n• notice of the charges, proceedings, or other actions against them and\n• a meaningful opportunity to be heard by a neutral decision-maker. Public employees are only entitled to these procedures if they have a property interest in their employment. This exists when the employee (1) has an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receives assurances of continued employment. But since none of these conditions apply to a seasonal (ie, probationary) public employee, the man had no property interest entitling him to due process. Therefore, his suit requesting a statement of reasons and a hearing will likely fail. (Choice B) Under the Tenth Amendment, all powers not expressly granted to the federal government by the Constitution—including the power to set the conditions of state employment—are reserved to the states. But when exercising such powers, states must comply with other constitutional provisions (eg, procedural due process). (Choice C) A bill of attainder is a legislative act that punishes a specified person or group without a judicial trial. But since the agency's procedure is applied against random persons, it is not a bill of attainder. (Choice D) The equal protection clause prohibits the government from treating similarly situated people differently—even unintentionally—without adequate justification. But when, as here, the government does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, its actions will likely be upheld under rational basis scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the man has no property interest entitling him to procedural due process."},"questionZh":"国家行政机构长期和季节性地雇用个人。一些季节性员工在工作结束后会被重新雇用，但大多数人不会。在极少数和随机的情况下，该机构会给没有被重新雇用的季节性员工一份理由说明和听证会的机会。没有法规或机构规则要求说明理由或听证会的机会。一名被聘为该机构季节性雇员的男子在工作期满后没有被重新雇用。该男子没有得到理由陈述或听证会的机会。该男子向联邦法院起诉该机构，要求陈述理由并举行听证会。男方的诉讼可能会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该男子没有财产利益，没有权利让他接受正当程序。","B":"不，因为第十修正案保留各州设定州就业条件的权力。","C":"是的，因为该机构的程序构成了剥夺公民权法案。","D":"是的，因为该机构的程序剥夺了该男子受到法律的平等保护。"},"explanationZh":"该男子的主张援引了第十四修正案的程序正当程序权，该权利要求各州在故意剥夺一个人的生命、自由或财产时遵循某些程序。这些程序包括向人员提供：\n• 针对他们的指控、诉讼或其他行动的通知以及\n• 中立决策者发表意见的有意义的机会。公职人员只有在其工作中拥有财产权益时才有权享受这些程序。当雇员 (1) 拥有持续的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证时，就会存在这种情况。但由于这些条件均不适用于季节性（即试用期）公职人员，因此该男子没有财产权益，使他有权诉诸正当程序。因此，他要求陈述理由和举行听证会的诉讼很可能会失败。 （选项 B）根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力（包括设定州就业条件的权力）均保留给各州。但在行使此类权力时，各州必须遵守其他宪法规定（例如程序性正当程序）。 （选项C） 剥夺公民权法案是一种未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为。但由于该机构的程序适用于随机的人，因此它不是剥夺公民权法案。 （选项 D） 平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）禁止政府在没有充分理由的情况下（即使是无意的）区别对待处境相似的人。但是，当政府没有像这里一样对基本权利或受保护阶层产生实质性影响时，其行为可能会在合理基础的审查下得到支持。\n\n教育目标：如果公职人员 (1) 拥有持续有效的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证，那么他们在工作中的财产利益就受到保护。因此，季节性（即试用期）公职人员可以在未经正当程序的情况下被解雇。\n\n参考文献\n• Bd。州立大学摄政王。 v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 576–77 (1972)（解释公共雇员在继续就业中何时享有受保护的利益）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果公职人员 (1) 拥有持续有效的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证，那么他们在工作中的财产利益就受到保护。因此，季节性（即试用期）公职人员可以在未经正当程序的情况下被解雇。","iracZh":{"issue":"男方的诉讼可能会成功吗？","rule":"如果公职人员 (1) 拥有持续有效的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证，那么他们在工作中的财产利益就受到保护。因此，季节性（即试用期）公职人员可以在未经正当程序的情况下被解雇。","analysis":"该男子的主张援引了第十四修正案的程序正当程序权，该权利要求各州在故意剥夺一个人的生命、自由或财产时遵循某些程序。这些程序包括向人员提供：\n• 针对他们的指控、诉讼或其他行动的通知以及\n• 中立决策者发表意见的有意义的机会。公职人员只有在其工作中拥有财产权益时才有权享受这些程序。当雇员 (1) 拥有持续的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证时，就会存在这种情况。但由于这些条件均不适用于季节性（即试用期）公职人员，因此该男子没有财产权益，使他有权诉诸正当程序。因此，他要求陈述理由和举行听证会的诉讼很可能会失败。 （选项 B）根据第十修正案，宪法未明确授予联邦政府的所有权力（包括设定州就业条件的权力）均保留给各州。但在行使此类权力时，各州必须遵守其他宪法规定（例如程序性正当程序）。 （选项C） 剥夺公民权法案是一种未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为。但由于该机构的程序适用于随机的人，因此它不是剥夺公民权法案。 （选项 D） 平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）禁止政府在没有充分理由的情况下（即使是无意的）区别对待处境相似的人。但是，当政府没有像这里一样对基本权利或受保护阶层产生实质性影响时，其行为可能会在合理基础的审查下得到支持。\n\n教育目标：如果公职人员 (1) 拥有持续有效的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证，那么他们在工作中的财产利益就受到保护。因此，季节性（即试用期）公职人员可以在未经正当程序的情况下被解雇。\n\n参考文献\n• Bd。州立大学摄政王。 v. Roth, 408 U.S. 564, 576–77 (1972)（解释公共雇员在继续就业中何时享有受保护的利益）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为该男子没有财产利益，没有权利让他接受正当程序。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-c4dba56348078ce8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":217,"sourceQuestionPage":648,"sourceQuestionPages":[648],"sourceExplanationPages":[649,650],"sourceAnswerPages":[649,650],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-218","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A number of psychotherapists routinely send mailings to victims of car accidents informing the victims of the possibility of developing post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) as the result of the accidents, and offering psychotherapy services. Although PTSD is a possible result of a car accident, it is not common. Many accident victims in a particular state who received the mailings complained that the mailings were disturbing and an invasion of their privacy. These victims also reported that as a result of the mailings, their regard for psychotherapists and for psychotherapy as a form of treatment had diminished. In response, the state enacted a law prohibiting any licensed psychotherapist from sending mailings that raised the concern of PTSD to any car accident victim in the state until 30 days after the accident. The state justified the law as an effort to address the victims' complaints as well as to protect the reputation of psychotherapy as a form of treatment. Is this law constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because the law singles out one type of message for prohibition while allowing others.","B":"No, because the mailings provide information to consumers.","C":"Yes, because mailings suggesting the possibility of developing PTSD as the result of an accident are misleading.","D":"Yes, because the law protects the privacy of accident victims and the public regard for psychotherapy without being substantially more restrictive than necessary."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Commercial speech encompasses all types of economically-oriented expression (eg, advertisements). And it receives intermediate First Amendment protection so long as it is not false or misleading and does not concern unlawful activity. Therefore, government regulation of protected commercial speech is only constitutional if it (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is narrowly drawn—ie, not substantially more restrictive than necessary—to serve that interest. Here, the state law prohibits psychotherapists from sending mailings about PTSD (including offers of services) to car-accident victims within 30 days of their accidents. These commercial mailings concern a lawful activity and are not false or misleading since they only suggest the possibility (not the likelihood) of developing PTSD (Choice C). And since a 30-day waiting period (1) directly advances the state's substantial interests in protecting victims' privacy and psychotherapy's reputation and (2) is not substantially more restrictive than necessary, the law is constitutional. (Choice A) Laws that prohibit one type of message while allowing others (ie, content-based restrictions) are generally struck down under strict scrutiny. But since the state law concerns commercial speech—which only undergoes intermediate scrutiny—it permissibly targets PTSD mailings. (Choice B) The mailings are protected commercial speech because they seek to provide consumers with information about PTSD—not to mislead them. But the government can still regulate the content of the mailings because the regulation meets intermediate scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: Commercial speech that is false/misleading or concerns unlawful activity can be freely regulated. But any other commercial speech can only be regulated if the government shows, under intermediate scrutiny, that its regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is narrowly drawn to serve that interest.\n\nReferences\n• Fla. Bar v. Went for It, Inc., 515 U.S. 618, 634–35 (1995) (upholding a state bar rule prohibiting attorneys from mailing solicitations to car-accident victims for 30 days after their accidents).","educationalObjective":"Commercial speech that is false/misleading or concerns unlawful activity can be freely regulated. But any other commercial speech can only be regulated if the government shows, under intermediate scrutiny, that its regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is narrowly drawn to serve that interest.","references":"• Fla. Bar v. Went for It, Inc., 515 U.S. 618, 634–35 (1995) (upholding a state bar rule prohibiting attorneys from mailing solicitations to car-accident victims for 30 days after their accidents).","rule":"Commercial speech that is false/misleading or concerns unlawful activity can be freely regulated. But any other commercial speech can only be regulated if the government shows, under intermediate scrutiny, that its regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is narrowly drawn to serve that interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is this law constitutional?","rule":"Commercial speech that is false/misleading or concerns unlawful activity can be freely regulated. But any other commercial speech can only be regulated if the government shows, under intermediate scrutiny, that its regulation (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is narrowly drawn to serve that interest.","analysis":"Commercial speech encompasses all types of economically-oriented expression (eg, advertisements). And it receives intermediate First Amendment protection so long as it is not false or misleading and does not concern unlawful activity. Therefore, government regulation of protected commercial speech is only constitutional if it (1) directly advances a substantial government interest and (2) is narrowly drawn—ie, not substantially more restrictive than necessary—to serve that interest. Here, the state law prohibits psychotherapists from sending mailings about PTSD (including offers of services) to car-accident victims within 30 days of their accidents. These commercial mailings concern a lawful activity and are not false or misleading since they only suggest the possibility (not the likelihood) of developing PTSD (Choice C). And since a 30-day waiting period (1) directly advances the state's substantial interests in protecting victims' privacy and psychotherapy's reputation and (2) is not substantially more restrictive than necessary, the law is constitutional. (Choice A) Laws that prohibit one type of message while allowing others (ie, content-based restrictions) are generally struck down under strict scrutiny. But since the state law concerns commercial speech—which only undergoes intermediate scrutiny—it permissibly targets PTSD mailings. (Choice B) The mailings are protected commercial speech because they seek to provide consumers with information about PTSD—not to mislead them. But the government can still regulate the content of the mailings because the regulation meets intermediate scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the law protects the privacy of accident victims and the public regard for psychotherapy without being substantially more restrictive than necessary."},"questionZh":"许多心理治疗师定期向车祸受害者发送邮件，告知受害者因事故而可能患上创伤后应激障碍（PTSD），并提供心理治疗服务。尽管创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）可能是车祸的结果，但并不常见。特定州的许多收到邮件的事故受害者抱怨这些邮件令人不安并侵犯了他们的隐私。这些受害者还报告说，由于这些邮件，他们对心理治疗师和心理治疗作为一种治疗形式的重视程度有所减弱。作为回应，该州颁布了一项法律，禁止任何有执照的心理治疗师在事故发生后 30 天内向该州的任何车祸受害者发送引起 PTSD 担忧的邮件。该州认为该法律是为了解决受害者的投诉并保护心理治疗作为一种治疗形式的声誉。这部法律合宪吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为法律只列出一种类型的信息予以禁止，同时允许其他类型的信息。","B":"不，因为邮件向消费者提供信息。","C":"是的，因为暗示事故可能导致创伤后应激障碍的邮件具有误导性。","D":"是的，因为法律保护事故受害者的隐私和公众对心理治疗的尊重，但没有过多的限制。"},"explanationZh":"商业言论包括所有类型的经济导向的表达方式（例如广告）。只要它不虚假或不具误导性并且不涉及非法活动，它就会受到第一修正案的中间保护。因此，政府对受保护的商业言论的监管只有在以下情况下才符合宪法：(1) 直接促进政府的重大利益，并且 (2) 是狭隘的——即没有比必要的限制更多——以服务于该利益。州法律禁止心理治疗师在事故发生后 30 天内向车祸受害者发送有关 PTSD 的邮件（包括提供服务）。这些商业邮件涉及合法活动，不存在虚假或误导性，因为它们仅表明存在发生 PTSD 的可能性（而非可能性）（选项 C）。由于 30 天的等待期 (1) 直接促进了国家在保护受害者隐私和心理治疗声誉方面的实质性利益，并且 (2) 并没有比必要的限制更多，因此该法律是符合宪法的。 （选择A）禁止一种类型的消息而允许其他类型的消息（即基于内容的限制）的法律通常会在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下被废除。但由于州法律涉及商业言论（仅经过中间审查），因此它可以针对 PTSD 邮件。 （选择 B）这些邮件是受保护的商业言论，因为它们试图向消费者提供有关 PTSD 的信息，而不是误导他们。但政府仍然可以规范邮件的内容，因为该规定经过了中间审查。\n\n教育目标：可以自由监管虚假/误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论。但任何其他商业言论只有在政府经过中间审查表明其监管（1）直接促进重大政府利益并且（2）狭隘地服务于该利益的情况下才能受到监管。\n\n参考文献\n• Fla. Bar v. Went for It, Inc., 515 U.S. 618, 634–35 (1995)（维护州律师协会规则，禁止律师在事故发生后 30 天内向车祸受害者邮寄请愿书）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"虚假/误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论可以受到自由监管。但任何其他商业言论只有在政府经过中间审查表明其监管（1）直接促进重大政府利益并且（2）狭隘地服务于该利益的情况下才能受到监管。","iracZh":{"issue":"这部法律合宪吗？","rule":"虚假/误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论可以受到自由监管。但任何其他商业言论只有在政府经过中间审查表明其监管（1）直接促进重大政府利益并且（2）狭隘地服务于该利益的情况下才能受到监管。","analysis":"商业言论包括所有类型的经济导向的表达方式（例如广告）。只要它不虚假或不具误导性并且不涉及非法活动，它就会受到第一修正案的中间保护。因此，政府对受保护的商业言论的监管只有在以下情况下才符合宪法：(1) 直接促进政府的重大利益，并且 (2) 是狭隘的——即没有比必要的限制更多——以服务于该利益。州法律禁止心理治疗师在事故发生后 30 天内向车祸受害者发送有关 PTSD 的邮件（包括提供服务）。这些商业邮件涉及合法活动，不存在虚假或误导性，因为它们仅表明存在发生 PTSD 的可能性（而非可能性）（选项 C）。由于 30 天的等待期 (1) 直接促进了国家在保护受害者隐私和心理治疗声誉方面的实质性利益，并且 (2) 并没有比必要的限制更多，因此该法律是符合宪法的。 （选择A）禁止一种类型的消息而允许其他类型的消息（即基于内容的限制）的法律通常会在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下被废除。但由于州法律涉及商业言论（仅经过中间审查），因此它可以针对 PTSD 邮件。 （选择 B）这些邮件是受保护的商业言论，因为它们试图向消费者提供有关 PTSD 的信息，而不是误导他们。但政府仍然可以规范邮件的内容，因为该规定经过了中间审查。\n\n教育目标：可以自由监管虚假/误导性或涉及非法活动的商业言论。但任何其他商业言论只有在政府经过中间审查表明其监管（1）直接促进重大政府利益并且（2）狭隘地服务于该利益的情况下才能受到监管。\n\n参考文献\n• Fla. Bar v. Went for It, Inc., 515 U.S. 618, 634–35 (1995)（维护州律师协会规则，禁止律师在事故发生后 30 天内向车祸受害者邮寄请愿书）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为法律保护事故受害者的隐私和公众对心理治疗的尊重，但没有过多的限制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-58c77a48304d1e8c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":218,"sourceQuestionPage":651,"sourceQuestionPages":[651],"sourceExplanationPages":[652,653],"sourceAnswerPages":[652,653],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-219","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"A state law provides that a person who has been divorced may not marry again unless he or she is current on all child-support payments. A woman who was refused a marriage license pursuant to this law sued the appropriate state officials. What standard should the court apply in reviewing the constitutionality of this law?","choices":{"A":"The state must show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.","B":"The state must show that the law is substantially related to an important government interest.","C":"The woman must show that the law serves no important public purpose.","D":"The woman must show that the legislature did not have a rational basis for enacting the law."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives A government action that treats similarly situated individuals differently can be challenged under the equal protection clause. The standard under which a court will review this challenge depends on the alleged discrimination. A law that discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right (eg, marriage) will be subject to strict scrutiny. Here, a state law discriminates against divorced persons by requiring them to be current on all child-support payments before remarriage—thereby substantially impacting the\n\nfundamental right to marry. As a result, the court will review that law under the strict scrutiny standard and will only uphold it if the state proves that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest. (Choices B & C) Only laws that impact a quasi-suspect class (ie, sex/gender, nonmarital birth) require the court to apply intermediate scrutiny. Under this standard of review, the government must show that the law is substantially related to an important government interest (eg, an important public purpose). (Choice D) The woman would have had to show that the legislature did not have a rational basis for enacting the law (ie, rational basis review) if the law had not impacted a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class.\n\nEducational objective: Discriminatory laws that impact a fundamental right (eg, marriage) or suspect class must satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, the government must show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Zablocki v. Redhail, 434 U.S. 374, 387–88 (1978) (holding that strict scrutiny applies when state action impacts the fundamental right of marriage).","educationalObjective":"Discriminatory laws that impact a fundamental right (eg, marriage) or suspect class must satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, the government must show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• Zablocki v. Redhail, 434 U.S. 374, 387–88 (1978) (holding that strict scrutiny applies when state action impacts the fundamental right of marriage).","rule":"Discriminatory laws that impact a fundamental right (eg, marriage) or suspect class must satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, the government must show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"What standard should the court apply in reviewing the constitutionality of this law?","rule":"Discriminatory laws that impact a fundamental right (eg, marriage) or suspect class must satisfy the strict scrutiny test—ie, the government must show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives A government action that treats similarly situated individuals differently can be challenged under the equal protection clause. The standard under which a court will review this challenge depends on the alleged discrimination. A law that discriminates against a suspect class or substantially impacts a fundamental right (eg, marriage) will be subject to strict scrutiny. Here, a state law discriminates against divorced persons by requiring them to be current on all child-support payments before remarriage—thereby substantially impacting the\n\nfundamental right to marry. As a result, the court will review that law under the strict scrutiny standard and will only uphold it if the state proves that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest. (Choices B & C) Only laws that impact a quasi-suspect class (ie, sex/gender, nonmarital birth) require the court to apply intermediate scrutiny. Under this standard of review, the government must show that the law is substantially related to an important government interest (eg, an important public purpose). (Choice D) The woman would have had to show that the legislature did not have a rational basis for enacting the law (ie, rational basis review) if the law had not impacted a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class.","conclusion":"Answer A: The state must show that the law is necessary to serve a compelling government interest."},"questionZh":"州法律规定，离婚者不得再婚，除非他或她能及时支付所有子女抚养费。一名根据该法律被拒绝领取结婚证的妇女起诉了有关国家官员。法院应当以什么标准来审查该法律的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"国家必须证明法律对于服务政府的迫切利益是必要的。","B":"国家必须证明该法律与重要的政府利益密切相关。","C":"该妇女必须表明法律不服务于重要的公共目的。","D":"该妇女必须证明立法机关制定该法律没有合理的基础。"},"explanationZh":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 政府以不同方式对待处境相似的个人的行为可能会根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。法院审查这一质疑的标准取决于所指控的歧视。歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利（例如婚姻）的法律将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这里，一项州法律歧视离婚者，要求他们在再婚前及时支付所有子女抚养费，从而严重影响了离婚者的生活。\n\n结婚的基本权利。因此，法院将在严格的审查标准下审查该法律，并且只有在州证明该法律对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的时才会维持该法律。 （选项 B 和 C）只有影响准嫌疑人类别（即性/性别、非婚生育）的法律才要求法院进行中间审查。根据这一审查标准，政府必须证明该法律与重要的政府利益（例如重要的公共目的）实质上相关。 （选项D）如果该法律没有影响基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层，那么该妇女就必须证明立法机关没有制定该法律的合理基础（即合理基础审查）。\n\n教育目标：影响基本权利（例如婚姻）或可疑阶层的歧视性法律必须满足严格的审查测试，即政府必须表明该法律对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Zablocki v. Redhail, 434 U.S. 374, 387–88 (1978)（认为当国家行为（state action）影响婚姻基本权利时，适用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"影响基本权利（例如婚姻）或可疑阶层的歧视性法律必须满足严格的审查测试，即政府必须表明该法律对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应当以什么标准来审查该法律的合宪性？","rule":"影响基本权利（例如婚姻）或可疑阶层的歧视性法律必须满足严格的审查测试，即政府必须表明该法律对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","analysis":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 政府以不同方式对待处境相似的个人的行为可能会根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。法院审查这一质疑的标准取决于所指控的歧视。歧视可疑阶层或严重影响基本权利（例如婚姻）的法律将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这里，一项州法律歧视离婚者，要求他们在再婚前及时支付所有子女抚养费，从而严重影响了离婚者的生活。\n\n结婚的基本权利。因此，法院将在严格的审查标准下审查该法律，并且只有在州证明该法律对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的时才会维持该法律。 （选项 B 和 C）只有影响准嫌疑人类别（即性/性别、非婚生育）的法律才要求法院进行中间审查。根据这一审查标准，政府必须证明该法律与重要的政府利益（例如重要的公共目的）实质上相关。 （选项D）如果该法律没有影响基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层，那么该妇女就必须证明立法机关没有制定该法律的合理基础（即合理基础审查）。\n\n教育目标：影响基本权利（例如婚姻）或可疑阶层的歧视性法律必须满足严格的审查测试，即政府必须表明该法律对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Zablocki v. Redhail, 434 U.S. 374, 387–88 (1978)（认为当国家行为（state action）影响婚姻基本权利时，适用严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 A：国家必须证明法律对于服务政府的迫切利益是必要的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":219,"sourceQuestionPage":654,"sourceQuestionPages":[654],"sourceExplanationPages":[655,656],"sourceAnswerPages":[655,656],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-220","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"The Federal Family Film Enhancement Act assesses an excise tax of 10% on the price of admission to public movie theaters when they show films that contain actual or simulated scenes of human sexual intercourse. Which of the following is the strongest argument against the constitutionality of this federal act?","choices":{"A":"The act imposes a prior restraint on the freedom of speech protected by the First Amendment.","B":"The act imposes a tax solely on the basis of the content of speech without adequate justification and, therefore, it is prohibited by the freedom of speech clause of the First Amendment.","C":"The act is not rationally related to any legitimate national interest.","D":"The act violates the equal protection concepts embodied in the due process clause of the Fifth Amendment because it imposes a tax on the price of admission to view certain films and not on the price of admission to view comparable live performances."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Speech regulations\n\nType Definition Test\n\nContent-\n• Facial discrimination Strict scrutiny based\n• Primary purpose targets\n• Necessary & narrowly tailored message, subject, or idea to achieve compelling interest Content-\n• Regulates time, place, or Intermediate scrutiny neutral manner of speech\n• Narrowly tailored to achieve important interest\n• Alternative avenues of communication remain The First Amendment free speech clause protects the right to freely communicate and receive information and ideas. To ensure such protection, content-based restrictions on speech are presumptively unconstitutional and subject to strict scrutiny. This means that the government can only restrict speech based on what is being said (ie, its message or ideas) if it can prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest—a nearly impossible task. Here, the act is a content-based restriction on speech because it imposes an excise tax on the price of admission to theaters when they show films that depict human sexual intercourse. And since it is highly unlikely that this act can survive strict scrutiny, the strongest argument against its constitutionality is that it imposes a tax based solely on the content of speech without adequate justification. (Choice A) A prior restraint on speech is a highly disfavored government action that prohibits speech before it even occurs (eg, injunction). But the act here is not a prior restraint on speech because it does not prevent the films from being shown—it merely imposes a tax. (Choice C) If the federal act is not rationally related to a legitimate national interest, it cannot pass rational basis scrutiny—let alone strict scrutiny. But this argument would likely fail since laws almost always survive rational basis review. (Choice D) This act may trigger the equal protection concepts embodied in the Fifth Amendment, which apply when the government treats similarly situated people differently. But since the movie theater patrons who must pay this tax are not part of a protected class, this is not a strong argument for challenging the act's validity.\n\nEducational objective: Content-based restrictions on speech are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny. This incredibly stringent standard requires the government to prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Ark. Writers' Project, Inc. v. Ragland, 481 U.S. 221, 231 (1987) (applying strict scrutiny to a tax scheme that exempts certain magazines based on content).","educationalObjective":"Content-based restrictions on speech are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny. This incredibly stringent standard requires the government to prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","references":"• Ark. Writers' Project, Inc. v. Ragland, 481 U.S. 221, 231 (1987) (applying strict scrutiny to a tax scheme that exempts certain magazines based on content).","rule":"Content-based restrictions on speech are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny. This incredibly stringent standard requires the government to prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the strongest argument against the constitutionality of this federal act?","rule":"Content-based restrictions on speech are unconstitutional unless they survive strict scrutiny. This incredibly stringent standard requires the government to prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest.","analysis":"Speech regulations\n\nType Definition Test\n\nContent-\n• Facial discrimination Strict scrutiny based\n• Primary purpose targets\n• Necessary & narrowly tailored message, subject, or idea to achieve compelling interest Content-\n• Regulates time, place, or Intermediate scrutiny neutral manner of speech\n• Narrowly tailored to achieve important interest\n• Alternative avenues of communication remain The First Amendment free speech clause protects the right to freely communicate and receive information and ideas. To ensure such protection, content-based restrictions on speech are presumptively unconstitutional and subject to strict scrutiny. This means that the government can only restrict speech based on what is being said (ie, its message or ideas) if it can prove that the restriction is necessary and narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling government interest—a nearly impossible task. Here, the act is a content-based restriction on speech because it imposes an excise tax on the price of admission to theaters when they show films that depict human sexual intercourse. And since it is highly unlikely that this act can survive strict scrutiny, the strongest argument against its constitutionality is that it imposes a tax based solely on the content of speech without adequate justification. (Choice A) A prior restraint on speech is a highly disfavored government action that prohibits speech before it even occurs (eg, injunction). But the act here is not a prior restraint on speech because it does not prevent the films from being shown—it merely imposes a tax. (Choice C) If the federal act is not rationally related to a legitimate national interest, it cannot pass rational basis scrutiny—let alone strict scrutiny. But this argument would likely fail since laws almost always survive rational basis review. (Choice D) This act may trigger the equal protection concepts embodied in the Fifth Amendment, which apply when the government treats similarly situated people differently. But since the movie theater patrons who must pay this tax are not part of a protected class, this is not a strong argument for challenging the act's validity.","conclusion":"Answer B: The act imposes a tax solely on the basis of the content of speech without adequate justification and, therefore, it is prohibited by the freedom of speech clause of the First Amendment."},"questionZh":"《联邦家庭电影增强法案》规定，当公共电影院放映包含真实或模拟人类性交场景的电影时，将征收 10% 的消费税。以下哪一项是反对该联邦法案合宪性的最有力论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"该法案对受第一修正案保护的言论自由施加了事先限制。","B":"该法案仅根据言论内容征税，没有充分的理由，因此受到第一修正案言论自由条款的禁止。","C":"该行为与任何合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。","D":"该法案违反了第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）中体现的平等保护概念，因为它对观看某些电影的入场费征税，而不是对观看类似现场表演的入场费征税。"},"explanationZh":"言论规定\n\n类型定义测试\n\n内容-\n• 面部歧视 基于严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）\n• 主要目的目标\n• 必要且精心定制的信息、主题或想法，以实现引人注目的兴趣 内容-\n• 规定时间、地点或中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）中立的言论方式\n• 为实现重要利益而进行的狭隘定制\n• 替代沟通途径仍然存在 第一修正案言论自由条款保护自由沟通和接收信息和想法的权利。为了确保这种保护，基于内容的言论限制被推定为违宪，并受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这意味着，如果政府能够证明限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的，那么政府只能根据所说的内容（即其信息或想法）来限制言论——这几乎是不可能的任务。在这里，该法案是基于内容的言论限制，因为当剧院放映描绘人类性交的电影时，它会对影院的入场费征收消费税。由于该法案不太可能通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此反对其合宪性的最有力论据是，它仅根据言论内容征税，没有充分的理由。 （选择 A） 事先限制言论是一种非常不受欢迎的政府行为，它在言论发生之前就禁止言论（例如，禁令）。但这里的法案并不是对言论的事先限制，因为它并不阻止电影的放映——它只是征税。 （选项C）如果联邦法案与合法的国家利益没有合理的联系，它就无法通过合理基础的审查——更不用说严格的审查了。但这一论点很可能会失败，因为法律几乎总是能经受住合理基础审查。 （选项D）该法案可能会触发第五修正案中体现的平等保护概念，该概念适用于政府以不同方式对待处境相似的人的情况。但由于必须缴纳这项税款的电影院顾客不属于受保护阶层，因此这并不是质疑该法案有效性的有力论据。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论限制是违宪的，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这一极其严格的标准要求政府证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。\n\n参考文献\n• Ark. Writers' Project, Inc. v. Ragland, 481 U.S. 221, 231 (1987)（对根据内容免除某些杂志的税收计划进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于内容的言论限制是违宪的，除非它们能够通过严格的审查。这一极其严格的标准要求政府证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项是反对该联邦法案合宪性的最有力论据？","rule":"基于内容的言论限制是违宪的，除非它们能够通过严格的审查。这一极其严格的标准要求政府证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。","analysis":"言论规定\n\n类型定义测试\n\n内容-\n• 面部歧视 基于严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）\n• 主要目的目标\n• 必要且精心定制的信息、主题或想法，以实现引人注目的兴趣 内容-\n• 规定时间、地点或中度审查（intermediate scrutiny）中立的言论方式\n• 为实现重要利益而进行的狭隘定制\n• 替代沟通途径仍然存在 第一修正案言论自由条款保护自由沟通和接收信息和想法的权利。为了确保这种保护，基于内容的言论限制被推定为违宪，并受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这意味着，如果政府能够证明限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的，那么政府只能根据所说的内容（即其信息或想法）来限制言论——这几乎是不可能的任务。在这里，该法案是基于内容的言论限制，因为当剧院放映描绘人类性交的电影时，它会对影院的入场费征收消费税。由于该法案不太可能通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），因此反对其合宪性的最有力论据是，它仅根据言论内容征税，没有充分的理由。 （选择 A） 事先限制言论是一种非常不受欢迎的政府行为，它在言论发生之前就禁止言论（例如，禁令）。但这里的法案并不是对言论的事先限制，因为它并不阻止电影的放映——它只是征税。 （选项C）如果联邦法案与合法的国家利益没有合理的联系，它就无法通过合理基础的审查——更不用说严格的审查了。但这一论点很可能会失败，因为法律几乎总是能经受住合理基础审查。 （选项D）该法案可能会触发第五修正案中体现的平等保护概念，该概念适用于政府以不同方式对待处境相似的人的情况。但由于必须缴纳这项税款的电影院顾客不属于受保护阶层，因此这并不是质疑该法案有效性的有力论据。\n\n教育目标：基于内容的言论限制是违宪的，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。这一极其严格的标准要求政府证明该限制是必要的，并且是为了实现令人信服的政府利益而量身定制的。\n\n参考文献\n• Ark. Writers' Project, Inc. v. Ragland, 481 U.S. 221, 231 (1987)（对根据内容免除某些杂志的税收计划进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","conclusion":"答案 B：该法案仅根据言论内容征税，没有充分的理由，因此受到第一修正案言论自由条款的禁止。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":220,"sourceQuestionPage":657,"sourceQuestionPages":[657],"sourceExplanationPages":[658,659],"sourceAnswerPages":[658,659],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-221","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"A city school board issued a rule authorizing public school principals to punish, after a hearing, students who violate the board's student behavior code. According to the rule, violators of the behavior code may be punished in a variety of ways, including being required to sit in designated school-confinement rooms during all school hours, with their hands clasped in front of them, for a period of up to 15 school days. A fifth-grade student in a city elementary school was charged with placing chewed bubble gum on a classmate's chair, a violation of the student behavior code. He had never violated the code before and was otherwise an attentive and well-behaved student. After a hearing on the charges, the student's principal determined that the student had violated the behavior code in the manner charged and ordered the student to spend the next 15 school days in the school-confinement room with his hands clasped in front of him. The student's parents file suit in federal court challenging, solely on constitutional grounds, the principal's action in ordering the student to spend the next 15 school days in the school- confinement room with his hands clasped in front of him. Which of the following arguments would be most helpful to the student's parents in this suit?","choices":{"A":"Because application of the school board rule in this case denies the student freedom of movement and subjects him to bodily restraint in a manner grossly disproportionate to his offense and circumstances, it violates the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"Because the school board rule is enforced initially by administrative rather than judicial proceedings, it constitutes a prohibited bill of attainder.","C":"Because the school board rule is substantially overbroad in relation to any legitimate purpose, it constitutes a facial violation of the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"Because the school board rule limits the freedom of movement of students and subjects them to bodily restraint, it denies them a privilege and immunity of citizenship guaranteed them by Article IV, Section 2."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Fourteenth Amendment protections\n\nClause Applicability\n\nEqual protection Discriminatory treatment of similarly situated people\n\nDue process Substantive – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification\n\nProcedural – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate process\n\nPrivileges or Interference with rights of national citizenship immunities*\n\n*Rarely a correct answer.\n\nSubstantive due process prohibits the government from depriving persons of life, liberty, or property (eg, freedom of movement) without adequate justification. At a minimum, due process requires that any such government action be rationally related to a legitimate government interest (ie, rational basis review). A government action that is grossly disproportionate to a person's offense or circumstances cannot satisfy this standard of review. Here, the principal applied a school board rule and ordered the student to spend 15 days in confinement after he placed gum on a classmate's chair. But the student had never violated the behavior code before and was otherwise attentive and well-behaved. As a result, the parents' best argument is that the application of the school board rule violates due process because it subjects the student to bodily restraint in a manner grossly disproportionate to his offense and circumstances. (Choice B) State and federal governments are prohibited from passing bills of attainder— ie, legislative acts that punish a specific person or group without a judicial trial. But since this school board rule allows punishment of any student who violates the behavior code— not just a specific student or group—it is not a bill of attainder. (Choice C) A rule is facially invalid (eg, substantially overbroad) if no application of the rule is constitutional. But the school board rule is not a facial violation of the equal protection clause since it applies equally to all students. (Choice D) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state residents by denying them a right of state citizenship. This clause does not apply here since the student is a resident of the school district—not an out-of-state resident.\n\nEducational objective: Substantive due process prohibits government deprivations of life, liberty, or property that are not rationally related to a legitimate government interest—eg, grossly disproportionate punishments.\n\nReferences\n• Cooper Indus. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., 532 U.S. 424, 433–35 (2001) (explaining that the due process clause prohibits government punishments that are \"grossly disproportional\" to the offense).","educationalObjective":"Substantive due process prohibits government deprivations of life, liberty, or property that are not rationally related to a legitimate government interest—eg, grossly disproportionate punishments.","references":"• Cooper Indus. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., 532 U.S. 424, 433–35 (2001) (explaining that the due process clause prohibits government punishments that are \"grossly disproportional\" to the offense).","rule":"Substantive due process prohibits government deprivations of life, liberty, or property that are not rationally related to a legitimate government interest—eg, grossly disproportionate punishments.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments would be most helpful to the student's parents in this suit?","rule":"Substantive due process prohibits government deprivations of life, liberty, or property that are not rationally related to a legitimate government interest—eg, grossly disproportionate punishments.","analysis":"Fourteenth Amendment protections\n\nClause Applicability\n\nEqual protection Discriminatory treatment of similarly situated people\n\nDue process Substantive – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification\n\nProcedural – deprivation of life, liberty, or property without adequate process\n\nPrivileges or Interference with rights of national citizenship immunities*\n\n*Rarely a correct answer.\n\nSubstantive due process prohibits the government from depriving persons of life, liberty, or property (eg, freedom of movement) without adequate justification. At a minimum, due process requires that any such government action be rationally related to a legitimate government interest (ie, rational basis review). A government action that is grossly disproportionate to a person's offense or circumstances cannot satisfy this standard of review. Here, the principal applied a school board rule and ordered the student to spend 15 days in confinement after he placed gum on a classmate's chair. But the student had never violated the behavior code before and was otherwise attentive and well-behaved. As a result, the parents' best argument is that the application of the school board rule violates due process because it subjects the student to bodily restraint in a manner grossly disproportionate to his offense and circumstances. (Choice B) State and federal governments are prohibited from passing bills of attainder— ie, legislative acts that punish a specific person or group without a judicial trial. But since this school board rule allows punishment of any student who violates the behavior code— not just a specific student or group—it is not a bill of attainder. (Choice C) A rule is facially invalid (eg, substantially overbroad) if no application of the rule is constitutional. But the school board rule is not a facial violation of the equal protection clause since it applies equally to all students. (Choice D) The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state residents by denying them a right of state citizenship. This clause does not apply here since the student is a resident of the school district—not an out-of-state resident.","conclusion":"Answer A: Because application of the school board rule in this case denies the student freedom of movement and subjects him to bodily restraint in a manner grossly disproportionate to his offense and circumstances, it violates the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"市学校董事会发布了一项规定，授权公立学校校长在听证会后对违反董事会学生行为守则的学生进行惩罚。根据该规定，违反行为守则的人可能会受到多种惩罚，包括被要求在所有上课时间坐在指定的学校禁闭室中，双手紧握在身前，为期最多 15 个教学日。市一所小学的一名五年级学生被指控将嚼过的泡泡糖放在同学的椅子上，违反了学生行为守则。他以前从未违反过校规，在其他方面是一个细心、行为良好的学生。在就指控举行听证会后，该学生的校长认定该学生违反了被指控的行为守则，并命令该学生在接下来的 15 个教学日中双手紧握在身前在学校禁闭室里度过。该学生的家长仅以宪法为由向联邦法院提起诉讼，对校长命令该学生在接下来的 15 个教学日中双手紧扣在学校禁闭室中的行为提出质疑。以下哪项论点对本次诉讼中的学生家长最有帮助？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于在本案中适用学校董事会规则剥夺了学生的行动自由，并以与其罪行和情况严重不相称的方式对学生进行身体限制，因此违反了第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","B":"由于学校董事会规则最初是通过行政程序而不是司法程序执行的，因此它构成了禁止的剥夺公民权法案。","C":"由于学校董事会的规则对于任何合法目的来说都过于宽泛，因此它构成了对第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）的表面违反。","D":"由于学校董事会的规定限制了学生的行动自由并使他们受到身体约束，因此剥夺了他们第四条第二节所保障的公民特权和豁免权。"},"explanationZh":"第十四修正案的保护\n\n条款适用性\n\n平等保护 对处境相似的人的歧视性待遇\n\n正当程序 实质性 – 在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n程序性——未经充分程序剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n特权或干扰国家公民豁免权*\n\n*很少有正确答案。\n\n实质性正当程序禁止政府在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺他人的生命、自由或财产（例如迁徙自由）。至少，正当程序要求任何此类政府行为与合法的政府利益合理相关（即合理的基础审查）。与个人的罪行或情况严重不相称的政府行为不能满足这一审查标准。这名学生在同学的椅子上放了口香糖，校长根据学校董事会的规定，下令对其进行 15 天的监禁。但该学生此前从未违反过行为准则，其他方面也很专心、表现良好。因此，家长们的最佳论点是，学校董事会规则的适用违反了正当程序，因为它以与学生的罪行和情况严重不相称的方式对学生进行身体限制。 （选择 B）禁止州和联邦政府通过剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为。但由于学校董事会的这项规定允许对任何违反行为准则的学生（而不仅仅是特定的学生或群体）进行惩罚，因此这并不是一项剥夺公民权利的法案。 （选项C）如果规则的应用不符合宪法，则该规则表面上无效（例如，实质上过于宽泛）。但学校董事会的规定并不是表面上违反平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它平等地适用于所有学生。 （选项 D） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止各州通过剥夺州外居民的州公民权来歧视他们。该条款不适用于此处，因为学生是学区居民，而不是州外居民。\n\n教育目标：实质性正当程序禁止政府剥夺与合法政府利益不合理相关的生命、自由或财产，例如极不相称的惩罚。\n\n参考文献\n• 库珀工业。 v. Leatherman Tool Grp., 532 U.S. 424, 433–35 (2001)（解释说正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止与犯罪行为“严重不成比例”的政府处罚）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"实质性正当程序禁止政府剥夺与合法政府利益不合理相关的生命、自由或财产，例如极不相称的惩罚。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项论点对本次诉讼中的学生家长最有帮助？","rule":"实质性正当程序禁止政府剥夺与合法政府利益不合理相关的生命、自由或财产，例如极不相称的惩罚。","analysis":"第十四修正案的保护\n\n条款适用性\n\n平等保护 对处境相似的人的歧视性待遇\n\n正当程序 实质性 – 在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n程序性——未经充分程序剥夺生命、自由或财产\n\n特权或干扰国家公民豁免权*\n\n*很少有正确答案。\n\n实质性正当程序禁止政府在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺他人的生命、自由或财产（例如迁徙自由）。至少，正当程序要求任何此类政府行为与合法的政府利益合理相关（即合理的基础审查）。与个人的罪行或情况严重不相称的政府行为不能满足这一审查标准。这名学生在同学的椅子上放了口香糖，校长根据学校董事会的规定，下令对其进行 15 天的监禁。但该学生此前从未违反过行为准则，其他方面也很专心、表现良好。因此，家长们的最佳论点是，学校董事会规则的适用违反了正当程序，因为它以与学生的罪行和情况严重不相称的方式对学生进行身体限制。 （选择 B）禁止州和联邦政府通过剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为。但由于学校董事会的这项规定允许对任何违反行为准则的学生（而不仅仅是特定的学生或群体）进行惩罚，因此这并不是一项剥夺公民权利的法案。 （选项C）如果规则的应用不符合宪法，则该规则表面上无效（例如，实质上过于宽泛）。但学校董事会的规定并不是表面上违反平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），因为它平等地适用于所有学生。 （选项 D） 第四条特权和豁免条款禁止各州通过剥夺州外居民的州公民权来歧视他们。该条款不适用于此处，因为学生是学区居民，而不是州外居民。\n\n教育目标：实质性正当程序禁止政府剥夺与合法政府利益不合理相关的生命、自由或财产，例如极不相称的惩罚。\n\n参考文献\n• 库珀工业。 v. Leatherman Tool Grp., 532 U.S. 424, 433–35 (2001)（解释说正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止与犯罪行为“严重不成比例”的政府处罚）。","conclusion":"答案 A：由于在本案中适用学校董事会规则剥夺了学生的行动自由，并以与其罪行和情况严重不相称的方式对学生进行身体限制，因此违反了第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":221,"sourceQuestionPage":660,"sourceQuestionPages":[660],"sourceExplanationPages":[661,662],"sourceAnswerPages":[661,662],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-222","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"Public schools in a state are financed, in large part, by revenue derived from real estate taxes imposed by each school district on the taxable real property located in that district. Public schools also receive other revenue from private gifts, federal grants, student fees, and local sales taxes. For many years, the state has distributed additional funds, which come from the state treasury, to local school districts in order to equalize the funds available on a per-student basis for each public school district. These additional funds are distributed on the basis of a state statutory formula that considers only the number of students in each public school district and the real estate tax revenue raised by that district. The formula does not consider other revenue received by a school district from different sources. The school boards of two school districts, together with parents and schoolchildren in those districts, bring suit in federal court to enjoin the state from allocating the additional funds from the state treasury to individual districts pursuant to this formula. They allege that the failure of the state, in allocating this additional money, to take into account a school district's sources of revenue other than revenue derived from taxes levied on real estate located there violates the equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. The complaint does not allege that the allocation of the additional state funds based on the current statutory formula has resulted in a failure to provide minimally adequate education to any child. Which of the following best describes the appropriate standard by which the court should review the constitutionality of the state statutory funding formula?","choices":{"A":"Because classifications based on wealth are inherently suspect, the state must demonstrate that the statutory formula is necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest.","B":"Because no fundamental right or suspect or quasi-suspect classification is implicated in this case, the plaintiffs must demonstrate that the funding allocation formula bears no rational relationship to any legitimate state interest.","C":"Because the funding formula inevitably leads to disparities among the school districts in their levels of total funding, the plaintiffs must only demonstrate that the funding formula is not substantially related to the furtherance of an important state interest.","D":"Because the statutory funding formula burdens the fundamental right to education, the state must demonstrate that the formula is necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Laws that unevenly distribute benefits can be challenged on equal protection grounds. These challenges are generally subject to rational basis review, where the challenger must show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest. But intermediate scrutiny is used for laws that substantially impact a quasi- suspect class. And strict scrutiny is used for laws that substantially impact a suspect class or fundamental right. Here, a state statute calculates the distribution of funds from the state treasury based on the number of students in each public school district and the real estate tax revenue raised by that district. This formula has a disparate impact on school districts because it fails to account for other sources of revenue. But since this law does not affect a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class, rational basis review will apply. (Choices A & D) The state must show that its law is necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny) when the law substantially impacts a suspect class or fundamental right. In San Antonio Indep. Sch. Dist. v. Rodriguez, the Supreme Court held that (1) wealth-based classifications do not impact suspect or quasi-suspect classes and (2) education is not a fundamental right. Therefore, rational basis scrutiny will apply here. (Choice C) The state would have had to demonstrate that its statutory funding formula is substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny) had the formula impacted a quasi-suspect class (ie, gender, legitimacy). But the statute does not do so.\n\nEducational objective: Under the equal protection clause, a discriminatory law that does not substantially impact a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class will be reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the challenging party must show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1 (equal protection clause).\n\n• San Antonio Indep. Sch. Dist. v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973) (explaining that education is not a fundamental right and wealth is not a quasi-suspect or suspect class).","educationalObjective":"Under the equal protection clause, a discriminatory law that does not substantially impact a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class will be reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the challenging party must show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1 (equal protection clause).\n\n• San Antonio Indep. Sch. Dist. v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973) (explaining that education is not a fundamental right and wealth is not a quasi-suspect or suspect class).","rule":"Under the equal protection clause, a discriminatory law that does not substantially impact a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class will be reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the challenging party must show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best describes the appropriate standard by which the court should review the constitutionality of the state statutory funding formula?","rule":"Under the equal protection clause, a discriminatory law that does not substantially impact a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class will be reviewed under the rational basis test—ie, the challenging party must show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest.","analysis":"Laws that unevenly distribute benefits can be challenged on equal protection grounds. These challenges are generally subject to rational basis review, where the challenger must show that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest. But intermediate scrutiny is used for laws that substantially impact a quasi- suspect class. And strict scrutiny is used for laws that substantially impact a suspect class or fundamental right. Here, a state statute calculates the distribution of funds from the state treasury based on the number of students in each public school district and the real estate tax revenue raised by that district. This formula has a disparate impact on school districts because it fails to account for other sources of revenue. But since this law does not affect a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class, rational basis review will apply. (Choices A & D) The state must show that its law is necessary to vindicate a compelling state interest (ie, strict scrutiny) when the law substantially impacts a suspect class or fundamental right. In San Antonio Indep. Sch. Dist. v. Rodriguez, the Supreme Court held that (1) wealth-based classifications do not impact suspect or quasi-suspect classes and (2) education is not a fundamental right. Therefore, rational basis scrutiny will apply here. (Choice C) The state would have had to demonstrate that its statutory funding formula is substantially related to an important state interest (ie, intermediate scrutiny) had the formula impacted a quasi-suspect class (ie, gender, legitimacy). But the statute does not do so.","conclusion":"Answer B: Because no fundamental right or suspect or quasi-suspect classification is implicated in this case, the plaintiffs must demonstrate that the funding allocation formula bears no rational relationship to any legitimate state interest."},"questionZh":"州公立学校的资金大部分来自每个学区对位于该学区的应税不动产征收的房地产税。公立学校还从私人礼物、联邦补助金、学生费用和地方销售税中获得其他收入。多年来，国家一直将来自国库的额外资金分配给当地学区，以平衡每个公立学区每个学生的可用资金。这些额外资金是根据州法定公式分配的，该公式仅考虑每个公立学区的学生人数以及该学区筹集的房地产税收入。该公式不考虑学区从不同来源获得的其他收入。两个学区的学校董事会以及这些学区的家长和学童向联邦法院提起诉讼，要求州政府根据此公式将国库的额外资金分配给各个学区。他们声称，州政府在分配这笔额外资金时，没有考虑到学区的收入来源，而不是对学区的房地产征税所产生的收入，这违反了第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。申诉并没有声称根据现行法定公式分配额外的国家资金导致未能为任何儿童提供最低限度的充分教育。以下哪一项最准确地描述了法院审查州法定拨款公式是否符合宪法的适当标准？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于基于财富的分类本质上是值得怀疑的，因此国家必须证明法定公式对于维护令人信服的国家利益是必要的。","B":"由于本案不涉及基本权利或可疑或准可疑分类，原告必须证明资金分配公式与任何合法的国家利益不存在合理关系。","C":"由于资助公式不可避免地导致学区之间的总资助水平存在差异，原告只需证明资助公式与促进重要的国家利益没有实质性关系即可。","D":"由于法定资助公式对受教育的基本权利造成负担，因此国家必须证明该公式对于维护令人信服的国家利益是必要的。"},"explanationZh":"利益分配不均的法律可以基于平等保护的理由受到质疑。这些挑战通常需要经过合理的基础审查，挑战者必须证明法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。但中间审查适用于对准嫌疑阶层产生重大影响的法律。对严重影响可疑阶层或基本权利的法律进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这里，州法规根据每个公立学区的学生人数和该学区筹集的房地产税收入来计算国库资金的分配。这个公式对学区有不同的影响，因为它没有考虑其他收入来源。但由于该法不影响基本权利、可疑类别或准可疑类别，因此将适用合理基础审查。 （选择 A 和 D）当法律对可疑阶级或基本权利产生重大影响时，国家必须证明其法律对于维护令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。在圣安东尼奥独立。施。区。在罗德里格斯诉罗德里格斯一案中，最高法院认为（1）基于财富的分类不会影响可疑或准可疑阶层，（2）教育不是一项基本权利。因此，合理基础审查将适用于此。 （选择C）如果该公式影响了准可疑阶层（即性别、合法性），国家必须证明其法定资助公式与重要的国家利益（即中间审查）密切相关。但法规并没有这样做。\n\n教育目标：根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层没有实质性影响的歧视性法律将根据合理基础测试进行审查，即质疑方必须证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理关系。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XIV，§ 1（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• 圣安东尼奥独立。施。区。 v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973)（解释说教育不是一项基本权利，财富也不是准嫌疑人或可疑阶层）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层没有实质性影响的歧视性法律将根据合理基础测试进行审查，即质疑方必须证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项最准确地描述了法院审查州法定拨款公式是否符合宪法的适当标准？","rule":"根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层没有实质性影响的歧视性法律将根据合理基础测试进行审查，即质疑方必须证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理关系。","analysis":"利益分配不均的法律可以基于平等保护的理由受到质疑。这些挑战通常需要经过合理的基础审查，挑战者必须证明法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。但中间审查适用于对准嫌疑阶层产生重大影响的法律。对严重影响可疑阶层或基本权利的法律进行严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在这里，州法规根据每个公立学区的学生人数和该学区筹集的房地产税收入来计算国库资金的分配。这个公式对学区有不同的影响，因为它没有考虑其他收入来源。但由于该法不影响基本权利、可疑类别或准可疑类别，因此将适用合理基础审查。 （选择 A 和 D）当法律对可疑阶级或基本权利产生重大影响时，国家必须证明其法律对于维护令人信服的国家利益（即严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））是必要的。在圣安东尼奥独立。施。区。在罗德里格斯诉罗德里格斯一案中，最高法院认为（1）基于财富的分类不会影响可疑或准可疑阶层，（2）教育不是一项基本权利。因此，合理基础审查将适用于此。 （选择C）如果该公式影响了准可疑阶层（即性别、合法性），国家必须证明其法定资助公式与重要的国家利益（即中间审查）密切相关。但法规并没有这样做。\n\n教育目标：根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对基本权利、可疑阶层或准可疑阶层没有实质性影响的歧视性法律将根据合理基础测试进行审查，即质疑方必须证明该法律与任何合法的国家利益没有合理关系。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XIV，§ 1（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• 圣安东尼奥独立。施。区。 v. Rodriguez, 411 U.S. 1, 28, 37 (1973)（解释说教育不是一项基本权利，财富也不是准嫌疑人或可疑阶层）。","conclusion":"答案 B：由于本案不涉及基本权利或可疑或准可疑分类，原告必须证明资金分配公式与任何合法的国家利益不存在合理关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"suspect classification","ipa":"/sʌspˈɛkt klˌæsʌfʌkˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"sus·pect clas·si·fi·ca·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"可疑分类：如种族分类，通常触发严格审查","contextualMeaning":"可疑分类：如种族分类，通常触发严格审查","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Because no fundamental right or suspect or quasi-suspect classification is implicated in this case, the plaintiffs must demonstrate that the funding allocation formula bears no rational relationship to any legitimate state interest.","synonyms":["suspect categorization","suspect categorisation","suspect compartmentalization"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0a9a97f020437e5f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":222,"sourceQuestionPage":663,"sourceQuestionPages":[663],"sourceExplanationPages":[664,665],"sourceAnswerPages":[664,665],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-223","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"A city zoning ordinance requires that anyone who proposes to operate a group home obtain a special-use permit from the city zoning board. The zoning ordinance defines a group home as a residence in which four or more unrelated adults reside. An individual applied for a special-use permit to operate a group home for convicted felons during their transition from serving prison sentences to their release on parole. Although the proposed group home met all of the requirements for the special-use permit, the zoning board denied the individual's application because of the nature of the proposed use. The individual sued the zoning board seeking declaratory and injunctive relief on constitutional grounds. Which of the following best states the appropriate burden of persuasion in this action?","choices":{"A":"Because housing is a fundamental right, the zoning board must demonstrate that denial of the permit is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.","B":"Because the zoning board's action has the effect of discriminating against a quasi- suspect class in regard to a basic subsistence right, the zoning board must demonstrate that the denial of the permit is substantially related to an important government interest.","C":"Because the zoning board's action invidiously discriminates against a suspect class, the zoning board must demonstrate that denial of the permit is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.","D":"Because the zoning board's action is in the nature of an economic or social welfare regulation, the individual seeking the permit must demonstrate that the denial of the permit is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"State and municipal (eg, city) government actions that treat similarly situated persons differently (ie, discriminate) can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. To comply with this clause, the challenged action must satisfy the appropriate level of scrutiny:\n• rational basis scrutiny (default test) – the challenger has the burden to prove that the government action is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest\n\n• intermediate scrutiny (quasi-suspect class) – the government has the burden to prove that its action is substantially related to an important government interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny (fundamental right or suspect class) – the government has the burden to prove that its action is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.\n\nHere, the zoning board denied the individual's application for a permit to operate a group home for convicted felons—even though the proposal met all the permit requirements. The denial was discriminatory because it was based solely on the nature of the individual's proposed use. But housing is not a fundamental right (Choice A), and convicted felons are not a quasi-suspect or suspect class (Choices B & C). As a result, the individual must show that this economic or social welfare regulation does not satisfy rational basis scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: Equal protection challenges that do not involve a fundamental right, quasi-suspect class, or suspect class are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny requires the challenger to show that the government action is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.\n\nReferences\n• City of Cleburne v. Cleburne Living Ctr., Inc., 473 U.S. 432, 439–41 (1985) (explaining that discriminatory economic and social regulations are analyzed under rational basis scrutiny).","educationalObjective":"Equal protection challenges that do not involve a fundamental right, quasi-suspect class, or suspect class are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny requires the challenger to show that the government action is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","references":"• City of Cleburne v. Cleburne Living Ctr., Inc., 473 U.S. 432, 439–41 (1985) (explaining that discriminatory economic and social regulations are analyzed under rational basis scrutiny).","rule":"Equal protection challenges that do not involve a fundamental right, quasi-suspect class, or suspect class are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny requires the challenger to show that the government action is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the appropriate burden of persuasion in this action?","rule":"Equal protection challenges that do not involve a fundamental right, quasi-suspect class, or suspect class are reviewed under rational basis scrutiny. This level of scrutiny requires the challenger to show that the government action is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","analysis":"State and municipal (eg, city) government actions that treat similarly situated persons differently (ie, discriminate) can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. To comply with this clause, the challenged action must satisfy the appropriate level of scrutiny:\n• rational basis scrutiny (default test) – the challenger has the burden to prove that the government action is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest\n\n• intermediate scrutiny (quasi-suspect class) – the government has the burden to prove that its action is substantially related to an important government interest or\n\n• strict scrutiny (fundamental right or suspect class) – the government has the burden to prove that its action is necessary to serve a compelling government interest.\n\nHere, the zoning board denied the individual's application for a permit to operate a group home for convicted felons—even though the proposal met all the permit requirements. The denial was discriminatory because it was based solely on the nature of the individual's proposed use. But housing is not a fundamental right (Choice A), and convicted felons are not a quasi-suspect or suspect class (Choices B & C). As a result, the individual must show that this economic or social welfare regulation does not satisfy rational basis scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer D: Because the zoning board's action is in the nature of an economic or social welfare regulation, the individual seeking the permit must demonstrate that the denial of the permit is not rationally related to a legitimate government interest."},"questionZh":"城市分区条例要求任何打算经营集体住宅的人都必须获得城市分区委员会的特殊使用许可证。分区条例将集体住宅定义为四个或四个以上无关成年人居住的住所。一名个人申请了特殊用途许可证，以便在被定罪的重罪犯从服刑到假释的过渡期间经营一个集体之家。尽管拟议的集体住宅满足了特殊用途许可证的所有要求，但分区委员会因拟议用途的性质而拒绝了个人的申请。该人起诉分区委员会，寻求基于宪法的宣告性和禁令性救济。以下哪一项最好地说明了此行动中适当的说服责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于住房是一项基本权利，分区委员会必须证明拒绝许可对于服务政府的迫切利益是必要的。","B":"由于分区委员会的行为在基本生存权方面具有歧视准嫌疑阶层的效果，因此分区委员会必须证明拒绝发放许可证与重要的政府利益密切相关。","C":"由于分区委员会的行为对可疑阶层进行了令人不快的歧视，因此分区委员会必须证明拒绝许可证对于服务于令人信服的政府利益是必要的。","D":"由于分区委员会的行动具有经济或社会福利监管的性质，因此寻求许可证的个人必须证明拒绝许可证与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。"},"explanationZh":"根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），州和市（例如市）政府对处境相似的人采取不同对待（即歧视）的行为可能会受到质疑。为了遵守本条款，受到质疑的行动必须满足适当级别的审查：\n• 合理基础审查（违约测试）——挑战者有责任证明政府行为与合法政府利益不合理相关\n\n• 中间审查（准嫌疑级）——政府有责任证明其行为与重要的政府利益或重大利益相关。\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（基本权利或可疑类别）——政府有责任证明其行动对于服务政府的迫切利益是必要的。\n\n在此，分区委员会拒绝了该个人为已定罪重罪犯经营集体之家的许可证申请，尽管该提案满足了所有许可证要求。这种拒绝是歧视性的，因为它完全基于个人提议用途的性质。但住房不是一项基本权利（选择 A），被定罪的重罪犯也不是准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层（选择 B 和 C）。因此，个人必须证明这种经济或社会福利监管不符合合理基础的审查。\n\n教育目标：在合理基础上审查不涉及基本权利、准可疑阶层或可疑阶层的平等保护挑战。这种级别的审查要求挑战者证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有合理关系。\n\n参考文献\n• City of Cleburne v. Cleburne Living Ctr., Inc., 473 U.S. 432, 439–41 (1985)（解释说，歧视性经济和社会法规是在合理基础审查下进行分析的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"不涉及基本权利、准可疑阶层或可疑阶层的平等保护挑战将在合理基础上进行审查。这种级别的审查要求挑战者证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有合理关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项最好地说明了此行动中适当的说服责任？","rule":"不涉及基本权利、准可疑阶层或可疑阶层的平等保护挑战将在合理基础上进行审查。这种级别的审查要求挑战者证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有合理关系。","analysis":"根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），州和市（例如市）政府对处境相似的人采取不同对待（即歧视）的行为可能会受到质疑。为了遵守本条款，受到质疑的行动必须满足适当级别的审查：\n• 合理基础审查（违约测试）——挑战者有责任证明政府行为与合法政府利益不合理相关\n\n• 中间审查（准嫌疑级）——政府有责任证明其行为与重要的政府利益或重大利益相关。\n\n• 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）（基本权利或可疑类别）——政府有责任证明其行动对于服务政府的迫切利益是必要的。\n\n在此，分区委员会拒绝了该个人为已定罪重罪犯经营集体之家的许可证申请，尽管该提案满足了所有许可证要求。这种拒绝是歧视性的，因为它完全基于个人提议用途的性质。但住房不是一项基本权利（选择 A），被定罪的重罪犯也不是准嫌疑人或嫌疑人阶层（选择 B 和 C）。因此，个人必须证明这种经济或社会福利监管不符合合理基础的审查。\n\n教育目标：在合理基础上审查不涉及基本权利、准可疑阶层或可疑阶层的平等保护挑战。这种级别的审查要求挑战者证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有合理关系。\n\n参考文献\n• City of Cleburne v. Cleburne Living Ctr., Inc., 473 U.S. 432, 439–41 (1985)（解释说，歧视性经济和社会法规是在合理基础审查下进行分析的）。","conclusion":"答案 D：由于分区委员会的行动具有经济或社会福利监管的性质，因此寻求许可证的个人必须证明拒绝许可证与合法的政府利益没有合理的关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0a9a97f020437e5f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":223,"sourceQuestionPage":666,"sourceQuestionPages":[666],"sourceExplanationPages":[667,668],"sourceAnswerPages":[667,668],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-224","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A man, who is a resident noncitizen, applied for employment as a state trooper with the state highway patrol. After the man passed a psychological examination and completed the requisite training courses, he was denied employment. The state highway patrol agency explained that the man was ineligible for employment as a state trooper pursuant to a policy that the agency had enacted six months prior. The policy disqualifies all noncitizens from employment with the state highway patrol, restricting employment to U.S. citizens. The man has filed an action for a declaratory judgment that the agency's policy is unconstitutional as applied to him. Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action?","choices":{"A":"The agency must demonstrate a rational relationship between the citizenship requirement and a legitimate state interest.","B":"The agency must demonstrate that the citizenship requirement is the least restrictive means to achieve a compelling government interest.","C":"The man must demonstrate that the citizenship requirement is not narrowly tailored to achieve a compelling state interest.","D":"The man must demonstrate that the citizenship requirement is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest."},"answer":"D","explanation":"State laws and policies that discriminate against lawful residents of the United States based on their citizenship can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. Since citizenship is a suspect class, such laws and policies are generally subject to strict scrutiny review and struck down. However, a growing exception exists for state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions that would allow (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion—eg, serving on a jury, voting, employment as a police officer or public school teacher. Under this political-function exception, such laws are subject to rational basis review and will be upheld unless the challenger proves that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest. Here, the agency enacted a policy prohibiting noncitizens from employment as state troopers. State troopers are officers of the law that directly participate in the execution of public policy and exercise broad discretion, so the political-function exception applies. This means that the agency's policy is subject to rational basis review, which places the burden on the man to demonstrate that the citizenship requirement is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest. (Choice A) As the challenger, the man—not the agency—bears the burden of proof under rational basis scrutiny. (Choices B & C) When the political-function exception does not apply, state laws and policies that discriminate on the basis of citizenship are subject to strict scrutiny review. Under strict scrutiny review, the government has the difficult burden of demonstrating that the citizenship requirement is necessary (ie, narrowly tailored, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling state interest.\n\nEducational objective: State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis review applies to state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in certain government functions—eg, employment as a police officer.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1 (equal protection clause).\n\n• Foley v. Connelie, 435 U.S. 291, 300 (1978) (holding that the political-function exception is applicable to a state law restricting resident noncitizens from employment as police officers).","educationalObjective":"State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis review applies to state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in certain government functions—eg, employment as a police officer.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1 (equal protection clause).\n\n• Foley v. Connelie, 435 U.S. 291, 300 (1978) (holding that the political-function exception is applicable to a state law restricting resident noncitizens from employment as police officers).","rule":"State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis review applies to state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in certain government functions—eg, employment as a police officer.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the burden of persuasion in this action?","rule":"State laws that discriminate based on citizenship are generally subject to strict scrutiny. But under the political-function exception, rational basis review applies to state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in certain government functions—eg, employment as a police officer.","analysis":"State laws and policies that discriminate against lawful residents of the United States based on their citizenship can be challenged under the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. Since citizenship is a suspect class, such laws and policies are generally subject to strict scrutiny review and struck down. However, a growing exception exists for state laws that restrict a noncitizen's participation in government functions that would allow (1) direct formulation, execution, or review of public policy or (2) exercise of broad discretion—eg, serving on a jury, voting, employment as a police officer or public school teacher. Under this political-function exception, such laws are subject to rational basis review and will be upheld unless the challenger proves that the law is not rationally related to any legitimate state interest. Here, the agency enacted a policy prohibiting noncitizens from employment as state troopers. State troopers are officers of the law that directly participate in the execution of public policy and exercise broad discretion, so the political-function exception applies. This means that the agency's policy is subject to rational basis review, which places the burden on the man to demonstrate that the citizenship requirement is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest. (Choice A) As the challenger, the man—not the agency—bears the burden of proof under rational basis scrutiny. (Choices B & C) When the political-function exception does not apply, state laws and policies that discriminate on the basis of citizenship are subject to strict scrutiny review. Under strict scrutiny review, the government has the difficult burden of demonstrating that the citizenship requirement is necessary (ie, narrowly tailored, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling state interest.","conclusion":"Answer D: The man must demonstrate that the citizenship requirement is not rationally related to a legitimate state interest."},"questionZh":"一名非居民男子申请担任州公路巡逻队的州警。该男子通过心理检查并完成必要的培训课程后，被拒绝就业。州公路巡逻机构解释说，根据该机构六个月前颁布的政策，该男子没有资格担任州警察。该政策取消了所有非公民在州高速公路巡逻队就业的资格，限制了美国公民的就业。该男子已提起诉讼，要求做出宣告性判决，认为该机构的政策适用于他是违宪的。以下哪项最能说明此行动中的说服负担？","choicesZh":{"A":"该机构必须证明公民身份要求与合法国家利益之间存在合理关系。","B":"该机构必须证明公民身份要求是实现令人信服的政府利益的限制最少的手段。","C":"该人必须证明公民身份要求并非狭隘地为了实现令人信服的国家利益而量身定制。","D":"该人必须证明公民身份要求与合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。"},"explanationZh":"基于公民身份歧视美国合法居民的州法律和政策可以根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。由于公民身份是一个可疑的阶层，此类法律和政策通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）并被废除。然而，限制非公民参与政府职能的州法律存在越来越多的例外情况，这些政府职能允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策，或（2）行使广泛的自由裁量权，例如担任陪审团、投票、担任警察或公立学校教师。根据这一政治功能例外，此类法律将接受合理基础审查，并将得到维持，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法国家利益没有合理关系。在这里，该机构制定了一项政策，禁止非公民受雇担任州警。国家警察是直接参与公共政策执行并行使广泛自由裁量权的法律官员，因此政治职能例外适用。这意味着该机构的政策要接受合理基础审查，这使得该人有责任证明公民身份要求与合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。 （选项A）作为挑战者，个人（而不是机构）在合理基础的审查下承担举证责任。 （选项 B 和 C）当政治职能例外不适用时，基于公民身份的歧视性州法律和政策将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在严格的审查下，政府承担着证明公民身份要求对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即狭隘的、限制性最小的手段）的艰巨任务。\n\n教育目标：基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，合理基础审查适用于限制非公民参与某些政府职能（例如担任警察）的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XIV，§ 1（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Foley v. Connelie, 435 U.S. 291, 300 (1978)（认为政治职能例外适用于限制非公民居民担任警察的州法律）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，合理基础审查适用于限制非公民参与某些政府职能（例如担任警察）的州法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项最能说明此行动中的说服负担？","rule":"基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，合理基础审查适用于限制非公民参与某些政府职能（例如担任警察）的州法律。","analysis":"基于公民身份歧视美国合法居民的州法律和政策可以根据第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑。由于公民身份是一个可疑的阶层，此类法律和政策通常会受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）并被废除。然而，限制非公民参与政府职能的州法律存在越来越多的例外情况，这些政府职能允许（1）直接制定、执行或审查公共政策，或（2）行使广泛的自由裁量权，例如担任陪审团、投票、担任警察或公立学校教师。根据这一政治功能例外，此类法律将接受合理基础审查，并将得到维持，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法国家利益没有合理关系。在这里，该机构制定了一项政策，禁止非公民受雇担任州警。国家警察是直接参与公共政策执行并行使广泛自由裁量权的法律官员，因此政治职能例外适用。这意味着该机构的政策要接受合理基础审查，这使得该人有责任证明公民身份要求与合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。 （选项A）作为挑战者，个人（而不是机构）在合理基础的审查下承担举证责任。 （选项 B 和 C）当政治职能例外不适用时，基于公民身份的歧视性州法律和政策将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。在严格的审查下，政府承担着证明公民身份要求对于实现令人信服的国家利益是必要的（即狭隘的、限制性最小的手段）的艰巨任务。\n\n教育目标：基于公民身份的歧视的州法律通常受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。但在政治职能例外情况下，合理基础审查适用于限制非公民参与某些政府职能（例如担任警察）的州法律。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。 XIV，§ 1（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• Foley v. Connelie, 435 U.S. 291, 300 (1978)（认为政治职能例外适用于限制非公民居民担任警察的州法律）。","conclusion":"答案 D：该人必须证明公民身份要求与合法的国家利益没有合理的关系。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-0acbaed72d2e7de4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":224,"sourceQuestionPage":669,"sourceQuestionPages":[669],"sourceExplanationPages":[670,671],"sourceAnswerPages":[670,671],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-225","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Congressional Powers","question":"A Senate investigative committee released a report identifying three U.S. citizens as individuals who were organizing support for terrorist activities. All three were employed by the U.S. government as park rangers. In response, Congress enacted a statute naming these three individuals and providing that they could not hold any position of employment with the federal government. Which of the following constitutional provisions provides the best means for challenging the constitutionality of the statute?","choices":{"A":"The bill of attainder clause.","B":"The due process clause.","C":"The ex post facto clause.","D":"The takings clause."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Prohibited legislation\n\nBill of attainder Prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment against clauses identified persons (or groups of persons) without a trial\n\nEx post facto Prohibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that: clauses\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof The Article I bill of attainder clauses prohibit federal and state legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment on named persons—or easily identifiable groups of persons—without a judicial trial. Here, the federal statute bars three named park rangers from federal government employment. And since this constitutes a civil punishment that is imposed without a trial, the statute is an unconstitutional bill of attainder. (Choice B) The Fifth Amendment due process clause requires the government to provide persons with notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard before depriving them of a life, liberty, or property interest. Public employment is a property interest when the employee (1) has an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receives assurances of continued employment—none of which apply here. (Choice C) The ex post facto clauses bar legislative acts that impose retroactive criminal punishments—ie, punish conduct that occurred before the law was enacted. Although the rangers' alleged support of terrorism occurred before the statute was enacted, their exclusion from government employment is a civil punishment. (Choice D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. But this clause applies to real or personal property—not interests in public employment.\n\nEducational objective: The bill of attainder clauses prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment on named individuals—or easily identifiable groups of individuals—without a trial. Exclusion from a profession or vocation is a type of civil punishment.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. art. I, § 9 (prohibits the federal government from enacting bills of attainder).\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315–16 (1946) (holding that a congressional act that excludes a specific person from a profession or vocation without a trial is an unconstitutional bill of attainder).","educationalObjective":"The bill of attainder clauses prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment on named individuals—or easily identifiable groups of individuals—without a trial. Exclusion from a profession or vocation is a type of civil punishment.","references":"• U.S. Const. art. I, § 9 (prohibits the federal government from enacting bills of attainder).\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315–16 (1946) (holding that a congressional act that excludes a specific person from a profession or vocation without a trial is an unconstitutional bill of attainder).","rule":"The bill of attainder clauses prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment on named individuals—or easily identifiable groups of individuals—without a trial. Exclusion from a profession or vocation is a type of civil punishment.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following constitutional provisions provides the best means for challenging the constitutionality of the statute?","rule":"The bill of attainder clauses prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment on named individuals—or easily identifiable groups of individuals—without a trial. Exclusion from a profession or vocation is a type of civil punishment.","analysis":"Prohibited legislation\n\nBill of attainder Prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment against clauses identified persons (or groups of persons) without a trial\n\nEx post facto Prohibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that: clauses\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof The Article I bill of attainder clauses prohibit federal and state legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment on named persons—or easily identifiable groups of persons—without a judicial trial. Here, the federal statute bars three named park rangers from federal government employment. And since this constitutes a civil punishment that is imposed without a trial, the statute is an unconstitutional bill of attainder. (Choice B) The Fifth Amendment due process clause requires the government to provide persons with notice and a meaningful opportunity to be heard before depriving them of a life, liberty, or property interest. Public employment is a property interest when the employee (1) has an ongoing employment contract, (2) can only be fired for cause, or (3) receives assurances of continued employment—none of which apply here. (Choice C) The ex post facto clauses bar legislative acts that impose retroactive criminal punishments—ie, punish conduct that occurred before the law was enacted. Although the rangers' alleged support of terrorism occurred before the statute was enacted, their exclusion from government employment is a civil punishment. (Choice D) The Fifth Amendment takings clause prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. But this clause applies to real or personal property—not interests in public employment.","conclusion":"Answer A: The bill of attainder clause."},"questionZh":"参议院调查委员会发布了一份报告，确认三名美国公民正在组织支持恐怖活动。这三人都受雇于美国政府担任公园管理员。作为回应，国会颁布了一项法令，指定了这三人的名字，并规定他们不能在联邦政府担任任何职位。以下哪项宪法条款提供了质疑该法规合宪性的最佳手段？","choicesZh":{"A":"剥夺公民权法案。","B":"正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","C":"事后条款。","D":"征收条款（Takings Clause）（Takings Clause）。"},"explanationZh":"禁止的立法\n\n剥夺公民权利法案 禁止在未经审判的情况下对特定个人（或群体）施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为\n\n事后禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法： 条款\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 剥夺公民权法案第一条禁止联邦和州立法行为在未经司法审判的情况下对指定人员（或易于识别的群体）施加民事或刑事处罚。在这里，联邦法规禁止三名指定的公园护林员受雇于联邦政府。由于这构成了未经审判而实施的民事处罚，因此该法规是一项违宪的剥夺公民权法案。 （选项 B）第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求政府在剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产利益之前向他们提供通知和有意义的表达意见的机会。当雇员 (1) 拥有持续的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证——这些都不适用时，公共就业是一种财产权益。 （选项C） 事后条款禁止实施追溯性刑事处罚的立法行为，即惩罚在法律颁布之前发生的行为。尽管护林员被指控支持恐怖主义是在该法规颁布之前发生的，但将他们排除在政府工作之外是一种民事处罚。 （选项D） 第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私人财产供公共使用。但该条款适用于不动产或个人财产，不适用于公共就业中的利益。\n\n教育目标：剥夺公民权利法案条款禁止在未经审判的情况下对指定个人或易于识别的个人群体施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为。禁止从事某种职业或职业是民事处罚的一种。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 9（禁止联邦政府颁布剥夺公民权法案）。\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315-16 (1946)（认为未经审判将特定个人排除在职业或职业之外的国会法案是违宪的剥夺公民权法案）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"剥夺公民权法案条款禁止在未经审判的情况下对指定个人或易于识别的个人群体施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为。禁止从事某种职业或职业是民事处罚的一种。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项宪法条款提供了质疑该法规合宪性的最佳手段？","rule":"剥夺公民权法案条款禁止在未经审判的情况下对指定个人或易于识别的个人群体施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为。禁止从事某种职业或职业是民事处罚的一种。","analysis":"禁止的立法\n\n剥夺公民权利法案 禁止在未经审判的情况下对特定个人（或群体）施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为\n\n事后禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法： 条款\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 剥夺公民权法案第一条禁止联邦和州立法行为在未经司法审判的情况下对指定人员（或易于识别的群体）施加民事或刑事处罚。在这里，联邦法规禁止三名指定的公园护林员受雇于联邦政府。由于这构成了未经审判而实施的民事处罚，因此该法规是一项违宪的剥夺公民权法案。 （选项 B）第五修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求政府在剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产利益之前向他们提供通知和有意义的表达意见的机会。当雇员 (1) 拥有持续的雇佣合同，(2) 只能因故被解雇，或 (3) 获得继续雇佣的保证——这些都不适用时，公共就业是一种财产权益。 （选项C） 事后条款禁止实施追溯性刑事处罚的立法行为，即惩罚在法律颁布之前发生的行为。尽管护林员被指控支持恐怖主义是在该法规颁布之前发生的，但将他们排除在政府工作之外是一种民事处罚。 （选项D） 第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私人财产供公共使用。但该条款适用于不动产或个人财产，不适用于公共就业中的利益。\n\n教育目标：剥夺公民权利法案条款禁止在未经审判的情况下对指定个人或易于识别的个人群体施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为。禁止从事某种职业或职业是民事处罚的一种。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。艺术。 I，§ 9（禁止联邦政府颁布剥夺公民权法案）。\n\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315-16 (1946)（认为未经审判将特定个人排除在职业或职业之外的国会法案是违宪的剥夺公民权法案）。","conclusion":"答案 A：剥夺公民权法案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":225,"sourceQuestionPage":672,"sourceQuestionPages":[672],"sourceExplanationPages":[673,674],"sourceAnswerPages":[673,674],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-226","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A large privately owned and operated shopping mall is open to the public and includes small shops, major department stores, and restaurants that are located around a pedestrian area. It also has several movie theaters, an ice-skating rink, a small amusement park, and a branch of the local public library. The mall is advertised as \"a small town with a big-town feel.\" During shopping hours, a group of 10 protesters gathered in the pedestrian area near the entrance to a department store to denounce the sale of animal-fur products in that store. The protesters were peaceful and did not interfere with traffic into or out of the store, but they carried signs and vocally communicated their message to people walking in the area. Mall management quickly broke up the protest and required the protesters to leave the mall. The protesters have sued the mall, claiming that their right to freedom of speech guaranteed by the First and Fourteenth Amendments was violated. Should the protesters prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the mall is private property, and there was no state action to which the freedom of speech guarantees of the First and Fourteenth Amendments apply.","B":"No, because the prohibition of protests adjacent to the entrance of a department store during shopping hours is a constitutionally proper limitation on the time, place, and manner of speech.","C":"Yes, because the mall is functionally equivalent to a town and, therefore, its actions are subject to the Constitution's guarantees of freedom of speech and assembly.","D":"Yes, because the mall's restriction on the protesters' speech was broader than necessary to ensure proper access to the department store."},"answer":"A","explanation":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control First Amendment free speech protections—applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause—are triggered by government (not private) action. But under the state-action doctrine, private action is treated as government action when either:\n• the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or\n• the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Here, the protesters can only sue the privately owned shopping mall for violating their freedom of speech if the state-action doctrine applies. But operating a shopping mall is not a traditional and exclusive government function. And though the mall houses a branch of the local public library, this does not amount to significant government involvement. Therefore, the mall cannot be treated as a government actor and the protesters' constitutional claim should fail. (Choices B & D) The government can only limit the time, place, and manner of speech if it passes intermediate scrutiny—ie, the limitation (1) is narrowly tailored (ie, no greater than necessary) to achieve a substantial government interest and (2) leaves open ample alternative avenues of communication. But the private shopping mall need not comply with constitutional requirements when limiting access to its stores. (Choice C) Although the mall is advertised as \"a small town with a big-town feel,\" the Supreme Court held in Hudgens v. NLRB that a shopping mall is not functionally equivalent to a town.\n\nEducational objective: Free speech protections are triggered by government—not private—action. But under the state-action doctrine, private action is treated as government action when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.\n\nReferences\n• Hudgens v. NLRB, 424 U.S. 507, 520–21 (1976) (holding that the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not apply to a shopping center since it is not functionally equivalent to a municipality).","educationalObjective":"Free speech protections are triggered by government—not private—action. But under the state-action doctrine, private action is treated as government action when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.","references":"• Hudgens v. NLRB, 424 U.S. 507, 520–21 (1976) (holding that the First and Fourteenth Amendments do not apply to a shopping center since it is not functionally equivalent to a municipality).","rule":"Free speech protections are triggered by government—not private—action. But under the state-action doctrine, private action is treated as government action when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.","irac":{"issue":"Should the protesters prevail?","rule":"Free speech protections are triggered by government—not private—action. But under the state-action doctrine, private action is treated as government action when (1) the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities.","analysis":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control First Amendment free speech protections—applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause—are triggered by government (not private) action. But under the state-action doctrine, private action is treated as government action when either:\n• the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or\n• the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. Here, the protesters can only sue the privately owned shopping mall for violating their freedom of speech if the state-action doctrine applies. But operating a shopping mall is not a traditional and exclusive government function. And though the mall houses a branch of the local public library, this does not amount to significant government involvement. Therefore, the mall cannot be treated as a government actor and the protesters' constitutional claim should fail. (Choices B & D) The government can only limit the time, place, and manner of speech if it passes intermediate scrutiny—ie, the limitation (1) is narrowly tailored (ie, no greater than necessary) to achieve a substantial government interest and (2) leaves open ample alternative avenues of communication. But the private shopping mall need not comply with constitutional requirements when limiting access to its stores. (Choice C) Although the mall is advertised as \"a small town with a big-town feel,\" the Supreme Court held in Hudgens v. NLRB that a shopping mall is not functionally equivalent to a town.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the mall is private property, and there was no state action to which the freedom of speech guarantees of the First and Fourteenth Amendments apply."},"questionZh":"一个大型私人拥有和经营的购物中心向公众开放，包括位于步行区周围的小商店、主要百货商店和餐馆。它还拥有几家电影院、一个溜冰场、一个小型游乐园和当地公共图书馆的分馆。该购物中心的广告语是“一座有大城镇感觉的小镇”。购物期间，10名抗议者聚集在一家百货公司入口附近的步行区，谴责该店销售的动物毛皮产品。抗议者是和平的，没有干扰进出商店的交通，但他们举着标语，并用声音向在该地区行走的人们传达他们的信息。商场管理人员迅速驱散了抗议活动，并要求抗议者离开商场。抗议者起诉该商场，声称第一修正案和第十四修正案保障的言论自由权受到侵犯。抗议者应该获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为购物中心是私有财产，并且不存在适用第一修正案和第十四修正案的言论自由保障的国家行为。","B":"不，因为禁止在购物时间在百货公司入口附近进行抗议是宪法对时间、地点和言论方式的适当限制。","C":"是的，因为商场在功能上相当于一个城镇，因此，其行为受到宪法保障言论和集会自由的约束。","D":"是的，因为商场对抗议者言论的限制超出了确保正确进入百货公司所需的范围。"},"explanationZh":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍介入私人实体的管理或控制 第一修正案的言论自由保护（通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）是由政府（而非私人）行为触发的。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行动被视为政府行动：\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能，或者\n• 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。在这里，如果国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用，抗议者只能起诉私营购物中心侵犯他们的言论自由。但经营购物中心并不是传统的、专有的政府职能。尽管该购物中心设有当地公共图书馆的分馆，但这并不意味着政府的大力参与。因此，商场不能被视为政府行为者，抗议者的宪法主张应该失败。 （选择 B 和 D）政府只有在通过中间审查的情况下才能限制言论的时间、地点和方式，即限制 (1) 是狭隘地定制的（即，不大于必要的），以实现重大的政府利益，并且 (2) 留有充足的替代沟通途径。但私人购物中心在限制进入其商店时无需遵守宪法要求。 （选项C） 尽管该购物中心被宣传为“具有大城镇感觉的小镇”，但最高法院在哈金斯诉NLRB一案中认为，购物中心在功能上并不等同于城镇。\n\n教育目标：言论自由保护是由政府而非私人行为触发的。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为被视为政府行为。\n\n参考文献\n• Hudgens v. NLRB, 424 U.S. 507, 520–21 (1976)（认为第一修正案和第十四修正案不适用于购物中心，因为它在功能上不等同于市政府）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"言论自由保护是由政府而非私人行为触发的。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为被视为政府行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"抗议者应该获胜吗？","rule":"言论自由保护是由政府而非私人行为触发的。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为被视为政府行为。","analysis":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍介入私人实体的管理或控制 第一修正案的言论自由保护（通过第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）适用于各州）是由政府（而非私人）行为触发的。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行动被视为政府行动：\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能，或者\n• 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。在这里，如果国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用，抗议者只能起诉私营购物中心侵犯他们的言论自由。但经营购物中心并不是传统的、专有的政府职能。尽管该购物中心设有当地公共图书馆的分馆，但这并不意味着政府的大力参与。因此，商场不能被视为政府行为者，抗议者的宪法主张应该失败。 （选择 B 和 D）政府只有在通过中间审查的情况下才能限制言论的时间、地点和方式，即限制 (1) 是狭隘地定制的（即，不大于必要的），以实现重大的政府利益，并且 (2) 留有充足的替代沟通途径。但私人购物中心在限制进入其商店时无需遵守宪法要求。 （选项C） 尽管该购物中心被宣传为“具有大城镇感觉的小镇”，但最高法院在哈金斯诉NLRB一案中认为，购物中心在功能上并不等同于城镇。\n\n教育目标：言论自由保护是由政府而非私人行为触发的。但根据国家行为学说，当（1）私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能或（2）政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为被视为政府行为。\n\n参考文献\n• Hudgens v. NLRB, 424 U.S. 507, 520–21 (1976)（认为第一修正案和第十四修正案不适用于购物中心，因为它在功能上不等同于市政府）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为购物中心是私有财产，并且不存在适用第一修正案和第十四修正案的言论自由保障的国家行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":226,"sourceQuestionPage":675,"sourceQuestionPages":[675],"sourceExplanationPages":[676,677],"sourceAnswerPages":[676,677],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-227","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state law imposed substantial regulations on insurance companies operating within the state with respect to their rates, cash reserves, and financial practices. A privately owned insurance company operating within the state advertised that it wanted to hire a new data processor. After reviewing applications for that position, the company hired a woman who appeared to be well qualified. The company refused to consider the application of a man who was better qualified than the woman, because he was known to have radical political views. The man sued the company, alleging only a violation of his federal constitutional right to freedom of expression. Is the man likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because hiring decisions are wholly discretionary and thus are not governed by the First Amendment.","B":"No, because the company is not subject to the provisions of the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","C":"Yes, because the company is affected with a public interest.","D":"Yes, because the company is substantially regulated by the state, and thus its employment decisions may fairly be attributed to the state."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Constitutional amendments (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) only apply to government actors. But a private actor can be treated as a government actor and be subject to the Constitution under the state-action doctrine. This doctrine applies when (1) a private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. The private actor can then be sued for violating the Constitution—including the First and Fourteenth Amendment guarantee of freedom of expression. Here, the private insurance company does not perform a traditional and exclusive government function since insurance has historically been provided by private companies. And state regulation of the company's financial practices does not involve the state in the company's hiring practices. Even if it did, government regulation and funding (however substantial) do not make private action attributable to the state (Choice D). As a result, the man's suit will likely fail because the company is not a state actor subject to the First and Fourteenth Amendments. (Choice A) Discretionary government decisions (including hiring decisions) are governed by the First Amendment. But since the private insurance company's hiring practices are not attributable to the state, the man will not prevail. (Choice C) Private companies or industries affected with a public interest (eg, insurance companies) are subject to government regulations for the public good. But such regulations do not amount to significant government involvement that will trigger the state-action doctrine.\n\nEducational objective: Under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution when the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. But mere government regulation or funding (no matter how substantial) does not make a private party's actions attributable to the state.\n\nReferences\n• Am. Mfrs. Mut. Ins. Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999) (holding that private insurance companies are not government actors unless the government is significantly involved in their activities).","educationalObjective":"Under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution when the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. But mere government regulation or funding (no matter how substantial) does not make a private party's actions attributable to the state.","references":"• Am. Mfrs. Mut. Ins. Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999) (holding that private insurance companies are not government actors unless the government is significantly involved in their activities).","rule":"Under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution when the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. But mere government regulation or funding (no matter how substantial) does not make a private party's actions attributable to the state.","irac":{"issue":"Is the man likely to prevail?","rule":"Under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is treated as a government actor and bound by the Constitution when the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. But mere government regulation or funding (no matter how substantial) does not make a private party's actions attributable to the state.","analysis":"Constitutional amendments (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) only apply to government actors. But a private actor can be treated as a government actor and be subject to the Constitution under the state-action doctrine. This doctrine applies when (1) a private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or (2) the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities. The private actor can then be sued for violating the Constitution—including the First and Fourteenth Amendment guarantee of freedom of expression. Here, the private insurance company does not perform a traditional and exclusive government function since insurance has historically been provided by private companies. And state regulation of the company's financial practices does not involve the state in the company's hiring practices. Even if it did, government regulation and funding (however substantial) do not make private action attributable to the state (Choice D). As a result, the man's suit will likely fail because the company is not a state actor subject to the First and Fourteenth Amendments. (Choice A) Discretionary government decisions (including hiring decisions) are governed by the First Amendment. But since the private insurance company's hiring practices are not attributable to the state, the man will not prevail. (Choice C) Private companies or industries affected with a public interest (eg, insurance companies) are subject to government regulations for the public good. But such regulations do not amount to significant government involvement that will trigger the state-action doctrine.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the company is not subject to the provisions of the First and Fourteenth Amendments."},"questionZh":"州法律对在州内运营的保险公司的费率、现金储备和财务实践施加了严格的规定。一家在该州运营的私营保险公司在广告中表示，它希望雇用一名新的数据处理器。在审查了该职位的申请后，该公司雇用了一名似乎非常合格的女性。该公司拒绝考虑比该女性更合格的男性的申请，因为众所周知，他有激进的政治观点。该男子起诉该公司，指控该公司侵犯了他的联邦宪法言论自由权。男人有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为招聘决定完全是酌情决定的，因此不受第一修正案的约束。","B":"不可以，因为公司不受第一修正案和第十四修正案规定的约束。","C":"是的，因为公司受到公共利益的影响。","D":"是的，因为公司实质上是受国家监管的，因此其雇佣决定完全可以归因于国家。"},"explanationZh":"宪法修正案（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为者。但私人行为者可以被视为政府行为者，并根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）遵守宪法。该原则适用于以下情况：(1) 私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。然后，私人行为者可能会因违反宪法而被起诉——包括第一修正案和第十四修正案对言论自由的保障。在这里，私人保险公司并不履行传统的、排他性的政府职能，因为保险历来都是由私人公司提供的。国家对公司财务行为的监管并不涉及公司的招聘行为。即使确实如此，政府监管和资金（无论数额如何）也不会使私人行为归属于国家（选择 D）。因此，该男子的诉讼可能会失败，因为该公司不是受第一修正案和第十四修正案约束的国家行为体。 （选项 A）政府酌情决定（包括雇用决定）受第一修正案管辖。但由于私人保险公司的雇佣行为不归咎于国家，因此该男子不会获胜。 （选择C） 受公共利益影响的私营公司或行业（例如保险公司）应遵守政府出于公共利益的法规。但此类法规并不意味着政府的重大参与将引发国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）。\n\n教育目标：根据国家行为学说，当政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。但仅仅政府监管或资金（无论数额有多大）并不能使私人团体的行为归因于国家。\n\n参考文献\n• 上午。制造商。穆特。插入。 Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999)（认为私人保险公司不是政府行为者，除非政府大力参与其活动）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据国家行为原则，当政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。但仅仅政府监管或资金（无论数额有多大）并不能使私人团体的行为归因于国家。","iracZh":{"issue":"男人有可能获胜吗？","rule":"根据国家行为原则，当政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。但仅仅政府监管或资金（无论数额有多大）并不能使私人团体的行为归因于国家。","analysis":"宪法修正案（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为者。但私人行为者可以被视为政府行为者，并根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）遵守宪法。该原则适用于以下情况：(1) 私人行为者履行传统且排他性的政府职能，或 (2) 政府大量参与私人行为者的活动。然后，私人行为者可能会因违反宪法而被起诉——包括第一修正案和第十四修正案对言论自由的保障。在这里，私人保险公司并不履行传统的、排他性的政府职能，因为保险历来都是由私人公司提供的。国家对公司财务行为的监管并不涉及公司的招聘行为。即使确实如此，政府监管和资金（无论数额如何）也不会使私人行为归属于国家（选择 D）。因此，该男子的诉讼可能会失败，因为该公司不是受第一修正案和第十四修正案约束的国家行为体。 （选项 A）政府酌情决定（包括雇用决定）受第一修正案管辖。但由于私人保险公司的雇佣行为不归咎于国家，因此该男子不会获胜。 （选择C） 受公共利益影响的私营公司或行业（例如保险公司）应遵守政府出于公共利益的法规。但此类法规并不意味着政府的重大参与将引发国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）。\n\n教育目标：根据国家行为学说，当政府大量参与私人行为者的活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者并受宪法约束。但仅仅政府监管或资金（无论数额有多大）并不能使私人团体的行为归因于国家。\n\n参考文献\n• 上午。制造商。穆特。插入。 Co. v. Sullivan, 526 U.S. 40, 52 (1999)（认为私人保险公司不是政府行为者，除非政府大力参与其活动）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为公司不受第一修正案和第十四修正案规定的约束。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-dfb752435e8daf8e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":227,"sourceQuestionPage":678,"sourceQuestionPages":[678],"sourceExplanationPages":[679,680],"sourceAnswerPages":[679,680],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-228","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Ex Post Facto & Bills of Attainder","question":"A state law makes it a criminal offense for any state employee to \"knowingly provide educational services or extend welfare benefits\" to a foreign national who was in the United States in violation of U.S. immigration laws. The principal of a public elementary school is prosecuted under the law for enrolling and providing education to several foreign nationals he knew to be in the country illegally. All of these actions took place before the law was adopted. No federal statute applied to the principal's actions. What constitutional provision would be most helpful to the principal's defense?","choices":{"A":"The due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","B":"The equal protection clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"The ex post facto clause of Article I, Section 10.","D":"The privileges or immunities clause of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Ex post facto clauses\n\n(applies to federal (art. 1, § 9) & state (art. 1, § 10) governments)\n\nProhibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that:\n\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof The Article I, § 10 ex post facto clause prohibits state governments from enacting retroactivecriminal laws—eg, laws that punish conduct that occurred beforethe law became effective (thereby criminalizing previously legal conduct). And because the principal enrolled foreign nationals before the law under which he is being prosecuted was adopted, the ex post facto clause would be most helpful to his defense. (Choice A) The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires that states enact reasonable laws. Laws that substantially burden a fundamental right are only reasonable if they pass strict scrutiny—which rarely occurs. But since the principal has no fundamental right to enroll foreign nationals in his school, this law need only pass rational basis scrutiny—which is easily done. Therefore, due process would not be helpful to his defense. (Choice B) The equal protection clause prohibits states from enacting laws that treat similarly situated people differently (as seen here). But since this state law does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, the law need only pass rational basis scrutiny—ie, be rationally related to any legitimate government interest. Therefore, an equal protection challenge would likely fail. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from infringing on the very limited rights of national citizenship—none of which are at issue here.\n\nEducational objective: The ex post facto clauses prohibit federal and state governments from enacting retroactive criminal laws—eg, laws that punish conduct that occurred before the law became effective.\n\nReferences\n• Lindsey v. Washington, 301 U.S. 397, 401 (1937) (explaining that the ex post facto clause \"forbids the application of any new punitive measure to a crime already consummated\").","educationalObjective":"The ex post facto clauses prohibit federal and state governments from enacting retroactive criminal laws—eg, laws that punish conduct that occurred before the law became effective.","references":"• Lindsey v. Washington, 301 U.S. 397, 401 (1937) (explaining that the ex post facto clause \"forbids the application of any new punitive measure to a crime already consummated\").","rule":"The ex post facto clauses prohibit federal and state governments from enacting retroactive criminal laws—eg, laws that punish conduct that occurred before the law became effective.","irac":{"issue":"What constitutional provision would be most helpful to the principal's defense?","rule":"The ex post facto clauses prohibit federal and state governments from enacting retroactive criminal laws—eg, laws that punish conduct that occurred before the law became effective.","analysis":"Ex post facto clauses\n\n(applies to federal (art. 1, § 9) & state (art. 1, § 10) governments)\n\nProhibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that:\n\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof The Article I, § 10 ex post facto clause prohibits state governments from enacting retroactivecriminal laws—eg, laws that punish conduct that occurred beforethe law became effective (thereby criminalizing previously legal conduct). And because the principal enrolled foreign nationals before the law under which he is being prosecuted was adopted, the ex post facto clause would be most helpful to his defense. (Choice A) The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires that states enact reasonable laws. Laws that substantially burden a fundamental right are only reasonable if they pass strict scrutiny—which rarely occurs. But since the principal has no fundamental right to enroll foreign nationals in his school, this law need only pass rational basis scrutiny—which is easily done. Therefore, due process would not be helpful to his defense. (Choice B) The equal protection clause prohibits states from enacting laws that treat similarly situated people differently (as seen here). But since this state law does not substantially impact a fundamental right or protected class, the law need only pass rational basis scrutiny—ie, be rationally related to any legitimate government interest. Therefore, an equal protection challenge would likely fail. (Choice D) The Fourteenth Amendment privileges or immunities clause prohibits states from infringing on the very limited rights of national citizenship—none of which are at issue here.","conclusion":"Answer C: The ex post facto clause of Article I, Section 10."},"questionZh":"州法律规定，任何州雇员“故意向在美国的外国人提供教育服务或提供福利”，违反美国移民法，即构成刑事犯罪。一所公立小学的校长因招收数名他明知非法入境的外国人并为其提供教育而受到法律起诉。所有这些行动都发生在该法律通过之前。没有联邦法规适用于委托人的行为。哪些宪法条款对校长的辩护最有帮助？","choicesZh":{"A":"第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","B":"第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。","C":"第一条第 10 节的事后条款。","D":"第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款。"},"explanationZh":"事后条款\n\n（适用于联邦（第 1 条第 9 条）和州（第 1 条第 10 条）政府）\n\n禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法：\n\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 第 I 条第 10 款事后条款禁止州政府制定追溯性刑法，例如惩罚在法律生效之前发生的行为的法律（从而将先前合法的行为定为刑事犯罪）。而且，由于校长在起诉他的法律通过之前就已经招收了外国人，因此事后条款对他的辩护最有帮助。 （选项 A） 第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求各州制定合理的法律。对基本权利造成重大负担的法律只有通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）才是合理的——而这种情况很少发生。但由于校长没有在学校招收外国人的基本权利，这项法律只需要通过合理基础的审查——这很容易做到。因此，正当程序对他的辩护没有帮助。 （选项 B）平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）禁止各州颁布法律以区别对待处境相似的人（如此处所示）。但由于该州法律不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，因此该法律只需要通过合理基础审查，即与任何合法的政府利益合理相关。因此，平等保护挑战很可能会失败。 （选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州侵犯国民公民的非常有限的权利——这些权利都不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：事后条款禁止联邦和州政府制定追溯性刑法，例如惩罚在法律生效之前发生的行为的法律。\n\n参考文献\n• Lindsey v. Washington, 301 U.S. 397, 401 (1937)（解释事后条款“禁止对已经完成的犯罪适用任何新的惩罚措施”）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"事后条款禁止联邦和州政府颁布追溯性刑法，例如惩罚在法律生效之前发生的行为的法律。","iracZh":{"issue":"哪些宪法条款对校长的辩护最有帮助？","rule":"事后条款禁止联邦和州政府颁布追溯性刑法，例如惩罚在法律生效之前发生的行为的法律。","analysis":"事后条款\n\n（适用于联邦（第 1 条第 9 条）和州（第 1 条第 10 条）政府）\n\n禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法：\n\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 第 I 条第 10 款事后条款禁止州政府制定追溯性刑法，例如惩罚在法律生效之前发生的行为的法律（从而将先前合法的行为定为刑事犯罪）。而且，由于校长在起诉他的法律通过之前就已经招收了外国人，因此事后条款对他的辩护最有帮助。 （选项 A） 第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求各州制定合理的法律。对基本权利造成重大负担的法律只有通过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）才是合理的——而这种情况很少发生。但由于校长没有在学校招收外国人的基本权利，这项法律只需要通过合理基础的审查——这很容易做到。因此，正当程序对他的辩护没有帮助。 （选项 B）平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）禁止各州颁布法律以区别对待处境相似的人（如此处所示）。但由于该州法律不会对基本权利或受保护群体产生实质性影响，因此该法律只需要通过合理基础审查，即与任何合法的政府利益合理相关。因此，平等保护挑战很可能会失败。 （选项 D）第十四修正案的特权或豁免条款禁止各州侵犯国民公民的非常有限的权利——这些权利都不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：事后条款禁止联邦和州政府制定追溯性刑法，例如惩罚在法律生效之前发生的行为的法律。\n\n参考文献\n• Lindsey v. Washington, 301 U.S. 397, 401 (1937)（解释事后条款“禁止对已经完成的犯罪适用任何新的惩罚措施”）。","conclusion":"答案 C：第一条第 10 节的事后条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"illegally","ipa":"/ˌɪlˈiɡəli/","syllables":"il·le·gally","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 非法地, 不合法地, 违法地","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：[法] 非法地, 不合法地, 违法地","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The principal of a public elementary school is prosecuted under the law for enrolling and providing education to several foreign nationals he knew to be in the country illegally.","synonyms":["illicitly","lawlessly"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":228,"sourceQuestionPage":681,"sourceQuestionPages":[681],"sourceExplanationPages":[682,683],"sourceAnswerPages":[682,683],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-229","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Privileges & Immunities","question":"A doctor is a resident of state A and is licensed to practice medicine in State A as well as neighboring State B. The doctor finds that the most convenient place to treat her patients who need hospital care is in a publicly owned and operated hospital located in State B. For many years, the doctor had successfully treated her patients in that hospital. Early this year, the hospital adopted a new rule in conformance with all required procedures. The new rule stated that every doctor who practices in that hospital must be a resident of State B. The doctor was then notified that she could no longer treat patients in that hospital because she was not a resident of State B. Which of the following constitutional provisions would be most helpful to the doctor in an action to challenge her exclusion from the State B hospital solely on the basis of this hospital rule?","choices":{"A":"The bill of attainder clause.","B":"The due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment.","C":"The ex post facto clause.","D":"The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Rights of state citizenship\n\n(Article IV privileges & immunities clause)\n\n• Travel/reside in state\n• Pursue employment\n• Practice commercial trade/business\n• Own/transfer property\n• Seek medical services\n• Access state courts\n• Civil liberties\n• Creditor's rights\n• Tax exemptions The Article IV privileges and immunities clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship. But such discrimination is valid if the state can show:\n• a substantial reason for the difference in treatment—ie, nonresidents cause or contribute to the problem that the state is attempting to solve and\n• the discrimination against nonresidents bears a substantial relationship to the state's objective. Here, the State B hospital rule discriminates against out-of-state doctors by excluding them from practicing in that hospital. This denies out-of-state doctors a right of state citizenship—to practice their profession in State B. And since there is no evidence that out- of-state doctors contribute to any problem the state is trying to solve, the hospital rule likely violates the Article IV privileges and immunities clause. Therefore, this clause would be most helpful to the State A doctor in challenging her exclusion. (Choice A) The hospital rule is not a prohibited bill of attainder—ie, a legislative act that punishes a specified person or group without a judicial trial—since it is a legislative act and does not seek to punish the doctor. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause prohibits states from depriving persons of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. When no fundamental right is involved (as seen here), the state action need only be rationally related to a legitimate state interest. And since state action is almost always upheld under this rational basis test, the hospital rule likely complies with due process.\n\n(Choice C) The Article I ex post facto clauses bar state and federal governments from enacting criminal statutes that punish actions retroactively. But the hospital rule is not an ex post facto law since it is civil in nature.\n\nEducational objective: The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state citizens by denying them a right of state citizenship unless (1) nonresidents cause or contribute to the problem that the state is attempting to solve and (2) the discrimination bears a substantial relationship to the state's objective.\n\nReferences\n• Ward v. Maryland, 79 U.S. 418, 430 (1870) (holding that the Article IV privileges and immunities clause protects the right of non-state citizens to enter another state to conduct business or practice a trade).\n\n• 16B Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 800 (listing examples of privileges and immunities of state citizenship).","educationalObjective":"The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state citizens by denying them a right of state citizenship unless (1) nonresidents cause or contribute to the problem that the state is attempting to solve and (2) the discrimination bears a substantial relationship to the state's objective.","references":"• Ward v. Maryland, 79 U.S. 418, 430 (1870) (holding that the Article IV privileges and immunities clause protects the right of non-state citizens to enter another state to conduct business or practice a trade).\n\n• 16B Am. Jur. 2d Constitutional Law § 800 (listing examples of privileges and immunities of state citizenship).","rule":"The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state citizens by denying them a right of state citizenship unless (1) nonresidents cause or contribute to the problem that the state is attempting to solve and (2) the discrimination bears a substantial relationship to the state's objective.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following constitutional provisions would be most helpful to the doctor in an action to challenge her exclusion from the State B hospital solely on the basis of this hospital rule?","rule":"The Article IV privileges and immunities clause prohibits states from discriminating against out-of-state citizens by denying them a right of state citizenship unless (1) nonresidents cause or contribute to the problem that the state is attempting to solve and (2) the discrimination bears a substantial relationship to the state's objective.","analysis":"Rights of state citizenship\n\n(Article IV privileges & immunities clause)\n\n• Travel/reside in state\n• Pursue employment\n• Practice commercial trade/business\n• Own/transfer property\n• Seek medical services\n• Access state courts\n• Civil liberties\n• Creditor's rights\n• Tax exemptions The Article IV privileges and immunities clause generally prohibits states from discriminating against citizens of other states by denying them a right of state citizenship. But such discrimination is valid if the state can show:\n• a substantial reason for the difference in treatment—ie, nonresidents cause or contribute to the problem that the state is attempting to solve and\n• the discrimination against nonresidents bears a substantial relationship to the state's objective. Here, the State B hospital rule discriminates against out-of-state doctors by excluding them from practicing in that hospital. This denies out-of-state doctors a right of state citizenship—to practice their profession in State B. And since there is no evidence that out- of-state doctors contribute to any problem the state is trying to solve, the hospital rule likely violates the Article IV privileges and immunities clause. Therefore, this clause would be most helpful to the State A doctor in challenging her exclusion. (Choice A) The hospital rule is not a prohibited bill of attainder—ie, a legislative act that punishes a specified person or group without a judicial trial—since it is a legislative act and does not seek to punish the doctor. (Choice B) The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause prohibits states from depriving persons of life, liberty, or property without adequate justification. When no fundamental right is involved (as seen here), the state action need only be rationally related to a legitimate state interest. And since state action is almost always upheld under this rational basis test, the hospital rule likely complies with due process.\n\n(Choice C) The Article I ex post facto clauses bar state and federal governments from enacting criminal statutes that punish actions retroactively. But the hospital rule is not an ex post facto law since it is civil in nature.","conclusion":"Answer D: The privileges and immunities clause of Article IV."},"questionZh":"一名医生是 A 州的居民，并拥有 A 州以及邻近的 B 州的行医执照。该医生发现，治疗需要住院治疗的患者的最方便的地方是位于 B 州的一家公立和经营的医院。多年来，该医生一直在该医院成功治疗她的患者。今年年初，医院采取了符合所有必要程序的新规则。新规则规定，在该医院执业的每位医生都必须是 B 州居民。然后，该医生被告知，她不能再在该医院治疗患者，因为她不是 B 州居民。以下哪项宪法条款对于该医生仅根据该医院规则对她被排除在 B 州医院提出质疑的诉讼中最有帮助？","choicesZh":{"A":"剥夺公民权法案。","B":"第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）。","C":"事后条款。","D":"第四条的特权和豁免条款。"},"explanationZh":"国家公民的权利\n\n（第四条特权与豁免条款）\n\n• 在州内旅行/居住\n• 谋求就业\n• 实践商业贸易/商业\n• 拥有/转让财产\n• 寻求医疗服务\n• 进入州法院\n• 公民自由\n• 债权\n• 免税 第四条特权和豁免条款一般禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的权利来歧视其他国家的公民。但如果国家能够证明：\n• 造成待遇差异的实质性原因——即非居民造成或促成国家试图解决的问题，以及\n• 对非居民的歧视与国家目标密切相关。在这里，B 州医院的规定歧视外州医生，禁止他们在该医院执业。这剥夺了州外医生的州公民权，即在 B 州执业的权利。而且，由于没有证据表明州外医生对该州试图解决的任何问题做出了贡献，医院的规定可能违反了第四条的特权和豁免条款。因此，该条款对于 A 州医生质疑她的排除是最有帮助的。 （选项A） 医院规则不是一项被禁止的剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为，因为它是一项立法行为，并不寻求惩罚医生。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺他人的生命、自由或财产。当不涉及基本权利时（如此处所示），国家行为只需与合法的国家利益合理相关。由于在这种合理基础测试下，国家行为（state action）几乎总是得到支持，因此医院规则可能符合正当程序。\n\n（选项 C）第一条事后条款禁止州和联邦政府颁布追溯性惩罚行为的刑事法规。但医院规则并不是事后法律，因为它具有民事性质。\n\n教育目标：第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺州外公民的国家公民权利来歧视他们，除非 (1) 非居民造成或促成国家试图解决的问题，并且 (2) 歧视与国家目标有重大关系。\n\n参考文献\n• Ward v. Maryland, 79 U.S. 418, 430 (1870)（认为第四条特权和豁免条款保护非州公民进入另一州开展业务或从事贸易的权利）。\n\n• 上午 16 点。尤尔。 2d 宪法第 800 条（列出国家公民特权和豁免权的示例）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺州外公民的国家公民权利来歧视他们，除非（1）非居民造成或促成国家试图解决的问题，并且（2）歧视与国家的目标有实质性关系。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项宪法条款对医生提起诉讼，仅根据该医院规则而将她排除在 B 州医院之外最有帮助？","rule":"第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺州外公民的国家公民权利来歧视他们，除非（1）非居民造成或促成国家试图解决的问题，并且（2）歧视与国家的目标有实质性关系。","analysis":"国家公民的权利\n\n（第四条特权与豁免条款）\n\n• 在州内旅行/居住\n• 谋求就业\n• 实践商业贸易/商业\n• 拥有/转让财产\n• 寻求医疗服务\n• 进入州法院\n• 公民自由\n• 债权\n• 免税 第四条特权和豁免条款一般禁止国家通过剥夺其他国家公民的权利来歧视其他国家的公民。但如果国家能够证明：\n• 造成待遇差异的实质性原因——即非居民造成或促成国家试图解决的问题，以及\n• 对非居民的歧视与国家目标密切相关。在这里，B 州医院的规定歧视外州医生，禁止他们在该医院执业。这剥夺了州外医生的州公民权，即在 B 州执业的权利。而且，由于没有证据表明州外医生对该州试图解决的任何问题做出了贡献，医院的规定可能违反了第四条的特权和豁免条款。因此，该条款对于 A 州医生质疑她的排除是最有帮助的。 （选项A） 医院规则不是一项被禁止的剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判而惩罚特定个人或群体的立法行为，因为它是一项立法行为，并不寻求惩罚医生。 （选项 B） 第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）禁止各州在没有充分理由的情况下剥夺他人的生命、自由或财产。当不涉及基本权利时（如此处所示），国家行为只需与合法的国家利益合理相关。由于在这种合理基础测试下，国家行为（state action）几乎总是得到支持，因此医院规则可能符合正当程序。\n\n（选项 C）第一条事后条款禁止州和联邦政府颁布追溯性惩罚行为的刑事法规。但医院规则并不是事后法律，因为它具有民事性质。\n\n教育目标：第四条特权和豁免条款禁止国家通过剥夺州外公民的国家公民权利来歧视他们，除非 (1) 非居民造成或促成国家试图解决的问题，并且 (2) 歧视与国家目标有重大关系。\n\n参考文献\n• Ward v. Maryland, 79 U.S. 418, 430 (1870)（认为第四条特权和豁免条款保护非州公民进入另一州开展业务或从事贸易的权利）。\n\n• 上午 16 点。尤尔。 2d 宪法第 800 条（列出国家公民特权和豁免权的示例）。","conclusion":"答案 D：第四条的特权和豁免条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":229,"sourceQuestionPage":684,"sourceQuestionPages":[684],"sourceExplanationPages":[685,686],"sourceAnswerPages":[685,686],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-230","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Substantive Due Process","question":"A state generally provides funding for the medical care of its residents who cannot afford such care. State law, however, prohibits use of this state funding for surgery for any person who has resided in the state for less than one year, except in emergency situations. A woman moved to the state two months ago seeking permanent employment. Her physician recommends non-emergency surgery to treat a medical condition. The surgery would qualify for state funding if the woman had resided in the state for a year. The woman has sued to invalidate the state law that prohibits state funding of her surgery. Should the woman prevail in her action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the law reasonably conserves the state's limited resources.","B":"No, because the law reasonably prevents the expenditure of state funds on transient nonresidents.","C":"Yes, because the law burdens the woman's fundamental right to health care.","D":"Yes, because the law burdens the woman's fundamental right to travel."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A woman who recently moved to the state was denied access to state funding for a surgery due to a durational residency requirement. Laws with this requirement are generally reviewed under the equal protection clause since they treat two classes of persons differently—those who have resided in the state for a designated period and those who have not. And since these laws substantially burden the fundamental right to travel by discouraging persons from moving into the state, they are subject to strict scrutiny and almost always held unconstitutional. Therefore, the woman should prevail in her action to invalidate the law. (Choices A & B) A law's ability to reasonably achieve a legitimate state interest (eg, conservation of state resources or funds) would validate the law under the rational basis test. But this test only applies when a law does not substantially impact a fundamental right or suspect class (which triggers strict scrutiny) or a quasi-suspect class (which triggers intermediate scrutiny). (Choice C) There is no fundamental right to health care, so the law will not be invalidated on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: Since durational residency requirements burden the fundamental right to travel, they are strictly scrutinized and almost always held unconstitutional under the equal protection clause.\n\nReferences\n• Shapiro v. Thompson, 394 U.S. 618, 627 (1969) (invalidating durational residency requirement under strict scrutiny equal protection analysis).","educationalObjective":"Since durational residency requirements burden the fundamental right to travel, they are strictly scrutinized and almost always held unconstitutional under the equal protection clause.","references":"• Shapiro v. Thompson, 394 U.S. 618, 627 (1969) (invalidating durational residency requirement under strict scrutiny equal protection analysis).","rule":"Since durational residency requirements burden the fundamental right to travel, they are strictly scrutinized and almost always held unconstitutional under the equal protection clause.","irac":{"issue":"Should the woman prevail in her action?","rule":"Since durational residency requirements burden the fundamental right to travel, they are strictly scrutinized and almost always held unconstitutional under the equal protection clause.","analysis":"A woman who recently moved to the state was denied access to state funding for a surgery due to a durational residency requirement. Laws with this requirement are generally reviewed under the equal protection clause since they treat two classes of persons differently—those who have resided in the state for a designated period and those who have not. And since these laws substantially burden the fundamental right to travel by discouraging persons from moving into the state, they are subject to strict scrutiny and almost always held unconstitutional. Therefore, the woman should prevail in her action to invalidate the law. (Choices A & B) A law's ability to reasonably achieve a legitimate state interest (eg, conservation of state resources or funds) would validate the law under the rational basis test. But this test only applies when a law does not substantially impact a fundamental right or suspect class (which triggers strict scrutiny) or a quasi-suspect class (which triggers intermediate scrutiny). (Choice C) There is no fundamental right to health care, so the law will not be invalidated on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the law burdens the woman's fundamental right to travel."},"questionZh":"国家通常为无力承担医疗费用的居民提供医疗费用。然而，州法律禁止将州资助用于在该州居住不足一年的任何人进行手术，紧急情况除外。一名妇女两个月前搬到该州寻求永久就业。她的医生建议进行非紧急手术来治疗健康状况。如果该妇女在该州居住了一年，则该手术将有资格获得国家资助。该妇女已提起诉讼，要求使禁止国家资助她的手术的州法律无效。女人的行为应该占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为法律合理地保护了国家有限的资源。","B":"不可以，因为法律合理地阻止国家资金用于临时非居民。","C":"是的，因为法律规定了妇女获得医疗保健的基本权利。","D":"是的，因为法律规定了妇女的基本旅行权利。"},"explanationZh":"一名最近搬到该州的妇女因长期居住要求而被拒绝获得国家手术资金。具有这一要求的法律通常会根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）进行审查，因为它们以不同的方式对待两类人：在该州居住了指定期限的人和没有在该州居住过指定期限的人。由于这些法律阻止人们进入该州，从而对基本旅行权利造成了沉重负担，因此它们受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且几乎总是被认为违宪。因此，妇女在主张法律无效的诉讼中应胜诉。 （选项 A 和 B） 法律合理实现合法国家利益（例如，保护国家资源或资金）的能力将在合理基础测试下验证该法律。但这一测试仅适用于法律不会对基本权利或可疑类别（引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））或准可疑类别（引发中间审查）产生实质性影响的情况。 （选项C） 没有基本的医疗权利，因此法律不会因此而失效。\n\n教育目标：由于长期居住要求对基本旅行权利造成负担，因此受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且在平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）下几乎总是被视为违宪。\n\n参考文献\n• Shapiro v. Thompson, 394 U.S. 618, 627 (1969)（在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）平等保护分析下使持续居住要求无效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"由于长期居住要求对基本旅行权构成负担，因此它们受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）几乎总是被视为违宪。","iracZh":{"issue":"女人的行为应该占上风吗？","rule":"由于长期居住要求对基本旅行权构成负担，因此它们受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）几乎总是被视为违宪。","analysis":"一名最近搬到该州的妇女因长期居住要求而被拒绝获得国家手术资金。具有这一要求的法律通常会根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）进行审查，因为它们以不同的方式对待两类人：在该州居住了指定期限的人和没有在该州居住过指定期限的人。由于这些法律阻止人们进入该州，从而对基本旅行权利造成了沉重负担，因此它们受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且几乎总是被认为违宪。因此，妇女在主张法律无效的诉讼中应胜诉。 （选项 A 和 B） 法律合理实现合法国家利益（例如，保护国家资源或资金）的能力将在合理基础测试下验证该法律。但这一测试仅适用于法律不会对基本权利或可疑类别（引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））或准可疑类别（引发中间审查）产生实质性影响的情况。 （选项C） 没有基本的医疗权利，因此法律不会因此而失效。\n\n教育目标：由于长期居住要求对基本旅行权利造成负担，因此受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），并且在平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）下几乎总是被视为违宪。\n\n参考文献\n• Shapiro v. Thompson, 394 U.S. 618, 627 (1969)（在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）平等保护分析下使持续居住要求无效）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为法律规定了妇女的基本旅行权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-bfe47b1d10f919a6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":230,"sourceQuestionPage":687,"sourceQuestionPages":[687],"sourceExplanationPages":[688,689],"sourceAnswerPages":[688,689],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-231","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Free Exercise Clause","question":"A police officer was employed on a city's police force for 10 years. When the officer accepted the job, the city's employee benefit plan provided a death benefit to the spouse of any employee who died as a result of any job-related injury. Last year, the city amended its employee benefit plan to deny its death benefit in cases where the death \"was caused by the employee's refusal to accept, for any reason, reasonably available medical care prescribed by a physician.\" After this amendment took effect, the officer was shot while on duty. Because of a sincerely held religious belief, the officer refused to allow a prescribed blood transfusion and, as a result, died from loss of blood. When the officer's spouse applied for the death benefit, the city denied the application on the basis of the amendment to the employee benefit plan. The officer's spouse has challenged the amendment, claiming that, as applied to the officer, it violated the officer's constitutional right to the free exercise of religion. Is the court likely to find the amendment to the employee benefit plan constitutional as applied to the officer?","choices":{"A":"No, because it effectively discriminates against a religious practice.","B":"No, because it violates the vested contractual rights of city employees who were hired before the amendment took effect.","C":"Yes, because it is generally applicable and religiously neutral, as well as a reasonable limitation on the award of such benefits.","D":"Yes, because it imposes a condition only on the award of a government benefit and does not impose a penalty on an individual's conduct."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The First Amendment free exercise clause prohibits governments from denying benefits (or imposing burdens) based on an individual's religious belief or conduct (Choice D). The level of scrutiny applied to laws or policies that burden religion depends on whether that burden was:\n• direct (intentional) – triggering strict scrutiny, which invalidates a law unless the government proves that it is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• incidental (generally applicable and religiously neutral) – triggering rational basis scrutiny, which upholds a law unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.\n\nBut even an incidental burden will trigger strict scrutiny when the law requires an individualized assessment of a person's actions. Here, the employee benefit plan only imposes an incidental burden since the plan is generally applicable to all employees and does not intentionally target religion (ie, is religiously neutral). That is because the plan denies benefits when the refusal to accept medical care was for any reason—it does not single out religious reasons (ie, no individualized assessment). And since the plan is reasonably related to the city's legitimate interest in limiting the award of benefits to job-related deaths, it is likely constitutional. (Choice A) Although the benefit plan effectively discriminates against the officer's religious practice, the plan will likely be upheld since it is generally applicable and there is no evidence that it intentionally interfered with his religion. (Choice B) The contracts clause prohibits state and municipal legislatures from enacting laws that substantially impair vested contract rights. But it does not apply to amendments to employee benefit plans since they are unrelated to lawmaking or rulemaking.\n\nEducational objective: Direct burdens on religion are strictly scrutinized and struck down unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. In contrast, incidental burdens are upheld unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 884–85 (1990) (affirming the government's ability to enforce a generally applicable law that incidentally burdens religion).","educationalObjective":"Direct burdens on religion are strictly scrutinized and struck down unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. In contrast, incidental burdens are upheld unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.","references":"• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 884–85 (1990) (affirming the government's ability to enforce a generally applicable law that incidentally burdens religion).","rule":"Direct burdens on religion are strictly scrutinized and struck down unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. In contrast, incidental burdens are upheld unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to find the amendment to the employee benefit plan constitutional as applied to the officer?","rule":"Direct burdens on religion are strictly scrutinized and struck down unless the government proves that the law is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. In contrast, incidental burdens are upheld unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.","analysis":"The First Amendment free exercise clause prohibits governments from denying benefits (or imposing burdens) based on an individual's religious belief or conduct (Choice D). The level of scrutiny applied to laws or policies that burden religion depends on whether that burden was:\n• direct (intentional) – triggering strict scrutiny, which invalidates a law unless the government proves that it is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• incidental (generally applicable and religiously neutral) – triggering rational basis scrutiny, which upholds a law unless the challenger proves that the law has no rational relation to any legitimate government interest.\n\nBut even an incidental burden will trigger strict scrutiny when the law requires an individualized assessment of a person's actions. Here, the employee benefit plan only imposes an incidental burden since the plan is generally applicable to all employees and does not intentionally target religion (ie, is religiously neutral). That is because the plan denies benefits when the refusal to accept medical care was for any reason—it does not single out religious reasons (ie, no individualized assessment). And since the plan is reasonably related to the city's legitimate interest in limiting the award of benefits to job-related deaths, it is likely constitutional. (Choice A) Although the benefit plan effectively discriminates against the officer's religious practice, the plan will likely be upheld since it is generally applicable and there is no evidence that it intentionally interfered with his religion. (Choice B) The contracts clause prohibits state and municipal legislatures from enacting laws that substantially impair vested contract rights. But it does not apply to amendments to employee benefit plans since they are unrelated to lawmaking or rulemaking.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because it is generally applicable and religiously neutral, as well as a reasonable limitation on the award of such benefits."},"questionZh":"一名警官在一个城市的警察部队工作了 10 年。当该官员接受这份工作时，该市的员工福利计划向因工伤死亡的任何员工的配偶提供死亡抚恤金。去年，该市修改了其员工福利计划，在死亡“因员工出于任何原因拒绝接受医生开具的合理医疗护理而导致”的情况下，拒绝提供死亡抚恤金。该修正案生效后，该警官在执行任务时被枪杀。由于真诚地持有宗教信仰，该警官拒绝接受规定的输血，结果因失血过多而死亡。当该官员的配偶申请死亡抚恤金时，该市以雇员福利计划的修订为由拒绝了该申请。该警官的配偶对该修正案提出质疑，声称该修正案适用于该警官，侵犯了该警官自由行使宗教信仰的宪法权利。法院是否可能认为适用于该官员的员工福利计划修正案符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为它实际上是对宗教活动的歧视。","B":"不可以，因为这侵犯了修正案生效前雇用的城市雇员的既得合同权利。","C":"是的，因为它普遍适用且宗教中立，并且对授予此类福利有合理的限制。","D":"是的，因为它只对政府福利的授予施加了条件，并且不对个人行为施加惩罚。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）禁止政府基于个人的宗教信仰或行为拒绝福利（或施加负担）（选项 D）。对宗教负担的法律或政策的审查程度取决于该负担是否：\n• 直接（有意）——引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这会使法律无效，除非政府证明有必要实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 偶然（普遍适用且宗教中立）——引发合理基础审查，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系，否则该法律将得到维护。\n\n但当法律要求对一个人的行为进行个性化评估时，即使是偶然的负担也会引发严格的审查。在这里，员工福利计划仅施加附带负担，因为该计划通常适用于所有员工并且无意针对宗教（即宗教中立）。这是因为，当出于任何原因拒绝接受医疗服务时，该计划都会拒绝提供福利——它没有单独指出宗教原因（即没有个性化评估）。由于该计划与该市限制与工作相关的死亡福利发放的合法利益合理相关，因此它可能符合宪法。 （选项A）虽然该福利计划实际上歧视该官员的宗教信仰，但该计划很可能会得到维持，因为它具有普遍适用性，并且没有证据表明该计划故意干扰其宗教信仰。 （选项 B）合同条款禁止州和市立法机构颁布严重损害既得合同权利的法律。但它不适用于员工福利计划的修改，因为它们与立法或规则制定无关。\n\n教育目标：除非政府证明法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则宗教的直接负担将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）和消除。相反，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的政府利益没有合理关系，否则附带负担将得到维持。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 884–85 (1990)（确认政府有能力执行普遍适用的法律，但同时也给宗教带来了负担）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则宗教的直接负担将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）和消除。相反，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的政府利益没有合理关系，否则附带负担将得到维持。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否可能认为适用于该官员的员工福利计划修正案符合宪法？","rule":"除非政府证明该法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则宗教的直接负担将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）和消除。相反，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的政府利益没有合理关系，否则附带负担将得到维持。","analysis":"第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）禁止政府基于个人的宗教信仰或行为拒绝福利（或施加负担）（选项 D）。对宗教负担的法律或政策的审查程度取决于该负担是否：\n• 直接（有意）——引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny），这会使法律无效，除非政府证明有必要实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 偶然（普遍适用且宗教中立）——引发合理基础审查，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法政府利益没有合理关系，否则该法律将得到维护。\n\n但当法律要求对一个人的行为进行个性化评估时，即使是偶然的负担也会引发严格的审查。在这里，员工福利计划仅施加附带负担，因为该计划通常适用于所有员工并且无意针对宗教（即宗教中立）。这是因为，当出于任何原因拒绝接受医疗服务时，该计划都会拒绝提供福利——它没有单独指出宗教原因（即没有个性化评估）。由于该计划与该市限制与工作相关的死亡福利发放的合法利益合理相关，因此它可能符合宪法。 （选项A）虽然该福利计划实际上歧视该官员的宗教信仰，但该计划很可能会得到维持，因为它具有普遍适用性，并且没有证据表明该计划故意干扰其宗教信仰。 （选项 B）合同条款禁止州和市立法机构颁布严重损害既得合同权利的法律。但它不适用于员工福利计划的修改，因为它们与立法或规则制定无关。\n\n教育目标：除非政府证明法律对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的，否则宗教的直接负担将受到严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）和消除。相反，除非挑战者证明该法律与任何合法的政府利益没有合理关系，否则附带负担将得到维持。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 884–85 (1990)（确认政府有能力执行普遍适用的法律，但同时也给宗教带来了负担）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为它普遍适用且宗教中立，并且对授予此类福利有合理的限制。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"lawmaking","ipa":"/lˈɔmˌeɪkɪŋ/","syllables":"law·mak·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 立法","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 立法","wordForm":"词；原形 lawmake；词形 i:lawmaking/0:lawmaking/1:i","sourceSentence":"But it does not apply to amendments to employee benefit plans since they are unrelated to lawmaking or rulemaking.","synonyms":["legislation","legislating"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-713dc2d0de798834"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":231,"sourceQuestionPage":690,"sourceQuestionPages":[690],"sourceExplanationPages":[691,692],"sourceAnswerPages":[691,692],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-232","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A high priest belonged to a small religious cult whose sincere beliefs required an annual animal sacrifice. The sacrifice required the priest to dissect the animal while it was still alive, which caused the animal excruciating pain and suffering. During one such sacrifice, the priest was arrested. He was charged and convicted under a state statute prohibiting animal cruelty. The priest appealed his conviction. Will the appellate court likely overturn the priest's conviction?","choices":{"A":"No, because the First Amendment does not protect animal sacrifices.","B":"No, because the state statute is rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in protecting animals.","C":"Yes, because the state statute directly burdened the priest's sincerely held religious belief in animal sacrifice.","D":"Yes, because the state statute is not necessary to achieve the state's compelling interest in protecting animals."},"answer":"B","explanation":"First Amendment free exercise of religion\n\nBurden Applicability Level of scrutiny\n\nIncidental Generally applicable to all persons Rational basis scrutiny:\n\n• burden rationally related to\n• legitimate government interest Direct Intentionally targets religious Strict scrutiny: belief/practice\n• burden necessary to achieve\n• compelling government interest The First Amendment free exercise clause, applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, prohibits government interference with or discrimination against sincere religious beliefs or conduct. As a result, criminal prosecution under a statute that burdens a defendant's religion can be challenged on these constitutional grounds. However, the defendant's likelihood of success depends on whether that burden is:\n• direct – the law intentionally targets religion and is usually unconstitutional under strict scrutiny or\n\n• incidental – the law generally applies to all and is usually constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.\n\nHere, the priest's sincere religious practice of animal sacrifice was burdened when he was convicted under the state's animal-cruelty statute. Since this statute applies equally to all— regardless of their religion—that burden is merely incidental (Choice C). And that burden is constitutional since the statute is rationally related to a legitimate state interest in protecting animals (Choice D). As a result, the priest's sincere religious belief does not excuse him from prosecution under this valid statute, and his conviction will likely be sustained by the appellate court. (Choice A) The free exercise clause applies to all religious practices—even those that are nontraditional or unpopular (eg, animal sacrifices). So had the state statute directly burdened the priest's religious beliefs and/or practices, it likely would be unconstitutional under strict scrutiny. But since it only incidentally burdens this practice, it is likely constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise a sincerely held religious belief, but it does not excuse persons from complying with an otherwise valid law. As a result, persons can be convicted of violating a law that incidentally burdens religion so long as the law is rationally related to a legitimate government interest.\n\nReferences\n• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–80 (1990) (upholding a generally applicable law that prohibits the use of peyote even though that law incidentally burdens Native American religious ceremonies).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise a sincerely held religious belief, but it does not excuse persons from complying with an otherwise valid law. As a result, persons can be convicted of violating a law that incidentally burdens religion so long as the law is rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","references":"• Emp't Div. v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–80 (1990) (upholding a generally applicable law that prohibits the use of peyote even though that law incidentally burdens Native American religious ceremonies).","rule":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise a sincerely held religious belief, but it does not excuse persons from complying with an otherwise valid law. As a result, persons can be convicted of violating a law that incidentally burdens religion so long as the law is rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","irac":{"issue":"Will the appellate court likely overturn the priest's conviction?","rule":"The First Amendment protects the right to freely exercise a sincerely held religious belief, but it does not excuse persons from complying with an otherwise valid law. As a result, persons can be convicted of violating a law that incidentally burdens religion so long as the law is rationally related to a legitimate government interest.","analysis":"First Amendment free exercise of religion\n\nBurden Applicability Level of scrutiny\n\nIncidental Generally applicable to all persons Rational basis scrutiny:\n\n• burden rationally related to\n• legitimate government interest Direct Intentionally targets religious Strict scrutiny: belief/practice\n• burden necessary to achieve\n• compelling government interest The First Amendment free exercise clause, applicable to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, prohibits government interference with or discrimination against sincere religious beliefs or conduct. As a result, criminal prosecution under a statute that burdens a defendant's religion can be challenged on these constitutional grounds. However, the defendant's likelihood of success depends on whether that burden is:\n• direct – the law intentionally targets religion and is usually unconstitutional under strict scrutiny or\n\n• incidental – the law generally applies to all and is usually constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.\n\nHere, the priest's sincere religious practice of animal sacrifice was burdened when he was convicted under the state's animal-cruelty statute. Since this statute applies equally to all— regardless of their religion—that burden is merely incidental (Choice C). And that burden is constitutional since the statute is rationally related to a legitimate state interest in protecting animals (Choice D). As a result, the priest's sincere religious belief does not excuse him from prosecution under this valid statute, and his conviction will likely be sustained by the appellate court. (Choice A) The free exercise clause applies to all religious practices—even those that are nontraditional or unpopular (eg, animal sacrifices). So had the state statute directly burdened the priest's religious beliefs and/or practices, it likely would be unconstitutional under strict scrutiny. But since it only incidentally burdens this practice, it is likely constitutional under rational basis scrutiny.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the state statute is rationally related to the state's legitimate interest in protecting animals."},"questionZh":"一位大祭司属于一个小型宗教团体，其真诚的信仰要求每年举行一次动物祭祀。祭祀要求祭司在动物还活着的时候解剖它，这给动物带来了难以忍受的痛苦。在一次这样的祭祀中，牧师被捕了。他根据禁止虐待动物的州法规被指控并定罪。神父对他的定罪提出上诉。上诉法院可能会推翻神父的定罪吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为第一修正案不保护动物献祭。","B":"不，因为国家法规与国家保护动物的合法利益合理相关。","C":"是的，因为国家法规直接加重了牧师对动物祭祀的真诚宗教信仰。","D":"是的，因为州法规对于实现州保护动物的迫切利益并不是必要的。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案自由行使宗教信仰\n\n负担 适用性 审查级别\n\n附带条款 一般适用于所有人 理性依据审查：\n\n• 合理相关的负担\n• 合法的政府利益 直接 有意针对宗教 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：信仰/实践\n• 实现目标所需的负担\n• 引人注目的政府利益 第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）通过第十四修正案适用于各州，禁止政府干涉或歧视真诚的宗教信仰或行为。因此，根据对被告的宗教造成负担的法规进行的刑事起诉可能会因这些宪法依据而受到质疑。然而，被告胜诉的可能性取决于该负担是否：\n• 直接——法律故意针对宗教，并且在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）或审查下通常是违宪的。\n\n• 附带的——法律一般适用于所有人，并且在合理基础上的审查下通常是符合宪法的。\n\n在这里，当牧师根据该州的虐待动物法规被定罪时，他真诚地进行动物献祭的宗教实践受到了负担。由于该法规平等地适用于所有人（无论其宗教信仰如何），因此这种负担只是偶然的（选择 C）。这种负担是符合宪法的，因为该法规与保护动物的合法国家利益合理相关（选择 D）。因此，该神父真诚的宗教信仰并不能免除他根据这项有效法规受到起诉的责任，上诉法院很可能维持对他的定罪。 （选项 A） 宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）适用于所有宗教活动，甚至包括那些非传统或不受欢迎的活动（例如动物祭祀）。因此，如果州法规直接对牧师的宗教信仰和/或实践施加负担，那么在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，它可能会违宪。但由于它只是偶然地给这种做法带来负担，因此在合理基础的审查下它可能是符合宪法的。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利，但并不免除人们遵守其他有效法律的责任。因此，只要法律与合法的政府利益合理相关，人们就可能因违反偶然给宗教带来负担的法律而被定罪。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–80 (1990)（维护一项普遍适用的法律，禁止使用佩奥特仙人掌，尽管该法律无意中给美洲原住民的宗教仪式带来了负担）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利，但它并不免除人们遵守其他有效法律的责任。因此，只要法律与合法的政府利益合理相关，人们就可能因违反偶然给宗教带来负担的法律而被定罪。","iracZh":{"issue":"上诉法院可能会推翻神父的定罪吗？","rule":"第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利，但它并不免除人们遵守其他有效法律的责任。因此，只要法律与合法的政府利益合理相关，人们就可能因违反偶然给宗教带来负担的法律而被定罪。","analysis":"第一修正案自由行使宗教信仰\n\n负担 适用性 审查级别\n\n附带条款 一般适用于所有人 理性依据审查：\n\n• 合理相关的负担\n• 合法的政府利益 直接 有意针对宗教 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：信仰/实践\n• 实现目标所需的负担\n• 引人注目的政府利益 第一修正案宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）通过第十四修正案适用于各州，禁止政府干涉或歧视真诚的宗教信仰或行为。因此，根据对被告的宗教造成负担的法规进行的刑事起诉可能会因这些宪法依据而受到质疑。然而，被告胜诉的可能性取决于该负担是否：\n• 直接——法律故意针对宗教，并且在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）或审查下通常是违宪的。\n\n• 附带的——法律一般适用于所有人，并且在合理基础上的审查下通常是符合宪法的。\n\n在这里，当牧师根据该州的虐待动物法规被定罪时，他真诚地进行动物献祭的宗教实践受到了负担。由于该法规平等地适用于所有人（无论其宗教信仰如何），因此这种负担只是偶然的（选择 C）。这种负担是符合宪法的，因为该法规与保护动物的合法国家利益合理相关（选择 D）。因此，该神父真诚的宗教信仰并不能免除他根据这项有效法规受到起诉的责任，上诉法院很可能维持对他的定罪。 （选项 A） 宗教宗教自由行使条款（Free Exercise Clause）（Free Exercise Clause）适用于所有宗教活动，甚至包括那些非传统或不受欢迎的活动（例如动物祭祀）。因此，如果州法规直接对牧师的宗教信仰和/或实践施加负担，那么在严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）下，它可能会违宪。但由于它只是偶然地给这种做法带来负担，因此在合理基础的审查下它可能是符合宪法的。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案保护自由行使真诚宗教信仰的权利，但并不免除人们遵守其他有效法律的责任。因此，只要法律与合法的政府利益合理相关，人们就可能因违反偶然给宗教带来负担的法律而被定罪。\n\n参考文献\n• 空缺部门。 v. Smith, 494 U.S. 872, 878–80 (1990)（维护一项普遍适用的法律，禁止使用佩奥特仙人掌，尽管该法律无意中给美洲原住民的宗教仪式带来了负担）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为国家法规与国家保护动物的合法利益合理相关。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":232,"sourceQuestionPage":693,"sourceQuestionPages":[693],"sourceExplanationPages":[694,695],"sourceAnswerPages":[694,695],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-233","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state has a state employee grievance system that requires any state employee who wishes to file a grievance against the state to submit that grievance for final resolution to a panel of three arbitrators chosen by the parties from a statewide board of 13 arbitrators. In any given case, the grievant and the state alternate in exercising the right of each party to eliminate five members of the board, leaving a panel of three members to decide their case. At the present time, the full board is composed of seven male arbitrators and six female arbitrators. A female state employee filed a sexual harassment grievance against her male supervisor and the state. The state's attorney exercised all of her five strikes to eliminate five of the female arbitrators. At the time she did so, the state's attorney stated that she struck the five female arbitrators solely because she believed women, as a group, would necessarily be biased in favor of another woman who was claiming sexual harassment. Counsel for the state employee eliminated four males and one female arbitrator, all solely on grounds of specific bias or conflicts of interest. As a result, the panel was all male. When the panel ruled against the state employee on the merits of her case, she filed an action in an appropriate state court, challenging the panel-selection process as a gender- based denial of equal protection of the laws. In this case, how should the court rule as to the constitutionality of the panel-selection process?","choices":{"A":"Constitutional, because the gender classification used by the state's attorney in this case satisfies the requirements of the rational basis test.","B":"Constitutional, because the gender classification used by the state's attorney in this case satisfies the requirements of the strict scrutiny test.","C":"Unconstitutional, because the gender classification used by the state's attorney in this case denies the grievant the right to a jury made up of her peers.","D":"Unconstitutional, because the gender classification used by the state's attorney in this case does not satisfy the requirements of intermediate scrutiny."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives When a government action is challenged under the equal protection clause, it must survive intermediate scrutiny if it discriminates against a quasi-suspect class—ie, gender/sex, nonmarital birth. Intermediate scrutiny requires the state to prove that its action was substantially related to an important government interest. Here, the state's attorney removed female arbitrators from the panel (government action) because she believed that women would be biased in favor of another woman claiming sexual harassment (gender discrimination). But as the Supreme Court reasoned in J.E.B. v.\n\nAlabama ex rel. T.B., a juror's or arbitrator's competence and impartiality (important government interest) cannot be evaluated based on gender (no substantial relationship). Therefore, the attorney's actions cannot survive intermediate scrutiny and should be ruled unconstitutional. (Choice A) The rational basis test requires the challenger to prove that the government action has no rational relationship to a legitimate government interest. But since this test only applies when the government action does not affect a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class, it does not apply here. (Choice B) Strict scrutiny requires the state to prove that its action is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest when it substantially impacts a fundamental right or suspect class—not a quasi-suspect class (as seen here). (Choice C) Most criminal and civil litigants have the right to a jury made up of their peers— ie, an impartial group of citizens selected from a fair cross-section of the community. But this right does not apply to non-judicial proceedings like arbitration (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Gender discrimination in the jury (or arbitrator) selection process cannot survive intermediate scrutiny since it is not substantially related to the government's important interest in having grievances decided by competent and impartial individuals. Therefore, such discrimination violates the equal protection clause.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• J.E.B. v. Alabama ex rel. T.B., 511 U.S. 127, 136 (1994) (explaining that the government cannot rely on gender stereotypes to justify gender-based discrimination).","educationalObjective":"Gender discrimination in the jury (or arbitrator) selection process cannot survive intermediate scrutiny since it is not substantially related to the government's important interest in having grievances decided by competent and impartial individuals. Therefore, such discrimination violates the equal protection clause.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. XIV (equal protection clause).\n\n• J.E.B. v. Alabama ex rel. T.B., 511 U.S. 127, 136 (1994) (explaining that the government cannot rely on gender stereotypes to justify gender-based discrimination).","rule":"Gender discrimination in the jury (or arbitrator) selection process cannot survive intermediate scrutiny since it is not substantially related to the government's important interest in having grievances decided by competent and impartial individuals. Therefore, such discrimination violates the equal protection clause.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, how should the court rule as to the constitutionality of the panel-selection process?","rule":"Gender discrimination in the jury (or arbitrator) selection process cannot survive intermediate scrutiny since it is not substantially related to the government's important interest in having grievances decided by competent and impartial individuals. Therefore, such discrimination violates the equal protection clause.","analysis":"Equal protection\n\n(standards of review)\n\nStandard Applicability Test\n\nRational basis Default test Challenger must show\n\n• action is not rationally related\n• to legitimate government interest Intermediate Discriminates against quasi-suspect Government must show scrutiny class:\n• action is\n• Sex/gender substantially related\n• Legitimacy\n• to important government interest Strict scrutiny Discriminates against suspect class: Government must show\n\n• Ethnicity\n• action is necessary\n• Citizenship (state law only) (ie, least restrictive\n• Race means)\n• Nationality\n• to achieve Impacts fundamental right (First VIP): compelling government interest\n• First Amendment rights\n• Voting\n• Interstate travel\n• Privacy: family, marriage, parental rights, sexual acts, contraceptives When a government action is challenged under the equal protection clause, it must survive intermediate scrutiny if it discriminates against a quasi-suspect class—ie, gender/sex, nonmarital birth. Intermediate scrutiny requires the state to prove that its action was substantially related to an important government interest. Here, the state's attorney removed female arbitrators from the panel (government action) because she believed that women would be biased in favor of another woman claiming sexual harassment (gender discrimination). But as the Supreme Court reasoned in J.E.B. v.\n\nAlabama ex rel. T.B., a juror's or arbitrator's competence and impartiality (important government interest) cannot be evaluated based on gender (no substantial relationship). Therefore, the attorney's actions cannot survive intermediate scrutiny and should be ruled unconstitutional. (Choice A) The rational basis test requires the challenger to prove that the government action has no rational relationship to a legitimate government interest. But since this test only applies when the government action does not affect a fundamental right, suspect class, or quasi-suspect class, it does not apply here. (Choice B) Strict scrutiny requires the state to prove that its action is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest when it substantially impacts a fundamental right or suspect class—not a quasi-suspect class (as seen here). (Choice C) Most criminal and civil litigants have the right to a jury made up of their peers— ie, an impartial group of citizens selected from a fair cross-section of the community. But this right does not apply to non-judicial proceedings like arbitration (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Unconstitutional, because the gender classification used by the state's attorney in this case does not satisfy the requirements of intermediate scrutiny."},"questionZh":"州有州雇员申诉系统，要求任何希望对州提出申诉的州雇员将该申诉提交给由各方从 13 名全州仲裁员委员会中选出的 3 名仲裁员组成的小组，以获得最终解决方案。在任何特定案件中，申诉人和国家交替行使各自的权利，消除委员会的五名成员，留下由三名成员组成的小组来决定他们的案件。目前，整个委员会由七名男性仲裁员和六名女性仲裁员组成。一名女性国家雇员对其男性主管和国家提出性骚扰申诉。该州检察官行使了全部五次罢免，罢免了五名女仲裁员。当时，州检察官表示，她袭击五名女仲裁员只是因为她相信女性作为一个群体必然会偏向另一名声称受到性骚扰的女性。国家雇员的律师取消了四名男性和一名女性仲裁员，所有这些都仅仅是因为特定偏见或利益冲突。结果，小组成员全部是男性。当该小组根据该州雇员的案情做出不利于她的裁决时，她向相应的州法院提起诉讼，对小组的选择过程提出质疑，认为这是基于性别的拒绝法律平等保护。在这种情况下，法院应当如何判定专家组选举程序的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"符合宪法，因为州检察官在本案中使用的性别分类满足合理基础测试的要求。","B":"符合宪法，因为州检察官在本案中使用的性别分类满足了严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）测试的要求。","C":"违宪，因为州检察官在本案中使用的性别分类剥夺了申诉人获得由同龄人组成的陪审团的权利。","D":"违宪，因为州检察官在本案中使用的性别分类不符合中间审查的要求。"},"explanationZh":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 当政府行为根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑时，如果它歧视准可疑阶层（即性别、非婚生育），则必须通过中间审查。中间审查要求国家证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。在这里，州检察官将女性仲裁员从专家组中剔除（政府行动），因为她认为女性会偏向另一名声称性骚扰的女性（性别歧视）。但正如最高法院在 J.E.B. v.\n\n阿拉巴马州前亲属。 T.B.，陪审员或仲裁员的能力和公正性（重要的政府利益）不能根据性别（没有实质性关系）来评估。因此，律师的行为不能通过中间审查，应被裁定为违宪。 （选项A）合理基础测试要求挑战者证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有理性关系。但由于该测试仅适用于政府行为不影响基本权利、可疑类别或准可疑类别的情况，因此不适用于此处。 （选择 B）严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）要求国家证明，当其行动对基本权利或可疑阶层（而不是准可疑阶层）产生重大影响时，其行动对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。 （选项C） 大多数刑事和民事诉讼当事人有权获得由其同行组成的陪审团，即从社区中公平选出的公正的公民群体。但这项权利不适用于仲裁等非司法程序（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：陪审团（或仲裁员）选择过程中的性别歧视无法通过中间审查，因为它与政府让有能力和公正的个人决定申诉的重要利益没有实质关系。因此，这种歧视违反了平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• J.E.B. v. 阿拉巴马州前亲属。 T.B., 511 U.S. 127, 136 (1994)（解释说政府不能依靠性别陈规定型观念来证明基于性别的歧视的合理性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"陪审团（或仲裁员）选择过程中的性别歧视无法通过中间审查，因为它与政府让有能力和公正的个人决定申诉的重要利益没有实质关系。因此，这种歧视违反了平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种情况下，法院应当如何判定专家组选举程序的合宪性？","rule":"陪审团（或仲裁员）选择过程中的性别歧视无法通过中间审查，因为它与政府让有能力和公正的个人决定申诉的重要利益没有实质关系。因此，这种歧视违反了平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。","analysis":"平等保护\n\n（审查标准）\n\n标准适用性测试\n\n合理基础 默认测试 挑战者必须展示\n\n• 行动没有理性关联\n• 为了合法的政府利益 中级 歧视准嫌疑人 政府必须表现出审查级别：\n• 行动是\n• 性/性别显着相关\n• 合法性\n• 重要的政府利益 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny） 歧视可疑阶层：政府必须表明\n\n• 种族\n• 必须采取行动\n• 公民身份（仅限州法律）（即限制最少\n• 种族手段）\n• 国籍\n• 实现影响力基本权利（第一 VIP）：令人信服的政府利益\n• 第一修正案权利\n• 投票\n• 州际旅行\n• 隐私：家庭、婚姻、父母权利、性行为、避孕药具 当政府行为根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）受到质疑时，如果它歧视准可疑阶层（即性别、非婚生育），则必须通过中间审查。中间审查要求国家证明其行为与重要的政府利益密切相关。在这里，州检察官将女性仲裁员从专家组中剔除（政府行动），因为她认为女性会偏向另一名声称性骚扰的女性（性别歧视）。但正如最高法院在 J.E.B. v.\n\n阿拉巴马州前亲属。 T.B.，陪审员或仲裁员的能力和公正性（重要的政府利益）不能根据性别（没有实质性关系）来评估。因此，律师的行为不能通过中间审查，应被裁定为违宪。 （选项A）合理基础测试要求挑战者证明政府行为与合法政府利益没有理性关系。但由于该测试仅适用于政府行为不影响基本权利、可疑类别或准可疑类别的情况，因此不适用于此处。 （选择 B）严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）要求国家证明，当其行动对基本权利或可疑阶层（而不是准可疑阶层）产生重大影响时，其行动对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。 （选项C） 大多数刑事和民事诉讼当事人有权获得由其同行组成的陪审团，即从社区中公平选出的公正的公民群体。但这项权利不适用于仲裁等非司法程序（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：陪审团（或仲裁员）选择过程中的性别歧视无法通过中间审查，因为它与政府让有能力和公正的个人决定申诉的重要利益没有实质关系。因此，这种歧视违反了平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。第十四条（平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause））。\n\n• J.E.B. v. 阿拉巴马州前亲属。 T.B., 511 U.S. 127, 136 (1994)（解释说政府不能依靠性别陈规定型观念来证明基于性别的歧视的合理性）。","conclusion":"答案 D：违宪，因为州检察官在本案中使用的性别分类不符合中间审查的要求。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"impartiality","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˌɑɹʃiˈælɪti/","syllables":"im·par·tial·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 公平, 无私, 不偏；[法] 公正无私, 公平, 公正","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 公平, 无私, 不偏；[法] 公正无私, 公平, 公正","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":", a juror's or arbitrator's competence and impartiality (important government interest) cannot be evaluated based on gender (no substantial relationship).","synonyms":["nonpartisanship"]},{"term":"arbitration","ipa":"/ˌɑɹbɪtɹˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"ar·bi·tra·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 仲裁, 公断；[计] 仲裁","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 仲裁, 公断；[计] 仲裁","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:arbitrations","sourceSentence":"But this right does not apply to non-judicial proceedings like arbitration (as seen here).","synonyms":["arbitrament","arbitrement"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":233,"sourceQuestionPage":696,"sourceQuestionPages":[696],"sourceExplanationPages":[697,698],"sourceAnswerPages":[697,698],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-234","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Constitutional Law","question":"A state university adopted a new regulation prohibiting certain kinds of speech on campus. Students, staff, and faculty convicted by campus tribunals of violating the regulation were subject to penalties that included fines, suspensions, expulsions, and termination of employment. The regulation was widely unpopular, and there was a great deal of public anger directed toward the two tenured professors who had drafted and promoted it. The following year, the state legislature approved a severable provision in the appropriations bill for the university declaring that none of the university's funding could be used to pay the two professors, who were specifically named in the provision. In the past, the professors' salaries had always been paid from funds appropriated to the university by the legislature, and the university had no other funds that could be used to pay them. If the professors challenge the constitutionality of the appropriations provision, is the court likely to uphold the provision?","choices":{"A":"No, because it amounts to the imposition by the legislature of a punishment without trial.","B":"No, because it is based on conduct the professors engaged in before it was enacted.","C":"Yes, because the Eleventh Amendment gives the state legislature plenary power to appropriate state funds in the manner that it deems most conducive to the welfare of its people.","D":"Yes, because the full faith and credit clause requires the court to enforce the provision strictly according to its terms."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Prohibited legislation\n\nBill of attainder Prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment against clauses identified persons (or groups of persons) without a trial\n\nEx post facto Prohibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that: clauses\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof Article I of the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder—ie, legislative acts that inflict punishment (criminal or civil) on specified individuals or groups of individuals without a judicial trial. Here, the appropriations provision forbids the state university from paying two named professors. And since withholding the specified professors' salaries constitutes a civil punishment and no trial is afforded, the provision is an unconstitutional bill of attainder that the court will likely invalidate. (Choice B) The Article I ex post facto clauses prohibit laws that impose retroactive criminal punishments. Here, the state provision punishes the professors for conduct that occurred before the provision was adopted. But since withholding the professors' pay constitutes a civil punishment, the provision will not be struck down on this basis. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties (and foreign governments) from suing a state in federal court without the state's express consent. And though the Tenth Amendment gives states the power to establish laws for the welfare, health, and safety of their people, that power is not plenary (ie, absolute). (Choice D) The Article IV full faith and credit clause requires courts to recognize the valid judgments, legislative acts, and records of other states. But since the court is considering its own state law, this clause does not apply. Even if it did, a court is never required to enforce an unconstitutional law.\n\nEducational objective: Article I of the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder—ie, legislative acts that inflict punishment (civil or criminal) on specified individuals without a trial.\n\nReferences\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315–16 (1946) (holding that a legislative act that withheld the salaries of named government employees without a trial constituted a prohibited bill of attainder).","educationalObjective":"Article I of the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder—ie, legislative acts that inflict punishment (civil or criminal) on specified individuals without a trial.","references":"• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315–16 (1946) (holding that a legislative act that withheld the salaries of named government employees without a trial constituted a prohibited bill of attainder).","rule":"Article I of the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder—ie, legislative acts that inflict punishment (civil or criminal) on specified individuals without a trial.","irac":{"issue":"If the professors challenge the constitutionality of the appropriations provision, is the court likely to uphold the provision?","rule":"Article I of the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder—ie, legislative acts that inflict punishment (civil or criminal) on specified individuals without a trial.","analysis":"Prohibited legislation\n\nBill of attainder Prohibit legislative acts that inflict civil or criminal punishment against clauses identified persons (or groups of persons) without a trial\n\nEx post facto Prohibit enactment of retroactive criminal laws that: clauses\n• criminalize previously legal conduct\n• impose greater punishment than previously prescribed\n• eliminate previously available defenses\n• decrease prosecution's previous burden of proof Article I of the Constitution prohibits bills of attainder—ie, legislative acts that inflict punishment (criminal or civil) on specified individuals or groups of individuals without a judicial trial. Here, the appropriations provision forbids the state university from paying two named professors. And since withholding the specified professors' salaries constitutes a civil punishment and no trial is afforded, the provision is an unconstitutional bill of attainder that the court will likely invalidate. (Choice B) The Article I ex post facto clauses prohibit laws that impose retroactive criminal punishments. Here, the state provision punishes the professors for conduct that occurred before the provision was adopted. But since withholding the professors' pay constitutes a civil punishment, the provision will not be struck down on this basis. (Choice C) The Eleventh Amendment bars private parties (and foreign governments) from suing a state in federal court without the state's express consent. And though the Tenth Amendment gives states the power to establish laws for the welfare, health, and safety of their people, that power is not plenary (ie, absolute). (Choice D) The Article IV full faith and credit clause requires courts to recognize the valid judgments, legislative acts, and records of other states. But since the court is considering its own state law, this clause does not apply. Even if it did, a court is never required to enforce an unconstitutional law.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because it amounts to the imposition by the legislature of a punishment without trial."},"questionZh":"一所州立大学通过了一项新规定，禁止在校园内发表某些类型的言论。被校园法庭判定违反规定的学生、教职员工将受到包括罚款、停职、开除和终止雇佣关系在内的处罚。这项规定很不受欢迎，公众对起草和推广该规定的两位终身教授感到非常愤怒。第二年，州立法机关批准了大学拨款法案中的一项单独条款，宣布大学的任何资金都不能用于支付条款中特别提到的两位教授的工资。过去，教授的薪金一直是由立法机关拨给大学的经费支付，而大学没有其他资金可以用来支付教授的薪金。如果教授们质疑拨款条款的合宪性，法院是否可能支持该条款？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为这相当于立法机关未经审判就施加惩罚。","B":"不，因为它是基于教授在颁布之前所从事的行为。","C":"是的，因为第十一条修正案赋予州立法机关以其认为最有利于人民福祉的方式拨款国家资金的全权。","D":"是的，因为完整的信用条款要求法院严格按照其条款执行该条款。"},"explanationZh":"禁止的立法\n\n剥夺公民权利法案 禁止在未经审判的情况下对特定个人（或群体）施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为\n\n事后禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法： 条款\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 《宪法》第一条禁止剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判就对特定个人或群体实施惩罚（刑事或民事）的立法行为。在这里，拨款条款禁止州立大学向两名指定教授支付报酬。由于扣留特定教授的工资构成民事处罚，并且不会进行审判，因此该条款是一项违宪的剥夺公民权法案，法院很可能会宣布其无效。 （选项B） 第一条事后条款禁止法律追溯刑事处罚。在这里，国家规定对教授在该规定通过之前发生的行为进行惩罚。但由于扣留教授工资构成民事处罚，因此该规定不会因此而被废除。 （选项 C）第十一修正案禁止私人团体（和外国政府）在未经州明确同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。尽管第十修正案赋予各州为其人民的福利、健康和安全制定法律的权力，但该权力不是全权（即绝对）。 （选项D） 第四条充分诚信条款要求法院承认其他国家的有效判决、立法行为和记录。但由于法院正在考虑自己的州法律，因此该条款不适用。即使确实如此，法院也永远不需要执行违宪法律。\n\n教育目标：《宪法》第一条禁止剥夺公民权法案，即未经审判对特定个人施加惩罚（民事或刑事）的立法行为。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315-16 (1946)（认为未经审判扣留指定政府雇员工资的立法行为构成禁止剥夺公民权法案）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"《宪法》第一条禁止剥夺公民权法案，即未经审判对特定个人施加惩罚（民事或刑事）的立法行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果教授们质疑拨款条款的合宪性，法院是否可能支持该条款？","rule":"《宪法》第一条禁止剥夺公民权法案，即未经审判对特定个人施加惩罚（民事或刑事）的立法行为。","analysis":"禁止的立法\n\n剥夺公民权利法案 禁止在未经审判的情况下对特定个人（或群体）施加民事或刑事处罚的立法行为\n\n事后禁止颁布具有以下性质的追溯刑法： 条款\n• 将以前的合法行为定为刑事犯罪\n• 施加比先前规定更严厉的惩罚\n• 消除以前可用的防御措施\n• 减轻检方先前的举证责任 《宪法》第一条禁止剥夺公民权法案，即未经司法审判就对特定个人或群体实施惩罚（刑事或民事）的立法行为。在这里，拨款条款禁止州立大学向两名指定教授支付报酬。由于扣留特定教授的工资构成民事处罚，并且不会进行审判，因此该条款是一项违宪的剥夺公民权法案，法院很可能会宣布其无效。 （选项B） 第一条事后条款禁止法律追溯刑事处罚。在这里，国家规定对教授在该规定通过之前发生的行为进行惩罚。但由于扣留教授工资构成民事处罚，因此该规定不会因此而被废除。 （选项 C）第十一修正案禁止私人团体（和外国政府）在未经州明确同意的情况下在联邦法院起诉该州。尽管第十修正案赋予各州为其人民的福利、健康和安全制定法律的权力，但该权力不是全权（即绝对）。 （选项D） 第四条充分诚信条款要求法院承认其他国家的有效判决、立法行为和记录。但由于法院正在考虑自己的州法律，因此该条款不适用。即使确实如此，法院也永远不需要执行违宪法律。\n\n教育目标：《宪法》第一条禁止剥夺公民权法案，即未经审判对特定个人施加惩罚（民事或刑事）的立法行为。\n\n参考文献\n• United States v. Lovett, 328 U.S. 303, 315-16 (1946)（认为未经审判扣留指定政府雇员工资的立法行为构成禁止剥夺公民权法案）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为这相当于立法机关未经审判就施加惩罚。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"tenured","ipa":"/tˈɛnjɝd/","syllables":"tenured","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"终身的；长期保有的","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：终身的；长期保有的","wordForm":"词；原形 tenure；词形 0:tenure/1:p","sourceSentence":"The regulation was widely unpopular, and there was a great deal of public anger directed toward the two tenured professors who had drafted and promoted it.","synonyms":["land tenure","term of office","incumbency"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":234,"sourceQuestionPage":699,"sourceQuestionPages":[699],"sourceExplanationPages":[700,701],"sourceAnswerPages":[700,701],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-235","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state enacted a statute providing for the closure of the official state records of arrest and prosecution of all persons acquitted of a crime by a court or against whom criminal charges were filed and subsequently dropped or dismissed. The purpose of this statute is to protect these persons from further publicity or embarrassment relating to those state proceedings. However, this statute does not prohibit the publication of such information that is in the possession of private persons. A prominent businessman in the state was arrested and charged with rape. Prior to trial, the prosecutor announced that new information indicated that the charges should be dropped. He then dropped the charges without further explanation, and the records relating thereto were closed to the public pursuant to the state statute. A newspaper in the state conducted an investigation to determine why the businessman was not prosecuted but was refused access to the closed official state records. In an effort to determine whether the law enforcement agencies involved were properly doing their duty, the newspaper filed suit against appropriate state officials to force opening of the records and to invalidate the statute on constitutional grounds. Which of the following would be most helpful to the state in defending the constitutionality of this statute?","choices":{"A":"The argument that the rights of the press are no greater than those of citizens generally.","B":"The argument that the state may seal official records owned by the state on any basis its legislature chooses.","C":"The fact that the statute only prohibits public access to these official state records and does not prohibit the publication of information they contain that is in the possession of private persons.","D":"The fact that the statute treats in an identical manner the arrest and prosecution records of all persons who have been acquitted of a crime by a court or against whom criminal charges were filed and subsequently dropped or dismissed."},"answer":"C","explanation":"There is no constitutional right to access government information. Therefore, the government can deny the public (and media) access to official state records. But since there is a First Amendment right to publish truthful information, any government restriction on this right is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Therefore, the fact that the statute only prohibits public access to state records and does not prohibit the publication of information they contain would help the state defend the statute's constitutionality. (Choice A) The argument that the press has no greater rights than citizens generally, while true, merely shows that the same standard applies to claims by the press and ordinary citizens. But it does not explain why the newspaper's claim will fail. (Choice B) A state may seal its official records on any legal or constitutional basis—not any basis that its legislature chooses. (Choice D) The fact that the statute treats the records of all persons charged with, but not convicted of, a crime identically shows that the statute complies with the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But this fact will not help the state because it does not address the First Amendment issue—accessing and publishing those records.\n\nEducational objective: Although there is no right to access government information, there is a First Amendment right to publish truthful information. As a result, any government restriction on this right is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny.\n\nReferences\n• U.S. Const. amend. I (free press).\n\n• Houchins v. KQED, Inc., 438 U.S. 1, 14–15 (1978) (holding that there is no constitutional right to access government information).","educationalObjective":"Although there is no right to access government information, there is a First Amendment right to publish truthful information. As a result, any government restriction on this right is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny.","references":"• U.S. Const. amend. I (free press).\n\n• Houchins v. KQED, Inc., 438 U.S. 1, 14–15 (1978) (holding that there is no constitutional right to access government information).","rule":"Although there is no right to access government information, there is a First Amendment right to publish truthful information. As a result, any government restriction on this right is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following would be most helpful to the state in defending the constitutionality of this statute?","rule":"Although there is no right to access government information, there is a First Amendment right to publish truthful information. As a result, any government restriction on this right is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny.","analysis":"There is no constitutional right to access government information. Therefore, the government can deny the public (and media) access to official state records. But since there is a First Amendment right to publish truthful information, any government restriction on this right is unconstitutional unless it survives strict scrutiny—ie, the government proves that the restriction is necessary to achieve a compelling government interest. Therefore, the fact that the statute only prohibits public access to state records and does not prohibit the publication of information they contain would help the state defend the statute's constitutionality. (Choice A) The argument that the press has no greater rights than citizens generally, while true, merely shows that the same standard applies to claims by the press and ordinary citizens. But it does not explain why the newspaper's claim will fail. (Choice B) A state may seal its official records on any legal or constitutional basis—not any basis that its legislature chooses. (Choice D) The fact that the statute treats the records of all persons charged with, but not convicted of, a crime identically shows that the statute complies with the Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clause. But this fact will not help the state because it does not address the First Amendment issue—accessing and publishing those records.","conclusion":"Answer C: The fact that the statute only prohibits public access to these official state records and does not prohibit the publication of information they contain that is in the possession of private persons."},"questionZh":"某州颁布了一项法规，规定关闭所有被法院宣告无罪或被提起刑事指控但随后被撤销或驳回的人的逮捕和起诉的官方国家记录。该法规的目的是保护这些人免受与这些国家诉讼有关的进一步公开或尴尬。然而，该法规并不禁止公布私人拥有的此类信息。该州一名著名商人被捕并被指控强奸。审判前，检察官宣布新信息表明应撤销指控。然后，他在没有进一步解释的情况下撤销了指控，并且根据州法规，相关记录不向公众公开。该州的一家报纸进行了调查，以确定为什么这名商人没有被起诉，但被拒绝查阅封闭的官方记录。为了确定相关执法机构是否适当履行职责，该报对相关州官员提起诉讼，要求强制公开记录，并根据宪法废除该法规。以下哪一项最有利于国家捍卫该法规的合宪性？","choicesZh":{"A":"认为新闻界的权利并不高于一般公民的权利的论点。","B":"认为国家可以根据其立法机关选择的任何基础封存国家拥有的官方记录的论点。","C":"事实上，该法规仅禁止公众获取这些官方国家记录，并不禁止公布其中包含的私人拥有的信息。","D":"事实上，该法规以相同的方式对待所有已被法院宣告无罪或受到刑事指控但随后被撤销或驳回的人的逮捕和起诉记录。"},"explanationZh":"宪法没有规定获取政府信息的权利。因此，政府可以拒绝公众（和媒体）访问官方国家记录。但由于第一修正案规定了发布真实信息的权利，政府对这项权利的任何限制都是违宪的，除非它经过严格的审查——即政府证明这种限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。因此，该法规仅禁止公众获取国家记录，而不禁止公开其中包含的信息，这一事实将有助于国家捍卫该法规的合宪性。 （选项A） 媒体并不比一般公民拥有更大权利的论点虽然正确，但仅仅表明同样的标准适用于媒体和普通公民的主张。但它并没有解释为什么该报的主张会失败。 （选择 B）一个州可以根据任何法律或宪法基础（而不是其立法机关选择的任何基础）封存其官方记录。 （选项 D）该法规对所有被指控但未定罪的人的记录一视同仁，这一事实表明该法规符合第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但这一事实对国家没有帮助，因为它没有解决第一修正案的问题——访问和发布这些记录。\n\n教育目标：虽然没有获取政府信息的权利，但第一修正案赋予发布真实信息的权利。因此，政府对这项权利的任何限制都是违宪的，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。我（新闻自由）。\n\n• Houchins v. KQED, Inc., 438 U.S. 1, 14–15 (1978)（认为宪法没有获取政府信息的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"尽管没有获取政府信息的权利，但第一修正案赋予发布真实信息的权利。因此，政府对这项权利的任何限制都是违宪的，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项最有利于国家捍卫该法规的合宪性？","rule":"尽管没有获取政府信息的权利，但第一修正案赋予发布真实信息的权利。因此，政府对这项权利的任何限制都是违宪的，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。","analysis":"宪法没有规定获取政府信息的权利。因此，政府可以拒绝公众（和媒体）访问官方国家记录。但由于第一修正案规定了发布真实信息的权利，政府对这项权利的任何限制都是违宪的，除非它经过严格的审查——即政府证明这种限制对于实现令人信服的政府利益是必要的。因此，该法规仅禁止公众获取国家记录，而不禁止公开其中包含的信息，这一事实将有助于国家捍卫该法规的合宪性。 （选项A） 媒体并不比一般公民拥有更大权利的论点虽然正确，但仅仅表明同样的标准适用于媒体和普通公民的主张。但它并没有解释为什么该报的主张会失败。 （选择 B）一个州可以根据任何法律或宪法基础（而不是其立法机关选择的任何基础）封存其官方记录。 （选项 D）该法规对所有被指控但未定罪的人的记录一视同仁，这一事实表明该法规符合第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。但这一事实对国家没有帮助，因为它没有解决第一修正案的问题——访问和发布这些记录。\n\n教育目标：虽然没有获取政府信息的权利，但第一修正案赋予发布真实信息的权利。因此，政府对这项权利的任何限制都是违宪的，除非经过严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）。\n\n参考文献\n• 美国宪法。修正。我（新闻自由）。\n\n• Houchins v. KQED, Inc., 438 U.S. 1, 14–15 (1978)（认为宪法没有获取政府信息的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 C：事实上，该法规仅禁止公众获取这些官方国家记录，并不禁止公布其中包含的私人拥有的信息。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-f1cee4509ccc2d9c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":235,"sourceQuestionPage":702,"sourceQuestionPages":[702],"sourceExplanationPages":[703,704],"sourceAnswerPages":[703,704],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-236","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"Under a state law, a drug company that makes a false factual claim about a prescription drug is strictly liable in tort to any user of the drug. In an advertisement promoting sales of a particular drug, a drug company claimed that the drug was safe for children. Suit was filed against the company on behalf of a child who allegedly was harmed as a result of taking the drug. At the time the child took the drug, the available medical studies supported the company's claim that the drug was safe for children, but later research proved that the drug actually was harmful to children. The company has moved to dismiss the suit on First Amendment grounds. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because false or misleading commercial speech is not constitutionally protected.","B":"No, because the drug business is subject to extensive health and safety regulation.","C":"Yes, because liability cannot be imposed for false statements without a showing of actual malice.","D":"Yes, because the company's claims about the drug were a matter of public concern."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Commercial speech is speech that does no more than propose a commercial transaction (eg, advertisements). Although this type of speech is afforded some First Amendment protections, these protections do not extend to commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity—even if these statements are made without knowledge or reckless disregard of their falsity or illegality (ie, without actual malice) (Choice C). Here, the company's advertisement innocently claimed that its drug was safe for children. But this advertisement lost any First Amendment protections when later research proved that the company's drug safety claim was false. Therefore, the company's motion to dismiss the false advertisement suit on First Amendment grounds should be denied. (Choice B) The fact that the drug business is subject to extensive health and safety regulation does not exclude its speech from First Amendment protections. (Choice D) Commercial speech that is false or misleading (as seen here) or concerns unlawful activity never receives First Amendment protection, regardless of whether that speech pertains to a matter of public concern.\n\nEducational objective: The First Amendment does not protect commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity—regardless of whether the speaker knew (or recklessly disregarded) that the speech was false, misleading, or unlawful.\n\nReferences\n• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980) (explaining that false and misleading commercial speech is not protected by the First Amendment).","educationalObjective":"The First Amendment does not protect commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity—regardless of whether the speaker knew (or recklessly disregarded) that the speech was false, misleading, or unlawful.","references":"• Cent. Hudson Gas & Elec. Corp. v. Pub. Serv. Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980) (explaining that false and misleading commercial speech is not protected by the First Amendment).","rule":"The First Amendment does not protect commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity—regardless of whether the speaker knew (or recklessly disregarded) that the speech was false, misleading, or unlawful.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"The First Amendment does not protect commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity—regardless of whether the speaker knew (or recklessly disregarded) that the speech was false, misleading, or unlawful.","analysis":"Commercial speech is speech that does no more than propose a commercial transaction (eg, advertisements). Although this type of speech is afforded some First Amendment protections, these protections do not extend to commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity—even if these statements are made without knowledge or reckless disregard of their falsity or illegality (ie, without actual malice) (Choice C). Here, the company's advertisement innocently claimed that its drug was safe for children. But this advertisement lost any First Amendment protections when later research proved that the company's drug safety claim was false. Therefore, the company's motion to dismiss the false advertisement suit on First Amendment grounds should be denied. (Choice B) The fact that the drug business is subject to extensive health and safety regulation does not exclude its speech from First Amendment protections. (Choice D) Commercial speech that is false or misleading (as seen here) or concerns unlawful activity never receives First Amendment protection, regardless of whether that speech pertains to a matter of public concern.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because false or misleading commercial speech is not constitutionally protected."},"questionZh":"根据州法律，对处方药做出虚假事实声明的制药公司应对该药物的任何使用者承担严格的侵权责任。在推销某种药物的广告中，一家制药公司声称该药物对儿童是安全的。该公司代表一名据称因服用该药物而受到伤害的儿童提起诉讼。当孩子服用该药物时，现有的医学研究支持该公司声称该药物对儿童是安全的，但后来的研究证明该药物实际上对儿童有害。该公司已以第一修正案为由驳回诉讼。法院应该批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为虚假或误导性的商业言论不受宪法保护。","B":"不可以，因为药品行业受到广泛的健康和安全监管。","C":"是的，因为如果没有表现出真正的恶意，就不能对虚假陈述承担责任。","D":"是的，因为该公司有关该药物的声明引起了公众的关注。"},"explanationZh":"商业演讲是指仅提出商业交易的演讲（例如广告）。尽管此类言论受到第一修正案的一些保护，但这些保护不适用于 (1) 虚假或误导性或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论，即使这些言论是在不知情或不顾后果地无视其虚假性或非法性的情况下做出的（即没有实际恶意）（选项 C）。在这里，该公司的广告天真地声称其药物对儿童是安全的。但当后来的研究证明该公司的药品安全声明是错误的时，该广告就失去了第一修正案的任何保护。因此，该公司以第一修正案为由驳回虚假广告诉讼的动议应予驳回。 （选择 B）药品行业受到广泛的健康和安全监管这一事实并不排除其言论受到第一修正案的保护。 （选项 D）虚假或误导性的（如此处所示）或涉及非法活动的商业言论永远不会受到第一修正案的保护，无论该言论是否涉及公众关注的问题。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案不保护以下商业言论：(1) 虚假或误导性的，或 (2) 涉及非法活动的，无论演讲者是否知道（或鲁莽地忽视）该言论是虚假的、误导性的或非法的。\n\n参考文献\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980)（解释虚假和误导性商业言论不受第一修正案保护）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第一修正案不保护以下商业言论：(1) 虚假或误导性的，或 (2) 涉及非法活动的——无论演讲者是否知道（或鲁莽地忽视）该言论是虚假的、误导性的或非法的。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该批准该动议吗？","rule":"第一修正案不保护以下商业言论：(1) 虚假或误导性的，或 (2) 涉及非法活动的——无论演讲者是否知道（或鲁莽地忽视）该言论是虚假的、误导性的或非法的。","analysis":"商业演讲是指仅提出商业交易的演讲（例如广告）。尽管此类言论受到第一修正案的一些保护，但这些保护不适用于 (1) 虚假或误导性或 (2) 涉及非法活动的商业言论，即使这些言论是在不知情或不顾后果地无视其虚假性或非法性的情况下做出的（即没有实际恶意）（选项 C）。在这里，该公司的广告天真地声称其药物对儿童是安全的。但当后来的研究证明该公司的药品安全声明是错误的时，该广告就失去了第一修正案的任何保护。因此，该公司以第一修正案为由驳回虚假广告诉讼的动议应予驳回。 （选择 B）药品行业受到广泛的健康和安全监管这一事实并不排除其言论受到第一修正案的保护。 （选项 D）虚假或误导性的（如此处所示）或涉及非法活动的商业言论永远不会受到第一修正案的保护，无论该言论是否涉及公众关注的问题。\n\n教育目标：第一修正案不保护以下商业言论：(1) 虚假或误导性的，或 (2) 涉及非法活动的，无论演讲者是否知道（或鲁莽地忽视）该言论是虚假的、误导性的或非法的。\n\n参考文献\n• 分。哈德逊天然气与电力公司公司诉酒吧。服务。 Comm'n, 447 U.S. 557, 566 (1980)（解释虚假和误导性商业言论不受第一修正案保护）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为虚假或误导性的商业言论不受宪法保护。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"illegality","ipa":"/ˌɪliɡˈælɪti/","syllables":"il·le·gal·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 非法行为, 犯规；[法] 违法, 不合法, 非法","contextualMeaning":"在本题语境中指：n. 非法行为, 犯规；[法] 违法, 不合法, 非法","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:illegalities","sourceSentence":"Although this type of speech is afforded some First Amendment protections, these protections do not extend to commercial speech that (1) is false or misleading or (2) concerns unlawful activity—even if these statements are made without knowledge or reckless disregard of their falsity or illegality (ie, without actual malice) (Choice C).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-58c77a48304d1e8c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":236,"sourceQuestionPage":705,"sourceQuestionPages":[705],"sourceExplanationPages":[706,707],"sourceAnswerPages":[706,707],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-237","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Procedural Due Process","question":"A city ordinance requires a taxicab operator's license to operate a taxicab in the city. The ordinance states that the sole criteria for the issuance of such a license are driving ability and knowledge of the city's geography. Applicants are tested by the city for these qualifications with detailed questionnaires, written and oral examinations, and practical behind-the-wheel demonstrations. The ordinance does not limit the number of licenses that may be issued. It does, however, allow any citizen to file an objection to the issuance of a particular license, but only on the ground that an applicant does not possess the required qualifications. City licensing officials are also authorized by the ordinance to determine, in their discretion, whether to hold an evidentiary hearing on an objection before issuing a license. A woman applies for a taxicab operator's license and is found to be fully qualified after completing the usual licensing process. Her name is then posted as a prospective licensee, subject only to the objection process. A man who is already a licensed taxicab driver files an objection to the issuance of such a license to the woman solely on the ground that the grant of a license to her would impair the value of his existing license. He demands a hearing before a license is issued to the woman so that he may have an opportunity to prove his claim. City licensing officials refuse to hold such a hearing, and they issue a license to the woman. The man petitions for review of this action by city officials in an appropriate court, alleging that the Constitution requires city licensing officials to grant his request for a hearing before issuing a license to the woman. For whom should the court rule in this case?","choices":{"A":"City officials, because the licensing ordinance does not give the man any property interest in being free of competition from additional licensees.","B":"City officials, because the man had the benefit of the licensing ordinance and, therefore, may not now question actions taken under it.","C":"The man, because the determination of whether to hold a hearing may not constitutionally be left to the discretion of the same officials whose action is being challenged.","D":"The man, because the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment requires all persons whose property may be adversely affected by governmental action to be given an opportunity for a hearing before such action occurs."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Due process rights\n\nRight Applicability Examples\n\nLife Government deprives person of life\n• Execution\n\nLiberty Government significantly restrains person's\n• Incarceration physical freedom, exercise of fundamental\n• Commitment to mental rights, or freedom of choice institution\n• Loss of parental rights Property Government deprives benefit from\n• Suspension from public person legally entitled to receive it school\n• Termination of welfare benefits\n• Loss of public employment (not at will) Procedural due process gives persons the right to notice and a hearing when the government intentionally deprives them of a life, liberty, or property interest. Persons have a property interest in any benefit that they are legally entitled to receive—eg, specific benefits associated with a business or professional license. But a unilateral expectation of, or desire for, a benefit does not amount to a property interest. Here, the ordinance gives any citizen the right to object to the issuance of a license. And the man's license gives him the benefit of operating a taxicab in the city. But neither gives him a property interest in being free of competition from additional licensees (eg, the woman). Nor does his desire for that benefit create a protected interest. Therefore, the court should rule for the city officials and find that they were not required to grant a hearing. (Choice B) The licensing ordinance gave the man the benefit of a taxicab license. But that does not preclude him from challenging actions taken under it. Instead, he can challenge an attempt to deprive him of his license (not the woman's) on due process grounds or file an objection to the issuance of a particular license (like any other citizen). (Choice C) Had the man possessed a property interest, the determination of whether to hold a hearing could have been left to the discretion of the same officials whose action is being challenged. But those officials would have had to use the Mathews v. Eldridge balancing test when determining what process was due.\n\n(Choice D) Due process often requires an opportunity for a hearing when a government action deprives a person of his/her property interest. However, a government action that merely has an adverse effect on such an interest is not sufficient to trigger due process safeguards.\n\nEducational objective: The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires notice and a hearing before the government deprives a person of a life, liberty, or property interest. A property interest includes benefits a person is legally entitled to receive—not merely expecting or desiring.\n\nReferences\n• Barry v. Barchi, 443 U.S. 55, 64 (1979) (holding that the plaintiff had a property interest in his state-issued horse trainer's license).","educationalObjective":"The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires notice and a hearing before the government deprives a person of a life, liberty, or property interest. A property interest includes benefits a person is legally entitled to receive—not merely expecting or desiring.","references":"• Barry v. Barchi, 443 U.S. 55, 64 (1979) (holding that the plaintiff had a property interest in his state-issued horse trainer's license).","rule":"The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires notice and a hearing before the government deprives a person of a life, liberty, or property interest. A property interest includes benefits a person is legally entitled to receive—not merely expecting or desiring.","irac":{"issue":"For whom should the court rule in this case?","rule":"The Fourteenth Amendment due process clause requires notice and a hearing before the government deprives a person of a life, liberty, or property interest. A property interest includes benefits a person is legally entitled to receive—not merely expecting or desiring.","analysis":"Due process rights\n\nRight Applicability Examples\n\nLife Government deprives person of life\n• Execution\n\nLiberty Government significantly restrains person's\n• Incarceration physical freedom, exercise of fundamental\n• Commitment to mental rights, or freedom of choice institution\n• Loss of parental rights Property Government deprives benefit from\n• Suspension from public person legally entitled to receive it school\n• Termination of welfare benefits\n• Loss of public employment (not at will) Procedural due process gives persons the right to notice and a hearing when the government intentionally deprives them of a life, liberty, or property interest. Persons have a property interest in any benefit that they are legally entitled to receive—eg, specific benefits associated with a business or professional license. But a unilateral expectation of, or desire for, a benefit does not amount to a property interest. Here, the ordinance gives any citizen the right to object to the issuance of a license. And the man's license gives him the benefit of operating a taxicab in the city. But neither gives him a property interest in being free of competition from additional licensees (eg, the woman). Nor does his desire for that benefit create a protected interest. Therefore, the court should rule for the city officials and find that they were not required to grant a hearing. (Choice B) The licensing ordinance gave the man the benefit of a taxicab license. But that does not preclude him from challenging actions taken under it. Instead, he can challenge an attempt to deprive him of his license (not the woman's) on due process grounds or file an objection to the issuance of a particular license (like any other citizen). (Choice C) Had the man possessed a property interest, the determination of whether to hold a hearing could have been left to the discretion of the same officials whose action is being challenged. But those officials would have had to use the Mathews v. Eldridge balancing test when determining what process was due.\n\n(Choice D) Due process often requires an opportunity for a hearing when a government action deprives a person of his/her property interest. However, a government action that merely has an adverse effect on such an interest is not sufficient to trigger due process safeguards.","conclusion":"Answer A: City officials, because the licensing ordinance does not give the man any property interest in being free of competition from additional licensees."},"questionZh":"城市法令要求出租车运营商持有执照才能在城市运营出租车。该法令规定，颁发此类驾照的唯一标准是驾驶能力和对城市地理的了解。市政府通过详细的调查问卷、笔试和口试以及实际的驾驶演示来对申请人的资格进行测试。该条例不限制可颁发的许可证数量。然而，它确实允许任何公民对特定许可证的颁发提出反对，但只能以申请人不具备所需资格为理由。该法令还授权城市许可官员自行决定是否在颁发许可证之前就反对意见举行证据听证会。一名妇女申请了出租车运营商执照，并在完成通常的许可程序后被认为完全合格。然后，她的名字将作为潜在被许可人发布，仅接受异议程序。已经是有执照的出租车司机的男子提出反对向该女子颁发此类执照，其理由仅仅是向她颁发执照会损害他现有执照的价值。他要求在向该女子发放许可证之前举行听证会，以便他有机会证明他的主张。市执照官员拒绝举行此类听证会，但他们向该女子颁发了执照。该男子请求市政府官员在适当的法院审查这一行为，声称宪法要求市许可官员在向该女子颁发许可证之前必须批准他的听证会请求。本案法院该为谁作出裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"市政府官员，因为许可条例并未赋予该男子任何财产利益，使其免受其他许可人的竞争。","B":"市政府官员，因为该男子受益于许可条例，因此，现在可能不会质疑根据该条例采取的行动。","C":"这名男子，因为根据宪法，是否举行听证会的决定不能由其行为受到质疑的同一官员自行决定。","D":"这名男子，因为第十四修正案的正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求所有其财产可能受到政府行为不利影响的人在此类行为发生之前有机会举行听证会。"},"explanationZh":"正当程序权利\n\n正确的适用性示例\n\n生命政府剥夺人的生命\n• 执行\n\n自由政府极大地限制个人的\n• 监禁期间的人身自由、行使基本权利\n• 对精神权利或选择制度自由的承诺\n• 失去父母权利 财产政府剥夺了以下权利：\n• 暂停合法有权上学的公众资格\n• 福利金的终止\n• 失去公共就业（非自愿） 当政府故意剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产利益时，程序正当程序赋予人们通知和听证的权利。个人对其合法有权获得的任何利益拥有财产权益，例如与商业或专业执照相关的特定利益。但对利益的单方面期望或渴望并不等于财产利益。在这里，该法令赋予任何公民反对颁发许可证的权利。该男子的执照使他可以在城里经营出租车。但两者都没有给他带来不受其他被许可人（例如，那位女士）竞争的财产利益。他对这种利益的渴望也不会产生受保护的利益。因此，法院应作出有利于市政府官员的裁决，并裁定他们无需举行听证会。 （选项 B） 执照条例赋予该男子获得出租车执照的好处。但这并不妨碍他质疑根据该法案采取的行动。相反，他可以以正当程序为由对剥夺他的执照（而不是该妇女的执照）的企图提出质疑，或对颁发特定执照提出反对（像任何其他公民一样）。 （选择 C）如果该男子拥有财产权益，是否举行听证会的决定可以由其行为受到质疑的同一官员自行决定。但这些官员在确定应采取何种程序时，必须使用马修斯诉埃尔德里奇案的平衡测试。\n\n（选择 D） 当政府行为剥夺某人的财产权益时，正当程序通常需要举行听证会的机会。然而，仅对此类利益产生不利影响的政府行为不足以触发正当程序保障措施。\n\n教育目标：第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求政府在剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产利益之前发出通知并举行听证会。财产权益包括一个人合法有权获得的利益，而不仅仅是期望或渴望的利益。\n\n参考文献\n• Barry v. Barchi, 443 U.S. 55, 64 (1979)（认为原告在其国家颁发的驯马师执照中拥有财产权益）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求政府在剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产利益之前必须发出通知并举行听证会。财产权益包括一个人合法有权获得的利益，而不仅仅是期望或渴望的利益。","iracZh":{"issue":"本案法院该为谁作出裁决？","rule":"第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求政府在剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产利益之前必须发出通知并举行听证会。财产权益包括一个人合法有权获得的利益，而不仅仅是期望或渴望的利益。","analysis":"正当程序权利\n\n正确的适用性示例\n\n生命政府剥夺人的生命\n• 执行\n\n自由政府极大地限制个人的\n• 监禁期间的人身自由、行使基本权利\n• 对精神权利或选择制度自由的承诺\n• 失去父母权利 财产政府剥夺了以下权利：\n• 暂停合法有权上学的公众资格\n• 福利金的终止\n• 失去公共就业（非自愿） 当政府故意剥夺人们的生命、自由或财产利益时，程序正当程序赋予人们通知和听证的权利。个人对其合法有权获得的任何利益拥有财产权益，例如与商业或专业执照相关的特定利益。但对利益的单方面期望或渴望并不等于财产利益。在这里，该法令赋予任何公民反对颁发许可证的权利。该男子的执照使他可以在城里经营出租车。但两者都没有给他带来不受其他被许可人（例如，那位女士）竞争的财产利益。他对这种利益的渴望也不会产生受保护的利益。因此，法院应作出有利于市政府官员的裁决，并裁定他们无需举行听证会。 （选项 B） 执照条例赋予该男子获得出租车执照的好处。但这并不妨碍他质疑根据该法案采取的行动。相反，他可以以正当程序为由对剥夺他的执照（而不是该妇女的执照）的企图提出质疑，或对颁发特定执照提出反对（像任何其他公民一样）。 （选择 C）如果该男子拥有财产权益，是否举行听证会的决定可以由其行为受到质疑的同一官员自行决定。但这些官员在确定应采取何种程序时，必须使用马修斯诉埃尔德里奇案的平衡测试。\n\n（选择 D） 当政府行为剥夺某人的财产权益时，正当程序通常需要举行听证会的机会。然而，仅对此类利益产生不利影响的政府行为不足以触发正当程序保障措施。\n\n教育目标：第十四修正案正当程序条款（Due Process Clause）（Due Process Clause）要求政府在剥夺某人的生命、自由或财产利益之前发出通知并举行听证会。财产权益包括一个人合法有权获得的利益，而不仅仅是期望或渴望的利益。\n\n参考文献\n• Barry v. Barchi, 443 U.S. 55, 64 (1979)（认为原告在其国家颁发的驯马师执照中拥有财产权益）。","conclusion":"答案 A：市政府官员，因为许可条例并未赋予该男子任何财产利益，使其免受其他许可人的竞争。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":237,"sourceQuestionPage":708,"sourceQuestionPages":[708],"sourceExplanationPages":[709,710],"sourceAnswerPages":[709,710],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-238","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of Speech","question":"A man intensely disliked his neighbors. One night, intending to frighten them, he spray- painted their house with racial epithets and threats to kill them. The man was arrested and prosecuted under a state law providing that \"any person who threatens violence against another person with the intent to cause that person to fear for his or her life or safety may be imprisoned for up to five years.\" In defense, the man claimed that he did not intend to kill his neighbors, but only to scare them so that they would move away. Can the man constitutionally be convicted under this law?","choices":{"A":"No, because he was only communicating his views and had not commenced any overt action against the neighbors.","B":"Yes, because he was engaged in trespass when he painted the words on his neighbors' house.","C":"Yes, because his communication was a threat by which he intended to intimidate his neighbors.","D":"Yes, because his communication was racially motivated and thus violated the protections of the Thirteenth Amendment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Unprotected speech\n\n• Fighting words/threats\n• Inciting crime\n• Defamation\n• Obscenity Mnemonic: FIDO\n\nThe First Amendment free speech clause generally prohibits the government from regulating speech based on its content (ie, the message being communicated). But certain categories of speech fall outside First Amendment protection and can be freely regulated by the government. This includes fighting words and threats of violence that are intended to intimidate the recipient—regardless of whether the speaker acts on the threat or intends to carry it out (Choice A). And since the man threatened to kill his neighbors so that they would be scared and move away, he can be constitutionally convicted under the state law. (Choice B) The fact that a person engages in unlawful conduct (eg, trespassing) as an incident to speech has no bearing on whether the person can be constitutionally punished for the content of that speech. (Choice D) The Thirteenth Amendment prohibits both government and private actors from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude. But racially motivated threats (as seen here) do not violate this amendment.\n\nEducational objective: Threats of violence that are communicated with the intent to intimidate the recipient are not protected by the free speech clause—regardless of whether the speaker acts on the threat or intends to carry it out.\n\nReferences\n• Virginia v. Black, 538 U.S. 343, 359–60 (2003) (explaining that the First Amendment permits the government to ban true threats of violence).","educationalObjective":"Threats of violence that are communicated with the intent to intimidate the recipient are not protected by the free speech clause—regardless of whether the speaker acts on the threat or intends to carry it out.","references":"• Virginia v. Black, 538 U.S. 343, 359–60 (2003) (explaining that the First Amendment permits the government to ban true threats of violence).","rule":"Threats of violence that are communicated with the intent to intimidate the recipient are not protected by the free speech clause—regardless of whether the speaker acts on the threat or intends to carry it out.","irac":{"issue":"Can the man constitutionally be convicted under this law?","rule":"Threats of violence that are communicated with the intent to intimidate the recipient are not protected by the free speech clause—regardless of whether the speaker acts on the threat or intends to carry it out.","analysis":"Unprotected speech\n\n• Fighting words/threats\n• Inciting crime\n• Defamation\n• Obscenity Mnemonic: FIDO\n\nThe First Amendment free speech clause generally prohibits the government from regulating speech based on its content (ie, the message being communicated). But certain categories of speech fall outside First Amendment protection and can be freely regulated by the government. This includes fighting words and threats of violence that are intended to intimidate the recipient—regardless of whether the speaker acts on the threat or intends to carry it out (Choice A). And since the man threatened to kill his neighbors so that they would be scared and move away, he can be constitutionally convicted under the state law. (Choice B) The fact that a person engages in unlawful conduct (eg, trespassing) as an incident to speech has no bearing on whether the person can be constitutionally punished for the content of that speech. (Choice D) The Thirteenth Amendment prohibits both government and private actors from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude. But racially motivated threats (as seen here) do not violate this amendment.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because his communication was a threat by which he intended to intimidate his neighbors."},"questionZh":"一个人非常不喜欢他的邻居。一天晚上，为了吓唬他们，他在他们的房子上喷上了种族歧视的绰号，并威胁要杀死他们。该男子根据一项州法律被捕并受到起诉，该法律规定“任何人威胁对他人使用暴力，意图使他人担心自己的生命或安全，可能会被判处最高五年的监禁。”该男子辩解称，他无意杀死邻居，只是为了吓唬他们，让他们走开。根据该法律，该男子是否可以根据宪法被定罪？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为他只是表达自己的观点，并没有对邻居采取任何公开的行动。","B":"是的，因为当他在邻居的房子上画这些字时，他就犯了非法侵入罪。","C":"是的，因为他的通讯是一种威胁，他打算以此来恐吓邻居。","D":"是的，因为他的交流是出于种族动机，因此违反了第十三修正案的保护。"},"explanationZh":"不受保护的言论\n\n• 对抗言语/威胁\n• 煽动犯罪\n• 诽谤\n• 淫秽助记符：FIDO\n\n第一修正案言论自由条款一般禁止政府根据言论内容（即所传达的信息）来监管言论。但某些类别的言论不受第一修正案的保护，可以由政府自由监管。这包括旨在恐吓接受者的争斗言论和暴力威胁，无论说话者是否按照威胁采取行动或打算实施（选择 A）。由于该男子威胁要杀死他的邻居，让他们害怕并搬走，根据州法律，他可能会被宪法定罪。 （选择B）一个人作为言论的附带行为而从事非法行为（例如侵入）这一事实与该人是否可以因该言论的内容而受到宪法惩罚无关。 （选项 D） 第十三修正案禁止政府和私人行为者从事奴役或非自愿奴役。但出于种族动机的威胁（如此处所示）并不违反该修正案。\n\n教育目标：以恐吓接收者为目的而发出的暴力威胁不受言论自由条款的保护，无论说话者是否对威胁采取行动或打算实施。\n\n参考文献\n• Virginia v. Black, 538 U.S. 343, 359–60 (2003)（解释说第一修正案允许政府禁止真正的暴力威胁）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"旨在恐吓接受者的暴力威胁不受言论自由条款的保护——无论说话者是否对威胁采取行动或打算实施。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据该法律，该男子是否可以根据宪法被定罪？","rule":"旨在恐吓接受者的暴力威胁不受言论自由条款的保护——无论说话者是否对威胁采取行动或打算实施。","analysis":"不受保护的言论\n\n• 对抗言语/威胁\n• 煽动犯罪\n• 诽谤\n• 淫秽助记符：FIDO\n\n第一修正案言论自由条款一般禁止政府根据言论内容（即所传达的信息）来监管言论。但某些类别的言论不受第一修正案的保护，可以由政府自由监管。这包括旨在恐吓接受者的争斗言论和暴力威胁，无论说话者是否按照威胁采取行动或打算实施（选择 A）。由于该男子威胁要杀死他的邻居，让他们害怕并搬走，根据州法律，他可能会被宪法定罪。 （选择B）一个人作为言论的附带行为而从事非法行为（例如侵入）这一事实与该人是否可以因该言论的内容而受到宪法惩罚无关。 （选项 D） 第十三修正案禁止政府和私人行为者从事奴役或非自愿奴役。但出于种族动机的威胁（如此处所示）并不违反该修正案。\n\n教育目标：以恐吓接收者为目的而发出的暴力威胁不受言论自由条款的保护，无论说话者是否对威胁采取行动或打算实施。\n\n参考文献\n• Virginia v. Black, 538 U.S. 343, 359–60 (2003)（解释说第一修正案允许政府禁止真正的暴力威胁）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为他的通讯是一种威胁，他打算以此来恐吓邻居。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":238,"sourceQuestionPage":711,"sourceQuestionPages":[711],"sourceExplanationPages":[712],"sourceAnswerPages":[712],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-239","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A city zoning ordinance contains provisions restricting places of \"adult entertainment\" to two specified city blocks within the commercial center of the city. These provisions define \"adult entertainment\" as \"live or filmed nudity or sexual activity, real or simulated, of an indecent nature.\" An entrepreneur proposes to operate an adult-entertainment establishment outside the two-block area zoned for such establishments but within the commercial center of the city. When his application for permission to do so is rejected solely because it is inconsistent with the adult-entertainment provisions of the zoning ordinance, he sues the appropriate city officials, seeking to enjoin them from enforcing those provisions against him. He asserts that these provisions violate the First Amendment as made applicable to the city by the Fourteenth Amendment. In this case, is the court likely to hold that the adult-entertainment provisions of the zoning ordinance are constitutional?","choices":{"A":"No, because they prohibit in the commercial area of the city adult entertainment that is not \"obscene\" within the meaning of the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","B":"No, because zoning ordinances that restrict freedom of speech may be justified only by a substantial interest in preserving the quality of a community's residential neighborhoods.","C":"Yes, because adult entertainment of the kind described in these provisions is not protected by the free speech guarantee of the First and Fourteenth Amendments.","D":"Yes, because they do not prohibit adult entertainment everywhere in the city, and the city has a substantial interest in keeping the major part of its commercial center free of uses it considers harmful to that area."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The First Amendment free speech protections—applicable to local governments through the Fourteenth Amendment—extend to adult entertainment (as seen here) (Choice C). As a result, government regulation of such businesses is subject to constitutional review. The level of review depends on whether the restriction is either:\n• content-based (targets what is being said) – strict scrutiny requires that the regulation be necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• content-neutral (targets time, place, or manner of speech) – intermediate scrutiny requires that the regulation be narrowly tailored (ie, no greater than necessary) to achieve an important or substantial government interest and leave open ample alternative avenues of communication.\n\nIn Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., the Supreme Court held that zoning ordinances for adult-entertainment businesses (as seen here) target the secondary effects that these businesses have on the surrounding community—not the acceptability of their content. As a result, they are considered content-neutral restrictions subject to intermediate scrutiny. Here, the ordinance can pass intermediate scrutiny because it furthers the city's substantial interest in keeping its commercial center free of harmful uses. And it is no more restrictive than necessary to achieve that interest since it does not prohibit adult entertainment everywhere. Instead, it provides ample alternative avenues of communication by allowing these businesses in a two-block area within the city's commercial center. Therefore, the court is likely to hold that the ordinance is constitutional. (Choice A) Although the ordinance restricts adult entertainment that is not obscene—and therefore is protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments—that restriction is constitutional because it can survive intermediate scrutiny. (Choice B) Zoning ordinances that impose content-neutral restrictions on speech may be justified by any substantial government interest—not just preserving residential areas.\n\nEducational objective: Zoning ordinances affecting adult-entertainment businesses are content-neutral speech restrictions that are subject to intermediate scrutiny—ie, the ordinance must be (1) narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) provide ample alternative avenues of communication.\n\nReferences\n• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, 475 U.S. 41, 48–50 (1986) (applying intermediate scrutiny to a zoning ordinance prohibiting adult theaters in certain parts of the city since it targets the secondary effects of adult theaters).","educationalObjective":"Zoning ordinances affecting adult-entertainment businesses are content-neutral speech restrictions that are subject to intermediate scrutiny—ie, the ordinance must be (1) narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) provide ample alternative avenues of communication.","references":"• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, 475 U.S. 41, 48–50 (1986) (applying intermediate scrutiny to a zoning ordinance prohibiting adult theaters in certain parts of the city since it targets the secondary effects of adult theaters).","rule":"Zoning ordinances affecting adult-entertainment businesses are content-neutral speech restrictions that are subject to intermediate scrutiny—ie, the ordinance must be (1) narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) provide ample alternative avenues of communication.","irac":{"issue":"In this case, is the court likely to hold that the adult-entertainment provisions of the zoning ordinance are constitutional?","rule":"Zoning ordinances affecting adult-entertainment businesses are content-neutral speech restrictions that are subject to intermediate scrutiny—ie, the ordinance must be (1) narrowly tailored to serve a substantial government interest and (2) provide ample alternative avenues of communication.","analysis":"The First Amendment free speech protections—applicable to local governments through the Fourteenth Amendment—extend to adult entertainment (as seen here) (Choice C). As a result, government regulation of such businesses is subject to constitutional review. The level of review depends on whether the restriction is either:\n• content-based (targets what is being said) – strict scrutiny requires that the regulation be necessary and narrowly tailored (ie, the least restrictive means) to achieve a compelling government interest or\n\n• content-neutral (targets time, place, or manner of speech) – intermediate scrutiny requires that the regulation be narrowly tailored (ie, no greater than necessary) to achieve an important or substantial government interest and leave open ample alternative avenues of communication.\n\nIn Renton v. Playtime Theatres, Inc., the Supreme Court held that zoning ordinances for adult-entertainment businesses (as seen here) target the secondary effects that these businesses have on the surrounding community—not the acceptability of their content. As a result, they are considered content-neutral restrictions subject to intermediate scrutiny. Here, the ordinance can pass intermediate scrutiny because it furthers the city's substantial interest in keeping its commercial center free of harmful uses. And it is no more restrictive than necessary to achieve that interest since it does not prohibit adult entertainment everywhere. Instead, it provides ample alternative avenues of communication by allowing these businesses in a two-block area within the city's commercial center. Therefore, the court is likely to hold that the ordinance is constitutional. (Choice A) Although the ordinance restricts adult entertainment that is not obscene—and therefore is protected by the First and Fourteenth Amendments—that restriction is constitutional because it can survive intermediate scrutiny. (Choice B) Zoning ordinances that impose content-neutral restrictions on speech may be justified by any substantial government interest—not just preserving residential areas.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because they do not prohibit adult entertainment everywhere in the city, and the city has a substantial interest in keeping the major part of its commercial center free of uses it considers harmful to that area."},"questionZh":"城市分区条例包含将“成人娱乐”场所限制在城市商业中心内的两个指定城市街区的规定。这些条款将“成人娱乐”定义为“现场或拍摄的裸体或性活动，无论是真实的还是模拟的，具有不雅性质”。一位企业家提议在划定此类场所的两个街区之外、但在城市商业中心内经营一家成人娱乐场所。当他的这样做的许可申请仅仅因为不符合分区条例的成人娱乐规定而被拒绝时，他起诉相应的城市官员，试图禁止他们对他执行这些规定。他声称这些规定违反了通过第十四修正案适用于该城市的第一修正案。在这种情况下，法院是否可能认为分区条例中的成人娱乐条款符合宪法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为他们禁止在城市商业区进行非第一修正案和第十四修正案所指的“淫秽”的成人娱乐活动。","B":"不，因为限制言论自由的分区条例可能只有出于维护社区住宅区质量的重大利益才合理。","C":"是的，因为这些条款中描述的那种成人娱乐不受第一修正案和第十四修正案的言论自由保证的保护。","D":"是的，因为他们并不禁止城市各处的成人娱乐，而且该市对于保持其商业中心的主要部分不被用于它认为对该地区有害的用途有着重大利益。"},"explanationZh":"第一修正案的言论自由保护——通过第十四修正案适用于地方政府——延伸到成人娱乐（如此处所示）（选项 C）。因此，政府对此类企业的监管须接受宪法审查。审查级别取决于限制是否：\n• 基于内容（针对所说的内容）——严格的审查要求监管是必要的，并且是严格定制的（即限制性最少的手段），以实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 内容中立（针对时间、地点或言论方式）——中间审查要求对监管进行严格调整（即不超过必要的范围），以实现重要或实质性的政府利益，并留出充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n在 Renton 诉 Playtime Theatres, Inc. 案中，最高法院认为，成人娱乐企业的分区条例（如此处所示）针对的是这些企业对周围社区产生的次要影响，而不是其内容的可接受性。因此，它们被认为是内容中立的限制，需要接受中间审查。在这里，该法令可以通过中间审查，因为它进一步促进了该市在保持其商业中心免受有害用途方面的重大利益。而且它的限制并不比实现这种兴趣所需的限制更多，因为它并没有禁止任何地方的成人娱乐。相反，它允许这些企业在城市商业中心的两个街区内开展业务，从而提供了充足的替代沟通途径。因此，法院很可能认为该法令符合宪法。 （选项 A）尽管该法令限制非淫秽的成人娱乐，因此受到第一修正案和第十四修正案的保护，但该限制是符合宪法的，因为它可以通过中间审查。 （选择 B）对言论施加内容中立限制的分区条例可能是出于任何重大的政府利益——而不仅仅是保护住宅区。\n\n教育目标：影响成人娱乐业务的分区条例是内容中立的言论限制，需要接受中间审查，即该条例必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益，并且 (2) 提供充足的替代沟通途径。\n\n参考文献\n• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, 475 U.S. 41, 48–50 (1986)（对禁止在城市某些地区开设成人剧院的分区条例进行中间审查，因为它针对的是成人剧院的次要影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"影响成人娱乐业务的分区条例是内容中立的言论限制，需要接受中间审查，即该条例必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益，并且 (2) 提供充足的替代沟通途径。","iracZh":{"issue":"在这种情况下，法院是否可能认为分区条例中的成人娱乐条款符合宪法？","rule":"影响成人娱乐业务的分区条例是内容中立的言论限制，需要接受中间审查，即该条例必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益，并且 (2) 提供充足的替代沟通途径。","analysis":"第一修正案的言论自由保护——通过第十四修正案适用于地方政府——延伸到成人娱乐（如此处所示）（选项 C）。因此，政府对此类企业的监管须接受宪法审查。审查级别取决于限制是否：\n• 基于内容（针对所说的内容）——严格的审查要求监管是必要的，并且是严格定制的（即限制性最少的手段），以实现令人信服的政府利益或\n\n• 内容中立（针对时间、地点或言论方式）——中间审查要求对监管进行严格调整（即不超过必要的范围），以实现重要或实质性的政府利益，并留出充足的替代沟通渠道。\n\n在 Renton 诉 Playtime Theatres, Inc. 案中，最高法院认为，成人娱乐企业的分区条例（如此处所示）针对的是这些企业对周围社区产生的次要影响，而不是其内容的可接受性。因此，它们被认为是内容中立的限制，需要接受中间审查。在这里，该法令可以通过中间审查，因为它进一步促进了该市在保持其商业中心免受有害用途方面的重大利益。而且它的限制并不比实现这种兴趣所需的限制更多，因为它并没有禁止任何地方的成人娱乐。相反，它允许这些企业在城市商业中心的两个街区内开展业务，从而提供了充足的替代沟通途径。因此，法院很可能认为该法令符合宪法。 （选项 A）尽管该法令限制非淫秽的成人娱乐，因此受到第一修正案和第十四修正案的保护，但该限制是符合宪法的，因为它可以通过中间审查。 （选择 B）对言论施加内容中立限制的分区条例可能是出于任何重大的政府利益——而不仅仅是保护住宅区。\n\n教育目标：影响成人娱乐业务的分区条例是内容中立的言论限制，需要接受中间审查，即该条例必须 (1) 严格定制以服务于政府的重大利益，并且 (2) 提供充足的替代沟通途径。\n\n参考文献\n• Renton v. Playtime Theatres, 475 U.S. 41, 48–50 (1986)（对禁止在城市某些地区开设成人剧院的分区条例进行中间审查，因为它针对的是成人剧院的次要影响）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为他们并不禁止城市各处的成人娱乐，而且该市对于保持其商业中心的主要部分不被用于它认为对该地区有害的用途有着重大利益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-11044fa688539d23"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":239,"sourceQuestionPage":713,"sourceQuestionPages":[713],"sourceExplanationPages":[714,715],"sourceAnswerPages":[714,715],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-240","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state statute provides that assessments of real property for tax purposes must represent the \"actual value\" of the property. In one county of the state, the county tax commission, in making its assessments, has uniformly and consistently determined the \"actual value\" of real property solely by reference to the price at which the particular property was last sold. In recent years, the market values of real property in the county have been rising at the rate of 15% per year. An owner is required to pay real estate taxes on her home in the county that are 200% to 300% higher than those paid by many other owners of similar homes in similar neighborhoods in that county, even though the current market values of their respective homes and the owner's home are nearly identical. The reason the taxes on the owner's home are higher than those imposed on the other similar homes in similar neighborhoods is that she bought her home much more recently than the other owners and, therefore, it is assessed at a much higher \"actual value\" than their homes. Persistent efforts by the owner to have her assessment reduced or the assessments of the others raised by the county tax commission have failed. The owner has now filed suit against the county tax commission, claiming only that the comparative overvaluation of her property by the commission is unconstitutional. What is the owner's strongest argument to support her claim?","choices":{"A":"The tax assessment constitutes a taking of private property for public use without just compensation.","B":"The tax assessment constitutes an ex post facto law.","C":"The tax assessment deprives the owner of a privilege or immunity of national citizenship.","D":"The tax assessment deprives the owner of the equal protection of the laws."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Tax assessments that treat similarly situated property differently can be challenged under the equal protection clause. Since these assessments are generally subject to rational basis review, they must rationally relate to a legitimate state interest. Rough equality in implementing tax assessments will satisfy this standard of review. But intentional and substantial inequality will not. Here, the owner's real estate taxes are 200% to 300% higher than those paid by owners of similar homes (substantial inequality). And the county tax commission has repeatedly denied the owner's requests to reduce her assessment or raise the others' assessments (intentional inequality). Therefore, the owner's strongest argument is that the tax assessment deprives her of the equal protection of the laws. (Choice A) The Fifth Amendment prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. A taking occurs when the government (1) actually/physically appropriates the property or (2) extinguishes all economic benefits/uses of the property. But since the commission did neither, no taking occurred. (Choice B) The Constitution prohibits the enactment of ex post facto laws—ie, criminal legislation that has a retroactive and punitive effect. But the tax assessment is not an ex post facto law because it is civil in nature. (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment bars states from interfering with the narrowly defined privileges or immunities of national citizenship—none of which are affected here.\n\nEducational objective: Equal protection challenges to unequal tax assessments are subject to rational basis review. Although some inequality in tax assessments is permissible, intentional and substantial inequality is unconstitutional.\n\nReferences\n• Allegheny Pittsburgh Coal Co. v. Comm'r of Webster Cty., 488 U.S. 336, 345–46 (1989) (holding that an equal protection violation occurred when a tax commission intentionally and substantially undervalued similar property).","educationalObjective":"Equal protection challenges to unequal tax assessments are subject to rational basis review. Although some inequality in tax assessments is permissible, intentional and substantial inequality is unconstitutional.","references":"• Allegheny Pittsburgh Coal Co. v. Comm'r of Webster Cty., 488 U.S. 336, 345–46 (1989) (holding that an equal protection violation occurred when a tax commission intentionally and substantially undervalued similar property).","rule":"Equal protection challenges to unequal tax assessments are subject to rational basis review. Although some inequality in tax assessments is permissible, intentional and substantial inequality is unconstitutional.","irac":{"issue":"What is the owner's strongest argument to support her claim?","rule":"Equal protection challenges to unequal tax assessments are subject to rational basis review. Although some inequality in tax assessments is permissible, intentional and substantial inequality is unconstitutional.","analysis":"Tax assessments that treat similarly situated property differently can be challenged under the equal protection clause. Since these assessments are generally subject to rational basis review, they must rationally relate to a legitimate state interest. Rough equality in implementing tax assessments will satisfy this standard of review. But intentional and substantial inequality will not. Here, the owner's real estate taxes are 200% to 300% higher than those paid by owners of similar homes (substantial inequality). And the county tax commission has repeatedly denied the owner's requests to reduce her assessment or raise the others' assessments (intentional inequality). Therefore, the owner's strongest argument is that the tax assessment deprives her of the equal protection of the laws. (Choice A) The Fifth Amendment prohibits the government from taking private property for public use without just compensation. A taking occurs when the government (1) actually/physically appropriates the property or (2) extinguishes all economic benefits/uses of the property. But since the commission did neither, no taking occurred. (Choice B) The Constitution prohibits the enactment of ex post facto laws—ie, criminal legislation that has a retroactive and punitive effect. But the tax assessment is not an ex post facto law because it is civil in nature. (Choice C) The Fourteenth Amendment bars states from interfering with the narrowly defined privileges or immunities of national citizenship—none of which are affected here.","conclusion":"Answer D: The tax assessment deprives the owner of the equal protection of the laws."},"questionZh":"州法规规定，出于税收目的对不动产的评估必须代表该财产的“实际价值”。在该州的一个县，县税务委员会在进行评估时，仅参考特定财产最后出售的价格来统一一致地确定不动产的“实际价值”。近年来，该县房地产市场价值以每年15%的速度上涨。业主需要为其在该县的房屋缴纳房地产税，该税比该县类似社区的许多其他类似房屋业主所缴纳的房产税高出 200% 至 300%，尽管他们各自房屋的当前市场价值与业主的房屋几乎相同。业主的房屋所缴纳的税款高于对类似社区的其他类似房屋征收的税款的原因是，她比其他业主购买房屋的时间要晚得多，因此，该房屋的评估“实际价值”比他们的房屋高得多。业主一直试图减少她的评估或县税务委员会提高其他人的评估，但没有成功。业主现已对县税务委员会提起诉讼，仅声称该委员会对其财产的相对高估是违宪的。业主支持其主张的最有力论据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"税收评估构成了在没有公正补偿的情况下征用私人财产供公共使用。","B":"税收评估构成事后法律。","C":"税收评估剥夺了所有者的公民特权或豁免权。","D":"税收评估剥夺了所有者受到法律平等保护的权利。"},"explanationZh":"根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对类似财产进行不同对待的税收评估可能会受到质疑。由于这些评估通常要经过合理的基础审查，因此它们必须与合法的国家利益合理地相关。实施税收评估的大致平等将满足这一审查标准。但故意的和实质性的不平等则不会。在这里，业主缴纳的房地产税比类似房屋业主缴纳的房地产税高出200%至300%（严重不平等）。县税务委员会一再拒绝业主减少自己的评估或提高其他人的评估（故意不平等）的请求。因此，业主最有力的论据是，税收评估剥夺了她受到法律平等保护的权利。 （选项A） 第五修正案禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下将私有财产用于公共用途。当政府 (1) 实际/实际占用财产或 (2) 消除财产的所有经济利益/用途时，就会发生征用。但由于委员会两者都没有这样做，所以没有发生任何征用。 （选项B） 宪法禁止制定事后法律，即具有追溯力和惩罚性效果的刑事立法。但税收评估不是事后法律，因为它具有民事性质。 （选项 C）第十四修正案禁止各州干涉狭义定义的国民公民特权或豁免权——这些特权或豁免权在这里均不受影响。\n\n教育目标：对不平等税收评估的平等保护挑战进行合理的基础审查。尽管税收评估中存在一定程度的不平等是允许的，但故意和实质性的不平等是违宪的。\n\n参考文献\n• Allegheny Pittsburgh Coal Co. v. Comm'r of Webster Cty., 488 U.S. 336, 345–46 (1989)（认为当税务委员会故意大幅低估类似财产时，就会发生平等保护违规行为）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对不平等税收评估的平等保护挑战需要经过合理的基础审查。尽管税收评估中存在一定程度的不平等是允许的，但故意和实质性的不平等是违宪的。","iracZh":{"issue":"业主支持其主张的最有力论据是什么？","rule":"对不平等税收评估的平等保护挑战需要经过合理的基础审查。尽管税收评估中存在一定程度的不平等是允许的，但故意和实质性的不平等是违宪的。","analysis":"根据平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause），对类似财产进行不同对待的税收评估可能会受到质疑。由于这些评估通常要经过合理的基础审查，因此它们必须与合法的国家利益合理地相关。实施税收评估的大致平等将满足这一审查标准。但故意的和实质性的不平等则不会。在这里，业主缴纳的房地产税比类似房屋业主缴纳的房地产税高出200%至300%（严重不平等）。县税务委员会一再拒绝业主减少自己的评估或提高其他人的评估（故意不平等）的请求。因此，业主最有力的论据是，税收评估剥夺了她受到法律平等保护的权利。 （选项A） 第五修正案禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下将私有财产用于公共用途。当政府 (1) 实际/实际占用财产或 (2) 消除财产的所有经济利益/用途时，就会发生征用。但由于委员会两者都没有这样做，所以没有发生任何征用。 （选项B） 宪法禁止制定事后法律，即具有追溯力和惩罚性效果的刑事立法。但税收评估不是事后法律，因为它具有民事性质。 （选项 C）第十四修正案禁止各州干涉狭义定义的国民公民特权或豁免权——这些特权或豁免权在这里均不受影响。\n\n教育目标：对不平等税收评估的平等保护挑战进行合理的基础审查。尽管税收评估中存在一定程度的不平等是允许的，但故意和实质性的不平等是违宪的。\n\n参考文献\n• Allegheny Pittsburgh Coal Co. v. Comm'r of Webster Cty., 488 U.S. 336, 345–46 (1989)（认为当税务委员会故意大幅低估类似财产时，就会发生平等保护违规行为）。","conclusion":"答案 D：税收评估剥夺了所有者受到法律平等保护的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["conlaw-chart-07528abbab3c00cf"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":240,"sourceQuestionPage":716,"sourceQuestionPages":[716],"sourceExplanationPages":[717,718],"sourceAnswerPages":[717,718],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-241","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Equal Protection","question":"A state statute permits a person's name to appear on the general election ballot as a candidate for statewide public office if the person pays a $100 filing fee and provides proof from the State Elections Board that he or she was nominated in the immediately preceding primary election by one of the state's two major political parties. It also permits the name of an independent candidate or a candidate of a smaller party to appear on the general election ballot if that person pays a filing fee of $1,000 and submits petitions signed by at least 3% of the voters who actually cast ballots for the office of governor in the last state election. The state maintains that these filing requirements are necessary to limit the size of the election ballot, to eliminate frivolous candidacies, and to help finance the high cost of elections. Historically, very few of the state's voters who are members of racial minority groups have been members of either of the two major political parties. Recently, a new political party has been formed by some of these voters. Which of the following constitutional provisions would be most helpful to the new political party as a basis for attacking the constitutionality of this state statute?","choices":{"A":"The First Amendment.","B":"The Thirteenth Amendment.","C":"The Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"The Fifteenth Amendment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"State-election restrictions\n\nRestriction Examples Test\n\nOrdinary\n• Voter registration Rational basis: challenger must show\n• Photo-ID restriction lacks rational relationship (nondiscriminatory) to legitimate state interest requirement\n• Disallowing write-in voting Severe\n• Poll tax Strict scrutiny: state must show\n• Disallowing third- restriction is necessary to achieve (discriminatory) compelling state interest party candidacies\n• Property-ownership requirement States have the power to regulate their own elections. But electoral regulations (as seen here) must comply with two constitutional provisions:\n• First Amendment freedom of association – protecting the right to freely engage in group expression and\n• Fourteenth Amendment equal protection – requiring that similarly situated people be treated equally. If an electoral regulation is challenged under either constitutional provision, the standard for evaluating that regulation depends on whether the burden imposed is ordinary (triggering rational basis review) or severe (triggering strict scrutiny). And since the First Amendment is applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, the Fourteenth Amendment would be most helpful in attacking this state statute (Choice A). (Choice B) The Thirteenth Amendment prohibits both government and private actors from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude—neither of which is at issue here. (Choice D) The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits both state and federal governments from denying any citizen the right to vote on account of race, color, or previous condition of servitude. Here, the statute places a higher burden on independent and small-party candidates seeking ballot access—but it does not deny anyone the right to vote.\n\nEducational objective: State electoral regulations must comply with the First Amendment freedom of association (applicable through the Fourteenth Amendment) and Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clauses. The standard for reviewing such regulations depends on whether the burden imposed is ordinary (rational basis) or severe (strict scrutiny).\n\nReferences\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992) (explaining that the level of scrutiny of an election law depends on the extent to which it burdens First and Fourteenth Amendment rights).","educationalObjective":"State electoral regulations must comply with the First Amendment freedom of association (applicable through the Fourteenth Amendment) and Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clauses. The standard for reviewing such regulations depends on whether the burden imposed is ordinary (rational basis) or severe (strict scrutiny).","references":"• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992) (explaining that the level of scrutiny of an election law depends on the extent to which it burdens First and Fourteenth Amendment rights).","rule":"State electoral regulations must comply with the First Amendment freedom of association (applicable through the Fourteenth Amendment) and Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clauses. The standard for reviewing such regulations depends on whether the burden imposed is ordinary (rational basis) or severe (strict scrutiny).","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following constitutional provisions would be most helpful to the new political party as a basis for attacking the constitutionality of this state statute?","rule":"State electoral regulations must comply with the First Amendment freedom of association (applicable through the Fourteenth Amendment) and Fourteenth Amendment equal protection clauses. The standard for reviewing such regulations depends on whether the burden imposed is ordinary (rational basis) or severe (strict scrutiny).","analysis":"State-election restrictions\n\nRestriction Examples Test\n\nOrdinary\n• Voter registration Rational basis: challenger must show\n• Photo-ID restriction lacks rational relationship (nondiscriminatory) to legitimate state interest requirement\n• Disallowing write-in voting Severe\n• Poll tax Strict scrutiny: state must show\n• Disallowing third- restriction is necessary to achieve (discriminatory) compelling state interest party candidacies\n• Property-ownership requirement States have the power to regulate their own elections. But electoral regulations (as seen here) must comply with two constitutional provisions:\n• First Amendment freedom of association – protecting the right to freely engage in group expression and\n• Fourteenth Amendment equal protection – requiring that similarly situated people be treated equally. If an electoral regulation is challenged under either constitutional provision, the standard for evaluating that regulation depends on whether the burden imposed is ordinary (triggering rational basis review) or severe (triggering strict scrutiny). And since the First Amendment is applied to the states through the Fourteenth Amendment, the Fourteenth Amendment would be most helpful in attacking this state statute (Choice A). (Choice B) The Thirteenth Amendment prohibits both government and private actors from engaging in slavery or involuntary servitude—neither of which is at issue here. (Choice D) The Fifteenth Amendment prohibits both state and federal governments from denying any citizen the right to vote on account of race, color, or previous condition of servitude. Here, the statute places a higher burden on independent and small-party candidates seeking ballot access—but it does not deny anyone the right to vote.","conclusion":"Answer C: The Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"州法规允许某人的名字作为全州公职候选人出现在大选选票上，前提是该人支付了 100 美元的申请费，并提供州选举委员会的证明，证明他或她在前一次初选中被该州两个主要政党之一提名。它还允许独立候选人或小党候选人的名字出现在大选选票上，前提是该候选人支付 1,000 美元的申请费，并提交由至少 3% 的在上次州选举中实际为州长职位投票的选民签署的请愿书。该州坚持认为，这些备案要求对于限制选举选票的规模、消除无意义的候选人资格以及帮助资助高昂的选举成本是必要的。从历史上看，该州少数族裔选民中很少有是两大政党的成员。最近，其中一些选民组建了一个新的政党。以下哪项宪法条款最有利于新政党作为攻击该州法规合宪性的基础？","choicesZh":{"A":"第一修正案。","B":"第十三修正案。","C":"第十四修正案。","D":"第十五修正案。"},"explanationZh":"州选举限制\n\n限制示例测试\n\n普通\n• 选民登记 合理基础：挑战者必须表明\n• 照片身份限制与合法的国家利益要求缺乏理性关系（非歧视性）\n• 不允许写入投票 严重\n• 人头税 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：州政府必须表明\n• 禁止第三方限制对于获得（歧视性）令人信服的国家利益党候选人资格是必要的\n• 财产所有权要求 国家有权规范自己的选举。但选举法规（如此处所示）必须符合两项宪法规定：\n• 第一修正案结社自由 – 保护自由参与群体表达和参与的权利\n• 第十四修正案平等保护——要求同等对待处境相似的人。如果某项选举规定在任何一项宪法条款下受到质疑，评估该规定的标准取决于所施加的负担是普通（引发合理基础审查）还是严重（引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。由于第一修正案通过第十四修正案适用于各州，因此第十四修正案将最有助于攻击该州法规（选项 A）。 （选项 B）第十三修正案禁止政府和私人行为者从事奴役或非自愿奴役——这两者都不是这里的问题。 （选项 D）第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府因种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况而剥夺任何公民的投票权。在这里，该法规给寻求投票权的独立和小党候选人带来了更高的负担，但它并不否认任何人的投票权。\n\n教育目标：州选举法规必须符合第一修正案结社自由（通过第十四修正案适用）和第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。审查此类规定的标准取决于施加的负担是普通（合理基础）还是严重（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。\n\n参考文献\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992)（解释说选举法的审查程度取决于它对第一和第十四修正案权利的负担程度）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"州选举法规必须符合第一修正案的结社自由（通过第十四修正案适用）和第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。审查此类规定的标准取决于施加的负担是普通（合理基础）还是严重（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项宪法条款最有利于新政党作为攻击该州法规合宪性的基础？","rule":"州选举法规必须符合第一修正案的结社自由（通过第十四修正案适用）和第十四修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。审查此类规定的标准取决于施加的负担是普通（合理基础）还是严重（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。","analysis":"州选举限制\n\n限制示例测试\n\n普通\n• 选民登记 合理基础：挑战者必须表明\n• 照片身份限制与合法的国家利益要求缺乏理性关系（非歧视性）\n• 不允许写入投票 严重\n• 人头税 严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny）：州政府必须表明\n• 禁止第三方限制对于获得（歧视性）令人信服的国家利益党候选人资格是必要的\n• 财产所有权要求 国家有权规范自己的选举。但选举法规（如此处所示）必须符合两项宪法规定：\n• 第一修正案结社自由 – 保护自由参与群体表达和参与的权利\n• 第十四修正案平等保护——要求同等对待处境相似的人。如果某项选举规定在任何一项宪法条款下受到质疑，评估该规定的标准取决于所施加的负担是普通（引发合理基础审查）还是严重（引发严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。由于第一修正案通过第十四修正案适用于各州，因此第十四修正案将最有助于攻击该州法规（选项 A）。 （选项 B）第十三修正案禁止政府和私人行为者从事奴役或非自愿奴役——这两者都不是这里的问题。 （选项 D）第十五修正案禁止州和联邦政府因种族、肤色或先前的奴役状况而剥夺任何公民的投票权。在这里，该法规给寻求投票权的独立和小党候选人带来了更高的负担，但它并不否认任何人的投票权。\n\n教育目标：州选举法规必须符合第一修正案结社自由（通过第十四修正案适用）和第十四修正案平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。审查此类规定的标准取决于施加的负担是普通（合理基础）还是严重（严格审查（strict scrutiny）（strict scrutiny））。\n\n参考文献\n• Burdick v. Takushi, 504 U.S. 428, 433–34 (1992)（解释说选举法的审查程度取决于它对第一和第十四修正案权利的负担程度）。","conclusion":"答案 C：第十四修正案。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":241,"sourceQuestionPage":719,"sourceQuestionPages":[719],"sourceExplanationPages":[720,721],"sourceAnswerPages":[720,721],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-242","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Freedom of the Press","question":"A private university is owned and operated by a religious organization. The university is accredited by the department of education of the state in which it is located. This accreditation certifies that the university meets prescribed educational standards. Because it is accredited, the university qualifies for state funding for certain of its operating expenses. Under this funding program, 25 percent of the university's total operating budget comes from state funds. A professor at the university was a part-time columnist for the local newspaper. In one of her published columns, the professor argued that \"religion has become a negative force in society.\" The university subsequently discharged the professor, giving as its sole reason for the dismissal her authorship and publication of this column. The professor sued the university, claiming only that her discharge violated her constitutional right to freedom of speech. The university moved to dismiss the professor's lawsuit on the ground that the U.S. Constitution does not provide the professor with a cause of action in this case. Should the court grant the university's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the accreditation and partial funding of the university by the state are sufficient to justify the conclusion that the state was an active participant in the discharge of the professor.","B":"No, because the U.S. Constitution provides a cause of action against any state- accredited institution that restricts freedom of speech as a condition of employment.","C":"Yes, because the action of the university in discharging the professor is not attributable to the state for purposes of the Fourteenth Amendment.","D":"Yes, because the First and Fourteenth Amendments protect the right of the university to employ only individuals who share and communicate its views."},"answer":"C","explanation":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control Constitutional protections (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) only apply to government conduct. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is considered a government actor (and bound by the Constitution) when:\n• the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or\n• the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities (more than funding or regulating). Therefore, the professor can only sue the private university for violating her constitutional right to free speech if the state-action doctrine applies. The university does not perform a traditional and exclusive government function since education has long been undertaken by private entities. And the state's accreditation and funding of the university do not involve the state in the university's employment decisions. But even if they did, state accreditation, funding, or regulation—no matter how substantial—does not make private conduct state action (Choice A). And since the university's discharge of the professor is not attributable to the state for Fourteenth Amendment purposes, her lawsuit should be dismissed. (Choice B) The Constitution does not provide a cause of action against any state-accredited institution that restricts freedom of speech as a condition of employment. Instead, such constitutional claims can only be asserted against private institutions if the state-action doctrine applies. (Choice D) The First and Fourteenth Amendments generally bar a government actor from dismissing its employees for not sharing and communicating its views. But the professor could only sue the private university on these constitutional grounds if she could first establish that the university is a state actor.\n\nEducational objective: Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are treated as government actors when they perform a traditional government function or the government is significantly involved in their activities. But government accreditation, funding, or regulation—no matter how substantial—does not trigger this doctrine.\n\nReferences\n• Rendell-Baker v. Kohn, 457 U.S. 830, 841–43 (1982) (explaining that the state- action doctrine did not apply to a private school's conduct despite substantial government funding and regulations).","educationalObjective":"Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are treated as government actors when they perform a traditional government function or the government is significantly involved in their activities. But government accreditation, funding, or regulation—no matter how substantial—does not trigger this doctrine.","references":"• Rendell-Baker v. Kohn, 457 U.S. 830, 841–43 (1982) (explaining that the state- action doctrine did not apply to a private school's conduct despite substantial government funding and regulations).","rule":"Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are treated as government actors when they perform a traditional government function or the government is significantly involved in their activities. But government accreditation, funding, or regulation—no matter how substantial—does not trigger this doctrine.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the university's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Under the state-action doctrine, private actors are treated as government actors when they perform a traditional government function or the government is significantly involved in their activities. But government accreditation, funding, or regulation—no matter how substantial—does not trigger this doctrine.","analysis":"State-action doctrine\n\nTraditional\n• Private actor performs traditional & exclusive government government function function (eg, running elections)\n\nSignificant\n• Government & private actor have mutually beneficial government relationship (eg, joint activity or venture) involvement\n• Government creates nexus by affirmatively facilitating or authorizing private action (eg, police officer acting under color of law)\n• Government is pervasively intertwined in private entity's management or control Constitutional protections (excluding the Thirteenth Amendment) only apply to government conduct. But under the state-action doctrine, a private actor is considered a government actor (and bound by the Constitution) when:\n• the private actor performs a traditional and exclusive government function or\n• the government is significantly involved in the private actor's activities (more than funding or regulating). Therefore, the professor can only sue the private university for violating her constitutional right to free speech if the state-action doctrine applies. The university does not perform a traditional and exclusive government function since education has long been undertaken by private entities. And the state's accreditation and funding of the university do not involve the state in the university's employment decisions. But even if they did, state accreditation, funding, or regulation—no matter how substantial—does not make private conduct state action (Choice A). And since the university's discharge of the professor is not attributable to the state for Fourteenth Amendment purposes, her lawsuit should be dismissed. (Choice B) The Constitution does not provide a cause of action against any state-accredited institution that restricts freedom of speech as a condition of employment. Instead, such constitutional claims can only be asserted against private institutions if the state-action doctrine applies. (Choice D) The First and Fourteenth Amendments generally bar a government actor from dismissing its employees for not sharing and communicating its views. But the professor could only sue the private university on these constitutional grounds if she could first establish that the university is a state actor.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the action of the university in discharging the professor is not attributable to the state for purposes of the Fourteenth Amendment."},"questionZh":"私立大学由宗教组织拥有和运营。该大学获得其所在州教育部的认可。该认证证明该大学符合规定的教育标准。由于该大学获得了认可，因此有资格获得国家资助其某些运营费用。根据该资助计划，大学总运营预算的 25% 来自国家资金。该大学的一位教授是当地报纸的兼职专栏作家。这位教授在她发表的一篇专栏中指出，“宗教已经成为社会的消极力量”。该大学随后解雇了这位教授，并以解雇她的作者身份和发表本专栏为唯一理由。这位教授起诉该大学，仅声称她被开除侵犯了她的宪法言论自由权。该大学提出驳回教授的诉讼，理由是美国宪法没有为该教授提供在此案中的诉讼理由。法院是否应该批准大学的驳回动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为国家对大学的认可和部分资助足以证明国家是教授解职的积极参与者的结论。","B":"不可以，因为美国宪法规定了针对任何以限制言论自由作为就业条件的国家认可机构的诉讼理由。","C":"是的，因为根据第十四修正案，大学解雇教授的行为不应归咎于州政府。","D":"是的，因为第一修正案和第十四修正案保护大学仅雇用分享和传达其观点的个人的权利。"},"explanationZh":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍介入私人实体的管理或控制 宪法保护（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行为者被视为政府行为者（并受宪法约束）：\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能，或者\n• 政府大力参与私人行为者的活动（不仅仅是资助或监管）。因此，只有在国家行为原则适用的情况下，教授才能起诉私立大学侵犯了宪法赋予的言论自由权。由于教育长期以来由私人实体承担，大学不履行传统的、排他性的政府职能。而且国家对大学的认可和资助并不涉及国家参与大学的就业决策。但即使他们这样做了，国家认证、资助或监管——无论规模有多大——也不会使私人行为成为国家行为（state action）（选择A）。由于根据第十四修正案的规定，大学解雇该教授的行为不应归咎于州政府，因此她的诉讼应被驳回。 （选项B）宪法没有规定针对任何以限制言论自由作为就业条件的国家认可机构的诉讼理由。相反，只有在国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用的情况下，这种宪法主张才能针对私人机构。 （选项 D） 第一修正案和第十四修正案一般禁止政府行为者因不分享和沟通其观点而解雇其雇员。但如果教授能够首先证明该大学是国家行为者，她就只能根据这些宪法理由起诉该私立大学。\n\n教育目标：根据国家行为学说（state-action doctrine），当私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或政府大力参与其活动时，他们被视为政府行为者。但政府的认可、资助或监管——无论规模有多大——都不会引发这一学说。\n\n参考文献\n• Rendell-Baker v. Kohn, 457 U.S. 830, 841–43 (1982)（解释说，尽管有大量政府资助和监管，但国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）并不适用于私立学校的行为）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或政府大量参与其活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者。但政府的认可、资助或监管——无论规模有多大——都不会引发这一学说。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应该批准大学的驳回动议？","rule":"根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），当私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或政府大量参与其活动时，私人行为者被视为政府行为者。但政府的认可、资助或监管——无论规模有多大——都不会引发这一学说。","analysis":"国家行为学说（state-action doctrine）\n\n传统\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能（例如，进行选举）\n\n重大\n• 政府和私人参与者具有互惠互利的政府关系（例如联合活动或合资企业）参与\n• 政府通过积极促进或授权私人行动（例如，警察以法律名义行事）来建立联系\n• 政府普遍介入私人实体的管理或控制 宪法保护（不包括第十三修正案）仅适用于政府行为。但根据国家行为原则（state-action doctrine），在以下情况下，私人行为者被视为政府行为者（并受宪法约束）：\n• 私人行为者履行传统且专有的政府职能，或者\n• 政府大力参与私人行为者的活动（不仅仅是资助或监管）。因此，只有在国家行为原则适用的情况下，教授才能起诉私立大学侵犯了宪法赋予的言论自由权。由于教育长期以来由私人实体承担，大学不履行传统的、排他性的政府职能。而且国家对大学的认可和资助并不涉及国家参与大学的就业决策。但即使他们这样做了，国家认证、资助或监管——无论规模有多大——也不会使私人行为成为国家行为（state action）（选择A）。由于根据第十四修正案的规定，大学解雇该教授的行为不应归咎于州政府，因此她的诉讼应被驳回。 （选项B）宪法没有规定针对任何以限制言论自由作为就业条件的国家认可机构的诉讼理由。相反，只有在国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）适用的情况下，这种宪法主张才能针对私人机构。 （选项 D） 第一修正案和第十四修正案一般禁止政府行为者因不分享和沟通其观点而解雇其雇员。但如果教授能够首先证明该大学是国家行为者，她就只能根据这些宪法理由起诉该私立大学。\n\n教育目标：根据国家行为学说（state-action doctrine），当私人行为者履行传统的政府职能或政府大力参与其活动时，他们被视为政府行为者。但政府的认可、资助或监管——无论规模有多大——都不会引发这一学说。\n\n参考文献\n• Rendell-Baker v. Kohn, 457 U.S. 830, 841–43 (1982)（解释说，尽管有大量政府资助和监管，但国家行为原则（state-action doctrine）并不适用于私立学校的行为）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为根据第十四修正案，大学解雇教授的行为不应归咎于州政府。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":242,"sourceQuestionPage":722,"sourceQuestionPages":[722],"sourceExplanationPages":[723,724],"sourceAnswerPages":[723,724],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-conlaw-243","subject":"Constitutional Law","topic":"Takings Clause","question":"In order to combat terrorism, Congress enacted a statute authorizing the president to construct surveillance facilities on privately owned property if the president determines that the construction of such facilities is \"necessary to safeguard the security of the United States.\" The statute provides no compensation for the owner of the land on which such facilities are constructed and provides that the surveillance facilities are to be owned and operated by the United States government. Pursuant to this statute, the president has determined that the construction of a surveillance facility on a very small, unused portion of an owner's large tract of land is necessary to safeguard the security of the United States. The construction and operation of the facility will not affect any of the uses that the owner is currently making of the entire tract of land. The owner has filed suit to challenge the constitutionality of the construction of a surveillance facility on the portion of land at issue without compensation. How should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"The construction and operation of the facility would not interfere with any use the owner is currently making of the entire tract of land, and therefore the owner would not be entitled to any compensation.","B":"The construction of the facility would be a taking of the owner's property for which the owner must be compensated.","C":"The construction of the facility would be valid without any compensation, because it has been determined to be necessary to protect a compelling government interest in national security.","D":"The construction of the facility would single out the owner for adverse treatment in violation of the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nA physical taking occurs when the government permanently and physically occupies private property—regardless of the size of the property or how the owner has used it. And the Fifth Amendment takings clause bars the government from taking private property unless (1) the taking is for a public use and (2) the owner receives just compensation (ie, the property's fair market value). Here, the federal government seeks to construct and operate a surveillance facility on the owner's land (permanent and physical occupation) to combat terrorism (public use). Although the facility will only occupy a small, unused portion of the owner's land and will not interfere with his use of the rest of the land, the government's action constitutes a taking (Choice A). Therefore, the court should rule that the owner is entitled to just compensation. (Choice C) Almost all takings require just compensation—even when the taking is necessary to protect a compelling government interest (eg, national security). The only minor exception to this rule is an imminent public emergency. But since there is no indication that a terror attack is imminent, this exception does not apply. (Choice D) This government action may trigger the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment, which applies when the federal government treats similarly situated persons differently. But this component is not violated since the government action is rationally related to the government's legitimate interest in national security.\n\nEducational objective: The Fifth Amendment takings clause bars the government from taking—eg, permanently and physically occupying—private property for public use without just compensation, regardless of the size or use of the property.\n\nReferences\n• Loretto v. Teleprompter Manhattan Catv Corp., 458 U.S. 419, 434 (1982) (holding that a permanent, physical invasion of private property by the government always constitutes a taking).\n\n• 26 Am. Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 10 (2019) (explaining permanent and physical occupation).","educationalObjective":"The Fifth Amendment takings clause bars the government from taking—eg, permanently and physically occupying—private property for public use without just compensation, regardless of the size or use of the property.","references":"• Loretto v. Teleprompter Manhattan Catv Corp., 458 U.S. 419, 434 (1982) (holding that a permanent, physical invasion of private property by the government always constitutes a taking).\n\n• 26 Am. Jur. 2d Eminent Domain § 10 (2019) (explaining permanent and physical occupation).","rule":"The Fifth Amendment takings clause bars the government from taking—eg, permanently and physically occupying—private property for public use without just compensation, regardless of the size or use of the property.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule?","rule":"The Fifth Amendment takings clause bars the government from taking—eg, permanently and physically occupying—private property for public use without just compensation, regardless of the size or use of the property.","analysis":"Fifth Amendment takings\n\nType Attributes\n\nPhysical\n• Possession or permanent physical invasion of property\n\nRegulatory\n• Deprivation of all economically beneficial uses\n• Restricts use under totality of circumstances: – character of government action – economic impact – reasonable investment-back expectations Exaction\n• Condition to obtain building/development permit\n\nDestruction\n• Destruction of property or property interest (eg, easements, liens)\n\nA physical taking occurs when the government permanently and physically occupies private property—regardless of the size of the property or how the owner has used it. And the Fifth Amendment takings clause bars the government from taking private property unless (1) the taking is for a public use and (2) the owner receives just compensation (ie, the property's fair market value). Here, the federal government seeks to construct and operate a surveillance facility on the owner's land (permanent and physical occupation) to combat terrorism (public use). Although the facility will only occupy a small, unused portion of the owner's land and will not interfere with his use of the rest of the land, the government's action constitutes a taking (Choice A). Therefore, the court should rule that the owner is entitled to just compensation. (Choice C) Almost all takings require just compensation—even when the taking is necessary to protect a compelling government interest (eg, national security). The only minor exception to this rule is an imminent public emergency. But since there is no indication that a terror attack is imminent, this exception does not apply. (Choice D) This government action may trigger the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment, which applies when the federal government treats similarly situated persons differently. But this component is not violated since the government action is rationally related to the government's legitimate interest in national security.","conclusion":"Answer B: The construction of the facility would be a taking of the owner's property for which the owner must be compensated."},"questionZh":"为了打击恐怖主义，国会颁布了一项法规，授权总统在私人财产上建造监视设施，如果总统确定建造此类设施“对于维护美国的安全是必要的”。该法规没有为建造此类设施的土地所有者提供任何补偿，并规定监视设施应由美国政府拥有和运营。根据该法规，总统确定，为了保障美国的安全，有必要在所有者大片土地上一小块未使用的土地上建造监视设施。该设施的建设和运营不会影响业主目前对整片土地的任何用途。业主已提起诉讼，质疑在争议土地上无偿建造监控设施的合宪性。法院应该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"该设施的建设和运营不会干扰业主目前对整块土地的任何使用，因此业主无权获得任何补偿。","B":"该设施的建设将占用业主的财产，业主必须得到补偿。","C":"该设施的建设无需任何补偿即可有效，因为它被确定为保护政府在国家安全方面的重大利益所必需的。","D":"该设施的建设将针对业主进行不利待遇，违反了第五修正案的平等保护条款（Equal Protection Clause）（Equal Protection Clause）。"},"explanationZh":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当政府永久地、实际地占用私人财产时，无论财产的大小或所有者如何使用它，都会发生实物征用。第五修正案征用条款禁止政府征用私人财产，除非（1）征用是为了公共用途并且（2）所有者得到公正的补偿（即财产的公平市场价值）。在这里，联邦政府寻求在所有者的土地上（永久和实际占用）建造和运营监视设施，以打击恐怖主义（公共使用）。尽管该设施仅占用所有者土地的一小部分未使用的部分，并且不会干扰他对其余土地的使用，但政府的行为构成了征用（选项A）。因此，法院应判决业主有权获得公正的赔偿。 （选项C）几乎所有征用都需要补偿——即使征用是为了保护令人信服的政府利益（例如国家安全）所必需的。该规则唯一的小例外是迫在眉睫的公共紧急状态。但由于没有迹象表明恐怖袭击即将发生，因此该例外情况不适用。 （选项 D）这一政府行为可能会触发第五修正案的平等保护部分，该部分适用于联邦政府以不同方式对待处境相似的人的情况。但这并不违反这一组成部分，因为政府行为与政府在国家安全方面的合法利益合理相关。\n\n教育目标：第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用（例如永久实际占用）私人财产供公共使用，无论该财产的规模或用途如何。\n\n参考文献\n• Loretto v. Teleprompter Manhattan Catv Corp., 458 U.S. 419, 434 (1982)（认为政府对私有财产的永久、实际侵犯始终构成侵占）。\n\n• 上午26 点。尤尔。 2d 征用权 § 10 (2019)（解释永久和实际占领）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用（例如，永久地、实际占用）私人财产供公共使用，无论该财产的规模或用途如何。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该如何判决？","rule":"第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用（例如，永久地、实际占用）私人财产供公共使用，无论该财产的规模或用途如何。","analysis":"第五修正案征收\n\n类型属性\n\n物理征收\n• 占有或永久侵犯财产\n\n监管\n• 剥夺所有经济利益用途\n• 在总体情况下限制使用： – 政府行为的性质 – 经济影响 – 合理的投资支持预期 征收\n• 获得建筑/开发许可证的条件\n\n毁灭\n• 财产或财产权益的毁坏（例如地役权、留置权）\n\n当政府永久地、实际地占用私人财产时，无论财产的大小或所有者如何使用它，都会发生实物征用。第五修正案征用条款禁止政府征用私人财产，除非（1）征用是为了公共用途并且（2）所有者得到公正的补偿（即财产的公平市场价值）。在这里，联邦政府寻求在所有者的土地上（永久和实际占用）建造和运营监视设施，以打击恐怖主义（公共使用）。尽管该设施仅占用所有者土地的一小部分未使用的部分，并且不会干扰他对其余土地的使用，但政府的行为构成了征用（选项A）。因此，法院应判决业主有权获得公正的赔偿。 （选项C）几乎所有征用都需要补偿——即使征用是为了保护令人信服的政府利益（例如国家安全）所必需的。该规则唯一的小例外是迫在眉睫的公共紧急状态。但由于没有迹象表明恐怖袭击即将发生，因此该例外情况不适用。 （选项 D）这一政府行为可能会触发第五修正案的平等保护部分，该部分适用于联邦政府以不同方式对待处境相似的人的情况。但这并不违反这一组成部分，因为政府行为与政府在国家安全方面的合法利益合理相关。\n\n教育目标：第五修正案征用条款禁止政府在没有公正补偿的情况下征用（例如永久实际占用）私人财产供公共使用，无论该财产的规模或用途如何。\n\n参考文献\n• Loretto v. Teleprompter Manhattan Catv Corp., 458 U.S. 419, 434 (1982)（认为政府对私有财产的永久、实际侵犯始终构成侵占）。\n\n• 上午26 点。尤尔。 2d 征用权 § 10 (2019)（解释永久和实际占领）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该设施的建设将占用业主的财产，业主必须得到补偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已按截至 2026-08-02 的美国最高法院公开判例与 Constitution Annotated 进行规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续判例。","authorities":[{"title":"U.S. Supreme Court — Opinions of the Court, October Term 2025","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/slipopinion/25"},{"title":"Congress.gov — Constitution Annotated","url":"https://constitution.congress.gov/"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Consititution Law(1).pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":243,"sourceQuestionPage":725,"sourceQuestionPages":[725],"sourceExplanationPages":[726,727],"sourceAnswerPages":[726,727],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"}];